window.__QB=(window.__QB||[]);window.__QB.push("ll,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Horseshoe abscess\",\"e\":\"This option is an ischiorectal abscess that extends to both sides of the anal canal so forming a horseshoe shape.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intersphincteric abscess\",\"e\":\"Intersphincteric abscesses sit between the internal and external sphincters. They may not be visible as they can be completely contained within the anal canal. In this instance either digital rectal examination or examination under anaesthetic (EUA) is required for diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ischiorectal abscess\",\"e\":\"The ischiorectal (or ischioanal) fossae are prism-shaped fascia-lined potential spaces adjacent to the rectum. They are bordered by the levator ani muscle (superiorly and medially), the anal canal (medially), the perinea skin (inferiorly), and the ischial tuberosity (laterally). Ischiorectal abscesses are the second most common type of anorectal sepsis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Perianal abscess\",\"e\":\"These are most common. They are superficial, do not transverse the external sphincter but sit just under the skin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supralevator abscess\",\"e\":\"These are the least common, they form from an upward extension of an intersphincteric abscess into the supralevator space. They may also form from an intra-abdominal inflammation (eg appendicitis) tracking down.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old woman attends the clinic with a history of right upper quadrant pain. She has an ultrasound scan of the abdomen that reveals a normal liver, a common bile duct diameter of 5 mm and multiple gallstones within a contracted thick-walled gall-bladder.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding this patient?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A laparoscopic cholecystectomy should be recommended\",\"e\":\"Given the patient's ongoing symptoms and thick-walled appearance to the gall-bladder, cholecystectomy should be considered. Differentials for a thick-walled gall-bladder include the normal physiological state after food, cholecystitis (acute and chronic), infiltrative gall- bladder cancer, and liver conditions such as cirrhosis:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gallbladder carcinoma can be excluded\",\"e\":\"This condition cannot be fully excluded without biopsy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"She has acute cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Acute cholecystitis presents with right upper quadrant pain, fever and raised inflammatory markers. Murphy's sign is usually positive (a positive Murphy's sign is when pressure under the right costal margin on inspiration results in pain and the patient 'catching' their breath). There can be fluid surrounding the gall-bladder as part of the inflammatory process.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"She has Mirizzi's syndrome which is a gallstone that is eroding through the gallbladder wall into the ileum\",\"e\":\"Mirizzi's syndrome is obstructive jaundice caused by a large gallstone in Hartmann's pouch compressing the common bile duct. Mirizzi's syndrome is one of the indications to convert from laparoscopic cholecystectomy to an open procedure, often leaving Hartmann's pouch behind to fibrose.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The common bile duct is dilated\",\"e\":\"Six millimetres is the upper limit of normal for common bile duct diameter, and allowances of a few millimetres more may be made in elderly patients.\\n\\nA 30-year-old man with ulcerative colitis attends the coloproctology clinic following a Gastroenterology referral for restorative proctocolectomy\\n\\nWhich one of the following is most accurate regarding restorative proctocolectomy?\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old woman presents to the clinic with a lump in the groin. The lump is reducible and has a cough impulse. It is located above and medial to the pubic tubercle.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true about this type of hernia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true about this type of hernia?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"In women a direct hernia is more common than in men\",\"e\":\"Direct hernias are rare in women (man to woman ratio 10:1), they result from an acquired weakness of the wall of the inguinal canal formed by the transversalis fascia. Indirect hernias occur through the internal inguinal ring where there is an inherent weakness. Direct hernias occur usually in the elderly population, whereas indirect hernias can occur at any age and can be congenital.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Open repair can require the fixation of mesh to the reflected edge of the inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"An inguinal hernia emerges above and medial to the pubic tubercle, so in this patient we suspect an inguinal hernia. The inguinal canal in women contains the round ligament of the uterus and a remnant of the processus vaginalis. A persistent processus vaginalis in women forms the canal of Nuck. The floor of the inguinal canal is formed by the reflected edge of the inguinal ligament. As part of the Lichtenstein procedure the lower part of the mesh is fixed to this reflected edge of the inguinal ligament with the mesh above this positioned to reinforce the posterior wall of the canal\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Repair requires preservation of the round ligament\",\"e\":\"During repair the round ligament can be divided.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The diagnosis is that of an femoral hernia which is more common than an inguinal hernia in women\",\"e\":\"Femoral hernia accounts for 20% of hernias in women and 5% of hernias in men The man to woman ratio of inguinal hernia is 10 to 1.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The hernia can be repaired with a local anaesthetic block to L2 and L3\",\"e\":\"Inguinal hernia can be repaired by a local anaesthetic block that requires infiltration of the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves (T12 and L1), 2 cm medial from the anterior superior iliac spine, the genitofemoral nerve (L1 and L2), 1-2 cm lateral to the pubic tubercle and local infiltration of the subcutaneous tissues along the line of incision.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"A 55-year old man with cirrhosis of the liver due to chronic alcohol use presents to the acute surgical take. He has had fresh per rectum (PR) bleeding, which has now resolved, and is found to have a mass in the low rectum on PR.\\n\\nWhat would be the next step in his management?\",\"lead\":\"What would be the next step in his management?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Computerized tomography scan of the abdomen and pelvis\",\"e\":\"This is not the best initial diagnostic investigation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Capsule endoscopy\",\"e\":\"Capsule endoscopy is a useful investigation to image the small intestines, however for a low colonic mass a different endoluminal investigation would be far more useful.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Colonoscopy\",\"e\":\"The differential here is rectal neoplasm, polyp and varices. The best investigation would be an endoluminal one, ie colonoscopy. Biopsies should only be considered if the mass is not of a vascular nature as attempting to biopsy a varices would likely lead to heavy bleeding. In patients that are unfit for colonoscopy axial imaging may be considered.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Low anterior resection\",\"e\":\"This may be the treatment of choice, for example if a malignancy is diagnosed. It is not. however, a diagnostic investigation\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the pelvis\",\"e\":\"This is part of the imaging required for the local staging of rectal cancers, it is not the best initial diagnostic investigation.\\n\\nAlong with the differentials of rectal neoplasm or polyp, this patient's history of cirrhosis means that varices must also be considered on the differential list. Common sites for varices are as follows:\\n\\n• In the gastro-oesophageal region, the oesophageal tributaries of the left gastric vein anastomose with the oesophageal veins, which empty into the azygos vein. • In the paraumbilical region the paraumbilical veins in the falciform ligament anastomose with the subcutaneous veins in the anterior abdominal wall. • In the anorectal region the superior rectal vein anastomoses with the middle and inferior rectal veins, which are anastomoses of the internal iliac and internal pudendal veins.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"A 2-week-old boy presents with projectile vomiting, palpable epigastric mass and failure to thrive. He is diagnosed with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and undergoes Ramstedt's pyloromyotomy.\\n\\nWhich one is the deepest layer of the pyloric wall to be divided?\",\"lead\":\"Which one is the deepest layer of the pyloric wall to be divided?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Circular muscle\",\"e\":\"The circular muscle is the last layer to be divided. Ramstedt's pyloromyotomy is indicated in hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. An incision is made along the length of the hypertrophic muscle, and the layers of longitudinal and circular muscle are divided until the mucosa is exposed.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Longitudinal muscle\",\"e\":\"The longitudinal muscle sits outside the circular muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mucosa\",\"e\":\"The mucosa is left intact.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Parietal peritnoneum\",\"e\":\"The peritoneum is divided to gain access to the greater curvature of the stomach.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serosal surface\",\"e\":\"The serosa is cut through before the muscular layers being divided.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old presents with a two-week history of diarrhoea, abdominal pain and fever. Examination reveals generalised peritonitis. He undergoes emergency laparotomy, during which several intestinal perforations are noted and treated with resection. The pathology report confirms the presence of Salmonella typhi within Peyer's patches.\\n\\nWhere is the most likely site of his perforation?\",\"lead\":\"Where is the most likely site of his perforation?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Duodenum\",\"e\":\"Peyer's patches are mostly found within the ileum, therefore this answer is incorrect.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastric body\",\"e\":\"The patient is most likely to be infected with Saimonella typhi, which accumulates in the ileum, not the gastric body.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ileum\",\"e\":\"This patient has typhoid fever, caused by Salmonella typhi. The bacterium accumulates within Peyer's patches and causes mucosal ulceration. Treatment is usually with oral antibiotics, typically ciprofloxacin, although multi-drug resistant strains do exist so sensitivity analysis should be completed. Left untreated, these ulcers can perforate and cause secondary peritonitis. The ileum has the highest concentration of Peyer's patches within the gastrointestinal tract.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Jejunum\",\"e\":\"Peyer's patches are mostly found within the ileum, therefore this answer is incorrect.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sigmoid colon\",\"e\":\"Infiltration of the Peyer's patches causes inflammation and subsequent narrowing of the ileum, where Peyer's patches are located.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":60,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman attends the Coloproctology Clinic with a 4-month history of intermittent, painful, fresh per rectum (PR) bleeding: She is found to have anal spasm and a chronic anal fissure.\\n\\nWhat will be the most appropriate first line management?\",\"lead\":\"What will be the most appropriate first line management?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Advancement flap\",\"e\":\"An advancement flap is considered in low pressure anal fissure, but is not first-line treatment\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Examination under anaesthesia and Botulinum A toxin\",\"e\":\"Examination under anaesthesia is not usually indicated for primary anal fissure - it may, however, be necessary in elderly patients or other groups in whom a secondary cause is suspected. Additionally, in the paediatric group if abuse is considered, a possible cause examination under anaesthesia (EUA) may be indicated. Botulinum toxin is a useful treatment causing relaxation of the sphincter, improving blood flow and therefore healing time, but is usually considered after other topical treatments have failed.\\n\\nWhilst a high fibre diet is important in both prevention and treatment of primary anal fissure it is not thought to offer much benefit in the treatment of chronic anal fissures. Additionally a flexible sigmoidoscopy is not usually a first-line measure in young patients when primary anal fissure is suspected. However, in elderly patients, secondary causes such as anal or rectal cancer are more likely and further investigation may be indicated.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Flexible sigmoidoscopy and high fibre diet\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lateral sphincterotomy\",\"e\":\"Lateral sphincterotomy can be considered should topical treatments or Botox management not prove successful. The aim is to reduce the pressure of the sphincter, which aids blood flow to the area and therefore healing.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"2% Diltiazem ointment\",\"e\":\"The accepted treatment protocol for anal fissures is firstly topical 0.2% glyceryl trinitrate (GTN) or 2% Diitiazem followed by Botox and then lateral sphincterotomy or advancement flap. The latter is considered in women with low pressure anal fissure. Anterior anal fissures are commoner in women due to the lack of support to the anal canal anteriorly, but posterior fissures are most common overall.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old woman presents to casualty with severe upper abdominal pain. On questioning she admits to several similar episoces of upper abdominal pain radiating to her back over the past 4 years that have all required admission. On examination, she is in in severe pain and looks thin and unkempt. A diagnosis of chronic pancreatitis is made.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is the most likely atiology in this woman?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is the most likely atiology in this woman?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alcohol\",\"e\":\"The most common cause of chronic pancreatitist is chronic excess alcoholt\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Cholelithiasis\",\"e\":\"Cholelithiasis (gallstones) and choledocholithiasis (gallstones in the common bile duct) are common causes of acute pancreatitis, which with alcohol accounts for approximately 80% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypocalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypocalcaemia is a complication of acute pancreatitis, as opposed to hypercalcaemia which is a cause.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypothermia\",\"e\":\"Hypothermia is a recognised cause of acute pancreatitis but alcohol is the most likely cause in this scenario.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Mumps\",\"e\":\"Viral infections such as mumps are also well recognised causes of acute pancreatitis, but alcohol is the most likely cause in this scenario.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with profuse hematemesis. She is a heavy crinker and has been for several years. On examination, she appears confused and has obvious ascites.\\n\\nWhich one of the following procedures should be completed first in her management?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following procedures should be completed first in her management?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Administration of vasoactive drugs such as somatostatin or octreotide\",\"e\":\"If urgent endoscopy is available then this should occur as soon as possible in a patient with active variceal bleeding. If not, then vasoactive drugs such as somatostatin, octreotide or Glypressin should be considered. These would require an IV line to be placed to be administered.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Balloon tamponade using a Sengstaken-Blakemore tube\",\"e\":\"Balloon tamponade is considered normally only if band ligation or sclerotherapy fails to adequately control the patient's variceal haemorrhage, or in an emergency setting when endoscopy is not available.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Performing TIPS procedure as an emergency\",\"e\":\"Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) procedure can be used to reduce portal pressure by diverting portal blood flow into the hepatic vein and may be used if other methods firstly cannot control bleeding:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Performing an urgent oesophago-gastroduodenoscopy (OGD) and endoscopic band ligation\",\"e\":\"In patients with variceal bleeding this procedure is the most important immediate definitive management, however it does not supersede a basic ABCDE (Airway maintenance with cervical spine protection, Breathing and ventilation, Circulation with haemorrhage control, Disability: neurological status, Exposure/environmental control) assessment and resuscitation of the patient.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Securing IV access\",\"e\":\"The clinical vignette suggests a patient with decompensated liver disease (ascites. confusion) as a result of heavy drinking. The suspicion must therefore be that this patient has presented with a variceal haemorrhage. Oesophageal varices may present with profuse haematemesis and this patient should be initially approached with an ABCDE assessment. As part of this, IV access should be secured and bloods sent including an urgent crossmatch.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"You are seeing a patient in clinic with complete rectal prolapse, and she asks about the potential complications of conservative treatment of this condition. Which one of the following statements would you tell her is the commonest?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements would you tell her is the commonest?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Incarceration\",\"e\":\"Incarceration is a complication of rectal prolapse, however, it is not the commonest of the correct options listed here.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Incontinence\",\"e\":\"The commonest of the correct options listed here is faecal incontinence (75%), which may actually be the patient's presenting symptom. Alternatively constipation can very commonly be either the presenting symptom or a complication. Rectal prolapse is a condition that most commonly affects older women, but can occur in any age. Partial rectal prolapse (prolapse of the mucosal layer only) occurs more commonly in children while complete rectal prolapse (prolapse of all layers of the rectum) is typical in elderly women. Patients may present because they have felt/noticed the prolapse, often while straining to pass stool. Additionally they may present with symptoms such as incontinence, constipation, per rectal (PR) bleeding or pain. Diagnosis may be simple with the prolapse visualised either at rest or on straining/performing the Valsalva manoeuvre, Imaging may be required in some patients, for example an evacuating proctogram.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Rectal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Occasionally a prolapse can be a presenting feature of rectal carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Strangulation\",\"e\":\"This is a complication of rectal prolapse that requires emergency surgery. It presents with a painful non-reducible prolapse. It is not the most common of the options listed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulceration\",\"e\":\"Ulceration and necrosis of the rectal wall are common complications, albeit not the most common listed option.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"An 18-month-old child is brought to see you in clinic as his mother has noted mucus and blood staining on nappies, and has seen a bright red mass interm ttently protruding from his anus. You diagnose a partial rectal prolapse.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding partial rectal prolapses?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding partial rectal prolapses?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It involves all layers of the rectal waif\",\"e\":\"Complete rectal prolapse involves all layers of the rectal wall and is more commonly seen in elderly women. Partial rectal prolapse is more common in children and involves the mucosal layer only.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is associated with a significant increased risk of rectal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"There is an associated risk of rectal carcinoma in adults with complete rectal prolapse, but this risk has not been reported in children with partial rectal prolapse, it may be more commonly associated with cystic fibrosis, Ehlers-Danlos or Hirschsprung's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is more common in adults than children\",\"e\":\"In adults complete rectal prolapse is more common, in children partial rectal prolapse is more common.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It should be treated with Delorme's procedure\",\"e\":\"Delorme's procedure is excision of the mucosal component of a prolapse with plication of the muscle from below, and is generally used in adults.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The majority resolves with conservative treatment\",\"e\":\"Most cases of partial rectal prolapse in children resolve with conservative treatment.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man from Uganda presents with a high perianal fistula. He is a non-smoker who has had a chronic cough for 3 months and has noticed weight loss of 2 stone in 6\\n\\nWhat is the most likely associated condition leading to this high anal fistula?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely associated condition leading to this high anal fistula?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma\",\"e\":\"This option is a possibility, and should certainly be considered given the weight loss red flag. It is unlikely in a patient of this age, and does not explain his cough and therefore is not the most likely option.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"All the options listed are possible causes of high fistula. Crohn's disease is a recognised cause in this age group and the weight loss would fit with this diagnosis. Crohn's disease is, however, more common in smokers and this option does not explain the chronic cough.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diverticular disease\",\"e\":\"Ail the options listed are possible causes of high fistula. Diverticular disease is possible, but uncommon in younger patients.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tuberculosis\",\"e\":\"The patient's country of origin, Uganda, where tuberculosis (TB) is endemic, along with the history of chronic cough and weight loss are highly suspicious for tuberculosis infection. Low and high fistula are defined by the level at which they pass through the anal sphincters. A low fistula's internal orifice is below puborectalis. A high fistula's orifice begins above this. The former are common, relatively easy to manage and do not usually threaten the patient's sphincteric muscle function (and therefore continence). High fistulae are infrequent, complex to manage and can lead to incontinence.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"Ulcerative colitis is a recognised cause in this age group and the weight loss would fit with this diagnosis, however, this option also does not explain the chronic cough from which the patient has been suffering.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"An 88-year-old woman presents with recurrent painless abdominal distension and vomiting. Her bowels have not opened for 5 days. Examination reveals a distended soft abdomen with reduced bowel sounds. X-rays show distended large bowel loops, unchanged from those on previous admissions. A diagnosis of colonic pseudo-obstruction is made.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A barium enema is likely to demonstrate a mechanical cause\",\"e\":\"Pseudo-obstruction is not caused by a mechanical obstruction, instead it is a result of ineffective peristalsis of the intestine.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The condition is not commonly associated with physical trauma\",\"e\":\"Physical trauma is a common cause of pseudo-obstruction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The condition is rarely associated with electrolyte disturbances\",\"e\":\"Weight loss and electrolyte disturbances are common occurring as a result of loss of appetite, frequent vomiting and poor nutritional intake. Additionally, the causative conditions in secondary pseudo-obstruction (eg Parkinson's disease, cerebral palsy, immobility, or chronic renal failure) may impair the patient's ability to take on adequate/appropriate nutrition or may directly lead to electrolyte disturbances themselves.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Institutionalised and immobile elderly patients are at increased risk\",\"e\":\"This is correct, other risk factors include cerebral palsy, Parkinson's disease or other neurological disorders, chronic renal, pulmonary or cardiac disease; prolonged immobility; and prolonged use of narcotic drugs and other anticholinergic drugs that reduce bowel motility. Chronic colonic pseudo-obstruction is characterised by symptoms of recurrent bowel obstruction, abdominal bloating and/or pain, evidence of bowel obstruction on radiographic imaging, and no evidence of anatomic/structural abnormality. Possible complications include diarrhoea (and associated electrolyte disturbances, intestinal perforation, nutritional deficiencies and weight loss.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The condition may successfully be treated by gastroscopy\",\"e\":\"Colonoscopy is often successful in decompressing the colon, although symptomatic relief from this is often temporary.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old man with a long history of alcohol abuse is admitted to hospital with acute abdominal pain and is diagnosed clinically as having acute pancreatitis. This is confirmed by finding a high serum amylase activity and by the results of an ultrasound scan. He has not had a similar illness before. His serum is seen to be lipaemic: serum triglyceride concentration is 26 mmol/1 (normal <1.7 mmol/l\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is the most likely cause of the hypertriglyceridaemia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is the most likely cause of the hypertriglyceridaemia?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased pancreatic lipase secretion\",\"e\":\"Pancreatic lipase is normally secreted into the gut and higher plasma levels are found in pancreatitis*\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Deficiency of apolipoprotein coll\",\"e\":\"Apolipoprotein C-ll activates lipoprotein lipase, which is responsible for the clearance of very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) and chylomicrons from the plasma. Both lipase deficiency and apolipoprotein C-ll deficiency are rare, inherited causes of severe hypertriglyceridaemia, which usually present in childhood\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Deficiency of LDL (low-density lipoprotein) receptors\",\"e\":\"LDL receptors are involved in the transport of cholesterol from the plasma to the interior of cells: deficiency is the cause of familial hypercholesterolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Deficiency of lipoprotein lipase\",\"e\":\"Lipoprotein lipase deficiency is a rare inherited cause of severe hypertriglyceridaemia. The condition usually presents in childhood. Lipoprotein lipase is responsible for the clearance of very-low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) and chylomicrons from the plasma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased synthesis of VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein)\",\"e\":\"Hypertriglyceridaemia occurs frequently in association with a high alcohol intake, and is due to the increased synthesis of triglyceride for incorporation into VLDL in the liver. This is thought to be as a result of the altered redox state as a consequence of alcohol metabolism, which favours fat synthesis\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"An infant should pass meconium at or soon after birth. It results from materials ingested by the neonate whilst it remains in utero.\\n\\nThe commonest underlying cause for meconium ileus in a newborn is?\",\"lead\":\"The commonest underlying cause for meconium ileus in a newborn is?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hirschsprung's disease\",\"e\":\"Hirschsprung's disease results from abnormal development of the intrinsic nerves of the colon and results in megacolon, rather than meconium ileus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intussusceptions\",\"e\":\"Intussusception is a common paediatric cause of obstruction, classically presenting with passing of a 'red currant jelly' stool and signs of obstruction. It results from part of the bowel 'folding' into an adjacent section of bowel such that it becomes trapped within the lumen of the adiacent section.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Duodenal atresia\",\"e\":\"This condition is the congenital absence of the duodenal lumen. It may be detected antenatally as it is a cause of polyhydramnios, in newborns it will present with features of intestinal obstruction.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Imperforate anus\",\"e\":\"This is a congenital absence or obstruction of the anal passage, which will be quickly detected as part of the routine newborn screening examination.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cystic fibrosis\",\"e\":\"Most newborn babies with meconium iles have cystic fibrosis. Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an autosomal-recessive disorder associated with the production of abnormal mucous with high salt content. The Guthrie tests done in the first days of life include a spot test for CF however, a sweat chloride test is used to confirm the diagnosis. Meconium ileus occurs in CF as a result of the following: the pancreas undergoes dysplasia, so with the loss of pancreatic enzymes and presence of abnormal mucous, thick meconium forms. This is thick enough to causes intraluminal intestinal obstruction usually at the level of the terminal ileum. Affected bowel will be dilated and filled with heavy meconium making it prone to volvulus.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"Oesophageal cancer is a severe diagnosis requiring risky surgery where oncological success is not always assured. Benign lesions are rare\\n\\nThe commonest benign tumour of the oesophagus is?\",\"lead\":\"The commonest benign tumour of the oesophagus is?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CL Fibroma\",\"e\":\"Fibromas do occur, but are very rare and certainly not as common as leiomyomas.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pseudopolyps\",\"e\":\"Pseudopolyps can occur, these are typically inflammatory in nature as a result of repetitive inflammation/ulceration followed by deposition of granular tissue/regrowth of normal tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Adenoma\",\"e\":\"Adenomas do occur, but are very rare and certainly not as common as leiomyomas.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Leiomyoma\",\"e\":\"Leiomyoma is the commonest benign tumour that occurs commonly in the lower part of the oesophagus, although it is still a relatively rare growth. Overall benign tumours of the oesophagus make up approximately 1% of all oesophageal tumours, and leiomyomas are around two-thirds of this, or 0.6% of all oesophageal tumours. The majority of these are asymptomatic. The commonest symptomatic presentation is dysphagia. The tumours are well encapsulated. Endoscopic resection for small pedunculated lesions and localised resection for larger lesions is the treatment if symptomatic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Fibrous polyps\",\"e\":\"The second commonest benign tumours are fibrous or fibrovascular polyps that occur commonly in the upper third of the oesophagus.\\n\\nMost of the tumours of the oesophagus are malignant and symptomatic benign tumours account for less than 1% of oesophageal tumours.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"Crohn's disease has distinct pathological features. This differentiates from other gastrointestinal pathologies Which one of the following is not a pathological characteristic of Crohn's disease?\",\"lead\":\"This differentiates from other gastrointestinal pathologies Which one of the following is not a pathological characteristic of Crohn's disease?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"There are abnormal areas intervening between normal mucosa 'skip lesions\",\"e\":\"In Crohn's disease there are often abnormal areas intervening between normal mucosa, these are called 'skip lesions'.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Usually limited to mucosa\",\"e\":\"Crohn's is typically a transmural inflammatory process.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ulceration in the bowel is usually deep\",\"e\":\"The inflammation is transmural and ulceration are typically deep. This situation leads to a predisposition to fistulae formation, perforations, abscesses and strictures.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Non-caseating granuloma\",\"e\":\"Granuloma are a histological feature of Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"May affect any part of the alimentary tract\",\"e\":\"In ulcerative colitis the rectum is always affected, however in Crohn's disease any part of the alimentary tract may be affected. Perianal disease is common in Crohn's disease presenting with features such as fissures, abscesses and fistulae. Mouth ulcers can also be a presenting feature.\\n\\nCrohn's disease is an inflammatory bowel disease that affects any part of the alimentary tract. Patients are typically diagnosed before the age of 30, and it affects any ethnic group, but mostly white patients and Ashkenazi Jews. Family history of the disease is a risk factor. and current cigarette smoking is a very strong risk factor and also associated with more severe disease and an increased likelihood of needing surgery. Presenting symptoms of gastrointestinal disease (ie not extra-intestinal manifestations) frequently include weight loss, loss of appetite, abdominal pain, diarrhoea with blood in stool. Additionally, mouth ulcers and perianal disease are common in Crohn's presenting with features such as fissures, abscesses and fistulae. The inflammation is transmural affecting the whole thickness of the affected bowel and non-caseating granulomas are typically found. The terminal ileum is affected in nearly 75% of the cases. As the inflammation is transmural and the ulcers associated with the disease are deep, fistulae formation, perforations, abscesses and strictures are not unusual. Inflammatory bowel diseases are associated with extra-intestinal manifestations that affect the skin (pyoderma gangrenosum, erythema nodosum), eyes (iritis). joints (sacroiliitis, ankylosing spondylitis), biliary tree (sclerosing cholangitis, chronic active hepatitis).\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":92,\"stem\":\"Blockage of biliary outflow from the liver results in dilated intrahepatic ducts. There is interruption of the enterohepatic circulation and concentration of bilirubin increases in the blood. For a patient with obstructive jaundice, which one of the following statements is true?\",\"lead\":\"For a patient with obstructive jaundice, which one of the following statements is true?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Clinically detectable jaundice does not occur until the bilirubin is above 60 umol\",\"e\":\"Clinically detectable jaundice occurs above 30 umol/l, at 60 umol/1 the jaundice should be obvious.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bile salt deposition in the skin can cause intense itching\",\"e\":\"Biliary obstruction leads to accumulation of bile salts with backflow into the liver and systemic circulation, raising their blood concentration and leading to skin deposition and pruritus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Most biliary strictures are due to inflammatory scarring after cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Most biliary strictures are iatrogenic after surgery, only small numbers are post inflammatory disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cholangiocarcinoma is the commonest neoplasm causing obstructive jaundice\",\"e\":\"Adenocarcinoma of the head of the pancreas is the commonest neoplasm leading to obstructive jaundice. Ninety per cent of pancreatic carcinomas are adenocarcinomas derived from the ductal cells of the exocrine pancreas, these have only a 5% 5-year survival rate.\\n\\nCholangiocarcinoma is a rare tumour arising from biliary epithelium. Unlike adenocarcinoma of the pancreas these tumours are slow growing and often metastasise late so that complete resection is often possible - nevertheless they have a poor prognosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PS) is more commonly seen in Crohn's disease than in Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"Primary sclerosing cholangitis is a rare condition thought to be of autoimmune origin; it results in progressive fibrosis of the biliary system. PSC may arise spontaneously but is usually in association with long-standing UC, rather than Crohn's disease. Areas of short localised disease can be treated with an indwelling stent.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":95,\"stem\":\"A senior house officer (SHO) working in a surgical ward developed hepatitis, which resolved in 2 weeks. His blood tests showed that he had hepatitis A. Six months after this episode a liver biopsy is performed for this SHO.\\n\\nWhat is the biopsy most likely to show?\",\"lead\":\"What is the biopsy most likely to show?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Central necrosis\",\"e\":\"Central necrosis is a finding of acute hepatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chronic septal fibrosis\",\"e\":\"This option a part of the histological findings of chronic hepatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lobular fibrosis\",\"e\":\"This option is a part of the histological findings of chronic hepatitis:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Normal architecture\",\"e\":\"Hepatitis A virus has no known chronic carrier state and does not lead to chronic hepatitis or cirrhosis. If a liver biopsy is performed after the patient has completely recovered from this infection it will therefore show normal architecture\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Periportal fibrosis\",\"e\":\"This option is a part of the histological findings of chronic hepatitis. It is also found in schistosomiasis.\\n\\nHepatitis A virus is a single-stranded RNA picornavirus. This virus is the most common cause of acute viral hepatitis and is particularly common in children and young adults. In some countries more than 75% of adults have been exposed. Hepatitis A virus spreads primarily by faecal-oral contact and so can occur in areas of poor hygiene. Water-borne and food-borne epidemics occur, especially in underdeveloped countries. Eating contaminated raw shellfish is sometimes responsible. Sporadic cases are also common, usually as a result of person-to- person contact. Faecal shedding of the virus occurs before symptoms develop and usually ceases a few days after symptoms begin. This means that infectivity often has already ceased when the hepatitis becomes clinically evident. Hepatitis A virus has no known chronic carrier state and does not lead to chronic hepatitis or cirrhosis. If a liver biopsy is performed after the patient has completely recovered from this infection it will therefore show normal architecture\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man with jaundice had laboratory investigations performed which showed: total bilirubin 7.0 mg/dL, (normal 0.3 to 1.0 mg/dL), unconjugated bilirubin 5 mg/dl (normal < 0.2 mg/dL) and anaemia. What is the most likely cause of his jaundice?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of his jaundice?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fibrosis of the common bile duct\",\"e\":\"This option would cause an obstructive pattern of jaundice, the patient's direct (conjugated) bilirubin would be expected to be significantly more elevated than here.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemolysis\",\"e\":\"Haemolysis results in the destruction of red cells therefore anaemia. Destruction of red cells also leads to the increased production of unconjugated bilirubin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hepatitis\",\"e\":\"Hepatitis typically causes a non-obstructive pattern of jaundice, as this patient's blood tests suggest. However, it does not explain the anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sclerosing cholangitis.\",\"e\":\"This option would cause an obstructive pattern of jaundice, the patient's direct (conjugated) bilirubin would be expected to be significantly more elevated than here.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Schistosomiasis\",\"e\":\"Schistosomiasis typically causes a non-obstructive pattern of jaundice, as this patient's blood tests suggest. However, it does not explain the anaemia.\\n\\nHyperbilirubinaemia results from increased bilirubin production, decreased liver uptake or conjugation, or decreased biliary excretion. Total bilirubin normally is mostly unconjugated, with values of <1.2 mg/dl (<20 umol/I). Fractionation can measure the proportion of bilirubin that is conjugated (or direct, ie measured directly). Fractionation is required only in neonatal jaundice or if the bilirubin is elevated, but the other liver test results are normal, suggesting that hepatobiliary disease is not the cause. Unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia (indirect bilirubin fraction > 85%) reflects increased bilirubin production (for example when haemolysis is the case, as in this scenario) or defective liver uptake or conjugation (eg in hepatitis, or Gilbert's syndrome): Such increases in unconjugated bilirubin are generally less. than five-fold (<6 mg/dl or <100 umol/I) unless there is concurrent liver disease. Conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia typically suggests decreased biliary excretion, also known as obstructive jaundice. Common causes of this condition include gall stones, cancers (such as head of pancreas tumours), chronic inflammatory diseases such as primary sclerosis cholangitis, and iatrogenically following procedures such as ERCP\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":101,\"stem\":\"A 43-year-old man has developed sclerosing cholangitis and iron deficiency anaemia secondary to chronic bloody diarrhoea. They undergo colonoscopy as an outpatient.\\n\\nA colonic biopsy would be most likely to show which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"A colonic biopsy would be most likely to show which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Colonic adenocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"This answer is not correct, it does not explain the sclerosis cholangitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diverticulitis\",\"e\":\"Diverticulitis is not usually a chronic complaint, it is an acute inflammation of diverticula typically in the distal colon It presents typically with left iliac fossa tenderness, bloody diarrhoea, and fever.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Granulomatous inflammation\",\"e\":\"Granulomatous inflammation suggests Crohn's disease, however, Crohn's is not classically associated with sclerosing cholangitis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Villous adenoma\",\"e\":\"A villous adenoma is a non-malignant polyp that is most commonly found within the colon. They can result in diarrhoea, typically not bloody diarrhoea though. They have considerable potential to develop into malignant adenocarcinoma.\\n\\nUlcerative colitis affects the mucosa and submucosa of the rectum and colon, with a sharp border between normal and affected tissue. Only in severe disease is the muscularis layer involved. In early cases, the mucous membrane is erythaematous, finely granular and friable, with loss of the normal vascular pattern and often with scattered haemorrhagic areas. Large mucosal ulcers with copious purulent exudate are characteristic of severe disease. Islands of relatively normal or hyperplastic inflammatory mucosa project above areas of ulcerated mucosa - these are name pseudopolyps. Fistulas and abscesses do not occur, unlike in Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pseudopolyps\",\"e\":\"Pseudopolyps are a classic finding of ulcerative colitis. This diagnosis is suggested by presence of sclerosing cholangitis (an extra-intestinal manifestation) along with the bloody diarrhoea in the scenario above.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":104,\"stem\":\"A 38-year-old man with ulcerative colitis develops pruritus and fatigue. His alkaline phosphatase is elevated. The biliary tree appears to have a 'beaded' appearance on barium X-ray.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Cholecystitis presents with Murphy's positive right upper quadrant pain and signs of infection. On imaging the gall-bladder may appear thick-walled and with some surrounding fluid.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chronic cholelithiasis\",\"e\":\"Chronic cholelithiasis may well be asymptomatic, or may present with symptoms of biliary colic. Imaging will reveal gallstones within the gall-bladder.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cholesterosis\",\"e\":\"Otherwise known as 'strawberry gall-bladder', this condition gives the wall of the gall- bladder a strawberry-like colour and visual texture as a result of high cholesterol.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gallstone ileus\",\"e\":\"Gallstone ileus is mechanical obstruction of the bowel due to a gallstone.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sclerosing cholangitis\",\"e\":\"Primary sclerosing cholangitis is a chronic cholestatic syndrome characterised by patchy inflammation, fibrosis and strictures of the intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts. These patchy affects are what led to the classic 'beaded' appearance mentioned in the scenario above. Around 80% of patients with PSC have inflammatory bowel disease, often ulcerative colitis. Symptoms of fatigue and pruritus develop late. The diagnosis is based on contrast cholangiography (with endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography) or magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography. The disease leads to eventual obliteration of the bile ducts, with cirrhosis, hepatic failure and sometimes cholangiocarcinoma.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"You are asked to examine a 42-year-old man in the surgical out-patient clinic who gives a history of pain in the upper central abdomen initially relieved by eating but that occurs 2-3 h after meals. He also tells you that he often wakes up during the night with a similar pain. Based on this history, what do you feel is the most likely cause of the pain?\",\"lead\":\"Based on this history, what do you feel is the most likely cause of the pain?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Duodenal ulcer\",\"e\":\"Duodenal ulcers tend to be associated with a fairly consistent pattern of pain. Pain is absent when the patient awakens but appears mid-morning, is relieved by food, but recurs 2-3 h after a meal. Pain that awakens a patient at night is common and is highly suggestive of duodenal ulcert. Duodenal ulcers are more common than gastric ulcers.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Gastritis\",\"e\":\"Gastritis pain is usually intermittent and described as indigestion or 'burning' pain, it is typically worsened shortly after eating food.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastric ulcer\",\"e\":\"Gastric ulcer pain also tends to be relieved by food, and come back a few hours later. Pain at night being common. They are not, however, as common as duodenal ulcers.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pyloric stenosis\",\"e\":\"Pyloric stenosis is extremely rare in adults. It presents with vomiting after meals and weight loss as patients are not usually able to adequately nourish themselves.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome\",\"e\":\"This is a rare condition. Gastrinomas within either the pancreas or duodenum secrete the hormone gastrin, this hormone results in increased acid production within the stomach uncontrolled by normal feedback loops. Excessive acid production then results in large numbers of gastric and duodenal ulcers.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":110,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old woman presenting with symptoms of severe gastro-oesophageal reflux disease has recently had an upper gastrointestinal (Gl) endoscopy. She has now been given a diagnosis of Barrett's oesophagus.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements most accurately describes this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements most accurately describes this condition?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Occurs in approximately 40% of patients with gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD) in the long term\",\"e\":\"Around 5-15% of patients with gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD) will develop Barrett's oesophagus (metaplasia), which in turn is associated with an increased risk of oesophageal malignancy (neoplasia).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is caused by chronic reflux of gastric acid into the lower desophagus causing metaplasia of the normal squamous epithelium of the lower oesophagus to columnar and goblet cell epithelium\",\"e\":\"The chronic reflux of gastric acid into the lower oesophagus causes chronic inflammation. As a result of this inflammation there is metaplasia of the affected oesophageal epithelium from squamous epithelium to columnar epithelium with goblet cells. Later dysplasia can occur and eventually this can develop into malignancy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Presents with malignancy in one-third of cases\",\"e\":\"The risk of developing oesophageal cancer in people with Barrett's is 0.13 %. About 1 in 860 patients with Barrett's go on to develop cancer (Cancer Research UK).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is diagnosed by the position of the z line which rises from its normal position\",\"e\":\"The histological changes (from squamous to columnar epithelium with goblet cells) together with elevation of the Z line on oesophago-gastro-duodenoscopy (OGD) is diagnostic. The Z line usually lies 3 cm from the lower oesophageal sphincter and is identified by a change in colour trom salmon pink to a deeper red.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Histamine H2 blockers are the treatment of choice\",\"e\":\"The treatment of choice is with proton pump inhibitors and close follow up.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":113,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man presents to the acute surgical team with tenderness and pain in the left lower quadrant, fever, nausea, diarrhoea and an increase white cell count. You suspect colonic diverticular disease.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding colonic diverticular disease?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding colonic diverticular disease?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is frequently associated with electrolyte disturbances\",\"e\":\"Diverticulae are very common and often asymptomatic. Presenting features can include persistent pain, irregular bowel habit including diarrhoea or constipation (or alternating). Complications are also common presenting features, most commonly diverticulitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is a premalignant disease\",\"e\":\"Colonic diverticular disease is not related to colon cancer but shares common dietary factors with colorectal carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is usually an inherited condition\",\"e\":\"Colonic diverticular disease is not inherited. The biggest risk factor is lack of dietary fibre and it usually occurs in older patients.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It may cause a vesicovaginal fistula\",\"e\":\"Fistula to bladder, skin, vagina and other loops of bowel are common but not vesicovaginal fistulae.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It may present with large bowel obstruction\",\"e\":\"Obstruction is unusual but well recognised. Often this is a result of fibrous tissue development and stricturing following an episode, or several episodes, of diverticulitis. Diverticular perforation is a common and important complication of diverticular disease and can be managed medically or surgically depending on the Hinchey Classification. Fistulae to bladder, skin, vagina and other loops of bowel are also common complications.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":116,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old woman presents with weight loss, nausea, vomiting and jaundice and is diagnosed with carcinoma of the gall-bladder. She has a history of gallstones. Which one of the following would suggest a better prognosis in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would suggest a better prognosis in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Age\",\"e\":\"Age is a recognised risk factor for carcinoma of the gall-bladder, with the incidence rising with age. It is most commonly diagnosed in those greater than 65 years of age.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Female\",\"e\":\"Female gender is a recognised risk factor for carcinoma of the gall-bladder, with a 2:1 female to male ratio. This may be due to the increased frequency of cholelithiasis in women.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Presence of gallstones\",\"e\":\"Over 75% of patients with gall-bladder cancer have cholelithiasis. Other important predisposing conditions include chronic cholecystitis, porcelain gall-bladder, gall-bladder polyps and choledochal cysts.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"TNM stage I disease\",\"e\":\"While most gall-bladder carcinoma presents at a late stage and has a poor survival, if detected early prognosis is much better. At stage 1A (T1, NO, MO) - simple cholecystectomy. should be curative. At stage IB (T2, NO, MO) extended cholecystectomy is performed conveying a 5-year survival rate of 70-90%. The tumour is usually an adenocarcinoma, although in 10% of patients squamous cell carcinoma may occur. Adenocarcinoma, particularly papillary adenocarcinoma, is associated with the best prognosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"TNM of T4, any N, MO\",\"e\":\"Gall-bladder carcinoma usually presents at a late stage and stage II! disease (T4, any N. MO) is generally not surgically curable (a 1-year survival rate for advanced gall-bladder cancer is less than 5% with a median survival of 2-4 months).\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":119,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man presents with jaundice, abdominal ascites, loss of weight and abdominal pain. You suspect hepatocellular carcinoma.\\n\\nWhich one of the following would you expect in hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would you expect in hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Women are affected more than men\",\"e\":\"Men are more commonly affected than women. Worldwide this ratio varies between 2:1 and 4:1\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"in hepatitis B virus endemic areas. HCC usually presents above the age of 50\",\"e\":\"In the UK the average age of presentation is 66, in countries with endemic hepatitis B there are peaks in the age distribution at 45 and 65.\\n\\nIn highly endemic areas, HBV transmission is nearly all from mother to newborn, and as many as 90% of babies develop chronic infection. This differs from areas that have a low prevalence of HBV, where transmission is horizontal, through sexual and parenteral rates in adulthood. In high risk countries, HCC can arise before the age of 2.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is associated with HIV\",\"e\":\"Common viral risk factors include hepatitis B and C viruses (HBV and HCV). In Europe and North America anti-HCV antibodies are present in around 70% of patients with hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). In countries with endemic hepatitis B, 90% of patients with HCC are positive for hepatitis B. HIV is not an independent risk factor for HCC although co- nfection with HCV and HIV may lead to more rapid progression to HCC.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is associated with alcoholic liver disease\",\"e\":\"Alcoholic liver disease is strongly associated with hepatocellular carcinoma. It is considered a causative factor in up to 45% of hepatocellular carcinoma cases (in American and Italian studies. Alcohol is causative through two possible mechanisms - direct genotoxicity and because excessive chronic alcohol consumption leads to cirrhosis.\\n\\nClinical features of hepatocellular carcinoma include weight loss, anorexia, fever, a right upper quadrant pain and ascites. Chemotherapy and radiotherapy are generally unhe pful. Survival, except in very selected groups, is seldom more than 6 months.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serum a-fetoprotein levels (AFP) are normal\",\"e\":\"AFP levels are usually raised. Patients with cirrhosis due to HBV, HCV, alcohol, primary biliary cirrhosis or haemochromatosis should be offered a 6-monthly abdominal ultrasound scan and serum AFP test as screening for hepatocellular carcinoma.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":122,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old man presents with a large mass in his anus, which is ulcerated and bleeds easily. He is concerned and seeking your advice about treatment options.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) of the anal canal is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) of the anal canal is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It characteristically presents with faecal incontinence\",\"e\":\"Rectal bleeding is the most common initial symptom (45% of cases); 30% of patients complain of pain or a sensation of a mass.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It does not metastasise\",\"e\":\"Anal cancer can metastasize. The nodal metastatic locations depend on the lymphatic drainage, which is dependent on where the anal cancer originates: above the dentate line, flows to the perirectal and paravertebral nodes; below the dentate line, drainage is through the inguinal and femoral nodes. The most common site of distal metastasis is the liver.\\n\\nAnal carcinoma is relatively radiosensitive. Presently, combination radiotherapy and chemotherapy [fluorouracil (5-FU) = mitomycin or 5-FU + cisplatin] is used as initial standard first-line treatment. Abdominoperineal (AP) resection is reserved for recurrent or resistant tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is relatively radioresistant\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is related to increased dietary fat intake\",\"e\":\"Increased dietary fat intake is thought to be a risk factor for colorectal cancer, not for anal cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is associated with human papillomavirus infection\",\"e\":\"The human papillomavirus (usually type 16) may cause intraepithelial neoplasia, which with time progresses to anal carcinoma. This virus is found in approximately 70% of patients Other risk factors for anal carcinoma include a history of cervical or vaginal cancer, history of sexually transmitted disease and immunosuppression following solid organ transplantation.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":125,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old man presents with ongoing indigestion, abdominal pain and loss of appetite. An oesophago-gastroduodenoscopy (OGD) reveals gastric cancer.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding incidence of gastric cancer is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding incidence of gastric cancer is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Incidence is increasing in Europe\",\"e\":\"The incidence of gastric cancer is decreasing in Europe.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Incidence is lower in people with H. pylori infection\",\"e\":\"Risk factors for developing gastric cancer include the presence of H. pylori, blood group A, pernicious anaemia, previous gastric surgery and the presence of gastric polyps.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Incidence shows no preference for diets high or low in fresh fruit\",\"e\":\"Fresh fruit and vitamin C may be protective, but there is no consistent evidence to support\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Incidence is higher in those with blood group Bor\",\"e\":\"Blood group A may be associated with higher incidence, not groups Bor O.\\n\\nGastric cancer in Western Europe often presents in patients over 55 years of age with new- onset dyspepsia/reflux and upper abdominal pain. Other red flags include upper abdominal mass and dysphagia, - these patients should be considered for urgent gastroscopy. Patients may also have nausea, vomiting, anaemia, treatment-resistant dyspepsia or weight loss.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Incidence is higher in patients with pernicious anaemia\",\"e\":\"Risk factors for developing gastric cancer include the presence of H. pylori, blood group A, pernicious anaemia, previous gastric surgery and the presence of gastric polyps.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":128,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man presents with multiple colorectal polyps. He has a family history of familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP). At colonoscopy he has multiple colorectal polyps. Which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"FAP is inherited as an autosomal recessive trait\",\"e\":\"FAP is an inherited disease transmitted as an autosomal dominant trait.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"FAP has an approximate 60% risk of progression to adenocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"In FAP, the risk of progression of an adenomatous polyp to adenocarcinoma is virtually 100%.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"FAP is diagnosed by computed tomography (CT) scan\",\"e\":\"FAP is normally diagnosed at colonoscopy when a patient is found to have more than 100 polyps alongside a strong family history and genetic testing.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"FAP can present with other extra-colonic connective tissue abnormalities\",\"e\":\"Gardner's syndrome is a variant of FAP and can present with extra-colonic abnormalities including multiple osteomas, epidermal cysts, desmoid tumours, lipomas and other connective tissue abnormalities. Additionally it is associated with small bowel cancer, thyroid cancer, pancreatic cancer, biliary cancer and hepatoblastoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The APC gene is an oncogene\",\"e\":\"An oncogene is a gene that is causative in cancer - either through activation or higher expression levels. APC is a tumour suppressor gene - mutation results in inactivation. It is responsible for the production of adenomatous polyposis coli.\\n\\nFAP is an autosomal dominant inherited disease resulting in multiple adenomatous polyps within the colon and a near 100% chance of progression to adenocarcinoma. It is estimated to have an incidence at between 1:7000 to 1:22 000. Less than 1% of colorectal cancers is due to FAP. Patients with known FAP should have yearly endoscopic screening at a minimum and will usually require a colectomy at some point in their disease progression (as the number of polyps increases, the risk of cancer increases), Even after colectomy, surveillance is still required at between 6-monthly and 4-yearly testing depending on how much rectal tissue is left.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":131,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old woman presents with hypergastrinaemia, duodenal ulcers and diarrhoea. She is subsequently diagnosed with a gastrinoma neuroendocrine tumour. Which one of the following statements concerning gastrinoma is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements concerning gastrinoma is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are malignant in 10% of cases\",\"e\":\"Around 60% of cases are malignant\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They occur at extrapancreatic sites in 30% of cases\",\"e\":\"Although gastrin is usually produced by gastric antral G cells, tumours of G cells most commonly originate in the pancreas. Around 10% occur in extrapancreatic sites, eg in the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They originate from Hells\",\"e\":\"They originate from G cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They are associated with multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN)\",\"e\":\"Gastrinoma may occur as one of the MEN syndromes (ie typically MEN 1):\\n\\nMEN type Features\\n\\nParathyroid Pancreas MENT Pituitary\\n\\nMENZa Parathyroid Phaeochromocytoma Medullary thyroid cancer\\n\\nMEN2D Marfanoid/mucosal neuroma Phaeochromocytoma Medullary thyroid cancer\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They secrete hydrochloric acid\",\"e\":\"G cells secrete gastrin. Gastrin is responsible for stimulating hydrochloric acid secretion from the parietal cells of the stomach. Excess gastrin production results in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, resulting in widespread peptic ulceration.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":134,\"stem\":\"You are discussing a patient in the colorectal multi-disciplinary team (MDT). This young man has presented with profuse bloody diarrhoea and progressive weight loss. Colonic biopsies are suggestive of inflammatory bowel disease.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is more suggestive of ulcerative colitis than Crohn's disease?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is more suggestive of ulcerative colitis than Crohn's disease?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cancer\",\"e\":\"The risk of developing cancer is higher in ulcerative colitis (UC) than in Crohn's disease - the main associated risks are duration of disease and extent of disease (ie total colitis is greater than partial colitis). After 10 years of total colitis the risk of cancer is approximately 4-5%.\\n\\nFistula formation, as well as intra-abdominal or peri-anal abscesses, are a frequent complication of Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fistula formation\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Obstruction\",\"e\":\"Obstruction is a frequent complication of Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Perianal involvement\",\"e\":\"Perianal problems (fissures, fistulae, skin tags) are much more commonly seen in Crohn's disease than in UC.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stricture formation\",\"e\":\"Strictures are a frequent complication of Crohn's disease.\\n\\nCrohn's disease is a granulomatous inflammatory disorder involving the whole thickness of the bowel wall (any part of the gastrointestinal tract from mouth to anus). Ulcerative colitis is a non-granuloma process that only involves the mucosa. This difference may explain the higher incidence of stricture, obstruction and fistula formation in Crohn's disease. Perianal problems (fissures, fistulae, skin tags) are much more commonly seen in Crohn's disease than in UC. UC invariably involves the rectum and extends proximally towards the caecum, and can sometimes cause a 'backwash ileitis'.\\n\\nUlcerative colitis Crohn's disease\\n\\nAffects the rectum, extending contiguously Any part of the alimentary tract from mouth to Segments proximally a variable anus, most commonly the terminal ileum affected amount but limited to the colon\\n\\nPattern of Skip lesions Continuous disease\\n\\nPseudopolyps, very friable Macroscopic Cobblestones, aphthous and linear ulcers, findings abscesses, luminal narrowing mucosa, toxic megacolon\\n\\nSubmucosal or mucosal Microscopic inflammation, crypt Transmural inflammation, granulomas findings abscesses\\n\\nCommon Abdominal pain, diarrhoea, weight loss, mouth Bloody diarrhoea, weight symptoms ulcers, perianal disease loss, abdominal pain\\n\\nPerianal disease (fissures, fistulae, tags, Extra- PSC, arthritis and abscesses), mouth ulcers, oral aphthous ulcers, intestinal spondyloarthropathies, erythema nodosum, arthritis and uveitis, episcleritis features spondyloarthropathies, uveitis, episcleritis\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":138,\"stem\":\"You are performing a laparoscopic appendicectomy and notice an inflamed structure approximately 60 cm from the terminal ileum. The appendix appears normal. You diagnose an inflamed Meckel's diverticulum. Which one of the following statements is true of a Meckel's' diverticulum?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true of a Meckel's' diverticulum?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is a common cause of malabsorption\",\"e\":\"Most cases of Meckel's diverticulum are asymptomatic, but 20% contain heterotopic gastric or pancreatic mucosa. Complications of Meckel's diverticulum include infection/inflammation, intussusception, ulceration and gastrointestinal bleeding, intestinal obstruction and perforation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is found in over 20% of the general population\",\"e\":\"Meckel's diverticulum occurs in 2% of the population and affects men twice as often as women.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"it is invariably found in the jejunum\",\"e\":\"It is classically found in the distal ileum, 60 cm from the ileocaecal valve on the antimesenteric border of the small intestine and is approximately 5 cm long.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It may lead to macrocytic anaemia\",\"e\":\"The common causes of macrocytic anaemia include vitamin B12 or folate deficiency, alcohol, liver disease or reticulocytosis - Meckel's diverticulum does not cause a macrocytic anaemia.\\n\\nMeckel's diverticulum is a true diverticulum with a mucous membrane and a muscular coat and may be connected to the umbilicus by either a fibrous band or a complete fistula - remnants of the vitellointestinal duct. If a Meckel's diverticulum is found incidentally, it should be left alone, especially if it is non-inflamed with a wide neck.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It may present with rectal bleeding\",\"e\":\"Some cases of Meckel's diverticulum are asymptomatic, but 1 in 2 contain either heterotopic gastric or pancreatic mucosa. Most symptomatic cases will present before the age of 2 years. Complications of Meckel's diverticulum include diverticulitis, intussusception, ulceration and gastrointestinal bleeding, intestinal obstruction and perforation. Meckel's diverticulum, the rule of 2s:\\n\\n• occurs in 2% of the population • 2:1 male female ratio • approximately 2 inches long • found 2 feet from the ileocaecal junction • 1 in 2 will contain ectopic tissue (gastric or pancreatic) • only 2% are symptomatic • important cause of rectal bleeding in the under 2s.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":141,\"stem\":\"You are assessing a middle-aged woman in your clinic. She initially presented to you via casualty with progressive weight loss and abdominal pain together with intermittent diarrhoea and constipation. Her computed tomography (CT) scan reveals a stenosing lesion in the sigmoid colon. Which one of the following statements is true regarding colorectal carcinoma?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding colorectal carcinoma?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Irritable bowel syndrome is associated with an increased incidence of colorectal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Colorectal carcinoma is associated with inflammatory bowel disease, especially ulcerative colitis, rather than irritable bowel syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left sided colonic tumours usually present with anaemia\",\"e\":\"Right-sided colonic tumours tend to be exophytic and often present with anaemia as the caecum is of a large calibre so rarely obstructs. In contrast, left-sided cancers tend to be stenotic.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"More than 80% of colonic carcinomas arise within adenomatous polyps\",\"e\":\"More than 80% of colorectal cancers arise from pre-existing polyps, which makes the disease suitable for screening. The initial screening test used in the UK is the faecal occult blood test, given to 60-74-year-olds every 2 years. In patients with a positive test or symptoms suggestive of colorectal cancer, endoscopy with colonoscopy or flexible sigmoidoscopy and biopsy is the diagnostic investigation of choice.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Left-sided colonic tumours tend to be exophytic\",\"e\":\"Right-sided colonic tumours tend to be exophytic and often present with anaemia as the caecum is of a large calibre so rarely obstructs. In contrast, left-sided cancers tend to be stenotic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The commonest genetic abnormality in colonic carcinoma is p53\",\"e\":\"The commonest genetic abnormality is in the APC gene - it is a tumour suppressor gene. Mutations in the TP53 gene, which codes for the p53 protein - another tumour suppressor gene - are also common. There are also several syndromes that have a strong association with this cancer [Gardener's syndrome, hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC) and FAP].\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":144,\"stem\":\"You are admitting a young man who has presented with 1 week of severe abdominal pain following a binge drinking session on holiday. A diagnosis of acute pancreatitis is made.\\n\\nWhich one of the following are correct regarding complications of acute pancreatitis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following are correct regarding complications of acute pancreatitis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gastrointestinal haemorrhage with resultant haematemesis and melaena is usually the result of rupture of the posterior duodenal artery due to stress\",\"e\":\"Stress-induced gastric erosions frequently occur - these can result in haemorrhage and resultant haematemesis and melaena. This is the most common cause of haemorrhage but occur outside the setting of pancreatitis. Proton pump inhibitors (PPls) or H, antagonists are prophylactically used to prevent stress-induced gastric erosions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypocalcaemia is relatively common and is caused by fat saponification\",\"e\":\"Acute pancreatitist is an inflammatory process that is caused by the releaset of pancreatic enzymes from the pancreatic acini. This causes both localised and systemic damaget. The release of pancreatic enzymest into the body's circulation results in fat necrosis and saponification - the free fatty acids released by this process bind calcium and result in systemic hypocalcaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pancreatic pseudocysts are formed in the greater sac of the abdomen\",\"e\":\"Pancreatic pseudocysts are enzyme rich collections, which are formed in the lesser sac of the abdomen following acute and/or chronic pancreatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Relative hypoxia in acute pancreatitis is due to multiple sub-clinical pulmonarv emboli due to hypercoagulability\",\"e\":\"Hypoxia is usually due to hypo-ventilation due to pain, however, ARDS can also occur due to release of inflammatory mediators.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Splenic rupture is a common complication\",\"e\":\"Splenic rupture is a very rare complication of acute pancreatitis. Common complications include: pancreatic pseudocysts, pancreatic abscess, stress-induced gastric erosions with haematemesis or melaena, acute renal failure, toxic psychosis, multiple organ failure, hypocalcaemia and chronic pancreatitis.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":147,\"stem\":\"You are trying to give the family of a young woman who has endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP.)-related pancreatitis, some indication of her disease severity. You decide to perform additional tests so that you can determine the modified Glasgow score (Imrie criteria)\\n\\nWhich one of the following thresholds scores a point in the modified Glasgow criteria (Imrie criteria) for acute pancreatitis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following thresholds scores a point in the modified Glasgow criteria (Imrie criteria) for acute pancreatitis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Age >40\",\"e\":\"Age > 55 would score a point.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) > 200 IU/I\",\"e\":\"ALP is not part of the scoring system.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Corrected calcium < 2 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"Prognosis* of pancreatitis* (Ranson's criteria) One point is given for each criterion present, severe pancreatitis = three or more points.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Haematocrit < 45\",\"e\":\"A haematocrit count is not part of the scoring system.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"PO) < 10\",\"e\":\"P. (Oz) < 8 would score a point. The modified Glasgow criteria enable early diagnosis of severe acute pancreatitist. The following parameters are part of the scoring system:\\n\\n• age > 55 • blood glucose > 10 mmol/l - no history of diabetes • white cell count (WCC) > 15.5 × 109%1 • lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) > 600 IU/I • aspartate aminotransferase (AST) > 200 U/l • corrected calcium < 2 mmol/l • urea > 16 mmol/l • Pa (O=) < 8 kPa (60 mmHg) • albumin < 32 g/l.\\n\\nThe mortality+ per positive criterion:\\n\\n• 0-2 < 5% mortality • 3-4 20% mortality • 5-640% mortality • 7-8 100% mortality.\\n\\nThe modified Glasgow criteria does not include serum amylaset , haematocrit or pH.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":150,\"stem\":\"You are seeing a 43-year-old patient with frank jaundice, pruritus, pale stools and dark urine. Blood tests show a pattern consistent with obstructive jaundice and an ultrasound scan shows dilated intra- and extra-hepatic ducts.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding obstructive jaundice?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding obstructive jaundice?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gallstones are the commonest cause of obstructive jaundice in the UK\",\"e\":\"Gallstones are the commonest cause of obstructive jaundice in the United Kingdom. In this patient, jaundice is caused by an obstructing stone in the common bile duct (CBD) - the pressure in the system proximal to this area leads to dilatation of intra- and extra-hepatic biliary ducts proximal to the CBD.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"In this case an urgent laparoscopic cholecystectomy is indicated as the next stage in management\",\"e\":\"Investigations such as magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) or endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) are often needed before removal of the gall-bladder is attempted to confirm the pathology and the level of obstruction. ERCP can also be used therapeutically to remove the obstructing stones making the subsequent planned cholecystectomy more straightforward.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mirizzi's syndrome is caused by a floppy Hartmann's pouch of the gall-bladder that obstructs the common hepatic duct\",\"e\":\"Mirizzi's syndrome is a rare condition in which a stone becomes impacted in the cystic duct or neck of the gall-bladder causing external compression and obstruction of the common bile duct.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The dark urine is due to an excess of unconjugated bilirubin\",\"e\":\"Unconjugated bilirubin is largely bound to albumin in the blood, so is not filtered by the kidneys. Bile contains bilirubin that has been conjugated in the liver to make it water soluble. When there is a biliary obstruction that prevents excretion of conjugated bilirubin, some of the conjugated bilirubin will re-enter the blood circulation. This excess systemic conjugated bilirubin is filtered by the kidneys and gives the urine a dark colour.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"There is an excess of urobilinogen in the urine\",\"e\":\"Bile contains bilirubin that has been conjugated in the liver to make it water soluble. Normally, as part of the enterohepatic circulation, this is excreted into the bowel via the biliary tract and converted by bacteria within the gut to urobilinogen. Some of this urobilinogen is reabsorbed and then excreted in the urine, while some is further digested to produce urobilin and stercobilin which give faces its brown colour. Because of the obstruction no bile gets into the small bowel so no urobilinogen is formed in the bowel and therefore no urobilinogen is found in the urine. Additionally stools will become pale because no bile pigments are formed.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":153,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. During the operation he asks you questions to test your knowledge. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding biliary pathology?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding biliary pathology?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pigment stones area the commonest type of gallstones\",\"e\":\"Cholesterol gallstones are the most common but with haemolytic conditions, such as sickle- cell anaemia, pigment stones are also common.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sickle-cell disease causes largely acalculous cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Sickle-cell disease results in increased red-cell haemolysis and turnover, leading to a preponderance to develop pigment stones.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"80% of gallstones are symptomatic\",\"e\":\"Approximately 70% of gallstones are thought to be asymptomatic and are found on routine ultrasound examination.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Murphy's sign is right upper quadrant (RUQ) pain on palpation, causing sudden cessation of inspiration and forcing the patient to catch breath\",\"e\":\"Murphy's sign is an indication of acute cholecystitis. The patient catches breath due to pain on inspiration while the right hypochondrium is deeply palpated, but not when the left hypochondrium is deeply palpated, due to downward movement of the inflamed gall- bladder onto the examining hand during inspiration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A common infecting agent in cholecystitis is Salmonella\",\"e\":\"Secondary infection within stagnant bile converts cholelithiasis to cholecystitis. Common organisms are E coli, Klebsiella, Enterococcus faecalis and Bacteroides. orn\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":156,\"stem\":\"You are seeing an elderly man in the Emergency Department with gross abdominal distension and prominent superficial abdominal veins. He has shifting dullness and you diagnose ascites secondary to chronic liver disease.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding ascites?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding ascites?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Malignancy causing ascites is a transudate\",\"e\":\"Malignancy would normally cause an exudate, as defined by a low serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) < 1.1.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"When associated with oedema this is usually non-pitting\",\"e\":\"Pitting oedema is a clinical finding whereby pressure applied to an oedematous area leaves an indentation. Non-pitting oedema results most commonly from lymphoedema, while pitting oedema occurs most commonly secondary to conditions such as chronic venous insufficiency or deep vein thrombosis, or systemic conditions such as congestive cardiac failure, cirrhosis, in renal failure or nephrosis, secondary to medication (eg calcium-channel blockers), or can be idiopathic.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A high serum-ascites albumin gradient (>11) indicates a transudate such as pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"A transudate is defined by high serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) >1.1. Examples of transudates include liver disease (such as cirrhosis of any cause), heart failure (such as constrictive pericarditis), and portal vein thrombosis or Budd-Chiari syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A low serum-ascites albumin gradient (<1.1) indicates an exudate such as hereditary angioedema\",\"e\":\"An exudate ascites is defined by a low serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) < 1.1. Exudative ascites are associated with malignancy, pancreatitis and hereditary angioedema.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is due to the introduction of sepsis following an ascitic tap\",\"e\":\"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SP) is the development of peritonitis in the absence of an obvious cause. It occurs commonly in people with portal hypertension secondary to liver cirrhosis and patients with nephrotic syndrome.\\n\\nAscites is the presence of fluid within the peritoneal cavity. The causes of ascites can be divided according to the type of ascitic fluid. The serum-ascites albumin gradient is the concentration of serum albumin minus the concentration of ascitic fluid albumin. A high gradient (>1.1) implies a transudate, usually due to high portal venous pressure - the increased hydrostatic pressure within the hepatic portal vasculature results in leakage of fluid through vessel walls and therefore accumulation of fluid within the peritoneal cavity. As the vasculature itself remains functionally intact, proteins (such as albumin) are largely kept within the vessel walls. Causes of raised portal venous pressure include liver disease (such as cirrhosis of any cause), heart failure (such as constrictive pericarditis), and portal vein thrombosis or Budd-Chiari syndrome. A low gradient (<1.1) indicates that, as well as fluid, protein is also leaking through the vessel walls into the peritoneal cavity - this can be due to malignancy (such as hepatocellular carcinoma), inflammation (pancreatitis, peritonitis, tuberculosis) - it can also be due to lowered systemic albumin such as in nephrotic syndrome\\n\\nAscites can also be assessed according to its appearance. Straw-coloured fluid is due to malignancy, cirrhosis, infection, hepatic-vein obstruction, chronic pancreatitis, congestive cardiac failure and hypoproteinaemia. Chylous ascitic fluid is due to obstruction of the main lymphatic duct. Haemorrhagic ascites is due to malignancy, ruptured ectopic pregnancy. abdominal trauma and acute pancreatitis. Treatment of ascites can be challenging - in patients with portal hypertension it is important that the patient maintains a low sodium diet and avoids overhydration (fluid restrictions may be necessary, for example. Medical management of these patients include diuretics, commonly spironolactone is the first line approach with furosemide the second line. Ascites that is recurrent despite these methods or symptomatic (abdominal pain, difficulty breathing due to diaphragmatic splinting) may require drainage percutaneously. Definitive management is correction of the underlying abnormality - in cirrhosis this includes the transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) procedure and consideration of transplant.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":160,\"stem\":\"A patient with cirrhosis requires a laparotomy. The surgical team are making a decision on the risks and benefits of surgical vs conservative treatment. The Child-Pugh classification of the severity of chronic liver disease includes which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The Child-Pugh classification of the severity of chronic liver disease includes which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ascites\",\"e\":\"See overall explanation for tables showing the full scoring system and prognosis depending on score.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Aspartate transaminase\",\"e\":\"See overall explanation for tables showing the full scoring system and prognosis depending on score.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bleeding varices\",\"e\":\"See overall explanation for tables showing the full scoring system and prognosis depending on score.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cholesterol\",\"e\":\"See overall explanation for tables showing the full scoring system and prognosis depending on score.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Clotting time\",\"e\":\"See overall explanation for tables showing the full scoring system and prognosis depending on score.\\n\\nThe Child-Pugh classification takes into account bilirubin level, albumin level, ascites, encephalopathy, and prothrombin time.\\n\\nC A B\\n\\nBilirubin <34 >51 34-51\\n\\n>35 28-35 Albumin <28\\n\\nAscites None Controlled Refractory\\n\\nAdvanced Encephalopathy None Minimal\\n\\nProthombin time <4 >6 4-6\\n\\n5 Year 10 Year Child-Pugh Child-Pugh 1 Year Survival Survival grade Score Survival\\n\\nIndicates a well-functioning 44% 27% 84% A 5-6 liver\\n\\nIndicates significant 10% 20% 7-9 62% B functional compromise\\n\\nIndicates decompensation of 42% 21% 0% 10-15 C the liver\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":164,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old man presents with severe right-sided abdominal pain together with peritonism in the right illac fossa. His clinical picture and haematological profile are consistent with appendicitis but at operation an inflamed Meckel's diverticulum is identified.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding Meckel's diverticulum?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding Meckel's diverticulum?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is a partial persistence of the median umbilical ligament\",\"e\":\"A Meckel's diverticulum is a partial persistence of the vitellointestinal duct and there may be a fibrous connection to the umbilicus. The median umbilical ligament or 'urachus' is a connection between the umbilicus and the bladder\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It arises on the mesenteric border of the small bowel\",\"e\":\"Meckel's diverticulum arises on the antimesenteric border of the small bowel.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It typically lies about 60 cm from the ileocaecal valve\",\"e\":\"It is classically found in the distal ileum, within 100 cm (typically 60 cm) from the ileocaecal valve on the antimesenteric border of the small intestine and is approximately 5 cm leng Meckel's diverticulum is a true diverticulum with a mucous membrane and a muscular coat and may be connected to the umbilicus by either a fibrous band or a complete fistula - remnants of the vitellointestinal duct. If a Meckel's diverticulum is found incidentally, it should be left alone, especially if it is non-inflamed with a wide neck. Meckel's diverticulum, the rule of 2s:\\n\\n• occurs ir 2% of the population • 2: male male: female ratio • approximately 2 inches long • found 2 feet from the ileocaecal junction • 1 in 2 will contain ectopic tissue (gastric or pancreatic) • only 2% are symptomatic • important cause of rectal bleeding in the under 2s.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is present in 0.2% of individuals\",\"e\":\"It is present in 2% of individuals and affects men twice as often as women.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It contains heterotopic pancreatic tissue in 40% of cases\",\"e\":\"Some cases of Meckel's diverticulum are asymptomatic, but 50% contain heterotopic gastric or pancreatic mucosa. Complications of Meckel's diverticulum include intussusception, ulceration and gastrointestinal bleeding, intestinal obstruction and perforation.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":167,\"stem\":\"An elderly man presents with progressive dysphagia and weight loss. He is a lifetime heavy smoker and his GP is concerned.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements best represents his diagnosis of oesophageal carcinoma?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements best represents his diagnosis of oesophageal carcinoma?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It has wide geographical variation in incidence rates and is increasing in frequency in the UK\",\"e\":\"Oesophageal carcinoma in the UK is increasing in incidence. There is a great deal of geographical variation in the incidence, with the highest risk areas being central Asia and South/East Africa. Common risk factors include smoking, alcohol, ingestion of caustic agents (including very hot drinks), age, obesity and previous radiotherapy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Squamous-cell carcinomas of the osophagus usually occur in the upper two-thirds of the oesophagus and are associated with GORD\",\"e\":\"If found in the lower one-third of the oesophagus, it is usually of the adenocarcinoma type and associated with gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD). If found in the upper two- thirds of the oesophagus is of similar origin as head and neck cancers and is of squamous- cell origin. This type, however, is usually associated with smoking not GORD as the above answer states.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The development of a hoarse or husky voice is a concerning feature likely to be due to direct invasion of the aryepiglottic folds\",\"e\":\"A hoarse voice is a worrying feature but is usually due to involvement of the recurrent laryngeal nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Palliation of symptoms is seldom necessary as most patients die soon after developing dysphagia\",\"e\":\"Palliation is an important aim of all treatment for oesophageal cancers when cure is not possible. Overall survival for oesophageal cancer is 40% at 1 year, 15% at 5 years (cancer research UK statistics). However, survival varies by type (squamous-cell carcinoma or adenocarcinoma) and by stage of presentation. Early cancers may be fully resectable with oesophagectomy or endoscopic mucosal resection for very early cancer. Adjuvant and neoadjuvant radiotherapy and chemotherapy are also used in oesophageal cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Diagnosis is usually established by a barium swallow alone\",\"e\":\"Diagnosis may be suggested by a barium swallow but definitive diagnosis requires histology obtained via an oesophago-gastro- duodenoscopy (OGD)\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":170,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old woman presents with symptoms of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease. She has tested positive for Helicobacter pylori infection and been started on proton pump inhibitor (PPI) and eradication therapy: Which one of the following tests, in common usage, has the highest combined sensitivity and specificity when used to detect Helicobacter pylori infection?\",\"lead\":\"She has tested positive for Helicobacter pylori infection and been started on proton pump inhibitor (PPI) and eradication therapy: Which one of the following tests, in common usage, has the highest combined sensitivity and specificity when used to detect Helicobacter pylori infection?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Culture of biopsy material\",\"e\":\"Culture of biopsies may yield the presence of H. pylori, but this is not routinely used.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"McNemar's test <i\",\"e\":\"McNemar's test is a non-parametrical statistical test and nothing to do with H. pylori.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The biopsy urease test\",\"e\":\"Biopsy of the stomach wall is the basis of the urease test ('Clo-test'), which takes up to 30 min to get a result and depends on the presence of urease in the bacterium to alter the substrate from orange to pink. Sensitivity is the highest of the presented options (98%), but it is not highly specific (82%).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The urea breath test\",\"e\":\"The urea breath test detects radiolabelled carbon dioxide in the breath after ingesting radiolabelled urea (sensitivity 95%, specificity 96%).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serology/stool testing\",\"e\":\"Serology testing is used, but serological testing (sensitivity 92%, specificity 83%) performs less well when compared with breath testing, which is the most sensitive and specific (sensitivity 95%, specificity 96%) and stool testing (sensitivity 95%, specificity 94%)\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":172,\"stem\":\"A 76-year-old lifelong smoker presented to the Emergency Department with haematemesis. Following stabilisation, an oesophago-gastro- duodenoscopy (OGD) was performed with the presumed diagnosis of a gastric ulcer. A large polypoid mass was found in the distal oesophagus adjacent to the gastro-oesophageal junction with evidence of a co-existent hiatus hernia. Biopsy confirmed the diagnosis of oesophageal adenocarcinoma. Which one of the following confers the highest relative risk of developing this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following confers the highest relative risk of developing this condition?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Barrett's oesophagus\",\"e\":\"Barrett's oesophagus is considered a pre-malignant lesion associated with a risk of 6-14% of developing oesophageal adenocarcinoma and requires regular endoscopic surveillance. The relative risk of oesophageal cancer is approximately 11.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Human papillomavirus (HPV) infection\",\"e\":\"The relative risk of oesophageal squamous-cell carcinoma is between 2.4 and 3.6 in human papillomavirus (HPV) infected patients\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Alcohol\",\"e\":\"Alcohol is a recognised risk factor for squamous carcinoma, the risk increases with volume drunk with relative risk of between 1.8-7.4.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease\",\"e\":\"The relative risk of adenocarcinoma is between 4.9 for patients who have weekly symptoms, to 7.4 for patients who have daily symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Helicobacter pylori infection\",\"e\":\"This infection is associated with gastric cancer rather than oesophageal cancer:\\n\\nThe most common presenting symptom of oesophageal cancer is dysphagia, usually this will progressively worsening and symptoms will start with dysphagia to food and progress on to dysphagia for liquids. Cancer in the distal third of the oesophagus is most frequently adenocarcinoma, which is most commonly associated with gastro-oesophageal reflux disease and especially Barrett's oesophagus. Cancers of the upper two-thirds of the oesophagus are classically squamous-cell carcinomas.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":175,\"stem\":\"Intussusception is much more common in the paediatric population. Often a lesion in the bowel will cause the pathological process to take place\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most common pathological lead point in intussusception?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most common pathological lead point in intussusception?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dance's sign\",\"e\":\"Dance's sign, (a feeling of emptiness on palpation), with viscera absent in the right lower quadrant on abdominal X-ray is seen in 15% of cases. It is not, however, a 'lead point' in intussusception.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intraluminal haematoma\",\"e\":\"An intraluminal haematoma in Henoch-Schönlein purpura may trigger intussusception.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Meckel's diverticulum\",\"e\":\"Meckel's diverticulum and polyps are identified as a pathological lead point in 10% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypertrophic Peyer's patch\",\"e\":\"Peyer's patches in the ileum are the pathological lead patch in up to 90% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Polyps\",\"e\":\"Meckel's diverticulum and polyps are identified as a pathological lead point in 10% of cases.\\n\\nIntussusception is a condition where a part of the bowel, normally small bowel, telescopes inside an adjacent part of the bowel. It is most common in infants aged 4 months to 1 year, with peak incidence between 6-9 months. Risk factors, aside from age, include previous intussusception, or family history of the condition, and intestinal malrotation. It presents with abdominal pain, 'redcurrant jelly' stool and signs of obstruction and sepsis. Diagnosis can be made on ultrasound scan (USS) or on air or contrast enema. The enema can serve a dual purpose to also decompress the intussuscepted segment. If this fails surgery may be required. Pathologically the theory is that a lead point' is usually to blame that instigates the telescoping - it is thought that up to 90% of cases are as a result of Peyer's patches.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":178,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a 1-day-old with abdominal distension and bile stained vomiting. He has not yet passed meconium. You suspect Hirschsprung's disease.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"There is a congenital ganglion absence in the rectum\",\"e\":\"In Hirschsprung's disease there is a defective neural crest-cell migration resulting in a congenital absence of the ganglia that normally make up Auerbach (myenteric) plexus and Meissner (submucosal) plexus to part of the colon. The result of this absence is increased tone in the affected part of the colon, and an absence of appropriate peristalsis that causes a functional (rather than mechanical) obstruction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"There will be dilation of the affected segment\",\"e\":\"Dilatation occurs proximal to the affected segment. The affected segment is hypertonic and therefore stenotic so faces accumulate proximal to this.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"This affects both sexes equally\",\"e\":\"Hirschsprung's disease occurs in 1 in 5000 births and has an M:F ratio of 5:1.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"This is diagnosed by cold-cup rectal biopsy\",\"e\":\"Suction biopsy of the narrowed segment is first line, this is examined for absence of Auerbach and Meissner plexus. Full-thickness rectal biopsy is required if suction biopsy is inconclusive.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"This usually presents in patients greater than 10-years of age\",\"e\":\"Most commonly Hirschsprung's disease is diagnosed shortly after birth because of failure to pass meconium within 48 h of delivery. Other symptoms can include signs of obstruction such as abdominal distension and vomiting. Additionally diagnosis can be delayed, presenting later in childhood with chronic constipation.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":181,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with jaundice. On closer examination, the presence of Kayser-Fleischer rings is noted.\\n\\nThis is pathognomonic of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This is pathognomonic of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Autoimmune hepatitis\",\"e\":\"Autoimmune hepatitis has no specific associated eye signs, it can present as an acute or chronic hepatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sclerosing cholangitis\",\"e\":\"This condition is frequently associated with inflammatory bowel disease, especially ulcerative colitis. It may present before or after diagnosis of inflammatory bowel disease: there are no specific eye signs. Symptoms are mostly related to liver disease,\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Primary biliary cirrhosis (BC)\",\"e\":\"Patients with PBC frequently develop xerostomia and dry eyes. It typically presents in middle-aged women with intense itching and signs of liver disease such as jaundice.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Haemochromatosis V\",\"e\":\"There are no specific eye signs associated with this condition. Presentation can include liver disease, cardiac failure, arthritis, diabetes, tray-bronze skin and other endocrine complaints.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wilson's disease\",\"e\":\"In Wilson's disease, the presence of Kayser-Fleischer rings indicates neurological impairment. It consists of copper deposition in the cornea and may be seen as a greenish or golden brown ring around the cornea on slit lamp examination. Symptoms mostly relate to deposition of copper in the liver (causing liver disease) and brain (causing neuropsychiatric symptoms, such as parkinsonism due to deposition in the basal ganglia).\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":184,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old woman presents to her GP two weeks after an appendicectomy with a 24 h history of right upper quadrant pain, fever and shortness of breath. The pain radiates to her right shoulder tip. On examination, temperature is 38.9°C, pulse is 106/min and she is tender over the right hypochondric region. Chest X-ray reveals right basal atelectasis and pleural effusion.\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Emphysema of right lung\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary emphysema is a chronic lung condition that, along with chronic bronchitis, is now more commonly known clinically as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). In emphysema there is dilation of the airways distal to the terminal bronchioles due to destruction of elastase within the supporting connective tissue of the distal airways, most commonly it is associated with smoking although a less predisposing factor is alpha 1- antitrypsin deficiency Patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) are predisposed to lower respiratory tract infections which would explain the shortness of breath, fevers and chest X- ray changes, but would not normally cause RUQ tenderness with pain radiating to the shoulder.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acute pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"Acute pancreatitis is an important differential of the acute abdomen, however, this patient's recent surgery and confirmed appendicitis, combined with atypical signs for pancreatitis (ie RUQ pain) means this diagnosis is not the most likely. It rarely presents with rigors.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sclerosing cholangitis\",\"e\":\"Sclerosing cholangitis is a chronic inflammatory condition, usually found in the presence of inflammatory bowel disease. It can lead to chronic liver disease through obstruction of the biliary ducts. It is not a likely diagnosis in this acute scenario especially without evidence of liver disease (eg jaundice).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Subphrenic abscess\",\"e\":\"Subphrenic abscess usually arises 3 to 6 weeks following abdominal surgery, mainly to the biliary tract, duodenum or stomach, or following a perforated viscus or anastomotic leakage. The subphrenic space is in direct contact with the para-colic gutter thereby allowing peritoneal contamination such as bile, blood or bowel contents to spread. Subphrenic abscess is right-sided in about 50%, left-sided in 25% and bilateral in 25% of patients. Clinical features include pyrexia* with chills and rigors, anorexia, loss of appetite and loss of weight. Diaphragmatic irritation may affect the lung, resulting in chest pain, dyspnoea and non- productive cough. Basal atelectasis, pneumonia and pleural effusion are recognised complications of this condition. Additionally, diaphragmatic irritation can cause referred pain to the shoulder because the phrenic nerve (which innervates the diaphragm) shares nerve roots with the dermatomes overlying the shoulder (ie roots C3-5). Ultrasound scan is the investigation of choice to diagnose subphrenic abscess, and, if an abscess is identified, ultrasound guided percutaneous drainage catheter may be placed at the same time.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pyonephrosis\",\"e\":\"Pyonephrosis is a collection within the renal pelvis. It is a recognised complication of pyelonephritis, renal stones or hydronephrosis but can also occur as a primary condition. You would expect the patient's pain to be over the renal angle more that the right hypochondrium, urinary symptoms may be present, and chest signs would not be typical.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":188,\"stem\":\"An 87-year-old women is referred with a 24 h history of sudden onset abdominal pain. She is pale, cold and appears to be in shock. Blood pressure is 102/68 mmHg and pulse is 92/min and irregular. Abdomen is soft and mildly tender, with absent bowel sounds. She was noted to pass a few bloody stools. Arterial blood gas reveals:\\n\\nInvestigations Result Normal value\\n\\n7.35-7.45 7.24 pH\\n\\n20 mmol Bicarbonate 22-26 mmol\\n\\nBase excess -10 mmol -2 to +2 mmol\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Sigmoid volvulus\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acute small bowel obstruction\",\"e\":\"Acute small bowel obstruction normally presents with colicky abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting failure to pass stool/flatus occurring after this as the obstruction may be relatively proximal. Mostly commonly it is secondary to adhesions, hernias or malignancy. Other causes of small bowel obstruction include Crohn's disease, volvulus, and foreign bodies (including gallstone ileus and swallowed foreign bodies).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Toxic megacolon\",\"e\":\"Toxic megacolon presents as a complication to conditions such as inflammatory bowel disease or Clostridium difficile infection most commonly. It presents with severe abdominal pain, distention and signs of sepsis. With no antecedent history of a causative condition this option is not the most likely.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm\",\"e\":\"A leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm may present acutely with haemorrhagic shock, or subacutely less commonly. Additionally the rupture may be contained within the retroperitoneum or uncontained - the latter allowing for much greater and faster blood loss. Classic signs include abdominal pain, back pain, hypotension and a palpable expansile mass in the abdomen. This diagnosis could account for the patient's relatively shocked state, but does not explain the fever or dark stool.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Mesenteric infarction (Ischaemic bowel disease)\",\"e\":\"The history, signs and symptoms are classical of mesenteric infarction, which is part of the spectrum of ischaemic bowel disease. While ischaemic bowel disease is a spectrum of acute and chronic conditions resulting in inadequate blood flow to the bowel (either large or small), mesenteric infarction specifically implies an obstruction to blood flow of the small bowel (which is supplied by the mesenteric vessels) that has resulted in cell death. The risk factors include age, atrial fibrillation, previous myocardial infarction, heart failure and thrombophilia - the ischaemia then caused by an embolic event. Mesenteric infarction typically presents with persistent and generalised abdominal pain. Vomiting may or may not be present. Some patients may present with shock, with the shock being out of proportion to the clinical symptoms. The infarcted bowel may shed the 'non-viable mucosa', which mixed with mucus, results in dark-coloured (also known as plum-coloured) stools. The inflammatory markers such as the white cell count and C-reactive protein may be elevated Arterial blood gas analysis is a very useful investigation, which may reveal a metabolic acidosis. This condition is a surgical emergency as the patient rapidly become septic and may die from septic shock unless the infarcted bowel ('dead gut') is removed.\\n\\nThis condition typically presents with abdominal distension and failure to pass stool or flatus (absolute constipation), lower abdominal pain is also a frequent symptom. Their abdomen will be clinically distended but may not be tender. Complications include ischaemia of the section affected, perforation and peritonitis. Risk factors include being elderly, chronic constipation and male gender.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":191,\"stem\":\"A 74-year-old man presents with a 4-month history of vague lower abdominal pain, distension and constipation. He reports a change in bowel habit, a sense of incomplete evacuation of the bowel and his stools are mixed with blood. He has lost a stone in weight. Examination reveals a soft abdomen with a mass in the left iliac fossa. Abdominal X-ray shows a markedly dilated colon.\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Toxic megacolon\",\"e\":\"Toxic megacolon normally presents secondary to a predisposing condition, such as ulcerative colitis or C. difficile infection. It is an acute presentation. with abdominal pain and distension and signs of sepsis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction\",\"e\":\"The chronicity of the history makes acute colonic pseudo-obstruction unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sigmoid volvulus\",\"e\":\"Sigmoid volvulus usually presents with a rapid onset abdominal distension and absolute constipation. The chronic history to this presentation makes a sigmoid volvulus unlikely, it would also not explain the per rectum (PR) bleeding nor the weight loss.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Colonic carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Elderly patients with per rectal bleeding, change in bowel habit, and weight loss should be considered to have colonic cancer unless proven otherwise\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Diverticular disease\",\"e\":\"While diverticular disease can explain the patient's change in bowel habit and PR bleeding, it does not explain the patient's weight loss.\\n\\nThe clinical presentation of patients with colonic malignancy depends on the site of the tumour. Right-sided colonic carcinoma commonly presents with anaemia, tiredness, malaise, pallor and loss of weight, while the left-sided colonic carcinoma presents with change in the bowel habits, bleeding per rectum and intestinal obstruction. Rectal carcinoma, in addition to the features seen in left-sided colonic carcinoma, is associated with a sense of incomplete evacuation of the bowel (tenesmus). Increase in age is a risk factor for developing colonic cancer. The other risk factors, among others, include a family history of colon cancer, familial adenomatous polyposis, diet rich in red meat and long standing ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease. The investigation of choice for suspected colonic malignancy is a colonoscopy with biopsy of suspicious lesions. If this is not possible, usually due to patient's lack of fitness for the examination, then flexible sigmoidoscopy or computed tomography (CT) colonoscopy is usually the next line. Carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) is the commonly used tumour marker to diagnose colonic malignancy and subsequently to assess the progress, including recurrence.\\n\\nObviously, in a case of acute obstruction due to suspected malignancy a different initial set of investigations will be required, likely to include basic bloods and plain abdominal X-ray (AXR), with CT scan likely required acutely if surgery is contemplated. Dukes' classification for colorectal cancers is useful to be aware of, however, in clinical practice the TNM classification is now more frequently used\\n\\nClassification Disease extent\\n\\nLimited to muscular propria Duke A\\n\\nDuke B Extending beyond muscularis propria\\n\\nLymph node involvement Duke C\\n\\nDuke D Distant Metastatic spread\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":195,\"stem\":\"A 33-year-old man presents with a 6-week history of upper abdominal pain and diarrhoea. He reports a few episodes of 'dark-coloured' vomitus during this period. Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy reveals multiple ulcers in the stomach and duodenum.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carcinoid tumour\",\"e\":\"This is a neuroendocrine tumour most commonly found in the small bowel. It can cause local symptoms (eg in the bowel it can cause bowel obstruction, bleeding or diarrhoea), and symptoms as a result of release of hormones such as serotonin into the bloodstream (carcinoid syndrome - diarrhoea, abdominal pain, flushing, tachycardia, wheeze). They can be sporadic, but are also part of MEN-1. Diagnosis is by 24-h urinary collection to detect unusually high levels of urinary carcinoid breakdown products.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastrinoma\",\"e\":\"Gastrinomas, which occur primarily in the pancreas and duodenum, are malignant in nearly two-thirds of cases. The patients may present with upper abdominal/epigastric pain and vomiting; the vomitus may be 'coffee-ground' due to bleeding from the ulcers. Ninety per cent of patients with gastrinomas develop peptic ulceration* due to excess gastrin release by the tumour - gastrin stimulates gastric acid hypersection, which causes ulceration. Gastrinomas may either arise sporadically or as part of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (peptic ulceration, gastric acid hypersecretion and islet-cell tumour of the pancreas). Sporadic Zollinger-Ellison occurs most frequently in the fifth decade of life. Approximately 20% of patients with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome have MEN type I syndrome. An elevated basal gastric acid output > 15 mEg/h and a serum gastrin > 1000 pg/ml are suggestive of a gastrinoma. If it is difficult to make a diagnosis, a secretin stimulation test may be indicated. Lesions are localised by somatostatin-receptor scintography. A CT scan may be indicated to exclude metastases. The treatment of this condition is either conservative (high dose proton pump inhibitors) or surgical. Surgical resection may be aided by intra-operative ultrasound and/or intra-operative endoscopy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Somatostatinoma\",\"e\":\"This is a tumour of the delta cells (the cells responsible for production of somatostatin) of the pancreas. The clinical result of this is a result of the inhibitory actions of somatostatin on various pancreatic and gastrointestinal hormones: suppression of insulin - diabetes, suppression of CCK and secretin - steatorrhoea and gallstones, suppression of gastrin - hypochlorhydria.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vasoactive intestinal peptide tumour\",\"e\":\"VIPomas develop from pancreatic islet cells. Release of VIP results in sometimes profound watery diarrhoea, hypokalaemia and associated symptoms, abdominal pain and weight loss.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"ACTH secreling Lumour\",\"e\":\"ACTH tumours are normally pituitary tumours, however, exogenous ACTH production can result from other tumours (notably certain lung cancers). Excess ACTH production results in excess stimulation of cortisol production in the adrenals and therefore Cushing's syndrome.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":198,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old woman presents with a 3-month history of crampy lower abdominal pain, diarrhoea (12-15 times/day), mouth ulcers and loss of appetite. She has lost nearly a stone in weight. She smokes 20 cigarettes/day. Abdominal examination reveals a mildly tender mass in the right illac fossa. Abscesses with sinus formation are noted in the perianal region.\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)\",\"e\":\"This is an infection involving the uterus, fallopian tubes and ovaries. PID can be asymptomatic, may present with mild abdominal pain, vaginal discharge or bleeding, dysuria or dyspareunia, or with severe pain and sepsis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pseudomembranous colitis\",\"e\":\"Pseudomembranous colitis is usually caused by C. difficile infection. It presents with bloody diarrhoea, abdominal pain and clinical signs of infection. Patient's require isolation and antibiotic treatment - usually oral metronidazole or vancomycin, sometimes IV metronidazole is required in addition to oral vancomycin in severe cases.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"Ulcerative colitis is an inflammatory disease that affects the rectum and a variable but continuous length of large bowel proximal to this. It can also sometimes cause backwash ileitis. While it does have extra intestinal features, it does not typically affect other parts of the gastrointestinal (GI) tract making the patient's mouth ulcers and perianal disease atypical for the presentation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"The signs and symptoms in this patient are very suggestive of Crohn's disease. Crohn's disease can affect the whole of the gastrointestinal (Gl) tract, leading to ulcers in the mouth and perianal disease as classic presenting symptoms. The terminal ileum is the most frequently affected segment of bowel, explaining the patient's right iliac fossa (RIF) pain - the inflammation around this area can also result in a palpable mass. Low-grade fever, loss of appetite, loss of weight, anaemia and general fatigability are also very common. Diarrhoea may be troublesome, which is usually non-bloody and intermittent. If the colon is involved, patients may present with diffuse abdominal pain accompanied by mucus, blood and pus in the stool. Perianal fissures or fistulae (eg, entero-colic, entero-cutaneous), intra-abdominal abscesses and adhesions, and intestinal obstruction may develop with progression of the disease. Risk factors for Crohn's disease include a strong positive family history, a variety of food, smoking (increases the risk by three folds), and infective agents such as Mycobacterium and cell wall-deficient organisms such as Pseudomonas.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma of the colon\",\"e\":\"The clinical presentation of patients with colonic malignancy depends on the site of the tumour. Right-sided colonic carcinoma commonly presents with anaemia, tiredness, malaise, pallor and loss of weight, while the left-sided colonic carcinoma presents with change in the bowel habits, bleeding per rectum and intestinal obstruction. Rectal carcinoma, in addition to the features seen in left-sided colonic carcinoma, is associated with a sense of incomplete evacuation of the bowel (tenesmus). It should be most suspected in patient's with a strong family history or elderly patient's with a change in bowel habit, new iron deficiency anaemia or frank per rectal (PR) bleeding.\\n\\nTable: Clinical and pathological differences in Crohn's and ulcerative colitis\\n\\nTable: Clinical and pathological differences in Crohn's and ulcerative colitis\\n\\nCrohn's disease Ulcerative colitis\\n\\nAny part of the alimentary tract Affects the rectum, extending Segments from mouth to anus, most contiguously proximally a variable affected commonly the terminal ileum amount but limited to the colon\\n\\nPattern of Skip lesions Continuous disease\\n\\nCobblestones, aphthous and linear Pseudopolyps, very friable mucosa, Macroscopic ulcers, abscesses, luminal findings toxic megacolon narrowing\\n\\nTransmural inflammation, Microscopic Submucosal or mucosal inflammation, findings crypt abscesses granulomas\\n\\nCommon Abdominal pain, diarrhoea, weight Bloody diarrhoea, weight loss, symptoms abdominal pain loss, mouth ulcers, perianal disease\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":202,\"stem\":\"A 44-year-old woman presents with a 72 h history of abdominal pain and general malaise. She reports pale stools and dark urine. On examination, she is jaundiced with right upper quadrant tenderness: A magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatogram shows a dilated common bile duct (CBD), intrahepatic duct dilatation, a fistula between the gall-bladder and CBD, and a large calculus in the CBD distal to the fistula. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma of the head of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"This is the commonest malignant cause of obstructive jaundice classically causing 'painless jaundice' and presenting late. The acute history and tenderness is not typical for this presentation, additionally the imaging findings are not in keeping with this diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cholangiocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"This is a relatively uncommon malignant cause of obstructive jaundice. The acute history and tenderness is not typical for this presentation, additionally the imaging findings are not in keeping with this diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mirizzi's syndrome\",\"e\":\"Mirizzi's syndrome is caused due to impaction of gallstones either in the cystic duct or the Hartmann pouch of the gall-bladder, leading to compression of the common hepatic duct from the outside, resulting in symptoms of obstructive jaundice. Impaction of gallstones in the Hartmann pouch or cystic duct results in the Mirizzi syndrome either by:\\n\\n• chronic and/or acute inflammatory changes leading to contraction of the gall-bladder and stenosis of the common hepatic duct, or • cholecystocholedochal fistula formation due to direct pressure necrosis of adjacent duct walls from large impacted stones.\\n\\nPatients may present with pain over the right upper quadrant of the abdomen, vomiting, fever, recurrent cholangitis, cholecystitis or pancreatitis. Pale stools and dark urine result from obstruction of the flow of bile into the intestine. Treatment of this condition is exploration of the common bile duct by either open or laparoscopic cholecystectomy and placement of a T-tube.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hepatocellular carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) does not typically cause obstructive jaundice. In the UK it is normally found in patient's with underlying liver disease or cirrhosis, for example due to hepatitis B, C or alcohol. Aflatoxin ingestion is a notable cause in South-East Asia and sub- Saharan Africa - this is a toxin produced by fungal growth in certain grains. Presentation of HCC may be on routine screening of patients with known liver disease, with local physical symptoms such as right upper quadrant pain, or with signs of liver failure - either due to decompensation of underlying liver disease or massive infiltration of the liver by the carcinoma cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma of the ampulla of Vater\",\"e\":\"This is a rare cancer arising from the ampulla of Vater. It most commonly presents with obstructive jaundice, however, the acute history and tenderness is not typical for this presentation, additionally the imaging findings are not in keeping with this diagnosis...\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":205,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man, who drinks 60-70 units of alcohol per week, presents with severe abdominal pain and 2-3 episodes of haematemesis. He is pale with a pulse rate of 110/min. Examination reveals dilated anterior abdominal wall veins, ascites, a large spleen and a small nodular liver.\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chronic pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"Chronic pancreatitis can present either due to chronic insufficiency of it is exocrine function (causing steatorrhoea, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency and weight loss), endocrine function (most notably causing insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus) or due to distinct episodes of acute-on-chronic inflammation presenting with severe epigastric abdominal pain and often vomiting. Alcohol is the commonest cause of this condition in the UK\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)\",\"e\":\"In the UK this condition is normally found in patient's with underlying liver disease or cirrhosis, for example due to hepatitis B, C or alcohol. Aflatoxin ingestion is a notable cause in South-East Asia and sub-Saharan Africa - this is a toxin produced by fungal growth in certain grains. Presentation of HCC may be on routine screening of patients with known liver disease, with local physical symptoms such as right upper quadrant pain, or with signs of liver failure - either due to decompensation of underlying liver disease or massive infiltration of the liver by the carcinoma cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Amoebic hepatitis\",\"e\":\"Entamoeba histolytica can cause localised abscesses or a diffuse hepatitis. Presentation normally includes right upper quadrant tenderness and hepatomegaly, fever, and possibly jaundice. Bloods will show evidence of hepatitis. Liver infection will normally follow an episode of intestinal amebiasis/amoebic dysentery. Risk factors for this condition include old age, recent tropical travel and immunosuppression (including chronic alcohol use, steroids, HIV, etc.)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Alcoholic liver cirrhosis\",\"e\":\"Alcoholic liver cirrhosis can cause portal hypertension; which causes the stigmata this patient presents with. Portal hypertension is defined as an increase in the portal vein pressure of more than 10 mmHg (the normal portal vein pressure is in the range of 5-10 mmHg). Among other causes, cirrhosis of the liver is one of the important causes of portal hypertension and currently accounts for up to 90% of cases in the UK. As a result of the portal hypertension collateral channels develop between the portal system and systemic circulation - common sites for this include the lower end of oesophagus (resulting in oesophageal varices), distal rectum, and anterior abdominal wall (resulting in dilated tortuous veins in the anterior abdominal wall known as caput medusae). Bleeding from varies can be profuse and life-threatening - bleeding from oesophageal varies is particularly common particularly oesophageal varices leading to haematemesis and/or melaena Treatment requires urgent resuscitation, definitive management is usually endoscopic Some of the signs and symptoms of portal hypertension include abdominal pain, ascites, jaundice, splenomegaly and signs of cirrhosis (such as spider naevi, gynaecomastia, palmar erythema and testicular atrophy).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Myeloproliferative disorder\",\"e\":\"Myeloproliferative disorders encompass chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML), essential thrombocythaemia, polycythaemia (rubra) vera and myelofibrosis. They can all cause hepatosplenomegaly and portal hypertension (which explains this patient's presentation) - however, in a patient drinking as much alcohol as this portal hypertension caused by liver cirrhosis is the most likely cause.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":208,\"stem\":\"Gallbladder pathology commonly presents on the acute general surgical take. A spectrum of diseases exist.\\n\\nWhich one of the following clinical conditions is most likely to be associated with identification of Aschoff-Rokitansky sinuses?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following clinical conditions is most likely to be associated with identification of Aschoff-Rokitansky sinuses?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Acute cholecystitis is associated with increased vascular permeability and infiltration with acute inflammatory cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cholesterosis of the gall-bladder (strawberry gall-bladder)\",\"e\":\"Cholesterol-laden macrophages in the lamina propria of the gall-bladder mucosa are a feature of cholesterosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Chronic cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Chronic cholecystitis is associated with a fibrotic thick walled gall-bladdert. Within the thickened wall are Aschoff-Rokitansky sinuses, mucosal herniations often containing inspissated bilet\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mucocele of the gall-bladder\",\"e\":\"Mucocele of the gall-bladder normally occurs in a thin walled non-inflamed gall-bladder.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis is a rare form of chronic cholecystitis characterised by huge numbers of lipid-laden macrophages and giant-cells. Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis can easily be mistaken for carcinoma.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":210,\"stem\":\"Inflammatory bowel diseases are systemic conditions. The significance of other signs and symptoms can point more clearly to the underlying disease process. Which one of the following associated conditions is far more suggestive of ulcerative colitis than Crohn's disease?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following associated conditions is far more suggestive of ulcerative colitis than Crohn's disease?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ankylosing spondylitis\",\"e\":\"Arthropathy and ankylosing spondylitis are both features of ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. Ankylosing spondylitis is slightly more common in ulcerative colitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Arthropathy\",\"e\":\"Arthropathy and ankylosing spondylitis are both features of ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Erythema nodosum\",\"e\":\"Erythema nodosum is the commonest cutaneous manifestation of inflammatory bowel disease and occurs slightly more commonly in Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Primary sclerosing cholangitis\",\"e\":\"Primary sclerosing cholangitis is far more common in ulcerative colitis than in Crohn'st disease. It is characterised by a progressive fibrous inflammatory reaction within the biliary tree leading to irregularity with multiple stenosis and biliary obstruction. There is no apparent relationship between the duration or severity of ulcerative colitis and ultimate progression to liver failure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pyoderma gangrenosum\",\"e\":\"Pyoderma gangrenosum is slightly more prevalent in ulcerative colitis than Crohn's disease, however, primary sclerosing cholangitis is far more common still in ulcerative colitis.\\n\\nThe following table summarises several key differences between ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\\n\\nThe following table summarises several key differences between ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.\\n\\nCrohn's disease Ulcerative colitis\\n\\nAffects the rectum, extending contiguously Segments Any part of the alimentary tract from mouth to proximally a variable affected anus, most commonly the terminal ileum amount but limited to the colon\\n\\nPattern of Continuous Skip lesions disease Macroscopic Cobblestones, aphthous and linear ulcers, Pseudopolyps, very friable findings mucosa, toxic megacolon abscesses, luminal narrowing Submucosal or mucosal Microscopic inflammation, crypt Transmural inflammation, granulomas findings abscesses\\n\\nCommon Abdominal pain, diarrhoea, weight loss, mouth Bloody diarrhoea, weight symptoms loss, abdominal pain ulcers, perianal disease PSC, pyoderma Perianal disease (fissures, fistulae, tags, Extra- gangrenosum, arthritis and abscesses), mouth ulcers, oral aphthous ulcers, intestinal spondyloarthropathies, erythema nodosum, arthritis and features spondyloarthropathies, uveitis, episcleritis uveitis, episcleritis\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":214,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old woman with chronic abdominal pain was noticed to be pale at the time of assessment in the surgical out-patient clinic. Investigations showed that she had a decreased serum iron and total iron-binding capacity (TIBC) in association with a normal serum ferritin. These findings are most indicative of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"These findings are most indicative of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anaemia of chronic disease\",\"e\":\"The serum iron, iron-binding capacity and saturation are all low, but there is plenty of storage iron that is not being utilised so ferritin is normal. These symptoms are typical of anaemia of chronic disease - you would also expect to find a normal mean corpuscular volume (MCV) (normocytic).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia\",\"e\":\"In haemolytic anaemia the haemolysed cells release haemoglobin that is usually not lost from the body and is recycled, so the iron studies are usually normal. The patient's MCV will normally be increased due to reticulocytosis (macrocytic), while LDH and bilirubin will be increased and haptoglobin decreased.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Chronic blood loss\",\"e\":\"Chronic blood loss can lead to iron deficiency because of the loss of iron, with a low serum iron, high TIBC and low ferritin. The MCV is typically decreased (microcytic).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Malabsorption\",\"e\":\"Malabsorption, particularly in disease processes involving the duodenum, can occasionally explain iron deficiency anaemia, which has a high TIBC and low ferritin. Malabsorption involving the distal ileum can produce a megaloblastic anaemia through malabsorption of vitamin Biz (macrocytic).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vitamin Big deficiency\",\"e\":\"Vitamin B12 and folate deficiencies lead to a megaloblastic anaemia that is not associated with abnormalities in iron metabolism. The bone marrow contains megaloblastic cells, while peripheral blood contains large red blood cells (macrocytoss) and hypersegmented neutrophils:\\n\\nThe following table explains the expected results of iron studies in several conditions:\\n\\nIron TIBC TIBC saturation Ferritin MCV\\n\\nIron deficiency Low High Low Decreased Low\\n\\nNormal Normal Anaemia of chronic disease Low Low Low\\n\\nHigh Normal Low Low Acute phase response Low\\n\\nNormal Haemochromatosis/iron overload High Low High High\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":217,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man is diagnosed with right-sided synchronous colonic tumours, which are mucinous and poorly differentiated. His mother had died at an early age of ovarian cancer and his maternal grandfather had died aged approximately 40 years of advanced caecal\\n\\nFrom the following list of inherited syndromes which one is most likely to be prevalent in this family?\",\"lead\":\"From the following list of inherited syndromes which one is most likely to be prevalent in this family?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cowden's syndrome\",\"e\":\"This is an autosomal-dominant condition associated with multiple hamartomas (especially in the breast and thyroid tissue, skin and mucosal membranes), and an increased incidence of certain cancers (breast and endometrial in women, as well as thyroid and renal cancers).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Familial adenomatous polyposis\",\"e\":\"This condition is inherited, resulting in the development of 100s of polyps in the large bowel, with an associated increased risk of colorectal cancer: Additionally, patients are at increased risk of other cancers including pancreatic and stomach, and have other characteristic findings such as osteomata, sebaceous cysts and fibromas. A specific collection of signs/symptoms in familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is known as Gardner syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer\",\"e\":\"Hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC) is an inherited condition and is inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion - there are various genes that may be involved but they are all characteristically involved in DNA mismatch repair. Colonic tumours associated with HNPCC tend to have certain distinguishing pathological features. They occur on average 20 years before the peak incidence of sporadic tumours and are frequently synchronous and metachronous with a predilection for the proximal colon. They tend to be mucinous, poorly differentiated and 'signet-ring' in appearance. Ovarian cancer is associated with HNPCC along with cancers of the endometrium and stomach.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Juvenile polyposis\",\"e\":\"Juvenile polyposis is a genetic condition in which multiple hamartomas develop within the gastro intestinal tract (GI) tract with an associated increased risk in cancer of the Gl tract (from the stomach to rectum).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome\",\"e\":\"This autosomal-dominant condition is characterised by multiple patches of hyperpigmentation on the lips/oral mucosa and hands and feet, and hamartomas within the GI tract. Patients are also at increased risk of cancer in the pancreas, liver and lung, in men also in the testicles and in women the breast, uterus and ovaries.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":220,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old woman presents with right upper quadrant discomfort and elevated serum transaminases. A diagnosis of hepatitis C virus infection is made following detection of antihepatitis C virus antibodies.\\n\\nWhich one of the following represents the most likely subsequent event if left untreated?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following represents the most likely subsequent event if left untreated?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Complete recovery\",\"e\":\"In total, 25-50% of patients with acute hepatitis C will completely clear the virus and therefore make a complete recovery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Development of chronic hepatitis C\",\"e\":\"In total, 50-80% of those with acute hepatitis C will develop a chronic infection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"End stage liver disease\",\"e\":\"In total, 20% of those individuals affected by chronic hepatitist C will develop progressive cirrhosis and resultant end-stage liver failure. Additionally, hepatocellular carcinoma occurs in 1-4% of those with associated cirrhosis per year.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transmission to her monogamous partner\",\"e\":\"Transmission to monogamous partners and vertical transmission is uncommon and accounts for the minority of new cases of hepatitis C virus infection. Hepatitis C virus is transmitted by parenteral or per mucosal exposure to infected blood or body fluids. Many patients have a history of intravenous drug abuse or transmission of blood products before the implementation of antihepatitis C virus screening of blood donors in 1992\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vertical transmission to potential children\",\"e\":\"Transmission to monogamous partners and vertical transmission is uncommon and accounts for the minority of new cases of hepatitis C virus infection. Hepatitis C virus is transmitted by parenteral or per mucosal exposure to infected blood or body fluids. Many patients have a history of intravenous drug abuse or transmission of blood products before the implementation of antihepatitis C virus screening of blood donors in 1992.\\n\\nHepatitis C virus has risk factors for infection in the UK patient population including intravenous drug use with sharing of paraphernalia, blood product transfusion (before screening for HCV in 1992), tattoos or piercings, while sexual and vertical transmission are also routes they are less common. Acute hepatitis C is defined as being the first 6 months of infection after the incubation period, while chronic hepatitis C is an infection longer than this period. Most patients are asymptomatic during this period - however, testing for hepatitis C should be considered in patients with clinical features of hepatitis (RUQ pain, jaundice, flu- like symptoms, diarrhoea and vomiting), risk factors, or deranged liver function tests. The presence of HCV antibodies show whether a patient has ever been infected with HCV, while the presence of HCV RNA shows an active infection.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":223,\"stem\":\"You are in the general surgical clinic seeing a patient with histology results after biopsy of a lesion at the anal verge. The tissue sample is in keeping with a diagnosis of anal cancer.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding anal cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding anal cancer?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"First-line trealment is by radical surgery\",\"e\":\"Anal carcinoma is relatively radiosensitive Presently, combination radiotherapy and chemotherapy [fluorouracil (5-FU) = mitomycin or 5-FU + cisplatin] is used as initial standard first-line treatment. Abdominoperineal (AP) resection is reserved for recurrent or resistant tumours or in combination with neoadjuvant chemo-radiotherapy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It accounts for 50% of anorectal malignancies\",\"e\":\"Anal cancers account for approximately 10% of anorectal malignancies.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Most anal malignancies are squamous cell carcinomas\",\"e\":\"Of anal cancers, 80% of them are squamous-cell carcinomas. Rectal bleeding is the most common initial symptom (45% of cases); 30% of patients complain of pain or sensation of a mass.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Perianal papilloma is not related to the development of anal cancer\",\"e\":\"Perianal papillomas have been shown to predispose to the development of anal cancer. The human papillomavirus (usually type 16) may cause papillomas/intraepithelial neoplasia, which with time progresses to anal carcinoma. This virus is found in approximately 70% of patients. Other risk factors for anal carcinoma include a history of cervical or vaginal cancer, history of sexually transmitted disease and immunosuppression following solid organ transplantation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tumours above the dentate line spread to the para-aortic lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"The nodal metastatic locations depend on the lymphatic drainage, which is dependent on where the anal cancer originates: above the dentate line, flows to the perirectal and internal iliac nodes; below the dentate line, drainage is through the inguinal and femoral nodes. The most common site of distal metastasis is the liver.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":226,\"stem\":\"You are in the general surgery clinic reviewing a 27-year-old patient referred with an abdominal mass and you suspect colorectal carcinoma. They are subsequently referred for urgent colonoscopy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding colorectal carcinoma?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding colorectal carcinoma?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is associated with a high fibre, low fat diet\",\"e\":\"The opposite is true, a high fat and low fibre diet predisposes to colorectal carcinoma. Other risk factors include diet high in red/processed meat, obesity, increasing age, alcohol and tobacco use. Additionally other conditions such as inflammatory bowel disease, and inherited conditions such as familial adenomatous polyposis syndrome and hereditary non- polyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC) syndrome are significant risk factors.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is associated primarily with Krukenberg's tumours\",\"e\":\"Krukenberg's tumours are malignant metastases within the ovaries - their primary site is normally gastrointestinal (GI), primarily it is associated with stomach carcinomas spreading to ovaries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is best treated by abdominal-perineal resection if the tumour is up to 15 cm from the anal canal\",\"e\":\"Abdominal-perineal resections are used for low rectal tumours, where tumours are within 8 cm from the anal canal. Left-sided tumours above this point may undergo left hemi- colectomy, while transverse and right-sided hemi-colectomies may be used in these respective areas. Right-sided hemi-colectomy is also the operation of choice for caecal tumours. Radiotherapy is frequently used in rectal cancers too - either as neoadjuvant or adjuvant therapy, but not commonly in other cancers of the colon. Chemotherapy may be used in all sites of colorectal cancer, depending on type/stage.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It has a palpable mass on digital rectal examination in over 60% of cases of rectal tumours\",\"e\":\"Colorectal cancer presents as a palpable rectal mass in 60% of cases of rectal tumours. Other common symptoms of a rectal cancer include peri-rectal (PR) bleeding, change in bowel habit, tenesmus, or abdominal discomfort.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is found in the rectum in 10% of cases\",\"e\":\"Approximately 45% of colorectal tumours are found in the rectum.\\n\\nIt is useful to be aware of the Dukes' staging system for colorectal cancer (although this system has largely been replaced by the TNM staging system clinically it is a frequent exam question!). Please see the link for a comprehensive description of colorectal cancer staging by the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC, 7th edition). Modified Astler-Coller staging system:\\n\\n• Stage A: Limited to mucosa • Stage B1: Extending into the muscularis propria but not penetrating through it; nodes are not involved • Stage B2: Penetrating through the muscularis propria; nodes are not involved. • Stage C1: Extending into the muscularis propria but not penetrating through it. Nodes are involved • Stage C2: Penetrating through the muscularis propria. Nodes are involved. • Stage D: Distant metastatic spread.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":230,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing the results of a colonoscopy with a patient in the general clinic. They are concerned about their future risk of significant disease.\\n\\nWhich one of these conditions puts the patient at increased risk of colorectal cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these conditions puts the patient at increased risk of colorectal cancer?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"13. Haemormoids\",\"e\":\"Haemorrhoids are swollen or inflamed vascular structures within the anal canal. They can present with pain, itching and per rectal (PR) bleeding - the major risk factor for these is constipation or straining at stool, as well as being common in pregnancy. They do not increase the risk of colorectal cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hamartoma\",\"e\":\"Hamartomas are benign focal growths within an organ comprised of cells/tissue that normally arise from that organ but have developed into an abnormally structured growth. They resemble neoplasms but are do not have malignant potential. There are, however, syndromes in which multiple hamartomas are present that can result in increased risk of malignancy. Cowden's syndrome is characterized by multiple hamartomas throughout the gastrointestinal (GI) tract, skin, thyroid and other organs, and while the hamartomas themselves are benign, the patient has an increased risk of malignancy due to mutations in the PTEN tumour suppressor gene.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Angiodysplasia\",\"e\":\"Angiodysplasia are arteriovenous malformations within the gastrointestinal tract which may be single or multiple. They are usually asymptomatic but can present with acute episodes of painless bleeding or malena (depending on their position within the Gl tract). Chronic bleeding may lead to anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Solitary poye RY\",\"e\":\"Solitary juvenile polyps are benign hamartomatous polyps, most commonly found in the rectum and occurring before the age of 20 (although they can occur at any age). The term juvenile describes the polyp, not the age of the patient. They may present with rectal bleeding, but do not predispose to cancer. This is in contrast to juvenile polyposis syndrome where multiple mostly hamartomatous polyps are found either within the rectum and colon, or throughout the GI tract - with an increased risk of adenocarcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"There is malignant potential in ulcerative colitis - after 10 years the risk is around 2%, then 8% at 20 years and 18% at 30 years. Those individuals with ulcerative colitis for 10 years or longer are offered surveillance at between 1-5-yearly intervals depending on whether they are deemed low, intermediate or high risk of developing colorectal cancer.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":233,\"stem\":\"A 74-year-old Asian man presents with a 6-month history of weight loss and epigastric pain. An oesophago-gastro-duodenoscopy (OGD) shows an ulceration lesion in the fundus of the stomach.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true regarding the incidence of gastric carcinoma?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding the incidence of gastric carcinoma?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is decreased in patients with chronic atrophic gastritis\",\"e\":\"Chronic atrophic gastritis is a condition where chronic inflammation of the gastric mucosa leads to a gastric glandular atrophy and loss of parietal cells (therefore a decrease in production of peptic acid and intrinsic factor), replaced by intestinal metaplasia and fibrous tissue deposition. This condition can present with chronic stomach pain, nausea, vomiting and weight loss, and with a megaloblastic anaemia due to lack of vitamin B2 absorption. The commonest predisposing conditions are H. pylori infection and autoimmune gastritis. Chronic atrophic gastritis and resultant intestinal metaplasia both lead to increased gastric cancer risk.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is higher among women\",\"e\":\"The male to female ratio is approximately 2:1.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is highest in India\",\"e\":\"The highest rates of gastric cancer occur in Japan, Mongolia and Korea, while most cases world-wide are in developing countries.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is reduced in patients with blood group A\",\"e\":\"The incidence of stomach cancer has been shown to be 20% higher in those with blood group A compared to those with blood group O.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The incidence is declining world-wide\",\"e\":\"This statement is correct, the incidence of gastric cancer is declining world-wide. The incidence of gastric cancer in the UK is between 6500-7000 cases a year and is also declining, the highest risk age group being 85-90-year-olds.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":236,\"stem\":\"A 6-month-old male infant presents with fresh rectal bleeding (resembling redcurrant jelly). vomiting, abdominal pain and a palpable mass. The parents want to know whether or not their son will need an operation.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anal fissure\",\"e\":\"Anal fissures present with pain and fresh per rectal (PR) bleeding on passing stool, often noticed most on wiping rather than in the stool itself. The main risk factor is constipation. The pain is felt at the site of the fissure rather than intra-abdominally (although this may be difficult to determine in a 6-month-old infant. This condition would not explain the vomiting. 'redcurrant jelly' or the abdominal mass.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hamartomatous polyp\",\"e\":\"Hamartomas are benign focal growths within an organ comprised of cells/tissue that normally arise from that organ but have developed into an abnormally structured growth. They resemble neoplasms but do not have malignant potential. Within the gastrointestinal (GI) tract they may present with Gl bleeding, but the description of 'red currant jelly stool' is classical for intussusception.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inflammatory bowel disease\",\"e\":\"Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) could explain this patient's symptoms (Crohn's disease, for example, can present with an inflammatory right iliac fossa mass), however, it would be unusual to present in such a young child and usually presents between the ages of 15-25. It is by no means the most likely diagnosis offered here.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intussusception\",\"e\":\"Intussusception is a condition in which a portion of the bowel telescopes within an adjacent segment of bowel. Most commonly this is the ileum* entering the caecum. Sometimes an anatomical lead point is identified as causing the intussusception - this may be a Meckel's diverticulum, polyp or a hyper plastic Peyer's patch (ie from a recent viral infection). It classically presents with the triad of rectal bleeding, vomiting and abdominal pain. The rectal bleeding is often described as like red currant jelly. An abdominal mass (the intussuscepted segment) may be palpable.\\n\\nUltrasound is the investigation of choice initially, and treatment is with either barium or water-soluble enema (which will usually both visualise and reduce the intussusception). If this fails surgical reduction may be required.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rectal ulceration\",\"e\":\"This option is a rare, benign condition in childhood that usually presents with per rectal (PR) bleeding and mucous, lower abdominal and perineal pain, and constipation. It would not explain the patients abdominal mass or vomiting. The most likely diagnosis in this scenario is intussusception.\\n\\nOther causes of rectal bleeding in children not considered above include Meckel's diverticulum, vascular malformations, and Henoch-Schönlein purpura.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":239,\"stem\":\"A 20-year-old diabetic man presents to the surgical take with anal pain, pruritus ani and discharge. He has had repeat admissions for anal abscesses and is awa ting an examination under anaesthesia (EUA) for anal fistula\\n\\nOf the following statements regarding anal fistula, which one is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following statements regarding anal fistula, which one is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They always start from an abscess\",\"e\":\"A fistula is an abnormal communication between two epithelialized surfaces. Up to 80% of anal fistulas are due to an anorectal abscesses. Other causes include post-surgical complications, trauma, penetrating injury, tuberculosis (TB) infection or Crohn's disease\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are always associated with Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"They can be caused by Crohn's disease. Most, however, start from an abscess, while others develop insidiously or from causes such as haemorrhoidectomy, trauma, penetrating injury. TB infection or tumours\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are initially managed with antibiotics\",\"e\":\"Antibiotics are used in cases complicated by cellulitis or sepsis, or if the patient is immunocompromised. The treatment of the fistula itself in simple cases is with fistulotomy. while in other cases other techniques may be required/indicated such as the Ligation of the Intersphincteric Fistula Tract (LIFT) procedure or Seton sutures.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"In the case of complex fistulae the investigation of choice is a contrast CT\",\"e\":\"Complex fistulae are best imaged by a magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"When following Goodsall's rule, in the case of an anterior fistula the internal opening follows a direct path\",\"e\":\"Goodsall's rule states that an external opening lying anterior to Goodsall's line (the transverse anal line) is usually associated with a straight tract, whereas an external opening lying posterior to it may follow a curving tract*.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":242,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old diabetic man presents to the surgical take with anal pain, pruritus ani and discharge. He is awaiting an EUA for anal fistula.\\n\\nWhen looking back through his medical history what are you most likely to see of particular note?\",\"lead\":\"When looking back through his medical history what are you most likely to see of particular note?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma\",\"e\":\"This option is a cause, but not the most common.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"Fistula are common in Crohn's disease, however, because inflammation in ulcerative colitis is confined to the mucosa and submucosa (rather than being transmural as in Crohn's) fistula are not classically seen in ulcerative colitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Recurrent abscess\",\"e\":\"A fistula is an abnormal communication between two epithelialised surfaces. Up to 80% of anal fistulast are due to an anorectal abscesses. Other causes include post-surgical complications, trauma, penetrating injury, tuberculosis (TB) infection or Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Trauma\",\"e\":\"This option is a cause, but not the most common.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tuberculosis\",\"e\":\"This option is a cause, but not the most common.\\n\\n1299\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":244,\"stem\":\"A 24-year-old woman presents with a recurrent cyst over her natal cleft. She has had multiple previous incisions and drainage and has excessive scarring as a result. She is obese and hirsute.\\n\\nWith reference to the description of the patient above, what is most unusual given her diagnosis of pilonidal sinus?\",\"lead\":\"With reference to the description of the patient above, what is most unusual given her diagnosis of pilonidal sinus?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Age of patient\",\"e\":\"Pilonidal sinus is a condition predominantly affecting young adults between the ages of 20 and 40 years.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hirsutism\",\"e\":\"Risk factors include hirsutism, obesity, prolonged sitting as part of normal daily routine, family history, male sex, and deep natal cleft.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Female sex\",\"e\":\"Men are affected three to four times more often than women\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Recurrence of condition following surgery\",\"e\":\"If an abscess forms then treatment is incision and drainage, while chronic discharging sinuses or recurrent abscesses may require removal of the tract or laying it open and allowing it to heal by secondary intention. Reported recurrence rates vary but may be up to 50%\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Obesity\",\"e\":\"Patients may frequently be described as hairy and may also be obese.\\n\\nA pilonidal sinus is usually found in the midline in the natal cleft. The cause is unclear although a loose hair piercing the skin is thought to be attributable. These may be asymptomatic, or present acutely with abscess formation, or chronically with ongoing discharge.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":247,\"stem\":\"A patient is listed on the day case endoscopy list for surveillance colonoscopy for familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP). Your consultant quizzes you on this disease. How is FAP most usually diagnosed in children of affected individuals?\",\"lead\":\"How is FAP most usually diagnosed in children of affected individuals?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"DNA screening - gene present on short arm of chromosome 15\",\"e\":\"The gene is present on the short arm of chromosome 5.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Male children only of known FAP patients need will be screened\",\"e\":\"FAP, due to mutation of the APC gene, is an autosomal-dominant condition that affects men and women equally.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ophthalmoscopy or DNA screening\",\"e\":\"DNA screening is 95% predictive. In addition, it is important to note that many patients with FAP are found to have bilateral lesions of congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium (CHRPE) on ophthalmoscopy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sigmoidoscopy and biopsy after adenomas become symptomatic\",\"e\":\"Diagnosis in new patients with new mutations and no family history is usually by sigmoidoscopy and biopsy after the adenomas become symptomatic. If 100 or more adenomas are present, FAP is presumed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Such children are most likely to present with carcinoma of the colon or rectum\",\"e\":\"The risk of malignancy in FAP is almost 100%. However, this risk increases with age, and children rarely present with carcinoma. Polyps may be visible from age 7, but the risk of cancer is 7% at 21, rising to almost 100% by age 50.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":249,\"stem\":\"A 79-year-old patient who tested positive on faecal occult blood test, presents for sigmoidoscopy. A colonic polyp is noted.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements concerning colonic polyp is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements concerning colonic polyp is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hamartomatous polyps are made up of smooth muscle cells\",\"e\":\"Hamartomas are benign focal growths within an organ comprised of cells/tissue that normally arise from that organ but have developed into an abnormally structured growth - they resemble neoplasms but do not have malignant potential.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Over 25% of the population over 45 years of age may have asymptomatic adenomas\",\"e\":\"Approximately 25% of men and 15% of women over the age of 50 years may have an adenoma identified at colonoscopy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They usually bleed on contact\",\"e\":\"Benign adenomas do not usually bleed on contact, bleeding is more common if the polyp is malignant.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Their malignant potential is indirectly related to the size of the lesion\",\"e\":\"The guidelines from NICE recommend ongoing screening for all patients with polyps found on colonoscopy at 5-yearly intervals. However, if any of the polyps found are *10mm in diameter then this screening should be at 3- or 1-yearly intervals, depending on the number of polyps.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pseudopolyps can be inflammatory\",\"e\":\"An inflammatory polyp is an island of inflamed mucosa surrounded by ulcerated tissue, a classic example of this is in ulcerative colitis - called pseudopolyps.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":252,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old man presents to clinic with pain on defecation and point bleeding on toilet paper. You examine him and find haemorrhoids.\\n\\nWhat is the best conservative treatment you would advise for first-degree haemorrhoids?\",\"lead\":\"What is the best conservative treatment you would advise for first-degree haemorrhoids?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bedrest and analgesia\",\"e\":\"Thrombosed strangulated haemorrhoids may be treated conservatively with analgesia, bedrest, elevated legs and an icepack or by surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Elevated legs and an ice pack\",\"e\":\"Thrombosed strangulated haemorrhoids may be treated conservatively with analgesia, bedrest, elevated legs and an icepack or by surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"No treatment should be given\",\"e\":\"Asymptomatic haemorrhoids should not be treated but reassurance and advice regarding a high-fibre diet is important.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Surgery\",\"e\":\"Thrombosed strangulated haemorrhoids may be treated conservatively with analgesia, bedrest, elevated legs or an icepack. Symptomatic first- and second-degree haemorrhoids that have persisted despite medical intervention can be treated with rubber band ligation or injection sclerotherapy, whereas third-degree haemorrhoids are ideally managed by formal haemorrhoidectomy\\n\\nHaemorrhoids are swollen vascular cushions that affect approximately 40% of patients at some point. The most common presenting symptoms are noticeable prolapse and fresh rectal bleeding, they may also cause pruritus or pain. Risk factors include constipation and straining at stool, increasing age, or increased intra-abdominal pressure (eg pregnancy. belvic masses).\\n\\nHaemorrhoids can be internal or external, external arising below the dentate line. If internal they can then be described as first- through to fourth-degree:\\n\\n1st - Haemorrhoids within lumen of the anal canal, no prolapse.\\n\\n2nd - Haemorrhoids prolapse on straining but spontaneously reduce when straining is stopped. 3rd - Haemorrhoids prolapse but are fully reducible with manual pressure. 4th - Haemorrhoids are irreducibly prolapsed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reassurance and advice on eating an increased fibre diet\",\"e\":\"First-degree haemorrhoids may respond to increasing fibre in the diet or to bulking agents. Reassurance that symptoms are due only to haemorrhoids is important.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":255,\"stem\":\"A 74-year-old man is brought into the Emergency Department resuscitation unit acutely unwell with severe abdominal pain. On examination he looks poorly, has a weak pulse and is hypotensive and tachycardic. He has a diffusely tender abdomen with widespread guarding and an erect chest X-ray confirms air under the diaphragm. He is taken for a laparotomy and this shows a perforated diverticulum.\\n\\nWhich one of the following sites does diverticular disease most commonly affect?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following sites does diverticular disease most commonly affect?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ascending colon\",\"e\":\"Right-sided diverticula are present in fewer than 20% of cases in the Western population. However, in the Asian population, right-sided diverticula are more common, although this is changing with the adoption of a Western diet.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Caecum\",\"e\":\"Right-sided diverticula are present in fewer than 20% of cases in the Western population. However, in the Asian population, right-sided diverticula are more common, although this is changing with the adoption of a Western diet.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Rectum\",\"e\":\"Rectal diverticula are extremely rare, presenting in less than 1% of cases\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sigmoid colon\",\"e\":\"Almost 50% of cases affect the sigmoid alone while a further nearly 25% of cases affect the sigmoid and descending colon. In total the sigmoid colon is involved in over 80% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transverse colon\",\"e\":\"The most common site for diverticula is the sigmoid colon.\\n\\nDiverticulosis describes the presence of pouches of protruding mucosa through the colonic wall. They primarily occur in the large bowel where the longitudinal outer layer of muscle is arranged as distinct bands called 'taenia coli' that give rise to sites of relative weakness in- between the bands. Presence of diverticula increases with age - it affects approximately 50% by 50 years of age and 70% by 80 years of age. It is most common in the descending and sigmoid colon in the Western population, but can occur throughout the colon. The most important modifiable risk factor appears to be lack of dietary fibre and resultant constipation.\\n\\nDiverticular disease is the term used to refer to symptomatic diverticulosis - the most common symptoms being altered bowel habit (often alternating between constipation and diarrhoea) and lower abdominal pain. It is not a premalignant condition but can co-exist with colorectal cancers. Diverticulitis is an acute inflammatory exacerbation of pre-existing diverticulosis. This usually presents with left iliac fossa pain, fever and can also present with per rectal (PR) bleeding.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":258,\"stem\":\"A 51-year-old alcoholic is admitted with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, fevers, dehydration and jaundice. He undergoes scoring and it is deemed that he should be admitted directly to HDU.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a poor prognostic factor in pancreatitis according to Ranson's criteria?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a poor prognostic factor in pancreatitis according to Ranson's criteria?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amylase 2600 IU/l\",\"e\":\"Amylase+ (normal value < 200 U/I) is a useful diagnostic investigation for pancreatitis. It is not, however, prognostically useful.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Arterial p(Oz) of 7 kPa\",\"e\":\"This is correct - arterial partial pressure of oxygen of < 8 (< 60 mmHg) is one of Ranson's criteria. Ranson's criteria are used to predict severe pancreatitis* and a prognosis of approximate mortality. It was initially described for alcoholic (ie non-gallstone) pancreatitis* Several factors are assessed atpresentation* and again at 48 h* to give the full score. 1 point is given for each criterion present, and this allows the following prognosis:\\n\\n• 0-2 points = 1% mortality from this episode • 3-4 points = 15% mortality • 5-6 points = 40% mortality • 7 or more points = 100% mortality.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Corrected serum calcium of 2.6 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"Corrected serum calcium of ‹ 2.0 mmol/l scores a point in Ranson's criteria and would therefore indicate poor prognosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Leukocyte count of 5 × 109/l\",\"e\":\"A white-cell count greater than 16 × 109/| scores a point in Ranson's criteria and would therefore indicate poor prognosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rise in packed-cell volume by more than 10%\",\"e\":\"Packed-cell volume indicates the volume of red cells circulating as a percentage of total blood volume. It is raised most commonly in dehydration or in excess production of red cells - it is not part of Ranson's criteria. A fall in haematocrit at 48 h by > 10% from admission does score, however\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":261,\"stem\":\"A 63-year-old woman presenting with colorectal cancer asks about risk factors for the disease. She is due to undergo colonoscopy for change in bowel habit\\n\\nWhich of the following is associated with the greatest attributable burden of disease in colorectal cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is associated with the greatest attributable burden of disease in colorectal cancer?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)\",\"e\":\"Less than 1% of colorectal cancer is attributable to FAP\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC)\",\"e\":\"This accounts for approximately 2-4% of colorectal cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Other familial colon cancer syndrome (excluding FAP/HNPCC)\",\"e\":\"Inheritance is a factor in up to one-third of colorectal cancer cases; however, only 5% of colorectal cancers arise in the setting of an inherited colorectal cancer syndrome - this number includes FAP and NPCC, which are the most common.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pre-existing inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)\",\"e\":\"IBD-related colorectal cancer accounts for < 2% of all colorectal cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sporadic factor\",\"e\":\"Around 65% of colorectal cancers are thought to be sporadic - ie the genetic mutations present within these cancers are not inherited. Important risk factors in developing sporadic colorectal cancer include age, diet rich in red meat and low in fibre, smoking and obesity.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":263,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man presents with abdominal pain and diarrhoea, with episodes up to 12 time a day. He is diagnosed with Crohn's disease. Which one of the following is true of both Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of both Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are more common in urban areas than rural areas\",\"e\":\"Both Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis are more common in those living in urban areas compared with rural areas. The following table summarises key aspects of Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis.\\n\\nCrohn's disease Ulcerative colitis\\n\\nPrevalence 145 per 100:000 240 per 100 000\\n\\nAffects the rectum, extending Any part of the alimentary tract from mouth Segments contiguousiy proximally a to anus, most commonly the terminal ileum. affected variable amount but limited Commonly spares the rectum to the colon\\n\\nPattern of Skip lesions Continuous disease\\n\\nPseudopolyps, very friable Cobblestones, aphthous and linear ulcers, mucosa, featureless colon, Macroscopic 'rose-thorn' uicers, abscesses, luminal decreased haustrae, findings narrowing or stricturing shortened colon, toxic megacolon\\n\\nSubmucosal or mucosal Microscopic Transmural inflammation, granulomas inflammation, crypt findings abscesses\\n\\nCommon Bloody diarrhoea, weight Abdominal pain, diarrhoea, weight loss, symptoms mouth ulcers, perianal disease loss, abdominal pain\\n\\nPrimary sclerosing Perianal disease (fissures, fistulae, tags, Extra- cholangitis, arthritis and abscesses), mouth ulcers, oral aphthous intestinal ulcers, erythema nodosum, arthritis and spondyloarthropathies, features spondyloarthropathies, uveitis, episcleritis uveitis, episcleritis\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Any part of alimentary canal affected from mouth to anus\",\"e\":\"Crohn's disease can affect any part of the alimentary canal, with mouth ulcers and perianal disease common features. It classically produces skip lesions whereby different parts of the bowel are affected in a discontinuous manner. Ulcerative colitis affects the rectum and a variable but continuous amount of proximal large bowel. Backwash ileitis can occur, causing inflammation of the terminal ileum but otherwise the small bowel is not affected.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Small, shallow ulcers\",\"e\":\"Small, shallow ulcers are typical of ulcerative colitis, those seen in Crohn's disease tend to be very deep.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"In 30% of cases, only the ileum is affected\",\"e\":\"In Crohn's disease, only the ileum is affected in 30% of cases. Ulcerative colitis will not affect the ileum, except in some cases in which backwash ileitis can occur (less than 10%).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"60% of cases are confined to rectum and sigmoid\",\"e\":\"In ulcerative colitis, 60% of cases are confined to the rectum and sigmoid colon. Crohn's disease classically causes skip lesions throughout the entire alimentary tract from mouth to anus, but the rectum is commonly spared.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":267,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man presents with abdominal pain, cramps and diarrhoea. Inflammatory bowel disease is diagnosed. He has a had colonoscopy and is now having further investigations with a barium enema.\\n\\nWhich one of the following findings would suggest Crohn's disease rather than ulcerative colitis (UC)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following findings would suggest Crohn's disease rather than ulcerative colitis (UC)?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Affects rectum and spreads proximally\",\"e\":\"Crohn's disease can affect any part of the alimentary canal, with mouth ulcers and perianal disease common features. It classically produces skip lesions whereby different parts of the bowel are affected in a discontinuous manner. Ulcerative colitis usually affects the rectum and a variable but continuous amount of proximal large bowel, backwash ileitis can occur causing inflammation of the terminal ileum, but otherwise the small bowel is not affected.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cobblestone appearance of mucosa\",\"e\":\"Cobblestone appearance of the mucosa is classic of the macroscopic appearance of the bowel in Crohn's disease. This appearance results from deep longitudinal ulcerations interlaced with intervening normal mucosa. Barium enema is not appropriate in acute colitis due to the risk of perforation, which would be complicated by barium peritonitis. The gold standard investigation for diagnosis of ulcerative colitis is colonoscopy with biopsy. Distinguishing findings between Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis (UC) include the following:\\n\\nCrohn's disease Ulcerative colitis\\n\\n145 per 100 000 240 per 100 000 Prevalence\\n\\nAffects the rectum, extending Any part of the alimentary tract from mouth Segments contiguously proximally a to anus, most commonly the terminal ileum. variable amount but limited affected Commonly spares the rectum to the colon\\n\\nPattern of Skip lesions Continuous disease\\n\\nPseudopolyps, very friable Cobblestones, aphthous and linear ulcers, mucosa, featureless colon, Macroscopic 'rose-thorn' ulcers, abscesses, luminal decreased haustrae, findings shortened colon, toxic narrowing or stricturing megacoion\\n\\nSubmucosal or mucosal Microscopic Transmural inflammation, granulomas inflammation, crypt findings abscesses\\n\\nCommon Bloody diarrhoea, weight Abdominal pain, diarrhoea, weight loss, symptoms mouth ulcers, perianal disease loss, abdominal pain\\n\\nPrimary sclerosing Perianal disease (fissures, fistulae, tags, Extra- abscesses), mouth ulcers, oral aphthous cholangitis, arthritis and intestinal ulcers, erythema nodosum, arthritis and spondyloarthropathies, features uveitis, episcleritis spondyloarthropathies, uveitis, episcleritis\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Featureless lead pipe' colon\",\"e\":\"A featureless 'lead pipe' colon can occur in chronic ulcerative colitis in affected segments due to the inflammation and oedema resulting in loss of haustrae.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thumbprinting\",\"e\":\"Thumbprinting is a relatively non-specific sign suggesting colonic oedema, which may be present in ulcerative colitis. It is, however, a feature of plain radiographs, not of barium enema.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Small ulcers\",\"e\":\"Ulcers in Crohn's disease are typically deep because inflammation is transmural and the mucosa has a cobblestoned appearance.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":271,\"stem\":\"A 16-year-old girl presents with weight loss, abdominal pain and diarrhoe mixed with blood and mucus. Which one of the following statements regarding ulcerative colitis is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding ulcerative colitis is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It has a prevalence of approximately six cases per 100 000 of the population\",\"e\":\"The prevalence in the UK is around 240 per 100 000 population.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It has a positive family history with those with a first degree relative affected having a 15% lifetime risk of developing the disease\",\"e\":\"Those with a first degree relative have up to a 15% lifetime chance of developing ulcerative colitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is always confined to the rectum\",\"e\":\"Ulcerative colitis always affects the rectum with a variable involvement proximally in a continuous manner. It is confined to the large bowel with the exception of occasional cases of backwash ileitis, in which the terminal ileum is inflamed. Otherwise, it does not affect the small bowel.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is investigated by ultrasound as the investigation of choice\",\"e\":\"Colonoscopy with biopsy is the gold standard investigation for diagnosis and sub- classification of inflammatory bowel disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is not a pre-malignant condition\",\"e\":\"There is malignant potential in ulcerative colitis - after ten years, the risk is around 2%, then 8% at 20 years and 18% at 30 years. Those with ulcerative colitis for ten years or longer are\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":273,\"stem\":\"An overweight 32-year-old woman presents with a short history of painless jaundice. There is no previous history of illness and she has no other signs of chronic liver disease. Initial investigations reveal:\\n\\nResult Investigation Normal Value\\n\\n12.7 g/dl Haemoglobin\\n\\n105 fl 80-96 fl Mean Corpuscular Volume (MCV)\\n\\n162 umol/l < 21 umol/l Serum bilirubin\\n\\nAspartate aminotransferase (AST) 175 U/l 10-40 U/I\\n\\n224 U/I 30-130 U/I Alkaline phosphate (ALP)\\n\\n< 65 U/l 200 U/1 g-Glutamyltransferase\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A holic liver disease\",\"e\":\"Jaundice with an elevation of both AST and alkaline phosphatase suggests mixed hepatocellular damage and cholestatic liver disease, typical of acute alcoholic hepatitis on a background of chronic liver disease (and is not excluded by the lack of physical signs). The high g-glutamyltransferase lends support to this as it is typically elevated in alcoholism (although it may be increased in liver disease of any cause). A mild macrocytosis is typical of chronic excessive alcohol intake and is not a feature of the other conditions. AST and alanine aminotransferase (ALT) are enzymes released from hepatocytes when they are damaged; therefore, when hepatocellular damage predominates, these enzymes will be raised. ALP is typically raised where cholestasis is present.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Autoimmune chronic hepatitis\",\"e\":\"Autoimmune chronic hepatitis typically causes a hepatocellular damage. You would therefore expect AST to be raised with much lesser change in ALP\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Non-metastatic carcinoma of the head of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"In patients presenting with painless jaundice, a diagnosis of pancreatic cancer causing an obstructive jaundice should be considered. The mixed picture of cholestasis and hepatocellular damage, however, does not fit with pancreatic cancer which normally causes a cholestasis - the ALP would be expected to be elevated with much lesser AST elevation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Chronic cholecystitis can cause jaundice but it would be unusual for there to be no history of acute episodes. Additionally, the cause of jaundice in these patients is normally due to obstruction, either due to a common biliary duct stone or because of local inflammation putting pressure on the biliary tree - an obstructive/cholestatic pattern would be more suggested by a high ALP and normal or only mildly elevated AST.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hepatitis A infection\",\"e\":\"In hepatitis A, a hepatocellular picture develops so AST is typically higher than alkaline phosphatase. This infection causes hepatitis and results directly in hepatocellular damage.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":277,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man presents with retention. On examination, he is found to have a large smooth prostate.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements about prostatic pathology is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about prostatic pathology is true?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) affects 50% of men over 50\",\"e\":\"The proportion of men affected increases with increasing age, and the prevalence of lower urinary tract symptoms also increases with age. However, many of the 50% will have only mild or moderate symptoms, which are acceptable to them without treatment\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The severity of symptoms is related to the size of the prostate gland\",\"e\":\"The severity of symptoms is not related to the size of the gland but to the encroachment on the prostatic urethra and the resulting obstruction to urinary flow.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Finasteride, a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor, will decrease the size of the prostate gland within a week and therefore allow a trial without a catheter\",\"e\":\"Finasteride is an alpha-reductase inhibitor, which blocks the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone and reduces the size of the hyperplastic glands. A 6-month trial of treatment is required and symptoms may be reduced or delayed or even avoid surgery. Alpha-adrenergic blockers can be used to relax bladder neck muscle fibres and fibres in the prostate gland, and take effect within a week.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Benign prostatic hyperplasia will not increase prostate-specific antigen (PSA)\",\"e\":\"Normal levels increase with age. Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) can cause a small rise in PSA, but the higher the rise in PSA the more specific it is for prostate cancer: > 4.1 ng/ml = 9.1%, > 10 = 55.5%.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The aim of transurethral prostatectomy is to core out the whole prostate\",\"e\":\"The aim of transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP) is to debulk the prostate gland surrounding the urethra and leave the normal peripheral tissue.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":280,\"stem\":\"A patient with an extensive family history of colorectal cancer is seen in the outpatient clinic. They are concerned about passing on the condition to their future children. Which one of the following is true for a patient of familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true for a patient of familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"F no mutation can be identified then family members should be screened for the ondition using colonoscopy or flexible sigmoidoscopy from 12-11 years of age\",\"e\":\"The British Society of Gastroenterology guidelines have recently been updated (2019) and suggest that relatives of the patient who have a 50% risk of FAP should be offered large bowel surveillance 1-3 yearly (depending on phenotype) commencing from the age of 12-14 until the age of 30. From then, 3-5-yearly screening until they are 60 is advised. As FAP is an autosomal-dominant condition, the children of the patient will have a 50% risk of the condition, so should be offered screening as above. A specific collection of signs/symptoms in FAP is known as Gardner syndrome* . In addition, upper GI surveillance should begin for FAP patients at age 25.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Desmoid tumours occurring as part of FAP are benign and do not cause any problems\",\"e\":\"Desmoid tumours, which are solid tumours of the connective tissues, particularly within the abdomen, occur in up to 15% of people with FAP. Although they are not malignant, they can cause major problems by aggressively invading surrounding tissues. Desmoid tumours account for up to 11% of deaths in those with FAP.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Colonic cancer is the only malignancy associated with FAP\",\"e\":\"Individuals affected with FAP risk developing a duodenal malignancy, other small bowel cancers, thyroid cancer, pancreatic cancer, adrenal cancer, and hepatoblastoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"FAP affects 1 in 1000 people\",\"e\":\"FAP affects 1 in 10 000 people. Men and women are equally affected.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"FAP adenomatous polyposis coli (APC) gene shows an autosomal-recessive form of inheritance\",\"e\":\"FAP is autosomal-dominant. About 20-30% of cases seem to arise from new mutations.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":283,\"stem\":\"Inflammatory bowel diseases cause significant morbidity. Specific variants have different optimal management plans.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true about the differences between Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true about the differences between Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Both Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis start then spread from the rectal mucosa\",\"e\":\"Ulcerative colitis always involves the rectum with disease then spreading proximally throughout the colon. There can be backwash ileitis where there is some involvement of the terminal ileum, but ulcerative colitis is really a colonic disease. Ulcerative colitis remains confined to the rectum in approximately 25% of cases. In the remainder of cases, it spreads proximally and contiguously. Pancolitis occurs in 10% of patients. Crohn's disease, conversely, consists of segmental involvement by a non-specific granulomatous inflammatory process. The most important pathological feature is involvement of all layers of the bowel, not just the mucosa, and the submucosa as is characteristic of ulcerative colitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Crohn's disease has a clear demarcation between involved and uninvolved mucosa, whereas ulcerative colitis shows skip lesions with normal mucosa in-between\",\"e\":\"Crohn's disease is discontinuous, with skip areas interspersed between one or more involved areas. Late in the disease, the mucosa develops a cobblestone appearance, which results from deep longitudinal ulcerations interlaced with intervening normal mucosa. The three major patterns of involvement in Crohn's disease are:\\n\\n• disease in the ileum and caecum, occurring in 40% of patients • disease confined to the small intestine, occurring in 30% of patients • disease confined to the colon, occurring in 25% of patients.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The incidence of gallstones and kidney stones is increased in Crohn's disease but not in ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"Gallstones are formed due to an increased cholesterol: bile ratio, caused by reduced bile salt reabsorption in the terminal ileum. Patients who have Crohn's disease with ileal disease or resection are likely to form calcium oxalate kidney stones. Fat malabsorption from ileal involvement allows calcium to bind to the unabsorbed long-chain fatty acids in the lumen. Oxalate is usually bound to calcium, but calcium preferentially binds to fatty acids instead The rising unbound oxalate is therefore reabsorbed in the colon, predisposing the individual to calcium oxalate stones. The following table shows key differences between Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis:\\n\\nCrohn's disease Ulcerative colitis\\n\\nPrevalence 145 per 100 000 240 per 100 000\\n\\nAffects the rectum, extending Any part of the alimentary tract from mouth Segments contiguously proximally a to anus, most commonly the terminal ileum. affected variable amount but limited Commonly spares the rectum to the colon\\n\\nPattern of Skip lesions Continuous disease\\n\\nPseudopolyps, very friable mucosa, featureless colon, Cobblestones, aphthous and linear ulcers, Macroscopic 'rose-thorn' ulcers, abscesses, luminal decreased haustrae, findings shortened colon, toxic narrowing or stricturing megacolon\\n\\nSubmucosal or mucosal Microscopic inflammation, crypt Transmural inflammation, granulomas findings abscesses\\n\\nAbdominal pain, diarrhoea, weight loss, Bloody diarrhoea, weight Common mouth ulcers, perianal disease loss, abdominal pain symptoms\\n\\nPerianal disease (fissures, fistulae, tags, Primary sclerosing Extra- abscesses), mouth ulcers, oral aphthous cholangitis, arthritis and intestinal ulcers, erythema nodosum, arthritis and spondyloarthropathies, features spondyloarthropathies, uveitis, episcleritis uveitis, episcleritis\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Weight loss is more commonly seen in ulcerative colitis than in Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"Weight loss is more common y seen with Crohn's disease due to the loss of absorptive capacity in the small bowel. Ulcerative colitis is more likely to present with bloody diarrhoe: and symptoms suggestive of an inflamed rectum, such as tenesmus and urgency.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Fistulae are often seen in both diseases\",\"e\":\"Fistulae are not usually seen in ulcerative colitis because there is only mucosal and submucosal involvement in the large bowel. Crohn's disease, however, has full-thickness involvement of the bowel wall and can therefore form a fistula with nearby structures, eg recto-vaginal, recto-vesical.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":288,\"stem\":\"A patient with an acute abdomen is not responding to conservative treatment. A plain abdominal X-ray demonstrates a very distended large bowel. Which one of the following is true of toxic megacolon?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of toxic megacolon?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Toxic megacolon is due to large bowel obstruction causing dilatation of the colon\",\"e\":\"The hallmarks of toxic megacolon, a potentially lethal condition, are non-obstructive colonic dilatation larger than 6 cm and signs of systemic toxicity.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Toxic megacolon does not occur in Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"Toxic megacolon may complicate any number of colitides, including inflammatory bowel disease - which is the most common aetiology (around 50%). Other causes include ischaemic, infectious (Clostridium difficile), radiation, and pseudomembranous colitis. It is more common in ulcerative colitis but can also occur in Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"On abdominal X-ray a transverse colonic diameter of 8cm is significant\",\"e\":\"The transverse colon is often used as the point of reference for dilatation, its normal diameter = 5.5-6.5 cm. Loss of colonic haustra is also seen, and possible thumbprinting - a coarse, irregular, mucosal pattern of the large bowel.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mortality rates in Toxic megacolon of any aetiology are 40%\",\"e\":\"Mortality rates have been falling due to better care and earlier surgical involvement, and are now quoted as being 5-20%. Once perforation has occurred mortality rates are thought to increase five-fold.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"First line treatment is surgical resection\",\"e\":\"Initial management is medical. During the initial resuscitation, fluid and electrolyte replacement, and transfusion should be aggressive. Broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics should be initiated. All medications that may affect colonic motility must be stopped. These include narcotics, antidiarrhoeals, and anticholinergic agents. The patient should be put on bowel rest, and a nasogastric tube placed to assist with gastrointestinal (Gl) decompression. If the patient has underlying inflammatory bowel disease then this should be treated too. Should medical management fail then surgical resection will be required.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":291,\"stem\":\"You are looking after a young man on the Intensive Care Unit (ITU) with severe pancreatitis. Initially when he was admitted he was fairly well, but continued to deteriorate after 48 hrs into his admission.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding pancreatitis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding pancreatitis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Feeding via the total parentral route is preferred as it prevents over stimulation of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"Opinion has recently changed regarding feeding in pancreatitis. It is now best practice to feed by the enteral route via a feeding tube placed past the pancreatic duct into the 3rd part of the duodenum. For milder cases, patients can have oral intake as this is tolerated.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Serum amylase is a reliable marker of severity and progression of pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"Although amylase and lipase levels are useful in the diagnosis of pancreatitis they have no role in assessing severity - for example, neither is used in the Ranson scoring system used to prognosticate acute pancreatitis. Lipase rises in 4-6 h and normalises in 7-14 days. Amylase may be normal in 10% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The APACHE II scale is the most commonly used prognostic scale in acute pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"The APACHE Il is a scoring system designed to assess disease severity patients admitted to the ITU. It has been well studied in acute pancreatitis, but by design is an admission score designed to assess disease severity in all adult patients admitted to general ITU. It uses the patients age, and various physiology and biochemical/laboratory values within the first 24 h of admission. A higher score corresponds to a higher severity of disease and a higher risk of death from this admission. The most frequently used prognostic classification for patients with acute pancreatitis is the modified Glasgow score.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The BALTHAZAR scoring system assesses severity of pancreatis using serial ultrasound scans\",\"e\":\"The BALTHAZAR score is a computed tomography (CT) scan-based scoring system that is used to grade the severity of pancreatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"If infected pancreatic necrosis develops the mortality rate is approximately 25%\",\"e\":\"This is correct, this rate compares with that of mild acute pancreatitis, which has a mortality of around 1%, and sterile pancreatic necrosis at 10%. About 20-30% of patients with acute pancreatitis develop complications of necrosis or organ failure. Due to the wide spectrum of the disease, several systems have been developed in an attempt to provide reliable prognostic classification for patients. The most frequently used of these is the modified Glasgow score. Modified Glasgow score criteria:\\n\\n• P, (02) ≤ 7.9 kPa • age > 55 years • neutrophils (white blood cell (WBC) > 15) • calcium < 2 mmol/l • urea > 16 mmol/l • LDH > 600 IU/l • albumin < 32 g/L (serum) • blood glucose > 10 mmol/l.\\n\\nIf three or more of these criteria are present in the first 48 h of admission then severe pancreatitis is likely. Various scores can also be used to estimate mortality:\\n\\n• score 0 to 2: 2% mortality • score 3 to 4: 15% mortality • score 5-6: 40% mortality • score 7 to 8: 100% mortality.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":295,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old woman presents with jaundice due to pancreatic cancer. Her pain is minimal and attended as her husband noticed she had gone yellow. She has lost a significant amount of weight recently. Which one of the following statements is true regarding her jaundice?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding her jaundice?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is due to excess bile production\",\"e\":\"Jaundice is a result of excess bilirubin. While bilirubin is excreted in the bile, excess bile production would not be expected to result in jaundice.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is due to haemolytic anaemia\",\"e\":\"Haemolytic anaemia is an example of pre-hepatic jaundice - it is not the correct answer in this instance.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is part of a paraneoplastic syndrome\",\"e\":\"Paraneoplastic syndromes are effects of malignancy not caused by the local effect of the cancer itself - they are frequently hormonal or immunological. Examples include Cushing syndrome in small-cell lung cancer caused by ectopic adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) production, or Lambert-Eaton myasthenia syndrome caused by antibodies produced against small-cell lung cancer that are active against pre-synaptic voltage gated calcium channels.\\n\\nParaneoplastic syndromes associated with pancreatic cancer include Cushing syndrome, carcinoid syndrome and dermatomyositis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is post-hepatic jaundice\",\"e\":\"This is correct - neoplasms classically within the head of the pancreas can compress or invade and obstruct the common bile duct. This leads to an obstructive or cholestatic jaundice. Bilirubin is principally derived from the degradation of haemoglobin within the spleen and is carried to the liver bound to albumin, as in its unconjugated form it is not water soluble. Within the liver, bilirubin is conjugated by glucuronyltransferase, making it water soluble, and is secreted into the duodenum. At the distal ileum the bilirubin is either converted to urobilinogen and excreted in the faces as stercobilinogen, or is re-absorbed and renally excreted. Jaundice becomes clinically apparent at serum levels >35-40 umol/I_\\n\\nJaundice may be classified as pre-hepatic, hepatic, or post-hepatic, depending on the site of the obstruction or metabolic abnormality\\n\\n• Pre-hepatic (haemolytic) jaundice: caused by disorders resulting in increased destruction of red cells, eg autoimmune haemolytic anaemia or sickle cell disease which overwhelms the livers ability to process the excess bilirubin. The excess bilirubin is unconjugated so no bilirubin is detected in the urine. Hepatic (parenchymal) jaundice: caused by any condition affecting hepatocyte function, eg acute hepatitis infection, alcoholic hepatitis, drug-induced hepatitis, or cirrhosis of any cause:\\n\\n• Post-hepatic (cholestatic) jaundice: caused by physical obstruction of the biliary system eg carcinoma of the head of pancreas or gallstones. Typically there is dark urine (the bilirubin is water soluble, so is excreted in the urine) and pale stools (due to steatorrhoea from lack normal biliary function in aiding fat absorption).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is secondary to tumour lysis syndrome\",\"e\":\"Tumour lysis syndrome is a potential treatment complication of most commonly, haematological cancers. Rapid lysis of leukaemia or lymphoma cells results in metabolic abnormalities including hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia, hypocalcamia, lactic acidosis and hyperuricaemia. It is potentially life-threatening - however, it does not usually result in jaundice.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":298,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old woman undergoes an investigation for jaundice. She first noticed this symptom 2 months ago, but for 4 months previously she had been experiencing generalised pruritus. The results of liver function tests are as follows:\\n\\nNormal vlaue Result Investigation\\n\\nSerum bilirubin 325 umol/l 0-22 umol/l\\n\\n15-42 U/ mmol/l Aspartate aminotransaminase 55 U/l\\n\\nAlkaline phosphatase 80-150 U/i 436 U/l\\n\\n11-51 U/l 82 U/I y-Glutamyltransferase\\n\\n36 g/l 30-50 g/l Albumin\\n\\nTotal protein 60-80 g/1 82 g/l\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alcoholic cirrhosis\",\"e\":\"Alcoholic cirrhosis is a common cause of the above picture but is unlikely (although not excluded) by the absence of history of alcohol excess, and the only slightly elevated g- glutamyltransferase (which is sensitive, although non-specific, for alcohol excess).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma of the head of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"Carcinoma of the head of the pancreas frequently presents in this manner (although weight loss is often present also), but the slight elevation in total protein with low-normal albumin suggests a high globulin concentration, which suggests autoimmune liver disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cholangiocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Cholangiocarcinoma is a rare tumour, although could present like this. As with carcinoma of the head of the pancreas, weight loss is often present also, and the slight elevation in total protein with low-normal albumin suggests a high globulin concentration, which suggests autoimmune liver disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Primary biliary cirrhosis\",\"e\":\"Primary biliary cirrhosis frequently presents first with progressive and insidious onset of pruritus and general malaise/lethargy, jaundice occurring later in the disease process. It is more common in women than men (ratio 9:1), classically presenting between 40-60 years of age. It is an autoimmune condition associated with antimitochondrial antibodies, and the presence of excessive antibodies is suggested by the slight elevation in total protein with low-normal albumin (this suggests a high globulin concentration). The disease process results in progressive destruction of the intrahepatic bile ducts, and therefore an obstructive/cholestatic jaundice supported by the raised alkaline phosphatase (ALP), but relatively normal aspartate transaminase (AST).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Primary sclerosing cholangitis\",\"e\":\"Primary sclerosing cholangitis is a possibility but is commoner in men than women (3:1), and in 75% of cases are associated with inflammatory bowel disease. In this case primary biliary cirrhosis is more likely\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":302,\"stem\":\"A 20-year-old man presents with mild jaundice following a flu-like illness. Following review by a gastroenterologist, he has been told that a diagnosis of Gilbert syndrome is probable. Which laboratory test is most likely to confirm this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which laboratory test is most likely to confirm this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Absence of bilirubin in the urine\",\"e\":\"Gilbertsyndrome is an inherited, autosomal-recessive condition resulting in abnormal liver metabolism of bilirubin. It is relatively commor affecting around 5% of the population. Gilbert syndrome is normally asymptomatic, but mild jaundice may develop when there is physiological stress (eg infection or fasting). In Gilbert syndrome, the excess bilirubin is unconjugated, and does not appear in the urine. You would also not expect to find bilirubin the urine in other causes of pre-hepatic jaundice, such as haemolytic disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased serum haptoglobin concentration\",\"e\":\"Decreased serum haptoglobin concentration is a sign of haemolytic anaemia. Haptoglobin will bind free haemoglobin released by lysed red cells and this complex is then removed by the spleen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Elevated serum aspartate aminotransferase (transaminase, AST) activity\",\"e\":\"Elevated AST is a non-specific sign of hepatocellular damage - the excess activity being detected in the blood a result of the release of these enzymes from their normal intracellular site within hepatocytes. This would be raised, for example, in alcoholic hepatitis, viral hepatitis, or drug-induced liver damage. Haemolysis may also cause a slight increase in serum aminotransferase (transaminase) activity as red blood cells also have a small amount of AST within them. AST is also found in skeletal muscle among other cell types.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased reticulocyte count\",\"e\":\"This is a sign of excess haemolysis - the rapid destruction of red blood cells resulting in rapid production of red blood cells and therefore an increased number of reticulates in the peripheral blood count.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased urinary urobilinogen excretion\",\"e\":\"However, in haemolytic jaundice, urinary urobilinogen is increased (increased production of bilirubin, and hence of urobilinogen), the reticulocyte count may be elevated and serum haptoglobin concentration decreased. Bilirubin is principally derived from the degradation of haemoglobin within the spleen and is carried to the liver bound to albumin, as in its unconjugated form it is not water soluble. Within the liver bilirubin is conjugated by glucuronyltransferase, making it water soluble, and is secreted into the duodenum. At the distal ileum the bilirubin is either converted to urobilinogen and excreted in the faces as stercobilinogen, or is re-absorbed and renally excreted. Jaundice becomes clinically apparent at serum levels >35-40 umol/l. Jaundice may be classified as pre-hepatic, hepatic, or post-hepatic, depending on the site of the obstruction or metabolic abnormality.\\n\\n• Pre-hepatic (haemolytic) jaundice: caused by disorders resulting in increased destruction of red cells eg autoimmune haemolytic anaemia or sickle cell disease which overwhelms the livers ability to process the excess bilirubin. The excess bilirubin is unconjugated so no bilirubin is detected in the urine. • Hepatic (parenchymal) jaundice: caused by any condition affecting hepatocyte function eg acute hepatitis infection, alcoholic hepatitis, drug-induced hepatitis, or cirrhosis of any cause. • Post-hepatic (cholestatic) jaundice: caused by physical obstruction of the biliary system eg carcinoma of the head of pancreas or gallstones. Typically there is dark urine (the bilirubin is water soluble, so is excreted in the urine) and pale stools (due to steatorrhoea from lack normal biliary function in aiding fat absorption).\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":306,\"stem\":\"You are asked to see an 82-year-old woman under the medical team with a lower respiratory tract illness and non-tender abdominal distension. There are dilated loops on her abdominal film. Blood tests reveal:\\n\\nResult Normal value Investigation\\n\\n138 mmol/l 135-145 mmol/l Sodium (Nat)\\n\\nPotassium (K=) 3.1 mmol/| 3.5-5 mmol/l\\n\\n10 mmol/l 2.5-6.5 mmol/l Urea\\n\\n50-120 umol/l 118 umol/l Creatinine\\n\\n< 5/mm= White cell count (WCC) 17/mmz\\n\\n< 3 mg/di C-reactive protein 11.5 mg/dl\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Colorectal cancer\",\"e\":\"Colorectal cancer, particularly left-sided lesions, can cause and therefore present with obstruction, which would give abdominal distension and dilated bowel loops. However, mechanical obstruction is frequently painful. Usually colorectal cancer presents insidiously with a change in bowel habit or PR bleeding, or with an otherwise unexplained microcytic anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"DAS Constipation\",\"e\":\"Constipation is a frequent complication for patients in hospital, both because of medication used (for example opiates) and because many patients are relatively immobile. In patients with constipation abdominal distension can occur. Frequently patients will have left iliac fossa pain. Dilated loops would not be typical and faecal loading may be seen in the colon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diverticulitis\",\"e\":\"Diverticulitis refers to inflammation of diverticulae, which are most frequently found in the sigmoid colon. It normally presents with left iliac fossa pain and fever, possibly with PR bleeding. Inflammatory markers would be elevated, although in this instance these are more likely to be explained by the already diagnosed lower respiratory tract infection.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pseudo-obstruction\",\"e\":\"Pseudo-obstruction is an example of non-mechanical bowel obstruction. It can be acute or chronic. Acute is also known as Ogilvie syndrome. In 80% of cases, colonic pseudo- obstruction is associated with other clinical conditions. The most common are cardio- respiratory illness, post-trauma or pelvic surgery, It usually presents as this clinical vignette with abdominal distension in the context of another acute condition, intermittent constipation and nausea or vomiting are frequent symptoms, colicky abdominal pain may be present. Imaging will reveal dilated bowel loops. Treatment should be based on correcting the associated cause such as the respiratory illness or correcting electrolyte imbalances - usually potassium, magnesium or phosphate. This may resolve the 'obstruction'. Otherwise consider endoscopic decompression. Neostigmine has also been used in some chronic cases.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Small bowel obstruction\",\"e\":\"Small bowel obstruction usually presents with vomiting, absolute constipation, and painful abdominal distension. The commonest causes are intra-abdominal adhesions and hernias, other causes include foreign bodies, gallstone ileus, volvulus, neoplasms and strictures (eg from previous ischaemia or from Crohn's disease). In small bowel obstruction you will usually see dilated small bowel loops proximal to the point of obstruction.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":310,\"stem\":\"A 62-year-old woman undergoes a gastric bypass. She attends the surgical assessment unit feeling unwell, is seen by the consultant and told she has dumping syndrome. She is unsure what this means and you have been asked to explain it to her.\\n\\nWhat are the likely symptoms that she has been suffering?\",\"lead\":\"What are the likely symptoms that she has been suffering?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Collapse and chest pain\",\"e\":\"This is not correct.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left upper quadrant pain, diarrhoea, chest pain\",\"e\":\"This is not correct.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pain before eating, dizziness and nausea\",\"e\":\"This is not correct, in particular symptoms occur after eating.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Postprandial pain, vomiting and dizziness\",\"e\":\"Dumping syndrome following gastrectomy is due to an absent gastric reservoir, abnormal postoperative gastric motor function and hormonal secretion alteration. After eating, symptoms are related to the rapid transit of hyperosmolar contents into the small bowel. It can be split into early dumping syndrome, occurring within 10-30 min after a meal, and late dumping syndrome, which occurs after 2-3 h. Early dumping syndrome results from rapid osmotic fluid shifts from the blood into the intestine resulting in decreased total blood volume and intestinal distension. Late dumping syndrome results from rapid absorption of sugar from the intestine followed by a rapid increase in insulin levels and a subsequent hypoglycaemic episode. Early dumping syndrome symptoms include abdominal pain, vomiting and nausea, diarrhoea, sweating and flushing, and dizziness: Late dumping syndrome symptoms include sweating, weakness, flushing and dizziness. Treatment is largely through managing dietary intake with several small meals a day (instead of large meals) and avoiding simple sugars. Additionally, liquids should be drunk in between meals not alongside meals. A high fibre diet can also help. Octreotide and proton pump inhibitors (PPls) can also help to slow gastric emptying if conservative management fails.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Shortness of breath and chest pain\",\"e\":\"This is not correct.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":313,\"stem\":\"A patient is undergoing a right hemicolectomy for a caecal tumour. The ileocaecal valve and part of the terminal ileum are resected before the anastomosis between ileum and transverse colon is made. Resection of the terminal ileum is most commonly associated with the malabsorption of which one of the following components?\",\"lead\":\"Resection of the terminal ileum is most commonly associated with the malabsorption of which one of the following components?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bile salts\",\"e\":\"Bile salts are primarily re-absorbed in the terminal ileum - loss of bile salts in faces has several knock-on effects. Bile salts released in the bile are normally responsible for solubilising dietary fat - so excess loss of bile salts results in fat malabsorption. One important effect of this is malabsorption of the fat soluble vitamins A, D, E and K. Additionally the terminal ileum is the primary site for absorption of vitamin B12 - loss of which results in a macrocytic anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"LAI Calcium\",\"e\":\"Calcium and folic acid are mainly absorbed in the jejunum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cholesterol\",\"e\":\"Dietary fat, including cholesterol, is primarily absorbed in the jejunum. However, excess loss of bile salts, which are absorbed in the ileum, can lead to fat malabsorption in the jejunum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Folic acid\",\"e\":\"Calcium and folic acid are mainly absorbed in the jejunum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sodium\",\"e\":\"Sodium is re-absorbed throughout the small bowel.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":315,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man patient is admitted to the surgical assessment unit with severe colicky right upper quadrant pain, he is not febrile and has no abdominal tenderness. His past medical history consists of Crohn's disease, previous laparotomy for small bowel resection and asthma.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely risk factor responsible for his symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely risk factor responsible for his symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"Explanation J\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Age\",\"e\":\"Increasing age is a risk factor for developing galistones, classically they become more likely especially above the age of 40.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diet\",\"e\":\"Dietary factors are not a major risk factor for developing gallstones.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lack of bile salts\",\"e\":\"This patient has gallstones because of a lack of bile salts which are usually involved in fat absorption. He has Crohn's disease, which resulted in a small bowel resection. This is most commonly in the terminal ileum where bile salts are most predominantly absorbed. Bile salts aid with the absorption of fat by forming micelles. Without the presence of bile salts in bile, cholesterol will more readily sediment out to form gallstones. Other risk factors include obesity, female sex, increasing age, diabetes mellitus, and premenopausal status or use of HRT/oral contraceptives.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased haemosiderin\",\"e\":\"Haemosiderin is a complex that stores ferritin; deposits are most commonly associated with iron overload conditions, such as hereditary haemochromatosis. They are not a risk factor for gallstones\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sex\",\"e\":\"Gallstones are more common in women than in men.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":318,\"stem\":\"A b8-year-old man presents with jaundice and itch. MRCP is taken which demonstrates a clear biliary tree.\\n\\nWhich one of the following clinical and biochemical characteristics of hepatic jaundice is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following clinical and biochemical characteristics of hepatic jaundice is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dark urine\",\"e\":\"The urine will be dark because, although primarily an unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia, hepatic jaundice also results in an elevation in conjugated bilirubin that is excreted in the urine giving it a darker colour.\\n\\nHepatic Pre-hepatic Post-hepatic\\n\\nVariable Variable Usually mild Jaundice\\n\\nUrine Dark Normal Dark\\n\\nNormal/Slightly pale Faeces Normal Pale\\n\\nUnconjugated Unconjugated and conjugated Serum bilirubin Conjugated\\n\\nSerum Normal Normal or increased Increased++ transaminases\\n\\nIncreased++ Serum ALP Normal Normal or increased\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Dark stool\",\"e\":\"Stool will be normal colour or slightly pale in hepatic jaundice - the normal brown colour of faces results from bile pigments, the metabolic products produced by bacteria metabolising excreted bilirubin. As less bilirubin enters the digestive tract there may be less pigment produced and therefore slightly pale stools.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A rise in conjugated serum bilirubin only\",\"e\":\"In the liver bilirubin is conjugated with glucuronic acid to make it water-soluble and allow its excretion into the bile. When there is significant hepatocellular damage or dysfunction as in hepatic jaundice this process is disrupted, resulting in a build up of unconjugated bilirubin within the body. Additionally, the inflammation and swelling within the liver results in some obstruction of the intrahepatic biliary tree - as a result even the bilirubin that is successfully metabolised will not be efficiently excreted and you will get a rise in conjugate bilirubin as well as the primarily unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Serum transaminase is normal\",\"e\":\"Release of intracellular transaminases from hepatocellular damage results in increased aspartate aminotransferase (AST) and alanine aminotransferase (ALT) concentrations in the serum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serum alkaline phosphatase is mildly decreased\",\"e\":\"Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) serum concentrations are increased primarily due to biliary tree obstruction. The greatest rises in this enzyme activity are detected in cholestatic/obstructive/post-hepatic jaundice. However, hepatocellular damage can result in increased release of this enzyme into the bloodstream, and the inflammation resulting within the liver can also result in some compression and obstruction of the intrahepatic biliary tree resulting in another cause for ALP increase. As a result, while serum ALP may be normal in hepatic jaundice, it may also be elevated though usually not as greatly as the transaminases in the same case.\\n\\n20.11\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":321,\"stem\":\"A 61-year-old woman presents with persistent vomiting of largely undigested food. She has lost a significant amount of weight, in the A&E department she is now in extremis.\\n\\nProlonged vomiting in gastric outflow obstruction is associated with which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Prolonged vomiting in gastric outflow obstruction is associated with which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased antidiuretic hormone (ADH) secretion\",\"e\":\"Loss of fluid and inability to take in oral fluids will result in dehydration and loss of intravascular fluid volume and therefore increased ADH secretion as the body tries to maintain its intravascular fluid volume.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypochloraemic alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Prolonged vomiting causes a hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis. The reason for this is that Ht and Cl-, from gastric secretions within the stomach, are lost and in addition the alkaline pancreatic and duodenal secretions are retained. The loss of H- obviously directly leads to alkalosis, however the loss of Cl also results in retention of the other major anion in the body, HCO= (bicarbonate). All together this results in a hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis. The loss of fluid volume causes a physiological response that includes sympathetic stimulation, increase in ADH secretion and activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. In small bowel obstruction, there is also loss of alkaline intestinal secretions, which prevents the development of metabolic alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"The loss of H+ and Cl- from gastric secretions results in hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Respiratory alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Respiratory alkalosis results from hyperventilation and therefore excess removal of carbon dioxide from the body, while the patient will be alkalotic this is not due to a respiratory cause.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemia\",\"e\":\"The patient will most likely become hypokalaemic. A relatively small amount of potassium will be directly lost in the vomit. However, more importantly, the patient will become alkalotic (metabolic alkalosis) - alkalosis results in a transcellular exchange of potassium going into the cells and hydrogen ions leaving the intracellular compartment, the increased reabsorption of H* in the kidneys also results in increased exchange of potassium into the urine. Finally the patient's hypovolaemia and resultant increase in aldosterone production also results in loss of potassium at the kidneys.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":324,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old man presents with a long history of intermittent abdominal discomfort and diarrhoea. On examination, he has a body mass index of 19 kg/m and is clinically anaemic. Coeliac disease is suspected.\\n\\nWhich one of the following investigations will most reliably diagnose this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following investigations will most reliably diagnose this condition?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Detection of antigliadin antibodies in serum\",\"e\":\"Sensitivity and specificity among studies for this test is highly variable but generally less than endomysial antibodies (EMA) and tissue transglutaminase (TTG) antibodies:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Detection of endomysial antibodies (EMA) in serum\",\"e\":\"Sensitivity and specificity are >95% making this a very good screening test, however, biopsy remains the gold standard. Endomysial antibodies have been identified as binding to a specific form of tissue transglutaminase within the endomysium.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Detection of tissue transglutaminase (TTG) antibodies in serum\",\"e\":\"Again sensitivity and specificity are >95% therefore this test is good for screening - but biopsy is the gold standard\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Microscopic examination of a small bowel biopsy specimen\",\"e\":\"The detection of the typical appearance of subtotal villous atrophy on a small bowel biopsy is regarded as the 'gold standard' for the diagnosis of coeliac disease. Other changes include increased numbers lymphocytes and crypt hyperplasia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Xylose absorption test\",\"e\":\"The xylose absorption test is a non-specific test for intestinal malabsorption. 2050\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":327,\"stem\":\"A. 21-vear-old male medical student who has been feeling non-specifically unwell for several days is noticed to have slightly icteric sclerae by his girlfriend and has liver function tests performed. The results of these are normal apart from a serum bilirubin concentration of 44 umol/L (normal range 3-17 umol/L). His urine does not contain bilirubin.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dubin-Johnson syndrome\",\"e\":\"Dubin-Johnson syndrome is an autosomal-recessive condition causing a conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia due to a defect in the secretion of bilirubin from the liver. The bilirubin that then accumulates in the plasma is conjugated, water-soluble and so is excreted in the urine. One key difference between this and Rctor syndrome is that in Dubin-Johnson syncrome the liver is classically pigmented black.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gilbert syndrome\",\"e\":\"Gilbert syndrome is a common (approximately 1:20) autosomal-recessive cause of mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia. This occurs due to defective conjugation within the liver. Normally mild jaundice develops when affected patients develop concomitant infections, are fasting or have other physiological stressors. As the hyperbilirubinaemia is unconjugated it will not be present in the urine.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hereditary spherocytosis\",\"e\":\"Hereditary spherocytosis is a chronic haemolytic disorder due to a defect in the red-ce!! membrane (most frequently in spectrin, a structural protein). It can present with a wide range of severity, from jaundice at birth to asymptomatic anaemia or jaundice in adults. It is pre-hepatic jaundice and will therefore be unconjugated with no bilirubin in the urine. However, it is much less common than Gilbert's syndrome so less likely (approximately 1:5000 in Northern Europeans).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Infectious mononucleosis\",\"e\":\"Infectious mononucleosis (IM) can cause hepatitis and jaundice but an elevated transaminase activity would be expected. It normally presents with a tonsillitis, general malaise, and cervical lymphadenopathy - the absence of these makes IM unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rotor syndrome\",\"e\":\"Rotor syndrome is an autosomal-recessive condition, causing a conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia due to defective transporter proteins from hepatocytes into the biliary tree. As the bilirubin that accumulates in the plasma is conjugated, water-soluble and therefore excreted in the urine.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":330,\"stem\":\"A 62-year-old man presents to his GP as his family have noticed he has 'gone yellow'. On further questioning he admits to generalised pruritus and around 2 stone of weight loss. He denies any abdominal or chest pain. On examination you note he has icteric sclera and widespread scratch marks.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Biliary colic\",\"e\":\"This normally presents with sudden onset, colicky right upper quadrant pain. Often associated with recent ingestion of large/fatty meals. It is unusual for jaundice to develop unless a gallstone has entered to common bile duct, or occasionally a gallstone within the neck of the gall-bladder or Hartmann's pouch can compress the common bile duct (CBD) and cause an obstructive jaundice (known as Mirizzi's syndrome). The absence of pain and insidious onset strongly goes against this diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ascending cholangitis\",\"e\":\"Ascending cholangitis is a medical emergency that can be recognised by Charcot's triad of fever, right upper quadrant abdominal pain and jaundice. This insidious presentation does not fit this diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Cholecystitis normally presents with constant right upper quadrant pain and fever, classically the patient is Murphy's positive in which deep inspiration while palpating the right upper quadrant (RUQ) results in the patient 'catching their breath' as the inflamed gall-bladder is lowered onto the palpating hand. Jaundice is uncommon unless a stone within the neck of the gall-bladder or Hartmann's pouch is compressing the CBD and can cause an obstructive jaundice (known as Mirizzi's syndrome), additionally the localised inflammation and swelling around the gall-bladder can result in compression of the CBD and therefore an obstructive jaundice.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gallstone ileus.\",\"e\":\"Gallstone ileus occurs when a gallstone enters the small bowel, usually through a fistula connecting the gall-bladder and small intestine. It can then cause a mechanical small bowel obstruction. It does not cause jaundice.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pancreatic cancer:\",\"e\":\"Pancreatic cancer classically presents insidiously with painless jaundice, often with a history of weight loss as the cancer usually presents at late stage. These cancers tend to be cancers of the head of the pancreas as it is through this portion of the pancreas that the CBD traverses. As this is the only condition listed that typically causes painless jaundice it is the correct answer. Obstructive jaundice classically produces dark urine and paler stools and it would be useful to know this from the history.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":333,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old man is being investigated for tremor and dysarthria. His GP has performed blood tests that show very deranged liver function tests (LFTs). On examination, he is noted to have a tremor, impaired memory and slit-lamp examination reveals Kayser-Fleischer rings.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alcoholic cirrhosis\",\"e\":\"Alcohol is a very common cause of deranged liver function tests, and can also present with confusion and ataxia as part of Wernicke's syndrome. However, this patient is very young to have developed this already, especially without any mention of a significant alcohol history, and alcohol does not cause Kayser-Fleischer rings (which are pathognomonic of Wilson's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency\",\"e\":\"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is an autosomal-recessive condition that predisposes affected patients to cirrhosis and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) (due to a predisposition to develop emphysematous changes in the lungs), it does not have neurological affects.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Autoimmune hepatitis\",\"e\":\"Autoimmune hepatitis can present as an acute hepatitis on a chronic hepatitis and eventually leads to cirrhosis. It is associated with autoantibodies, including antinuclear antibody (ANA) and smooth muscle autoantibodies, it is most common in women and in those aged 40-50 years. Additionally, it does not cause neurological disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hepatitis B\",\"e\":\"Hepatitis B is a viral hepatitis, which in the Uk is normally acquired as an adult through needle-sharing or sexual intercourse. In endemic countries the vertical transmission route is relatively much more important. In the majority (approx 95%) of those infected as adults it causes an acute infection that may be symptomatic or may cause hepatitis, but is eventually cleared successfully. The risk of chronic infection if infected perinatally at birth is around 90%. Those with a chronic infection can also develop cirrhosis (approx 20%) or reactivation of the disease periodically (approx 25%). These scenarios could account this patient's deranged LFTs, however, hepatitis B does not usually cause neurological effects and in the absence of stated risk factors hepatitis B infection is unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wilson's disease\",\"e\":\"Wilson's disease is an autosomal-recessive disorder that results in the toxic accumulation of copper in the liver and central nervous system (CNS) (in particular the basal ganglia) Children often present with liver disease, and adults with CNS features. Kayser-Fleischer rings are copper deposits in the iris (Descemet's membrane) that are pathognomic but not invariable and may require a slit-lamp to be seen. Blood tests would possibly reveal low ceruloplasmin and high serum copper, along with abnormal LFTs. First-line treatment is with d-penicillamine, a copper chelating drug, along with dietary advice on avoidance of copper- containing foods.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":336,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old businessman from Thailand presents to his GP with symptoms of malaise, weight loss and right upper quadrant pain. On examination he is noted to have hepatomegaly, which is irregular. A bruit is also heard over the liver. His blood tests show very raised a-fetoprotein (AFP) levels.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"visi Cholangiocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Cholangiocarcinoma most commonly presents with jaundice and deranged liver function tests, possibly with abdominal pain, all due to obstruction of the biliary tree, often on a background of weight loss. It can also present acutely with fever, jaundice and abdominal pain due to it predisposing towards ascending cholangitis. It is a rare cancer, affecting 3.58 per 100 000 population in England (National Cancer Intelligence Network statistics for rare and less common cancers). In addition, it would be unusual for this cancer to present with a palpable mass without having caused obstruction and resultant jaundice.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fibrosarcoma of the liver\",\"e\":\"Fibrosarcomas are tumours arising from fibroblasts: They are most common in the limbs, but even then are uncommon. The literature regarding these tumours affecting the liver is confined to only a few case reports in which they tend to present as large masses. The most common type of sarcoma affecting the liver is the angiosarcoma, which makes up around 0.3% of all liver cancers (Cancer Research UK statistics).\\n\\nGallstones normally present in the context of biliary colic - sudden onset, colicky right upper quadrant pain. Often associated with recent ingestion of large/fatty meals. They would not explain the bruit, or the palpable irregular hepatomegaly, nor the raised AFP or weight loss.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gallstones\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)\",\"e\":\"HCC is the commonest malignant tumour of hepatocytes accounting for 90% of primary liver cancers. World-wide it is most commonly caused by chronic hepatitis B, followed by hepatitis C infection, and its incidence is highest in Asia (such as Thailand where this patient is from and sub-Saharan Africa. In Europe, hepatitis C infection is the most common cause, however any cause of cirrhosis puts patients at increased risk of HCC - so alcoholism, autoimmune conditions such as primary biliary cirrhosis (PBC) and primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC), and genetic conditions such as haemochromatosis PBC are all common causes. Another important cause in Asia and sub-Saharan Africa is aflatoxins - these are toxins produced by fungi that contaminate foodstuff such as peanuts and grains. In the UK the incidence of HCC is around 4.3 per 100 000 (Cancer Research UK). It commonly presents on the background of cirrhosis and associated signs, possibly presenting due to a deterioration in the patient's chronic liver disease. Other symptoms suggestive of HCC include right upper quadrant pain, weight loss, and new onset ascites/jaundice. On examination they may be palpable as an irregular hepatomegaly and sometimes a bruit may be heard over them. The a-fetoprotein levels are raised in 80% of patients. Patients at high risk of HCC should be screened every 6-12 months with ultrasound liver and serum AFP - these patients include cirrhotic patients due to HBV/HCV, PBC, haemochromatosis and alcoholism.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Liver haemangioma\",\"e\":\"These are the most common benign liver tumour, mostly they are incidental findings on imaging for other indications, if they do present clinically it is normally with a right upper quadrant (RUQ) 'fullness' or pain - they would not, however, cause this patient's weight loss or raised AFP\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":340,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old man who works in the city presents with crampy abdominal pain and a 6-week history of bloody diarrhoea with mucus. He is referred for investigation from which a sigmoidoscopy reveals an inflamed mucosa and a rectal biopsy shows an inflammatory infiltrate with goblet-cell depletion and crypt abscesses.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"S.V Coeliac disease\",\"e\":\"Coeliac disease is a condition that affects the small bowel - chronic inflammation results from autoimmune reaction to gluten, with serum findings of antiendomysial, antitissue transglutaminase and antigliadin antibodies. It results in diarrhoea, normally without blood, abdominal pain and bloating, and malabsorption. Endoscopic findings in the small bowel include villous atrophy, increased numbers of lymphocytes and crypt hyperplasia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AAL Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"Crohn's disease presents between the ages of 10 and 40 years and is characterised by diarrhoea, usually without blood or mucous, and abdominal pain. Abdominal pain is often in the right iliac fossa, as the most common site of affected bowel is the terminal ileum. Features of malabsorption (eg anaemia secondary to low vitamin Big levels) are common. It can affect any region of the alimentary canal from mouth to anus, and causes 'skip' lesions. Endoscopy may show deep ulceration and cobblestone appearance, fissures, abscesses and stenosis are frequent findings, and histologically there may be an inflammatory infiltrate, non-caseating granulomas and transmural inflammation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Irritable bowel syndrome\",\"e\":\"Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) presents with long-term altered bowel function, such as diarrhoea or constipation or alternation between the two, often with abdominal pain or bloating. It is a functional condition, by definition with no discernible metabolic or mechanical cause - signs or symptoms such as bloody diarrhoea (which this patient has), anaemia, weight loss, evidence of malabsorption all point firmly away from this diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"Ulcerative colitis commonly presents between the ages of 15 and 25 years. It is characterised by diarrhoea often with mucus and blood. There may be a crampy abdominal pain, and the frequency of motions may be related to the severity of an attack. It may affect only the rectum (proctitis in approximately 50%), or spread proximally to involve part of the rest of the colon. Sigmoidoscopy shows an inflamed and friable mucosa from the rectum and a variable but continuous amount proximally, classically with pseudopolyps, and histologically there may be an inflammatory infiltrate, goblet-cell depletion, glandular distortion, mucosal ulcers and crypt abscesses.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Whipple's disease\",\"e\":\"Whipple's disease is caused by infection with Tropheryma whipp/ei, commonly it presents with diarrhoea (steatorrhoea) and weight loss as a result of malabsorption. Joint pain and arthritis are also common, usually a migratory non-deforming arthritis. Less common but recognised features also include skin pigmentation, endocarditis, uveitis and central nervous system (CNS) involvement. Diagnosis is made by endoscopy of the duodenum, and biopsy showing PAS-positive macrophages and non-acid-fast Gram-positive bacilli.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":343,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old A-level student presents with diarrhoea, abdominal pain and weight loss. On examination, she is tender in the right iliac fossa (RIF) and on further questioning she had a perianal abscess drained 1 year ago. A barium enema shows cobblestoning in the RIF\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Coeliac disease\",\"e\":\"Coeliac disease is a condition that affects the small bowel - chronic inflammation results from autoimmune reaction to gluten, with serum findings of antiendomysial, antitissue transglutaminase and antigliadin antibodies. It results in diarrhoea, normally without blood, abdominal pain and bloating, and malabsorption. Endoscopic findings in the small bowel include villous atrophy, increased numbers of lymphocytes and crypt hyperplasia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"Crohn's disease+ is a form of inflammatory bowel disease that has several features in common with ulcerative colitis and commonly presents between 10-40 years of age. However, it is characterised by transmural granulomatous inflammation and can affect any part of the gut. It is most commonly found in the terminal ileum (and therefore may produce discomfort in the RIF), but can affect anywhere from mouth to anus causing discontinuous skip lesions. Other features seen include diarrhoea and weight loss. Perianal disease is also common and may manifest as abscesses and fistulae. Barium enemas will classically show cobblestoning and 'rose-thorn' ulcers. A magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan can also be used to assess the extent of pelvic disease. Endoscopy may reveal deep ulceration and a cobblestone appearance, fissures, abscesses and stenosis are frequent findings. Histologically there may be an inflammatory infiltrate, non-caseating granulomas ano transmural inflammation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Irritable bowel syndrome\",\"e\":\"Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) presents with long-term altered bowel function, such as diarrhoea or constipation or alternation between the two, often with abdominal pain or bloating. It is a functional condition, by definition with no discernible metabolic or mechanical cause - signs or symptoms such as bloody diarrhoea, anaemia, weight loss, and evidence of malabsorption all point firmly away from this diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Whipple's disease\",\"e\":\"Whipple's disease is caused by infection with Tropheryma whipplei, commonly it presents with diarrhoea (steatorrhoea) and weight loss as a result of malabsorption. Joint pain and arthritis are also common, usually a migratory non-deforming arthritis. Less common but recognised features also include skin pigmentation, endocarditis, uveitis and CNS involvement. Diagnosis is made by endoscopy of the duodenum, and biopsy showing PAS- positive macrophages and non-acid-fast Gram-positive bacilli.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"Ulcerative colitis commonly presents between the ages of 15 and 30 years. It is characterised by diarrhoea often with mucus and blood. There may be a crampy abdominal pain, and the frequency of motions may be related to the severity of an attack. It may affect only the rectum (proctitis in approximately 50%), or spread proximally to involve part or the rest of the colon. Barium enema reveals button-shaped ulcers and pseudopolyps, thickened bowel- wall and haustra, filiform polyps and, in cases of chronic inflammation, a 'lead pipe' colon can occur (featureless colon with no normal haustral markings). Sigmoidoscopy shows an inflamed and friable mucosa from the rectum and a variable but continuous amount proximally. Histologically there may be an inflammatory infiltrate, goblet-cell depletion, glandular distortion, mucosal ulcers and crypt abscesses.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":347,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man presents with episodes of severe epigastric pain radiating through to his back for several weeks. He also reports he has noticed his stools have tended to float more recently and are very difficult to flush away. On examination erythema ab igne is noted over the epigastrium.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"Acute pancreatitis* classically presents with sudden onset of severe epigastric pain radiating through to the back, better on sitting forwards. Commonly patients will be nauseous and vomiting, and have evidence of localised peritonitis (such as rebound tenderness and guarding in the epigastrium). The most common causes are gallstones and excessive alcohol, other causes can be remembered by the mnemonic below. While the description of the pain suffered by the patient is certainly fitting with pancreatitis, there are several features that point towards chronic rather than acute pancreatitis - including the multiple episodes over several weeks, and the evidence of exocrine insufficiency causing steatorrhoea\\n\\nThis normally presents with sudden onset, colicky right upper quadrant pain. Often associated with recent ingestion of large/fatty meals: The site of the pain in this patient does not fit with this description, and biliary colic would not explain his stools.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Biliary colic\",\"e\":\"This normally presents with sudden onset, colicky right upper quadrant pain. Often associated with recent ingestion of large/fatty meals. The site of the pain in this patient does not fit with this description, and biliary colic would not explain his stools.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Coeliac disease\",\"e\":\"Coeliac disease is a condition that affects the small bowel - chronic inflammation results from autoimmune reaction to gluten, with serum findings of anti-endomysial, anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-gliadin antibodies. It results in diarrhoea (normally without blood), abdominal pain, bloating, weight loss, fatigue and mouth ulcers. In addition commonly malabsorption is present which may manifest as iron-deficiency anaemia or vitamin Bra/folate deficiency with macrocytic anaemia, or steatorrhoea (as in this patient). The severe pain, radiating into the patient's back and in multiple distinct episodes does not fit with coeliac disease which tends to present with a milder colicky abdominal pain, or indigestion-type pain.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Chronic pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"Chronic pancreatitis presents most commonly either with chronic epigastric pain, or multiple episodes of acute epigastric pain, often radiating into the back. The pain may be relieved by sitting forwards and is often made worse by eating. Patients may also report nausea and vomiting, bloating and pale/difficult to flush/malodorous stools (steatorrhoea) with weight loss. The later symptoms are a result of pancreatic exocrine insufficiency resulting in malabsorption. The patient may also have signs/symptoms of endocrine insufficiency resulting in high body mass (BM), with polyuria and polydipsia. The commonest cause of chronic pancreatitis is alcohol excess, while gallstones is another common cause. It can also idiopathic, due to trauma, autoimmune disease, cystic fibrosis or other genetic disorders There are a number of features of this case to suggest it is chronic pancreatitis rather than acute - its prolonged history and the patient has symptoms suggestive of steatorrhoea from exocrine insufficiency. The presence of erythema ab igne also fits with a chronic picture. This is a 'rash' consisting of reticular erythema and hyperpigmentation most often caused by chronic use of a hot water bottle to relieve pain and is often seen in the epigastrium or on the back\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pancreatic Cancer\",\"e\":\"Pancreatic cancer classically presents insidiously with painless jaundice. These cancers tend to be cancers of the head of the pancreas as it is through this portion of the pancreas that the common bile duct (CBD) traverses: Other symptoms of pancreatic cancer (of any anatomical position) are general malaise, poor appetite, significant weight loss, and epigastric abdominal pain or lower thoracic back pain. Multiple severe episodes of epigastric pain without a history of weight loss/malaise do not fit well with pancreatic cancer.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":351,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old woman presents to her GP with a history of vague abdominal pain and bloating, On further questioning she reports weight loss and passing offensive stools which are difficult to flush away. As part of her investigations a jejunal biopsy is performed. This shows crypt hyperplasia and subtotal villous atrophy.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bacterial overgrowth\",\"e\":\"Also known as small bowel bacterial overgrowth, this can mimic coeliac disease with partial villous atrophy, however the aspirate would demonstrate a high number of bacteria. It is less common than coeliac disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Coeliac disease\",\"e\":\"Coeliac disease is a T-cell-mediated autoimmune disease affecting the small bowel caused by gluten intolerance. It causes a variety of symptoms including steatorrhoea (due to an inability to absorb fats as evidenced in this case by offensive stools that are difficult to flush away), abdominal pain and bloating. It can be diagnosed by a jejunal biopsy, which classically shows subtotal villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia. The management involves a gluten-free diet, which can relieve symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"Crohn's disease is a form of inflammatory bowel disease and can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract from mouth to anus. It most commonly presents with diarrhoea, weight loss and abdominal pain. Offensive stools would be unlikely, although the patient may report blood or mucous in the stool. Furthermore, crypt hyperplasia and villous atrophy is indicative of coeliac disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"Ulcerative colitis is a form of inflammatory bowel disease affecting the large bowel (and occasionally the terminal ileum due to backwash ileitis). While it may present with weight loss and abdominal pain, jejunal histology would be unremarkable.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tropical malabsorption\",\"e\":\"There is no history of foreign travel in the scenario therefore this is unlikely.\\n\\n23111\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":354,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman is referred with a 1-month history of dysphagia. It has developed gradually but she is now struggling to swallow food. She has a past history of Raynaud's phenomenon. Examination reveals radial furrowing of the lips, and tapered fingers. She reports no weight loss or change in bowel habit, with no family history of note.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1S.V Achalasia.\",\"e\":\"Achalasia is a motility disorder of the oesophagus in which relaxation of the lower oesophageal sphincter fails preventing passage of food into the stomach. It presents with progressive dysphagia and regurgitation of undigested food. Lip and finger changes would not be seen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Myasthenia gravis\",\"e\":\"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune condition due to antibodies against the acetylcholine receptors of the neuromuscular junction, resulting in progressive muscle fatigue. There is no association with Raynaud's phenomenon and lip/finger changes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oesophageal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Oesophageal carcinoma would not result in radial furrowing of the lips or tapering of the fingers but may present with weight loss and progressive dysphagia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Scleroderma\",\"e\":\"Scleroderma is a likely autoimmune condition affecting the skin and connective tissue, causing hardening of tissue. It is characterised by increased dermal collagen and decreased tissue elasticity. It may be in a localised form, affecting the skin only, or systemic form. Systemic scleroderma may be either limited or diffuse: Limited cutaneous scleroderma, which includes calcinosis cutis, Raynaud's phenomenon, oesophophageal dysfunction, sclerodactyly and telangiectasia syndrome (CREST) syndrome, has a better prognosis. Diffuse cutaneous scleroderma represents a more severe form in which widespread organ involvement occurs at an early stage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Syringobulbia\",\"e\":\"This is a rare condition affecting the brainstem and spinal cord. Fluid-filled cavities enlarge and compress nerves often resulting in cranial nerve palsies. It would not cause lip or finger changes. Other peripheral neurological signs and symptoms would be expected.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":359,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man presents to his GP with a strange feeling of food getting stuck when he swallows. This has been present for around 3 months and initially was only noticed when eating solid foods such as bread or meat, but now he has problems swallowing liquids. He also reports significant unintended weight loss.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Achalasia\",\"e\":\"Achalasia is a motility disorder of the osophagus in which relaxation of the lower oesophageal sphincter fails preventing passage of food into the stomach. It presents with progressive dysphagia and regurgitation of undigested food\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Barrett's oesophagus\",\"e\":\"Adenocarcinoma is predisposed to by a metaplastic change in the oesophageal mucosa from squamous to columnar epithelium by chronic reflux (Barrett's oesophagus). However, in this case there is no history of reflux disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hiatus hernia\",\"e\":\"A hiatus hernia is likely to result in reflux symptoms and retrosternal pain, it would not cause dysphagia and weight loss.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Oesophageal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Presenting features of oesophageal carcinoma are classically progressive dysphagia (initially solids and then liquids), associated with significant weight loss. Retrosternal chest discomfort may occur. The most common forms of oesophageal cancer are squamous and adenocarcinoma. Adenocarcinoma now exceeds squamous carcinoma by a ratio of 2:1. The ratio was previously in the reverse direction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pharyngeal pouch\",\"e\":\"A pharyngeal pouch is a diverticulum through Killian's dehiscence. It presents with halitosis, dysphagia and regurgitation. It is common in elderly men, however significant weight loss would not necessarily be expected as described in the case above.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":362,\"stem\":\"A 41-year-old woman is urgently referred because of dysphagia, On assessment you note the patient is wearing gloves because she gets very painful hands in the cold. She describes a colour change going from white to blue to bright red. It is also noted she has very tight skin around her lips and she reports limited mouth opening. What disease process is likely to be present?\",\"lead\":\"What disease process is likely to be present?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Achalasia\",\"e\":\"Achalasia is a motility disorder of the oesophagus in which relaxation of the lower oesophageal sphincter fails, preventing passage of food into the stomach. It presents with progressive dysphagia and regurgitation of undigested food.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diffuse oesophageal spasm\",\"e\":\"Diffuse oesophageal spasm causes intermittent chest pain upon eating food as the oesophagus spasms against a food bolus. It can be very painful but would not cause true dysphagia or lip changes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Myasthenia gravis\",\"e\":\"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune condition due to antibodies against the acetylcholine receptors of the neuromuscular junction. It results in progressive muscle fatigue. There is no association with Raynaud's phenomenon and lip changes:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Oesophageal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Oesophageal carcinoma classically presents with progressive dysphagia (initially solids and then liquids). This is often associated with significant weight loss. Retrosternal chest discomfort may also occur. The key features in this history to differentiate are skin colour changes and lip changes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Scleroderma\",\"e\":\"The colour change describes the classic pattern in Raynaud's syndrome. This may be idiopathic, or associated with connective tissue disorders, vascular obstructive disorders or occupation. In this case, the patient has scleroderma: Calcinosis cutis, Raynaud's phenomenon, oesophophageal dysfunction, sclerodactyly and telangiectasia syndrome (CREST) syndrome is a form of scleroderma. Its five main features are associated with anticentromere antibodies.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":365,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old man presents to his GP with vague symptoms of abdominal pain and weight loss. On examination his abdomen is soft, but an irregular hepatomegaly is noted and on further assessment a hard lymph node is palpable in the left supraclavicular fossa.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Duodenal ulcer . )\",\"e\":\"While a duodenal ulcer may present with vague abdominal pain, there is unlikely to be significant weight loss and irregular hepatomegaly would not be seen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gallstones\",\"e\":\"Gallstones will result in right upper quadrant abdominal pain that is worse when consuming fatty meals. Virchow's node is indicative of an intra-abdominal malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastric cancer\",\"e\":\"Gastric cancer often starts with very non-specific symptoms - anorexia, weight loss, features of dyspepsia. Tumours of the antrum can cause gastric outlet obstruction and present with fullness, nausea and vomiting. Features found on examination can include signs of metastases - ascites, irregular hepatomegaly and Virchow's node (a hard lymph node in the left supraclavicular fossa).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD)\",\"e\":\"GORD would not present with weight loss or irregular hepatomegaly. It is a common condition resulting in indigestion that is worse after food and upon lying down.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Gastric ulcer\",\"e\":\"An ulcer would not cause irregular hepatomegaly or significant weight loss.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":367,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old man with known mental health problems is admitted to a burns unit after self- immolation. On arrival his burns were mostly full thickness and the surface area was 34%. He is moved to the Burns Intensive Care Unit (ICU) and is intubated and ventilated and requiring inotropic support. Six days later he develops an episode of haematemesis.\\n\\nWhat is likely to be the cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is likely to be the cause?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A'S' Curling's ulcer\",\"e\":\"A gastric ulcer that occurs in burn patients when severe depletion of the plasma volume results in ischaemia and cell necrosis of the mucosa is known as a Curling's ulcer. It is therefore important that all severe burn patients are managed with proton pump inhibitors to reduce the incidence of this.\\n\\nCushing's ulcer is a gastric ulcer occurring as a result of increased intra-cranial pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cushing's ulcer\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oesophageal burns\",\"e\":\"Oesophageal burns are rare. In cases of self-immolation tracheal burns are much more common.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mallory-Weiss tear\",\"e\":\"Mallory-Weiss tears occur due to persistent vomiting, resulting in a small tear of the mucosal lining and subsequent minor haematemesis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome\",\"e\":\"This syndrome is caused by a gastrinoma, which results in elevated stomach acid levels and subsequently peptic ulcer disease.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":369,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old man is referred to a dermatologist by his GP due to skin changes in his axillae. On careful inspection it is noted he has a pigmented, rough thickening of his skin in his axillae, with warty lesions.\\n\\nWhat other important conditions must be considered in the case?\",\"lead\":\"What other important conditions must be considered in the case?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Rheumatoid arthritis\",\"e\":\"Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune condition resulting in inflammation of the synovium causing joint pain and swelling. Cutaneous manifestations include palmar erythema skin atrophy, and brittle nails.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Coeliac disease\",\"e\":\"Coeliac disease is associated with dermatitis herpetiformis in which itchy blisters develop over the knees, elbows and scalp.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"Crohn's disease is associated with several skin changes including pyoderma gangrenosum and erythema nodosum:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gastrointestinal cancer\",\"e\":\"The skin changes described in this case is acanthosis nigricans and is associated with gastrointestinal (eg stomach) cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Chronic pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"Cutaneous manifestations of chronic pancreatitis are uncommon and often examination is unremarkable except for vague abdominal pain.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":371,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old woman with a body mass index (BMI) of more than 40 kg/m= undergoes a gastric bypass procedure. She rapidly begins to lose weight after the procedure.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate statement regarding this procedure?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most appropriate statement regarding this procedure?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Perioperative mortality associated with bariatric surgery is 3% and is an important consideration that should be discussed with the patient before surgery\",\"e\":\"Perioperative mortality for bariatric surgery is reported as less than 0.3% and is declining due to improved management in specialist bar atric centres with multidisciplinary care. Complications of surgery should always be discussed with the patient, in bariatric surgery patient expectations around weight loss following surgery can be unrealistic and must be managed.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An adjustable gastric band forms an integral part of the procedure\",\"e\":\"The surgery can be broadly classified into restrictive and malabsorptive. The former consists of gastric banding or vertical banded gastroplasty which reduces the size of the stomach The latter is achieved by bypass procedures to alter the digestive tract so that less absorption occurs. Most procedures are a combination of the two techniques.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Iron and vitamin Bız deficiency are common\",\"e\":\"Surgery for morbid obesity is considered in incividuals who have a body mass index (BMI) more than 40 kg/m?. Obesity surgery for patients with a body mass index (BMI) between 35-39 kg/m2 may be justified if they have a co-morbid condition that will be improved by weight loss. The decision to operate must be made within a dedicated multidisciplinary team comprising a bariatric surgeon, dietician, physician, anaesthetist, radiologist and clinical nurse specialist. the conditions that can be improved by weight loss include type II diabetes mellitus, hypertension and dyslipidaemia (the metabolic syndrome). Others include obstructive sleep apnoea, decreased mobility, osteoarthritis and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and poor quality of life. Importantly, it must be remembered that gastric bypass surgery can result in iron and vitamin Big deficiencies.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Jejuno-ileal bypass with cholecystectomy is the preferred option\",\"e\":\"Jejuno-ileal bypass has fallen from favour as it has been associated with serious complications.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Weight loss from a biliopancreatic diversion with duodenal switch is less effective han biliopancreatic diversion alone\",\"e\":\"A combined surgical approach of biliopancreatic diversion with duodenal switch is associated with greater weight loss.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":374,\"stem\":\"A 51-year-old man presents with an upper gastrointestinal (Gl) bleed. His systolic blood pressure is 118 mmHg and blood results show an haemoglobin (Hb) of 11 g/dl (normal 10.5- 13.5 g/dl). Endoscopy reveals a duodenal ulcer with a visible bleeding vessel which is successfully clipped and injected with adrenaline.\\n\\nWhich is the most significant factor in predicting re-bleeding in this case?\",\"lead\":\"Which is the most significant factor in predicting re-bleeding in this case?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Age\",\"e\":\"Age of 60 years or above does predict re-bleeding risk, however, this patient is ‹60 years old.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Blood pressure\",\"e\":\"This patient's blood pressure is within the normal range - while a low blood pressure does predict increased re-bleeding risk, a normal blood pressure does not.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ulcer size\",\"e\":\"Ulcer size does not influence re-bieeding risk according to the major guidelines and scoring systems.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urea\",\"e\":\"Ulcer size does not influence re-bleeding risk according to the major guidelines and scoring systems.\\n\\nThis option is part of the Blatchford scoring system that predicts the need for intervention, but has not been shown to predict re-bleeding risk, we do not know this patient's urea levels so cannot comment any further on it.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Visible bleeding vessel at the time of endoscopy\",\"e\":\"This patient had a visible bleeding vessel, this s a major stigmata of a bleeding peptic ulcer+ which is an important part of the Rockall scoring system that has been shown to predict risk of re-bleeding. There are two scores that National Institute of Clinical Excellence (NICE) recommends are used in assessing risk of patients who present with acute upper GI bleeding. The Blatchford score, which has been shown to predict the risk of requiring intervention (transfusion, endoscopic or surgical therapy) and the Rockall score, which predicts the risk of re-bleeding and death. Below is a table showing the Rockall score. The scores are summed, the higher the score the higher the risk of re-bleeding and the higher the risk of mortality.\\n\\nAdmission 3 points O points 1 points 2 points scores\\n\\n60-79 <60 80+ Age -\\n\\nSBP >100. SBP >100, - Shock pulse SBP <100, pulse >100 pulse <100 >100\\n\\nRenal or liver failure, Co- CCF, IHD or other None None disseminated morbidities major comorbidity malignancy\\n\\nPost- 1 points 2 points 3 points O points endoscopy Mallory-Weiss All other Malignancy of upper GI Diagnosis - tear, no lesion diagnoses tract\\n\\nBlood, adherent clot, Major None or dark - visible or bleeding - stigmata spot only vessel\\n\\nAs you can see from the scoring system - the most important factors in predicting re- bleeding are: the presence of significant endoscopic stigmata of recent haemorrhage with a visible bleeding vessel, haemodynamic instabil ty or shock, malignancy of the upper Gl tract, age over 60 years and certain comorbidities. The Blatchford score also includes systolic blood pressure and pulse, hepatic disease and cardiac failure, and additionally includes admission haemoglobin, urea, melaena, syncope.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":378,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old obese woman presents with right upper quadrant (RUQ) pain and nausea, she reports similar episodes in the past that have resolved spontaneously. On examination, she is afebrile and has a soft abdomen which is tender in the RUQ and a plain X-ray shows radio- opaque round opacities in the RUQ. These are thought to represent gallstones.\\n\\nWhat proportion of gallstones can be seen on a plain abdominal X-ray?\",\"lead\":\"What proportion of gallstones can be seen on a plain abdominal X-ray?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"Explanation bod\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1%\",\"e\":\"The correct answer is 10-15%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"15%\",\"e\":\"Around 10-15% of gallstones can be seen on plain imaging due to calcification of the stones. These often appear as rings due to calcium deposition around a central organic core. In some cases the gall-bladder may be calcified ('porcelain gall-bladder') and therefore visible. Porcelain gall-bladder is a type of chronic cholecystitis, its presence increases the risk of developing gall-bladder cancer and cholecystectomy is the usual treatment. The most common imaging modality used to detect gallstones is abdominal ultrasound - this has a much higher sensitivity of 85%, and specificity up to 100%\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"30%\",\"e\":\"Gallstones are not usually visualised with abdominal X-ray (AXR).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"50%\",\"e\":\"As above, however renal stones tend to be more radio-opaque.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"75%\",\"e\":\"This is an incorrect response.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":380,\"stem\":\"A 41-year-old man is referred from his GP with a long history of epigastric pain which is relieved by eating. He is otherwise well, and drinks alcohol only occasionally The only other feature of note is a long-standing history of diarrhoea, which has not responded to several changes in diet. An oesophago-gastroduodenoscopy (OGD) reveals multiple small duodenal ulcers.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Curling's ulcer\",\"e\":\"A Curling's ulcer is a stress ulcer within the stomach or duodenum that occurs acutely in association with severe skin burns. They are usually multiple and shallow.\\n\\nA Cushing's ulcer is an ulcer within the stomach or duodenum associated with raised intracranial pressure - raised intracranial pressure classically presents with headaches that are worse in the morning and on lying down, associated with nausea and blurred vision.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cushing's ulcer\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Duodenal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Duodenal carcinoma is uncommon [only 0.3% of gastrointestinal (Gl) malignancies], it is usually an adenocarcinoma and could present as an ulcerated lesion, commonly it presents with abdominal pain, as well as weight loss, nausea and vomiting, or with bleeding. It is unlikely to present as multiple small erosions as described here though, and most commonly presents in the sixth decade of life:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"VIPoma\",\"e\":\"This is a rare type of cancer of the pancreatic islets that results in secretion of vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP), It primarily presents with watery diarrhoea, that can result in hypokalaemia and dehydration. It is sometimes associated with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MENT).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome\",\"e\":\"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is the constellation of multiple, usually severe, peptic ulcers affecting the stomach and duodenum associated with a gastrinoma (a tumour of gastrin- secreting 'G' cells, usually in the pancreas but can also be in the stomach or duodenum). Excess gastrin promotes excess gastric acid secretion - this acid leads to peptic ulceration, and diarrhoea is a common symptom too. Patients complain of epigastric pain due to peptic ulceration which may be better with food. Around one-third of patients have MEN-1+ syndrome.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":383,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old man with known alcoholic liver disease, presents with confusion and is generally unwell. On examination he is noted to have a distended abdomen with shifting dullness. Imaging later confirms a mass in his liver that is thought to be malignant in origin.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely nature of this mass?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely nature of this mass?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cholangiocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Cholangiocarcinoma most commonly presents with jaundice and deranged liver function tests, possibly with abdominal pain, all due to obstruction of the biliary tree, often on a background of weight loss. It can also present acutely with fever, jaundice and abdominal pain due to it predisposing towards ascending cholangitis. It is a rare cancer, affecting 3.58 per 100 000 population in England (National Cancer Intelligence Network statistics for rare and less common cancers).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fibrolamellar carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Fibrolamellar carcinoma is a rare variant of HCC, around 1:5 million population incidence, affecting children and young adults without a history of background liver disease. Risk factors remain poorly understood.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemangioma\",\"e\":\"These are the most common benign liver tumour, mostly they are incidental findings on imaging for other indications, if they do present clinically it is normally with a RUQ 'fullness' or pain - they would not, however, cause this patient's signs of decompensated liver disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hepatic angiosarcoma\",\"e\":\"This cancer makes up around 2% of primary liver cancers, originating from endothelial cells within the liver. It usually presents with a mass and general signs such as weight loss and malaise. It can also present with rupture and bleeding. Risk factors include environmental toxins, such as vinyl chloride or arsenic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)\",\"e\":\"HCC is the commonest malignant tumour of hepatocytes accounting for 90% of primary liver cancers. It usually occurs on a background of cirrhosis, world-wide it is most commonly caused by chronic hepatitis B, followed by hepatitis C infection, and its incidence is highest in Asia and sub-Saharan Africa. In Europe, hepatitis C infection is the most common cause, however, any cause of cirrhosis puts patients at increased risk of HCC - so alcoholism, autoimmune conditions such as primary biliary cirrhosis (PBC) and primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC), and genetic conditions such as haemochromatosis are all common causes. Another important cause in Asia and sub-Saharan Africa is Aflatoxins - these are toxins produced by fungi contaminating foodstuff such as peanuts and grains. In the UK the incidence of HCC is around 4.3 per 100 000 (Cancer Research UK). It commonly presents on the background of cirrhosis and associated signs, possibly presenting due to a deterioration in the patient's chronic liver disease, for example with encephalopathy, ascites and reduced synthetic function. Other symptoms suggestive of HCC include right upper quadrant pain, weight loss, and new onset ascites/jaundice. On examination they may be palpable as an irregular hepatomegaly and sometimes a bruit may be heard over them. The a-fetoprotein levels are raised in 80% of patients. Increasingly patients are also detected on screening in the UK. Patients at high risk of HCC should be screened every 6-12 months with ultrasound liver and serum AFP - these patients include cirrhotic patients due to HBV/HCV. PBC, haemochromatosis and alcoholism (see BSG Guidelines for more information).\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":386,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old woman attends her general practitioner seeking advice on a heavy dependence on alcohol. She is currently low of mood, unemployed and complains of upper abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting and retching. Which one of the following symptoms are associated with chronic alcohol abuse?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following symptoms are associated with chronic alcohol abuse?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"iSil Hightened libido\",\"e\":\"Chronic alcohol use normally suppresses libido.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AM Menstrual disturbance\",\"e\":\"Excessive alcohol intake, ever in the absence of liver damage, can lead to altered menstrual cycle, from irregular periods to anovulation and altered hormone levels. One key function of the liver is to metabolise ostrogen, as liver function deteriorates in chronic alcohol abuse menstrual disturbance becomes very common. Additionally, menorrhagia may occur as liver damage causes coagulopathy. During end-stage liver disease, menstruation may cease completely\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Microcytosis\",\"e\":\"Macrocytosis often develops in alcoholism. While this is somet mes due to decreased folate levels or low vitamin Big from malnutrition associated with alcoholism, it is more frequently a direct result of alcohol on the bone marrow (though the mechanism is poorly understood).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Primary thrombocythemia\",\"e\":\"Alcohol has multiple haematological effects. It has a suppressive effect on the bone marrow, so can commonly cause thrombocytopenia, anaemia with macrocytosis, lymphopenia and immunosuppression. See the link for an in-depth discussion of the various haematological effects alcohol is known to have.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sydenham's chorea\",\"e\":\"Sydenham's chorea is a description of the choreiform movements that can present associated with acute rheumatic fever. The cause is due to an autoimmune reaction and inflammation within the basal ganglia after exposure to the Group A b-haemolytic streptococci. Central nervous system (CNS) effects associated with chronic alcohol abuse include Wernicke's encephalopathy (a reversible condition of ophthalmoplegia, confusion and ataxia) and Korsakoff's syndrome (irreversible dementia with marked anterograde amnesia). Both of these conditions are associated with low 'B' vitamins, especially thiamine, due to chronic malnutrition. Signs and symptoms of cerebellar ataxia are common in chronic alcoholism.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":389,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old woman is taken to the Emergency department by her family with severe abdominal pain and fevers. On examination, she appears unwell and is spiking temperatures. You note she is icteric and on palpation is most tender in the right upper quadrant (RUQ).\\n\\nWhat is the most likely disease process in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely disease process in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Biliary colic\",\"e\":\"This normally presents with sudden onset, colicky right upper quadrant pain. Often associated with recent ingestion of large/fatty meals. It is unusual for jaundice to develop unless a gallstonet has entered to common bile duct, or occasionally a gallstone within the neck of the gall-bladder or Hartmann's pouch can compress the CBD and cause an obstructive jaundice (known as Mirizzi's syndrome). Additionally, patients are not usually tender in the RUQ (although this is where the pain is located) and they should not be febrile.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cholangitis\",\"e\":\"Ascending cholangitis is a medical emergency that can be recognised by Charcot's triad of fever, right upper quadrant abdominal pain and jaundice. It is caused commonly by biliary obstruction that promotes biliary stasis and allows for infection to ascend from the duodenum. The commonest risk factor is a galistone but other causes include pancreatic head cancer or cholangiocarcinoma, strictures, stents or recent endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP). Patients can become very unwell with cholangitis and develop sepsis and subsequent septic shock. Treatment involves intravenous (IV) antibiotics, fluid resuscitation and an ERCP to relieve the biliary obstruction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Cholecystitis normally presents with constant right upper quadrant pain and fever, classically the patient is Murphy's positive where deep inspiration while palpating the RUQ results in the patient 'catching their breath' as the inflamed gall-bladder is lowered onto the palpating hand. Jaundice is uncommon unless a stone within the neck of the gall-bladder or Hartmann's pouch is compressing the common bile duct (CBD), which can cause an obstructive jaundice (known as Mirizzi's syndrome), additionally the localised inflammation and swelling around the gall-bladder can result in compression of the CBD and therefore an obstructive jaundice. Because jaundice is unusual this is not the most likely cause of this presentation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gallstone ileus\",\"e\":\"Gallstone ileus occurs when a gallstone enters to small bowel, usually through a fistula connecting the gall-bladder and small intestine. It can then cause a mechanical small bowel obstruction. It does not cause jaundice or fevers, tenderness would not be localised to the RUQ.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Renal colic\",\"e\":\"Renal colic is caused by renal stones within the urinary tract - it presents with severe colicky loin-to-groin pain, normally with blood on urine dipstick. Fevers do not occur except in superadded infection. The patient may be tender in the renal angle, but should not be tender in the RUQ. This certainly would not cause jaundice.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":392,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old woman presents with severe pain during defecation. She also reports a small amount of blood on the toilet paper following defecation. Her only past medical history is constipation, which has become worse because of the pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1. 5% Anal fissure\",\"e\":\"The history in the case is quite classical for an anal fissure where pain is the dominant symptom. Normally there is severe sharp anal pain on passing stool, with a burning pain afterwards: A small amount of blood on wiping may then occur, though is not always present. Examination should reveal an anal fissure, and exclude the other causes mentioned here. Common risk factors are constipation and hard stools, and Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anal fistula 1 : -\",\"e\":\"An anal fistula is a connection between the anal canal and the perianal skin. Most commonly they result secondary to perianal abscesses, though they can also be secondary to Crohn's disease, trauma, or cancer, among other causes. They can present with pain, itching, localised swelling, or discharge of pus. The absence of preceding history, and the predominance of pain at defecation makes anal fistula less likely diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemorrhoids\",\"e\":\"These may be asymptomatic, or present with itching/irritation, bright painless bleeding per rectum (PR), or a feeling of 'fullness'. Pain is not common unless the haemorrhoids are strangulated or thrombosed, in which case the pain will be severe and constant (not just when passing stool).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Perianal abscess\",\"e\":\"Perianal abscess presents normally with perianal pain and swelling, it may be worse when passing stool but will normally be present at other times too. Bleeding is not a common symptom. They are not related to constipation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pilonidal sinus\",\"e\":\"Pilonidal sinuses are normally present in the natal cleft - they can be asymptomatic, or present as a small non-tender pit or lump. They may also present as a painful and tender lump, possibly with purulent discharge as an acute pilonidal abscess. Symptoms are not usually related to passing stool, and bleeding is not a common symptom, the major risk factors include hirsutism and poor personal hygiene.\\n\\n2854\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":395,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man presents after being referred from the GP with a painful, discharging swelling around his anus. He has had a similar problem twice in the past and on examination a very tender, erythematous swelling is noted in the natal cleft, but separate from the anal verge. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ischiorectal abscess\",\"e\":\"The ischiorectal (or ischioanal) fossae are prism-shaped fascia-lined potential spaces adjacent to the rectum. They are bordered by the levator ani muscle (superiorly and medially), the anal canal (medially), the perineal skin (inferiorly), and the ischial tuberosity (laterally). Ischiorectal abscesses are the second most common type of anorectal sepsis (30% of anorectal abscesses), after perianal abscess. Like other anorectal abscesses the pain is predominantly perianal, and often worse just before/during bowel movements - the anatomical description of this patient's abscess does not fit with this diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hidradenitis suppurativa\",\"e\":\"This is a long-term condition with multiple abscesses caused by infections within sweat glands, they are most commonly found under the arm pits, around the buttocks and groin. As well as normally having multiple lesions, the chronicity of the condition normally means that there is evidence of old abscesses, such as scarring of the skin. This is unlikely because of the single lesion this patient suffers from.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Perianal abscess\",\"e\":\"Perianal abscesses are the commonest form of anorectal abscess (60% of total - and present normally with perianal pain and swelling), it may be worse when passing stool but will normally be present at other times too. They are frequently recurrent, as in this patient, but the description of its location fits better with pilonidal abscess.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pilonidal abscess\",\"e\":\"The commonest site for pilonidal abscesses is the natal cleft and there may be a history of recurrent episodes over months or years. Pilonidal means 'hair's nest' in Latin and theories regarding its atiology involve shed hairs getting trapped in pits and becoming a nidus for local infection. Clinically there may be openings in the midline or either side of it sometimes with tufts of hair. There may be inflammation of the adjacent skin and pus may be expressed via the openings. Management involves incision and drainage of the abscess and then other procedures can be done electively to try and prevent recurrence. Antibiotics are indicated if systemic signs of infection, or evidence of surrounding cellulitis is present.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supralevator abscess\",\"e\":\"This describes an abscess that forms from an upward extension of an intersphincteric abscess into the supralevator space. They may also form from an intra-abdominal inflammation (eg appendicitis) tracking down. They are very uncommon making up only 4% on anorectal abscesses. Like other anorectal abscesses the pain is predominantly perianal, and often worse just before/during bowel movements - as well as being a very uncommon diagnosis, the anatomical description of this patient's abscess does not fit with this diagnosis.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":398,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old woman presents with intermittent cramping abdominal pain and bloating that is relieved after opening her bowels. She reports intermittent episodes of diarrhoea, but no weight loss, rectal bleeding, or mucous. Colonoscopy is unremarkable. Blood tests reveal normal full blood count (FBC), urea and electrolytes (U&Es) and C-reactive protein (CRP)/erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR). What is the most likely cause of her symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of her symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Coeliac disease\",\"e\":\"Coeliac disease is an autoimmune inflammatory condition of the small bowel caused by ongoing gluten exposure. Symptoms include abdominal bloating and pain, diarrhoea, weight loss and signs of malabsorption - this may commonly include steatorrhoea or anaemia (iron deficiency, or vitamin Big or folate deficiency). Colonoscopy would be normal, although small bowel biopsy would reveal villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia and increased numbers of lymphocytes. Bloods tests typically show autoantibodies (anti-tTG, anti-gliadin and anti- endomysial), anaemia, and possibly raised inflammatory markers - a coeliac screen is recommended by the National Institute of Clinical Excellence (NICE) Guidance on inflammatory bowel disease (IBS) before making the diagnosis of IBS.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"4A Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"This classically presents with abdominal pain, often in the right iliac fossa (as the terminal ileum is the most frequently affected segment of bowel) and non-bloody diarrhea, weight loss and general malaise are also likely. On examination the patient is likely to have abdominal tenderness. Colonoscopy may be normal, as Crohn's can affect any site of the alimentary canal, classically causing skip lesions. Perianal disease (fistulae, abscesses, fissures) and mouth ulcers are also common. Bloods tests may reveal anaemia, in particular a macrocytic anaemia due to vitamin Big malabsorption if the terminal ileum is involved - additionally, evidence of inflammation is likely on the patient's blood tests.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Irritable bowel syndrome\",\"e\":\"Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is one of the commonest causes of abdominal pain and is a functional disorder with no discernible metabolic or mechanical cause resulting in a mixture of pain, discomfort, bloating and altered bowel habit. It does not cause per rectum (PR) bleeding or mucus, or weight loss and these symptoms are suggestive of organic pathology and should be investigated appropriately and not attributed to IBS. National Institute of Clinical Excellence (NICE) recommend FBC, ESR and CRP and a coeliac screen, although other bloods and imaging are unnecessary unless there are signs/symptoms concerning for organic disease. Certainiy, a colonoscopy done in a patient IBS will show a normal rectum and colon, and treatment focuses on symptomatic relief through dietary measures. antispasmodics and psychological approaches.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hiatus hernia\",\"e\":\"Hiatus hernia describes herniation of the stomach through the diaphragm - it is normally asymptomatic but can present with symptoms of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD) (indigestion/heart burn, nocturnal cough, acid brash). Blood tests and colonoscopy would be normal, but it would not usually cause the patient's abdominal symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"This classically presents with abdominal pain and tenderness with bloody diarrhoea, weight loss and general malaise: Ulcerative colitis (UC) affects the rectum, and a variable but continuous amount of colon proximal to this. Colonoscopy would therefore not be expected to be normal with ongoing symptoms as in this case, Bloods tests would also likely be abnormal, revealing raised inflammatory markers.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":401,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old man attends the colorectal clin c presenting with a 4-month history of a change in bowel habit with blood mixed with stool. He has no relevant past medical history.\\n\\nWhat is the most appropriate investigation?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate investigation?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Barium enema\",\"e\":\"A barium enema, although it could be useful, is not the best test for this patient. It may give an impression of a lesion in the bowel wall but does not give such accurate information as would colonoscopy in this case. Furthermore he would still require an endoscopic biopsy if a lesion was seen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Capsule endoscopy\",\"e\":\"Capsule endoscopy is an expensive test and not readily available. It is useful for looking for a source of gastrointestinal (GI) bleeding that occurs in the small bowel beyond the scope of the oesophago-gastroduodenoscopy (OGD) and proximal to the caecum/terminal ileum which cannot be accessed by colonoscopy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Colonoscopy\",\"e\":\"This 46-year-old man requires a lower gastrointestinal (GI) endoscopy for his red flag symptoms of change in bowel habit associated with per rectum (PR) bleeding. This test is the best investigation as it allows inspection of the bowel mucosa, biopsy if required and is potentially even therapeutic, for example, if a polyp is seen this can be removed by snare and sent for histology. It is an invasive test, requiring bowel preparation before the procedure but he is otherwise well and there are no contraindications in undertaking colonoscopy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Computed tomography (CT) scan\",\"e\":\"CT scans maybe useful for detecting gross bowel pathology and staging for cancers but would not be as useful as colonoscopy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Oesphagoduodenoscopy\",\"e\":\"Oesphagoduodenoscopy (OGD) is useful if an upper gastrointestinal (Gl) source is suspected but this patient has lower GI symptoms.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":404,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old woman with a history of chronic diarrhoea undergoes a flexible sigmoidoscopy. There is brownish discoloration on the wall of the sigmoid in a moiré pattern Biopsy reveals characteristic pigment-laden macrophages within the mucosa on periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) staining. She works as a swimwear model and is very concerned with her appearance.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\", in which Colonoscopic findings of Crohn's disease include cobblestone appearance, oedematous islands of mucosa are separated by fissures: These can then extend through all layers of the bowel. Transmural involvement can lead to stricture and fistulae formation. Skip lesions are common where there is healthy bowel between diseased segments. Microscopically there can be deep fissuring ulcers, oedema and inflammatory cell infiltrates with foci of lymphocytes and non-caseating granulomas. Pigment-laden macrophages within the mucosa on PAS staining is not a feature of Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Campylobacter infection\",\"e\":\"Diagnosis of Campylobacter infection is made on direct microscopy of faces or isolation of the organism. On sigmoidoscopy, the findings may be similar to those observed in pseudomembranous colitis or inflammatory bowel disease. These include mucosal oedema, hyperaemia, patchy petechiae and aphthod ulceration. Pigment-laden macrophages within the mucosa on PAS staining is not a feature of Campylobacter infection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diverticulitis\",\"e\":\"Diverticulitis may present with similar features as mentioned in the history. In the current era of advanced imaging most of the complications of diverticulitis are diagnosed by computed tomography (CT) scan. However lower gastrointestinal (Gl) endoscopy is required to rule out malignancy once the acute phase is over. Sigmoidoscopy may show openings of diverticulae. Pigment-laden macrophages within the mucosa on PAS staining is not a feature of diverticulitis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Melanosis coli\",\"e\":\"Brownish discoloration on the wall of the sigmoid in a moiré pattern and characteristic pigment-laden macrophages within the mucosa with PAS staining on mucosal biopsy are characteristic features of melanosis coli and indicate laxative abuse. Although, colonoscopic appearance does not have any negative sequela, chronic diarrhoea requires advice on stopping laxative abuse. Note her occupation as a model suggests she may feel pressurised to lose weight. Offer psychological support if required.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"On colonoscopy, inflammation is restricted to the mucosa and submucosa of the large bowel with ulcerative colitis. Abscesses develop at the base of the colonic crypts and once burst can lead to ulceration. Oedema of the intervening mucosa leads to pseudopolyp appearance. Histologically, there is chronic inflammatory cell infiltrate, crypt architectural distortion and goblet cell depletion. Granulomas are usually absent. Pigment-laden macrophages within the mucosa on PAS staining is not a feature of ulcerative colitis.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":407,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old woman presents with a change in bowel habit. She has had fresh bloody diarrhoea for the last 2 months. Flexible sigmoidoscopy revealed inflamed and ulcerated mucosa from the rectum to as high as the scope could extend in the splenic flexure. Biopsy shows superficial ulceration and diffuse mucosal inflammation.\\n\\nWhat is the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"Colonoscopic findings of Crohn's disease include a cobblestone appearance, in which oedematous islands of mucosa are separated by fissures. These can then extend through all layers of the bowel. Transmural involvement can lead to stricture and fistulae formation. Skip lesions are common where there is healthy bowel between diseased segments. Microscopically there can be deep fissuring ulcers, oedema and inflammatory cell infiltrates with foci of lymphocytes and non-caseating granulomas. Pigment-laden macrophages within the mucosa on periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) staining is not a feature of Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ischaemic colitis\",\"e\":\"Ischaemic colitis presents in a similar way to inflammatory bowel disease or infectious colitis. Endoscopic findings that distinguish ischaemic colitis from inflammatory bowel disease are the segmental distribution, rectum sparing and rapid resolution on serial examinations. Histological changes in ischaemic colitis include oedema, distorted crypts, mucosal and submucosal haemorrhage, and inflammatory infiltration into the lamina propria, granulation tissue, intravascular platelet thrombi and necrosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diverticulitis\",\"e\":\"Diverticulitis may present with similar features as mentioned in history. In the current era of advanced imaging most of the complications of diverticulosis are diagnosed by computed tomography (CT) scan. However lower gastrointestinal (Gl) endoscopy is required to rule out malignancy once acute phase is over. Sigmoidoscopy may show openings of diverticulae.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Salmonella infection\",\"e\":\"Diagnosis of Salmonella infection is made clinically and with microbiologica investigations. Colonoscopy may be required in some cases where complications such as bleeding occurs requiring endoscopic intervention. Colonoscopic findings include multiple variable-sized punched-out ulcers with slightly elevated margins. Moreover, the most commonly involved area is the terminal ileum, followed by the ileocecal valve and ascending/transverse colon. The left colon is usually spared.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"This patient has ulcerative colitis as evident in history, endoscopy findings with confluent inflammation, and histology results. This inflammatory disorder differs from Crohn's disease as granulomas are absent, fissures are rare and inflammation is superficial involving the mucosal layer.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":410,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old patient presents with urinary retention. He describes pneumaturia and faeculent debris in his urine. He has had significant weight loss and a change in bowel habit over the last two months. A contrast enema shows an obstructing lesion in the distal sigmoid colon. He is otherwise fit and well. What is the best definitive treatment in this case?\",\"lead\":\"What is the best definitive treatment in this case?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Catheterisation\",\"e\":\"This patient has developed a malignant colovesical fistula as a result of a sigmoid tumour. This patient will initially need a catheter but it is not the 'definitive' treatment requested. A fistula will not heal if there is distal obstruction - in this case an obstructing operative tumour.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Neostigmine\",\"e\":\"Neostigmine is an anticholinesterase agent that increases the effect of acetylcholine. It is helpful in the management of patients with neurogenic bladder having urinary retention. It is not helpful in patients having urinary retention due to a malignant colovesical fistula. Moreover, the question is regarding definitive management, which will be en bloc resection with or without pelvic exenteration; or faecal and urinary diversion in case of palliation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Stenting of the stricture\",\"e\":\"Stenting may help in relieving colonic obstruction but it is not the definitive management of malignant colovesical fistulae.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Surgical resection of colonic lesion\",\"e\":\"Definitive management of colovesical fistulae is surgical. It may be with curative or palliative intent depending upon the extent of local invasion. With curative intention it may include en bloc resection with or without pelvic exenteration. With palliative intent it may be urinary and faecal diversion leading to urostomy and colostomy respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy\",\"e\":\"In case of rectal tumour, necadjuvant chemoradiotherapy reduces the risk of recurrence. However, in the case of sigmoid tumours, neoadjuvant treatment is not recommended due to the high position of the tumour that can put the small bowel at risk of radiation enteritis.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":413,\"stem\":\"You are called to the delivery room with your registrar to see a baby who the midwife says has 'no abdominal wall'. When you get there you find a baby with an abdominal defect with loops of bowel outside the abdomen, and no covering layers. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Umbilical hernia\",\"e\":\"An umbilical hernia is covered by skin. The defect is herniation of the abdominal contents through the umbilical fibromuscular ring. Umbilical hernias usually occur in infants and reach their maximal size by the first month of life. Most hernias of this type close spontaneously by the first year of life, with only a 2-10% incidence in children older than 1 year.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastroschisis\",\"e\":\"In gastroschisis there is no peritoneal covering, and it is rarely associated with congenital defects seen in patients with omphalocole. However, sometimes it may be associated with intestinal atresia. Management initially consists of covering the defect with cling film to prevent heat and moisture loss, while establishing intravenous (IV) access for IV fluids.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Omphalocoele major:\",\"e\":\"An omphalocoele is a congenital defect of the anterior abdominal wall in which the abdominal contents are covered by a sac. The sac has three layers: the peritoneum. Wharton's jelly, and the amnion. The sac may contain intestinal loops, the bladder, and the liver. Unlike gastroschisis it is often associated with other congenital anomalies. Contents in the case of omphalocoele protrude through the central defect in the abdomen and there is no umbilicus. Depending on the size of the defect it may be labelled as major or minor. A defect of more than 4 cm is designated major, while less than 4 cm it is minor.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hernia of the umbilical cord\",\"e\":\"In a hernia of the umbilical cord a cuff of skin is seen extending from the abdominal wall onto the neck of the sac. The hernia sac comprises of the outer amnion and the inner peritoneal lining and contains contents varying from loops of bowel to any mobile intraperitoneal organs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Omphalocoele minor\",\"e\":\"Omphalocoele minor is a congenital defect of the anterior abdominal wall in which the abdominal contents are covered by a sac and the defect is less than 4 cm.\\n\\n3083\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":416,\"stem\":\"A 9-month-old baby presents with excessive crying and withdrawing of legs for the last 12 h. Between the attacks he looks quite healthy. He has vomited a few times and has passed mucous mixed with blood per rectum. A week ago he had a viral illness. Examination reveals a sausage-shaped mass in the upper abdomen.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pyloric stenosis\",\"e\":\"Presentation with vomiting may mimic pyloric stenosis, however pyloric stenosis usually presents around the age of 4-6 weeks. Vomiting is projectile, bleeding per rectum is rare but an olive-shaped mass may be palpable in epigastric region. Diagnosis is by ultrasound (US) scan of the abdomen. Electrolyte abnormalities include hypokalemia, hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis with paradoxical aciduria. Treatment is by open or laparoscopic pyloromyotomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intussusception\",\"e\":\"Intussusception is the commonest gastrointestinal surgical emergency in children between 3 months and 2 years. The ileocecal junction is the commonest location. A part of the bowel invaginates into the adjacent section, leading to obstruction. The patient usually presents with intermittent colicky abdominal pain lasting 1-2 min wich frequently recurs. It is associated with vomiting. Blood is passed PR, often described as red-currant jelly in appearance. The invaginated bowel segment may be palpable as a sausage-shaped mass.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastroenterts V\",\"e\":\"The presentation may mimic acute gastroenteritis, however a palpable sausage-shaped mass is suggestive of intussusception.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Midgut volvulus\",\"e\":\"Midgut volvulus may present as a surgical emergency. With a 12 h history the abdomen is distended and tender and the child is usually in a state of shock. Such patients require emergency laparotomy without any investigations.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Necrotising enterocolitis\",\"e\":\"Necrotising enterocolitis (NEC) is a condition that involves premature neonates. The usual presentation is greenish aspirates in a premature neonate who is in the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU). The abdomen becomes shiny, tender and distended. An X-ray of the abdomen may show pneumatosis intestinalis, Treatment is medical (with antibiotics) or surgical depending upon the severity of the NEC\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":419,\"stem\":\"You are treating a 4-week-old baby who has had jaundice since birth at full-term, weight 5 Ib. The child has developed palpable firm hepatosplenomegaly, pale stools and dark urine. Ultrasound scan (USS) shows no dilatation of the biliary tree. What is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Biliary atresia\",\"e\":\"There are numerous causes of jaundice in neonates including physiological, medical and surgical. Surgical causes include biliary atresia, choledochal cysts, spontaneous perforation of the bile duct, inspissated bowel within the common bile duct, and extrahepatic bile duct tumours. In biliary atresia the bile ducts are destroyed by inflammation. Clinical signs include jaundice, and the development of firm hepatosplenomegaly, pale stools and dark urine, which are not seen at birth. Children usually go to full term, but with a low birthweight. No single test is diagnostic of this condition.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Choledochal cysts\",\"e\":\"Choledochal cysts are congenital cystic dilatations of the biliary tree. Infants typically present with jaundice, pale stools and right upper quadrant mass. USS would classically reveal dilatation of the biliary tree.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Extrahepatic bile duct tumour\",\"e\":\"Bile duct tumours are uncommon and typically present with jaundice, affecting older patients with the average age of 60-65 years.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"lleal atresia\",\"e\":\"This is a malformation of the ileum resulting in a complete luminal obstruction and would typically be diagnosed within the first 24 h of birth if it had not been picked up on antenatal scans. The infant is unlikely to have passed stool, wili have a distended abdomen and may be vomiting.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spontaneous perforation of the bile duct\",\"e\":\"This is a rare condition affecting infants and children with the cause unknown. The infants can present acutely with biliary peritonitis or later with jaundice, pale stools and abdominal distension. An ultrasound would demonstrate ascites.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":422,\"stem\":\"A 4-day-old full-term neonate has developed abdominal distension and bilious aspirates. He passed meconium in the first 24 h but has not opened his bowels since. An abdominal X-ray shows distended small bowel loops and fluid levels with no air in the rectum.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Duodenal atresia\",\"e\":\"Duodenal atresia can be diagnosed antenatally. Abdominal distension is not marked as obstruction is proximal. In duodenal atresia a double gas bubble would be expected on abdominal X-ray.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hirschsprung's disease\",\"e\":\"In Hirschsprung's disease there is failure in passage of meconium in the first 48 h in most cases. The rest of the findings may match with the above description. Rectal stimulation and washouts help in emptying the bowel unless the pathology involves whole of the colon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Meconium ileus\",\"e\":\"In meconium ileus, passage of meconium fails to occur. X-ray findings may show a soap bubble appearance. Meconium ileus may be managed by rectal washouts in certain selected cases. Work up should include screening for cystic fibrosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Oesophageal atresia\",\"e\":\"With oesophageal atresia, excessive salivation and frothing occurs. A trial of passage of a nasogastric tube (NG) tube fails and X-rays show NG tube coiling in the oesophagus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Small bowel atresia\",\"e\":\"Small bowel atresia can be diagnosed antenatally. It presents with bilious vomiting. abdominal distension, and failure to pass further stools following passage of meconium. Plain abdominal X-ray shows distended loops of bowel with fluid levels.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":425,\"stem\":\"A 4-week-old full-term male baby is brought with history of projectile, non-bilious vomiting. Examination reveals a severely dehydrated baby with a scaphoid abdomen. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"15. Duodenal Atresia\",\"e\":\"Vomiting is bilious and presentation is earlier following birth.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hirschprung's disease\",\"e\":\"Prominent features are inability to open the bowels and abdominal distersion. Vomiting is a late feature and is bilious\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pyloric stenosis\",\"e\":\"Typical presentation is around 4-6 weeks in the first male baby of the family. Vomiting is projectile and non-bilious due to the high level of obstruction (the thickened pyloric muscle). which is above the entrance of the common bile duct into the duodenum. Distension is not usually a feature. Between the episodes the baby is eager to take feeds. Electrolyte disturbances include hypokalaemic, and hypochloraemic metabolic alkalesis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Malrotation\",\"e\":\"Presentation is with acute abdomen and bilious vomiting. If midgut volvulus has developed the baby requires emergency laparotomy. No further investigations should be done: Bilious vomiting in a newborn is a surgical emergency until proved otherwise. Malrotation is the diagnosis, which needs exclusion with an upper gastrointestinal (GI) contrast to look at the layout of the intestine, specifically whether the duodenal-jejunal flexure is on the correct side of the vertebrae - the left is the correct s de.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Meconium lleus\",\"e\":\"Presentation is at birth with abdominal distension, failure to pass stool and bilious aspirates.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":428,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old man presents with a history of weight loss, altered bowel habit and feeling generally unwell. Investigations reveal a caecal carcinoma. He undergoes a right hemicolectomy. Histology reveals a large tumour that has extended into the muscularis propria but spared the lymph nodes. What is the most likely Astler-Coller classification of Dukes' staging for this tumour?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely Astler-Coller classification of Dukes' staging for this tumour?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dukes' A\",\"e\":\"If the tumour is limited to the mucosa it is classified as Dukes' A\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Dukes' B1\",\"e\":\"According to the new Dukes' staging it is B1 once the tumour has spread into the muscularis propria but has not penetrated through it. If it has penetrated through he propria, it is staged as B2.\\n\\nThe original staging system derived by Cuthbert Dukes was\\n\\n• A - Invasion into but not through the bowel wall (90% 5-year survival) • B - Invasion through the bowel wall but not involving lymph nodes (70% 5-year survival) • C - Involvement of lymph nodes (30% 5-year survival) • D - Distant metastases.\\n\\nThis was revised by Astler-Coller in 1954:\\n\\n• A - Limited to mucosa • B1 - Into muscularis propria but not penetrating it. No nodes. • B2 - Penetrating muscularis propria. No nodes. • C1 - Metastasis to one to four regional lymph nodes • C2 - Metastasis to more than four lymph nodes. • D - Distant metastases.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Dukes' C1\",\"e\":\"If the tumour has spread into regional lymph nodes (between one and four it is staged as\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Dukes' C2\",\"e\":\"Spread to lymph nodes beyond the regional lymph nodes (more than four) is classified as C2.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Dukes' D\",\"e\":\"Distant metastasis is classified as stage D.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":431,\"stem\":\"An elderly man presents to your clinic with progressive weight loss, dysphagia and several episodes of hematemesis. An upper gastrointestinal endoscopy reveals a fungating growth in his distal oesophagus. Biopsy has been taken for histopathology.\\n\\nWhat would be the most likely histopathological finding?\",\"lead\":\"What would be the most likely histopathological finding?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Squamous cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Squamous cell carcinoma occurs in the upper part of the oesophagus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Malignant melanoma\",\"e\":\"Primary and metastatic malignant melanoma may occur in some patients, but it is a very rare problem. Moreover history and endoscopic findings are more suggestive of adenocarcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Adenocarcinoma of the intestinal type\",\"e\":\"The lower end of the oesophagus is prone to develop metaplastic changes in response to reflux oesophagitis. Change of normal squamous epithelium to columnar type is called as Barrett's oesophagus. These changes are reversible. However, in longstanding cases, dysplasia may occur that can lead to adenocarcinoma. As the mass in the summary is in the lower oesophagus then, most probably, malignant adenocarcinoma is the most likely diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Metaplastic columnar epithelium\",\"e\":\"This is suggestive of Barrett's oesophagus. Oesophageal squamous epithelium is replaced by columnar epithelium of the intestinal type with goblet cells. Adenocarcinoma may develop in longstanding cases of Barrett's oesophagus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Dysplastic columnar epithelium\",\"e\":\"This finding is suggestive of dysplasia in Barrett's oesophagus. It is a premalignant condition and can lead to adenocarcinoma of the lower end of the oesophagus and longstanding cases of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD). Oesophageal carcinoma is divided into an adenocarcinoma, which affects the distal one-third (50-80%) and squamous cell carcinomas (90-95%), which affect the upper two-thirds. Squamous cell carcinomas are associated with tobacco (as is adenocarcinoma) and also alcohol intake, whereas adenocarcinoma is closely linked to GORD and Barrett's oesophagus. Most oesophageal cancers present late and therefore are not suitable for minimally invasive surgery Sadly. palliation is often the only treatment available.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":434,\"stem\":\"You see a 67-year-old woman in your clinic. She has been complaining for 2 months of anorexia, abdominal bloating and 2 stones of weight loss. A computed tomography (CT) scan suggests a thickened stomach without any definitive mass. Endoscopic biopsy reveals malignant signet ring type cells with intracytoplasmic mucin.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Metastatic tumours of the stomach\",\"e\":\"These tumours may present as ulcers or diffused stomach thickening, however biopsy will reveal the site of origin of such metastasis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastrointestinal stromal tumour (GIST)\",\"e\":\"Small gastric GISTs appear as serosal, submucosal or intramural nodules. They are usually incidental findings during abdominal surgery or endoscopy. Histologically these are positive for KIT tyrosine kinase receptors.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastric carcinoid\",\"e\":\"Histology shows enterochromaffin like (ECL) cells that are a distinctive type of neuroendocrine cell in the gastric mucosa underlying the epithelium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gastric Lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Gastric lymphomas may be primary or secondary. Endoscopically, these are visible as enlarged gastric folds, or ulcers in the case of low grade lesions and a mass in the case of high grade lesions. Histology and immunohistochemistry may differentiate between different types of lymphomas. 5175\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Linitis plastica\",\"e\":\"Gastric linitis plastica is a diffuse type of cancer. Malignant cells invade throughout the stomach, resulting in the thickening and rigidity of the stomach wall. Most of the patients present late. Endoscopically the stomach looks thick with reduced distensibility. Biopsy reveals invasion of all the layers of the stomach in a diffuse pattern. Malignant cells look like a signet ring. Prognosis is poor and treatment may include radical surgery or palliative chemotherapy.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":437,\"stem\":\"You see a 67-year-old woman in your clinic. She has been complaining of 2 months of anorexia, abdominal bloating and 2 stones of weight loss. A computed tomography (CT) scan suggests a thickened stomach and oesophago-gastroduodenoscopy (OGD) reveal linitis plastica.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true of stomach cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true of stomach cancer?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"There is a strong association between heavy alcohol intake and stomach cancer\",\"e\":\"There is significant body of evidence suggesting that a heavy alcohol intake increases gastric cancer risk. Smoking also increases the risk from 40% increased risk for current smokers to 82% increased risk for heavy smokers. Other risk factors include autoimmune atrophic gastritis, pernicious anaemia, Menetrier disease and intestinal metaplasia.\\n\\nH. pylori infection is the main risk factor for 65-80% of gastric carcinomas but these only occur in approximately 2% of infections.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The majority of Hpylori infections result in stomach cancer\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastric carcinoma is commoner in women than men\",\"e\":\"Men are affected more commonly than women.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Linitis plastica tends to be associated with H pylori infection\",\"e\":\"Linitis plastica tends not to be associated with H. pylori and has a more genetic association.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Gastrointestinal stromal tumours form the most common histological subtype of gastric cancer\",\"e\":\"The most common subtype of gastric cancer is adenocarcinoma (90-95%). Other histological subtypes include: lymphoma (1-5%), gastrointestinal stromal tumours (2%), carcinoids (1%), adenoacanthomas (1%) and squamous cell carcinoma (1%).\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":439,\"stem\":\"You review a 37-year-old woman in the Emergency Department who presents with abdominal pain and vomiting. On examination, you note circumoral pigmentation and a distended abdomen. A computed tomography (CT) scan shows ileo-ileal intussusception.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is true?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is an autosomal-recessive condition\",\"e\":\"Peutz-Jeghers is an autosomal-dominant condition, It is associated with germ-line mutations in the STK11 gene on chromosome 19.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"it is characterised by polyps typically present in the small bowel\",\"e\":\"Polyps in Peutz-Jeghers are typically present in the small bowel but may affect the entire gastrointestinal (Gl) tract.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pigmentation is limited to the circumoral skin and buccal mucosa\",\"e\":\"Pigmentation can also affect the hands and genitalia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Histological examination of polyps adenomas\",\"e\":\"Polyps in Peutz-Jeghers in are hamartomas.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"There is no increased risk of cancer with this condition\",\"e\":\"Hamartomas are usually benign, there is a very small risk of malignant transformation. However, there is also an increased risk of pancreatic, breast, lung, ovarian and endometrial cancer associated with Peutz-Jeghers syndrome.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":441,\"stem\":\"A 59-year-old man presents with rectal bleeding and offensive stool. Investigations reveal a Meckel's diverticulum. Which one of the following is true regarding Meckel's diverticulum?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding Meckel's diverticulum?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It involves two layers of the intestinal wall only\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is seen in 2% of people, usually presenting in patients over two years of age\",\"e\":\"While it is present in 2% of people, most symptomatic cases will present before the age of 2.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is 60 cm from the ileocaecal valve\",\"e\":\"It is classically found in the distal ileum, 60 cm (2 feet) from the ileocaecal valve on the antimesenteric border of the ileum and is approximately 5 cm (2 inches) long*.\\n\\nA Meckel's diverticulum is an example of a true diverticulum and contains all the layers of the intestinal wall.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is usually found in the jejunum on the antimesenteric border and is approximately 5 cm long\",\"e\":\"It is classically found in the distal ileum, 60 cm from the ileocaecal vaive on the antimesenteric border and is approximately 5 cm long.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It occurs twice as frequently in women than in men\",\"e\":\"Meckel's diverticulum occurs in 2% of the population and affects men twice as often as women.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":443,\"stem\":\"An infant attends the paediatric surgical clinic with their parents as a follow up patient. They had a staged repair of a congenital abdominal wall defect.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements about exomphalos is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about exomphalos is true?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"In the vast majority of cases in which exomphalos is present it is the only congenital abnormalıty\",\"e\":\"The incidence of associated abnormalities is between 40-70%, including chromosomal (trisomies 13, 18 and 21), cardiac, genitourinary, gastrointestinal, craniofacial, and pulmonary hypoplasia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Maternal smoking decreases the risk of developing it\",\"e\":\"Both maternal alcohol use and heavy smoking are associated with an increased risk.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"it is associated with Edward syndrome\",\"e\":\"Associated syndromes include Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (features include macroglossia, macrosomia, omphalocele/exomphalos, umbilical hernia, ear pits, neonatal hypoglycaemia, hepatoblastoma), Edward syndrome (trisomy 18, features include heart defects, omphalocele, oesophageal atresia, microcephaly, micrognathia, cleft lip/palate, widely spaced eyes, ptosis, webbing of toes, undescended testicles) and Patau syndrome (trisomy 13, features include microcephaly, structural eye defects, polydactyly, low-set ears, omphalocele, rocker-bottom feet, abnormal genitalia and heart defects).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Its incidence is decreasing\",\"e\":\"Incidence is 1 in 5000 to 1 in 10 000 live births although the incidence seems to be increasing worldwide.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is associated with Potter syndrome\",\"e\":\"Potter syndrome or sequence relates to renal abnormalities, it is not associated with exomphalos. Oligohydramnios as a result of renal abnormalities, urinary tract obstruction or amniotic fluid leakage may lead to fetal compression with joint contractures (arthrogryposis), pulmonary hypoplasia and squashed facies.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":446,\"stem\":\"A surgical registrar is performing his third solo laparoscopic cholecystectomy in a 47-year- old woman. When attempting to dissect out Calot's triangle to identify the cystic artery great difficulty is experienced as the anatomy appears aberrant.\\n\\nWhat proportion of patients have anomalies of the gall-bladder and biliary tree?\",\"lead\":\"What proportion of patients have anomalies of the gall-bladder and biliary tree?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1%\",\"e\":\"Difficult biliary anatomy is relatively common, often making what is usually a routine operation very difficult.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"25%\",\"e\":\"This answer is not correct, the correct answer is 50%.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"50%\",\"e\":\"Calot's triangle, also known as the cystohepatic or hepatobiliary triangle is an anatomic space that is of great relevance when performing a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. It is bounded by the common hepatic duct medially, cystic duct laterally and liver superiorly, and is used to locate the cystic artery which runs within. Anomalies of the gall-bladdert or biliary tree are found within 50% of subjects and are important during surgery. There are a vast array of different anomalies that can occur, by way of example these anomalies include:\\n\\n• Anomalous ducts in around 12% - around 85% of which are found (and occasionally injured) within Calot's triangle: • Anomalous gall-bladder development - rarely agenesis of the gall-bladder (around 0.02%), more commonly multiple gall bladders around (1 in 3800), or other anomalies such as the gall-bladder opening directly into the side of the common bile duct ie the cystic duct is absent. • Variation in the blood vessel arrangement supplying the gall-bladder eg in 25% of people the right hepatic artery crosses in front of the common hepatic duct instead of behind it.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"75%\",\"e\":\"The variant anatomy encountered is less than this percentage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"90%\",\"e\":\"This estimate is not correct.\\n\\n382:\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":449,\"stem\":\"A neonate is noted to have persistent, progressive jaundice, pale stools and dark urine. On examination there is hepatosplenomegaly. The gall-bladder is absent on ultrasonography.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause of this?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of this?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"3\\\" Breast milk jaundice\",\"e\":\"Breast milk jaundice is a type of neonatal jaundice that is associated with breastfeeding. It is characterized by indirect hyperbilirubinemia in an otherwise healthy breastfed neonate. Signs of obstructive jaundice mentioned in the summary will be absent. Diagnosis is by exclusion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hepatocellular cholestasis\",\"e\":\"Hepatocellular cholestasis results from stasis of bile pigments within the liver. The underlying aetiology can include any of viral hepatitis, Alphal-antitrypsin deficiency, inborn errors of bile acid synthesis, drug-induced cholestasis or total parenteral nutrition (TPN) associated cholestasis. However, the clinical and biochemical picture differs as features of obstructive jaundice will be absent.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemolytic disease of the new-born\",\"e\":\"The jaundice is of non-obstructive variety. It results from rhesus incompatibility. Antibodies against Rh +ve red blood cells cross the placenta and cause haemolysis. Depending upon severity the disease may range from hydrops fetalis to neonatal jaundice at birth. Laboratory investigations may show indirect hyperbilirubinemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Paediatric biliary atresia\",\"e\":\"Biliary atresia is of unknown atiology. The extra-hepatic bile ducts are destroyed by inflammation. It presents with jaundice, pale stools and dark urine with hepatosplenomegaly. An absent gall bladder is characteristic finding of extrahepatic biliary atresia as it is atretic. Treatment requires a Kasal's procedure, this involves removal of all the extrahepatic tree and portoenterostomy, Outcome is poor if diagnosis is delayed\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Choledochal cyst\",\"e\":\"The presentation closely resembles that of extrahepatic biliary atresia. The differentiating point is presence of a gall-bladder. A choledochal cyst on ultrasound may also be visible.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":452,\"stem\":\"You receive the histology report from a specimen from a colonic resection. It reveals that the tumour extends into, but not through the muscularis propria. There is one local lymph node group involved. What is the Dukes' stage classification of the tumour according to the modified Astler- Coller system?\",\"lead\":\"What is the Dukes' stage classification of the tumour according to the modified Astler- Coller system?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"D\",\"e\":\"Stage D describes distant metastatic spread.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cl\",\"e\":\"Please see the NICE guidelines link for a comprehensive description of colorectal cancer staging by the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC, 7th edition): The modified Astler-Coller staging system:\\n\\n• Stage A: Limited to the mucosa • Stage B1: Extending into the muscularis propria but not penetrating through it; nodes are not involved. • Stage B2: Penetrating through the muscularis propria; nodes are not involved • Stage CI: Extending into the muscularis propria but not penetrating through it. Nodes are involved. • Stage C2: Penetrating through the muscularis propria. Nodes are involved. • Stage D: Distant metastatic spread.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"C2\",\"e\":\"In stage C2, the cancer penetrates through the muscularis propria and nodes are involved.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Bl\",\"e\":\"In stage B1 the cancer extends into the muscularis propria but does not penetrate through it; nodes are not involved.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"B2\",\"e\":\"In stage B2 the cancer penetrates through the muscularis propria, nodes are not involved.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":455,\"stem\":\"A 2-year-old boy is admitted with a history of intermittent rectal bleeding and complains of something protruding per anus that reduces spontaneously Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"A 2-year-old boy is admitted with a history of intermittent rectal bleeding and complains of something protruding per anus that reduces spontaneously Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anal fissure\",\"e\":\"Pain during defecation is the most prominent feature of fissure, with bright red blood, but not sensation of prolapse. Examination reveals fissure and usually no further investigations are needed. Management can include stool softeners and pain management and, if unresolving, surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intussusception\",\"e\":\"In intussusception the usual age of presentation is around 4-10 months. It comprises invagination of a proximal bowel segment into the distal bowel lumen. Paroxysms of abdominal pain occur along with characteristic red currant jelly-like stool. Intussusception may prolapse through rectum, however it is less likely to spontaneously reduce. Perineal examination would reveal that the finger can be passed between the prolapsed mass and anus (differentiating it from rectal prolapse).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hamartomatous polyp\",\"e\":\"Hamartomatous polyps (juvenile polyps) occur predominately in infants and children. The polyp is characteristically 1-2 cm in diameter with a smooth surface and slender stalk. Hamartomatous polyps mostly occur in the rectum and can prolapse through the anus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Meckel's diverticulitis\",\"e\":\"Bleeding per rectum (PR) in children younger than 2 years may occur due to Meckel's diverticulitis. Blood is typically dark red/maroon in colour. Unless associated with prolapse of intussusception, no mass protrudes per anus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)\",\"e\":\"IBD would be unusual at this age, and bleeding per rectum is not the usual presentation. Bleeding can occur in patients with known IBD. The history of protruding mass also makes this diagnosis unlikely.\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":458,\"stem\":\"A patient with Crohn's has had multiple resections for stricturing disease. They are developing further symptoms and attend the Emergency Department.\\n\\nIn a patient who has undergone an extensive ileocolic resection, which one of the following biochemical abnormalities would be most expected?\",\"lead\":\"In a patient who has undergone an extensive ileocolic resection, which one of the following biochemical abnormalities would be most expected?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A microcytic anaemia\",\"e\":\"The terminal ileum is the site of vitamin Big absorption - malabsorption leads to development of a macrocytic anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperparathyroidism\",\"e\":\"The terminal ileum is an important site of bile salt re-absorption as well as lipid soluble vitamins including Vitamin A, D. E and K. Extensive resection leads to short bowel syndrome. Vitamin D deficiency and the reduction of Ca absorption surface in the ileum can lead to hypocalcaemia and secondary hyperparathyroidism.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Vitamin K excess\",\"e\":\"The terminal ileum is an important site of bile salt re-absorption, and therefore loss results in fat and fat soluble vitamin (vitamins A, D. E and K) malabsorption.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased serum calcium\",\"e\":\"The terminal ileum is an important site of bile salt re-absorption as well as lipid soluble vitamins including Vitamin A, D, E and K. Extensive resection leads to short bowel syndrome: Vitamin D deficiency and the reduction of Ca absorption surface in the ileum can lead to hypocalcaemia and secondary hyperparathyroidism.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypermagnesaemia\",\"e\":\"The terminal ileum is an important site of vitamin B12 absorption as well as bile salt re- absorption. Loss of bile salts results in fat malabsorption and excess fatty acids within the distal bowel - these can chelate magnesium. This leads to hypomagnesaemia (not hypermagnesaemia).\"}]},{\"t\":16,\"p\":461,\"stem\":\"You are examining a 63-year-old man with abdominal pain, ascites and jaundice. An abdominal ultrasound scan (USS) shows signs suspicious of a hepatocellular carcinoma. Which one of the following is correct regarding hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Women are affected more than men\",\"e\":\"Men are more commonly affected than women, world-wide this varies between 2:1 and 4:1.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is associated with hepatitis A viruses\",\"e\":\"Common viral risk factors include hepatitis B and C viruses (HBV and HCV). In Europe and North America anti-HCV antibodies are present in around 70% of patients with hepatocellular carcinoma. While in countries with endemic hepatitis B 90% of patients with HCC are positive for hepatitis B. Alone, HIV is not a risk factor for HCC although co-infection with HCV and HIV may lead to more rapid progression to HCC.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is associated with type I diabetes\",\"e\":\"Type II diabetes with poor insulin control may be associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Serum a-fetoprotein (AFP) levels are in the normal range\",\"e\":\"Serum a-fetoprotein (AFP) levels are usually raised. Patients with cirrhosis due to HBV, HCV. alcohol, primary biliary cirrhosis or haemochromatosis should be offered a 6-monthly abdominal ultrasound scan and serum AFP test as screening for hepatocellular carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is not associated with age\",\"e\":\"Liver cancer incidence is strongly related to age, with the highest incidence rates being in older males and females. In the UK in 2012-2014, on average each year more than 4 in 10 (44%) cases were diagnosed in people aged 75 and over.\\n\\nClinical features include weight loss, anorexia, fever, a right upper quadrant pain, and ascites. Serum a-fetoprotein is usually raised. Surgical resection is occasionally possible. Chemotherapy and radiotherapy are unhelpful. Survival, except in very selected groups, is seldom more than 6 months.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old man attends the clinic with predominantly voiding lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) including hesitancy, poor flow, terminal dribbling and nocturia. He is counselled about further tests and investigations to further assess the cause of his symptoms.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is not one of the next steps/first-line investigations in the evaluation of a male patient with LUTS?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is not one of the next steps/first-line investigations in the evaluation of a male patient with LUTS?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Digital rectal examination (DRE)\",\"e\":\"A digital rectal examination is done to detect nodules that may indicate an underlying prostate cancer as well as to estimate the consistency and rough size of the prostate, which may indicate BPH. This must be done when assessing a patient with LUTS.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Transperineal prostate biopsy\",\"e\":\"This is not one of the first-line investigations that should be performed. The patient may subsequently go on to have a biopsy of the prostate gland, however it is an invasive procedure and should not be done without other vital information obtained through less invasive methods. The following investigations/tests are considered as first line in investigation of LUTS:\\n\\n• DRE • prostate specific antigen (PSA) • serum creatinine • post-void residual volume (PVR) • flow rate measurement • pressure flow studies • renal ultrasonography.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Prostate specific antigen (PSA)\",\"e\":\"PSA testing is important and a patient must be counselled on to what the results may then lead before obtaining this blood sample. There are acceptable PSA ranges according to a patient's age, other factors such as prostatitis, vigorous physical activity and instrument use can affect the PSA result.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Serum creatinine\",\"e\":\"Baseline measure of renal function as well as detection of renal failure secondary to high- pressure urinary retention is a very important first-line investigation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Post-void residual (PVR) urine volume and flow rate\",\"e\":\"Post-void residual urine volume and flow rate are important measurements as they give much information on how the urinary flow is affected. They are obligatory before undertaking any surgical treatment for benign prostatic hyperplasia. They also give a good picture of the severity of the symptoms and how they are likely to affect the patient's quality of life.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"An 80-year-old man is referred by his General Practitioner with symptoms of urinary frequency, nocturia, hesitancy and terminal dribbling. Digital rectal examination reveals a smooth bilobed enlarged prostate. The serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) level is 2 ng/ml. Which one of the following statements is true regarding the best initial management option for this patient with benign prostatic hyperplasia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding the best initial management option for this patient with benign prostatic hyperplasia?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Observation with a 6-monthly follow-up\",\"e\":\"This patient is currently symptomatic with LUTS, which are likely to be affecting his quality of life. Observation in 6 months without any intervention is unlikely to provide any benefit and, in fact, could result in worsening of symptoms resulting in unnecessary acute admissions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Transurethral resection\",\"e\":\"This is not the best management option, as conservative and medical therapy have not been trialled in the first instance. Surgical management should only be considered once conservative measures fail.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ultrasound-guided core biopsy\",\"e\":\"In this case, the patient's digital rectal examination and PSA results are not worrying currently for a prostate malignancy and would not merit a prostate biopsy at this point.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Diuretics\",\"e\":\"Diuretics are likely to worsen his symptoms and could potentially cause renal insult. There is absolutely no indication for stating a diuretic of any kind in this case. It is important also to note that we do not have a baseline serum creatinine result, which would be important\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"5-Alpha-reductase inhibitors or alpha-1-adrenergic antagonists\",\"e\":\"It is important to take into account the patient's co-morbidities before initiating drug therapy, as is ruling out other possible causes such as a urinary tract infection. For bothersome moderate-to-severe LUTS and prostate estimated >30 g or PSA > 1.4 ng/ml, consider the combination of an alpha-blocker plus 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor as a treatment option with the first review in 4-6 weeks time. The prevalence of benign prostatic hyperplasia is nil in men < 30 years, 50% in the 51-60 years age range and over 85% in men in their 80s. Proliferation of the transitional zone is seen with lateral lobe enlargement. Median lobe enlargement is due to periurethral stromal proliferation. Benign prostatic hyperplasia consists of 50% connective tissue, 25% smooth muscle and 25% epithelium. The risk of acute urinary retention is 1-2% per year and treatment consists of observation, use of drugs such as 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors, alpha-1-receptor antagonists and surgery: transurethral resection or prostatectomy\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old boy presents to the Emergency Department with a 2-h history of priapism. It is extremely painful and the emergency doctor is unfamiliar with the condition and its treatment. He contacts the Urology ST3 to enquire about his patient's problem and best treatment.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true regarding priapism?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding priapism?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Priapism can be classified as low flow, intermediate flow or high flow only\",\"e\":\"Priapism can be classified into three types:\\n\\n1. low flow (ischaemic): most common form due to veno-occlusion. Ischaemic priapism > 4 h requires emergency intervention 2. high flow (non-ischaemic): due to unregulated arterial blood flow, presenting with a semi-rigid painless erection (usually due to trauma) 3. recurrent: most commonly seen in sickle-cell disease patients.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Priapism is defined as a prolonged, unwanted erection in the absence of sexual desire or stimulus lasting for > 1h\",\"e\":\"Priapism is a prolonged, unwanted erection in the absence of sexual desire or stimulus lasting for > 4h.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Priapism is regularly associated with thalassaemia\",\"e\":\"Recurrent or stuttering priapism is associated with sickle-cell disease. It is usually high flow but may change to low flow. Some recent studies have shown some association with thalassemia major, but this is not the best answer here.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Low-flow priapism may be treated by urgent decompression by aspirating blood from the corpora with a 21-gauge cannula\",\"e\":\"Management of priapism:\\n\\n• low flow: urgent decompression with aspiration of blood from the corpora. If there is no change after 10 min, try intracavernosal injection of alpha-1-adrenergic agonist every 5-10 min until cetumescence occurs. Monitor blood pressure (BP) and pulse during administration. If these fail after 1.h, surgical intervention may be required • high flow: conservative treatment recommended in most cases. Traumatic or delayed presentations require arteriography, embolisation or ligation of fistula.\\n\\nPriapism, sustained painful penile erection, is commonly iatrogenic, occurring after the administration of intracavernosal agents used to treat erectile dysfunction: It may also occur in sickle-cell disease and affects only the corpora cavernosa. Non-operative treatment includes aspiration, irrigation with heparinised saline and/or administrat on of intracavernosal metaraminol or phenylephrine. Surgical techniques include the formation of a caverno-spongiosal shunt. Impotence may result, but is less likely if the condition is effectively treated\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Renal-cell carcinoma is the most common cancer associated with priapism\",\"e\":\"Prostate or bladder cancer that extends into the penis could cause priapism; this would be most unusual for renal cancer\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 2-year-old child attends the Paediatric Urology Clinic because the parents are worried that one of his testes is missing. They have not been able to palpate the right testicle since birth. On examination, the testis is absent from the right hemiscrotum. It is palpable within the inguinal canal; however it cannot be milked into the scrotum.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true about undescended testes (UDT)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true about undescended testes (UDT)?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The incidence of undescended testicles at 1 year of age is 3%\",\"e\":\"At birth (of a full-term neonate), the incidence of undescended testes is 4%, however many testicles will spontaneously descend and at 1 year of age the incidence drops to 1.3-1.8%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Undescended testicles can be categorised into three types: retractile, ectopic and atrophic\",\"e\":\"There are five common classifications for undescended testes\\n\\n• retractile: intermittently active cremasteric reflex results in a testis that retracts up and out of the scrotum • ectopic: abnormal migration of the testis below the external ring (ie perineum, base of penis, femoral triangle) • incomplete descent: intra-abdominal, intra-inguinal or pre-scrotal • atrophic (absent) • acquired UDT: testes that have ascended.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"High birthweight is a risk factor for developing undescended testes\",\"e\":\"Risk factors for undescended testes include:\\n\\n• family history • twins • pre-term infants • low birthweight.\\n\\nHigh birthweight is not a recognised risk factor and, in fact, infants with a high birthweight would be less likely to have undescended testes compared with their low birthweight counterparts.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The relative risk of testicular cancer is eight-fold higher. Reduced fertility is also a long-term complication.\",\"e\":\"Risk of cancer, reduced fertility and increased risk of testicular torsion or trauma are all long- term complications of undescended testes. By the age of 2, the testis is unlikely to descend. It is preferable to perform an orchidopexy at this stage before the child becomes of school age. Up to one-third of patients with a unilateral undescended testis have a contralateral patent processus vaginalis (and therefore increased risk of contralateral cryptorchidism), contralateral orchidopexy is therefore also performed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inguinal undescended testes is treated with orchidectomy between 6-18 months of age\",\"e\":\"Orchidopexy is the treatment of choice for undescended testes, and describes fixing of the testes within the scrotum. Orchidectomy is removal of the testis, indicated in testicular malignancy and occasionally when testicular trauma has occurred, most commonly in road traffic accidents.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old scaffolder presents to the Urology Clinic with a 3-month history of swelling in the left hemiscrotum. On further questioning, he reveals he had an orchidopexy for an undescended left testicle when he was an infant. Clinical examination reveals a smooth, hard lump in the left hemiscrotum that cannot be differentiated from the testicle itself. It does not transilluminate.\\n\\nWhat is the optimal management of this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the optimal management of this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Needle aspiration\",\"e\":\"This option is not appropriate as there is a suspicion that this might be a testicular malignancy. Aspiration is contraindicated as it has the possibility of seeding the cancer cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ultrasound scan and needle aspiration\",\"e\":\"An ultrasound of the testicle is not an unreasonable suggestion, however other important tests should be conducted. Aspiration is not appropriate here\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ultrasound scan, beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin (b-HCG) and alpha- fetoprotein blood tests and if positive orchidectomy and/or chemotherapy\",\"e\":\"A multi-disciplinary team (MDT) discussion is crucial before a decision of management of any cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ultrasound scan, beta-HCG and alpha-feroprotein blood tests and Jaboulay's repair\",\"e\":\"Jaboulay's repair is indicated for hydroceles, not in suspected testicular malignancy. An ultrasound scan is usually obtained before surgery to assess the size and confirm the extent of the hydrocele.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ultrasound scan, beta-HCG, lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) and alpha-fetoprotein blood tests and, if positive, a discussion in the multi-disciplinary team (MDT) meeting prior to orchidectomy\",\"e\":\"Initial blood test markers must be measured at presentation: alpha-fetoprotein, HCG and LDH. An ultrasound of the testes can further characterise the lump and a staging CT scan would assess the extent of cancer spread and be done prior to or after surgery. An MDT discussion is the gold standard and a very important part of all cancer management. A testicular lump in a young man should always evoke suspicion for a cancer, particularly in this patient with a positive risk factor, which is a history of undescended testicle which increases his risk by about eight-fold. Differential diagnoses of a testicular lump include:\\n\\n• hydrocele • epididymal cyst • testicular malignancy • indirect inguinal hernia\\n\\nA hydrocele is the abnormal accumulation of fluid within the tunica vaginalis. Secondary hydroceles may result from testicular injury, tumour, heart failure or obstruction of the retroperitoneal lymphatics. The Jaboulay procedure involves excision and eversion of the hydrocoele sac and Lord's procedure is plication of the sac. In this age group, a secondary hydrocoele caused by testicular malignancy is a real possibility. Non-seminomatous germ- cell tumours are commoner in the age group 20-30 years for which the tumour markers alpha-fetoprotein and beta-HCG are elevated.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old man is catheterised for urinary retention with a 14 French catheter, but insertion is difficult initially. As the Urology Registrar, you are called due to scrotal and perineal swelling. Blood is seen at the meatus and the catheter has not drained urine over the last 3 h. The patient is now in considerable discomfort.\\n\\nWhat will be the most appropriate management step?\",\"lead\":\"What will be the most appropriate management step?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Flexible or rigid cystoscopy\",\"e\":\"It is likely that the urethra has suffered trauma here resulting in a false passage. It is not appropriate to perform a cystoscopy as it could be difficult to ascertain in the acute setting the true lumen of the urethra. The patient is also in acute urinary retention, which needs managing.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cystourethrogram\",\"e\":\"This option would not be conducted in the acute setting. A retrograde urethrogram should be done electively once the injury to the urethra has had a chance to heal and can be assessed properly\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Surgical repair of the urethra on a CEPOD theatre\",\"e\":\"This is not appropriate in the acute setting. The patient is in acute urinary retention and this needs to be addressed first. Urine also needs to be diverted away from the urethra to allow it to heal over time. Repair in the acute setting will result in a high chance of complications.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Video urodynamics\",\"e\":\"Video urodynamics is important in assessing the behaviour and function of the bladder and urinary tract. It does not play a role in this particular case.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Suprapubic catheter under ultrasound guidance\",\"e\":\"Insertion of a suprapubic catheter under ultrasound guidance (which is the gold standard) will treat the acute urinary retention and relieve the patient of pain and discomfort. Secondarily, it will also divert the urine away from the urethra to give it a chance to heal over time. In this particular example, it is clear that a significant urethral injury has occurred, so there is little alternative to immediately drain the bladder. Traumatic catheter insertion may rupture the posterior and anterior urethra. Rupture of the spongy (penile) part of the urethra leaks urine into the superficial perineal fascia (of Colles), which is continuous with the membranous fascia (of Scarpa) in the anterior abdominal wall - such urine leakage may result in swelling of the perineum and scrotum. Blood may also be seen at the urethral meatus. A suprapubic catheter may be used to temporarily divert urine out of the bladder and away from the injured urethra. Investigation with a retrograde urethrogram and repair should be considered at 6-12 weeks post injury.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man presents with a 2-day history of left-sided lower abdominal and scrotal pain. He is pyrexial at 37.8°C and visibly in pain. On examination, it is evident that the left testicle is erythematous, hot to the touch and swollen.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is most commonly caused by a virus\",\"e\":\"Acute epididymitis is inflammation of the epididymis and is most commonly caused by bacterial infection\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"In men aged <35 years, the most common pathogen is Staphylococcus aureus\",\"e\":\"In sexually active men aged under 35 years, the most common pathogen is usually Neisseria gonorrhoea, Chlamydia trachomatis or coliform. In older men and children, Escherichia coli is more common.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Amiodarone can potentially cause this condition\",\"e\":\"A rare, non-infectious cause of epididymitis is amiodarone, the antiarrhythmic drug which accumulates in high concentrations within the epididymis, causing inflammation. It can be unilateral or bilateral and resolves on discontinuation of the drug\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Treatment of choice is usually a 7-day course of an antibiotic\",\"e\":\"Antibiotics is the mainstay of treatment until urine culture sensitivities are available, however it is typically given over a 2-week period. Doxycycline or ciprofloxacin is usually prescribed, depending on suspected aetiology.\\n\\nIS PAC\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A complication of this condition commonly includes haematoma formation\",\"e\":\"Recognised complications of epididymitis are:\\n\\n• abscess formation • infarction of the testis • chronic pain and infection • infertility\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"You are examining a 40-year-old man patient who presents with a painless scrotal lump which he has now had for 5 years and is increasing in size. On examination, it is non-tender, soft and is indistinuishable from the testis.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epidydimal cyst\",\"e\":\"Epididymal cysts are often felt distinctly from the testis itself and are often multiple.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gumma\",\"e\":\"Gummas are rare and are due to syphilis of the testis, resulting in a round, hard insensitive mass involving the testis which is difficult to distinguish from a tumour.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hydrocoele\",\"e\":\"This is the most likely diagnosis as it fits the criteria of a hydrocele:\\n\\n• painless • difficult or impossible to feel the testis, which is surrounded by a fluid collection • often chronic • slowly increases in size over time.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Teratoma\",\"e\":\"Usually a solid mass, unlikely to be the most likely diagnosis as it would have disastrous consequences if present for 5 years.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Varicocoele\",\"e\":\"It is arguable that this is a possible diagnosis, however not the most likely one. It does not feel cystic in nature and, conversely, is often described as feeling 'like a bag of worms' and can be responsible for a dragging sensation or an ache in the scrotum. This condition is often painful.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A concerned mother brings her 7-month-old infant to the Paediatric Urology Clinic She tells you she has noticed 'an opening of the water pipe', which, on examination, is confirmed to be a hypospadias.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding hypospadias?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding hypospadias?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is a congenital deformity in which the opening of the urethra (meatus) is sited on the dorsal part of the penis\",\"e\":\"In hypospadias, the opening of the urethral meatus in fact sites on the underside (ventral) part of the penis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It can be classified into anterior and posterior only\",\"e\":\"Hypospadias can be classified according to the anatomical location of the urethral meatus. There are three types:\\n\\n• anterior - most common (50-80%) • middle • posterior - penoscrotal, scrotal and perineal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Associated anomalies include undescended testes, inguinal hernia, disorders of sexual development (DSD) and hydroceles\",\"e\":\"Hypospadias are associated with other genitourinary anomalies such as undescended testes, inguinal hernia, DSD and hydroceles due to it being an embryological defect\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Repair is performed after 3 years of age\",\"e\":\"Surgery is indicated when the deformity is severe, interferes with voiding or is predicted to interfere with sexual function. Repair is performed between 6-18 months of age.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urethral diverticulum is not a known complication of surgical repair of hypospadias\",\"e\":\"Complications of surgical repair include urethrocutaneous fistula, urethral stricture, poor cosmesis, urethral diverticulum, meatal stenosis, spraying of urine and voiding dysfunction.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old woman attends the Urology Clinic complaining of a 2-week history of visible haematuria and increased urinary frequency as well as occasional flank pain. She has recently undergone an elective rigid cystoscopy and intradetrusor Botox injections. She has not been sexually active in the last year.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely pathogen responsible for her urinary tract infections (UTI)?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely pathogen responsible for her urinary tract infections (UTI)?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"E. coli\",\"e\":\"E. coli is responsible for most UTIs in the community however this patient reports recent instrumentalisation which makes Staphylococcus aureus more likely to be the organism responsible. UTIs are the most common bacterial infections in all age groups. Women are at greater risk of UTIs than men because a shorter urethra allows transfer of faecal flora; transfer of vaginal flora occurs during sexual intercourse and hormonal changes cause changes in urine composition during pregnancy and the menopause.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Streptococcus faecalis\",\"e\":\"Streptococcus faecalis (Enterobacteria) is a less common cause of acute uncomplicated UTI in both sexes and at all ages\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pseudomonas spp.\",\"e\":\"Pscudomonas is usually indicative of a foreign body ie: mesh, suture.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus saprophyticus\",\"e\":\"Staphylococcus saprophyticus is particularly seen in sexually active women and the question states that she has not been sexuaily active in the last year.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus aureus\",\"e\":\"Staphylococcus aureus is the most likely pathogen given a history of recent surgery on the urinary tract.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"A patient is being seen in urology clinic with suspected prostate cancer. They are concerned aboul their ongoing investigations.\\n\\nRegarding prostatic cancer (CaP), which one of the following statements is true?\\n\\nA Caucasians are at greatest risk of developing prostate cancer. Current detection strategies include the efficient use of a combination of digital rectal examination (DRE), serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) and transrectal ultrasound (TRUS) with prostatic biopsy.\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Caucasians are at greatest risk of developing prostate cancer. Current detection strategies include the efficient use of a combination of digital rectal examination (DRE), serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) and transrectal ultrasound (TRUS) with prostatic biopsy\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gleason score of 15 indicates poorly differentiated tumour\",\"e\":\"Gleason scoring does not go beyond 10; the range is between 2-10.\\n\\n• Gleason 2-6: Well differentiated • Gleason 7: Moderately differentiated • Gleason 8-10: Poorly differentiated\\n\\nGleason score is obtained by adding the primary and secondary grades together.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"PSA is the single most useful test for screening of CaP for men of all age group\",\"e\":\"Not all patients with an elevated PSA concentration have CaP because PSA is not cancer specific, it is prostate-specific.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"About 70% of CaP arises in the peripheral zone of the prostate gland and most are multifocal\",\"e\":\"About 75% of adenocarcinomas are located in the peripheral zone and most (85%) are multifocal.\\n\\nA Caucasians are at greatest risk of developing prostate cancer. Current detection strategies include the efficient use of a combination of digital rectal examination (DRE), serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) and transrectal ultrasound (TRUS) with prostatic biopsy.\\n\\nAfro-Caribbean men are at greatest risk, followed by Caucasians. The disease is uncommon in Asians and Oriental races, depending on migration to the West. The probability of CaP developing in a man under the age of 40 is 1 in 10 000; for men 40 to 59 it is 1 in 103; and 1 in 8 for men 60 to 79.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transurethral resection of prostate (TURP) is the treatment of choice for localised disease\",\"e\":\"The treatment options for localised CaP include watchful waiting, radical prostatectomy and radiotherapy.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old man has a serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) concentration of 15 ng/l. He has been referred into the urology clinic for further assessment.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true with regard to this result?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true with regard to this result?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A small proportion of poorly differentiated prostate cancers fail to express PSA\",\"e\":\"PSA generally increases with advancing stage and tumour volume, however a small proportion of tumours fail to express PSA.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is diagnostic of malignancy\",\"e\":\"PSA is prostate-specific, not prostate cancer specific and hence is not, in itself, diagnostic of malignancy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is likely to be invalidated if he underwent a digital rectal examination (DRE) 48 h before the blood sample was taken\",\"e\":\"In practice, a DRE only affects the PSA by <1 ng/ml and would not invalidate the result or account for an abnormally high result. Prostatitis and acute urinary retention can also cause a transient rise in PSA.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is prognostically highly significant\",\"e\":\"The absolute PSA concentration correlates poorly with prognosis in prostate cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is unremarkable for a man of his age\",\"e\":\"Over 50% of patients have extraprostatic disease if PSA >10 ng/mi hence this is a significantly raised PSA. In general, the higher the PSA, the greater the likelihood of malignancy, but some patients with malignancy have normal levels (often taken as less than 4 ng/1, but are actually age-dependent).\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with suprapubic discomfort and urinary retention. He has a serum creatinine concentration of 522 mmol/I (normal 50-120 umol/I). He is known to have chronic kidney disease stage 3\\n\\nWhich one of the following features, if present, would help to identify possible atiologies for his renal failure and suggest that it is chronic?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following features, if present, would help to identify possible atiologies for his renal failure and suggest that it is chronic?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anaemia\",\"e\":\"Anaemia (due to decreased erythropoietin secretion) and secondary hyperparathyroidism (decreased calcitriol secretion causes hypocalcaemia) are features, not causes, of chronic renal failure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Small kidneys on ultrasound examination\",\"e\":\"The kidneys are usually decreased in size in chronic renal failure, due to atrophy with a loss of nephrons and vascular tissue - except when this is due to amyloid or polycystic disease. Kidneys are usually normal in size in advanced diabetic nephropathy, whereas affected kidneys are initially enlarged from hyperfiltration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"High serum parathyroid hormone concentration\",\"e\":\"Secondary hyperparathyroidism (decreased calcitriol secretion causes hypocalcaemia) is a feature of chronic renal failure, not a cause.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypotension\",\"e\":\"Hypertension, not hypotension, can be both a cause and a consequence of renal failure. Hypotension is rarely associated with renal failure and is certainly not a cause of chronic renal failure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypouricaemia\",\"e\":\"Uric acid nephropathy can cause renal failure but hyperuricaemia is usual in chronic renal failure, due to decreased excretion.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man takes advantage of his employer's health benefits and undergoes a private health screen. Urinalysis is positive for protein. His serum urea and creatinine concentrations are normal\\n\\nWhich one of the following options would be the most important investigations to perform next?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options would be the most important investigations to perform next?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"History, blood pressure, protein creatinine ratio (PCR) and fasting blood glucose\",\"e\":\"To detect and identify proteinuria, use urine albumin/creatinine ratio (ACR) in preference to PCR, because it has greater sensitivity than PCR for low levels of proteinuria.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"History, blood pressure, albumin: creatinine ratio (ACR), fasting blood glucose and urine protein electrophoresis\",\"e\":\"This is the correct answer; a thorough work-up is important to ascertain the atiology of proteinuria. Its presence generally suggests renal disease or multiple myeloma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"History, blood pressure, mid-stream urine for culture and sensitivity and fasting blood glucose\",\"e\":\"Proteinuria is not an indication of a urine infection, but can be present concurrently. It is important to rule out an infection but is not one of the most important first line investigations.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"History, blood pressure, 24-h urine collection and urine protein electrophoresis\",\"e\":\"The main reason for performing urine protein electrophoresis is to find a light chain myeloma producing an excess of free light chains (Bence Jones protein), an important part of a myeloma screen. In this case, other investigations such as ACR should be done first.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"History, blood pressure and 24-hour urine collection\",\"e\":\"Albumin: creatinine ratios (ACR) are generally more useful than 24-hour collections hence are preferable. These collections are useful in metabolic screening for kidney stones.\\n\\nProteinuria can occur because of glomerular damage, tubular damage (hence the decreased absorption of the small amount of low molecular weight proteins that are normally filtered by the glomeruli), an increased concentration of low-molecular-weight proteins in the blood (eg immunoglobulin light chains in myeloma - Bence Jones - protein) and secretion of proteins into the urine (eg in urinary tract infection). Tubular proteins and Bence Jones protein can be detected by electrophoresis of concentrated urine. However, particularly in a young person, orthostatic proteinuria should be excluded before proceeding to further investigation.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"A 20-year-old man presents with a painless lump in his testicle. He undergoes an ultrasound which is suspicious for malignancy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true of testicular malignancies?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true of testicular malignancies?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Testicular tumours can present with gynaecomastia\",\"e\":\"Gynaecomastia is a rare presenting feature but can occur in sex hormone-secreting interstitial-cell tumours, and may be noticed before the lump is seen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Teratomas are the commonest type of testicular malignancy\",\"e\":\"Seminomas are germ-cell tumours arising from the seminiferous epithelium and are the commonest form of malignant testicular tumour, accounting for 40% of tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In pure seminomas a-fetoprotein (AFP) is markedly raised\",\"e\":\"AFP is not produced by pure seminomas, it is produced by yolk-sac elements, which are not present in seminomas. b-Human chorionic gonadotrophin (b-hCG) and AFP are used to aid diagnosis and monitoring of response to treatment in teratomas. In seminomas b-hCG is more useful as a tumour marker, along with lactate dehydrogenase, which is less specific but can give an idea of tumour burden.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Teratomas are less aggressive than seminomas\",\"e\":\"Teratomas are more aggressive than seminomas. Seminomas tend to spread by the lymphatics to the para-aortic nodes, whereas teratomas spread haematogenously and occasionally to lymph nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"An orchidectomy for malignancy should be performed through a scrotal incision\",\"e\":\"Any orchidectomy performed for malignancy must be performed through an inguinal incision, not a scrotal one. This is done to allow control of the blood vessels and the vas. These vessels must both be divided above a tourniquet placed on them. If the scrotum is incised there is a risk of seeding and recurrence. If there is doubt, a frozen section can be done but should also be through an inguinal incision.\\n\\n2504 м\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"An elderly man presents with painless hematuria. The last episode in the past was painful. Which one of the following statements is true about his possible diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true about his possible diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Painless haematuria cannot be a urinary calculus\",\"e\":\"Urinary calculi can cause painless haematuria and is one of the considerations when investigating a patient in the elective setting, although a painful haematuria is more commonly seen in the hospital setting, for example in renal colic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Squamous-cell carcinoma is associated with exposure to certain dyes, and smokers\",\"e\":\"Squamous-cell carcinoma arises from metaplastic squamous epithelium and is associated with chronic irritation of the bladder for example from long-term catheter use or in parts of the world where the parasitic infection schistosomiasis is more common. Transitional-cell carcinoma is associated with some aniline dyes in the textile industry, reagents in rubber, smoking and analgesic abuse.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Squamous-cell carcinomas are usually solid and invasive\",\"e\":\"Painless haematuria is the most common presentation of a bladder cancer and initial investigations may include imaging such as an ultrasound scan or a computed tomography (CT) urogram in addition to a flexible cystoscopy. At flexible cystoscopy, the squamous-cell carcinoma appears as a solid lesion commonly found on the trigone or lateral walls. It is typically very invasive and therefore often high grade at diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma in situ is not a precursor to invasive carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Carcinoma in situ is a type of non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer. It appears as flat red lesions within the bladder. It is high grade, meaning that it is more likely to recur and progress than other non-muscle-invasive bladder cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Metastatic lung carcinoma does not produce bladder secondaries\",\"e\":\"Secondary tumours, by haematogenous and lymphatic spread, can be found in the bladder; although they are rare. Direct spread from the cervix, prostate or rectum is more common.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"Prostate cancer is the most common cancer in men in the UK. A bulky and hard prostate may be found on examination.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true about prostate cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true about prostate cancer?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Prostate carcinoma arises in the paraurethral tissue\",\"e\":\"Carcinoma of the prostate arises in the peripheral glands rather than the paraurethral glands, which is why it often takes longer to become symptomatic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Malignant change may occur in the compressed normal tissue left behind after TURP\",\"e\":\"Malignant change can happen in any prostatic tissue left behind after transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP). If there are clinical concerns regarding prostate cancer then having had a previous TURP should not prevent the patient undergoing further assessment for\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Average survival after diagnosis of metastatic disease is 6 months\",\"e\":\"With asymptomatic metastatic disease the average survival is 2-3 years, this reduces to 12 months with symptomatic disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"In T, stage from the TNM classification a physician would be able to palpate a nodule on rectal examination\",\"e\":\"At the T, stage there is clinically inapparent tumour that is not palpable, usually diagnosed either incidentally from histology of resected prostatic tissue eg post TURP; or identified by biopsy. With a palpable abnormality of the prostate the clinical staging would be Ta or above.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is used as a screening tool for prostate cancer\",\"e\":\"PSA is not used as a screening tool for prostate cancer, it has both a high false-positive and false-negative rate. Other conditions that increase a patient's PSA include: benign prostatic enlargement; prostatitis, acute urinary retention; and catheterisation. Patients should be appropriately counselled before being offered a PSA test as it can lead to further invasive investigations that have significant morbidity:\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"A 13-year-old girl is seen in the gynaecology out-patients clinic with haematocolpos. She noticed a mass in her abdomen.\\n\\nWhich of the following conditions is most likely to be associated with haematocolpos?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following conditions is most likely to be associated with haematocolpos?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cervical condyioma\",\"e\":\"Cervical condylomas are related to human papillomavirus (HPV) infections and are pre- cancerous lesions. Treatment is with excisional biopsy, diathermy or laser vaporisation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cervical gonorrhoea\",\"e\":\"Gonorrhoea is a sexually transmitted infection that can affect the mucous membranes of the cervix, uterus, fallopian tubes and urethra. Untreated gonorrhoea can cause pelvic inflammatory disease, which can lead to pelvic abscesses and chronic pelvic pain.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Endometriosis\",\"e\":\"Endometriosis is the presence of endometrial mucosa in abnormal location (other than the uterus). It is most commonly associated with dysmenorrhoea and menorrhagia but without another abnormality would not result in the accumulation of menstrual blood in the vagina.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Imperforate hymen\",\"e\":\"Haematocolpos (accumulation of menstrual blood in the vagina) can cause the vagina to bulge and in turn result in haematometra (accumulation of blood in the uterus), which can cause uterine distension or a mass. It is most likely to be associated with an imperforate hymen. In about 1 in 2000 women, the hymen fails to develop any opening at all. This condition is called an imperforate hymen and if it does not spontaneously resolve itself before puberty a physician will need to make a hole in the hymen (hymenotomy) to allow menstrual discharge to escape.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ruptured Bartholin's cyst\",\"e\":\"A Bartholin's cyst is a small fluid-filled sac just inside the opening of the vagina, it has the potential to become infected and if ruptured would then drain pus. It would not lead to haematocolpos (accumulation of menstrual blood in the vagina).\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man presented in the surgical outpatient clinic complaining of a left scrotal swelling. On examination, he had a painless, transilluminant swelling at the upper pole of the left testis. No cough impulse was visible or palpable.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Haematocele\",\"e\":\"Testicular haematocele are usually a result of trauma and occur when the bleeding remains within the tunica vaginalis, if the tunica vaginalis is broken then a scrotal haematoma would form. On examination there is typically a swelling with overlying ecchymosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inguinal hernia\",\"e\":\"It is possible to differentiate between a scrotal swelling and inguinal hernia on examination alone. If the examiner can get above the swelling then it is arising from the scrotum, if not then it is more likely to be an inguinal hernia. In addition hernias when not incarcerated would have a cough impulse.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pyocoele\",\"e\":\"Pyocoeles are purulent fluid collections typically in the scrotal sac that can occur as a complication of epididymo-orchitis. It is an uncommon presentation but must be recognised and quickly treated as it can lead to Fournier's gangrene. They typically present with a short history of scrotal swelling, pain and erythema.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spermatocele\",\"e\":\"A spermatocele is a painless fluid-filled cyst that usually occurs at the upper pole of the testis, adjacent to the epididymis and appears as a cystic scrotal mass. A large spermatocele might be difficult to differentiate from a hydrocoele, which is also cystic, painless and transilluminates. Ultrasonography can be helpful in reaching a diagnosis. Surgical excision is indicated if the spermatocele becomes large and symptomatic..\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Varicocele\",\"e\":\"A varicocele is an enlargement of the pampiniform venous plexus in the scrotum. On examination the classical description is that the scrotal sac feels like a bag of worms. Do not forget that a left-sided varicocele could be the only presenting symptom of a left renal cancer, as the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old man presents with a 1 h history of severe testicular pain and is rushed to theatre for an emergency scrotal exploration. The testis appears to be torted and is repaired in the usual fashion. At closure, the surgeon is satisfied that normal perfusion has been restored\\n\\nThe patient has an increased risk of which condition?\",\"lead\":\"The patient has an increased risk of which condition?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chronic groin pain\",\"e\":\"Chronic groin pain is not a typical complication of testicular torsion or scrotal exploration but can happen following inguinal hernia repair. Chronic scrotal pain is associated with testicular torsion and scrotal exploration.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased risk of cancer in the contralateral testis\",\"e\":\"Evidence exists to indicate that, following testicular torsion, there is an increased risk of testicular cancer in both the ipsilateral and contralateral testis. The reason for this is unclear.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inguino-scrotal hernia\",\"e\":\"There is no link between testicular torsion or scrotal exploration and inguino-scrotal hernia. However, there is a link with open appendicectomy and inguinal hernias thought to be due to damage to the nerve supply during the incision resulting in weakness of the deep inguinal ring.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Epididymal cyst\",\"e\":\"Epididymal cysts are fluid-filled cysts arising from the epididymis. They are a common finding and do not require treatment unless symptomatic. The reason for their development is unclear but there is no known link with testicular torsion\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Varicocele\",\"e\":\"There is no reported risk of varicocele after testicular torsion. Varicocele can cause discomfort and may be a differential to consider in a patient presenting with testicular pain, but should be easy to distinguish from a torsion on examination with its classical description of a bag of worms.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old man presents to Accident and Emergency with acute pain passing urine, haematuria and a dull ache over his flank. He is known to have cystinuria.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements about cystinuria is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about cystinuria is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Can be prevented with allopurinol therapy\",\"e\":\"Allopurinol therapy can be used to help prevent uric acid stones but are not useful for cystine stones. However, one treatment option for cystinuria is alkalinisation of the urine with citrate supplementation or acetazolaminde, or dietary modification to reduce salt and protein intake (especially methionine)\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Renal stones may be demonstrated by ultrasonography\",\"e\":\"Renal stones can be detected on ultrasonography, which is particularly useful in young patients and pregnant patients when you do not want to expose them to the radiation of a computed tomography (CT) scan of the kidneys, ureters and bladder (KUB). Up to 40% of patients suffering from cystinuria may develop mixed calculi that will also contain calcium oxalate, calcium phosphate or struvite.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"May be caused by d-Penicillamine\",\"e\":\"d-Penicillamine can be used to help prevent cystine stones and therefore does not cause them. It combines with cystine to form a soluble complex preventing stone formation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Occur more commonly than uric acid stones\",\"e\":\"The incidence of uric acid stones is 8-10% with cystine stones occurring in 1-2% of patients.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tend to precipitate in alkaline urine\",\"e\":\"Cystine stones tend to precipitate in acidic urine. Alkalinisation with potassium citrate is a treatment for cystinuria.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old man presents to the Urology department with excruciating intermittent pain in his left flank radiating to his groin. He has associated sweating, nausea, vomiting, and urinary urgency. A diagnosis of renal stones is made.\\n\\nIn the management of renal stones, which one of the following is a contraindication to percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL)?\",\"lead\":\"In the management of renal stones, which one of the following is a contraindication to percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL)?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abdominal aortic aneurysms\",\"e\":\"Abdominal aortic aneurysms are not a contraindication to PCNL but are to extracorporea! shock wave lithotripsy due to the risk of rupture.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Clotting anormalities\",\"e\":\"Clotting abnormalities should be corrected before PCNL, this diagnosis is also a contraindication for extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy. In patients with an obstructing calculus requiring emergency intervention and clotting abnormalities, a ureteric stent in the first instance would be appropriate to decompress the kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Large-volume stones\",\"e\":\"PCNL is the treatment of choice for large-volume stones in the kidney. It should be considered first line for stones >20 mm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Stones in a transplanted kidney\",\"e\":\"PCNL is a safe treatment for stones in a transplanted kidney but should be carried out in a specialist centre.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Patients with urinary diversion in the form of an ileal conduit\",\"e\":\"PCNL is often considered in patients with kidney stones that have undergone urinary diversion. A retrograde approach can also be considered if access to the ureters can be gained.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old smoker presents with painful haematuria and weight loss. He is diagnosed with squamous-cell carcinoma of the bladder.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is associated with bladder exstrophy\",\"e\":\"Bladder exstrophy is a congenital abnormality when the bladder is open and exposed on the outside of the abdomen. It is most commonly associated with adenocarcinoma of the\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It accounts for 25% of all bladder cancers in the UK\",\"e\":\"Transitional-cell carcinoma is the most common type of bladder carcinoma. Squamous-cell carcinoma accounts for 3-7% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It can occur in patients with long-term indwelling catheters\",\"e\":\"A risk factor for the development of squamous-cell carcinoma is chronic inflammation and irritation, which can be caused by long-term catheters.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is associated with acute infection\",\"e\":\"Squamous-cell carcinoma is not associated with acute infection although symptoms of acute infection such as dysuria and frequency without evidence of infection on urinalysis can be an indication of malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is uncommon in areas where schistosomiasis is endemic\",\"e\":\"Schistosomiasis is a risk factor for squamous-cell bladder cancer and in regions where schistosomiasis is endemic squamous-cell carcinoma can account for up to 70% of bladder cancers.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old man presents with haematuria, nocturia and urinary frequency. He is diagnosed with prostatic cancer.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in prostatic cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct in prostatic cancer?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It almost never develops in men castrated before puberty\",\"e\":\"Testosterone and its metabolite dihydrotestosterone (DHT) are essential for normal prostate growth. Prostate cancer is exceptionally rare in men castrated before puberty.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Prostate cancer is typically treated with chemotherapy\",\"e\":\"Not all prostate cancer is clinically significant meaning that low-grade prostate cancers often require no treatment and rather need surveillance as they are unlikely to progress to a point that makes the patient symptomatic or effects their mortality. In those cases that are clinically significant depending on the grade, staging and patient factors the main treatment options are hormonal treatments, radiotherapy and surgery. Chemotherapy agents are only used in selected cases usually after other failed treatment options.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is staged with the Gleason system\",\"e\":\"Staging is based on the TM classification system, which combines examination, histological and imaging findings usually in the form of magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) with other modalities such as a bone scan if indicated\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It can be staged with ultrasound\",\"e\":\"Ultrasound is not used for staging prostate cancer but can be used to assess the size of the prostate, which is occasionally done before surgery in benign prostatic hyperplasia or to take biopsies (transrectal ultrasound guided biopsy).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The incidence among Japanese men is high\",\"e\":\"Black men have the highest incidence rate of prostate cancer. Japanese men show one of the lowest incidence of prostate cancer.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old man presents with a testicular lump, and describes a heaviness' You suspect testicular seminoma\\n\\nIn testicular seminoma, which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"In testicular seminoma, which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Never present with acute testicular pain\",\"e\":\"Testicular tumours are typically painless although they can present with acute testicular pain and this should not be forgotten when assessing an acute scrotum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It presents with a lump in the scrotum in 8% cases\",\"e\":\"The most common presentation of any testicular tumour is a painless lump.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is radioinsensitive\",\"e\":\"Seminomas are radio-sensitive and radiotherapy is offered in select cases after radical inguinal orchidectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It may secrete B-HCG\",\"e\":\"5-10% of seminomas secrete B-HCG, which can cause gynaecomastia or mastitis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The primary treatment of any testicular tumour is always radical inguinal orchidectomy\",\"e\":\"This is true with one exception. If a patient presents with widespread metastatic disease typically identified by a preoperative chest X-ray or if available on a computed tomography (CT) scan of the chest, abdomen and pelvis. The patient should then be immediately discussed with the oncology team and will likely undergo chemotherapy before surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":76,\"stem\":\"A 44-year-old man presents with a testicular lump. On examination his testis feels firm and bosselated. Scrotal ultrasonography confirms the diagnosis of a testicular tumour\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true in testicular tumours?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true in testicular tumours?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Sertoli cell tumours always present with gynaecomastia\",\"e\":\"Sertoli cell tumours are rare and like all other testicular tumours most commonly present as a painless lump, they can but not always present with gynaecomastia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Teratomas are extremely radiosensitive\",\"e\":\"Primary treatment for testicular tumours usually begins with radical inguinal orchidectomy. Post-operatively chemotherapy is often offered depending on staging. Radiotherapy is not part of treatment for teratoma but is used for seminoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The measurement of tumour markers is useful in the management of teratomas\",\"e\":\"Tumour markers a-fetaprotein and B-hCG are elevated in approximately 50% and 40%, respectively, of non-seminomatous germ-cell tumours which includes teratomas. If elevated at first presentation then they should be repeated 7 days post-orchidectomy and are used to monitor response to treatment and recurrence.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Teratomas are the most common tumour\",\"e\":\"Seminomas are the most common type of testicular tumours and form approximately half of all cases.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Testicular tumours never present with a hydrocele\",\"e\":\"Patients with testicular tumours can develop secondary hydroceles in response. When assessing a patient with a hydrocele it is important to ensure the underlying testicle is normal with an ultrasound before surgical repair.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":79,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old man is diagnosed with prostate cancer as an incidental finding on digital rectal examination following presentation with lower urinary tract symptoms. He wants to know what the ongoing management plan wili be.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in prostatic cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct in prostatic cancer?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It may be diagnosed by a serum blood test\",\"e\":\"Prostate specific antigen (PSA) is a protein produced by prostatic cells. It is produced by both normal and cancerous cells and is therefore not specific to cancer. While changes in the PSA can be a marker of prostate cancer and is used in active surveillance, PSA can also be increased in benign conditions such as benign prostatic hyperplasia and prostatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is an incidental finding in 90% of routine transurethral resections of the prostate (TURPs)\",\"e\":\"It has been reported that up to 15% of patients undergoing transurethral resection of the prostate will have an incidental finding of prostate cancer on histology.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is a rare incidental post-mortem finding\",\"e\":\"Prostate cancer is a very common finding at post mortem, in approximately 80% of men over the age of 80, although figures vary depending between studies.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"There is no screening programme in the UK\",\"e\":\"There is currently no screening programme in the UK. PSA screening does not fulfil the World Health Organization (WHO) screening criteria and European studies have suggested that it results in high levels of over-treatment of the disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is characterised by skeletal metastases in 70% of patients at the time of diagnosis\",\"e\":\"Approximately 5% of patients have skeletal metastases at the time of diagnosis of prostate cancer. This figure has been decreased and has been attributed to the rise in the number of patients undergoing PSA testing to identify low-grade prostate cancer and the detection of low-grade cancer in histology following bladder outflow obstruction surgery such as transurethral resection of the prostate or holmium enucleation of the prostate.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":82,\"stem\":\"A 49-year-old man presents with haematuria, flank mass, abdominal pain, weight loss and back pain. They are referred to the local emergency urology service. Which one of the following investigation findings would most likely be associated with this presentation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following investigation findings would most likely be associated with this presentation?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyponatraemia\",\"e\":\"Hyponatraemia is not caused by renal-cell carcinoma although there is some evidence as with many other medical conditions that hyponatraemia at presentation is a poor prognostic indıcator.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypercalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypercalcaemia is the most common paraneoplastic syndrome in renal-cell carcinoma affecting up to 20% of patients. It is thought to be due to the secretion of parathyroid hormone by the tumour leading to increased bone resorption and decreased renal clearance of calcium.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypocalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypocalcaemia is not seen with renal-cell carcinoma, it is hypercalcaemia that is one of the paraneoplastic syndromes\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lymphopaenia\",\"e\":\"Renal-cell carcinoma does not cause lymphopaenia but there is some evidence that it is an indicator of poor prognosis for patients with papillary renal-cell carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Polycythaemia rubra vera\",\"e\":\"Polycythaemia is one of the paraneoplastic syndromes associated with renal-cell carcinoma, but not polycythaemia rubra vera, which is a specific myeloproliferative disorder.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":85,\"stem\":\"A 7-year-old boy presents with fever, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting and haematuria. Wilms' tumour is diagnosed. Which one of the following statements about Wilms' tumour is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about Wilms' tumour is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It commonly presents at between 5 and 10 years of age\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It most commonly presents with haematuria\",\"e\":\"Wilms' tumour presents as an abdominal mass in 90% of cases, although it may also present with haematuria (30%) and abdominal pain (20%).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is bilateral in 40% of cases\",\"e\":\"Wilms' tumour is bilateral in approximately 5% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Metastases commonly involve the liver and lung\",\"e\":\"Metastases in Wilms' tumour are typically from haematogenous spread. The most common site of spread for Wilms' tumour is the lung. Metastatic spread can happen to the liver and brain but this is not common.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Nephrectomy with adjuvant chemotherapy is the mainstay of treatment\",\"e\":\"If treated early with nephrectomy and postoperative chemotherapy, it carries a favourable prognosis of around 80% survival rate at 3 years.\\n\\nWilms' tumour or nephroblastoma is the commonest intra-abdominal malignancy in children under 10 years. It affects about 1 in 10 000 live births and commonly presents between 0 and 3 years.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":87,\"stem\":\"You are treating a 52-year-old man with renal calculi. He has already had three admissions to A&E with renal colic.\\n\\nWhat can predispose individuals to renal tract calculi?\",\"lead\":\"What can predispose individuals to renal tract calculi?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyperthyroidism\",\"e\":\"Thyroid dysfunction does not typically encourage renal stone formation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gout\",\"e\":\"Stones in the renal tract occur due to an increased concentration of urinary solutes such as calcium, uric acid and oxalic acid, or due to an increased concentration of urine during dehydration. Gout is caused by high uric acid levels and patients with gout are also at an increased risk of forming uric acid stones as uric acid deposits build in the kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoparathyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hyperparathyroidism not hypoparathyroidism is linked with renal calculi formation due to increased serum calcium and subsequent hypercalciuria, which lead to the formation of renal calculi.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hypothyroidism has no proven association with renal calculi although it is associated with reduced glomerular filtration rate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Osteomalacia\",\"e\":\"Osteomalacia reduces serum calcium and is therefore not linked to renal calculi formation..\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"You are checking a patient's results from a 99Tcm diethylenetriamine penta-acetic acid (DTPA)-renogram. The report states that there is a hypervascular flush.\\n\\nWhat condition is the patient most likely to have?\",\"lead\":\"What condition is the patient most likely to have?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A functioning renal transplant\",\"e\":\"DTPA renograms are used to assess the overall function of a transplanted kidney. They are important in the early identification and management of renal transplant complications.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chiv Obstructive uropathy\",\"e\":\"With obstructive uropathy the DTPA renogram will not demonstrate drainage of the affected kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Simple renal cysts\",\"e\":\"Simple renal cysts appear as 'cold spots' on the DTPA renogram as they have no blood supply.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Space-occupying lesion of the kidney\",\"e\":\"Increased flow within an area of the kidney (hypervascular flush) is indicative of a renal tumour.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vesicoureteric reflux\",\"e\":\"DTPA renograms can determine the presence or absence of vesicoureteric reflux while assessing the function of the kidney.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":91,\"stem\":\"You are checking a patient's blood results and notice raised prostate specific antigen (PSA) levels. They do not complain of any symptoms of prostatism.\\n\\nWhat can significantly elevate PSA levels?\",\"lead\":\"What can significantly elevate PSA levels?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bedrest\",\"e\":\"There is some evidence suggesting that PSA levels decrease with bedrest although this is not proven.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acute urinary retention\",\"e\":\"Acute urinary retention can elevate PSA levels. A mildly elevated PSA level with acute urinary retention should be repeated before any further investigations or treatment, but a significantly elevated PSA level should raise concerns for advanced prostate cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Laparoscopic cholecystectomy\",\"e\":\"Laparoscopic cholecystectomy itself has not been linked with a rise in PSA although one study has suggested a rise in PSA level with the pneumoperitoneum created in laparoscopic surgery, but the evidence is poor.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hydrocoele\",\"e\":\"PSA is not affected by the presence of a hydrocele.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Digital rectal examination\",\"e\":\"Digital rectal examination can raise PSA levels but in a series of 202 patients the PSA level only changed by 0.26 ng/ml, which has been deemed insignificant.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":93,\"stem\":\"You send a urine specimen for analysis from one of your patients who is on warfarin, and it is reported as showing microscopic haematuria. They otherwise appear fairly well.\\n\\nWhat should you do?\",\"lead\":\"What should you do?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arrange a flexible cystoscopy\",\"e\":\"Flexible cystoscopy is one of the first line investigations for haematuria and should be used to investigate this patient despite the patient being on warfarin. Depending on the indication for warfarin and local protocol it may need to be stopped before the cystoscopy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Arrange an abdominal X-ray\",\"e\":\"First line imaging investigation for haematuria is either with ultrasound or computed tomography (CT) urogram..\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Arrange a 24 hour urine collection.\",\"e\":\"A 24 hour urine collection is not recommended to investigate microscopic haematuria.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Nothing as the patient is taking warfarin\",\"e\":\"Microscopic haematuria should not just be attributed to the patient taking warfarin, up to 25% of these patients will have an underlying problem such as malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Immediately commence antibiotics\",\"e\":\"There is no indication from the information given that this patient should be started on antibiotics. The patient is not symptomatic and the urine specimen has only shown microscopic haematuria, which alone does not suggest infection\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":95,\"stem\":\"You are treating a 67-year-old man with neuropathic bladder dysfunction. The patient has an underactive detrusor and urethra.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cerebrovascular accident (CA) „Cr\",\"e\":\"Lesions above the pons cause loss of cerebral inhibition and may produce an overactive detrusor leading to urgency, frequency and urge incontinence. Although evidence has shown that symptoms often improve after the first few weeks.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diabetes mellitus\",\"e\":\"Detrusor underactivity is defined as a contraction of reduced strength and/or duration resulting in prolonged bladder emptying and/or failure to achieve complete bladder emptying. Poorly controlled diabetes mellitus can lead to peripheral neuropathy. Lesions of the sacral cord or peripheral nerves cause an underactive detrusor and urethra.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Multiple sclerosis\",\"e\":\"Multiple sclerosis (MS) is an inflammatory-demyelinating disease of the central nervous system. Patients commonly experience detrusor overactivity with symptoms of frequency, urgency and urge incontinence. Involuntary contraction of the bladder often occurs simultaneously to varying degrees of contraction of the external urinary sphincter, with consequent sphincter asynchrony.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spina bifida\",\"e\":\"Spina bifida can lead to dyssynergy so that the sphincter is closed when the detrusor contracts, creating high pressures within the bladder but low flow rates.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vesicoureteric reflux\",\"e\":\"Vesicoureteric reflux can be a complication of a neurogenic bladder, typically with detrusor external sphincter dyssynergia, leading to high bladder storage and voiding pressures. When the detrusor filling pressure is high ureteral drainage into bladder deteriorates, leading to hydronephrosis and vesicoureteric reflux. As a consequence the patient can develop recurrent urinary tract infections and renal insufficiency.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"You are treating a 53-year-old man who has renal calculi. He otherwise has been fairly healthy throughout his life.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is associated with the greatest increased risk of renal calculi?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is associated with the greatest increased risk of renal calculi?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypocalcaemia .C.\",\"e\":\"Hypocalcaemia does not classically result in an increased risk of renal stones. However, there are some conditions with blood hypocalcaemia but urine hypercalciuria. This can lead to deposits of calcium in the kidney and so stone formation, but this is rare.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperparathyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hyperparathyroidism is one of the most common causes of renal calculi. Hyperparathyroidism causes hypercalcaemia and consequently hypercalciuria which leads to the formation of renal calculi.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hypothyroidism is associated with reduced glomerular filtration rate but there is no proven association with renal calculi.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"ORL Metanephrines\",\"e\":\"Metanephrines are not associated with an increased risk of renal calculi. Urinary metanephrines are often requested when a diagnosis of phaeochromocytoma is being\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Peptic ulcer disease\",\"e\":\"Although peptic ulcer disease treated historically with milk and bicarbonate resulted in the milk-alkali syndrome (ie associated with renal stones), the pathology of peptic ulcer disease itself is not directly associated with renal calculi.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":101,\"stem\":\"You are treating a 69-year-old man in erectile dysfunction clinic. He is keen to continue to pursue an active sex life.\\n\\nWhat is the commonest organic cause of impotence?\",\"lead\":\"What is the commonest organic cause of impotence?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cirrhosis\",\"e\":\"Cirrhosis can cause impotence but is not the commonest organic cause. Damage to the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis and impaired liver function are the main causes of sexual dysfunction in patients with cirrhosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diabetes\",\"e\":\"Diabetes is the commonest organic cause of impotence. The pathophysiology is complex but is thought to be due to neuropathy impairing blood flow. Other important and common organic causes are: vascular disease, drugs, pelvic surgery, pelvic or spinal injury, neurological disease and hormonal problems.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Priapism\",\"e\":\"Long-term tissue damage from priapism is a rare but important cause of impotence, and must therefore be treated rapidly.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Psychological trauma\",\"e\":\"Psychological trauma is not an organic cause of impotence but can exacerbate an organic cause.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Streptococcal pneumonia\",\"e\":\"There is no proven link between streptococcal pneumonia and erectile dysfunction.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":103,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old man presents to his GP with problems passing urine. He reports hesitancy and terminal dribbling, further questioning reveals he has been getting up several times at night to pass urine. An ultrasound guided prostate biopsy reveals localised prostate cancer and the patient is planned to have radiotherapy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is an appropriate symptom relieving treatment option?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is an appropriate symptom relieving treatment option?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dutasteride\",\"e\":\"Dutasteride is a 5a-reductase inhibitor; 5a-reductase converts testosterone to its more active form dihydrotestosterone, which is involved in prostatic growth. Over time with drugs such as dutasteride the prostate can reduce in size and improve lower urinary tract symptoms but it would not be the first-line choice in this case.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Finasteride\",\"e\":\"Finasteride is a 5a-reductase inhibitor that can reduce the size of the prostate and improve lower urinary tract symptoms. It is often used in combination with Tamsulosin for patients with large prostates that are trialling medical therapy first. It would not be the most appropriate treatment for this patient who is about to undergo radiotherapy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pelvic exenteration\",\"e\":\"Peivic exenteration is radical surgery removing all organs from the pelvis used to treat cancer. This patient is undergoing radiotherapy for the treatment of their prostate cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tamsulosin\",\"e\":\"Tamsulosin is an a-blocker causing a relaxation of the smooth muscle in the bladder neck and prostate proven to improve lower urinary tract symptoms. It is typically used as first line therapy for patients with troublesome lower urinary tract symptoms when surgery is not indicated but would not be suitable in this patient about to undergo radiotherapy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP)\",\"e\":\"In patients undergoing radiotherapy with significant lower urinary tract symptoms there is a risk the patient will go into retention and they require definitive treatment before radiotherapy. A TURP would be sufficient in this case.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":106,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old retired schoolteacher presents with nocturia and poor urinary flow. He is diagnosed with benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH).\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate treatment for him?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most appropriate treatment for him?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alfuzosin\",\"e\":\"Alfuzosin is a selective Alpha-blocker that relaxes prostatic smooth muscles and is typically used as first line medical therapy for troublesome lower urinary tract symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Amlodipine\",\"e\":\"Amlodipine is a calcium-channel blocker typically used in the treatment of hypertension and not for troublesome lower urinary tract symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Goserelin\",\"e\":\"Goserelin is a luteinising hormone-releasing hormone (LHRH) agonist that has a role in the treatment of prostatic cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Methyldopa\",\"e\":\"Methyldopa is an anti-hypertensive medication.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Oxybutynin\",\"e\":\"Oxybutynin is an anticholinergic drug that is used in the treatment of urinary frequency due to detrusor instability (overactive bladder).\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":108,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man is being consented by the Urology specialist registrar (SpR) for a transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP). The patient is anxious about the procedure.\\n\\nWhich one of the following risks should he be warned about?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following risks should he be warned about?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypernatraemia\",\"e\":\"TURP syndrome is reported as having a 0.5-2% risk in patients undergoing traditional monopolar TURP with glycine irrigation. It is caused by a combination of dilutional hyponatraemia, fluid overload and glycine toxicity.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemia\",\"e\":\"The main electrolyte risk with TURP is hyponatraemia due to TURP syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypomagnesaemia\",\"e\":\"The patient should be warned specifically about TURP syndrome, which classically causes dilutional hyponatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal failure\",\"e\":\"TURP is not associated with a risk of renal failure and does not form part of the consent.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Retrograde ejaculation\",\"e\":\"Patients should be warned of retrograde ejaculation when semen flows back into the bladder. This is usually prevented by internal muscles close to the bladder neck that are often damaged during TURP.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":110,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man undergoes a very prolonged transurethral resection of prostate (TURP). He becomes unwell on the ward post-operatively\\n\\nWhich one of the following options may occur?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options may occur?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"ExUlalalıull T\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SH Bradycardia\",\"e\":\"With prolonged TURP there is a greater risk of TURP syndrome which is in part due to fluid overload. Fluid overload causes hypertension and reflex bradycardia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AMI Hyperglycaemia\",\"e\":\"Hyperglycaemia is not associated specifically with prolonged TURP.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypernatraemia\",\"e\":\"There is a risk of TURP syndrome with prolonged surgery. The effects are due to dilutional hyponatraemia, fluid overload and glycine toxicity\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased osmolality of the plasma\",\"e\":\"With prolonged surgery there is a risk of TURP syndrome as a result of absorption of the irrigation fluid glycine which has a lower osmolality than plasma. This can decrease the osmolality of plasma and this puts the patient at risk of cerebral oedema, which is life threatening.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased total body sodium\",\"e\":\"This is a risk of hyponatraemia with prolonged TURP as a result of TURP syndrome.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":112,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man has a clinical diagnosis of a testicular malignancy in a previously maldescended testis.\\n\\nFrom the following list of testicular malignancies, which is the most likely histological diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"From the following list of testicular malignancies, which is the most likely histological diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Interstitial (Leydig) cell tumour\",\"e\":\"Leydig cell tumours are rare testicular tumours. They is a type of sex cord stromal tumour that can occur in prepubertal boys but are most common in men aged 30-60 years.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Testicular lymphomas are a rare extranodal presentation of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. It has a peak incidence of 60-70 years and is more common than primary testicular cancer in men over 50 years.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Seminoma\",\"e\":\"Seminomas are a type of germ cell tumour along with teratomas they account for 85-90% of testicular tumours. Peak incidence of seminomas is 40-60 years. Seminomas are the commonest testicular tumours to develop in maldescended testicles with a 10-40 times greater risk.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Teratoma\",\"e\":\"Teratomas are of germ cell origin with a peak incidence of 20-30 years, but it is not the most common testicular tumour in a maldescended testis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Yolk sac tumour\",\"e\":\"Yolk sac tumours occur before the age of 3 years and are the commonest testicular malignancy in children.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":115,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man presents with a hard, non-tender lump in his left testicle. Both a- fetoprotein (AFP) and f-human chorionic gonadotrophin (B-HCG) are raised.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely nature of the lump?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely nature of the lump?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Haematoma\",\"e\":\"Scrotal haematoma is usually a result of trauma and can present as a firm lump but is often painful. It does not elevate serum tumour markers.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Abscess\",\"e\":\"A scrotal abscess would typically present as a fluctuant usually painful swelling with raised inflammatory markers. The serum tumour markers (AFP and b-HCG) would not be elevated as a result of an abscess alone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Teratoma\",\"e\":\"Teratoma would be the most common testicular tumour in this age group and can cause both AFP and B-HCG levels to be elevated.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Seminoma\",\"e\":\"Seminomas can, but not always, elevate the tumour markers B-HCG and LDH, however a pure seminoma does not elevate AFP.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hydrocoele\",\"e\":\"A hydrocele is accumulation of fluid within the tunica vaginalis. A simple hydrocele would not be associated with raised tumour markers. But it should not be forgotten that hydroceles can be secondary to testicular tumours and in that case could be associated with raised tumour markers.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":118,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman presents with right flank mass and hamaturia. She also complains of depression, constipation and vomiting.\\n\\nWhat is the underlying diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the underlying diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Colorectal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Renal stones\",\"e\":\"Renal stones can present asymptomatically but in patients with renal colic typically present with loin to groin pain and haematuria. These patients would not present with a mass.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Renal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"This collection of symptoms can all be explained by renal carcinoma with hypercalcaemia as a paraneoplastic syndrome. Paraneoplastic syndromes are non-metastatic systemic effects caused by cancer. Hypercalcaemia causes depression, abdominal pain, lethargy and constipation.\\n\\nPatients with colorectal carcinoma could have a palpable abdominal mass but typically present with a change in bowel habit.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Bladder carcinoma\",\"e\":\"The most common presentation of bladder cancer is haematuria occurring in 90% of patients. These patients would not present with a flank mass and paraneoplastic syndromes are rare.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pyelonephritis\",\"e\":\"Pyelonephritis would be more likely to present with fever, flank pain and urinary symptoms such as dysuria and frequency.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":120,\"stem\":\"You are discussing cases in the urology MDT. The pathologist discusses the details of some of the cancers encountered at the meeting.\\n\\nWhich one of the following malignancies is most commonly multi-centric?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following malignancies is most commonly multi-centric?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Testicular cancer\",\"e\":\"Synchronous testicular cancers are very rare.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Renal cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Renal cell carcinomas are sometimes bilateral or multi-centric, but not often.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Squamous cell carcinoma of penis\",\"e\":\"Generally, penile carcinomas are solitary (but infiltrative) mass lesions:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transitional cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Transitional cell carcinomas can occur anywhere in the urothelium and multi-centricity and recurrence are common.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wilms' tumour of the kidney\",\"e\":\"Wilms' tumour usually presents as a solitary mass, though about 10% are bilateral or multi- centric at the time of diagnosis.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":122,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old man who had been feeling unwell and pyrexial for 10 days was seen in the Emergency Department. He described dull pain on palpation of his left lower back. He had burning dysuria. A full blood count revealed an elevated white blood cell count with a left shift.\\n\\nWhich one of the following urinalysis findings would be most diagnostic for his renal condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following urinalysis findings would be most diagnostic for his renal condition?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyaline casts\",\"e\":\"Hyaline casts (formed from solidified mucoprotein) are the most common casts found in the urine on microscopy and can be seen in healthy patients. Increased numbers may occur as a result of chronic renal disease, strenuous exercise, and diuretic use.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Oval fat bodies\",\"e\":\"This refers to the presence of lipids in the urine and is most commonly seen as a result of nephrotic syndrome. Burning dysuria, back pain and pyrexia would not be seen with nephrotic syndrome\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Renal tubular epithelial cells\",\"e\":\"These are seen often in virai renal disease (such as cytomegalovirus (CMV) nephritis) and kidney transplant rejection.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"White blood cell casts\",\"e\":\"White blood cell casts indicate an inflammatory process within the kidney. This includes glomerulonephritis and tubulointerstitial nephritis. The most common causes of tubulointerstitial nephritis are; allergic reactions, toxins/medication reactions (NSAIDS, phenytoin, penicillins and more), infection (pyelonephritis). Tubulointerstitial nephritis is a primary injury to the renal tubules and interstitium that results in decreased renal function.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Proteinuria\",\"e\":\"Proteinuria is non-specific and could be as a result of many different conditions, such as nephrotic syndromes, glomerular disease, and autoimmune nephropathies.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":125,\"stem\":\"A 38-year-old man reports sudden-onset, severe, right flank pain that comes in waves. His urine dipstick shows no ketones, glucose, protein, nitrite or urobilinogen; the urine contains blood, and a few white blood cells. The specific gravity of the urine is 1.015 (normal value 1.002-1.030) and the pH is 5.5 (normal value 6).\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Benign prostatic hyperplasia\",\"e\":\"Benign prostatic hypertrophy is seen in men of advancing age in which they present with lower urinary tract symptoms such as poor flow, hesitancy, and dribbling. Urinalysis may reveal microscopic haematuria.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Membranous glomerulonephritis\",\"e\":\"Membranous glomerulonephritis will not present flank pain\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Renal angiomyolipoma\",\"e\":\"This is a renal parenchymal tumour, which is often asymptomatic and is more common in women.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transitional cell carcinoma of bladder\",\"e\":\"Transitional cell carcinoma of the bladder would be uncommon in a man of this age and would not present with renal colic, instead it presents with hamaturia (either frank or microscopic) usually above the age of 60, more commonly in men. The main two risk factors are smoking and occupational exposures (rubber, leather dye, paint, printing and aromatic amines).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ureteric calculus\",\"e\":\"This is a classic history of ureteric colic. During passage through the ureter a calculus irritates the ureter and can become lodged, obstructing urine flow and causing hydroureter and hydronephrosis. There are three anatomical narrowings in the ureter, where stones are most likely to become lodged; the ureteropeivic junction, the ureteral crossing of the iliac vessels and the ureterovesical junction. Distal stones and stones ‹ 5 mm diameter are more likely to pass spontaneously. Even partial obstruction causes decreased glomerular filtration, which can persist briefly after the calculus has passed. With hydronephrosis and elevated glomerular pressure, renal blood flow declines, this further worsens renal function. Generally. however, permanent renal dysfunction occurs only after about 28 days of complete obstruction. Secondary infection can occur with longstanding obstruction. The three main indications for hospital admission with ureteric stones are:\\n\\n• fever - any evidence infection in the presence of obstruction will usually require hospital admission and surgical intervention to relieve obstruction (nephrostomy. retrograde ureteric stent, antegrade ureteric stent) • impaired renal function • pain requiring IV analgesia • single kidney - post-nephrectomy or non-functioning contralateral kidney.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":128,\"stem\":\"A young man, who is training for a marathon, was admitted to the Emergency Department after passing dark brown coloured urine. The urine dipstick test for blood was positive but no red blood cells were seen on urine sediment microscopy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following conditions is most likely to be associated with these findings?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following conditions is most likely to be associated with these findings?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Myoglobinuria\",\"e\":\"Myoglobinuria is the presence of myoglobin in the urine, usually associated with rhabdomyolysis or muscle destruction. Trauma, including electrical injuries and burns, vascular problems, excessive exercise, venoms and certain drugs can destroy or damage the muscle, releasing myoglobin into the circulation and so to the kidneys. Under ideal situations myoglobin will be filtered and excreted with the urine, but if too much myoglobin is released into the circulation it can overwhelm the kidneys. This can cause casts to form (solid masses of myoglobin) which can occlude the renal filtration system, leading to acute tubular necrosis and acute kidney injury. Patients who have undergone severe rhabdomyolysis (eg crush injury victims) should receive high volumes of IV fluids in an aim to prevent cast formation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis\",\"e\":\"While this can be seen in younger patients there is no preceding history of streptococcal illness such as a sore throat in this case history.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Renal infarction\",\"e\":\"Renal infarction is rare and in a young man it would be unlikely as it is most commonly due to thromboemboli.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal papillary necrosis\",\"e\":\"Papillary necrosis is most commonly caused by analgesic nephropathy (caused by excessive ingestion of simples analgesics such as NSAIDs and paracetamol). These patients can sometimes present with renal colic, as the necrotic tissue can obstruct ureteric drainage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ureteric lithiasis\",\"e\":\"Renal stones would present with renal colic and the presence of microscopic haematuria that would include red blood cells.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":131,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the urology clinic with a malignant tumour. They are due to be discussed at the urology MDT Regarding urological malignancy, which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"They are due to be discussed at the urology MDT Regarding urological malignancy, which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Prostatic carcinoma commonly metastasizes to bone\",\"e\":\"Prostatic carcinoma characteristically produces metastases in the lumbar vertebrae as a result of the prostatic venous plexus drainage into the internal vertebral plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Prostatic tumours are most commonly squamous cell carcinomas\",\"e\":\"Prostatic tumours are adenocarcinomas with a variable degree of differentiation. Prostate cancer is graded using the Gleason Score - where tissue is graded between 0-5, the two most abundant types of tissue are added together to form a score out of 10. Higher Gleason scores reflect more aggressive local and metastatic spread.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Renal cancers are most commonly bilateral\",\"e\":\"Renal cell carcinomas may occasionally occur bilaterally, but they are most commonly unilateral.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal cancers spread to the lungs via the thoracic duct\",\"e\":\"Renal cancer often infiltrates the adrenal gland, more distal spread is usually haematogenous not via the lymphatic system.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Testicular tumours usually spread to inguinal lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"Testicular tumours mostly spread to the para-aortic nodes, and not to the inguinal lymph nodes.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":134,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man life-long smoker undergoes an urgent cystoscopy for haematuria. A bladder tumour is found and biopsied.\\n\\nWhat is the most common type of bladder cancer in the UK?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most common type of bladder cancer in the UK?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adenocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Less than 5% of bladder tumours are adenocarcinomas.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"SiVi Benign tumour\",\"e\":\"Similar to sarcomas, benign tumours of the bladder are rare.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Sarcoma is a rare form of bladder cancer:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)\",\"e\":\"Only 5% of bladder tumours are squamous cell carcinomas. Squamous cell carcinoma is associated with chronic bladder irritation - such as long-term catheters, bladder stones and schistosomiasis infection.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transitional cell carcinoma (TCC)\",\"e\":\"Ninety per cent of bladder cancers are TCC, 5% are SCC and the rest are adenocarcinomas. Sarcoma is rare and benign tumours even more uncommon. TCC is an urothelial tumour of the type that lines the entire urinary tract. Ninety-five per cent affect the bladder whilst 5% affect the upper tract. Carcinoma of the bladder is more common in men (ratio of men.women = 3:1) in whom it is the fourth most common cancer (after prostate, lung and colorectal tumours). Haematuria is commonly painless although some may present with cystitis, or ureteric colic (if the tumour obstructs ureteric flow). Superficial tumours may be managed with trans-urethral resection of bladder tumours (TURBT) in conjunction with intra-vesical therapy such as Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG) vaccine or mitomycin. These patients require frequent surveillance with cystoscopy. Muscle invasive tumours are generally managed by radical cystectomy, assuming that the patient has a reasonable performance status.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":137,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old man is seen under the urologists with recurrent urinary tract infections. Imaging for colicky right flank pain shows a staghorn renal tract calculus.\\n\\nWhich feature is most likely to be present in relation to this calculi?\",\"lead\":\"Which feature is most likely to be present in relation to this calculi?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bacteroides organism\",\"e\":\"Urease-producing bacteria such as proteus, Klebsiella, Pseudonomas and Enterobacter (not bacteroides) increase the risk of stag horn calculi.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased calcium\",\"e\":\"Staghorn calculi can be formed of struvite or calcium carbonate apatite, and therefore increased calcium is likely to be seen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"High level of urease-producing bacteria\",\"e\":\"Urease-producing bacteria split urea into ammonia and hydroxide. These stones are commonly formed of magnesium ammonium phosphate (struvite) or calcium carbonate apatite. They are radiopaque and form in the renal pelvis hence the staghorn shape. Staghorn calculi are usually seen in the setting of recurrent urinary tract infection with urease-producing bacteria (eg Proteus. Klebsiella, Pseudomonas and Enterobacter)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Low in phosphate\",\"e\":\"Struvite is formed of magnesium ammonium phosphate and therefore is high in phosphate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Radio-occult\",\"e\":\"Staghorn calculi are radiopaque and therefore can be seen on a range of imaging, including plain film, computed tomography (CT) scan and ultrasound\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":139,\"stem\":\"Some 24 h after sustaining severe skeletal and soft tissue trauma in a road traffic accident, a 19-year-old man becomes oliguric. The nurses ask you to review him as his urine output is now persistently minimal. Which one of the following would most suggest that this is pre-renal in origin, rather than being due to acute tubular necrosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would most suggest that this is pre-renal in origin, rather than being due to acute tubular necrosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"is i Glycosuria\",\"e\":\"Trauma can cause transient glycosuria because of a stress-induced rise in blood glucose concentration.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemia\",\"e\":\"Tissue breakdown and decreased urine production can cause both hyperkalaemia and hyperuricaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyperuricaemia\",\"e\":\"As discussed, hyperuricaemia can occur following tissue breakdown from trauma, however it does not provide an indication as to whether the origin of reduced renal function is intra- renal or pre-renal.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urinary osmolality less than 250 mmol/kg\",\"e\":\"Urine osmolality is a measure of concentration of the urine and if the patient is suffering from pre-renal failure then the osmolality would be high due to reabsorption of water from the kidneys under the actions of ADH.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urinary sodium concentration less than 20 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"A urinary sodium concentration less than 20 mmol/i suggests that renal tubular function is intact. Whereas if oliguria is due to acute tubular necrosis, it would be expected that urinary sodium concentration will be over 80 mmol/l, due to leaching of sodium from the tubules with a urine plasma osmolality of 1:1.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":142,\"stem\":\"A patient has a routine urine dip. They had presented to the Emergency Department with indeterminate abdominal pain.\\n\\nWhich one of the following renal functions will be assessed if you are measuring the urine specific gravity?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following renal functions will be assessed if you are measuring the urine specific gravity?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Blood flow\",\"e\":\"Urine specific gravity refers to the concentration of solutes in the urine. Accurate measurement of a patient's renal blood flow is challenging with renal function used as a proxy for adequate renal blood flow.\\n\\nFiltration is often measured clinically through the estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR).\\n\\nReabsorption that occurs in the kidney is often measured through proxy measurements of renal function.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Concentration\",\"e\":\"Renal concentrating ability is reflected by specific gravity. In humans, normal specific gravity values range from 1.002 g/ml to 1.028 g/ml. Increased specific gravity (ie increased concentration of solutes in the urine) is associated with dehydration, glucosuria and the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH). Decreased specific gravity (ie decreased concentration of solutes in urine) is associated with renal failure, pyelonephritis, diabetes insipidus, acute tubular necrosis, interstitial nephritis and excessive fluid intake. Paired osmolality is more commonly used to assess renal concentrating ability.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Filtration\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reabsorption\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Secretion\",\"e\":\"Urine specific gravity is a measure of renal concentrating ability.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":145,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman presents with abdominal pain and is referred as appendicitis. She has painful urination and a positive urine dip.\\n\\nWhich one of the following symptoms would you also expect to find in an irritative lower urinary tract infection?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following symptoms would you also expect to find in an irritative lower urinary tract infection?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hesitancy\",\"e\":\"Voiding symptoms consist of hesitancy, poor flow, a feeling of incomplete emptying and postmicturition dribble. These are classed as obstructive symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Incomplete voiding\",\"e\":\"Incomplete voiding is classed as a voiding, or obstructive symptom.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Nocturia\",\"e\":\"Lower urinary tract symptoms can be divided into filling (irritative) and voiding (obstructive) symptoms. Filling symptoms consist of frequency, nocturia, urgency (with or without urge incontinence) and suprapubic pain.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Overflow incontinence\",\"e\":\"Overflow incontinence occurs when there is involuntary release of urine from an overfull bladder. It is an obstructive symptom.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Poor flow\",\"e\":\"Poor flow is a voiding symptom. i788\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":147,\"stem\":\"You are on an Intensive Care Unit (ITU) ward round where you review a patient admitted with severe sepsis and multiorgan failure. As a result he has low urine output and peripheral oedema. He is acidotic and hyperkalaemic.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding renal replacement therapy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding renal replacement therapy?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It can only be delivered via a central line\",\"e\":\"Renal replacement therapy can be delivered via a central line, tunnelled lines, arteriovenous fistulae or through intraperitoneal catheters.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is a cure for kidney disease\",\"e\":\"Renal replacement therapy can be used to extend a patient's life in the setting of chronic renal failure, or as a temporary measure during severe acute kidney injury. It is not, however, a cure for kidney disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It may involve replacing filtered fluid in the peritoneal cavity\",\"e\":\"Renal replacement therapy is indicated in intractable fluid overload, hyperkalaemia, acidosis and uraemia when medical therapy has failed. It can be administered as a holding measure to cover an acute crisis or in the chronic setting. In the acute setting, such as on ITU, haemofiltration is usually used, in which the patient's own blood pressure drives the formation of an ultrafiltrate. The advantage over haemodialysis is that the machinery involved is less complex and it can continue for many hours. In the chronic setting, in which the maintenance of a reasonable lifestyle of the patient is requisite and the process must be rapid/intermittent, haemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis is commonly used. Haemodialysis like haemofiltration involves the patient's blood being pumped through an artificial kidney where waste products are exchanged with dialysis fluid, which surrounds the semi-permeable tubes through which the blood is passed. Often an arteriovenous fistula is formed surgically to allow easy vascular access for haemodialysis. Peritoneal dialysis involves fluid being pumped into the abdominal cavity into which waste products diffuse. The fluid must be changed episodically (often four times per day).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It should be considered first line for fluid overload\",\"e\":\"Renal replacement therapy (RRT) should only be considered for intractable fluid overload. Conservative and medical therapy, such as fluid restriction and diuretics, should be attempted before RRT. Other indications for renal replacement therapy in the acute setting include: severe acidosis (< 7.1), intractable hyperkalaemia, certain drug overdoses and signs of uraemia (eg pericarditis or encephalopathy).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rapid ultrafiltration of the blood cannot be undertaken\",\"e\":\"Rapid ultrafiltration can be undertaken with renal replacement therapy through haemodialysis.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":150,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes and hypertension is referred to a renal clinic due to concerns regarding his renal function. It is decided to monitor his glomerular filtration rate (GFR) as an outpatient.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is used in clinical practice to estimate GFR?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is used in clinical practice to estimate GFR?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Creatinine clearance\",\"e\":\"Creatinine clearance is not used to estimate GFR in routine clinical practice.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Serum creatinine level\",\"e\":\"The GFR varies during a lifetime. Adult levels are reached at 2 years of age and decline linearly from 40 years due to glomerular sclerosis. In clinical practice, GFR is estimated by measuring the serum creatinine and the MDRD equation. A 50% increase in GFR occurs during pregnancy, GFR increases after a protein-rich meal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inulin clearance\",\"e\":\"In research laboratories, GFR can be measured by inulin clearance. Inulin is a polymer of fructose that is not secreted, metabolised or reabsorbed by the kidney. This means that the amount of inulin excreted in the urine (per min) equals the amount of inulin filtered at the glomerulus (per min).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urea levels\",\"e\":\"Urea levels provide an indication of renal function but they are not used in the calculation of estimated GFR.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urine output\",\"e\":\"Urine output provides a gross indication of renal function, however it is not used to estimate GFR.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":153,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old with end stage renal failure (ESRF) presents 2 days post dialysis with chills, fever and nausea. You refer them onto the nephrologists.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding renal replacement therapy is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding renal replacement therapy is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"May involve replacing filtered fluid in the peritoneal cavity - so-called haemofiltration\",\"e\":\"Renal replacement therapy can include installation of dialysis fluid into the peritoneal cavity. utilising the peritoneal lining as a filtration membrane. This is called peritoneal dialysis, not haemofiltration.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"May be indicated first line for fluid overload\",\"e\":\"Medical therapy should be trialled before initiation of renal replacement therapy. This can include fluid restriction and diuretics.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"May be indicated in hypokalaemia\",\"e\":\"Renal replacement therapy can be used to treat intractable hyperkalaemia, not hypokalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"May require the formation of an arteriovenous fistula\",\"e\":\"In the chronic setting, where the maintenance of a reasonable lifestyle of the patient is requisite and the process must be rapid/intermittent, haemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis is commonly used. Haemodialysis, like haemofiltration, involves the patient's blood being pumped through an artificial kidney where waste products are exchanged with dialysis fluid, which surrounds the semi-permeable tubes through which the blood is passed. Often an arteriovenous fistula is formed surgically to allow easy vascular access for haemodialysis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Should not be considered in cases where rapid ultrafiltration is required\",\"e\":\"Rapid ultrafiltration can be achieved through either haemodialysis or haemofiltration.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":156,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old woman is admitted with severe community-acquired pneumonia and goes into acute renal failure. She is initially managed with IV fluid.\\n\\nWhich one of the following can be used to estimate the glomerular filtration rate (GFR)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following can be used to estimate the glomerular filtration rate (GFR)?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ADH\",\"e\":\"Anti-diuretic hormone (ADH) plays an important role in fluid homeostasis, however it cannot be used to estimate GFR\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glucose\",\"e\":\"Glucose in normal circumstance is not filtered by the kidney and is actively reabsorbed, therefore cannot be used to estimate GFR\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inulin\",\"e\":\"inulin (not insulin) can be used to measure the glomerular filtration rate. Inulin is freely filtered across the glomerulus into Bowman's capsule. It is neither reabsorbed, secreted nor metabolised by the cells of the nephron. These serve as criteria for the measurement of the GFR. Inulin is usually used in experimental studies. Creatinine can be used to estimate GFR in clinical practice, creatinine is a by-product of skeletal muscle and is produced at a relatively constant rate, any amount produced is proportional to the muscle mass. In most clinical situations, creatinine clearance provides a reasonably accurate measure of GFR.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Para-amino hippuric acid\",\"e\":\"Para-amino hippuric acid is an organic ion filtered across the glomerulus and is used to measure renal plasma flow.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sodium\",\"e\":\"Sodium is filtered and reabsorbed by the kidney through various mechanisms to maintain plasma homeostasis of sodium ions and so would not be a helpful in estimating the GFR\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":159,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man presents with pain in his left scrotum that has been increasing for the past 2 days. He is systemically well and on examination his abdomen is soft and non-tender. On palpation the left testicle is slightly tender with no palpable lumps, but he is very tender slightly above and behind the testicle.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epididymo-orchitis\",\"e\":\"Epididymo-orchitis is an inflammatory condition of the testicle and epididymis secondary to infection. This may occur as a result of a viral infection such as mumps, or bacterial infection due to a urinary tract infection (UTI) or sexually transmitted disease eg Chlamydia infection or gonorrhoea. The epididymis is often swollen and tender.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Hydrocoele\",\"e\":\"In a hydrocoele the testicle is often impalpable and it is usually painless.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inguinal hernia\",\"e\":\"An inguinal hernia extending into the scrotum would cause a lump and it is not possible to 'get above it'.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Testicular tumour\",\"e\":\"Testicular tumours often present as painless, palpable lumps often found on self- examination.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Torsion\",\"e\":\"Testicular torsion is an acute surgical emergency with an exquisitely tender testicle.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":161,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old man is being investigated for subfertility and, on further questioning, reports a dragging sensation in his scrotum that is exacerbated on standing. On examination the right testicle feels normal, but on the left distended blood vessels are seen that feel like a bag of worms. However, on lying down, they disappear. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hydrocoele\",\"e\":\"In a hydrocoele the testicle is often impalpable and it is usually painless.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Indirect inguinal hernia\",\"e\":\"An inguinal hernia extending into the scrotum would cause a lump and it is not possible to 'get above it'\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Orchitis\",\"e\":\"Orchitis refers to inflammation of the testicle, most commonly seen in the context of epididymo-orchitis. Epididymo-orchitis is an inflammatory condition of the testicle and epididymis secondary to infection. This may occur as a result of a viral infection such as mumps, or bacterial infection due to a urinary tract infection (UTI) or a sexually transmitted disease eg Chlamydia infection or gonorrhoea. The epididymis is often swollen and tender.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Testicular tumour\",\"e\":\"Testicular tumours often present as painless, palpable lumps often found on self- examination.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Varicocele\",\"e\":\"A varicocele may be secondary to an incompetence of the valves between the left testicular vein and left renal vein causing dilated veins of the pampiniform plexus. These often present as a dull ache in the scrotum and feel like a 'bag of worms' in the scrotum. They become more obvious when the patient stands up. They can be treated if they are symptomatic or in cases in which patients have reduced fertility (although this does not seem to affect pregnancy rates) by surgery or embolisation.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":164,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old man attends the Emergency Department following an episode of haemoptysis. He is a non-smoker and was previously well. On examination his chest and abdomen are unremarkable, and a small 1 × 1cm non-tender lump is found in his left testicle that he has not previously noted. His chest X-ray shows multiple opacities in the lung fields. He is thought to have a testicular tumour.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely TNM staging of this tumour?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely TNM staging of this tumour?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"T4N1M1\",\"e\":\"T4 staging would suggest the tumour is invading the scrotum. That is not the case from the question stem.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"T1N2MO\",\"e\":\"MO staging means there are no distant metastasis. The question stem indicates lung metastasis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"TisN2M1b\",\"e\":\"The question stem says the patient has a palpable mass. Intra-tubular germ cell neoplasis indicated by Tis is non palpable. We are not given enough information to assess the lymph node status. He has lung metastasis which wouid give a staging of Mla not M1b\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"T2NxM1a\",\"e\":\"The British Association of Urologist use the TM + S staging system.\\n\\nWe are told the tumour is limited to the testis. We are told there are lung metastasis but we can not assess the lymph node status with this information.\\n\\nTx Primary tumour cannot be assessed\\n\\nTO No evidence of primary tumour (eg histological scar in testis)\\n\\nTis Intra-tubular germ cell neoplasia (intra-epithelial neoplasia)\\n\\nTi Tumour limited to testis & epididymis without vascular/lymphatic invasion; tumour may invade tunica albuginea but not tunica vaginalis T-Stage (orchidectomy T2 Tumour limited to testis & epididymis with vascular/lymphatic invasion; specimen) or tumour extending through tunica albuginea with involvement of tunica vaginalis T3 Tumour involving spermatic cord with or without vascular/lymphatic invasion\\n\\nT4 Tumour invading scrotum with or without vascular/lymphatic invasion\\n\\nNx Regional lymph nodes cannot be assessed NO no regional lymph node metastases N1 Metastasis in a lymph node mass ≥2cm in greatest dimension & 5 or N-Stage fewer positive nodes, none >2cm in greatest dimension (CT/PET N2 Metastasis in lymph node mass 2cm but < 5cm in greatest dimension; scanning) or >5 nodes positive, none >5cm; or evidence of extra-nodal extension of tumour\\n\\nN3 Metastasis with a lymph node mass >5cm in greatest dimension\\n\\nMx Distant metastases cannot be assessed\\n\\nMO No distant metastases M-Stage M1 Distant metastasis (CT/PET scanning) Mla Non-regional lymph node(s) or lung\\n\\nMlb Metastases in other sites\\n\\nSx Serum tumour markers not available or not performed\\n\\nSO Serum tumour markers within normal limits\\n\\nLDH (U/L) B-HCG (mIU/mL) AFP (ng/mL)\\n\\n< 5,000 & < 1.5-times norm & S1 <1,000 S-Stage (blood tests) 1,000-10,000 S2 1.5-10-times norm or 5,000-50,000 or\\n\\n>50,000 or S3 > 10-times norm or >10,000\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T1N2M2\",\"e\":\"M2 classification does not exist.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":169,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old woman with previous renal colic presents with severe left loin pain radiating to her groin. She reports rigors and on examination looks unwell with a temperature of 38.5°C. Urine dip shows blood 3+ nitrites and leukocytes. A CT scan shows a 7 mm stone in the left pelviureteric junction with proximal hydronephrosis.\\n\\nWhat would be the most appropriate management for this?\",\"lead\":\"What would be the most appropriate management for this?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Catheterisation with an irrigation catheter\",\"e\":\"This treatment can be used when there is obstruction of the bladder, however, the CT scan demonstrates that the stone is in the left ureter.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Flexible cystoscopy\",\"e\":\"Flexible cystoscopy allows visualisation of the bladder, but not the ureters.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Insertion of a nephrostomy\",\"e\":\"This case demonstrates an obstructed and infected urinary tract and as such constitutes a urological emergency with a danger of pyonephrosis developing unless the obstruction is urgently relieved. A nephrostomy tube can be placed percutaneously to allow drainage of the kidney. In addition to this, other measures include intravenous (IV) antibiotics and fluid resuscitation. A ureteric stent would be another option.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Laparoscopy\",\"e\":\"Laparoscopy is not indicated at present, instead a percutaneous nephrostomy tube should be placed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Open surgery\",\"e\":\"Open surgery carries significant risk and recovery times and is not required in this setting. Placement of the nephrostomy tube and treatment with IV fluids and antibiotics is sufficient at this stage.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":171,\"stem\":\"A 71-year-old man presents to his GP with a history of frank haematuria. He denies any associated pain and is generally well in himself. Examination is unremarkable and his only past medical history of note is chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) secondary to a long smoking history.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause for the haematuria?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause for the haematuria?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bladder cancer\",\"e\":\"Painless haematuria in any patient above the age of 50 should be considered to be the result of bladder cancer until proven otherwise. The main risk factor for transitional-cell carcinoma (TCC) of the bladder in the UK at present is smoking, of which this man has a significant history\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Benign prostatic hypertrophy (BPH)\",\"e\":\"BPH presents with lower urinary tract symptoms such as increased frequency, hesitancy and poor flow. Haematuria is uncommon in BPH and should raise the suspicion of bladder cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Prostate cancer\",\"e\":\"Similarly, prostate cancer presents with lower urinary tract symptoms, not haematuria.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal colic\",\"e\":\"Renal colic usually presents with loin to groin pain with microscopic haematuria.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urinary tract infection (UTI)\",\"e\":\"UTI may present with haematuria, however this is often microscopic and associated with dysuria and other systemic symptoms of infection.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":173,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old man presents to his GP with a swelling in his scrotum. This is painless, but is becoming a nuisance due to its increasing size. On examination there is a diffuse swelling of the scrotum, and it is not possible to distinctly palpate the testicle. When using a pen-torch it glows red.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epididymo-orchitis\",\"e\":\"Epididymo-orchitis is an inflammatory condition of the testicle and epididymis secondary to infection. This may occur as a result of a viral infection such as mumps, or bacterial infection due to a urinary tract infection (UTI) or a sexually transmitted disease eg Chlamydia infection or gonorrhea. The epididymis is often swollen and tender.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hydrocoele\",\"e\":\"A hydrocoele consists of fluid accumulating within the tunica vaginalis and can occur secondary to a patent processus vaginalis in neonates, (congenital), or be idiopathic in origin, (primary). In a hydrocoele it is often difficult to palpate the testicle separately. They transilluminate well to produce a red glow when a pen-torch is used\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hernia\",\"e\":\"An indirect hernia may extend into the scrotum causing discomfort. On examination, it is difficult to palpate above the hernia on the affected side, in contrast with a hydrocele where one can get above it\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spermatocoele\",\"e\":\"A spermatocoele, or epididymal cyst, is a fluid-filled cyst found within the epididymis. On examination the testicle would be clearly palpable with a swelling noted above and behind the affected testicle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Testicular tumour\",\"e\":\"Testicular tumours present as painless, non-tender lumps within the testicle and are often found on self-examination.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":176,\"stem\":\"A 77-year-old man with HIV presents with a 6-h history of severe right loin to groin pain and is unable to get comfortable. On examination he is clearly in pain, but afebrile with normal observations. His urinalysis shows blood 2+, but his blood tests, plain imaging and a computed tomography (CT) scan of the kidneys, ureters and bladder (KUB) are all unremarkable.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bladder stone\",\"e\":\"A bladder stone would tend not to cause the classic loin to groin pain associated with renal colic, as the stone is in the bladder and therefore not causing intermittent ureteric obstruction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Calcium oxalate stone\",\"e\":\"Calcium oxalate, cysteine and triple phosphate stones are radio-opaque and therefore should be seen on imaging.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"crystaine son, V\",\"e\":\"A cysteine stone would be seen on radiographic imaging.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Indinavir stone\",\"e\":\"Indinavir is an antiretroviral agent used in HIV that can classically cause radiolucent stones. Given the clear history of renal colic and known diagnosis of HIV in the absence of a stone on imaging, an indinavir stone is the most likely diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Triple phosphate stone\",\"e\":\"A triple phosphate stone is radio-opaque and therefore visible on CT imaging.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":178,\"stem\":\"You are called to review a 74-year-old man on the orthopaedic ward who is complaining of severe pain in the tip of his penis, following catheterisation for urinary retentior after a total hip replacement. On examination the prepuce is retracted with the glans swollen and oedematous..\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"Explanation wt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Balanitis\",\"e\":\"Balanitis is an acute inflammation of the glans (while 'balanoposthitis' implies inflammation of the glans and foreskin) often caused by a bacterial infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Balanitis xerotica obliterans (BXO)\",\"e\":\"BXO is a condition in which white plaques are present on the glans and prepuce making retraction of the foreskin difficult. It is an indication for circumcision.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Paraphimosis\",\"e\":\"A paraphimosis occurs when the prepuce is retracted beyond the glans and cannot be replaced. This results in the prepuce acting as a tight band and preventing venous return, which can result in swelling and oedema of the glans. It may be caused by not returning the retracted foreskin to its original position after urethral catheterisation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Phimosis\",\"e\":\"A phimosis occurs when the foreskin is tight, making retraction difficult.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Traumatic catheterisation\",\"e\":\"Traumatic catheterisation is likely to result in frank haematuria and pain.\\n\\n2518\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":180,\"stem\":\"A 15-year-old boy is brought into A&E by his parents who are concerned he has started limping and complaining of pain in the lower abdomen. He also gives a history of nausea and vomiting. On examination his abdomen is soft throughout, but his right testicle is noted to be very tender, swollen and noted to be drawn up in the scrotum. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Appendicitis\",\"e\":\"Appendicitis would present with abdominal pain and tenderness on examination. It can cause a child to limp, however due to inflammation of the peritoneum in the right iliac fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diverticulitis\",\"e\":\"Diverticulitis would be extremely unlikely in a 15-year-old boy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Epididymo-orchitis\",\"e\":\"Epididymo-orchitis is an inflammatory condition of the testicle and epididymis secondary to infection. This may occur as a result of a viral infection such as mumps, or bacterial infection due to a urinary tract infection (UTI) or sexually transmitted disease, eg Chlamydia or gonorrhoea. The epididymis is often swollen and tender.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Meckel's diverticulum\",\"e\":\"Meckel's diverticulum will result in an acute abdomen, however this patient's abdomen is soft and there would not be tenderness of the testicle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Torsion\",\"e\":\"When considering causes of a limping child it is important to think of causes beyond the musculoskeletal system, such as those relating to general surgical and urological conditions. These can include causes of an acute abdomen such as appendicitis or a Meckel's diverticulum. However, in this case the abdomen is soft throughout and the right testicle is noted to be exquisitely tender and drawn up. In any boy of this age presenting with this history and examination findings, a torted testicle must be considered. Torsion is a urological emergency and the testis can infarct within hours if not recognised and treated. Treatment consists of surgical exploration and if a torted testicle is seen, it should be untwisted and then secured in place (orchidopexy) or if it is found to be not viable an orchidectomy performed. The other testicle must also be secured as well to prevent possible future torsion of that testicle.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":183,\"stem\":\"A 3-year-old boy is referred from his GP as his parents have noted some haematuria and weight loss. On examination, he is noted to have a visible left-sided abdominal mass.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause for this?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause for this?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Nephroblastoma\",\"e\":\"Nephroblastomas (or Wilms' tumours) occur in children < 5 years and are an undifferentiated embryonic tumour. They are the commonest intra-abdominal tumours in children under 10 years of age and are bilateral in 10%. Presentation can be with failure to thrive and a visible abdominal mass. Around one-third of patients will have haematuria.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Neuroblastoma\",\"e\":\"Neuroblastomas are tumours derived from neuroendocrine cells and arise from neural crest elements of the sympathetic nervous system. They most commonly arise from the adrenal glands, but can develop elsewhere. In most cases there will be alterations in catecholamine levels:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Renal cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Renal cell carcinoma most commonly present as asymptomatic, unilateral tumours in adults, not children.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transitional cell carcinoma of the left ureter\",\"e\":\"Transitional cell carcinoma (TCC) would be extremely unlikely in a 3-year-old child and instead is a disease of adults of advancing age.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Meckel's diverticulum\",\"e\":\"Meckel's diverticulum will result in an acute abdomen with pain and tenderness. A visible left-sided abdominal mass would not be seen.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":186,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old woman with chronic glomerulonephritis requires a renal transplant as she has end-stage renal failure (ESRF). A suitable kidney has become available in the last 4 hours. The renal artery and vein of the donor are most likely to be anastomosed to which vessels of the recipient in end to side anastomosis?\",\"lead\":\"The renal artery and vein of the donor are most likely to be anastomosed to which vessels of the recipient in end to side anastomosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aorta and inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"This would require placement of the transplant kidney deep into the abdominal cavity which would prevent easy future access for biopsies and post-operative complications.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"External iliac artery and vein\",\"e\":\"The kidney is placed extraperitoneally in the right or left iliac fossa, usually on the right side (dictated by existing scars or previous transplants and because it is relatively straightforward to access for biopsies and surgery in the event of post-operative problems). Renal vessels are anastomosed (end to side) to the recipient's external iliac vessels. The ureter is taken down into the pelvis where it is anastomosed to the bladder mucosa, either directly (extravesical approach) or by threading it through a submucosal tunnel and suturing from inside the bladder through a separate incision in the bladder wall (intravesical approach):\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Femoral artery and vein\",\"e\":\"The femoral vessels start below the level of the inguinal ligament and therefore it would not be accessible for a transplanted kidney in the iliac fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Internal iliac artery and vein\",\"e\":\"The internal iliac vessels run deep in the pelvis close to the sacrum and therefore are suboptimal vessels to perform the anastomosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Renal artery and vein\",\"e\":\"The transplanted kidney is placed in the right or left iliac fossa and therefore would not be anastomosed with the renal artery and vein.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":189,\"stem\":\"A 39-year-old woman presents with fever, pain on passing urine and abdominal pain that radiates along the flank. She is desperate for treatment and cannot cope at home.\\n\\nWhat is the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cystitis . C-\",\"e\":\"Cystitis is common in women due to the short urethra and presents with dysuria. Systemic symptoms such as fever are not seen, with pain being localised to the suprapubic region, not the flank.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pelvic abscess\",\"e\":\"A pelvic abscess would cause pain and fever, however, the pain would not radiate along the flank which is suggestive of pyelonephritis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pyelonephritis\",\"e\":\"Pyelonephritis is an ascending urinary tract infection that has ascended to affect the kidney. Severe cases can lead to pyonephritis, urosepsis and kidney failure. Treatment is with intravenous antibiotics and fluids.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urethritis\",\"e\":\"Urethritis would cause dysuria only, with fever and abdominal pain unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lower urinary tract infection\",\"e\":\"Given this patient's fever and abdominal pain, it suggests the urinary tract infection (usually confined to the bladder) has extended proximally through the ureter and is now affecting the kidney.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":191,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old woman receives a course of quinolone antibiotics for a urinary tract infection. She subsequently develops abdominal pain with foul smelling watery stools.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding the diagnosis or treatment of Clostridium difficile?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding the diagnosis or treatment of Clostridium difficile?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Treatment is with high-dose benzylpenicillin\",\"e\":\"Treatment is usually with oral metronidazole as a first line treatment, not benzylpenicillin.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diagnosis is with C. difficile toxin in faces\",\"e\":\"C. difficile infection should be considered in any patient who develops diarrhoea and who is taking an antibiotic, or who has received a course of antibiotics in the past few weeks. In the laboratory, the most reliable way of confirming the diagnosis is by the detection of the toxin produced by C. difficile in the faces of patients. Therefore, one or more faces specimens should be sent to the microbiology laboratory requesting that a specific investigation for C. difficile is undertaken. Loperamide should be avoided as it may slow down the rate at which toxins are cleared and may worsen C. difficile infection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diagnosis is with C. difficile organism in faces\",\"e\":\"Laboratory testing involves detection of the C. difficile toxin in the faces, not the organism itself.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Treatment is with steroids\",\"e\":\"Treatment with steroids is not recommended for C. difficile infection.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Symptoms can be alleviated with loperamide\",\"e\":\"Loperamide can be used as a symptomatic treatment for diarrhoea, however it should not be used in the presence of infection\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":194,\"stem\":\"You are examining the external genitalia of a newborn and note the presence of hypospadias. You are concerned of the presence of a disorder of sexual development.\\n\\nWhat is the most common location for the meatus in this condition?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most common location for the meatus in this condition?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dorsal surface\",\"e\":\"The external urethral meatus is located on the ventral, not dorsal, surface of the penis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glanular\",\"e\":\"In hypospadias the external urethral meatus is located on the ventral surface of the penis: 70% are glanular, 10% penile and 20% scrotal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Penile\",\"e\":\"Only 10% of hypospadias are penile.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Healed\",\"e\":\"Perineal placement is associated with anorectal anomalies.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Scrotal\",\"e\":\"Scrotal placement of hypospadias occurs in around 20% of cases.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":196,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient with torsion of the appendage of the testis. At exploration you note the abnormality and excise it.\\n\\nWhich appendage is most commonly affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which appendage is most commonly affected?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epididymal appendix\",\"e\":\"The epididymal appendix is a remnant of the Wolffian (or mesonephric) duct and can become torted, however it is more common for the hydatid of Morgagni to become torted.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hydatid of Morgagni\",\"e\":\"The testicular appendix (also known as hydatid of Morgagni) is a remnant of the Müllerian duct. It is the most common testicular appendage to tort, accounting for around 90% of cases. Can sometimes be noted on transillumination of the testis as a blue spot.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Organ of Giraldés\",\"e\":\"This organ is a vestigial structure located on the distal portion of the spermatic cord, however it is often not seen on imaging and is likely to be of little clinical significance.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vas aberrans\",\"e\":\"Vas aberrans is a testicular appendage located in a fissure between the epididymis and testis consisting of aberrant vessels. It is however of little clinical significance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Paradidymis\",\"e\":\"The paradidymis is also known as the organ of Giraldés and does not frequently become torted.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":198,\"stem\":\"A 2-year-old boy presents with a palpable left-sided renal mass, associated with a varicocele The child is pyrexial and hypertensive. A urine dipstick is negative for blood.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Massive hydronephrosis\",\"e\":\"Massive hydronephrosis may be diagnosed pre-nataly, approximately 50% will regress by birth, with a further 15% resolving by age 3 years.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Neuroblastoma\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Rhabdomyosarcoma\",\"e\":\"Rhabdomyosarcoma is a mixed solid and cystic genitourinary malignant tumour, which tends to present with haematuria or vaginal bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Simple renal cyst\",\"e\":\"Simple renal cysts may be an incidental finding, they are less likely to be palpable and not usually associated with pyrexia and hypertension. 3105\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wilms' tumour\",\"e\":\"Wilms' tumour is the most common solid renal mass in children. The commonest presentation is with a palpable renal mass in a pre-school age child. Haematuria is rarely seen. Fever and hypertension may be present. Left-sided tumours can be associated with varicoceles.\\n\\nNeuroblastomas cross the midline and nearly all produce catecholamines, which can be tested for in the urine.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":200,\"stem\":\"You are examining a 2-week-old boy, and are unable to palpate the testis in the right side of the scrotum. You suspect an ectopic testis.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely site that you will find the ectopic testis lying at?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely site that you will find the ectopic testis lying at?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Base of the penis\",\"e\":\"Ectopic testes are extremely rare. They can be found at the base of the penis however this is less common than within the superficial inguinal pouch.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral region\",\"e\":\"Ectopic testes have left the normal path of descent and can be found in the femoral region, however, the superficial inguinal pouch is more common.\\n\\nUndescended testes can be found along the path of descent and testes in the abdominal cavity are in this category.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Intra-abdominal\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Perineum\",\"e\":\"Testes in the perineum are considered ectopic testes, however the most likely site to find ectopic testes is the superficial inguinal pouch.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superficial inguinal pouch\",\"e\":\"Testes that are not palpable in the scrotum are undescended, which can occur with an incidence of 2-3%. Undescended testes can be retractile, ectopic or incompletely descended. Attempts should be made to manipulate retractile testes back into the scrotum during examination. Ectopic testes have left the normal course of descent of the testis and approximately 80% of undescended testes fall into this category. They can be found in the superficial inguinal pouch, (most common location), base of penis. perineum, and femoral region.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":203,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old woman with myasthenia gravis presents having recently experienced urinary incontinence for the first time. She has four children and consumes caffeine daily. She reports urgency for the last 2 years and is unable to cope with her symptoms. This is the first time she has sought medical treatment.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding this condition?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is usually caused by detrusor overactivity\",\"e\":\"Overactive bladder (OAB) is a symptom syndrome that includes urgency with or without urge incontinence and is usually caused by detrusor overactivity but can be due to other causes of voiding dysfunction. OAB affects 17% of the population aged >40 years in Europe. Symptoms include urinary urgency with or withcut incontinence, frequency and nocturia. The prevalence increases with age. Conservative management such as lifestyle factors and behavioural modifications is first line. Second line management includes use of anticholinergics like oxybutynin, solifenacin and tolterodine. Contraindications are mentioned above. Before starting patients on this medication, they need to be advised of common side- effects including dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision, urinary retention and cognitive impairment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The prevalence of the condition decreases with age until the age of 30\",\"e\":\"Overactive bladder incidence increases with age.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bladder training does not play a role in initial management of this condition\",\"e\":\"Behavioural modification such as:\\n\\n• modifying fluid intake - not to drink past 7 pm • avoiding stimulants like caffeine and alcohol • bladder training - delaying micturition for increasing periods of time\\n\\nhave all been effective at improving symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"This patient may be started on non-selective anticholinergic medication should behavioural modifications fail\",\"e\":\"Anticholinergic medication is the mainstay of medical treatment. There are now more selective agents such as tolterodine that reduce systemic side-effects. However, antimuscarinics are contraindicated in the following cases:\\n\\n• uncontrolled closed angle glaucoma • myasthenia gravis • bladder outflow obstruction • bowel disorders.\\n\\nThis patient has myasthenia gravis, hence anticholinergics are contraindicated.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Diarrhoea is one of the common gastrointestinal (GI) side-effects of anticholinergics\",\"e\":\"Constipation is one of the side-effects that patients should be warned about, not diarrhoea\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":206,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man attends the Emergency Department complaining of left flank pain and dysuria. He is visibly in pain despite 10 mg of intravenous (IV) morphine. His urine dip is positive for nitrites and blood, and he is pyrexial at 38°C.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct about his most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct about his most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The first step in management would be getting a CTKUB\",\"e\":\"Always manage a patient, as in this case for example, who is likely to be septic using an ABCDE (Airway maintenance with cervical spine protection, Breathing and ventilation, Circulation with haemorrhage control, Disability: neurological status, Exposure/environmental control) approach to conduct a safe assessment and address any issues in that order.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is most likely to be due to a calcium oxalate calculus\",\"e\":\"80-85% of all kidney stones are made of calcium oxalate and is therefore the most likely composition of the kidney stone. Stone disease is becoming a growing problem in the population. Intrinsic and extrinsic factors both play a role in predisposition. Risk factors for stone disease include:\\n\\n• diet: fluid intake, meat consumption, high uric acid levels • drugs: corticosteroids, chemotherapeutic agents • urinary tract infection (UTI) • immobility • systemic disease: gout, primary hyperparathyroidism, sarcoidosis • family history: cystinuria • renal anatomy • previous bowel resection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is not necessary to send off a urine culture as the urine dip is positive for nitrites\",\"e\":\"It is essential that the urine is cultured so ensure an midstream specimen of urine (MSU) is always sent off. This is useful to know in terms of antimicrobial treatment and will guide future treatment should repeated episodes occur.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Corticosteroids are not a known risk factor in stone formation\",\"e\":\"Corticosteroids are a known risk factor as they increase enteric absorption of calcium, leading to hypercalciuria accelerating stone formation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"First line imaging would be an ultrasound of the renal tract\",\"e\":\"Renal stones are not always detected on renal tract ultrasonography (USKUB) hence a plain abdominal radiograph is preferable, especially in this case as it is an acute presentation with no contraindication to radiation for example, a pregnant woman. In some trusts, a computed tomography (CT) KUB is done as first line imaging however this should be considered on a case by case basis taking into account radiation risk.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":209,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man patient who is being investigated for gallstones has an ultrasound scan (USS) of his abdomen. The radiologist notes a right-sided renal mass which 'appears benign' on the report.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true of benign renal masses?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true of benign renal masses?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Simple renal cysts are benign and decrease in incidence with age\",\"e\":\"Simple renal cysts increase in incidence with age; they are a common finding at autopsy, or an incidental finding at USS\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Most benign renal tumours are rare, the two most clinically important are oncocytoma and angiomyolipoma\",\"e\":\"Benign renal tumours are rare. Oncocytoma and angiomyolipomas are the most clinically important.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oncocytomas can often be distinguished radiologically from renal-cell carcinoma (RCC) J\",\"e\":\"They often cannot be distinguished radiologically from RCCs and may even coexist with RCCs.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Both adult and childhood polycystic disease are not associated with cysts in the liver\",\"e\":\"Both forms of polycystic kidney disease can have liver cysts. In adult polycystic kidney disease the liver cysts have no functional significance: In childhood polycystic kidney disease, there may be more significant liver disease ranging from bile duct proliferation and cysts to hepatic fibrosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ten per cent of angiomyolipomas (AMLs) are associated with tuberous sclerosis\",\"e\":\"About 20% of AMLs are associated with the autosomal-dominant syndrome tuberous sclerosis which characterised by mental retardation, epilepsy, adenoma sebaceum and other hamartomas.\"}]},{\"t\":17,\"p\":212,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old man was diagnosed with renal cell carcinoma. The tumour was seen to be extending into the renal vein on computed tomography.\\n\\nWhat is the clinical T stage of this tumour?\",\"lead\":\"What is the clinical T stage of this tumour?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"Explanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"T1\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"T2\",\"e\":\"These tumours are greater than 7 cm in size and limited to the kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"T3a\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"T3b\",\"e\":\"These tumours can be any size but extend into <he inferior vena cava below the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T3c\",\"e\":\"These tumours can be any size but extend into the inferior vena cava above the diaphragm.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old man presents with acute back pain after lifting a heavy weight. His past medical history includes a previous lumbar intervertebral disc prolapse.\\n\\nWhich myotome would be weak in the context of an isolated L4/L5 disc lesion?\",\"lead\":\"Which myotome would be weak in the context of an isolated L4/L5 disc lesion?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ankle plantar flexion\",\"e\":\"Ankle plantar flexion is mediated by S1 and 2. Ankle Dorsiflexion is mediated by L4/L5 Though L5 weakness would be expected with an L4/L5 lesion, the contribution of level L4 would maintain strength in this myotome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Eversion of the foot\",\"e\":\"Eversion of the foot is mediated by S1. In the lumbar spine, exiting nerve roots emerge below their respective vertebrae. Therefore weak foot eversion (mediated by S1) would be expected with an L5S1 lesion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Extension of great toe\",\"e\":\"In the lumbar spine (in contrast to the cervical spine) nerve roots emerge below their respective vertebrae: so the majority of L4/5 disc prolapses would be expected to affect the L5 root and the majority of L5/S1 disc prolapses would affect the S1 nerve root. The L5 nerve root, which would be affected in this case, also contributes to hip abduction and extension, knee flexion and ankle dorsiflexion. The weakness seen in these myotomes is often minimal because of the contribution of other roots to these movements and is maximal in extension of the toes, particularly the great toe.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inversion of the foot\",\"e\":\"Inversion of the foot is med ated by L4 alone, therefore isolated weakness in this myotome would be expecting with an L3/L4 lesion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Knee extension\",\"e\":\"Knee extension is mediated primarily by L3/4, therefore isolated weakness in this myotome would be expected with an L2/3 or L3/L4 lesion.\\n\\n280\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with pins and needles on the lateral and anterior aspect of his left thigh. On examination, there is no motor deficit. There is no history of trauma. Which of the following is likely to be causing the problem?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is likely to be causing the problem?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh lesion\",\"e\":\"The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies the antero-lateral aspect of the thigh. It has no motor branches. Meralgia paraesthetica is a condition in which there is irritation of the nerve causing sensory changes in the distribution of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh without any motor changes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"L2 root lesion\",\"e\":\"L2 and L3 supply part of the dermatome described, but both have motor branches.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"L3 root lesion\",\"e\":\"While the L3 nerve root supplies the dermatome, it also has motor branches, therefore if the L3 nerve root were affected weakness could be expected. The main muscle innervated by L3 is the quadriceps femoris.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Femoral nerve lesion\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve supplies the quadriceps muscle therefore a lesion to the femoral nerve would result in weakness of knee extension.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Saphenous nerve lesion\",\"e\":\"The saphenous nerve is a terminal cutaneous branch of the femoral nerve. It runs with the saphenous vein to supply an area of skin below the knee on the medial aspect of the leg. Saphenous nerve neuralgia can be seen after varicose vein surgery. Damage can also occur after vein harvest for coronary artery bypass graft (CABG).\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with a history of back pain that developed 3 months ago when he got up suddenly from a seated position. The pain radiates down the leg towards the knee. On examination he has weakness of the quadriceps, reduced knee jerk reflex and reduced sensation over the patella.\\n\\nWhere is the lesion likely to be?\",\"lead\":\"Where is the lesion likely to be?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Sciatic nerve compression\",\"e\":\"The sciatic nerve innervates the muscles of the posterior compartment of the thigh and the muscles of the leg. It provides sensory innervation for the posterior thigh, the leg and the foot.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ilioinguinal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve supplies a small area of skin on the medial aspect of the upper thigh as well as the scrotum and penis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"L3 nerve root\",\"e\":\"The history suggests a prolapsed intervertebral disc. The quadriceps are supplied by the femoral nerve whose root value is L2-L4. The skin over the patella is usually part of the L3 dermatome, and the rcot value of the knee jerk is L3/L4..\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"L5 nerve root\",\"e\":\"Compression of the L5/S1 nerve root produces symptoms of sciatica which would not result in a reduced knee jerk reflex.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Compression of the femoral nerve at the inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"Femoral nerve compression at the level of the inguinal ligament is unlikely, given the history of injury and back pain.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A patient complains of sensory deficit halfway down the anterior surface of the thigh. The patient is very disconcerted by their presentation.\\n\\nWhich one of the following would cause this sensory deficit?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would cause this sensory deficit?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Damage to the sciatic nerve\",\"e\":\"This would result in sciatica in which pain is experienced in the lower back with extension of the pain down the posterior thigh.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Compression of the ventral roots of L5 to S2\",\"e\":\"Compression of the L5-S2 nerve root would result in posterior sciatica symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Damage to a nerve accompanying the artery in the adductor canal\",\"e\":\"The saphenous nerve (a branch of the femoral nerve) can be found in the adductor canal. It has no motor function but provides sensory supply to the medial aspect of the lower half of the leg.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Could be the result of nerve damage during surgical procedures in the femoral sheath\",\"e\":\"The femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve can be damaged within the femoral sheath. This supplies skin around the femoral triangle area.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Damage to the nerve that innervates the pectines muscle\",\"e\":\"The anterior surface of the thigh receives its sensory innervation from the femoral nerve so this is the nerve most likely to be injured. The pectineus is supplied by the second, third and fourth lumbar nerves through the femoral nerve. Occasionally, it receives a branch from the obturator nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A young man sustains a skull-base fracture at the middle cranial fossa that injures his right abducens (VI) nerve.\\n\\nWhich signs are most likely to be present on clinical examination?\",\"lead\":\"Which signs are most likely to be present on clinical examination?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"There is posis on the right side\",\"e\":\"Ptosis would be as a result of damage to the oculomotor nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The pupil on the right side is constricted and fails to respond to light\",\"e\":\"The pupillary light reflex is detected by the optic nerve and pupillary constriction is initiated by the oculomotor nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The right eyelid is numb\",\"e\":\"The trigeminal nerve is responsible suppling facial sensation including to the right eyelid.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The patient is unable to deviate his right eye medially\",\"e\":\"The oculomotor nerve via the medial rectus muscle is responsible for adduction of the eye.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The patient is unable to deviate his right eye laterally\",\"e\":\"The abducens nerve innervates the lateral rectus muscle of the eye exclusively; the sole effect of damage to this nerve is that the patient is unable to abduct (laterally deviate) the eye.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"In performing a lumbar puncture, the operator needs to be familiar with the anatomy involved. There are important structures in the area that can be inadvertently damaged.\\n\\nWhich anatomical feature is relevant to this procedure?\",\"lead\":\"Which anatomical feature is relevant to this procedure?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"In the newborn baby, the spinal cord occupies the full length of the dural sac\",\"e\":\"In the newborn, it terminates at L3.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The dural sac in the adult terminates at the lower end of the sacral canal\",\"e\":\"The dural sac in the adult extends to the level of the second sacral segment.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In the normal adult, the commonest level for the termination of the spinal cord is the disc space between L1 and L2\",\"e\":\"During a lumbar puncture, a needle most commonly passes through the L3/4 intervertebral space (usually found at the height of the iliac crest) to avoid the spinal cord\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The spinal cord in the average male is 12 inches (30 cm) in length\",\"e\":\"The spinal cord in both adult men and women is 18 inches (45 cm) in length.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The extradural space comprises a thin layer of avascular connective tissue\",\"e\":\"The spinal extradural space contains loose fat (which allows the ready diffusion of local anaesthetic in an extradural block), together with the extensive vertebral venous plexus of veins.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A cerebral angiogram is performed on a 37-year-old woman, following a suspected aneurysmal bleed. She presented with reduced GCS and headache without a history of trauma\\n\\nWhich anatomical feature should be considered when interpreting the angiogram?\",\"lead\":\"Which anatomical feature should be considered when interpreting the angiogram?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The middle cerebral artery is the largest artery among the cerebral arteries\",\"e\":\"The middle cerebral artery is indeed the largest artery among the cerebral arteries, being, in effect, the termination of the internal carotid.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The posterior cerebral artery is clearly seen on a lateral carotid angiogram\",\"e\":\"The posterior cerebral artery arises from the termination of the basilar artery, which itself arises from the two vertebral arteries that meet on the under surface of the brainstem - so the posterior cerebral artery can only be visualised by vertebral angiography.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The vertebral arteries meet at the foramen magnum to form the basilar artery\",\"e\":\"The vertebral arteries meet under the surface of the brainstem.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The middle cerebral artery courses over the lateral aspect of the temporal lobe of the cerebrum\",\"e\":\"It passes through the lateral sulcus of the cerebrum between the temporal and frontal lobes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The middle meningeal artery is an extracranial branch of the internal carotid artery\",\"e\":\"The middle meningeal artery is a branch of the maxillary artery, one of the terminal branches of the external carotid.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"In clinic, you see a 36-year-old man referred by his General Practitioner (GP) with a feeling of numbness in his left leg. On examination, he has decreased position sense and light touch and vibration sensation affecting his left leg to the upper part of the thigh. No other neurological deficit is demonstrable.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause of this presentation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely cause of this presentation?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left dorsal column lesion\",\"e\":\"The dorsal columns carry ipsilateral proprioception, light touch and vibration sensation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left spinothalamic tract lesion\",\"e\":\"Spinothalamic tract lesions cause contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Peripheral polyneuropathy\",\"e\":\"Peripheral polyneuropathy is usually bilateral.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Partial section of the spinal cord\",\"e\":\"Partial section of the cord tends to cause contralateral pain and temperature sensation and ipsilateral loss of the modalities carried in the dorsal columns; there may also be upper motor neurone weakness. A clinical example is Brown-Séquard syndrome, caused by damage to one-half of the spinal cord.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sensory root compression\",\"e\":\"Sensory root compression typically causes pain in the dermatome supplied by the root\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"A patient has been diagnosed with a fast-growing pituitary adenoma. Magnetic resonance image (MRI) scanning reveals suprasellar extension.\\n\\nWhich structure is most likely affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure is most likely affected?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abducens nerve\",\"e\":\"The lateral walls of the pituitary fossa are formed by the cavernous sinuses which contain the internal carotid arteries and cranial nerves III, IV, V1, V2 and VI. The abducens nerve is less likely to be compressed than the optic nerve as adenomas extend upwards.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypothalamus\",\"e\":\"The hypothalamus is located superiorly to the pituitary gland and the optic chiasm within the brain parenchyma. With a rapidly growing adenoma, the optic nerve becomes compressed between the hypothalamus and the adenoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oculomotor nerve\",\"e\":\"The oculomotor nerve forms part of the lateral wail of the pituitary fossa; however, as adenomas usually extend upwards, it is less likely to be affected than the optic chiasm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Third ventricle\",\"e\":\"Even if the third ventricle were compressed by a rapidly growing adenoma, there is unlikely to be any clinical significance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Optic chiasm\",\"e\":\"The pituitary gland occupies the sella turcica, which is a cup-shaped depression in the basisphenoid bone. The roof of the sella is formed by the diaphragma sella, a fold of dura, which is perforated to allow passage of the pituitary stalk. Above the diaphragm lies the suprasellar cistern, the optic chiasm and the anterior cerebral arteries. The optic chiasm is most likely to be compressed by a pituitary adenoma. Classically, this produces a bitemporal hemianopia:\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"You review a 56-year-old woman in the Outpatient Clinic following myocardial revascularisation using the internal thoracic arteries. Despite a strong recovery and good postoperative course, she complains that since the operation she has had chronic pain in her shoulder and that her shoulder is becoming weaker. When you examine her, you notice winging of the scapula.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause of these symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of these symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Damage to the axillary nerve\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Damage to the long thoracic nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the long thoracic nerve causes winging of the scapula due to weakness of the serratus anterior.\\n\\nThe axillary nerve contains fibres of C5 and C6 to supply predominantly the deltoid muscle. It conveys some sensory fibres to the lateral aspect of the forearm.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Damage to the radial nerve\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve supplies the extensor carpi radialis and ulnaris, plus extensor digitorum (via the posterior interosseous nerve), so its injury results in wrist drop.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Damage to the spinal accessory nerve\",\"e\":\"Spinal accessory nerve damage will cause weakness in shrugging the shoulders.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Damage to the whole brachial plexus\",\"e\":\"Damage to the whole brachial plexus would produce a wide range of symptoms including significant motor and sensory deficits.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old man has collapsed following a subarachnoid haemorrhage. After initial resuscitation and investigations, he is taken to theatre and the aneurysm is clipped. After waking from anaesthesia his Glasgow Coma Score (GCS) is 15/15 and he is taken to the Neurological Intensive Care Unit (ITU) for monitoring: When you see him on the ward round the next day his GCS has fallen to 12/15 and a computed tomography (CT) scan shows dilated ventricles.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct about cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) circulation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct about cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) circulation?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It flows between the third and fourth ventricles via the aqueduct of Monro\",\"e\":\"The aqueduct of Sylvius connects the third and fourth ventricles, the foramen of Monro connects the lateral ventricles.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is produced at a rate of 2 mi/min\",\"e\":\"CSF is produced at a rate of 0.3-0.5 ml/min.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is produced by the arachnoid granulations\",\"e\":\"The arachnoid granulations (villi) are involved in the reabsorption of the CSF into the venous sinuses and so into the systemic circulation. Cerebrospinal fluid is predominantly produced by the choroid plexuses in the ventricles.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is sterile\",\"e\":\"Cerebrospinal fluid is usually sterile. It is only non-sterile in the context of infection, for example meningitis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It normally has a higher protein content than plasma\",\"e\":\"The protein content of serum is much higher than that of the CSF.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old man presents with sensory loss of his right medial leg distal to the knee. On physical exam, he is unable to extend his right knee and his patellar reflex is absent.\\n\\nWhich nerve injury would produce these symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve injury would produce these symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A common peroneal nerve injury\",\"e\":\"Common peroneal (or fibular) nerve injuries often occur from pressure from plaster casts and lateral knee ligament injuries. Superficial peroneal nerve injuries impair eversion but not dorsiflexion, as this action is subserved by the deep peroneal nerve. Peroneal nerve injury does not cause the pattern of injury described here.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A lesion of L3 nerve root\",\"e\":\"The L3 nerve root supplies a dermatomal area across the mid-thigh and extends to just beyond the knee. Given the sensory deficit is distal to the knee, it is the L4 nerve root that is more likely affected.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A lesion of S1\",\"e\":\"S1 lesions result in a weak plantar flexion, loss of ankle reflex and sensory loss over the sole\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"L4 nerve root compression\",\"e\":\"Lesions affecting the L3/4 nerve root also results in a decreased knee reflex, and sensory loss over the anterior thigh and medial part of the leg. In this particular scenario, it is also more likely that is the L4 root, rather than the L3 root, that is affected due to the pattern of sensory loss described.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sciatic nerve injury\",\"e\":\"Sciatic nerve injury, which can occur through trauma during surgery or with gluteal intramuscular injection, results in sensory deficit on the posterior aspect of the thigh and does not extend beyond the knee. It can also present with foot drop.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old man falls sustaining a hyperextension injury to his neck. On examination, he has weakness in his upper and lower limbs, but sensation is preserved\\n\\nWhich spinal tract injury explains this presentation?\",\"lead\":\"Which spinal tract injury explains this presentation?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The dorsal column\",\"e\":\"The dorsal columns are white matter tracts formed by the fasciculus gracilis and fasciculus cuneatus. The fasciculus gracilis lies medially to the fasciculus cuneatus. They carry fine touch and proprioception.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The lateral spinothalamic tract\",\"e\":\"The lateral spinothalamic tracts carry crossed axons conducting pain and temperature sensations.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The ventral spinothalamic tract\",\"e\":\"The ventral (or anterior) spinothalamic tract contains ascending fibres that transmit crude touch and firm pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The lateral corticospinal tract\",\"e\":\"The lateral corticospinal tract transmits voluntary motor signals to the limbs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The ventral corticospinal tract\",\"e\":\"The ventral (or anterior) corticospinal tract contains descending fibres and control axial voluntary muscles. It is much smaller than the lateral corticospinal tract and decussate at the level of the spinal cord.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A young man sustains brachial plexus injury following a stabbing incident. He presents with weakness of the elbow extension and wrist dorsiflexion.\\n\\nWhich part of the brachial plexus has been transected?\",\"lead\":\"Which part of the brachial plexus has been transected?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The superior trunk\",\"e\":\"The superior (or upper) trunk is derived from the C5/6 nerve roots. Damage to this area would result in limited elbow flexion, medial rotation of the arm, forearm pronation, and loss of sensation over the deltoid and the lateral upper limb.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The posterior cord\",\"e\":\"The posterior cord of the brachial plexus gives off the radial nerve, which supplies the triceps and the extensor carpi radialis. The brachial plexus is derived from the anterior rami of C5, 6, 7, 8, T1. It is described in terms of roots, trunks, divisions, cords and nerves. The musculocutaneous nerve (C5, 6, 7) is a continuation of the lateral cord, the median nerve (C5, 6, 7, 8, T1) is derived from the medial and lateral cords, the ulnar (C8, T1) nerve is a continuation of the medial cord, and the radial nerve (C5, 6, 7, 8, T1) a continuation of the posterior cord.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The axillary nerve\",\"e\":\"The axillary nerve is not involved in elbow extension and wrist dorsiflexion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"I he ulnar nerve\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve is involved n innervation of the forearm and hand and, therefore, would not be involved in elbow extension or wrist dorsiflexion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"5 nerve root\",\"e\":\"The C5 nerve supplies nerves to the brachial plexus, however transection of the C5 nerve root would result in weakness of shoulder abduction and external rotation.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"An elderly man with pre-existing cervical spondylosis falls sustaining hyperextension injury to his neck. On examination he has motor deficit that is worse in the upper limb than the lower limb\\n\\nWhich spinal cord injury explains this presentation?\",\"lead\":\"Which spinal cord injury explains this presentation?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Complete spinal cord injury\",\"e\":\"In contrast with all the above, a complete spinal cord injury would feature a complete loss of sensory and motor function below the level of lesion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anterior cord syndrome\",\"e\":\"Anterior cord syndrome is generally caused by ischaemic injury to the anterior spinal injury and is characterised by paraplegia below the level of the lesion (due to corticospinal tract involvement) and a dissociated sensory loss with loss of pain and temperature sensation (due to lateral spinothalamic tract involvement). Position, vibration and deep pressure sensations, all functions of the posterior column, are preserved. Anterior cord syndrome has the poorest prognosis of all incomplete spinal injuries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Brown-Séquard syndrome\",\"e\":\"Brown-Séquard syndrome, resulting from hemisection of the cord, usually causes ipsilateral motor loss and contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation, there is also associated loss of position sense.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Central cord syndrome\",\"e\":\"Central cord syndrome typically occurs following cervical hyperextension injury, in which anterior and posterior cord compression can lead to oedema, haemorrhage or ischaemia. It is thought to be a result of vascular compromise of the cord in the distribution of the anterior spinal artery. Infarction of the cord in the territory of this artery could also result in anterior cord syndrome. Central cord syndrome is characterised by a disproportionately greater loss of motor power in the upper extremities than in the lower extremities, with varying degrees of sensory loss. The arms and hands are most severely affected as the motor fibres to the cervical segments are topographically arranged towards the centre of the cord.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior cord syndrome\",\"e\":\"Posterior cord syndrome is a rare, incomplete lesion with primary damage to the posterior cord, featuring preservation of motor function, pain and temperature sensations with loss of proprioception and fine touch sensation below the level of the lesion. It can occur secondarily to a posterior spinal artery infarct..\\n\\n420\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"The patellar reflex is a monosynaptic deep tendon reflex arc that helps maintain posture and balance. Normally it can be easily elicited with a tendon hammer.\\n\\nAn absent knee reflex is most likely to be due to which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"An absent knee reflex is most likely to be due to which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dorsal column demyelination\",\"e\":\"Dorsal column lesions only affect central sensory processing as collaterals subserving spinal reflexes are preserved.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral nerve transection\",\"e\":\"The patellar reflex is mediated by the femoral nerve formed from the posterior divisions of the L2-L4 anterior spinal rami and is, therefore, lost after femoral nerve and L2-L4 dorsal root damage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"L1-L2 disc prolapse\",\"e\":\"L1-L2 disc prolapse is too high to cause absent patellar reflex: With L2 prolapse the reflex may be slightly diminished, but should not be absent.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"S2-S4 dorsal nerve root avulsions\",\"e\":\"The patellar reflex synapses in the L2-4 anterior spinal rami, and as such the S2-4 nerve roots, are too low to affect the patellar reflex. Furthermore, the anterior nerve roots would need to be affected, not the dorsal (or posterior) nerve roots.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T12 level cord transection\",\"e\":\"Although an acute T12 level cord transection may result in a transient loss of reflexes, T12 cord lesions typically result in an upper motor neurone lesion with exaggerated reflexes.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man had poliomyelitis as a child that left him with total paralysis of the left deltoid muscle. Which feature is most likely to be present on clinical examination?\",\"lead\":\"Which feature is most likely to be present on clinical examination?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anaesthesia over the superior surface of the left shoulder\",\"e\":\"This would be as a result of damage to the supraclavicular nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The acromion process of the scapula forms the most lateral bony landmark of the left shoulder\",\"e\":\"The most lateral bony landmark of the shoulder is the greater tubercle of the head of the numerus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Drooping of the left shoulder compared to the right side\",\"e\":\"Drooping of the shoulder would be due to damage to the accessory nerve, causing weakness in the trapezius muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Detectable weakness flexing the shoulder and internally rotating the shoulder when this is compared with the right side\",\"e\":\"The deltoid is the primary abductor of the arm. Even if the supraspinatus is fully functional, it is far too weak a muscle to be able to abduct the whole weight of the arm. The deltoid, in addition to being the powerful abductor of the humerus, also assists in flexion/extension and medial/lateral rotation of the shoulder. Weakness of these movements compared with the normal side can be detected on careful examination.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Full abduction of the shoulder can be achieved by the action of the intact supraspinatus muscle on the left side\",\"e\":\"The supraspinatus muscle is far too weak to fully abduct the whole arm. It can initiate the first 10-15° of abduction.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"A patient attends the clinic complaining of pins and needles affecting the radial (lateral) three digits of his hand. The symptoms are worse at night and also after driving long distances.\\n\\nWhich one of the following motor signs would confirm the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following motor signs would confirm the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inability to flex the radial three fingers\",\"e\":\"Flexion of the radial three fingers would not be affected as the nerve supply to the long flexors comes from the median nerve proximal to the carpal tunnel. The lateral terminal branch of the median nerve (which passes through the carpal tunnel) innervates the flexor pollicis brevis, however this is responsible for flexion of the thumb, whereas the question refers to flexion of the fingers.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inability to adduct all the fingers of his hand\",\"e\":\"Abduction and adduction of the fingers is effected by the interossei, which are supplied by the ulnar nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inability to abduct all the fingers of his hand\",\"e\":\"Abduction of the fingers is effected by the interossei, which are supplied by the ulnar nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Weakness of opposition of the thumb\",\"e\":\"The history described above is typical of carpal tunnel syndrome. Entrapment of the median nerve at the carpal tunnel affects the muscles of the thenar eminence. These are the abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis and opponens pollicis. The nerve supply of the flexor pollicis brevis is extremely variable however, so the best test to perform is assessment of opposition. In addition, the motor branch of the median nerve after the level of the carpal tunnel also innervates the radial two lumbricals.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The likely diagnosis would not cause a motor deficit\",\"e\":\"The median nerve supplies muscles within the thenar eminence including the abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis and opponens pollicis and so there would be a motor deficit.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old woman presents to your breast surgery clinic with progressive tingling and numbness affecting the posterior aspect of her right arm and forearm and dorsolateral aspect of her hand. She also has a wrist drop. She had previously fallen asleep with her arm draped over her chair and noticed the numbness when she awoke.\\n\\nThe nerve most likely to be affected by this mechanism of injury is supplied by which nerve roots?\",\"lead\":\"The nerve most likely to be affected by this mechanism of injury is supplied by which nerve roots?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"C8, T1\",\"e\":\"C8 and T1 form the inferior trunk of the brachial plexus, however, the radial nerve takes its supply from the entire brachial plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"C5, 6, 7\",\"e\":\"The brachial plexus, which supplies the radial nerve, is formed from C5-8 and T1 nerve roots.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"C7, 8 and T1\",\"e\":\"This would only form the middle and inferior trunk of the brachial plexus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"C5, 6, 7, 8 and T1\",\"e\":\"The brachial plexus is located partly in the neck and partly in the axilla. It is formed by rami, trunks, divisions, cords, and their branches. The supraclavicular part is located in the posterior triangle of the neck, and the infraclavicular part in the axilla. The brachial plexus is formed by the union of the ventral rami of nerves C5-C8 and the greater part of the T1 ventral ramus. C5 and C6 form the superior trunk, C7 the middle trunk, and C8 and T1 the inferior trunk. The anterior divisions supply the flexor parts of the upper limb and the posterior divisions the extensor parts. This clinical palsy is often described as 'Saturday night palsy' and is consequent to damage to the radial nerve. Clinically, this produces a wrist drop and numbness in the dorsum of the hand and wrist. Numbness of the posterior aspect of the arm and forearm may also be seen.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"C4, 5\",\"e\":\"C4 does not form part of the brachial plexus\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old woman has presented to your clinic at almost 4 years following her right- sided mastectomy and axillary clearance. She had been transferred to your care from another hospital due to a change in address. On routine examination you note that she a winged right scapula:\\n\\nWhich nerve roots are responsible for the likely pathology?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve roots are responsible for the likely pathology?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"C7, 8\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve is supplied by nerve roots C5, 6 and 7.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"C5,6\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve is supplied by C7 as well as C5 and 6.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"C6, 7, 8 and T1\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve is not supplied by C8 or T1.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"C7, 8 and T1\",\"e\":\"The long thoracic nerve is supplied by C5, 6, and 7, not C8 or T1.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"C5, 6,7\",\"e\":\"An injury to the long thoracic nerve (C5, 6, 7), which can occur during weight lifting, thoracic surgery, or during an axillary lymph node clearance during a mastectomy, can result in paralysis of the serratus anterior muscle and 'winging' of the scapula. The nerve usually passes along the chest wall in the fascia over the serratus anterior, le in the medial wail of the axilla. Patients usually experience minimal symptoms of posterior shoulder or scapular pain, which generally needs no further treatment.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"You have been asked to see a young man in the Emergency Department who has been admitted following a fall out of a tree. To break his fall, he catches hold of a branch on the way down, jarring his shoulder. On presentation he shows signs of Klumpke's paralysis.\\n\\nHow would you describe this man's neurological presentation?\",\"lead\":\"How would you describe this man's neurological presentation?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"You have been asked to see a young man in the Emergency Department who has been admitted following a fall out of a tree. To break his fall, he catches hold of a branch on the way down, jarring his shoulder. On presentation he shows signs of Klumpke's paralysis.\\n\\nHow would you describe this man's neurological presentation?\\nExplanation be\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It may be associated with Horner's syndrome and pupillary dilation on the ipsilateral side\",\"e\":\"If there is T1 involvement, Horner's syndrome may be present with pupillary constriction (miosis), not dilation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is often associated with eyelid retraction\",\"e\":\"Eyelid retraction is not seen, instead ptosis is often present.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The motor defect includes paralysis of the wrist extensors\",\"e\":\"Wrist flexors, not extensors may be affected with Klumpke's syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The classic presentation is one of a claw hand\",\"e\":\"Klumpke's syndrome is a partial palsy of the lower roots of the brachial plexus (C8 and T1) after or before they have joined to form the lower trunk. This is a rare condition and has a variable recovery time and some may never resolve (especially following birth injuries).\\n\\nThe paralysis affects the intrinsic muscles of the hand (notably the interossei, thenar and hypothenar muscles). The clinical presentation often described is that of 'claw hand':\\n\\n• the wrist is held in extreme extension, as the wrist extensors are acting unopposed • hyperextension at the MCPJs is present due to the loss of the hand intrinsics • flexion at the IPJs is present due to the loss of hand intrinsics.\\n\\nForearm pronators and wrist flexors may also be affected. The clinical picture is the claw hand where the forearm is supinated and the wrist and fingers are flexed. The sensory deficit is in the ulnar nerve distribution and a Horner's syndrome with ptosis and miosis (pupillary constriction) may also be seen due to T1 involvement.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The sensory deficit is in the distribution of the median nerve\",\"e\":\"Sensory deficit is seen in the distribution of the ulnar, not median nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 32-week pregnant woman presents to your clinic with disabling tingling in the thumb, index, middle and ring fingers of her dominant hand. She is dropping light items and cannot grip a cup of tea.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements best represents her likely pathology?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements best represents her likely pathology?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Symptoms are a recognised complication of osteoarthritis\",\"e\":\"Rheumatoid arthritis is more commonly associated with carpal tunnel syndrome due to the associated synovial inflammation seen with rheumatoid disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Symptoms are frequently caused by a cervical rib\",\"e\":\"Cervical rib, as well as cervical spondylosis, could be included in the differential diagnosis for this presentation, however, this patient only has symptoms distal to the wrist, these other differentials would produce symptoms and sign proximal to the wrist.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The abductor pollicis brevis will be spared\",\"e\":\"The median nerve supplies motor fibres to the abductor policies brevis muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The diagnosis can be made with confidence on review of nerve conduction studies\",\"e\":\"The likely diagnosis is carpal tunnel syndrome. The causes of carpal tunnel include pregnancy, rheumatoid arthritis, hypothyroidism, and fractures in the region of the wrist. The syndrome results from compression of the median nerve in the tunnel under the flexor retinaculum and if conservative measures fail, treatment is by surgical division of the retinaculum. Nerve conduction studies will demonstrate a reduced conduction velocity across flexor retinaculum in the median nerve indicating compression given the clinical history. Conduction studies of the radial and ulnar nerve will be normal. Importantly, while nerve conduction can confirm the diagnosis often in primary care the diagnosis is clinical.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Treatment is always by division of the extensor retinaculum\",\"e\":\"Treatment options for carpal tunnel syndrome include conservative measures, such as physiotherapy, corticosteroid injections and subsequently surgery if conservative measures fail. Surgery involves division of the flexor, not extensor, retinaculum.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old woman is thrown from a horse she is riding and lands heavily on her left shoulder. She has no other injuries but reports difficulty in moving her left shoulder and elbow. On examination she is unable to abduct the left shoulder or flex the elbow, and the shoulder is medially rotated with the palm facing posterior. Which one of the following best describe this clinical pattern?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describe this clinical pattern?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Claw hand\",\"e\":\"Claw hand is more commonly seen with Klumpke's palsy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Erb's palsy\",\"e\":\"Most brachial plexus injuries are due to severe traction, such as the trauma described in this scenario, however Erb's palsy is most commonly in neonates following traumatic birth injuries. Of note, fractures of the clavicle rarely damage the plexus. An Erb's palsy is caused by damage to C5, C6 and sometimes C7 roots. In an injury to the superior trunk of the brachial plexus the characteristic portion of the limb is a waiter's tip position' in which there is weakness in shoulder abduction, external rotation, elbow flexion and supination. This leads to the limb being held in the described position.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Klumpke's palsy\",\"e\":\"A Klumpke's palsy is due to damage to C8 andTl, and gives rise to weak wrist and finger flexors, intrinsic hand muscles are paralysed with loss of sensation in the ulnar forearm and hand. There may be an associated Horner's syndrome. This palsy is most commonly encountered in neonates following traumatic birth injuries:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Horner's syndrome\",\"e\":\"Horner's syndrome occurs due to a deficiency of sympathetic activity and develops symptoms on the ipsilateral side to the lesion. The characteristic features are ptosis miosis, anhidrosis and enophthalmos.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sudeck's atrophy\",\"e\":\"Sudeck's atrophy is another name for type 1 complex regional pain syndrome\\n\\n552\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old rugby player has undergone surgery for a comminuted proximal humerus fracture in which it is noted anatomical reduction was very difficult. Six months following surgery it is noted he has weakness of elbow flexion and altered sensation over the lateral aspects of his forearm. No other areas of weakness or sensory deficit are noted Which one of the following nerves is most likely to be injured?\",\"lead\":\"No other areas of weakness or sensory deficit are noted Which one of the following nerves is most likely to be injured?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Axillary\",\"e\":\"The axillary nerve can be damaged following anterior-inferior dislocations of the shoulder and fractures of the surgical neck of humerus, however this would present with weakness in shoulder abduction and weak flexion, extension, and rotation of the shoulder.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Median\",\"e\":\"By far the most common cause of median nerve palsy is carpal tunnel syndrome, however supracondylar elbow fractures can also cause a median nerve palsy. It is rarely injured at the level of the proximal humerus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Musculocutaneous\",\"e\":\"The musculocutaneous nerve supplies the coracobrachialis, biceps brachii and brachialis muscles. It pierces the deep fascia just proximal to the elbow joint and becomes superficial. It is then called the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm, supplying skin on the lateral aspect of the forearm. It is probable that, due to the difficult reduction of the fracture intraoperatively, the musculocutaneous nerve has been damaged\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Radial\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve innervates the posterior compartment of the upper arm and forearm. A radial nerve palsy would present with weakness in forearm extension, loss of extension of the fingers and wrist drop. These features are not described above, moreover the radial nerve is more commonly injured following a mid-shaft humeral fracture.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulnar\",\"e\":\"The unar nerve is more commonly injured at the elbow and would present with weakness in flexion of the hand at the wrist and a claw hand deformity at rest.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"A 24-year-old soldier sustained a gunshot wound to the right proximal thigh. Neurological examination revealed he had an absent knee jerk reflex, numbness over the medial and anterior thigh, and medial malleolus. The cremasteric reflex is intact\\n\\nWhich is the most likely nerve to have been injured?\",\"lead\":\"Which is the most likely nerve to have been injured?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"Femoral nerve injury is rare and usually secondary to stab or gunshot wounds. Motor fibres supply the quadriceps femoris muscle, the contracting fibres of which elicit the knee jerk. The nerve supplies the L2-L4 dermatomes over the anterior skin of the thigh. The genitofemoral nerve mediates the cremasteric reflex:\\n\\nThe genitofemoral nerve supplies the L2 dermatome and damage to this nerve would not impact on the knee jerk reflex.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Genitofemoral nerve\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh\",\"e\":\"The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies the L2 dermatome, but again damage to this nerve would not impact on the knee jerk reflex.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"L2 nerve root\",\"e\":\"Given the anatomical location of the gunshot wound, damage to the L2 nerve root is unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Saphenous nerve\",\"e\":\"The saphenous nerve (L4), a branch of the femoral nerve, innervates the skin over the medial malleolus, however an isolated saphenous nerve injury would not affect the knee jerk reflex as it is distal to the femoral nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old man with a long standing history of back pain presents acutely with shooting pains to the outer aspect of his right foot, reduced sensation to the sole of the right foot, weakness of plantar flexion of the right ankle and an absent ankle jerk reflex on the right side. There is no bladder or bowel incontinence.\\n\\nWhich nerve root is most likely to be compressed?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve root is most likely to be compressed?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cauda equina\",\"e\":\"As this is a unilateral problem and there is no evidence of bowel or bladder incontinence then there is no evidence of cauda equina\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left S1 nerve root\",\"e\":\"The symptoms and signs described are present on the right, not the left. Decussation takes place in the spinal cord, therefore signs and symptoms are present are ipsilateral to the affected nerve root.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Right L1 nerve root\",\"e\":\"Compression of the L1 nerve root would produce pain in the groin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right L5 nerve root\",\"e\":\"Compression of the L5 nerve root may present with pain in a similar distribution to that of S1 compression, however, in L5 compression the great toe and medial border of the foot is affected, not the lateral aspect.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right S1 nerve root\",\"e\":\"Signs of S1 nerve root compression include reduced sensation in the 51 dermatome (sole of the foot), weakness of plantar flexion of the ankle, and absent or reduced ankle jerk.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"You are in the neurosurgical clinic reviewing a patient who has had a left temporal lobe tumour resected and now has a suspected abscess. He has raised inflammatory markers, fever and headache.\\n\\nA dominant hemisphere temporal lobe abscess is most likely to cause which symptom?\",\"lead\":\"A dominant hemisphere temporal lobe abscess is most likely to cause which symptom?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Motor disturbance in the contralateral arm\",\"e\":\"The primary motor cortex is located at the posterior border of the frontal lobe, given this patient's abscess is located in the temporal lobe, motor function is likely to be preserved.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Problems with balance\",\"e\":\"Issues with balance are more likely to occur with disorders of the cerebellum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Problems with speech (dysphasia)\",\"e\":\"Cerebral abscesses act as space-occupying lesions and produce focal neurological deficits. They may also cause meningitis, epilepsy and intracranial herniation. Dysphasia occurs with dominant (most commonly left) temporal lobe lesions. The 'important' cortical areas of the temporal lobe area are the auditory cortex and temporal association area (responsible for the recognition of auditory stimuli and integration with other modalities)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sensory disturbance in the contralateral leg\",\"e\":\"The sensory cortex is located in the parietal lobe and is unlikely to be affected by temporal lobe pathology.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inferior homonymous quadrantanopia\",\"e\":\"A temporal lobe lesion would produce an upper homonymous quadrantanopia, while a parietal lobe lesion would cause an inferior homonymous quadrantanopia.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":73,\"stem\":\"Following a left carotid endarterectomy a 74-year-old patient is noted to have developed speech problems. He seems able to understand what is said but unable to get the words out.\\n\\nWhich area of the brain is most likely to be affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which area of the brain is most likely to be affected?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Basal ganglia\",\"e\":\"The putamen and globus pallidus are together known as the lentiform nucleus and form part of the basal ganglia. Damage to the basal ganglia produces movement disorders.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Broca's area\",\"e\":\"The primary motor cortex is in the precentral gyrus and the primary sensory cortex in the postcentral cortex. Broca's area lies in the posterior part of the inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere. Lesions to Broca's area cause expressive dysphasia, classically described as knowing what to say, but being unable to get the words out.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Occipital cortex\",\"e\":\"The occipital cortex functions as the primary visual cortex, and there are no visual disturbances described in the case history\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Oractory cortex 21\",\"e\":\"The olfactory impulses travel to the temporal lobe in the region of the uncus, and no olfactory disturbances are described.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wernicke's area\",\"e\":\"The Wernicke's area is tasked with the comprehension of speech, and damage to this area would result in a receptive dysphasia, however, this man has an expressive dysphasia suggesting a damage to Broca's area.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":76,\"stem\":\"Different areas of the brain are responsible for different functions. The presenting neurological symptoms and signs a patient may have can point towards where the lesion is. Lesions in the occipital area of the brain may cause which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Lesions in the occipital area of the brain may cause which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Personality changes\",\"e\":\"Lesions of the frontal lobe are associated with personality changes, urinary incontinence and mono or hemiparesis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"MAAU Hemiparesis\",\"e\":\"Hemiparesis occurs as a result of damage to the primary motor cortex which is located in the frontal lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Non-fluent aphasia\",\"e\":\"Non-fluent aphasia occurs in lesions of the left frontal lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Visual field defects\",\"e\":\"Visual field defects are usually associated with lesions in the occipital region of the brain.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urinary incontinence\",\"e\":\"Damage to the frontal lobe can result in urinary incontinence, probably the best example of this is frontotemporal dementia which often presents with significant cognitive impairment and incontinence.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":78,\"stem\":\"You are asked to see a patient with new onset neurological deficits. By the time you go down to assess them they seem to be markedly improving compared to the documentation by the A&E SHO. Which of the following is the most common cause of a transient ischaemic attack?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most common cause of a transient ischaemic attack?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Thrombophilia\",\"e\":\"Thrombophilia is less common than atherosclerosis of the carotid arteries as a cause of transient ischaemic attacks (TIA)\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Atrial fibrillation\",\"e\":\"Anticoagulation is utilised to reduce the risk of TIA and stroke in atrial fibrillation due to the increased risk of thromboembolism, however atherosclerosis remains a more common cause for TIA.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Myocardial infarction\",\"e\":\"Myocardial infarction may result in the production of mural thrombus, which may embolise through the arterial tree and occlude the cerebral circulation, however, atherosclerosis of the carotid artery is a more common cause.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Paradoxical embolus\",\"e\":\"Atherosclerosis of the carotid artery is a more common cause of a transient ischaemic attack (TIA).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Atherosclerosis of the carotid bifurcation\",\"e\":\"Although all the above may give rise to embolisation within the arterial tree travelling to the cerebral circulation and presenting as a transient ischaemic attack, a 70% stenosis of the carotid bifurcation or proximal internal carotid artery is by far the commonest cause. Ultrasound imaging of the carotid arteries following a TIA is important, and carotid endarterectomy may be indicated in some patients. Secondary prevention is also important with medications to control blood pressure and cholesterol.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":81,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted with new onset neurological signs. A computed tomography (CT) head demonstrates a posterior circulation stroke\\n\\nCerebellar lesions produce which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Cerebellar lesions produce which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Waddling gait\",\"e\":\"Weakness of proximal lower limb muscles (eg in polymyositis or muscular dystrophy) leads to difficulty in rising from sitting or squatting. Once upright, the patient walks with a waddling gait, as each lower limb, as it carries the full weight of the body, does not adequately support the pelvis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Festinant gait\",\"e\":\"Festinant, or hurried gait occurs in Parkinson's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ataxic gait\",\"e\":\"In disease of the lateral cerebellar lobes, the stance becomes broad based, unstable and tremulous ie ataxic. The gait tends to veer towards the side of the more affected cerebellar lobe. The underlying causes of cerebellar ataxia are extensive but can include; multiple sclerosis, infection, infarction, vasculitis and autoimmune conditions.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Scissors gait\",\"e\":\"Spasticity causes stiffness and jerkiness while walking - scissors gait.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"High-stepping gait\",\"e\":\"Broad-based, high stepping or stamping gait develops in peripheral sensory lesions (eg polyneuropathy) when there is loss of proprioception.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man, with a two year history of back pain presents with worsening symptoms: He has been referred to the acute spinal service for urgent assessment.\\n\\nWhich one of the following signs is most suggestive of acute cauda equina syndrome?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following signs is most suggestive of acute cauda equina syndrome?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Muscle wasting\",\"e\":\"Muscle wasting suggests chronic cauda equina syndrome (CES), this question is specifically discussing acute CES.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperactive lower limb reflexes\",\"e\":\"Hyperactive lower limb reflexes and a positive Babinski sign are upper motor neurone signs and therefore make the diagnosis of CES unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Loss of sensation to the lateral aspect of the foot\",\"e\":\"Although sensory changes may be noted in acute cauda equina syndrome this affects the saddle area (S3-S5). Loss of sensation to the lateral foot would implicate S1-S2.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Loss of perianal tone\",\"e\":\"CES is caused when there is compression of the lumbosacral nerves below the level of the spinal cord. It typically presents with low back pain, unilateral or bilateral sciatica, bladder or bowel dysfunction and perianal sensory loss or hypoaesthesia. Loss of perianal tone is characteristic of CES.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Positive Babinski sign\",\"e\":\"A positive Babinski is an upper motor neurone sign and therefore makes the diagnosis of CES unlikely\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":85,\"stem\":\"A 53-year-old man who suffers from long standing alcoholism has had a history of multiple falls over the last 2 months. He complains of a progressive headache. A computed tomography (CT) scan does not reveal any skull fractures\\n\\nWhich other findings is this patient most likely to have?\",\"lead\":\"Which other findings is this patient most likely to have?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Air in the cranial cavity\",\"e\":\"Air in the cranial cavity would suggest a significant skull fracture and the CT scan has ruled this out.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anosmia\",\"e\":\"Anosmia is associated with acute subdural haematoma and traumatic brain injury.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gait disturbance\",\"e\":\"Chron c subdural haematomas are most commonly found in the older age groups. Chronic alcohol ingestion is also an associated factor due to the frequent incidence of falls. Chronic subdural haematomas are collections of altered blood and are most frequently triggered by minor trauma: The symptoms include progressive headache, gait disturbance, failing intellect, hemiparesis and a fluctuating conscious level.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Meningitis\",\"e\":\"Meningitis is an acute presentation with worsening headache, neck stiffness and photophobia. If not treated early and aggressively is associated with high mortality and morbidity.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rhinorrhoea\",\"e\":\"Acute subdural haematoma is more commonly associated with anosmia and rhinorrhoea as there is usually a concomitant skull fracture.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":88,\"stem\":\"A 33-year-old man involved in an altercation is brought to the Emergency Department and is diagnosed with an extradural haematoma. His GCS has remained stable at 14 since admission and his case has been referred urgently to the local neurosurgical centre.\\n\\nWhich anatomical structures are most likely to limit the spread of the haematoma?\",\"lead\":\"Which anatomical structures are most likely to limit the spread of the haematoma?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Intracranial venous sinuses\",\"e\":\"Due to the arterial nature of an extradural haematoma, the venous sinuses with their much lower blood pressure will not limit an expanding haematoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The falciform fold\",\"e\":\"The falciform fold is a congenital fold from the disc to the ciliary region of the retina and is unlikely to play any role in limiting the expansion of an extradural haematoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Suture lines\",\"e\":\"Extradural haematoma is associated with fracture and is usually the result of damage to the anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery. The dura has strong attachments to the cranium along the suture lines and as a result, these attachments can limit the extent of the haematoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tentorium cerebelli\",\"e\":\"The tentorium cerebelli is an extension of the dura mata separating the inferior parietal lobes from the cerebellum. An expanding haematoma supratentorially is not going to be limited by the tentorium cerebelli, indeed, by the time the tentorium cerebelli limits the expansion uncal herniation and compression of the brainstem will have already occurred.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The ventricular space\",\"e\":\"The ventricular space in an extradural haematoma will compress allowing expansion, not limitation, of the haematoma\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":91,\"stem\":\"A 16-year-old girl is admitted to the Emergency Department having fallen and injured her head while out with friends. She is clearly intoxicated. Her friends say she passed out, and the patient does not recall what happened.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements reflects NICE guidelines regarding the management of head injuries?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements reflects NICE guidelines regarding the management of head injuries?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"All patients who have had loss of consciousness (LOC) must be admitled\",\"e\":\"The following patients meet the criteria for admission to hospital following a head injury:\\n\\n• Patients with new, clinically significant abnormalities on imaging. • Patients who have not returned to GCS 15 after imaging, regardless of the imaging results. • When a patient fulfils the criteria for computed tomography (CT) scanning, but this cannot be done within the appropriate period, either because CT is not available or because the patient is not sufficiently co-operative to allow scanning. • Continuing worrying signs of concern to the clinician (for example, persistent vomiting, severe headaches) • Other sources of concern to the clinician (eg drug or alcohol intoxication, other injuries, shock, suspected non-accidental injury, meningism, cerebrospinal fluid leak).\\n\\nAs such, not all patients who have experienced a loss of consciousness will require admission.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"All patients who have had LOC need a CT scan\",\"e\":\"A single episode of loss of consciousness is common following a head injury and does not warrant CT scanning according to NICE guidelines.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"All intoxicated patients with LOC must be admitted\",\"e\":\"An intoxicated patient may warrant admission for observation following a head injury only if there is significant concern from the assessing clinician, however, this may mean some patients will not require admission.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"All patients with a Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) ‹ 13 at any point since the injury should have aCT scan\",\"e\":\"The following reflects the NICE guidelines for head injuries and their management (CG176 January 2014). Patients who have sustained a head injury and present with any one of the following risk factors should have computed tomography (CT) scanning of the head immediately requested\\n\\n• Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) less than 13 on initial assessment in the Emergency Department • GCS less than 15 at 2 h after the injury • suspected open or depressed skull fracture • any sign of basal skull fracture (haemotympanum, 'panda' eyes, cerebrospinal fluid, otorrhoea, Battle's sign) • post-traumatic seizure • focal neurological deficit • more than one episode of vomiting • for head injuries in children who have none of the above but more than one of the following • loss of consciousness lasting more than 5 mins (witnessed) • abnormal drowsiness • dangerous mechanism of injury eg traffic accident at high speed • amnesia (antegrade or retrograde lasting more than 5 mins (this is not possible in preverbal children and very difficult in any child under 5).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"All patients with continuing symptoms such as nausea or vomiting, and brief lapses in memory can go home provided they are accompanied and can be observed by a responsible adult\",\"e\":\"The following patients meet the criteria for admission to hospital following a head injury:\\n\\n• Patients with new, clinically significant abnormalities on imaging • Patients who have not returned to GCS 15 after imaging, regardless of the imaging results. • When a patient fulfils the criteria for CT scanning but this cannot be done within the appropriate period, either because CT is not available or because the patient is not sufficiently co-operative to allow scanning. • Continuing worrying signs of concern to the clinician (for example, persistent vomiting, severe headaches) • Other sources of concern to the clinician (eg drug or alcohol intoxication, other injuries, shock, suspected non-accidental injury, meningism, cerebrospinal fluid leak).\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":95,\"stem\":\"A paraganglioma is a rare neuroendocrine neoplasm which presents as a painless mass. You are discussing this diagnosis with a patient in clinic\\n\\nWhich one of the following options is a correct description of a paraganglioma?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is a correct description of a paraganglioma?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are always secretory\",\"e\":\"Paragangliomas are non-secretory approximately 50% of the time; producing catecholamines.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They arise from non-chromaffin paraganglionic cells and commonly metastasise\",\"e\":\"They arise from non-chromaffin paraganglionic cells and rarely metastasise.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They arise from preganglionic chromaffin cells derived from the neural crest\",\"e\":\"They arise from non-chromaffin paraganglionic cells, not preganglionic chromaffin cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They may be associated with pheochromocytomas\",\"e\":\"Paragangliomas are carotid body tumours which arise from nests of non-chromaffin paraganglionic cells derived from the neural crest. There is a high incidence in the people of Peru (altitude 2000-5000 m), Mexico City and Colorado. In addition to bilateral tumours and associated phaeochromocytomas there is a strong family tendency.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They only occur in the parasympathetic nervous system\",\"e\":\"They can be seen in both the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous system.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":97,\"stem\":\"A patient is referred to the general surgical clinic with excessive sweating. She is considered for operative intervention.\\n\\nRegarding hyperhidrosis, which one of the following options is true?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding hyperhidrosis, which one of the following options is true?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"If affecting the axilla it may be treated with thoracoscopic ablation of the T1 and T2 ganglia\",\"e\":\"The first (T1) thoracic ganglion controls facial sweating, the second (T2) and third (T3) thoracic ganglia are responsible for palmar sweating\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"If affecting the palms it may be treated with thoracoscopic ablation of the T4 and T5 ganglia\",\"e\":\"The fourth (T4) thoracic ganglion controls axillary sweating.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It may be treated with intra-dermal Botox injections\",\"e\":\"Patients most often complain of excessive sweating of the palms, soles and axillae. Topical rather than oral aluminium chloride may be used as well as intra-dermal Botox injections:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It may be treated with oral 20% aluminium chloride hexahydrate\",\"e\":\"Topical rather than oral aluminium should be used to reduce systemic side-effects.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It most commonly affects the chest\",\"e\":\"The most commonly affected areas are the palms, soles of the feet and axillae, not the chest.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":99,\"stem\":\"You are consenting a patient for thoracoscopic sympathectomy. They have high expectations for a successful procedure.\\n\\nWhich one of these complications is the most common reason for dissatisfaction following this operation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these complications is the most common reason for dissatisfaction following this operation?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bradycardia\",\"e\":\"Thoracoscopic sympathectomy can cause bradycardia but this not commonly symptomatic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Compensatory sweating (hyperhidrosis affecting previously uninvolved areas of the body)\",\"e\":\"The cause of compensatory and gustatory sweating remains an enigma and it is important to make this possibility clear to the patient before operation. It should be noted that this is a controversial procedure and outcomes are not predictable and extremely variable.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Horner syndrome\",\"e\":\"Horner syndrome results if the stellate ganglion is accidentally injured, however, this is uncommon.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intercostal neuralgia\",\"e\":\"Intercostal neuralgia is not a common post operatively.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"As the pleural cavity is entered during this procedure a small pneumothorax does occur. however, this will usually resolve without intervention following the operation and is not a common cause for dissatisfaction.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":101,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient with a pituitary tumour, which is affecting the posterior portion of the gland on CT scans. They are beginning to develop systemic manifestations of the disease.\\n\\nSecretion of which hormone is most likely to be affected by this tumour?\",\"lead\":\"Secretion of which hormone is most likely to be affected by this tumour?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)\",\"e\":\"The anterior pituitary secretes adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), growth hormone, thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), prolactin and LH. The secretion of these is governed by releasing and inhibitory factors released into the pituitary portal system. The hypothalamus regulates levels of these specific factors by monitoring circulating levels of hormones produced by the target organ. For example - levels of FSH and luteinizing hormone (LH) are controlled by luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone (LHRH) from the hypothalamus in response to circulating levels of ostrogens and androgens.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Follicle stimulating hormone (FSH)\",\"e\":\"As discussed, FSH is secreted by the anterior pituitary.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oxytocin\",\"e\":\"The posterior pituitary secretes oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (ADH) into the bioodstream. Oxytocin stimulates lactation in response to suckling.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prolactin\",\"e\":\"Prolactin is secreted by the anterior pituitary.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)\",\"e\":\"TSH is secreted by the anterior pituitary. \\\"FO¿\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":104,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old man with a long-standing history of alcohol abuse was admitted to hospital because he was difficult to rouse. On examination, he is confused and ataxic. Examination of the eyes reveals nystagmus and ophthalmoplegia.\\n\\nDeficiency of which vitamin is most likely to have caused his symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"Deficiency of which vitamin is most likely to have caused his symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Vitamin A\",\"e\":\"Vitamin A deficiency is a common cause of night blindness in the developing world but is rare in the developed world. The case history is not consistent with vitamin A deficiency:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Vitamin B,\",\"e\":\"A common cause of the amnesic syndrome is the Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome resulting from thiamine (vitamin B.) deficiency in association with chronic alcoholism or, occasionally, malnutrition or malabsorption. The Wernicke phase of this disorder is characterised by confusion, nystagmus, abducent and conjugate gaze palsies (ophthalmoplegia) as well as ataxia. These features are commonly accompanied by peripheral neuropathy. Prompt treatment with thiamine replacement is vital to avert a chronic and disabling amnesic disorder (the Korsakoff syndrome).\\n\\nAgain, while chronic alcoholism is a risk for micro-nutrient and vitamin deficiencies the case history described is that of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Vitamin Bs\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vitamin B12\",\"e\":\"Vitamin Big deficiency most commonly results in macrocytic anaemia, rarely, neurological complications can occur. Interestingly, chronic alcoholism can cause a mild macrocytosis even in the absence of Bi deficiency.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Folic acid\",\"e\":\"Folate deficiency is accelerated by alcoholism and clinical presentation can be varied, however, it would not be associated with nystagmus or ophthalmoplegia.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"You are in the Emergency Department reviewing an elderly male patient with sudden onset left arm weakness and upper motor neuron signs which resolved over a few minutes. He has a bruit over his left carotid on careful examination.\\n\\nWhich one of the following regarding transient ischaemic attack (TIA) is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regarding transient ischaemic attack (TIA) is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increases the risk of future stroke\",\"e\":\"The risk is of further stroke is increased and brain imaging, ideally a magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan and an urgent carotid Doppier test are needed.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An indication for carotid endarterectomy if associated with a less than 70% stenosis of the appropriate internal carotid artery\",\"e\":\"Carotid endarterectomy is indicated if there is a stenosis of greater than 70%.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Any neurological deficit lasting more than 2\",\"e\":\"A TIA is a focal neurological deficit without acute infarction or permanent tissue injury. Operationally, it is considered to last less than 24 h.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is best treated with antiplatelet agents only\",\"e\":\"A combination of antiplatelet therapy and endarterectomy may be indicated depending on the severity of the stenosis and whether it corresponds to the clinical syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Most commonly results from cerebral hypo-perfusion secondary to a very tight stenosis in the carotid artery\",\"e\":\"When in the area of the carotid artery it is most commonly due to embolisation from atheromatous plaque at the carotid bifurcation.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":109,\"stem\":\"A middle-aged patient is admitted to A&E resus and the acute stroke team are asked to assess them urgently. They have developed dysarthria, nystagmus and a tremor that worsens with directed movement.\\n\\nThis patient most probably has which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This patient most probably has which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cerebellar disease\",\"e\":\"The patient in this description has cerebellar tremor as suggested by the signs and symptoms. Cerebellar tremor is a slow, broad tremor of the extremities that occurs at the end of a purposeful movement, such as trying to press a button or touching a finger to the tip of one's nose. Cerebellar tremor is caused by lesions in or damage to the cerebellum resulting from stroke, tumour or disease such as multiple sclerosis or some inherited degenerative disorder. It can also result from chronic alcoholism or overuse of some medicines. In classic cerebellar tremor, a lesion on one side of the brain produces a tremor in that same side of the body that worsens with directed movement. Cerebellar damage can also produce a 'wing-beating' type of tremor called rubral or Holmes' tremor - a combination of rest, action and postural tremors. The tremor is often most prominent when the affected person is active or is maintaining a particular posture. Cerebellar tremor may be accompanied by dysarthria (speech problems), nystagmus (rapid, involuntary rolling of the eyes), gait problems and postural tremor of the trunk and neck.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Damage to pontine and caudate nuclei\",\"e\":\"Damage to the pontine and caudate nuclei would cause a resting tremor that lessens on voluntary movement, which is not the case described. It is also unlikely to be associated with nystagmus and dysarthria.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyperthyroidism\",\"e\":\"Tremor may be present in hyperthyroidism, however, it would not be associated with dysarthria or nystagmus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Parkinsonism\",\"e\":\"The tremor associated with parkinsonism is a resting tremor that ceases on voluntary movement, this is contrary to the tremor described in the case history that worsens on voluntary movement.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spinal cord transection\",\"e\":\"Spinal cord transection is more commonly caused by significant trauma and is associated with focal neurological deficit (such as paralysis and paraesthesia) below the level of the injury-\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":112,\"stem\":\"You are seeing a patient in the neurosurgical clinic with a posterior cerebral artery infarction. They have debilitating symptoms and are unable to lead a normal life.\\n\\nWhat symptoms would you expect to find?\",\"lead\":\"What symptoms would you expect to find?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Nystagmus\",\"e\":\"Nystagmus and dysdiadochokinesis are cerebellar signs.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Dysdiadochokinesis\",\"e\":\"Nystagmus and dysdiadochokinesis are cerebellar signs.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Dysphasia\",\"e\":\"Speech would be unaffected as Broca's and Wernicke's areas, which are responsible for the comprehension and production of speech, are located in the frontal and temporal lobes which are largely supplied by the middle cerebral artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Loss of light reflex\",\"e\":\"The light reflex is a primitive reflex which circuits through the midbrain and therefore would be unaffected by a posterior cerebral artery territory infarction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Homonymous hemianopia\",\"e\":\"The posterior cerebral artery supplies the cerebral peduncle and the optic tract. It supplies the inferomedial surface of the temporal and occipital lobes. Homonymous hemianopia is caused by a lesion beyond the optic chiasm opposite to the field defect, eg due to stroke or a tumour.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":114,\"stem\":\"You are assessing a patient who has suffered a blunt head injury approximately 2 h ago. You suspect raised intracranial pressure.\\n\\nWhich one of the following signs would be most consistent with this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following signs would be most consistent with this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bloody lap following lumbar puncture\",\"e\":\"Lumbar puncture is contraindicated in raised intracranial pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased blood pressure\",\"e\":\"Hypertension, not hypotension, is associated with increased intracranial pressure as part of Cushing's triad; hypertension, bradycardia and irregular respiration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased pulse rate\",\"e\":\"As discussed, raised intracranial pressure can result in Cushing's triad, bradycardia, hypertension and irregular respiration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Irregular breathing\",\"e\":\"Rising intracranial pressure presents with headache, drowsiness, vomiting and seizures and there is often a history of trauma. Signs include listlessness, irritability, drowsiness, falling pulse, rising blood pressure, coma, irregular breathing (respiratory depression from compression of the medulla) and, later, papilloedema. Cushing's triad also indicates raised intracranial pressure; bradycardia, hypertension and irregular respiration\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neck stiffness\",\"e\":\"Neck stiffness is associated more commonly with meningitis, not raised intracranial pressure.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":116,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man comes off his motorbike at 65 mph and is admitted with a large subdural haematoma. He undergoes intracranial pressure monitoring. The early phase of increased intracranial pressure (ICP) causes which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The early phase of increased intracranial pressure (ICP) causes which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased pulse pressure\",\"e\":\"Raised intracranial pressure may give rise to Cushing's triad with decrease in respiratory rate, bradycardia, hypertension and increased pulse pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperventilation\",\"e\":\"Rising intracranial pressure may result in Cheyne-Stokes respiration and subsequent respiratory depression, but not hyperventilation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypotension\",\"e\":\"As discussed, hypertension (as part of Cushing's triad) may ensue, not hypotension.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pupillary constriction\",\"e\":\"Pupils constrict initially due to external compression of overlying parasympathetic fibres, but later dilate when the oculomotor nerve is compressed, Complete third nerve palsy may also occur. As the herniation progresses, the contralateral oculomotor nerve may be compressed, producing bilateral pupil dilation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Bradycardia\",\"e\":\"Bradycardia is associated with the late stages of rising intracranial pressure.\\n\\n2225\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":118,\"stem\":\"A 37-year-old man is admitted with headache and papilloedema. An urgent computed tomography (CT) scan is requested\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true of a space-occupying lesion in the left cerebral hemisphere?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of a space-occupying lesion in the left cerebral hemisphere?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"May reduce intracranial pressure\",\"e\":\"The skull is a rigid compartment and its three main contents (brain, blood and cerebrospinal fluid) are in dynamic equilibrium (Monroe-Kellie doctrine). An uncompensated increase in one component will result in raised intracranial pressure (ICP).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"May shift the third ventricle to the left\",\"e\":\"Space-occupying lesions cause a shift of cerebral matter to the opposite side and so may compress the lateral ventricle..\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"May cause nausea and vomiting\",\"e\":\"Nausea and vomiting are signs of raised ICP.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Must be large before it causes symptoms\",\"e\":\"Depending on location, small lesions can cause symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rarely presents with symptoms\",\"e\":\"Relatively large lesions may develop in the frontal lobes before any signs of raised ICP are seen, however, other symptoms such as nausea and headache are likely to be present.\\n\\n2228\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":120,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old woman is referred to the Endocrine Clinic with amenorrhoea for 9 months after giving birth. She stopped breast feeding 4 months ago. Blood tests reveal hyponatraemia, low thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) and T3/T4 levels. She had a heavy post-partum haemorrhage requiring a massive transfusion.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis in this case?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis in this case?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Empty-sella syndrome\",\"e\":\"The pituitary gland is normally located within the sella turcica and empty-sella syndrome occurs in which the pituitary gland shrinks or becomes flattened meaning it cannot be seen on an MRI scan. The sella turcica is often filled with cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) which may have leaked, causing the pituitary gland to shrink or flatten. In most causes there are no symptoms or loss of pituitary function.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Kallmann's syndrome\",\"e\":\"Kallmann's syndrome is a rare genetic disorder resulting in a failure to start or complete puberty. Given this woman has managed to bear a child, this answer is incorrect\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pituitary apoplexy\",\"e\":\"This is a result of bleeding from a pituitary gland tumour and presents with a sudden onset headache, diplopia and visual field defect due to compression of the optic chiasm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sheehan syndrome\",\"e\":\"Sheehan's syndrome is also known as Simmonds' syndrome or post-partum pituitary gland necrosis. It is now a rare condition which occurs following pituitary infarction due to severe post-partum haemorrhage. The pituitary gland is vulnerable as it relies on a low pressure portal venous and hyperplasia and hypertrophy of lactotrophs in pregnancy cause the anterior pituitary to increase in size. Patients can have loss of all pituitary hormones or a selective loss, and the symptoms may only manifest at a later date.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Subacute thyroiditis\",\"e\":\"Subacute thyroiditis is usually associated with a goitre and presenting symptoms can include fever, myalgia, and malaise which are not described in the case history.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":123,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old woman is undergoing a magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan for neurological symptoms that are thought to represent early multiple sclerosis. The radiologist notes he is unable to visualise the pituitary gland and advises urgent clinical assessment. This reveals no symptoms of endocrine dysfunction and normal blood tests. What is the most likely cause for this appearance on an MRI scan?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause for this appearance on an MRI scan?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Empty-sella syndrome\",\"e\":\"The pituitary gland is normally located within the sella turcica and empty-sella syndrome occurs in which the pituitary gland shrinks or becomes flattened meaning it cannot be seen on an MRI scan. The sella turcica is often filled with cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), which may have leaked in causing the pituitary gland to shrink or flatten. In most causes there are no symptoms or loss of pituitary function.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Kallmann's syndrome\",\"e\":\"Kallmann's syndrome is a rare genetic disorder resulting in a failure to start or complete puberty.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pituitary apoplexy\",\"e\":\"Pituitary apoplexy is referred to as bleeding from a pituitary gland tumour which presents with a sudden onset headache, diplopia and visual field defect due to compression of the optic chiasm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sheehan's syndrome\",\"e\":\"Sheehan's syndrome is also known as Simmonds' syndrome or post-partum pituitary gland necrosis. It is now a rare condition which occurs following pituitary infarction due to severe post-partum haemorrhage. The pituitary gland is vulnerable as it relies on a low pressure portal venous and hyperplasia and hypertrophy of lactotrophs in pregnancy cause the anterior pituitary to increase in size. Patients can have loss of all pituitary hormones or a selective loss, and the symptoms may only manifest at a later date.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Simmonds syndrome\",\"e\":\"As discussed, Simmonds' syndrome is also known as Sheehan's syndrome..\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":126,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man presented with urinary retention and saddle paraesthesia. His symptoms began 12 h ago. He has a cardiac pacemaker (of unknown. make) in situ. Examination revealed reduced anal tone.\\n\\nWhat is the next step in his management?\",\"lead\":\"What is the next step in his management?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CT myelogram of the lumbar spine C\",\"e\":\"This patient has classic symptoms of cauda equina syndrome. The most important next step is to identify the source of compression in the lumbar spine. The study of choice is a magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan. However in patients who are unable to undergo an MRI scan, such as those with non MRI safe pacemakers, a CT myelogram should be performed. Cauda equina syndrome is a complex of low back pain, bilateral or unilateral sciatica, saddle anaesthesia, motor weakness and bowel/bladder dysfunction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"High dose corticosteroids\",\"e\":\"Cauda equina is a surgical emergency. Decompression of the lumbar spine should be performed within 48 h from the onset of symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Immediate surgical decompression of the cervical spine\",\"e\":\"Cauda equina syndrome affects the cauda equina which can be found in the lumbar spinal column not the cervical spine.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"MRI scan of the lumbar spine\",\"e\":\"While some cardiac pacemakers are now MRI safe, given that the make of this man's pacemaker is unknown, an MRI is contraindicated and he should proceed instead to a CT myelogram.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Physiotherapy, analgesia, and out-patient follow-up\",\"e\":\"Cauda equina is a surgical emergency: Decompression of the lumbar spine should be performed within 48 h from the onset of symptoms.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":129,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man is involved in a road traffic collision and sustains a neck injury with C4/C5 dislocation. On examination he has loss of motor function on one side and loss of pain and temperature sensation on the contralateral side.\\n\\nWhich spinal cord syndrome is associated with these findings?\",\"lead\":\"Which spinal cord syndrome is associated with these findings?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior cord syndrome\",\"e\":\"Anterior cord syndrome occurs when there is loss of the anterior cord resulting in complete loss of motor function and pain and temperature sensation below the level of the injury. however, proprioception, vibration and fine touch will be preserved as these are carried in the posterior cord\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Brown-Séquard syndrome\",\"e\":\"Brown-Séquard syndrome is caused by complete cord hemitransection. On examination patients have loss of motor power, proprioception and vibration sensation on one side and contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation. This syndrome has the best prognosis of all spinal cord injuries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Central cord syndrome\",\"e\":\"Central cord syndrome is one of the incomplete spinal cord injuries. Patients are often elderly with pre-existing spinal degeneration. The patient usually complains of loss of dexterity and hand weakness more pronounced than at shoulder and lower limbs. Prognosis is usually good with patients ambulatory at final visit but some loss of manual dexterity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neurological shock\",\"e\":\"This occurs when there is complete disruption of the cord usually at the cervical or high thoracic level resulting in loss of sympathetic tones, subsequently there is hypotension and bradycardia due to peripheral vasodilation. This is not described in the case history above.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior cord syndrome\",\"e\":\"Posterior cord syndrome is very rare and occurs when the posterior cord is damaged, usually through ischaemia. Clinically this presents as loss of proprioception, vibration sense and light touch. This is not described in the case history above.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":132,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man presented with bilateral buttock and leg pain, worse with stand ng and walking, and better with leaning forward. On examination, he has normal power in his legs, straight leg raising and pedal pulses are norma.\\n\\nWhat is this clinical presentation consistent with?\",\"lead\":\"What is this clinical presentation consistent with?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cauda equina syndrome\",\"e\":\"Cauda equina syndrome is a complex of low back pain, bilateral or unilateral sciatica, saddle anaesthesia, motor weakness (note this patient has normal power in his legs) and bowel/bladder dysfunction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Disc prolapse\",\"e\":\"Lumbar disc prolapse is a condition occurring in fit young adults. It occurs acutely when lifting a heavy weight or while straining. The pain is felt in the lower back, sometimes radiating to the buttock or down the leg. In total, 90% of patients will have improvement of symptoms within 3 months with non-operative care. MRI is the modality of choice for diagnosis of lumbar disc herniations.\\n\\nThis is a bony infection of the spine and, as this patient has no signs of infection, this is unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Osteomyelitis of the spine\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Peripheral vascular disease\",\"e\":\"Pedal pulses are normal in this patient and makes peripheral vascular disease unlikely as a cause for his symptoms\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spinal stenosis\",\"e\":\"Spinal stenosis is due to narrowing of the spinal canal by either bony or soft tissue structures. Patients present with neurogenic claudication. MRI is the best investigation. Treatment is with analgesia and steroid injection, or surgical with decompression. It is important to differentiate between neurogenic and vascular claudication.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":135,\"stem\":\"Extradural bleeding is associated with severe head injury and can result in permanent brain damage if not recognised and managed immediately. The condition strips the dura mater from the periosteum of the skull\\n\\nWhat is the main radiological feature of extradural haematoma?\",\"lead\":\"What is the main radiological feature of extradural haematoma?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Absence of a fracture on plain X-rays\",\"e\":\"Extradural haematomas are usually associated with skull fractures.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Biconvex looking haematoma on computed tomography (CT) scan\",\"e\":\"Extradural haematomas are biconvex and do not cross suture lines, as the dura is very adherent to the cranium. They are usually associated with a fracture of the pterion, which is formed by the meeting of the frontal, parietal, temporal, sphenoid bones (located just posterior to the temple). This is the weakest part of the cranium and the anterior branch of the middle meningea artery runs just beneath the pterion and so is often damaged (causing an extradural haematoma) with trauma affecting this area of the skill. Both extradural and subdural haematomas and any condition leading to cerebral oedema could lead to a rise in ICP. In the early stages of head injury, there is a non-linear relationship between an expanding haematoma and elevation of ICP - a haematoma may expand without any significant rise in pressure. Once this early compliance is lost, the pressure will rise rapidly This severely jeopard ses cerebral perfusion: cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) is equal to mean arterial blood pressure minus ICP (normal CPP is approximately 70 mmHg and the normal ICP in adults is 10-15 mmHg), The Monro-Kellie hypothesis: the sum of intracranial volumes of blood, brain, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and other components is constant, an increase in any one of these must be offset by a corresponding decrease in another, or else the ICP will rise\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bleeding into the ventricles on CT\",\"e\":\"Bleeding occurs between the dura and skull. Bleeding into the ventricles would suggest a sub-arachnoid bleed.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Bleeding that crosses suture lines\",\"e\":\"Extradural haematomas do not cross suture lines due to the adherent nature of the dura mata to the cranium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Crescentic shape of the bleed on CT\",\"e\":\"Subdural haematoma are crescentic in shape.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":138,\"stem\":\"A 20-year-old man sustains a head injury while skiing without protective head gear. Initially he showed signs of concussion, but recovered and did not seek medical attention. 2 days later he is brought to the emergency department with a GCS of 6.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cerebral contusion\",\"e\":\"Cerebral contusions can have varying presentations depending on location and severity. The risk is off raised intracranial pressure due to cerebral swelling. It is unlikely to present with reduced conscious level 2 days post injury, and more likely to present in the first 12 h following injury.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diffuse axonal injury\",\"e\":\"Diffuse axonal injury will present immediately following the incident and is a result of extensive lesions in the white matter tracts.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Extradural haematoma\",\"e\":\"There are a number of different mechanisms of brain injury including hypoxia, ischaemia, contusion and diffuse axonal injury. Focal injuries include extradural, subdural and intracerebral haematomas and contusions. Extradural haematomas often result from a trivial blow, and children and young adults are more at risk. Typically, initially there are signs of concussion, then a lucid interval, then rapid decompensation as intracranial pressure rises:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intracerebral haematoma\",\"e\":\"An intracerebral haematoma would likely present immediately with a focal neurological deficit following injury due to the disruption of the cerebral cortex.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Subdural haematoma\",\"e\":\"Subdural haematomas are more common in the elderly due to the fragility of the bridging veins, so it would be unlikely in a 20-year-old.\\n\\nThere are a number of different mechanisms of brain injury including hypoxia, ischaemia, contusion and diffuse axonal injury. Focal injuries include extradural, sudural and intracerebral haematomas and contusions. Extradural haematomas often result from a trivial blow, and children and young adults are more at risk. Typically, initially there are signs of concussion, then a lucid interval, then rapid decompensation as intracranial pressure rises.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":141,\"stem\":\"You are called to assess a patient on the ward who demonstrates abnormal flexion, is making incomprehensible sounds and opens her eyes to pain. She was admitted following a fall\\n\\nWhat is the patient's Glasgow Coma Scale score (GCS)?\",\"lead\":\"What is the patient's Glasgow Coma Scale score (GCS)?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"* Explanation (L\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"4\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"5\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"6\",\"e\":\"The patient's GCS is 7 according to the clinical information given, please attempt to recalculate.\\n\\nThe patient's GCS is 7 according to the clinical information given, please attempt to recalculate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"7\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"8\",\"e\":\"The patient’s GCS is 7 according to the clinical information given, please attempt to recalculate.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":145,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man presents to the ENT clinic. On audiology, it is apparent that he has unilateral sensorineural deafness and tinnitus.\\n\\nWhat intracranial lesion is most likely to cause this?\",\"lead\":\"What intracranial lesion is most likely to cause this?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Astrocytoma\",\"e\":\"This is the most common form of brain neoplasm and is a subtype of gliomas. It would not cause sensorineural hearing loss.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ependymoma\",\"e\":\"This is a subtype of gliomas and it would not cause sensorineural hearing loss.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medulloblastoma\",\"e\":\"Again, this is a subtype of gliomas and it would not cause sensorineural hearing loss.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Oligodendroma\",\"e\":\"This is a type of glioma which is relative slow growing but survival rates are poor. It would not cause sensorineural hearing loss.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Schwannoma\",\"e\":\"Schwannoma, otherwise known as acoustic neuroma, usually arise from cranial nerve VIII. The diagnosis of schwannoma must be considered in all patients presenting with a unilateral sensorineural deafness.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":147,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old man is admitted with an ulnar-sided wrist incised wound having fallen onto glass while intoxicated. He is no longer able to move his hand normally. Which of the following is a sign of ulnar nerve transection?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is a sign of ulnar nerve transection?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Loss of flexor digiti min mi function. Y\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve supplies flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU) and medial half of flexor digitorum profundus in the forearm. In the hand it gives off a superficial and deep branch. The deep branch supplies the hypothenar muscles, opponens digiti minimi, abductor digiti minimi and flexor digiti minimi as well as the third and fourth lumbrical muscles, dorsal and palmar interossei, adductor pol icis and deep head of flexor pollicis brevis. The superfical branch of the ulnar nerve supplies palmaris brevis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Loss of the index finger flexor digitorum\",\"e\":\"The unar nerve only supplies the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus function and the index finger would represent the lateral half.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Loss of sensation in the radial 3 digits\",\"e\":\"Sensation in the radial 3 digits would be supplied by the median nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Loss of sensation over the 1st dorsal web space\",\"e\":\"This area is supplied by the radial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Loss of thumb abduction\",\"e\":\"Thumb abduction by abductor pollicis brevis is inserted by the recurrent branch of the median nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":149,\"stem\":\"A previously well 61-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a marked right upper limb weakness and right facial droop. He is also noted to have a dysphasia. On taking a history from his wife it emerges he is due to attend hospital the following day for an elective inguinal hernia repair. He is normally on warfarin for a mechanical valve, which he stopped 6 days ago on the advice of his GP. What complication has occurred?\",\"lead\":\"What complication has occurred?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amaurosis fugax.\",\"e\":\"Amaurosis fugax is the painless loss of vision in one or both eyes, this is not the case described above.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Embolic stroke\",\"e\":\"Mechanical valvular prostheses require lifelong anticoagulation to prevent emboli and valvular obstruction with thrombus: Heparin may be used to anticoagulate should warfarin need to be withheld for any reason. Warfarin is a teratogen, and so mechanical valves should be avoided in women who plan to have children.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemorrhagic stroke\",\"e\":\"The patient has stopped taking warfarin so reducing his risk of a haemorrhagic stroke.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prosthetic endocarditis\",\"e\":\"Prosthetic valve endocarditis is notoriously difficult to treat and will often require redo valve replacement. So antibiotic prophylaxis before instrumentation is very important in those patients with prosthetic valves. While this may lead to abscess formation in the brain due to seeding from the valve endocarditis, the case history is of a stroke.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Subdural haematoma\",\"e\":\"This usually occurs following trauma of which there is none described in this case history.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":151,\"stem\":\"You are examining a newborn baby, who was suspected to have anomalies on prenatal screening. You note a sac protruding from the lumbosacral region of the baby's back. The spinal cord is unfolded within. What associated condition is most commonly seen in children with this?\",\"lead\":\"What associated condition is most commonly seen in children with this?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Biliary atresia\",\"e\":\"Biliary atresia describes malformation of the biliary tree in which some bile ducts may be blocked, stenosed or absent. It does not have an association with myelomeningoceles.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cleft palate\",\"e\":\"Cleft palate is most commonly associated with Pierre Robin sequence (PRS). This is characterised by micrognathia, retraction of the tongue and upper airways obstruction\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Down syndrome\",\"e\":\"Down's syndrome is a chromosomal abnormality as a result of trisomy 21. While there is an association between neural tube defects and Down's syndrome, hydrocephalus is more common.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gastroschisis\",\"e\":\"Gastroschisis occurs when the small intestine fails to return to the abdominal cavity, as such the neonate is born with a section of bowel protruding through the abdominal wall. It is not associated with neural tube defects.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hydrocephalus\",\"e\":\"The baby has a myelomeningocele, which is a neural tube defect. There is complete unfolding of the cord, which lies on the childs back, most commonly at the lumbosacral junction. 80% of patients with myelomeningocele also have hydrocephalus.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":153,\"stem\":\"On examining a newborn you notice a fluid-filled meningeal sac filled with cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), but no evidence of the cord, over the lower lumbar region. The mother is very anxious about her child and wants your advice.\\n\\nWhat condition does the patient have?\",\"lead\":\"What condition does the patient have?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cerebral palsy\",\"e\":\"Cerebral palsy is a broad term which refers to permanent movement disorders due to abnormal development of the brain: It usually presents in early childhood with movement difficulties, not with a meningeal sac.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hydrocephalus\",\"e\":\"Hydrocephalus is a condition in which there is excess accumulation of cerebrospinal fluid in the brain causing dilatation of the ventricular system and increased intracranial pressure. It does not present with a meningeal sac.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Meningocele\",\"e\":\"The incidence of neural tube defects has decreased over the last 30 years. These defects are broadly classified into three types: myelomeningocele, meningocele and spina bifida occulta. A fluid filled sac without the cord contained is a meningocele.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Myelomeningocele\",\"e\":\"The presence of the cord within the sac would classify as a myelomeningocele.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spina bifida occulta\",\"e\":\"In spina bifida occulta there is a covering of skin and no fluid filled sac.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":155,\"stem\":\"A pregnant woman has increased levels of serum and amniotic fluid alpha-fetoprotein. Ultrasound scan (USS) reveals that the neural tube has failed to close rostrally\\n\\nWhat condition will the fetus have?\",\"lead\":\"What condition will the fetus have?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anencephaly\",\"e\":\"Neural tube defects can be detected pre-natally. The neural tube closure begins centrally and extends rostrally on day 24 and caudally on day 26 of gestation. Failure to close rostrally results in anencephaly. Alpha-fetoprotein levels can be raised in a number of conditions, and forms part of Down's syndrome screening, and screening for neural tube and abdominal wall defects.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AVI Exomphalos\",\"e\":\"Exomphalos is a condition in which the primitive bowel loop fails to return to the abdominal cavity.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Meningocele\",\"e\":\"A fluid-filled meningeal sac without the cord contained is a meningocele.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Myelomeningocele\",\"e\":\"The presence of a meningeal sac and spinal cord protruding through an opening would classify as a myelomeningocele.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spina bifida occulta\",\"e\":\"Failure of the neural tube to close caudally results in spina bifida, which can result in three distinct anomalies; myelomeningocele, meningocele, spina bifida occulta. In spina bifida occulta there is a covering of skin and no fluid filled meningeal sac.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":157,\"stem\":\"You are treating a neonate born with a myelomeningocele. You were not expecting this on the baby check and perform a more thorough examination and investigations to check for any additional congenital defects.\\n\\nWhat is the commonest associated condition that you should check for?\",\"lead\":\"What is the commonest associated condition that you should check for?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"iS i Anencephaly\",\"e\":\"Anencephaly can be part of rachischisis, the most severe form of spina bifida cystica, but is not associated with myelomeningocele.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AlVI Exomphalos\",\"e\":\"Exomphalos is a condition in which the primitive bowel loop fails to return to the abdominal cavity, and does not appear to have an association with myelomeningoceles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hip dislocation\",\"e\":\"Myelomeningocele in association with hip dislocation is not as common as it's association with hydrocephalus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hydrocephalus\",\"e\":\"In myelomeningocele there is complete unfolding of the spinal cord. It is more common than meningocele. There are a number of conditions associated with it including syringomyelia and hip dislocation, but hydrocephalus is the most common, seen in 80-90% of those with myelomeningocele.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Syringomyelia\",\"e\":\"Syringomyelia is a broad term used to refer to formation of a cavity or cyst within the spinal cord, and while it can be associated with a myelomeningocele, hydrocephalus is a more common association.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":159,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man was referred by his GP with back pain, urinary retention and saddle paraesthesia for 10 h. On examination, he has bilateral altered sensation in his legs and reduced anal tone.\\n\\nWhat is the next step in management?\",\"lead\":\"What is the next step in management?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"nís' Caudal epidural steroid injection\",\"e\":\"This would not be helpful in this case, he requires imaging to assess for cauda equina syndrome and then urgent decompression.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Computed tomography (CT) myelogram of the lumbar spine\",\"e\":\"CT myelogram of the lumbar spine is an alternative choice for those unable to undergo a magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan, however, MRI is the investigation of choice.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Immediate surgical decompression of the cervical spine\",\"e\":\"If the patient had compression of the spinal cord at the cervical spine the presenting signs and symptoms would be very different, upper limb neurological deficit would be expected. However, this man clearly has evidence of compression of the cauda equina in the lumbar spine.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"MRI scan of the lumbar spine\",\"e\":\"This patient has classic symptoms of cauda equina syndrome. The most important next step is to confirm the diagnosis with an MRI scan to evaluate neurological compression. In patients who are unable to undergo an MRI such as those with pacemakers and Claustrophobia, a CT myelogram is an alternative. Cauda equina syndrome is a complex of low back pain, bilateral or unilateral sciatica, saddle anaesthesia, motor weakness and bowel/bladder dysfunction. Studies have shown improved outcomes in bowel and bladder function and resolution of motor and sensory deficits when decompression performed within 48 h of the onset of symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Physiotherapy, analgesia, and out-patient tollow up\",\"e\":\"The patient has multiple 'red flags' for acute cauda equina syndrome and requires immediate investigation with an MRI scan. This course of action would therefore be inappropriate and associated with high morbidity.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":162,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman presents with an acute episode of low backache which started on lifting a heavy object at home. Her pain is radiating down one leg.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Facet dislocation\",\"e\":\"Facet dislocation can occur with or without the presence of an associated fracture, however, it usually occurs due to forced flexion of the cervical spine as this is the most mobile section of the spinal column. Given this patient has lower back pain, facet dislocation is unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lumbar disc herniation\",\"e\":\"Lumbar disc prolapse is a condition occurring in fit young adults. It occurs acutely when lifting a heavy weight or while straining. The pain is felt in the lower back, sometimes radiating to the buttock or down the leg. For 90% of patients, improvement of symptoms will have occurred within 3 months with non-operative care. MRI is the modality of choice for diagnosis of lumbar disc herniations.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Multiple myeloma\",\"e\":\"Multiple myeloma is a neoplastic haematological condition most commonly seen in the elderly which often presents with bone pain (often affecting the spine) due to presence of lytic lesions within the bone. It would be rare in a woman of this age.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spinal stenosis\",\"e\":\"Spinal stenosis is an abnormal narrowing of the spinal canal and is most commonly seen in the elderly in the context of arthritis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spondylolisthesis\",\"e\":\"Spondylolisthesis is the displacement of one vertebrae in relation to another, and has a number of atiologies, including trauma and degenerative. It would be uncommon given the case history.\"}]},{\"t\":18,\"p\":165,\"stem\":\"Following a road traffic collision, a patient presents with loss of motor function in the upper limbs and less so on the lower limbs. The patient complains of a loss of dexterity in his hands.\\n\\nWhich spinal cord syndrome is associated with these findings?\",\"lead\":\"Which spinal cord syndrome is associated with these findings?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior cord syndrome\",\"e\":\"Anterior cord syndrome occurs when there is loss of the anterior cord resulting in complete loss of motor function and pain and temperature sensation below the level of the injury, however, proprioception, vibration and fine touch will be preserved as these are carried in the posterior cord. The loss of dexterity is more indicative of central cord syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Brown-Séquard syndrome\",\"e\":\"Brown-Séquard syndrome occurs when there is hemisection of the spinal cord. It results in loss of motor function and proprioception on the ipsilateral side, while loss of pain and temperature sensation of the contralateral side of the injury. This scenario is not described in the case above.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Central cord syndrome\",\"e\":\"Central cord syndrome is one of the incomplete spinal cord injuries. Patients are often elderly with pre-existing spinal degeneration. The patient usually complains of loss of dexterity and hand weakness more pronounced than at shoulder and lower limbs. Prognosis is usually good with patients' ambulatory at final visit but some loss of manual dexterity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neurological shock\",\"e\":\"This event occurs when there is complete disruption of the cord usually at the cervical or high thoracic level resulting in loss of sympathetic tones, subsequently there is hypotension and bradycardia due to peripheral vasodilation. This scenario is not described in the case history above.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior cord syndrome\",\"e\":\"Posterior cord syndrome is very rare and occurs when the posterior cord is damaged, usually through ischaemia. Clinically this presents as loss of proprioception, vibration sense and light touch. This is not described in the case history above.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A patient develops common fibular nerve palsy after treatment with a below-knee cast for 6 weeks for an undisplaced ankle fracture.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The patient is unable to stand on tiptoe\",\"e\":\"Standing on tiptoes requires the functioning of muscles in the posterior compartment of the lower limb, mainly the gastrocnemius muscle; the tibial nerve supplies these muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"There would be complete sensory loss below the knee\",\"e\":\"Sensation below the knee is supplied by a number of different peripheral nerves (deep and superficial peroneal nerve, tibial nerve, saphenous nerve and sural nerve). Sensory loss in a below-knee distribution is more suggestive of peripheral neuropathy then isolated damage to a single nerve injury.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"There would be no dorsiflexion of the foot\",\"e\":\"Dorsiflexion of the foot requires the functioning of the muscles in the anterior compartment of the lower limb; the motor supply of these muscles comes from the deep fibular nerve, which is a branch of the common fibular nerve. The common fibular nerve (also known as the common peroneal nerve) can be compressed by a below-knee cast at the level of the fibula neck.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The patient would complain of complete sensory loss on the sole of the foot\",\"e\":\"The sensory fibres of the tibial nerve and its terminal branches the medial and lateral planter nerve supply the sole of the foot. The tibial nerve supplies the posterior compartment of the lower leg.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sensation would be normal on the dorsum of the foot\",\"e\":\"The common fibular nerve supplies sensation over the dorsum of the foot. The deep fibular nerve supplies the skin over the first dorsal web space and the superficial fibular nerve supplies the rest of the dorsum of the foot.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"Following open reduction internal fixation of both the distal tibia and the distal fibula, a patient complains of numbness along the lateral side of the foot. This was not present before their surgery.\\n\\nWhich nerve is likely to have been injured?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve is likely to have been injured?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Sural nerve\",\"e\":\"The sural nerve is made up of collateral branches of the tibial and common fibular nerve and supplies sensation to the lateral aspect of the leg. It runs alongside the short saphenous veins, which can be located posterior to the lateral malleolus, therefore they may be injured during the approach to the distal fibular.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Saphenous nerve\",\"e\":\"The saphenous nerve is the largest cutaneous branch of the femoral nerve and supplies sensation to the medial aspect of the leg It runs alongside the long saphenous vein, which runs anteriorly to the medial malleolus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Deep fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The deep fibular nerve is a mixed motor/sensory nerve that branches from the common fibular nerve and supplies the muscles of the anterior lower leg compartment and the skin over the first dorsal web space.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superficial fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The superficial fibular nerve is a mixed motor/sensory nerve that branches from the common fibular nerve and supplies the muscles of the lateral lower leg compartment and the skin over the dorsal of the foot, except the first dorsal web space.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tibial nerve.\",\"e\":\"The tibial nerve is a mixed motor/sensory nerve that branches from the sciatic nerve and supplies the muscles of the posterior lower leg compartment and the skin over the planter aspect of the foot.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A young patient is tilting her pelvis while walking. They are having difficulty maintaining independent mobility. Which clinical test is used to assess the hip abductors?\",\"lead\":\"Which clinical test is used to assess the hip abductors?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Barlow's test\",\"e\":\"Barlow's test is performed on infants to screen for developmental dysplasia of the hip and assessing the stability of the hip.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ober's test\",\"e\":\"Ober's test is performed to assess the tightness of the iliotibial band.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ortolani's test\",\"e\":\"Ortolani's test is performed on infants to screen for developmental dysplasia of the hip, which is performed along with the Barlow's test.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thomas' test\",\"e\":\"Thomas' test is performed to assess for hip flexion contracture and psoas syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trendelenburg's test\",\"e\":\"Trendelenburg's test assesses for weak or paralysed the hip abductor muscles. This test is performed with the patient standing. The patient is asked to raise one leg; the test is positive if the pelvis on the raised side drops. A positive test suggests weakness of the abductors of the other hip.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old man presents with mechanical hip pain. He undergoes routine plain AP and lateral X-ray of the hip. What is the first X-ray sign to appear in osteoarthritis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the first X-ray sign to appear in osteoarthritis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Osteopenia\",\"e\":\"Osteopenia is a condition in which bone mineral density is lower than normal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Osteophytes\",\"e\":\"Osteophyte formation is a radiographic sign of osteoarthritis, but it is not the first to appear. As the disease progresses, subluxation may occur and osteophytes may form.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Soft tissue swelling\",\"e\":\"Rest pain, stiffness and bony swelling are clinical features of osteoarthritis. Soft tissue swelling is more commonly assocated with rheumatoid arthritis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Subchondral sclerosis\",\"e\":\"Subchrondal sclerosis occurs as cartilage loss increases and appears as an area of increased density on the radiograph. The classic radiographic signs of osteoarthritis are joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, osteophytes and subchondral cysts. Osteopenia and soft tissue swelling are often associated with rheumatoid arthritis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Narrowing joint space\",\"e\":\"Osteoarthritis is a degenerative joint disorder, which results from a loss of hyaline cartilage in synovial joints. The first radiographic sign of osteoarthritis is non-uniform joint space narrowing.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 10-year-old girl is treated for a tibia and fibula fracture with above-knee plaster. She is recovering on the ward, but the nurses are concerned and have asked you to review her overnight.\\n\\nWhat early clinical signs of compartment syndrome in the leg do you look for?\",\"lead\":\"What early clinical signs of compartment syndrome in the leg do you look for?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Loss of the dorsalis pedis pulse\",\"e\":\"Pulses are normally present in compartment syndrome. Absent pulses are usually due to systemic hypotension, arterial occlusion or vascular injury. In the context of compartment syndrome absent pulses are a late finding.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mottling of the overlying skin\",\"e\":\"Mottling of the skin would be a late clinical sign of compartment syndrome. Local trauma causes bleeding and soft tissue swelling, which compromises venous return and gives rise to a mottled appearance of skin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pain out of proportion to injury on passive dorsiflexion of the foot\",\"e\":\"Compartment syndrome is a clinical diagnosis defined as increased pressure within a fascial compartment that causes reduced tissue perfusion and cellular damage. Dorsiflexion of the foot stretches muscles in the posterior compartment, which causes an out-of-proportion pain response in the presence of high compartment pressures as myoneural blood supply is compromised. Clinical signs of compartment syndrome include pain out of proportion of the injury, pain on passive starching, pain despite analgesia, pain despite elevation and pain despite removal of constrictive dressings.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Paralysis of the foot\",\"e\":\"The loss of motor function is a late sign of compartment syndrome. Local trauma causes bleeding and soft tissue swelling, which increases compartmental pressures. This compromises venous return and propagates swelling and further increases compartmental pressures causing microvascular occlusion resulting in myoneural ischaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduced sensation in the foot\",\"e\":\"The sensory loss is a late sign of compartment syndrome. Local trauma causes bleeding and soft tissue swelling, which increases compartmental pressures. This compromises venous return and propagates swelling and further increases compartmental pressures causing microvascular occlusion resulting in myoneural ischaemia\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man complains of having to swing his body to help him walk after a long session of exercise in the gym. Trendelenburg's test is performed.\\n\\nIf the left side of the patient's pelvis drops while he stands on his right leg, what is the likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"If the left side of the patient's pelvis drops while he stands on his right leg, what is the likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A shortened right femur\",\"e\":\"In the case of a shortened femur the upper body is shifted slightly over the leg in the stance phase If a patient has a shortened right femur and was standing on their right leg, their body weight would be shifted to right raising the pelvis on the opposite side.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Weakness of the right hip abductor muscles\",\"e\":\"Trendelenburg's test assesses for weak or paralysed hip abductor muscles. This test is performed with the patient standing. The patient is asked to raise one leg; the test is positive if the pelvis on the raised side drops. A positive test suggests weakness of the abductors of the other hip. When the gluteal muscles are compromised (weakened as a result of a hip dislocation, due to paralysis, or following hip operations) with functional deficits, they are no longer able to support the pelvis on the weight-bearing side. The pelvis than drops down on the normal, non-weight-bearing side.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Weakness of the left hip abductor muscles\",\"e\":\"Trendelenburg's test assesses the hip abductor muscles on the side that the patient is standing, therefore weakness of the left hip abductor muscles would result in a patient's pelvis dropping while standing on their left leg.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An injury to the left gluteus medius muscle\",\"e\":\"Trendelenburg's test assesses the hip abductor muscles, these include the glutes medius, tensor fasciae latae, gluteus minimus, piriformis and obturator internus. Injury to the left gluteus medius would result in the right side of the pelvis dropping when standing on the\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"bilateral hip dysplasia\",\"e\":\"Patients with bilateral hip dysplasia walk with a 'waddling gait' (myopathic gait) and the Trendelenburg's test will be positive on both sides. Trendelenburg's test is used to assess stability of the hip. An abnormal Trendelenburg's test is seen with any painful disorder of the hip. It is also present with a dislocated or subluxed hip and in other conditions in which the proximal femoral anatomy is abnormal (short femoral neck with high riding trochanter). Weak abductor muscles will also lead to an abnormal test. The abductors of the hip include the piriformis, gluteus medius and minimis muscles. The pectines muscle adducts the hip. The other adductors of the hip are the gracilis, and adductor longus, brevis and magnus muscles.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A 38-year-old builder's labourer sustained a severe fracture of his left elbow, which damaged the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus. A month later, he still has total ulnar nerve paralysıs.\\n\\nWhich clinical sign is most likely to be present on examination?\",\"lead\":\"Which clinical sign is most likely to be present on examination?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Sensory loss over the unar 3% digits..\",\"e\":\"Damage to the ulnar nerve would result in sensory loss over the ulna 1½ digits.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inability to grip a sheet of paper between his fingers\",\"e\":\"To grip a sheet of paper requires finger adduction and the functioning of the palmar interossei muscles, which are supplied by the deep branch of the unar nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Excessive sweating over the unar border of the left hand\",\"e\":\"Injury to the unar nerve may result in sympathetic interruption, with absence of sweating in the affected area\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ring and little fingers on the affected side are held in the claw position\",\"e\":\"Although the ring and little finger are held in the clawed position when the ulnar nerve is injured at the wrist, a high injury at the level of the elbow paralyses the long flexors to these two fingers and results in the loss of this sign (the ulnar paradox).\\n\\nThe recurrent motor branch of the median nerve supplies the thenar eminence. Wasting of the thenar eminence would suggest damaged to the median nerve, and can be seen in carpal tunnel syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Marked wasting of the thenar eminence\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 49-year-old woman has been complaining of a tingling feeling in her right hand at night and is under the impression that her hand is swollen although there is no obvious oedema. In the last few days she has noticed numbness in her right index finger and the tip of her thumb, especially while working. Which nerve is most likely to be responsible for her symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve is most likely to be responsible for her symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ulnar nerve\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve supplies sensation to the ulna 1½ digits, motor function to the intrinsic muscles of the hand, the ulna half of flexor digitorum profundus and the flexor carpi ulnaris muscle in the forearm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Median nerve\",\"e\":\"The median nerve supplies sensation to the palm and the palmer surface of the radial 3½ digits. The symptoms described are suggestive of carpal tunnel syndrome. This often spares sensory loss to the palm as the palmer branch of the median nerve comes off before it enters the carpal tunnel. The digital branches and the thenar muscles will be affected. The usual presentation of carpal tunnel syndrome is with acroparaesthesias. This consists of numbness, tingling and burning sensations felt in the hand and fingers; the pain sometimes radiates up the forearm as far as the elbow or even as high as the shoulder or root of the neck. Although the paraesthesias are sometimes restricted to the radial fingers, they may affect all the digits as some fibres from the median nerve are distributed to the fifth finger through a communication with the ulnar nerve in the palm. The attacks of pain and paraesthesias are most common at night and often wake the patient from sleep. They are then relieved by shaking the hand. The hand tends to feel numb and useless on waking in the morning but recovers after it has been used for some minutes. The symptoms may recur during the day following use, or at other times if the patient sits with the hands immobile. Such symptoms of acroparaesthesias may persist for many years without the appearance of symptoms of median nerve damage. In other patients, weakness of the thenar muscles develops, particularly with abduction of the thumb, and is associated with atrophy of the lateral aspect of the thenar eminence. Sensory loss may appear over the tips of the median innervated fingers.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Radial nerve\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve supplies sensation to the first dorsal web space in the hand and motor function to the extensor muscle of the forearm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Nerve root C7\",\"e\":\"The C7 nerve root is most strongly associated with elbow extension and sensation along the middle finger.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Nerve root C8\",\"e\":\"The C8 nerve root is most strongly associated with finger flexion and sensation along the ring and little finger.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old falls out of a tree and injures his arm. Subsequently his hand appears clawed On examination, his hand is extended at all the metacarpal (MCP) joints and all the interphalangeal joints are in fixed flexion. The wrist flexors appear to be slightly weaker.\\n\\nWhat is likely to be the injury?\",\"lead\":\"What is likely to be the injury?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1 Si Ulnar nerve injury\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve originates from C8-T1 and innervates the flexor carpi ulnaris muscle and the flexor digitorum profundus muscle of the ring and little finger, as well as the intrinsic muscles of the hand. Ulnar nerve injury results in clawing of the ring and little finger from denervation of the ulna two lumbricals and unopposed action of the flexor digitorum profundus resulting in extended metacarpophalangeal joints and flexed interphalangeal joints. If the injury occurs more proximally, the flexor digitorum profundus to the ring and little finger is also denervated and the claw-like appearance is less apparent, this is known as the ulnar paradox.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"C8, T1 root injury\",\"e\":\"Lower brachial plexus injuries (C8, T1) are caused by traction on an abducted arm resulting in Klumpke's palsy. The classical presentation is of a claw hand in which the forearm is supinated and the wrist and all fingers are flexed. This is caused by the paralysis of the intrinsic muscles of the hand and the flexors of the wrist and fingers. Involvement of T1 may also result in Horner syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Median nerve injury\",\"e\":\"The median nerve originates from C5-T1 segments and innervates the flexors of the forearm (except flexor carpi ulnaris and flexor digitorum profundus muscles* to the ring and little finger) and the lumbricals 1 and 2, opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis and flexor pollicis brevis muscles. Distal median nerve injuries can result in an ape hand deformity at rest, which presents as an inability to abduct the thumb, leaving it resting in adduction. Proximal median nerve injuries result in the hand of benediction when attempting to form a fist. The index and middle finger remain extended at the metacarpophalangeal joints and interphalangeal joints because of denervation of the lateral lumbricals and flexor digitorum profundus respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"C5, C6 root injury\",\"e\":\"Upper brachial plexus injuries (C5, C6) are caused by excessive lateral flexion of the neck resulting in Erb's palsy. The classical presentation is of a waiter's tip position whereby the limb hangs by the side, medially rotated with the forearm extended and pronated.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Radial nerve injury\",\"e\":\"The radial nerve originates from C5-T1 segments and innervates the extensors of the arm and forearm. Radial nerve injury results in a wrist drop due to the inability to extend the hand and fingers.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A 37-year-old woman is seen in the hand clinic with paraesthesia in the thumb, index and middle fingers together with a positive Tinel's and Phalen's test at the wrist. A diagnosis of carpal tunnel syndrome is made. Which one of the following is correct with regard to this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct with regard to this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"May be caused by metabolic disorders such as gout, diabetes and hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"Carpal tunnel syndrome presents with paraesthesia of the radial 3.5 digits and weakness of the thenar muscle from compression of the median nerve within the carpal tunnel. Sensation within the palm is unaffected, as the palmer branch of the median nerve does not travel through the carpal tunnel. Tinel's, Phalen's and Durkan's tests are all provocation tests used clinically to assess for carpal tunnel syndrome. Carpal tunnel syndrome is associated with pregnancy, arthritis, acromegaly, hypothyroidism, diabetes, gout, alcoholism, tumours and connective tissue disorders.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"May be caused by inflammation of palmaris longus tendon\",\"e\":\"The palmaris longus tendon runs superficially within the forearm and inserts onto the transverse carpal ligament, which forms the roof of the carpal tunnel. It can be used clinically for tendon grafting, although about 10% of the general population lack a palmaris longus tendon. The median nerve in the forearm runs between the flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundus, deep to the palmaris longus tendon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"May cause anaesthesia of the little finger\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve supplies sensation to the little finger and ulna half of the ring finger, and passes through Guyon's cannel at the wrist. Guyon's canal is ulnar to the carpal tunnel and contains the ulnar artery and nerve. The canal is bordered on the ulnar side by the pisiform and on the radial side by the hook of the hamate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"May cause wasting of abductor digiti minimi (ADM)\",\"e\":\"The abductor digiti minimi is a muscle of the Hypothenar eminence in the hand and is supplied by the ulnar nerve, which is unaffected in carpal tunnel syndrome. The median nerve enters the hand via the carpal tunnel and dives into two branches, the recurrent branch (innervating thenar muscles) and the palmar digital branch (innervating palmar surface and lateral three and a half digits). Wasting of the thenar muscles, in particular abductor pollicis brevis muscles can be seen in carpal tunnel syndrome, but this would not typically give rise to wasting of abductor digiti minimi muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Occludes the ulnar artery\",\"e\":\"Ulnar artery occiusion (also known as hypothenar hammer syndrome) is a rare post- traumatic digital ischaemia due to thrombosis of the ulnar artery at Guyon's canal. It presents with pain over the hypothenar eminence and paraesthesia in an ulnar nerve distribution in the hand\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old man is seen in the Out-patient Hand Clinic with a long history of progressively worsening flexion contracture of his left ring and little finger. He cannot grip objects and has difficulty doing his buttons. Which one of the following is correct regarding his condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding his condition?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Central cords displace the neurovascular bundle\",\"e\":\"In Dupuytren's contracture, central cords are found in the digits and insert onto the flexor sheath at the proximal interphalangeal joint (PIPJ) level causing metacarpophalangeal joint (MCPJ) contracture. Central cords are not involved with the neurovascular bundle. Spiral cords travel up the sides of the digits displacing the neurovascular bundle centrally and superficially, and cause contractures at the PIPJ.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is caused by contraction of the palmar fascia\",\"e\":\"Dupuytren's contracture is a common benign fibroproliferative disorder affecting the palmer fascia. It is a progressive disorder, presenting initially with thickening of the palmer fascia, after which a firm painless nodule develops, which normally precedes cord formation, which causes gradual contraction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is more common in people of African origin than European\",\"e\":\"Dupuytren's contracture commonly affects elderly men of northern European descent with a pronounced genetic predisposition. It is rare in those people from African origin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"May be associated with retroperitoneal fibrosis\",\"e\":\"Dupuytren's contracture is associated with knuckle pads (Garrod's pads), penile fibrous plaques (Peyronie's disease) and plantar fibromatosis (Ledderhose's disease). Retroperitoneal fibrosis, however, is not associated with Dupuytren's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Most commonly affects the little finger\",\"e\":\"The ring finger is most commonly affected in Dupuytren's contracture followed by the little finger, middle finger, then index finger.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man is admitted to the day case unit for release of carpal tunnel under local anaesthesia. Your consultant has asked that you start the procedure and will be in shortly.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carpal tunnel syndrome is associated with Bennett's fracture\",\"e\":\"A Bennett's fracture is an intra-articular fracture of the base of the first metacarpal, which extends into the carpometacarpal joint and is not associated with carpal tunnel syndrome\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Carpal tunnel syndrome is associated with Golfer's elbow\",\"e\":\"Golfer's elbow is also known as medial epicondylitis commonly caused by repetitive activities that cause micro-trauma to the insertion of the flexor-pronator mass. Golfer's elbow is not associated with carpal tunnel syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"During the procedure, a longitudinal incision is made ulnar to the interthenar eminence and should not cross the distal wrist crease\",\"e\":\"The skin incision for a carpal tunnel decompression is made in line with the radial border of the ring finger from the distal wrist crease to Kaplan's line (a line from the apex of the interdigital fold between thumb and index finger to the hook of the hamate). Crossing the wrist crease at right angles may result in a hyper-trophic and painful scar, as well as a wrist flexion contracture.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Opening of Guyon's canal leads to release of pressure on the median nerve\",\"e\":\"Opening the Guyon's canal leads to release of pressure on the ulnar nerve. Guyon's canal is medial to the carpal tunnel and contains the ulnar artery and ulnar nerve. The canal is bordered on the ulnar side by the pisiform and on the radial side by the hook of the hamate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Palmaris longus passes through the carpal tunnel\",\"e\":\"The palmaris longus tendon runs superficially within the forearm and inserts onto the transverse carpal ligament, and does not pass through the carpal tunnel. The deep carpal arch forms the floor of the carpal tunnel and the transverse carpal ligament forms the roof The scaphoid and trapezium and the hook of the hamate form the lateral border and the pisiform the medial border. The carpal tunnel contains the tendon of the flexor pollicis longus, four tendons of the flexor digitorum profundus, four tendons of the flexor digitorum superficialis and the median nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"The rotator cuff stabilises the otherwise highly unstable shoulder joint. There are four of these mucles in total: infraspinatus, supraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis.\\n\\nWhich muscle of the rotator cuff is mainly involved in impingement of the shoulder?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscle of the rotator cuff is mainly involved in impingement of the shoulder?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Infraspinatus . (-\",\"e\":\"The infraspinatus muscle has its origin at the infraspinous fossa and inserts onto the middle facet of the greater tuberosity. The suprascapular nerve from the upper trunk of the brachial plexus innervates the infraspinatus muscle and its action is the external rotation of the shoulder. It is not involved with shoulder impingement.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Supraspinatus\",\"e\":\"The supraspinatus muscle has its origin at the supraspinous fossa and its tendon passes through the subacromial space before attaching to the superior facet on the greater tuberosity. The suprascapular nerve from the upper trunk of the brachial plexus innervates the supraspinatus muscle and its action initiates shoulder abduction. Shoulder impingement results from extrinsic compression of the supraspinatus tendon as it passes through the subacromial space resulting in a 'painful arc' occurring during 50° to 130° of abduction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Teres minor\",\"e\":\"The teres minor muscle has its origin at the lateral border of the scapular and inserts onto the inferior facet of the greater tuberosity. The axiliary nerve supplies the teres minor muscle and its action is the external rotation of the shoulder. The teres minor muscle is not involved with shoulder impingement.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Subscapularis\",\"e\":\"The subscapularis muscle has it origin from the subscapular fossa and inserts on to the lesser tuberosity. The upper and lower subscapularis nerves from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus supply the subscapularis muscles and its action is to internally rotate the shoulder.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"None of the above\",\"e\":\"The shoulder joint is a synovial ball and socket joint surrounded by a think and loose fibrous capsule that permits a wide range of movement. The blood supply of the shoulder joint is derived from branches of the anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries from the axillary artery and the suprascapular artery from the subclavian artery. The rotator cuff consists of the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis. Shoulder impingement results from supraspinatus tendonitis, which normally presents with a 'painful arc' occurring during 50° to 130° of abduction.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"A poorly fitting crutch has injured the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. The patient attends the fracture clinic with new onset of neurological deficit in the affected upper limb.\\n\\nWhich one of the following clinical pictures is this likely to produce?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following clinical pictures is this likely to produce?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"A poorly fitting crutch has injured the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. The patient attends the fracture clinic with new onset of neurological deficit in the affected upper limb\\n\\nWhich one of the following clinical pictures is this likely to produce?\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Weak shoulder abduction and wrist drop\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lack of sensation of half the ring finger and the whole little finger together with hypothenar muscle wasting\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The upper limb adopting the 'waiters tip' position\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Winging of the scapula\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Weak elbow flexion and the 'Benedictine sign'\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A man presents to your orthopaedic clinic with a 'claw hand' following an injury to his forearm. He is a manual worker and has found that the defect is life changing, he wants a diagnosis. Which nerve roots or their combinations are associated with this neurological injury?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve roots or their combinations are associated with this neurological injury?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"C6, C7 and C8\",\"e\":\"C6 gives rise to musculocutaneous nerve and the lateral root of median nerve. C7 gives rise to the axillary and radial nerve. C8 gives rise to the ulna and medial root of median nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"C7, C8 and T1\",\"e\":\"C7 gives rise to the axillary and radial nerve. C8 and T1 gives rise to the ulnar and medial root of median nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"C6 and C7\",\"e\":\"C6 gives rise to musculocutaneous nerve and the lateral root of median nerve. C7 gives rise to the axillary and radial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"C7 and C8\",\"e\":\"C7 gives rise to the axillary and radial nerve. C8 gives rise to the ulna and medial root of median nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"C8 and T1\",\"e\":\"The claw hand of a distal ulnar nerve palsy is caused by paralysis of the interossei and the ulna two lumbricals with the resultant unopposed action of extensors and flexor digitorum profundus (which would also be affected in a proximal ulnar nerve injury, eg at the elbow). The ulnar nerve is the direct continuation of the medial cord (C8 and T1).\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old woman presents to the Orthopaedic Clinic with problems using her left hand. She sustained a disiocated left elbow 1 month previously which was reduced in the Emergency Department. On examination she has normal wrist extension, thumb abduction, and elbow flexion. However, Froment's test is positive.\\n\\nWhich nerve is Froment's test assessing?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve is Froment's test assessing?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SI Axillary nerve\",\"e\":\"The axillary nerve can be assessed by testing the deltoid function (shoulder abduction) and sensation in the regimental badge area (the skin covering the inferior region of the deltoid), which is innervated by the superior lateral cutaneous nerve, a branch of the axillary nerve. The axillary nerve can be damaged following a shoulder dislocation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Median nerve\",\"e\":\"Getting the patient to make an 'OK' sign ensuring the tip of the index finger and thumb touch assesses median nerve. This tests the function of the index finger flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) and flexor pollicis longus (FPL) muscles, which are supplied by the anterior interosseous nerve, a branch of the median nerve. Assessing sensation of the radial border of the index finger can test the sensory function of the median nerve. The median nerve can be damaged following a displaced distal radius fracture, both bone forearm fracture and or supracondylar fracture.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Musculocutaenous nerve\",\"e\":\"Testing the bicep brachii, brachialis and coracobrachialis muscles with elbow flexion and forearm supination assesses the motor function of the musculocutaneous nerve. Assessing sensation of the lateral side of the forearm from the elbow to wrist tests the sensory function of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm, a branch of the musculocutaneous nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Radial nerve\",\"e\":\"Getting the patient to extend their wrist or to give a 'thumbs up' assesses the function of the wrist extensors and extensor pollicis longus (EPL) respectively, which are supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve, a branch of the radial nerve. Assessing sensation of the first dorsal web space can test the sensory function of the superficial branch of the radial nerve The radial nerve can be injured following a mid-shaft humerus fracture, as it passes through the radial groove.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulnar nerve\",\"e\":\"Froment's test assesses the motor function of the unar nerve by testing the action of adductor pollicis. The patient is asked to hold a piece of paper between the thumb and a flat palm as the paper is pulled away. An abnormal test is demonstrated by recruitment of the flexor pollicis longus muscle, seen with flexing at the interphalangeal joint of the thumb to maintain grip pressure. Assessing sensation of the lateral border of the little finger can test the sensory function of the ulnar nerve. The ulnar nerve runs posterior to the medial epicondyle within the cubital tunnel at the elbow and is prone to injury following elbow dislocation.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"A 43-year-old woman presents with pain and paraesthesia of the medial aspect of the foot, exacerbated by walking. She is diagnosed with tarsal syndrome and receives appropriate management.\\n\\nWhich nerve has been injured?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve has been injured?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Common fibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The common fibular (peroneal) nerve branches into the deep and superficial fibular nerves supplying the anterior and lateral compartments of the lower limb respectively. Motor function can be assessed by ankle dorsal flexion, which tests the tibialis anterior muscle. Sensory function can be assessed by testing sensation to the dorsum of the foot, the deep fibular nerve innervates the first dorsal web space and the rest is supplied by the superficial fibular nerve: The common peroneal nerve can be injured by a fibular neck fracture, an incorrectly fitted below knee plaster or external compression from being in a lithotomy boot during general anaesthesia\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The femoral nerve supplies the muscles of anterior compartment of the thigh, which assist in hip flexion and knee extension. The femoral nerve also supplies sensation to the skin overlying the anterior thigh. Clinically the femoral nerve and its branches can be blocked to interrupt pain signals, particularly useful in hip fracture patients.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh\",\"e\":\"The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh is a nerve from the lumbar plexus (L2-3) that innervates the skin on the lateral part of the thigh.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sural nerve\",\"e\":\"The sural nerve originates from branches from the tibial and common peroneal nerve, it travels along with the short saphenous vein and supplies sensation to the lateral aspect of the foot and ankle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tibial nerve\",\"e\":\"The tibial nerve originates from the sciatic nerve. It runs inferior to the flexor retinaculum of the foot alongside the tibial artery and vein, the tibialis posterior muscle, and the flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum longus muscles. If the nerve is compressed within the tarsal tunnel, then the clinical syndrome described above can result.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"A 1-day-old boy is referred to the paediatricians with omphalocoele, microcephaly, difficulty feeding and cryptorchidism. He is diagnosed with trisomy 18 after further investigation. Which lower limb abnormality is associated with this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which lower limb abnormality is associated with this condition?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amelia\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Congenital talipes equinovarus\",\"e\":\"Trisomy 18 is also known as Edwards' syndrome, and presents with a variety of developmental defects. It is associated with talipes equinovarus (or 'club foot\\\") and polydactyly.\\n\\nAmelia is a rare birth defect of lacking one or more limbs.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Genu valgus\",\"e\":\"Genu valgus is commonly called 'knocked knee' and is a condition in which the knees angle in to touch each other and the distal limb is bent out laterally.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Genu varus\",\"e\":\"Genu varus is commonly called 'bowed legs' and is a condition in which the knees bow outwards and the distal limb is bent in medially.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Meromelia\",\"e\":\"Meromelia is a birth defect of lacking a part, but not all, of one or more limbs with the presence of a hand or foot. It results in a shrunken and deformed extremity.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old woman felt a pop at the back of her leg while playing tennis and she presents with pain and weakness. On examination, she has a positive Thompson-Simmonds test.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Thompson's test is lack of plantar flexion when calf is squeezed\",\"e\":\"Thompson's test is positive in Achilles' tendon rupture, which is lack of plantar flexion when calf is squeezed\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Thompson's test is lack of dorsiflexion when calf is squeezed\",\"e\":\"Thompson's test is lack of plantar flexion when calf is squeezed.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The condition affects women more than men\",\"e\":\"Achilles' tendon rupture is more common in men.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The condition is most commonly due to direct impact trauma\",\"e\":\"Achilles' tendon rupture is more commonly due to sudden dorsiflexion or plantar flexion of the ankle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Plantar flexion is exaggerated by the condition\",\"e\":\"Dorsiflexion is exaggerated as it is normally limited by the Achilles' tendon. Plantar flexion is limited.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"A patient develops a painful calf following surgery for a fractured tibia. They have increasing morphine requirements to remain comfortable.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding compartment syndrome is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding compartment syndrome is true?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pressure monitoring is required to confirm the diagnosis before surgical intervention\",\"e\":\"Compartment syndrome is a clinical diagnosis. While pressure monitoring may confirm raised pressures, it is not required and can be misleading in certain cases.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pain is aggravated by active stretch of the muscle group within the compartment\",\"e\":\"Pain is made worse by passive stretching. Usually there will be a degree of pain on active stretching, however pain on passive stretch is a more concerning finding.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"If significant rhabdomyolysis occurs, this could result in hepatic failure\",\"e\":\"Rhabdomyolysis would cause renal failure, not hepatic failure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Compartment syndrome is an emergency and immediate treatment is required\",\"e\":\"Compartment syndrome should be treated as soon as possible with fasciotomies. Failure to treat leads to complications including tissue ischaemia and necrosis, Volkmann's contracture, and rhabdomyolysis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Orthopaedic casts should remain on until the patient is taken to theatre to prevent infection\",\"e\":\"Orthopaedic casts should be removed as soon as possible as this will reduce the intra- compartmental pressure instantly\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"You are assisting the paediatric orthopaedic consultant. They ask you about likely cases you will see in the clinic.\\n\\nWhat is the commonest pathological cause of the limping child aged 4-8 years?\",\"lead\":\"What is the commonest pathological cause of the limping child aged 4-8 years?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Congenital dislocation of the hip (CH)\",\"e\":\"Congenital dislocation of the hip has an incidence of approximately 5-20 in 1000 live births, falling to roughly 1-2 per 1000 infants 3 weeks later due to spontaneous stabilisation. It is more common in girls. It usually presents in the first 3 years of life, although most cases are now diagnosed with neonatal screening.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis (JIA) -\",\"e\":\"JIA is the classification for autoimmune arthritis in childhood replacing juvenile chronic/rheumatoid arthritis' and Still's disease. Those affected are usually very young children, or older boys (aged 9 years and upwards).\\n\\nLeukaemia should be considered in the differential diagnosis for 'the limping child', but it is not the most common pathological cause in this group.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Leukaemia\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Perthes' disease\",\"e\":\"Perthes' disease is a condition in which avascular necrosis of the femoral head occurs. The incidence is approximately 1: 10 000, the male to female ratio is 4: 1. It presents between the ages of 4 and 8 years with a limp. The characteristic X-ray appearances may not become evident for up to 3 months after initial presentation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Slipped upper femoral epiphysis (SUFE)\",\"e\":\"Slipped upper femoral epiphysis presents between the ages of 10 and 16 years and is more common in boys than girls.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":60,\"stem\":\"A patient is referred to you from the obstetric baby checking service. They suspect a lower limb developmental abnormality.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH)?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Both hips are affected in the majority of patients\",\"e\":\"Both hips are affected in up to one-third of patients.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"In a newborn the femoral head will show on an ultrasound scan\",\"e\":\"Ultrasound scanning will show both the position of the femoral head and the shape of the acetabulum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In the newborn the femoral head will show on a plain radiograph\",\"e\":\"X-ray examination is of value in established cases, but in the newborn the femoral head is cartilaginous so it does not show on a plain radiograph.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It affects about 1 in 10000 new borns\",\"e\":\"The incidence of developmental DDH is about 1 in 1000 newborns.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is equally common in boys and girls\",\"e\":\"DDH is more common in giris.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"Hip surgery makes up a major part of orthopaedic emergency and elective work. Avascular necrosis of the femoral head is a severe complication.\\n\\nIt is most commonly seen following which one of these injuries?\",\"lead\":\"It is most commonly seen following which one of these injuries?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior dislocation of the hip\",\"e\":\"Anterior dislocation of the hip does not typically disrupt the vascular supply to the head of the femur and is not associated with avascular necrosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Displaced mid-shaft femoral fracture\",\"e\":\"A displaced mid-shaft femoral fracture does not disrupt the blood supply to the femoral head.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Intertrochanteric fracture of the femoral neck\",\"e\":\"Intracapsular, more than extracapsular, neck of femur fractures are associated with disruption of the vascular supply to the femoral head.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intracapsular fracture of the femoral neck\",\"e\":\"Intracapsular neck of femur fractures are associated with disruption of the vascular supply to the femoral head. The incidence of avascular necrosis (AVN) is up to 15% in non-displaced fractures and increases to nearly 90% with untreated, completely displaced fractures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Posterior dislocation of the hip\",\"e\":\"Posterior dislocation of the hip does not typically disrupt the vascular supply to the head of the femur and is not associated with avascular necrosis.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"Osteoarthritis and fractures in the hip are common. Arthroplasty is a definitive treatment From the following options, which one is the most common postoperative complication of total hip arthroplasty?\",\"lead\":\"Arthroplasty is a definitive treatment From the following options, which one is the most common postoperative complication of total hip arthroplasty?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Asymptomatic Deep Vein Thrombosis\",\"e\":\"Thromboembolism occurs in 50% of cases of hip replacement, despite prophylaxis, but these rarely progress to fatal pulmonary embolism.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Dislocation\",\"e\":\"Dislocation occurs in 2-5% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Heterotropic ossification →\",\"e\":\"Heterotopic ossification affects approximately 10% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Leg length discrepancy with Trendelenburg gait\",\"e\":\"While this may occur after a total hip arthroplasty, thromboembolism is the more common postoperative complication.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prosthesis infection\",\"e\":\"Postoperative infection of the prosthetic joint is uncommon (<1%).\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"Total hip arthroplasty is a routine procedure, however it can cause major complicat ons. There are different techniques to complete the procedure, each with benefits and disadvantages.\\n\\nWhich nerve is potentially at greatest danger of iatrogenic injury during total hip arthroplasty via the posterior approach?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve is potentially at greatest danger of iatrogenic injury during total hip arthroplasty via the posterior approach?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1'5' Femoral nerve\",\"e\":\"latrogenic injury to the femoral nerves is rare, accounting for less than 10% of all nerve injuries following hip surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh\",\"e\":\"Lateral skin incisions should be planned to avoid the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Obturator nerve\",\"e\":\"latrogenic injury to the obturator nerves is rare, accounting for less than 10% of all nerve injuries following hip surgery-\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sciatic nerve\",\"e\":\"Sciatic nerve injury is the most common peripheral nerve injury occurring in total hip replacement.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior gluteal nerve\",\"e\":\"The superior gluteal nerve is retracted with the muscle intra-operatively and is unlikely to be injured\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"Pes cavus causes a foot deformity. In severe cases corrective surgery may be indicated.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding pes cavus is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding pes cavus is true?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pes cavus can be diagnosed using non-weight-bearing X-rays\",\"e\":\"X-rays should be taken while weight bearing.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Compensatory utilisation of extensor digitorum longus to dorsiflex the ankle results in clawing of the toes\",\"e\":\"Clawing of the toes occurs when the intrinsic muscles develop contractures and the long extensor to the toes (extensor digitorum longus), is recruited to assist in ankle dorsiflexion. This causes claw toe deformity due to a change in its axis of pull.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pes cavus is almost always asymptomatic and does not require treatment\",\"e\":\"Pes cavus may cause significant pain due to metatarsal compression.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Most cases are idiopathic\",\"e\":\"The aetiology of pes cavus can be identified approximately 80% of the time. The remaining 20% of cases are idiopathic and non-progressive. The causes include malunion of calcaneal or talar fractures, burns, sequelae resulting from compartment syndrome, residual clubfoot, and neuromuscular disease. Identifying the actiology is essential to determine if the deformity is progressive, which assists in operative planning.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pes cavus is a high arch of the foot that flattens with weight bearing\",\"e\":\"Pes cavus is a high arch of the foot that does not flatten with weight bearing.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"In pes planus, the whole foot is everted around ts longitudinal axis and the medial arch comes into contact with the ground when standing.\\n\\nWhich one of these anatomical structures is the dynamic stabilizer of the medial plantar arch?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these anatomical structures is the dynamic stabilizer of the medial plantar arch?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acetabulum pedis\",\"e\":\"The 'acetabulum pedis' maintains the medial longitudinal arch and acts as an important static stabilizer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Calcaneo-navicular (spring) gament\",\"e\":\"The spring ligament complex is the most frequently affected static stabilizer in symptomatic pes planus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lisfranc joint\",\"e\":\"The Lisfranc joint connects the tarsals and metatarsals. It does not have a role in stabilising the medial plantar arch.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Plantar fascia\",\"e\":\"The plantar fascia is the aponeurosis that connects the heel to the toes. It does not have a role in stabilising the medial plantar arch.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tibialis posterior\",\"e\":\"The tibialis posterior muscle and corresponding tendon are crucial to hindfoot position and foot flexibility during the gait cycle.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"Dislocation of the hip joint in young adults is an orthopaedic emergency. Without prompt reduction this can result in a life changing injury.\\n\\nWhich one of these complications increases in incidence the longer the hip remains dislocated?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these complications increases in incidence the longer the hip remains dislocated?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Avascular necrosis of the femoral head\",\"e\":\"Avascular necrosis of the femoral head occurs in 2-17% of patients. The longer it takes to relocate a hip, the higher the risk of avascular necrosis. Early relocation of a hip can make the difference between a healthy joint and a chronically disabled joint.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Osteoarthritis\",\"e\":\"Osteoarthritis is a recognised complication of hip joint dislocation, but its incidence is not related to delayed relocation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary embolism\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary embolism is a common complication for hospital patients - especially those who are postoperative or are poorly mobilising: There is no proven association, however, with delayed relocation following hip joint relocation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Recurrent hip dislocation\",\"e\":\"There is no known association between delayed hip relocation and recurrent hip dislocation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sciatic nerve injury\",\"e\":\"Sciatic nerve injury is a recognised complication of hip joint dislocation, but its incidence has not been shown to increase with delayed relocation. Sciatic nerve injury may present as a foot drop.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":76,\"stem\":\"Osteochondritis of the navicular bone is known as Kohler's disease. It presents with pain in the middle part of the foot.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding Kohler's disease is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding Kohler's disease is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Avascular necrosis is the main pathological process that is thought to cause this condition\",\"e\":\"It is thought to be due to a disturbance of the blood supply causing avascular necrosis and delay in ossification.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Typically occurs in children aged 11-18 years old\",\"e\":\"Kohler's disease typically occurs in children aged 3-5 years old.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ligamentous strain is the main pathological process that is thought to cause this condition\",\"e\":\"Avascular necrosis is the main pathological process that is thought to cause this condition.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Patients rarely become symptom free following treatment\",\"e\":\"Normally symptoms disappear after a few weeks of strapping the foot and restricting activity, but rest in a cast may be necessary if there is severe pain. Eventually the foot becomes normal clinically and radiologically over a period of months.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Children complain of pain on the lateral side of the foot.\",\"e\":\"They complain of pain over the medial side of the foot and may present with a limp.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":78,\"stem\":\"Developmental dysplasia of the hip where the acetabulum does not fully cover the femoral head. Left untreated it can cause early onset arthritis.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a test used to examine children with this condition and causes the hip to give a distinctive click when the hip is flexed and then abducted?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a test used to examine children with this condition and causes the hip to give a distinctive click when the hip is flexed and then abducted?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Barlow's test\",\"e\":\"Barlow's test causes the clunk of dislocation as the hip is adducted.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Thomas' test\",\"e\":\"Thomas' test is used to exclude hip flexion contracture.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Kernig's test\",\"e\":\"Kernig's test is used to test for meningism and is performed by flexing the hip to 90° and extending the knee.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Homan's\",\"e\":\"Homan's test, although not recommended generally, is a calf squeeze test to screen for deep vein thrombosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ortolani's test\",\"e\":\"Ortolani's test causes the clunk of reduction as the hip is flexed and then abducted.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"Osteoporosis results in easy fractures. Recognition and treatment can prevent subsequent need for orthopaedic procedures.\\n\\nIt is most reliably diagnosed by which one of the following techniques?\",\"lead\":\"It is most reliably diagnosed by which one of the following techniques?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DEXA)\",\"e\":\"DEXA is currently the most precise and accurate method for diagnosing osteoporosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Measurement of serum osteocalcin\",\"e\":\"Biochemical indices of bone formation (eg osteocalcin) are of value in monitoring the response of patients to treatment, but not for diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Measurement of urinary collagen telopeptides\",\"e\":\"Biochemical indices of bone breakdown (eg N-terminal and C-terminal crosslinking telopeptides of type-1 collagen) are of value in monitoring the response of patients to treatment, but not for diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Computed tomography (CT) scan\",\"e\":\"Quantitative CT scan of the lumbar spine is less precise (and more expensive, and involves a greater exposure to radiation).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Quantitative ultrasonography\",\"e\":\"Quantitative ultrasound measurements on the calcaneum are being evaluated but have not been proven to be superior to DEXA.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":82,\"stem\":\"You notice an incidental bony lesion in a plain radiography. On showing the film to the orthopaedic consultant they recommend urgently bringing the patient to clinic.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most common benign bone tumour affecting individuals under the age of 21 years?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most common benign bone tumour affecting individuals under the age of 21 years?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chondromyxoid fibroma\",\"e\":\"Chondromyxoid fibroma is an extremely rare cartilaginous tumour.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AM Osteochondroma\",\"e\":\"An osteochondroma is a benign tumour that often protrudes from the outer contour of bones and is capped by growing cartilage. Around 80% of these lesions are noted before the age of 21 years.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Giant-cell tumour\",\"e\":\"Giant-cell tumours are uncommon, mostly benign bone tumours that typically present in the fourth or fifth decade of life.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Aneurysmal bone cyst\",\"e\":\"Aneurysmal bone cysts are tumour-like lesions that typically present in childhood or adolescence, but are not as common as osteochondromas.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Osteogenic sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Osteogenic sarcomas are not benign. They are aggressive malignant bone tumours of mesenchymal origin.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":84,\"stem\":\"A patient presents to the Orthopaedic Out-patient Clinic complaining of pain in his left tibia for the past 8 months. He said that the pain was particularly severe at night and was relieved by non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. An X-ray is taken and is suggestive of an osteoid\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true regarding osteoid osteomas?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding osteoid osteomas?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Osteoid osteomas are more common in women\",\"e\":\"Osteoid osteomas are about three times more common in men.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Osteoid osteomas typically present in the elderly\",\"e\":\"Osteoid osteomas tend to affect young adults.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The characteristic appearance on X-ray is a small radiopaque zone surrounded by a larger blastic zone\",\"e\":\"The characteristic appearance on X-ray is a small radiolucent zone surrounded by a larger sclerotic zone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Osteoid osteomas are typically benign tumours\",\"e\":\"Osteoid osteomas are typically benign tumours arising from osteoblasts.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Osteoid osteoma is more common in the small bones of the body.\",\"e\":\"Osteoid osteoma is more common in the long bones.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"A patient presented to the Orthopaedic Out-patient Clinic complaining of pain in his right tibia, with a palpable swelling. X-ray of the affected bone is suggestive of a giant-cell tumour and revealed a lytic lesion involving the epiphysis and extending into the soft tissues.\\n\\nWhich one of the following regarding giant-cell tumours is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regarding giant-cell tumours is true?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Giant-cell tumours are typically benign\",\"e\":\"Rarely, a giant-cell tumour can metastasise, even though it remains histologically benign.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Giant-cell tumours typically present in the elderly\",\"e\":\"Giant-cell tumours most commonly affect people in their twenties and thirties.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Giant-cell tumours typically occur in the diaphysis\",\"e\":\"Giant-cell tumours typically occur in the epiphyses.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Giant-cell tumours appear osteoblastic on X-ray\",\"e\":\"Benign giant-cell tumours appear lytic on X-ray.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Giant-cell tumours rarely recur\",\"e\":\"Giant-cell tumours are notorious for their tendency to recur.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":88,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman typist in a busy legal firm notices that she develops tingling and numbness over the palmar surface of her thumb, index and middle fingers after several hours at her computer workstation. Pain in the same area often occurs at night as well.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements about the diagnosis is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about the diagnosis is true?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The diagnosis is suspected further with a positive Tinel's sign or Phalen's sign\",\"e\":\"Carpel tunnel syndrome is supported by Tinel's sign (median nerve paraesthesia is reproduced by tapping at the volar surface of the wrist over the site of the median nerve in the carpal tunnel) and Phalen's sign (reproduction of tingling with wrist flexion).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The causes of symptoms are due to compression of the ulnar nerve\",\"e\":\"The symptoms of carpal tunnel syndrome are caused by compression of the median nerve in the carpal tunnel.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Osteoporosis is often associated with carpal tunnel syndrome\",\"e\":\"Carpal tunnel syndrome is associated with rheumatoid arthritis. Other associations include diabetes mellitus, hypothyroidism, acromegaly, amyloidosis and pregnancy-induced oedema in the carpal tunnel.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The condition is most common in the elderly\",\"e\":\"Carpal tunnel syndrome is most common in people aged 30-50 years.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The condition is more common in men\",\"e\":\"Carpal tunnel syndrome is more common in women.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":90,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old woman was treated non-operatively following an open fracture of the radial shaft. She returns to the hospital after having self-discharged despite the injury. Which one of the following early complications would you most likely expect to see?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following early complications would you most likely expect to see?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Infection\",\"e\":\"Infection is an early complication of fractures, especiaily when open. Ideally, open fractures should be fixed within 4-6 h of injury. All patients with open fractures should be given antibiotics.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Myositis ossificans\",\"e\":\"Myositis ossificans is a form of heterotopic calcification occurring in soft tissue, notably after dislocation of the elbow or a blow to the brachialis, deltoid or quadriceps muscles. Treatment includes initial rest, then gentle active movements. Occasionally, excision of the bony mass is required.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tencon rupture\",\"e\":\"Tendon rupture is a well-recognised late complication of fractures. Extensor pollicis longus rupture occurs (6-12 weeks) after fracture of the lower radius.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Volkmann's ischaemic contracture\",\"e\":\"Volkmann's ischaemic contracture occurs following an arterial injury or compartment syndrome n which the muscle is replaced by inelastic fibrous tissue. This may lead to claw hand or toes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Algodystrophy\",\"e\":\"Algodystrophy (Sudek's atrophy) is characterised by a painful post-traumatic extremity. Late atrophic changes are seen, with features of patchy osteoporosis on X-ray.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":93,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old man with known long-standing Paget's disease presents with increasing pain, especially at night, in his left femur. He also complains of weight loss and night sweats. On clinical examination, there is a palpable mass over the distal third of the femur.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abscess\",\"e\":\"The vignette is about a patient who is a known case of Paget's disease and has developed a mass in the femur. The differential ranges from an abscess to malignancy. However, night sweats and weight loss are more suggestive of the development of malignancy, and it is osteosarcoma in such cases.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ewing's Sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Development of a mass associated with night sweats and weight loss in a patient with a long-standing history of Paget's disease indicates a malignancy. Ewing's sarcoma is not known to develop in such patients.\\n\\nDevelopment of a mass associated with night sweats and weight loss in a patient with a long-standing history of Paget's disease indicates a malignancy. Ewing's sarcoma is not known to develop in such patients.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fibrosarcoma\",\"e\":\"Features in the vignette are suggestive of the development of malignancy, Fibrosarcoma does not develop in long-standing cases of Paget's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Osteochondroma\",\"e\":\"Osteochondroma does not develop in long-standing cases of Paget's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Osteosarcoma\",\"e\":\"Osteosarcoma is a rare, but well-recognised, complication of Paget's disease, and this accounts for the second peak in incidence around the sixth decade. It is most commonly found in the metaphysis of long bones and presents in the second or third decade of life in young adults, in the absence of Paget's disease. Osteosarcomas may be osteolytic or osteoblastic or show a mixed picture on X-ray. Sun-ray spicules represent new bone formation once the cortex has been destroyed. Bone formation in Codman's triangle is due to elevation of the periosteum by invasion of the tumour through the cortex.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":96,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old woman has been diagnosed with an atrophic non-union in the mid-shaft of her left humerus. She complains of minimal discomfort at the fracture site, but she is unable to make best use of her arm, as there is movement at the fracture site. She feels depressed, as she would like to go back to work as a cashier at the local bank as soon as possible. She is otherwise fit and well.\\n\\nWhat is the most appropriate line of treatment?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate line of treatment?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Electrical stimulation\",\"e\":\"The cause of non-union in this post-menopausal woman is most probably osteoporosis. Electrical stimulation is less likely to induce osteoblastic activity and new formation, and hence union rates are not high. This patient requires autologous bone graft with some sort of bone fixation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intramedullary nailing\",\"e\":\"In osteoporotic bone, the union rate is less for intramedullary nailing alone (66%), when compared with autologous bone graft combined with plating (98%).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bracing for 6 weeks\",\"e\":\"Bracing for 6 weeks will not induce new bone formation. The best management with the highest success rate is plating with autologous bone graft.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Plating and bone grafting\",\"e\":\"The highest union rate (98%) is observed in osteoporotic patients where autologous bone graft is used in combination with plating.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vitamin D and calcium supplements\",\"e\":\"The reason for non-union is most probably osteoporosis. Prescription of vitamin D and calcium supplements is less likely to induce new bone formation.\\n\\nNon-union is caused by: unrecognised delayed union; wide distraction and separation of the fracture surfaces; soft tissue interposition, excessive movement at the fracture site; and poor local blood supply. Painless movement at the fracture site is diagnostic of non-union, as distinct from delayed union which is painful. Two types of non-union are described: hypertrophic (bulbous bone ends) and atrophic (no calcification at bone ends).\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":99,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old man presents with a painful lump in his proximal femur. He also reports headaches and deafness. X-rays show new bone formation and sclerosis, with cortical thickening and coarse trabeculae. Laboratory results reveal increased alkaline phosphatase. Histology results demonstrate excessive osteoclast activity and paratrabecular fibrosis.\\n\\nWhich of the following is the first-line agent for treatment of this patient's condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the first-line agent for treatment of this patient's condition?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bisphosponates\",\"e\":\"Bisphosphonates suppress the osteoclastic activity. These can help regulate bone growth and reduce pain. Examples include risedronate, zoledronate and pamidronate. The most common side-effect of risedronate is gastritis, while that of pamidronate is flu-like symptoms\\n\\nHistory, examination and radiological findings are suggestive of Paget's disease of bone. It is characterised by enlargement and thickening of the bone, but the internal architecture of the bone is abnormal and the bone is unusually brittle. It affects men and women equally, most commonly from the age of 50 years. The most commonly affected sites are the pelvis and tibia. An elevated alkaline phosphatase level indicates increased osteoblastic activity. Complications include nerve compression, fractures, osteoarthritis, osteosarcoma, high- output cardiac failure and hypercalcaemia. Treatment focuses on suppressing bone turnover and includes the use of bisphosphonates as a first-line agent and calcitonin. Surgery may be required for pathological fractures, osteoarthritis and nerve entrapment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Calcitonin\",\"e\":\"Calcitonin is a second-line treatment for suppression of osteoclastic activity. It is prescribed in patients who do not tolerate bisphosphonates. Calcitonin is given as a daily injection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Calcium and Vitamin D\",\"e\":\"Calcium and vitamin D alone do not relieve the symptoms of Paget's disease. These are given to all the patients, especially those on bisphosphonates.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cortisol\",\"e\":\"Cortisol may have some role in the management of cardiac failure in patients with Paget's disease; however, it is not a first-line treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Parathyroid hormone\",\"e\":\"Parathyroid hormone causes bone resorption, hence it will worsen the symptoms. Calcitonin is a treatment of Paget's disease which blocks the effects of parathyroid hormone.\\n\\n1132\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"A patient suffers an ankle injury. On a plain X-ray, an avulsion fracture is present.\\n\\nWhich one of the following mechanisms is associated with avulsion fractures?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following mechanisms is associated with avulsion fractures?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ivis Axial loading\",\"e\":\"Axial loading causes compression fractures. It is common in falling from a height with landing on the feet. This can lead to crush fractures of the spine. The common findings are compression and/or wedging of the vertebral bodies.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Direct blow\",\"e\":\"Depressed fractures suggest a direct blow or shearing force. The forces tend to be localised. For example, a blow from a hammer to the skull can cause a depressed skull fracture. Comminuted fractures are usually caused due to a high-velocity injury. In this type of fracture, the bone is shattered into pieces, consisting of small and large fragments.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cortical bone destruction\",\"e\":\"Metastasis to the long bones can lead to cortical bone destruction. Even a trivial force can cause a pathological fracture. Occasionally, pathological bone fractures are the first presentation of an underlying malignancy. Bone biopsy in such cases may help in the diagnosis of an occult malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Twisting\",\"e\":\"Twisting can cause spiral fractures. A typical example is when the foot is caught during playing football and the player rotates the leg on a fixed foot. It leads to a spiral fracture of the tibia.\\n\\nBecause of the flexible nature of the bones, a greenstick fracture is more common in children. This type of fracture does not extend to both cortices of the bone. The healing in this type of fracture is usually quick and efficient. In compound fractures, the fractured bone protrudes through the skin, or a soft tissue wound leads to the fracture site. The risk for infection is highest in compound fractures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Powerful muscle contractions\",\"e\":\"Avulsion fractures are caused when the muscle or tendon contracts vigorously, so avulsing the piece of bone at the insertion of the bone. This type of fracture is common in the knee and shoulder joints.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":105,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old man, who is an active smoker, is seen in the Combined Orthoplastics Clinic 4 months after having had a tibial nail and free anterolateral thigh (ALT) flap for an open tibial fracture. There is no evidence of callus formation at the fracture site on plain radiography. However, the surgeons plan for no additional intervention.\\n\\nWhich of the following is most likely the cause of delayed union?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is most likely the cause of delayed union?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Micro-movement at the fracture site\",\"e\":\"Although too much movement at a fracture site can delay healing, 'micro-movements' promote callus formation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Smoking\",\"e\":\"Smoking can impair fracture union, wound healing and the success of a free flap. It is likely that smoking exerts these effects through impairing blood flow to healing tissues.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Myocutaneous flap cover\",\"e\":\"Myocutaneous flaps have better outcomes than fasciocutaneous flaps. However, success of flaps depends on the integrity of the blood supply. Cigarette smoking decreases the chances of success.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Internal fixation over external fixation\",\"e\":\"In Gustilo type IIIB open tibial fractures, in the absence of infection, better outcomes are seen with internal fixation and ALT free flaps. This approach reduces the risk for complications and the inconvenience of external fixators.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Use of gentamicin beads at time of debridement\",\"e\":\"Gentamicin beads are used to prevent infection and improve fracture healing. Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), however, can cause impairment of blood supply to the graft and increased chances of non-union/delayed union.\\n\\nDelayed union is the term applied when a fracture has not healed within the period of time that would be considered adequate for bone healing for that particular site. It is expected that union will eventually occur without further intervention. For tibial fractures, healing should occur in 16 weeks. Non-union is the term applied to a fracture that will not unite without additional intervention. The time limit is usually 6-9 months for tibial fractures. The causes of a delayed union of fracture include inadequate blood supply, infection (including acute and chronic osteomyelitis), excessive shearing movements between the fragments, loss of apposition between the fragments, over-distraction of the bony fragments by excessive traction, dissolution of the fracture haematoma by synovial fluid (may occur in fractures within joints), the presence of a foreign body in the fracture site and the presence of a tumour in the fracture site.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":108,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old man undergoes intramedulary nailing for a closed fracture of his left tibia. During the night, you are asked to see him as he complains of pain and his usual medications are not settling him. On examination, his left leg is swollen and tense. Distal pulses are not palpable.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely finding on dipstick urinalysis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely finding on dipstick urinalysis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Haemoglobin\",\"e\":\"The mechanism of trauma, the surgical procedure and the findings on clinical examination are suggestive of compartment syndrome. Ischaemic injury to muscle leads to rhabdomyolysis. Myoglobin is released in the urine and detected as haemoglobin on the urine dipstick - the dipstick would be false positive for haemoglobin. Compartment syndrome is typically due to trauma, eg haemorrhage after fracture, surgery or blunt trauma or from post-ischaemic swelling. It can occur after any injury, including crush injuries, following prolonged compression of a limb, such as a tight cast, and prolonged surgery. It occurs more commonly in the leg and forearm. The most important symptom is pain out of proportion to the injury. Unless an arterial injury has occurred, peripheral pulses remain normal in most cases of acute compartment syndrome. The 4 Ps are pain, paraesthesiae, paralysis and pulselessness; however, these appear late and it is recommended not to wait till these signs appear. Management is emergency fasciotomy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Alkalotic urine\",\"e\":\"Urine is acidic in compartment syndrome due to associated metabolic acidosis. Alkalinisation of urine is a therapeutic measure to prevent renal damage. It makes myoglobin soluble in the urine and help in excretion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Red blood cell (RBC) casts\",\"e\":\"RBC casts are a feature of glomerulonephritis, and not of compartment syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"0 Glucosuria\",\"e\":\"There is no cause for glucose to be found in the urine. The patient is not noted to be diabetic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"High urinary creatine phosphokinase (CPK)\",\"e\":\"CPK is a measure in serum, and not in urine. Increased levels of serum CPK in the range of 1000-5000 units/ml or greater suggest compartment syndrome. Combined with the presence of myoglobinuria, it is diagnostic of compartment syndrome. The appearance of CPK may suggest impending renal failure.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":111,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old woman presents with a painful knee. The pain has been gradually increasing, is worse at the end of the day and with exercise and is associated with a swollen joint. Her full blood count (FBC), erythrocyte sedimentation rate. (ESR) and C-reactive protein (CRP) are all normal.\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most likely condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most likely condition?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Septic arthritis\",\"e\":\"Septic arthritis is inflammation of a joint caused by a bacterial infection. Septic arthritis has a more acute presentation. Any joint can be affected by septic arthritis, but it is most common in the knees and hips. Symptoms include pain and tenderness over a joint, pain on moving the joint and feeling unwell. It is an uncommon, but serious, infection. Urgent treatment is needed. This includes antibiotic medicines and drainage of the infected fluid from the joint to prevent permanent joint damage.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Osteoarthritis\",\"e\":\"Osteoarthritis presents with a long-standing history. The pain is worse with exercise and occurs towards the end of the day. As it is not an inflammatory condition, so it does not cause any such blood result abnormalities. The knee is very commonly affected by osteoarthritis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Rheumatoid arthritis (RA)\",\"e\":\"RA is a condition that causes inflammation in many joints of the body, which particularly affects the hands, feet, wrists, ankles and knees: It tends to occur symmetrically. The pain is relieved by exercise and is worse on waking/resting. RA causes anaemia of chronic disease and, during flare-ups, raised ESR and CRP levels.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gout\",\"e\":\"Gout is a form of inflammatory arthritis characterised by recurrent attacks of a red, tender, hot and swollen joint. Pain typically comes on rapidly in < 12 hours. The joint at the base of the big toe is most commonly affected. Diagnosis is by finding high levels of uric acid in blood and negatively birefringent crystals on synovial fluid examination. Treatment of an acute attack is by painkillers. Prevention of attacks depends upon the underlying cause of gout, ie increased production vs decreased excretion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reactive arthritis.\",\"e\":\"Reactive arthritis is a chronic form of arthritis that often occurs following an infection of the genital, urinary or gastrointestinal system, It may involve the knee joint. However, there is no association with physical activity.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":114,\"stem\":\"A 16-year-old girl presents with a 4-month history of an aching discomfort in the distal right femur, relieved with simple analgesia. A plain X-ray of the knee shows an area of slight sclerosis.\\n\\nWhich is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chondroma\",\"e\":\"Chondromas are made up almost entirely of cartilage and are common in the hands and feet. Radiological appearance is seen as a saucerisation of the adjacent bony cortex, with a sclerotic periosteal reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fibroma\",\"e\":\"A non-ossifying fibroma (also called fibroxanthoma) is a common benign bone tumour in children and adolescents. These are well-circumscribed lytic lesions. No treatment is needed in asymptomatic patients, and spontaneous remission with replacement by bone tissue may happen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Osteochondroma\",\"e\":\"Osseocartilaginous exostoses are the most common benign tumours of the bones. These often appear as a bony pedicle growing away from the epiphyseal plate, covered in a large cartilage cap. Classic radiographic features include orientation of the lesion away from the physis and medullary continuity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Osteoclastoma\",\"e\":\"Osteoclastomas are commonly found lying close to the epiphyseal plate, with destruction of the overlying cortex. These are also called giant cell tumours. Common sites are around the knee joint. A typical radiological finding is a soap bubble appearance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Osteoid osteoma\",\"e\":\"Osteoid osteomas are most common in the femur and tibia. They are unusual as benign tumours, in that they produce a constant aching pain which is classically relieved by simple analgesia. They can be difficult to see on plain radiographs.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":117,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old woman presents with a fragility fracture due to osteoporosis. While age and the female sex are non-modifiable risk factors for developing osteoporosis, an entire cohort of modifiable risk factors also exist.\\n\\nWhich of the following is most commonly associated with the development of osteoporosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is most commonly associated with the development of osteoporosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hormone replacement therapy (HRT))\",\"e\":\"HRT reduces the risk for osteoporosis. HRT is aimed at correcting the ostrogen deficiency which plays a major part in the pathogenesis of postmenopausal osteoporosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoparathyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hyperparathyroidism, and not hypoparathyroidism, causes secondary osteoporosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Thyrotoxicosis\",\"e\":\"Thyrotoxicosis causes acceleration of bone remodelling, and hence secondary osteoporosis. The extent of reduction in bone density is variable; however, it is reversible.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Osteoarthritis\",\"e\":\"Osteoporosis and osteoarthritis are two very different medical conditions. There is little in common. These concitions develop differently, have different symptoms, are diagnosed differently and are treated differently. No relationship could be found between the two. If anything, the relationship is inverse.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Warfarin\",\"e\":\"Although in tially suspected, long-term warfarin use has not been found to increase the risk for osteoporosis. Causes of osteoporosis are primary - related to the ageing process and decreased gonadal activity (most common).\\n\\nSecondary causes can be subdivided into:\\n\\n• nutritional: scurvy, malabsorption, malnutrition • endocrine: hyperparathyroidism, thyrotoxicosis, Cushing's disease, gonadal insufficiency • drug-related: corticosteroids, alcohol, heparin • malignant disease: carcinomatosis, leukaemia, multiple myeloma • systemic disease: rheumatoid arthritis (RA), tuberculosis (TB), chronic liver disease (especially primary biliary cirrhosis), ankylosing spondylitis • idiopathic: juvenile osteoporosis, post-climacteric osteoporosis.\\n\\nWeight-bearing exercise and ostrogen, whether endogenous or as HRT, reduce the risk for osteoporosis.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":123,\"stem\":\"An 18-month-old child presents with features suggestive of septic arthritis of the left hip. They are taken to theatre for a washout of the affected joint.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely causative organism?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely causative organism?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Neisseria gonorrhoeae\",\"e\":\"Neisseria gonorrhoeae is the most common organism among younger sexually active individuals, therefore it is not the most common cause in children less than 2 years of age.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemophilus influenzae\",\"e\":\"Haemophilus influenza used to be a common cause of septic arthritis in children less than 2 years of age, however due to introduction of vaccination, its incidence has decreased.\\n\\nReactive arthritis is seen commonly in patients with gastrointestinal (GI) or genitourinary infections. The most common pathogens are of Gl origin however Chlamydia trachomatis genitourinary infection is the second most common cause of reactive arthritis. Reactive arthritis is immunological in origin and is not septic.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus aureus\",\"e\":\"Staphylococcus aureus infection is the most common cause of acute bacterial arthritis in children and adults. The most common predisposing condition to this infection in adults is prosthetic joints.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Chlamydia trachomatis\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Salmonella\",\"e\":\"This organism is a more common cause of septic arthritis and osteomyelitis in sickle-cell disease patients.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":125,\"stem\":\"Infection in orthopaedic surgery is a notoriously difficult complication to manage. Maximal precautions are taken perioperatively to reduce the chance of infection for these cases.\\n\\nWhich one of the following orthopaedic procedures is an absolute indication for prophylactic antibiotics?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following orthopaedic procedures is an absolute indication for prophylactic antibiotics?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carpal tunnel decompression\",\"e\":\"Antibiotics are not required for carpal tunnel decompression as the procedure is grouped as clean and it does not involve insertion of any implant or prosthetic material.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diagnostic arthroscopy\",\"e\":\"Diagnostic arthroscopy does not require antibiotic prophylaxis. It is a clean procedure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diagnostic aspiration of a joint for infection\",\"e\":\"Antibiotics are contraindicated before joint aspiration for infection as they could give false- negative results.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intra-articular joint infection\",\"e\":\"Antibiotics are not usually required for joint injection unless the patient is immunocompromised.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Total hip replacement\",\"e\":\"Prophylactic antibiotics are indicated in any joint replacement and whenever a prosthesis/foreign body is implanted\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":127,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old farmer sustained a penetrating injury to his index finger. Two days later, the digit is swollen, and painful to move. On examination the index finger is held in slight flexion, passive extension of the digit is painful and there is tenderness throughout the flexor sheath of the index finger.\\n\\nWhich one of the following organism is most likely responsible for this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following organism is most likely responsible for this condition?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Herpes virus\",\"e\":\"Clinical findings are suggestive of pyogenic tenosynovitis. Herpes virus causes herpetic whitlow infection of the hand, usually on the fingers. Healthcare workers are prone to develop this as their hands are exposed to the saliva of patients carrying herpes virus. Findings include small, swollen, painful blood-tinged blisters, and sometimes numbness. The condition usually resolves in several weeks without any treatment or morbidity.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Candida albicans\",\"e\":\"Candida albicans causes chronic paronychia. It is more likely to occur with chronic immersion in water (as in dishwashers), previous trauma, or nail defects. It can be treated with topical antifungal agents. Occasionally, excision of a portion of the nail or removal of the entire nail may be necessary.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Eikenella corrodens\",\"e\":\"Eikenella corrodens is a fastidious facultative anaerobic Gram-negative bacillus that is present as endogenous microbiota in the mouth and upper respiratory tract as well as on other mucosal surfaces: It is associated with hand infections caused by human or animal bites.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pasteurella multocida\",\"e\":\"Pasteurella multocida is a small, Gram-negative bacteria. It is responsible for hand infections caused by animal bite, scratch, or lick.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus aureus\",\"e\":\"The most probable diagnosis is bacterial flexor tenosynovitis. It is usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus following a penetrating injury. Bacterial flexor tenosynovitis should be distinguished from stenosing tenosynovitis ('trigger finger) of the digital flexor tendons. Stenosing tenosynovitis mostly affects the thumb or ring finger but can involve any digit. Bacterial flexor tenosynovitis, on the other hand, results from penetrating wounds to the hand and the majority are due to Staphylococcus aureus infection. Any digit can be affected depending on the site of injury. Infections of the finger flexor tendons are serious, with the potential for substantial permanent functional impairment. Finger movements are severely restricted and the four cardinal signs of tendon sheath infection are: partially flexed posture of the finger; fusiform swelling of the finger; tenderness along the entire flexor tendon sheath; and severe pain from passive extension of the finger. The tenosynovitis can rapidly destroy the synovial gliding surfaces and result in healing with restrictive adhesions. A fulminating infection can lead to tendon necrosis. Early tenosynovitis can be treated with intravenous antibiotics, elevation and splinting. However, if no definite signs of improvement are seen after 24 h, surgical exploration or drainage must be considered as the consequences of a fulminating flexor tenosynovitis are so severe and permanent. Initial drainage should be with a limited proximal incision in the distal palm over the tendon sheath, through which a small catheter can be introduced. A small distal incision for outflow is then made to create a through-and-through irrigation system with minimal surgical damage. For the vast majority of cases these measures, along with antibiotics and supportive care will bring the situation promptly under control. In severe cases, however, radical drainage by opening the whole finger through a mid-axial incision and opening the sheath while carefully preserving the pulleys may be required.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":130,\"stem\":\"A 33-year-old intravenous (IV) drug user (IVDU) is admitted to the Emergency Department with septic shock and rapidly progressing cellulitis of his right upper limb. After admission infection has spread to the abdominal wall over a period of few hours.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is most important step in management of this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most important step in management of this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antibiotic therapy\",\"e\":\"This patient is suffering from necrotising fasciitis. Antibiotic therapy is part of treatment. However, definitive treatment requires liberal debridement\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Wide excision of the skin and necrotic fascia\",\"e\":\"Management of necrotising fasciitis requires liberal debridement of the skin and underlying fascia. Skin loss will require plastic procedures and negative wound therapy to cover the skin loss following debridement. In the early stages of necrotising fasciitis the skin appears normal; if untreated subcutaneous oedema develops with gangrene of the dermal layer. The infection is typically polymicrobial eg streptococci, bacteroides, haemolytic streptococci. often developing from a simple inoculation, or occurring in a post-operative wound. It requires aggressive treatment, with resuscitation, high-dose antibiotics, and radical debridement of the fascial planes affected. Antibiotic therapy alone is insufficient to limit the spread of necrotising fasciitis due to its aggressive nature and the definitive treatment remains early and aggressive surgical intervention. Delay often results in increased morbidity and mortality. If the perineum is involved, a colostomy is beneficial to help maintain wound cleanliness. The mortality is in the order of 30% and has remained static despite advances in intensive care. This is likely to be due to the difficult nature of diagnosing necrotising fasciitis early.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyperbaric oxygen therapy\",\"e\":\"There is no role of hyperbaric oxygen therapy in management of necrotising fasciitis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Conservative wound debridement\",\"e\":\"Liberal debridement is required. Infection is usually beyond the level of skin discoloration. The underlying fascia should be debrided until it starts bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Femoral angioplasty\",\"e\":\"Clinical presentation is not suggestive of acute thrombo/embolic event of the limb. Hence there is no role of angioplasty.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":133,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old woman with known insulin-dependent diabetes and obesity presents with erythema and swelling of the face. She rapidly becomes unwell. Examination reveals crepitus in the subcutaneous tissue. Contrast computed tomography (CT) reveals air pockets in the underlying muscles.\\n\\nWhich one of the followings is the most likely pathogen?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the followings is the most likely pathogen?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Streptococcus pneumoniae\",\"e\":\"It causes respiratory infection in diabetics.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Clostridium perfringens\",\"e\":\"The presence of the air pockets in the underlying muscles is suggestive of gas gangrene. Air pockets limited to subcutaneous tissues suggest necrotising fasciitis. The organism involved in gas gangrene is Clostridium perfringens. It may occur after trauma or surgery when it is wound related, In certain immunocompromised patients C. perfringens can causes spontaneous gas gangrene. These include patients with colorectal malignancy, diabetics and patient with colitis. Treatment includes antibiotics, wound debridement and hyperbaric oxygen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mycobacterium tuberculosis\",\"e\":\"It causes pulmonary or extra pulmonary tuberculosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pseudomonas\",\"e\":\"Pseudomonas is responsible for malignant or necrotising otitis externa in diabetic patients older than 35 years. It also causes diabetic foot infections. However, the description n the vignette is suggestive of gas gangrene for which the organism is C perfringens.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus aureus\",\"e\":\"Staphylococcus aureus causes diabetic foot infections and necrotising fasciitis. The involvement of muscles makes this organism unlikely.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":136,\"stem\":\"A 7-year-old boy is referred to an orthopaedic clinic with pain in his left forearm that has been getting progressively worse. His only medical history of note is a fracture of his left distal radius requiring manipulation and K-wire fixation 6 months earlier: X-rays of the left distal radius shows changes consistent with osteomyelitis. Which one of the following organisms is most likely to be responsible?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following organisms is most likely to be responsible?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gonococcus\",\"e\":\"Gonococcus (Neisseria gonorrhoeae) causes sexually transmitted disease (STD)-related arthritis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Meningococcus\",\"e\":\"Meningococcus (Neisseria meningitidis) does not cause any bone infections:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pneumococcus\",\"e\":\"Bone infection caused by a pneumococcus (Streptococcus pneumoniae) is extremely rare. It accounts for less than 2% of acute osteomyelitis in children.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Staphy/ococcus\",\"e\":\"Staphylococcus aureus infection is the most common cause of acute bacterial arthritis in children and adults. The most common predisposing condition to this infection in adults is prosthetic joints. Presence of a K-wire at fracture site makes it vulnerable to infection by Staphylococcus aureus. Staphylococcus aureus infection is the most common cause of acute bacterial arthritis in children and adults. The most common predisposing condition to this infection in adults is prosthetic joints. Positive blood cultures are only found in about 50% of proven cases of infection. Staphylococci are the most common causative organisms, but Streptococcus, gonococcus and pneumococcus are also responsible. Haematogenous spread may occur from a skin abrasion, a tooth abscess, a boil, any indwelling line or catheter, and in newborns an infected umbilical cord.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Salmonella Typhi\",\"e\":\"This is the common cause of septic arthritis and osteomyelitis in sickle-cell disease patients.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":139,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man has presented with discharge from his right foot. Examination reveals a red swollen foot with weak pulses and impaired sensation. The patient has had diabetes for last 15 years.\\n\\nWhich one of the following investigations is of highest diagnostic value?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following investigations is of highest diagnostic value?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ankle-brachial pressure index\",\"e\":\"It may suggest impaired arterial blood flow, however it has limited value in the diagnosis of underlying osteomyelitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Bone scan\",\"e\":\"The role of bone scans is controversial in the diagnosis of osteomyelitis as the numbers of false-positive and false-negative results are high.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan\",\"e\":\"Magnetic resonance imaging is the most sensitive and most specific imaging modality for the detection of osteomyelitis and provides superb anatomic detail and more accurate information of the extent of the infectious process and soft tissues involved. The diagnostic imaging of osteomyelitis can require the combination of diverse imaging techniques for an accurate diagnosis. Conventional radiography should always be the first imaging modality to start, as it provides an overview of the anatomy and the pathological conditions of the bone and soft tissues of the region of interest. Ultrasonography is most useful in the diagnosis of fluid collections, periosteal involvement, and surrounding soft tissue abnormalities and may provide guidance for diagnostic or therapeutic aspiration, drainage, or tissue biopsy. A computed tomography scan can be a useful method to detect early osseous erosion and to document the presence of sequestrum, foreign body, or gas formation but generally is less sensitive than other modalities for the detection of bone infection. Nuclear medicine imaging is particularly useful in identifying multifocal osseous involvement.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Radiographs\",\"e\":\"Plain radiographic studies of the diabetic foot may demonstrate demineralisation and Charcot joint and occasionally may suggest the presence of osteomyelitis. However it has high false-negative rate as it may fail in picking up osteomyelitis presenting at an early stage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transcutaneous oxygen pressures\",\"e\":\"Transcutaneous tissue oxygen studies are done in cases of vaso-occlusive disease. The indications include borderline situations in which the advisability of arterial bypass surgery may be unclear. It has no role in investigation of osteomyelitis. Transcutaneous oxygen pressures (TcpO,) are considered to be the gold standard to assess wound healing potential.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":142,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old male patient has sustained a single minimally displaced fracture of the tibial shaft. Their case is being discussed at the morning trauma meeting. Which one of the following is a risk factor for delayed union?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a risk factor for delayed union?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A closed fracture\",\"e\":\"This is favourable for early healing.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A high consumption of dairy products\",\"e\":\"This helps in bone healing.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"An intact fellow bone\",\"e\":\"Distraction of fragments causes delayed fracture healing. Application of traction, or intact fellow bone (in this case the fibula) causes distraction of bone fragments and hence delayed union.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pain at the fracture site.\",\"e\":\"Pain is not directly related to non/delayed union, however, there are some controversial reports about non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDS) and opioids: Studies have reported that fracture healing may be delayed due to the anti-inflammatory effects of analgesics.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Treatment in an above-knee cast\",\"e\":\"Minimal manipulation of the fracture haematoma is protective. Hence when indicated an above-knee cast is protective. In contrast, internal fixation can disturb the fracture haematoma and can cause delayed union. Causes of delayed union include an inadequate blood supply, infection, incorrect immobilisation and an intact fellow bone (such as the fibula in a tibial fracture). The fracture site is usually tender and the fracture remains visible, with very little callus formation, periosteal reaction or sclerosis at the bone-ends. Continued treatment is required and includes functional bracing, an excellent method of promoting bony union. A fracture should undergo internal fixation and bone grafting if union is delayed for more than 6 months and in the absence of any callus formation.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":145,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man presents with pain in the lower back. Examination reveals loss of lumbar lordosis, and restriction of spinal movements. He also has a positive Schober's test and reduced chest expansion.\\n\\nOf the following options, what is the most likely diagnosis based on this presentation?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following options, what is the most likely diagnosis based on this presentation?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ankylosing spondylitis\",\"e\":\"Loss of lumbar lordosis with restriction of spinal movements in a young patient are highly suggestive of ankylosing spondylitis. Ankylosing spondylitis is a systemic chronic autoimmune disease, causing sacroillitis, progressive spinal deformities and hip osteoarthritis. Patients present with stiffness: Radiographs show the classical bamboo spine appearance. Treatment is symptomatic, with physiotherapy. Spinal decompression and fusion is needed if there is a progressive neurological deficit.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cauda equina syndrome\",\"e\":\"The presentation of cauda equina syndrome is with sudden onset of pain in the lower limbs, associated with loss of control on bowel and bladder function. It is a surgical emergency and requires emergent decompression. The causes include any condition that causes narrowing of the spinal canal and compresses lower nerve roots.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lumbar disc herniation\",\"e\":\"Most disc herniations occur either at the L4-L5 or L5-S1 discs. Presentation may vary depending upon the level and severity of nerve root compression. Loss of lumbar lordosis may occur due to spasm and contraction of paravertebral muscles, however movements of the spine are not compromised. A magnetic resonance injury (MRI) scan is diagnostic. Treatment may be conservative or surgical depending upon severity of disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spinal stenosis\",\"e\":\"Any narrowing of the spinal canal can lead to spinal stenosis. It may be a developmental abnormality or a degenerative process, symptoms can mimic cauda equina syndrome Degenerative spinal stenosis is a disease of the elderly. Restriction of spinal movement is not a feature.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spondylolisthesis\",\"e\":\"The abnormal forward slip of one vertebral body on another is called spondylolisthesis. Severe cases can cause a narrowing of the spinal canal and lead to cauda equina syndrome. Loss of movements of the spine is not a feature.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":148,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man is admitted with a glass injury to the wrist. Examination reveals an inability to flex the middle finger at the distal interphalangeal joint (DIP). Which one of the following structures is most likely to be divided?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures is most likely to be divided?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ulnar nerve\",\"e\":\"Injury of ulnar nerve at wrist will lead to paralysis of all the muscles supplied by ulnar nerve in hand\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Median nerve\",\"e\":\"Injury of the median nerve at the wrist will lead to paralysis of all the muscles supplied by the median nerve in hand, it also well covered at the wrist and unlikely to be injured as it passes through the carpal tunnel.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tendon of the flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) to the middle finger\",\"e\":\"Tendons of FDP insert into the cistal phalanges of the four fingers and flexes the distal interphalangeal joint.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tendon of flexor d.gitorum superficialis (FDS) to the middle finger\",\"e\":\"Tendons of FDS insert into middle phalanx of the four fingers. It flexes proximal interphalangeal joints and assists in flexing the metacarpophalangeal joints. The tendon of FDP is the only flexor tendon that inserts into the distal phalanx and flexes the distal interphalangeal joint.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Flexor carpi radialis x-axis\",\"e\":\"Flexor carpi radialis inserts into the base of the second and third metacarpal bones. It flexes and abducts hand at wrist joint. It is not involved in the movement of the digits.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":151,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old woman presents with burning pain associated with tingling and numbness in the right hand. She wakes up at night due to pain. Examination reveals altered sensation on the palmar aspect of the right hand in the area of thumb, index and middle fingers, and radial half of the ring finger.\\n\\nCompression of which nerve is responsible for this patient's symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"Compression of which nerve is responsible for this patient's symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Axillary nerve\",\"e\":\"The diagnosis of the condition described is carpel tunnel syndrome. The axillary nerve does not supply hand and does not pass below the flexor retinaculum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Median nerve\",\"e\":\"The median nerve is the only nerve that passes through the carpal tunnel. It supplies sensation to palmar surface of thumb and radial two and a half fingers including nails. Compression in carpal tunnel leads to altered sensation in the area of distribution. It spares thenar eminence as sensory branch from median nerve to this area passes above the flexor retinaculum. In advanced cases muscles supplied by median nerve in hand also become weak.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Musculocutaneous nerve\",\"e\":\"The musculocutaneous nerve does not supply the hand.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Radial nerve\",\"e\":\"The diagnosis of the condition described is carpel tunnel syndrome. The radial nerve supplies the thumb and radial one and half fingers on the dorsum surface of the hand, excluding nails. Moreover it does not pass below the flexor carpal retinaculum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulnar nerve\",\"e\":\"The ulnar nerve passes outside of the flexor retinaculum. In the hand it supplies the skin over the medial one and half fingers both on the palmar and dorsal aspects.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":154,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old man presents with a deep wound to his hand while using a circular saw. The bleeding has been controlled with pressure, but he has altered median nerve sensation. He is unable to flex his middle and ring fingers. On examination, the wound is 5 cm long and situated medial to the thenar eminence - starting at the wrist crease.\\n\\nWhat zone of tendon injury would be present in this position?\",\"lead\":\"What zone of tendon injury would be present in this position?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Zone 1\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Zone\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Zone I|l\",\"e\":\"Zone | injuries include tendon injuries occurring in the area between the distal interphalangeal (DIP) and proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint creases. Zone I injuries involve the FDP tendons only. The median nerve is not involved in this area.\\n\\nZone Il injuries include tendon injuries occurring in the area between the midpoint of the middle phalanx and distal palmar crease. Zone I must be carefully treated as there is a close relationship with the FDS and the FDP, which can cause adhesion formation and failed repair. The median nerve is not involved in this area.\\n\\nZone III injuries include tendon injuries occurring in the area between the distal palmar crease and the distal margin of the carpal tunnel. The median nerve is not involved in this area.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Zone IV\",\"e\":\"Zone IV injuries include tendon injuries occurring in the area overlying the carpal tunnel. Bleeding and involvement of median nerve suggests injury to this area. Although neurovascular structures are involved both in zones IV and V injuries. However in the description the injury lies distal to wrist crease which is an area of zone IV. Zone V injuries lie proximal to wrist crease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Zone V\",\"e\":\"Zone V injuries include tendon injuries occurring in the area between the forearm and wrist up to the proximal border of the carpal tunnel. Bleeding and involvement of median nerve suggests injury to this area. Although neurovascular structures are involved both in zones IV and V injuries. However in the description the injury lies distal to wrist crease which is an area of zone IV Zone V injuries lie proximal to wrist crease.\\n\\nManagement of tendon injuries depend on the area where tendons are involved. The zones for flexor and extensor injuries are different. For a flexor tendon injury the zones as described by Verden, are:\\n\\n• Zone | - Between the distal interphalangeal (DIP) and proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint creases. • Zone II - Between the midpoint of the middle phalanx and distal palmar crease. • Zone III - Between the distal palmar crease and the distal margin of the carpal tunnel. • Zone IV - Overlying the carpal tunnel. • Zone V - The forearm and wrist up to the proximal border of the carpal tunnel.\\n\\nThis patient also has signs of a median nerve injury which is vulnerable in this location and another important reason to formally explore the wound in theatre.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":157,\"stem\":\"A 10-year-ald child is brought in by his mother with a three-day history of sudden onset hip pain and inability to weight bear. He is pyrexial and feels unwell. Blood tests show an elevated white-cell count and a raised C-reactive protein level.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hip dysplasia\",\"e\":\"Hip dysplasia is a non-infective condition and presentation is not like the one described in the vignette. Developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH) is termed congenital hip dislocation or hip dysplasia. Screening is done at birth to detect this condition. Without early diagnosis the outcome is poor.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Irritable hip\",\"e\":\"Irritable hip is a non-infective condition and the presentation is not like the one described in vignette. It is also termed transient synovitis. It is the most common cause of a limp in children. It presents with an acute onset of hip pain and a limp that gradually resolves.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Perthes disease\",\"e\":\"Perthes' disease is a non-infective condition and presentation is not like the one described in the vignette. Perthes' disease is also known as Legg-Calvé-Perthes' disease or avascular necrosis of head of femur. It is a self-limiting disease of the femoral head comprising of necrosis, collapse, repair, and remodelling.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reactive arthritis\",\"e\":\"Reactive arthritis is a non-infective condition and the presentation is not like the one described in the vignette. It is also known as Reiter's syndrome. It develops in response to urinary or gastrointestinal tract infection. It may take a few months to resolve.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Septic arthritis\",\"e\":\"Septic arthritis presents with an acute onset of pain and inability to weight bear. The child lies with the affected leg in flexion and external rotation to reduce pain. The most common causative organism is Staphylococcus aureus. Diagnosis could be confirmed with joint aspiration. Treatment is with urgent wash-out of the hip joint and intravenous antibiotics. Complications include femoral head destruction and osteonecrosis.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":160,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old woman presents with pain in both hands and they are dropping. Examination reveals that her middle finger proximal interphalangeal joint is fixed in flexion of approximately 30° and the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint is fixed in hyperextension.\\n\\nHow would you describe this finding?\",\"lead\":\"How would you describe this finding?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bouchard's node\",\"e\":\"Bouchard's nodes are seen in osteoarthritis. Although found at proximal interphalangeal joints (PIP), these are hard, bony outgrowths and not a fixed flexion/ extension deformity.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Boutonnière deformity\",\"e\":\"The deformity described in the vignette is characteristic of a boutonnière deformity. Boutonnière is the French word for 'buttonhole'.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Heberden's nodes\",\"e\":\"Heberden's nodes are seen in osteoarthritis. These are hard, bony outgrowths similar to Bouchard's nodes but are found on the distal interphalangeal joints.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mallet finger\",\"e\":\"Mallet finger occurs due to injury to the extensor mechanism to the distal phalanx. It is also known as baseball finger, however any force that causes hyperextension at the DIP can lead to a mallet finger. The resultant deformity is fixed flexion at DIP.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Swan neck deformity\",\"e\":\"Both the proximal interphalangeal and DIP are involved in a swan neck deformity. Characteristically, there is hyperextension at the proximal interphalangeal and fixed flexion at DIP. Looking at finger gives the impression of swan neck. This deformity is the opposite to that found in boutonnière's deformity.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":162,\"stem\":\"An 8-year-old boy presents to the emergency department with pain in the left hip and a limp. The symptoms have been present for 24 h, but the boy has had previous episodes of hip pain intermittently for the past 3 months. He is small for his age, systemically well, does not recall any injury and his bloods are normal.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"S aare east swamenan\",\"e\":\"Developmental dysplasia of the hip is most commonly diagnosed in babies, and is screened for, but may present late usually when the child starts walking.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Perthes disease\",\"e\":\"Perthes' disease is a transient disruption in the blood supply to the femoral head, which is more common in boys, those who are small for their age and between the ages of 3-12 years. Onset is over weeks to months and pain is intermittent. The hip is tender on palpation and flexion and internal rotation are commonly affected.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Septic arthritis\",\"e\":\"Septic arthritis can occur at any age, but is more common in younger babies, it is generally associated with systemic upset and raised inflammatory markers.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Slipped upper femoral epiphysis\",\"e\":\"Slipped upper femoral epiphysis is also more common in boys, but tends to occur in older children, most commonly 11-16 years, at the time of increased pubertal growth.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transient synovitis\",\"e\":\"Irritable hip (transient synovitis) is a non-infective condition. It is the most common cause of a limp in children. Onset is acute and over a few days. Pain occurs at the end of the arc of motion. The child is otherwise well. However, transient synovitis is a diagnosis of exclusion. The condition usually resoives without recurrence/ consequences.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":165,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old man presents with pain in his right shoulder. The pain started 2 days ago, after he had painted a ceiling. The pain is present on forward flexion and abduction between 70- 120°. Hawkins' test is positive, Jobe's test, internal and external rotation lag signs, and Gerber's lift-off tests are negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acromotieur (A) in arents\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acute calcific tendonitis\",\"e\":\"Acute calcific tendonitis tends to present with sudden, acute, severe shoulder pain, with pain present even at rest and on all movements.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glenohumeral joint arthritis\",\"e\":\"AC joint arthritis present with pain over the superior aspect of the shoulder. Pain is often worse with internal rotation which is tested by asking the patient to place the arm behind the back. Radiography and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan of the shoulder may be required to make diagnosis.\\n\\nOsteoarthritis of the main shoulder joint cavity presents with deep joint pain that is exacerbated by extensive use, often described as an ache. Range of motion is reduced and examination may reveal stiffness and crepitus. X-rays and other imaging studies are helpful in diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Rotator cuff tear\",\"e\":\"The rotator cuff consists of four muscles (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis). Individual muscles may be tested for possible tear. Jobe's test is positive for the supraspinatus muscle, the internal rotation lag sign and Gerber's lift-off test are positive for the subscapularis muscle; and the external rotation lag test is positive in case of infraspinatus muscle involvement. Absence of these clinical signs make rotator cuff tears less likely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Subacromial impingement\",\"e\":\"Subacromial impingement is tested by Hawkins-Kennedy impingement sign. During this test, the patient's shoulder is placed into 90° of forward flexion; the elbow is placed in 90° flexion and then passively internally rotated. Presence of pain suggests that space between the humeral head and acromion process has been reduced. Internal rotation causes the supraspinatus tendon or the subacromial bursa to become compressed and elicit a painful response.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":168,\"stem\":\"Examination of a newborn reveals a 'clunk' when a slight gentle posterior pressure is applied to the hips in adducted position. The midwife is concerned about developmental dysplasia of the hip and asks the orthopaedic team to review the child\\n\\nWhat is the most appropriate next step in the management of this baby?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate next step in the management of this baby?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Immediate ultrasound (US) scan of the hips\",\"e\":\"Examination findings suggest developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH). Test described in the vignette is called Barlow's test. US on urgent basis may lead to high false-positive rates due to lax capsules. The optimum time is 4-6 weeks. Within the first 2-3 weeks most babies' hips stabilise spontaneously without the need for treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ultrasound (US) scan of the hips at 4-6 weeks\",\"e\":\"If hip examination in a newborn reveals abnormalities, then a US scan is requested. The timing of the scan is at 4-6 weeks, to reduce splint age in children that do not require it, as the majority of lax capsules will tighten up by this stage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ultrasound (US) scan of hips at 14 weeks\",\"e\":\"Delaying the US scan by 14 weeks will lead to delay in diagnosis and management (application of harness/ brace).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"X-ray of hips at 2 weeks\",\"e\":\"The femoral capital epiphysis does not begin to ossify until 3-4 months of age, therefore X- rays are only helpful in diagnosis if presentation is delayed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"X-ray of hips at 4 weeks\",\"e\":\"X-rays are only helpful in diagnosis if presentation is delayed.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":170,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man sustained a transverse displaced tibia fracture following a football injury. The fracture was manipulated in theatre and placed in an above-knee back slab.\\n\\nWhich symptom is the earliest sign of a developing compartment syndrome of the leg?\",\"lead\":\"Which symptom is the earliest sign of a developing compartment syndrome of the leg?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pain on active flexion of toes\",\"e\":\"Pain on passive stretching is more suggestive of compartment syndrome, Pain on active movement of toes may be present even in the absence of compartment syndrome due to fracture of the bone.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pain out of proportion to injury to the clinical situation\",\"e\":\"The most common early symptom of impending compartment syndrome is pain out of proportion to injury.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Paralysıs\",\"e\":\"Pulselessness, paralysis, pallor, and paraesthesia are all late symptoms and signs and should not be waited for to make the diagnosis of compartment syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased pulses in the foot\",\"e\":\"Presence of distal pulses are not reassuring. One should not wait till disappearance of pulses as it is a late sign and irreversible damage may have occurred by this time.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Palpable swelling\",\"e\":\"Swelling is common after injuries and may occur in the absence of compartment syndrome. However, a tense tender leg should raise suspicion of compartment syndrome.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":172,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man sustains a displaced intracapsular fracture of his neck of femur following a motor bike accident. You are consenting him for cannulated hip screws and he has some concerns about what is being proposed.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely complication of this injury?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely complication of this injury?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Avascular necrosis\",\"e\":\"Femoral neck fractures are high-energy injuries in young patients. Avascular necrosis (AVN) is the most common complication after femoral neck fractures due to the disruption of blood supply. The risk of AVN in a displaced intracapsular neck of the femur fracture is approximately 30-40%. Risk increases with delayed surgical fixation and therefore emergent operative treatment is recommended in the young patient to enable fixation and avoid arthroplasty.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral artery thrombosis\",\"e\":\"Injury to femoral vessels in trochanteric fractures is rare. The profunda femoris artery is most commonly involved. The mechanisms include pressure of a sharp bone fragment (the lesser trochanter), the tip of protruding cortical screws (extramedullary implant) or the distal locking screw (gamma nail)\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Non-union\",\"e\":\"Conservative treatment of a displaced fracture can lead to a painful non-union, hence operative management is recommended. A recent metanalysis has shown a non-union (NU) rate of about 9% in intracapsular hip fractures in young adults.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Post-traumatic osteoarthritis\",\"e\":\"Secondary osteoarthritis is very rare in patients with hip fractures (less than 0.5%).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sciatic nerve palsy\",\"e\":\"Sciatic nerve injury is rare. It may be associated with hip replacement and displacement of the implant. It leads to foot drop and carries significant lower limb morbidity. Its incidence in total hip replacement varies from zero to 2.8%.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":175,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old woman tripped and fell at home. She was brought to the emergency department with a painful hip. On examination her leg was shortened and externally rotated. X-rays demonstrated an intertrochanteric proximal femoral fracture.\\n\\nWhich method of treatment is best for this injury?\",\"lead\":\"Which method of treatment is best for this injury?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1S: Cannulated screws fixation\",\"e\":\"Cannulated screw fixation is used for intracapsular neck of femur fractures.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hip hemiarthroplasty\",\"e\":\"Intracapsular fracture are treated with hip hemiarthroplasty.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hip spica cast\",\"e\":\"It is not a preferred treatment due to the high failure rate and immobility-associated complications.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Internal fixation with Dynamic Hip Screw\",\"e\":\"Internal fixation with dynamic hip screw is associated with highest chances of fracture healing. It provides a chance for early mobilisation and weight bearing. The blood supply to the femoral head comes through the capsule. Therefore extracapsular fractures do not usually disturb the vascularisation. This situation means that extracapsular fractures can be fixed with a high union rate. A dynamic hip screw is the most commonly used device for intertrochanteric neck of femur fractures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Skin traction\",\"e\":\"Non-operative treatment of neck of femur fractures are associated with very high complications rate due to immobility, and not recommended except in patients who are not fit for anaesthetics.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":177,\"stem\":\"An otherwise fit and well 25-year-old patient fractured his right tibia and is being treated non-operatively in an above-knee plaster. You see him in the clinic after his admission and the check X-ray demonstrates good progress.\\n\\nWhat is the minimum time interval required for callus to become visible on radiographs?\",\"lead\":\"What is the minimum time interval required for callus to become visible on radiographs?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1 week\",\"e\":\"After fracture, the inflammatory process starts rapidly and lasts until fibrous tissue, cartilage, or bone formation begins (1-7 days post fracture). Initially, there is haematoma formation and inflammatory exudation from ruptured blood vessels. Visibility of callus requires the formation of scaffold that occurs during the second to third weeks.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"2-3 weeks\",\"e\":\"Callus is formed and fluffy opacity appears on X-ray. This phase is labelled as phase of callus formation, which involves formation of scaffolding for new bone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"4-6 weeks\",\"e\":\"Union phase occurs during this period. A bridge of cartilage or immature bone is formed between the two ends. At this stage the fracture is stable but still weak.\\n\\nThis is a phase of consolidation. Callus is replaced by bone and immature bone is replaced by lamellar bone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"6-8 weeks\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"1-2 years\",\"e\":\"This is the stage of remodelling. It is the final stage. Continued osteoblast and osteoclast activity causes reshaping of the fracture site. As a result the fracture site achieves best bone density and shape.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":179,\"stem\":\"An 87-year-old woman is referred to an orthopedic clinic with ongoing left hip pain that has persisted for the last year following a fractured hip. This was treated with cannulated screws and an X-ray shows changes consistent with avascular necrosis of the femoral head\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding avascular necrosis is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding avascular necrosis is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fractures of the scaphoid bone may be complicated by avascular necrosis\",\"e\":\"A scaphoid fracture often severs the proximal fragment from the blood supply entering the distal aspect of the bone.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The neck of femur is particularly susceptible to avascular necrosis\",\"e\":\"The head (not the neck) of the femur undergoes necrosis when the sub-retinacular arteries are severed following fractures of the neck.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bisphosphonates protect against avascular necrosis\",\"e\":\"Bisphosphonates are associated with avascular necrosis of the jaw.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Females are more commonly affected\",\"e\":\"Males are more commonly affected\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Symptoms develop quickly after the process of avascular necrosis begins\",\"e\":\"Patients are often asymptomatic at the beginning of avascular necrosis.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":181,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with an open fracture, they have travelled from abroad back to the UK and therefore operation is delayed. Post-operatively they are diagnosed with osteomyelitis.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding osteomyelitis is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding osteomyelitis is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The long bones are the least likely to be affected by osteomyelitis\",\"e\":\"The long bones (of the upper and lower limbs) are the most likely to be affected by osteomyelitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Salmonella infection is the most common cause of osteomyelitis in the general population\",\"e\":\"Salmonella is more commonly found as a causative species in patients with sickle-cell anaemia\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ultrasound is typically the first line imaging investigation of osteomyelitis\",\"e\":\"Plain radiographs are more commonly used as a first-line imaging investigation in osteomyelitis. Ultrasound can be used to assess soft tissue changes, but cannot image within the bone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus aureus is the most common causative species in the general population\",\"e\":\"In both adults and children, Staphylococcus aureus is the most common causative species that causes osteomyelitis. Note that a patient may develop osteomyelitis without an open fracture.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trauma is a rare cause of osteomyelitis\",\"e\":\"Trauma can account for up to 47% of osteomyelitis cases.\"}]},{\"t\":19,\"p\":184,\"stem\":\"A 13-year-old boy has been attending tennis lessons over the last 3 months. He weighs 70 kg and is 1.65 m tall. His mother brings him in to see his GP after noticing him limping slightly and reporting worsening pain over his left groin. He is referred to a Paediatric Orthopaedic Clinic following failure of conservative management. There, he is diagnosed with a slipped upper femoral epiphysis (SUFE).\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding SUFE?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding SUFE?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SUFE typically affects: males aged between 12 and 16 years old and females aged between 10 and 14 years old\",\"e\":\"SUFE is the most common hip disorder in adolescents and usually develops during periods of rapid growth, shortly after the onset of puberty in the absence of trauma. It can also occur following minor trauma or a fall. Its incidence is 10/100 000 children per year and can be categorised as stable (90% of cases) or unstable (10%). Radiological classification is based on the degree - Grades I-III (mild, moderate or severe). Early diagnosis and treatment is important in preserving overall function and achieving a good prognosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"First line investigations in investigating SUFE would be blood tests and a magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan of the femur\",\"e\":\"Blood tests would be an important first line investigation as it can rule out endocrine or metabolic disorders which can account for the symptoms. The most important imaging in diagnosing SUFE is an X-ray of the pelvis, hip and thigh. Anteroposterior (AP) and 'frog leg' lateral X-rays show widening of the epiphyseal line or displacement of the femoral head. MRI scan is not necessary as a first line investigation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"in the majority of cases of sure, surgical intervention is not necessary\",\"e\":\"The aim of treatment is to prevent the mildly displaced femoral head from slipping any further. To accomplish this, surgical intervention is necessary Conservative therapy is no longer recommended. Depending on the severity of the case (mild, moderate or severe). there are a few different surgical options including open reduction internal fixation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hyperthyroidism and hyperpituitarism are risk factors for developing SUFE\",\"e\":\"Risk factors for SUFE are: obesity, radio/chemotherapy. septic arthritis, trauma, hypo/hyperthyroidism and vitamin D deficiency.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The right hip is more commonly affected and it is never bilateral\",\"e\":\"The left hip is more commonly affected; it is bilateral in 20-80% of cases.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"In breast cancer histological information can have a big influence on subsequent treatment. In particular with regards to receptors.\\n\\nWhich receptor will indicate the likelihood of responding to antihormonal treatment such as Tamoxifen?\",\"lead\":\"Which receptor will indicate the likelihood of responding to antihormonal treatment such as Tamoxifen?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Oestrogen Receptor\",\"e\":\"The presence of ostrogen receptor (ER) is detected using immunohistochemistry and its presence indicates the likelihood of benefiting from hormonal treatment with tamoxifen. The use of such treatment reduces recurrence by nearly 50%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"II receptor\",\"e\":\"Her II positive tumours are targeted using the new treatment, Herceptin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Progesterone recepto!\",\"e\":\"The presence of progesterone receptor (PR) reflects on the functionality of the ER Therefore if the tumour is ER positive and PR positive it will respond better to hormonal treatment compared to an ER positive PR-negative tumour.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tam-l receptor\",\"e\":\"The TAM receptors are a unique family of tyrosine kinases and function as homeostatic regulators in adult tissues. They play no role in determining likelihood of response to tamoxifen.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"CK5/6\",\"e\":\"CK5/6 is a marker for squamous-cell differentiation. This does not play a role in determining the likelihood of a response to tamoxifen.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 61-year-old man who is a keen gardener presents with a lesion on his lower lip, which has bled intermittently for several months. On examination, there is a red area with overlying thickened skin. Further examination reveals enlarged, non-tender lymph nodes in the anterior cervical chain, which measures 4cm. A punch biopsy confirms the lesion to be a squamous- cell carcinoma.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely first step in the management of lymph node metastasis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely first step in the management of lymph node metastasis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chemotherapy\",\"e\":\"Chemotherapy would not typically be used to manage localised lymph node metastasis of oral cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chemoradiotherapy\",\"e\":\"Chemoradiotherapy would not typically be used to manage localised lymph node metastasis of oral cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Radical neck dissection\",\"e\":\"Squamous-cell carcinoma (SCC) of the mouth is related to betel nut chewing, common in Asia. Leucoplakia is a risk factor, especially if associated with severe epithelial dysplasia. Due to the genetic basis of cancer, anyone is at risk; however it is rare in non-smokers. Patients who develop lymph node metastases are treated with radical neck dissection, usually followed by radiotherapy. Based on the vignette, the TNM classification of the lesion would be N2a (metastasis in a single ipsilateral node, 3-6 cm), which would make it a stage !!! cancer.\\n\\nLymph node metastasis is treated with radical neck dissection first and is then usually followed by radiotherapy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Radiotherapy\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wide local excision\",\"e\":\"Radical neck dissection would be the treatment of choice for localised lymph node spread. Wide local excision would not treat the lymph node spread.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A patient has primary hyperparathyroidism. A tissue sample is required for a full work up. To confirm adenoma, the best biopsy type would be which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"To confirm adenoma, the best biopsy type would be which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A -needle aspiration cytology\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"CT guided biopsy\",\"e\":\"The difficulty with parathyroid biopsy is the variable location of the glands, making them inaccessible to simple biopsy techniques, even with image guidance such as computed tomography (CT) scanning.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Core biopsy\",\"e\":\"The difficulty with parathyroid biopsy is the variable location of the glands, making them inaccessible to simple biopsy techniques, such as core biopsy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"US guided biopsy\",\"e\":\"The difficulty with parathyroid biopsy is the variable location of the glands, making them inaccessible to simple biopsy techniques, even with image guidance such as ultrasound.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Intra-operative frozen section\",\"e\":\"Intra-operative frozen section is the only way of being certain that parathyroid tissue has been sampled.\\n\\nThe difficulty with parathyroid biopsy is the variable location of the glands, making them inaccessible to simple biopsy techniques, such as fine-needle aspiration.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"During an oncology department multi-disciplinary meeting the consultant oncologist mentions that a patient has a very radiosensitive tumour. The consultant therefore recommends radiotherapy as part of the management plan.\\n\\nTo which one of the following tumours is this consultant most likely to be referring?\",\"lead\":\"To which one of the following tumours is this consultant most likely to be referring?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chondrosarcoma\",\"e\":\"Chondrosarcoma is a cancer of cells that produce cartilage. This rare tumour of bones and joints is radioinsensitive. The mainstay of treatment is often surgical and chemotherapy in a specialist sarcoma centre.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Endometrial carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Progestins are used in the treatment of endometrial carcinoma, in addition to surgical interventions. Endometrial carcinomas are relatively radiotherapy-insensitive.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastric carcinoma\",\"e\":\"The most common gastric carcinoma is adenocarcinoma. Most adenocarcinomas are radiotherapy-insensitive.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ovarian malignancy\",\"e\":\"Ovarian malignancy is typically radiotherapy-insensitive. Surgical interventions, cisplatinum and cyclophosphamide are effective in the treatment of ovarian cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Seminoma\",\"e\":\"Seminoma is a very radiosensitive tumour. Standard treatment for seminoma after unilateral orchidectomy is radiation therapy, usually 20-40 Gy to the para-aortic regions up to the diaphragm (a higher dose is used for patients with a nodal mass); the ipsilateral ilioinguinal region is not treated routinely. Occasionally, the mediastinum and left supraclavicular regions are also irradiated, depending on the clinical stage.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the surgical clinic who is undergoing chemotherapy. One of the drugs they are taking is chlorambucil, an alkylating agent. Which one of the following is correct regarding alkylating agents?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding alkylating agents?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Can cause immunosuppression in large doses only\",\"e\":\"Alkylating agents can still cause immune suppression in very low doses and cyclophosphamide is used in this way, for example, to treat systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), Wegener's granulomatosis and many other autoimmune disorders.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Are not toxic to normal cells\",\"e\":\"Alkylating agents also affects normal cells, which are dividing rapidly.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"include cyclophosphamide\",\"e\":\"Alkylating agents are perhaps the oldest class of anticancer drug and are still in frequent use. Alkylating agents, such as cyclophosphamide, form covalent bonds in cellular DNA, thereby resulting in their de-activation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prevent spindle formation\",\"e\":\"Vinca alkaloids are a separate class of chemotherapeutic agent and prevent spindle formation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prevents microtubule assembly\",\"e\":\"Docetaxel is a chemotherapeutic that prevents microtubule disassembly and limits the amount of tubulin available for cell replication to occur.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"You are trying to audit the outcomes of your department in managing colorectal cancer. You review the relevant cancer registries..\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding cancer registries?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding cancer registries?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ignore hospital death certificates and post mortems\",\"e\":\"Cancer registries obtain their data by identifying all death certificates that state 'cancer' as the cause of or contributing to death and then studying the notes of these patients. Post- mortem findings contribute greatly to the accuracy of the data collected by cancer registries.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Are only useful for retrospective studies\",\"e\":\"Among other things, cancer registries can monitor the outcomes of ongoing treatment trials and compare current therapies and centres.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Are most useful at a local level\",\"e\":\"While cancer registries are a source of valuable local data, they are of particular value in providing a large information source for the collection of national data, thereby enabling national features and trends to be identified and studied and regional variations to be highlighted.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Can monitor 5-year survival rates\",\"e\":\"Cancer registries monitor key clinical outcome data, including 5-year survival rates.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Have no value in cancer screening\",\"e\":\"Cancer registries enable at-risk families to be identified for study and possibly screening.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 33-year-old woman underwent a total unilateral thyroid lobectomy for a suspicious dominant nodule. Subsequent histology demonstrated multi-focal papillary cancer with 18 mm and 4 mm foci. Resection margins were clear, with no lymphadenopathy noted during the operation.\\n\\nWhich one of the following treatment options is the most appropriate next stage of treatment?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following treatment options is the most appropriate next stage of treatment?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Completion total thyroidectomy and radical neck dissection\",\"e\":\"There is no evidence to support radical block dissection of the neck for cases of localised papillary thyroid cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Completion total thyroidectomy\",\"e\":\"Larger tumours (>10 mm), especially when multi-focal, should be considered for completion thyroidectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Local radiotherapy\",\"e\":\"Excision of locally infiltrated structures may be required with extensive extra-thyroidal disease, along with radiotherapy. However, in above case of localised papillary thyroid cancer, the treatment of choice is completion total thyroidectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Radioactive iodine (131) imaging and ablation\",\"e\":\"Radioactive iodine is a useful means of detecting metastatic disease after total thyroidectomy. It would not be indicated for localised papillary thyroid cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Regular out-patient follow-up with thyroglobulin measurements\",\"e\":\"Micropapillary tumours (<10 mm diameter) are frequently co-incidental findings and can be safely managed without total thyroidectomy in the absence of clinically overt contralateral or metastatic disease. Measurement of thyroglobulin is a sensitive indicator of recurrent disease after total thyroidectomy when the patient is on full thyroxine replacement therapy\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 59-year-old long-term smoker is referred with haemoptysis and weight loss. A computed tomography (CT) scan of the thorax confirms a 6.2 cm tumour within the lung parenchyma with no evidence of localised invasion.\\n\\nWhich one of the following terms best describes the stage of this tumour using the TM classification for lung cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following terms best describes the stage of this tumour using the TM classification for lung cancer?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"TI\",\"e\":\"The latest TNM staging system for lung cancer (8th edition, 201€) defines a T1 lesion as one that is contained within the lung. The Tla stage describes a cancer 1cm or less at its widest part. A Tlb stage cancer is 1-2 cm across. A Tic stage cancer is 2-3 cm across.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"T2\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"TS\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"T4\",\"e\":\"The latest TNM staging system for lung cancer (8th edition, 2016) defines a T4 lesion as one that is >7 cm in size, in more than one lobe of the lung or various other minor criteria that signify significant local spread.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T5\",\"e\":\"The latest TM staging system for lung cancer (8th edition, 2016) has no definition for a T5 lesion. The staging score goes from 0-4 for tumour size.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the colorectal clinic with bad side-effects from chemotherapy. They are having difficulty continuing their treatment regimen. Which one of the following is generally correct regarding the toxic effects of chemotherapy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is generally correct regarding the toxic effects of chemotherapy?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are better if the renal function is worse\",\"e\":\"Creatinine clearance is a measure of renal function. Drugs excreted by the kidneys are therefore removed more rapidly when renal function is good.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are reduced by combination with other chemotherapeutic agents\",\"e\":\"Combination chemotherapy is used to combat drug resistance. Combinations of drugs have at least as much toxicity, if not more, than single agents.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are worse if the hepatic function is better\",\"e\":\"Many chemotherapy agents, such as doxorubicin and vinca alkaloids, are metabolised in the liver so toxicity would be increased in liver impairment, but less likely the better the liver function.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They may be treated with serotonin antagonists if vomiting is prominent\",\"e\":\"Highly emetogenic drugs require treatment with the serotonin antagonists (5-HT3) such as ondansetron.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They include permanent hair loss\",\"e\":\"Hair loss from chemotherapy is almost always reversible after the course of treatment, even if the quality of regrowth may be altered.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the clinic several weeks after wide local excision and radiotherapy to the breast and axilla. She is having a number of problems. Which of the following is a generalised effect of radiotherapy rather than being specific to the local field?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is a generalised effect of radiotherapy rather than being specific to the local field?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Breast discomfort\",\"e\":\"Localised radiotherapy in the region of the breasts can cause breast discomfort, mostly related to skin irritation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Frozen shoulder \\\\7\",\"e\":\"Shoulders exposed to radiotherapy can develop frozen shoulder symptoms. This is notoriously difficult to treat and the emphasis will be on physiotherapy and adequate analgesia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lethargy\",\"e\":\"Radiotherapy can cause general effects such as lethargy and loss of appetite as well as localised effects.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pneumonitis\",\"e\":\"Pneumonitis can occur secondary to localised radiation damage to lungs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rib fracture\",\"e\":\"Radiotherapy can rarely cause osteonecrosis of the ribs predisposing to rib fractures.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"You are in the oncology MDT discussing new referrals of colorectal cancer. A patient with a rectal tumour with likely threatened margins is being discussed. Considering the staging of malignant tumours, which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Considering the staging of malignant tumours, which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Staging is frequently made solely on the basis of a histological specimen\",\"e\":\"Accurate tumour staging cannot be made on the basis of a histological specimen alone, and requires additional information on nodal and distant disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Staging does not correlate with prognosis\",\"e\":\"Staging allows determination of a statistical outcome, which correlates with prognosis. The main reason for staging is to help determine appropriate management given likely prognosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Staging does not take into account the local anatomy for individual primary tumour\",\"e\":\"Staging of tumours takes into account local tissue anatomy for individual primary tumour sites, including: invasion of tumours into the different layers of the wall of any viscus, and invasion into adjacent local structures.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Staging does not always require details of distant sites for accuracy\",\"e\":\"Accurate staging requires details of distant sites and any possible involvement.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Staging may be altered by response to irradiation treatment\",\"e\":\"Pathological staging may be altered by the response to chemotherapy or irradiation treatment, and is known as 'downstaging'\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"You are at the Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) meeting and one of the patients is to be referred for radiotherapy. You see them in the clinic after their treatment and they have markedly improved. Radiotherapy can be the sole primary treatment for which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Radiotherapy can be the sole primary treatment for which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A denocarcinoma of oesopagus\",\"e\":\"Oesophageal adenocarcinoma is quite radiotherapy insensitive (as with most adenocarcinomas). Surgical options and chemotherapy are more commonly used in adenocarcinoma of the oesophagus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cystosarcoma phylloides breast tumour\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastric carcinoma\",\"e\":\"The primary treatment for gastric carcinoma is surgical resection. An adjuvant role for radiotherapy has been found by some studies of gastric cancer, particularly with regards to locoregional control. Radiotherapy is not, however, the primary mode of treatment of gastric cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Rectal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"The primary treatment for rectal carcinoma is surgical resection. 1280\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vocal fold (T1) tumour\",\"e\":\"Approximately 90% of oral and oropharyngeal tumours are squamous-cell carcinoma in origin, including those of the vocal folds. Early vocal fold tumours can be treated initially with radiotherapy. Advanced tumours may benefit from palliative radiotherapy.\\n\\nCystosarcoma phylloides tumour is a mixed connective tissue and epithelial tumour of the breast. It is fast-growing but only 15% are malignant. Treatment is local excision, but it may require chemotherapy or radiotherapy in metastatic cases.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"Your 43-year-old patient has had a mastectomy and axillary dissection for 20 mm of grade 3 invasive ductal carcinoma with has 4/18 nodes positive, She has had adjuvant chemotherapy and radiotherapy and is now to be started on hormonal therapy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following neoplasms and appropriate hormonal therapies (primary or adjuvant) are correctly paired?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following neoplasms and appropriate hormonal therapies (primary or adjuvant) are correctly paired?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Endometrial carcinoma - diethylstilboestrol\",\"e\":\"Progestins are used in the treatment of endometrial carcinoma, whereas ostrogens eg diethylstilbesterol can cause and promote this malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Carcinoid - Octreotide\",\"e\":\"Neuroendocrine tumours may be treated with somatostatin analogues such as octreotide or lanreotide.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Phaeochromocytoma - aminoglutethimide\",\"e\":\"Phenoxybenzamine can be given to patients with phaeochromocytoma to provide blockade of alpha receptors, this will be before giving beta blockers. This combination ensures blood pressure control before any operative intervention.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pre-menopausal ER+ve breast cancers - letrozole\",\"e\":\"The hormonal therapies used in the management of breast cancer include tamoxifen (an oestrogen antagonist), and aromatase inhibitors such as anastrozole, letrozole and exemestane. The aromatase inhibitors are only used in post-menopausal breast cancer and are contraindicated in pre-menopausal breast cancer in which tamoxifen is used.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prostatic carcinoma - aromatase inhibitors\",\"e\":\"Gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GNRH) analogues such as goserelin and buserelin are currently used in the treatment of prostatic carcinoma.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"You are seeing an elderly woman in the breast clinic. She has a large fungating tumour, which is beginning to cause her social embarrassment. it is too large for surgical intervention and the decision is made that radiotherapy may be a viable option. Which one of the following tumours are radiosensitive?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following tumours are radiosensitive?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gastric carcinomas\",\"e\":\"The most common gastric carcinoma is adenocarcinoma. Most adenocarcinomas are radiotherapy insensitive.\\n\\nAll tumours of the adult kidney, including hypernephroma are relatively radioresistant.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypernephroma 1: -\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Malignant melanoma\",\"e\":\"Malignant melanoma responds unpredictably to radiotherapy - radical surgical excision is the treatment of choice. Both basal-cell carcinoma and squamous-cell carcinoma are radiosensitive.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal-cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"All tumours of the adult kidney, including renal-cell carcinoma are relatively radioresistant.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Testicular germ-cell tumours\",\"e\":\"The treatment of malignant testicular tumours is surgical in the first instance. Both chemotherapy and radiotherapy are used in the adjuvant setting. Adjuvant radiation therapy consists of delivery of 20-30 Gy to the infradiaphragmatic area, including the para-aortic lymph nodes and in some cases the ipsilateral ilioinguinal nodes. Median failure rates from 20% with surgery alone to 3% when surgery is used in combination with radiotherapy.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"You are seeing an elderly man on the ward who has been admitted with neutropenic sepsis following adjuvant chemotherapy for a Dukes C adenocarcinoma of the rectum. Which one of the following tumour types is not particularly sensitive to modern day cytotoxic chemotherapy regimens?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following tumour types is not particularly sensitive to modern day cytotoxic chemotherapy regimens?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Invasive ductal carcinoma of the breast\",\"e\":\"In breast cancer, cyclophosphamide, 5-fluorouracil, methotrexate, mitomycin C and adriamycin are effective chemotherapeutic agents.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Malignant melanoma\",\"e\":\"Surgical excision is the treatment of choice for malignant melanoma. In recent years, the advent of immunotherapy has been a huge step forward for the management of patients with advanced stages of melanoma. Chemotherapy though, is not particularly useful in the management of malignant melanoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) of the anus\",\"e\":\"Anal squamous-cell carcinoma (SCC) is primarily treated by radiotherapy and chemotherapy (5-fluorouracil and mitomycin C).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Testicular teratoma\",\"e\":\"Several cytotoxic drugs are used in the treatment of testicular teratoma, these include bleomycin, etoposide and cisplatinum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ovarian carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Cisplatinum and cyclophosphamide are effective in the treatment of ovarian cancer.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"You are about to see a young man in the post-operative clinic following a recent diagnosis of a cancer. The Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) meeting outcome had been for discussion with the patient about adjuvant treatment.\\n\\nChemotherapy can achieve long-term survival in which tumour types?\",\"lead\":\"Chemotherapy can achieve long-term survival in which tumour types?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cancer of the oesophagus\",\"e\":\"Long-term survival in oesophageal cancer remains poor and chemotherapy regimens to date have not achieved long-term survival in this patient group.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypernephroma\",\"e\":\"Chemotherapy does not help achieve long-term survival in patients with hypernephroma. The mainstay of management is surgical excision.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Testicular carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Normal lifespans have been achieved using chemotherapy in the following: acute leukaemia in children, Hodgkin's disease, histiocytic lymphoma, testicular carcinoma, embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, Ewing's sarcoma, Wilms' tumour, Burkitt's lymphoma, retinoblastoma and choriocarcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thyroid carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Chemotherapy does not help achieve long-term survival in patients with thyroid carcinoma. The mainstay of management is surgical excision.\\n\\n1518\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Malignant melanoma\",\"e\":\"Surgical excision is the treatment of choice for malignant melanoma. In recent years, the advent of immunotherapy has been a huge step forward for the management of patients with advanced stages of melanoma. Chemotherapy though, is not particularly useful in the management of malignant melanoma.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"You are seeing a very anxious young man in your clinic, who has presented with a completely reducible left-sided inguinal hernia. It is slightly tender and you put him on the list for primary repair. Your waiting list is approximately 9 weeks but he is extremely concerned and says 'that's a long time to wait doc, is there any risk of it turning cancerous?' Which one of the following statements is correct regarding conditions with an increased incidence of malignant tumours?\",\"lead\":\"' Which one of the following statements is correct regarding conditions with an increased incidence of malignant tumours?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypospadias gives an increased risk of penile cancer\",\"e\":\"There is no known association between hypospadias and subsequent development of penile cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Paget's disease of bone is associated with an increased risk of sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Paget's disease of bone is associated with an increased incidence of osteosarcoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Peptic ulcer of the duodenum has a strong association with duodenal cancer\",\"e\":\"There is no known association between duodenal peptic ulcer disease and subsequent development of duodenal cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Undescended testes increases the risk of testicular cancer but is reversible by orchidopexy\",\"e\":\"Undescended testes are at increased risk of germ-cell tumours. Even though the risk is reduced by orchidopexy, the risk is not eliminated entirely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Barrett's oesophagus is not associated with an increased risk of oesophageal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Barrett's oesophagus (metaplasia of the squamous distal oesophageal epithelium to a columnar epithelium in the presence of chronic gastroesophageal reflux) carries a risk of developing into adenocarcinoma of the oesophagus.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in fracture clinic who had fixation of a pathological limb fracture 2 weeks previously and has just started radiotherapy for a lower limb bone metastasis. Early complications of radiotherapy include which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Early complications of radiotherapy include which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bone marrow failure\",\"e\":\"The early complications of radiotherapy reflect the sensitivity of rapidly growing cells to radiation damage. They include desquamation lesions of the skin and gastrointestinal tract (inflammation, bleeding and ulceration), infertility and bone marrow suppression, which can lead to bone marrow failure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Constipation\",\"e\":\"Radiotherapy to the abdominal area can result in constipation but most radiotherapy machines are much more advanced now and aim to deliver radiotherapy more directed towards the lesion, therefore this would not be expected if delivering to the lower limb area.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hair growth\",\"e\":\"Localised hair loss can occur after radiotherapy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hypothyroidism is a late complication of exposure of the thyroid gland to radiation, and may also complicate radioodine treatment for thyroid malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Secondary malignancies\",\"e\":\"The production of secondary malignancies is now recognised as a late complication of radiotherapy and may develop from 3 years after therapy.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman with known node positive breast cancer and a positive family of the disease has undergone a mastectomy, axillary clearance and chemotherapy. She returns for the pathology result and asks you about her 5-year prognosis. Which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The Nottingham Prognostic Index is purely a mathematical equation and should not be applied to the individual patient\",\"e\":\"The Nottingham prognostic index was constructed in 1982 and divides patient prognosis into good (83% 10-year survival), moderate (52% 10-year survival) and poor (13% 10-year survival). It is used to compare survival outcomes between breast units.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A high risk of recurrence should be expected given her strong family history\",\"e\":\"A strong family history has not been shown to be predictive of a high risk of recurrence of breast cancer:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The Nottingham Prognostic Index is calculated from size of the lesion, the number of lymph nodes involved and tumour grade\",\"e\":\"The Nottingham prognostic index gives a numerical score to individual breast cancers and can act as a guide for the need for chemotherapy and give patients prognostic information that they can understand. It divides cancers into five prognostic groups. It is calculated from size in cm (x0.2) + grade (1-3) + number of involved lymph nodes (0 = 1, 1-3 = 2, >3 = 3)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is impossible to predict risk of breast cancer recurrence\",\"e\":\"Oncotype Dx is a prognostic test that has been developed to stratify the risk of breast cancer recurrence. It is recommended by NICE for patients with ostrogen receptor (ER)- positive, lymph node (LN)-negative, and human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2)- negative breast cancer to guide chemotherapy decisions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Immune modulators would prolong survival in this patient\",\"e\":\"For patients who have undergone a mastectomy, axillary clearance and chemotherapy, there is currently no evidence for additional hormonal therapy.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A 69-year old woman returns to clinic after a mastectomy and axillary clearance for breast cancer. She wishes to discuss cancer staging, though she does not wholly understand the implication of stage on her overall survival.\\n\\nWhat is the best way to explain staging to her?\",\"lead\":\"What is the best way to explain staging to her?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Staging cannot be assessed without full radiological imaging including a computed tomography (CT) chest/abdomen/pelvis and positron emission tomography (PET) scan\",\"e\":\"Radiology imaging does play a very important role in staging for cancers, in particular to look for potential metastatic deposits. However, staging can still be assessed without these radiology investigations and, in the TNM staging system, patients are designated as Mx (metastasis that cannot be evaluated) in the absence of imaging.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Staging is a medical term used for cancer research purposes, it has no implication on clinical disease-free survival period\",\"e\":\"Staging of a disease allows the identification of those patients who would benefit from entry into trials as well as informing the patient on their likely disease-free survival period.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Breast cancer has four stages, in which stage 4 has a better prognosis than stage 1\",\"e\":\"There are four stages of breast cancer. Stage 1 is the earliest stage and stage 4 is the most advanced stage, and it therefore carries a worse prognosis than stage 1. Stage 1 is cancer confined only to breast tissue. Stage 2 is cancer within the breast or axillary lymph nodes or both. Stage 3 has spread from the breast to the overiying skin or local lymph nodes of the chest wall, which can be known as 'locally advanced'. Stage 4 is breast cancer spread to other parts of the body. As this lady has had an axillary clearance, she has at least stage 2 cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Staging is based on histopathological analysis of the cancer and follows the TNM classification, where T is tumour size, N is nodal involvement and M is metastasis\",\"e\":\"Stage of disease is determined by histological analysis of the tumour specimen and lymph nodes. The most common staging method is based on the TNM model, which can be used for multiple types of cancer, including breast.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"An axillary dissection should be performed to establish whether she has lymph node metastasis to fully stage her disease\",\"e\":\"Sentinel lymph node biopsy has eliminated the need for axillary lymph node dissection. For patients with no sentinel node involvement, then axillary dissection of the lymph nodes is not advised. In addition, she has already had an axillary clearance, therefore does not require further axillary surgery for staging.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old woman consults you regarding breast screening as she is unsure whether she should take this up. She has a friend who had a bad experience with mammography. Which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Breast screening will be able to tell her if she does or does not have breast cancer\",\"e\":\"No screening test is unfortunately 100% sensitive and specific, therefore every single screening test may miss some people with a condition and/or falsely pick up people without the condition.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A true positive is a positive test result in the presence of the disease\",\"e\":\"A positive test result in the presence of disease is a true positive result.\\n\\nDisease\\n\\nPresent Absent\\n\\nPositive A: True positive B: False positive Screening test Negative C: False negative D: True negative\\n\\nBreast screening has been extended to include patients between the ages of 47 and 73 years. It involves 3-yearly mammograms, which are taken in two views and are also double read. The main aim of screening is to detect early stage breast cancers, which are still localised to the breast and therefore amenable to treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A true negative is a positive test result in the absence of disease\",\"e\":\"A positive test result in the absence of disease is a false positive result.\\n\\nDisease\\n\\nPresent Absent\\n\\nPositive B: False positive A: True positive Screening test Negative C: False negative D: True negative\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A false-positive is a positive result in the presence of the disease\",\"e\":\"A positive result in the presence of disease is a true positive result.\\n\\nDisease\\n\\nAbsent Present\\n\\nA: True positive B: False positive Positive Screening test Negative C: False negative D: True negative\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A false-negative is a negative result in the absence of the disease\",\"e\":\"A negative result in the absence of disease is a true negative result.\\n\\nDisease\\n\\nAbsent Present\\n\\nPositive B: False positive A: True positive Screening test Negative C: False negative D: True negative\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a mastectomy and a level two axillary clearance operation. You have permitted to perform the clearance.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements best describes the anatomical level to which clearance should be gained?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements best describes the anatomical level to which clearance should be gained?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inferolateral to and behind pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"Axillary node clearance is the established operative management for disease control in invasive breast cancer, obtaining prognostic information and influencing post-operative therapy including the choice of breast cancer trials. Axillary node clearance can be defined as clearing the axillary contents bounded by the axillary skin laterally, latissimus dorsi, teres major and subscapularis posteriorly, the lower border of the axillary vein superiorly. pectoralis muscles anteriorly, and the chest wall medially. The levels of axillary nodes are anatomically defined as level one (inferolateral to pectoralis minor), level two (posterior to pectoralis minor) and level three (superomedial to pectoralis minor).\\n\\nAbove pectoralis minor would be defined as a level three axillary clearance operation. The levels of axillary nodes are anatomically defined as level one (inferolateral to pectoralis minor), level two (posterior to pectoralis minor) and level three (superomedial to pectoralis minor).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Above pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medial to pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"Medial to pectoralis minor would be defined as a level three axillary clearance operation. The levels of axillary nodes are anatomically defined as level one (inferolateral to pectoralis minor), level two (posterior to pectoralis minor) and level three (superomedial to pectoralis minor).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"To the outer border of the first rib\",\"e\":\"The outer border of the first rib would be superior to the pectoralis minor. Therefore this would be defined as a level three axillary clearance operation. The levels of axillary nodes are anatomically defined as level one (inferolateral to pectoralis minor), level two (posterior to pectoralis minor) and level three (superomedial to pectoralis minor).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Up to the medial border of pectoralis major\",\"e\":\"Up to the medial border of pectoralis major would be medial to the pectoralis minor muscles and defined as a level three axillary clearance operation. The levels of axillary nodes are anatomically defined as level one (inferolateral to pectoralis minor), level two (posterior to pectoralis minor) and level three (superomedial to pectoralis minor). In reality, level 1,2, and 3 nodes are in continuity with each other but the concept of axillary node levels is useful when discussing the extent of axillary node surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":60,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman asks to see you in clinic to discuss further management as she has recently tested BRCA1 positive. She is very keen to reduce her chance of developing cancer since her mother died aged 37. She has not yet completed her family and is hoping to have another child.\\n\\nWhat is the most appropriate advice or intervention for her to consider at present?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate advice or intervention for her to consider at present?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Advice on self-examination\",\"e\":\"The BRCAT gene is found on chromosome 17 and the overall risk for breast cancer in patients who have this gene is 50-80% by age 80 years old. As a result, it is important to be aware of advice to offer patients. This patient wants to reduce the chance of developing cancer, therefore self-examining and screening may a low earlier detection but will not reduce the chance of her developing cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mammographic screening\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Risk reducing mastectomy\",\"e\":\"Risk-reducing mastectomy that would meet the request by this patient. It is important to note that this does not remove the risk completely hence 'risk-reducing'. It would reduce her risk to 5% over her lifetime, which is less than that of the general population.\\n\\nWhile mammography would offer a means of screening and possibly allow earlier detection, this in itself will not reduce the chance of her developing cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Risk reducing bilateral salpingooophorectomy\",\"e\":\"In addition to the risk of breast cancer, the BRCAl gene is associated with a 40-60% lifetime risk of ovarian cancer, which increases from the age of 40 years.\\n\\nRisk reducing bilateral salpingooophorectomy would reduce the chance of her developing ovarian cancer but is offered after the age of 40 when the risk increases and currently she still wishes to extend her family. Women who have their ovaries removed before menopause also reduce their risk of breast cancer by 50%, even when hormone replacement therapy is given.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tamoxifen\",\"e\":\"Tamoxifen is teratogenic so not suitable in this case as chemoprophylaxis\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"During a 5-yearly follow-up for colorectal cancer, a computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen in a 50-year-old woman reveals a 3.5 cm incidentaloma. On further assessment in the multidisciplinary team (MDT) meeting, the incidentaloma represents a right adrenal gland mass.\\n\\nWhat is the best next step in investigation or management?\",\"lead\":\"What is the best next step in investigation or management?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Assessment of function\",\"e\":\"The first step in adrenal lesions is to determine whether it is a metastatic lesion, this would be performed with further MRI imaging. Functional assessment would then take place after this. Functional assessment requires the measurement of 24-h urinary catecholamine and metanephrine levels, serum cortisol and aldosterone levels. It is extremely important to assess the lesion for the possibility of being a phaeochromocytoma as this has bearing on preoperative preparation such that hypertensive crisis can be prevented.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fine-needle aspiration,\",\"e\":\"Fine-needle aspiration is of limited value for adrenal lesions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Laparoscopic adrenalectomy\",\"e\":\"The incidence of primary malignancy increases with size. Surgery is now recommended for lesions > 3 cm in size in patients under 50 years of age and is performed by laparoscopic adrenalectomy. However, before proceeding to surgery, assessment is required to establish whether this is a metastatic lesion and then the functional status of the lesion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"MRI scan\",\"e\":\"Incidentalomas of the adrenal gland are diagnosed in up to 4.5% of patients undergoing imaging for other reasons. The first step is to determine whether the lesion is a metastasis. Once excluded it is necessary to assess the functional status of the lesion. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is useful in distinguishing benign or secondary malignant deposits. This would be the next investigation of choice.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Yearly follow-up\",\"e\":\"Yearly follow-up with no further investigation currently would not be the right answer. The next step would be to assess if this is a metastatic lesion and then the functional status of this adrenal lesion. 2397\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man presents with enlargement of the left tonsil, which has been gradually increasing in size for 2 months. The mass is confined to the tonsil. Biopsy reveals this to be a sarcoma.\\n\\nWhat is the most appropriate treatment?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate treatment?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chemotherapy\",\"e\":\"While chemotherapy has been tried in some cases of tonsillar sarcoma, currently radiotherapy and surgical treatment are considered the first line management options.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Phototherapy\",\"e\":\"Phototherapy has not been shown to be of any benefit in the treatment of tonsillar sarcomas.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tonsillectomy\",\"e\":\"Tonsillar sarcomas are highly radiosensitive tumours therefore any management should always include radiotherapy to the sarcoma lesion\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tonsillectomy and radiotherapy\",\"e\":\"Tonsillar sarcomas are highly radiosensitive tumours, and sometimes small tumours are treated with radiotherapy alone. If it is confined to the tonsil, then excision can be achieved with tonsillectomy then radictherapy to decrease the chance of recurrence.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Watchful waiting\",\"e\":\"Sarcoma of the tonsil is very rare but has significant metastatic potential, therefore watchful waiting is not an option.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old woman presents with right upper quadrant pain and intermittent nausea and vomiting. She reports that recently she has lost weight and recalls an ultrasound scan several years ago, which showed gallstones but as she was asymptomatic at the time, she did not undergo surgery A cholecystectomy is now performed and the histology for this shows a tumour of the gall-bladder.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely type of tumour of the gall-bladder?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely type of tumour of the gall-bladder?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adenocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Adenocarcinomas are the most common tumour type accounting for 90% of gall-bladder tumours. Around 85% of tumours of the gall-bladder are associated with gallstones. The initial features can be very similar to chronic cholecystitis with right upper quadrant pain. nausea and vomiting. Patients can also develop weight loss, obstructive jaundice and a right upper quadrant mass may be palpable.\\n\\nIt is reported that 1-5% of adults may have a hamangioma in their liver or biliary tree, but these cause no symptoms and after often only incidentally picked up on imaging investigations.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemangioma\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Gall-bladder sarcomas are exceptionally rare.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Squamous-cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Squamous-cell carcinomas are the second most common type of gall-bladder cancers, and account for less than 10% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transitional-cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Transitional-cell carcinomas occur in the genitourinary tract and account of 90% of bladder and ureteric malignancies\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man undergoes an emergency laparotomy for large bowel obstruction. This reveals an obstructing sigmoid tumour. This is resected and a Hartmann's' procedure is completed. Histology for the specimen reveals the tumour has extended through all the muscle layers, but no lymph node involvement is noted What Dukes' stage would this tumour be classified as?\",\"lead\":\"Histology for the specimen reveals the tumour has extended through all the muscle layers, but no lymph node involvement is noted What Dukes' stage would this tumour be classified as?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Stage A\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Stage B\",\"e\":\"Dukes' stage B colorectal cancer would have advanced through the bowel wall, including the muscle layers, but with no lymph node involvement. The Dukes' system is one of the most well-known staging methods for colonic carcinoma. Dukes' staging (A-D) is still in common usage as an adjunct to TNM staging in determining the management of colorectal cancer.\\n\\nDukes' stage A colorectal cancer would be confined to the bowel wall.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Stage C\",\"e\":\"Dukes' stage C colorectal cancer would have local lymph node involvement but no evidence of distant metastatic spread\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Stage D\",\"e\":\"Dukes' stage D colorectal cancer would have evidence of distant metastatic spread.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stage E\",\"e\":\"There is no Stage E in the Dukes' staging classification:\\n\\n5-year survival % Stage Description\\n\\n93.2% A Confined to bowel wall\\n\\nB Through bowel wall - lymph node not involved 77%\\n\\n47.7% Lymph nodes involved, no other metastases\\n\\n6.6% Distant metastases D\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a mastectomy and a level three axillary clearance operation. The consultant permits you to undertake the axillary clearance.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements best describes the anatomical level to which clearance should be gained?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements best describes the anatomical level to which clearance should be gained?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Superomedial to pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"Axillary node clearance is the established operative management for disease control in invasive breast cancer, obtaining prognostic information and influencing post-operative therapy including the choice of breast cancer trials. Axillary node clearance can be defined as clearing the axillary contents bounded by the axillary skin laterally, latissimus dorsi, teres major and subscapularis posteriorly, the lower border of the axillary vein superiorly, pectoralis muscles anteriorly, and the chest wall medially. Superomedial to the pectoralis minor would be defined as a level three axillary clearance operation. The levels of axillary nodes are anatomically defined as level one (inferolateral to pectoralis minor), level two (posterior to pectoralis minor) and level three (superomedial to pectoralis minor)\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferolateral to pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"Inferolateral to the pectoralis minor would be defined as a level one axillary clearance operation. The levels of axillary nodes are anatomically defined as level one (inferolateral to pectoralis minor), level two (posterior to pectoralis minor) and level three (superomedial to pectoralis minor).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"To the superior border of the fourth rib\",\"e\":\"The superior border of the fourth rib would be inferolateral to the pectoralis minor and be defined as a level one axillary clearance operation. The levels of axillary nodes are anatomically defined as level one (inferolateral to pectoralis minor), level two (posterior to pectoralis minor) and level three (superomedial to pectoralis minor).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Up to the medial border of pectoralis major\",\"e\":\"The levels of axillary nodes are anatomically defined as level one (inferolateral to pectoralis minor), level two (posterior to pectoralis minor) and level three (superomedial to pectoralis minor)\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Up to the medial border of pectoralis minor\",\"e\":\"Up to the lateral border of pectoralis major would be medial to the pectoralis minor muscles and defined as a level one axillary clearance operation. The levels of axillary nodes are anatomically defined as level one (inferolateral to pectoralis minor), level two (posterior to pectoralis minor) and level three (superomedial to pectoralis minor). In reality, levels 1, 2, and 3 nodes are in continuity with each other but the concept of axillary node levels is useful when discussing the extent of axillary node surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man undergoes an anterior resection. The resection specimen shows invasion into the muscularis propria but no nodal involvement.\\n\\nWhat is his 5-year survival according to Dukes' stage?\",\"lead\":\"What is his 5-year survival according to Dukes' stage?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"Explanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"6%\",\"e\":\"A 6% 5-year survival would be expected for Dukes' Stage D colorectal cancer. Dukes' staging (A-D) is still in common usage as an adjunct to TNM staging in determining the management of colorectal cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"47%\",\"e\":\"A 47% 5-year survival would be expected for Dukes' Stage C colorectal cancer. Dukes' staging (A-D) is still in common usage as an adjunct to TNM staging in determining the management of colorectal cancer\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"77%\",\"e\":\"This patient has Dukes' stage B colon cancer defined by invasion into the muscularis propria but not beyond to involve lymph nodes on the histology specimen. This equates to a 5 year survival of 77% Dukes' staging (A-D) is still in common usage as an adjunct to TNM staging in determining the management of colorectal cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"93%\",\"e\":\"A 93% 5-year survival would be expected for Dukes' Stage A colorectal cancer. Dukes' staging (A-D) is still in common usage as an adjunct to TNM staging in determining the management of colorectal cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"100%\",\"e\":\"A 100% 5-year survival is not associated with any of the Dukes' Stages for colorectal cancer. Dukes' staging (A-D) is still in common usage as an adjunct to TNM staging in determining the management of colorectal cancer.\\n\\n5-year survival % Stage Description\\n\\n93.2% Confined to bowel wall\\n\\nB Through bowel wall - lymph node not involved 77%\\n\\n47.7% Lymph nodes involved, no other metastases\\n\\nDistant metastases 6.6% D\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old, previously healthy, man presents with a palpable anorectal mass and rectal bleeding. On endoscopic examination, you notice a large mass 1 cm proximal to the dentate line, which was 3 cm from the anal verge. Following staging investigations, the multidisciplinary team (MDT) elect to offer curative surgery. Which one is the most appropriate procedure?\",\"lead\":\"Which one is the most appropriate procedure?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abdominoperineal excision\",\"e\":\"Abdominoperineal excision is the most appropriate surgical treatment of anorectal cancers that lie within 5 cm of the anal verge.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hartmann's procedure\",\"e\":\"Hartmann's procedure is resection of the sigmoid colon, colostomy, and oversewing of the distal rectal stump. It is often performed as an emergency operation when bowel obstruction occurs, or if there is bowel perforation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Low anterior resection\",\"e\":\"A low anterior resection is typically performed for cancers in the upper two-thirds of the rectum, which allows resection of the tumour while leaving the rectal sphincter intact.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right hemicolectomy\",\"e\":\"A right hemicolectomy would typically be the operation of choice in a right-sided colon cancer and not for a cancer of the rectum or anus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transanal endoscopic mucosal resection\",\"e\":\"Transanal endoscopic mucosal resection can be used to excise superficial rectal polyps or small neoplasms.\\n\\n2680 -\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"A 61-year-old heavy smoker presents with recurrent chest infections treated with antibiotics from the GP. A mass is seen on his chest X-ray and a bronchoscopy confirms this is malignant in nature.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a contraindication to resection?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a contraindication to resection?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"COPD\",\"e\":\"The presence of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is not a contraindication to lung resection for patients with lung cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"FEV/FVC of 70%\",\"e\":\"A FEV, (forced expiratory volume)/forced vital capacity (FVC) ratio of 70% would not preclude from surgery. Generally a FEV1 of <50% and pO_ <8 kPa would suggest the patient is not fit for surgery. For the majority of patients, chemotherapy is the treatment of choice, small-cell lung cancers are rarely suitable for surgical management. The 5-year survival rate following complete resection of non-small-cell lung cancer is in the region of 30-40%\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ipsilateralhilar lymphadenopathy\",\"e\":\"Involvement of ipsilateral hilar lymph nodes is not usually a contraindication to resection.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Malignant pleural effusion\",\"e\":\"A malignant pleural effusion precludes from surgery as this implies a more advanced stage of tumour. A lung tumour must be sufficiently localised to be suitable for resection. Involvement of ipsilateral hilar lymph nodes is not usually a contraindication to resection but the presence of malignant cells in a pleural effusion is a contraindication.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ongoing smoking status\",\"e\":\"While ongoing smoking status is certainly associated with worse outcomes following surgical operations, this is not in itself an absolute contraindication to operative intervention.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old woman reports a small lump adjacent to a scar in her right breast. She had wide local excision and sentinel node biopsy for grade 3 invasive ductal cancer with node negativity 1 year previousiy with adjuvant radiotherapy and chemotherapy, followed by Tamoxifen. She is still having periods. Imaging suggests a 2cm recurrence. Biopsy reveals grade 3 carcinoma that is ostrogen receptor positive.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding her condition and its treatment?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding her condition and its treatment?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The most appropriate treatment is a wide local excision of the tumour followed by radiotherapy to treat the remaining breast\",\"e\":\"This patient has already had a wide local excision for an invasive breast cancer and developed a recurrence on hormonal treatment. She would not be able to have a repeat wide local excision and radiotherapy as she has already had radiotherapy before. The most appropriate treatment would be mastectomy:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"She should have her Tamoxifen changed to Anastrozole in combination with her surgical management\",\"e\":\"Anastrozole is of no value in pre-menopausal women as, in these patients, the vast majority of ostrogen comes from the ovaries and reducing peripheral production by Anastrozole would cause a reflex increase in the amount produced by this source because of the negative feedback mechanism.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"She does not need further axillary assessment as her previous sentinel node was negative\",\"e\":\"Further axillary assessment would definitely be required and the investigation of choice would likely be an ultrasound scan. The axilla would then likely have to be treated as part of any surgical procedure dependent on the ultrasound scan results.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"This woman should be counselled for a mastectomy = immediate reconstruction\",\"e\":\"This patient has already had a wide local excision for an invasive breast cancer and developed a recurrence on hormonal treatment. The management of this is therefore a mastectomy and discussion of reconstruction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Staging is not indicated as it would not change subsequent operative management\",\"e\":\"Before surgery staging, computed tomography (CT) scans and a bone scan should be undertaken to assess for distant metastases. If these are present it may alter the surgical treatment offered.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old woman presents to your clinic with a craggy mass in her epigastrium, 2 stone of weight loss over 4 months and general malaise. You are concerned that this may represent a gastric malignancy. As part of her assessment you notice a velvety dark brown pigmentation in her axillae and groin creases that you recognise as acanthosis nigricans.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding paraneoplastic syndromes?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding paraneoplastic syndromes?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are the consequence of microscopic deposits of tumour cells, which act ocally to produce symptoms\",\"e\":\"Paraneoplastic syndromes are diseases or syndromes, which are a consequence of cancer cells in the body but not due to the local action of cancer cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome is often found with ovarian carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome is found as a consequence of small-cell lung cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cushing syndrome associated with small cell lung carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Cushing syndrome is associated with small-cell lung carcinoma and occurs due to ectopic secretion of adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Trousseau's sign of malignancy is not associated with gastric carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Trousseau's sign of malignancy (sometimes called Trousseau's syndrome) is when multiple crops of tender venous nodules occur, usually on lower limb, caused by hypercoagulability and venous thrombosis. This is associated with bronchogenic carcinoma, gastric and pancreatic carcinoma. This is different to Trousseau's sign of latent tetany which indicates carpal spasm when the upper arm of a patient is compressed by a blood pressure cuff. This particular sign would be associated with hypocalcaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Necrolytic migratory erythema is associated with insulinomas\",\"e\":\"Necrolytic migratory erythema is associated with glucagonomas 5180\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":92,\"stem\":\"Your team has recently performed a right mastectomy and level 3 axillary node clearance for a 43-year-old woman. Histology reveals a 43-mm grade 3 carcinoma with 13/15 nodes positive. The tumour is ER/PR/Her-2 positive. Following discussion at the multidisciplinary team (MDT) meeting she has been recommended for radiotherapy, chemotherapy, Herceptin and then hormonal therapy in due course. Which one of the following statements is true regarding chemotherapy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding chemotherapy?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Herceptin is a monoclonal antibody immunotherapy directed against the IGFR-3 receptor which is found in 25-30% of breast cancers\",\"e\":\"Herceptin is a monoclonal antibody against the HER-2 receptor otherwise known as he EGRF-2 receptor. This is found in 25-30% of breast cancer cells and is a poor prognostic marker.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chemotherapy medications can only be used to treat cancers\",\"e\":\"Chemotherapy when in the context of cancer should be delivered by oncologists but can also be active against a variety of other conditions if sufficiently aggressive/severe. These include psoriasis, arthritis, multiple sclerosis, Crohns disease and systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Alkylating agents bind covalently to DNA and act by inducing apoptosis if the cell attempts to divide or repair itself\",\"e\":\"Alkylating agents, such as cyclophosphamide, crosslink DNA and prevent further division by mitosis. An example of a platinum-based alkylating agents is cisplatin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Antimetabolites promote the production of antimetabolic enzymes, which act preferentially on rapidly dividing cells (ie cancer)\",\"e\":\"Antimetabolites have a similar structure to purines and pyrimidines and impede DNA/RNA synthesis. They prevent mitosis and induce apoptosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neo-adjuvant chemotherapy can be given to consolidate primary surgical treatment\",\"e\":\"Neo-adjuvant chemotherapy is given before local therapy, eg surgery to shrink the tumour or in cases in which micrometastasis is likely.\\n\\n3181\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":95,\"stem\":\"You have been asked to see a postoperative patient who recently had a right hemicolectomy for node-positive cancer. The multidisciplinary team (MDT) has recommended chemotherapy in the adjuvant setting. Your patient wants to ask you questions about the toxicity and side- effects of this adjuvant treatment as they are understandably concerned.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding the side-effects of chemotherapy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding the side-effects of chemotherapy?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The majority of septic episodes associated with chemotherapy are due to commensal microorganisms\",\"e\":\"85% of chemotherapy-associated infections are normally due to commensal organisms from the gastrointestinal tract and oral cavity. They only turn into pathogens when the state of low immunity is introduced.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Typhlitis is a life-threatening complication of chemotherapy characterised by intravascular dissemination and haemorrhagic pulmonary infiltrates\",\"e\":\"Typhlitis is the potential life-threatening gastrointestinal complication of chemotherapy associated with nausea, vomiting, abdominal distension, fever chills and pain.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Due to potential interactions with chemotherapeutic agents, antiemetics are only used in extreme cases for induction of chemotherapy\",\"e\":\"Nausea and vomiting is such a common and troublesome complication that antiemetics are usually part of standard chemotherapy regimens:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Chemotherany induces hair loss at the same rate in both man and womer\",\"e\":\"Hair loss following chemotherapy is very common and affects women more than men. Regeneration usually occurs a few weeks after the last treatment and can change colour, texture and style.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"emotherapy is only potentially teratogenic after the first trimester, as this represents the period of most rapid growth for a developing fetus\",\"e\":\"The teratogenic effects of chemotherapy are most evident in the first trimester. In cases of malignancy during pregnancy, chemotherapy if undertaken is not usually started until the later stages of pregnancy.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"You see a 78-year-old woman in the breast clinic who presents with a large fungating mass in her right breast. She is confined to a wheelchair due to severe respiratory and cardiac disease and has paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea requiring three pillows to sleep at night. Following discussion with your consultant you start her on Anastrozole 1 mg once daily.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding ostrogen receptor positive breast cancers?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding ostrogen receptor positive breast cancers?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anastrozole is an appropriate treatment choice in the adjuvant setting for node- positive high risk pre-menopausal breast cancers\",\"e\":\"Anastrozole is of no value in pre-menopausal women as in these patients the vast majority of ostrogen comes from the ovaries and reducing peripheral production by Anastrozole would cause a reflex increase in the amount produced by this source because of the negative feedback mechanism\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anastrozole is recommended in the adjuvant setting for post-menopausal breast cancers for 10 years\",\"e\":\"The current recommendation is for 5 years of adjuvant aromatase inhibitor. Aromatase inhibitors are used to treat ostrogen-receptor positive, post-menopausal breast cancers in the neo-adjuvant or adjuvant settings.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tamoxifen increases the risk of endometrial carcinoma from 1:1000 to 1:500\",\"e\":\"In post-menopausal women, tamoxifen increases the risk of endometrial cancer and as a result this is very much second line as hormonal treatment for breast cancer patients in this age group.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tamoxifen lowers the HDL:LDL cholesterol ratio\",\"e\":\"Tamoxifen increases the HDL:LDL ratio and as such are cardioprotective.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Approximately 90% of breast cancers in post-menopausal women are ostrogen receptor positive\",\"e\":\"Ostrogen receptor-positive breast cancers make up approximately two-thirds of cancers in post-menopausal women.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":101,\"stem\":\"You are seeing a patient in the joint oncology clinic who is about to start a course of radiotherapy for adjuvant treatment of breast cancer. While waiting for the oncologist she asks you questions regarding radiotherapy as a general principle. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding radiotherapy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding radiotherapy?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"in the context of breast cancer, radiotherapy is given following surgery to complete he local treatment before chemotherapy is then administered, if needed\",\"e\":\"Following breast cancer surgery, the order of treatment assuming all are needed is chemotherapy then radiotherapy and hormonal treatment. It can be given pre-, intra- or postoperatively. In sarcoma management, radiotherapy is indicated for positive margins, high-grade tumours and recurrent disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Radiotherapy works to induce local damage and thereby induce macrophages and natural killer cells who effect tumour regression\",\"e\":\"The commonest type of ionising radiation used in clinical practice is X-rays, which are produced artificially by linear accelerators. The severity of DNA damage is oxygen dependent; hypoxic cells are more resistant to radiotherapy than well oxygenated cells. The Gray (Gy) is the unit of dose that describes the energy absorbed per unit mass Radiotherapy works by damaging the DNA of exposed tissue leading to cell death. The effect is either direct or indirect ionisation of the atoms that make up the DNA chain. Indirect damage happens as a result of the ionisation of water, forming free radicals, notably hydroxyl radicals causing damage to DNA.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Brachytherapy is a slow duration method of administering radiotherapy for patients with significant, eg pulmonary, pathology and therefore need a more gentle regimen\",\"e\":\"Brachytherapy is used to deliver a rapid high local dose of radiation as in cervical or prostatic carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Temporary tattoos are placed as part of planning to guide beam placement during treatment\",\"e\":\"Radiotherapy spares adjacent tissue by using shaped beams that are designed during planning to intersect to give a large absorbed dose in the area of interest. The beams are kept constant over treatments by using permanent tattoos, which ensure standardisation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The major side-effect of radiotherapy is lethargy and skin reactions\",\"e\":\"Cancer patients are treated by external beam interstitial or intracavity radiotherapy. The effects of radiotherapy depend upon the dose, the tumour size, the tumour proliferation rate, the tumour radiosensitivity and the presence of anaemia and vascular disease. Radiotherapy can be palliative or curative. Lethargy is the most common side-effect. Skin effects are becoming less common as the more advanced radiotherapy machines are able to deliver peak dose below the level of the skin.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":104,\"stem\":\"You are treating a 62-year-old man patient who presents with pain in the right hip. X-rays reveal a sclerotic lesion in the subtrochanteric region. What is the most likely primary diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely primary diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Breast cancer\",\"e\":\"The most common type of bone tumour are secondary tumours (metastases). There are five types of tumour that commonly metastasize to bone, these are breast, prostate, thyroid, renal, and lung cancers. Breast cancer tends to present with lytic or mixed bone lesions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Colorectal cancer\",\"e\":\"Colorectal cancer rarely metastasises to bone tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mycloma\",\"e\":\"Multiple myeloma is a primary tumour of bone giving rise to lytic lesions.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prostate cancer\",\"e\":\"The most common type of bone tumour are secondary tumours (metastases). There are five types of tumour that commonly metastasise to bone, these are breast, prostate, thyroid, renal, and lung cancers. While most of the above cancers cause mixed lytic and sclerotic lesions. Prostate cancer tends to more commonly give rise to sclerotic metastases.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Renal cancer\",\"e\":\"The most common type of bone tumour are secondary tumours (metastases). There are five types of tumour that commonly metastasise to bone, these are breast, prostate, thyroid, renal, and lung cancers. Renal cancer tends to present with lytic or mixed bone lesions.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":106,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old woman has undergone a right-sided mastectomy for breast carcinoma and requires postoperative radiotherapy. She is being consented for the ongoing treatment and she has some concerns about side effects.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a known side-effect of radiotherapy to the breast?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a known side-effect of radiotherapy to the breast?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gallstones\",\"e\":\"There is no association between localised radiotherapy to the breasts and formation of gallstones.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Head hair loss\",\"e\":\"Radiotherapy, unlike chemotherapy, causes site-specific hair loss. Radiotherapy to the breast may result in chest and underarm hair being lost rather than head hair.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased risk of cancer metastasis\",\"e\":\"Radiotherapy should decrease the risk of cancer metastasis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased risk of skin cancer\",\"e\":\"Side-effects of radiotherapy localised to the breast include risk of skin cancer in the irradiated field and future development of pulmonary fibrosis. In the field of delayed breast reconstruction, post-operative radiotherapy affects the quality of recipient vessels making anastomoses more difficult.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Osteomalacia\",\"e\":\"There is no association between radiotherapy to the breasts and development of osteomalacia.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":108,\"stem\":\"You are the orthopaedic CT1 on call and are asked to see a 64-year-old woman with known breast cancer and spinal metastases. She is complaining of bladder dysfunction. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding spinal metastases?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding spinal metastases?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The majority of patients with spinal metastases present with motor or sensory neurology\",\"e\":\"Approximately 90% present with back/bone pain followed by radicular pain. Sensory or motor neurology occurs in 50%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are found in 10% of patients with a palliative cancer\",\"e\":\"Spinal metastases are found in 30% of all patients with a palliative cancer. The common primaries include breast, bronchus, kidney, thyroid, prostate and haematological malignancies.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They may cause Brown-Séquard syndrome\",\"e\":\"The clinical features of spinal metastases include pain, urinary retention, spastic paraplegia, Brown-Séquard syndrome and other neurological symptoms and signs. Approximately 90% present with back/bone pain followed by radicular pain. Sensory or motor neurology occurs in 50%. Brown-Séquard syndrome occurs when there is damage caused to one-half of the spinal cord. This results in ipsilateral loss of motor function and proprioception and contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation below the level of the spinal lesion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They most commonly affect the cervical spine\",\"e\":\"Spinal metastases most commonly occur in the thoracic region.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Unlike spinal trauma, compression is poorly treated with high-dose corticosteroids\",\"e\":\"High-dose steroid medication is the immediate treatment of choice, to help reduce oedema around area of cord compression. This is often followed by radiotherapy to relieve cord compression. Complete paraplegia lasting longer than 24 h is a relative contraindication to surgical decompression.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":111,\"stem\":\"You are advising a patient on the potential need for further treatment following his proposed low anterior resection for a high rectal cancer. On the MRI pelvis it appears the surgical margin may be threatened\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding adjuvant chemotherapy for rectal carcinoma?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding adjuvant chemotherapy for rectal carcinoma?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Commonly includes oral 5-fluorouracil\",\"e\":\"Adjuvant chemotherapy in rectal cancer most commonly involves intravenous 5-fluorouracil.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Indicated in a patient with a Dukes A tumour\",\"e\":\"Adjuvant chemotherapy is unlikely to benefit patients with low-risk tumours such as Dukes A tumours, as they are unlikely to have metastases.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is of benefit in patients with proven local recurrence\",\"e\":\"Chemotherapy in the presence of a proven recurrence is not adjuvant.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Likely to be of benefit for the majority of patients who receive it\",\"e\":\"It is likely to benefit only a small fraction of those receiving it, as many will already have been truly cured and some will relapse despite receiving it.\\n\\n3100\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Commonly lasts 6 to 12 months following surgery\",\"e\":\"Adjuvant therapy is given to patients following apparently curative resection. Treatment is usually in cycles lasting up to a year in total.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":113,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman is undergoing chemotherapy for a lymph node positive breast cancer. She is on cyclophosphamide/methotrexate/fluorouracil (CMF) chemotherapy regimen and asks about hair loss, which is a recognised side-effect of cyclophosphamide. Which one of the following statements is also true of cyclophosphamide?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is also true of cyclophosphamide?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It binds to the protein tubulin, thereby preventing the formation of microtubules\",\"e\":\"Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent that is activated by microsomal enzymes of the liver. Docetaxel, another chemotherapeutic agent binds to tubulin and prevents formation of microtubules.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is an alkylating agent that is activated by microsomal enzymes of the liver\",\"e\":\"Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent that is activated by microsomal enzymes of the liver. Cyclophosphamide is used in the treatment of cancers of the ovary, breast, lung and testis. It is also used to treat sarcomas and lymphomas.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is derived from 4-hydroxycyclophosphamide via hepatic enzymes\",\"e\":\"Cyclophosphamide undergoes metabolic activation by cytochrome P450 enzymes in the liver to 4-hydroxycyclophosphamide, the active component.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is less myelotoxic than ifosfamide\",\"e\":\"Ifosfamide, which is equally effective is less myelotoxic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Its metabolites include 2-mercaptoethane sulfonate Na (MESNA)\",\"e\":\"The adverse effects include alopecia, nausea, vomiting, cardiotoxicity, testicular damage, myelotoxicity and carcinogenicity, Cyclophosphamide metabolites can also cause haemorrhagic cystitis, which can be prevented by maintaining a high urine flow and the administration of MESNA.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":116,\"stem\":\"A 31-year-old woman with lymphoma is commenced on cyclophosphamide by her oncology team. She is seen in clinic with significant side effects and would like to be put on alternative chemotherapy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following side-effects can occur with alkylating agents?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following side-effects can occur with alkylating agents?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cardiomegaly\",\"e\":\"Cardiomyopathy may follow anthracycline chemotherapy. This is not a known side-effect of alkylating agents.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gout\",\"e\":\"Alkylating agents can cause hyperuricaemia, which presents clinically as gout. Other common side-effects include nausea and vomiting, bone marrow suppression and alopecia. They are also teratogenic.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lymphoedema\",\"e\":\"Lymphoedema is not a known side-effect of alkylating agents. This can occur following radiotherapy or surgery to localised areas of lymphatics such as the axillary region resulting in upper limb lymphoedema.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary hypertenion\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary hypertension is not a known side-effect of alkylating agents. This can occur in the longer term secondary to interstitial lung disease, caused by agents such as bleomycin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Systemic hypertension\",\"e\":\"Systemic hypertension is not a known side-effect of alkylating agents. Decreased renal function and subsequent hypertension may be caused by platinum-containing chemotherapy agents.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":119,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old woman presents with primary breast cancer of her left breast and undergoes mastectomy and axillary node sampling in which two oul of the four nodes were positive for cancer cells. Following discussion at the multidisciplinary team (MDT) meeting, she is put forward for chemotherapy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely side-effect of adjuvant chemotherapy for breast cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely side-effect of adjuvant chemotherapy for breast cancer?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cardiomyopathy\",\"e\":\"Cardiomyopathy may follow anthracycline chemotherapy. The incidence of chemotherapy related cardiomyopathy following anathracycline treatment is estimated at less than 5%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased renal function\",\"e\":\"Decreased renal function may be caused by platinum-containing chemotherapy agents. However, these are not commonly used in the treatment of breast carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Severe hyponatremia\",\"e\":\"Severe hyponatremia has been seen in cases of Vinorelbine administration (agent mainly used for the treatment of non-small cell lung cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Secondary leukaemia\",\"e\":\"Population-based studies suggest that adjuvant chemotherapy for breast cancer carries an increased risk of developing a secondary non-breast cancer, 1 in 20 patients will develop a secondary non-breast cancer in 10 years, which corresponds to a 22% increase in the relative risk. Chemotherapy has been linked particularly to the development of secondary acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary fibrosis\",\"e\":\"Incidence among cancer patients is about 10%. It would follow that the incidence of Chemotherapy related cardiomyopathy is lower. Pulmonary fibrosis may result from bleomycin containing chemotherapy regimens.\\n\\n3102\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":122,\"stem\":\"You have just seen a 66-year-old woman with a thickening and distortion in the inferior aspect of her right breast. She has previously had a wide local excision in that quadrant treated with adjuvant radiotherapy.\\n\\nWhat is likely to be the most common side-effect of radiotherapy to the breast?\",\"lead\":\"What is likely to be the most common side-effect of radiotherapy to the breast?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cough\",\"e\":\"A cough or breathlessness may continue years after treatment due to changes in lung tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Damage to heart and lungs?\",\"e\":\"Damage to the heart and lungs is rare, however it may account for increased deaths from other causes in large population studies of these patients.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Alopecia\",\"e\":\"Head hair loss is a side-effect associated with most chemotherapeutic agents but not with localised radiotherapy treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Local skin reactions\",\"e\":\"The most common side-effect is localised erythema and skin reactions, which may be permanent although often diminish over time. With newer radiotherapy machines delivering doses of radiation below the skin, skin reactions are decreasing in frequency.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lymphoedema\",\"e\":\"Lymphoedema of the arm is seen from axillary radiotherapy especially when combined with axillary surgery. Lymphoedema is a side-effect that can occur secondary to radiotherapy, however this is not as common as localised skin reactions.\"}]},{\"t\":20,\"p\":124,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman has recently been diagnosed with a palpable node positive breast cancer initially treated by a wide local excision and axillary clearance. Her tumour is 26 mm, grade 3 with 3/19 nodes positive. Her pre-chemotherapy staging scans [computed tomography (CT) and bone scan] show indeterminate lung nodules. She is enquiring about her prognosis and rationale for adjuvant therapy.\\n\\nWhich one scoring system can be used for breast cancer to help provide information for this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one scoring system can be used for breast cancer to help provide information for this patient?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Nottingham prognostic index\",\"e\":\"The Nottingham prognostic index gives a numerical score to individual breast cancers and can act as a guide for the need for chemotherapy. It gives patients prognostic information, which they can understand. It divides cancers into four prognostic groups.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Karnorsky performance scale\",\"e\":\"This is a scoring system specifically designed to measure physical status for patients who are receiving palliative care. The scale is from 0-100, with maximal performance status at 100.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Dukes' staging system\",\"e\":\"The Dukes staging system provides a classification for colorectal cancer. It divides colorectal cancers into four prognostic groups.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"ECOG performance status\",\"e\":\"The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) performance status is widely used to assess functional status of a patient for discussion at multidisciplinary team meetings. The score ranges from 0-5, with O being full active without any restrictions and decreased functional status associated with each increasing score.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Gleason score\",\"e\":\"The Gleason grading system is used to evaluate grade of prostate cancer, to help predict prognosis and guide therapy.\"}]},{\"t\":21,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"An adult patient with cirrhosis presents to clinic. He has a family history of hereditary haemochromatosis (HH).\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most reliable diagnostic tool for HH as the cause?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most reliable diagnostic tool for HH as the cause?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Liver biopsy\",\"e\":\"In HH, excess iron is primarily found in parenchymal cells, whereas with secondary iron overload, accumulation tends to be in Kupffer cells. Liver biopsy can demonstrate this, allows assessment of liver damage and is of prognostic value.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Serum ferritin concentration\",\"e\":\"Serum ferritin concentrations are almost always markedly elevated, but elevations can occur in any inflammatory condition (including other liver diseases). Ferritin concentration higher than 1000 ug/I suggests liver damage with fibrosis or cirrhosis.\\n\\nSerum iron concentration is normal in approximately 25% of patients with HH and can be elevated in healthy individuals or people with secondary iron overload.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Serum iron concentration.\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Computed tomography (CT) scan of the liver\",\"e\":\"CT scan is neither sensitive nor specific for the detection of mild hepatic iron overload.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transferrin saturation\",\"e\":\"Haemochromatosis is suggested by a persistently elevated transferrin saturation in the absence of other causes of iron overload. Its use in diagnostics is limited as it is influenced by other liver diseases and inflammation\"}]},{\"t\":21,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old woman has been referred to the orthopaedic team with bone pain. On further investigation, she is found to have renal failure and pneumococcal pneumonia.\\n\\nIn this setting, a bone marrow biopsy is most likely to show numerous:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Myeloblasts\",\"e\":\"Myeloblasts are the precursors of granulocytes. The heavy presence of these in a biopsy may indicate acute myeloblastic leukaemia, which presents with symptoms of fever, lethargy and easy bruising\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Granulomas\",\"e\":\"Granulomas are a relatively uncommon finding in the bone marrow but can be found in a wide array of conditions such as infection and sarcoidosis and do not particularly assist with diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Plasma cells\",\"e\":\"The features are suggestive of multiple myeloma. Multiple myeloma is a malignancy of plasma cells that produce monocional immunoglobulin and invade and destroy adjacent bone tissue. Common manifestations include bone pain, renal insufficiency, hypercalcaemia, anaemia and recurrent infections. Plasma cell clones produce a monoclonal (M) protein leading to renal failure caused by light chains or hyperviscosity. Diagnosis requires the demonstration of M-protein (sometimes present in urine and not serum) and either lytic bone lesions, light-chain proteinuria or excessive marrow plasma cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reed-Sternberg cells\",\"e\":\"Reed-Sternberg cells are large abnormal lymphocytes that may contain more than one nucleus. They are typically found in patients with Hodgkin's lymphoma, which often presents with a painless swelling classically of the neck, armpit or groin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Auer rods\",\"e\":\"Auer rods are needle-like bodies seen within myeloblasts and are pathognomonic for a diagnosis of acute myeloid leukaemia with patients often presenting with vague symptoms of fever, fatigue, weight loss, anaemia and easy bruising.\"}]},{\"t\":21,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 51-year-old woman was referred to the clinic with lymphoedema of her leg. She is beginning to have significant difficulty in being able to mobilise independently.\\n\\nWhat is the most common cause of secondary lymphoedema in the UK?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most common cause of secondary lymphoedema in the UK?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chemotherapy\",\"e\":\"Chemotherapy does not cause lymphoedema.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Filariasis\",\"e\":\"Lymphatic filariasis (also known as elephantiasis) is a parasitic disease caused by roundworms that only live in the lymphatic system and cause blockage to lymph drainage resulting in lymphoedema. The parasites are spread by mosquito bites. In the UK the condition is uncommon, but over 120 million people are affected through Asia, Africa, Western Pacific and part of the Caribbean and South America:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoplastic lymphatics\",\"e\":\"Lymphatic hypoplasia is a rare condition and can cause primary lymphoedema due to incomplete development of lymphatic vessels and/or nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Malignant neoplastic lymphatic infiltration\",\"e\":\"Obstruction to the flow of lymphatic drainage causes lymphoedema with the most common cause being secondary to malignant neoplastic lymphatic infiltration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Milroy's disease\",\"e\":\"Milroy's disease is an autosomal-dominant inherited condition characterised by primary lymphoedema.\"}]},{\"t\":21,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old girl presents to her GP with increased fatigue and general lethargy. He organises several blood tests including a full blood count, fasting plasma glucose and thyroid function tests. Her only past medical history of note is a splenectomy for hereditary spherocytosis as a young child. Which one of the following haematological consequences of a splenectomy might you find long-term in a patient post-splenectomy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following haematological consequences of a splenectomy might you find long-term in a patient post-splenectomy?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased circulating Howell-Jolly bodies\",\"e\":\"Howell-Jolly bodies are left-over nuclear remnants in red blood cells. They are typically removed in the spleen. Therefore, you would see an increased presence of Howell-Jolly bodies post-splenectomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased circulating Howell-Jolly bodies\",\"e\":\"Howell-Jolly bodies are typically removed by the spleen, so after splenectomy there is an increase in circulating Howell-Jolly bodies.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Leucocytosis\",\"e\":\"An early leucocytosis (usually neutrophils) is seen within hours and may last for several weeks but does not persist in the long term.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduced platelet adhesiveness\",\"e\":\"There is increased platelet adhesiveness and platelet dysfunction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduced sideroblasts\",\"e\":\"Sideroblasts are atypical nucleated erythroblasts (precursor red blood cells) that have stainable deposits of iron within their cytoplasm. They typically enter the circulation from the bone marrow after maturing into a red blood cell. They are not affected by splenectomy.\"}]},{\"t\":21,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 13-year-old child with idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura undergoes splenectomy: You are discussing her case with the paediatricians.\\n\\nWhat peri-operative and long term prophylaxis is indicated?\",\"lead\":\"What peri-operative and long term prophylaxis is indicated?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amoxicillin until age 15 only - recommenced at first sign of febrile illness\",\"e\":\"The risk of severe infection post-splenectomy is greatest in the first 2 years following splenectomy. However, in high risk individuals such as in this case (as the patient is under 16 years of age), lifelong antibiotic prophylaxis would be recommended. All patients should carry a supply of appropriate antibiotics for emergency use. Patients developing infection must be given systemic antibiotics and admitted urgently to hospital.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Immunisation with Haemophilus influenzae type-B (Hib), meningococcal and neumococcal vaccines plus lifelong amoxicillin\",\"e\":\"Administration of all routine vaccinations should be checked, in addition patients should also receive Hib, meningococcal, pneumococcal and influenza vaccines. Lifelong prophylactic antibiotics following splenectomy is still routinely recommended in all patients. Amoxicillin in the non-allergic patient would be a suitable choice, but phenoxymethylpenicillin remains the first recommended choice\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Immunisation with Hib, meningococcal and pneumococcal vaccines with boosters at 5-10 years plus malaria prophylaxis\",\"e\":\"Booster doses of pneumococcal vaccines are recommended every 5 years. Patients who have undergone splenectomy are at risk of severe malaria and should avoid travel to prevalent regions but if unavoidable should take the necessary prophylaxis at that time.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lifelong amoxicilin only\",\"e\":\"Amoxicillin would be a suitable choice of antibiotic prophylaxis in the non-penicillin allergic patient. Lifelong prophylactic antibiotics is now recommended in all patients, not only those deemed to be high risk such as in this case; those <16 years or >50 years, poor response to pneumococcal vaccination, previous invasive pneumococcal illness, underlying haematological malignancy. Antibiotics alone would not be the only recommendation this patient also requires some vaccinations.\\n\\na07C\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vaccination against Hib, meningococcus B and C, and pneumococcus, annual influenza vaccinations, lifelong oral penicillin\",\"e\":\"These are currently all of the recommended vaccinations for splenectomy patients. Lifelong prophylactic antibiotics should be offered to all patients, not just those considered at continued high risk of pneumococcal infection, using oral penicillins or macrolides. This advice should be regularly reviewed in the light of local pneumococcal resistance patterns..\"}]},{\"t\":21,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 33-year-old patient presents to clinic complaining of lower leg lymphoedema. His main complaint is aching pain and skin discoloration.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the likely cause of his leg lymphoedema?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the likely cause of his leg lymphoedema?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Angio-oedema . S\",\"e\":\"Angio-oedema is a separate pathological process to lymphoedema. Angio-oedema is a local self-limiting oedema that is a result of increased leakage of plasma from the capillaries located in the deeps layers of the skin and mucosae. This can happen in anaphylaxis. Lymphoedema is an accumulation of tissue fluid as a result of a defect in the lymphatic system.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome\",\"e\":\"Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome is a congenital condition that affects the development of blood vessels, soft tissues and bones. It is characterised by vein malformations such as varicose veins, a large port-wine stain and abnormal overgrowth of soft tissues and bones. A complication of this condition is the accumulat on of fluid (lymphoedema).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Raynaud's disease\",\"e\":\"In Raynaud's disease smaller arteries supplying your skin narrow, limiting blood circulation The affected areas of skin such as fingers or toes typically turn white then blue and feel cold and numb. When they warm and circulation improves they classically turn red can be swollen and painful. Raynaud's disease does not cause lymphoedema.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sentinel node biopsy\",\"e\":\"Sentinel lymph node biopsy is performed in cancer surgeries such as breast wide local excision in which a radioactive blue dye is injected near the tumour to identify the position of the sentinel lymph node, which can be then be removed intraoperatively and examined to determine the presence of any cancer cells. If cancer cells are present then the patient may go on to have axillary lymph node dissection, which is associated with a risk of lymphoedema.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tuberculosis\",\"e\":\"Tuberculosis is a bacterial infection caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. While it classically manifests in respiratory disease it can affect areas outside of the lungs with symptoms depending on the area affected, for example in the spine causing back pain. Chronic infections like tuberculosis can cause secondary lymphoedema due to lymphatic obstruction or destruction. While there are some case reports in the literature, it is not widely recognised and, with the advancements in treatment, would be a very unlikely presentation of tuberculosis.\\n\\n1207\"}]},{\"t\":21,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 15-year-old girl attends the vascular clinic with recent right leg swelling. On examination, she has confluent swelling to the thigh with pitting oedema. Examination of the groin and abdomen is unremarkable, as is ultrasound of the abdomen and groin. Lymphatic abnormality was seen on lymphoscintigraphy. Which one is the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one is the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Kippel-Trenaunay Syndrome\",\"e\":\"Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome is a rare congenital disorder that affects the development of blood vessels, soft tissues and bones. It is characterised by vein malformations such as varicose veins, a large port-wine stain and abnormal overgrowth of soft tissues and bones. Klippel-Trenaunay would be present from birth and may be suspected in children at an early age with the presence of port-wine stains but it is often not confirmed until the child is walking and the varicose eins and limb hypertrophy become more apparent.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lymphoedema praecox\",\"e\":\"Lymphoedema praecox is an example of a primary lymphoedema. It is the most common form of primary lymphoedema and classically appears at puberty/adolescence. It is four times more common in women than men with the majority of cases involving one leg only. It is the recent diagnosis in the 15-year-old girl that makes this diagnosis most likely in this patient along with any obvious aetiology.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lymphedema tarda\",\"e\":\"Lymphoedema tarda is a primary lymphoedema sımilar to lymphoedema praecox but unlike praecox it typically presents in patients after the age of 35\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Milroy's disease\",\"e\":\"Milroy's disease is an example of a primary lymphoedema, which is present at or soon after birth. It tends to affect the legs but can occur in multiple limbs or even affect the face. It typically occurs on both sides of the body:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Secondary lymphoedema\",\"e\":\"Secondary lymphoedema is caused by damage to the lymphatic system or problems with the movement and drainage of fluid in the lymphatic system. It occurs as a consequence of another atiology or surgery. For example; malignancy, radiotherapy, severe cellulitis, inflammatory conditions eg rheumatoid arthritis and venous diseases eg deep vein thrombosis.\"}]},{\"t\":21,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"During clinical examination of the liver the patient is asked to take a deep breath so that the diaphragm may push the liver below the costal margin rendering it palpable. You examine a patient and can feel their liver.\\n\\nIn which of the following cases would you be most likely to palpate hepatosplenomegaly?\",\"lead\":\"In which of the following cases would you be most likely to palpate hepatosplenomegaly?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A 55-year-old man with alcoholism\",\"e\":\"Hepatosplenomegaly results from an increase in cell size, not number. Intracellular water increases, while extracellular water stays the same. Splenomegaly occurs as a result of portal hypertension.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A 39-year-old man with acute hepatitis A virus\",\"e\":\"Hepatitis A virus can present with fever, hepatomegaly and jaundice. It is unlikely to give splenomegaly unless chronic liver disease becomes a feature.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A newborn with Gaucher's disease\",\"e\":\"Gaucher's disease is a genetic lysosomal storage disorder in which glucosylceramide accumulates in cells and certain organs, resulting in hepatosplenomegaly. Hepatosplenomegaly is not usually palpable at birth but appears in the first year of life.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A 30-year-old woman with sickle-cell disease\",\"e\":\"In sickle-cell disease vaso-occlusive crises are caused by sickle-shaped red blood cells thal obstruct capillaries and restrict blood flow to an organ, resulting in ischaemia, pain and organ damage. Because of its narrow vessels and its function in clearing defective red blood cells, the spleen is frequently affected. Initially splenomegaly occurs but it is usually undergoes progressive atrophy, leading to autosplenectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A 15-year-old boy with Sjögren syndrome\",\"e\":\"Sjögren syndrome is a chronic autoimmune disease that particularly affects glands that produce moisture in the eyes and mouth. It does not affect the liver or spleen.\"}]},{\"t\":21,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A 7-week-old baby is referred to clinic with lymphoedema of the right lower leg. There is a strong family history for the condition. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PT Cellulitis\",\"e\":\"Cellulitis in a 7-week-old baby would be unusual, but Milroy's disease can be complicated by recurrent cellulitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"MS Lymphoedema praecox\",\"e\":\"Lymphoedema praecox also known as Meige's disease is the most common form of primary lymphoedema. Most cases present during puberty with all occurring before the age of 35. Most cases are unilateral and involving the left lower leg.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Milroy's disease\",\"e\":\"Milroy's disease is an autosomal-dominant primary lymphoedema that presents at birth. It commonly affects the right lower leg and the overlying skin appearing rosy and is warm to touch. It can lead to recurrent cellulitis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Secondary lymphoedema\",\"e\":\"Secondary lymphoedema will develop when the lymphatic system becomes damaged and most commonly occurs later in life as a result of treatment for cancer including surgery and/or radiotherapy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lymphoedema tarda\",\"e\":\"Lymphoedema tarda is the rarest form of primary lymphedema and presents in patients over the age of 35. It is not clear whether the defect is congenital or acquired.\\n\\n3950\"}]},{\"t\":21,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old man who was previously fit and well has an emergency splenectomy after a road traffic accident.\\n\\nWhich one of the following would be found on his blood film after surgery?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would be found on his blood film after surgery?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Absence of Howell-Jolly bodies\",\"e\":\"After splenectomy Howell-Jolly bodies are expected and absence would suggest the presence of functional splenic activity. Howell-Jolly bodies are round basophilic bodies in red blood cells and are residual nuclear material usually removed by the spleen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Presence of schistocytes\",\"e\":\"Schistocytes are fragmented red blood cells and are not seen after splenectomy. They are present, however, on a blood film of patients with disseminated intravascular coagulation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Thrombocytopenia\",\"e\":\"Almost all patients after splenectomy have an acute thrombocytosis, which increases their risk of a thrombotic event.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Presence of Pappenheimer bodies\",\"e\":\"Pappenheimer bodies are basophilic granules composed of iron found in red blood cells typically removed by the spleen and would therefore be seen in patients post splenectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased mean corpuscular volume\",\"e\":\"After splenectomy mean corpuscular volume is unchanged.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"Abdominal aortic aneurysm rupture is a life threatening pathology. The only viable treatment is urgent surgical repair.\\n\\nRecognised complications of abdominal aortic aneurysm repair include all of the following, except:\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Incisional hernia\",\"e\":\"Incisional hernias are a very common complication following open abdominal aortic aneurysm repair, at the midline laparotomy site. It is important to note that only a few are symptomatic and will require further treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ischaemic colitis\",\"e\":\"Ischaemic colitis is a known complication following abdominal aortic aneurysm, occurring at a rate of 1-3% with an open repair, with the risk being lower with EVAR. Intra-operative ligation of the inferior mesenteric artery can cause left colon ischaemia in the absence of patent collaterals with the superior mesenteric artery to maintain flow.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Madura foot\",\"e\":\"Madura foot, also known as mycetoma, describes a localised subcutaneous infection by fungi or filamentous bacteria. Most common cause is infection with actinomycetes. It typically presents in agricultural workers following minor trauma to the foot, but has not been associated with abdominal aortic aneurysm repair.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Paraplegia\",\"e\":\"Paraplegia is one of the recognised side-effects of aortic aneurysm repair. This can be the result of damage to the artery of Adamkiewicz intra-operatively. This artery is also known as the great anterior radiculomedullary artery arises most commonly from the abdominal aorta at the level of the gth to 12th intercostal artery and supplies the spinal cord from T8 to conus medullaris. During abdominal aorta aneurysm repair it is at risk of ischaemia or occlusion. If this occurs, anterior spinal artery syndrome may develop with symptoms such as faecal/urinary incontinence and paraplegia. Sensory function in the lower limbs can sometimes be preserved to a certain degree\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trash foot\",\"e\":\"Trash foot is a term used to describe acute lower limb ischaemia following aortic surgery. It is thought to be due to cholesterol emboli, dislodging from the atheromatous plaque found in the affected abdominal aorta and blocking distal arteries. It presents with the triad of pain, intact peripheral pulses and the classical purple lacy discoloration of the affected leg (livedo reticularis).\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"Once the abdominal aorta is > 3cm it is considered aneurysmal. If left, they have an increasing chance of rupture.\\n\\nHow are abdominal aortic aneurysms most commonly identified?\",\"lead\":\"How are abdominal aortic aneurysms most commonly identified?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Patient presents with acute lower limb ischaemia\",\"e\":\"Acute lower limb ischaemia and trash foot are the result of a thrombosis or embolus released from the aneurysm. They are uncommon presentations of abdominal artery aneurysm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Patient presents with a gastrointestinal (GI) bleed\",\"e\":\"A fistula can form between the aneurysm wall and the bowel - an aorto-enteric fistula. This is a very rare complication of an abdominal aortic aneurysm and can present with gastrointestinal (Gl) bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Incidental finding\",\"e\":\"About 75% of patients with an abdominal aorta aneurysm are unaware of the presence of the aneurysm and remain asymptomatic. The diagnosis is either with clinical examination or radiological, usually by the NHS National screening programme, involving an abdominal ultrasound offered to all men over the age of 65.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Patient presents with severe abdominal pain radiating to the back\",\"e\":\"This can sometimes be a presentation of an abdominal aorta aneurysm which often relates to the aneurysm expanding or if very acute and severe, impending rupture. Patients may also present with a pulsating abdominal mass.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Patient presents with trash foot\",\"e\":\"Trash foot, is acute lower limb ischaemia from a cholesterol embolus thrown from the atherosclerotic plaque in the abdominal aorta aneurysm. This is a rare presentation and most commonly occurs post-operatively when the aneurysm has been disrupted.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"Inflammatory aortic aneurysms are possibly a more severe form of degenerative abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA). They can be life threatening if untreated.\\n\\nWhich one of the following may lead you to confirm or suspect inflammatory aortic aneurysm?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following may lead you to confirm or suspect inflammatory aortic aneurysm?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are always associated with an elevated procalcitonin level\",\"e\":\"Inflammatory AAAs are associated with an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate in 90% of the cases. Abdominal pain in the presence of an AAA, weight loss, fever and a raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) is pathognomonic of inflammatory AAA. In the quest for identifying biomarkers sensitive in differentiating inflammatory AAAs and infected AAAs, procalcitonin appears elevated in the latter. Procalcitonin is produced ubiquitously by endotoxin or mediators released in respons to bacterial infections; therefore you would expect procalcitonin levels to be low in an inflammatory AAA.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They can present with abdominal pain\",\"e\":\"Inflammatory abdominal aortic aneurysms are a variant of AAA, forming 5-10% of all AAA cases. They are most commonly found in younger patients (presentation age 5-10 years younger than presentation of atherosclerotic AAA), in the infrarenal abdominal aorta and are symptomatic. Symptoms include abdominal and/or back pain, fevers and weight loss. Risk factors include: male gender, smoking and family history. They are commonly associated with a raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) (90% of cases) and inflammatory markers. They are detected with CT scans/MRI, showing a cuff of perianeurysmal tissue and inflammatory changes. These spare the posterior wall. Radiological appearances relate to thickening of the adventitia with abundant inflammatory cells (lymphocytes, macrophages, plasma cells) Initial treatment may require smoking cessation, steroids and immunosuppressive medication but definitive treatment is via surgery (open repair or EVAR). The inflammation involves the retroperitoneum as well leading to retroperitoneal fibrosis, causing entrapment of the ureters and progressively renal failure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are more common in women\",\"e\":\"Inflammatory abdominal aorta aneurysms are more commonly seen in men. Other risk factors include smoking and genetic predisposition. An association of inflammatory AAA with Takayasu's arteritis has also been reported in the literature.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They are responsible for 20% of all abdominal aortic aneurysms in smokers\",\"e\":\"Inflammatory AAA are more common in smokers but are not however responsible for as high as 20% of all AAAs in smokers. They generally represent a 5-10% of all AAAS.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They are most commonly detected intra-operatively\",\"e\":\"With the advances in imaging modalities, the majority of inflammatory abdominal aortic aneurysm diagnoses occur pre-operatively. However, there is still one in four inflammatory abdominal aortic aneurysms detected intra-operatively Computed tomography (CT) scanning typically shows the aneurysm and the thickened aortic wall with associated periaortic inflammation and fibrosis (known as the 'mantle sign'). Further FGD-PET scanning is also able to illustrate the active inflammation around the aortic wall.\\n\\n81\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old woman presents with a pulsatile abdominal swelling and undergoes duplex imaging. This reveals a 4.5 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm that appears to be infrarenal. Her abdomen is soft and non-tender, Hypertension is her only co-morbidity.\\n\\nWhat is the most appropriate management?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate management?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anticoagulation\",\"e\":\"There is no indication to start the patient on anticoagulation treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Discharge to primary care for blood pressure control\",\"e\":\"The patient has hypertension and it is important to ensure this is well controlled, to reduce the risk of rupture of the abdominal aortic aneurysm. However, the patient should not be discharged as according to the current guidelines she requires 6-monthly surveillance of the aneurysm with ultrasonography.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Endovascular aneurysm repair\",\"e\":\"Currently, clinical practice in the UK recommends elective surgical repair of an abdominal aortic aneurysm of a diameter of > 5.5 cm or aneurysms that are > 4.5 cm in diameter but have increased more than 0.5 cm in a 6-montn period. Endovascular repair is recommended for patients with unruptured, infrarenal aneurysms for whom surgical intervention is appropriate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Open aneurysm repair\",\"e\":\"Currently, clinical practice in the UK recommends elective surgical repair of an abdominal aortic aneurysm of a diameter of > 5.5 cm or aneurysms that are > 4.5 cm in diameter but have increased more than 0.5 cm in a 6-montn period. It is performed when the aneurysm has ruptured. In unruptured aneurysms the decision of whether to perform open repair or endovascular is a clinical decision based on several patient and aneurysm characteristics.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Surveillance\",\"e\":\"The Vascular Society and National Screening Committee recommend that patients with aneurysms of 3-4.4 cm in diameter should have repeat surveillance scans with ultrasonography every 12 months. Patients with an aneurysm of 4.5-5.4 cm diameter should have a 6-monthly surveillance with ultrasonography, If an aneurysm is found and is 5.5 cm or larger, then the patient should be referred to a surgeon in the vascular network.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old woman presents with lipodermatosclerosis, haemosiderosis and an ulcer on the medial aspect of the left calf. She is diagnosed with venous insufficiency and undergoes surgery. What is the surface anatomical location of the sapheno-femoral junction?\",\"lead\":\"What is the surface anatomical location of the sapheno-femoral junction?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1cm medial to the femoral pulse\",\"e\":\"The structure found 1 cm medial to the femoral pulse is the femoral vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"2 cm inferior to the mid-inguinal point\",\"e\":\"The mid-inguinal point, halfway between the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) and the pubis, is relevant in locating the femoral artery for palpation and intervention.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"2 cm inferior to the mid-point of the inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"The midpoint of the inguinal ligament, found halfway between the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) and the pubic tubercle, is relevant as a landmark for the deep inguinal ring and identification of indirect inguinal hernias.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"2 cm medial and inferior to the pubic tubercle\",\"e\":\"The sapheno-femoral junction is found 1-4 cm lateral and inferior to the pubic tubercle, not medial.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"1-4 cm lateral and inferior to the pubic tubercle\",\"e\":\"The sapheno-femoral junction is found 1-4 cm inferior and lateral to the pubic tubercle and is describes as the site where the saphenous vein pierces through the fascia lata to join the femoral vein.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old pregnant woman presents with bilateral painful lower limbs with varicosities in the long saphenous vein distribution at the level of the perforating veins bilaterally. She remains symptomatic despite wearing compression stockings for over 6 months.\\n\\nWhat is the diagnostic investigation of choice for this woman?\",\"lead\":\"What is the diagnostic investigation of choice for this woman?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ankle Brachial Pressure Index (ABPI)\",\"e\":\"This is the first step in assessment of a patient presenting with symptoms of claudication and a diagnosis of peripheral vascular disease/atherosclerotic arterial disease is suspected.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lower limb arteriogram\",\"e\":\"This is the investigation of choice when suspecting a diagnosis of peripheral vascular disease/arteriosclerotic arterial disease. It has no role in the assessment and diagnosis of varicose veins.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lower limb contrast computed tomography (CT) angiogram\",\"e\":\"The patient is pregnant and a CT should be avoided unless absolutely necessary as the radiation exposure carries risk to the fetus. However, even if the patient was not pregnant contrast CT angiography is used to evaluate patency of lower limb arteries in the work-up of peripheral vascular disease. Contrast venography can be used in patients who have a deep vein thrombosis (DVT) or post thrombotic syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lower limb duplex venous ultrasound\",\"e\":\"Duplex venous ultrasound scanning is the gold standard pre-operative investigation for assessment of the veins of the lower limbs in patients with a suspected diagnosis of venous thrombosis or varicosities. It is non-invasive and safe to perform in pregnancy. A deep vein thrombosis in this patient is high in the differential list given the hypercoagulable state of pregnancy. It is also important to note that varicose veins are more common in pregnancy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lower limb MRI angiogram\",\"e\":\"An MR angiogram has a role in providing diagnostic information of the lower limb arteries by visualisation of the vessels in more detail than with conventional angiography. This however has no role in assessment and diagnosis of varicose veins.\\n\\nRate this question: O\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"Your consultant asks you to consent a patient for sclerotherapy for varicose veins. They have had previous varicose vein surgery, but the varicose veins have recurred. The complications you should make the patient aware of specific to this treatment include which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The complications you should make the patient aware of specific to this treatment include which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Trash foot and brown discolouration of the skin\",\"e\":\"Although brown discoloration of the skin is a recognised complication of sclerotherapy for varicose veins, trash foot is not, making this option incorrect.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Brown discolouration of the skin, deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and skin ulceration\",\"e\":\"Sclerotherapy is indicated for residual and recurrent varicose veins, following varicose vein surgery. Sclerosing foam agent is injected into the veins under ultrasound guidance aiming to damage the endothelial surface, cause scarring and lead to blocking of the vein. The procedure can be carried out using local anaesthesia. Complications of the procedure include:\\n\\n• brown discoloration of the skin, secondary to extravasation of the sclerosing agent, causing tissue damage. • superficial thrombophlebitis • thromboembolism (both deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and PE) • pain at the injection site • localised reaction to the sclerosing agent/ Urticaria • cutaneous necrosis leading to ulceration of the skin • nerve injury: saphenous and sural nerve • neurological complications: increased risk of stroke in patients with patent foramen ovale, visual disturbances, TIA, migraine • Anaphylaxis\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Trash foot, DVT and Sudeck's atrophy\",\"e\":\"Trash foot and Sudeck's atrophy are not complications of sclerotherapy treatment for varicose veins. Trash foot can be a complication of arterial procedures with emboli occluding the foot's blood supply. Sudeck's atrophy is described as a complex regional pain syndrome, as a complication of trauma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"All of the above\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect. Even though skin discoloration, deep vein thrombosis and skin ulceration are complications of sclerotherapy for varicose veins, trash foot and Sudeck's atrophy are not\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"None of the above\",\"e\":\"Brown discoloration of the skin, deep vein thrombosis and skin ulceration respectively, are recognised complications associated with sclerotherapy of varicose veins.\\n\\nRate this question: C\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"Aneurysmal disease can be life threatening following rupture. Often until this occurs they can be relatively asymptomatic.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true of aneurysm pathology?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of aneurysm pathology?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A false aneurysm is the same thing as a dissecting aneurysm\",\"e\":\"A false aneurysm (also known as pseudoaneurysm) is a collection of blood found between the muscularis and adventitia layers of an artery resulting from an arterial puncture or trauma to the artery. In contrast a dissecting aneurysm occurs following a tear in the intima of the aorta allowing blood to collect in the intima-media space and propagating the tear along the wall of the vessel, dissecting its layers. Dissecting aneurysms are most commonly seen in the elderly or patients with Marfan syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"25% of people with one aneurysm will also have other aneurysms elsewhere\",\"e\":\"Having one aneurysm, increases the risk of having an aneurysm elsewhere in the body. The rate that this occurs varies with different aneurysms. In total, 10-15% of patients with an abdominal aortic aneurysm are found to have a popliteal artery aneurysm and conversely 30-50% of patients with a popliteal aneurysm are found to have an abdominal aortic aneurysm. Also most patients with a common iliac artery aneurysm are found to have an abdominal aortic aneurysm as well; 15-30% of patients with a Berry (intracerebral) aneurysm, are found to have an aneurysm elsewhere in the body.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The intimal entry tear in a dissecting aneurysm is usually in a diseased area of the aorta\",\"e\":\"The ageing process of the aorta involves a reduction in elastin and an increase in the collagen elastin ratio, reducing its ability to withhold sheer stress and increasing the risk of dissection. In most cases of dissecting aneurysm, the intimal tear, that initiates the process of dissection, occurs in a disease-free area of the aorta but quite commonly adjacent to a diseased area like an atheromatous plaque. However there is evidence that the ageing process of the aorta, with reduction of elastin and an increase in the collagen:elastin ratio, predispose the aorta to the formation of a dissection as the patient ages.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The commonest location for mycotic aneurysms is the aorta\",\"e\":\"Mycotic aneurysms can complicate infective endocarditis or other bacteraemia, resulting from bacterial infection of the vessel wall and weakening. They are false aneurysms, making them highly likely to rupture. They most commonly occur in the femoral artery followed by the aorta and the intracranial arteries, however they can affect any artery of the body.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Syphilitic aneurysms often result in rupture\",\"e\":\"Syphilitic aneurysms (or luetic aneurysms) usually occur in the aorta during the stage of tertiary syphilis following a chronic syphilitic aortitis. They are very rare and generally do not result in rupture. Instead they cause aortic incompetence and are surgically repaired.+\\n\\n183 Rate this question:\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"The infrarenal abdominal aorta is the commonest site for abdominal aortic aneurysm. Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) is the 13h commonest cause of death in the Western world.\\n\\nWhat is the most common cause of AAA?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most common cause of AAA?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PY Atherosclerosis\",\"e\":\"Abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAA) are described as focal dilations in the diameter of the abdominal aorta of more than 50% compared to the vessel's normal diameter, arising usually as the result of degeneration of the media of the wall leading to a continuous dilation of the lumen. They are much more common in men and prevalence increases with age. It is thought to be a degenerative process and the most common cause of this remains atherosclerosis.\\n\\nThere are however other causes of AAA such as: inflammation, vasculitis, connective tissue disorders (Marfan's syndrome or Ehlers-Danlos syndrome), chronic aortic dissection, endocarditis/bacteraemia leading to mycotic aneurysm, syphilis and trauma. Risk factors include: smoking, male gender, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), previous aneurysm repair or peripheral aneurysm, hypertension, coronary artery disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"iviS Diabetes mellitus\",\"e\":\"Diabetes mellitus is not considered a cause of an abdominal aortic aneurysm, However, there is a higher incidence of atherosclerosis in diabetics, which can in turn lead to an abdominal aortic aneurysm.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inflammatory\",\"e\":\"Inflammatory abdominal aortic aneurysms account for 5-10% of all AAA cases. They commonly occur in younger patients, with risk factors being man gender, smoking and positive family history. They are usually symptomatic and can present with the following triad: abdominal pain, weight loss and raised ESR\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Marfan syndrome (\",\"e\":\"Even though connective tissue disorders, such as Marfan's and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes are known causes of abdominal aortic aneurysms, atherosclerosis remains far more common\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Syphilis\",\"e\":\"Syphilis can cause an AAA during the stage of tertiary syphilis, but since antibiotic prophylaxis and higher surveillance of syphilis infections it is now an extremely rare cause of an aneurysm.+\\n\\n965 Rate this question: €\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man is admitted with ischaemic rest pain in both feet. There is no history of preceding intermittent claudication. He is a heavy smoker. Femoral and popliteal pulses are palpable. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atherosclerosis\",\"e\":\"Atherosclerosis tends to manifest at a later age, and vascular disease associated with atherosclerosis presents with progressively worsening symptoms of intermittent claudication.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fibromuscular dysplasia\",\"e\":\"Fibromuscular dysplasia is an angiopathy affecting medium sized vessels, most commonly in women of reproductive age. Most common sites invoived are the renal and carotid arteries. Only in 5% of cases the arteries of the limbs are affected. It is usually asymptomatic or can present with symptoms according to which vessel is affected eg in renal angiopathy the patient can present with refractory hypertension whereas in carotid artery disease they can present with headache, dizziness, vision problems, tinnitus and facial weakness.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Polyarteritis nodosa\",\"e\":\"Polyarteritis nodosa is a systemic vasculitis disorder affection small and medium sized vessels. Patients affected are predominantly men ages 45-65. Symptoms are usually present for a few weeks to months before diagnosis and include:\\n\\n• non-specific symptoms (eg fever, malaise, anorexia, myalgia, arthralgia) • central nervous system symptoms (transient monocular blindness and cerebral arteritis leading to subarachnoid haemorrhage) • peripheral nervous system symptoms (peripheral neuropathy).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Takayasu's arteritis\",\"e\":\"Takayasu's arteritis is a rare vasculitis, affecting large vessels, predominantly the aorta and its main branches. It is most commonly seen in women of reproductive age. It usually presents with a combination of:\\n\\n• cardiac/vascular manifestations such as claudication, Raynaud's syndrome and a carotid bruit • neurological features headache, visual disturbances, TIA/stroke • dermatological features: erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum.\\n\\n^ 72-year-old man has a swelling behind his knee consistent with a popliteal artery aneurysm. He has pulses distally, in both the dorsalis pedis and posterial tibial arteries Which one of the following is the strongest indication for surgical intervention?\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thromboangiitis obliterans (Burger's disease)\",\"e\":\"Thromboangitis obliterans, also called Buerger's disease, is an inflammatory arteritis affecting the tunica intima of small to medium sized arteries, as well as veins. It most commonly occurs in male patients at the age of 20-45. It is characterised by small segmental occlusions of the vessels, most commonly in the lower limb. This leads to rest pain, Raynaud's syndrome, painful ulceration or gangrene of toes, thrombophlebitis. Intermittent claudication can be present, but not usually a major feature. Risk factors include: smoking, chronic gum disease, male gender, age ‹ 45. It is essential to stop smoking.+\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the vascular out-patient clinic with an ulcer on their lower limb and you are trying to determine if this is arterial or venous in atiology. You undertake a careful examination and document your findings.\\n\\nWhich one of the following best describes venous ulcers?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describes venous ulcers?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They have sloping edges\",\"e\":\"Venous ulcers are usually found around the medial malleolus. They are large, shallow and painless unless they become infected and are associated with aching, oedematous lower limbs. They have sloping edges as opposed to arterial ulcers that have punched out edges.\\n\\nVenous ulcers are surrounded by mottled brown/black skin or areas of venous eczema. They are also associated with lipodermatosclerosis and hyperkeratosis of the skin.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are most commonly found on the dorsum of the foot\",\"e\":\"Venous ulcers are most commonly found around the medial malleolus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are usually very painful\",\"e\":\"Venous ulcers are usually painless uniess they become infected. It is arterial ulcers that are painful.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They are usually associated with absent pulses\",\"e\":\"With venous ulceration, the arterial pulses are usually intact. It is with arterial ulcers that the peripheral pulses can become diminished or absent\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They cannot be diagnosed in the absence of superficial varicose veins\",\"e\":\"Varicose veins are a risk factor increasing a patient's risk of getting a venous ulcer in the lower limbs. 50% of venous ulcers are associated with varicose veins. However, there are other risk factors such as history of deep vein thrombosis (DVT), prolonged standing or sitting, multiple pregnancies, hypertension, previous leg surgery, lower limb fractures, obesity, immobility and chronic leg oedema.*\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with an incompetent sapheno-popliteal junction (SP). They are due to undergo an intervention to correct this.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is imperative?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is imperative?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Closure of popliteal fascia\",\"e\":\"Closure of the popliteal fascia prevents the patient from having a bulge on the posterior side of the knee. This is more important for cosmetic reasons but not imperative for the success of the operation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Flush ligation of the SPJ\",\"e\":\"Flush ligation is generally thought to reduce the rate of recurrence of varicose veins, it is however difficult to achieve and carries a significant risk of injury to surrounding vessels and nerves.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Identification of the Giacomini vein (upward continuation of the short saphenous vein)\",\"e\":\"The Giacomini vein is an anatomical variation, thought to be present in 75% of the population. It is also called the thigh extension of the short saphenous vein. It is a distinct branch of the short saphenous vein that continues into the thigh. It is important to identify this if present and evaluate it for venous disease during pre-operative ultrasound (US) scans but given it is an anatomical variation it has not got an imperative role in the success of the operation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pre-operative marking of the SPJ with duplex imaging\",\"e\":\"As the location of the SPJ is extremely variable, therefore pre-operative assessment with duplex ultrasound imaging is essential to mark the accurate location of the SPJ on the patient to guide where the surgical incision is made. This helps reduce risk of injury to surrounding nerves and vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stripping of the short saphenous vein\",\"e\":\"Stripping of the short saphenous vein is generally not recommended due to the significant risk of injury to the sural nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"An 81-year-old man with newly diagnosed atrial fibrillation (AF) develops sudden onset left leg pain and pallor. He attends A&E due to the amount of discomfort he is in.\\n\\nWhat is the underlying pathology?\",\"lead\":\"What is the underlying pathology?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Thrombophilia\",\"e\":\"Thrombophilia is more commonly associated with deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Atheroma\",\"e\":\"Leg ischaemia secondary to an atheroma is chronic and presents with a history of progressively worsening claudication symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Thrombocytopenia\",\"e\":\"Thrombocytopenia presents with bleeding, ecchymoses and petechiae, not with an acutely ischaemic leg.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thrombosis\",\"e\":\"Though a significant number of acute limb ischaemia cases are secondary to a thrombus at sites of atherosclerosis, given the patient in this scenario has newly diagnosed AF an arterial embolus is the most likely mechanism of blockage of the arterial supply.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Embolus\",\"e\":\"Patients with atrial fibrillation are at risk of developing a left atrial thrombus. This can fragment and send emboli through the systemic circulation to any part of the body and cause ischaemia. In this case the patient presents with an acutely ischaemic leg. A similar picture can occur from a mural thrombus following an myocardial infarction (MI), or from an aneurysm+.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"An 81-year-old man, an ex-smoker, presents with a 2-year history of left calf pain after walking 500 m. He attends the clinic as his symptoms have steadily worsened during this time. What is the underlying pathological process?\",\"lead\":\"What is the underlying pathological process?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Embolus\",\"e\":\"An arterial embolus causes an acute episode of limb ischaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Thrombophilia\",\"e\":\"Thrombophilia is a hypercoagulable associated with a greater risk of developing deep vein thromboses and pulmonary emboli.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Atherosclerosis\",\"e\":\"The patient being a smoker and elderly, presents with symptoms of intermittent claudication. This is described as pain in the calves on exertion, relieved by a short period of rest. The timing of onset of symptoms is usually constant with the distance walked. Intermittent claudication is secondary to narrowing of the arterial lumen due to build-up of an atheromatous plaque. Symptoms are chronic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thrombocytopenia\",\"e\":\"Thrombocytopenia presents with bleeding, ecchymoses and petechiae, not with an acutely ischaemic leg.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thrombosis\",\"e\":\"In this scenario the patient is experiencing intermittent claudication associated with the presence of an atheromatous plaque in the arteries. The artery has not been occluded by a thrombus and distal blood flow remains\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"An 81-year-old ex-smoker presents with a 2-day history of worsening left calf pain even at rest. He has been getting pain for the last 10 years but was able to walk 500 m.\\n\\nWhat is the underlying pathological process?\",\"lead\":\"What is the underlying pathological process?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Embolus\",\"e\":\"An arterial embolus causes an acute episode of limb ischaemia. Symptom onset is acute - this patient has had symptoms for 2 days on background of claudication symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"NS Thrombophilia\",\"e\":\"Thrombophilia is a hypercoagulable condition, associated with a greater risk of developing deep vein thromboses and pulmonary emboli.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Vasculitis\",\"e\":\"Generally vague symptoms including joint pain, rashes and bleeding which this patient does not have. The symptoms stated do fit in with Buerger's disease, but this patient is an ex- smoker and too old for the classical presentation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thrombocytopenia\",\"e\":\"Thrombocytopenia presents with bleeding, ecchymoses and petechiae, not with an acutely ischacmic leg.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thrombosis\",\"e\":\"The patient presents with acute on chronic limb ischaemia. This has been caused by rupture of the atheromatous plaque at a site of atherosclerosis and superimposed thrombus formation, causing an obstruction to distal blood flow and ischaemia.\\n\\nLeg ischaemia secondary to an atheroma is chronic and presents with a history of progressively worsening claudication symptoms.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old man with hypertension presents with sudden onset severe chest pain that radiates to his back and arms. His blood pressure is 180/80 mmg in his right arm, but no reading is obtainable from the left arm. Cardiac examination reveals a murmur of aortic insufficiency (regurgitation). Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute aortic dissection\",\"e\":\"Aortic dissection* is a result of a tear in the intima of the aorta and propagation of dissection by blood collection in the intima-media space, leading to separation of the layers within the wall. It is a severe condition with high mortality and management is needed immediately. The biggest risk factor for developing an aortic dissection is hypertension. Other risk factors include age, coarctation of the aorta, atherosclerosis, connective tissue disorders, previous heart surgery and vasculitis. Symptoms associated with aortic dissection include: severe sudden onset tearing chest pain radiating to the back, dyspnoea, anxiety, hemiparesis, dysphasia, syncopal episode, abdominal pain (if abdominal aorta becomes involved) and flank pain (if renal arteries become involved). Examination findings include: hypertension, hypotension, interarm blood pressure difference of more than 20 mmHg, signs of aortic regurgitation (diastolic murmur, wide pulse pressure as in the scenario), asymmetrical\\n\\nChest X-ray may reveal a widened mediastinum and there might be a haemothorax if rupture has occurred. Transoesophageal echocardiography can be used to classify and evaluate a dissection. Computed tomography (CT) scan with contrast is the definitive diagnostic tool only if the patient is haemodynamically stable, however MRI carries a higher sensitivity. Treatment of stable chronic patients is with aggressive antihypertensive regimes: In an acute: aortic dissection, as in this scenario, urgent operation is required with segmental resection and interposition of a synthetic graft. Discrepancy in blood pressure readings between arms is seen in aortic dissection as well as congenital heart disease, peripheral vascular disease and some neuromuscular abnormalities.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acute myocardial intarction\",\"e\":\"An acute myocardial infarction can present with sudden onset central, 'crushing' chest pain that can radiate to the jaw and down the left arm. It can radiate to the back. It does not typically cause a discrepancy in blood pressure between the arms. Additionally, an acute myocardial infarction can cause a murmur, but this is systolic in nature secondary to rupture of the interventricular septum or papillary muscle, and not a diastolic murmur of aortic insufficiency.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Embolus to the right subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"A patient with an embolus to the right subclavian artery would present with an ischaemic+ right upper limb.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary embolism\",\"e\":\"A pulmonary embolism presents with sudden onset chest pain, unilateral that can radiate to the back, pleuritic in nature associated with shortness of breath, It does not present with interarm blood pressure discrepancy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spontaneous pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"A spontaneous pneumothorax presents with sudden onset chest pain, pleuritic in nature and shortness of breath. It rarely occurs in patients over the age of 40. A spontaneous pneumothorax does not cause interarm discrepancy in blood pressure nor a murmur of aortic incompetence.\\n\\nA patient was admitted with a mycotic aneurysm. He is being considered for an operation.\\n\\nWhich one of the following conditions is most likely to be associated with a mycotic aneurysm?\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old woman was referred to the vascular clinic with absent pulses and trophic changes in one hand. Symptoms were worse on exposure to cold weather. You diagnose Raynaud's phenomenon. Which one of the following is a recognised cause of Reynaud's phenomenon?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a recognised cause of Reynaud's phenomenon?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Idiopathic trombocytopenic purpura (ITP)\",\"e\":\"Raynaud's phenomenon may be idiopathic or secondary Secondary causes include connective tissue disorders, medications, or vessel obstruction. This includes those that cause a hypercoagulable state such as thrombocytosis/polycythaemia and not thrombocytopenia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intra-arterial papaverine administration\",\"e\":\"Papaverine is an antispasmodic medication used in the treatment of smooth muscle spasm that occurs in vessels, ureters or intestine. It is a vasodilator, therefore does not cause Raynaud's phenomenon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Carpal tunnel syndrome\",\"e\":\"Carpal tunnel syndrome affects the median nerve and is not associated with vascular spasm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Phenoxybenzamine therapy\",\"e\":\"Phenoxybenzamine, an a-blocker, used in the treatment of hypertension secondary to phaeochromocytoma causes vasodilation and therefore not Raynaud's phenomenon.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thoracic outlet syndrome\",\"e\":\"Thoracic outlet syndrome is a condition associated with axillary-subclavian artery symptoms resulting from compression from a cervical rib. Repetitive arterial compression leads to changes in the artery and thromboembolic complications Thoracic outlet syndrome can present with Raynaud's phenomenont or symptoms secondary to damage in the sympathetic chain of the subclavian bundle (coldness, cyanosis and oedema).\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis is complaining of intermittent attacks of discolouration of fingers in her hands. She has been referred to the vascular clinic.\\n\\nHow is primary Raynaud's disease different from secondary Raynaud's syndrome?\",\"lead\":\"How is primary Raynaud's disease different from secondary Raynaud's syndrome?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It more commonly affects both hands\",\"e\":\"Primary Raynaud's phenomenon (also known as Raynaud's disease) is characterised as recurrent vasospasm of the fingers and toes following cold exposure or stress alone, without association with another illness. The sequency of colour changes is WBC - White, Blue, Crimson (red). It is characterised by the following:\\n\\n• attacks triggered by exposure to cold or stress • bilateral arm involvement\\n\\n• no necrosis\\n\\n• no underiying cause • no antinuclear antibodies (ANA) • normal inflammatory markers and normal capillaries.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is more common in men than women\",\"e\":\"Primary Raynaud's phenomenon is seen more commonly in women aged 20-30. Other risk factors include family history and living in cold climates.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is typically associated with the presence of a cervical rib\",\"e\":\"Primary Raynaud's phenomenon occurs in the absence of an underlying condition and is of unknown aetiology. Secondary Raynaud's can be caused by a cervical rib.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It occurs in workers using vibrating tools\",\"e\":\"Vibration injury can be a cause of secondary Raynaud's but not primary\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is routinely treated with cervical sympathectomy\",\"e\":\"Cervical sympathectomy is reserved for patients with severe symptoms who have failed to improve with pharmacological treatment and show symptoms of critical ischaemia. Usually patients find improvement with medication.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"Ulcers of the ankle and foot could have several atiologies. The treatment for these varies depending on the cause.\\n\\nWhich one feature is suggestive of neuropathic ulcer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one feature is suggestive of neuropathic ulcer?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It affects the 'gaiter area\",\"e\":\"The gaiter area is affected by venous ulceration. Neuropathic ulcers occur at the sole of the foot, beneath the metatarsal heads and bony prominences.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is aggravated by an erect posture.\",\"e\":\"Erect posture usually aggravates venous ulçers, not neuropathic.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is frequently a consequence of deep vein thrombosis\",\"e\":\"A deep vein thrombosis can lead to a venous ulcer. Neuropathic ulcers are most commonly caused by diabetes and are often secondary to minor trauma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is usually associated with oedema of the leg\",\"e\":\"Tissue surrounding neuropathic ulcers is usually well perfused and appears normal. Oedema of the leg is usually associated with venous ulceration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It typically occurs under metatarsal heads\",\"e\":\"Neuropathic ulcers are deep, painless with a punched-out appearance and bleed easily. They are usually found on the sole of the foot, beneath the heads of the metatarsals and bony prominences.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"A 59-year-old woman who is obese is being assessed for chronic venous insufficiency in the vascular clinic. She is concerned about the facets of the disease she may go on to develop.\\n\\nWhat is the most common complication to be aware of?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most common complication to be aware of?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"Explanation it\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"St Cellulitis\",\"e\":\"Cellulitis can occur in chronic venous insufficiency when venous ulcers become superimposed with infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AM Leg ulceration\",\"e\":\"The most common complication of venous insufficiency is venous ulceration, which usually occurs around the medial or lateral malleolus (gaiter area. Venous ulcers are painless with sloughed ends. They are more common in women and are surrounded by skin inflammation and oedema. Other complications associated with chronic venous insufficiency include: recruitment of collateral veins, venous thromboembolism, lymphoedema, recurrent cellulitis and skin discoloration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"DVT\",\"e\":\"DVT can be both a cause and a complication of chronic venous insufficiency, but this complication is not the most common.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Osteomyelitis\",\"e\":\"Untreated chronic venous insufficiency can lead to venous ulceration. Venous ulcers can become superimposed with infection and if left untreated can progress to affect bone and cause osteomyelitis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary embolism\",\"e\":\"Chronic venous insufficiency can increase the risk of both DVT and pulmonary embolism (PE), but this complication is not the most common.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient with a rash on their legs and a diagnosis of thrombophlebitis migrans is made. Additional investigations may be required With which of the following is this associated?\",\"lead\":\"Additional investigations may be required With which of the following is this associated?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma of pancreas\",\"e\":\"Thrombophlebitis migrans, also known as Trousseau's sign of malignancy, is a migratory thrombophlebitis causing recurrent episodes of superficial thrombophlebitis in the low extremities. It has a strong association with visceral carcinoma, most commonly adenocarcinoma of the pancreas, lung, stomach and genital tract.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diabetes\",\"e\":\"Thrombophlebitis migrans is associated with visceral carcinomas, most commonly pancreatic and lung.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glioblastoma\",\"e\":\"Thrombophlebitis migrans is associated with visceral carcinomas most commonly pancreatic and lung carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Phlegmasia cerulae dolens:\",\"e\":\"Phlegmasia cerulae dolens is a severe form of deep vein thrombosis affecting the major veins of one of the extremities as well as its collateral veins. This condition is not associated with thrombophlebitis migrans.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Venous insufficiency\",\"e\":\"Thrombophlebitis migrans is associated with visceral carcinomas, especially pancreatic and lung.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient with Raynaud's disease, but suspect it is secondary Raynaud's phenomenon. They have intermittent episodes where fingers become white and uncomfortable.\\n\\nWith which one of the following is Raynaud's phenomenon associated?\",\"lead\":\"With which one of the following is Raynaud's phenomenon associated?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypertension\",\"e\":\"Hypertension does not directly cause secondary Raynaud's phenomenon, however hypertension increases the risk of developing atherosclerosis, which can in turn lead to Raynaud's disease:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AM Lupus\",\"e\":\"Connective tissue disorders such as systemic lupus erythamatosus, rheumatoid arthritis and scleroderma can be a cause of secondary Raynaud's phenomenon* Additionally, smoking and exposure to chemical such as vinyl chloride increase the risk of developing secondary Raynaud's phenomenon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Psoriatic arthritis\",\"e\":\"Psoriatic arthritis is not known to cause Raynaud's disease. Rheumatoid arthritis on the other hand is a known cause of secondary Raynaud's phenomenon.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sickle cell disease\",\"e\":\"Sickle-cell disease is not a known cause of Raynaud's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tuberculosis\",\"e\":\"Tuberculosis is not a known cause of Raynaud's disease.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"A 63-year-old woman with insulin dependent diabetes develops severe sepsis and is transferred to the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) with intropic support. She subsequently develops necrosis of her fingertips. Which one of the following is descriptive of dry gangrene?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is descriptive of dry gangrene?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is caused by Clostridium infection\",\"e\":\"a necrotising infection most commonly due to Clostridium infection causes gas gangrene, Clostridium perfringens, which releases toxins and leads to tissue damage. It presents with extreme pain associated with purplish skin discoloration, bullae, profuse serous discharge and crepitations of the tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It occurs when both venous and arterial obstruction are present\",\"e\":\"Both arteries and veins are affected in wet gangrene. Dry gangrene is caused by arterial obstruction. Dry gangrene treatment involves restoration of blood supply and, if this is unsuccessful, amputation takes place.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It occurs when the tissues are desiccated by gradual slowing of the bloodstream\",\"e\":\"Gangrene is a process where there is necrosis and decay of body tissue It results from reduced blood supply to the tissues secondary to vascular disease, usually relating to atherosclerosis, or due to vasculitis. The area involved becomes cold, pale or erythematous, numb and there is no discharge. It eventually becomes brown or black and will slough off.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It smells\",\"e\":\"Wet gangrene, the result of infection of the tissues from staphylococci and streptococci species that cause inflammation and obstruct the blood flow in both vein and arteries supplying the area. This change leads to propagation of the infection and discharge. The discharge is usually malodorous. Other characteristics of wet gangrene include swelling and erythema, initially, and then the tissue turns black. Wet gangrene is treated with analgaesia, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and finally surgical debridement, if all the above fail. Dry gangrene, being without discharge, does not smell.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"There will be crepitus and septicaemia from toxin release\",\"e\":\"Crepitus and septicaemia from toxin release is associated with gas gangrene caused by Clostridium perfringens.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"Thrombophlebitis is the inflammation of a vein secondary to formation of a thrombus within that vein. It is often seen as a complication of cannulation:\\n\\nWhat is a well recognised factor in inciting thrombophlebitis?\",\"lead\":\"What is a well recognised factor in inciting thrombophlebitis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Intracranial malignancy\",\"e\":\"Thrombophlebitis is described as superficial venous inflammation that can occur in the presence or absence of a clot. It occurs most commonly in the lower limbs. Conditions that cause a hypercoagulable state such as malignancy and pregnancy are the main causes of thrombophlebitis: Additionally, conditions that lead to venous stasis such as immobility post- surgery or during long haul flights, as well as venous incompetence and varicosities. Signs and symptoms include:\\n\\n• swelling, erythaema and tenderness along the affected vein • hardness if there is a clot involved.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Early mobilisation\",\"e\":\"Postoperatively, early mobilisation is one of the factors that prevents venous stasis and therefore prevents thrombophlebitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Local anaesthetics\",\"e\":\"Thrombophlebitis can occur at intravenous injection or infusion sites as well as sites of trauma. Local anaesthetics are not injected into vessels (apart from intravenous Bier's block) and therefore are rare causes of thrombophlebitis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prophylaxis\",\"e\":\"Prophylaxis of thromboembolic disease, such as TED stockings and low-molecular-weight heparin injections given postoperatively reduce the risk of thrombus formation within veins and therefore reduce the risk of thrombophlebitis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Short haul flights\",\"e\":\"Long haul flights, as they lead to prolonged immobility, increase venous stasis and can lead to the development of thrombophlebitis.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old man with Marfan syndrome presents with sudden onset chest pain radiating to his back. A plain chest X-ray reveals a widened mediastinum.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a recognised complication of an ascending aortic dissection?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a recognised complication of an ascending aortic dissection?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute myocardial infarction\",\"e\":\"Dissectiont of the ascending aorta (DeBakey Types | and II, Stanford type A) can disrupt the ostia of the coronary arteries and therefore affect the blood supply to the heart, leading to myocardial ischaemia or infarction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemothorax\",\"e\":\"Very rarely dissection of the ascending aorta can lead to a right sided haemothorax if it ruptures. This however leads to sudden death and is a post-mortem finding, therefore it is not as recognised as acute myocardial infarction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mitral valve rupture\",\"e\":\"Aortic dissection can lead to dilation of the aortic root and annulus and therefore lead to aortic valve incompetence and regurgitation. The mitral valve is not normally affected.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pericardial transudate effusion\",\"e\":\"A transudative pericardial effusion is the result of congestive heart failure, nephrotic syndrome or myxoedema. Ascending aorta dissection can lead to a haemorrhagic pericardial effusion, or haemopericardium which leads to cardiac tamponade and can cause syncope.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary embolus\",\"e\":\"Dissection of the arch of the aorta can lead to compression of the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries, however a pulmonary embolus is not a recognised complication of aortic dissection.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing an elderly male patient in the general surgical clinic and are giving advice on the risk factors for peripheral vascular disease which he could modify. He gets pain in his calves when sleeping at night. Which of these is most appropriate to tell the patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which of these is most appropriate to tell the patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"If he has two risk factors his risk of arterial disease is doubled\",\"e\":\"If a patient has two risk factors, the risk of arterial disease is increased several fold and not in a linear fashion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"He shouldn't worry about his cholesterol level as this is not a risk factor\",\"e\":\"Dyslipidaemia and hypercholesterolaemia increase the risk of developing peripheral arterial disease. It is important to control cholesterol levels.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"His risk will be less as he gets older\",\"e\":\"Risk of peripheral vascular disease increases with increasing age.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"His wife is at more risk of peripheral arterial disease than him, all other factors being equal\",\"e\":\"Men are at a significantly higher risk of developing peripheral vascular disease than women.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stopping smoking will reduce his risk of peripheral arterial disease\",\"e\":\"Smoking is the strongest risk factor associated with peripheral vascular disease. Stopping smoking leads to a significant reduction in the incidence of claudication. After one year of quitting smoking the European Stroke Association quotes that inc dence drops to the equivalent of a non-smoker. Other risk factors for developing peripheral arterial disease include: diabetes mellitus (x5 fold increase), male gender, increasing age, hypertension and dyslipidaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"You are asked to review a patient who has an arterial ulcer by the tissue viability nurses. The wound is not healing despite conservative measures. Which of the following are associated with arterial ulceration?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following are associated with arterial ulceration?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Absolute pedal pressures are over 100 mmHg\",\"e\":\"A normal ABPI (Ankle brachial pressure index) is 1.0-1.4, meaning that in normal individuals the ankle blood pressure is the same or higher than brachial systolic pressure. Therefore in arterial ulceration, where the ABPI is <0.9, absolute pedal pressures must be lower than systolic brachial pressure, therefore generally less than 100mmHg.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Age less than 65\",\"e\":\"Ulceration and gangrene are complications of peripheral arterial disease that commonly affects older men with atherosclerotic disease. It is not commonly seen in younger patients.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"History of smoking cigarettes\",\"e\":\"The most common cause of peripheral arterial disease and arterial ulceration * is atherosclerosis. Smoking is the strongest independent risk factor for developing peripheral arterial disease. Other risk factors include: age > 50, male gender, hypertension, diabetes, coronary heart disease, cerebrovascular disease and dyslipidaemia. Characteristics of arterial ulcers:\\n\\n• on the dorsum • painful, pain worse at night - patients report finding relief by hanging their leg out of the bed at night.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Negative Burger's test\",\"e\":\"A positive Burger's test is a valuable examination finding in the diagnosis of peripheral arterial disease. It is performed with the patient initially supine. Both legs are raised up to 45 degrees and maintained in that position until pallor ensues in the feet (usually 1-2 minutes, pallor indicates ischaemia and occurs when the arterial pressure is inadequate to overcome effects of gravity). An angle of <20 degrees elevation causing pallor indicates severe ischaemia. Then the patient is asked to sit up and allow his/her legs to dangle off the side of the examination couch. The time required for pinkness to return in the feet is measured. 10- 15 seconds is the normal time that indicates competent arteries. In peripheral arterial disease, feet become blue (deoxygenated blood) and then red (hyperaemia, from post hypoxic vasodilation). This rubor is also a positive Buerger's test.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Varicose veins\",\"e\":\"The presence of varicose veins indicates venous insufficiency and chronic venous hypertension, a risk factor for developing venous ulceration ie ulcers in the gaiter region, shallow with ill-defined borders, painless unless infected, with surrounding granulation tissue. Venous ulcers can become very large before reported as they are usually painless.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":92,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old woman presents to you in the vascular clinic with unsightly, tortuous superficial veins on the medial aspect of her right lower limb. You send the patient for a Venous Duplex and this confirms incompetence of the right long saphenous vein.\\n\\nWhich one of the following would be a definite contraindication to surgical treatment?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would be a definite contraindication to surgical treatment?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Occluded deep veins confirmed on the venous duplex\",\"e\":\"Occluded deep veins confirmed on the venous duplex are an absolute contraindication to surgical treatment of varicose veins. This is because the superficial veins allow collateral circulation, to which flow in the affected limb is dependent.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Previous deep vein thrombosis in the same leg\",\"e\":\"Previous deep vein thrombosis in the same leg is a relative contraindication to varicose vein surgery, mainly because of the increased risk of recurrence of the deep vein thrombosis which will compromise venous circulation in the leg. However, in cases where the clot has resolved and forward flow is demonstrated on venous duplex, surgery can occur. If there is reversal of flow and therefore valve incompetence in the deep system, it is a riskier operation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Previous saphenofemoral disconnection\",\"e\":\"Varicose veins commonly recur following surgery. Previous varicose vein surgery, in the form of saphenofemoral junction ligation and disconnection is not a contraindication for further surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"She has a strong family history of breast and ovarian malignancy\",\"e\":\"This is not a contra-indication to surgery. However it is important to note that varicosities and lymphoedema may present due to an undiagnosed pelvic malignancy, and in a patient with family history this should be investigated.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The varicose veins first appeared during pregnancy\",\"e\":\"This is not a contra-indication to surgery. Varicose veins are more common in the pregnant population than in the general population of the same age. Varicose vein surgery during pregnancy is contraindicated, as in many cases varicosities resolve after delivery. However, if these ensue and are problematic the patient should be assessed for surgical intervention.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":95,\"stem\":\"A patient arrives in resus moribund with a suspected ruptured AAA. He is taken immediately to the operating theatre.\\n\\nWhat is the mortality rate associated with ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAA) presenting to hospital?\",\"lead\":\"What is the mortality rate associated with ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAA) presenting to hospital?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"30%\",\"e\":\"Only 50% of the patients that present to hospital with a ruptured aortic aneurysm survive repair. There is therefore a 50% intra-hospital mortality rate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"40%\",\"e\":\"Only 50% of the patients that present to hospital with a ruptured aortic aneurysm survive repair. There is therefore a 50% intra-hospital mortality rate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"50%\",\"e\":\"Of all patients with a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, 50% make it to hospital alive. There is therefore a 50% pre-hospital mortality rate. Additionally, out of the ones presenting to hospital, only 50% survive repair. There is therefore a 50% intra-hospital mortality rate. Overall mortality associated with a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm is therefore 75%.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"60%\",\"e\":\"Only 50% of the patients that present to hospital with a ruptured aortic aneurysm survive repair. There is therefore a 50% intra-hospital mortality rate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"70%\",\"e\":\"Only 50% of the patients that present to hospital with a ruptured aortic aneurysm survive repair. There is therefore a 50% intra-hospital mortality rate.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"A 76-year-old woman attends for review of an ulcer that appeared 2 weeks ago over the medial aspect of her lower left leg. This is shallow with sloping edges. On examination, there is hyperpigmentation of surrounding skin. She takes warfarin for a previous recurrent DVT.\\n\\nWhat is the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"St Arterial ulcer\",\"e\":\"An arterial ulcer typically presents secondary to reduced arterial supply in the lower limb. It is found on the distal foot and toes, with a 'punched-out' appearance. It is painful and the surrounding skin may show signs of arterial compromise such as: pallor, loss of hair, absent pulses. Patients tend to report that the pain is worse at night.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Marjolin's ulcer\",\"e\":\"Marjolin's ulcer is a term used to describe a tumour that presents in an area of chronic inflammation or tissue injury. It is a slow growing tumour that develops over months to years. They are suspected in cases of chronic ulceration that fails to heal with basic wound care and may be associated with lymphadenopathy. Diagnosis is via biopsy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Post-phlebitic ulcer\",\"e\":\"This patient has a post-phlebitic ulcer - a venous ulcer - because of symptomatic chronic venous insufficiency after a DVT. Post-phiebitic syndrome usually presents in the first 2 years following a DVT and signs include: leg pain, oedema, skin induration and discolouration, which if left untreated can lead to venous ulceration. Venous ulceration occurs in the gaiter area (ie the region between the knee and the ankle) and most commonly around the medial malleolus: A venous ulcer is usually shallow, with irregular (sioping) edges, painful (although generally less painful than arterial ulcers) with a moist granulating base. They can be malodorous and are commonly affected by superimposed infection. The skin hyperpigmentation described in the scenario is called lipodermatosclerosis and relates to hemosiderin deposition.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neuropathic ulcer\",\"e\":\"A neuropathic ulcer is found over pressure points, commonly over the 1st or 5th metatarsophalangeal joints and sole of the foot. Neuropathic ulcers have punched out edges and a deep sinus, surrounded by chronic inflammation. They are painless.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vasculitic ulcer\",\"e\":\"Vasculitic ulcers are rare and difficult to diagnose as they resemble other forms of ulceration. Causes include: idiopathic vasculitis, infection and autoimmune. They present initially with localised cutaneous vasculitis in an area that was initially erythematous. If remains untreated the skin can develop widespread purpura, skin necrosis and eventually ulceration. Diagnosis is via biopsy.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":101,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man is transferred to your vascular centre with a suspected abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA). Fast scan at the initial hospital Emergency Department showed a 9 cm suspected aneurysm. Vital signs are as follows, heart rate 100 bpm, blood pressure 117 systolic, 02sats 94% on room air.\\n\\nWhat is the next appropriate step?\",\"lead\":\"What is the next appropriate step?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CT scan\",\"e\":\"The patient is at the moment haemodynamically stable therefore there is no need to rush into theatre before appropriate radiological investigation. For the investigation of AAA, the first investigation to order is an abdominal ultrasound, this has been performed via a FAST scan and an AAA detected. CT has a sensitivity nearing 100% for detection of AAA. A CT scan of the abdomen is indicated in this scenario to evaluate in more detail the size and shape of the aneurysm and look for signs of aneurysmal leak or rupture such as the crescent sign (blood within the thrombus), retroperitoneal haematoma, wall discontinuity or extravasation of contrast in the peritoneal cavity. CT also measures the length of the neck of the aneurysm, which can be used in defining suitability of the patient for EVAR. This information will help guide the vascular surgeon with regards to how to manage the patient.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Elective repair\",\"e\":\"Elective repair is generally recommended for AAA that are more than or equal to 5-5.5 cm in diameter. Given the size of the aneurysm in this scenario and the extremely high risk of rupture, the procedure needs to be performed as an emergency.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Theatre for emergency endovascular aortic repair (EVAR)\",\"e\":\"Following the results of the CT, results of the anatomy of the aneurysm can be reviewed by the surgeon and if the patient remains stable, the possibility of performing an emergency EVAR can be examined.\\n\\nThe patient will require surgical intervention for repair of his abdominal aortic aneurysm and if unsuitable for EVAR this will be performed by open repair. Additionally, if the patient becomes haemodynamically unstable and there is suspicion of a ruptured AAA, then immediate transfer to theatre for open repair will be necessary.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Theatre for emergency open repair\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Repeat Fast Scan\",\"e\":\"Given the Fast scan has already been performed, there would be no benefit in repeating this as it would not add any new information that would affect the management of the patient.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":104,\"stem\":\"A worried mother brings her child to see you. He has just started walking and she has noticed that he has a gait abnormality, as his right leg appears longer than the other. He has prominent varicosities on his right leg and a bluish 'port-wine stain' on his face. What is the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Deep vein thrombosis \\\"C..\",\"e\":\"Deep vein thrombosis usually presents in patients over the age of 40.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Kippel-Trenaunay syndrome\",\"e\":\"Kippel-Trenauay syndrome, also known as angio-osteohypertrophy syndrome, is a rare disorder associated with sporadic mutations in the somatic cells during fetal development. It is a syndrome affecting the development of bone, soft tissues and blood vessels and presents in infancy or early childhood. It is associated with the following triad:\\n\\n• port wine stain (or capillary hemangioma) - often the first sign and noted at birth • varicose veins and venous malformations that can be either superficial or affecting the deep veins • soft tissue or bone hypertrophy affecting an extremity, all of which are described in this scenario. Limb discrepancy of up to 12 cm has been reported in the literature.\\n\\nDiagnosis is clinical and management is symptomatic.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Milroy disease\",\"e\":\"Milroy disease is a autosomal dominant condition leading to abnormal function of the lymphatic vessels in the lower limbs and is characterised by lymphoedema presenting at birth or soon after. Other features that can be associated with this condition include: hydrocele, vein prominence, urethral anomalies in males. Cellulitis and superimposed infection can affect these patients. Treatment is with compression hosiery and bandaging to reduce the oedema and the risk of complications.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Popliteal aneurysm\",\"e\":\"The mean age of presentation of a popliteal aneurysm is 65 years of age with a male to female ratio of about 20: 1. It is very unlikely to be the diagnosis in this case. Patients with popliteal aneurysms are usually asymptomatic. Popliteal aneurysms are often found in patients with abdominal aortic aneurysms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Secondary lymphoedema\",\"e\":\"Secondary lymphoedema usually occurs following cancer especially with radiotherapy treatment, surgery, infection, trauma and varicose vein surgery with vein stripping. This causes obstruction to the lymphatic flow. There is no history of any of the above in this case.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"You are called to see an 8-year-old girl admitted overnight following an emergency open reduction and internal fixation of her right supracondylar fracture. She is complaining of severe pain and tingling in her right hand. On examination, there is pain on passive extension of the fingers.\\n\\nWhat is the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arteriovenous malformation\",\"e\":\"An arteriovenous malformation is unlikely in this case as these present with focal neurological abnormalities, a seizure or stroke symptoms. They can be asymptomatic. Even though there are focal symptoms in the arm of the patient given the previous surgery and the clinical findings, compartment syndrome is the most likely diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Axillary vein thrombosis\",\"e\":\"Axillary vein thrombosis is unlikely in this patient. There are no risk factors in the above scenario associated with axillary vein thrombosis such as: central venous catheter, compression in the thoracic outlet, malignancy, diabetes, obesity, intense sports activity, oral contraceptive use or pregnancy. Findings associated with axillary vein thrombosis include: oedema of the arm and hand, hand cyanosis, dilated veins in chest and upper arm, jugular vein distention, palpable vein/fullness in the supraclavicular fossa.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Axillary artery aneurysm\",\"e\":\"Axillary artery aneurysms are rare and occur either secondary to atherosclerosis or multiple trauma to the artery (usually blunt use of crutches). These tend to present in older individuals.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Compartment syndrome\",\"e\":\"Acute compartment syndrome* is a surgical emergency caused by fractures, injury. prolonged use of constricting bandages, muscle hypertrophy, burns, infection and iatrogenic causes (secondary to IM injections). Most common cause is a fracture in the long bones Compartment syndrome develops when the pressure of the tissue in a closed compartment exceeds perfusion pressure and therefore impedes tissue oxygenation. This leads to muscle and nerve ischaemia followed by necrosis, if not dealt with promptly. Common sites of compartment syndrome include the lower leg and the arm distal to the elbow (forearm, wrist, hand). Signs and symptoms include: pain out of proportion for the injury, described as burning and exacerbated by passive extension of the muscle compartment. Tissue oedema and paraesthesia can occur. A test with high specificity for the diagnosis is a decreased 2- point discrimination test. If it remains untreated the affected limb shows signs of acute ischaemia. Treatment is with immediate release of pressure. In this scenario the plaster cast needs to be split immediately and if symptoms do not improve then a fasciotomy will be necessary. If not treated promptly compartment syndrome can lead to contractures resulting from necrosis of the muscles and nerves.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Secondary lymphoedema\",\"e\":\"Secondary lymphoedema usually occurs following cancer especially with radiotherapy treatment, surgery, infection, trauma and varicose vein surgery with vein stripping. Whatever the cause, it is the result of lymphatic obstruction. Even though this can occur following surgery, it is more of a chronic complication and unlikely to present within the first few days of an operation.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":110,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old smoker attends for review due to intermittent pain in the back of the right calf on walking. After 30 yards she needs to stop and rest to ease the pain Which is her likely ankle brachial pressure index (ABPI) value?\",\"lead\":\"After 30 yards she needs to stop and rest to ease the pain Which is her likely ankle brachial pressure index (ABPI) value?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"LAWiIal 1CGALIVGl Le) ae\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1\",\"e\":\"Normal ABPI is usually in the range of 1-1.2 ABPI of >1.3 is consistent with calcified arteries making them non-compressible and commonly seen in diabetic patients.\\n\\nABP| of 1 is normal.\\n\\nABPI of 1 is normal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"x\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"0.9 - 0.5\",\"e\":\"This patient presents with symptoms of intermittent claudication, pain in the calves on walking or exertion resulting from diminished circulation. ABPI is used in the assessment of peripheral arterial disease and the severity of it as well. ABPI is calculated by dividing the systolic blood pressure at the ankle (dorsalis pedis or posterior tibial artery by the systolic blood pressure of the arm measured at the brachial artery. ABPI of <0.9 indicates the presence of peripheral artery disease. A value of <0.5 indicates the presence of critical ischaemia (defined as severe impairment in circulation that can lead to limb loss). Intermittent claudication has a variable 5 year prognosis:\\n\\n• 10-20% have worsening symptoms • 5-10% develop critical ischaemia • 1-2% may require amputation • the remainder have stable symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"0.5- 0:3\",\"e\":\"Patients with an ABPI of 0.5-0.3 indicates severe arterial disease and patients tend to present with rest pain.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"0.2\",\"e\":\"An ABPI of <0.3 indicates critical limb ischaemia and threatened limb loss if untreated. Patients tend to present with ulceration or gangrene. These patients require immediate referral to vascular surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":113,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old lifelong smoker presents with a punched-out deep ulcer on the ball of his foot. On examination the foot is cool and hairless. The dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulse are difficult to feel\\n\\nWhat is the likely aetiology?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely aetiology?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"15\\\" Arterial\",\"e\":\"This patient presents with peripheral arterial disease causing reduced arterial supply of the lower limb, which has in turn led to the formation of an arterial ulcer. Arterial ulcers are typically found on the ankle and foot over pressure areas. Arterialulcerst are deep, with a 'punched-out' appearance and are very painful. Patients tend to report that the pain is worse at night.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diabetic\",\"e\":\"A diabetic ulcer is the most common form of neuropathic ulcers. A neuropathic ulcer is found over pressure points, commonly over the 1st or 5th metatarsophalangeal joints and sole of the foot. Neuropathic ulcers have punched-out edges and a deep sinus, surrounded by chronic inflammation. They are painless.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Infective\",\"e\":\"Infective ulcers are usually secondary to bacterial or fungal infection. There is usually associated erythema, discharge and the patient may show systemic signs of infection. They require swabbing and culture to detect the exact causative organism. Common causes include: Staphylococcus and Streptococcus. Infection can superimpose any other kind of uicer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Malignant\",\"e\":\"Malignant skin ulcers can be the result of primary malignancy or secondary (most commonly breast, head, neck). They occur in patients usually over the age of 70 with known metastatic malignancy. They are either cavitating forming a crater, proliferative tumours forming a nodular 'cauliflower-like' structure on the skin or both.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Venous\",\"e\":\"Venous ulcers are the most commonly encountered ulcers and are associated with chronic venous insufficiency. Venous ulceration occurs in the gaiter area (ie the region between the knee and the ankle) and most commoniy around the medial malleolus. A venous ulcer is usually shallow, with irregular (slopping) edges, painful with a moist granulating base. They can be malodorous and are commonly affected by superimposed infection. A venous ulcer can also be associated with other features of chronic venous insufficiencyt\\n\\nThis patient has peripheral arterial disease with typical deep punched out ulcers. The limb has signs of chronic ischaemia with absent pulses. These ulcers usually occur in pressure areas such as the ball of the foot or heel.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":116,\"stem\":\"The medical team ask you to review a patient on the ward who has developed a swollen painful pale oedematous leg. Looking through the notes you see the patient was diagnosed with a deep vein thrombosis last week. Pulses are present.\\n\\nWhich is the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which is the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atherosclerotic ischaemia\",\"e\":\"Atherosclerotic ischaemia is a chronic condition* and unlikely in the above scenario.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Deep vein thrombosis\",\"e\":\"The diagnosis of a deep vein thrombosis is already known. The presentation of the patient in the above scenario is related to a complication from the deep vein thrombosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lymphedema tarda\",\"e\":\"Lymphoedema tarda is the rarest form of primary lymphoedema usually presenting in patients above the age of 35. An inherited abnormality in the valves of the lymphatic vessels, which is then exacerbated by an event such as trauma, is thought to be the cause, causing lymphatic incompetence and problematic drainage. It is commonly seen in patients with cutaneous syndromes such as neurofibromatosis and haemangiomas. It is unlikely to be the diagnosis in this scenario as this involves a chronic accumulation of fluid and peripheral oedema.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Phlegmasia alba dolens\",\"e\":\"This is an example of phlegmasias alba dolens (meaning painful white inflammation/oedema), a complication of an extensive deep vein thrombosis. It only affects deep veins and not collateral veins, therefore there is still some venous drainage. The superficial venous system is not however able to manage the large volume, high pressure blood delivered to the leg* from the arterial system resulting in sudden onset of a painful. oedematous, white leg (alba = white). Phlegmasia alba dolens is part of a spectrum and if untreated can develop to phlegmasia cerulea dolens and eventually gangrene. Treatment is with thrombolysis or thrombectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pheigmasia cerulea dolens\",\"e\":\"Phlegmasia cerulea dolens (meaning painful blue oedema) is the progression of untreated phlegmasia alba dolens. The build-up of oedema causes occlusion of the superficial venous system and therefore results in complete lack of venous drainage and massive congestion. This affects the arterial supply and leads to ischaemia and eventually gangrene. Treatment is with thrombolysis or thrombectomy\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":119,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant with a femoral-popliteal bypass. Your consultant is contemplating where to site the distal anastomosis and also what nature of conduit to use for the graft.\\n\\nWhich one of the following has the best patency rate?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following has the best patency rate?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Above knee anastomosis using long saphenous vein\",\"e\":\"Success of the revascularisation depends on a good inflow vessel (adequate pressure and flow), outflow vessel (least diseased and with outflow to the foot), and the conduit. In general, above knee anastomoses have a better patency rate than infrapopliteal anastomoses. Vénous grafts are superior to any of the available synthetic grafts. If the saphenous vein is not suitable for bypass grafting or is absent, a polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) graft is the best alternative.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Above knee anastomosis using polytetrafluorothylene (PTFE)\",\"e\":\"PTFE is the most commonly used synthetic graft, but is inferior to using a vein autograft.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Below knee anastomosis using Dacron\",\"e\":\"Synthetic grafts such as PTFE and Dacron are inferior to venous autografts. Additionally above knee anastomoses are better than below knee.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Below knee anastomosis using long saphenous vein\",\"e\":\"Vein grafts are superior to any synthetic conduits, with the long saphenous vein being the most commonly used autograft. Vein grafts in infrapopliteal positions have a 1-year patency of 70-80%, whereas synthetic grafts have an average 1-year patency of 30-50% when used in the infrapopliteal region.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Below knee anastomosis using PTFE\",\"e\":\"PTFE is the most commonly used synthetic graft, but vein grafts are superior to any synthetic graft.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":122,\"stem\":\"Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of a patients' abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) are being discussed at a vascular multidisciplinary team (MDT). The decision is being made as to whether it is suitable for endovascular repair.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct for endovascular aneurysm repair (EVAR) of AAA?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct for endovascular aneurysm repair (EVAR) of AAA?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Technically feasible in all patients with infra-renal abdominal aortic aneurysms\",\"e\":\"EVAR is a minimally invasive procedure that involves a stent-graft insertion within the aneurysm, though a small incision in the femoral artery. Not all patients with infra-renal abdominal aortic aneurysms are candidates for treatment with EVAR. Suitability is defined by various anatomical parameters assessed by computed tomography (CT), and include aneurysm neck angle, length and diameter as well as distance of the common iliac artery length and diameter. Reports suggest EVAR is possible in only 40-60% of patients.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Not possible for juxta-renal aneurysms\",\"e\":\"EVAR is possible in the management of juxta-renal and supra-renal aneurysms by the use of fenestrated graft stents.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has a higher 30-day mortality than conventional open aneurysm repair\",\"e\":\"Mortality associated with EVAR is reported as being about one-third of that with conventional open aneurysm repair in the short term. However, trials have shown that the rate remains the same in the medium and longer terms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is cheaper than conventional open aneurysm repair\",\"e\":\"The EVAR procedure is more expensive than the conventional open aneurysm repair.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Requires long-term follow-up\",\"e\":\"Patients who undergo EVAR, are at risk of developing endovascular leaks also called endoleaks, and the main two types of endoleak of concern are type I (where the graft does not seal tightly to the vessel leading to continuous blood flow though the aneurysm and, type I| (caused by back-filling of the aneurysm from small vessels in its wall). There is also a risk of complication from stent migration. Due to these risks, patients who undergo EVAR, need follow-up with computed tomography (CT) or ultrasound scans to screen the development of complications. Patients who undergo traditional open surgical repair do not require regular follow-up.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":125,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient on the ward who is unwell following an endovascular aneurysm repair (EVAR). They are clinically euvolaemic. What is the most likely cause of renal impairment in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of renal impairment in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Accidental stent-graft occlusion of renal artery origins\",\"e\":\"Stent-graft occlusion of renal artery origins is a recognised complication of EVAR, but remains uncommon. This is typically due to partial occlusion of the renal artery or stent migration occluding the renal artery origins partly or completely. This leads to delayed renal dysfunction and can be seen as early as 1 week postoperatively.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Contrast nephrotoxicity from stenting procedure\",\"e\":\"To correctly position a stent graft during an EVAR procedure, it is necessary to use iodinated contrast medium. This contrast is toxic to the kidney and in contact with the stent graft, acute kidney injury can develop particularly in those with risk factors such as diabetes, or in those already taking nephrotoxic drugs.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pre-renal renal failure\",\"e\":\"Pre-renal renal failure is associated with hypovolaemia or low blood pressure leading to reduced perfusion of the kidney and so reduced urine output. Causes include: conditions of systemic vasodilation such as sepsis, hypercalcaemia, hypovolaemia, cardiorenal or hepatorenal syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Type Tondoleak 2' V\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Type 2 endoleak\",\"e\":\"Type I endoleak is a complication of EVAR but does not cause acute renal failure. Type I endoleak is associated with inadequate apposition of the stent graft onto the aneurysm wall leading to an incomplete seal. These allows continuous blood flow and enlargement of the aneurysm sac.\\n\\nType II endoleak is a complication arising following EVAR but does not cause acute renal failure. It is the most common endoleak associated with repair of abdominal aortic aneurysms. In this case the aneurysm sac continues to fill up from small vessel branches and can continue to expand\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":128,\"stem\":\"You are in the vascular clinic reviewing a man who has calf pain while walking. You take a history, measure the ankle-brachial pressure indices and make a diagnosis of intermittent claudication.\\n\\nRegarding the epidemiology and natural history of this condition, which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding the epidemiology and natural history of this condition, which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Has a prevalence of 50% in those aged over 60 years\",\"e\":\"It is estimated from population studies that intermittent claudication, and therefore peripheral arterial disease, has a prevalence of about 20% in those aged over the age of 60.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Has a 5 year mortality of 5% *\",\"e\":\"Patients with peripheral arterial disease with symptoms of intermittent claudication have a 5 year mortality of more than 30%, mainly from coronary and cerebrovascular events.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has a shorter life expectancy, the lower their ankle-brachial pressure index\",\"e\":\"The lower the ankle-brachial pressure index (ABPI) the worse the disease present, therefore the more likely the patient will die from a complication related to the peripheral artery disease or a coronary/cerebrovascular event.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Will improve or remain stable in 50% of cases\",\"e\":\"Most people with intermittent claudication where the risk factors are addressed and managed appropriately continue to have stable symptoms over the course of 5 years. Conversely, 10-20% of patients develop worsening symptoms and 5-10% will develop critical ischaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Will require major amputation in 50% of cases\",\"e\":\"Over a 5 year period 1-2% of patients with intermittent claudication will require a lower limb amputation. This is however higher in patients with diabetes, reaching 5%.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":131,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the Emergency Department with a painful, pulsatile swelling in the groin. You suspect a pseudo-aneurysm.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is most appropriate regarding these lesions?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most appropriate regarding these lesions?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are less common in intravenous drug users (IVDU)\",\"e\":\"False aneurysms (pseudo-aneurysms) occur secondary to a disruption in the vessel wall integrity, commonly occurring with arterial puncture. They are therefore more common in intravenous drug users compared to the general population.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are commonly treated by aorto-bifemoral grafting\",\"e\":\"A pseudo-aneurysm can be a complication following aorto-bifemoral grafting for vascular stenosis. Pseudo-aneurysms that fail conservat ve management are commonly treated by surgical repair or thrombin injection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They can be complications of angioplasty\",\"e\":\"Pseudo-aneurysms in the femoral artery are the most commonly encountered pseudo- aneurysm, because the femoral artery is used as an access site for both coronary and peripheral angiography and angioplasty. A false aneurysm (also known as pseudo-aneurysm) is a collection of blood found between the muscularis and adventitia layers of an artery resulting from an arterial puncture or trauma to the artery. Due to the breech in the vessel wall the blood leaks through but remains contained by the adventitia. They present as a painful, pulsatile mass in the groin. It can occur in up to about 8% of procedures involving femoral puncture.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They most commonly occur in the axilla\",\"e\":\"A pseudo-aneurysm most commonly presents in the groin, where the femoral artery resides, as this is an access site for both coronary and peripheral angioplasty and angiography.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They have more wall layers than true aneurysms\",\"e\":\"A pseudo-aneurysm does not contain all the layers of the vessel wall, and occurs following a breach in the vessel wall leading to a collection of blood contained by the adventitia or surrounding perivascular soft issue. A true aneurysm involves all the layers of the vessel wall.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":134,\"stem\":\"You are called to see a patient on the wards with a cold, pale, pulseless leg. You make a diagnosis of acute limb ischaemia. What feature in the history and examination would make you suspect this was acute ischaemia caused by an embolus as opposed to a more chronic occlusion?\",\"lead\":\"What feature in the history and examination would make you suspect this was acute ischaemia caused by an embolus as opposed to a more chronic occlusion?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"Explanation t\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Difficult to palpate contralateral pulses\",\"e\":\"Difficulty to palpate contralateral pulses suggests bilateral symptoms and presence of peripheral arterial disease, favouring the diagnosis of a chronic occlusion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"History of claudication pain\",\"e\":\"History of claudication pain suggests presence of peripheral arterial disease and therefore favouring the diagnosis of a chronic occlusion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Normal ECG\",\"e\":\"A normal ECG reduces the risk of an embolus. If the ECG showed evidence of an irregularly irregular pulse, ie evidence of atrial flutter (AF), then that would favour the diagnosis of an embolus. In some patients with AF there is an atrial thrombus, part of which can detach and lodge in distal arteries causing an acute embolic event.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Recent myocardial infarction\",\"e\":\"In a patient with a recent myocardial infarction there is an increased risk of the presence of a thrombus within the cardiac chambers. This can embolise and lodge into a distal artery, such as in this scenario.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Smokes 20 cigarettes per day\",\"e\":\"History of heavy smoking is the strongest risk factor associated with peripheral artery disease and makes the diagnosis of a chronic arterial occlusion more likely than an acute embolic event.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":137,\"stem\":\"You are in the neurosurgical clinic and review a patient with dysaesthesia, aching in the arm and a weak hand grip. There is a cervical rib reported on the plain X-ray of the shoulder.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding thoracic outlet syndrome?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding thoracic outlet syndrome?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Always requires surgical decompression\",\"e\":\"Thoracic outlet syndrome requires surgical decompression in a minority of cases in which there is progressively worsening neurological dysfunction or acute vascular insufficiency. In the remaining cases, non-operative measures should suffice. In the acute phase, it is usually recommended to take rest, NSAIDs, physiotherapy to strengthen the cervico-scapular region and increase range of motion as well as advice on better posture. Other forms of non- operative treatment include ultrasound and transcutaneous nerve stimulation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is more common in men\",\"e\":\"Thoracic outlet syndrome affects up to 8% of the population and it is more common in women. Those at increased risk are women with poor posture or poor muscle development.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"May present with axillary vein thrombosis\",\"e\":\"Thoracic outlet syndrome (causing venous compression), placement of an indwelling venous catheter or thrombophilia can lead to axillary vein thrombosis. The patient typically presents with acute oedema, cyanosis and pain in the affected limb. The most common presentation is relating to neurological bundle compression (95% of cases). Patients present with pain anywhere between the neck/chest and arm and paraesthesia in the affected upper limb. Patients may also present with reduced sensation, weakness and fatigue. If there is venous thrombosis (occurring in 1-2% of presentations) the patient presents with Paget-Schroetter syndrome and arm oedema, cyanosis and feeling of heaviness. In arterial compression the patient presents with symptoms of claudication, pain in the hand, pallor and cold intolerance.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Most commonly presents with symptoms related to arterial compression\",\"e\":\"Thoracic outlet syndrome presentation depends on the structures most affected by the compression, vascular or neurological.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is always due to a cervical rib\",\"e\":\"Thoracic outlet syndrome is a term used to describe a group of symptoms caused by compression of the neurovascular structures that run through the thoracic outlet. The borders of the thoracic outlet are: the scalene muscles, the clavicle and the first rib. It can be caused by a variety of reasons as follows:\\n\\n• Bony: a cervical rib, cervical/clavicular exostoses, a hypoplastic 1st thoracic rib. • Trauma and post-traumatic conditions such as haematoma. • Soft tissue: congenital fibrous muscle bands, hypertrophy of the scalene muscles in athletes, space occupying lesion.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":140,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted to the Emergency Department complaining of severe back pain. He rapidly goes into hypovolaemic shock. A diagnosis of ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) is made.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is not a risk factor for this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is not a risk factor for this condition?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atherosclerotic vascular disease\",\"e\":\"Atherosclerotic vascular disease is a major risk factor for abdominal aortic aneurysms, due to weakening of the aortic wall adjacent to the atherosclerotic plaque. Other risk factors include: male gender, increasing age, hypertension, smoking, coronary artery disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), previous aneurysm repair, presence of peripheral aneurysm and connective tissue disorders.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Female gender\",\"e\":\"Male gender is a risk factor for the development of AAA.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypertension\",\"e\":\"Hypertension is one of the major risk factors for abdominal aortic aneurysm, because of the high pressure to which the vessel is subjected. Other risk factors include: atherosclerotic vessel disease, man gender, increasing age, smoking, coronary artery disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). previous aneurysm repair, presence of peripheral aneurysm, connective tissue disorders.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Marfan syndrome\",\"e\":\"Marfan syndrome is an autosomal-dominant condition of the connective tissue, caused by a mutation in FBN-1 (fibrillin 1), affecting the extracellular matrix and predisposing to the formation of aneurysms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Syphilis\",\"e\":\"Tertiary syphilis can lead to the formation of a syphilitic or leutic aneurysm. The pathogenic organism Treponema pallidum causes an inflammation of the vasa vasorum (outer layer) of the aorta, a process called aortitis. The vasa vasorum undergoes hyperplasia and as a result becomes ischaemic. Cell necrosis causes loss of elasticity and formation of an aneurysm.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":143,\"stem\":\"A 59-year-old, poorly-controlled diabetic, is admitted with sepsis secondary to a necrotic first and second toe and undergoes radical debridement Which one of the following statements on the diabetic foot is correct?\",\"lead\":\"A 59-year-old, poorly-controlled diabetic, is admitted with sepsis secondary to a necrotic first and second toe and undergoes radical debridement Which one of the following statements on the diabetic foot is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ankle brachial pressure index (ABPI) is low\",\"e\":\"Ankle brachial pressure index is falsely elevated in diabetic foot disease because of the calcification of the vessels; the arterial wall becomes stiff, resisting compression.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is usually painful\",\"e\":\"Diabetic foot disease is usually painless because it is associated with neuropathy leading to reduced sensation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is associated with a peripheral neuropathy\",\"e\":\"Diabetic foot is associated with peripheral neuropathy (affecting motor, sensory and autonomic nerve fibres) and ischaemia (peripheral artery disease). Given the sensory deficit associated with peripheral neuropathy, the patient loses awareness of pain, which guards against injury, initiating the development of a leg ulcer. Ulcers can become infected and lead to sepsis. Diabetic foot disease is usually painless, ulcers are punched out with a thick callus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lumbar sympathectomy increases blood supply to the muscles\",\"e\":\"A lumbar sympathectomy increases the blood flow to the skin and not the muscles as the sympathetic nerves supply mainly the cutaneous vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Once root ulcers are established strict diabelic control will not help in the healing process\",\"e\":\"Strict diabetic control will assist the healing process and prevent further complications from the diabetic ulcer.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":146,\"stem\":\"You have reviewed a 42-year-old woman in the vascular clinic and your consultant asks you to consent her for a Trendelenburg's operation. The patient has several questions regarding this procedure and their diagnosis.\\n\\nFor what condition is this procedure?\",\"lead\":\"For what condition is this procedure?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abdominal aortic aneurysm\",\"e\":\"Trendelenburg's operation is for primary varicose vein disease and not for the repair of an abdominal aortic aneurysm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Arteriovenous fistula\",\"e\":\"The Trendelenburg's operation is for primary varicose vein disease and not for the repair of an arteriovenous fistula.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) with varicose veins\",\"e\":\"Trendelenburg's operation should not be performed in the presence of deep vein thrombosis with varicose veins. The long saphenous vein provides collaterals for the obstructed deep veins and therefore ligation of the long saphenous vein can precipitate venous claudication\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Primary varicose vein surgery\",\"e\":\"Trendelenburg's operation is for primary varicose vein disease secondary to sapheno- femoral valve incompetence. It is performed under spinal anaesthesia with the patient in supine position and a 30° head-down tilt. It identifies the long saphenous vein at the sapheno-femoral junction as well as its tributaries and is flush ligated to the femoral vein Following this the upper 10 cm length of the long saphenous vein is excised. It is associated with a high recurrence rate.\\n\\nThe Trendelenburg's operation should not be performed in the presence of deep vein thrombosis, in which the long saphenous vein provides collaterals, as it can precipitate venous claudication.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Varicocele\",\"e\":\"The Trendelenburg's operation is for primary varicose veins and not for repair of a varicocele.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":149,\"stem\":\"You are in vascular clinic and are reviewing a pregnant patient with varicose veins. They are keen to have surgical intervention. The registrar asks you about risk factors for varicose veins, which one of the following is a risk factor?\",\"lead\":\"The registrar asks you about risk factors for varicose veins, which one of the following is a risk factor?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Male sex\",\"e\":\"Varicose veins are five times more common in women.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mediterranean origin\",\"e\":\"Northern European origin increases the risk of developing varicose veins.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Previous history of deep vein thrombosis\",\"e\":\"A deep vein thrombosis can cause permanent damage to the veins of the lower limb by scarring or narrowing of the vein, but can also affect the valves of the veins and lead to retrograde flow and valve incompetence and therefore varicose veins.\\n\\nOther risk factors include: female gender, increasing age, inactivity or prolonged periods of standing, leg trauma, pregnancy, smoking, HRT/oral contraceptive pill, a positive family history, obesity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Professional cyclist\",\"e\":\"Being active is a protective factor for varicose veins. Being inactive and prolonged periods of standing increase the risk of developing varicose veins.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Young age\",\"e\":\"Incidence of varicose veins increases with increasing age.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":152,\"stem\":\"A 31-year old intoxicated patient presents with an acutely ischaemic limb having cut through the brachial artery and undergoes vascular grafting. He is a smoker.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is most likely to adversely affect the outcome of vascular reconstruction?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most likely to adversely affect the outcome of vascular reconstruction?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Continued smoking\",\"e\":\"Stopping smoking is the most important factor affecting post vascular grafting prognosis: If patients continue smoking the overall prognosis is poor. Additionally there are other measures to reduce risk of further cardiovascular problems:\\n\\n• exercise • healthy diet and good nutritional status assists in healing and rehabilitation • controlling cholesterol • preventing and controlling diabetes and hypertension.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Good left ventricular run off\",\"e\":\"Good cardiac function favours reconstructive surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Good distal run off\",\"e\":\"Good distal run-off favours reconstructive surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prolonged splinting\",\"e\":\"Splinting is aimed at providing support, reducing pain and enhancing healing.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Strict glycaemic control\",\"e\":\"Strict glycaemic control is essential in aiding the healing process and ensuring a good prognosis.\\n\\nContinued smoking in patients with ischaemic limbs results in poor overall prognosis. Proximal vessel disease (ie above the inguinal ligament), good distal run-off and cardiac function favour reconstructive surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":155,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old heavy smoker with type II diabetes mellitus presents to the Emergency Department with central chest pain radiating to his back. While in resus, he is noted to drop his blood pressure, and on careful examination his jugular venous pressure (JVP) is increased on inspiration and his pulse fades.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely complication of aortic dissection that has developed?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely complication of aortic dissection that has developed?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aortic regurgitation\",\"e\":\"Aortic dissection can lead to dilation of the aortic root and annulus and therefore aortic regurgitation*\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cardiac tamponade\",\"e\":\"Aortic dissection is a result of a tear in the intima of the aorta and propagation of dissection by blood collection in the intima-media space, leading to separation of the layers within the wall. It is a severe condition with high mortality and management is needed immediately.\\n\\nProximal aortic dissection can extend into the aortic root and produce a haemopericardium and cardiac tamponade. These patients have a significant mortality and requires immediate management.\\n\\nSigns of pericardial involvement include: pericardial rub, jugular vein distention and pulsus paradoxus (during inspiration cardiac beating is detected on auscultation, but there is no palpable radial pulse and a raised JVP), hypotension and syncope.\\n\\nIn haemodynamically stable patients cardiac tamponade can be managed with pericardiocentesis, however if the patient is haemodynamically unstable surgical repair is preferred.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Myocardial ischaemia\",\"e\":\"Myocardial ischaemia can occur as the dissection progresses in the ascending aorta and can affect blood flow in the coronary arteries. This can lead to myocardial ischaemia and/or infarction. Depending on which branches become affected, mesenteric or limb ischaemia can also occur.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Myocardial infarction\",\"e\":\"Myocardial infarction can occur as the dissection progresses in the ascending aorta and can affect blood flow in the coronary arteries. This situation can lead to myocardial ischaemia and/or infarction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right ventricular failure\",\"e\":\"Right ventricular failure can occur secondary to pericardial tamponade in the context of this scenario if tamponade is not treated promptly.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":158,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man presents with tearing chest pain radiating to his back. He has a past medical history of poorly controlled hypertension and is a heavy smoker. Imaging of his thorax shows a Stanford type B aortic dissection\\n\\nHow is this type of dissection normally managed?\",\"lead\":\"How is this type of dissection normally managed?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Endovascular repair\",\"e\":\"Type B dissections are usually managed medically but if any of the following is present, an endovascular repair with a stent can be considered.\\n\\nIndications for endovascular repair include:\\n\\n• persistent pain • increasing aortic diameter • evidence of malperfusion of organs supplied by branch vessels • evidence of periaortic/mediastinal haematoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hybrid procedure\",\"e\":\"The hybrid procedure for repair of aortic dissection involves debranching and endovascular aortic repair in a combined technique, used to manage type A aortic dissection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lifestyle advice\",\"e\":\"An aortic dissection is a severe condition that if left untreated can lead to significant mortality. It is important to give the patient lifestyle advice such as quitting smoking, weight loss and reducing cholesterol in the diet, but the most important factor in preventing propagation of the dissection is strict blood pressure control, which is achieved by medication.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Medical treatment<\",\"e\":\"The two most common classifications of aortic dissection are the Stanford classification and the DeBakey classification. The DeBakey classification divides dissections into\\n\\n• type I: involving both the ascending and descending aorta (Stanford A) • type II: involving the ascending aorta only (Stanford A) • type III: involving descending aorta only and starts after the origin of the left subclavian (Stanford B).\\n\\nThe Stanford classification divides dissections into:\\n\\n• type A: Affects Ascending Aorta and Arch (carry increased risk of cardiac tamponade) • type B: Begins Beyond Brachiocephalic vessels.\\n\\nStanford type A dissections (DeBakey types I and II) require immediate open surgical repair to prevent the development of haemopericardium secondary to rupture, as this can lead to cardiac tamponade and carries the risk of very high mortality Stanford type B dissections (DeBakey type III), such as the one in the above scenario can be managed medically. It is essential that patients with a proven dissection keep their systolic blood pressure (BP) below 120 mmHg. This level is achieved initially by intravenous antihypertensives and a central line in the High Dependency Unit (HDU), followed by change to oral medication. This approach decreases the risk of propagation of the dissection. First-line medication include the use of b-blockers (labetalol, propranolol). Adequate analgaesia is also vital, usually in the form of opioids\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Open surgery\",\"e\":\"Open surgery is the management of choice for type A aortic dissections that carry a high mortality rate due to the high risk of haemopericardium and cardiac tamponade.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":162,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old diabetic man presents with chest pain radiating to the back and an initial chest X-ray shows a widened mediastinum. Urgent computed tomography (CT) angiography demonstrates a DeBakey type I thoracic aortic dissection and the decision is made for surgical management.\\n\\nWhich incision allows the best access to the descending thoracic aorta?\",\"lead\":\"Which incision allows the best access to the descending thoracic aorta?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Median sternotomy\",\"e\":\"A median sternotomy is used in the surgical repair of a DeBakey type Il aortic dissection as the ascending aorta is a mediastinal structure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Rooftop incision\",\"e\":\"A rooftop incision in parallel to the costal margin and is used for oesophagectomy. gastrectomy, adrenalectomy, liver operations or transplant.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Thoracoabdominal\",\"e\":\"Thoracoabdominal incision is performed from the 7th intercostal space to the costal arch, then following a paramedian line (2 cm of the linea alba) to the umbilicus and is used for the exposure of thoracic, abdominal and retroperitoneal organs.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thoracoscopic\",\"e\":\"Thoracoscopic incisions are made for thoracoscopic resection of small wedge areas of the lungs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Left thoracotomy\",\"e\":\"The two most common classifications of aortic dissection are the Stanford classification and the DeBakey classification. The DeBakey classification divides dissections into:\\n\\n• type I: involving both the ascending and descending aorta (Stanford A) • type II: involving the ascending aorta only (Stanford A) • type III: involving descending aorta only and starts after the origin of the left subclavian (Stanford B)\\n\\nThe Stanford classification divides dissections into:\\n\\n• type A: Affects Ascending Aorta and Arch (carry increased risk of cardiac tamponade) • type B: Begins Beyond Brachiocephalic vessels.\\n\\nType A dissections (DeBakey types 1 and II, such as the one in the above scenario require immediate open surgical repair to prevent the development of haemopericardium secondary to rupture, as this can lead to cardiac tamponade and carries a very high risk of mortality.\\n\\nType B dissections (DeBakey type III) can be managed medically or with an endovascular stent. They commence distal to the left subclavian artery. For type | and type III DeBakey dissections, a left thoracotomy is performed, giving the surgeon very good exposure to the mediastinal and cardiac structures. A left thoracotomy gives better access to the descending thoracic aorta as this is a posterior mediastinal structure, when compared with the median sternotomy, giving better access to anterior mediastinal structures.\"}]},{\"t\":22,\"p\":165,\"stem\":\"An 88-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with sudden-onset upper back pain. He is known to have hypertension, and bloods show severe acute renal failure. An Emergency Consultant orders a computed tomography (CT) scan, an image of which is shown here:\\n\\nSource: Chestatlas.com What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"com What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":\"data:image/jpeg;base64,|data:image/jpeg;base64,\",\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abdominal aortic aneurysm\",\"e\":\"This slice is at the level of T4, the level at which the pulmonary trunk bifurcates. Therefore, an abdominal aortic aneurysm cannot be diagnosed on this film, as it does not show the abdominal aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Type A aortic dissection\",\"e\":\"Type A aortic dissection (according to the Stanford classification) involves the ascending aorta and the arch. The false lumen can be seen on this CT angiogram slice in the ascending aorta and the descending aorta. This means the dissection involves the ascending aorta, the arch and the descending limb. This is consistent with a type A dissection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Type B aortic dissection\",\"e\":\"Type B aortic dissection involves the descending aorta, distal to the subclavian artery. The saying 'B begins beyond brachiocephalic vessels' serves as an aide-memoire. This image shows that dissection has started proximal to this at the ascending aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thoracic spine fracture\",\"e\":\"There is no evidence of fracture of the spinal vertebra in this image. Fractures would appear as areas of radiolucency in the vertebral body, lamina, transverse processes or pedicles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Nil abnormal detected\",\"e\":\"An aortic dissection is visible on this film. Dissection can present with non-specific symptoms such as back and chest pain. A high index of suspicion is required in older. hypertensive patients, especially those with evidence of end-organ dysfunction. n these patients, close attention should be paid for the possibility of an aortic dissection on a CT angiogram, which is shown by a false lumen adjacent to the true lumen.\"}]},{\"t\":23,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old woman sustains a myocardial infarction. ST elevation and Q waves are present in leads Va-Vs. I and AVL. Which one of the following aspects of the heart is most likely to have been involved in the infarct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following aspects of the heart is most likely to have been involved in the infarct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior\",\"e\":\"A purely anterior infarct will involve chest leads only, usually V2 - Vs.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anterolateral\",\"e\":\"This combination suggests an anterolateral infarct. The anterior surface is represented by leads V1-V4. Hence the involvement of chest leads Va suggests an anterior infarct. However, as leads V5, V6, 1 and AVL are also involved this adds a lateral element to the infarct, rather than it being only anterior.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anteroseptal\",\"e\":\"Anteroseptal infarcts cause ischaemic patterns in chest leads only, typically V -Vs.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inferior\",\"e\":\"Inferior infarcts cause ischaemic patterns in leads I!, Ill and AVF.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lateral\",\"e\":\"Lateral infarcts cause ischaemic patterns in chest leads I, Il and AVL.\"}]},{\"t\":23,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"The ECG strip of a patient shows sagging of the ST segment, depression of the T-wave and elevation of the U-wave. What is the most likely electrolyte abnormality responsible for these ECG findings?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely electrolyte abnormality responsible for these ECG findings?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemia\",\"e\":\"ECG changes in hyperkalaeria include.\\n\\n• QRS widening • fusion of QRS-T • loss of the ST segment • P waves widened and of low amplitude • tall tented T-waves.\\n\\nAt potassium concentrations of >7.5 mmol/L atrial and ventricular fibrillation can occur.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypermagnesaemia\",\"e\":\"High serum magnesium levels are rare and often iatrogenic. It can be caused by renal failure, IV or oral magnesium or laxative overuse. ECG changes include an increased PR and QTc interval with prolonged QRS complex, peaked T-waves and flattened P-waves. Complete arteriovenous (AV) block and asystole can occur.\\n\\nIS PASTU IS PAS A ECG changes in hypocalcaemia include:\\n\\n• narrowing of QRS complex • reduced PR interval • T-wave flattening and inversion • prolongation of the QT interval • prominent U-wave • prolonged ST and ST depression.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypocalcaemia\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemia\",\"e\":\"Cardiac effects of hypokalaemia are usually minimal until plasma potassium levels are less than 3mmol/l. Hypokalaemia leads to sagging of the ST segment, depression of the T-wave and elevation of the U-wave. With marked hypokalaemia, the T-wave become progressively smaller and the U-wave becomes increasingly larger. Sometimes, a flat or positive T-wave merges with a positive U-wave, which can be confused with qT prolongation. Hypokalaemia can also cause premature ventricular and atrial contractions, ventricular and atrial tachyarrhythmias and second- or third-degree atrioventricular block. Such arrhythmias become more severe with increasingly severe hypokalaemia, eventually, ventricular fibrillation can occur. Patients with significant pre-existing heart disease and/or those receiving digoxin are at risk of cardiac conduction abnormalities, even with mild hypokalaemia\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypomagnesaemia\",\"e\":\"The primary ECG abnormality is a prolonged QTe interval. This can lead on to atrial and ventricular ectopy and torsades de pointes. Correction of low magnesium is vital to correct these arrhythmias. Remember magnesium must be corrected alongside potassium which is often concurrently low and causing arrhythmias:\\n\\nNormal values of electrolytes:\\n\\n• Nat: 135-145 mmol/l • K*: 3.5-5.0 mmol/l • urea: 2.5-6.5 mmol/l • creatinine: 50-120 umol/l • corrected Cat: 2.20-2.60 mmol/l • POL: 0.70-1.40 mmol/l • Mg:: 0.75-1.00 mmol/l.\"}]},{\"t\":23,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man suffered a myocardial infarction, following which the velocity of impulse: conduction through the atrioventricular node decreased.\\n\\nThis patient will have which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This patient will have which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atrial fibrillation\",\"e\":\"Atrial fibrillation has a number of causes including myocardial infarction resulting in atrial remodelling.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased PR interval\",\"e\":\"For a decreased PR interval, the velocity of impulse conduction through the atrioventricular node would be increased.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Disappearance of the T-wave\",\"e\":\"The T wave represents ventricular repolarisation. Flattened T waves can occur in hypokalaemia\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased heart rate\",\"e\":\"The sinoatrial node regulates heart rate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased PR interval\",\"e\":\"Damage to the atrioventricular node resulting from ischaemia or inflammation may increase the PR interval from a normal value of about 0:12-0.20 s to as much as 0.25-0.40 s. If conduction through the arteriovenous node becomes severely impaired, complete block of impulses may occur, in which case the atria and ventricles will beat independently.\"}]},{\"t\":23,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman is seen in the Emergency Department with extreme generalised oedema. She has marked expansion of the extracellular space within the subcutaneous tissues, visceral organs and body cavities. The woman has which one of the following conditions?\",\"lead\":\"The woman has which one of the following conditions?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anasarca\",\"e\":\"Anasarca (or 'generalised oedema') is a condition characterised by widespread swelling of the skin due to effusion of fluid into the extracellular space: It is usually caused by either cardiac failure, liver failure or renal failure. It can also be created from the administration of exogenous intravenous fluid. Certain plant-derived anticancer chemotherapeutic agents, such as docetaxel, cause anasarca through a poorly understood capillary leak syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Angioedema\",\"e\":\"Angioedema is an area of swelling in the deeper layers of skin typically as a response to allergy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Apoptosis\",\"e\":\"Apoptosis is programmed cell death. This happens to millions of cells on a daily basis. Abnormalities n the apoptosis pathway can result in disease, for example inhibition can result in cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hyperthecosis\",\"e\":\"Hyperthecosis is the presence of nests of luteinised theca cells in the ovarian stroma due to differentiation of the ovarian interstitial cells into active luteinised stromal cells. The result is greater production of androgens and women typically present with hirsutism and virilisation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Haemochromatosis\",\"e\":\"Haemochromatosis is an autosomal-recessive inherited condition in which there is abnormal accumulation of iron. The classical skin manifestation is hyperpigmentation.\"}]},{\"t\":23,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"You have a post-operative patient, in the intensive care unit, who has a history of coronary artery disease and decreased coronary blood flow. The intensive care consultant asks you to tell her what increases the coronary blood flow.\\n\\nHow do you respond?\",\"lead\":\"How do you respond?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)/vasopressin\",\"e\":\"ADH/vasopressin causes coronary vasoconstriction resulting in decreased coronary blood flow.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Distension of a hollow viscus (eg bladder)\",\"e\":\"Hollow viscus distension has been shown to stimulate receptors in their wall causing reflex decreased coronary blood flow.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increasing pCO\",\"e\":\"An increase in coronary artery p(CO2) results in coronary vasodilatation and so increased coronary blood flow.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pain\",\"e\":\"Coronary blood flow is decreased by pain.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serotonin (5HT)\",\"e\":\"Serotonin has been shown to result in coronary vasodilatation in normal endothelium but effect. when the endothelium is damaged to have a vasoconstricting\"}]},{\"t\":23,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with critical ischaemia of the foot. They are found to have new onset atrial fibrillation (AF) and undergo an arteriotomy then embolectomy with a fogarty catheter. Functionally there is no long term damage to the foot. Features of AF include which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Features of AF include which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Absent P waves\",\"e\":\"There are no P waves in atrial fibrillation because the atrial rate is so fast and the action potentials produced are of low amplitude.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Regular pulse 1: -\",\"e\":\"In atrial fibrillation the pulse is irregularly irregular.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"T wave changes\",\"e\":\"Atrial fibrillation does not produce any T wave changes. T wave changes are associated with ischaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A regular heart rhythm\",\"e\":\"Atrial fibrillation is associated with an irregular heart rhythm.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Will convert to sinus rhythm with carotid massage\",\"e\":\"Carotid sinus massage is used in the treatment of supraventricular tachycardia not atrial fibrillation.\"}]},{\"t\":23,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man, who has been stabbed in the left side of the chest, just below the fifth rib is brought to the Emergency Department as a trauma call. It is suspected that the patient has cardiac tamponade.\\n\\nWhich one of the following signs would be consistent with this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following signs would be consistent with this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"globular' cardiac outline on chest X-ray (CXR)\",\"e\":\"The classical symptom triad of cardiac tamponade (Beck's triad) includes muffled heart sounds, distended neck veins and hypotension, and is seen in the majority of patients. In cardiac tamponade, the cardiac outline on CR is classically globular.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Charcot's triad\",\"e\":\"Charcot's triad refers to fever, jaundice and abdominal pain, and is suggestive of cholangitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Collapsed neck veins\",\"e\":\"Cardiac tamponade results in distended neck veins as a result of a rise in venous pressure as blood backs up.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypertension\",\"e\":\"Hypotension results from cardiac tamponade due to a decreased stroke volume and cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prominent heart sounds\",\"e\":\"In cardiac tamponade, classically the heart sounds are muffled.\\n\\n16.25\"}]},{\"t\":23,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old woman is recovering from a hip hemiarthroplasty 2 days ago when she suddenly loses consciousness. An electrocardiogram (ECG) shows a heart rate of 36, with no association between the P waves and QRS complexes. A diagnosis of complete heart block is made and she is listed for a pacemaker. It is also noted that the QRS complexes are wider.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause for this widening of the QRS complex?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely cause for this widening of the QRS complex?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Accessory pathway\",\"e\":\"In patients that have an accessory pathway such as Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome the classical ECG findings are short PR interval and the delta wave (a slurred upstroke in the QRS complex).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Conduction defect\",\"e\":\"The QRS complex represents depolarisation of the ventricles. Wide QRS complexes suggest conduction defects.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Myocardial infarct\",\"e\":\"ECG changes during acute myocardial infarction include hyperacute T waves, ST elevation, T wave inversion and later pathological Q waves.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Myocardial ischaemia\",\"e\":\"With myocardial ischaemia, the typical ECG findings are ST depression.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ventricular hypertrophy\",\"e\":\"Ventricular hypertrophy is suggested by tall QRS complexes.\"}]},{\"t\":23,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"Your consultant asks you to note the jugular venous pressure (JVP) in a 59-year-old diabetic with known heart failure. This is noted to be high. Which one of the following is a possible cause?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a possible cause?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atrial fibrillation\",\"e\":\"The JVP is not raised with atrial fibrillation but the a-wave, which is produced by contraction of the right atrium, disappears.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Complete heart block\",\"e\":\"Complete heart block results in large a-waves within the JVP complex (cannon waves), but not a raised JVP.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypovolaemia\",\"e\":\"The JVP is a measure of pressure in the right atrium and usually reflects intravascular volume and is therefore elevated in fluid overload not hypovolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Left Ventricular Hyoertrophy Tricuspid stenosis\",\"e\":\"This does not typically cause an elevated JVP. Tricuspid stenosis resuits in a slow y-descent of the JVP complex but not an elevated JVP.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior vena cava (SVC) obstruction\",\"e\":\"SVC obstruction results in a build-up of venous return despite collateral pathways, resulting in a raised JVP.\"}]},{\"t\":23,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 76-year-old man has been generally unwell for the last two weeks. He attends the Emergency Department with severe abdominal pain, fever and nausea. On examination, he has a pulsatile abdominal mass.\\n\\nFrom which one of the following infections could this patient be suffering?\",\"lead\":\"From which one of the following infections could this patient be suffering?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"E. coli\",\"e\":\"Haemolytic uraemic syndrome can complicate E. coli infection. It results in acute renal failure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Clostridium difficile\",\"e\":\"Clostridium difficile infection can cause comp ications including toxic megacolon and pseudomembranous colitis. There is a risk of bowel perforation, but this is inconsistent with the history of a pulsatile abdominal mass.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Salmonella\",\"e\":\"The patient's presentation suggests an abdominal aortic aneurysm. Mycotic aneurysms although rare can be caused by infections such as Staphylococcus aureus, Salmonella and Streptococcus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Shigella\",\"e\":\"Shigella is a Gram-negative bacteria similar to E. coli. Infection causes diarrhoea, fever and vomiting.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rotavirus\",\"e\":\"Rotavirus causes gastroenteritis, most notably in children.\"}]},{\"t\":24,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A patient is referred into the neurosurgical clinic with weakness. On examination they are barely able to keep their eyes open. Antibodies to which of the following are found in patients with myasthenia gravis?\",\"lead\":\"Antibodies to which of the following are found in patients with myasthenia gravis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acetylcholine esterase\",\"e\":\"Acetylcholine esterase is not a target for autoantibodies in this disease. Antibodies to acetylcholine esterase would, in theory, increase the amount of available acetylcholine.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acetylcholine receptors\",\"e\":\"Myasthenia is characterised by fatigability of striated muscle. It is an autoimmune disease with antibodies against acetylcholine receptors.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Myelin\",\"e\":\"Myelin is not a primary target for autoantibodies in this disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Striated muscle\",\"e\":\"Striated muscle is not a primary target for autoantibodies in this disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tensilon\",\"e\":\"i anticholinesterase activity and is used to aid diagnosis of the Edrophonium has short-acting disease.\\n\\n2172\"}]},{\"t\":24,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old man sustains a severe head injury in a road traffic accident. CT imaging demonstrates an extradural haematoma with midline shift.\\n\\nThe cardiovascular effects of raised intracranial pressure include which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The cardiovascular effects of raised intracranial pressure include which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased blood pressure, decreased heart rate\",\"e\":\"A rise in blood pressure aims to maintain cerebral perfusion pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased blood pressure, increased heart rate\",\"e\":\"A rise in blood pressure aims to maintain cerebral perfusion pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased blood pressure, increased heart rate\",\"e\":\"Hypertension evokes a bradycardia via the baroreceptor reflex\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased blood pressure, decreased heart rate\",\"e\":\"A rise in blood pressure maintains cerebral perfusion pressure. This elevated blood pressure evokes a bradycardia via the baroreceptor reflex.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased blood pressure, increased heart rate, increased cerebral perfusion pressure\",\"e\":\"A rise in blood pressure maintains cerebral perfusion pressure.\"}]},{\"t\":24,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A patient is referred to the neurosurgical clinic with new onset cranial nerve palsies. CT imaging of the head demonstrates a space occupying lesion. Which one of the following systemic effects is most likely to be caused by this?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following systemic effects is most likely to be caused by this?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"Explanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bradycardia\",\"e\":\"The effects of raised intracranial pressure are hydrocephalus, cerebral ischaemia (due to decreased cerebral perfusion pressure) and systemic effects. The systemic effects include hypertension, bradycardia, slowed respiration and gastric ulceration (Cushing's ulcer).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypotension\",\"e\":\"Hypertension occurs to maintain cerebral perfusion pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tachycardia\",\"e\":\"Bradycardia occurs as a response to hypertension.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tachypnoea\",\"e\":\"Respiratory depression occurs with brainstem compression. This is a late sign of raised ICP.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Low intracranial pressure\",\"e\":\"The cranium is a fixed volume containing blood, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and brain tissue in equilibrium. Increases in one component can be compensated by a decrease in the other components without increasing intracranial pressure (the Monroe-Kellie doctrine). Beyond a certain point, this compensation is insufficient, and raised intracranial pressure results (greater than 10-15 mmHg).\"}]},{\"t\":24,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted with a lancunar stroke. On careful clinical examination and on reviewing imaging, you feel the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) is involved in their presentation.\\n\\nA lesion of the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus will affect which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"A lesion of the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus will affect which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hearing\",\"e\":\"The LGN of the thalamus is a part of the brain that is the primary processor of visual information. The LGN has no known associations with hearing. Sound is perceived in the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Smell\",\"e\":\"The LGN of the thalamus is a part of the brain that is the primary processor of visual information. The LGN has no known associations with olfaction. Smell is processed in the anterior olfactory nucleus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Taste\",\"e\":\"The LGN of the thalamus is a part of the brain that is the primary processor of visual information. The LGN has no known associations with taste. Taste is processed in the solitary nucleus with information arriving from the glossopharygneal nerve and the chorda tympani.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cutaneous sensation\",\"e\":\"The LGN of the thalamus is a part of the brain that is the primary processor of visual information. The LGN has no known associations with cutaneous sensation. Cutaneous sensation is processed in the somatosensory cortex.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vision\",\"e\":\"The LGN of the thalamus is a part of the brain that is the primary processor of visual information, received from the retina, in the central nervous system. The LGN receives information directly from the retina and sends projections directly to the primary visual cortex.\"}]},{\"t\":24,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following systemic effects is most likely to be disrupted by a lesion of the suprachiasmatic nucleus of hypothalamus?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following systemic effects is most likely to be disrupted by a lesion of the suprachiasmatic nucleus of hypothalamus?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Autonomic function\",\"e\":\"The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) does not have direct autonomic function.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Regulation of circadian rhythm\",\"e\":\"The SCN is a region of the brain, located in the hypothalamus, which is responsible for controlling endogenous circadian rhythms. The SCN receives inputs from specialized photoreceptive retinal ganglion cells, via the retinohypothalamic tract. Destruction of the SCN leads to a complete loss of circadian rhythm.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Regulation of water balance\",\"e\":\"Water balance is monitored and controlled by the hypothalamus and pituitary gland.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Temperature regulation\",\"e\":\"Temperature regulation is controlled in the hypothalamus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sexual maturity\",\"e\":\"A frontal lobe brain lesion may render a patient disinhibited.\"}]},{\"t\":24,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man is seen in the Emergency Department with a lower motor neurone lesion of long standing. He has obvious clinical signs on examination in keeping with this.\\n\\nThis patient will likely have which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This patient will likely have which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyperaesthesia\",\"e\":\"A pure motor lesion would not affect sensation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyper-reflexia\",\"e\":\"Hyporeflexia, not hyper-reflexia would indicate a lower motor neurone lesion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Muscle wasting\",\"e\":\"Neurogenic atrophy (wasting) of skeletal muscle is a sign of a lower motor neurone lesion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Positive Babinski's sign\",\"e\":\"A positive Babinski sign would indicate an upper motor neurone lesion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spasticity\",\"e\":\"Flaccid weakness (hypotonia) would be seen.\\n\\nLower motor neurones (LMNs) are the motoneurons connecting the brainstem and spinal cord to muscle fibres, bringing the nerve impulses from the upper motor neurones out to the muscles. LMN are classified based on the type of muscle fibres they innervate. Alpha-motor neurones (a-MNs) innervate extrafusal muscle fibres, the most numerous type of muscle fibre and the one most involved in contraction of a muscle. Gamma-motor neurones (y-MNs) innervate intrafusal muscle fibres, which are involved with muscie spindles and the sense of body position. Damage to lower motor neurones is indicated by abnormal electromyographic potentials, fasciculations, paralysis, weakening muscles and neurogenic atrophy (wasting) of skeletal muscle(s).\"}]},{\"t\":24,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A patient is referred to the neurosurgical clinic with a new onset of focal neurological deficits. CT imaging of the head demonstrates a lesion within the ventromedial nucleus of the hypothalamus.\\n\\nIf this area of the brain is destroyed the affected individual will have which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"If this area of the brain is destroyed the affected individual will have which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Loss of appetite\",\"e\":\"The ventromedial nucleus of the hypothalamus is responsible for satiety. A lesion will cause over-eating, not loss of appetite.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Loss of circadian rhythm ;\",\"e\":\"The suprachiasmatic nucleus is responsible for circadian rhythm.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Loss of regulation of antidiuretic hormone secretion\",\"e\":\"Antidiuretic hormone is synthesised in the supraoptic nuclei of the hypothalamus and is released by the posterior pituitary gland\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Loss of satiety\",\"e\":\"The ventromedial nucleus of the hypothalamus is responsible for satiety. If destroyed, this Will lead to overeating as there is nothing telling the body that it is full.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Loss of vision\",\"e\":\"The lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus is the location of synapse between the optic tract and radiation.\\n\\n2:27\"}]},{\"t\":24,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old man suffered a stroke that mainly affected his thalamus. On CT imaging you can see clear ischaemic changes in this area.\\n\\nWhich of the following abnormalities is most likely to be seen in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following abnormalities is most likely to be seen in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased sexual drive\",\"e\":\"Stroke involving the frontal lobe can cause disinhibition, but this is not common in thalamic strokes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased temperature\",\"e\":\"Temperature is regulated by the hypothalamus, not the thalamus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyperaesthesia\",\"e\":\"Stroke can cause thalamic syndrome (Dejerine-Roussy syndrome), which results in contralateral hemianaesthesia, burning or aching sensation (hyperaesthesia), often accompanied by mood swings.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypotonia\",\"e\":\"In stroke, initially there is flaccid weakness (hypotonia), but this progressively becomes spastic weakness (hypertonia) with time.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thirst\",\"e\":\"Thirst is regulated by the hypothalamus, not the thalamus.\"}]},{\"t\":24,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the neurosurgical clinic who has had a severe traumatic brain injury some months previcusly. The GP has noted 'upper motor neuron signs' throughout.\\n\\nWhich one of the following would you expect to find on examination?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would you expect to find on examination?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Reduced tone in the affected limbs\",\"e\":\"Tone is increased (spasticity) in an upper motor neurone lesion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperreflexia\",\"e\":\"Hyperreflexia is seen in upper motor neurone lesions. This is due to disinhibited reflex circuits in the spinal cord.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Marked wasting\",\"e\":\"Wasting is seen in lower motor neurone lesions.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fasciculations\",\"e\":\"Fasciculations are seen in lower motor neurone lesions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Extensor contractures\",\"e\":\"Flexure contractures are usually seen:\"}]},{\"t\":24,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 79-year-old woman presents with an ulcer over the medial aspect of her lower right leg. Although she has had this for a few years, recently it has become increasingly painful. The edges of the ulcer have become raised and thickened.\\n\\nWhich one of the options is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the options is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Marjolin's Ulcer\",\"e\":\"This is a Marjolin's ulcer, in which a chronic venous ulcer has undergone malignant change. This is a type of squamous-cell carcinoma and is diagnosed by the description of change in a longstanding ulcer that is tender with thickened edges. Diagnosis is by biopsy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Venous ulcer\",\"e\":\"Venous ulcers classically occur at the medial malleolus. They are typically shallow and have sloping edges and granulation at their bases.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Traumatic Ulcer\",\"e\":\"Traumatic ulcers have variable location and appearance depending on atiology (eg impact, or burns).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Arterial ulcer\",\"e\":\"Arterial ulcers appear as punched out lesions with a necrotic base and tend to be painful..\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Diabetic Ulcer\",\"e\":\"Diabetic ulcers tend to occur over pressure points and have surrounding callouses.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A biopsy result from a colonoscopy reveals changes throughout the layers of the bowel wall including a non-caseating epithelioid granuloma and fissuring ulcers. On colonoscopy, the mucosa appeared to have a cobblestone appearance.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Crohn's\",\"e\":\"Histological investigation of endoscopic biopsies remains one of the key investigations for potential cases of inflammatory bowel disease. The presence of granuloma, cobblestone appearance of mucosa and ulcers make diagnosis of Crohn's disease most likely.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ulcerative colitis\",\"e\":\"Ulcerative colitis is usually limited to the mucosa, has smaller/shallower ulcers and granuloma are much less common than in Crohn's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pseudomembranous colitis\",\"e\":\"Tissue damage occurs due to toxin production from Clostridium difficile species. Damage is centred around the intestinal crypts and pseudomembranes are formed from necrotic epithelial cells, mucus, fibrin and neutrophils.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Familial adenomatous polyposis\",\"e\":\"Typically tubular or tubulovillous adenomas. Associated with higher number of aberrant crypt foci. But not granuloma or ulcers.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ischaemic colitis\",\"e\":\"Can also get cobblestone appearance in ischaemic colitis. However, microscopic examination of biopsy usually shows few inflammatory cells. Typical findings are of necrosis and granulation tissue extending into the submucosa and surrounding smooth muscle fibres of the muscularis mucosa.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A lump was excised and sent for histopathological examination. The histopathologist reported seeing what appeared to be an abnormal amount and arrangement of normal tissue, compared to what is normally appropriate for the area in which the tissue arises. Which one of the following options most reflects the above histopathology report?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options most reflects the above histopathology report?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":\"data:image/jpeg;base64,|data:image/jpeg;base64,\",\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carcinosarcoma\",\"e\":\"Malignant tumours consisting of a mixture of carcinoma (epithelial cell cancer) and sarcoma (connective tissue cancer) cells that may be different to surrounding cell types.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Embryonal tumour\",\"e\":\"Embryonal tumours form in embryonic cells and remain after birth. They consist of a mixture of different cell types that may be different to surrounding cell types.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hamartoma\",\"e\":\"A hamartoma is composed of tissues that are normal to an area but growing in a disorganised manner.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mxed tumour.?.\",\"e\":\"A mixed tumour is derived from one cell type that has divergent differentiation, such as a pleomorphic.adenoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Teratoma\",\"e\":\"A teratoma is a tumour with tissue from more than one germ layer. Therefore these cells may be different to surrounding cell types.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"You have recently admitted a farmer, who is on holiday from the Western Isles of Scotland, with severe abdominal pain. On ultrasound of his abdomen a large cystic structure is seen within the left lobe of his liver and enzyme linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) analysis confirms hydatid disease.\\n\\nWhich of the following applies to this disease?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following applies to this disease?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Diagnosis is made on needle aspiration of suspected lesions\",\"e\":\"Diagnosis should be made on serological testing or from the typical appearances of cyst septation and daughter cysts on computed tomography. Needle biopsy is associated with the risk of anaphylaxis and dissemination of infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hepatic lesions are rarely asymptomatic\",\"e\":\"Hepatic lesions are often asymptomatic and discovered by chance on investigation of other problems. Emergent embryos pass through the intestinal wall into the portal system and liver, and to other organs, where they develop into hydatid cysts.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Humans are accidental intermediate hosts\",\"e\":\"The intermediate host is usually sheep. Dogs become infested by eating sheep offal and subsequently pass tapeworm eggs in their stool, which in turn can contaminate their hair. This may lead to accidental human ingestion after touching their hair. Therefore, humans act as an accidental intermediate host.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The causative bacteria is Echinococcusgranulosus\",\"e\":\"Hydatid disease is caused by Echinococcus granulosus, but this is a tapeworm and not a bacterium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The normal intermediate host is the dog\",\"e\":\"The intermediate host is usually a herbivore, eg a sheep.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"An elderly man on the ward is recovering from a laparotomy following a right hemicolectomy. He has profuse green diarrhoea. A diagnosis of Clostridium difficile infection is made.\\n\\nWhich of the following statements applies to this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements applies to this condition?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It may be adequately treated with a single dose of oral metronidazole\",\"e\":\"The two principal therapies are vancomycin and metronidazole. For severe cases, usually a combination of oral vancomycin and intravenous metronidazole are used. A single dose of oral metronidazole is unlikely to adequately treat C. difficile infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It may be adequately treated with oral gentamicin\",\"e\":\"Gentamicin is not effective in the treatment of C. difficile infection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It may lead to colonic perforation\",\"e\":\"Complications of C. difficile infection include electrolyte disturbances, paralytic ileus and, if pancolitis develops, toxic megacolon, endotoxic shock and bowel perforation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is usually diagnosed by Gram staining of the faces\",\"e\":\"Clostridium difficile infection is usually detected by identification of the cytotoxin. Gram staining of faces is unhelpful in detecting the organism as it cannot distinguish C. difficile from many of the other gut organisms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It should be followed up by stool culture in asymptomatic patients\",\"e\":\"There is no evidence to follow-up with repeat stool cultures in asymptomatic patients, following completion of a course of treatment.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old Vietnamese street chef presents with right upper quadrant pain. He reports diarrhoea and weight loss for the past few months. On examination he has little abdominal fat and a palpable liver edge is apparent. Ultrasound shows multiple cysts suggestive of hydatid disease. Which of the following is most true of this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is most true of this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carries a risk for hepatocellular carcinoma\",\"e\":\"There is no known association of hydatid disease to hepatocellular carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cannot cause peritonitis\",\"e\":\"Rupture of hydatid cyst can present with peritonitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"May cause seizures\",\"e\":\"1% of hydatid disease can disseminate to the brain and present with seizures due to space- occupying effect of these cysts.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The mature form is found in humans\",\"e\":\"The adult, mature worm resides in the small intestine of a definitive host, which includes dogs and other carnivores.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The worm stage is found in humans\",\"e\":\"Eggs are released that are passed in the faces of the host and ingested by an intermediate host, such as humans and other mammals. Human infection with E. granulosus frequently occurs in early childhood by direct contact with infected dogs or by eating uncooked or improperly washed vegetables contaminated with canine faces.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old woman with severe Crohn's disease presents with gallstones. Biliary colic is diagnosed and she is discharged with a date for an elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy.\\n\\nWhat is the likely constituent of her gallstones?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely constituent of her gallstones?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bile pigment\",\"e\":\"Gallstones can be either cholesterol, pigment or mixed stones. Bile pigments are excreted in bile with the most important being bilirubin and biliverdin. Pigment stones can be found in anyone but are more common in patients with haemolytic anaemias such as sickle-cell disease. Patients with Crohn's disease are more likely to form cholesterol stones.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bilirubin\",\"e\":\"Bilirubin is a bile pigment which forms pigment stones, patients with Crohn's disease are more likely to have cholesterol stones.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Calcium bilirubinate\",\"e\":\"Calcium bilirubinate is a constituent of a pigment stone that patients with Crohn's disease can form but are not at any increased risk when compared with the general population, they have an increased risk of cholesterol stones.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cholesterol\",\"e\":\"Patients with terminal ileal Crohn's disease are at increased likelihood of developing gallstones. Normally, bile acids that are excreted into the small bowel from the liver and gall- bladder are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum so that they may be secreted again. This is known as the entero-hepatic circulation. So in terminal ileal Crohn's disease this recycling of the bile salts is impaired and there is a net loss of the available bile salt pool and the body is unable to secrete significant concentrations of bile salts. Gallstones are at increasing likelihood of forming in such situations as there is an insufficient concentration of bile salts to emulsify the cholesterol and the cholesterol leaches out of solution, forming gallstones.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urobilinogen\",\"e\":\"Urobilinogen is one of the breakdown products of bilirubin and does not contribute to gallstone formation.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old woman presents to clinic having difficulty with her diet following a partial gastrectomy. You think she has dumping syndrome and offer her simple lifestyle advice in the first instance.\\n\\nThe initial symptoms of this condition in patients with intestinal short circuits such as anastomosis of the jejunum to the stomach are caused by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The initial symptoms of this condition in patients with intestinal short circuits such as anastomosis of the jejunum to the stomach are caused by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased blood pressure\",\"e\":\"Dumping syndrome occurs when food passes quickly from the stomach to the duodenum. Blood pressure does not trigger the symptoms of dumping syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increase secretion of glucagons\",\"e\":\"Glucagon is produced by a-cells in the pancreas and works to prevent hypoglycaemia. Rebound hypoglycaemia is responsible for late dumping syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased secretion of CCK\",\"e\":\"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is secreted by I-cells mostly in the duodenum and jejunum It is released following a meal with the greatest stimulator being a fatty meal. It is responsible for stimulating the secretion of pancreatic digestive enzymes. Rapid gastric emptying, which causes dumping syndrome, can decrease the secretion of CCK.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fluid shifts\",\"e\":\"Dumping syndrome (DS) is characterised by the rapid gastric transit of chyme. The chyme of the patients with DS is unique, in that it is both voluminous and hyperosmolar. This is because chyme that is rapidly transited through the stomach is incompletely digested. These characteristics together may increase the stimulus for the secretion of gastric hormones. One of the effects of hormones secreted during digestion is to shift fluid from the general circulation into the intestinal lumen and intestinal blood supply, helping to digest the chyme. This fluid shift if also the result of a parasympathetic response to chyme in the intestines, and the intestinal distension which occurs during digestion. This effect is called splanchnic blood pooling. Normally, compensatory sympathetic activity, with a concomitant increase in heart rate, vasoconstriction and plasma norepinephrine levels, keeps blood pressure relatively constant. However, it has been hypothesized that the shift in fluid from general circulation to Gl circulation is so dramatic in DS that it overwhelms compensatory mechanisms and causes symptoms such as fatigue, weakness, dyspnoea and if severe, hypotension, faintness and 'blackouts'\\n\\nThere are two types of dumping syndromes resulting from rapid gastric emptying:\\n\\n• early dumping which manifests by dizziness, sweating and palpitations within 5-45 min after eating • late dumping occurs 2-4 h after eating and is due to rebound hypoglycaemia, small meals and glucose help to improve symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hyperglycemia\",\"e\":\"It is a rebound hypoglycaemia that typically occurs 2-4 h after eating and causes late dumping syndrome.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old obese woman presents to the surgical out-patient clinic complaining of right upper quadrant abdominal pain, which occurs whenever she takes a fatty meal. Which one of the following substances may be involved in the pathophysiology of this woman's complaint?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following substances may be involved in the pathophysiology of this woman's complaint?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cholecystokinin\",\"e\":\"The patient in the vignette has symptoms suggestive of gallstonest . Intraluminal fats or amino acids in the intestine stimulate cholecystokinin (CCK) cells in the duodenum to release CCK; which causes sustained gall-bladder contractions and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi. In the presence of inflammation, CCK can aggravate abdominal pain through gall- bladder contractions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chymotrypsin\",\"e\":\"Chymotrypsin is a proteolytic enzyme. It is secreted in the inactive form of chymotrypsinogen to prevent unwanted destruction of cellular proteins.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastrin\",\"e\":\"Gastrin is a peptide hormone released by the G-cells of the stomach and stimulates secretion of gastric acid from the parietal cells of the stomach.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Secretin\",\"e\":\"Secretin is a hormone released from the duodenum and regulates water homeostasis throughout the body.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Somatostatin\",\"e\":\"Somatostatin is produced by the delta cells of the islets of Langerhans of the pancreas. It is also called growth hormone inhibiting hormone (GHIH) and inhibits the secretion of growth hormone from somatotrope cells.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old woman has had dry eyes and dry mouth for many years and is diagnosed with Sjögren syndrome. Which one of the following would be true regarding her saliva?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would be true regarding her saliva?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Flow rate would increase during nausea\",\"e\":\"Sjögren's syndrome is a systemic chronic inflammatory disorder characterised by lymphocytic infiltrates in exocrine organs. Most common presentation is with sicca symptoms, ie xerophthalmia (dry eyes, xerostomia (dry mouth) and parotid gland enlargement. Salivary flow increases with nausea, both in patients with and without Sjögren's.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Secretion from the parotid gland would be predominantly mucous\",\"e\":\"The parotid gland produces predominantly serous saliva, the sublingual glands produce mainly mucous saliva and the submandibular glands produce a mixture of the two.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Secretion would cease altogether at night\",\"e\":\"Although secretion of saliva is reduced during periods of sleep, it does not stop completely.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Volume production is reduced to 1.5 litres per day\",\"e\":\"Salivary glands normally produce 0.75-1.5 litres of saliva per day, in Sjogren's syndrome this figure is much reduced and would be less than 0.75 litres per day.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It would contain only a low concentration of potassium\",\"e\":\"Due to reduced volume of saliva produced and active transport against a concentration gradient, saliva contains a higher concentration of potassium compared with plasma.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man has a history of recurring attacks of pancreatitis. He does not drink alcohol and has had thorough investigations for gallstones which have been normal. He is noted to have eruptive xanthomas and increased plasma triglyceride levels (2000 mg/dl) associated with chylomicrons.\\n\\nDeficiency of which one of the following is the likely cause of these symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"Deficiency of which one of the following is the likely cause of these symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ApoB-100 receptors\",\"e\":\"Apolipoprotein B100 plays a role in cholesterol transport around the body. Genetic mutations in apoB-100 are associated with familial hypercholesterolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"ApoB-48 receptors\",\"e\":\"Apolipoprotein B48 is produced by the small intestine and is a smaller version of apoB-100. While apoB-48 deficiency may affect chylomicron levels, this would not cause the significant hypertriglyceridaemia seen in the vignette.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"HMG-CoA reductase enzyme\",\"e\":\"HMG-CoA reductase is an enzyme that catalyses the reaction of acetyl-CoA to mevalonate, as part of the pathway for cholesterol production. Statins are HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors and serve to lower cholesterol production by inhibiting this pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Low-density lipoprotein (LDL) receptors\",\"e\":\"Disorders of LDL receptors result in familial hypercholesterolaemia and would not have picture of elevated triglycerides that is seen in the vignette.\\n\\n2163\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lipoprotein lipase enzyme\",\"e\":\"The features presented in this vignette are suggestive of hypertriglyceridaemia secondary to lipoprotein lipase deficiency. Lipoprotein lipase is an enzyme that hydrolyses lipids in lipoproteins, like those found in chylomicrons and very-low-density lipoproteins (VLDL), into three fatty acids and one glycerol molecule. It requires apoC-ll as a co-factor. LPL deficiency leads to hypertriglyceridaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A 37-year-old woman is urgently referred from her General Practitioner (GP) with symptoms of dysphagia, retrosternal discomfort and regurgitation. On further questioning, she admits to having lost weight. A barium swallow shows a dilated tapering oesophagus with a 'rat's tail' appearance.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"I Achalasia\",\"e\":\"Achalasia is a rare condition in which there is failure of the lower oesophageal sphincter to relax causing dysphagia. It is caused by loss of ganglion cells within the oesophagus. Above the lower sphincter, the oesophagus becomes hypertrophied and dilated as a result. This gives rise to its classic appearance on barium swallow - a dilated tapering oesophagus known as a 'rat's tall' or 'bird's beak' appearance. Manometry shows incomplete relaxation of the lower oesophageal sphincter.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Barrett's oesophagus\",\"e\":\"Barrett's oesophagus occurs when squamous epithelium changes to columnar epithelium. The process of metaplasia to dysplasia and then adenocarcinoma can occur. The risk of progression to adenocarcinoma of the oesophagus is estimated to be around 1% risk per year in patients who have Barrett's oesophagus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastric cancer\",\"e\":\"This is the fifth most common cancer world-wide and, as with most other gastrointestinal tract cancers, this typically presents late with insidious symptoms such as weight loss, fatigue, nausea and loss of appetite.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease\",\"e\":\"Gastro-oesophageal reflux is a presumptive clinical diagnosis in patients who describe heartburn and regurgitation. Endoscopy often then reveals evidence of acid reflux causing oesophagitis in these patients.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Oesophageal cancer\",\"e\":\"The two most common types of primary osophageal cancer are adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma. They typically present with a progressive dysphagia, initially to larger solids as well as systemic features such as malaise and weight loss. The clinical features as well as the barium imaging results are much more in keeping with achalasia.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old obese woman is referred to endoscopy because of ongoing symptoms of reflux despite conservative measures and medication. Endoscopy reveals the distal besophagus has a 'velvety' appearance and histology confirms Barrett's oesophagus.\\n\\nThis is an example of what histological process?\",\"lead\":\"This is an example of what histological process?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anaplasia\",\"e\":\"Anaplasia is the loss of differentiation of cells, and of their orientation to each other.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Dysplasia\",\"e\":\"Dysplasia is the alteration in the size, shape and organisation of adult cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Metaplasia\",\"e\":\"Metaplasia is the change in differentiation of a cell type from one form to another. For example, in Barrett's oesophagus the normal squamous epithelium lining the oesophagus is replaced with columnar epithelium due to chronic reflux.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Necrosis\",\"e\":\"Necrosis occurs following cell injury and leads to premature death of cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neoplasia\",\"e\":\"Neoplasia is the formation of new tissue, this can be benign or malignant.\"}]},{\"t\":25,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"An overweight 32-year-old woman presents with a brief history of jaundice and right upper quadrant pain. There is no previous history of illness and, apart from the jaundice, she has no signs of chronic liver disease Initial investigations reveal\\n\\nResult Normal value Investigation\\n\\n115-160 g/| 127g/l Haemoglobin (Hb)\\n\\n105 fl 78-100 fl Mean corpuscular volume (MCV)\\n\\n3-17 mmol/l 162 mmol/l Serum bilirubin\\n\\n10-40 /I 145 U/I Aspartate aminotransferase (AST)\\n\\n224 U/I Alkaline phosphatase 30-130 U/l\\n\\n6-42 U/I 200 U/l Gamma-glutamyltransferase\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alcoholic hepatitis\",\"e\":\"Jaundice with an elevation of both aspartate aminotransferase (AST) and alkaline phosphatase suggests mixed hepatocellular damage and cholestatic liver disease, typical of acute alcoholic hepatitis, which can occur without a background of chronic liver disease. Although it should be noted that lack of physical signs do not exclude the presence of chronic liver disease. The high gamma-glutamyltransferase lends support to recent alcohol intake (although it may be increased in liver disease of any cause). Macrocytosis is typical of chronic excessive alcohol intake and is not a feature of the other conditions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Autoimmune hepatitis\",\"e\":\"While this is certainly a diagnosis to bear in mind given the age and gender of the patient in the vignette, some things are common and alcohol-related liver disease would be much more common than autoimmune hepatitis: Also, macrocytosis is not usually seen in autoimmune hepatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma of the head of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"This option would typically present with painless jaundice and the liver function tests would typically be cholestatic in nature, with raised levels of alkaline phosphatase and normal liver transaminases.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"Chronic cholecystitis can cause jaundice but it would be unusual for there to be no history of acute episodes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hepatitis A infection\",\"e\":\"In hepatitis A, the liver function tests show a hepatitis picture, with aspartate aminotransferase (AST) typically higher than alkaline phosphatase and there may be history of meat consumption to fit with faeco-oral contamination and transmission of the virus.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A pathologist presents cellular abnormalities for a cancer patient during the colorectal MDT. They understand you are due to take your MRCS exam and have a question for you. Which one of the following provides an example of concomitant hyperplasia and hypertrophy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following provides an example of concomitant hyperplasia and hypertrophy?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Breast enlargement at puberty\",\"e\":\"Breast tissue enlargement resulting from hormonal influences is due solely to an increase in cell numbers (hyperplasia).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cystic hyperplasia of the endometrium\",\"e\":\"As the name would suggest this condition results in excessive proliferation of cells in the endometrium (hyperplasia) alone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Enlargement of skeletal muscle in athletes\",\"e\":\"in athletes, enlargement of skeletal muscle occurs through hypertrophy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Left ventricular cardiac hypertrophy\",\"e\":\"In cardiac muscle, an increase in cell size (hypertrophy) is seen in response to volume overload or systemic hypertension. 2:08\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Uterine growth during pregnancy\",\"e\":\"In uterine growth during pregnancy, both cell proliferation involving the endometrial glands and muscle enlargement of the uterine wall occur. These processes offer models of both hyperplasia and hypertrophy. When both are present, DNA synthesis is markedly accelerated. Hyperplasia is an increase in the number of cells, whereas hypertrophy is an increase in cell size.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"Certain surgical instruments are sterilised with ultraviolet light. This is used as an antimicrobial physical agent because it does which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This is used as an antimicrobial physical agent because it does which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acts as an alkylating agent\",\"e\":\"The addition of alkyl groups to cellular components is caused by ethylene oxide or formaldehyde.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Causes the formation of pyrimidine dimers\",\"e\":\"The mode of action of ultraviolet light on micro-organisms is related to its absorption by the DNA. This absorption leads to the formation of covalent bonds between adjacent pyrimidine bases. These pyrimidine dimers alter the form of the DNA and so interfere with normal base pairing during the synthesis of DNA.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Disrupts the bacterial cell membrane\",\"e\":\"Disruption of the bacterial cell membrane is caused by detergents.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is a common protein denaturant\",\"e\":\"Protein denaturation is commonly caused by heat or alcoholic compounds.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Removes free sulfhydryl groups\",\"e\":\"Removal of free sulfhydryl groups is caused by heavy metals such as soluble salts of mercury.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old woman presents to the pre-assessment clinic before elective lymph node dissection for melanoma. She gives a urine sample, which is urine dipstick tested. Which one of the following can be identified?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following can be identified?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amylase\",\"e\":\"Amylase cannot be identified on urine dipstick, but a urine could be analysed for amylase in pathology.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Myoglobin\",\"e\":\"Urine myoglobin can be detected in conditions such as rhabdomyolysis but would not be identified on urine dipstick. It may be detected as blood on the dipstick.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Platelets\",\"e\":\"Platelets are typically measured on a full blood count and although there are some reports of the presence of urinary platelets being associated with IgA nephropathy it is not a commonly used investigation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Polymorphs\",\"e\":\"These are detected at urine culture not on a urine dipstick. E60\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Specific gravity\",\"e\":\"Urinalysis demonstrates the specific gravity of urine as well as the presence of nitrites; haemoglobin, leucocyte esterase, protein, glucose and ketones.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 63-year-old man with a history of squamous cell carcinoma re-presents with a swelling in his neck. You carry out a fine needle aspiration (FNA) for cytology. Which one of the following statements regarding this procedure is most accurate?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding this procedure is most accurate?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It can be used in combination with other tests to diagnose metastases\",\"e\":\"Malignant metastatic cells (with mitotic figures, increased nuclei and decreased cellular cohesion) can be diagnosed by needle aspiration cytology:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"If negative it can be used to exclude malignancy\",\"e\":\"The exclusion of malignancy should not be based on the findings of needle aspiration cytology alone and should be combined with clinical and radiographic findings.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is able to differentiate invasive from in-situ carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Features of invasion such as capsular or vascular invasion require histological evaluation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Always need to be done under ultrasound guidance\",\"e\":\"Ultrasound guidance is not a requirement and FNA can be performed using other image modalities such as computed tomography (CT) scan.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Routinely undergoes Gram staining\",\"e\":\"The Romanowsky-type stain (air dried), and the Papanicolaou stain (wet fixed), are more commonly used for cytological specimens. The Gram stain is used to stain for the bacterial cell wall in Gram-positive organisms.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A patient has tissue sent off for biopsy. This comes back two weeks later showing evidence of granuloma. They are reviewed in clinic and must be referred to the appropriate specialty. Granuloma is seen in which one of the following conditions?\",\"lead\":\"Granuloma is seen in which one of the following conditions?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Syphilis\",\"e\":\"A granuloma is a collection of macrophages, giant-cells as a nidus of chronic inflammation. The centre may necrotise to form caseation, classically in tuberculosis. There is a long list of infective and immunological conditions in which a granulomatous response may be seen, including tertiary syphilis, sarcoidosis, Crohn's and Wegener's granulomatosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Typhoid\",\"e\":\"Typhoid fever is predominantly caused by Salmonella enterica and classically presents with fever, malaise, diffuse abdominal pain and constipation. It does not result in granuloma formation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cholera\",\"e\":\"Cholera is caused by Vibrio cholera and presents with profuse diarrhoea as a result of enterotoxin formation that promotes secretion of fluid and electrolytes into the small intestine.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Amoebiasis\",\"e\":\"Amoebiasis is caused by Entamoeba histolytica a parasite that causes proteolysis and tissue lysis. It can induce apoptosis and present with dysentery with the potential to involve other organs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Shigellosis\",\"e\":\"Shigella infection induces acute inflammation accompanied by polymorphonuclear cell infiltration resulting in destruction of the colonic mucosa.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A patient has significant abnormalities on their chest X-ray and has been feeling non- specifically unwell for some time. Tissue biopsy and laboratory testing is performed. The presence of non-caseating granulomas and hypercalcaemia is most suggestive of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The presence of non-caseating granulomas and hypercalcaemia is most suggestive of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Histoplasmosis\",\"e\":\"In histoplasmosis, granulomas may form in the pulmonary parenchyma, hilar and mediastinal nodes. These are usually caseating and may develop both calcification and fibrosis. However. it does not typically cause hypercalcaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sarcoidosis\",\"e\":\"Sarcoidosis is the commonest cause of non-caseating granulomas. it is also associated with hypercalcaemia due to an increased synthesis of vitamin D by macrophages and a subsequent increased absorption of calcium in the intestine:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tuberculosis\",\"e\":\"Tuberculosis is the classic disease associated with caseating granulomas.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Berylliosis\",\"e\":\"Berylliosis results in non-caseating granulomas in the lungs, it is however less common than sarcoidosis and hypercalcaemia is also less common than in sarcoidosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Eosinophilic granuloma\",\"e\":\"Eosinophilic granuloma is a form of neoplasia and not a granuloma.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man is seen in clinic complaining of a tender right forearm lump. He was involved in a road traffic accident 6 months previously, when several windscreen fragments entered his forearm, which were removed at the time in the Emergency Department. At elective surgery another glass fragment is recovered from his right forearm. Which of the following cell types would be most characteristic of the inflammatory response in this situation?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following cell types would be most characteristic of the inflammatory response in this situation?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Eosinophils\",\"e\":\"Eosinophils are typical of parasitic and allergic inflammatory responses, not of inflammatory responses to foreign bodies.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Giant cells\",\"e\":\"The glass fragment is a foreign material and produces a foreign body reaction characterised by giant cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mast cells\",\"e\":\"Mast cells are not numerous in tissues and do not proliferate significantly as part of the foreign body inflammatory response.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neutrophils\",\"e\":\"A few neutrophils might be present, but giant cells are more specific for a foreign body inflammatory response.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Plasma cells\",\"e\":\"Plasma cells are more typical of chronic inflammation and not as part of a foreign body inflammatory response.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man who has been non-specifically unwell with constitutional symptoms attends the outpatient clinic. A tissue biopsy of an abnormal area on CT scanning is taken. The histopathology report for a granulomatous lesion suggests chronic inflammation. Which cell types are most commonly seen in tissue undergoing this level of inflammation?\",\"lead\":\"Which cell types are most commonly seen in tissue undergoing this level of inflammation?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"RS Y Eosinophils\",\"e\":\"Eosinophils are classically seen in parasitic infections.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"The predominant cells types in chronic inflammation are macrophages and lymphocytes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mast cells\",\"e\":\"Mast cells release histamine and are the predominant cell type for allergic reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neutrophils\",\"e\":\"Neutrophils are the predominant cell type in acute inflammation and are recruited to the site within minutes following trauma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Platelets\",\"e\":\"Platelets are involved in clotting and recruited when there is vascular injury.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old patient's histopathology report describes the presence of granulation tissue in a lesion..\\n\\nWhich one of the following features is characteristic of granulation tissue?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following features is characteristic of granulation tissue?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Giant cells and fibroblasts\",\"e\":\"The extracellular matrix of granulation tissue is formed by fibroblasts but giant cells are seen in granulomas\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Giant cells and lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"Giant cells and lymphocytes are found in granulomas rather than granulation tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Giant cells, plasma cells and lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"Granulation tissue is not to be confused with granulomatous inflammation or granuloma in which giant cells, plasma cells and lymphocytes are seen, Granulomas are the hallmark of a form of chronic inflammation in which healing and the stimulation for damage occur concurrently.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neutrophils and necrotic tissue\",\"e\":\"Neutrophils and necrosis are seen in necrotic not granulation tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Proliferation of new capillaries, with fibroblasts and new collagen formation\",\"e\":\"Granulation tissue is the first phase of the healing process at the end of acute inflammation. New capillaries proliferate in the tissue, with fibroblasts and the first laying-down of new collagen, which eventually will become largely avascular scar tissue. Granulation tissue is a normal response in the normal healing process. During the proliferative phase of wound healing, granulation tissue is:\\n\\n• light red or dark pink in colour, being perfused with new capillary loops or 'buds' • soft to the touch • moist • granular in appearance.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A 37-year-old woman presents to casualty with severe right upper quadrant pain. An ultrasound scan is suggestive of acute cholecystitis. Her blood tests show s gnificantly raised serum inflammatory markers. This inflammatory process is characterised by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This inflammatory process is characterised by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A significant delay whilst inflammatory mediators are formed\",\"e\":\"Inflammatory mediators are released immediately after the onset of injury/infection leading to acute inflammation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An inflammatory response that is independent of the burden of tissue injury\",\"e\":\"The inflammatory response is dependent on the extent of tissue injury.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decreased vascular permeability\",\"e\":\"Inflammation results in increased vascular permeability and resulting in exudate formation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased blood flow\",\"e\":\"Inflammation is characterised by vasodilatation and therefore increased blood flow to the affected area.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Recruitment of cells that phagocytose microbes and damaged tissue and are responsible for the humoural immune system\",\"e\":\"Macrophages play a part in phagocytosis and also act as antigen presenting cells. They do not however have a role in the humoral immune system.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"A neonate is born with an abnormal mass. The parents and midwife are concerned and request an emergency review by the paediatric surgical team. Which one of the following sites in this patient would most commonly give rise to tumours derived from all three germ-cell layers?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following sites in this patient would most commonly give rise to tumours derived from all three germ-cell layers?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Central nervous system\",\"e\":\"Teratomas of the central nervous system can present at any age but are very rare.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mediastinum\",\"e\":\"Mediastinal teratomas are the most common extra-gonadal germ-cell tumours most commonly located in the anterior part. They can present in patients of a wide age range but are rare in children and newborns.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ovaries\",\"e\":\"Ovarian teratomas (also called dermoid cysts) can occur at any age but are most commonly seen in patients of childbearing age.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sacrococcygeal area\",\"e\":\"Tumours derived from all three cell layers are called 'teratomas'. In a newborn infant, the most common location is the sacrococcygeal area. Overall, however, a hamangioma is the most common tumour in infancy and most of these regress over time.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Testis\",\"e\":\"Testicular teratomas account for around 40% of testicular cancers and are most common in patients who are 20-30 years old.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old woman presents to the clinic with a hamartoma. She is concerned the lump may be cancerous. Which one of the following is correct when describing these lesions?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct when describing these lesions?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arise at the vermilion border of the lip in Peutz-Jeghers syndrome\",\"e\":\"The polyps of Peutz-Jeghers syndrome are gastrointestinal hamartomas, while the associated lesions at the vermilion border of the lips are due to increased pigmentation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Often metastasise\",\"e\":\"Hamartomas do not typically metastasise to other parts of the body. They require surgery if they cause obstruction, intussusception or bleeding:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Contain a variety of normal tissue components\",\"e\":\"Hamartomas are benign overgrowths of fully differentiated normal components of the tissues in which they arise. In a hamartoma, the proportion and composition of these normal tissue components differ greatly from the normal tissue structure. The most common hamartomas are vascular and include birthmarks such as port-wine stains.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Grow autonomously\",\"e\":\"Although not neoplastic, hamartomas are mass lesions and, unlike true neoplasms, they cannot grow autonomously.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Grow rapidly in size\",\"e\":\"Hamartomas can take over a decade to double in size.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted with sepsis of unknown origin, they undergo extensive microbiological investigation. A swab result subsequently shows a Gram-positive aerobic coccus, which is coagulase negative. Which one of the following is the most likely organism?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely organism?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"i's\\\" staphylococcus aureus\",\"e\":\"Staphylococci are aerobic Gram-positive cocci. Staphylococcus aureus is coagulase positive.\\n\\nStreptococci are aerobic Gram-positive cocci. Haemolytic groups are used to subdivide streptococci. Streptococcus viridans is an a-haemolytic Streptococcus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Streptococcus viridans\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus epidermidis\",\"e\":\"Staphylococci are aerobic Gram-positive cocci. The presence of the coagulase enzyme is a test to subdivide the Staphylococcus micro-organisms. Staphylococcus epidermidis is coagulase negative.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Streptococcus faecalis\",\"e\":\"Streptococci are aerobic Gram-positive cocci. The coagulase enzyme is a test to subdivide Staphylococcus not Streptococcus micro-organisms:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Enterococcus faecalis\",\"e\":\"Enterococcus faecalis is an anaerobic microorganism.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"An 80-year-old patient on intravenous (IV) cefuroxime develops diarrhoea. The infection control team are anxious to know whether or not this is infectious in nature. The organism that you should test the stool sample for is which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The organism that you should test the stool sample for is which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Clostridium difficile\",\"e\":\"The patient is on antibiotics that increase the risk of pseudomembranous colitis, cephalosporins are particularly linked to this, and Clostridium difficile is the causative organism.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Clostridium pertringens\",\"e\":\"Clostridium perfringens can cause food poisoning but can also be ingested without causing harm. In this patient in the context of recent antibiotic use it is a less likely cause.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"clostridium tetani V\",\"e\":\"Clostridium tetani causes tetanus characterised by hypertonia, painful muscular contractions and generalised muscle spasms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Clostridium botulinum\",\"e\":\"Clostridium botulinum causes botulism, an acute neurological disorder that causes potentially life-threatening neuroparalysis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Escherichia coll\",\"e\":\"E. coli can cause diarrhoea, but is a less likely cause in this patient.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old white man is diagnosed with a liver abscess. He has no recent history of foreign travel, or significant past medical history. Which one of the following micro-organisms is most likely to be the causative agent?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following micro-organisms is most likely to be the causative agent?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Candida albicans\",\"e\":\"Fungal abscesses are associated with immunosuppression.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Echinococcus\",\"e\":\"Echinococcus is a parasitic disease and causes hydatid disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Entamoeba histovica!\",\"e\":\"Worldwide, amoebic abscesses are the commonest cause of liver abscess with 10% of the world's population infected with Entamoeba histolytica.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Escherichia coll V\",\"e\":\"Pyogenic abscesses account for 75% of liver abscesses in developed counties. Most pyogenic liver abscesses are secondary to coliforms such as E. coli and Klebsiella. The most common cause is cholangitis associated with biliary stones followed by diverticulitis. Most are polymicrobial with Gram-negative aerobic and anaerobic organisms predominating.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Streptococcus milleri\",\"e\":\"Streptococcus milleri can cause liver abscesses but is rare, it is also the causative organism in some cases of bacterial endocarditis.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"A 9-year-old boy ruptures his spleen when he falls off his push bike badly. He undergoes emergency splenectomy and his life is saved. At discharge, the need for lifelong immune support is discussed with the boy's parents. Splenectomy increases susceptibility to which of the following organisms?\",\"lead\":\"Splenectomy increases susceptibility to which of the following organisms?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Streptococcus pyogenes\",\"e\":\"Streptococcus pyogenes is not an encapsulated organism. Following splenectomy there is increased susceptibility to encapsulated organisms.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bacteroides fragilis\",\"e\":\"Bacteroides fragilis is an anaerobic Gram-negative bacterium and is non-encapsulated. Splenectomy patients have an increased risk of infection with encapsulated bacteria.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Neisseria meningitidis\",\"e\":\"The spleen plays an important role in the removal of dead and dying erythrocytes and in the defence against microbes. Removal of the spleen (splenectomy) leaves the host susceptible to a wide array of pathogens, but especially to encapsulated organisms. Certain bacteria have evolved ways of evading the human immune system. One way is through the production of a 'slimy' capsule on the outside of the bacterial cell wall. Such a capsule resists phagocytosis and ingestion by macrophages and neutrophils. This allows them not only to escape direct destruction by phagocytes but also to avoid stimulating T-cell responses through the presentation of bacterial peptides by macrophages. The only way that such organisms can be defeated is by making them more palatable by coating their capsular polysaccharide surfaces in opsonising antibody. The production of antibody against capsular polysaccharide primarily occurs through T-cell-independent mechanisms. The spleen plays a central role in both the initiation of the antibody response and the phagocytosis of opsonised encapsulated bacteria from the bloodstream. This helps to explain why following a splenectomy the host is most susceptible to infection by encapsulated organisms, notably Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus). Ne/sseria meningitidis (meningococcus) anc Haemophilus influenzae.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus aureus\",\"e\":\"Splenectomy increases the risk of infection with encapsulated organisms. Although with the vaccinations now given to post-splenectomy patients the infections they typically get are often from common organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Schistosoma haematobium K\",\"e\":\"Schistosoma haematobium causes schistosomiasis, a parasitic infection. It preferably infects the bladder, but with other forms of Schistosoma there can be hepatosplenic involvement with some evidence that splenectomy can be used as treatment.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"The histopathologist reports the presence of 'epithelioid cells' in a biopsied cervical lymph node from a patient with tuberculosis. They are commenced on tuberculosis eradication therapy and their condition improves. From which one of the following are epithelioid cells transformed?\",\"lead\":\"From which one of the following are epithelioid cells transformed?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epithelial cells\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Eosinophils\",\"e\":\"Eosinophils are predominantly found in patients with parasitic infections not tuberculous granulomas.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"Lymphocytes are part of the immune system and the predominant cell type in lymph.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Macrophages\",\"e\":\"luberculous granulomas are often called tubercles' and consist of round, plump, mononuclear phagocytes, Langhans' cells and epithelioid cells. The enlarged macrophages are called 'epithelioid cells' because of their abundant cytoplasm and their tendency to arrange themselves very closely together, which makes them resemble epithelial cells:\\n\\nEpitheloid cells are not transformed from epithelial cells, but take a similar name because their appearance resembles epithelial cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neutrophils\",\"e\":\"Neutrophils are the predominant cell type in acute inflammatory response.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"In an experiment, Streptococcus pneumoniae organisms are added to a solution containing leukocytes. Enguifment and phagocytosis of the microbes is observed to occur. A substance is then added that enhances engulfment. Which one of the following substances is most likely to produce this effect?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following substances is most likely to produce this effect?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Complement C3b\",\"e\":\"The C3b is a protein fragment released from complement component C3. It serves as an effective opsonin. Soluble C3 convertase, also known as 'iC3Bb', catalyses the proteolytic cleavage of C3 complement component into C3a and C3b as part of the alternative complement system. C3b can then bind to microbial cell surfaces within an organism's body. This can lead to the production of surface-bound C3 convertase and so more C3b components. Also known as 'C3bBb', this convertase is similar to soluble C3 convertase, except that it is membrane bound. Alternatively, bound C3b can help in the opsonisation of the microbe by macrophages. Complement receptor-1 (CR1) on macrophages allows the engaging of C3b-covered microbes\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glutathione peroxidase\",\"e\":\"Glutathione peroxidase does not act as an opsonin, but is involved in scavenging free radicals.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"immunoglobalin\",\"e\":\"IgM does not act as an opsonin, although IgG does.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"P-selectin\",\"e\":\"Selectins aid in the initial binding of leukocytes to endothelial surfaces.\\n\\nNADPH oxidase in leukocytes aids in the killing of phagocytosed microbes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"NADPH oxidase\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted under the infectious diseases team with unusual symptoms. Eventually he is diagnosed with Blastomyces dermatitidis and started on eradication therapy. Which one of the following is the most common portal of entry in this infection?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most common portal of entry in this infection?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"Explanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Central nervous system\",\"e\":\"Disseminated blastomycosis can cause lesions to develop in the central nervous system but that is not the most common portal of entry.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Circulatory system\",\"e\":\"Blastomycosis enters the body through the respiratory system but eventually invades the circulatory system to cause disseminated disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mouth\",\"e\":\"Blastomycosis can cause lesions in the mouth that can mimic squamous cell carcinoma but it does not enter the body through the mouth.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Respiratory tract\",\"e\":\"Biastomycosis is a pulmonary and occasionally a haematogencus fungal infection caused by the organism Giastomyces dermaiitidis. Cndemic in parts.of North America, blastomycosis causes Clinical symptoms similar to histoplasmosis. Infection occurs by inhalation of the spores of the fungus from its 1atura! soil habitat. Once inhaled into the lungs, the fungus multiplies and can disseminate through the blood and lymphatics to other organs, including the skin, bone, genitourinary tract and brain. The incubation period is 30-100 days, although infection can be asymptomatic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Skin\",\"e\":\"Blastomycosis can cause skin lesions to develop but most commoniy enters the body through the lungs\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man is being discussed at the colorectal MDT with a probably sigmoid cancer. The decision is made to proceed to resection. At operation the appearances are odd and the surgeon wonders whether the tumour has perforated due to the presence of an abscess. This is subsequently proven to contain sulphur granules.\\n\\nWhat is the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Actinomycosis\",\"e\":\"Actinomycosis is usually caused by Actinomyces israeli and is frequently misdiagnosed as a neoplasm. In health, A. israelii colonises the vagina, colon and mouth. Infection is established first by a breach of the mucosal barrier through various procedures (eg dental, gastrointestinal), aspiration, or pathological conditions such as diverticulitis. The chronic phase of this disease is also known as the 'classic phase' because the acute early phase is often missed. This is characterised by slow contiguous growth that ignores tissue planes and forms a sinus tract that can spontaneously heal and recur, leading to a densely fibrotic lesion. This lesion is often described as 'wooden'. • Sulphur granules form in a central purulence surrounded by neutraphils. This conglomeration of organisms is virtually diagnostic of A. israelii. Oral-cervicofacial disease is the most common form of actinomycosis. It is characterised by a painless 'lumpy jaw'. Lymphadenopathy is uncommon in this form of the disease. Another form of actinomycosis is thoracic disease, which is often misdiagnosed as a neoplasm, as it forms a mass that extends to the chest wall. It arises through aspiration of organisms from the oropharynx. Symptoms include chest pain, fever and weight loss. Abdominal disease is another manifestation of actinomycosis. This can lead to a sinus tract that drains to the abdominal wall or the perianal area. Pelvic actinomycosis is often caused by intrauterine devices. Symptoms include fever, abdominal pain and weight loss.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"TAN Amoebiasis\",\"e\":\"Amoebiasis is a parasitic infection that can cause the development of liver abscesses forming well circumscribed firm cavities. The abscess contains acellular proteinaceous debris as a consequence of induced apoptosis and is surrounded by a rim of amoebic trophozoites invading the tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Brucellosis\",\"e\":\"Brucellosis can affect any organ in the body and abscesses can therefore present in many different locations. They do not contain sulphur granules.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Histoplasmosis\",\"e\":\"Histoplasmosis is a fungal infection, on microscopy intracellular organisms are present and are surrounded by chronic inflammatory cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus aureus infection\",\"e\":\"Staphylococcus aureus is a Gram-positive coccus that may be detected on a pus swab from an abscess.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"A 38-year-old, HIV-positive man presents in the Emergency Department complaining of weight loss associated with chronic, persistent, watery, non-bloody diarrhoea and loss of appetite. Numerous acid-fast cysts are found in his stool sample. Which one of the following organisms is most likely to be responsible for this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following organisms is most likely to be responsible for this condition?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cryptosporidium parvum\",\"e\":\"Cryptosporidium parvum is one of several species that cause cryptosporidiosis. C. parvum infection is a protozoal infection that causes an acute diarrhoea in immunocompromised patients. In HIV infection, it can cause a watery diarrhoea, which can be associated with anorexia, nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain. There is no effective treatment (other than supportive treatment) for this infection apart from paromomycin antibiotics, this treatment can have a limited effect on the diarrhoea..\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Entamoeba histolytica\",\"e\":\"Entamoeba histolytica is a parasitic infection causing amoebiasis. When symptomatic patients can have frequent watery and/or bloody stools, but only about 10-20% of infected people show symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Giardia lamblia\",\"e\":\"Giardia lamblia causes giardiasis which presents with watery diarrhoea, abdominal cramping, malaise, nausea and anorexia. Trophozoites may be found in fresh stools but disintegrate rapidly. Cysts can also be intermittently seen in the stool and are more apparent when stained with iodine.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mycobacterium avium complex\",\"e\":\"Mycobacterium avium complex typically affects individuals who are immunocompromised but is primarily a pulmonary pathogen. It generally presents with a productive cough, weight loss, fever and lethargy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Toxoplasma gondii\",\"e\":\"Toxoplasma gondii causes toxoplasmosis that in immunosuppressed patients such as those with HIV can be rapidly fatal. In 50% of cases it involves the central nervous system causing seizures and focal neurological deficits. It does not typically result in diarrhoea. i230\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old patient who was receiving chemotherapy for cancer developed a disseminated varicella-zoster infection. They are given oral and topical aciclovir with some improvement in their symptoms. Which one of the following is the most likely reason for this viral infection?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely reason for this viral infection?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Deficiency in the third component of complement\",\"e\":\"Deficiency in the third component of complement is associated with enhanced susceptibility to pyogenic extracellular bacteria, but not to viruses.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypogammaglobulinaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypogammaglobulinaemia has not been shown to cause disseminated varicella-zoster infection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Outgrowth of virus from varicella-zoster immunisation\",\"e\":\"The varicella vaccine is a live vaccine which can reactivate later in life although this is very rare.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Synergism between varicella-zoster and chemotherapy\",\"e\":\"Primary infection with the varicella-zoster virus causes chickenpox. Immunity develops, but the virus remains in the body. It is immunosuppression by chemotherapy that reactivates the varicella-zoster virus and causes shingles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T-cell deficiency\",\"e\":\"An undesirable side-effect of chemotherapy used to treat malignancies is the destruction of T cells, which play a key role in the development of immunity against viral infections. This is especially true for viral diseases in which the causative agent remains dormant in the body. Varicella-zoster is an excellent example of such a virus and flare-ups of varicella-zoster infections are well known occurrences in cancer patients who receive chemotherapy. The varicella-zoster virus is a single entity. It is a medium-sized (100-200 nm), double-stranded DNA virus of the herpesvirus group, with only one serological type.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"Oncoviruses can cause up to 20% of cancers. These can be prevented with vaccinations.\\n\\nWhich DNA oncogenic virus may be prevented through vaccination?\",\"lead\":\"Which DNA oncogenic virus may be prevented through vaccination?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) and non-Hodgkin lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is a member of the herpes virus family and is associated with Burkitt's lymphoma and nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hepatitis C virus and liver cancer\",\"e\":\"Hepatitis C virus (HCV) is associated with hepatocellular carcinoma; however the mechanism of oncogenesis remains unclear. There is currently no vaccine for HCV.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Human herpes virus 8 and Kaposi's sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Human herpes virus 8 (HHV-8) causes Kaposi's sarcoma most commonly in patients with AIDS, there is no vaccine.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Human papilloma virus (HPV) and cervical cancer\",\"e\":\"There are around 50 types of human papillomavirus (HPV). The virus is associated with anal and cervical cancer. The human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine is now issued to all girls between the ages of 12 and 18.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Human T-cell leukaemia virus (HTLV-1)\",\"e\":\"Human T-cell leukaemia virus (HTLV-1) is the only known RNA virus that causes a human malignancy, most often haematological in nature. There is currently no vaccine.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following conditions exhibits autosomal-dominant inheritance, and is characterised by involvement of the nervous system?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following conditions exhibits autosomal-dominant inheritance, and is characterised by involvement of the nervous system?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)\",\"e\":\"FAP is inherited in an autosomal-dominant fashion in which numerous adenomatous polyps form mainly in the epithelium of the large bowel. Without treatment, development of colorectal carcinoma is inevitable in FAP\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fanconi's anaemia\",\"e\":\"Fanconi's anaemia is an autosomal-recessive condition that predisposes to lymphoid malignancies:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemophilia A\",\"e\":\"Haemophilia A is a bleeding disorder inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neurofibromatosis\",\"e\":\"Neurofibromatosis is inherited in an autosomal-dominant fashion. Type 1 disease is characterised by neurofibromas of the peripheral nervous system. In type 2 disease Schwann cell tumours are more common.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Xeroderma pigmentosum\",\"e\":\"Xeroderma pigmentosum is an autosomal-recessive disorder of DNA repair that predisposes to skin malignancies.\\n\\n1243\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"You are undertaking the World Health Organization (WHO) checklist in theatre before starting your routine elective list. One of your patients for a semi-elective inguinal hernia repair has acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) secondary to human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection and you bring this to the team's attention although universal precautions apply.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding this infective process?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding this infective process?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It carries a risk of seroconversion following a needlestick injury from an HIV-positive patient of approximately 1 in 25\",\"e\":\"The risk of seroconversion in someone following a needlestick injury from an HIV-positive patient is about 0.3%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is caused by a parvovirus-like agent\",\"e\":\"HIV is a single-stranded RNA virus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is more infectious following a needlestick injury than hepatitis B\",\"e\":\"The risk of seroconversion in someone following a needlestick injury from an HIV-positive patient is about 0.3%, and from a hepatitis B-positive patient it is approximately 30%. Hepatitis B is therefore potentially more infectious following a needlestick injury than HIV.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It may be present in high titre in the blood of asymptomatic carriers\",\"e\":\"HIV is present in high titre in the blood of asymptomatic carriers, and in many asymptomatic HIV-positive patients.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It may be diagnosed by Southern blotting\",\"e\":\"Definitive diagnosis is made by western blotting following a positive enzyme linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA). More recently, polymerase chain reaction (PCR) techniques have been used.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"You are clerking a man on the surgical take who is HIV positive but poorly compliant with his medication. He has been referred to the surgical team with a painful abdomen.\\n\\nInfectious causes of abdominal pain in HIV-infected individuals include which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Infectious causes of abdominal pain in HIV-infected individuals include which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epstein-Barr virus\",\"e\":\"Patients with HIV are at increased risk of developing Epstein-Barr virus associated lymphoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cryptococcus\",\"e\":\"Cryptococcus infection is one of the most common opportunistic infections in patients with HIV with most presenting with central nervous system (CNS) manifestations. Signs and symptoms include headache, fever, malaise, visual disturbance and cranial neuropathies.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cytomegalovirus\",\"e\":\"Numerous organisms can cause infection and abdominal pain in HIV-infected individuals. The well documented organisms include: cryptosporidia, cytomegaloviruses, Mycobacterium tuberculosis; Salmonella, Shigella and Campylobacter: spp., Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Treponema pallidum, Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare, Listeria monocytogenes, Entamoeba histolytica, Giardia lamblia, Isospora belli, Candida albicans, Histoplasma spp. and herpes simplex viruses.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Kaposi's sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Kaposi's sarcoma is a type of cancer more common in patient with AIDS. Lesions are usually discoloured nodules typically on the skin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Mycobacterium bovis\",\"e\":\"Mycobacterium bovis is an important cause of tuberculosis (TB) infection in HIV but would not present with abdominal pain. Mycobacterium tuberculosis can manifest with abdominal symptoms.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old patient is reviewed with a postoperative wound infection and an abscess. Following debridement, swabs grow Streptococcus pyogenes.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding this organism?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding this organism?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It belongs to Lancefield group A\",\"e\":\"Streptococcus pyogenes belongs to Lancefield group A. It is associated with rheumatic fever, carditis and glomerulonephritis as immunological sequelae. The Lancefield classification is based on the composition of bacterial antigens found the organisms' cell walls.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It cannot produce any toxins\",\"e\":\"Streptococcus spp. produces streptolysins O and S, hyaluronidase, streptokinase, leukocidin and an erythrogenic toxin that causes scarlet fever.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is found in clusters\",\"e\":\"Staphylococci are found in clusters; streptococci are found in pairs or chains.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is sensitive to tetracycline\",\"e\":\"It is sensitive to penicillins.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is alpha-haemolytic\",\"e\":\"Streptococcus pyogenes is beta-haemolytic.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"You are in the urology clinic and a patient is asking about a screening test for prostate cancer they have read about in the newspapers. Their best friend has recently been diagnosed with the disease and is due to undergo surgery. In devising a suitable screening test for malignant cancer, which of the following would be most useful?\",\"lead\":\"In devising a suitable screening test for malignant cancer, which of the following would be most useful?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The natural history of the disease should not be known\",\"e\":\"Preconditions for a good screening test include having an adequate understanding of the natural history of the disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The test should have high sensitivity\",\"e\":\"Preconditions for a good screening test include:\\n\\n• the disease must be an important health problem • there should be an accepted treatment • facilities for diagnosis and treatment must be available • there should be a latent stage • the screening test should have both high sensitivity and specificity • the test should be acceptable to the population • the natural history of the disease should be adequately understood • there should be an agreed policy on which patients should be treated • diagnosis and treatment should be relatively cost effective • the screening should be an ongoing process.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The test should have low specificity\",\"e\":\"A screening test should have both a high sensitivity and specificity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The tumour should be of anaplastic type\",\"e\":\"An anaplastic carcinoma would carry such a poor prognosis that any screening test would make no difference to the outcome.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The tumour should have a short latent phase\",\"e\":\"For a screening test to be effective, early detection and treatment must lead to fewer deaths. Earlier detection of tumours with a short latent stage is unlikely to improve the outcome.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":70,\"stem\":\"A fit and well 25-year-old patient presents with a superficial abscess on their arm. It is easily managed with incision and drainage in SAU using a freeze spray and lidocaine.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely causative organism?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely causative organism?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bacteroides\",\"e\":\"Bacteroides is unusual in superficial abscesses but is implicated in deep abscesses.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Escherichia coli\",\"e\":\"E. coli is often implicated in deep not superficial abscesses:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pseudomonas aeruginosa.\",\"e\":\"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a Gram-negative infection and an important cause in the immunocompromised. It is common in patients who have had a prolonged hospital stay.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus aureus\",\"e\":\"An abscess is collection of pus within soft tissues and occurs when the host's response to infection is inadequate. Predisposing factors include foreign bodies, haematoma formation and poor blood supply. An abscess contains bacteria, acute inflammatory cells, protein exudate and necrotic tissue and is surrounded by granulation tissue (the 'pyogenic membrane'). The organisms usually involved cause:\\n\\n• superficial abscesses - Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes • deep abscesses - Gram-negative species (eg Escherichia coli) and anaerobes (eg Bacteroides).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Streptococcus pneumoniae\",\"e\":\"Streptococcus pneumoniae is not typically implicated in abscesses but is the most common bacterial cause of pneumonia. 1267\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":73,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man is readmitted with fevers and rigors around 2 weeks following an appendicectomy. Imaging with a CT scan shows a collection. Following incision and drainage, cultures grow actinomycetes after 8 days. Which one of the following sites is the most common site of actinomycosal infection?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following sites is the most common site of actinomycosal infection?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"St Abdomen\",\"e\":\"Actinomycetes are commensals of the lower gut, infection can occur in the abdomen but is not the most common infection site.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cervicofacial\",\"e\":\"Actinomycetes are Gram-positive microaerophilic bacteria, present as part of the normal flora of the mouth, lower gut and female genital tract. It is therefore an endogenous infection. The commonest site of actinomycosis infection is cervicofacial (about 50% of cases). Other sites of infection include the abdomen, thorax and female genital tract. The commonest organism producing infection is Actinomyces israelii, although Actinomyces propionica is occasionally responsible. Culture of the organism is slow, and it takes a week or more to produce positive cultures. The discharge contains characteristic sulphur granules.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Female genital tract.\",\"e\":\"Actinomycetes rarely cause infection of the female genital tract, although are a commensal here.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Male genital tract\",\"e\":\"Actinomycetes are commensal of the female genital tract and can, although not commonly, cause infection, but are not found in the male genital tract.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thorax\",\"e\":\"Thoracic actinomycosis is uncommon but can be seen in immunocompromised patients.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":76,\"stem\":\"A 79-year-old woman is seen in her GP surgery 7 days after a hip replacement as the wound is erythematous and tender. A previous swab shows only normal skin flora and she is referred to the orthopaedic team.\\n\\nWhich one of the following sites are normally colonised by commensal bacteria?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following sites are normally colonised by commensal bacteria?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"iSil Aqueous humour:\",\"e\":\"Aqueous humour is free of commensal bacter a\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lung parenchyma\",\"e\":\"The lung parenchyma is free of commensal bacteria unlike the upper respiratory tract.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oropharynx\",\"e\":\"The internal organs of the body are free of conmensal bacteria, apart from the alimentary tract, upper respiratory tract and oropharynx and genital tract. The skin, external auditory meatus and conjunctiva are also colonised with commensals.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Peritoneal cavity\",\"e\":\"There are no commensal bacteria in the peritoneal cavity, if bacteria are present this would cause peritonitis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Retina\",\"e\":\"The retina is not colonised with commensal bacteria. Infectious retinitis occurs as a result of bacterial infection of the retina.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":78,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old man with idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura undergoes splenectomy. He returns to hospital with fever, low blood pressure (BP), tachycardia and reduced responsiveness. What is the most common causative organism of post-splenectomy sepsis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most common causative organism of post-splenectomy sepsis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Candidiasis\",\"e\":\"Candidiasis is caused by infection with the fungus Candida but is a rare cause of sepsis after splenectomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AM AM E coli\",\"e\":\"E. coli is sometimes isolated in post-splenectomy sepsis patients but is not the most common organism.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemophilus influenza\",\"e\":\"Haemophilus influenza is an encapsulated bacterium for which there is increased risk of sepsis after splenectomy but it is not the most commonly isolated organism.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neisseria meningitidis\",\"e\":\"Neisseria meningitidis is a Gram-negative encapsulated bacterium that can cause meningitis and has been isolated in septic patients post splenectomy but is not the most commonly isolated pathogen.\\n\\n1275\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Streptococcus oneumoniae\",\"e\":\"Patients who have undergone splenectomy are 60 times more likely to develop overwhelming infection than the normal healthy population. Sepsis is more common in patients after splenectomy for conditions that are partially improved by the procedure eg thalassemia and rare in patients after splenectomy for trauma or hereditary spherocytosis. The sepsis is more common in younger patients and is usually of insidious onset, pursues a fulminant course and may progress to death in 24-36 h, with a 50-80% mortality. Streptococcus pneumoniae is the causative organism in 55% of cases. Sepsis may be prevented by vaccination, preferably administered before surgery. Serious infections are most likely to occur within the first 3 years post splenectomy. It is now recommended that regular amoxicillin should be given as prophylaxis until the age of 15 (not lifelong) after which it should be commenced at the first sign of febrile illness.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":81,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old woman attends the pre-operative clinic. She is awaiting a knee replacement and is found to be methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) positive on wound swab. MRSA transmission may be best prevented by which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"MRSA transmission may be best prevented by which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Barrier nursing\",\"e\":\"Once detected, barrier nursing should be put into place to prevent spread but is not the best preventative measure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"VF51 Eradication of carriage\",\"e\":\"Mupirocin will offer short-term decolonisation of the nose, and chlorhexidine hair and body wash is also a useful tool but this is not the best preventative measure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Good hand hygiene\",\"e\":\"MRSA is Gram-positive coccus. Over the last 20 years the prevalence of MRSA in hospitals has fluctuated and is now found in around 50% of hospitals in the UK. The mecA gene encodes an additional penicillin binding protein (PBP), which confers methicillin resistance. It classically grows on culture medium in clusters as opposed to streptococci, which grow in chains. The routine and consistent use of handwashing and/or alcohol gels is the most effective preventative measure, as most transmission is skin to skin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intravenous vancomycin 1 g twice daily\",\"e\":\"Systemic MRSA infections may require appropriate antibiotics. Intravenous vancomycin 1 g twice daily may be considered if, for example, MRSA is detected in abdominal cavity or in blood cultures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Patient screening\",\"e\":\"Patient screening is important especially if having elective surgery with prosthetic implants and, once detected, surgery can be delayed or otherwise isolation of the carrier or infected patients can occur (barrier nursing).\\n\\n1270\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":84,\"stem\":\"Commensal bacteria flora are organisms that are routinely found in a particular organ that can cause sepsis. Due to this, one may predict which organisms are responsible for different causes of sepsis.\\n\\nWhich one of the following pairings is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following pairings is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Large bowel and candidiasis\",\"e\":\"Many anaerobic bacteria colonise the colon, especially Bacteroides fragilis:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lower respiratory tract and streptococci\",\"e\":\"The lower respiratory tract is usually sterile\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oropharynx and streptococci\",\"e\":\"The predominant bacterial flora of the oropharynx are Streptococcus viridans, coryneforms and Neisseria spp.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Skin and coliforms\",\"e\":\"The predominant commensal bacteria of the skin is Staphylococcus epidermidis. Others include Staphylococcus aureus, micrococci, coryneforms and anaerobic cocci. Aerobic bacteria such as Escherichia coli and Enterococcus spp. are also present in large numbers.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagina and E. coli\",\"e\":\"Lactobacilli are the predominant species in the vagina. Others include Gardnerella vaginalis and anaerobes.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"Endotoxins are large molecules comprising a lipid and a polysaccharide and are found on the outer surface of Gram-negative bacteria Which one of the following descriptions of the activity of endotoxins is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Endotoxins are large molecules comprising a lipid and a polysaccharide and are found on the outer surface of Gram-negative bacteria Which one of the following descriptions of the activity of endotoxins is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are excreted by living bacteria\",\"e\":\"Endotoxins form part of the cell wall of Gram-negative bacteria.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They cause systemic hypertension\",\"e\":\"Bradykinin release in response to endotoxins causes vasodilatation, increased vascular permeability and hypotensive shock\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They inhibit the alternative complement pathway\",\"e\":\"Endotoxins result in activation of the alternative pathway of complement cascade which mediates inflammation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They inhibit the release of tumour necrosis factor by macrophages\",\"e\":\"Endotoxins induce secretion of tumour necrosis factor.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They stimulate the release of interleukin-1 by macrophages\",\"e\":\"Endotoxins are lipopolysaccharides forming integral parts of the cell walls of Gram-negative bacteria. Interleukin-1 and tumour necrosis factor which are released by macrophages, mediate most of the biological effects of endotoxins. Bradykinin release causes vasodilatation, increased vascular permeability and hypotensive shock. The activation of the alternative pathway of complement cascade mediates inflammation. The activation of Hageman factor triggers the coagulation cascade and release of vasoactive kinins. They differ from exotoxins, which are excreted by living bacteria.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"Amyloidosis is a term used to collectively define a group of disorders in which protein structures change, causing them to fold over n a characteristic form. They can be present in serious pathological processes.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is an example of amyloidosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is an example of amyloidosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"15.v Acute pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"Acute pancreatitist involves acute inflammation of the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Papillary carcinoma of the thyroid\",\"e\":\"Papillary carcinoma is the most common form of thyroid cancer. Causes include exposure to radiation and genetics.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Osteoarthritis\",\"e\":\"Osteoarthritis is caused by the inflammation, breakdown and eventual loss of joint cartilage. Risk factors for developing osteoarthritis include obesity, trauma and age\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Soft tissue sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Soft tissue sarcomas are malignant tumours of mesenchymal origin with the majority occurring in the extremities. The majority of soft tissue sarcomas are sporadic but in rare cases there is a genetic predisposition.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Multiple myeloma\",\"e\":\"Amyloidosis is a disorder in which extracellular proteinaceous substances (protein fibrils) are deposited in body tissues, locally or systemically. Amyloidosis may be of primary or secondary atiology, and is linked with chronic disease conditions that are usually inflammatory (rheumatoid arthritis, Crohn's disease) or caused by infections (tuberculosis); it is also associated with multiple myeloma, Hodgkin's disease and Waldenström's macroglobulinaemia. Other forms of amyloidosis are familial and isolated (as in Alzheimer's disease.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":92,\"stem\":\"A 16-year-old girl who started at sixth-form boarding school 2 months ago, has a fever of 38°C, sore throat, malaise and generalised lymphadenopathy. Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection is suspected\\n\\nWhat is the most common condition associated with EBV?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most common condition associated with EBV?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Burkitt's lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Epstein-Barr virus may predispose to the formation of Burkitt's lymphoma, more commonly in the immunocompromised patients.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Guillain-Barré syndrome\",\"e\":\"Guillain-Barré is a rare demyelinating neuropathy classically presenting with ascending weakness.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hodgkin's lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Hodgkin's lymphoma is a malignancy arising from B cells, although not clear there is some evidence that it is linked with EBV. Hodgkin's lymphoma causes a painless swelling of the neck, weight loss and night sweats.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Infectious mononucleosis\",\"e\":\"The most common condition associated with Epstein-Barr virus infection is infectious mononucleosis, which is common in young adults. It is also known as glandular fever or 'the kissing disease' because it passes via saliva. The complications of infectious mononucleosis secondary to Epstein-Barr virus infection include Guillain-Barré syndrome, renal failure, pneumonitis, meningitis, encephalitis, autoimmune haemolytic anaemia and hepatitis. The Epstein-Barr virus genome has been found in Reed-Sternberg cells of Hodgkin's lymphoma and Epstein-Barr virus is also associated with nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Squamous cell carcinoma of the throat\",\"e\":\"The most common type of oropharyngeal cancer is squamous cell cancer of the oropharynx. Predisposing factors include smoking or chewing tobacco and human papillomavirus.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":95,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old microbiology student has been drinking to excess and partying during her first year at university. She presents with fever and right upper quadrant pain, she is suspected to have a contracted Epstein-Barr virus.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a commonly recognised feature?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a commonly recognised feature?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Stomatitis\",\"e\":\"Stomatitis is an inflammation of the mouth and lips. It can have a wide variety of causes including the herpes simplex virus 1 resulting in gingivostomatitis. It is not a feature of infectious mononucleosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Jaundice 1: -\",\"e\":\"Infectious mononucleosis (or glandular fever) is caused by the Epstein-Barr virus (human herpes virus 4, HHV-4). A penicillin will cause a rash in infected patients. It can cause splenomegaly, haemolytic anaemia and hepatitis (subsequently jaundice).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Shingles\",\"e\":\"Shingles occurs with reactivation of herpes zoster (human herpes virus 3).. It typically presents with a painful dermatomal rash.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Quinsy\",\"e\":\"Quinsy is a peri-tonsillar abscess most commonly caused by Streptococcus, Staphylococcus and Haemophilus species.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Parotid gland swelling\",\"e\":\"Parotid gland sweiling is the most common symptom of mumps. In infectious mononucleosis the initial infection is at the salivary glands but it does not present with parotid gland swelling.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old homeless man presents with a laceration of his forehead having been assaulted with a rusty tin, wielded by a football hooligan. He receives a tetanus vaccine. Which one of the following is correct regarding clostridia species?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding clostridia species?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are Gram-negative\",\"e\":\"Clostridia bacteria are Gram-positive.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They secrete endotoxins\",\"e\":\"Clostridia are exotoxin-producing. Endotoxins are part of bacterial cell walls and are released following bacterial death, so inducing a response.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are aerobic\",\"e\":\"Clostridia are anaerobes with varying degrees of resistance to the toxic effects of oxygen.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They are spore bearing\",\"e\":\"Clostridia are spore bearing bacteria. Clostridium tetani produces a potent biological toxin, retanospasmin, which causes painful muscular spasms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They are spherical\",\"e\":\"Clostridia are rod-shaped bacteria. Clostridium tetani is box-car shaped.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":100,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old butcher is brought in unwell to the surgical department with abdominal pain. He is in acute renal failure and haemolytic uremic syndrome is suspected. Which one of the following is produced by Escherichia coli?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is produced by Escherichia coli?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Verotoxin\",\"e\":\"Verotoxin is produced by the enterohaemorrhagic Escherichia coli and is similar to the shiga toxin produced by Shigella dysenteriae.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyaluronidase\",\"e\":\"Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes and Clostridium perfringens produce hyaluronidase. S. aureus utilises hyaluronidase to break down the connective tissue matrix.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Toxic-shock syndrome toxin!\",\"e\":\"Toxic-shock syndrome toxin is a superantigen produced by Staphylococcus aureus causing toxic-shock syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Exotoxin A\",\"e\":\"Exotoxin A is associated with Pseudomonas aeruginosa.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neurotoxin\",\"e\":\"Neurrotoxins are produced by bacteria such as Clostridium botulinum. 1317\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"You are asked to see a 47-year-old man on the orthopaedic ward. He has had a recent plate fixation of a fractured clavicle. He appears flushed and his blood pressure is 80/64 mmHg. What is a recognised toxin produced by Staphylococcus aureus?\",\"lead\":\"What is a recognised toxin produced by Staphylococcus aureus?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Botulinum toxin\",\"e\":\"Botulinum toxin is produced by the bacterium Clostridium botulinum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Amylase\",\"e\":\"Amylase is an enzyme released by the pancreas and a raised amylase can be an indication of pancreatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemolysin\",\"e\":\"Staphylococcus aureus produces haemolysins, fibrinolysin, hyaluronidase (may assist spread of infection), leucocidin (destroys polymorphonuclear leukocytes), coagulase, enterotoxin (heat-stable proteins causing vomiting), toxic-shock syndrome toxin (related to enterotoxins) and epidermolytic toxins (cause splitting of epidermis and blister formation).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Shiga toxin\",\"e\":\"Shiga toxins are produced by the bacteria Shigella dysenteriae and certain strains of E. coli.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Verotoxin\",\"e\":\"Verotoxin (also known as Shiga-like toxin) is produced by some strains of E. coli and is associated with haemolytic uremic syndrome.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":104,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old HIV-positive man presents with pain, swelling and fever in the mandibular region following a tooth extraction. He is admitted under the maxillofacial surgical team for intravenous antibiotics while cultures are awaited.\\n\\nWhich of the following options best describes actinomycosis infection in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following options best describes actinomycosis infection in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Common\",\"e\":\"It is a rare bacterial infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Involves Gram negative rods\",\"e\":\"Actinomycosis is a Gram-positive, non-acid-fast, anaerobic to microaerophilic bacterial infection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Less common in diabetics\",\"e\":\"It is commoner in diabetics and immunosuppressed people and is highly sensitive to penicillin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Non-granulomatous inflammation\",\"e\":\"Actinomycosis causes granulomatous and suppurative inflammation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pus contains sulphur granules\",\"e\":\"Actinomycosis bacterial infections cause pus containing sulphur granules.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":106,\"stem\":\"A 62-year-old diabetic and hypertensive man suffered a stroke that affected his speech and movement in the right arm and leg. A cerebral angiogram revealed an occlusion of his left middle cerebral artery. Months later, a CT scan shows a large, 5 cm cystic area in his left parietal lobe cortex.\\n\\nThis CT scan finding most likely demonstrates a lesion that is the consequence of resolution of which one of the following events?\",\"lead\":\"This CT scan finding most likely demonstrates a lesion that is the consequence of resolution of which one of the following events?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Apoptosis\",\"e\":\"Apoptosis is single-cell necrosis that does not result in a grossly visible cystic area.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Atrophy\",\"e\":\"Atrophy would be a more generalised process, whereas a single cystic area in the brain suggests a remote infarction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Caseous necrosis\",\"e\":\"Caseous necrosis is more typical of granulomatous inflammation with Mycobacterium tuberculosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Coagulative necrosis\",\"e\":\"Coagulative necrosis is more typical of parenchymal organs such as the kidney or spleen. which do not have as high a lipid content as the brain.\\n\\n1409\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Liquefactive necrosis\",\"e\":\"The brain undergoes liquefactive necrosis with infarction. As it resolves, a cystic area forms in the region of infarction.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":108,\"stem\":\"Metaplasia is a reversible change in which one adult cell type (epithelial or mesenchymal) is replaced by another adult cell type. In certain areas it may represent a pre-malignant condition. In which one of the following situations is the process of epithelial metaplasia most likely to have occurred?\",\"lead\":\"In which one of the following situations is the process of epithelial metaplasia most likely to have occurred?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute myocardial infarction\",\"e\":\"An acute myocardial infarction will lead to cardiac muscle fibre necrosis, which will heal with the production of fibrous scar tissue, but this is not a reversible metaplasia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lactation following pregnancy\",\"e\":\"Lactation following pregnancy is a form of physiological hyperplasia of breast lobules that occurs as a result of hormonal influences.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tanning of the skin following sunlight exposure\",\"e\":\"Tanning of the skin is a physiological event resulting from the accumulation of melanin pigment - there is no change from one cell type to another involved.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urinary obstruction due to an enlarged prostate\",\"e\":\"The enlarged prostate represents primarily glandular hyperplasia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vitamin A deficiency\",\"e\":\"Vitamin A is necessary to maintain epithelia in the respiratory tract and squamous metaplasia can occur if there is a deficiency.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":111,\"stem\":\"A patient is found to have a small kidney on one side and has worsening renal function. They undergo a renal biopsy which demonstrates atrophy of the kidney. Which one of the following findings on a microscopic slide is most in keeping with atrophy of an organ?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following findings on a microscopic slide is most in keeping with atrophy of an organ?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A greater number of autophagic vacuoles\",\"e\":\"Autophagic vacuoles appear to form when breakdown of intracellular components and organelles occurs. Separation of damaged cell substances in the form of autophagic vacuoles appears to be an adaptive response in atrophy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased myofilament size\",\"e\":\"Myofilaments form muscles, an increase in the size of fibres results in muscle hypertrophy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A greater number of mitochondria\",\"e\":\"Mitochondria are important for energy production. The number of mitochondria differs between different cell types and there is some evidence that the number of mitochondria decreases with atrophy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A greater number of rough endoplasmic reticulum\",\"e\":\"Rough endoplasmic reticulum is important for protein synthesis and in atrophy there would mean fewer numbers.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A greater number of smooth endoplasmic reticulum\",\"e\":\"Atrophy results in fewer smooth endoplasmic reticulum, which is involved in cell transport and phospholipid synthesis.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":114,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old woman has a core biopsy of a screen-detected right breast lesion. Unfortunately, she develops a large bruise, which resolves over time. Which one of the following statements most accurately describes the process involved?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements most accurately describes the process involved?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Eosinophils are key players in remodelling after tissue injury\",\"e\":\"Eosinophils are involved in allergic reactions but are not important for remodelling after tissue injury.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mast cells produce antibodies that recruit phagocytic cells to the area\",\"e\":\"Mast cells and eosinophils are involved in allergic reactions but do not produce antibodies, these are produced by B cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Monocytes are responsible for phagocytosing effete red blood cells\",\"e\":\"Following an injury, the body recruits cells such as neutrophils, monocytes and macrophages which act to phagocytose the effete material to start the process of regeneration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Natural killer cells work in tandem with mast cells to initiate a type IV hypersensitivity reaction\",\"e\":\"Natural killer cells are a type of cytotoxic lymphocyte that provide a rapid response to virally infected cells or tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Giant cells are formed by the coalescence of mast cells\",\"e\":\"Giant cells are formed by the coalescence of macrophages in chronic inflammation in granulomatous diseases.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":117,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old woman falls down the stairs and sustains a laceration to her shin Unfortunately, the wound becomes infected. As part of the healing process macrophages are recruited. Which one of the following statements most accurately describes these essential cells?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements most accurately describes these essential cells?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are derived from the spieen C\",\"e\":\"Macrophages are part of the mononuclear phagocytic system and are derived from bone marrow haemopoletic cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are important in humoral immunity\",\"e\":\"Macrophages play a vital role in several aspects of the innate immune response. They take up antigen and degrade it to short polypeptides that can be expressed on their surface. Furthermore, macrophages play a role in delayed-type hypersensitivity reactions, and also have antitumour activity. In addition, they produce various colony-stimulating factors that promote the growth and development of different white blood cells, and some factors that act on other cells (monokines), such as interleukin-1 (IL-1), which stimulates helper T cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are rarely found in granulomas\",\"e\":\"Macrophages are involved in granulomatous and chronic inflammation, whereby macrophages and monocytes differentiate into epithelioid cells.\\n\\nGiant cells are formed by the fusion of macrophages in response to exogenous insoluble material (such as talc and silica) and in reaction to endogenous insoluble material, such as keratin, fat and cholesterol, and to certain organisms such as those causing tuberculosis and syphilis bacteria. Megaloblasts are abnormal precursors of red blood cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They can form megaloblasts\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They have phagocytic properties\",\"e\":\"Macrophages are well known phagocytic cells. The function of macrophages includes antimicrobial defence against intracellular organisms such as Mycobacterium, Listeria and Histoplasma spp.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":120,\"stem\":\"You undertake a local anaesthetic list where you excise a skin lesion and send it for histology. You close the wound directly with vertical mattress sutures and prolene.\\n\\nWhich one of the following cells is involved in the process of wound healing in the proliferative stage?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following cells is involved in the process of wound healing in the proliferative stage?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adipocytes\",\"e\":\"Adipocytes are fat cells and there is no clear role for them in wound healing.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Platelets\",\"e\":\"Platelets are invoived in wound healing in the haemostasis stage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fibroblasts\",\"e\":\"Wound healing is the process by which damaged tissue is restored, as closely as possible, to its normal state. The steps in wound healing include: haemostasis (first few minutes), inflammation (0-3 days), proliferative (3 -5 days), maturation and remodelling (5 days to 6 months). The various cell types which take part in wound healing and time of appearance in wound as follows:\\n\\n• Platelets: Immediate • Neutrophils: 0-1 day • Macrophages: 1-2 days • Fibroblasts: 2-4 days • Myofibroblasts: 2-4 days • Endothelial cells: 3-5 days.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Keratinocyte(\",\"e\":\"Keratinocytes are keratin-producing epidermal cells and have a role in the healing of wounds in the regeneration/remodelling stage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Megakaryocytes\",\"e\":\"Megakaryocytes are platelet precursors and are not involved in wound healing. 142G\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":123,\"stem\":\"A 19-year-old woman attends the clinic with a large keloid scar to her ear from where it has previously been pierced. Which one of the following statements regarding these scars is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding these scars is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are typically caused by wound haematoma and infection\",\"e\":\"Keloids are dermoproliferative disorders unique to humans. The atiology of keloids is unclear, although various theories including familial tendency such as an autosomal- dominant or -recessive inheritance, hormonal influence, altered immunological response, enhanced role of transforming growth factor-B, abnormality of keratinocyte control over fibroblasts, and downregulation of apoptosis-related genes have been purported. Factors such as haematoma, infection and wound dehiscence predispose to hypertrophic scar formation (not keloids).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are characterised by decreased collagen synthesis\",\"e\":\"Collagen synthesis is three times higher in keloids than in hypertrophic scars, and 20 times higher in keloids than in normal skin. The absolute amount of collagen is also increased in keloids, indicative of increased collagen synthesis or decreased collagen degradation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are more common in children and young adults\",\"e\":\"Keloids commonly affect children and young adults, and they undergo rapid growth during puberty and increase in size during pregnancy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They do not extend beyond the margins of the original scars\",\"e\":\"Keloids extend beyond the original scar margins, while hypertrophic scars are confined to the borders of the original wound.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They usually develop within 1-2 months of initial injury\",\"e\":\"Hypertrophic scars generally develop within weeks of injury, whereas keloids can develop up to 1 year later.\\n\\n-\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":126,\"stem\":\"You are asked to close a cheek laceration in the Emergency Department\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding cutaneous wound healing is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding cutaneous wound healing is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Healing by secondary intention occurs by a combination of granulation, epithelialisation and wound contraction\",\"e\":\"In healing by secondary intention, the wound closes by both wound contraction, granulation and epithelialisation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Primary closure is recommended for the surgical wound after incision and drainage of a perianal abscess\",\"e\":\"Grossly contaminated wounds, wounds with extensive soft tissue loss, or wounds after some surgical procedures (eg laparostomy) are left to heal by secondary intention (secondary healing).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Primary healing occurs when a wound is closed within 48-72 h of its creation\",\"e\":\"Primary healing (healing by first intention) occurs when a wound is closed within 12-24 h of its creation as in a clean surgical incision or a clean laceration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Granulation tissue plays a crucial role in primary healing\",\"e\":\"Granulation tissue is an important part of wound healing in secondary intention,\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Split-thickness donor graft sites heal by secondary intention\",\"e\":\"Healing of split-thickness donor graft sites does not occur by secondary intention but by epithelialisation. In this type of injury, as the basal layer of cells remains uninjured, the epithelial cells within the dermal appendages, hair follicles, and sebaceous glands replicate to cover the exposed dermis.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":129,\"stem\":\"A patient has lived with GORD for many years, even declining prescriptions of omeprazole from their GP. Their symptoms have recently worsened and an OGD is organised which demonstrates changes in the distal oesophagus. A diagnosis of Barrett's is made.\\n\\nIn Barrett's oesophagus, what is the pathological process that converts squamous cells to columnar cells?\",\"lead\":\"In Barrett's oesophagus, what is the pathological process that converts squamous cells to columnar cells?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dysplasia\",\"e\":\"Dysplasia is the abnormal development of cells, which is often a pre-malignant state.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypertrophy\",\"e\":\"Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size..\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyperplasia\",\"e\":\"Hyperplasia is an increase in cell number.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Apoptosis\",\"e\":\"Apoptosis is programmed cell death.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Metaplasia\",\"e\":\"Metaplasia is the reversible replacement of one differentiated cell type with another, as in this case.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":131,\"stem\":\"A 16-year-old woman returns to the clinic 8 weeks after an emergency appendicectomy with the formation of an unsightly keloid scar.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a risk factor for these scars?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a risk factor for these scars?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Light skin\",\"e\":\"Keloid scars are more common on dark skin, burns and trauma wounds and in those with a past history of keloid scars.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Older age range\",\"e\":\"Keloid scars are more common in younger patients.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tension on wound\",\"e\":\"Tension in the wound is a risk factor for the development of keloid scars. Other risk factors include dark pigmented skin, past history of keloid scars, male sex, site of the wound (sternum, shoulders, head and neck) and delayed healing.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Use of Langer's lines\",\"e\":\"The use of Langer's lines for incisions is a preventative measure for keloid scars.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Use of pressure garments\",\"e\":\"Careful use of pressure garments are preventative measures.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":133,\"stem\":\"A 17-year-old girl presents to the clinic with a keloid scar following a belly button piercing. She wants the lump removed but the consultant is reluctant to excise the lesion as they are concerned it will come back again. Which one of the following is characteristic of these scars?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is characteristic of these scars?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It may be re-excised with good results\",\"e\":\"Re-excision usually leads to recurrence unless steroids (triamcinolone) are injected locally to reduce scar formation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is more common in pigmented skir\",\"e\":\"Keloid scars are 15 times more common in patients with darkly pigmented skin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is most common on the flexure surfaces of the limbs\",\"e\":\"Keloid is most common on the sternum and deltoid area.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is unlikely to be prevented with pressure dressings\",\"e\":\"Pressure dressings can be used as a preventative measure for the formation of keloid scars.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It occurs within the limits of the surgical wound\",\"e\":\"Keloid scars extend beyond the previous wound (hypertrophic scars are confined to the wound).\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":135,\"stem\":\"During a marathon attempt, a 27-year-old man collapses and is admitted to hospital with severe acute dehydration. This patient most likely has which one of the following as a result of severe dehydration?\",\"lead\":\"This patient most likely has which one of the following as a result of severe dehydration?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased baroreceptor firing rate\",\"e\":\"There is a decrease in plasma volume in acute dehydration leading to an inhibition of the baroreceptors and a lower firing rate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased plasma osmolarity\",\"e\":\"Acute dehydration results in decreased plasma volume and increased plasma osmolarity, as more water than salt is lost in sweat.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"High renal water excretion\",\"e\":\"An increase in water permeability results in the reabsorption of more water by the kidneys and renal water excretion is low.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Low plasma adenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) levels\",\"e\":\"The increase in plasma osmolarity leads to increased ACTH secretion and high plasma ACTH levels.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Low water permeability of the renal collecting ducts\",\"e\":\"As a result of high plasma ADH levels there is increased water permeability of the collecting duct cells that allows further reabsorption of water.\\n\\n1758\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":137,\"stem\":\"During routine antenatal check-up of a pregnant patient at 26 weeks' gestation it was discovered that she had high blood pressure, proteinuria (400 mg/day), serum albumin of 20g/l, creatinine creatinine umol/l and peripheral oedema. She had normal blood pressure on her first antenatal Check-up.\\n\\nWhich one of the following diagnoses is most appropriate for this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following diagnoses is most appropriate for this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chronic renal failure\",\"e\":\"Chronic renal failure can be associated with proteinuria and hypertension, which can get worse in pregnancy. But, in the context of this patient having a normal blood pressure at the first antenatal check, it is not the most likely diagnosis. Most patients would have their chronic renal failure identified either before pregnancy or at their first check.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glomerulonephritis\",\"e\":\"In glomerulonephritis an immunological mechanism triggers inflammation of the glomerular tissue. Pregnancy can reveal underlying renal disease such as glomerulonephritis with proteinuria typically before 16 weeks.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Nephrotic syndrome\",\"e\":\"Nephrotic syndrome is the combination of proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia and oedema. In nephrotic syndrome proteinuria is >3 g/day.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Polycystic kidney disease\",\"e\":\"Polycystic kidney disease is a genetic disorder that can be inherited in either an autosomal- dominant or autosomal-recessive fashion. Autosomal-recessive inheritance is much rarer and problems tend to present in childhood. With autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease it is usually only picked up when the cysts are large enough to affect kidney function, typically between the ages of 30 and 60. Patients with polycystic kidney disease are more likely to develop proteinuria and hypertension earlier in their pregnancy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pre-eclampsia\",\"e\":\"Pre-eclampsia is said to be present when hypertension arises in pregnancy (pregnancy- induced hypertension) in association with significant protein in the urine, usually after the 20th week of pregnancy. Its cause remains unclear, although the principal cause appears to be a substance or substances from the placenta causing endothelial dysfunction in the maternal blood vessels. While blood pressure elevation is the most visible sign of the disease, it involves generalised damage to the maternal endothelium, kidneys and liver; with the release of vasopressive factors only secondary to the original damage. Pre-eclampsia may develop at varying times within pregnancy and its progress differs among patients; most cases present pre-term. It has no known cure apart from ending the pregnancy (induction of labour or abortion). It may also present up to 6 weeks post-partum. It is the most common, dangerous complication of pregnancy and it may affect both the mother and the fetus. Pre-eclampsia is diagnosed when a pregnant woman develops high blood pressure (two separate readings taken at least 6 h apart of 140/90 or more) and 300 mg of protein in a 24-h urine sample (proteinuria). Swelling or oedema, (especially in the hands and face) was originally considered an important sign for a diagnosis of pre-eciampsia, but in current medical practice only hypertension and proteinuria are necessary for a diagnosis. However, unusual swelling, particularly of the hands, feet or face, notable by leaving an indentation when pressed on, can be significant and should be investigated.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":140,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old man who underwent a liver transplant 6 years earlier presents to his GP with a lesion on his lower lip that bleeds intermittently. On examination it is a small ulcerated lesion with raised edges. He reports it has been present for 4 months and is not healing.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely nature of this lesion?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely nature of this lesion?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Candida albicans\",\"e\":\"Candida fungal infection of the skin is more common in immunosuppressed patients and causes a red itchy rash most commonly on skin folds.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Kaposi sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Kaposi sarcoma is caused by infection with the human herpes virus 8 and usually occurs in immunosuppressed patients due to HIV/AIDS or post organ transplantation. They typically present as red/purple skin patches or inside the mouth, over time they can become nodular and merge into each other.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Melanoma\",\"e\":\"Melanomas are mainly caused by exposure to ultraviolet (UV) light and are twice as likely in the immunosuppressed patient. They commonly appear as a new mole or change in an existing mole and are more likely if the following features are present:\\n\\n• asymmetry • border irregularity • colour (uneven) • diameter > 6 mm • elevation/enlargement.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Squamous cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Long-term immunosuppression for successful organ transplantation carries a number of important side-effects such as malignant disease in particular squamous cell carcinoma. This has a number of different appearances, but can be a non-healing ulcer often with slightly everted edges. Transplant patients are often followed up long-term to screen for the development of skin cancers secondary to immunosuppression.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Gingival hypertrophy\",\"e\":\"Gingival hypertrophy is an increase in the size of the gums.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":143,\"stem\":\"You are treating a 15-year-old man who has presented with failure to fully develop secondary sexual characteristics and gynaecomastia. Genetic testing reveals an extra chromosome (XXY). What condition does he have?\",\"lead\":\"What condition does he have?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH)\",\"e\":\"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia can affect both men and women, and is an autosomal- recessive condition. It results in a deficiency of an enzyme involved in cortisol synthesis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fragile-X syndrome\",\"e\":\"Fragile-X syndrome is inherited in an X-linked dominant pattern, and usually presents with a range of developmental problems.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Klinefelter syndrome\",\"e\":\"Klinefelter syndrome usually presents at puberty, and only affects boys. It is a result of an extra sex chromosome, the patient is XXY. Affected men fail to fully develop secondary sexual characteristics, including small testes and lack of facial hair. Gynaecomastia, infertility, mild mental retardation and increased height can also be features.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Testicular feminisation\",\"e\":\"Testicular feminisation patients present as women, but are XY, and can present with an inguinal hernia containing a testis or amenorrhoea.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Turner syndrome\",\"e\":\"In Turner syndrome those affected are woman who are XO.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":146,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old woman is about to undergo a coronary artery bypass graft for four-vessel coronary artery disease. She has a past medical history of asthma and smoking history of 45 pack-years.\\n\\nWith regards to smoking which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"With regards to smoking which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Four weeks' abstinence pre-operatively will return ciliary function to near normal\",\"e\":\"Ciliary function does not return to normal for at least 8 weeks.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Nicotine decreases myocardial oxygen demand\",\"e\":\"Nicotine increases myocardial oxygen demand via an increase in heart rate and blood pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"There is no benefit to smoking cessation 12 h pre-operatively\",\"e\":\"Smoking cessation for 12 h pre-operatively results in a significant decrease in carboxyhaemoglobin levels.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Smoking increases the risk of postoperative deep vein thrombosis (DVT)\",\"e\":\"Smoking is a risk factor for deep vein thrombosis (DVT).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Smoking more than 10 cigarettes a day doubles postoperative morbidity\",\"e\":\"More than 10 cigarettes a day increases the postoperative morbidity six-fold.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":148,\"stem\":\"You undertake a day-case simple excision of sebaceous cyst from the cheek of a young girl. When seen in clinic a week later, she complains of pain and discharge. Which one of the following organisms are likely to be implicated in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following organisms are likely to be implicated in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Candida\",\"e\":\"Candidiasis of the skin typically presents as an erythematous rash on the skin folds, genitals. trunk, buttocks or inframammary folds.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Klebsiella\",\"e\":\"Common Klebsiella infections include UTI and pneumonia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Micobacterium tuberculosis\",\"e\":\"Mycobacterium tuberculosis can cause cutaneous infection but would be an unlikely cause in this case.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus aureus\",\"e\":\"Staphylococcus aureus is commonly implicated in skin infections and post-operative wound infections.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pneumocystis carinii\",\"e\":\"Pneumocystis carinii is an opportunistic respiratory infection found typically in the immunocompromised\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":150,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old man who is HIV positive is admitted with appendicitis. During open surgery it is found the appendix is stuck down and very difficult to remove. In this process the SHO sustains a needle stick injury.\\n\\nWhat is the transmission rate for HIV following these injuries?\",\"lead\":\"What is the transmission rate for HIV following these injuries?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"Explanation ol\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"0.001%\",\"e\":\"The risk of transmission of HIV after a needlestick injury is 0.3%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"0.1%\",\"e\":\"The risk of transmission after mucocutaneous exposure is 0.1%.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"0.3%\",\"e\":\"Occupationally acquired HIV infection in health workers occurs as a result of sharps injury. with hollow needles carrying a much greater risk. The overall risk of transmission of HIV infection is about 0.3% of all needlestick injuries from HIV-positive patients. At 6-12 weeks following HIV infection, there is a rise in antigen titre but no detectable antibody. At 3 months, 85% of HIV-infected patients mount an antibody response and their antigen levels fall. In HIV infection the CD4 (receptors found on helper T cells and macrophages) count falls. Hepatitis B virus can be up to 100 times more infectious than HIV.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"1.8%\",\"e\":\"There is a 1.8% risk of transmission of hepatitis C virus following a needlestick injury, greater than a patient with HIV.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"30%\",\"e\":\"The risk of transmission of hepatitis B virus after needlestick can be as high as 30%, it is much less in HIV.\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":153,\"stem\":\"Your consultant quizzes you on the initial stages of the wound healing of a scar during an appendicectomy. Which one of the following options is routinely involved in the process of wound healing?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is routinely involved in the process of wound healing?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SPI Erythrocytes\",\"e\":\"Erythrocytes (red blood cells) do not take part in wound healing\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fibroblasts\",\"e\":\"The process of wound healing may be described in the order of haemostasis, inflammation, proliferation and maturation:\\n\\n• Haemostasis: Within the first few minutes of injury, platelet activation occurs, resulting in fibrin activation and platelet aggregation, forming a blood clot to reduce further haemorrhage. • Inflammation: Phagocytosis of damaged/dead cells and tissue occurs, and platelet- derived growth factors are released into the wound. This causes the migration and division of cells during the proliferative phase. • Proliferation: In this phase, angiogenesis, collagen deposition, granulation, epithelialisation, and wound contraction occur. In angiogenesis, vascular endothelial cells form new blood vessels. During fibroplasia and granulation tissue formation, fibroblasts grow and form a new provisional extracellular matrix by excreting collagen and fibronectin. Concurrently, re-epithelialisation of the epidermis occurs, in which epithelial cells proliferate on top of the wound bed, providing cover for the new tissue. In wound contraction, myofibroblasts progressively decrease the size of the wound. • Maturation (remodelling): During maturation and remodelling, collagen is realigned along tension lines, and cells that are no longer needed are removed by apoptosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Megakaryocytes\",\"e\":\"Megakaryocytes are not involved in wound healing directly, they are the precursors of platelets, which are important in the haemostasis required for wound healing:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Osteoblasts\",\"e\":\"Osteoblasts are important for bone production but not wound healing.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Dendritic cells\",\"e\":\"Dendritic cells are antigen-presenting cells and act as messengers between innate and adaptive immunıty.\\n\\nA 40-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with vomiting and epigastric pain. An image from her computed tomography (CT) scan is shown below:\\n\\nSource: radiopaedia.org What abnormality in her blood biochemistry is most specific to her diagnosis?\"}]},{\"t\":26,\"p\":161,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man suffered a massive myocardial infarction that was complicated by shock and prolonged hypotension. On arrival in the Emergency Department, he was found to have focal neurological deficits in addition to features consistent with low-output cardiac failure. Despite the best efforts of the medical team he died the next day. At autopsy, the most likely change you would expect to see in a brain biopsy would be which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"At autopsy, the most likely change you would expect to see in a brain biopsy would be which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute haemorrhagic change\",\"e\":\"The history of this patient having prolonged hypotension would fit with hypoxic cell death that does not evoke acute haemorrhagic changes. This would be more common with bleeds from traumatic brain injuries.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Coagulative necrosis\",\"e\":\"Coagulative necrosis is a type of cell death caused by ischaemia or infarction. It is important to note that while ischaemia in most tissues of the body will cause coagulative necrosis, in the central nervous system it causes liquefactive necrosis, as there is very little structural framework in neural tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Granulomatous change\",\"e\":\"Granulomatous changes are caused by a variety of conditions including infection, autoimmune, allergic and neoplastic conditions, this does not apply in this case.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lacunar infarct\",\"e\":\"Lacunar infarcts are small infarcts in the deep cerebral white matter, basal ganglia or pons. They are thought to occur due to occlusion of a single small perforating artery. In this case there has been prolonged hypotension that would result in global brain ischaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Liquefactive necrosis\",\"e\":\"Liquefactive necrosis is characteristic of focal bacterial, or occasionally fungal, infections, because microbes stimulate the accumulation of inflammatory cells. Hypoxic death of cells within the central nervous system evoke liquefactive necrosis. Although the reasons for this are obscure, it is thought to happen due to the reduced structural framework of neural tissues. Whatever the pathogenesis, liquefaction completely digests the dead cells. The enc result is transformation of the tissue into a viscous liquid mass. If the process was initiated by acute inflammation the material is frequently creamy yellow in colour because of the presence of dead white cells and this is called 'pus'.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman who had been on oral contraceptives for nearly 3 years presented in the gynaecology outpatient clinic with abnormal vaginal bleeding. On speculum examination, an endocervical polypoid mass was discovered, which was biopsied and sent for histopathology reporting. Which one of the following will be reported by the histopathologist?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following will be reported by the histopathologist?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Clear cell adenocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Clear cell adenocarcinoma is a subtype of vaginal carcinoma. This is a rare tumour, often linked to pre-natal exposure to diethylstillbestrol (DES), a drug which was prescribed in high-risk pregnancies. Clear cell adenocarcinoma accounts for approximately 10% of primary vaginal malignancies, and cases are mainly diagnosed between the ages of 15 and 31.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ectopic pregnancy\",\"e\":\"An ectopic pregnancy would not present with an endocervical polypoid mass.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Endocervical adenocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Endocervical polyps are usually reddish-pink, measure less than 1 cm in all dimensions and are friable. They are rarely malignant. It does not seem to be associated with nulliparity or age at menopause, and its relationship to long-term use of oral contraception is still being explored.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Microglandular hyperplasia\",\"e\":\"Microglandular hyperplasia (endocervical polyp) occurs secondary to oral contraceptives (progestogens) and must be distinguished from adenocarcinoma histologically.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sarcoma botryoides\",\"e\":\"This option describes a malignant sarcoma of the vagina or other hollow mucosal-lined structures. It most likely presents before 8 years of age.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"Endometrial biopsy of a woman reveals endometrial hyperplasia. They are referred to the gynaecology service for further management. Which one of the following neoplasms is most likely to be associated with endometrial hyperplasia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following neoplasms is most likely to be associated with endometrial hyperplasia?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Choriocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"This is a germ-cell tumour that most likely arises from a molar pregnancy. Symptoms mimic pregnancy and as the choriocarcinoma produces high levels of 5 hCG hyperemesis is common.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fibrothecoma\",\"e\":\"A fibrothecoma is a benign ovarian tumour. The thecoma component of the neoplasm gives the tumour a yellowish appearance because of the lipid content and can also produce oestrogen, which is responsible for the endometrial hyperplasia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Krukenberg tumour\",\"e\":\"This tumour is a subtype of metastatic carcinoma to the ovary, with the stomach and the colon being the most likely primary site.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mature cystic teratoma\",\"e\":\"These lumours are germ-cell Lumours that are the most common tumour of the ovary: They contain multiple germ-cellayers.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour\",\"e\":\"This is a rare sex cord stromal tumour that presents with masculinisation due to the testosterone it produces.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man is diagnosed with a germ-cell tumour of the testis. Orchidectomy is arranged for the patient with a curative intent. He gets a good oncological outcome and subsequently has a prosthesis placed. Which one of the following histological subtypes is most likely to be found on histopathological examination of the involved testis after orchidectomy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following histological subtypes is most likely to be found on histopathological examination of the involved testis after orchidectomy?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Choriocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Embryonal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Embryonal carcinomas are responsible for 10-25% of testicular germ-cell tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Seminoma\",\"e\":\"Seminoma is the commonest germ-cell tumour of the testes accounting for 40% of cases.\\n\\nChoriocarcinomas are rare in the testes accounting for 1% of all germ-cell tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Teratocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Teratocarcinomas cause around 25% of testicular tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Teratoma\",\"e\":\"Teratomas are responsible for around 5% of testicular germ-cell tumours:\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old smoker presents with painless haematuria and weight loss. He is diagnosed with bladder cell carcinoma.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct with regard to this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct with regard to this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Squamous-cell carcinoma is the most common type in the UK\",\"e\":\"Transitional-cell carcinomas account for 90-95% of all bladder cancers in the UK.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Schistosomiasis is associated with adenocarcinoma of the bladder\",\"e\":\"Schistosomiasis is related to squamous-cell carcinoma of the bladder not adenocarcinoma of the bladder.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"There is a higher incidence in women\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The most common chromosomal abnormality is the deletion of chromosome 9\",\"e\":\"Deletion of chromosome 9 is the most common chromosomal abnormality, along with alterations in the tumour suppressor genes p53 and Rb.\\n\\nThere is a three-times higher incidence in men compared with women.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The most common chromosomal abnormality is the deletion of chromosome 19\",\"e\":\"It is the deletion of chromosome 9, not 19, that is the most common chromosomal abnormality.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 49-year-old man presents with haematuria, flank pain and an abdominal mass. CT imaging demonstrates a mass arising from the right kidney and the patient is subsequently discussed at the urology MDT. The decision is made to biopsy the lesion before further treatment. Which one of the following is correct in renal cell carcinoma?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct in renal cell carcinoma?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is more common in women\",\"e\":\"There is a 2:1 ratio towards men.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It often presents with a right-sided varicocele\",\"e\":\"A left-sided renal tumour may present with a left varicocele as the left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein, which may be occluded by the tumour.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is a feature of von Recklinghausen's disease\",\"e\":\"Renal cell carcinoma is a feature of von Hippel-Lindau syndrome rather than von Recklinghausen's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It accounts for 5% of all renal tumours\",\"e\":\"Approximately 90% of renal tumours are renal cell carcinomas.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It presents with metastasis in 30% of all cases\",\"e\":\"Approximately 30% of patients have metastasis at presentation.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A 9-year-old child presents with a large abdomen, abdominal pain, fever and haematuria. A diagnosis of a Wilms' tumour is made and it is arranged that the patient undergo surgical resection.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is associated with nephroblastoma (Wilms' tumour)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is associated with nephroblastoma (Wilms' tumour)?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Accounts for 20% of all genitourinary tumours in the under-15 age group\",\"e\":\"Wilms' tumour is rare (1 in 10 000) but accounts for 80% of all genitourinary (GU) tumours in children under the age of 15\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It has a very poor prognosis\",\"e\":\"The survival rate is generally good, ranging from 55 to 97% depending upon the tumour stage and histology\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is more common in men\",\"e\":\"Men and women are equally affected, 20% are familial.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is bilateral in 50% of cases\",\"e\":\"Only 5% of cases are bilateral.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is associated with the mutation or deletion of both copies of the WT/ tumour suppressor gene on chromosome 11p\",\"e\":\"The mutation or deletion of the WTI tumour suppressor gene is responsible for nephroblastomas in more than 20% of cases\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old woman comes to you asking about her risk of developing bladder cancer given that her father, aged 55, has just been diagnosed with the disease. You counsel her on risk factors and give her a leaflet on the presenting features of urological malignancies. Which one of the following is a risk factor for bladder cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a risk factor for bladder cancer?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anastrozole\",\"e\":\"This is an aromatase inhibitor given to post-menopausal women as a treatment for hormone receptor-positive breast cancer. It has not been linked to an increased risk of bladder cancer:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Benzidine\",\"e\":\"Benzidine is an industrial carcinogen linked with bladder cancer. Smoking is the leading cause of bladder cancer. Cyclophosphamide has been linked with an increased risk of developing bladder cancer, and more specifically an increased rate of developing muscle- invasive bladder cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Co-proxamol\",\"e\":\"Co-proxamol has not been linked to an increased risk of bladder cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Coffee drinking\",\"e\":\"Coffee has been implicated as a risk factor, but the link is as yet unproven.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tea drinking\",\"e\":\"There is no link between tea drinking and bladder cancer. 1401\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 73-year-old man presents with haematuria, nocturia and urinary frequency, all suggestive of prostatic cancer. On examination the diagnosis is confirmed when a hard craggy prostate is palpated on DRE\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"A 73-year-old man presents with haematuria, nocturia and urinary frequency, all suggestive of prostatic cancer. On examination the diagnosis is confirmed when a hard craggy prostate is palpated on DRE\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding this diagnosis?\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is the most common cause of death from cancer in men\",\"e\":\"Prostate cancer is the most common cancer in middle-aged men, but is second to lung cancer as a cause of death. Post-mortem studies show that microscopic foci of prostate cancer are present in up to 80% of men aged 80, however risks of prostate cancer as a cause of death is about 3% - men are more likely to die 'with' the disease than 'of' the disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is most commonly found in the transitional zone of the prostate\",\"e\":\"It is most commonly found in the peripheral zone rather than the transitional zone of the prostate; only 20% develop in the transitional zone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is most commonly found in the peripheral zone of the prostate\",\"e\":\"The peripheral zone is responsible for 70% of prostate carcinomas.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is most commonly found in the central zone of the prostate\",\"e\":\"Prostate carcinoma is not found commonly in the central zone of the prostate; less than 10% of prostate carcinomas are found on the central zone.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Microscopic foci of prostate cancer are present in <10% of men in their fifties\",\"e\":\"Approximately 30% of men in their fifties have microscopic foci of prostate carcinoma.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man with renal colic is being treated. Imaging reveals a horseshoe kidney. What causes horseshoe kidney?\",\"lead\":\"What causes horseshoe kidney?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The kidneys fuse at the inferior pole before ascending\",\"e\":\"Embryologically horseshoe kidney results from the kidneys fusing at the inferior (medial) pole having their ascent arrested by the presence of the isthmus on the inferior mesenteric artery. Horseshoe kidney is more common in men. The anatomical location and blood supply is very variable. They are more prone to infection and calculi formation due to relative upper- tract stasis. They are also more prone to trauma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The kidneys fuse at the middle pole before ascending\",\"e\":\"The kidneys do not fuse at the middle pole rather they fuse at the inferior pole.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The kidneys fuse at the middle pole and fail to ascend\",\"e\":\"Horseshoe kidneys fuse at the inferior pole and have their ascent arrested by the isthmus and inferior mesenteric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The kidneys fuse at the superior pole and fail to ascend\",\"e\":\"Horseshow kidney fuse at the inferior pole and have their ascent arrested by the inferior mesenteric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The kidneys fuse at the superior pole before ascending\",\"e\":\"The kidneys fuse at the inferior pole rather than the superior pole.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old patient presents with renal colic, and renal calculi are seen on imaging. Testing the urine reveals it is acidic.\\n\\nWhat type of stones are most likely to form in this patient's urine?\",\"lead\":\"What type of stones are most likely to form in this patient's urine?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"15. Calcium phosphate\",\"e\":\"Treating calcium phosphate stones involves decreasing calcium intake. They are not related to acidic urine.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cystine calculi\",\"e\":\"Cystine and urate stones form in acidic urine and urine alkalisation forms part of the treatment of these types of stones. Cystine stones are poorly radiopaque on X-ray but can usually be seen on CT.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oxalate calculi\",\"e\":\"Oxalate stones are related to oxalate intake and treatment should decrease oxalate intake (chocolate, tea, rhubarb, spinach).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Staghorn calculi\",\"e\":\"Staghorn calculi (also known as 'struvite') are composed of magnesium, ammonium and phosphate. They form when urine has a pH > 7. They are associated with Proteus spp. and other Gram-negative organisms that contain the enzyme urease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Struvite calculi\",\"e\":\"Struvite calculi is another name for staghorn calculi.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A 79-year-old man presents with difficulty in passing urine and has a craggy prostate on DRE. The diagnosis is confirmed with a biopsy that demonstrates prostatic adenocarcinoma. From which histological structure is his disease most likely to have originated?\",\"lead\":\"From which histological structure is his disease most likely to have originated?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"aX\\\" Anterior fibromuscular zone\",\"e\":\"The anterior fibromuscular zone contains no glandular tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Central zone\",\"e\":\"The central zone is responsible for less than 5% of prostate carcinomas.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Peripheral zone\",\"e\":\"The peripheral zone of the prostate contains around 75% of all the glandular tissue. Accordingly. 70-80% of all prostate cancers originate here.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prostatic urethra\",\"e\":\"Prostate carcinoma does not originate from the prostatic urethra.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transitional zone\",\"e\":\"Only 10-20% of carcinomas arise from the transitional zone.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man presents with difficulty of micturition. Prostate-specific antigen levels are significantly raised and, on digital rectal examination, he is noted to have a hard, craggy prostate gland. In which histological portion of the prostate gland is adenocarcinoma most likely to develop?\",\"lead\":\"In which histological portion of the prostate gland is adenocarcinoma most likely to develop?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior fibromuscular zone\",\"e\":\"The anterior fibromuscular zone contains no glandular tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AAL Central zone\",\"e\":\"The central zone is responsible for less than 5% of prostate carcinomas.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Peripheral zone\",\"e\":\"The peripheral zone of the prostate contains around 75% of all the glandular tissue. Accordingly, 70-80% of all prostate cancers originate here.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transitional zone\",\"e\":\"Only 10-20% of carcinomas arise from the transitional zone.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urogenital sinus\",\"e\":\"The urogenital sinus is the embryological precursor of the gland.\\n\\n1EAI\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old man presents with abnormal penile curvature, erectile dysfunction and pain. He is diagnosed with Peyronie's disease.\\n\\nIn which penile structure is the pathology?\",\"lead\":\"In which penile structure is the pathology?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Corpora cavernosa\",\"e\":\"The corpora cavernosa is the sponge-like tissue in the penis that fills with blood during an erection. It is not affected by Peyronie's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Corpus spongiosum\",\"e\":\"The corpus spongiosum is the spongey tissue that surrounds the urethra in the penis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Penile bulb\",\"e\":\"The penile bulb has no role in Peyronie's disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Perineal raphe\",\"e\":\"This is the visible ridge of tissue that extends from the anus through the perineum and scrotum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tunica albuginea\",\"e\":\"Peyronie's disease is a chronic inflammation of the tunica albuginea, which surrounds the corpora cavernosa of the penis. With time, fibrous tissue develops which causes the abnormal curvature and the other symptoms as described.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"A 73-year-old man presents with painless haematuria and weight loss. He undergoes diagnostic cystoscopy and is diagnosed with transitional-cell carcinoma of the bladder.\\n\\nWhich occupational carcinogen increases the risk of this disease?\",\"lead\":\"Which occupational carcinogen increases the risk of this disease?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aluminium\",\"e\":\"Aluminium exposure is not associated with TCC\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Asbestos\",\"e\":\"Asbestos increases the risk of mesothelioma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glyphosate\",\"e\":\"Glyphosate has recently been linked to increases in the risk of some haematological cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Coal dust\",\"e\":\"Coal dust increases the likelihood of lung carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Benzene\",\"e\":\"People working in the rubber and dye industries, where benzene is used, are known to have a higner incidence of bladder cancer.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old retired man with bladder transitional-cell carcinoma is admitted for a TURBT.\\n\\nWhat previous occupation might be expected that has most likely exposed this patient to a carcinogen?\",\"lead\":\"What previous occupation might be expected that has most likely exposed this patient to a carcinogen?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Painter\",\"e\":\"Painting is linked to benzopyrene exposure and an increased risk of lung cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Peanut farmer\",\"e\":\"Peanut farming causes exposure to aflatoxin and increases the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Printer\",\"e\":\"Printing is linked to benzopyrene exposure and increases the risk of lung carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Dye worker\",\"e\":\"Beta-naphthylamine exposure is related to transitional-cell carcinoma, and this is used in the dye industry\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Miner\",\"e\":\"Mining is linked to chromium, asbestos, arsenic and nickel exposure, and increases the risk of lung cancer.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"A 43-year-old woman who has had recurrent urinary tract infections (UTIs) presents with loin pain. A CT scan of kidneys, ureters and bladder (KUB) shows a staghorn calculus. Which of the following is the most likely type of calculus?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most likely type of calculus?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"P Calcium\",\"e\":\"Calcium stones are associated with excess calcium absorption and bone disorders.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Struvite\",\"e\":\"Struvite stones are radio-opaque and are associated with infections with urea-splitting organisms eg Proteus. They are also more commonly responsible for staghorn calculi\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oxalate\",\"e\":\"Oxalate stones are related to oxalate intake and treatment should decrease oxalate intake, (chocolate, tea, rhubarb, spinach).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urate\",\"e\":\"Urate stones are radiolucent and are associated with high levels of uric acid such as gout.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Xanthine\",\"e\":\"Xanthine stones are radiolucent and associated with errors of metabolism.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old man was admitted to the Accident and Emergency department with exacerbation of his chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. An arterial blood gas on room air showed respiratory acidosis. The patient's plasma level of potassium will be expected to do which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The patient's plasma level of potassium will be expected to do which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fall, as the filtered load of potassium to the tubules falls in acidosis\",\"e\":\"The filtered load of potassium in the tubules will rise in acidosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Rise, as tubular secretion of potassium is inversely coupled to acid secretion\",\"e\":\"Increased excretion of hydrogen ions during metabolic compensation for respiratory acidosis will result in decreased secretion of potassium ions (or increased retention). This would potentially cause a rise in serum potassium.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Rise, as acid and potassium excretion are coupled\",\"e\":\"Acid and potassium are inversely related.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Rise, as acidosis is a stimulus to renin secretion by the juxtaglomerular apparatus\",\"e\":\"Renin is stimulated by hypotension, hyponatraemia and sympathetic nervous stimulation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rise, as acidosis increases the affinity of the aldosterone receptor for aldosterone\",\"e\":\"Increased acidosis and plasma potassium will increase aldosterone production, but it works by excreting potassium into the renal tubules.\\n\\n1734\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old student has severe diarrhoea following a holiday to the Far East. He loses 4.0 litres of intestinal fluid over a 6-h period, but is unable to drink any fluids. His urine output during the 6 his only 100 ml. Which one of the following physiological changes is most likely to be found in this patient at the end of the 6 h period as compared with the hour before the onset of diarrhoea?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following physiological changes is most likely to be found in this patient at the end of the 6 h period as compared with the hour before the onset of diarrhoea?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased plasma angiotensin II levels\",\"e\":\"Plasma angiotensin I levels will be increased as the patient is likely hypovolaemic and hypotensive.\\n\\nUrine osmolarity will be increased as the kidneys will be reabsorbing as much water as possible making the urine more concentrated.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased urine osmolarity\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased extracellular fluid volume\",\"e\":\"The extracellular fluid volume will be decreased due to dehydration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased glomerular filtration rate\",\"e\":\"The glomerular filtration rate (GFR) will be decreased due to hypotension.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased plasma aldosterone levels\",\"e\":\"Plasma aldosterone levels will be increased to increase water retention.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"An 82-year-old man falls and fractures his left hip. He is found on the floor the next morning. He undergoes fixation with a dynamic hip screw. The following day he develops a low urine output and peripheral oedema with bibasal lung crepitations are noted on chest auscultation. Which one of the following is correct in acute renal tubular dysfunction?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct in acute renal tubular dysfunction?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Following surgery, haemofiltration is frequently required\",\"e\":\"The need for haemofiltration is rare.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Following surgery, acute renal tubular dysfunction is usually due to cortical ischaemia\",\"e\":\"It is due to ischaemia of the medulla not the cortex.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It can be treated with osmotic diuretics\",\"e\":\"It can be initially treated with osmotic diuretics, but the majority of cases will resolve with supportive treatment and careful fluid balance monitoring.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is commonly due to renal vein thrombosis\",\"e\":\"Renal vein thrombosis is a rare cause of renal tubular acidosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It produces a high urine osmolality (> 350 mOsml/kg)\",\"e\":\"The urine has a low urine osmolality (< 150 mOsmol/kg).\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man is admitted following a road traffic accident (RTA) in which he sustains an open tibial fracture with heavy blood loss. He develops low blood pressure, is catheterised and resuscitated for low urine output\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in acute renal failure?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct in acute renal failure?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arterial p(COz) is increased\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Blood pH is increased\",\"e\":\"Acute renal failure causes metabolic acidosis, therefore the pH will be decreased.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Plasma bicarbonate is increased\",\"e\":\"Plasma bicarbonate will be decreased as it will be used as a buffer for the acidosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Potassium levels are decreased\",\"e\":\"Potassium levels will be increased in acute renal failure. The biochemical features of acute renal failure include a hyperkalaemia, hyponatraemia and elevated urea and creatinine. Phosphate is elevated and calcium lowered\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Respiratory rate is increased\",\"e\":\"The respiratory rate will be increased to attempt to blow off more CO_and correct the acidosis.\\n\\nThe patient will have a metabolic acidosis so arterial p(CO) will be decreased as the respiratory system compensates for the metabolic acidosis.\"}]},{\"t\":27,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"You have been referred a 29-year-old man who wished to start a family with his partner. Over the last 12 months he has been unable to maintain an erection.\\n\\nWhich one of these is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"High testosterone\",\"e\":\"Low testosterone, rather than high testosterone can cause impotence.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Streptococcal pneumonia\",\"e\":\"Pneumonia is unlikely to cause long-term impotence.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Omeprazole treatment\",\"e\":\"While proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) like omeprazole can cause impotence, diabetes is the more common cause given here.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Physiological due to age\",\"e\":\"Erectile dysfunction is more common as men age, however at 29 this patient is probably too young.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Diabetes\",\"e\":\"Diabetes can cause impotence by causing impairments in blood vessels, nerves and muscle function. Vascular impairment is a key cause of ED. Often, the first sign of a metabolic or cardiovascular condition is impairment of penile artery flow in an otherwise healthy patient. Investigating cardiovascular compromise is imperative in younger men in which diagnosing treatable conditions such as diabetes can be crucial in avoiding adverse outcomes later on in life. The main causes of erectile dysfunction are: diabetes, vascular disease, drugs, pelvic surgery, pelvic or spinal injury, neurological disease and hormonal problems. There are also psychological causes:\"}]},{\"t\":28,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"Regarding Chagas' disease, which of the following statements is NOT correct?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding Chagas' disease, which of the following statements is NOT correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The causative organism is Trypanosoma brucei\",\"e\":\"Chagas' disease is caused by the parasite Trypanosoma cruzi, which is found in South America. The parasite Trypanosoma brucei (along with Trypanosoma gambiense) causes sleeping sickness and is found in Africa\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The organism destroys the ganglionic cells of the myenteric plexus\",\"e\":\"Trypanosoma cruzi causes destruction of ganglionic cells at the time of infection, but also contributes to their loss over time. This progression is why the disease has two phases, an acute phase, and a chronic phase. The symptoms of the acute phase are non-specific, and Chagas' disease may not be picked up at the time of infection. With ageing, it becomes clinically apparent as the number of ganglionic cells falls, which can lead to megacolon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It can cause symptoms similar to achalasia\",\"e\":\"When Chagas' disease affects the oesophagus, it can mimic achalasia, and is a cause of 'pseudo-achalasia.' Pseudo-achalasia is the term used to describe dilatation of the oesophagus secondary to narrowing of the distal oesophagus. True achalasia is caused by denervation of the distal oesophagus, which leads to impaired relaxation of the lower oesophageal sphincter and aperistalsis of the lower oesophagus. This situation in turn causes a dilated oesophagus. Clinically and radiologically pseudo-achalasia and achalasia are very similar. Treatment is by cardiomyotomy, preferably laparoscopic, although in unfit patients or in more mild cases the alternative treatment is endoscopic pneumatic dilatation or botulinum toxin injections.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It can mimic the presentation of malignancy\",\"e\":\"This infection affects multiple systems. In the manner in which it affects the gastrointestinal (Gl) tract, it can mimic the presentation of malignancy eg megacolon (presents with abdominal pain, distension, change in bowel habit) or pseudo-achalasia (dysphagia).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is associated with cardiomyopathy\",\"e\":\"Many patients with Chagas' disease will present with cardiac manifestations, and these include dilated cardiomyopathy, which in turn can lead to arrhythmias and sudden death.\"}]},{\"t\":28,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man presents with diffuse lymphadenopathy, and has been referred to the surgical team for an excision biopsy. The histology from a iymph node biopsy reveals Reed- Sternberg cells. The patient has which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The patient has which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hodgkin's lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Reed-Sternberg cells are characteristic of Hodgkin's lymphoma. They are large, multi- nucleated, abnormal lymphoid cells (mature B cells). They are never seen in non-Hodgkin lymphomas.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Folicle centre cell lymphoma.\",\"e\":\"This is a type of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. Non-Hodgkin's lymphomas can arise from both B cells and T cells (unlike Hodgkin's, which arises from B cells only).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Langerhans cell histiocytosis\",\"e\":\"Langerhans cell histiocytosis is a neoplastic condition of the Langerhans cells causing lymphadenopathy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Burkitt's lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Burkitt's lymphoma is an aggressive type of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma is the collective term used to describe lymphomas that are not characterised by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells ie Hodgkin's lymphoma, There are many types of lymphomas, but they are broadly categorised into Hodgkin's and non- Hodgkin's lymphoma. They can present in similar ways eg fatigue, night sweats, lymphadenopathy. Biopsy distinguishes the two categories. The incidence of non-Hodgkin's increases with age, whereas there appear to be two distinct age groups that are affected by Hodgkin's - young adults in their 20s and those over the age of 55.\"}]},{\"t\":28,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman is seen with long-standing right lower limb swelling and thickened skin. After further investigation you diagnose primary lymphoedema and commence therapy to improve the condition. Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathology for this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathology for this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Breast cancer\",\"e\":\"The treatment of breast cancer is a secondary cause of lymphoedema, following axillary node clearance, and so presents in the arm of the side that the clearance was performed.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Infection\",\"e\":\"Infection is a secondary cause of lymphoedema In particular, cellulitis can cause lymphoedema, and cause the presentation described in the scenario.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoplastic lymphatics\",\"e\":\"Primary lymphoedema is a rare entity and can be classified into Milroy's disease (hereditary lymphoedema type I) or Meige's disease (hereditary lymphoedema type II). It is more common in women and is caused by hypoplastic lymphatics.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Trauma\",\"e\":\"Lymphoedema can be caused by any process that damages the lymph chains, and trauma is an example of this. It is a secondary cause though.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Worms\",\"e\":\"Filariasis is an infectious cause of lymphoedema, and is caused by round worms: It can cause gross lymphoedema known as elephantiasis.\"}]},{\"t\":28,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man is involved in a road traffic accident and undergoes an emergency splenectomy Several months later, a sample of his blood is sent off to the lab. Which one of the following may be seen in the blood film of this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following may be seen in the blood film of this patient?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Howell-Jolly bodies\",\"e\":\"One of the functions of the spleen is the sequestration and phagocytosis of old or abnormal red cells. Therefore, in a post-splenectomy patient, red cell inclusion bodies may be present, producing target cells, Howell-Jolly bodies and sideroblasts. Howell-Jolly bodies are nuclear remnants that are removed by the spleen, and are so seen in the blood post splenectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Leucopenia\",\"e\":\"Leucopenia is typically seen in hypersplenism ie overactivity of the spleen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Neutrophilia\",\"e\":\"The circulating neutrophil count may go down post splenectomy, however there may be an increase in absolute neutrophil numbers.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Punctate basophilia\",\"e\":\"Punctate basophilia is seen in patients with lead poisoning, thalassaemia, and haemolytic anaemia due to pyrimidine-5-nucleotidase deficiency. It occurs when erythropoiesis is dysfunctional.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rouleaux formation\",\"e\":\"Rouleaux formation is the tendency for red cells to stack up like a pile of coins. The amount of rouleaux formation is determined by the concentration of protein in the plasma and is therefore seen in myeloma, in which blood viscosity is high.\"}]},{\"t\":28,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man presents after an incidental finding of a superior mediastinal mass. A computed tomography (CT) scan reveals enlargement of the thymus and a positron emission tomography (PET) scan shows some high uptake in the central portion of the giand\\n\\nWhat is the most appropriate next step?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate next step?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Midline sternotomy and thymectomy\",\"e\":\"This is the safest option and will allow both tissue diagnosis and potential complete surgical excision. An enlarged thymus is an abnormal finding in anyone of this age. Tumours of the thymus gland are varied and can include both benign and malignant atiologies. A positive PET scan indicates malignant disease - irrespective of this, excision biopsy should be offered if the patient is fit enough.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN VATS thymectomy\",\"e\":\"This could be attempted but thymomas with local invasion into the innominate vein, lung, pericardium or even the aorta have the potential for catastrophic bleeding. When malignancy is not suspected and, in experienced hands, video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) may be offered.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"CT-guided biopsy\",\"e\":\"The thymus sits behind the sternum and is adjacent to major vessels, so the technique is not easy, and excision biopsy is recommended.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ultrasound-guided biopsy\",\"e\":\"The thymus sits behind the sternum and is adjacent to major vessels, so the technique is not easy, and excision biopsy is recommended.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Screening for myasthenia gravis (MG)\",\"e\":\"Although MG is linked to thymic hyperplasia and treatment with thymectomy can improve symptoms, this patient has evidence from the PET scan of malignancy, and any further delay is unnecessary.\"}]},{\"t\":28,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man is seen for a biopsy of an enlarged rubbery lymph node in his neck. A diagnosis of Hodgkin's lymphoma is made. Which of the below are probable risk factors for the disease?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the below are probable risk factors for the disease?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"5. HIV\",\"e\":\"Immune deficiency is a clear risk factor for Hodgkin's lymphoma, which was first described by Thomas Hodgkin in 1832.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Epstein-Barr virus\",\"e\":\"Up to 45% of cases of Hodgkin's lymphoma are linked to Epstein-Barr virus (EBV). Infectious mononucleosis is a common manifestation of EBV and seems to precede diagnosis of Hodgkin's lymphoma by about 3-4 years.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Solid organ transplant recipient\",\"e\":\"Patients who have received a solid organ transplant are about two to four times more likely to develop Hodgkin's lymphoma and their reduced resistance to the Epstein-Barr virus seems to be the cause.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduced exposure to childhood infections\",\"e\":\"Hodgkin's lymphoma is one of the few diseases that has a higher prevalence in more affluent people. It is believed that this could be due to reduced exposure to common infections in childhood. Being an only child also represents a risk factor for seemingly similar reasons.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"All of the above\",\"e\":\"All of the above are risk factors for Hodgkin's lymphoma.\"}]},{\"t\":28,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 12-year-old boy presents with extensive inguinal lymphadenopathy due to infection in his thigh. He is commenced on oral antibiotics and both the thigh and lymphadenopathy improve.\\n\\nWhat describes the flow of lymphatics best?\",\"lead\":\"What describes the flow of lymphatics best?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It flows from the peripheral body tissues centrally, and there it enters the venous circulation at multiple points\",\"e\":\"Lymphatics collect interstitial fluids, including lymph and emulsified fats, from the peripheries back towards the neck, where it enters the venous circulation. The largest point of entry is on the left via the thoracic duct into the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It flows peripherally to centrally, entering the venous circulation at a single point\",\"e\":\"Although the thoracic duct on the left is the larger of the entry points into the venous circulation, a similar duct enters on the right at the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"There is no significant flow of lymph and it is locally absorbed into the blood vessels\",\"e\":\"Lymph has a unidirectional flow back towards the neck and is a crucial part of the body's function.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It flows peripherally to centrally, entering both the venous and the arterial circulations at multiple points\",\"e\":\"The lymphatics enter the venous circulation and can do so due to the low pressure. A gradient between lymph and artery could not and does not exist.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It flows centrally to peripherally to supply organs\",\"e\":\"Lymphatics have a unidirectional flow, but this is centrally back towards the base of the neck at the confluence of the major veins.\"}]},{\"t\":28,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old boy is seen by the trauma team after falling off a trampoline. Abdominal tenderness leads to a computed tomography (CT) scan of his abdomen and a 3 cm splenic laceration is revealed, with no vascular involvement and no significant haemoperitoneum. He is not currentiy haemodynamically compromised and no other injury is found\\n\\nHow should this be managed?\",\"lead\":\"How should this be managed?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Diagnostic laparotomy\",\"e\":\"This would add little to the management and confuse any period of observation. As the injury is moderate at worst, it can be managed conservatively in the first instance.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Laparoscopic splenectomy\",\"e\":\"Performing laparoscopy in trauma is dangerous due to both the inability to control major bleeding, often the indication for surgery in the first place, and to the reduction in venous return caused by CO_ insufflation. There is also no indication for splenectomy because smaller injuries can be managed conservatively.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Open splenectomy\",\"e\":\"Historically, splenic injuries were treated in this way, but with greater understanding of both the morbidity of splenectomies and the ability to manage these smaller injuries conservatively, care has moved towards less aggressive treatments.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Observation\",\"e\":\"Smaller injuries without evidence of significant active bleeding, as in this case, can be safely managed conservatively.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Angiography and embolisation\",\"e\":\"This treatment would be appropriate for injuries with on-going bleeding or a localised blush of contrast on a CT scan.\\n\\nSplenic injuries are common and can be graded based on the size of the laceration, haematoma or involvement of the hilum. Large, actively bleeding spleens with evidence of devascularisation should be managed surgically, but smaller injuries (grade 1-3), such as the one here, can be managed conservatively in most cases. Splenectomies carry a significant morbidity due to the susceptibility to encapsulated bacteria.\\n\\n• Grade 1 = small (haematoma < 10% spleen volume, < 1-cm laceration) • Grade 2 = moderate (haematoma < 10-50% spleen volume, < 5-cm laceration) • Grade 3 = large > 50% haematoma, > 5 cm or expanding • Grade 4 = large with partial devascularisation (> 25%) • Grade 5 = complete devascularisation of spleen.\\n\\n5840\"}]},{\"t\":28,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A normally fit and well, 22-year-old, white woman is seen with left upper quadrant tenderness. She has had weight loss and night sweats for several months. An enlarged spleen is found\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Splenomegaly in someone of this age is most likely due to lymphoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Trauma\",\"e\":\"There is nothing in the history to suggest trauma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Abscess\",\"e\":\"Although this differential is quite high on the list, splenic abscesses are relatively rare and the most likely diagnosis would be lymphoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Rheumatoid arthritis (RA)\",\"e\":\"Splenomegaly can occur in RA and Felty syndrome (which includes low white cell count to complete the triad), It is not often tender and, in the absence of other symptoms, it is not likely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sarcoidosis\",\"e\":\"Splenomegaly occurs in around a quarter of patients with sarcoidosis, although this often happens later in the disease and in older patients.\"}]},{\"t\":28,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man known to have reflux disease presents with worsening dysphagia, and biopsy of his oesophagus reveals malignancy. He is discussed at the upper GI MDT and the decision is made to proceed to oesophagectamy.\\n\\nWhat is the drainage of the oesophagus?\",\"lead\":\"What is the drainage of the oesophagus?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Upper third to deep cervical lymph nodes, middle third to pre- and para-aortic lymph nodes, lower third to coeliac plexus\",\"e\":\"The lymphatic drainage of the oesophagus is important surgically and is divided roughly into thirds. Lymph drains to the cervical neck lymph nodes, nodes around the aorta and abdominal lymph nodes in the coeliac plexus. Lymphatic drainage follows the blood supply.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Upper half to pre- and para-aortic lymph nodes and lower half to coeliac plexus nodes\",\"e\":\"Although the lymphatics do drain to these two locations, the middle third drains to the pre- and para-aortic nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Upper half to cervical lymph nodes and lower half to pre- and para-aortic lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"Although the lymphatics do drain to these two locations, the lower third follows the blood supply into the coeliac plexus nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Upper two-thirds to pre- and para-aortic lymph nodes and lower third to coeliac plexus nodes\",\"e\":\"Although the lymphatics do drain to these two locations, the upper third travels to the cervical lymph nodes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Upper third to cervical lymph nodes and lower two-thirds to coeliac plexus nodes\",\"e\":\"Although the lymphatics of the osophagus do travel to these two locations, the middle third drains to the lymph nodes around the aorta.\"}]},{\"t\":29,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 15-year-old boy presents to the Orthopaedic clinic complaining of pain in his right leg for 2 months. He also had a low-grade fever. X-ray reveals a mass in the diaphyseal region of the femur with overlying cortical erosion and soft-tissue extension. A biopsy is performed and the lesion is seen on microscopy to be composed of numerous small, round, blue cells.\\n\\nWhat is the most probable diagnosis in this case?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most probable diagnosis in this case?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chondroblastoma\",\"e\":\"Chondroblastoma is a benign tumour of the bone that arises in the epiphyses or apophyses. It also affects young people. Chondroblasts are seen in biopsy samples.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ewing's sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Small round blue cell tumours are a group of tumours characterised by the presence of undifferentiated cells that stain blue at histology. In Ewing's sarcoma, plain X-rays do not usually reveal the full extent of bone involvement. Computed tomography scan and magnetic resonance imaging are better for defining the extent of disease and can help guide treatment. Many other benign and malignant tumours can have identical appearances, so diagnosis is made by biopsy. Ewing's sarcoma is commonly seen at this age. It is malignant. Most tumours develop in the extremities, but any bone can be involved. Ewing's tumour tends to be extensive, sometimes involving the entire bone shaft. Pain and swelling are the most common symptoms: Lytic destruction is the most common X-ray finding, but multiple layers of subperiosteal reactive new bone formation may produce an 'onion skin' appearance.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medulloblastoma\",\"e\":\"This is also a 'small round blue cell tumour, but occurs in the brain.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neuroblastoma\",\"e\":\"Neuroblastoma is a rare malignant tumour of nerve cells. It is also a 'small blue round tumour. It usually presents as a mass in the abdomen, as the tumour typically arises from the adrenal glands, although it can also arise as lesions on the bones. It usually affects children less than 5 years of age.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Osteoblastoma\",\"e\":\"Osteoblastoma is a rare benign tumour of the bones. It usually affects young adults in their 20s or 30s.\"}]},{\"t\":29,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman can feel a firm, non-tender, rounded subcutaneous mass, 1 cm in diameter, along the extensor surface of the forearm. Microscopically, the nodule shows an area of central necrosis surrounded by palisading epithelioid cells.\\n\\nWhich one of the following laboratory test findings is most likely to be positive in this woman?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following laboratory test findings is most likely to be positive in this woman?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acereholine receptor antibody\",\"e\":\"Patients with myasthenia gravis are positive for acetylcholine receptor antibody.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anti-Jo-1 (antihistidyl-tRNA synthetase)\",\"e\":\"This is a marker of antisynthetase syndrome, antisynthetase syndrome is a rare autoimmune condition that affects the muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Elevated antinuclear antibody\",\"e\":\"Testing for antinuclear antibodies is a non-specific way of looking for disease. Elevated levels may be present in individuals without disease. Its interpretation has to be undertaken in the context of the clinical picture and other tests.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Monoclonal gammopathy\",\"e\":\"This is the presence of monoclonal protein, or M protein, in the blood. In itself, it is not pathological, but it may be a sign of possible progression to a range of haematological malignancies including multiple myeloma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Positive rheumatoid factor\",\"e\":\"This is a rheumatoid nodule, a peculiar form of granulomatous inflammation that can be seen in the soft tissues of people with rheumatoid arthritis. Rheumatoid nodules develop in about 30% of patients with rheumatoid arthritis. They consist of a central necrotic area surrounded by palisaded histiocytic macrophages, all enveloped by lymphocytes, plasma cells and fibroblasts. Nodules and vasculitis can also develop in many of the visceral organs.\"}]},{\"t\":29,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"You review a 39-year-old sportsman who complains of knee pain. Arthroscopy reveals damage to the cartilage. Which one of the following stems best describes a property of hyaline cartilage?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following stems best describes a property of hyaline cartilage?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It has a blood supply from small arterioles\",\"e\":\"Hyaline cartilage is avascular, and relies on diffusion from synovial fluid for nutrients.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is rich in type 1 collagen\",\"e\":\"It is rich in type I! collagen and forms a meshwork containing proteoglycan molecules that retain water.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Chondrocytes secrete collagen only\",\"e\":\"Chondrocytes secrete proteoglycans as well as collagen. They are embedded in the cartilage and migrate to the joint surface along with the matrix that they produce.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is avascular\",\"e\":\"Hyaline cartilage forms the articular surface and is avascular, relying on diffusion from synovial fluid for nutrients\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pressure from normal joint loading accelerates damage to cartilage\",\"e\":\"Intermittent pressure from joint loading is essential to maintain normal cartilage function.\"}]},{\"t\":29,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"You are undertaking nerve studies on a patient with possible carpal tunnel syndrome. During the contraction of the muscle the electrical impulses are quantified.\\n\\nWhich of the following statements best describes the action potential of skeletal muscle?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements best describes the action potential of skeletal muscle?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Has a prolonged plateau phase\",\"e\":\"There is no plateau phase in the action potential of skeletal muscle. A plateau phase is seen in the action potential of cardiac muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Spread outwards to all parts of the muscle via the T tubes\",\"e\":\"The action potential of the skeletal muscle spreads out from the motor end plate, through the T tube system, this causes the mobilization of Ca?+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum to the cytoplasm.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Causes immediate uptake of Ca into the sarcoplasmic reticulum\",\"e\":\"The mobilization of Ca'+ is from the sarcoplasmic reticulum into the cytoplasm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is longer than the action potential of cardiac muscle\",\"e\":\"The action potential of cardiac muscle is longer than that of the skeletal muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is not essential for contraction\",\"e\":\"The action potential of skeletal muscle is essential for contraction.\\n\\n1152\"}]},{\"t\":29,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 79-year-old man complains of persistent back pain for several months. It is unrelated to physical activity. He reports some weight loss during this time. Laboratory findings include:\\n\\nNormal value Investigation Result\\n\\n6.7 × 10/1 4-11 × 109/1 White blood cell count (WCC)\\n\\n11.2 g/di 13.5-17.5 g/dl Haemoglobin (Hb)\\n\\nHaematocrit 38.8-50% 33.3%\\n\\n76-98 fl Mean corpuscular volume (MCV) 88 fl\\n\\n89 × 109/1 150-400 × 109/1 Platelet count\\n\\nHe has a peripheral leucoerythroblastic picture. Serum biochemistry profile is normal. A CT scan of the spine reveals scattered bright lesions of varying sizes in the vertebral bodies.\\n\\nWhich one of the following additional laboratory test findings is he most likely to have?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following additional laboratory test findings is he most likely to have?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Blood culture positive for Neisseria gonorrhoeae\",\"e\":\"The musculoskeletal manifestation of Neisseria gonorrhoeae is arthritic in nature (gonococcal arthritis), and gives symptoms in the joints.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Serum calcium of 1.37 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"The computed tomography (CT) findings are in keeping with bony metastases, which are a cause of hypercalcaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Serum prostate-specific antigen of 35 ng/ml\",\"e\":\"A prostatic adenocarcinoma should be the first guess (particularly in a man) with osteoblastic (bone-forming) tumour metastases. Extensive metastases can act in a myelophthisic manner that leads to peripheral blood leukoerythroblastosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A normal serum parathyroid hormone level\",\"e\":\"In the presence of bone metastases, calcium ions move from the serum into the metastatic bone cells, as they are blastic cells. This fall in serum calcium stimulates the parathyroid glands to release more parathyroid hormone (PTH), which plays an important role in bone and calcium homeostasis Its general purpose is to release calcium ions from the bone into the serum, so that it can be used in other processes in the body\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Positive serology for Borrelia burgdorferi\",\"e\":\"Borrelia burgdorferi is a bacterial infection that causes Lyme disease. Lyme disease can affect the knees (Lyme arthritis). 1503\"}]},{\"t\":29,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"You are examining a neonate's hips to check for developmental dysplasia of the hip. Which tests are routinely used to test for dislocatable and dislocated hips?\",\"lead\":\"Which tests are routinely used to test for dislocatable and dislocated hips?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Barlows' and Ortolani's tests both test for dislocated hips\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option; the two tests are distinct.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Barlows' and Ortolani's tests both test for dislocatable hips\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option; the two tests are distinct.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Barlows' test checks if the hip is dislocated, Ortolani's test checks if it is dislocatable\",\"e\":\"This option has the tests the wrong way round - Barlow's tests is a hip that can be dislocated, and Ortolani's test is to assess if the hip has already been dislocated.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Barlows' test checks if the hip is dislocatable, Ortolani's test checks if it is dislocated and relocatable\",\"e\":\"This is the correct option. Ortolani's test checks for dislocated hips that are relocatable. The femoral head is felt returning into the acetabulum. The hips and knees are flexed, and the hips slowly abducted, relocation occurs as full abduction is approached, if the hip is irreducible abduction is restricted.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Barlows' and Ortolani's tests are both only applicable if the hip is dislocated\",\"e\":\"This is not correct, as Barlow's test is checking for a hip that may be dislocated. The pelvis is fixed and backwards pressure applied to attempt to sublux/dislocate the hip. If the hip dislocates/subluxes then relocation is attempted with forward pressure or abduction of the hip.\"}]},{\"t\":29,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 6-month-old boy is seen by the orthopaedic team with a clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). It is a relatively common congenital deformity of the foot. What does this consist of?\",\"lead\":\"What does this consist of?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inversion, adduction of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot and plantarflexion\",\"e\":\"Clubfoot can be unilateral or bilateral and affects boys and girls equally. Treatment now consists of a series of plaster casts to slowly manipulate the foot back into the correct position (Ponsetti's method). It is linked to prune-belly syndrome, arthrogryposis and myelomeningocele.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inversion, adduction of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot, dorsiflexion\",\"e\":\"The foot is not dorsiflexed, but rather it is plantarflexed with a pointed toe position.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inversion, abduction of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot and plantarflexion\",\"e\":\"The foot is not abducted.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inversion, abduction of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot and dorsiflexion\",\"e\":\"The foot is neither dorsiflexed nor abducted.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Eversion, abduction of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot and plantarflexion\",\"e\":\"The foot is not abducted.\\n\\n3807\"}]},{\"t\":29,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 15-year-old student is seen in an orthopaedic clinic after an X-ray of his hip revealed a pattern of bone destruction and multiple concentric rings of new bony formation in his proximal femur What is the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"A 15-year-old student is seen in an orthopaedic clinic after an X-ray of his hip revealed a pattern of bone destruction and multiple concentric rings of new bony formation in his proximal femur What is the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ewing's sarcoma\",\"e\":\"This classic radiological finding is most commonly seen in Ewing's sarcoma, a malignant round-cell tumour of the long bones. It carries with it a relatively poor prognosis. If metastatic disease is present, 80% of patients will not make it to 5 years before death.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Osteosarcoma\",\"e\":\"Although osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignancy of bones, the radiological appearance of bone destruction and new bone formation produces the sunray sign, not the onion-ring sign.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Healing stress fracture.\",\"e\":\"The radiological features are not consistent with a healing fracture and stress fractures in this location of the femur are relatively rare.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Chrondrosarcoma\",\"e\":\"These tumours, which can emerge from pre-existing chrondromas, have a popcorn appearance on imaging. Treatment is by excision because they are unresponsive to chemotherapy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Malignant fibrous histiocytoma\",\"e\":\"This is an exceptionally rare form of bone cancer and more usually presents in middle age.\"}]},{\"t\":29,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"An 8-year-old boy is seen with a fracture of his distal radius. It is classified by the radiologist as a Salter-Harris type Ill fracture. What does this mean?\",\"lead\":\"What does this mean?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fracture line straight through the epiphyseal plate (the physis)\",\"e\":\"These fractures are most commonly seen in very young children and those with vitamin deficiencies such as scurvy. This would be a type I injury using the Salter-Harris system.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fracture line involving the epiphyseal plate (the physis) and bone proximal to the plate\",\"e\":\"This would be a type Il fracture and is the most common growth plate fracture pattern in children.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fracture line involving the epiphyseal plate (the physis) and epiphysis bone distal to the plate\",\"e\":\"A fracture involving the epiphyseal plate can be classified according to the Salter-Harris system. The place where the fracture line extends through the bone towards the joint space is designated type I!!.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"acture line involving the epiphyseal plate (the physis) and bone both proximal (metaphysis) and the bone distal to the plate (the epiphysis)\",\"e\":\"The Salter-Harris system would classify this as a type IV fracture through the epiphyseal plate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Compression at the physis\",\"e\":\"This would be a type V fracture.\"}]},{\"t\":29,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old woman is seen in fracture clinic after sustaining a Weber A fracture of her left ankle. She wants to know how long this will take to heal. In what order do the stages of bone healing occur?\",\"lead\":\"In what order do the stages of bone healing occur?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fracture haematoma, vascular granulation tissue, sub-periosteal osteoblast stimulation, bone matrix deposition, endochondral formation, callous formation, remodelling\",\"e\":\"The fracture line releases blood which creates a fracture haematoma, resulting in a sequence of events that ends in soft then hard callous formation. Remodelling due to on-going forces on the bone continues, sometimes for years after the original fracture.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fracture haematoma, sub-periosteal osteoblast stimulation, bone matrix deposition, vascular granulation tissue, endochondral formation, callous formation, remodelling\",\"e\":\"Vascular granulation tissue occurs after haematoma formation and is required for bone matrix deposition.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fracture haematoma, vascular granulation tissue, sub-periosteal osteoblast stimulation, endochondral formation, callous formation, bone matrix deposition, remodelling\",\"e\":\"Bone matrix deposition occurs to create a callous, which in turn depends on the process of endochondral ossification.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fracture haematoma, vascular granulation tissue, endochondral formation, callous formation, sub-periosteal osteoblast stimulation, bone matrix deposition, remodelling\",\"e\":\"Bone matr x deposition occurs to create a callous, which in turn depends on the process of endochondral ossification.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Fracture haematoma, callous formation, vascular granulation tissue, sub-periosteal teoblast stimulation, bone matrix deposition, endochondral formation. remodelling\",\"e\":\"Callous formation is the product of vascular granulation tissue and is the end-product of osteoblast activity. This callous then undergoes remodelling.\\n\\n3810\"}]},{\"t\":30,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old woman with a palpable 1.5 cm lump in the upper outer quadrant of her right breast had a biopsy that suggested the lump was a carcinoma. She is booked for surgery without delay to stop disease progression.\\n\\nWhich one of the following features of her carcinoma would suggest a poor prognosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following features of her carcinoma would suggest a poor prognosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Axillary lymph node metastases\",\"e\":\"Axillary nodal status is the most common factor indicating prognosis. Invasion of the lymphatics is a poor prognostic factor as this factor indicates tumour spread. Additionally, the greater the number of axillary nodes affected, the poorer the prognosis. Other factors associated with poor prognosis include: tumour size - the larger the tumour the poorer the prognosis; and tumour grade - the poorer the cellular differentiation the worse the prognosis.\\n\\nEthnicity still remains controversial, but studies have shown that Afro-Caribbean and Hispanic women have a decreased survival rate compared with White women.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Family history of breast carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Family history of breast carcinoma increases the risk of a woman having breast cancer but does not affect the prognosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lack of aneuploidy\",\"e\":\"Aneuploidy is associated with a poorer prognosis. Therefore the lack of aneuploidy can be considered a positive prognostic factor.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ostrogen receptor positivity\",\"e\":\"Ostrogen receptor positivity is a positive predictor as it increases the chance of the tumour being receptive to adjuvant chemotherapy in the form of tamoxifen and therefore improves the prognosis and cure rate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Presence of an in-situ component\",\"e\":\"In situ carcinomas (either ductal or lobular), are pre-cancerous conditions. If a tumour is detected at this stage then this situation is a positive predictor for prognosis as it can be detected early and treated, reducing the risk of progressing to a malignancy.\"}]},{\"t\":30,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old woman who has given birth to one child presents with a growing mobile lump in her right breast. It is painless with no associated nipple discharge. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CH Breast cyst\",\"e\":\"In this age group the second most likely diagnosis following a fibroadenoma would be a breast cyst. Breast cysts are fluid filled and develop as a result of fluid accumulation and entrapment in the glandular breast tissue. They are a very common benign condition of the breast, relating to normal hormonal breast tissue changes. They most commonly occur in women in the ages of 35-50 and tend to disappear after the menopause. They present as palpable lumps that are usually smooth, firm and mobile. Some can be tender. Some women have the tendency to develop recurrent cysts. Most breast cysts resolve on their own but if it is causing troublesome symptoms or is very large then aspiration is recommended. Taking the oral contraceptive or hormone replacement therapy can increase the risk of developing breast cysts.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ductal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Ductal carcinoma is the most commonly diagnosed form of breast cancer and infiltrates the surrounding tissue through the basement membrane of the ducts. The patient may remain asymptomatic or present with a breast lump that is immobile, hard and usually painless Peak presentation is around the age of 50-60 and it presents as a speculated hyperdense or an ovulated lesion on mammography.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Paget's disease\",\"e\":\"Paget's disease of the breast is a malignant disease affecting the nipple and the areola and usually presents in women aged 50-60. It presents with eczematous changes in the nipple and surrounding area such as: erythema, scaling and crusting. In most cases it is associated with malignant duct cells and therefore should always be treated as malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fibroadenoma\",\"e\":\"Fibroadenomas are the most common benign lesions seen in young women (< 30 years). They account for 90% of breast masses found in women under the age of 19 years. They arise from the breast lobule and contain both stromal and epithelial elements. They present clinically as a firm, smooth, mobile, well defined, painless mass of 1-5 cm in maximal diameter: Fibroadenomas can enlarge during pregnancy and can be multiple at presentation in 10-15% of cases. They tend to occur most commonly in the upper outer quadrant of the breast. On radiography, they appear well circumscribed, discrete, homogeneous lesions Diagnosis* is usually with a biopsy under ultrasound guidance and if no atypical features are found it can be followed up radiographically. If atypical features are found or lesions are large and symptomatic then surgical excision is preferred. The risk of malignant transformation of a fibroadenoma is < 1%.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Phyllodes tumour\",\"e\":\"A cystosarcoma phyllodes, also known as a phyllodes tumour, is a fibroepithelial sarcoma. It is rare, with an incidence of 1 in 100 000 women, and accounts for 0.5% of primary breast tumours. Phyllodes tumours can be benign, malignant or borderline. The benign form is encountered more often than the malignant forms. Phyllodes tumours are fast growing and women commonly present with a large, heterogenous breast mass that may be ulcerated. It is a locally invasive tumour with a tendency to recur. Up to 25% of the benign tumours undergo a malignant transformation. It usually occurs in women between the age of 40-60 years. Phyllodes tumours are surgically excised and are not sensitive to either chemotherapy or radiotherapy.\"}]},{\"t\":30,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old woman presents with a painless fixed lump in the upper outer quadrant of the right breast which has been present for the last four weeks. A mammogram has not revealed any abnormality but ultrasound has shown a 21 mm irregular mass.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Benign phyllodes\",\"e\":\"A phyllodes tumour, is a fibro-epithelial tumour occurring in women aged 40-60 and resembles a fibroadenoma. It is fast growing and a large breast mass is usually the presenting feature. Up to 25% of the benign tumours undergo a malignant transformation. Phyllodes tumours are surgically excised.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fibroadenoma\",\"e\":\"Fibroadenomas are the most common benign lesion seen in young women and they are rare after the age of 50 and menopause. Presentation is with a firm, smooth, mobile, painless mass. On radiography they appear well circumscribed, discrete, homogeneous lesions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Intraductal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Ductal carcinoma is the most common histological form of invasive breast cancer, accounting for 70-80% of invasive breast cancers. The patient may remain asymptomatic or present with a breast lump that is immobile, hard and usually painless. Peak presentation is around the age of 50-60 and it presents as a spiculated hyperdense or an ovulated lesion on mammography.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lobular carcinoma\",\"e\":\"A fixed lump and an ill-defined lesion on ultrasonography suggests malignancy. Lobular carcinoma is the most likely diagnosis in this scenario as these can be mammographically occult. Lobular carcinoma forms the second most common form of invasive breast cancer accounting for 5-15% of all invasive carcinomas. It presents in women on average older than those with ductal carcinoma, tumours at presentation tend to be larger, more invasive and have a higher percentage of positivity for ostrogen and progesterone receptors. It presents as a firm, fixed mass and can be bilateral or multilobular in 10-15% of cases at presentation. Radiographic features can include: ill-defined lesion, a mass of asymmetrical density. architectural distortions, microcalcifications unmay appear benign on mammography. Lobular carcinoma usually lacks microcalcifications and this lowers the sensitivity of detecting it with a mammogram. If there is a clinical suspicion then ultrasonography should be used as well as mammography if no lesion is detected on mammography. It was found that early menarche, late menopause and age 35 years at first full-term pregnancy were more significant risk factors in the case of lobular carcinoma of the breast than other types.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Radial scar\",\"e\":\"A radial scar is a rare proliferative breast lesion mostly affecting women aged 40-60. It is impalpable and the breast may appear normal. A radial scar is usually diagnosed on mammography as a spiculated lesion with a low density centre that allows visualisation of breast tissue. This differentiates it from a malignancy, which tend to be dense in the centre. Interestingly a radial scar does not relate to previous surgery, it is not a surgical scar.\"}]},{\"t\":30,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old woman with type I diabetes is seen in a breast clinic with an irregular firm lump in her right breast. There are no overlying skin changes and she is clinically well What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"There are no overlying skin changes and she is clinically well What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abscess\",\"e\":\"Although abscesses are common these would normally have a degree of skin change and can have systemic features.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lymphocytic lobulitis\",\"e\":\"Patients with type | diabetes are particularly at risk of this disorder. It is difficult to rule out malignancy due to its firm and irregular features, but a core biopsy would reveal the diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mastitis\",\"e\":\"This is more commonly generalised in a breast with pain and, although discrete masses are possible, they are not the common presenting feature.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fibroadenoma\",\"e\":\"Fibroadenomas are painless and smooth, historically called a breast mouse; they are benign.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Paget's disease\",\"e\":\"Paget's disease of the breast is a rare presentation involving the nipple; it does not present with a discrete lump, and this is why presentation is often delayed.\"}]},{\"t\":30,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old woman is seen in a breast clinic with invasive carcinoma of the left breast. Which of the factors below confers a lower risk of breast cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the factors below confers a lower risk of breast cancer?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"First-degree relative with breast cancer\",\"e\":\"Having a sister or mother with breast cancer confers a 25% lifetime risk of breast cancer. Around 1 in 300 women is a carrier for the BRCA mutation, which elevates risk further.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Female sex\",\"e\":\"Woman are around 100 times more likely to suffer from breast cancer than men.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Early menarche\",\"e\":\"Menarche occurring early, before the age of 11, increases the exposure of a woman to oestrogens, which are linked to a higher risk.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Early menopause\",\"e\":\"Early menopause results in lowering of the ostrogen levels, which correlates with the low risk of breast cancer: It is logical therefore to assume that hormone replacement. therapy increases the risk and is proportional to the length of treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Nulliparity\",\"e\":\"As with early menarche and late menopause, woman who do not have children are at an increased risk of developing cancer.\"}]},{\"t\":30,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 41-year-old woman is seen in the Breast Clinic with a lump in her right breast. She undergoes a triple assessment and the histology is reported as benign disease.\\n\\nWhich one of the following diagnoses is considered least benign?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following diagnoses is considered least benign?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Sclerosing adenosis\",\"e\":\"This benign condition is challenging to distinguish from carcinoma on mammography alone due to calcification.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cystic disease of the breast\",\"e\":\"This heterogeneous group of conditions is benign and associated with hormone replacement therapy and the perimenopausal period\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Intraductal papilloma\",\"e\":\"Although a neoplasm, this remains benign. Bloody or sometimes watery nipple discharge can be cured by local excision of the offending duct.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gynaecomastia\",\"e\":\"Affecting both men and woman, this is often triggered by medications such as spironolactone, but can also be an indicator of liver disease or occult malignancy elsewhere in the body.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Phyllodes tumour\",\"e\":\"Although these tumours have a wide range of characteristics, they can be malignant and arise from the stromal cells in the breast. They require wide local excision.\"}]},{\"t\":30,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old woman sees you in breast clinic after a diagnosis of invasive breast cancer. As her sister has also been diagnosed with breast cancer, genetic testing revealed a BRCAT mutation\\n\\nWhere is this mutation found?\",\"lead\":\"Where is this mutation found?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"X chromosome\",\"e\":\"BRCA1 is not found on the X chromosome and can be passed on via both maternal and paternal routes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chromosome 17\",\"e\":\"BRCAl is found on chromosome 17q21. It is inherited in an autosomal dominant manner and can be passed on via both maternal and paternal routes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Chromosome 13\",\"e\":\"BRCAl is not found on chromosome 13, but BRCA2 is.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Chromosome 21\",\"e\":\"Chromosome 21 does not carry BRAl or BRCA2 and is the smallest chromosome. Trisomy 21 results in Down syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Chromosome 7\",\"e\":\"Chromosome 7 carries the cystic fibrosis mutation, which is inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion.\\n\\n3514\"}]},{\"t\":30,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman is seen in the breast clinic with invasive breast cancer.\\n\\nWhich group of women should be routinely offered genetic testing for BRAT and BRCA2 according to National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidelines?\",\"lead\":\"Which group of women should be routinely offered genetic testing for BRAT and BRCA2 according to National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidelines?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Only women aged under 35 with triple-negative breast cancer\",\"e\":\"Although BRA gene mutation screening should be offered to patients aged under 35 with triple-negative breast cancer, it should also be offered to women aged under 40. Triple- negative cancers account for around 10-20% of breast cancers. This means that the tumours do not carry receptors for ostrogen, progesterone and HER2.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Only women aged under 40 with triple-negative breast cancer\",\"e\":\"Triple-negative breast cancer means that the tumour growth is not supported by hormones and, subsequently, the disease does not respond to commonly used therapies such as tamoxifen and trastuzumab. NICE guidelines suggest that genetic screening should be offered to all patients with a 10% risk of carrying BRCA1, BRCA2 or TP3. This correlates to women aged under 40 who present like this. BRCAl and BRCA2 mutations increase the risk of breast cancer in both men and women. Women with these mutations are more likely to present at an earlier age and with triple-negative breast cancer:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Only women aged under 35 with triple-positive breast cancer\",\"e\":\"Triple-positive cancer growth is stimulated by sex hormones and, although they do occur in patients with BRA mutations, triple-negative cancers are more commonly associated with the mutations.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Only women aged under 50 with triple-positive breast cancer\",\"e\":\"As above, triple-positive breast cancer patients do not require screening.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"All women who present with any breast cancer\",\"e\":\"Although genetic testing is a fast-moving field and its use is expanding year on year, routine screening should be offered to women aged under 40 with triple-negative breast cancers.\"}]},{\"t\":30,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old woman, who is otherwise fit and well, is seen with unilateral invasive breast cancer diagnosed on core biopsy. There is no evidence of axillary lymph node involvement on ultrasonography. She has no evidence of metastatic spread and surgery is planned.\\n\\nWhat technique should be used?\",\"lead\":\"What technique should be used?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Unilateral wide local excision alone\",\"e\":\"Although no evidence of lymph node involvement is found on ultrasonography, this approach would fail to identify cases with nodal involvement. Lymph node involvement wil! affect subsequent treatment options and prognosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Unilateral wide local excision and sentinel node sampling\",\"e\":\"The use of coloured radioactive dye allows identification and assessment of lymphatic drainage of the breast tissue where the cancer is located. Sampling of this node allows the identification of lymph node involvement with less morbidity than axillary node clearance.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Unilateral wide local excision and axillary node clearance\",\"e\":\"This historical approach has been usurped by the equally accurate sentinel node biopsy. The principle is similar in that local nodal involvement is investigated which then dictates further treatments\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Bilateral mastectomy and axillary lymph node clearance\",\"e\":\"In unilateral disease there is no need for bilateral treatment and lymph node clearance would add little to diagnosis and prognosis over sentinel node biopsies.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"No surgery is indicated\",\"e\":\"Although some patients with locally invasive disease may refuse surgery or have comorbidities that preclude them from surgical treatment, in this patient surgery would be indicated.\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with a hard lump in their neck with constitutional symptoms. It moves on swallowing and you suspect a thyroid cancer. The presence of 'orphan Annie' nuclei on histopathology is found in which type of thyroid cancer?\",\"lead\":\"The presence of 'orphan Annie' nuclei on histopathology is found in which type of thyroid cancer?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Papillary carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Orphan Annie nuclei are pale, empty looking nuclei that are typically seen in cases of papillary adenocarcinoma of the thyroid\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Follicular carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Orphan Annie nuclei are not seen in follicular carcinoma. Follicular carcinomas are typically solitary round nodules and feature capsular invasion (versus the non-capsular invasion seen in follicular adenomas).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anaplastic carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Orphan Annie nuclei are not seen in anaplastic carcinoma. The pathological features of anaplastic include necrosis, high mitotic activity and cellular pleomorphism.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Medullary carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Orphan Annie nuclei are not seen in medullary carcinoma. Medullary carcinomas are malignant tumours of the thyroid C-cells and are related to the MENa and MEN2b syndromes. Histology features include amyloid deposits.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Primary thyroid lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Orphan Annie nuclei are not seen in primary thyroid lymphoma. Primary thyroid lymphoma is rare, but is associated with Hashimoto's thyroiditis. It is typically of the non-Hodgkin's B- cell type\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A woman presents with a thyroid mass and symptoms of hyperthyroidism. She is initially managed by the endocrinology team but does not respond to treatment. She is therefore referred to the endocrine surgeons for consideration of thyroidectomy. Which one of the following is true of hyperthyroidism?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of hyperthyroidism?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Toxic nodular goitres are the commonest cause of hyperthyroidism\",\"e\":\"Graves' disease is the commonest cause of hyperthyroidism (thyrotoxicosis). It is usually associated with a diffuse goitre. The thyroid is moderately enlarged with increased vascularity.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Graves' disease is due to production of an autoantibody of the IgM class that binds to thyroid epithelial cells and mimics the action of thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)\",\"e\":\"The autoantibody in Graves' disease is an IgG antibody known as a long-acting thyroid stimulator (LATS). It stimulates the function and growth of the thyroid follicular epithelium.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Less than 1% of functioning adenomas shows enough secretory activity to cause hyperthyroidism\",\"e\":\"Thyroid adenomas may be so small that they only show up on a 13-radioistope scan.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"In Graves' disease, pretibial myxoedema is more common than exophthalmos\",\"e\":\"Pretibial myxoedema is rare, but exophthalmos is common.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hyperthyroidism can cause distal myopathy\",\"e\":\"Hyperthyroidism is known to cause proximal, rather than distal, myopathy.\\n\\n1500\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old man with urinary retention secondary to prostate cancer is admitted. The A&E team are unable to catheterise him resulting in further delay. You arrive and place a suprapubic catheter. By this time the patient has a potassium of 7.2 mmol/L Hyperkalaemia is usually associated with which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"2 mmol/L Hyperkalaemia is usually associated with which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acidosis\",\"e\":\"Hyperkalaemia is usually due to a metabolic acidosis such as diabetic ketoacidosis or renal failure\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Parathyroid adenoma\",\"e\":\"A parathyroid adenoma does not cause hyperkalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cushing syndrome\",\"e\":\"Cushing syndrome causes hypokalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Osmotic diuresis\",\"e\":\"Diuretics often cause hypokalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Injecting insulin\",\"e\":\"Insulin can cause hypokalaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"You are admitting a patient with suspected appendicitis. On examination and early bloods, you notice several abnormalities and wonder if there is a different cause to their presentation.\\n\\nWhich one of the following patients is most likely to have hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis, petechial haemorrhages and microinfarcts in the kidneys, in conjunction with a markedly elevated plasma renin?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following patients is most likely to have hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis, petechial haemorrhages and microinfarcts in the kidneys, in conjunction with a markedly elevated plasma renin?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A 56-year-old man with an acute myocardial infarction\",\"e\":\"An acute myocardial infarction could cause decreased cardiac output with decreased renal perfusion and ischaemia, leading to acute tubular necrosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A 6-year-old boy with albuminuria\",\"e\":\"The child is most likely to have minimal-change disease and the kidney will be grossly normal, will be normal on light microscopy and will show only fusion of foot processes on electron microscopy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A 62-year-old woman with end-stage renal disease\",\"e\":\"End-stage kidneys all look alike: thickened arteries, globally sclerotic glomeruli and interstitial scarring with chronic inflammation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A 15-year-old man with recent streptococcal infection\",\"e\":\"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis produces glomerular hypercellularity:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A 45-year-old woman with scleroderma\",\"e\":\"The diffuse form of scleroderma can be associated with hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis and malignant hypertension.\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old woman is diagnosed with a phaeochromocytoma and is booked for resectional surgery.\\n\\nShe is most likely to have increased serum levels of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"She is most likely to have increased serum levels of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aldosterone\",\"e\":\"Aldosterone levels are raised in adrenal adenoma (Conn syndrome).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cortisol\",\"e\":\"Serum cortisol is not raised in patients with phaeochromocytoma. Tumours causing elevated cortisol levels include Cushing's disease (a secretory pituitary adenoma) or small-cell lung cancer (paraneoplastic secretion of ACTH).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Noradrenaline\",\"e\":\"A phaeochromocytoma is a neuroendocrine tumour of the medulla of the adrenal glands originating in the chromaffin cells, which secrete excessive amounts of catecholamines, usually adrenaline and noradrenaline.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"ARY Oxytocin\",\"e\":\"Oxytocin serum levels are not increased in phaeochromocytoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vasopressin\",\"e\":\"Vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone (ADH)) is not elevated in phaeochromocytoma, but it is elevated in some cases of small-cell lung cancer.\\n\\n1740\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"Renin is a circulating enzyme released mainly by juxtaglomerular cells in the juxtaglomerular apparatus of the kidneys. Renin goes on to have important effects on the body's circulatory system. Plasma renin levels are decreased in patients with which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Plasma renin levels are decreased in patients with which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Heart failure\",\"e\":\"Secondary hyperaldosteronism is due to elevated renin levels and may be caused by heart failure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Primary hyperaldosteronism\",\"e\":\"Most patients with primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome) have an adrenal adenoma. The increased plasma aldosterone concentration leads to increased renal Nat reabsorption, which results in plasma volume expansion. The increase in plasma volume suppresses renin release from the juxtaglomerular apparatus and these patients usually have low plasma renin levels.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Renal artery stenosis\",\"e\":\"Secondary hyperaldosteronism is due to elevated renin levels and may be caused by renal artery stenosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Salt restriction\",\"e\":\"Salt restriction causes a decrease in renal perfusion pressure and therefore increased renin release from the juxtaglomerular apparatus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Upright posture\",\"e\":\"Upright posture causes a decrease in renal perfusion pressure and therefore increased renin release from the juxtagiomerular apparatus.\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old diabetic patient has poorly controlled diabetes mellitus, with low insulin levels. His GP is asked to improve the glucose control prior to going ahead with a high risk surgical procedure.\\n\\nWhich one of the following biochemical changes is most likely to be seen in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following biochemical changes is most likely to be seen in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abnormally rapid fall of blood glucose following a meal\",\"e\":\"Poorly controlled diabetes will result in a rapid rise of blood glucose following a meal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Abnormally low concentration of fatty acids in blood\",\"e\":\"There will be a high concentration of fatty acids in the blood. A deficiency in insulin results in increased fatty acid production from stored lipids, increased fatty acid release into the blood and decreased fatty acid uptake.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased protein breakdown\",\"e\":\"A deficiency of insulin causes protein catabolism. Glucose is synthesised (mostly from amino acids, released by protein catabolism).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"High rate of glycogen synthesis in liver\",\"e\":\"A lack of insulin will result in a low rate of glycogen synthesis in the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased gluconeogenesis\",\"e\":\"A lack of insulin will result in increased gluconeogenesis:\\n\\n1743 -\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old woman is diagnosed with primary hyperparathyroidism. She is seen in the urology clinic as her GP is concerned she may have formed a renal calculus. Of which one of the following symptoms may she complain?\",\"lead\":\"Of which one of the following symptoms may she complain?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Diarrhoea\",\"e\":\"Hypercalcaemia causes constipation rather than diarrhoea.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"1MU Muscle weakness\",\"e\":\"Muscle weakness is associated with hypocalcaemia rather than hypercalcaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Osteomalacia\",\"e\":\"Hypercalcaemia causes osteoporosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Euphoria\",\"e\":\"Depression is more typical of hypercalcaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Depression\",\"e\":\"Hypercalcaemiat can cause depression, fatigue, weakness and in extreme cases, confusion, seizures and coma.\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A teenager presents with excess hair and amenorrhoea. She is normotensive. Her prolactin levels are normal but she has a raised 17a-hydroxyprogesterone level.\\n\\nWhat is her diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is her diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Complete 21-hydroxylase deficiency\",\"e\":\"In the salt-losing form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (ie with complete deficiency of 21- hydroxylase, there is complete inability to produce cortisol and aldosterone), leading to an earlier presentation than in partial 21-hydroxylase deficiency.\\n\\n11B-Hydroxylase deficiencies lead to hypertension due to excessive mineralocorticoid production.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Complete l- hydroxylase deficiency\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Partial 21-hydroxylase deficiency\",\"e\":\"She has congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH). Partial deficiency of 21-hydroxylase (ie in the simple-virilising form of CAH) allows patients to produce near normal (or normal) amounts of cortisol due to increased ACTH output. Late-onset disease can be due to partial enzyme deficiency.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Partial 11B-hydroxylase deficiency\",\"e\":\"11ß-Hydroxylase deficiencies lead to hypertension due to excessive mineralocorticoid production.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"17a-Hydroxylase deficiency\",\"e\":\"17a-Hydroxylase deficiency is a rare form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia that features excess mineralocorticoid production but deficiency in the production of sex steroids and cortisol.\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man is found to have a carcinoid on routine histology. They are urgently brought back to the outpatient clinic to discuss the ongoing care. Which one of the following is correct regarding carcinoid syndrome?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding carcinoid syndrome?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is due to excessive secretion of somatostatin\",\"e\":\"Carcinoid syndrome is due to the excess secretion of serotonin by argentaffin cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"VS May cause constipation\",\"e\":\"Carcinoid syndrome typically causes diarrhoea\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"May result in cardiac valve lesions\",\"e\":\"Carcinoid heart disease is characterised by pathognomonic plaque-like deposits of fibrous tissue secondary to elevated levels of serum serotonin. These deposits occur most commonly on the endocardium of valvular cusps and leaflets, the cardiac chambers, and occasionally on the intima of the pulmonary arteries or aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Can be confirmed by elevated vanillyimandelic acid (VMA) levels\",\"e\":\"Diagnosis is by raised urinary excretion of 5-hydroxy indole-acetic acid (5-HIAA), a metabolite of serotonin. VMA levels are elevated in phaeochromocytoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is cured by excision of the appendix\",\"e\":\"The syndrome relates to serotonin produced by liver metastases and therefore appendicectomy cannot be curative.\\n\\n204t\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"You review a patient's biochemistry and notice their calcium levels are very high. You undertake further investigations to find a cause including bloods and imaging. Which one of the following is not typically a cause of hypercalcaemia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is not typically a cause of hypercalcaemia?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyperparathyroidism\",\"e\":\"In primary hyperparathyroidism there is excess production of parathyroid hormone (PTH). which results in hypercalcaemia. Although usually from a benign adenoma, this sometimes results from hyperplasia of the parathyroid glands and, in rare cases, a carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"Thyrotoxicosis can cause hypercalcaemia as well as osteoporosis..\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Milk-alkali syndrome\",\"e\":\"The milk-alkali syndrome can occur in patients who suffer from dyspepsia and drink milk and alkali-containing antacids, which may reduce the renal excretion of calcium.\\n\\nAround one-fifth of those people with sarcoid have increased calcium levels:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sarcoid\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Squamous-cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Various mechanisms cause raised hypercalcaemia of malignancy\\n\\n3.7.0\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old man is diagnosed with a pituitary adenoma. He has presented with weight gain, muscle weakness and easy bruising Which one of the following hormones is most likely to be secreted by this adenoma?\",\"lead\":\"He has presented with weight gain, muscle weakness and easy bruising Which one of the following hormones is most likely to be secreted by this adenoma?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Calcitonin\",\"e\":\"Calcitonin is produced by the parafollicular C-cells of the thyroid.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)\",\"e\":\"TSH is secreted by the anterior pituitary gland and can be raised in a secreting adenoma. An excess of TSH would result in the clinical features of hyperthyroidism - weight loss would occur rather than weight gain.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)\",\"e\":\"ACTH is secreted by the anterior pituitary gland and can be raised in a secreting adenoma - this is called Cushing's disease. Cushing's disease causes symptoms related to high cortisol levels. These include: upper body obesity, a rounded 'moon' face, myopathy, acne, hypertension, hyperglycaemia, fatigue, emotional disturbance, depression, immunosuppression, osteoporosis, amenorrhoea, hirsutism, striae, easy bruising, water retention, and a 'buffalo hump' (enlarged supraclavicular fat pads).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Growth hormone (GH)\",\"e\":\"Growth hormone is secreted by the anterior pituitary gland and can be raised in a secreting adenoma. This would manifest clinically as acromegaly.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prolactin\",\"e\":\"Prolactin is secreted by the anterior pituitary gland and can be raised in a secreting adenoma. A prolactin-secreting adenoma would cause milk production and menstrual cycle disturbance in women and reduce testosterone levels in men.\"}]},{\"t\":31,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following is a well-recognised feature of excessive glucocorticoid levels?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a well-recognised feature of excessive glucocorticoid levels?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypotension\",\"e\":\"Hypertension is a feature as a result of increased renal reabsorption of sodium and water.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoglycaemia\",\"e\":\"Hyperglycaemia is a feature as a result of glucocorticoid activity. Excessive glucocorticoid levels raise the blood glucose level by stimulating lipolysis, proteolysis, and hepatic glucose production.\\n\\nAcne and alopecia may occasionally be treated by glucocorticoid excess.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Alopecia\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Immunosuppression\",\"e\":\"Immunosuppression is a well-recognised feature of glucocorticoid excess. Patients with elevated glucocorticoid levels are at a higher risk of infection, of particular note, this includes the immediate post-operative period when endogenous cortisol levels are high following major surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Osteopetrosis.\",\"e\":\"Osteoporosis is a recognised feature of glucocorticoid excess. Patients with elevated glucocorticoid levels, either in disease or those taking long-term steroids, are at risk of osteoporotic fractures. Osteopetrosis is a phenomenon in which bones become more dense.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old farmer presents with a lesion on his left cheek that has been present for several months. On examination, it has nodular appearance with rolled edges and visible telangiectasia. Clinically it is thought to be a basal-cell carcinoma (BCC) and the decision is made to proceed with treatment.\\n\\nWhat proportion of BCCs occur in the head and neck region?\",\"lead\":\"What proportion of BCCs occur in the head and neck region?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"One per cent\",\"e\":\"Ninety per cent of all BCCs occur in the head and neck region.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ten per cent\",\"e\":\"Ninety per cent of all BCCs occur in the head and neck region.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Thirty percent\",\"e\":\"Ninety per cent of all BCCs occur in the head and neck region.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fifty per cent\",\"e\":\"Ninety per cent of all BCCs occur in the head and neck region.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ninety per cent\",\"e\":\"BCC is the commonest skin tumour on the face and 90% of all BCCs occur in the head and neck region.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old man consults his dentist with a persistent, non-healing ulcer at the base of his tongue which bleeds sporadically. The dentist is concerned this could represent a squamous- cell carcinoma and refers him for urgent investigation.\\n\\nWhich one of the following are risk factors for oral squamous-cell carcinoma?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following are risk factors for oral squamous-cell carcinoma?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"Explanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Diabetes mellitus\",\"e\":\"There is no association between diabetes and oral squamous-cell cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"HIV\",\"e\":\"In addition to tobacco and alcohol use, human papillomavirus (HPV) infection, immunodeficiency and possibly genetic changes represent risk factors for oral squamous- cell carcinoma in patients with HIV infection. Smoking and high alcohol intake are synergistic risk factors for oral squamous cell carcinoma (SCC), increasing the risk by six-fold. Other factors include syphilis, malnutrition, cirrhosis, Plummer- Vinson syndrome and leucoplakia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypertension\",\"e\":\"There is no association between steroid therapy and oral squamous-cell cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"There is no association between hypertension and oral squamous-cell cancer.\\n\\nThere is no association between hypothyroidism and oral squamous-cell cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Steroid therapy\",\"e\":\"There is no association between steroid therapy and oral squamous-cell cancer.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"Tumour markers are excellent at guiding a clinician to a diagnosis in malignancy of unknown origin. In addition they have utility in monitoring response to treatment or the possibility of recurrence.\\n\\nWhich one of the following tumour-associated antigens is linked with the correct cancer?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following tumour-associated antigens is linked with the correct cancer?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CA-125: Testicular teratoma\",\"e\":\"CA-125 is associated with ovarian epithelial cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"CA 19-9: Pancreatic cancer\",\"e\":\"CA 19-9 is associated with pancreatic and some colorectal cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Alpha-fetoprotein: Ovarian cancer\",\"e\":\"Alpha-fetoprotein is associated with hepatocellular carcinoma, hepatoblastoma, testicular teratoma and yolk sac tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hepatocellular carcinoma\",\"e\":\"CEA is associated with colorectal carcinoma and some head and neck and lung cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Breast cancer\",\"e\":\"HCG is associated with germ-cell ovarian tumours, choriocarcinoma, testicular teratoma and seminoma.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 49-year-old woman presents to her gynaecologist with CIN-3 changes. She has been reading on the Internet about how the human papillomavirus interferes with the programmed death of defective cells and may predispose to cancers. Which one of the following protein names best identifies the DNA-binding protein with which the papillomavirus interferes?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following protein names best identifies the DNA-binding protein with which the papillomavirus interferes?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"p52\",\"e\":\"Papillomavirus does not interfere with p52\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"p51\",\"e\":\"Papillomavirus does not interfere with p51.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"p53\",\"e\":\"p53 is a DNA-binding protein that induces the expression of a number of genes that are involved in programmed cell death. Clearly, p53 gene mutations or products that interfere with p53 protein functioning are implicated in carcinogenesis. Initial p53 gene expression causes genes involved in DNA repair to be expressed. If DNA repair is too slow or gene repair cannot be effected, then other genes involved in apoptosis take over, and programmed cell death ensues. Both the adenovirus E1B and papillomavirus E6 gene products bind to p53 and interfere with its functioning. Another protein involved in the cell cycle and triggering of apoptosis is the RB protein, the functioning of which is interfered with by a number of viruses including papillomavirus, adenovirus and the SV40 virus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"p54\",\"e\":\"Papillomavirus does not interfere with p54.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"p55\",\"e\":\"Papillomavirus does not interfere with p55.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"When planning the radiotherapy dosage for solid tumours, it has to be remembered that the division of cells is governed by the cell cycle, the mechanism of which is disrupted by radiation.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true concerning the cell cycle?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true concerning the cell cycle?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"M represents the phase of premitotic DNA synthesis\",\"e\":\"M is the mitotic phase.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"G, is a gap phase under the influence of the p53 gene\",\"e\":\"G, is a gap phase under the influence of the p53 gene.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"S is the mitotic phase\",\"e\":\"S represents DNA replication.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"G2 is a gap phase when cells contain half as much DNA as non-dividing cells\",\"e\":\"G¿ is a gap phase when cells contain twice as much DNA as non-dividing cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"In normal tissues, cells with significant damage to their DNA are arrested at the M phase\",\"e\":\"In normal tissues, cells with significant damage to their DNA are arrested at the G, phase.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"Various cell mechanisms have been proposed to contribute to the process of carcinogenesis. Establishing these mechanisms increases the chances of finding ways to reverse the disease process.\\n\\nWhat does 'sis' refer to in the discussion of cancer genetics?\",\"lead\":\"What does 'sis' refer to in the discussion of cancer genetics?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Growth factor oncogene\",\"e\":\"sis is the best studied growth factor oncogene. It leads to the overproduction of platelet- derived growth factor, which stimulates cells to grow.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Growth factor receptor\",\"e\":\"sis is not a growth factor receptor. Growth factor receptors are ormally turned 'on' and 'off\\\" by growth factors. the best known examples are erb B and erb B-2. These are sometimes known as epidermal growth factor receptor HER2/neu. HER2/neu gene amplification is an important abnormaility seen in about one third of breast cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Signal transducer\",\"e\":\"sis is not a signal transducer. Signal transducers are the intermediate pathways between the growth factor reception and the cell nucleus where the signal is recieved. Like growth factor receptors, these can be turned on or off. When they are abnormal in cancer cells, they are turned on. Two well known signal transducers are abl and ras. Abl is activated in chronic myelocytic leukemia and is the target of most successful drug for these diseases, imatnib or Gleevec. Abnormalities of ras are found in many cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transcription factor\",\"e\":\"sis is not a transcription factor. Transcription factors are the final molecules in the chain that tell the cell to divide. These molecules act on the DNA and control which genes are active in producing RNA and protein. The best known of these is called myc. In lung cancer, leukaemia, lymphoma and a number of other cancer types, myc is often overly activated and stimulated cell division.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Programmed cell death regulator\",\"e\":\"sis is not a programmed cell death regulator. PCDR molecules prevent a cell from committing suicide when it becomes abnormal. When these genes are overactivated they prevent the cell from going through the suicide process. This leads to an overgrowth of abnormal cells, which can then become cancerous. The most well described one is called bcl-2. It is often activated in lymphoma cells.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"Oncologists have an essential role in any cancer MDT. You are discussing a patient with a rectal adenocarcinoma and the oncologist decides to see them in clinic before surgery to discuss neoadjuvant chemotherapy. Which of the following is an example of a growth factor receptor oncogene?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is an example of a growth factor receptor oncogene?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PDCD-1\",\"e\":\"PDCD-1 is the gene that codes for programmed cell death 1 receptor protein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"B-2\",\"e\":\"erb B-2 is a growth factor receptor oncogene.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"ras\",\"e\":\"ras is a signal transducer\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"myc\",\"e\":\"myc is a transcription factor.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"bel-2\",\"e\":\"bcl-2 is a programmed cell death regulator.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an inherited condition that predisposes affected individuals to a wide array of neoplastic disease. Screening and genetic counselling is an important part of management.\\n\\nThe commonest gene mutation in this inherited cancer syndrome involves which gene?\",\"lead\":\"The commonest gene mutation in this inherited cancer syndrome involves which gene?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"3/75\\nExplanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"INK4a\",\"e\":\"INK4a is associated with melanoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"BRCA2\",\"e\":\"BRCA2 is associated with breast cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"WTI\",\"e\":\"WT/ is associated with Wilms' tumour.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"p55\",\"e\":\"p53 is associated with Li-Fraumeni syndrome\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"VHL\",\"e\":\"VHL is associated with renal malignancy.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old man has an adrenal mass noted on computed tomography (CT) scan. This is a metastasis as there is an obvious primary on the CT therefore it is not biopsied The most likely primary source is which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This is a metastasis as there is an obvious primary on the CT therefore it is not biopsied The most likely primary source is which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Clear cell renal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Kidney tumours more commonly metastasise to lung and bone. The specific subtype of clear-cell renal carcinoma commonly metastasises to lungs (76%), local lymph nodes (66%), bone (42%) and liver (41%).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Non-small cell lung cancer\",\"e\":\"Tumours commonly metastasising to the adrenal glands are lung and breast cancers. Isolated adrenal metastases are reported in 1.6% - 3.5% of patients with primary non-small cell lung cancer who present with resectable tumours. The incidence increases to up to 40% as the clinical course of the disease progresses. Other primaries that commonly metastasise to the adrenal glands are colorectal cancer and pancreatic cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Prostate\",\"e\":\"Prostate tumours more commonly metastasise to lung and bone. They can also metastasise to the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Papillary thyroid cancer\",\"e\":\"Papillary thyroid cancer is the most common subtype of thyroid cancer (~80% of cases). Cervical metastases (cervical lymph nodes) are present in 50% of small papillary carcinomas and more than 75% of large papillary thyroid carcinomas. However, distant metastases are rare in this type of cancer, but it typically involves the lungs, bones and liver.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Glioblastoma multiforme\",\"e\":\"Glioblastoma multiforme is the most aggressive subtype of intracranial tumour. This malignancy diffusely infiltrates the surrounding tissues, but does not typically involve blood vessles and very rarely spreads outside the central nervous system. The most common site for extraneural metastases (present in only 0.2% of cases) is the pleura.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old woman presents with increasing pain in the right femur. X-rays reveal a single lytic lesion consistent with bony metastasis.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is least likely to be the primary?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is least likely to be the primary?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"3/80\\nExplanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Colon\",\"e\":\"Colorectal cancer commonly metastasises to lung and liver, but not thebone*.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Breast\",\"e\":\"Breast cancer is one of the common cancers that spreads to bone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lung\",\"e\":\"Lung cancer is one of the common cancers that spreads to bone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Kidney\",\"e\":\"Kidney is one of the common cancers that spreads to bone.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyroid\",\"e\":\"Thyroid cancer is one of the common cancers that spreads to bone.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old man presents with a left testicular lump. They undergo initial investigations which includes tumour markers and an ultrasound.\\n\\nWhich one of the following tumour markers would be the least useful?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following tumour markers would be the least useful?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"37/82\\nExplanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alpha-fetoprotein\",\"e\":\"Alpha-fetoprotein, human chorionic gonadotropin (B-HCG) blood test and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) levels are elevated by non-seminomatous germ cell tumour (NSGCT).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"LDH\",\"e\":\"Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) levels are elevated by non-seminomatous germ cell tumour (NSGCT).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (B-HCG)\",\"e\":\"B-HCG can be elevated with seminoma and non-seminomatous germ cell tumour (NSGCT).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ca 15-3\",\"e\":\"Ca 15-3 is a tumour marker for breast cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase (GGT)\",\"e\":\"Gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase (GGT) can be elevated by testicular tumours, but is not commonly used in clinical practice.\\n\\n1094\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man with known cirrhosis is found to have developed a new lesion in the right lobe of the liver. This is discussed at the HPB MDT and deemed to most likely represent a hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). Which virus is most commonly associated with HCC?\",\"lead\":\"Which virus is most commonly associated with HCC?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hepatitis A\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hepatitis B\",\"e\":\"Hepatitis B is strongly associated with hepatocellular carcinoma, and patients are often HBsAg positive.\\n\\nHepatitis A is a self-limiting disease, not known to cause chronic liver disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hepatitis D\",\"e\":\"Hepatitis D requires HBsAg co-existence.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hepatitis E\",\"e\":\"Hepatitis E is a self-limiting disease, not known to cause chronic liver disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Epstein Barr Virus\",\"e\":\"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is linked to nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Burkitt's lymphoma and Hodgkin's lymphoma i..n=\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with hyponatraemia. The chest X-ray shows a lesion. The most likely type of lung cancer is which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The most likely type of lung cancer is which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Squamous cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH is not common in squamous cell carcinoma. Squamous cell tumours can cause ectopic parathyroid hormone (PTH) secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Adenocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH is not common in adenocarcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Giant cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH is not common in giant-cell carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Clear cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Syndrome of inappropriate ADH is not common in clear cell carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Small cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"The patient has a syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion secondary to the lung mass. Small cell (oat cell) tumours are most commonly associated with paraneoplastic syndromes (15% of patients have them at presentation). Increased ADH secretion and increased adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) secretion are the most common..\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A surgical pathology report indicates that a certain type of neoplasm is graded as grade I on a scale of Ito IV\\n\\nWhich one of the descriptions in the report would suggest that this neoplasm is graded in this way?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the descriptions in the report would suggest that this neoplasm is graded in this way?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It cannot have an in-situ component\",\"e\":\"It may have an in-situ component, eg in breast cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It has arisen only from epithelium\",\"e\":\"Grading and staging are most useful for epithelial malignancies, but are not reserved specifically for them.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is unlikely to be malignant\",\"e\":\"It may well be malignant.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is well differentiated and localised\",\"e\":\"A well differentiated and localised neoplasm usually has both a low grade and a low stage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It could spread via lymphatics\",\"e\":\"It can indeed spread to nodes, particularly if it is a carcinoma, but it is less likely to do so if it is a low-grade tumour and it has not already done so.\\n\\n1122\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"Some cancers are more common in families due to a genetic predisposition. In certain diseases screening and genetic counselling may be necessary BRCAT and BRCA2 genes are used as tumour markers for the diagnosis of a tumour of which of the following organs?\",\"lead\":\"In certain diseases screening and genetic counselling may be necessary BRCAT and BRCA2 genes are used as tumour markers for the diagnosis of a tumour of which of the following organs?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bladder\",\"e\":\"BRCAT and BRCAZ are not linked to bladder tumours\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Breast\",\"e\":\"Both BRCAl and BRCA2 are tumour suppressor genes. Certain variations of the BRCAl and BRCA2 genes lead to an increased risk for breast cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Kidney\",\"e\":\"BRCAT and BRCA2 are not linked to kidney tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lung\",\"e\":\"BRCAT and BRCA2 are not linked to lung tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rectum\",\"e\":\"BRCAT and BRCA2 are not linked to cancers of the rectum.\\n\\n:133\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man undergoes a liver transplant and is placed on lifelong immunosuppressive drugs. Years later they subsequently develop neoplastic disease.\\n\\nWhich one of the following types of cancers is most common in organ transplant recipients?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following types of cancers is most common in organ transplant recipients?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Breast cancer\",\"e\":\"There is no increase in risk of breast cancer following organ transplantation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lung cancer\",\"e\":\"There is no increase in risk of lung cancer following organ transplantation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pancreatic cancer\",\"e\":\"There is no increase in risk of pancreatic cancer following organ transplantation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prostate cancer\",\"e\":\"There is no increase in risk of prostate cancer following organ transplantation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Skin cancer\",\"e\":\"Carcinomas of the skin are the most common cancers in organ transplant recipients. There is a significantly higher in incidence in this population than that of the general population. The skin cancers differ from the carcinomas in the general population because they have increased aggressiveness, are of squamous cell origin and occur more frequently in relatively young patients.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"You are spending a 'taster week' in the pathology laboratories at your hospital and are reviewing specimens of a colorectal lesion resected by the general surgeons. The request form reads 'benign or malignant'.\\n\\nMalignant tumours may be distinguished from benign tumours by the presence of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Malignant tumours may be distinguished from benign tumours by the presence of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Capsule\",\"e\":\"Malignant tumours do not typically have a capsule.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Higher mitotic rate\",\"e\":\"Malignant tumours exhibit increased cellular turnover (hence high mitotic rate and high nuclear cytoplasmic ratio).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lack of new blood vessel formation\",\"e\":\"Tumours require angiogenesis to sustain them. As larger tumours are, in general, more likely to metastasize, the presence of new blood vessel formation may indicate malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Oncogene activation\",\"e\":\"Oncogene activation can occur in both benign and malignant tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Low nuclear: cytoplasm ratio\",\"e\":\"Malignant tumours exhibit increased cellular turnover (hence high mitotic rate and high nuclear cytoplasmic ratio).\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"You are undertaking a rotation in a tertiary paediatric surgical unit and are expecting to be exposed to some unusual pathology. In the clinic you review a teenage patient with a known neuroblastoma\\n\\nThese tumours may arise from which of the following locations?\",\"lead\":\"These tumours may arise from which of the following locations?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior root of the spinal cord\",\"e\":\"Neuroblastoma does not arise from the anterior root of the spinal cord.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Posterior root of the spinal cord\",\"e\":\"Neuroblastoma does not arise from the posterior root of the spinal cord.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Parasympathetic ganglia ~\",\"e\":\"Neuroblastoma does not arise from the parasympathetic ganglia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Adrenal medulla K\",\"e\":\"Neuroblastoma is a tumour of the sympathetic nervous system, and is usually highly malignant. As neuroblastic tumours are derived from neural crest cells, they can affect the adrenal medulla (and adjacent retroperitoneal tissue), sympathetic neurones and melanocytes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Peripheral nerves\",\"e\":\"Neuroblastoma does not arise from the peripheral nerves.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"A 45-year old man is reviewed in the cardiothoracic clinic and is found to have mesothelioma caused by asbestos exposure. The patient is booked for a pleurectomy/decortication procedure as an aggressive therapy for their disease.\\n\\nOther common carcinogens and their associated malignancies include?\",\"lead\":\"Other common carcinogens and their associated malignancies include?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ultraviolet radiation and basal-cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"There is a significant association between ultraviolet (UV) radiation and basal-cell carcinoma (skin cancer).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"B-Naphthylamine and gastric cancer\",\"e\":\"Aromatic amines such as B-naphthylamine are associated with urinary tract (especially bladder) tumours in chemical workers who are particularly involved with dyes and pesticides.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mobile phones and glioblastoma\",\"e\":\"There is no current good evidence to prove a link between the use of mobile phones and glioblastoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Benzene and colonic cancer\",\"e\":\"Painters, printers, mechanics and others working with petroleum derivatives and organic solvents (benzene exposure) are at an increased risk of developing leukaemia, lymphoma and multiple myeloma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Arsenic and oesophageal cancer\",\"e\":\"Arsenic is used in pesticide manufacture and exposure also occurs in metal smelters. It is associated with skin and lung cancers as well as bladder tumours.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"You are reading the pathology report for a post-operative patient. They have had a bowel lesion resected and it is an adenoma.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding adenomas?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding adenomas?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Are typically encapsulated\",\"e\":\"Consistent with their benign nature, they are typically encapsulated.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Can arise in transitional epithelial cells\",\"e\":\"Adenomas are benign tumours of ductal or glandular epithelial cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Typically invade the basement membrane\",\"e\":\"Consistent with their benign nature, adenomas do not invade the basement membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Are typically annular lesions\",\"e\":\"Annular lesions, which grow in hollow organs and can cause stricturing, are typically malignant.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Do not contain dysplastic cells\",\"e\":\"Adenomas can contain dysplastic cells.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man presents with a 5cm lump in his anterior thigh. It is hard to palpate, moves with the underlying muscle and does not transilluminate. Open biopsy a soft tissue sarcoma is confirmed.\\n\\nWhich one of the following best describes the invasive nature of sarcoma?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describes the invasive nature of sarcoma?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adventitia of large vessels are relatively vulnerable to invasion\",\"e\":\"They are locally invasive, but spread by 'pushing' along tissue planes rather than by direct invasion of major structures. Fascia, major nerve sheaths and adventitia of large vessels are relatively resistant to invasion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Characteristically spreads by direct invasion of major structures\",\"e\":\"They are locally invasive, but spread by 'pushing' along tissue planes rather than by direct invasion of major structures. Fascia, major nerve sheaths and adventitia of large vessels are relatively resistant to invasion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Frequently metastasises to lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"Unlike rhabdomyosarcoma and angiosarcoma, sarcoma rarely produce lymph node metastasis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spreads initially along fascial planes\",\"e\":\"Sarcoma spreads across fascial planes and metastasises via the bloodstream, the lungs are the most common site of metastatic deposits.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Usually invades via major nerve sheaths\",\"e\":\"They are locally invasive, but spread by 'pushing' along tissue planes rather than by direct invasion of major structures. Fascia, major nerve sheaths and adventitia of large vessels are relatively resistant to invasion.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a follow-up patient in the cardiothoracic clinic with an 'EGFR' mutation in their lung cancer, according to the pathology report in the notes. This is an example of an oncogene. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding oncogenes?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding oncogenes?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Code for proteins involved in cellular regulatory processes\",\"e\":\"Oncogenes tend to code for proteins (oncoproteins) involved in key cellular regulatory processes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Always encode for growth factors\",\"e\":\"Oncogene-derived proteins (oncoproteins) are not limited to growth factors.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Must be mutated for carcinogenesis to occur\",\"e\":\"Oncogenes can cause malignancy not only when their gene product is altered by mutation, but also when they are over- or underexpressed or expressed at an inappropriate stage of the cell cycle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Exhibit a high degree of evolutionary variability\",\"e\":\"Because of the key regulatory roles of their products, oncogenes tend to be fundamental to cellular activity and hence highly conserved throughout evolution.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cannot be transmitted by viruses\",\"e\":\"The discovery of tumour viruses containing viral analogues of these genes, derived from cellular oncogenes, act as vectors for their transmission and for malignant transformation of host cells. The H-ras oncogene in the Harvey rat sarcoma virus is found in a variety of common human cancers.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"You are in the colorectal MDT discussing a patient with liver metastases from their primary tumour. The decision is made for them to proceed to palliative chemotherapy.\\n\\nWhat does the ability of neoplastic cells to metastasise depend upon?\",\"lead\":\"What does the ability of neoplastic cells to metastasise depend upon?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Reduced expression of integrins\",\"e\":\"Adhesion to the basement membrane requires the expression of integrins (cell-surface receptors for basement membrane components)\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased cellular cohesion\",\"e\":\"Metastasis requires loss of cellular cohesion (loss of tight junction barrier function).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Protease secretion\",\"e\":\"To metastasise, cells must overcome their normal cohesion and be able to invade the tissue, a property facilitated by the secretion of protease enzymes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduced cellular adherence to the basement membrane\",\"e\":\"Cancer cells must adhere to the basement membrane to pass through.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased production of cadherins\",\"e\":\"Adhesion to the basement membrane requires the expression of integrins, but not cadherins, which enhance cell-to-cell adhesion.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"You are in a neuroradiology meeting and various primary tumours are being discussed. A diagnosis of malignant cancer is based on the evidence of CT scan.\\n\\nWhich one of the following tumours is malignant rather than benign?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following tumours is malignant rather than benign?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Leiomyoma\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Meningioma\",\"e\":\"A meningioma is typically a benign tumour of the meninges.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Rhabdomyoma\",\"e\":\"A rhabdomyoma is a benign tumour of striated muscle (its malignant counterpart is a rhabdomyosarcoma).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neuroma\",\"e\":\"A neuroma is a benign tumour arising from the tissue of or surrounding nerves. 1230\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Chondrosarcoma\",\"e\":\"A chondrosarcoma is a malignant tumour of cartilage of mesenchymal origin.\\n\\nA leiomyoma is a benign tumour of smooth muscle (its malignant counterpart is a leiomyosarcoma).\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing the post-operative histology for a patient that underwent an emergency surgical procedure where tissue was removed. You are surprised to see the report states that this is likely a carcinoid tumour, you therefore urgently bring the patient back to clinic. Which one of the following is correct concerning these tumours?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct concerning these tumours?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carcinoid tumours can be diagnosed on histology solely on argyrophil and argentaffin positivity\",\"e\":\"While argyrophil and argentaffin positivity supports the diagnosis, a pathological examination on both cytology and histology is required.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Carcinoid tumours are most common in the stomach\",\"e\":\"The small bowel and appendix are the most common primary sites for carcinoid tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Carcinoid tumours produce increased amounts of urinary catecholamines\",\"e\":\"Carcinoid tumours produce increased 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid and 5-hydroxytryptamine.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Carcinoid tumours can produce a similar appearance on barium enema to Crohn's disease\",\"e\":\"The barium enema in carcinoid syndrome can produce mucosal ulcers mimicking Crohn's disease. Carcinoids are occasionally found in the appendix, therefore all routine appendicectomy cases should have the histology checked in case a right hemicolectomy is required.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Carcinoid tumours respond well to cytotoxic chemotherapy\",\"e\":\"Carcinoid tumours do not respond well to cytotoxic chemotherapy. Optimal control results from removal of the carcinoid tumour. If this is not possible, biological activity and sometimes size may be diminished by hepatic artery embolisation, drug therapy or radiotherapv.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"Some orthopaedic surgeons are exposed to radiation when using fluoroscopy intraoperatively. Most wear protective lead coats, neck guards and make an effort to stand out of the way of the C-arm. Which malignancy is the most common following exposure to ionising radiation?\",\"lead\":\"Which malignancy is the most common following exposure to ionising radiation?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Glioma\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Colorectal cancer\",\"e\":\"There is no association between radiation exposure and colorectal cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gonadal cancer\",\"e\":\"Oocytes and spermatogenesis are radiosensitive, and radiation exposure may therefore impair fertility. However, the rate of cancer does not increase.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Leukaemia\",\"e\":\"Both leukaemia and solid tumours occur at an increased rate following exposure to ionising radiation.\\n\\nThere is no association between radiation exposure and glioma. However, there is an association with meningioma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Oral cancer\",\"e\":\"There is no association between radiation exposure and oral cancer.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"You are giving the diagnosis of a malignant tumour to a patient in the clinic. The patient asks about whether smoking may have contributed to their tumour.\\n\\nWhich of the following conditions is most strongly associated with smoking?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following conditions is most strongly associated with smoking?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute myeloid leukaemia\",\"e\":\"There is no known risk between smoking and leukaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma of the larynx\",\"e\":\"There is a significant association between smoking and carcinoma of the larynx.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma\",\"e\":\"There is no known risk between smoking and lymphoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Squamous-cell carcinoma of the cervix\",\"e\":\"There is an association between smoking and cervical cancer, but not as significant as the association between smoking and cancer of the larynx.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transitional-cell carcinoma of the bladder\",\"e\":\"There is an association between smoking and bladder cancer, but not as significant as the association between smoking and cancer of the larynx.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man is reviewed after resection of a carcinoid tumour. They would I ke to know what the future treatment may be. Which one of the following is correct regarding these tumours?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding these tumours?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carcinoid tumours are the most commonly found tumours of the appendix\",\"e\":\"Appendix tumours are most commonly of carcinoid histology, they rarely metastasise.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Carcinoid tumours are always associated with hypertension\",\"e\":\"The over-production of 5-hydroxytryptamine (5-HT) leads to vasodilation and hypotension. Adrenergic peptides can also be produced by carcinoid tumours and can result in hypertension.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cause flushing via production of adrenaline\",\"e\":\"The production of 5-hydroxytryptamine (5-HT), which is released into the systemic circulation, leads to symptoms of cutaneous flushing, diarrhoea, bronchoconstriction and right-sided cardiac valve disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Are most commonly found in the adrenal gland\",\"e\":\"Carcinoid tumours are most commonly found in the small intestine appendix.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Commonly cause constipation\",\"e\":\"The over-production of 5-hydroxytryptamine (5-HT) leads to diarrhoea.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"4. 45-year-old woman who had surgery recently is awaiting their diagnosis. They are extremely anxious to know whether or not what was removed was cancerous.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a malignant tumour?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a malignant tumour?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adenoma\",\"e\":\"An adenoma is a benign tumour of ductal or glandular epithelium. A malignant tumour of this origin is termed an adenocarcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hamartoma\",\"e\":\"Hamartomas are not true tumours but represent overgrowth of one or more cell types that have normal constituents. However, they are arranged in irregular fashion (examples are haemangioma, lymphangioma, lipoma and neurofibroma).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lipoma\",\"e\":\"A lipoma is a benign tumour of the adipose tissue. The malignant counterpart is a liposarcoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neurofibroma\",\"e\":\"A neurofibroma is a benign tumour of a nerve sheath.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sarcoma\",\"e\":\"Sarcoma is a malignant tumour arising in tissues of mesenchymal origin. Sarcomas of soft tissue and bone are rare and represent <1% of malignant neoplasms.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"You are in the colorectal clinic reviewing a patient with a lung metastasis. You advise the patient that there are no curative surgical options left and that they will see the oncologist for palliative chemotherapy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following tumour types would have the greatest metastatic potential?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following tumour types would have the greatest metastatic potential?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cholangiocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Cholangiocarcinoma is a malignant tumour and spreads via the lymphatics and the bloodstream.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chondroma\",\"e\":\"A chondroma is a benign cartilaginous tumour and does not metastasise.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Colonic adenoma\",\"e\":\"Adenomas are benign tumours but can undergo genetic mutations and progress to neoplasia. However, only 5% of colonic adenomas will progress to adenocarcinoma and malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fibroadenoma\",\"e\":\"Breast fibroadenoma does not have malignant potential.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lipoma\",\"e\":\"A lipoma is a benign tumour of adipose tissue and does not metastasise.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"You are attending a lecture on the natural history of cancer-associated lesions. You take note as you realise if diseases are treated earlier then the chances of a permanent cure are more likely. Which one of the following is a premalignant condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a premalignant condition?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acanthosis nigricans\",\"e\":\"Acanthosis nigricans is a skin condition that is associated with malignancy, but is itself not a premalignant condition. Patients typically develop pruritic hyperkeratotic plaques affecting flexor surfaces such as the axilla. The tumours associated with acanthosis nigricans are mostly intra-abdominal, classically gastric carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Familial adenomatous polyposıs\",\"e\":\"Familial adenomatous polyposis is an autosomal-dominant premalignant condition (accounting for 1% of all colorectal carcinomas). Multiple polyps are found throughout the colon and rectum by the early teens. If left untreated, the polyps will become malignant.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Keratoacanthoma\",\"e\":\"Histologically, a keratoacanthoma resembles a squamous-cell carcinoma. However, it is not premalignant. Typically, keratoacanthomas grow rapidly but do not infiltrate beyond the sweat glands. After several weeks of such growth a keratoacanthoma regresses.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Paget's disease of the nipple\",\"e\":\"Paget's disease of the nipple is a malignant condition nearly always associated with an intraductal carcinoma in situ that, if left alone, will progress to invasive cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Seborrheic keratosis\",\"e\":\"Seborrheic keratosis are benign tumours of the skin keratinocytes. There is no malignant potential.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"You have been asked to see an angry patient in clinic who has recently had a lipoma excised from her back. She received a letter from your consultant with her results that stated that she has a benign tumour. She is concerned regarding the term tumour\\n\\nWhich features are more characteristic of benign rather than malignant tumours?\",\"lead\":\"Which features are more characteristic of benign rather than malignant tumours?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anaplasia\",\"e\":\"Anaplasia is a feature of malignancy.\\n\\nInvasion is a feature of malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Capsule formation\",\"e\":\"Capsule formation is common in benign tumours but rare in malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Invasion of surrounding tissues\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A high mitotic index\",\"e\":\"A high mitotic index is a feature of malignancy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rapid growth\",\"e\":\"Most malignant tumours grow more rapidly than benign neoplasms\\n\\nAnaplasia, the potential for invasion, expansion and a high mitotic index are all features of malignancy 1200\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":70,\"stem\":\"On reviewing one of your patients' abdominal X-rays you notice calcification affecting the vessels, kidneys and stomach. You request that they be seen in clinic again rather than discharged.\\n\\nWhat one condition is most likely to be associated with this metastatic calcification?\",\"lead\":\"What one condition is most likely to be associated with this metastatic calcification?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aortic stenosis\",\"e\":\"Aortic stenosis describes the narrowing of the aortic valve and alone does not infer calcification. Aortic sclerosis (not synonymous with aortic stenosis) is calcification of the aortic valve.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Atherosclerosis\",\"e\":\"Calcification does occur in atherosclerosis, but this is dystrophic calcification.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Healing tuberculoustesions\",\"e\":\"Calcification does occur in old tuberculous lesions, but this is dystrophic calcification.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hyperparathyroidism.\",\"e\":\"Metastatic calcification is defined as 'calcification occurring in otherwise normal tissue, and mainly affects the vessels, kidneys, lungs and gastric mucosa. Renal calculi are more likely to occur in patients with hyperparathyroidism.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rheumatoid arthritis\",\"e\":\"Rheumatoid arthritis is not associated with metastatic calcification. Rheumatoid nodules are made up of collagen, fibroblasts and macrophages.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":72,\"stem\":\"You are giving the results of a recent operation to a woman who has had a 3 cm invasive ductal carcinoma excised from her breast. You describe it as a'malignant tumour in the breast:\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements correctly describes the growth of malignant tumours?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements correctly describes the growth of malignant tumours?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are characterised by contact inhibition\",\"e\":\"Malignant tumour cells lack normal contact inhibition by neighbouring cells, which normally arrest over-active growth in normal cell lines.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They contain cells which increasingly differentiate\",\"e\":\"New malignant cells produced tend to be less differentiated.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They grow because there is failure to respond to the constraints that regulate cell loss\",\"e\":\"Malignant tumours tend to proliferate in an autonomous fashion and have escaped normal external control mechanisms. This is one of the main differentiating factors between neoplasia and hyperplasia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They predominantly grow because their growth factors are greater than those in normal tissues\",\"e\":\"The growth factors of malignant cells are similar to non-malignant cells but the new cells produced tend to be less differentiated - they lack the normal phenotype.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They tend to be governed by exogenous stimulating factors\",\"e\":\"Some tumours respond to external stimuli (for example, ER +ve breast cancers respond to oestrogen). However, in most cases, malignant tumours continue to grow even if the stimulus is removed\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman is being counselled about her future risk of developing malignant disease. You suggest that surveillance scanning would be a safe option for them.\\n\\nFor which one of the following tumours is the malignant potential most often associated with tumour size?\",\"lead\":\"For which one of the following tumours is the malignant potential most often associated with tumour size?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Breast adenocarcinoma:\",\"e\":\"Although size is an important criterion in staging breast cancers, the relationship between tumour size and malignant potential is more marked for renal adenocarcinoma\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Colon adenocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"Although size is an important criterion in staging colon cancers, the relationship between tumour size and malignant potential is more marked for renal adenocarcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Prostate adenocarcinoma.\",\"e\":\"Although size is an important criterion in staging prostate cancers, the relationship between tumour size and malignant potential is more marked for renal adenocarcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal adenocarcinoma\",\"e\":\"The distinction between a benign renal adenoma and renal adenocarcinoma is commonly made on the basis of size, tumours less than 2 cm in size rarely being malignant and those greater than 3 cm in size behaving in a malignant fashion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung\",\"e\":\"Although size is an important criterion in staging lung cancers, the relationship between tumour size and malignant potential is more marked for renal adenocarcinoma.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":76,\"stem\":\"Your unit has been asked to take part in a phase Il clinical trial of a new chemotherapeutic agent. This product requires a detailed knowledge of the cell cycle.\\n\\nIn the cell cycle which one of the following statements is true?\",\"lead\":\"In the cell cycle which one of the following statements is true?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cytotoxic drugs act mainly on resting cells\",\"e\":\"Cytotoxic drugs usually act on cycling cells and can be phase-specific or non-phase-specific.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Duplication of cellular DNA occurs during G2 phase\",\"e\":\"Duplication (replication) of cellular DNA occurs during S phase, which is followed by a second growth phase (G2).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mitosis occurs during S phase\",\"e\":\"Mitosis occurs in M phase.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"RNA and proteins are synthesised during the G1 phase\",\"e\":\"RNA and protein synthesis occur during the first growth phase, G1\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The average duration of the cell cycle is approximately 10 days\",\"e\":\"The duration of the cell cycle varies from 20 to 100 h (20-24 h in rapidly growing cells).\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":78,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old man presents with increasing pain in the lumbar spine for 3 months with night pain. He has no neurological signs or symptoms. X-rays reveal a sclerotic lesion in the L4 vertebra.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely primary diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely primary diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Breast cancer\",\"e\":\"Breast cancer that spreads to bone tends to be mixed (iytic and sclerotic).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lung cancer\",\"e\":\"Lung cancer that spreads to bone tends to be mixed (lytic and sclerotic).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Prostate cancer\",\"e\":\"A prostate cancer is most likely in a man of this age, and prostate metastases tend to be sclerotic.\\n\\nCancers that commonly spread to the bone can be remembered by 'Particular Tumours Love Killing Bone':\\n\\n• Prostate • Thyroid • Lung • Kidney • Breast.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal cancer\",\"e\":\"Renal cancer that spreads to bone tends to be lytic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyroid cancer\",\"e\":\"Thyroid cancer that spreads to bone tends to be lytic.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":81,\"stem\":\"A young woman has come into clinic enquiring about testing for familial breast cancer. She has a mother who developed the disease at 65 and a grandmother who developed it at 43.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding the genetics of inherited cancers?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the genetics of inherited cancers?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Retinoblastoma (RB) is associated with inherited mutations in the RBl gene of Ch13 in 90% of cases\",\"e\":\"The RB1 gene is a tumour suppressor gene on Ch13. In inherited RB (approximately 40% of cases) there is a germ-line inactivation of the RB1 gene. This condition is usually bilateral. In the more common non-hereditary RB both of the mutations are somatic and this condition tends to affect only one eye.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"BRAT and BRCA2 mutations are associated with a 30% breast cancer risk.\",\"e\":\"The risk of developing breast cancer with the BRCA1 mutation is 60-90%, whereas with the BRCA2 mutation, this is 45-85%.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Familial adenomatous polyposis is associated with the APC (adenomatous polyposis coli) gene on Ch5\",\"e\":\"Familial adenomatous polyposis is a condition which increases the risk of colorectal carcinoma. The APC gene is found on Ch5 and is mutated in a large proportion of individuals with this condition.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Li-Fraumeni syndrome is associated with autosomal-recessive inheritance of p53 mutations\",\"e\":\"Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an autosomal-dominant condition associated with mutations in the TP53 gene (Ch17), which encodes for the p53 protein. It is associated with osteosarcoma, breast cancer, brain cancer, leukaemia and adrenocortical cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Autosomal dominant hereditary non-polyposis colon cancer (Lynch syndrome) is associated with a 45% lifetime risk of colorectal cancer\",\"e\":\"Lynch syndrome, is associated with colorectal cancer (80% lifetime risk), ovarian, endometrial, stomach, small intestine, hepatobiliary, upper urinary, brain and skin cancers. It is due to DNA mismatch repair impairment.\"}]},{\"t\":32,\"p\":84,\"stem\":\"You receive a report back from the pathology laboratory on a lesion that you excised. It states that the sample contained an overgrowth of normal cells with disordered architecture. What is the excised lesion an example of?\",\"lead\":\"What is the excised lesion an example of?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hamartoma\",\"e\":\"A hamartoma is overgrowth of normal mature cells, with disordered cell architecture.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperplasia\",\"e\":\"Hyperplasia is an increase in cell numbers, cells are of normal size and shape.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypertrophy\",\"e\":\"Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size, the number of cells remains the same.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Metaplasia\",\"e\":\"Metaplasia is reversible replacement of one cell type by another.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Teratoma\",\"e\":\"A teratoma is growth of cells from more than one germ cell line.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old man is referred with persistent haemoptysis and has a CT scan that shows probable lung cancer. Their case is being discussed at the lung MDT and it is felt a tissue biopsy should be taken first.\\n\\nWhich of the following is true regarding small-cell cancer of the lung?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true regarding small-cell cancer of the lung?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Accounts for less than 25% of all Jung cancers\",\"e\":\"Small-cell cancers of the lung account for less than one-quarter of all lung cancers. It is named after the small round cells that make up these tumours. Squamous-cell carcinoma makes up nearly 25%, adenocarcinoma 40% and large-cell carcinoma approximately 10% of all lung cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is common in elderly non-smokers\",\"e\":\"Small-cell carcinoma is a tumour of neuroendocrine origin that may exist in the classical oat- cell form or as an intermediate-cell type. Small-cell lung cancer is almost always caused by smoking while adenocarcinoma is seen in elderly non-smokers.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is frequently benign and localised at presentation\",\"e\":\"These tumours are highly malignant and are usually disseminated at presentation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is frequently associated with hypercalcemia\",\"e\":\"Small-cell lung cancers are often associated with ectopic hormone production and paraneoplastic syndromes such as adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) (Cushing's syndrome), syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (causes hyponatraemia) and myasthenic syndrome. Note that hypercalcaemia is much more frequently associated with non-small-cell lung tumours (ie due to parathyroid hormone- related peptide) than small-cell tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Has a median survival of 5 years if treated appropriately\",\"e\":\"The median survival of patients with small-cell cancer is 3 months if untreated and 12-18 months if treated.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"You are called to see a patient on the ward who is cyanosed. You look at the oxygen saturation probe and note the probe is showing 85%. Which one of the following statements best describes cyanosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements best describes cyanosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A blue colouration of the tissues\",\"e\":\"Cyanosis is a blue coloration of the tissues commonly due to the presence of deoxygenated haemoglobin, rarely it is due to methaemoglobin (reduced haemoglobin), a condition caused by drug reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Central if excess oxygen utilisation in the tissues outweighs their supply\",\"e\":\"Peripheral cyanosis occurs due to excess oxygen utilisation, outweighing supply in the peripheral tissues. Central cyanosis indicates profound arterial hypoxemia and should be treated with oxygenation while a cause is sought.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Common when there is profound anaemia\",\"e\":\"In profound anaemia there is too little haemoglobin for enough of it to be deoxygenated to generate visible blue colour. As such, cyanosis does not occur in profound anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Due to the presence of carboxyhaemoglobin in the tissues\",\"e\":\"It is the presence of deoxygenated haemoglobin, not the presence of carboxyhaemoglobin, that produces cyanosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Peripheral if the buccal mucosa is discoloured\",\"e\":\"Central cyanosis is distinguished from peripheral cyanosis by inspecting the mouth. Blue discolouration of the oral mucosa is indicative of central cyanosis.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old man is listed for an elective knee replacement. The anaesthetist requests pre- operative respiratory function tests due to a history of breathlessness.\\n\\nWhich of the following would be most consistent with a diagnosis of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following would be most consistent with a diagnosis of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Diffusion capacity decreased, FEV./FVC increased, total lung capacity reduced\",\"e\":\"Diffusion capacity and total lung capacity is characteristically decreased in restrictive lung disorders, such as idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. FEV1/FVC is either normal or slightly increased with restrictive disorders, but is always reduced in obstructive disorders.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diffusion capacity increased, FEV./FVC normal, total lung capacity increased\",\"e\":\"Total lung capacity is reduced in restrictive lung disease, while it is normal or increased in obstructive airways disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diffusion capacity norma, FEV/FVC reduced, total lung capacity reduced\",\"e\":\"FEV1/FVC reduction would be seen in obstructive airways disease, which would be reversible in asthma and irreversible in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Diffusion capacity decreased, FEV/FVC normal, total lung capacity normal\",\"e\":\"In idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, a restrictive lung disease, diffusion capacity would be decreased and the FEV1/FVC ratio would be normal or increased. In pulmonary fibrosis, however, total lung capacity would be reduced, not normal.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Diffusion capacity decreased, FEV/FVC increased, total lung capacity increased\",\"e\":\"In restrictive conditions FEV1/FVC ratio is normal or increased, but diffusion and lung capacity would be reduced.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 24-year-old woman is admitted with a ruptured ectopic pregnancy and loses a lot of blood during her treatment. She is eventually discharged, but her haemoglobin has not yet returned to normal.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true in a patient with anaemia and normal lungs?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true in a patient with anaemia and normal lungs?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arterial p(Oz) is reduced\",\"e\":\"A patient with anaemia and normal lungs typically has a normal arterial p(0z).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Arterial-venous O, concentration difference is increased\",\"e\":\"If the oxygen consumption and cardiac output are normal, the arterial-venous Oz concentration difference will also be normal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Arterial O, saturation is reduced\",\"e\":\"Because the position of the oxygen dissociation curve is typically normal, the arterial oxygen saturation is also normal.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cardiac ourput is reduced V\",\"e\":\"As a matter of fact, cardiac output is sometimes reflex increased in anaemia and if this occurs, the arterial-venous O, concentration difference will be decreased.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"p(Oz) of mixed venous blood is reduced\",\"e\":\"Although the arterial p(O,) is typically normal, the pOz) of mixed venous blood must fall. This is because mixed venous pO2 is related to oxygen delivery to the tissues. Anaemia reduces oxygen delivery, however the extraction by the tissues is the same. This results in a lower mixed venous po2.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man with end stage emphysema is admitted in respiratory failure and commenced on intermittent positive-pressure ventilation (IPPV). He slowly begins to improve and is eventually weaned from the support of the machine so that he can be discharged to a lower level of care.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is most accurate with regard to changes in physiology with IPPV?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most accurate with regard to changes in physiology with IPPV?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increases splanchnic blood flow\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreases cardiac output..\",\"e\":\"IPPV creates a positive intra-thoracic pressure (this is normally negative) and a compression tamponade, thereby reducing venous return, cardiac output and therefore blood pressure. Additionally, the alveoli may be subjected to high inflation pressures and result in subsequent p(CO) barotrauma and pneumothorax.\\n\\nIPPV leads to an increase in intra-thoracic pressure and a reduction in cardiac output, there a subsequent is reduction in liver, kidney and intestinal blood flow.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Results in hepatic failure\",\"e\":\"While blood flow is reduced to the liver, this would not necessarily result in hepatic failure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Results in hypertension\",\"e\":\"Due to the compression tamponade secondary to positive intra-thoracic pressure results in hypotension, not hypertension.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lowers intracranial pressure\",\"e\":\"Intermittent positive-pressure ventilation (IPPV) causes an increase in intracranial pressure, as do positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) ventilation and obstruction of central venous drainage.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man presents with severe rib fractures. Their pain is poorly controlled and their respiratory effort is deteriorating. An arterial blood gas (ABG) is performed to get a more accurate idea of what is happening. On ABG, the patient's p(COz) is 8 kPa (normal 4.6- 6.0 kPa). Carbon dioxide retention can cause which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Carbon dioxide retention can cause which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A decrease in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) pressure\",\"e\":\"Increased CO_ causes a rise in CSF pressure. Hyperventilation to reduce CO_ Will decrease CSF pressure in head injuries, however, studies have demonstrated that hyperventilation to induce hypocarbia does not impact on survival. Furthermore, hypocarbia results in cerebral vasoconstriction, reducing cerebral blood flow and potentially inducing ischaemia. Current guidance is to aim for a p(CO2) of 4.0 KPa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A faint pulse\",\"e\":\"The pulse is classically bounding and the patient may vasodilate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"An increase in serum potassium\",\"e\":\"CO_ retention causes a respiratory acidosis, which increases serum potassium, in addition to catecholamine release causing an increased risk of cardiac arrhythmias and sweating\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased cardiac output\",\"e\":\"Physiological studies have shown that heart rate, stroke volume, cardiac output, and mean arterial blood pressure (BP) are increased by hypercapnia: The direct and indirect effects of hypercarbia on the cardiovascular system are dual and opposite. Directly, the hypercarbia dilates the arterioles and depressed the myocardium, and indirectly, hypercarbia enhances sympathoadrenal activity by liberating catecholamines. This causes abnormal inotropic and chronotropic cardiac effects and thus concomitant increase in myocardial oxygen consumption. The indirect vascular effects of enhanced sympathoadrenal activity depend on the predominance of a- (norepinephrine) or B- (epinephrine) adrenergic activity. B- adrenergic cardiac and vascular effects prevail in most pople. This results in hypertension. tachycardia, increased cardiac output and stroke volume and decreased systemic vascular resistance.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduced plasma catecholamines\",\"e\":\"As discussed, raised CO_ increases plasma catecholamines.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"'STES: :_..\\n\\nA patient is treated with increased lung compliance. Which of the following causes this?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following causes this?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A general anaesthetic\",\"e\":\"A general anaesthetic will result in reduced compliance due to reduction in the function of the respiratory muscles as such patients require mechanical ventilation with positive airways pressures overcome the reduced compliance.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypervolaemia\",\"e\":\"Compliance increases in hypovolaemia (because there is less blood in lung and it is easier to expand).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary fibrosis\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary fibrosis makes the lung more difficult to expand, as does a general anaesthetic and any feet-up position.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The head-up position\",\"e\":\"Lung compliance is inversely proportional to the elasticity of the lung. For example, in emphysema, all elastic tissues have been destroyed, compliance increases and the lungs are easier to expand due to less elastic resistance. Lung compliance will increase when the patient is placed in the head-up position.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The lithotomy position\",\"e\":\"As discussed, compliance will decrease when the patient is supine, particularly if their feet are also raised.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"While reviewing lung function tests on a preoperative patient in clinic you note a decreased pulmonary functional residual capacity (FRC).\\n\\nWhat underlying condition could have caused this?\",\"lead\":\"What underlying condition could have caused this?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Asthma\",\"e\":\"Asthma is a reversible obstructive lung disease. In asthmatic lungs, there is obstruction of air on exhaling and therefore an increase in FRC is seen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chronic bronchitis\",\"e\":\"Again, bronchitis is an obstructive disease which limits the volume of air exhaled which subsequently increases FRC.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Emphysema\",\"e\":\"FRC is often increased in diseases where there is increased airway resistance, such as emphysema, chronic bronchitis and asthma. Additionally, in emphysema air trapping occurs in bullae adding to the functional residual capacity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Standing Upright\",\"e\":\"Standing up right aids alveoli recruitment thereby increasing FRC\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary fibrosis\",\"e\":\"FRC is the volume of gas remaining in the lung after a normal expiration. It can be calculated from the expiratory reserve volume plus the residual volume. FRC can be measured by the hel um dilution technique or body plethysmography. A reduced FRC is seen in patients with reduced lung compliance, as in diffuse interstitial fibrosis.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"You are called to review a ward patient who is short of breath. You diagnose acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).\\n\\nWhich of the following is an indirect cause of this?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is an indirect cause of this?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"The causes of ARDS can broadly be divided into direct and indirect insults to the lung. Indirect insults include massive blood transfusion, pancreatitis and fat embolus. Patients diagnosed with ARDS should be cared for in a High Dependency or Intensive Care Unit as it is associated with a high morbidity and mortality.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acute renal failure\",\"e\":\"Renal failure may be associated with ARDS, especially in the presence of multi-organ failure, however, it is not a cause of ARDS.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Aspiration\",\"e\":\"Direct insults include lung contusion, aspiration and pneumonia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lung contusion\",\"e\":\"A lung contusion would constitute a direct insult to the lung and can result in ARDS.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pneumonia\",\"e\":\"Again, pneumonia can result in ARDS and is considered a direct insult.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"You attend a post mortem for one of your patients who you suspect died of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). There is contention regarding the cause of death so a tissue biopsy from the lung is taken for further analysis.\\n\\nWhat histological features would help confirm this cause of death?\",\"lead\":\"What histological features would help confirm this cause of death?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"25-y Bulla formation\",\"e\":\"Bulla formation occurs in primarily in emphysematous disease due to destruction of the lung parenchyma's elastic tissues.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glandular hyperplasia\",\"e\":\"Glandular hyperplasia is non-specific and does not occur in ARDS.\\n\\nGranulomas are a non-specific immune reaction to either infection or foreign bodies. Macrophages and other immune cells surround either bacteria or the foreign body forming a granuloma. While granulomas may form in ARDS, they are non-specific.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Granulomas\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Interstitial fibrosis\",\"e\":\"Physiological and histological features of ARDS include: increased capillary permeability; interstitial and alveolar oedema; fibrin exudation; hyaline membrane formation, and, later, diffuse late interstitial and alveolar fibrosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Synovial membrane formation\",\"e\":\"Hyaline, not synovial, membrane formation occurs in ARDS. The synovial membrane is found in musculoskeletal joints.\"}]},{\"t\":33,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing the pulmonary function tests for one of your preoperative patients who is known to have airways disease.\\n\\nWhich pulmonary function test results would assist you in confirming whether this is restrictive or obstructive?\",\"lead\":\"Which pulmonary function test results would assist you in confirming whether this is restrictive or obstructive?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Forced expiratory volume: forced vital capacity (FEVI: FVC) ratio of < 0.75 typically occurs in obstructive disease\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary function tests are normally performed in a special lung function laboratory. They are not usually performed at the bedside. Peak expiratory flow rate (PER) can be used as a simple bedside test for obstruction to expiration. This approximates to the FEVI, the maximum amount of air a patient can exhale in the first second of expiration. FVC refers to the volume of air a patient can exhale after maximal inspiration. The ratio FEV1: FVC describes the proportion of the maximum amount of air a patient can hold in their lungs, which they can expel in the first second of respiration. In obstructive airways disease the FEV1 is usually decreased with a normal or reduced FVC, giving a decreased FEV1 : FVC ratio ie < 0.75.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Forced expiratory volume: forced vital capacity (FEV1: FVC) ratio of < 0.75 typically occurs in restrictive disease\",\"e\":\"A reduced FEV1 : FVC ration would be indicative of obstructive, not restrictive lung disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Obstructive disease typically results in an increased FEV1\",\"e\":\"Obstructive disease would result in a reduced FEV1 as air is impeded during the exhale so reducing the volume of air that can be exhaled in one second.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Obstructive disease typically results in an increased FVC\",\"e\":\"Obstructive airway disease results in a reduced FVC due to obstruction of air on the exhale.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Restrictive disease typically results in an increased FEV1\",\"e\":\"In restrictive airways disease the FEV1 and FVC are both usually decreased, giving a normal FEV1: FVC ratio.\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-oid woman attends a plastic surgery firm for resection of a suspected malignant melanoma. The patient wants to know what their prognosis is likely to be.\\n\\nWhich one of the following features suggests an aggressive malignant melanoma?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following features suggests an aggressive malignant melanoma?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amelanotic lesions\",\"e\":\"Amelanotic lesions are associated with a poor prognosis due to difficult recognitior but are not a sign of aggressiveness. In amelanotic melanomas the cells do not make melanin, the dark pigment that gives most lesions their brownish colour. They can be pink, purple, red or normal skin colour and are therefore difficult to recognise. Their atypical appearance often leads to delay in diagnosis and advanced lesions are more difficult to treat. The prognosis is poor and the rate of recurrence and metastases is high.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Located on the arm\",\"e\":\"The most aggressive cutaneous lesions are those on the head and neck as they are more commonly associated with vascular and neural invasion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Flat lesions\",\"e\":\"Flat lesions grow laterally before invading vertically. Nodular melanomas are the most aggressive type of melanoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Low mitotic rate\",\"e\":\"Lesions with higher mitotic rates (rapidly dividing cells) are more aggressive as they are larger with greater potential for metastases and therefore have a poorer prognosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Satellite lesions\",\"e\":\"Satellite lesions are areas of tumour located away from but within 2 cm of the primary site. It represents an aggressive tumour:\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the plastic surgery clinic who had a skin lesion excised and are. reading the pathology report. You need to decide how urgently they should be seen in the busy outpatient clinic.\\n\\nWhich one of the following has malignant potential?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following has malignant potential?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Basal-cell papilloma\",\"e\":\"Basal-cell papillomas also known as seborrhoeic keratoses are benign growths and have no malignant potential.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Keratoacanthoma\",\"e\":\"Keratoacanthoma is a rapidly growing benign lesion typically arising around a hair follicle. It has no malignant potential..\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Molluscum contagiosum\",\"e\":\"Molluscum contagiosum is a viral infective lesion that most commonly affects children. They are benign with no malignant potential.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Solar keratosis,\",\"e\":\"Solar or actinic keratosis is rough patches of skin caused by damage from ultraviolet light exposure. They have a potential to become squamous-cell carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spitz naevus\",\"e\":\"A Spitz naevus is a benign skin tumour without malignant potential. They are melanocytic lesions and can be mistaken for malignant melanoma.\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the plastic surgical clinic referred by their GP with a query of squamous-cell carcinoma. They are listed for surgical excision biopsy of the lesion.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of these tumours?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct in respect of these tumours?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Are a rare cause of skin cancer in the UK\",\"e\":\"Squamous-cell carcinomas are the second most common skin cancer in the UK behind basal- cell carcınoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Develop from Merkel cells\",\"e\":\"Squamous-cell carcinoma arises from the epidermis and extends into the dermis, it has nests of squamous epithelial cells. A Merkel-cell carcinoma is a rare but highly aggressive tumour derived from the Merkel-cell population, which are primitive neuroendocrine (APUD) cells in the skin. These tumours occur mostly in elderly people and usually appear as skin-coloured or bluish-red nodules often on the face, head or neck. The prognosis is poor, with local recurrence developing in 40% of cases and nodal metastases in up to 65%. Approximately half the patients are dead within 2 years.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Are often confused with dermatofibroma\",\"e\":\"A dermatofibroma is a benign fibrous nodule most often arising on the lower legs, its cause is unknown but has been attributed to insect bites. A squamous-cell carcinoma usually appears as a scaly red raised area of skin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"May develop from Bowen's disease\",\"e\":\"Bowen's disease is also called squamous-cell carcinoma in situ. The cancer cells are restricted to the epidermis. It predominantly occurs on the legs as a result of sun exposure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Are not caused by sun exposure\",\"e\":\"Sun damage is a major risk factor, resulting in carcinoma in situ, followed by an invasive carcinoma.\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A 12-year-old boy is being followed up in a dermatology clinic as he has a history of xeroderma pigmentosum and has previously developed multiple epitheliomas. You are happy with the progress he is making but ensure that you book him into the clinic for ongoing surveillance of his disease\\n\\nWhich one of the following other conditions also predisposes to cutaneous malignancies?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following other conditions also predisposes to cutaneous malignancies?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Actinic keratosis\",\"e\":\"Actinic keratosis, also known as solar keratosis, represents the cumulative effect of ultraviolet light exposure. The condition appears as rough patches of skin and the lesions have the potential to become squamous-cell carcinoma\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acne vulgaris\",\"e\":\"Acne vulgaris is a common skin condition involving blockage and/or inflammation of the hair follicles and their accompanying sebaceous glands. It most commonly affects the face but can also spread to the back and chest. It has no malignant potential.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Keratoacanthoma\",\"e\":\"Keratoacanthoma is a rapidly growing benign lesion typically arising around a hair follicie. It has no malignant potential.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Necrobiosis lipoidica\",\"e\":\"Necrobiosis lipoidica is often seen in patients with diabetes mellitus (although not exclusively). It is a disorder of collagen degeneration with a granulomatous response, fat deposition and thickening of blood vessel walls. It appears as a tender yellowish brown patch often on the lower legs. It does not predispose to cutaneous malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Seborrhoeic keratosis\",\"e\":\"Seborrhoeic keratosis is a benign skin condition with no malignant potential. It develops from a proliferation of epidermal cells and appears as waxy scaly elevated black/brown growths. Typically affecting the face, chest, shoulders and back.\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man is admitted following skin contact with azo dye. There is a significant skin reaction which eventually improves but you are concerned about possible future disease risk.\\n\\nOf the following conditions, which is known to be caused by b-naphthylamine?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following conditions, which is known to be caused by b-naphthylamine?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adenocarcinoma of the stomach\",\"e\":\"Infection with Helicobacter pylori which causes ulcers and increases the risk of stomach cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Breast cancer\",\"e\":\"There are several risk factors for the development of breast cancer including: advancing age, family history of breast or ovarian cancer, BRCA-1 or - 2 genes, ostrogen exposure (early menarche, late menopause, having no children or children later in life, HRT, OCP), obesity and radiation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bladder cancer\",\"e\":\"Beta-naphthylamine (used to make azo dyes) exposure has been strongly linked with bladder tumours. The human bladder mucosa secretes the enzyme B-glucuronidase, which splits B-naphthylamine and releases a carcinogen. Beta-naphthylamine is an aromatic amine and is a good example of remote carcinogenesis. The average latent period from exposure to disease development is 15 years. Other strong links with bladder carcinoma are benzidine exposure and smoking\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lymphoma\",\"e\":\"Risk factors for Hodgkin lymphoma include previous Epstein-Barr virus, weakened immune system and a family history. For non-Hodgkin lymphoma risk factors are advancing age, exposure to chemicals including some insecticides, radiation exposure, immunodeficiency, HIV infection, Epstein-Barr virus and Helicobacter pylori.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Small-cell carcinoma of the lung\",\"e\":\"The most significant risk factor is smoking.\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old school teacher presents with a dark irregularly pigmented lesion on her left forearm and palpable lymph nodes in the left axilla. Doctors are concerned that she may have metastatic melanoma and ensure she is discussed at the skin MDT.\\n\\nOf the following statements, which is commonly correct regarding metastases?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following statements, which is commonly correct regarding metastases?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Metastases commonly occurs transluminally\",\"e\":\"Metastasis is the spread of a tumour from one part of the body to another that is not directly interconnected. Various factors play a role: loss of cellular cohesion and cytoarchitecture, initiation of angiogenesis and adhesion molecules. Transluminal metastases are uncommon..\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Metastases to regional lymph nodes in the abdomen usually follows the pattern of venous drainage\",\"e\":\"Lymphatic drainage routes generally follow the path of arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Metastases via the lymphatics is a common feature of basal-cell carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Basal-cell carcinomas invade locally and metastases are rare occurring in only 0.1% of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Metastases via the lymphatics is a common feature of osteosarcoma\",\"e\":\"Osteosarcoma typically spreads via the bloodstream, most commonly to the lungs. It can also spread to other bones.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Metastases via the blood is a common feature of prostatic carcinoma\",\"e\":\"The extensive venous plexus surrounding the prostate makes haematogenous spread likely. Prostate cancer can also spread by direct extension or lymphatics.\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing an HIV-positive patient in the plastics clinic referred with a skin lesion, who is concerned that it may be malignant. You suspect Kaposi's sarcoma.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding Kaposi's sarcoma is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding Kaposi's sarcoma is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"In non-AIDS patients it is rapidly fatal within a year of diagnosis\",\"e\":\"The course of the disease in non-AIDS patients is slow, and frequently they survive for more than 10 years after the onset of the disease. This pattern is contradictory to that of AIDS- related disease, which can be rapidly fatal. With improvements in treatment, however it can now be controlled for many years.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"In the early stages it is characterised by a single firm plaque\",\"e\":\"Kaposi's sarcoma is a multicentric, proliferative disorder of vasoformative tissue that usually follows a malignant neoplastic course. In the early stages, the lesions present as purplish pigmented patches of purpura with haemosiderosis, mostly over the lower legs and feet, similar to a bruise. Unlike a bruise they do not lose their colour when pressed. Later, indurated plaques and firm vascular polypoid excrescences appear.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Its association with AIDS is mostly seen in older or middle-aged individuals\",\"e\":\"Kaposi's sarcoma is of two major types: AIDS related and non-AIDS related (classic, endemic, transplant related). AIDS-related disease is mostly seen in young people while non-AIDS- related disease generally affects older or middle-aged individuals.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is usually treated by radiotherapy\",\"e\":\"The treatment of choice for Kaposi's sarcoma depends on the type, size and the patient's general health. Not all non-AIDS-related Kaposi's sarcoma needs treating but if the lesions are large or very visible they can be treated with radiotherapy or chemotherapy. In transplant related Kaposi's sarcoma reducing or changing the immunosuppressive drugs can improve it, otherwise radiotherapy or chemotherapy may be necessary. In AIDS-related Kaposi's sarcoma treatment can be with chemotherapy or interferon but the side-effects of interferon treatment can be severe and not all patients will be able to tolerate this. There is little reason to excise Kaposi's sarcoma surgically since the disease is multicentric and recurs rapidly\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It should be biopsied to confirm diagnosis\",\"e\":\"Biopsy confirmation of the skin lesions is essential. However, caution should be exercised when handling such specimens in view of the potential for transmission of AIDS-related disease from blood products and biopsy tissue.\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman presents to outpatients with a darkly pigmented, 5mm lesion on her trunk. On further questioning, she reports occasional haemoptysis and a CT scan of the chest reveals obvious metastatic disease.\\n\\nA diagnosis of malignant melanoma is made but what is the stage?\",\"lead\":\"A diagnosis of malignant melanoma is made but what is the stage?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Stage O\",\"e\":\"Metastatic disease is stage IV. Stage O equates only to melanoma in situ.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Stage IA/B\",\"e\":\"Stages IA and IB relate to cancers with no lymph node or distant metastatic deposits. Lesions are <2 mm in thickness without ulceration or thinner than 1mm with ulceration. The 5-year survival rate is around 92-98%\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Stage IIA/B/C\",\"e\":\"Stage Il melanomas are categorised by a larger lesion, but with no local lymph node involvement and no evidence of metastatic disease. The 5-year survival rate is any where from 81% to 53%.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Stage III\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stage IV\",\"e\":\"Stage IV melanomas include any cancer with evidence of metastatic spread. The survival at 5 years is around 15-20%\\n\\nStage III includes any size lesion with any degree of ulceration. Its unique identifier is local nodal involvement. The 5-year survival rate varies from 78% to 40%\\n\\nStaging of cancers can be either clinical or pathological, but it is important to understand the implications of each on the patient's prognosis and management. Even locally advanced cancers may be treated with excision, whereas metastatic disease will often require systemic treatment. Malignant melanoma is a common cancer with clear guidelines.\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old man is seen with an ulcerating, 2.5mm deep, malignant melanoma with a single lymph node positive for disease. He has stage IIIB disease with T3bN1bMO.\\n\\nWhat is his estimated chance of 5-year survival?\",\"lead\":\"What is his estimated chance of 5-year survival?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"19%\",\"e\":\"Although metastatic melanoma carries with it a poor prognosis, even stage IV disease has a 40% survival rate at 5 years, dropping to 24% at 10 years\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"39%\",\"e\":\"This would be a sensible estimate for stage IV disease, which represents patients with evidence of metastatic disease.\\n\\nStage IIIA disease is where there is no evidence of ulceration of the primary lesion. This infers a less aggressive disease process and carries with it a much better prognosis than stage IIIB disease where the primary lesions are ulcerating.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"59%\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"79%\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"99%\",\"e\":\"Malignant melanoma, even if stage IA, has a higher mortality rate than this with only a 97% survival rate at 5 years.\\n\\nIt is important to understand the basic principles of staging of various common malignancies and to be able to sensibly judge a prognosis based on them. As a general rule higher staged cancers have a worse survival, but the tumour type is important.\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man is seen with a possible squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) on his chest. He would like to know what the rate of SCCs is in men versus women and where they tend to occur.\\n\\nWhich of the following best describes this?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following best describes this?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"More likely in men than women and more likely in sun-exposed areas\",\"e\":\"Although SCCs can occur in normal healthy skin, they are by far the most common in sun- exposed areas. Areas of actinic keratosis, leukoplakia in the mouth or even chronic ulcers can give rise to SCCs. Immunosuppression also leads to a higher rate of SCCs. Men have a rate of SCCs approaching double that of women; most of this variation can be explained by sun exposure, including on a thinning scalp.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"More likely in women than men and more likely in sun-exposed areas\",\"e\":\"SCCs are more common in men than women and this is mostly accounted for by increased sun exposure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"More likely in men than women but not more common in sun-exposed areas\",\"e\":\"SCCs are more common in men than women and this seems to be related to exposure to the main risk factor, sun exposure. Any chronic inflammation can lead to SCC formation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"More likely in women than men but not more common in sun-exposed areas\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect on both accounts as SCCs are related to sun exposure and accumulated damage. This damage is more common in men than women, which accounts for most of the variation in rates.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Equally common in men and women but not more common in sun-exposed areas\",\"e\":\"Sun exposure and other forms of chronic inflammation lead to the formation of SCCs. The exposure to the main risk factor, sunlight, is more common in men than women and reflects the higher rate of SCCs in men.\"}]},{\"t\":34,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman currently being treated for malignant melanoma has two first-degree relatives who have also been diagnosed - her mother and older sister. She has a young daughter and they are referred on to a genetic counselling service.\\n\\nWhat genes have been highlighted as being a high risk for development of melanoma?\",\"lead\":\"What genes have been highlighted as being a high risk for development of melanoma?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CDKN2A, BRCA1, CDK4\",\"e\":\"Familial melanoma is recognised in around 1-2% of patients when two first-degree relatives also have melanoma. Although many genetic causes are unrecognised and unstudied, many familial links have been found. BRCA1 carries with it a higher risk of melanoma, along with breast and ovarian cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gorlin syndrome, PTCH2\",\"e\":\"Both Gorlin syndrome and PTCH2 are associated with basal cell cancer formation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Rombo syndrome, Bazex-Dupré-Christol syndrome\",\"e\":\"Rombo syndrome is characterised by milia, atrophoderma and increased rates of basal cell cancer. Bazex-Dupré-Christol syndrome results in hypohidrosis, milia, follicular atrophoderma and basal cell cancers in teens and patients in their early 20s.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Treacher Collins syndrome\",\"e\":\"Treacher Collins syndrome, also known as mandibulofacial dysostosis, is an autosomal dominant syndrome that results in craniofacial defects with normal intelligence.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Polycystic kidney disease\",\"e\":\"Polycystic kidney disease is associated with intracranial aneurysms, renal failure and cyst formation in other organs.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"Nitric oxide is biologically derived from which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Nitric oxide is biologically derived from which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cyclic GMP\",\"e\":\"Its actions increase the levels of intracellular cyclic guanosine monophosphate (CGMP).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Endothelium-derived relaxing factor\",\"e\":\"Nitric oxide used to be called endothelium-derived relaxing factor.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"GTN\",\"e\":\"Glyceryl trinitrate (GTN) is a synthetic compound that relaxes vascular tone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"I-Arginine\",\"e\":\"Nitric oxide is derived from I-arginine by nitric oxide synthase.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Nitrous oxide\",\"e\":\"Nitrous oxide, also known as 'laughing gas', is often used in obstetrics and trauma for pain relief.\\n\\nNitric oxide (NO), which used to be known as 'endothelium-derived relaxing factor', is a local cellular messenger. It is derived from I-arginine (an amino acid) by nitric oxide synthase. Its actions increase the levels of intracellular cGMP (cyclic guanosine monophosphate), which has effects depending on which cell it is acting upon. Glyceryl trinitrate is a synthetic prodrug. NO has also been implicated in septic shock, adult respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) and inflammation. Nitrous oxide, also known as 'laughing gas', is often used in obstetrics and trauma for pain relief.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man on the high dependency unit is has had an arterial blood gas. They have an arterial line in situ to enable regular monitoring.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding the pH of the extracellular fluid?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding the pH of the extracellular fluid?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is increased in hypovolaemic shock\",\"e\":\"pH is decreased and the patient becomes more acidotic in any type of shock.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is maintained at between pH 7.45 and 7.65\",\"e\":\"It is maintained at pH7.35-7.45.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It can be regulated by the kidneys\",\"e\":\"pH is tightly controlled by buffer systems bicarbonate/carbonic anhydrase, phosphate, plasma proteins, and haemoglobin, with additional regulation by the lungs and kidneys.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is increased after a cardiac arrest\",\"e\":\"After a cardiac arrest the patient is often acidotic due to poor tissue perfusion and an increase in the anaerobic metabolism.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is controlled by the liver\",\"e\":\"The main control of pH is through buffers (bicarbonate, phosphate, proteins) and the lungs and kidneys.\\n\\npH falls (ie becomes more acid) in any type of shock because poor tissue perfusion results in a metabolic acidosis. Normal pH is 7.35-7.45. pH is tightly controlled by buffer systems (bicarbonate/carbonic anhydrase, phosphate and plasma proteins/haemoglobin) with additional regulation by the lungs and kidneys.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old woman is diagnosed with papillary thyroid cancer and undergoes resectional surgery. Post radical thyroidectomy, the patient is complaining of perioral tingling, pins and needles affecting her hands and has Chvosteks's sign.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely biochemical abnormality?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely biochemical abnormality?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"St Hyponatraemia\",\"e\":\"Hyponatraemia symptoms include confusion, nausea, headache, fatigue and irritability.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypercalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypercalcaemia presents with symptoms such as constipation, mood disturbance, thirst, kidney stones, fatigue and confusion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypocalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Symptoms of hypocalcaemia include tetany, paraesthesia (especially peri-oral numbness and tingling) and confusion. Chvosteks's sign may be elicited in hyporcalcaemic states and is the involuntary twitching of facial muscles in response to tapping over the facial nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemia\",\"e\":\"Hyperkalaemia presents with symptoms similar to hypokalaemia including palpitations, chest pain, fatigue, muscle weakness and numbness.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypokalemia\",\"e\":\"Hypokalacmia presents with symptoms including palpations, chest pain, fatigue, constipation and muscle weakness.\\n\\nCauses of hypocalcaemia include hypoparathyroicism, vitamin D deficiency and renal disease. Primary hypoparathyroidism is a state of inadequate parathyroid hormone (PTH) activity and is most often iatrogenic in atiology. Most commonly this occurs during thyroid surgery, secondary to accidental damage or removal of the parathyroid glands. Also associated with hypocalcaemia is Trousseau's sign, which is carpopaedal spasm seen following a reduction in blood flow to the hand that is elicited by the inflation a blood pressure cuff above systolic pressure for 3 min.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old man develops breathlessness on the ward and undergoes a chest X-ray. This shows a pleural effusion with less than 2.5 g of protein per 100 ml of fluid. This is most likely to be caused by which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This is most likely to be caused by which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bronchial carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Malignancy causes an exudative pleural effusion rather than a transudate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mitral regurgitation\",\"e\":\"Mitral regurgitation is likely to be associated with left ventricular failure causing back pressure on the pulmonary circulation. This then results in a transudate pleural effusion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pneumonia\",\"e\":\"Infections such as pneumonia would result in an exudative pleural effusion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tuberculosis\",\"e\":\"Tuberculosis would cause an exudative pleural effusion and would have protein levels >2.5 g per 100 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tricuspid regurgitation\",\"e\":\"Tricuspid regurgitation is often secondary to an enlarged right ventricle and causes a pulsatile liver, peripheral oedema and ascites.\\n\\nAn effusion with less than 2.5 g of protein per 100 ml is a transudate: Lights criteria describes an exudate if one of the following is present:\\n\\n• Pleural protein serum protein >0.5, • Pleural LDH:serum LDH >0.6, • Effusion LDH level greater than 2/3 of the upper range of serum LDH\\n\\nTypically, the cause of an exudative effusion includes infection, malignancy or inflammatory causes, whereas transudates are by cardiac failure, nephrotic syndrome or atelectasis, although exceptions do occur.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding carbon dioxide?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding carbon dioxide?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is carried on the haemoglobin molecule as carboxyhaemoglobin\",\"e\":\"Carbon monoxide, rather than carbon dioxide, is carried on haemoglobin as carboxyhaemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemoglobin acts as a buffer for CO,\",\"e\":\"Carbon dioxide produces H+ when dissolved in blood. This is buffered mainly by haemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"In the blood it increases the oxygen affinity of haemoglobin\",\"e\":\"It decreases the oxygen affinity of haemoglobin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is less soluble than oxygen in blood\",\"e\":\"Carbon dioxide is about 25 times more soluble in blood than oxygen.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It combines reversibly with myoglobin\",\"e\":\"Oxygen, rather than carbon dioxide, combines reversibly with myoglobin.\\n\\nCarbon dioxide (COz) decreases the oxygen binding power of haemoglobin (Hb) and it is 25 times more soluble in blood than is oxygen. CO_ is dissolved in the blood and via carbonic anhydrase forms hydrogen and bicarbonate ions. The bicarbonate diffuses out of the cell in exchange for chloride ions, the so-called chloride shift.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted with severe sepsis, they have a low urine output and you decide to give them a fluid challenge of the nearest crystalloid to hand. The nurse brings you a bag of Hartmann's solution.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is found in Hartmann's solution?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is found in Hartmann's solution?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Phosphate\",\"e\":\"Phosphate is not tound in Hartmann's solution\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Glucose\",\"e\":\"There is no glucose in Hartmann's solution.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lactate\",\"e\":\"28 mmol of lactate is found in 1 litre of Hartmann's solution. This undergoes gluconeogenesis to be turned into glucose.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Magnesium\",\"e\":\"Magnesium is not found in Hartmann's solution.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Bicarbonate\",\"e\":\"Bicarbonate is not found in Hartman's solution. However some of the lactate is oxidised using up hydrogen ions and generating bicarbonate.\\n\\nThe constituents of Hartmann's solution (per litre) include sodium 130 mmol, chloride 109 mmol, potassium 4 mmol, calcium 1.5 mmol and lactate 28 mmol. It does not contain any magnesium. Hartmann's solution is formulated to contain as many electrolytes as are essential for tissue function. Most of the lactate is converted by gluconeogenesis into glucose. This along with oxidation of the remaining lactate consumes hydrogen ions generating OH- which combines with CO_ to form bicarbonate. Therefore, the overall effect of lactate metabolism is a mild alkalinisation and together with the generation of glucose makes Hartmann's solution an ideal crystalloid for the resuscitation of patients.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following can result in a hypokalaemia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following can result in a hypokalaemia?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Salbutamol\",\"e\":\"Salbutamol results in the movement of potassium ions into the cells, causing hypokalaemia. It is used in the treatment of hyperkalaemia for this reason.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Potassium sparing diuretics\",\"e\":\"Potassium sparing diuretic such as spironolactone can cause hyperkalaemia rather than hypokalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Renal failure\",\"e\":\"Renal failure can precipitate hyperkalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors\",\"e\":\"ACE inhibitors can result in hyperkalaemia as they reduce renal function.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"Metabolic acidosis can result in hyperkalaemia due to the shift out of the cell with the hydrogen ions.\\n\\nThe sodium potassium ATPase pump exchanges intracellular sodium ions for extracellular potassium ions and is driven by the conversion of ATP to ADP. It is located on cell membranes and is the mechanism that ensures that sodium remains largely extracellular and potassium intracellular. It can also be driven by B sympathetic receptors. Therefore a B- agonist like salbutamol can reduce serum potassium levels and is used as a second line agent along with insulin in the emergency treatment of hyperkalemia.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following constituents is present in Hartmann's solution?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following constituents is present in Hartmann's solution?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"Explanation it\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Glucose\",\"e\":\"Glucose is not found in Hartmann's solution, however lactate can be converted by gluconeogenesis to create glucose.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"SI Zinc\",\"e\":\"Zinc is not found in any of the fluids used.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Magnesium\",\"e\":\"Magnesium is not present in Hartmann's solution.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"HCO;-\",\"e\":\"Bicarbonate is not present in Hartmann's solution. However the lactate can be metabolised to HCO5.\\n\\nThe composition of Hartmann's solution is as follows:\\n\\n• Na* = 130 mmol/l • C/- = 109 mmol/i • K* = 4 mmol/l • Cart = 1.5 mmol/l • Lactate = 28 mmol/l.\\n\\nThe osmolality of Hartmann's solution is equivalent to plasma. The lactate present in the solution is metabolised in vivo to form HCO: Bicarbonate is not added to Hartmann's solution as this would result in the precipitation of calcium carbonate in the storage container.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lactate\",\"e\":\"There is 28 mmol of lactate in Hartmann's solution:\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A patient is found to have euvolaemic hyponatraemia.\\n\\nWhich of the following conditions would be expected on their subsequent investigations?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following conditions would be expected on their subsequent investigations?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Diabetes insipidus\",\"e\":\"In diabetes insipidus there is excess water loss compared with sodium, which could lead to hypernatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH)\",\"e\":\"SIADH causes excess water retention over sodium retention by promoting water reabsorption in the collecting ducts of the kidneys. This results in hyponatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diabetes mellitus\",\"e\":\"Due to polyuria and polydipsia, there is excess water loss that may lead to hypernatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Conn syndrome\",\"e\":\"Conn syndrome (hyperaldosteronism) would cause hypernatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cushing syndrome\",\"e\":\"Cushing syndrome would cause hypernatraemia by promoting excess sodium retention over water retention.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following options is true regarding potassium?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is true regarding potassium?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Concentration in plasma is low in chronic adrenal insufficiency\",\"e\":\"In adrenal insufficiency the potassium is normally raised as aldosterone secretion is reduced.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is raised in metabolic alkalosis\",\"e\":\"It is raised in a metabolic acidosis rather than alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is the main extracellular cation\",\"e\":\"The main extracellular cation is sodium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Plasma level is a reliable index of total body potassium\",\"e\":\"As the majority of potassium is intracellular, the plasma level of potassium is not a reliable index of total body potassium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Potassium loss may be a feature of renal tubular dysfunction\",\"e\":\"The loss of potassium ions may feature in both type 1 (distal) and type 2 (proximal) renal tubular acidosis.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following would lower the plasma potassium concentration?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would lower the plasma potassium concentration?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute tubular necrosis (oliguric phase) Fiu\",\"e\":\"Acute tubular necrosis would reduce renal potassium excretion and sodium reabsorption, and thus elevate plasma potassium. However, there is a risk of hypokalaemia during the polyuric (recovery phase) of acute tubular necrosis disease progression\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Extracelular fluid depletion\",\"e\":\"Extracellular fluid depletion leads to activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, increasing renal sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoaldosteronism\",\"e\":\"Hypoaldosteronism would raise the potassium concentration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"In metabolic acidosis the potassium concentration is increased. It falls in metabolic alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Taking an angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor regularly\",\"e\":\"ACE inhibitors can cause hyperkalaemia as a side-effect.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"You are called to see an agitated 85-year-old man on the surgical ward admitted under the urology team after a operation on his prostate. His urea and electrolytes levels (U&Es) reveal: sodium 121 mmol/1 (135-45 mmol/I); potassium 4.1 mmol/1 (3.5-5.0 mmol/1) What is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"0 mmol/1) What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Diabetes insipidus\",\"e\":\"In diabetes insipidus there is excess water loss compared with sodium that could lead to a hypernatraemia rather than a hyponatraemia.\\n\\nExcess steroids lead to hypernatraemia and hypokalaemia due to their mineralocorticoid effects on the distal convoluted tubule, namely sodium and water retention and potassium\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Excess steroid use\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypovolaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypovolaemia leads to hypernatraemia, as the kidneys try to reabsorb sodium to retain the water.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pyrexia of unknown origin\",\"e\":\"Pyrexia leads to water loss and hence hypernatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"TURP syndrome\",\"e\":\"Irrigation during transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP) may lead to excessive water absorption and therefore hyponatraemia (the TURP syndrome).\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"You are doing the ward round on the surgical high dependency unit while your Consultant goes to start the clinic. The nursing staff tell you a patient in the bay is hypokalaemic.\\n\\nWhich one of the following options is correct regarding low serum potassium levels?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is correct regarding low serum potassium levels?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Produces peaked T-waves on the ECG\",\"e\":\"Peaked T-waves are a sign of hyperkalaemia on an ECG:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Causes the heart to arrest in diastole\",\"e\":\"Hyperkalaemia, rather than hypokalaemia inhibits the myocardium, leading to ventricular standstill, and can cause the heart to arrest in diastole.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is seen in metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"Metabolic acidosis results in hyperkalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"May occur following extensive muscle trauma\",\"e\":\"Extensive muscle trauma or rhabdomyolysis would cause hyperkalaemia due to the release of potassium from the intracellular space.\\n\\n1007\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"May be seen in pancreatic fistulae\",\"e\":\"The pancreatic juices are rich in potassium and the presence of a high-output fistula may lead to hypokalaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"You are asked to record fluid levels for the next 24 h for a surgical patient on the ward. The nurse specialist suggests a 5% dextrose intravenous infusion.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding this type of fluid?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding this type of fluid?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1S. Is isotonic on administration\",\"e\":\"It is an isotonic fluid on administration, however the glucose is quickly metabolised and the resulting solution is hypotonic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Remains within the extracellular intravascular space for at least 1 hour\",\"e\":\"The glucose is quickly metabolised leaving just water, which is hypotonic. It is then evenly distributed to all the fluid compartments.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is a good mode of resuscitation in the shocked patient\",\"e\":\"As it becomes hypotonic once the glucose is metabolised the fluid rapidly spreads out through all the fluid compartments. Only a small amount remains in the intravascular compartment, meaning it is a poor fluid choice for resuscitation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"May give rise to type II respiratory failure\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary oedema caused by fluid overload can result in type 1 (hypoxemic) respiratory failure - in which case the p. (CO.) level will be normal or low. This is the most common type of respiratory failure. Type Il (hypercapnic) respiratory failure is more commonly seen in conditions such as severe airway disorders, chest wall abnormalities and overdose.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Contains 60 kcal/I (~251 J)\",\"e\":\"It contains 200 kcal/1.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"You are asked to prescribe Hartmann's solution for one of your patients. They are currently tachycardic and you think it is a reasonable crystalloid to use as a fluid challenge.\\n\\nWhat are the constituents of Hartmann's?\",\"lead\":\"What are the constituents of Hartmann's?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hartmann's solution contains calcium, bicarbonate and potassium\",\"e\":\"Hartmann's contains no bicarbonate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hartmann's solution contains calcium, sodium and bicarbonate\",\"e\":\"Hartmann's solution contains calcium and sodium but no bicarbonate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hartmann's solution contains sodium, bicarbonate and potassium\",\"e\":\"Both sodium and potassium are present in Hartmann's solution, however bicarbonate is not part of the fluid\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hartmann's solution contains sodium, potassium and lactate\",\"e\":\"Sodium, potassium and lactate are all present within Hartmann's solution.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hartmann's solution contains sodium, potassium and modified gelatins\",\"e\":\"Hartmann's solution is a crystalloid not a colloid and gelatins are not present.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old woman presents with non-specific malaise and nausea. A routine electrocardiogram (ECG) shows inverted T-waves and her serum electrolytes reveal hypokalaemia.\\n\\nThis can result from which one of the following options?\",\"lead\":\"This can result from which one of the following options?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute tubular necrosis\",\"e\":\"Acute tubular necrosis is a cause of acute renal failure and would result in hyperkalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Addison's disease\",\"e\":\"Addison's disease leads to a hyperkalaemia due to inadequate aldosterone secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Conn's syndrome\",\"e\":\"In Conn's syndrome, there is excessive production of aldosterone which causes sodium retention and potassium loss leading to hypokalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Acute kidney injury\",\"e\":\"A patient with acute kidney injury (AKI) is more likely to have hyperkalaemia due to reduced renal excretion of potassium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ramipril\",\"e\":\"A side-effect of an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor is hyperkalaemia\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man consults his family doctor complaining of feeling 'tired all the time'. His haemoglobin is 12.5 g/dl (normal value 13-17 g/dl) and mean cell volume (MCV) is 100 fl (normal value 82-102 fl). Examination of cells does not indicate any megaloblastic changes.\\n\\nWhich one of the following investigations can help in the diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following investigations can help in the diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Measurement of red cell folate concentration\",\"e\":\"Folate deficiency is-associated with megaloblastic anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Measurement of serum ferritin concentration\",\"e\":\"Iron deficiency would present with a microcytic anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Measurement of serum thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) concentration\",\"e\":\"Given this patient's symptoms with only slightly low haemoglobin, another diagnosis to consider is hypothyroidism, which often has an insidious onset, or is discovered when subclinical. Therefore thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) levels would be the investigation of choice.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Measurement of serum vitamin Be concentration\",\"e\":\"Vitamin Biz deficiency, similar to folate deficiency, would present with megaloblastic anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Measurement of transferrin\",\"e\":\"An increased transferrin level is often seen in iron deficiency anaemia. The blood picture does not suggest iron deficiency, which is typically microcytic and hypochromic.\"}]},{\"t\":35,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman is diagnosed with haemolytic anaemia under the medical team. The surgeons are concerned as there is major concern about the health of the spleen and whether it may need to be surgically removed.\\n\\nWhich one of the following biochemical parameters would be reduced in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following biochemical parameters would be reduced in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Serum haptoglobin\",\"e\":\"In haemolysis, free haemoglobin combines with haptoglobin, so serum levels fall as a result.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Serum ferritin\",\"e\":\"Patients with haemolytic states do not become iron deficient.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)\",\"e\":\"Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is released from red blood cells that are undergoing haemolysis raising the serum LDH level.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Bilirubin\",\"e\":\"Bilirubin levels are increased in haemolysis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serum iron\",\"e\":\"Serum iron levels are unchanged in patients suffering from haemolysis.\\n\\nUnconjugated bilirubin is marker of haemolyis and is generated by the breakdown of the Haem ring from haemoglobin. In addition, LDH is released from red blood cells if haemolysis is intravascular.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"You are assessing a patient in pre-operation clinic who is listed for a total knee replacement. The patient tells you that he has haemophilia A.\\n\\nWhich clotting factor is deficient in haemophilia A?\",\"lead\":\"Which clotting factor is deficient in haemophilia A?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"Explanation i\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor V\",\"e\":\"Haemophilia A is a sex-linked inherited disorder characterised by a complete or partial deficiency of factor VIII or defects in its function. Severe disease (factor VIII < 1%) is characterised by painful joint and muscle bleeds that eventually lead to a chronic arthropathy. Rarer manifestations include pseudo-tumour formation following muscle bleeds, nerve entrapment, compartment syndromes, haematuria and post-traumatic intracranial bleeds. Significant mortality arises from replacement therapy associated with HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C infections\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Factor XI\",\"e\":\"Factor XI deficiency is otherwise known as haemophilia C and is also passed on in an autosomal-recessive pattern.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor VII\",\"e\":\"Alexander's disease is deficiency of factor VIl and is passed on in an autosomal-recessive pattern.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Factor VIII\",\"e\":\"Owren's deficiency is deficiency of factor V and is passed on in an autosomal-recessive pattern. It must not be confused with factor V Leiden, which is a hypercoagulable disorder.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor IX\",\"e\":\"Haemophilia B is a sex-linked disorder arising from deficiency of factor IX.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A patient is due to undergo a major colorectal resectional procedure. Pre-operative FBC demonstrates a low haemoglobin. You request iron studies, vitamin Big and folate levels: The folate level is reduced. What can cause folate deficiency?\",\"lead\":\"What can cause folate deficiency?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cushing's syndrome\",\"e\":\"Cushing's syndrome leads to a normochromic normocytic anaemia, whereas a folate deficiency leads to a macrocytic anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hypothyroidism may be associated with normocytic, microcytic or macrocytic anaemia but does not affect folate levels directly.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Methotrexate therapy\",\"e\":\"Methotrexate can reduce folate levels within the body, therefore patients receive folic acid supplementation during treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pernicious anaemia\",\"e\":\"Arising from destruction of the parietal cells in the stomach that produce intrinsic factor, this condition then reduces absorption in the ileum leading to a reduction of vitamin B1z.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thalassemia\",\"e\":\"Thalassaemia will present with microcytic anaemia, whereas folate deficiency will cause macrocytic anaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old man is treated with a haemoglobin concentration of 8.5 g/dl (normal value 13.5-17.5 g/dl) ard a decreased serum iron level on admission. Chronic iron deficiency anaemia is suspected.\\n\\nWhat would you expect to be seen on a blood film?\",\"lead\":\"What would you expect to be seen on a blood film?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A microcytic, hypochromic film with anisocytosis and poikilocytosis\",\"e\":\"Iron deficiency anaemia is microcytic and hypochromic on a blood film. Anisocytosis and poikilocytosis are features of iron deficiency anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A microcytic, hypochromic film with basophilic stippling\",\"e\":\"Basophilic stippling is seen in lead poisoning and thalassemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A microcytic, hypochromic film with sideroblasts\",\"e\":\"Sideroblasts are a feature of sideroblastic anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A microcytic, hypochromic film with spherocytes\",\"e\":\"Spherocytes are seen in haemolysis such as haemolytic anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A microcytic, hypochromic film with target cells\",\"e\":\"Target cells are seen in liver disease, thalassemia and sickle cell disease.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"You are treating a haemodynamically unstable polytrauma patient with an open book pelvic fracture. You want to prevent fibrinolysis.\\n\\nWhat is the commonest activator of fibrinolysis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the commonest activator of fibrinolysis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epsilon-aminocaproic acid\",\"e\":\"Epsilon-aminocaproic acid (EACA) inhibits the fibrinolytic system.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Factor XII\",\"e\":\"Factor XII acts upon prekallikrein to form kallikrein, which activates the fibrinolytic system through the intrinsic pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Protein C\",\"e\":\"Protein C is an important natural inhibitor of blood clotting.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tissue plasminogen activator\",\"e\":\"Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) is produced by endothelial cells. It binds to fibrin, converting fibrin-bound plasminogen to plasmin, which in turn degrades to fibrin. tPA is the commonest activator of fibrinolysis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tranexamic acid\",\"e\":\"Fibrin dissolution can be reduced by tranexamic acid, given in polytrauma protocols, which acts by inhibiting plasminogen activation and fibrinolysis. It is useful in cases in which it is difficult to stop haemorrhaging directly, as in prostatectomy.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient who has vitamin Big injections for Big deficiency, she asks you what vitamin B12 does. They have previously undergone a terminal ileal resection for Crohn's disease.\\n\\nHow might you respond?\",\"lead\":\"How might you respond?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is directly responsible for haemoglobin synthesis\",\"e\":\"It is not involved in haemoglobin synthesis directly but rather with haemopoiesis and the maturation of red blood cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is a fat soluble vitamin and is therefore mostly stored in the adipose tissue\",\"e\":\"Vitamin Biz is water soluble and is stored mainly in the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Its metabolically active form is pyridoxal 5-phosphate, which is involved in haemoglobin synthesis\",\"e\":\"The metabolically active form of vitamin B6 is pyridoxal 5-phosphate (PLP), which is involved in haemoglobin synthesis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is involved in the red cell maturation process\",\"e\":\"Vitamin Big is involved in the red cell maturation process and haemopoiesis. In deficient states, megaloblastic erythropoiesis results with a mean corpuscular volume (MCV) >96 fl.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is the same as intrinsic factor\",\"e\":\"Intrinsic factor is produced by the parietal cells in the stomach. Intrinsic factor binds to Biz and facilitates absorption in the ileum\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man is brought to hospital with severe abdominal pain. Examination reveals widespread guarding. A CT scan shows a perforated sigmoid diverticulum and he is taken for laparotomy. He takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation with an international normalised ratio (INR) of 3.2 (normal ratio 0.9-1.2), He is administered vitamin K Which one of the following clotting factors is vitamin K dependent?\",\"lead\":\"2), He is administered vitamin K Which one of the following clotting factors is vitamin K dependent?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor V\",\"e\":\"Factor V is not vitamin K dependant. If someone is suffering from factor V deficiency the only treatment for bleeding is FFP.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Factor VIII\",\"e\":\"Factor VIII is not vitamin K dependant. Otherwise known as haemophilia A, deficiency is treated with replacement factor VIII.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor IX\",\"e\":\"Clotting factors are predominantly synthesised in the liver - factors I!, VII, IX and X syntheses being vitamin K dependent. Coagulation is initiated in vivo by the exposure of circulating factor VII to tissue factor, which in turn activates factor X (extrinsic pathway). Activated factor X subsequently sustains and amplifies the pathway by activating more factor VIl and by activating factors XII, XI, IX, VIII and V (intrinsic pathway). Both pathways generate more activated factor X, which in sufficient concentration converts prothrombin to thrombin - this in turn converts fibrinogen to fibrin (common pathway), so producing a fibrin clot. The process is completed by factor XIII, which stabilises the fibrin clot. Clotting factors are predominantly synthesised in the liver - factors II, VII, IX and X syntheses being vitamin K dependent. The coagulation cascade is inhibited in vivo by antithrombin Ill and the protein S and C pathways. Deficiency of these proteins therefore predisposes to thrombosis. Deficiency of the extrinsic, intrinsic and common pathway factors predisposes to haemorrhage, with the exception of factor XII deficiency, which is asymptomatic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Factor XI:\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor XII\",\"e\":\"Factor XII deficiency is not dependant on vitamin K\\n\\nFactor XIII is not dependant on vitamin K. People with this haemophilia may suffer from menorrhagia, recurrent miscarriages and infertility in men.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A patient with significant clotting derangement is under your care. After arranging a blood film you seek advice from the haematologist on call as the patient may be higher risk for their surgical procedure.\\n\\nTesting for factor V Leiden is warranted in patients with which one of the following options?\",\"lead\":\"Testing for factor V Leiden is warranted in patients with which one of the following options?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1 Si Menorrhagia\",\"e\":\"Factor V Leiden is pro-thrombotic and would not cause menorrhagia as a symptom.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Recurrent DVTs\",\"e\":\"Factor V Leiden tests are indicated for venous thrombo-embolism presenting at a relatively young age, or in an unusual part of the body. They are also indicated when a patient has a strong personal or family history of recurrent deep vein thromboses (DVTs), or when they are experiencing unexplained miscarriages. In Britain, 5% of the population carry one or more genes for factor V Leiden. These patients are at an increased risk of venous thrombosis. This risk is five times greater than that of the general population.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"An arterial embolus\",\"e\":\"Factor V Leiden causes venous thromboembolic events (VTEs) rather than arterial embolisms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transient ischaemic attacks\",\"e\":\"Factor V Leiden testing is indicated for patients with recurrent DVTs, miscarriages or strong family history of VTE:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Haemophilia\",\"e\":\"Factor V Leiden is pro-thrombotic disorder rather than a haemophilia.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A patient underwent splenectomy as a child, but has been well since. Later in their adult life they are admitted to hospital with a simple community acquired pneumonia. The blood film of this patient will reveal which one of the following options?\",\"lead\":\"The blood film of this patient will reveal which one of the following options?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pappenheimer bodies\",\"e\":\"Pappenheimer bodies are abnormal basophilic granules of iron found inside red blood cells on routine blood stain. They are a type of inclusion body composed of ferritin aggregates, or mitochondria or phagosomes containing aggregated ferritin. They appear as dense, blue- purple granules within the red blood cell and there is usually one or two, located in the cell periphery. They stain on a Romanowsky stain because clumps of ribosomes are co- precipitated with the iron-containing organelles. A cell containing Pappenheimer bodies is a siderocyte. Reticulocytes often contain Pappenheimer bodies. They are mostly observed in diseases such as myelodysplastic syndrome, sideroblastic anaemia, hemolytic anaemia and sickle-cell disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Howell-Jolly bodies\",\"e\":\"Howell-Jolly bodies are nuclear fragments of condensed DNA that appear as spherical blue- black inclusions within red blood cells on Wright-stained smears. They are found in splenectomy patients as the spleen would normally filter out these type of red blood cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Auer rods\",\"e\":\"Auer rods are elongated, bluish-red rods present in the cytoplasm of myeloblasts and promyelocytes. Their presence in the peripheral blood film is indicative of acute myeloid leukemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Basophilic stippling\",\"e\":\"Basophilic stippling of red cells may be observed in lead poisoning, exposure to some drugs, anaemias, or septicaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hairy cells\",\"e\":\"Hairy cells are only seen in hairy-cell leukemia.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A 12-year-old boy was seen in the elective orthopaedic out-patient clinic with recurrent spontaneous joint haemorrhages.\\n\\nWhich one of the following laboratory findings are most likely to be seen in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following laboratory findings are most likely to be seen in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor V deficiency . C-\",\"e\":\"Factor V deficiency is rare, and most often presents with bruising, bleeding gums, nosebleeds and menorrhagia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Factor VIll deficiency\",\"e\":\"Joint haemorrhages (haemarthroses) are typical of haemophilia. Factor VIII deficiency is known as haemophilia A. The diagnosis might be suspected as coagulation testing reveals an increased partial thromboplastin time in the context of a normal prothrombin time and a normal bleeding time. The diagnosis is made in the presence of very low levels of factor VIII ( < 10 IU). A family history is frequently present, although not essential.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Platelet count of 100 000 per microlitre\",\"e\":\"Spontaneous bleeding is likely to occur in patients with platelet count of less than 20 000.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Positive protamine sulphate test\",\"e\":\"The protamine sulphate test is used to test for disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). However it is unlikely the patient has been suffering from DIC with recurrent haemiarthrosis and no other symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Prothrombin time (PT) twice normal control\",\"e\":\"A PT time of twice normal is equal to an INR of 2. Haemiarthrosis is extremely rare with an INR that low\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman has been diagnosed with deficiency of a protein that functions as a cofactor in the thrombin-induced activation of protein C in the anticoagulant pathway. She is referred to the haematologist for optimisation of her clotting prior to elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following proteins was deficient in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following proteins was deficient in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"a-2-Antiplasmin\",\"e\":\"a-2-Antiplasmin is a serine protease inhibitor responsible for inactivating plasmin, an important enzyme that participates in fibrinolysis.\\n\\nFibronectin is an extracellular matrix glycoprotein that binds to receptor proteins called integrins.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fibronectin\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"High-molecular-weight kininogen\",\"e\":\"High-molecular-weight kininogen is a plasma protein that participates in the initial coagulation cascade and in the generation of bradykinin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Prekallikrein\",\"e\":\"Prekallikrein is a serine protease that is the precursor of kallikrein. It is cleaved by activated factor XII.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thrombomodulin\",\"e\":\"Thrombomodulin is a membrane protein expressed on the surface of endothelial cells. It functions as a cofactor in the thrombin-induced activation of protein C in the anticoagulant pathway, ultimately leading to reduced blood coagulation. In a patient with thrombomodulin deficiency therefore, clotting could in theory be enhanced inappropriately.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve, who was on long-term warfarin therapy, was started on intravenous heparin before undergoing an elective cholecystectomy. She comes through the operation without complication and her warfarin is safely restarted on the first post-operative day, with bridging low molecular weight heparin.\\n\\nWhat is the mechanism of action of heparin?\",\"lead\":\"What is the mechanism of action of heparin?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Activation of antithrombin III\",\"e\":\"Heparin binds to the enzyme inhibitor, antithrombin III (AT-III), causing a conformational change that result in its active site being exposed. The activated AT-Ill then inactivates thrombin and other proteases involved in blood clotting, most notably factor Xa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Activation of protein C\",\"e\":\"Protein C. is activated by thrombomodulin and endothelial protein C receptor (EPCR).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Activation of protein S\",\"e\":\"Heparin has no action on protein S, rather it activates antithrombin III.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inhibition of fibrinogen degradation\",\"e\":\"Heparin does not inhibit fibrinogen degradation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Inhibition of synthesis of vitamin K-dependent clotting factors\",\"e\":\"Warfarin inhibits the synthesis of vitamin K-dependant clotting factors.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman who was admitted for elective excision of a preauricular dermoid cyst told the Senior House Officer (SHO) clerking her that she has a coagulation defect. This is known to be due to deficiency of a coagulation factor, which is activated by factor Xlla and is produced in the liver.\\n\\nWhich coagulation factor is deficient in this woman?\",\"lead\":\"Which coagulation factor is deficient in this woman?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor VIII\",\"e\":\"Factor VIII is produced by the liver and endothelial cells throughout the body. It is activated by thrombin (factor lla).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Factor IX\",\"e\":\"Factor IX is activated by either factor Xla or factor Vlla.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor X\",\"e\":\"Factor X is activated by factor IXa, rather than Xlla.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Factor XI\",\"e\":\"Factor Xi is activated by factor Xlla. Factor XI (or plasma thromboplastin antecedent) is one of the enzymes of the coagulation cascade. Like many other coagulation factors, it is a serine protease, produced by the liver and circulates in its inactive form. The zymogen factor is activated into factor Xla by factor Xlla (FXlia), thrombin and it is also autocatalytic. FXI is a member of the 'contact' pathway. Factor Xia activates factor IX, which in turn activates factor X.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor XIII\",\"e\":\"Factor XIII is activated by thrombin to form factor Xlla.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old patient with a bleeding tendency was investigated and diagnosed with gray platelet syndrome (GPS). They require an emergency open reduction and internal fixation of a spiral fracture of the humerus. You seek advice from the haematologist to optimise their clotting for the procedure\\n\\nWhich one of the following components is deficient in this patient's platelets?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following components is deficient in this patient's platelets?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alpha granules\",\"e\":\"In GPS there is a reduction or absence of alpha granules in the platelets. GPS is a very rare inherited disorder that is characterised by thrombocytopenia, abnormally large, agranular platelets in peripheral blood smears (which appear grey), splenomegaly, and myelofibrosis. Alpha platelet granules normally serve to promote platelet adhesion and wound healing. Patients with GPS may present with prolonged bleeding following injury, nosebleeds and easy bruising\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Canalicular system\",\"e\":\"The Canalicular system is not deficient in GPS.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Dense bodies\",\"e\":\"Dense bodies, otherwise known as delta granules, contain platelet activating chemicals and are not affected by GPS.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lysosomes\",\"e\":\"Lysosomes are still present in platelets affected by GPS.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Mitochondria\",\"e\":\"The mitochondria in platelets is not deficient in GPS.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man has had chronic back pain for several months. He has lost some weight. He is not febrile. A full blood count is performed which shows increased immature granulocytes, increased nucleated red blood cells and the following results:\\n\\nNormal Value Result Investigation\\n\\n9.8 × 10ª/ 4-11 × 10ª/I White cell count (WCC)\\n\\nNeutrophils 6.3 × 10% 2.5-7.58 × 109/|\\n\\n1.5-3.5 × 10%1 1.9 × 109%1 Lymphocytes\\n\\n0.8 × 10%% Monocytes 0.2-0.8 × 10%1\\n\\n12.2 g/dl 13.5-17.5 g/dl Haemoglobin\\n\\n78-100 fl Mean corpuscular volume (MCV) 84 fl\\n\\n124 × 109/1 150-400 × 109/1 Platelet count\\n\\n12.2 g/dl 13.5-17.5 g/dl Haemoglobin\\n\\n78-100 fl Mean corpuscular volume (MCV) 84 fl\\n\\n124 × 109/1 150-400 × 109/l Platelet count\\n\\nWhich one of the following conditions is most likely to be associated with these findings?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following conditions is most likely to be associated with these findings?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia\",\"e\":\"The main symptom of chronic lymphocytic leukaemia would be of a lymphocytosis which is not present in this patient.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemolytic anaemia\",\"e\":\"Haemolytic anaemia would often have an increased MCV due to the high number of reticulocytes in the bloodstream.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Metastatic carcinoma\",\"e\":\"The presence of nucleated red blood cells along with immature granular cells shows a leucoerythroblastic picture. This is due to space-occupying lesions in the bone marrow. including myeloma, myelofibrosis or in malignancy. The most likely underlying pathogenesis in this patient would be metastatic prostatic or lung carcinoma\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Previous splenectomy\",\"e\":\"Howell-Jowell bodies would be evident after a previous splenectomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Staphylococcus aureus osteomyelitis\",\"e\":\"Osteomyelitis would not lead to nucleated red blood cells.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"Low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH), is recommended as a prophylaxis against venothrombus disease in patients undergoing lower limb replacement arthroplasty. The course is usually prolonged due the massively increased risk of reduced patient mobility and likely damage to the endothelial layer of vessels.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true about the coagulation cascade?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true about the coagulation cascade?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"LMWH directly inhibits factor Xa\",\"e\":\"LMWH inhibits the coagulation process by binding with antithrombin, which in turn accelerates in inhibition of factor Xa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Antithrombin III and protein C deficiencies predispose to bleeding\",\"e\":\"Antithrombin III and protein C deficiencies are pro-thrombotic rather than causing bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor VIll is synthesised in the vascular endothelium\",\"e\":\"Factor Vill is synthesised by the sinusoidal cells of the liver as well as by the vascular endothelium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fibrinogen cleaves prothrombin to yield thrombin\",\"e\":\"Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The intrinsic pathway includes factor VIl and tissue factor\",\"e\":\"The extrinsic pathway includes factor VII and tissue factor.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old man is due to undergo an elective open aortic aneurysm repair. The potential for catastrophic bleeding is high and it must be ensured that blood is available before operating. They have anti-A and anti-B antibodies in their blood.\\n\\nTheir blood group genotype must be which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Their blood group genotype must be which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"OO\",\"e\":\"This patient would have both anti-A and anti-B crculating in their blood. As O is recessive, genotypically the patient must be O0, as AO would give a phenotypically A patient, and BO would give a phenotypically B patient.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AA\",\"e\":\"There would be no anti-A circulating in this patients' blood.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"AO\",\"e\":\"This patient would have anti-B circulating in their bloodstream but no anti-A.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"BB\",\"e\":\"This patient would have anti-A present but no anti-B.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"AB\",\"e\":\"This patient would have no circulating anti-A or anti-B.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"You have a patient who has been prescribed low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) subcutaneously throughout the perioperative period for deep vein thrombosis (DVT) prophylaxis. They are discharged safely without the complication of a venous thrombosis.\\n\\nWhat is the mechanism of action of LMWHs?\",\"lead\":\"What is the mechanism of action of LMWHs?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Its action predominantly affects factor Xa\",\"e\":\"LMWH acts predominantly upon factor Xa, via antithrombin I!!. LMWHs are prepared by the degradation of unfractionated heparin, so reducing their mean molecular weight to less than 8 kDa. LMWHs have a longer half-life than unfractionated heparin and better bioavailability after subcutaneous injection. Only a once-daily administration is required. Anti-factor Xa levels can be measured to monitor the effects of LMWH. Excretion is almost exclusively renal and so dose reduction is required in patients with renal failure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"1. i Affects vitamin K metabolism\",\"e\":\"This is the mechanism of action of warfarin\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Dilutional effect\",\"e\":\"LMWH does not inhibit coagulation by dilutional effect.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inhibits the production of thromboxane\",\"e\":\"Long-term aspirin use inhibits the production of thromboxane A2\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduces venous stasis\",\"e\":\"This is the mechanism of action of TED stockings.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man with von Willebrand's disease was admitted with a painful swollen knee due to haemarthrosis. They are referred to the orthopaedic team with a possible septic arthritis but given the underlying diagnosis, it is felt that a knee aspiration will just raise the chances of the haemarthrosis becoming infected.\\n\\nWhat are the main coagulation abnormalities in von Willebrand's disease?\",\"lead\":\"What are the main coagulation abnormalities in von Willebrand's disease?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abnormal factor VIll with platelet dysfunction\",\"e\":\"Von Willebrand's disease sufferers have platelet dysfunction and abnormal factor VIII. Von Willebrand's factor binds factor VIII and platelets to help form a platelet plug during clotting.\\n\\nVon Willebrand's disease is the commonest inherited bleeding disorder in white populations and is usually autosomal dominant in inheritance. Affected individuals have reduced or dysfunctional von Willebrand factor (vWF), a protein normally present in platelets and endothelium. vWF stabilises factor VIII in the circulation and mediates platelet adhesion at the site of vascular injury. The associated laboratory abnormalities therefore include a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT) (due to reduced factor VIII), a normal prothrombin time and a prolonged bleeding time (platelet adhesion defect) Clinically, affected individuals usually show a mild bleeding tendency with epistaxis, easy bruising, menorrhagia and gingival bleeding. Although factor VIII levels are characteristically reduced, levels are rarely sufficiently low to cause haemarthroses, the hallmark of haemophilia A. Preoperative management usually involves treatment with desmopressin (which liberates platelet stores of vWF) or factor replacement with either intermediate- purity factor VIII or a specific vWF concentrate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased factor IX\",\"e\":\"This is haemophilia B, or Christmas disease\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Decreased factor VIll alone\",\"e\":\"This is haemophilia A.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Factor Xa inhibition\",\"e\":\"This occurs with treatment by low-molecular-weight heparin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thromboxan dysfunction\",\"e\":\"Treatment with aspirin causes thromboxane dysfunction.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"The following results came back from a 39-year-old woman. The consultant expects you to arrange further investigation to search for a cause and to begin therapy to correct the underlying abnormality.\\n\\nResult Normal value Investigation\\n\\n11.5-15.5 g/dl 10.1 g/di Haemoglobin (Hb)\\n\\n76-98 fl Mean corpuscular volume 74 fl\\n\\n22 ng/ml Ferritin 22-400 ng/ml\\n\\n248 ng/i 160-900 ng/l Vitamin B12 Folate 5.3 ug/l 2.0-11.0 ug/l\\n\\n185-370 mg/di 500 mg/dl Transferrin\\n\\n248 ng/l Vitamin B12 160-900 ng/i\\n\\n2.0-11.0 ug/l 5.3 мg/1 Folate\\n\\nTransferrin 500 mg/dl 185-370 mg/dl\\n\\nWhat is the most probable diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most probable diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Iron deficiency anaemia\",\"e\":\"The low mean corpuscular volume (MCV), low ferritin and high transferrin suggest iron deficiency anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Folate deficiency anaemia\",\"e\":\"The folate level is normal in this scenario. Folate deficiency would lead to a macrocytic anaemia with an MCV >96 fi\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bi2 deficiency\",\"e\":\"The levels of Big are normal in this individual and B12 deficiency would lead to a macrocytic anaemia with an MCV >96 fl\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thalassaemia\",\"e\":\"Thalassaemia suffers would have a high ferritin level.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Anaemia of chronic disease\",\"e\":\"Anaemia of chronic disease would be normocytic rather than microcytic. The transferrin falls and ferritin is raised as negative and positive inflammatory markers respectively.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"A 2-year-old boy presents with a painful, swollen right knee. Apart from recurrent epistaxis, he is fit and well. His blood results reveal the following abnormalities:\\n\\nNormal value Investigation Result\\n\\n13.2 g/dl 11.5-13.5 g/dl Haemoglobin (Hb)\\n\\n95 fl 73.5-83.4 fi Mean corpuscular volume (MCV)\\n\\n8.3 x109/1 5-17 × 109/1 Whole cell count (WCC)\\n\\n250 × 109/1 150-400 × 109/1 Platelet count\\n\\nNormal Prothrombin\\n\\nProlonged Activated partial thromboplastin times\\n\\nWhat is the most probable diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most probable diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Septic arthritis\",\"e\":\"In septic arthritis you would expect a raised whole cell count (WCC). However the WCC in this patient is normal making septic arthritis unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Protein C deficiency\",\"e\":\"Protein C deficiency increases the likelihood of a patient forming clots as it is hypercoagulable state. Therefore haemarthrosis would not be a presenting symptom.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)\",\"e\":\"Platelets are low in DIC and they are normal in this patient.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Willebrand Disease (VWD)\",\"e\":\"The child has a bleeding disorder of the intrinsic pathway indicatec by the raised activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT). The recurrent epistaxis suggests VWD due to abnormality of platelets and factor VIII. Platelets cause mucosal bleeding, factor VIII deficiency causes deep bleeding, such as haemarthroses in this case.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor V Leiden\",\"e\":\"Factor V Leiden is a genetic condition leading to hypercoagulability therefore haemarthrosis would not be a symptom.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A Rh-negative woman with a Rh-positive husband is pregnant for the first time. The midwife tells the woman that subsequent babies may be at risk of developing haemolytic disease of the newborn.\\n\\nWhich one of the following agents would be the most probable protection for the baby from developing the disease?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following agents would be the most probable protection for the baby from developing the disease?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Albumin from Rh-positive donors\",\"e\":\"Albumin from Rh-positive donors would not protect the infant against anti-Rh antibodies.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anti-Rh antibody (Rh immunoglobulin)\",\"e\":\"The intramuscular administration of high-titre anti-Rh antibodies, also known as 'anti-Rh immunoglobulins', into an Rh-negative mother is effective in preventing the development of haemolytic disease of the newborn. Rhesus sensitisation happens when a mother with Rh- negative blood is exposed to Rh-positive blood, which may cross the placenta from a Rh- positive fetus. The mother responds to this by producing anti-Rh antibodies, and the next time the mother is exposed to Rh-positive blood, her body will produce anti-Rh antibodies immediately. If she is pregnant again with an Rh-positive fetus, the antibodies may cross the placenta, resulting in haemolytic disease of the newborn. Guidelines state that 'all rhesus negative pregnant women wha have not previously been sensitized should be offered routine antenatal prophylaxis with anti-D lg (RAADP) either with a single dose regimen at around 28 weeks, or two-dose regimen given at 28 and 34 weeks. Following any potentially sensitising events, such as amniocentesis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A transfusion of platelets T\",\"e\":\"Platelet transfusion would not protect the infant against anti-Rh antibodies.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Plasma from randomly selected donors\",\"e\":\"Plasma from randomly selected donors is useful in immunodeficiency diseases but not rhesus disease of the newborn. Some preparations could conceivably contain anti-Rh.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rh-positive erythrocytes\",\"e\":\"Infusion of Rh-positive red blood cells could increase further the risk of the mother producing anti-Rh antibodies. Trauma, ectopic pregnancy or delivery, anti-D should be offered within 72 h to reduce the risk of sensitisation and future pregnancies being at risk of haemolytic disease of the newborn.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"You are prescribing erythropoietin for a pre-operation patient who is a Jehovah's Witness and is willing to accept this form of treatment to optimise their pre-operation haemoglobin. You will aiso ensure a cell saver is present during their surgery.\\n\\nHow is erythropoietin produced normally?\",\"lead\":\"How is erythropoietin produced normally?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Erythropoietin production is stimulated by increased oxygen content in the renal arteries\",\"e\":\"Erythropoietin production is stimulated by reduced oxygen in the renal arterial circulation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"In adults, erythropoietin is produced by the peritubular fibroblasts of the kidney\",\"e\":\"Erythropoietin is a glycoprotein hormone produced primarily by the peritubular fibroblasts of the renal cortex, in response to hypoxia. In premature, as well as fuli-term infants, the liver is the primary site of erythropoietin production. The kidney becomes the primary site of erythropoietin synthesis shortly after birth. Small amounts of erythropoietin are also synthesized in the hepatocytes of healthy adults. Erythropoietin production is stimulated by reduced oxygen content in the renal arterial circulation. Some of the clinical conditions that give rise to tissue hypoxia include anaemia, lung disease and cyanotic heart disease, all of which stimulate erythropoietin production.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The kidney is the primary site of erythropoietin production in the fetus\",\"e\":\"The liver, rather than the kidney, is the site of development of erythropoietin in the fetus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The kidney is the primary site of erythropoietin production in a premature infant\",\"e\":\"Like full-term infants, it is the liver that produces erythropoietin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The liver is the primary site of erythropoietin production in adults\",\"e\":\"It is primarily produced by the peritubular fibroblasts of the kidney in adults, rather than the liver.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"A normal, healthy woman became pregnant. On her first antenatal visit she had a haemoglobin concentration of 12 g/dl. After 24 weeks' of gestation she had a haemoglobin level of 10g/dl (normal range 11.5-16.0 g/di) with a normal blood picture.\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most probable cause?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most probable cause?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Folate-deficiency anaemia\",\"e\":\"Folate deficiency would not present with a normal blood picture, instead the mean corpuscular volume (MCV) would be >96 fl.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Iron deficiency anaemia\",\"e\":\"Iron deficiency anaemia would lead to microcytic anaemia rather than a normal blood picture:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Thalassaemia\",\"e\":\"Thalassaemia would not present with a normal blood picture, the MCV would be microcytic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vitamin Ba deficiency\",\"e\":\"Vitamin Biz deficiency would lead to a macrocytic anaemia similar to folate deficiency.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Physiological response\",\"e\":\"In pregnancy, red cell volume also increases, but this frequently lags behind the plasma volume, resulting in a reduced haematocrit and haemoglobin concentration. This is referred to as the physiological anaemia of pregnancy.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 15-year-old adolescent youth with haemophilia A has suffered recurrent bleeding episodes into his joints. As a consequence he has arthropathies in his knees, elbows and wrists.\\n\\nWhat is the most probable coagulation deficiency causing his bleeding tendency?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most probable coagulation deficiency causing his bleeding tendency?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Thromboxane\",\"e\":\"Haemophilia A is factor VIII deficiency.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Factor X\",\"e\":\"Factor X deficiency is not the underlying pathogenesis of haemophilia A, rather is it deficiency of factor VIII.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Protein C\",\"e\":\"Protein C deficiency leads to a hypercoagulable state making haemiarthrosis unlikely\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Factor IX\",\"e\":\"Deficiency of factor IX leads to haemophilia B, rather than haemophilia A.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor VIII\",\"e\":\"Deficiency of factor VIII (haemophilia A) results in the clinical phenotype commonly known as haemophilia. Haemophilia principally presents with haematoma formation, easy bruising and bleeding at the site of venepuncture during the toddler period. The clinical features of haemophilia predominantly include bleeding into joints and soft tissues. Haemophilia exists in severe, moderate and mild forms. These are classified as such on the basis of a clinical laboratory blood coagulation test, which is performed to assess the level of functional coagulant protein (per cent activity of factor VIII or factor IX). The pathological problem in both haemophilia A. factor VIII deficiency and haemophilia B. factor IX deficiency (also called 'Christmas disease'), is the inability to form a functional tenase complex to activate factor X to factor Xa.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"Which of the following factors is involved in the extrinsic coagulation cascade?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following factors is involved in the extrinsic coagulation cascade?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor VII\",\"e\":\"Factor VIl and activated tissue factor comprise the extrinsic pathway. The clotting cascade is the ordered stepwise enzyme-controlled activation of soluble clotting factors to produce an insoluble fibrin mesh, a thrombus. There are two different pathways, intrinsic and extrinsic. The intrinsic pathway is so-called as all the elements necessary for its activation are in the blood. It is triggered by exposure of collagen in damaged vascular endothelium. The extrinsic pathway requires the release of tissue factors from damaged tissues to start the process. Both pathways converge in the common pathway. Sequential activation of factors XII, XI, IX and VIII comprises the intrinsic pathway. The extrinsic pathway involves tissue factor and activated factor VII. Both the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways activate factors X, lI and I to form fibrin and this is the common pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Factor VIII\",\"e\":\"Factor VIll is the last factor to be activated in the intrinsic pathway after factors XII, XI, and IX.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor IX\",\"e\":\"Factor IX is part of the intrinsic pathway, it is activated after factors XIl and XI.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Factor XI\",\"e\":\"Factor Xl is the second factor activated after factor XII in the intrinsic pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor XII\",\"e\":\"Factor XII is the first factor activated in the intrinsic cascade.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"Myeloid stem cells give rise to several different cell types. Which one of the following options do not arise from myeloid stem cells?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options do not arise from myeloid stem cells?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Neutrophils\",\"e\":\"Neutrophils arise from the myeloid stem cells along with eosinophils, basophils, monocytes and macrophages.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Monocytes\",\"e\":\"Myeloid stem cells give rise to monocytes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Eosinophils\",\"e\":\"Eosinophils are polymorphonuclear leukocytes and are derived from myeloid stem cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"Lymphocytes are derived from the lymphoid stem cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Macrophages\",\"e\":\"Macrophages descend from the myeloid stem cells.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man had a preoperative full blood count (FBC) performed before elective right hemicolectomy for a malignant growth in the ascending colon. The FBC revealed:\\n\\nResults Normal range 12.1 g/di 13.5-17.5 g/dl Haemoglobin\\n\\n35.8% 35-55% Haematocrit\\n\\n76-98 fl Mean corpuscular volume 90 fl\\n\\n4-11 × 109/1 13.5 × 10%1 White blood cell count\\n\\nPlatelet count 1000 × 109/| 150-400 × 109/1\\n\\nThese findings are most indicative of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"These findings are most indicative of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Myeloproliferative disorder\",\"e\":\"The myeloproliferative diseases are a group of diseases of the bone marrow in which excess cells are produced. They are related to, and can evolve into, myelodysplastic syndrome and acute myeloid leukaemia, although the myeloproliferative diseases on the whole have a much better prognosis than these conditions. All cell lines can be increased with myeloproliferative disorders. Thrombocytosis is one manifestation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chronic liver disease\",\"e\":\"Chronic liver disease would lead to thrombocytopaenia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hereditary spherocytosis\",\"e\":\"Hereditary spherocytosis would lead to a microcytic anaemia, whereas the mean corpuscular volume (MCV) in this patient is normai.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Postoperative haemorrhage\",\"e\":\"The blood count in this scenario was performed before surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Willebrand's disease\",\"e\":\"Von Willebrand's disease would not give a platelet count of 1000 × 109/I.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man who has had multiple episodes of deep venous thrombosis presented in the Emergency Department with a suspected pulmonary embolism. After his successful management the medical team ensure he is discharged with lifelong warfarin.\\n\\nWhich one of the following blood proteins is most likely to be deficient in this patient leading to the need for lifelong anticoagulation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following blood proteins is most likely to be deficient in this patient leading to the need for lifelong anticoagulation?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"a-2-Antiplasmin\",\"e\":\"a-2-Antiplasmin is responsible for inactivating plasmin. As plasmin is responsible for degrading blood clots, reduced inhibition of plasmin increases the risk of bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"a-2-Macroglobulin\",\"e\":\"a-2-Macroglobulin also inhibits plasmin. If levels of a-2-macroglobulin are reduced then there is reduced inhibition of plasmin therefore increasing the risk of bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Antithrombin III\",\"e\":\"Antithrombin III deficiency is a rare hereditary disorder that predisposes patients to clotting and often presents with recurrent deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Factor V\",\"e\":\"Factor V deficiency is also known as Owren's disease or parahemophilia. It's a rare bleeding disorder that results in poor clotting after an injury or surgery. Factor V deficiency shouldn't be confused with factor V Leiden mutation, a much more common condition that causes excessive blood clotting.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Fibrinogen\",\"e\":\"Fibrinogen deficiency (or factor I deficiency) is a rare bleeding disorder that would increase the risk of spontaneous bleeding.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":70,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old woman admitted for elective cholecystectomy revealed that she has factor IX deficiency Which of the following is true of factor IX?\",\"lead\":\"A 42-year-old woman admitted for elective cholecystectomy revealed that she has factor IX deficiency Which of the following is true of factor IX?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is a co-factor in the coagulation cascade\",\"e\":\"Factor IX is not a cofactor in the coagulation cascade, it is a main factor.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is activated by factor V\",\"e\":\"It is activated by factor Xla.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Requires prostacyclin for its activity\",\"e\":\"It requires calcium, membrane phospholipids and factor VIII as co-factors.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Acts by hydrolysing one arginine-isoleucine bond in factor X to form factor Xa\",\"e\":\"When activated into factor IXa, it acts by hydrolysing one arginine-isoleucine bond in factor X to form factor Xa.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Deficiency causes haemophilia A\",\"e\":\"Deficiency of factor VIII causes haemophilia A. Factor IX deficiency causes haemophilia B (Christmas disease).\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":72,\"stem\":\"Antithrombin Ill is a serine protease inhibitor with an important role in haemostasis.\\n\\nWhich one of the following substances enhances antithrombin III activity?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following substances enhances antithrombin III activity?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aspirin\",\"e\":\"Aspirin acts upon thromboxane A on platelets and has no effect upon antithrombin III activity.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Citrate\",\"e\":\"Heparin, rather than citrate, enhances antithrombin Ill activity.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Coumarin\",\"e\":\"Coumarin is another name for warfarin and has no effect on antithrombin Ill activity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Heparin\",\"e\":\"Heparin greatly enhances antithrombin Ill activity. Antithrombin Ill is a small molecule that inactivates several enzymes of the coagulation system. It is a glycoprotein produced by the liver. Antithrombin Ill is a serpin (serine protease inhibitor) that inactivates a number of enzymes from the coagulation system, namely the activated forms of factor X, factor IX, factor I! (thrombin), factor VII, factor Xl and factor XII. The rate of its reaction with these molecules (ie, its effectiveness) is greatly enhanced by heparin. Antithrombin Ill deficiency is a rare hereditary disorder that generally comes to light when a patient suffers recurrent venous thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Warfarin\",\"e\":\"Warfarin has no effect on antithrombin III. Instead it acts upon the vitamin K-dependant factors II, VII, IX, and X\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient before a hernia repair and discover on the pre-operative tests that their clotting is deranged. The patient is not on any anticoagulants. Which one of the following options is correct regarding the clotting cascade?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is correct regarding the clotting cascade?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The extrinsic pathway can by activated by collagen or glass\",\"e\":\"The intrinsic pathway can be initiated by glass or collagen rather than the extrinsic pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The clotting cascade can only be initiated by the extrinsic pathway\",\"e\":\"The clotting cascade can be initiated by either the intrinsic or the extrinsic pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"This culminates in the production of a plug of thrombin\",\"e\":\"The result of the coagulation cascade is a platelet plug strengthened by fibrin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is defective in von Hippel-Lindau syndrome\",\"e\":\"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome is a disease-associated with tumour formation and has no effect on the coagulation cascade.\\n\\n2084\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is dependent on calcium to function\",\"e\":\"Calcium is an essential co-factor in the coagulation cascade. The clotting cascade has two different pathways, the extrinsic (activated by tissue thromboplastin) and the intrinsic (activated by collagen and glass among others). These both culminate in the activation of the common pathway. This involves activation of factor X that in turn activates prothrombin, generating thrombin. The thrombin activates fibrinogen to produce fibrin. This fibrin polymerises under the effects of factor XIlI to form the fibrin plug that contributes the framework of the clot within which platelets and erythrocytes become enmeshed. Calcium is a co-factor and is vital for function of the clotting cascade. Citrate or EDTA are used as anticoagulants in blood sample bottles as they chelate calcium to stop clotting from occurring. Heparin acts by inhibiting thrombin. Warfarin inhibits the function of vitamin K, which is vital for the correct function of factors !!, VII, IX and X. The clotting cascade is deficient in haemophilia A (factor VIII), haemophilia B (factor IX) and von Willebrand's disease (a clotting protein which binds factor VIII). Von Hippel-Lindau's disease is a genetic abnormality associated with tumour formation.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":78,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man is admitted with a very high INR due to warfarin. They are likely to need surgery on this admission therefore elect to reverse the warfarin with intravenous vitamin K\\n\\nVitamin K is not involved in carboxylation of glutamic acid residues of which of the following clotting factors?\",\"lead\":\"Vitamin K is not involved in carboxylation of glutamic acid residues of which of the following clotting factors?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor XI\",\"e\":\"Factor XI is not dependant on vitamin K. Vitamin K is essential for activation of clotting factors II (prothrombin), VII, IX and X.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Prothrombin\",\"e\":\"Prothrombin (factor II) is a vitamin K-dependant clotting factor.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor VII\",\"e\":\"Factor VII is dependent on vitamin K to activate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Factor IX\",\"e\":\"Vitamin K is essential for activation of factor IX.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor X\",\"e\":\"Factor X is a vitamin K-dependant clotting factor.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"A 76-year-old woman presents with fatigue, shortness of breath and weight loss. She is found to have microcytic anaemia on routine blood tests. Which one of the following statements is correct with regard to her anaemia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct with regard to her anaemia?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alcoholism may be a cause\",\"e\":\"Alcoholism can lead to anaemia, which is macrocytic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It reduces the overall oxygen carrying capacity of blood\",\"e\":\"Anaemia is the reduction in the oxygen transport capacity of blood, usually due to a reduction of total circulating red cell mass.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It causes cardiac output to reduce\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output has to increase to keep up with physiological demands.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It can cause bradycardia\",\"e\":\"In severe anaemia a hyperdynamic circulation can be a result, leading to tachycardia and murmurs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It decreases total blood viscosity\",\"e\":\"Although haemoglobin (Hb) levels will be lower and red blood cell volume is decreased this can be compensated for by an increase of red cell number therefore viscosity may be unchanged.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":82,\"stem\":\"You are prescribing warfarin for one of your ward patients. You monitor their INR and ensure that on discharge they have outpatient follow up.\\n\\nHow does warfarin work?\",\"lead\":\"How does warfarin work?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"it impairs the synthesis or actor!. Y\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It impairs the synthesis of factor v!\",\"e\":\"Factor III is not affected by warfarin as it is not a vitamin K-dependant clotting factor.\\n\\nFactor V is not factor affected by warfarin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It inhibits the synthesis of fibrinogen\",\"e\":\"Fibrinogen is synthesised in the liver by the hepatocytes and is not affected by warfarin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It impairs the recycling of vitamin K\",\"e\":\"Warfarin acts by rapidly depleting hepatic vitamin K by impairing its recycling. It acts by preventing the synthesis of the vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX and X. this leads to a reduction in clotting factors II, VII, IX and X and therefore requires at least 48 h to become effective. Although warfarin treatment will prolong both the activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT) and the prothrombin time (PT), the PT is a more reliable index of anticoagulant activity. It is usual practice to express the PT as an international normalised ratio (INR) when measuring anticoagulation. Complications of treatment include bleeding and, rarely, skin necrosis. Warfarin has numerous interactions with other drugs, commonly antibiotics (which either reduce or enhance its bioavailability).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It requires 24 h to become effective\",\"e\":\"Warfarin requires at least 48 h to become effective.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":84,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old woman of Afro-Caribbean origin is seen in clinic. She is due to undergo a mastectomy for breast cancer. She is known to have a thalassaemia trait.\\n\\nWhat blood picture would you expect her to have?\",\"lead\":\"What blood picture would you expect her to have?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"All of the following\",\"e\":\"The anaemia will be hypochromic and microcytic\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypochromic microcytic\",\"e\":\"Thalassaemia will present with a microcytic and hypochromic anaemia. Thalassaemia is an inherited blood disorder in which the body makes an abnormal form of haemoglobin. It leads to an excess destruction of red blood cells, as a result red blood cells will appear small and abnormally shaped\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Macrocytic anaemia\",\"e\":\"Macrocytic anaemia is when the mean corpuscular volume (MCV) is >96 fl and occurs in conditions such as folate deficiency or vitamin Big deficiency.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Normocytic normochromic\",\"e\":\"This would be the picture of anaemia of chronic disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"None of the above\",\"e\":\"In thalassaemia the red blood cells will be microcytic and hypochromic.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"A 39-vear-old woman is seen in the pre-assessment clinic. Routine blood tests confirm that she has iron deficiency anaemia.\\n\\nWhich one of the following blood parameters reflect this?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following blood parameters reflect this?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Raised mean cell haemoglobin\",\"e\":\"Mean cell haemoglobin is reduced in iron deficiency anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Raised mean cell haemoglobin concentration\",\"e\":\"The mean cell haemoglobin concentration is low in iron deficiency anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Raised serum ferritin\",\"e\":\"Serum ferritin will be reduced in iron deficiency anaemia\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduced mean cell volume\",\"e\":\"Mean corpuscular volume (MCV) is decreased in iron-deficient anaemia. Normal range is usually 76-98 fl. Iron deficiency anaemia is the commonest cause of anaemia world-wide. Diagnosis is based on reduced levels of haemoglobin, mean cell volume, mean cell haemoglobin, mean ceil haemoglobin concentration, serum ferritin and serum iron. Microcytic, hypochromic red cells can be found under the microscope.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Macrocytic cells\",\"e\":\"Macrocytic cells occur when the MCV is > 96 fl. However in iron deficiency anaemia the MCV is low. Normal range is usually 76-98 fl.\"}]},{\"t\":36,\"p\":88,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man who is admitted for elective resection of a colonic carcinoma has the following blood results:\\n\\nInvestigation Result Normal value\\n\\n9.2 g/dl 13.5-18.0 g/dl Haemoglobin (Hb)\\n\\n1.5 × 109/1 4-11 × 109/| White blood cell count (WCC)\\n\\n78-100 fl Mean corpuscular volume (MCV) 68 fl\\n\\nHe requires optimisation of his anaemia pre-operatively.\\n\\nWhich one of the following options will be most prominent on this patient's peripheral blood smear?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options will be most prominent on this patient's peripheral blood smear?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"El Blasts\",\"e\":\"Blast cells are a feature of acute myeloid leukaemia and this would be associated with a leucocytosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyper-segmented neutrophils\",\"e\":\"MCV less than 78 fl points to a microcytic anaemia and hyper-segmented neutrophils would not be characteristic of this. Hyper segmented neutrophils are a characteristic of vitamin b 12 deficiency and a megaloblastic anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypochromic microcytic red blood cells\",\"e\":\"This would be the finding in iron deficient anaemia which is characterised the MCV would be less than 80 fl.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Nucleated red blood cells\",\"e\":\"Nucleated red blood cells are found in leucoerythroblastic pictures such as metastatic carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Schistocytes\",\"e\":\"Schistocytes are fragmented red blood cells and are often seen in patients with haemolytic anaemia.\\n\\n4392\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 20-year-old man has recurrent and/or severe infections. He has known hypogammaglobulinaemia. With which of the following organisms would make you suspect immunodeficiency associated with this disease?\",\"lead\":\"With which of the following organisms would make you suspect immunodeficiency associated with this disease?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Haemophilus influenzae\",\"e\":\"Hypogammaglobulinaemia patients present with recurrent bacterial infections often sinopulmonary infections. Infection with Haemophilus influenzae and Streptococcus pneumoniae occurs with this condition, not Streptococcus pyogenes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Streptococcus pyogenes\",\"e\":\"Hypogammaglobulinaemia patients present with recurrent bacterial infections often sinopulmonary infections. Infection with Haemophilus influenza and Streptococcus pneumoniae occurs with this condition, not Streptococcus pyogenes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Herpes simplex virus\",\"e\":\"Viral and fungal infections tend to be more common presentations in patients with T-cell immunodeficiency\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Candida albicans\",\"e\":\"Viral and fungal infections tend to be more common presentations in patients with T-cell immunodeficiency.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pneumocystis jiroveci\",\"e\":\"Pneumocystis jiroveci (formerly P. carinil) is associated with T-cell immunodeficiencies, the most common being acquired HIV infection.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A patient with an allergy to penicillin is given tazocin. They develop respiratory distress and exhibit angioedema. You suspect anaphylaxis with massive mast cell activation. Antigen binding to mast cells results in the release of both pre-formed and newly formed mediators.\\n\\nWhich of the following options is an example of newly formed mediators?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following options is an example of newly formed mediators?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"i'Si Thromboxane\",\"e\":\"Most mediators from mast cells are pre-formed, while others include cytokines and chemokines, or are newly formed following degranulation. Thromboxane is one of the newly formed lipid mediators following mast cell degranulation. Thromboxane causes vasoconstriction, platelet aggregation and bronchoconstriction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Histamine\",\"e\":\"Histamine is a pre-formed mediator in mast cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tryptase\",\"e\":\"Tryptase is a pre-formed mediator in mast cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Heparin\",\"e\":\"Heparin is pre-formed in mast cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Eosinophilic chemotactic factor of anaphylaxis\",\"e\":\"Eosinophil chemotactic factor is pre-formed in mast cells.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"Mucus in the intestinal tract is important for function. This is mediated through several factors.\\n\\nWhich interleukin promotes mucus secretion in the intestinal tract?\",\"lead\":\"Which interleukin promotes mucus secretion in the intestinal tract?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CL Interleukin-5\",\"e\":\"IL-5 is involved with the activation and function of eosinophils.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-4\",\"e\":\"IL-4 promotes helper T-cell proliferation and B cell lgE synthesis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Interkleukin-1\",\"e\":\"There are 11 members of the interleukin-1 family and most of these members are pro- inflammatory. Interleukin- is associated with mucus secretion from the intestinal tract.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-13\",\"e\":\"As for IL-4, IL-13 promotes helper T-cell proliferation and B cell IgE synthesis. IL-13 mediates mucus secretion within the respiratory tract in asthma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tumour necrosis factor\",\"e\":\"Tumour necrosis factor is part of the acute phase reaction and activates neutrophils, increases monocyte chemotaxis, as well as enhancing the production of other cytokines.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A patient with suspected appendicitis has high blood cells on their FBC. You look closer at the different cell types and note a neutrophilia. They proceed to surgery where a laparoscopic appendicectomy is performed.\\n\\nWhich enzyme is responsible for the production of free radicals in neutrophils?\",\"lead\":\"Which enzyme is responsible for the production of free radicals in neutrophils?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Superoxide dismutase\",\"e\":\"This is an antioxidant that is involved in the neutralisation of free radicals.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Vitamin E\",\"e\":\"This is an antioxidant that is involved in the neutralisation of free radicals.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gluthatione peroxidase.\",\"e\":\"This is an antioxidant that is involved in the neutralisation of free radicals.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Catalase\",\"e\":\"This is an antioxidant that is involved in the neutralisation of free radicals.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"NADPH oxidase\",\"e\":\"NADPH oxidase is found in the wall of the endocytic vacuole. It acts as an electron donor to reduce oxygen to superoxide and hydrogen peroxide.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A patient develops an erythematous rash under the dressing site 48 h post operatively. It is itchy but once the dressing is removed the rash settles. You do not think a wound infection has occurred.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely hypersensitivity reaction type?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely hypersensitivity reaction type?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Type I\",\"e\":\"Type I hypersensitivity is the immediate IgE-mediated response, as occurs in anaphylaxis.\\n\\nType II hypersensitivity is antibody-mediated towards antigen attached to cells, such as in antiglomerular basement membrane disease in the kidney (formerly called Goodpasture's syndrome).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Type II\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Type III\",\"e\":\"Type III hypersensitivity is antibody-mediated towards antigen not attached to cells, such as immune complex deposition in systemic lupus erythematosus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Type IV\",\"e\":\"Type IV, or delayed type hypersensitivity reaction, is T-cell-mediated and onset is typically between 24 and 72 h after exposure. This case presents a patient who has likely developed contact dermatitis from their dressing, which is a type IV hypersensitivity reaction*\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Type V\",\"e\":\"Type V hypersensitivity occurs when autoantibodies are stimulatory, such as in Grave's disease or myasthenia gravis.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A patient suffers status epilepticus and has a violent seizure. After such vigorous and prolonged muscle contraction there will be abnormalities in the blood.\\n\\nWhich of the following pro-inflammatory cytokines secreted by macrophages is also secreted by muscle?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following pro-inflammatory cytokines secreted by macrophages is also secreted by muscle?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-3\",\"e\":\"Predominantly causes monocyte production.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-4\",\"e\":\"I|-4 stimulates the differentiation of naïve T helper cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-5\",\"e\":\"Stimulates eosinophils.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-6\",\"e\":\"Interleukin-6 (IL-6) is a pro-inflammatory cytokine secreted by T cells and macrophages to stimulate the immune response to trauma especially burns or other tissue damage leading to inflammation. IL-6 is also a 'myokine', a cytokine produced by muscle and is elevated in response to muscle contraction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-9\",\"e\":\"Stimulates cell proliferation and prevents apoptosis. IG1G\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A patient has a very high CRP after undergoing debridement for necrotizing fasciitis. They slowly recover and the CRP drops prior to successful discharge.\\n\\nWhich of the following cytokines has anti-inflammatory properties?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following cytokines has anti-inflammatory properties?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF)\",\"e\":\"GM-CSF is a pro-inflammatory cytokine.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-2\",\"e\":\"Interleukin-2 is a pro-inflammatory cytokine.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-3\",\"e\":\"Interleukin-3 is a pro-inflammatory cytokine.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-10\",\"e\":\"Interleukin-10 (IL-10), also known as 'human cytokine synthesis inhibitory factor' (CSIF), is the classical anti-inflammatory cytokine. It is mainly expressed in monocytes, type-2 CD4 T helper cells and mast cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tumour necrosis factor (TNF)\",\"e\":\"TNF is a pro-inflammatory cytokine.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man is seen in the Outpatient Clinic 8 days following a hot oil burn to his arm. He is pyretic, complains of nausea and vomiting and pain to the area. When you inspect the wound, you notice it is covered in a layer of pus.\\n\\nOf the following, which goes up with inflammation and is not a protein?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following, which goes up with inflammation and is not a protein?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antitrypsin\",\"e\":\"This is an acute phase reactant protein that rises with acute infection or inflammation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Caeruloplasmin\",\"e\":\"This is an acute phase reactant protein that rises with acute infection or inflammation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cortisol\",\"e\":\"Cortisol goes up with inflammation and is a steroid hormone, not a protein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Complement\",\"e\":\"This is an acute phase reactant protein that rises with acute infection or inflammation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serum Amyloid P\",\"e\":\"This is a protein that can give rise to secondary amyloidosis, due to chronic inflammatory conditions such as rheumatoid arthritis and bronchiectasis.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"The orthopaedic ward has been closed off due to an outbreak of rotavirus. You discuss cases with the on call microbiologist, who provides advice on treatment measures so that the ward can be reopened sooner.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding the components of the complement system is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding the components of the complement system is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are needed for formation of membrane attack complexes\",\"e\":\"Complement functions can be grouped into: opsonisation, inflammation and membrane perforation. Components C5-C9 comprise the membrane attack complex, which perforates cell membranes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are heat stable\",\"e\":\"Components of the complement system are heat labile\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They disengage mast cells\",\"e\":\"Activated fragments of C3 and C5 have proinflammatory actions and will activate mast cells:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They have a classical pathway involving C14\",\"e\":\"C14 is not a recognised complement fragment involved in either the classical or alternative pathways:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They are not involved in parasitic infections\",\"e\":\"Many extracellular organisms, including helminths have surface components that will activate complement by the alternative pathway and fix C3b on their surface, which aids phagocytosis.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"A 63-year-old woman with known rheumatoid arthritis is admitted with a skin rash over an abdominal wound, after a laparoscopic abdominal operation the previous week. You make a diagnosis of contact dermatitis. Type IV hypersensitivity reactions may be mediated by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Type IV hypersensitivity reactions may be mediated by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antigen-antibody complex\",\"e\":\"Types 1, 2 and 3 hypersensitivity reactions all involve antibody-antigen interaction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Complement complexes\",\"e\":\"Types 2 and 3 hypersensitivity reactions involve complement\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Defects in suppressor cell numbers or function\",\"e\":\"Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune non-organ specific disease. Mechanisms of development of autoimmunity are unclear but may result from defects in suppressor cell numbers or function. However in this instance, the contact dermatitis is due to a type IV hypersensitivity reaction\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Leukotrienes\",\"e\":\"Leukotrienes are a group of biologically active inflammatory mediators produced by white blood cells. While their role in inflammatory disease pathogenesis is being investigated, they do not mediate type IV hypersensitivity reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tlymphocytes\",\"e\":\"Cell-mediated (type IV) hypersensitivity# reactions are also known as delayed hypersensitivity reactions. They are mediated by sensitised T-cells, particularly CD4 T-cells, and so the incidence is reduced in HIV infection.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"You are seeing a new patient with a pelvic mass and your consultant asks for the carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) level to be checked on the blood tests. The test is raised which has an influence on the decision making made at the colorectal MDT.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding serum CEA?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding serum CEA?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is useful for detection of lung cancer\",\"e\":\"It is not a useful diagnostic marker for cancers because it is increased in severe benign liver disease, inflammatory conditions (particularly of the gastrointestinal tract), trauma, infection, collagen diseases, renal impairment and smoking. It may however be used in detecting tumour recurrence, especially in the liver following colorectal cancer resection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is a lipid\",\"e\":\"CEA is a water-soluble glycoprotein\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is raised in 90% of cases of colorectal cancers\",\"e\":\"It is elevated in less than 5% of patients with Dukes' grade A colorectal cancer, 25% of Dukes' B, 44% of Dukes' C and approximately 65% of patients with distant metastases.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Has a mean half-life of 100 days\",\"e\":\"The mean half-life is about 10 days.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It can be elevated in smokers\",\"e\":\"CEA can be raised in smokers and in conditions such as ulcerative colitis, pancreatitis and liver cirrhosis:\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old boy is noted to have poorly controlled asthma after recurrent hospital admissions. Assessment shows he has a very poor inhaler technique and he is given a spacer to use with his inhalers and commenced on montelukast - a leukotriene receptor antagonist.\\n\\nWhich one of the following patterns best describe the actions of leukotrienes?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following patterns best describe the actions of leukotrienes?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased vascular permeability, bronchoconstriction, chemotaxis\",\"e\":\"Leukotrienes increase vascular permeability, bronchoconstriction and chemotaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased vascular permeability, bronchodilation, chemotaxis\",\"e\":\"Leukotrienes increase vascular permeability, bronchoconstriction and chemotaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased vascular permeability, bronchoconstriction, chemotaxis\",\"e\":\"Leukotrienes consist of a set of peptides that are coupled to a metabolite of arachidonic acid. They are important in the acute inflammatory reaction producing vasoconstriction, increased vascular permeability, bronchoconstriction and chemotaxis. Mast cells are a source of leukotrienes and these play a key role in diseases such as asthma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased vascular permeability, bronchodilation, chemotaxis\",\"e\":\"Leukotrienes increase vascular permeability, bronchoconstriction and chemotaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased vascular permeability, no change in bronchodilation or chemotaxis\",\"e\":\"Leukotrienes increase vascular permeability, bronchoconstriction and chemotaxis.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"You are clerking a patient with an unknown primary and multiple metastases on CT scan of the chest. You decide to send tumour markers on the blood panel.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding tumour markers?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding tumour markers?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CA-125 is always absent in the normal ovary\",\"e\":\"CA-125 can still be present in a woman with normal ovaries.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Prostate specific antigen (PSA) is not found in the normal prostate\",\"e\":\"High levels of PSA can be found in men with a normal prostate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are sufficiently specific for screening purposes in the majority of cases\",\"e\":\"Tumour markers are notorious for not having appropriate levels of specificity to be used for screening purposes in the majority of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They provide a definite diagnosis of malignancy\",\"e\":\"Tumour markers are substances that are present in the body at a concentration proportional to the tumour burden. They are not always tumour-specific and are certainly not absolutely a diagnostic for cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They provide diagnostic and prognostic information\",\"e\":\"Tumour markers are most commonly used to monitor therapeutic responses. However, they may also aid in diagnosis, have a role in the detection of relapse and provide prognostic information.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old woman is reviewed in the preoperative clinic with a strong family history of cancer who has had a variety of genetic tests. You are concerned they may have Li-Fraumeni syndrome.\\n\\nWhich one of these is a tumour-suppressor gene?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is a tumour-suppressor gene?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"Explanation i\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"c-Myc\",\"e\":\"c-Myc is an oncogene involved in lymphoma, breast and lung carcinomas.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"erbB2\",\"e\":\"erbB2 (Her2) is an oncogene involved in breast and ovarian cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glioblastoma\",\"e\":\"Glioblastoma is a type of brain tumour.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"K-ras\",\"e\":\"K-ras mutations are found in 80% of pancreatic cancers and in other adenocarcinomas.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"p53\",\"e\":\"Tumour-suppressor genes control abnormal cell proliferation and are inactivated in cancer. p53 is the gene most frequently involved in cancers (more than 50% of all cases). It is seen in the inherited Li-Fraumeni cancer syndrome, lung, breast, colon, and liver cancers as well as leukaemia and lymphomas.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"You are on the cardiothoracic team, looking after a patient with a lung metastasis and no known primary. Your consultant asks for 'tumour markers' to be sent and you are filling out the blood request form.\\n\\nWhich tumour marker and primary cancer are correctly paired?\",\"lead\":\"Which tumour marker and primary cancer are correctly paired?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Colorectal cancer and B-HCG\",\"e\":\"B-HCG is associated with testicular and trophoblastic carcinomas, as well as seminomatous germ cell tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Parathyroid tumours and calcitonin\",\"e\":\"Medullary thyroid carcinoma is known to secrete calcitonin. There are no known markers for parathyroid tumours, in clinical practice currently.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Prostate cancer and alkaline phosphatase\",\"e\":\"Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is associated with prostatic carcinoma. Acid phosphatase and not alkaline phosphatases associated with prostate cancer, but the association with PSA is superior.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Serous ovarian cancer and CA-125\",\"e\":\"CA-125 is associated with ovarian cancers and peritoneal tumours.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Teratoma and placental alkaline phosphatase\",\"e\":\"Alpha-fetoprotein is secreted in high amounts by hepatocellular carcinomas and teratomas. Placental alkaline phosphatase is associated with some tumours but its lack of specificity means that it is not a clinically useful marker.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing the pathology results for a patient with colorectal cancer and genetic analysis of the tissue has been performed. The report mentions the term 'tumour suppressor genes. Which one of the following is a tumour suppressor gene?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a tumour suppressor gene?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"St. c-Myc\",\"e\":\"c-Myc plays a role in gene amplification. It is found in normal cells but functions with less restriction in tumour cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"K-ras\",\"e\":\"K-ras is an oncogene implicated in colorectal, pancreatic and lung carcinomas as well as in leukaemia\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"MHC-II\",\"e\":\"Major histocompatibility complex class II molecules are found on antigen-presenting cells such as dendritic cells. These present peptides from antigens to CD4- T helper cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"TP5S\",\"e\":\"A tumour suppressor gene normally encodes products that inhibit growth and cell proliferation. Tumour protein 53 (TP53) is a tumour suppressor gene whose protein products cause cells to arrest in the G1 phase of the cell cycle. Mutations of this gene are common in cancers, occurring in more than 50% of instances (Li-Fraumeni syndrome; breast, gastric, lung and thyroid cancers).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"erbB2\",\"e\":\"This is an oncogene implicated in breast, ovarian, gastric and bladder carcinoma.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the clinic referred by their GP with raised a-fetoprotein (AFP) levels on blood testing. You must decide how quickly to see this patient as the clinic is always overbooked\\n\\nThis is associated with which one of the following conditions?\",\"lead\":\"This is associated with which one of the following conditions?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Colonic carcinoma\",\"e\":\"AFP is not associated with colonic carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia\",\"e\":\"AFP is not associated with congential adrenal hyperplasia:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hepatocellular carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Alpha-fetoprotein is the fetal equivalent of plasma albumin and is produced by the fetal liver. yolk sac and intestine. It can be elevated in hepatocellular carcinoma (up to 90% of cases), testicular teratoma, pancreatic, biliary, gastric and bronchial cancers.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Nephroblastoma\",\"e\":\"AFP is not associated with nephroblastoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pure seminoma of the testicle\",\"e\":\"A pure seminomatous germ-cell tumour does not produce AFP.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old woman presents to the spinal on-call team with sudden onset low back pain without a history of trauma. Plain films show a collapsed vertebra. Your registrar suspects she may have a malignancy and asks you to send serum samples for tumour markers.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statement is correct regarding these markers?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statement is correct regarding these markers?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CA 125 is elevated in over 50% of patients with stage-1 ovarian cancer\",\"e\":\"CA-125 is elevated in less than 50% of patients with stage-1 ovarian cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"CA 19.9 is elevated in up to 90% of patients with pancreatic carcinoma\",\"e\":\"CA-19-9 is elevated in 75-90% of patients with pancreatic carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Serum CEA is elevated in 10% of cases of Dukes' B colorectal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Serum CEA is elevated in fewer than 5% of patients with Dukes' A colorectal carcinoma, about 25% with Dukes' B and 44% of patients with Dukes' C colorectal carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Serum CEA is elevated in 75% of cases of Dukes' B colorectal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"Serum CEA is elevated in fewer than 5% of patients with Dukes' A colorectal carcinoma, about 25% with Dukes' B and 44% of patients with Dukes' C colorectal carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tumour markers are tumour specific\",\"e\":\"Tumour markers are not tumour specific, which is why the results can be difficult to interpret.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"Tumour suppressor genes are normal genes that slow down cell division, repair DNA mistakes, and induce apoptosis. If they malfunction then neoplastic disease becomes more likely. Abnormalities of which gene will prevent apoptosis?\",\"lead\":\"Abnormalities of which gene will prevent apoptosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"Explanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"R61\",\"e\":\"Retinoblastoma-1 (RB1) gene helps control cell growth and reproduction. Abnormalities in this gene can lead to retinoblastoma in infants.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"p53\",\"e\":\"Tumour-suppressor genes control abnormal cell proliferation and are inactivated in cancer. p53 is the gene most frequently involved in cancers (more than 50% of all cases). It is seen in the inherited Li-Fraumeni cancer syndrome, lung, breast, colon, and liver cancers as well as leukaemia and lymphomas.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"erbB2\",\"e\":\"erbB2 is growth factor receptor, which acts as an oncogene.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Abi\",\"e\":\"Abl is a signal transducer, which acts as an oncogene.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"APC\",\"e\":\"Adenomatous polyposis coli (APC) gene helps control cell growth and reproduction. Mutations in this gene can lead to colon cancer, as well as other cancers such as in the liver.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following options is true of endothelin-1?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is true of endothelin-1?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is a vascular cell-adhesion molecule\",\"e\":\"Levels of endothelin increase in times of stress and it plays a part in modulation of vascular tone, but it is not a vascular cell-adhesion molecule\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is a polypeptide\",\"e\":\"Endothelin-1 is a 21-amino acid polypeptide and is a highly potent vasoconstrictor.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is also known as ICAM-1\",\"e\":\"ICAM-1 is a cellular adhesion molecule.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is a heat-shock protein\",\"e\":\"Heat-shock proteins (HSPs) are also increased during tissue stress and have many different roles.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Was formally known as IL-1\",\"e\":\"IL-1 increases levels of ICAM-1 during episodes of inflammation.\\n\\n1327\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"You are helping your consultant debride a wound when he asks you on the role of phagocytes in wound healing. There are many areas of the body where phagocytes are prominent.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is an example of a phagocyte?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is an example of a phagocyte?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"Lymphocytes are non-phagocytic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Kupffer cells.\",\"e\":\"Phagocytosis is the process whereby phagocytic cells ingest solid particles such as bacteria, dead cells, foreign material and cellular debris. Neutrophils, macrophages and monocytes are phagocytic. Kupffer cells are specialised phagocytic cells that line the hepatic sinusoids.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Plasma cells\",\"e\":\"Plasma cells are non-phagocytic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Erythrocytes\",\"e\":\"Erythrocytes are non-phagocytic\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stellate cells\",\"e\":\"Stellate cells are any ceil that have a star-like shape, and these are non-phagocytic.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"You are discussing the body's immune response to infection. In particular the role that lymphocytes having in ensuring the body is not overwhelmed by pathogens.\\n\\nRegarding B cells and plasma cells, which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding B cells and plasma cells, which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"B cells and plasma cells have surface-bound IgG\",\"e\":\"B cells have surface-bound IgG but plasma cells do not. Plasma cells are fully differentiated from B cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"B cells and plasma cells have surface MHC class II\",\"e\":\"B cells have surface MHC class Il but plasma cells do not. Plasma cells are fully differentiated from B cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Plasma cells can undergo somatic hypermutation\",\"e\":\"B cells can undergo somatic hypermutation but plasma cells cannot. Plasma cells are fully differentiated from B cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"B cells can undergo isotype switching\",\"e\":\"B cells can undergo isotype switching, this is the process of switching through immunoglobulin classes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Plasma cells can undergo isotype switching\",\"e\":\"B cells can undergo isotype switching but plasma cells cannot. Plasma cells are fully differentiated from B cells.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following immune cells has perforin in its granules?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following immune cells has perforin in its granules?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"B lymphocyte\",\"e\":\"Perforin is found in cytotoxic T lymphocytes; it is not found in B lymphocytes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Eosinophil\",\"e\":\"Perforin is a pore forming cytotoxic protein. Perforin is not found in eosinophils.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Kupffer cell\",\"e\":\"Perforin is not found in Kupffer cells. The perforin protein is found is Natural Killer (NK cells) and cytotoxic (CD8) T cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mast cell\",\"e\":\"Perforin is not found in mast cells.\\n\\n1482\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Natural killer cell\",\"e\":\"Perforin is a cytolytic protein found in the granules of CD8 T cells and natural killer (NK) cells. During degranulation, perforin binds to the target cell's plasma membrane and forms pores in it. These pores then allow granzymes - pro-apoptotic proteases - into the cell. Perforin is structurally similar to complement C9\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman develops a deep vein thrombosis (DVT) 7 days following a panproctocolectomy for Crohn's disease. She is positive for anticardiolipin and lupus antıcoagulant antibodies.\\n\\nWhat is the likely underlying condition behind this DVT?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely underlying condition behind this DVT?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor V Leiden mutation\",\"e\":\"Factor V is a co-factor for factor Xa to allow thrombin activation. The Leiden variant of factor V cannot be inactivated therefore patients have a much higher risk of developing a thrombosis. However in this summary, the antibody profiles would be consistent with antiphospholipid syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Protein C deficiency\",\"e\":\"Protein C is a natural anticoagulant and its deficiency is associated with increased risk of thrombosis. However in this summary, the antibody profiles would be consistent with antiphospholipid syndrome.\\n\\nProtein S is a natural anticoagulant and its deficiency is associated with increased risk of thrombosis. However in this summary, the antibody protiles would be consistent with antiphospholipid syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Protein S deficiency\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome\",\"e\":\"The presence of the anticardiolipid and lupus anticoagulant antibodies indicate that this patient has the hypercoagulable state of antipnospholipid syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Antithrombin III deficiency\",\"e\":\"Antithrombin III is a natural anticoagulant and its deficiency is associated with increased risk of thrombosis. However in this summary, the antibody profiles would be consistent with antiphospholipid syndrome.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-cid woman develops nasal discharge and itching of the eyes each year in the spring. She was referred by her GP for allergy testing. Fifteen minutes after skin testing with a mixture of grass pollens, she developed erythema and a 15mm wheal at the site.\\n\\nThe skin test response is most likely to be a result of which one of the following mechanisms?\",\"lead\":\"The skin test response is most likely to be a result of which one of the following mechanisms?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antigen-antibody complexes being formed in blood vessels in the skin\",\"e\":\"Antigen-antibody complexes being formed in blood vessels do not explain the erythema seen in positive skin allergy testing.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Influx of phagocytic cells in response to injection of foreign proteins\",\"e\":\"Influx of phagocytic cells does not explain the erythema seen in positive skin allergy testing.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Release of histamine from mast cells\",\"e\":\"Skin allergy testing is a method used for medical diagnosis of allergies. A microscopic amount of an allergen is introduced to a patient's skin by:\\n\\n• pricking the skin with a needle or pin containing a small amount of the allergen (prick testing' or 'scratch testing') • applying a patch to the skin, where the patch contains the allergen • release of histamine from mast cells produces the characteristic response in a skin test.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Release of lymphokines by sensitised lymphocytes reacting with antigens\",\"e\":\"Release of lymphokines by sensitised lymphocytes does not explain the erythema seen in positive skin allergy testing at this early stage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Release of lymphokines from mast cells\",\"e\":\"Release of lymphokines from mast cells does not explain the erythema seen in positive skin allergy testing. 1484\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old woman shows a type-l hypersensitivity response of the immune system, accompanied by eosinophilia. Which one of the following is most likely to be found in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most likely to be found in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amyloidosis\",\"e\":\"Amyloid deposition does not result in an immune response.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis\",\"e\":\"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis can lead to a type-ll! (ie immune complex disease) reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Liver flukes\",\"e\":\"Parasitic infestations can be accompanied by a type-l hypersensitivity reaction. Liver flukes can be a cause of parasitic infection.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neoplasm\",\"e\":\"Natural immune mechanisms such as type l hypersensitivity reactions directed against neoplasia are not generally effective.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spirochaetes\",\"e\":\"Both cell-mediated and humoral immune mechanisms play a role in syphilis, but not specifically a type-l hypersensitivity reaction. 1495\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old man developed hyperacute rejection following kidney transplantation. The transplant team are devastated as the donor kidney has been wasted. Which one of the following statements is best associated with hyperacute rejection?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is best associated with hyperacute rejection?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A T-cell-mediated response in recipients with pre-existing antibodies to the donor\",\"e\":\"Hyperacute rejection is mediated by pre-existing antibodies but is a complement-mediated response and not T-cell mediated.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Best treated by a large dose of methy/prednisolone\",\"e\":\"Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes and the best treatment is for the transplant to be immediately removed to prevent a severe systemic inflammatory response.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Complement-mediated reaction\",\"e\":\"Hyperacute rejection is a complement-mediated response in recipients with pre-existing antibodies to the donor (for example, ABO blood group antibodies).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Characterised by profuse bleeding\",\"e\":\"Rapid coagulation of the blood occurs in hyperacute rejection.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Occurs within the first day or so after transplantation\",\"e\":\"Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes of a transplant. A prospective cytotoxic crossmatch is performed before kidney transplantation to ensure that antibodies to the donor are not present.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old woman with blood group type A, Rh-negative, is pregnant with her second child. Her first child is Rh-positive and the father is also Rh-positive.\\n\\nThe second child is most likely to be at risk of developing which one of the following conditions?\",\"lead\":\"The second child is most likely to be at risk of developing which one of the following conditions?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"An autoimmune disease\",\"e\":\"There is no known increased risk of autoimmune disease with rhesus incompatibility.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"ABO incompatibility\",\"e\":\"The above summary describes risk for rhesus incompatibility and not ABO incompatibility.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Drug-induced haemolytic anaemia\",\"e\":\"There is no known increased risk of drug-induced haemolytic anaemia with rhesus incompatibility.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neutropenia\",\"e\":\"There is no known increased risk of neutropenia with rhesus incompatibility.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Haemolytic disease of the newborn\",\"e\":\"The infant is at risk of developing haemolytic disease of the newborn. The mechanism involves passage of Rh-positive erythrocytes from the fetus into the blood circulation of a Rh-negative mother, which usually occurs near the time of delivery. The mother becomes sensitised and can begin producing anti-Rh antibodies. The firstborn child is generally not at great risk, because it is born before an appreciable level of immunoglobulin G (IgG) antibody is formed. However, in a subsequent pregnancy with an Rh-positive fetus, the mother can have a strong secondary immune response and IgG antibodies against Rh antigen can give rise to haemolytic disease of the newborn.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"You are finishing a long kidney transplant procedure. At the end of the procedure the graft does not look normal and you think that hyperacute graft rejection has occurred.\\n\\nIn which conditions/situations is antigen-antibody complex reactions seen?\",\"lead\":\"In which conditions/situations is antigen-antibody complex reactions seen?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ABO incompatible blood transfusion .\",\"e\":\"ABO incompatible blood transfusions are due to a type Il hypersensitivity reaction*. This occurs due to antibody-mediated towards antigen attached to cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Contact dermatitis\",\"e\":\"Type IV, or delayed type hypersensitivity reaction is T-cell-mediated, and onset is typically between 24 and 72 h after exposure. An example would be contact dermatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Delayed hypersensitivity skin reactions\",\"e\":\"Type IV, or delayed type hypersensitivity reaction is T-cell-mediated, and onset is typically between 24 and 72 h after exposure. An example would be contact dermatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intracellular destrúction of bacteria\",\"e\":\"There are many processes that can lead to intracellular degradation of bacteria and these are not limited to hypersensitivity reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hayfever and atopic asthma\",\"e\":\"Hayfever, asthma and eczema can occur in combination in atopy. Although not entirely understood, this in part appears to be due to a partial type I hypersensitivity reaction.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"You are clerking a patient in the pre-operative assessment clinic and they have a complement activation abnormality which is hereditary. You want to optimise them for their complex surgical procedure and refer them to the tertiary immunology service.\\n\\nRegarding the complement system, which of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding the complement system, which of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"C4 is activated by viruses in the alternative pathway\",\"e\":\"C4 activation occurs in the classical complement pathway and not the alterative pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is activated by bacteria but not viruses or fungi\",\"e\":\"The complement system can be activated by bacteria, viruses and fungi.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is not a part of the coagulation cascade\",\"e\":\"The complement system plays an important role in the coagulation cascade, particular with regards the contact activation pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The alternative pathway can be activated by endotoxins\",\"e\":\"The complement system consists of over 20 component proteins and is involved in bacterial killing. Complement can be activated by the classical pathway, alternative pathway or via the lectin pathway. In the alternate pathway, C3 is activated directly by endotoxins, viruses, bacteria, fungi and other parasites.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The classical pathway is independent of immunoglobulin for activation\",\"e\":\"The classical pathway is dependent on immunoglobulin for activation.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"You are measuring a patient's immunoglobulin levels, and they have a raised level of IgG. You are concerned immunological pathology may be present and seek advice from the tertiary immunology service.\\n\\nOn which cells are the Fc receptors for IgG found?\",\"lead\":\"On which cells are the Fc receptors for IgG found?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"T lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"T lymphocytes do not possess Fo receptors specific for IgG. T lymphocytes can only recognise, and be activated by, antigens bound to specialised cell-surface glycoproteins (HLA) molecules encoded by the major histocompatibility complex.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"B lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"B lymphocytes do not possess Fc receptors specific for IgG. B lymphocytes can mature into plasma cells that secrete large numbers of antibody molecules with the same antigenic specificity as the receptors of the B cells from which they arose.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Dendritic Cells\",\"e\":\"Dendritic cells are antigen presenting cells and therefore do not express Fc receptors.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Erythrocytes\",\"e\":\"Erythrocytes are haemoglobin containing, oxygen carrying cells. They do not express Fc receptors.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neutrophils and macrophages\",\"e\":\"Macrophages possess Fc receptors specific for IgG.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old boy is brought to the GP by his mum as he has had a sore throat for 5 days and had a fever for 10 days. He reports muscle aches and pains, and on examination he has lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. The GP diagnoses infectious mononucleosis and advises the patient appropriately. It is known that natural killer cells have an important immune role in virus infections such as Epstein-Barr virus (EBV).\\n\\nWhat type of immune cell is a natural killer cell?\",\"lead\":\"What type of immune cell is a natural killer cell?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Basophil\",\"e\":\"Basophils are granulocytes involved in many inflammatory reactions, particularly those that give rise to allergic symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Eosinophil\",\"e\":\"Eosinophils are granulocytes that play a leading role in allergic inflammatory reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lymphocyte\",\"e\":\"Natural killer (NK) cells are large granular lymphocytes that recognise virus products on cell membranes. They perforate cell membranes and produce rapid nuclear fragmentation\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neutrophil\",\"e\":\"Neutrophils are the most abundant granulocyte and play a key role in defence against bacterial organisms.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Mast cell\",\"e\":\"Mast cells are a type of granulocyte that is involved in inflammatory processes, such as allergy and anaphylaxis.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old woman is referred by her GP to a rheumatologist with a history of fatigue, widespread joint pain and swelling and a malar rash. Further blood tests confirm a diagnosis of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). The rheumatologist organises additional blood tests which show a deficiency in complement of the classical pathway.\\n\\nWhat type of immunity is conferred by the complement system?\",\"lead\":\"What type of immunity is conferred by the complement system?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acquired\",\"e\":\"Acquired immunity refers to the development or acquisition of antibodies to antigens. These can be developed following exposure to an antigen or acquired from transmission such as from mother to fetus.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Active\",\"e\":\"Active immunity is the process of triggering the immune system to produce antibodies to a pathogen orits antigen\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cellular\",\"e\":\"A protective immune response that does not involve antibodies but instead involves activation of cells and their respective cytokines and chemokines.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"0 Innate\",\"e\":\"Innate immunity is the body's first line of defence against antigens. It is non-specific and its role is not changed by repeated encounters with antigen. It includes lysozyme, complement, acute phase proteins, pathogens and natural killer (NK) cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Passive\",\"e\":\"Immunity develops immediately and is due to antibodies produced elsewhere, such as from maternal transmission to the fetus, or in immunoglobulin therapy.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"Phagocytes are cells that are involved in the immune response by ingesting harmful foreign bodies. They are critical the overall defence of the human body from pathogenic threats.\\n\\nWhich of the following is an example of these cells?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is an example of these cells?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"B lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"B lymphocytes and their subsequent maturation to plasma cells are involved in antibody production. They are not phagocytes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mast cells\",\"e\":\"Mast cells are granulocytes involved in inflammatory reactions such as allergy and anaphylaxis. They are not phagocytes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Megakaryocytes\",\"e\":\"Megakaryocytes are precursors to platelets. They are not phagocytes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Neutrophil polymorphs\",\"e\":\"Phagocytic cells include polymorphonuclear leukocytes (particularly neutrophils), monocytes and tissue macrophages. Kupffer cells are part of the reticulo-endothelial system involved in phagocytosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"T lymphocytes play a crucial role in the adaptive immune response. They are not phagocytes.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":73,\"stem\":\"Cytokines are small proteins used in the cell signalling pathway. They act through receptors and are of particular importance in the immune system.\\n\\nWhich of the following cytokines have pro-inflammatory properties?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following cytokines have pro-inflammatory properties?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-7\",\"e\":\"IL-7 is involved the development of thymocyte development. and is also involved in other non-immune and haemopoetic functions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-6\",\"e\":\"Pro-inflammatory cytokines such as interleukin-1, interleukin-6 and tumour necrosis factor-a are produced predominantly by activated immune cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-10\",\"e\":\"Anti-inflammatory cytokines such as interleukin-4, interleukin-10 and interleukin-13 are involved in the reduction of inflammatory reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-13\",\"e\":\"Anti-inflammatory cytokines such as interleukin-4, interleukin-10 and interleukin-13 are involved in the reduction of inflammatory reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transforming growth factor (TGF) B\",\"e\":\"TGF-6 is involved inflammatory responses, often as an anti-inflammatory agent. It can act as a pro-inflammatory agent in certain instances, however the predominant effect is thought to be anti-inflammatory.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"Cytotoxic T cells are white blood cells that have a role in the immune response. If they are deficient, the human body becomes susceptible to various types of infection.\\n\\nWhich of the following best describes this role?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following best describes this role?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They do not have a role in graft rejection\",\"e\":\"Cytotoxic T celis recognise foreign transplant antigens and have a major role in graft rejection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They play a major role in the protection of body surfaces\",\"e\":\"IgA is involved in the protection of mucosal surfaces.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are involved in atopic dermatitis\",\"e\":\"IgE is involved in the allergic response of atopic dermatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They are able to kill virally infected cells after prior sensitisation\",\"e\":\"Cytotoxic T lymphocytes are able to recognise antigens presented to them from virally infected cells and trigger immune processes to kill these cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They are not activated by antigen presenting cells\",\"e\":\"Cytotoxic T lymphocytes recognise antigens presented by major histocompatibility class I molecules in antigen presenting cells.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"A macrophage is a type of cell with diverse functions and plays a significant role in adaptive immunity, wound healing and muscle regeneration. They are present in larger number in various regions of the body.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct of these cells?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct of these cells?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are derived from blood lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"Most macrophages are derived from circulating blood monocytes\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are capable of phagocytosis\",\"e\":\"Most macrophages are derived from circulating blood monocytes and are capable of phagocytosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They have a shorter survival than neutrophils ex vivo\",\"e\":\"Macrophages live longer than neutrophils, which survive only 3 to 5 days outside the circulation. While some macrophages last only a few weeks, some survive much longer especially if they have ingested inert material such as carbon particles.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They produce immunoglobulins\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulins are produced by plasma cells not by macrophages.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They do not multiply\",\"e\":\"Multi-nucleate giant cells are formed by coalescence and fusion of macrophages but other macrophages can multiply.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":79,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old woman presents to her doctor with an extremely dry mouth that has stopped her taking part in a choir. On further questioning she reports her eyes are sore and her doctor suspects Sjögren syndrome.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true of the suspected diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of the suspected diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is usually self-limiting\",\"e\":\"Sjögren syndrome is a multi-system disorder that can affect the salivary glands and presents with exocrine involvement (upper respiratory tract, gastrointestinal genitourinary tract), or with non-exocrine involvement (skin, musculoskeletal, lungs, kidneys and neurological). Like other autoimmune conditions, once it develops, it is a chronic condition that the patient has for ife.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is treated with antibiotics\",\"e\":\"Sjögren syndrome can present with multi-system systemic symptoms. Management is mostly directed at controlling symptoms and disease control is achieved with use of immunosuppressive agents such as rituximab. Antibiotics are not typically used in the management of Sjögren syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"it is caused by tuberculosis\",\"e\":\"Tuberculosis is caused by infection with Mycobacterium tuberculosis. While some groups of individuals with Sjögren syndrome may be at slightly higher risk of developing tuberculosis, there is no current evidence that the Mycobacterium tuberculosis organism causes Sjögren syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is associated with elevated rheumatoid factor\",\"e\":\"Rheumatoid factor is an IgM autoantibody directed against IgG. It is found in a wide variety of autoimmune conditions and can be found in 1-2% of members of the population who do not have any autoimmune disease. Rheumatoid factor is often positive in patients with Sjögren syndrome. Sjögren syndrome is the third most common autoimmune disorder, after rheumatoid arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus. Sjogren syndrome is much more common in women with a reported ratio of 9:1. Sjögren syndrome is characterised by dry eyes and dry mouth. It is a mild illness and care consists primarily of symptom treatment, eg artificial tears. High leveis of antinuclear antibody and rheumatoid factor may be present as Sjögren is associated with other autoimmune diseases. Extra-glandular involvement most commonly affects the kidneys, causing tubulointerstitial nephritis. There is also possible adenopathy with pleomorphic lymph node infiltrates. The secretory glands show a periductal lymphocytic infiltrate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is associated with a macrophage infiltration of the secretory gland affected\",\"e\":\"Sjögren syndrome appears to be due to a complex interplay between genetic and environmental factors in a susceptible individual. Infiltration of salivary glands by T cells, in particular CD4 T helper cells seems to be key in triggering active Sjögren disease.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":82,\"stem\":\"A 15-year-old skateboard champion presents to the Emergency Department with an infected laceration on his knee. He reports that the initial injury occurred 3 days ago and it has swollen and become tender since then. You suspect a soft tissue infection.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true regarding leucocyte response to infection and inflammation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding leucocyte response to infection and inflammation?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adaptive immunity is only found in insects\",\"e\":\"The adaptive immune system is made up from highly specialised cells to eliminate or prevent growth of pathogens. This is a specific system, unlike the innate immune system. This adaptive immune system is found in jawed vertebrates and a more primitive version is found in jawless vertebrates.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Interferons induce apoptosis\",\"e\":\"Interferons are a group of signalling proteins, produced in response to pathogens such as viruses. Infected cells release interferons and help clear pathogens in a variety of different ways, one of which includes inducing programmed cell death, apoptosis. The adaptive immune system is composed of highly specialised, systemic cells and processes that eliminate or prevent pathogen growth. It creates immunological memory after an initial response to a specific pathogen, leading to an enhanced response to subsequent encounters with that same pathogen. Interferons are a family of glycoproteins that are important for a host's response to viral infection. Interferon and interleukin 2 enhance the cytotoxic activity of natural killer cells, which play a major role in the destruction of malignant cells, virally infected cells and some normal cells, without prior sensitisation. Natural killer cells provide rapid responses to viral-infected cells and respond to tumour tissue, and work by cytokine release, causing lysis or apoptosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Interferons primarily opsonise bacteria\",\"e\":\"Interferons are important for a host's response to viral infection.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Natural killer cells do not destroy malignant cells\",\"e\":\"Natural killer (NK) cells are a type of cytotoxic cell. They respond to infection and also play a key role in clearance of malignant cells. The function of NK cells is often stimulated by interferon gamma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Natural killer cells phagocytose virally infected cells\",\"e\":\"NK cells cause the death of virally infected cells either by apoptosis or through osmotic cell lysis. This does not involve the process of phagocytosis. NK cells have more recently been shown to have phagocytotic activity against fungi although phagocytic activity of NK cells is lower than that of professional phagocytes like macrophages.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":85,\"stem\":\"A 37-year-old man presents with malaise and fever. He has recently been working in sub- Saharan Africa in an oilfield. He has had unprotected sexual intercourse on multiple occasions.\\n\\nRegarding HIV immunocompromised patients, which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding HIV immunocompromised patients, which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are resistant to anorectal sepsis\",\"e\":\"Immunocompromised patients with HIV are more susceptible to infections and development of sepsis. HIV directly infects T helper cells. As well as reducing the number and function of these cells, it also impairs the ability of the immune system to deal with other indirect responses.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They have no impairment of wound healing\",\"e\":\"Immunocompromised patients with HIV have higher risk of impaired wound healing.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They have no increased complication rate following minor surgery\",\"e\":\"Immunocompromised patients with HIV have been shown to have higher complication rates following surgery, even following minor surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They should not undergo surgery for anorectal abscesses\",\"e\":\"The key to treatment for abscesses, is to ensure source control. This would involve surgical drainage of the abscess, regardless of whether the patient is immunocompromised.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They may develop pyomyositis\",\"e\":\"Pyomyositis presents with pain, tenderness and swelling of muscles, and tends to occur in greater frequency in patients who are immunocompromised.\\n\\nImmunodeficiency will delay wound healing and increases the risk of postoperative complications such as wound infection and breakdown. HIV, if left untreated or inadequately treated, can progress to Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome (AIDS). Patients with AIDS have a high incidence of anorectal sepsis but undergo the same treatment for anorectal sepsis as non-HIV-infected patients. Pyomyositis presents with pain, tenderness and swelling, with the overlying skin being smooth and shiny, thereby mimicking an abscess.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":88,\"stem\":\"Hypersensitivity is a set of reactions produced by a normal immune system in response to an invading pathogen that results in tissue damage.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is an example of an antibody-dependent cytotoxic hypersensitivity reaction?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is an example of an antibody-dependent cytotoxic hypersensitivity reaction?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Any type 5 hypersensitivity reactions\",\"e\":\"Type 5 hypersensitivity reactions* are stimulatory and occur when anti-receptor antibodies lead to stimulation of cell function. Examples are Graves' disease and myasthenia gravis. Type- 5 reactions can be considered as a subtype of type 2 reactions, due to binding to receptors rather than to the cell surface.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Blood transfusion reaction causing ABO incompatibility\",\"e\":\"Blood transfusion causing ABO incompatibility would be a type 2 hypersensitivity reaction due to antibody-mediated cell destruction. Antibody-dependent hypersensitivity reactions are type-ll reactions. Graves' is a type 5 hypersensitivity reaction (stimulating antibody) according to the Gell and Coombs classification. It involves IgG antibodies reacting with tissue receptors: Gell and Coombs classification:\\n\\nMediators Type Examples\\n\\nType 1 Asthma and peanut allergy, anaphylactic (include) Histamine, Leukotrienes (anaphylactic or shock immediate\\n\\nTransfusion reaction, Antibodies against intrinsic or extrinsic antigens on the cell surface or on other Type 2 (cytotoxic) autoimmune thrombocytopenia components\\n\\nType 3 (immune Immune complexes (antigen-antibody) SLE, acute formed either in the circulation or at complex- glomerulonephritis mediated) extravascular sites\\n\\nTB Mantoux test, Type 4 (cell Sensitised lymphocytes mediated/delayed) transplant rejection\\n\\nAnti-receptor antibodies lead to stimulation Graves' disease Type 5 myasthenia gravis (stimulatory) of cell function\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyperthyroidism in Graves' disease\",\"e\":\"Graves' disease occurs due to stimulating auto-antibodies leading to hyperthyroidism and this would be an example of type 5 hypersensitivity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Involves lgA antibodies reacting with tissue receptors\",\"e\":\"IgA antibody reactions with tissues is immune complex-mediated and would be an example of a type 3 hypersensitivity reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The primary mechanism of acute early rejection of a transplanted kidney\",\"e\":\"Acute (weeks to months) graft rejection of a transplanted kidney is due to sensitised T lymphocytes. This is an example of a type 4 hypersensitivity reaction.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":91,\"stem\":\"A 9-year-old boy is admitted to the Emergency Department in respiratory arrest following an anaphylaxis to a bee sting. He is given IM adrenaline in the resuscitation area and in addition is intubated and ventilated. He makes a full recovery on the PICU. Which one of the statements regarding hypersensitivity reactions is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the statements regarding hypersensitivity reactions is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anaphylactic hypersensitivity depends on the reaction of antigen with specific immunoglobulin E (IgE) antibody bound to the mast cell\",\"e\":\"Type 1 hypersensitivity+ is an immediate hypersensitivity reaction due to immunoglobulin E (IgE)-sensitised mast cells and basophils releasing mediators of inflammation which produce the symptom of allergy. Examples include hay fever, asthma, urticaria, drug allergy and bee and wasp venom anaphylaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Immune complex-mediated hypersensitivity (type 3) does not involve complement\",\"e\":\"Type 3 hypersensitivity is caused by IgG and IgM antibodies. Antibodies bind to soluble antigen and form antigen/antibody complexes, which activate complement cascades and cause inflammation wherever they are deposited, resulting in diseases such as nephritis, farmer's lung disease, serum sickness and autoimmunity.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemolytic anaemia is an example of type 1 hypersensitivity\",\"e\":\"Type 2 hypersensitivity is caused by IgG and IgM antibodies. Type 2 mechanisms are important in autoimmunity and organ transplantation. Examples include haemolytic anaemia, haemolytic disease of the newborn, drug reactions and antiglomerular basement membrane disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Steroids are effective against late type 3 reactions\",\"e\":\"Type 3 hypersensitivity is caused by IgG and IgM antibodies. Antibodies bind to soluble antigen and form antigen/antibody complexes, which activate complement cascades and cause inflammation wherever they are deposited, resulting in diseases such as nephritis, farmer's lung disease, serum sickness and autoimmunity. Steroid treatment is used to prevent late type 1 hypersensitivity reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The formation of chronic granulomata is an example of a type 5 reaction\",\"e\":\"Type 4 hypersensitivity can result in chronic granulomata and these reactions are based on T-cell-antigen interaction, which results in recruitment of other cells to the site. The time course of the reactions is delayed compared to the types 1 and 3, reaching a peak 24-48 h after injection or contact. The classic type 4 responses are the tuberculin and contact allergy (eg nickel, chromate, cosmetics and some drugs).\\n\\nType 5 hypersensitivity reactions are stimulatory and occur when anti-receptor antibodies lead to stimulation of cell function. Examples are Graves' disease and myasthenia gravis. Type 5 reactions can be considered as a subtype of type 2 reactions, due to the binding on receptors rather than the cell surface.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":94,\"stem\":\"A 12-year-old boy is admitted in acute respiratory distress following an asthma attack\\n\\nGell and Coombs type 1 hypersensitivity classically involves which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Gell and Coombs type 1 hypersensitivity classically involves which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"RI Immunoglobulin D(IgD)\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin E is responsible for type 1 hypersensitivity reactions. Immunoglobulin D is usually co-expressed with IgM, and the function of IgD is not well established but, given it is preserved across multiple different species, suggests an important adaptive immune system function. IgD is involved in B lymphocyte signalling and also seems to promote binding of basophils and mast cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Immunoglobulin A (IgA)\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin E is the classical antibody in type-l hypersensitivity reactions. Although IgE is the least abundant immunoglobulin in serum, it can trigger a significant inflammatory reaction, by binding to Fc receptors found on various cells, including mast cells and basophils. Type 1 reactions are those in which antigen interacts with immunoglobulin E (IgE) bound to tissue mast cells or basophils. This results in the release of many inflammatory mediators, including leukotrienes, histamine, prostaglandins, etc. which can result in a profound inflammatory response.\\n\\nImmunoglobulin A is the most abundant immunoglobulin produced by mucosal membranes. Decreased or absent lgA can result in immunodeficiency, however type 1 hypersensitivity reactions do not classically involve IgA.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Immunion E - I\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Macrophages\",\"e\":\"Macrophages are phagocytic cells that play a key role in the innate immune system. They are not the classical cell type implicated in type 1 hypersensitivity.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Osteoblasts\",\"e\":\"Osteoblasts are cells involved in the process of bone remodelling. They do not play a significant role in type 1 hypersensitivity reactions.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":97,\"stem\":\"The complement system is part of the innate immune system and enhances the ability of antibodies and phagocytic cells to clear pathogens.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a role of this system?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a role of this system?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SPI DNA repair\",\"e\":\"Complement is not involved in DNA repair mechanisms.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Phagocytosis is\",\"e\":\"Complement is not involved in phagocytosis. Complement factors Ca and C3a are anaphylatoxins, which are chemotactic and activate mast cells. The formation of the membrane attack complex (MAC) by the terminal components of the common pathway cause cell lysis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"RNA synthesis\",\"e\":\"Complement is not involved in RNA synthesis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Opsonisation\",\"e\":\"Complement coats (opsonises) antigen, making it more attractive for macrophages, and can fix antibody by the classical pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T-cell activation\",\"e\":\"Complement is not directly involved in T-cell activation. T-cell activation occurs by T-cell receptor (TCR)/antigen interaction in association with the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) on antigen-presenting cells.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":99,\"stem\":\"A 19-year-old girl develops lip swelling and stridor after administration of penicillin. Once the acute episode has resolved she is given a medical alert bracelet to warn clinicians about her severe reaction to penicillin and prevent further episodes.\\n\\nWhat is the type of hypersensitivity reaction seen here?\",\"lead\":\"What is the type of hypersensitivity reaction seen here?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Type 1\",\"e\":\"This is an anaphylactic reaction. Anaphylaxis is a type 1 hypersensitivity, which is an immediate hypersensitivity reaction* due to immunoglobulin E (IgE)-sensitised mast cells and basophils releasing mediators of inflammat on that produce the symptom of allergy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Type 2\",\"e\":\"Type 2 hypersensitivity is caused by antibodies binding to host cells. Type 2 mechanisms are important in autoimmunity and are cytotoxic. Examples include haemolytic anaemia, haemolytic disease of the newborn, drug reactions and antiglomerular basement membrane disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Type 3\",\"e\":\"Type 3 hypersensitivity is caused by IgG and IgM antibodies. Antibodies bind to soluble antigen and form antigen / antibody complexes, which activate complement cascades and cause inflammation wherever they are deposited, resulting in diseases such as nephritis, farmer's lung disease, serum sickness and autoimmunity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Type 4\",\"e\":\"Type 4 hypersensitivity can result in chronic granulomata and these reactions are based on T-cell-antigen interaction, which results in recruitment of other cells to the site. The time course of the reactions is delayed compared with the types 1 and 3, reaching a peak 24-48 h after injection or contact. The classic type-4 responses are the tuberculin and contact allergy (eg nickel, chromate, cosmetics and some drugs)\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Type 5\",\"e\":\"Type 5 hypersensitivity reactions are stimulatory and occur when anti-receptor antibodies lead to stimulation of cell function. Examples are Graves' disease and myasthenia gravis. Type 5 reactions can be considered as a subtype of type 2 reactions, due to binding to receptors rather than to the cell surface.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"A patient is given penicillin on the ward and soon after develops respiratory distress. The nurses call you and you note a diffuse rash as well as angioedema, you place an adult medical emergency call and ask for the anaphylaxis box.\\n\\nIn a patient with anaphylaxis, which of the following should be given to inhibit the important late-phase reaction?\",\"lead\":\"In a patient with anaphylaxis, which of the following should be given to inhibit the important late-phase reaction?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antihistamines\",\"e\":\"Anti-histamines do not affect the last-phase reaction response seen in patients with anaphylactic reactions. Anti-histamines are usually H,-histamine antagonists and typically used to reduce levels of histamine, which is a chemical released in the early response. However, there is little evidence to support their use despite them being commonly administered.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline\",\"e\":\"Adrenaline is typically used to reduce the reaction from chemicals that are released from the early response in type 1 hypersensitivity.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Leukotriene inhibitor\",\"e\":\"Leukotrienes are typically released in the early response in type 1 hypersensitivity and leukotriene receptor antagonists have been trialled to try and reduce this response. Their use, however, is not widespread.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hydrocortisone\",\"e\":\"Steroids are useful to reduce the late response of a type 1 hypersensitivity reaction. They should be given intravenously/intramuscularly at a dose of 100-200 mg. Type 1 hypersensitivity is characterised by an early response over minutes to hours and a late response which is characterised by release of chemical mediators with effects which can last several hours to days.\\n\\nChemical mediators of early response: (released Histamine, serotonin, proteases, prostaglandin D2. leukotriene Ca and Da from mast cells)\\n\\nChemical mediators of late response: (products Prostaglandins, bradykinin, leukotrienes from metabolism of arachidonic acid\\n\\nHydrocortisone blocks the generation of leukotrienes and prostaglandins, and hence prevents the late-phase reaction, often seen in asthma. Approximately 30% of deaths related to anaphylaxis occur because of this late-phase reaction..\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAID)\",\"e\":\"Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents do not help reduce the late response from a type 1 hypersensitivity reaction.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":105,\"stem\":\"A dental practice nurse is admitted for an elective operation. During induction with a general anaesthetic she develops tachycardia, rash and diffuse wheeze. She had mentioned that she has had rashes when assisting with anaesthetics at work.\\n\\nWhat is the likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Sł Systemic mastocytosis\",\"e\":\"This occurs due to a neoplastic proliferation of mast cells and typically presents with diarrhoea, abdominal pain, flushing of face and the classical picture of urticaria pigmentosa (production of wheals on rubbing areas of skin). Serum tryptase levels are markedly elevated as well as having high urinary histamine levels. Management involves treatment of both the hypersensitivity reaction and the underlying disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anaphylaxis\",\"e\":\"Anaphylaxis is a type 1 hypersensitivity reaction* that results from the rapid degranulation of mast cells and basophils, usually, but not always, caused by the parental administration of drugs such as penicillin, or insect stings. Anaphylaxis is a medical emergency that can result in death either by cardiovascular collapse, laryngeal oedema and/or bronchial smooth muscle constriction and anoxia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pseudoallergy\",\"e\":\"Presents similarly to a true allergy, but is caused by alterations in metabolism of histamine or by certain forms of food intolerance.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Serum sickness\",\"e\":\"Serum sickness is a type 3 hypersensitivity reaction mediated by immune complex deposition. This can be more common with chimaeric antibodies, which are at least partly derived from animais. Typically it presents with rashes, itching, joint pains and fevers, within a few days of exposure. On discontinuing medication, the symptoms usually settle within about 5 days. Corticosteroids, anti-histamines and analgesia can be used as adjunctive treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Contact dermatitis\",\"e\":\"Allergic contact dermatitis is caused by a type 4 delayed hypersensitivity reaction to a chemical in contact with the skin. Initial sensitisation can occur 7-10 days after the first contact with a potent allergen. However, it is more usually a consequence of many months or years of exposure to small amounts of the allergen. Once sensitised, contact with the allergen can produce dermatitis within 24-48 h, and all areas of the body are equally susceptible.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":108,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted with diverticulitis complicated by a local perforation. On admission they have a higher fever with tachycardia. They are clearly septic and managed initially with just IV antibiotics.\\n\\nWhich one of the following biochemical parameters will not rise in sepsis as part of the acute phase response?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following biochemical parameters will not rise in sepsis as part of the acute phase response?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"i'S' Glucose\",\"e\":\"Glucose rises partly because of the release of other stress hormones including cortisol and growth hormone.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Albumin\",\"e\":\"Albumin is a transport protein, which decreases in the acute phase response.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"C3\",\"e\":\"C3 is part of the complement proteins important in fighting infection. C3 is raised as part of the acute phase response.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Alpha-1-antitrypsin\",\"e\":\"Alpha-1-antitrypsin helps minimise tissue damage from proteinases that may be released following tissue damage and rises as part of the acute phase response.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Haptoglobin\",\"e\":\"Haptoglobin is an iron-binding protein that will pick up any released iron from damaged cells and prevent it being utilised by foreign micro-organisms.\\n\\nThe acute phase response is a humoral and biochemical response to any major insult on the. body including surgery, sepsis, pancreatitis and burns. This response is a stress response designed to optimise the body's response to trauma or infection and minimise ongoing tissue damage.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":111,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman with severe rheumatoid arthritis is reviewed by her rheumatologist. It is noted she has not responded to disease-modifying antirheumatoid drugs (DMARDs) and has been unable to tolerate infliximab. It is decided to commence tocilizumab, an interleukin- 6 inhibitor.\\n\\nWhich one of the following cell types is responsible for predominantly releasing interleukins?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following cell types is responsible for predominantly releasing interleukins?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"B cells\",\"e\":\"B cells (lymphocytes) are predominantly involved in the humoral component of the adaptive immune system and work by secreting antibodies.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fibroblasts\",\"e\":\"These are cells which provide the structural framework (stroma) for most animals and synthesise extracellular matrix and collagen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Eosinophils\",\"e\":\"Eosinophils are a subset of white blood cells that play a key role in allergy. Following activation, eosinophils degranulate to release a multitude of different cytotoxic granules.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Macrophages\",\"e\":\"Macrophages are phagocytic white blood cells that engulf and digest foreign substances and microbes. Interleukins are released predominantly by activated macrophages into the systemic circulation and have a wide range of effects. These include stimulation of acute phase proteins, stimulation of the hypothalamus to produce fever, and they may also have direct cytotoxic properties.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Mast cells\",\"e\":\"Mast cells are a type of white blood cell that contains granules rich in molecules such as histamine. Mast cells are very similar to basophils in structure and function. Mast cells play a key role in allergy and anaphylaxis\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":114,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old man is admitted to hospital for elective femoral angioplasty. He has blood samples taken for 'group and save'. His cells are not agglutinated by either anti-A or anti-B.\\n\\nBased on these data, which one of the following is most likely to be his genotype?\",\"lead\":\"Based on these data, which one of the following is most likely to be his genotype?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"Explanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"AB\",\"e\":\"Blood group AB means there are no antibodies in plasma and antigens A and B are present in red blood cells, therefore this would cause agglutination of cells of blood group A or B.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AO\",\"e\":\"Blood group AO means there are antibodies in plasma to antigen B and that antigen A is present in red blood cells. This option would not fit the above picture.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"BB\",\"e\":\"Blood group BB means there are antibodies in plasma to antigen A and that antigen B is present in red blood cells. This option would not fit the above picture.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"BO\",\"e\":\"Blood group BO means there are antibodies in plasma to antigen A and that antigen B is present in red blood cells. This would not fit the above picture.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"OO\",\"e\":\"Blood group 00 means there are antibodies in plasma to both antigens A and B, with neither of these antigens present in red blood cells. This option would fit the picture described above. If cells are not agglutinated by anti-A or anti-B antibodies, he must be blood group O. The blood group O allele is recessive and the genotype must therefore be O0. Group O individuals have naturally occurring antibodies to A and B blood group antigens, and it would therefore be expected that his serum would agglutinate cells bearing these antigens.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":117,\"stem\":\"Haemolytic disease of the newborn is typically restricted to the presence of Rhesus antigens on red cells rather than ABO antigens. Predominantly, such anti-Rh antibodies cross the placenta during the third trimester.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements best explains the background physiology?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements best explains the background physiology?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antibodies to ABO blood groups are IgM, whereas antibodies to Rhesus antigens are IgG\",\"e\":\"ABO antibodies are of the IgM subtype while antibodies to Rhesus antigens are of the IgG subtype. IgG antibodies to Rhesus antigens can cross the placenta during the last trimester. whereas ABO antibodies are IgM and hence cannot cross the placenta. The function of serum IgD is unknown. The transplacental passage of immunoglobulin only applies to IgG.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Antibodies to ABO blood groups are IgG, whereas antibodies to Rhesus antigens are IgM\",\"e\":\"ABO antibodies are of the IgM subtype while antibodies to Rhesus antigens are of the IgG subtype.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Antibodies to ABO blood groups are IgA, whereas antibodies to Rhesus antigens are IgG\",\"e\":\"ABO antibodies are of the IgM subtype while antibodies to Rhesus antigens are of the IgG subtype.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Antibodies to Rhesus antigens are IgD, whereas anti-ABO blood groups are IgM\",\"e\":\"ABO antibodies are of the IgM subtype while antibodies to Rhesus antigens are of the IgG subtype.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Antibodies to Rhesus antigens are IgE, whereas anti-ABO blood groups are IgG\",\"e\":\"ABO antibodies are of the IgM subtype while antibodies to Rhesus antigens are of the IgG subtype.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":120,\"stem\":\"Which of the following is the most influential cytokine involved in coagulation activation?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most influential cytokine involved in coagulation activation?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PA Interleukin-2\",\"e\":\"Interleukin-2 is a key driver of regulatory T-cell proliferation. There is subsequently great interest in using this as a potential novel therapeutic for treatment of autoimmune and autoinflammatory conditions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-3\",\"e\":\"Interleukin-3 is mainly released by Thi type CD4 T helper cells and stimulates production of monocytes from haematopoietic progenitor cells..\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-4\",\"e\":\"Interleukin-4 is predominantly released by regulatory T cells and Th2 type CD4 T helper cells and predominantly leads to antiinflammatory actions.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-5\",\"e\":\"Interleukin-5 is predominantly released by Th2 type CD4 T helper cells and stimulates B lymphocyte growth, differentiation and stimulation of eosinophils.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Interleukin-6\",\"e\":\"Interleukin-6 is released by macrophages and plays a key role in activation of lymphocytes as well as playing an important role in coagulation. IL-6 together with tumour necrosis factor are the most influential cytokines involved in coagulation activation.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":122,\"stem\":\"A medical SHO is required to give a blood sample to check his Hep B status. He received a course of vaccinations nine months ago.\\n\\nWhat is his blood test likely to show?\",\"lead\":\"What is his blood test likely to show?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anti-HBe\",\"e\":\"Anti-HBe implies seroconversion with infectivity declining on production of this antibody. If persistent this result indicates chronic infection\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anti-HBs\",\"e\":\"Anti-HBs implies either recovery from hepatitis B infection or immunity following administration of the hepatitis B vaccine.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anti-HBs + anti-HBc\",\"e\":\"Anti-HBs implies either recovery from hepatitis B infection or immunity following administration of the hepatitis B vaccine. Anti-HBc implies either previous or current infection.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"HBsAg + HBcAg\",\"e\":\"HBsAg is the first marker to appear and implies acute disease, while HBcAg is a marker of infectivity.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"IgM to HBcAg\",\"e\":\"IgM to HBcAg implies acute or recent infection within the last six months.\\n\\nSurface and core antigens (HBsAg, HBcAg) are detectable during acute infection. HBeAg (envelope) is a good marker of high infectivity, whilst anti-HBeAg suggests a patient who is less infective. Acute infection is also implied by IgM to HBcAg, whilst IgG to HBcAg suggests a previous infection. Virus clearance and recovery correlate with the disappearance of antigens and the appearance of antibodies. Previous vaccination is suggested by the presence of only anti-HBs.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":125,\"stem\":\"A patient is being managed for severe Crohn's disease. They are commenced on immunoglobulin therapy and make a good improvement and this delays the need for future surgery. Which of the following regions forms the antigen binding site of the immunoglobin structure?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following regions forms the antigen binding site of the immunoglobin structure?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The heavy chain\",\"e\":\"The antigen-binding site is situated within the variable region of the immunoglobulin structure, made up of both the heavy and light chains, specifically within the hypervariable regions:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The light chain\",\"e\":\"The antigen-binding site is situated within the variable region of the immunoglobulin structure, made up of both the heavy and light chains, specifically within the hypervariable regions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The constant region of one heavy and one light chain\",\"e\":\"The antigen-binding site is situated within the variable region of the immunoglobulin structure, made up of both the heavy and light chains, specifically within the hypervariable regions:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The hinge region\",\"e\":\"The immunoglobulin hinge (interdomain) region is a region in the central part of the heavy chains of lgA and IgA immunoglobulin classes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The variable region of one heavy and one light chain\",\"e\":\"The antigen-binding site is situated within the variable region of the immunoglobulin structure, made up of both the heavy and light chains, specifically within the hypervariable regions.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":128,\"stem\":\"Which immunoglobulin can fix complement via the alternative pathway?\",\"lead\":\"Which immunoglobulin can fix complement via the alternative pathway?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"IgA\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin A is an antibody that plays a key role in mucous membrane immune function and can fix complement via the alternative complement pathway\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"IgM\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin M is an antibody produced by B cells and is the largest antibody. It is typically the first antibody to appears following initial exposures to an antigen. IgM can fix complement via the classical pathway through the Fc portion of the immunoglobulin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"IgG\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin G is the most abundant antibody in humans. It is released by plasma B cells via a variety of mechanisms in response to pathogens such as bacteria, viruses, fungi and so forth. IgG can fix complement via the classical pathway through the Fc portion of the immunoglobulin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"IgE\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin E is synthesised by plasma cells and consists of two heavy chains and two light chains. It plays a significant role in type I hypersensitivity reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"IgD\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin D is produced by immature B lymphocytes and is usually coexpressed by IgM. The function of IgD is still relatively unknown.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":130,\"stem\":\"A patient is diagnosed with sepsis. They mount a systemic response to the infection and pull through from the infection without requiring intervention.\\n\\nWhich one of the following activates the classical complement pathway?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following activates the classical complement pathway?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bacteria\",\"e\":\"The indirect pathway is activated by micro-organisms such as bacteria. Bacterial cell surface molecules interact with the plasma proteins and trigger complement activation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Imunoglobulins\",\"e\":\"Direct activation of complement occurs with activated immunoglobulins.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"T lymphocytes\",\"e\":\"T lymphocytes are not involved in the direct activation of the complement pathway\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Natural killer cells\",\"e\":\"Natural killer cells are not involved in the direct activation of the complement pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Monocytes\",\"e\":\"Monocytes are involved in activation of complement via the alternative pathway. The complement cascade is an enzyme-driven process involving the activation of precursors that promote inflammation, phagocytosis and the direct killing of micro-organisms. There are two pathways - the classical and the alternative - also known as direct and indirect. Direct activation of complement occurs with activated immunoglobulins. The indirect pathway is activated by micro-organisms such as bacteria Bacterial cell surface molecules interact with the plasma proteins and trigger complement activation.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":132,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following cells is cytotoxic?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following cells is cytotoxic?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CD4 T-cells\",\"e\":\"CD4 T cells are T helper cells that recognise antigen only in the context of class II MHC.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"CD8 T-cells\",\"e\":\"CD8 T cells are cytotoxic and recognise cell-bound antigens only in association with class | MHC.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"B cells\",\"e\":\"B lymphocytes (or plasma cells) secrete antibodies.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tal cells\",\"e\":\"Tel ceils are a subset of CD4 T helper cells, characterised by secretion of a typical set of cytokines. These cytokines include interferon-g, interleukin-2, interleukin-3 and tumour necrosis factor - a and b\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"T.2 cells\",\"e\":\"T_2 cells are a subset of CD4 T helper cells, characterised by secretion of a typical set of cytokines. These cytokines include interleukin-4, interleukin -5, interleukin-6, interleukin-9, interleukin-10 and interleukin-13.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":134,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old woman is admitted to hospital for elective inguinal hernia repair. Her blood is taken for 'group and save'. Considering the ABO and rhesus (Rh) systems, which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Considering the ABO and rhesus (Rh) systems, which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"If the patient blood group is AB, his serum will have anti-A and anti-B antibodies\",\"e\":\"In AB blood group, the patient will have A and B antigen but not anti-A and anti-B antibodies.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Naturally occurring anti-A and anti-B antibodies are usually IgG\",\"e\":\"ABO blood group antibodies are usually IgM and are present on most of body cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A patient with O blood group blood can receive a donation from any other ABO group.\",\"e\":\"O group individuals are universal donors (they have anti-A and anti-B antibodies), while AB can receive blood from all of the other groups (no antibodies).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The presence of the D antigen makes the subject rhesus positive\",\"e\":\"This is the definition of being rhesus positive, these individuals should not donate their blood to rhesus-negative patients - particularly in women of child bearing age as it can precipitate haemolytic disease of the newborn.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rhesus antibodies are naturally occurring antibodies\",\"e\":\"Rhesus antibodies do not occur naturally and are formed as immune antibodies upon exposure to antigen.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":136,\"stem\":\"A patient is infected with a viral illness which their immune system is able to overcome. Several months later they are exposed to the same pathogen, however the immune system overcomes the virus before any symptoms manifest\\n\\nImmunity conferred by immune responses involving plasma cells is described as which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Immunity conferred by immune responses involving plasma cells is described as which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Complement immunity\",\"e\":\"The complement system is a part of the innate immune system that enhances the ability of antibodies and phagocytic cells to clear pathogens and damaged cells (opsonization), promote inflammation, and activate the cell-killing membrane attack complex.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Cellular immunity\",\"e\":\"Cellular immunity is a protective immune response that does not involve antibodies but instead involves activation of cells and their respective cytokines and chemokines.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Humoral immunity\",\"e\":\"Plasma cells are terminally differentiated B lymphocytes that secrete antibodies. Humoral immunity refers to antibody-mediated responses.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Innate immunity\",\"e\":\"Innate immunity is the body's first line of defence against antigens. It is non-specific and its role is not changed by repeated encounters with antigen. It includes lysozyme, complement, acute phase proteins, pathogens and natural killer (NK) cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Passive immunity\",\"e\":\"Passive immunity develops immediately and is due to antibodies produced elsewhere, such as from maternal transmission to the fetus.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":138,\"stem\":\"A patient undergoes a successful kidney transplant with a compatible donor. They can look forward to many years of better health however they will require lifelong immunosuppression. They are commenced on ciclosporin\\n\\nThis drug acts via which immune mechanism of action?\",\"lead\":\"This drug acts via which immune mechanism of action?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inhibiting release of interleukin (IL)-2 from T cells\",\"e\":\"Ciclosporin works by inhibiting release of interleukin (IL)-2 from T-cells, thereby inhibiting their proliferation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increasing IL-2 release\",\"e\":\"IL-2 plays a critical role in the development and stimulation of regulatory T-cells. Therapeutic use of IL-2 is an exciting area but still remains a novel approach restricted to the research setting.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increasing IL-10 production?\",\"e\":\"IL-10 is a natural anti-inflammatory cytokine but no successful analogues are available in clinical practice.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreasing CD4 expression\",\"e\":\"CD4 is expressed by helper T-cells. Ciclosporin has no effect on CD.4 expression.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreasing expression of the T cell receptor\",\"e\":\"The T-cell receptor is found on the surface of T lymphocytes and plays a key role in helping recognise antigens presenting by major histocompatibility complexes. Ciclosporin has no effect on the T-cell receptor.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":140,\"stem\":\"A patient is undergoing a renal transplant, it is a long procedure but thought to be successful. As the abdomen is starting to be closed you notice the graft does not look as it should. You are concerned the patient may have hyperacute allograft rejection.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is associated this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is associated this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cytotoxic T-cells\",\"e\":\"Cytotoxic T-cells can play a role in acute graft rejection as well as possibly playing a role in chronic graft rejection. They are not the drivers of hyperacute graft rejection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decay-accelerating factor\",\"e\":\"Decay-accelerating factor has been implicated in regulating activation of complement. This does not play a role in hyperacute graft rejection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class II\",\"e\":\"MHC class I plays a key role in hyperacute graft rejection, not the class II subtype.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Natural killer (NK) cells\",\"e\":\"NK cells play a role in acute graft rejection but not a significant role in hyperacute graft rejection.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pre-existing humoral antibodies\",\"e\":\"Hyperacute graft rejection occurs within minutes of transplantation and is a consequence of pre-existing humoral antibodies. These are usually due to pre-sensitisation to MHC class I or to blood group incompatibility.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":142,\"stem\":\"A patient requires a graft for tissue that is not functioning normally. An allograft is available immediately and you must advise them whether or not to take this opportunity.\\n\\nWhich one of the following options best describes an allograft?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options best describes an allograft?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Tissue from one individual transplanted to another\",\"e\":\"An allograft is an organ graft between individuals of the same species.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Tissue which is identically matched for human leucocyte-associated antigen (HLA)\",\"e\":\"In practice most allografts are matched as closely as possible for human leucocyte- associated antigen (HLA), but are rarely identical.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tissue from an animal transplanted to a human\",\"e\":\"A xenograft is an organ transplant from another species eg from pig to a human.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tissue transplanted from one site to another in a single individual\",\"e\":\"An autologous transplant is when the organ eg skin is moved from one part of the body to another.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Artificial tissue implanted into an individual\",\"e\":\"Xenotransplantation refers to transplant from foreign tissues into a host body.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":144,\"stem\":\"A patient is given many units of packed red cells due to massive haemorrhage, the patient is known to be HIV positive. Subsequently they develop fever and a rash, this is subsequently diagnosed as graft-versus-host disease.\\n\\nThis occurs due to which one of the following immune mechanisms?\",\"lead\":\"This occurs due to which one of the following immune mechanisms?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"When immunocompetent cells from the recipient react with the donor tissue\",\"e\":\"Immunocompetent cells from the recipient react with the donor tissue in all graft rejection reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"When immune cells form within a newly transplanted organ and subsequently react with donor tissue\",\"e\":\"Development of immune cells reacting with donor tissue occurs in acute and chronic graft rejection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"If the transplant recipient has already been sensitised to the donor tissue\",\"e\":\"If transplant recipient has already been sensitised to a donor then this can result in a hyperacute graft rejection, not graft-versus-host disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"When immunocompetent stem cells are transplanted to an individual\",\"e\":\"Graft-versus-host disease occurs when immunocompetent stem cells in the transplanted organ, eg bone marrow, attack the recipient. In practice this is prevented by irradiation of the recipient bone marrow and by T-cell depletion of the transplanted bone marrow.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"When natural killer cells are present in the donor tissue transplant\",\"e\":\"Natural killer cells are not the key cells involved in graft-versus-host\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":146,\"stem\":\"Skin grafts between individuals after burns rarely reject even when they are not fully human leucocyte-associated antigen (HLA) matched. This means there is rarely any shortage for skin graft tissue. Which one of the following options best explains why this happens?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options best explains why this happens?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The skin has a poor blood supply,\",\"e\":\"The skin has an excellent blood supply and this does not explain why rejection is rare with skin grafts.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Local immunosuppressive factors are produced by the transplant\",\"e\":\"Local immunosuppressive factors are not the reason why rejection is rare with skin grafts.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"HLA expression is low in the skin\",\"e\":\"HLA expression is high in the skin\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Immune cells are unable to penetrate the superficial layers of skin\",\"e\":\"Immune cells are able to penetrate superficial layers of the skin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tissue that is 'burned' is relatively immunosuppressed\",\"e\":\"Immune suppression early (3 days) after burn injury is associated with glucocorticoid- mediated T cell apoptosis and anti-inflammatory cytokine responses These hormones are cytokines are driven by a compensatory anti-inflammatory response syndrome (CARS) following the systemic inflammatory response syndrome: Certain tissues appear to do well after transplantation even after modest HLA matching. Liver transplants are thought to express less major histocompatibility complex (MC) class I and so HLA matching appears not to correlate with outcome. The cornea is relatively avascular so protecting it from rejection.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":148,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old woman with lupus is found to have double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) antibodies and grade 3 glomerulonephritis. She is referred to the nephrologists for management of her renal impairment.\\n\\nWhich one of the following options most accurately describes the immunopathological process?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options most accurately describes the immunopathological process?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Activation of the alternative complement pathway\",\"e\":\"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) typically results in activation of the classical complement pathway rather than the alternative pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Type II hypersensitivity reaction\",\"e\":\"Type Il hypersensitivity reactions are cytotoxic-mediated reactions such as occurs in antiglomerular basement membrane disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Type I hypersensitivity reaction\",\"e\":\"Type I hypersensitivity reactions are allergic reactions such as anaphylaxis\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Activation of the classical complement pathway\",\"e\":\"Activation of the classical complement pathway occurs in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) due to the large number of dsDNA immune complexes that form and are able to fix complement. It could also be described as a type III hypersensitivity reaction. Complement complexes are deposited in the kidneys and other organs, where they attract other components of the immune system and tissue damage ensues. Complement consumption is common in active disease in these cases.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Complement deficiency\",\"e\":\"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) results in complement activation and is not due to complement deficiency.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":150,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man presents with fever, rash and arthralgia. He has ++ protein and + blood on urinalysis. Investigations show: erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) of 32 mm/h (normal <15 mm/h), C-reactive protein (CRP) 12 mg/| (normal 0-10 mg/I), Full blood count (FBC) and urea and electrolytes (U+E) are normal but antistreptolysin O (ASO) titres are raised.\\n\\nWhat immunological phenomenon is taking place?\",\"lead\":\"What immunological phenomenon is taking place?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Type I hypersensitivity reaction\",\"e\":\"Type I hypersensitivity is an immediate hypersensitivity reaction due to immunoglobulin E (IgE)-sensitised mast cells and basophils releasing mediators of inflammation which produce the symptom of allergy. Examples include hay fever, asthma, urticaria, drug allergy and bee and wasp venom anaphylaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Type II hypersensitivity reaction\",\"e\":\"A type Il hypersensitivity reaction occurs due to antibody mediated towards antigen attached to cells, such as in ABO incompatible blood transfusions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Type III hypersensitivity reaction\",\"e\":\"This patient has a post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, an immune complex disease. Other infectious causes of immune complex disease include hepatitis. B and C virus infection, cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection and malaria.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Type IV hypersensitivity reaction\",\"e\":\"Type IV, or delayed type, hypersensitivity reaction is T-cell mediated, and onset is typically between 24 and 72 h after exposure. An example would be contact dermatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cryoglobulinaemia\",\"e\":\"Occurs when cryoglobulins (predominantly composed of immunoglobulins) become insoluble at reduced body temperatures and are typically assoc ated with conditions such as myeloma and hepatitis C virus infection.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":153,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old woman attends the Emergency Department with an acute presentation of hereditary angioedema. It is not easily reversed and she requires rapid sequence induction and an endotracheal tube to secure her airway.\\n\\nWhat is the practical immediate management?\",\"lead\":\"What is the practical immediate management?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antihistamines\",\"e\":\"They do not treat itching as this is not a type hypersensitivity reaction, and therefore does not respond to antihistamines.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline\",\"e\":\"Intramuscular adrenaline would the treatment of choice in anaphylactic shock but hereditary angio-oedema does not respond to this treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fresh-frozen plasma\",\"e\":\"This condition needs to be treated with the inhibitor, which is present in fresh-frozen plasma or in recombinant preparations. The recombinant preparation is expensive and often is not available immediately. therefore the most practical answer is fresh-frozen plasma. This condition is due to the deficiency of C1 esterase inhibitor. This causes uncontrolled activation of the classical pathway resulting in angioedema. Patients often present with oro-facial swelling which can cause respiratory obstruction, and abdominal pain. C2 and C4 often fall during an acute attack while C3 levels remain unchanged.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Recombinant Cl esterase inhibitor\",\"e\":\"This needs to be treated with the inhibitor, which is present in fresh-frozen plasma or in recombinant preparations. The recombinant preparation is expensive and often is not available immediately therefore the most practical answer is fresh-frozen plasma rather than recombinant C1 esterase inhibitor preparations.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Aspirin\",\"e\":\"Aspirin has not been shown to have any use in the acute management of hereditary angio- oedema.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":156,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted with atypical abdominal pain, and they are noted to be HIV positive with poorly controlled disease. Subsequently their condition is found to be due to uncontrolled CMV infection.\\n\\nWhich chemokine receptor is the coreceptor for HIV viral entry into T cells?\",\"lead\":\"Which chemokine receptor is the coreceptor for HIV viral entry into T cells?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Methyl-accepting chemotaxis protein (MCP)-1\",\"e\":\"This is a transmembrane sensor for the protein of bacteria. It is not involved in HIV sensing.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"CD40\",\"e\":\"CD40 is a costimulatory protein found on antigen presenting cells. This is not a co-receptor for HIV entry into T cells\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"CCR-5\",\"e\":\"C-C chemokine receptor 5 is a protein on the surface of white blood cells that acts as a co- receptor for HIV viral entry into T cells. CCR-5 has been identified as the coreceptor with CD4 for HIV entry into T cells. It was discovered after a group of prostitutes in Thailand failed to contract the disease despite high exposure to the virus. These individuals subsequently were shown to be deficient in the chemokine receptor. In vitro HIV infection could also be inhibited by excess chemokine ligand, which effectively competed for binding at the receptor. Novel strategies for CCR-5 receptor blockage in HIV are being examined.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Interleukin (IL)-8\",\"e\":\"Interleukin-8 is a chemokine produced by macrophages that causes neutrophils and other granulocytes to migrate towards sites of infection.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"CD4\",\"e\":\"CD4 is a glycoprotein found on the surface of T helper lymphocytes. The CD4 count is an accurate marker of HIV disease control and progression and is routinely used in clinical practice.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":159,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted with atypical abdominal pain, they are noted to be HIV positive with I intentroled disease. Subsequently their condition is found to be due to uncontrolled CMV infection.\\n\\nIn HIV, to what level does the CD4 count normally drop, before an individual is at risk of opportunistic infection?\",\"lead\":\"In HIV, to what level does the CD4 count normally drop, before an individual is at risk of opportunistic infection?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"Explanation J\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"800\",\"e\":\"The CD 4 count is a marker of T helper cell numbers and is normally above 800.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"200\",\"e\":\"A CD4 count less than 200 is typically thought to be the threshold below which individuals are a significantly greater risk of developing an opportunistic infection and an AIDS-defining illness. The CD4 count is a marker of T helper cell numbers and is normally above 800. Although there is often a transient mild drop on seroconversion, the count may be maintained for up to 10 years (and longer if on treatment) before i: falls. If untreated, a linear fall in the CD4 count is associated with the development of a number of AIDS-defining diseases. The first is usually Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia (PCP) when the count is about less than 200-300. This is a reliable marker of an at-risk individual and is used as a clinical indication for PCP prophylaxis. Other AIDS-defining diagnoses are less predictable by CD4 count such as lymphoma, Kaposi's sarcoma and TB.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"100\",\"e\":\"CD4 counts of around 100 put an individual at risk of toxoplasmos s and Cryptosporidium infections.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"50\",\"e\":\"CD4 counts of less than 50 indicate susceptibility to the atypical tuberculoses (TBs) such as avian intracellular TB.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"10\",\"e\":\"CD4 counts less than 10 would greatly increase risk of most opportunistic infections.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":162,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old woman patient has been referred to hospital with jaundice. On examination there is hepatosplenomegaly. She does not take any medication nor drinks alcohol. Her sister, who is 10 years older, had similar problems. Following serum testing, antibodies to which antigen are most likely to be detected in her serum?\",\"lead\":\"Following serum testing, antibodies to which antigen are most likely to be detected in her serum?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Double-stranded DNA\",\"e\":\"Double-stranded DNA antibodies are usually used to test for patients with suspected systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Proteinase 3\",\"e\":\"Proteinase -3 ANCA antibodies are elevated in conditions such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly called Wegener's granulomatosis).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mitochondria\",\"e\":\"Anti-mitochoncrial antibodies (AMA) are associated with autoimmune hepatitis, which should be suspected in this case given the demographics of our patient. AMA are detectable in serum before abnormalities in liver function and long before the onset of symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"T cells\",\"e\":\"This would not be associated with autoimmune liver disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Immunoglobulins\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin levels may be raised in autoimmune hepatitis but would be a non-specific feature of disease.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":164,\"stem\":\"Which of the following is true concerning complement activation?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is true concerning complement activation?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"IgG and lgE are the main antibody classes involved in classical pathway activation\",\"e\":\"IgG and IgM are the main antibody classes that activate the classical pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cla binds to the Fab regions of antigen-complexed IgG antibodies\",\"e\":\"Clq binds to the Fc rather than the Fab fragment that binds antibody. Fc is the crystallisable fragment and Fab is the antigen-binding fragment.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The alternative, but not the classical C3 convertase enzyme involves C3b\",\"e\":\"While the alternative pathway C3 convertase contains the components C3b and Bb, the classical pathway C3 convertase contains different proteins of the complement system - C4b and C2a\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Elevated serum 3dg is a good marker of complement activation\",\"e\":\"Clotting of blood can lead to complement activation, and hence complement conversion products must be measured from plasma and not serum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The membrane-attack complex involves polymerisation of C7\",\"e\":\"It is C9 that polymerises in the membrane-attack complex.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":166,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following statements is true concerning kinins?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true concerning kinins?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"prin They are lipids\",\"e\":\"Kinins are polypeptides, proteins and enzymes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They promote vasoconstriction\",\"e\":\"Kinins promote vasodilation and play a key role in helping to regulate systemic blood pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They decrease the permeability of blood vessels\",\"e\":\"Kinins increase the permeability of blood vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They attract phagocytes\",\"e\":\"Kinins are a group of polypeptides, proteins and enzymes that - amongst other functions - attract phagocytes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They perforate invading bacteria\",\"e\":\"Complement (not kinins) perforates invading bacteria, dilates blood vessels, stimulates histamine release and attracts neutrophils.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":168,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old, atopic, health-care worker presents with red weals and itchy hands within 20 minutes of wearing latex gloves. It is advised that she go to A&E for further management as her symptoms may progress. Which one of the following mechanisms is most likely to be relevant?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following mechanisms is most likely to be relevant?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Contact dermatitis\",\"e\":\"Type IV hypersensitivity can result in chronic granulomata and these reactions are based on T cell-antigen interaction, which results in recruitment of other cells to the site. The time course of the reactions is delayed compared with the types I and I!!, reaching a peak 24-48 h after injection or contact. The classic type IV responses are due to tuberculin and contact allergy (eg nickel, chromate, cosmetics and some drugs).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Complement-mediated\",\"e\":\"Type II (complement-mediated) hypersensitivity is caused by IgG and IgM antibodies. Type lI mechanisms are important in autoimmunity and organ transplantation. Examples include haemolytic anaemia, haemolytic disease of the newborn, drug reactions, and anti-glomerular basement membrane disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Immune complex-mediated\",\"e\":\"Type III (immune complex-mediated) hypersensitivity is caused by IgG and IgM antibodies. The antigen is soluble and antigen/antibody complexes, which activate complement cascades and causesinflammation wherever deposited, resulting in diseases such as nephritis, Farmer's lung disease, serum sickness and autoimmunity-\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Delayed-type hypersensitivity\",\"e\":\"Type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity can result in chronic granulomata and these reactions are based on T cell-antigen interactions, which result in recruitment of other cells to the site. The time course of the reactions is delayed compared with types I and Ill, reaching a peak 24-48 h after injection or contact. The classic type IV responses are due to tuberculin and contact allergy (eg nickel, chromate, cosmetics and some drugs).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"IgE-mediated sensitivity\",\"e\":\"Type I hypersensitivity+ is an immediate hypersensitivity reaction due to immunoglobulin E (IgE)-sensitised mast cells and basophils releasing mediators of inflammation that produce the symptoms of allergy. Complement-mediated (type-2 hypersensitivity) and immune complex (type-3 hypersensitivity) reactions typically produce vascular damage, and therefore bullae and petechiae would predominate in the skin.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":171,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following immunoglobulin isotypes has the highest concentration in serum?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following immunoglobulin isotypes has the highest concentration in serum?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"Explanation it\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"IgA\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin A is an antibody that plays a key role in mucous membrane immune function and can fix complement via the alternative complement pathway. IgA is the most predominant immunoglobulin in mucous secretions. Immunoglobulin A represents about 15% of total antibody levels.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"IgD\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin Dis produced by immature B lymphocytes and is usually co-expressed by IgM. The function of IgD is still relatively unknown. IgD represents about 0.25% of total antibody levels.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"IgE\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin E is synthesised by plasma cells and consists of two heavy chains and two light chains. It plays a significant role in type l hypersensitivity reactions. IgE represents about 0.05% of total antibody levels.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"IgG\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin G is a type of antibody that is the most abundant antibody in humans. It is released by plasma B cells via a variety of mechanisms in response to pathogens such as bacteria, viruses, fungi and so forth. IgG represents about 75% of total antibody levels.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"IgM\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulin M is an antibody produced by B cells and is the largest sized antibody. It is typically the first antibody to appears following initial exposure to an antigen. IgM represents about 9-10% of total antibody levels.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":174,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man with AIDS develops Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia.\\n\\nHe is deficient of what immunological component?\",\"lead\":\"He is deficient of what immunological component?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Complement\",\"e\":\"Complement deficiency typically presents with bacterial infections with organisms such as Neisseria.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"T cells\",\"e\":\"Patients with HIV have a deficiency in CD4 lymphacytes. The risk of developing P. jiroveci pneumonia is greatest with a CD4 count of 200 × 10%/l or below.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"B cells\",\"e\":\"B-cell immunodeficiency typically presents with bacterial infections such as sinopulmonary infections.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"igM\",\"e\":\"IgM deficiency presents with recurrent sinopulmonary infections with encapsulated bacteria, particularly Pseudomonas species. There are associations with atopic disease, chronic dermatitis and impetigo.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"IgA\",\"e\":\"IgA deficiency presents with recurrent sinus infections due to encapsulated bacteria such as Haemophilus.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":176,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old man is investigated due to symptoms in keeping with vasculitis. The rheumatology team send for an autoantibody screen, which shows a high titre of anti- neutrophilic cytoplasmic autoantibodies in this patient.\\n\\nThis would be most suggestive of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This would be most suggestive of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Wegner's granulomatosis\",\"e\":\"Anti-neutrophillic cytoplasmic autoantibodies are most commonly seen in patients with granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly called Wegener's granulomatosis), eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangitis (formerly called Churg-Strauss syndrome), and microscopic polyangiitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lupus erythematosus\",\"e\":\"Patients with systemic lupus erythematosus usually demonstrate anti-nuclear antibodies as well as antibodies to dsDNA in their serum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Rheumatoid arthritis\",\"e\":\"The most sensitive antibody for detection of rheumatoid arthritis would be anti-CCP antibodies, which are more specific and present earlier in disease than rheumatoid factor antibodies.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Systemic sclerosis\",\"e\":\"Typical antibodies associated with systemic sclerosis include; anti-centromere (limited systemic sclerosis) and anti-Scl-70 (diffuse systemic sclerosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Drug-induced vasculitis\",\"e\":\"Drug-induced vasculitis typically is an ANCA-negative vasculitis.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":178,\"stem\":\"A 39-year-old man develops facial swelling, difficulty breathing and a skin rash following peanut ingestion. He is recognised as having developed an anaphylactic reaction and is appropriately managed. Which immunoglobulin mediates this immediate hypersensitivity reaction?\",\"lead\":\"Which immunoglobulin mediates this immediate hypersensitivity reaction?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"Explanation tt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"IgA\",\"e\":\"IgA is primarily secreted into the gastrointestinal and respiratory tracts where it prevents microbes from binding to epithelial cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"IgD\",\"e\":\"The function of IgD is still unclear.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"IgE\",\"e\":\"Immunoglobulir E is the key immunoglobulin involved in mediating hypersensitivity reactions such as anaphylaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"IgG\",\"e\":\"IgG and IgM are involved in complement activation and opsonisation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"IgM\",\"e\":\"IgG and IgM are involved in complement activation and opsonisation.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":180,\"stem\":\"A healthcare worker is wearing latex gloves. The following day they notice a rash on their hands in the distribution of the gloves.\\n\\nIn case of contact dermatitis from latex-based gloves, which one of the following is the related hypersensitivity reaction?\",\"lead\":\"In case of contact dermatitis from latex-based gloves, which one of the following is the related hypersensitivity reaction?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gell and Coombs Type I\",\"e\":\"Type I hypersensitivity is an immediate hypersensitivity reaction due to immunoglobulin E (IgE)-sensitised mast cells and basophils releasing mediators of inflammation which produce the symptom of allergy. Examples include hay fever, asthma, urticaria, drug allergy and bee and wasp venom anaphylaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gell and Coombs Type lI\",\"e\":\"Type II hypersensitivity is caused by IgG and IgM antibodies. Type II mechanisms are important in autoimmunity and organ transplantation. Examples include haemolytic anaemia, haemolytic disease of the newborn, drug reactions, and antiglomerular basement membrane disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gell and Coombs Type III\",\"e\":\"Type III hypersensitivity is caused by IgG and IgM antibodies: Antigen is soluble and antigen/antibody complexes, which activate complement cascades and cause inflammation wherever deposited, result in diseases such as nephritis, Farmer's lung disease, serum sickness and autoimmunity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gell and Coombs Type IV\",\"e\":\"Type IV, or delayed type hypersensitivity reaction is T cell mediated, and onset is typically between 24 and 72 h after exposure. An example would be contact dermatitis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Un-classified by Gell and Coombs\",\"e\":\"Newer types of hypersensitivity reactions have been described since Gell and Coombs' original classification, such as those caused by excitatory antibodies. However, in this vignette, contact dermatitis would come under a type IV hypersensitivity. Gell and Coombs classified hypersensitivity reactions in to four categories: Type I immediate hypersensitivity due to overproduction of lgE antibody on mast cells or basophils;\\n\\n• Type II causes antibody to cell-bound antigen; • Type III causes immune complex reactions; • Type IV is delayed hypersensitivity mediated by T cells.\\n\\nLatex, as the sensitising antigen or hapten, becomes bound to skin proteins. This complex is presented to T lymphocytes in association with MHC class il antigen by Langerhans' cells. Induction of T cells usually occurs after months of exposure to small amounts of antigen with cytokine release in the epidermis. Inflammation and induration is affected at the contact site.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":183,\"stem\":\"A 49-year-old man with severe diabetic nephropathy receives a renal transplant. He develops a hyperacute rejection to the transplanted organ\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements concerning hyperacute rejection after renal transplantation is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements concerning hyperacute rejection after renal transplantation is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is successfully treated with ciclosporin A\",\"e\":\"Cyciosporin A has no role in the treatment of hyperacute reactions: the only treatment is removal of the organ.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is caused by cyclosporin A\",\"e\":\"Preformed circulating antibodies react with MHC class 1 antigens on the transplanted kidney. causing hyperacute rejection. Cyclosporin A has no role in development of the rejection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is mediated by preformed circulating antibodies\",\"e\":\"Hyperacute rejection is mediated by pre-existing antibodies in recipient.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is largely a B-cell-mediated response\",\"e\":\"Hyperacute rejection is mediated by pre-existing antibodies in recipient\\n\\nHyperacute rejection of a donor kidney may only take a few minutes once the organ has been vascularised. A reaction ensues involving complement molecules, an influx of polymorphs and the aggregation of platelets. Blood vessels supplying the organ become obstructed causing ischaemia. However, circulating antibodies mean the next organ must be MHC I matched. Matching for MHC II is well known to prolong graft survival. The use of cyclosporin has revolutionised donor organ survival. It modifies T-cell responses, and is itself nephrotoxic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Occurs at least 3 days after surgery\",\"e\":\"Hyperacute rejection typically occurs within minutes to hours of the donor organ being re- vascularised. Surgeons may be able to observe macroscopic changes in the new organ intraoperatively.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":186,\"stem\":\"A patient collapses during induction with a general anaesthetic. The anaesthetic team think this is an anaphylactic reaction and initially manage the patient with IM adrenaline.\\n\\nWhich one of the following investigations will be most useful in subsequently establishing an lgE-mediated process (anaphylactic mechanism)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following investigations will be most useful in subsequently establishing an lgE-mediated process (anaphylactic mechanism)?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Elevated serum tryptase at approximately 1 h after collapse\",\"e\":\"Although tryptase measurements indicate mast-cell degranulation, they do not point to the triggering mechanism.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AVI Total serum lgE level\",\"e\":\"Total serum IgE is a test with little clinical value except in the interpretation of specific IgE measurements.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Skin-prick tests to anaesthetic agents\",\"e\":\"Skin-prick tests performed at neat and 1:10 dilutions. This is the recognised investigation to explore cause of an IgE mediated hypersensitivity reaction to anaesthetic agents.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Serum/plasma C3 and C4 levels\",\"e\":\"Plasma complement levels are rarely helpful in the setting of anaphylaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Specific |gE to latex\",\"e\":\"Specific IgE to latex for investigating latex allergy may be helpful but is unlikely to be the cause of a reaction at induction of anaesthesia.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":188,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man receives a renal transplant from an unrelated donor for dialysis- dependent renal disease. The transplanted organ is placed in the right iliac fossa.\\n\\nWhat type of transplant is this?\",\"lead\":\"What type of transplant is this?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"C\\\", Autograft\",\"e\":\"An autograft is transplant of tissue from one location to another in the same individual.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Isograft\",\"e\":\"An isograft is a transplant of tissue between genetically identical individuals eg identical twins.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Heterotopic graft\",\"e\":\"A heterotopic graft involves transplantation of a donor organ to a different anatomical site than that of the existing organs in a recipient.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Orthotopic graft\",\"e\":\"An orthotopic graft involves transplantation of a donor organ to the same anatomical site in a recipient.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Xenograft\",\"e\":\"A xenograft involves transplantation of organs between different species.\"}]},{\"t\":37,\"p\":190,\"stem\":\"Macrophages have a role in both innate and adaptive immunity. Which one of the following options is a function of a macrophage?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is a function of a macrophage?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Production of antibodies\",\"e\":\"Antibodies are produced by plasma cells following maturation from B lymphocytes.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Release of NETs\",\"e\":\"Neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) are produced by neutrophils.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Granulomatous inflammation.\",\"e\":\"Macrophages are well known phagocytic cells. Macrophages are known to be involved in granulomatous inflammation and in the chronic inflammatory reaction. Activated macrophages secrete numerous factors including neutral proteases, lysozyme, cytokines, chemotactic factors, arachidonic acid metabolites and complement components.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Formation of membrane attack complex\",\"e\":\"The membrane attack complex is a structure formed following activation of the complement cascade, as is involved in clearance of pathogenic bacterial cells.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Synthesis and secretion of collagen\",\"e\":\"Fibroblasts, not macrophages, synthesise and secrete collagen but macrophages are involved in wound healing (removal of dead tissue, releasing chemical mediators to attract other cells).\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 43-year-old man has a basal-cell carcinoma excised and closed from his cheek under local anaesthetic. The wound has healed poorly and he is referred onto the plastic surgeons for consideration of a graft. Which one of the following occurs with normal wound healing?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following occurs with normal wound healing?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Albumin is not a good indicator of how well a patient will heal\",\"e\":\"Low albumin in a stable patient can indicate poor nutritional status, which can delay wound healing*\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperbaric oxygen significantly accelerates wound healing\",\"e\":\"Hyperbaric oxygen is being used in some centres for cases of gas gangrene (as oxygen is toxic to anaerobes) but has not been proved to significantly accelerate wound healing. although it may help if there is an ischaemic element.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypertrophy of scar is part of the normal wound healing process\",\"e\":\"Hypertrophic scarring is a complication of wound healing.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Myofibroblasts are responsible for wound contraction\",\"e\":\"Myofibroblasts are the cells responsible for wound contracture.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sutured surgical wounds heal by secondary intention\",\"e\":\"A sutured, clean surgical wound heals by primary intention. Open wounds heal by secondary intention.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old woman attends the plastic surgery day unit with a dressing over a shallow sacral wound. The consultant decides that this wound should be allowed to heal by secondary intention. How does this type of healing occur?\",\"lead\":\"How does this type of healing occur?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"At the same rate as healing by primary intention\",\"e\":\"The process of wound healing by secondary intention is much slower than primary intention.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"By epithelialisation from the surrounding wound edges\",\"e\":\"In healing of secondary intention, the base of the wound has to fill with granulation tissue, and epithelialisation occurs from the wound edges.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"By infiltration of lymphocytes to the wound\",\"e\":\"Lymphocytes are not major contributors to wound healing.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"only in the presence of infection\",\"e\":\"Significant tissue loss is another indication for closure by secondary intention.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"When the wound edges are tightly apposed\",\"e\":\"Wound healing by secondary intention occurs when the wound edges are not brought together.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"The plastic surgery consultant is in theatre, revising a wound which has not healed properly. There have been chronic infections and the patient is exasperated with the impact on their life. Concerning wound healing, which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Concerning wound healing, which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Most skin wounds regain their pre-injury strength with time\",\"e\":\"Wounds of the skin, subcutaneous tissues, muscle, fascia and tendon only ever regain 80- 90% of their pre-injury strength.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Scars will not mature until around 6 months from the injury\",\"e\":\"Scar maturation starts well before 6 months, and can last from months to years.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The maturation phase is followed by a proliferative phase\",\"e\":\"The proliferative phase precedes the maturation phase\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Type III collagen is stronger than type | collagen\",\"e\":\"Type Ill collagen is produced during the early phase of wound healing, and is weaker than the later-appearing type I collagen (maturation stage).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wound strength returns to a maximal of 90% of pre-injury strength, typically at about 2-3 months from injury in uncomplicated cases\",\"e\":\"Wounds only ever regain 80-90% of their pre-injury strength\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department requiring wound closure. The patient has a clean, laceration on their leg that they sustained about 1 hour ago. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the healing of this wound?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the healing of this wound?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The wound healing process will be in the proliferative phase.\",\"e\":\"At 1 hour post-injury, the wound healing process will be in the inflammatory phase.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Myofibroblasts will be the predominant cell at work at this stage\",\"e\":\"Myofibroblasts increase at days 2-4 post-injury\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Neutrophils will be involved in at this stage of wound healing\",\"e\":\"At 1 hour post-injury, the wound healing process will be in the inflammatory phase and neutrophils will be a predominant cell type.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Macrophages will be the predominant cell at work at this stage.\",\"e\":\"Macrophages increase on days 1-2 and their role in the acute inflammatory phase, includes phagocytosis and growth factor secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Fibroblasts will be the most predominant cell at work at this stage.\",\"e\":\"Fibroblasts tend to migrate to the wound on days 2-4, and allow for synthesis of extracellular matrix components.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"Apoptosis is a process by which cells die. Errors in the apoptotic pathway result in uncontrolled cell proliferation and cancer.\\n\\nWhich one of the following regarding apoptosis is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regarding apoptosis is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is always pathological\",\"e\":\"Apoptosis may be either physiological (programmed cell death) or pathological. Necrosis is always pathological.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"it involves loss of cellintegrity\",\"e\":\"In apoptosis cell integrity is preserved.\\n\\nApoptosis is a highly-regulated process ('programmed cell death')\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is not regulated\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is energy dependent\",\"e\":\"Apoptosis is energy dependent, necrosis is not.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Apoptosis is typically associated with inflammation.\",\"e\":\"Necrosis is more typically associated with inflammation\\n\\nThe table below compares apoptosis to necrosis\\n\\nNecrosis Apoptosis\\n\\nPhysiological or pathological Always pathological\\n\\nEnergy dependent Energy independent\\n\\nRegulated process Non- regulated process\\n\\nCell integrity intact Cell integrity breakdown\\n\\nDNA cleavage by specific activated enzymes No DNA cleavage\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"The ovum is the largest cell in the human body. It is typically visible to the naked eye without the aid of a microscope or other magnification device. Which one of the following is true regarding the ova?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding the ova?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"They are developed from the primitive germ cells\",\"e\":\"The ova are developed from the primitive germ cells, which are embedded in the substance of the ovaries.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"They are released by the rupture of the corona radiata at the time of ovulation\",\"e\":\"By the enlargement and subsequent rupture of a follicle at the surface of the ovary, an ovum is liberated and conveyed by the uterine tube to the cavity of the uterus.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They give rise to oogonia\",\"e\":\"Each primitive germ cell gives rise, by repeated divisions, to a number of smaller cells called oogonia, from which the ova or primary oocytes are developed.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They measure about 2mm in diameter\",\"e\":\"Human ova are extremely small, measuring about 0.1-0.2 mm in diameter and are enclosed within the egg follicles of the ovaries.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They undergo routine further development after liberation\",\"e\":\"Unless it is fertilised it undergoes no further development and is discharged from the uterus, but if fertilisation take place it is retained within the uterus and develops into an embryo.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"The cell cycle is a series of events that result in the division of a cell and duplication of its genetic material. Errors in this cycle can ultimately result in the pathogenesis of cancer. Which one of the following statements regarding this cycle is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding this cycle is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cells in the G1 phase are in the resting phase\",\"e\":\"Cells in the GO phase are in the resting phase.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"DNA synthesis occurs in the GO phase\",\"e\":\"DNA synthesis occurs in the S phase.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"RNA synthesis occurs in the S phase\",\"e\":\"RNA synthesis occurs in both the G1 and G2 phases.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"RNA synthesis occurs in the G2 phase\",\"e\":\"RNA synthesis occurs in both the G1 and G2 phases:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"p53 prevents entry of the cell into the G2 phase\",\"e\":\"The tumour-suppressor gene p53 is known as 'the guardian of the genome' and prevents a cell from entering the S phase of the cell cycle.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"A small child requires assessment for a deep wound on their leg after falling onto glass. The wound is clean.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding the management of this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the management of this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Damaged nerves are repaired 6 weeks later\",\"e\":\"In a clean wound, damaged nerves and tendons are repaired urgently (within 72 h ideally).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Healing should be by secondary intention\",\"e\":\"Healing of lacerated wounds is by primary intention, ie direct closure. This is achieved by appropriate wound irrigation, debridement and primary suture. Devitalised wounds, ulcers and abscess cavities may heal by second intention, ie granulation from the base upwards but leave worse scars.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"skin edges are excised routinely\",\"e\":\"Skin is preserved except for devitalised tissue, which is trimmed before wound closure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Steroids do not impair wound healing in children\",\"e\":\"Steroids impair wound healing + at any age.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The distal pulse is always examined\",\"e\":\"It is good practice to examine the neurovascular status of the limb distal to the injury. if the vascular supply has been compromised by the injury, this will need to be repaired if possible before closure.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"You take a blood sample from a patient in the pre-assessment clinic. You consider how the puncture wound you have created is going to heal. Which one of the following regarding this process is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regarding this process is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cell proliferation is the first phase of wound healing\",\"e\":\"Bleeding occurs after tissue injury, and the first thing to occur is haemostasis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemostasis depends only on platelet-plug formation\",\"e\":\"Haemostasis is achieved through constriction of the blood vessels (vasoconstriction). platelet-plug formation and the intrinsic and extrinsic coagulation pathways.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"During the inflammatory phase neutrophils are the first leukocytes to appear in the wound\",\"e\":\"The inflammatory phase is dominated initially by neutrophils that appear in the first few hours and reach a peak in 24-48 h. Their main function is phagocytosis and cleaning the wound of bacteria and foreign material.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The maturation and remodelling starts as early as 7 days after injury and is complete by day 21\",\"e\":\"The remodelling phase starts a few days after injury and can last up to 18 months.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The tensile strength of the wound will reach up to 100% of that of normal skin after healing\",\"e\":\"The tensile strength of any wound will never again be as strong as the premorbid state.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old man with severe Crohn's disease undergoes formation of a colostomy. In the future he is hoping that this can be reversed, however not until he is fully recovered from this operation.\\n\\nWhich one of the following cells is most likely to appear in the wound 3-5 days into the healing process?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following cells is most likely to appear in the wound 3-5 days into the healing process?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1 5% Endothelial cells\",\"e\":\"Endothelial cells appear around days 3-5 and migrate into the wound to form new capillaries.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Neutrophils\",\"e\":\"Neutrophils appear at day 1-2 and are part of the initial inflammatory response.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Macrophages\",\"e\":\"Macrophages appear around days 1-2 and are involved in the inflammatory phase.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Myofibroblasts\",\"e\":\"Myofibroblasts appear around weeks 2-4 and are important in contraction of the wound.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Platelets\",\"e\":\"Platelets appear immediately and form the platelet plug to achieve primary haemostasis.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"In contrast with adult wounds, fetal skin wounds repair rapidly and in the absence of scar formation. The exact mechanisms of scarless fetal wound healing remain largely unknown.\\n\\nOf the following statements regarding the mechanism of fetal wounds, which one of the following is known to be correct?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following statements regarding the mechanism of fetal wounds, which one of the following is known to be correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Involves a reduced inflammatory response\",\"e\":\"Fetal wounds have reduced pro-inflammatory signals so the inflammatory infiltrate during the early phase of healing is inhibited.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Involves a decreased synthesis of total collagen\",\"e\":\"Fetal fibroblasts synthesise more total collagen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Involves a decreased expression of transforming growth factor b3\",\"e\":\"The expression of transforming growth factor b3, a growth factor associated with reduced scarring, is higher in fetal wounds.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Involves a reduction in migration of fibroblasts\",\"e\":\"Fetal fibroblasts migrate at a faster rate than adult fibroblasts. Their increased migration velocity during repair is likely to affect collagen deposition and crosslinking.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Involves a decrease in hyaluronic acid synthesis\",\"e\":\"Fetal fibroblasts synthesise more hyaluronic acid than their adult counterparts 11=2\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old smoker has a basal cell carcinoma (BCC) removed from his forehead and direct closure. He is seen in the dressing clinic with pain, erythema and discharge. Which one of the following is seen in the proliferation phase of wound healing?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is seen in the proliferation phase of wound healing?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Complement activation\",\"e\":\"Complement activation is seen in the initial inflammatory phase.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Influx of inflammatory cells\",\"e\":\"There is an influx of inflammatory cells much earlier than the proliferative phase. This occurs in the inflammatory phase of wound healing.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Matrix remodelling\",\"e\":\"There is remodelling of the extracellular matrix in the maturation (or 'remodelling') phase that follows the proliferative phase\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Migration and proliferation of fibroblasts into the wound\",\"e\":\"Fibroblasts appear in the proliferative phase of wound healing and produce collagen.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Release of cytokines and growth factors\",\"e\":\"A release of cytokines and growth factors is seen in the initial inflammatory phase of wound healing.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A 37-year-old woman returns to the dressings clinic following an excision of a basal cell carcinoma (BCC) and local flap to her forehead. The wound is erythematous and painful. There is pus discharge. Which one of the following is not a risk factor for wound complications?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is not a risk factor for wound complications?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A body mass index (BMI) of 22\",\"e\":\"Obesity is a risk factor for poor wound healing*. A body mass index (BMI) of 22 is within the normal range.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diabetes\",\"e\":\"Diabetes is a recognised risk factor for poor wound healing and complications.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Immunosuppression\",\"e\":\"Immunosuppression is a recognised risk factor for poor wound healing and complications.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Obesity\",\"e\":\"Obesity is a recognised risk factor for poor wound healing and complications.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pyrexia not manifesting until >48 h post surgery\",\"e\":\"Clinically wound infections do not present in the first 48 h - most commonly after 3-5 days, so a high temperature commencing more than 2 days after surgery should raise the suspicion of a possible wound infection. Wound infections following cardiac or vascular surgery are potentially very serious as they can lead to graft failure.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"Apoptosis is the process of programmed cell death that can occur in multicellular organisms. Necrosis is another process by which cells die in multicellular organisms: Which one of the following statements regarding apoptosis is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Necrosis is another process by which cells die in multicellular organisms: Which one of the following statements regarding apoptosis is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is associated with a tragmented cell membrane\",\"e\":\"The cell membrane remains intact in the process of breaking down cells into apoptotic bodies along the cascade of programmed cell death.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is characterised by cell necrosis\",\"e\":\"Necrosis differs from apoptosis. Necrosis is not programmed ceil death, but is rather is pathological cell death due to exogenous injury.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is characterised by nuclear condensation and fragmentation\",\"e\":\"Apoptosis (programmed cell death) is characterised histologically by nuclear condensation. fragmentation and by defects in the cell membrane. Programmed cell death is a feature of all cells and is associated with a variety of enzymes including endonuclease, granzyme B and ICE (interleukin (IL)-1ß-converting enzyme).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inflammation is a characteristic feature of apoptosis\",\"e\":\"Necrosis is an inflammatory process whereas apoptosis is not.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Apoptosis is always a pathological process:\",\"e\":\"Apoptosis is programmed cell death. Physiological examples of apoptosis include embryological development and cellular turnover.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old woman presents with lymphoedema in the breast clinic. Last year she underwent axillary clearance for breast cancer. Which one of the following is the most important physiological function of the lymphatic system?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most important physiological function of the lymphatic system?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"To transport fluid and proteins away from the interstitium to the blood\",\"e\":\"In the absence of a lymphatic system, the interstitial fluid protein concentration would increase greatly, causing widespread extracellular oedema.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"To concentrate proteins in the lymph\",\"e\":\"This is a function of the lymphatic system, but not the most important.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"To remove particulate materials from the interstitium\",\"e\":\"This is a function of the lymphatic system, but not the most important.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"To transport antigenic materials to lymph nodes\",\"e\":\"This is a function of the lymphatic system, but not the most important.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"To create negative pressure in the free interstitial fluid\",\"e\":\"This is a function of the lymphatic system, but not the most important.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"Endometrial biopsy from a 24-year-old healthy woman with regular menstrual cycle shows the presence of highly coiled arteries with oedema. This is consistent with the secretory phase of the menstrual cycle. Which one of the following statements is true regarding this stage?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding this stage?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The secretory phase of the menstrual cycle begins soon before ovulation occurs\",\"e\":\"The secretory phase of the menstrual cycle begins after ovulation occurs:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Progesterone peaks at around day 14\",\"e\":\"Within the secretory phase, progesterone peaks 7 days after ovulation (day 21). Progesterone levels can be used to confirm ovulation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"There is a temperature decrease in the secretory phase\",\"e\":\"There is a basal body temperature increase (0.5°C) in the secretory phase.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The secretory phase follows menstruation\",\"e\":\"Menstruation follows the secretory phase\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Progesterone maintains the hyperplastic endometrium\",\"e\":\"In the secretory phase, the endometrium is maintained under the influence of progesterone.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old woman presents mid-cycle with right iliac fossa pain. Menstrual bleeding is a consequence of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Menstrual bleeding is a consequence of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Progesterone withdrawal\",\"e\":\"In the absence of a pregnancy and without human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG), the corpus luteum demises and inhibin and progesterone levels fall. Progesterone withdrawal leads to menstrual shedding (progesterone-withdrawal bleeding).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Proliferative phase of endometrium\",\"e\":\"The endometrium is in a process of hyperplasia (building) rather than breakdown in the proliferative phase. Menstrual bleeding is a consequence of endometrial shedding.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Prostaglandin withdrawal\",\"e\":\"Prostaglandin withdrawal is not the cause of menstruation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Secretory phase of endometrium\",\"e\":\"The menstrual phase occurs after the secretory phase. The endometrium is maintained in the secretory phase.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thickening of the endometrium\",\"e\":\"In menstruation there is a breakdown of the endometrium, not thickening. 1959\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"The placenta is essential for the growing fetus. On a biochemical level, transfer of molecules occurs between mother and child\\n\\nWhich one of the following substances is transported by facilitated diffusion across the placental barrier?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following substances is transported by facilitated diffusion across the placental barrier?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amino acids\",\"e\":\"Active transport systems exist for amino acids, conjugated steroids, nucleotides and water- soluble vitamins.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Steroid hormones\",\"e\":\"Simple diffusion exchange is determined by concentration gradient for low-molecular-weight molecules, such as blood gases, sodium, water, urea, non-polar molecules such as cholesterol and steroid hormones.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glucose\",\"e\":\"Hexose sugars (eg, glucose) are transported by facilitated diffusion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Nucleotides O\",\"e\":\"Active transport systems exist for amino acids, conjugated steroids, nucleotides and water- soluble vitamins.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Plasma proteins\",\"e\":\"Pinocytosis occurs for transfer of plasma proteins, immunoglobulins and lipoproteins.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"Capacitation of sperm takes place in the uterus in vivo. This increases the ability of the sperm to achieve fertilisation of the ovum.\\n\\nCapacitation allows which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Capacitation allows which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased energy metabolism.\",\"e\":\"Capacitation requires increased energy metabolism.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Enhanced motility\",\"e\":\"Glycoprotein molecules coating the surface of the sperm cell are solubilised by uterine fluid in the process of capacitation. Capacitation enhances motility and allows the head to penetrate an egg.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"inhibit t reactio\",\"e\":\"Capacitation enables the acrosome reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Release of FSH\",\"e\":\"Capacitation does not affect follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) release.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Release of LH\",\"e\":\"Capacitation does not affect luteinising hormone (LH) release.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old boy has generally well controlled asthma but needs to use his inhaler before playing football. At present, he only uses a salbutamol inhaler Which one of the following adrenoceptor subtypes does salbutamol act mainly on?\",\"lead\":\"At present, he only uses a salbutamol inhaler Which one of the following adrenoceptor subtypes does salbutamol act mainly on?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ai\",\"e\":\"Salbutamol does not act at this receptor. Catecholamines signal through this receptor.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"a2\",\"e\":\"Salbutamol does not act at this receptor. Catecholamines signal through this receptor.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"B1\",\"e\":\"Salbutamol does not act at this receptor. Agonists include dobutamine, isoprenaline and adrenaline, while antagonists include b-blockers.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"2\",\"e\":\"Salbutamol acts on B2 adrenergic receptors. These receptors are found on the smooth muscle lining airways of the lungs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"BS\",\"e\":\"83 receptors are typically found in adipose tissue. Salbutamol does not act at this receptor\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"A dysfunctional cell cycle can ultimately lead to the pathogenesis of cancer. Eukaryotic cells are more complex than prokaryotes. Which one of the following statements regarding the eukaryotic cell cycle is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding the eukaryotic cell cycle is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"DNA is made in the G1 phase.\",\"e\":\"DNA is synthesised in the S phase. The G1 phase (part of interphase) represents synthesis and growth of proteins required for DNA synthesis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"DNA is synthesised in the S phase\",\"e\":\"DNA is synthesised in the S phase:\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"G2 phase commences as G1 finishes\",\"e\":\"The S phase occurs after G1 and before G2.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"G2 determines variability in the length of the cell cycle in humans\",\"e\":\"The G1 phase determines the variability of the cycle length.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"M phase signifies meiosis\",\"e\":\"Mitosis, not meiosis, occurs in the M phase of the eukaryotic cell cycle\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"By which process may particles move along a concentration gradient across a selectively permeable membrane?\",\"lead\":\"By which process may particles move along a concentration gradient across a selectively permeable membrane?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Endocytosis\",\"e\":\"Endocytosis is the active process by which a cell consumes external material. An example of endocytosis is the uptake of low-density lipoprotein to the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diffusion\",\"e\":\"Fat-soluble molecules, such as glycerol, can diffuse through the membrane easily. They dissolve in the phospholipid bilayer and pass through it in the direction of the concentration gradient, from a high concentration to a low concentration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Exocytosis\",\"e\":\"Exocytosis is the active and controlled process by which a cell releases material. An example of this is the release of acetylcholine from the pre-synaptic membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Osmosis\",\"e\":\"Osmosis is the diffusion of water (not particles) across a membrane, in which water diffuses into a solution having a greater solute concentration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Phagocytosis\",\"e\":\"Phagocytosis is the process by which a cell engulfs harmful cells or material. For example neutrophils and macrophages are phagocytes. 1079\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"DNA polymerases are enzymes that synthesize DNA. If this process becomes dysfunctional. ultimately pathogenesis of significant disease can occur. What is the 3'* 5' exonuclease activity possessed by some DNA polymerases called that enables the enzyme to replace misincorporated nucleotides?\",\"lead\":\"What is the 3'* 5' exonuclease activity possessed by some DNA polymerases called that enables the enzyme to replace misincorporated nucleotides?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Proofreading\",\"e\":\"Proofreading' refers to the process by which genetic errors are corrected. DNA polymerases are able to detect a base pair mismatch and repace it with an appropriate match, allowing replication to continue.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Replication\",\"e\":\"Replication refers to the normal process of the DNA sequence being copied, but this term does not imply error.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Recombination\",\"e\":\"Recombination refers to the process by which the combination of two DNA sequences generates a unique, new sequence. This process occurs in meiosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Retrotransposition\",\"e\":\"Retrotransposition is transposition via an RNA intermediate (transposition is the movement of a genetic element from one site to another in a DNA molecule).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Splicing\",\"e\":\"Splicing is when the primary transcript of mRNA is processed and introns are removed, with the remaining exons joined together. A mature transcript of mRNA is formed\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man with ascites undergoes an ascitic tap to assist them in their symptoms. The ascites re-accumulates within a week or so. Which one of the following factors does not influence net filtration of water across a capillary wall?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following factors does not influence net filtration of water across a capillary wall?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The hydrostatic pressure gradient across the vessel wall\",\"e\":\"As the intravascular hydrostatic pressure rises, the net filtration towards the extravascular space will increase\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure gradient across the vessel wall\",\"e\":\"As the intravascular oncotic pressure rises, the net filtration towards the extravascular space will decrease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The thickness of the layer of smooth muscle cells in the vessel wall\",\"e\":\"By definition, capillaries are only one endothelial cell thick and do not contain smooth muscle\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The surface area of the vessel wall\",\"e\":\"A larger surface area will allow a larger net filtration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The hydraulic conductivity of the vessel wall\",\"e\":\"Hydraulic conductivity (Lp) is the filtration rate per unit of pressure across the membrane. As Lp increases, the net filtration will increase also.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"What is the main method by which intracellular pH is regulated?\",\"lead\":\"What is the main method by which intracellular pH is regulated?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The bicarbonate buffer system 2 I\",\"e\":\"In the interstitial (ie extracellular and extravascular) compartment, the bicarbonate system is the main mechanism of pH buffering.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The phosphate buffer system\",\"e\":\"The phosphate buffer system (HPO 2-+ Ht « H2PO4) acts in the intravascular space.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cytoplasmic proteins\",\"e\":\"Cytoplasmic proteins provide the main contribution to pH buffering of the intracellular compartment.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cerbonic anhydrase\",\"e\":\"The bicarbonate system is an intravascular buffer: CO2 + H2O « HaCO; «H+ + HCO; catalysed by the enzyme carbonic anhydrase.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The globin component of haemoglobin\",\"e\":\"The globin component of haemoglobin acts as a buffer in the intravascular space.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":54,\"stem\":\"An organelle is a discrete structure of a cell having specialised functions. There are many types of organelles, particularly in the eukaryotic cells of higher organisms. Which one of the following statements about mitochondria is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about mitochondria is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Mitochondria do not contain RNA.\",\"e\":\"Mitochondria contain RNA that is coded for by their own DNA.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mitochondria regenerate and replicate spontaneously\",\"e\":\"They have the ability to regenerate and replicate spontaneously as required.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mitochondria are not bound by a membrane\",\"e\":\"Mitochondria are enclosed by a double (inner and outer) membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mitochondria do not consume ATP\",\"e\":\"Mitochondria do consume ATP while simultaneously generating ATP via the electron transport chain\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"There is typically only one mitochondrion per cell\",\"e\":\"The number of mitochondria per cell varies, with up to 2000 found in a human hepatocyte.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old man attends his GP with symptoms of urinary hesitancy, straining to void and dribbling. He is found to have a large prostate on DRE. Hypertrophy of cells is characterised by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Hypertrophy of cells is characterised by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cells with malignant potential\",\"e\":\"Cells with malignant potential undergo genetic mutations, but this is not a feature of hypertrophy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increase in cell number\",\"e\":\"An increase in cell number is called hyperplasia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increase in the number of mitoses\",\"e\":\"Mitosis is how cells divide and increase in number (cell division), but is not a feature of hypertrophy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increase in tissue size\",\"e\":\"Hypertrophy is the increase in size of a tissue due to the increase in size of the cells present.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Once established, is irreversible\",\"e\":\"Hypertrophy is reversible if the stimulus is removed.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old man has acute kidney injury with deranged sodium, potassium, urea and creatinine. A renal cause is diagnosed for the acute kidney injury. The active transport mechanisms of the kidney will have been affected by the acute kidney injury.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding active transport?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding active transport?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It does not involve the expenditure of energy\",\"e\":\"Active transport occurs at the expense of energy (adenosine triphosphate, ATP).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It does not require specific carrier proteins\",\"e\":\"Carrier proteins are required to transport molecules across the cell membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is required to move molecules from an area of high concentration to one of low concentration\",\"e\":\"Active transport is required to move molecules from an area of low concentration to one of high concentration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Active transport can only occur by the hydrolysis of ATP\",\"e\":\"Active transport requires either hydrolysis of ATP (primary active transport), or a pre- existent osmotic gradient, such as the resting intracellular/extracellular Na* gradient (secondary active transport or coupled transport)\\n\\nAn example of this latter type is when glucose is resorbed glucose against its concentration gradient in the kidney. The energy to drive the reabsorption is from the passage of Nat along the pre-existing concentration gradient\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is vital to maintain the resting Na* gradient\",\"e\":\"Active transport is required to polarise the cell membrane and to maintain the resting Nat gradient.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":60,\"stem\":\"An individual runs for the bus and has heavy breathing. Their respiratory rate settles as they recover.\\n\\nWhich one of the following changes will decrease the rate of diffusion of a substance?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following changes will decrease the rate of diffusion of a substance?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A decrease in the molecular weight of the substance\",\"e\":\"A decrease in the molecular weight of the substance will increase the diffusion rate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An increase in the concentration gradient\",\"e\":\"The rate of diffusion of a substance is proportional to the difference in the concentration of the diffusing substance between the two sides of the membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"An increase in temperature\",\"e\":\"An increase in temperature will increase the diffusion rate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An increase in the molecular weight of the substance\",\"e\":\"An increase in the molecular weight of the substance will decrease the diffusion rate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"An increase in membrane permeability\",\"e\":\"An increase in membrane permeability will increase the diffusion rate.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"A patient takes beta-blockers to control their heart rate. Biochemical mechanisms of signal transduction follow the pharmacological subdivisions of the adrenergic receptors. Which one of the following statements regarding the stimulation of 6-receptors is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding the stimulation of 6-receptors is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Activates adenylate cyciase\",\"e\":\"Stimulation of either 61- or B2-receptors activates adenylate cyclase.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Noradrenaline predominantly stimulates B2-receptors\",\"e\":\"While adrenaline stimulates ß1- and ß2-receptors, noradrenaline predominantly stimulates al- receptors.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"B2-receptor stimulation causes systemic vasoconstriction\",\"e\":\"B2-receptor stimulation causes systemic vasodilation\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"62-receptor stimulation causes bronchoconstriction\",\"e\":\"B2-receptor stimulation causes bronchodilation. This underpins the mechanism of salbutamol.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"B-Adrenergic responses typically result from decreased production of cAMP\",\"e\":\"B-Adrenergic responses typically result from increased production of cAMP.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old patient with familial adenomatous polyposis requires counselling, Inherited diseases are the result of mutations in chromosomes that are then carried through families in a variety of inheritance patterns. Which one of the following diseases displays an autosomal-dominant inheritance?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following diseases displays an autosomal-dominant inheritance?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Beta-thalassemia\",\"e\":\"Beta-thalassemia is autosomal recessive.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cystic fibrosis\",\"e\":\"Cystic fibrosis shows recessive inheritance. The gene defect is on chromosome 7 and is carried by approximately 1 in 20 Whites.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemophilia\",\"e\":\"Haemophilia is a sex-Inked recessive disorder. Haemophilia A is caused by a deficiency clotting factor VIII. Haemophilia B is caused by a deficiency of clotting factor IX\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hereditary spherocytosis\",\"e\":\"In hereditary spherocytosis there is a defect of the structural proteins in the red cells. It is inherited as an autosomal-dominant trait.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Trisomy 21\",\"e\":\"Trisomy 21 occurs when an extra chromosome 21 is present and can occur due to a non- dysjunction or mosaicism. It is not an inherited disease.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"The cell cycle is a series of programmed events which take place to ensure the duplication and division of genetic material to produce two daughter cells. During one phase of mitosis the paired chromosomes separate and are dragged to the opposite sides of the cell by the microtubules.\\n\\nWhat is this phase known as?\",\"lead\":\"What is this phase known as?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anaphase\",\"e\":\"In anaphase the paired chromosomes separate and are dragged to the opposite sides of the cell by the microtubules.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cytokinesis\",\"e\":\"In cytokinesis, an actin fibre forms around the centre of the cell and contracts, pinching it into two daughter cells, each with 23 pairs of chromosomes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Interphase\",\"e\":\"Interphase comprises phases G1, S and G2 when the cell is in preparation for division:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Metaphase\",\"e\":\"Metaphase follows on from prophase and prometaphase. The spindle fibres align with chromosomes along the metaphase plate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Telophase\",\"e\":\"In telophase, the chromatids arrive at the opposite poles of the cell and disperse after new nuclear membranes are formed\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man has orculatory failure due to sepsis: A fluid challenge is given and their ability to perfuse their tissues is increased by the supportive therapy administered Which one of the following best describes the physiological principles that determine movement of fluid across a capillary?\",\"lead\":\"A 45-year-old man has orculatory failure due to sepsis: A fluid challenge is given and their ability to perfuse their tissues is increased by the supportive therapy administered Which one of the following best describes the physiological principles that determine movement of fluid across a capillary?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fluid movement is determined by the difference in plasma and tissue oncotic pressure only\",\"e\":\"Fluid movement is dependent on oncotic pressure (plasma and tissue), hydrostatic pressure (plasma and tissue) and the permeability of the membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fluid movement is determined by capillary permeability only\",\"e\":\"Fluid movement is dependent on oncotic pressure (plasma and tissue), hydrostatic pressure (plasma and tissue) and the permeability of the membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fluid movement is determined by the difference in hydrostatic pressure between the capillary and the tissue\",\"e\":\"Fluid movement is dependent on oncotic pressure (plasma and tissue), hydrostatic pressure (plasma and tissue) and the permeability of the membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fluid movement is determined by the net filtration pressure (capillary hydrostatic pressure - tissue hydrostatic pressure) minus the net oncotic pressure (capillary oncotic pressure - tissue oncotic pressure)\",\"e\":\"This is Starling's equation. Fluid movement will reflect the difference between net filtration and net oncotic pressures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sodium is the most important determinant of intravascular oncotic pressure\",\"e\":\"Albumin is the most important determinant of intravascular oncotic pressure.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":70,\"stem\":\"A 38-year-old woman is recovering in a Burns ICU with 37% burns sustained in a house fire. It is noted she has a negative nitrogen balance on her charts. Which one of the following circumstances is associated with a positive nitrogen balance?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following circumstances is associated with a positive nitrogen balance?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"During growth\",\"e\":\"A positive nitrogen balance is required in growth. Nitrogen balance is a measure of nitrogen input minus nitrogen output. Positive nitrogen balance is associated with periods of growth, hypothyroidism, tissue repair and pregnancy. This means that the intake of nitrogen into the body is greater than the loss of nitrogen from the body, so these is an increase in the total body pool of protein. This is the equivalent of stating that a patient is 'anabolic'.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Following a bone fracture\",\"e\":\"Following a fracture there is a negative nitrogen balance due to increased demands from the reparative process. Trauma induces a hypermetabolic state, which leads to a persistently negative nitrogen balance (as the total protein intake is insufficient to meet the body's demands).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Following exercise A 1.\",\"e\":\"In exercise the metabolic rate and nitrogen demand of the body increases. Dietary protein is in a constant state of flux in the body, being broken down into amino-acids, transformed into other compounds, and sometimes reassembled into other proteins. They are also used up as energy. a mechanism that is more prevalent when energy consumption is high (such as in sustained exercise). Muscle protein can thus become a source of energy. thus leading to a negative nitrogen balance.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Post-operative following major surgery\",\"e\":\"Major surgery results in iatrogenic injury and inflammation that increases the metabolic rate, increased urea production and so the nitrogen demands of the body.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sepsis\",\"e\":\"Following sepsis there is a negative nitrogen balance due to the increased requirement from the inflammatory processes.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":73,\"stem\":\"A trauma patient presents having crashed their car on an icy road. It took a prolonged period for the crew to extricate that patient from the damaged car and on arrival they are hypothermic. Which one of the following does not normally occur as a response to a decrease in core body temperature?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following does not normally occur as a response to a decrease in core body temperature?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bradycardia\",\"e\":\"Bradycardia occurs due to depression of cardiac pacemaker cells.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Peripheral vasoconstriction\",\"e\":\"Peripheral vasoconstriction is a normal response to hypothermia and prevents heat loss.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A decrease in brain metabolism\",\"e\":\"A fall in core body temperature is associated with a decrease in brain metabolism and activity. Therapeutic cooling is one of several methods that can be used to prevent secondary traumatic brain injury.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A reduction in plasma catecholamine levels\",\"e\":\"There is an increase in the release of catecholamines in response to hypothermia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A rise in plasma thyroxine\",\"e\":\"There is an increase in the release of thyroxine in response to hypothermia.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"You are experiencing a summer heat wave and many of your elderly patients are finding this difficult to cope with. The surgical assessment unit becomes full of medical patients suffering from dehydration. Exposure to a hot environment causes which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Exposure to a hot environment causes which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cutaneous vasoconstriction\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased respiration\",\"e\":\"By various negative-feedback mechanisms involved in homeostasis, a rise in ambient temperature is typically accompanied by increased respiratory rate.\\n\\nHyperthermia causes cutaneous (peripheral) vasodilation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased appetite\",\"e\":\"Hyperthermia causes a decrease in appetite:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased catecholamine secretion\",\"e\":\"Hypothermia causes an increase in catecholamine secretion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased skeletal muscle activity\",\"e\":\"Hyperthermia causes a reduction in skeletal muscle activity.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"A patient is being considered for bariatric surgery. They have not yet been given lifestyle advice to mitigate their need for an operation. They have questions on what they can do to imprave their health through their diet. Which one of the following statements best characterises low-density lipoproteins (LDL)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements best characterises low-density lipoproteins (LDL)?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The major fat in low-density lipoproteins is triglyceride\",\"e\":\"The major fat in low-density lipoproteins is cholesterol.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Their concentration is highly correlated with dietary cholesterol content\",\"e\":\"Their concentration is determined mainly by the rate of hepatic synthesis and peripheral clearance; dietary saturated fat intake correlates more strongly with LDL-cholesterol concentration than dietary cholesterol intake\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"They are involved in reverse cholesterol transport\",\"e\":\"It is HDL-cholesterol that is involved in reverse cholesterol transport.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"They are synthesised de novo in the liver\",\"e\":\"LDLs are formed from VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) and IDL (intermediate-density lipoprotein) by successive removal of triglyceride and modification in the circulation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"They contain apolipoprotein B-100\",\"e\":\"Each particle of LDL contains one molecule of apolipoprotein B-100, the ligand for the LDL receptor.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":79,\"stem\":\"You have just finished a ward round with an endocrine surgeon. Over coffee she starts to quiz you on metabolic rate Which one of the following is true with regards to metabolic rate?\",\"lead\":\"Over coffee she starts to quiz you on metabolic rate Which one of the following is true with regards to metabolic rate?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Falls after a meal\",\"e\":\"Metabolic rate increases after a meal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Falls in a cold environment\",\"e\":\"Metabolic rate increases in a cold environment.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Higher in adults than children\",\"e\":\"Metabolic rate is greater in children compared with adults.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased by thyroid hormones\",\"e\":\"Metabolic rate is affected by a number of factors including muscular exertion (most important), ingestion of food, sex, age, height, weight, surface area, body temperature, environmental temperature, emotional state. Thyroid hormones and catecholamines (eg adrenaline and noradrenaline) will increase the metabolic rate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Unaffected by catecholamines\",\"e\":\"Catecholamines increase the metabolic rate.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":81,\"stem\":\"A young patient presents with pyloric stenosis. They have intractable vomiting and a Ramstedt pyloromyotomy is performed. With respect to vomiting which one of the following statements is the best answer?\",\"lead\":\"With respect to vomiting which one of the following statements is the best answer?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The main receptor in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) is adrenergic\",\"e\":\"The main receptors are dopamine, histamine and serotonin\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The CTZ is outside the blood brain barrier\",\"e\":\"The CTZ is outside the blood-brain barrier, in the floor of the fourth ventricle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"5HT3 agonists may be effective in controlling cisplatin induced vomiting\",\"e\":\"5HT3 antagonists (eg ondansetron), not agonists, are effective in controlling vomiting in this context.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"H2 receptors are abundant in the vomiting centre\",\"e\":\"H1 receptors have been identified in the vomiting centre.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The vomiting centre is present in the reticular formation of the mid brain\",\"e\":\"The vomiting centre is present in the reticular formation of the medulla.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man is reviewed in the neurosurgical clinic with muscle spasms. They have diffuse fasciculations in addition to this. Which one of the following is true regarding skeletal muscle contraction?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding skeletal muscle contraction?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Occurs spontaneously in response to high serum calcium levels\",\"e\":\"Spontaneous contractions (tetany) of skeletal muscle may occur in response to low (not high) serum calcium levels.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is prolonged and maintained in fast twitch fibres\",\"e\":\"There are two types of muscle fibres: slow, which produce prolonged, maintained contraction, and fast, which produce transient fatigueable contraction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Depends only on thick myofilaments moving\",\"e\":\"Both thick and thin myofilaments are required to move along one another.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Occurs in response to depolarisation of the sarcolemma\",\"e\":\"Depolarisation of the plasma membrane triggers passive release of calcium ions from the sarcoplasmic reticulum through specific channels along a gradient.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ends when calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum\",\"e\":\"Contraction is sustained until the calcium ions are actively transported back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum and relaxation occurs.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":85,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man, who weighs 70 kg. presents with suspected appendicitis and is made 'nil by mouth' until a theatre slot can be found. In the meantime a doctor writes up maintenance fluids for him Which one of the following is true regarding normal physiology in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"In the meantime a doctor writes up maintenance fluids for him Which one of the following is true regarding normal physiology in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The body's water content is 75%\",\"e\":\"Body water content is approximately 60% in the adult man and this equates to about 45 litres. Neonates comprise 75% water.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The body's plasma volume is approximately 5 litres\",\"e\":\"The plasma volume measures about 3 litres. The total blood volume is around 5 litres.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Approximately 1.5 litres of water are lost daily from the lungs\",\"e\":\"Approximately 400ml of water is lost daily from the lungs.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The intracellular fluid volume is approximately two-thirds of the total body water\",\"e\":\"The intracellular fluid is about two-thirds of total body water, that is 30 litres out of a total body water content of 45 litres\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The daily potassium requirement is approximately 3.5-5.0 mmol/kg\",\"e\":\"Daily potassium requirements are approximately 1 mmol/kg. The normal serum potassium concentration is around 3.5-5.0 mmol/l.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":87,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man has been involved in a road traffic accident and was brought into the Emergency Department as a trauma call. Following the collision, his car became submerged in cold water and he spent approximately 30 min in water at freezing temperatures At what temperature do thermoregulatory mechanisms begin to fail?\",\"lead\":\"Following the collision, his car became submerged in cold water and he spent approximately 30 min in water at freezing temperatures At what temperature do thermoregulatory mechanisms begin to fail?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"20°C\",\"e\":\"A body temperature of 20°C is incompatible with life, regulatory mechanisms have long failed by this point and death is likely to have occurred sooner due to cardiac arrhythmias or respiratory arrest.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"28°C\",\"e\":\"Below 28°C temperature regulatory mechanisms have already failed, the patient is typically unconscious and there is a high risk of cardiac arrhythmias.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"30°C\",\"e\":\"Temperature regulatory mechanisms fail at 30°C and the condition is often life threatening after this point.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"32°C\",\"e\":\"Temperature regulatory mechanisms are still working, patients begin to develop bradycardia, hypotension and respiratory depression.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"35°C\",\"e\":\"Symptoms of hypothermia start to appear below 35°C and include shivering. Temperature regulatory mechanisms are working to maintain core body temperature.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man was the driver of a car travelling at 70 mph that hit the central reservation. In the Emergency Department, he complains of severe abdominal pain. On arrival, he is anxious. respiratory rate 35 breaths/min, heart rate 125 beats/min, blood pressure 88/46 mmHg.\\n\\nWhat stage of haemorrhagic shock is he in?\",\"lead\":\"What stage of haemorrhagic shock is he in?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"This patient is not in haemorrhagic shock\",\"e\":\"From the vital signs (change of mental state, haemodynamic instability) and mechanism of injury this patient is in shock until proven otherwise. As per the history given in the description, the most likely cause of this is from massive blood loss with one possibility being a splenic injury.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Stage I\",\"e\":\"This patient is in stage III haemorrhagic shock demonstrated by his anxious state and observations. At this stage patients ere hypotensive, tachycardic and tachypneoic with a bleod loss of 1500-2000 mi (30-40% of blood volume).\\n\\nClass of haemorrhagic shock\\n\\n| I! i\\\" IV\\n\\nBlocd loss (ml) Up to 750 750-1500 1500-2000 >2000\\n\\nBlond less <8. Hise\\\" Up to 15 15-30, 30-40 >40 volume)\\n\\nHeart rate/min <100 100-120 120-140 >140\\n\\nBlood pressure Normal Normal Decreased Decreased\\n\\nPulse pressure (mmHg) Normal/increased Decreased Decreased Decreased\\n\\nRespiratory rate ; ws a - > (breaths/min) 14-20 20-30 30-40 35\\n\\nUrine output (mi/h) >30 20-30 5-15 Negligible\\n\\nCentral nervous system Slightly anxious Mildly ANHIOUS: Confused, enxious confused lethargic\\n\\nStage | haemorrhagic shock occurs with blood loss up to 750 ml (15% of blood volume). At this stage the patient still has normal observations, albeit appearing slightly anxious.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Stage II\",\"e\":\"Stage Il haemorrhagic shock occurs with blood loss of 750-1500 ml (15-30% of blood volume). The patient begins to become tachycardic and tachypnoeic but is still able to maintain blood pressure. At this stage patients begin to become anxious.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Stage III\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stage IV\",\"e\":\"Stage IV haemorrhagic shock occurs with blood loss of more than 2000 ml (40% of blood volume). The patient is often confused and lethargic with HR >140 beats/min, RR >35 breaths/min and hypotensive.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":92,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old man presents with severe right flank pain. Observations are respiratory rate 22 breaths/min, heart rate 105 beats/min, blood pressure 105/82 mmg, temperature 38.2°C. He is diagnosed with renai colic. Blood results are below:\\n\\nResult Investigation Normal Value\\n\\nSodium (Na\\\") 136 mmol/l 135-145 mmol/l\\n\\n6.6 mmol/l 3.5-5.0 mmol/l Potassium (K)\\n\\n640 umol/l 50-120 umol/l Creatinine\\n\\nWhat electrocardiogram (ECG) change might this patient have?\",\"lead\":\"What electrocardiogram (ECG) change might this patient have?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1 SEлм-0105507\",\"e\":\"This patient has hyperkalaemia for which the classical ECG findings include peaked T-waves, loss of P-waves and widened QRS complexes predisposing the patient to cardiac arrhythmias.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"J-waves\",\"e\":\"J-waves are positive deflections that occur between the QRS complex and ST segment. They are associated with hypothermia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Delta-wave\",\"e\":\"Delta-waves are most commonly associated with Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome. They are seen as a slurred upstroke in the QR$ complex.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Narrowed QRS complex\",\"e\":\"This patient has hyperkalaemia, which would typically produce a widened QRS complex.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Flattened T-wave\",\"e\":\"In hyperkalaemia there are peaked T-waves. Flattened T-waves can be found in myocardial ischaemia and hypokalaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":94,\"stem\":\"You are summoned to the ward by the Foundation Year 1 (FY1) doctor to review a patient who is 1-week post-sigmoid colectomy. She tells you that the patient is septic and thinks they may need to be transferred to the high dependency unit. Which one of the following parameters would score as part of the quick Sequential (sepsis- related) Organ Failure Assessment (qSOFA) score for patients with sepsis to identify high- risk patients for mortality?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following parameters would score as part of the quick Sequential (sepsis- related) Organ Failure Assessment (qSOFA) score for patients with sepsis to identify high- risk patients for mortality?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Temperature 38.2°C\",\"e\":\"aSOFA is used as a predictor of mortality and not to identify sepsis and so temperature does not form part of its assessment. A temperature of greater than 38°C formed part of the systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) criteria, which are no longer recommended for the diagnosis of sepsis. Normal value 36.1-37.2°C.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Platelets 50 × 10/l\",\"e\":\"Platelets do not form part of the SOFA assessment but are used as a predictor of mortality in the longer sequential organ failure assessment (SOFA) assessment which is primarily used for patients in intensive care. Normal value 150-400 × 109/1.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Systolic blood pressure (BP) 105 mmHg\",\"e\":\"Systolic BP does form part of the SOFA assessment to identify septic patients with a high risk of mortality. However, it only scores for a systolic BP ≤100 mmHg. Normal value <120 mmHg.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"White blood-cell count 3200/mm3\",\"e\":\"Blood parameters do not form part of the SOFA assessment. In the extended SOFA assessment used for intensive care, patients' platelets, bilirubin and creatinine are all considered. White blood-cell count was previously used as part of the SIRS criteria to identify septic patients. Normal value 6000-15 000/mm• (6-15 x 10°/l).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Respiratory rate 22 breaths/min\",\"e\":\"The SOFA assessment is a quick screening tool with three parameters to identify high-risk patients with sepsis outside of intensive care. A point is given for each of the following; Glasgow Coma Score (GCS) <15, respiratory rate ≥22 breaths/min (normal 12-20 breaths/min) and systolic BP ≤100 mmHg (normal <120 mmHg).\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":97,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old woman sustained an abdominal injury during a rugby match. On arrival to Accident and Emergency she has severe left upper quadrant pain and initial observations show respiratory rate 25 breaths/min, heart rate 116 beats/min, blood pressure 82/47 mmHg. temperature 37.2°C.\\n\\nWhich one of the following compensatory mechanisms would be in action in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following compensatory mechanisms would be in action in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased antidiuretic hormone (ADH)\",\"e\":\"ADH is secreted from the posterior pituitary gland in response to a decreased circulating blood volume. ADH acts to increase water permeability and causes vasoconstriction resulting in increased total peripheral resistance and therefore increased blood pressure (BP) = cardiac output (CO) x total peripheral resistance (TPR).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased renin secretion\",\"e\":\"From the history and vital signs there would be concern that this patient has sustained a splenic injury and is in hypovolaemic/haemorrhagic shock. A decrease in afferent arteriole pressure would occur as a result of decreased blood volume resulting in the secretion of renin. The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system stimulates the reabsorption of sodium and water to increase the circulating volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)\",\"e\":\"ANP is released in response to an increase in extracellular fluid. In this hypovolaemic patient it would not be stimulated. The actions of ANP would lead to fluid loss by increased glomerular filtration and the inhibition of reabsorption of sodium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased aldosterone secretion\",\"e\":\"The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system helps to increase circulating volume. In this patient there would be increased renin secretion which stimulates the conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin 1 which is then converted to angiotensin II. Angiotensin I| stimulates the release of aldosterone, therefore. there would be increased aldosterone secretion in this patient.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased glomerular filtration rate\",\"e\":\"A decreased circulating volume would result in a decreased glomerular filtration rate\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":100,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old woman who has had multiple previous abdominal surgeries presents with profuse vomiting, abdominal distension and bowels not open for three days. An arterial blood gas (ABG) test has been performed. The patient's renal function and serum lactate are within the mornal range:\\n\\nWhich one of the following would be most likely in this presentation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would be most likely in this presentation?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"7.42; p,(O≥) 7.8 kPa; pa(CO:) 5.3 kPa; HCO; 28 mmol/l; lactate 0.9 mmol/\",\"e\":\"This represents a type 1 respiratory failure with only the pa(Oz) being abnormal. Causes include pulmonary embolism and pneumonia. In this patient profuse vomiting could result in an aspiration pneumonia but there is no suggestion of that in the history and it would not be the most likely finding on an ABG test at this stage.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"7.32; p,(Oz) 11 kPa; pa(CO2) 7.2 kPa; HCO, 26 mmol/l; lactate 1.2 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"This represents a respiratory acidosis with a low pH and high pa(CO2). Causes include chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and neuromuscular disorders such as motor neurone disease.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"7.52; p, (0 2) 11,6 kPa; pa(COz) 5.1 kPa; HCO: 33 mmol/l; lactate 2.0 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"The patient's history is consistent with bowel obstruction. The profuse vomiting is likely to resuit in an arterial blood gas (ABG) similar to this showing metabolic alkalosis (high pH, high HCO:). Vomiting causes this by a loss of gastric secretions which are rich in hydrochloric acid. The loss of hydrogen ions results in the dissociation of carbonic acid into hydrogen and bicarbonate ions (Henderson-Hasselbalch equation).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"6.9; pa (02) 10.7 kPa; pa(CO2) 3.3 kPa; HCO;- 12 mmol/l; lactate 7.2 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"This ABG demonstrates a metabolic acidosis with partial respiratory compensation (low pH. low HCOs, low pa(COz)). Causes include diabetic ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis and profuse diarrhoea (resulting in HCO; losses).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"7.49; Pa(02) 10.5; pa(CO2) 2.7; HCO: 25 mmol/l; lactate 1.4 mmol/!\",\"e\":\"Respiratory alkalosis is caused by hyperventilation and there is no evidence of this from the history.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"A 38-year-old woman with an ileostomy presents with abdominal pain and lethargy. On examination, she has a tender abdomen but is not peritonitic. Arterial blood gas shows:\\n\\nResult Normal Value Investigation 7.12 7.35-7.45 ó PASTEST pH\\n\\n10.5-13.5 kPa pa(O=) 10.5\\n\\n5.7 4.6-6.0 kPa Pa(CO=) 15 24-30 mmol/l HCO= 135-145 mmol/l 136 Sodium (Na*)\\n\\n5.5-5.0 mmol/l Potassium (K+) 4.8\\n\\n96-106 mmol/l 101 Chloride (Cl-)\\n\\nni 5- im Lactate 3.2\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Salmonella infection\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Appendicitis\",\"e\":\"Appendicitis, without evidence of perforation does not typically cause acid-base disturbance and is not the most likely diagnosis here.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"High output stoma\",\"e\":\"Metabolic acidosis can result from excess H+ production, impaired H+ excretion or excess loss of base. With excess base loss, as would happen with a high output stoma, the anion gap would be maintained not increased as in this case. The anion gap is normal because the decrease in HCOs is matched by increased Cl.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Diabetic ketoacidosis\",\"e\":\"This is the most likely diagnosis. Diabetic ketoacidosis results in a metabolic acidosis with a high anion gap as a result of acid production. Anion gap is calculated by (Na* + K*) - (HCO; + Cl-). in this case (136 + 4.8) - (15 + 101) = 24.8. A normal anion gap is between 4 and 12 mmol/l.\\n\\nHyperemesis gravidarum is characterised by profuse vomiting, which would result in a metabolic alkalosis due to acid loss rather than a metabolic acidosis.\\n\\nSalmonella infection can result in profuse diarrhoea. The gastrointestinal losses would result in a metabolic acidosis as in this case. However, once a metabolic acidosis is identified the anion gap should be calculated to identify a cause. With a decrease in HCOs there is an increase in serum Cl-resulting in a normal anion gap of between 4 and 12 mmol/l ((Na* + K*) - (HCO: + Cl-)). In this case there is a high anion gap indicating another more likely diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"E eremesis gravidarum\",\"e\":\"\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":105,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man develops ileus after a right hemicolectomy for caecal cancer. He has had limited nutrition for seven days, so the team are concerned and would like to commence feeding him\\n\\nWhat is the most appropriate management for this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate management for this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Continue to observe as he is unlikely to require feeding\",\"e\":\"With limited nutrition for a week it is important to consider an alternative method of feeding to improve patient outcome\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastrostomy feeding\",\"e\":\"Perculaneous endoscopic gastrustomy or radiologically inserted gastrostory is performed in patients that require long-term feeding and have swallowing difficulties, for example following certain types of stroke.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Nasogastric feed\",\"e\":\"The patient would not absorb nasogastric feed. A nasogastric tube would be useful for symptomatic relief of vomiting and distension in patients with post-operative ileus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Total parenteral nutrition (TPN)\",\"e\":\"In ileus the gastrointestinal tract is not functional and the patient is unlikely to absorb any enteral feed. When commencing TPN it would be important to closely monitor magnesium, phosphate and potassium levels, as patients that have had a prolonged period of starvation are at risk of refeeding syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Feeding jejunostomy\",\"e\":\"A feeding jejunostomy would require a working gastrointestinal system for absorption of feed, which is not the case here. This is typically done in combination with operations of the upper gastrointestinal system and avoids feed passing through the stomach, delivering feed directly to the jejunum.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"You are asked to prescribe maintenance fluids for a patient who is awaiting a laparoscopic cholecystectomy and is nil by mouth. You decide to prescribe the patient Hartmann's solution\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the correct composition of Hartmann's solution?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the correct composition of Hartmann's solution?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"131 mmol/1; K* mmol/l; Ca+ Ca2+ mmol/I; CI: CI:111 mmol/I; HCO; 29 mmol/l; glucose 0 mmol/l; osmolarity 280 mOsm/kg\",\"e\":\"This is the correct composition of Hartmann's sclution, HCO: is actually in the form of lactate and is metabolised to bicarbonate in the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Na+ O mmol/l; K* 0 mmol/l; Ca?+ O. mmol/l; Cl- 0 mmol/l, HCO;- 0 mmol/l; glucose 278 mmol/1 (50 g); osmolarity 278 mOsm/kg\",\"e\":\"This is the normal composition of 5% dextrose.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Na* 154 mmol/l; K* 0 mmol/l; Ca?+ 0 mmol/l; CI-154 mmal/I; HCO, 0 mmol/l; glucose 0 mmol/I; osmolarity 308 mOsm/kg\",\"e\":\"This is the composition of normal saline\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Na* 150 mmol/l; K* 0 mmol/l; Ca?+ 0 mmol/I; Cl- 0 mmol/I; HCO:- 150 mmol/l; glucose 0 mmol/l: osmolarity 300 mOsm/kg\",\"e\":\"This is the composition of 1.26% sodium bicarbonate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Na* 140 mmol/l; K* 4 mmol/l; Ca?+ 2.5 mmol/l; CI- 100 mmol/I; HCO; 28 mmol/I; glucose 5 mmol/!; osmolarity 285 mOsm/kg\",\"e\":\"This is a normal composition of plasma, not Hartmann's solution, although Hartmann's solution is close to the physiological normal. 3362\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":109,\"stem\":\"An 8-year-old boy who weighs 32 kg presents with migratory right iliac fossa pain and loss of appetite. He is thought to have appendicitis and is booked and consented for an appendicectomy. There is an emergency laparotomy in theatre and he will be the next case The staff nurse asks you to prescribe maintenance fluid. What would you prescribe?\",\"lead\":\"What would you prescribe?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"4% dextrose/0.18% saline at 60 ml/h\",\"e\":\"Hypotonic fluid is no longer recommended for maintenance fluid in children and has been associated with morbidity/mortality secondary to hyponatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"0.9% saline at 60 ml/h\",\"e\":\"This is neither the correct maintenance fluid for children or at the correct rate, which should be calculated according to the patient's weight.\\n\\nWhile saline is suitable for use as maintenance fluid in children, this rate is too fast and would result in overload. The volume for maintenance fluid for children in 24 hours is calculated using their weight. For the first 10 kg, fluid requirement is 100 ml/kg, for 11-20 kg the requirement is 50 ml/kg and from 20 kg the requirement is 20 ml/kg. This boy weighs 32 kg and therefore replacement per hour is (1000 + 500+ (12 × 20))/24 = 73 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"0.9% saline at 80 milk\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"5% dextrose/0.9% saline at 60 ml/h\",\"e\":\"5% dextrose/0.9% saline is a suitable fluid for maintenance in neonates. The fluid maintenance volume required in 24 hours for children is calculated using their weight. For the first 10 kg. fluid requirement is 100 ml/kg, for 11-20 kg the requirement is 50 ml/kg and from 20 kg the requirement is 20 ml/kg. This boy weighs 32 kg and therefore replacement per hour is (1000 + 500 + (12 × 20))/24 = 73 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hartmann's at 73 ml/h\",\"e\":\"This is the most appropriate fluid maintenance in children and has been calculated using the boy's weight of 32 kg (100 ml/kg for first 10 kg; 50 ml/kg for 10-20 kg; 20 ml/kg for >20 kg child's weight).\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":112,\"stem\":\"A 63-year-old man with pulmonary fibrosis is awaiting an elective repair of a large inguinal hernia. The anaesthetist has arranged for him to undergo lung function tests. The volume of gas in his lungs at the end of maximal expiration is referred to as what?\",\"lead\":\"The volume of gas in his lungs at the end of maximal expiration is referred to as what?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inspiratory reserve volume\",\"e\":\"The inspiratory reserve volume is the additional volume of air that can be inspired at the end of normal inspiration\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Vital capacity\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity is the difference of volume of air between maximum inspiration to maximum expiration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Expiratory reserve volume\",\"e\":\"The expiratory reserve volume is the additional volume of air that can be expired from the lungs after normal expiration to reach maximum expiration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Functional residual capacity\",\"e\":\"The functional residual capacity is the volume of air left in the lungs at the end of normal expiration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Residual volume\",\"e\":\"This is correct, the residual volume is the volume of air left in the lungs at the end of maximum expiration.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":114,\"stem\":\"An 88-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is awaiting surgery for a right neck of femur fracture. He develops pneumonia and becomes hypoxic. He receives 15 litres of oxygen to maintain his saturations. Three hours later he is drowsy and the oxygen is taken off. Arterial blood gas results show:\\n\\nNormal Result\\n\\n7.35-7.45 pH 7.32\\n\\n7.1 kPa 10.5-13.5 kPa\\n\\n4.6-6.0 kPa p (COz) 7.3 kPa\\n\\nHCO:- 28 mmol/l 24-30 mmol/l\\n\\nWhich one of the following receptors will be directly stimulated based on these blood gas levels?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following receptors will be directly stimulated based on these blood gas levels?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carotid body chemoreceptors\",\"e\":\"This arterial blood gas represents a type 2 respiratory failure ('ventilatory failure'). This has probably been caused by the administration of high flow oxygen in a patient with COPD, resulting in respiratory depression and subsequent hypercapnia. The carotid body chemoreceptors increase ventilation and are activated in response to reduced pa(02) and/or elevated pa(CO2) or H*.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Aortic baroreceptors\",\"e\":\"Aortic baroreceptors are stimulated by arterial stretch. An increase in baroreceptor discharge occurs in an attempt to lower blood pressure\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"The chemoreceptors located in the aortic body would be stimulated as a result of the type 2 respiratory failure. Afferent fibres are relayed to the vagus nerve to regulate breathing, but it is the chemoreceptors, not the vagus nerve that are stimulated first.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Macula densa\",\"e\":\"The macula densa cells are located within the distal convoluted tubule of the kidney and they are sensitive to changes in the concentration of sodium\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Beta-1 adrenergic receptors\",\"e\":\"Beta-1 receptors increase heart rate and contractility and would not be stimulated by type 2 respiratory failure.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":116,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old woman has coronary artery disease, type two diabetes and is an ex-smoker (30 pack-years). 48 hours ago, she underwent a coronary artery bypass graft and is on the Intensive Care Unit.\\n\\nWhich one of the following hormones would you expect to have decreased secretion?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following hormones would you expect to have decreased secretion?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline\",\"e\":\"The sympathetic nervous system is activated as part of the stress response and adrenaline release causes tachycardia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Thyroxine\",\"e\":\"Immediately after surgery there is a reduction in thyroid hormone secretion: This would be expected to normalise within the first few days.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cortisol\",\"e\":\"During an acute stress response there is activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary axis. which stimulates secretion of cortisol. Cortisol has a number of functions including anti- inflammatory effects and glucose control.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Antidiuretic hormone\",\"e\":\"There is increased secretion of antidiuretic hormone resulting in sodium and water retention.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cytokines\",\"e\":\"Cytokines are small proteins, not hormones, and there is increased secretion after surgery associated with tissue damage. Their effects include haemostasis and healing.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":118,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old man attends the Out-patient Clinic with a history of abdominal pain, particularly after eating and diarrhoea. He has an endoscopic desophagogastroduodenoscopy which shows severe ulceration of the stomach and duodenum. and the surgeon is concerned he may have Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is an effect of the hormone involved in this syndrome?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is an effect of the hormone involved in this syndrome?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Stomach distension\",\"e\":\"Distension of the stomach is a physiological stimulus for gastrin secretion and not an action of gastrin itself.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inhibition of gastric acid secretion\",\"e\":\"Gastrin stimulates the secretion of gastric acid by the parietal cells in the stomach.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pepsinogen secretion\",\"e\":\"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome there is excessive secretion of gastrin. Gastrin stimulates pepsinogen secretion by chief cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Somatostatin secretion\",\"e\":\"Gastrin does not impact the secretion of somatostatin. Somatostatin inhibits the release of gastrin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased gastric motility\",\"e\":\"Gastrin secretion aids digestion by increasing gastric motility.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":120,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old patient presents to the Emergency Department with severe colicky right flank pain. She has a computed tomography scan of kidneys, ureters and bladder (CTKUB). which demonstrates severe right hydronephrosis with an 8 mm stone at the right pelviureteral junction. What will happen to the renal blood flow over the first 4-5 h in an obstructed kidney?\",\"lead\":\"What will happen to the renal blood flow over the first 4-5 h in an obstructed kidney?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased renal blood flow\",\"e\":\"Over the first 1-2 h there is an increase in renal blood flow due to vasodilatation in an attempt to increase capillary hydrostatic pressure to maintain glomerular filtration rate. However, this is followed by a sustained decrease in renal blood flow unless the obstruction can be cleared.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased renal blood flow\",\"e\":\"Renal blood flow begins to decline after 3-4 h\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Initial decrease followed by an increase in renal blood flow\",\"e\":\"It is an initial increase in renal blood flow in an attempt to maintain glomerular filtration rate followed by the subsequent decline in renal blood flow.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Initial increase followed by a decrease in renal blood\",\"e\":\"Prostaglandin release and vasodilatation leads to an initial increase in renal blood flow. A few hours later, this is followed by vasoconstriction and therefore decreased renal blood flow.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"No change in renal blood flow\",\"e\":\"In an obstructed kidney the renal blood flow changes in an attempt to maintain glomerular filtration rate. 3322\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":122,\"stem\":\"A 96-year-old woman underwent right hip hemiarthroplasty one week ago. She has had a productive cough for 2 days. Blood cultures are negative. She has a temperature of 39.4°C, respiratory rate 26/min, heart rate 111/min and blood pressure 132/76 mmHg. On examination she has right basal crackles.\\n\\nWhich one of the following best describes this woman's condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describes this woman's condition?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Post-operative atelectasis\",\"e\":\"The history is not consistent with a simple atelectasis. The patient has a productive cough, is febrile, tachypnoeic and tachycardic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Uncomplicated infection\",\"e\":\"From the information you have been given this patient appears to have a chest infection. the patient is febrile, tachypnoeic and tachycardic. There is no evidence of organ dysfunction or hypotension, therefore it is not a sepsis or septic shock. The SOFA score is 1.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sepsis\",\"e\":\"Sepsis exists in the presence of infection with evidence of organ dysfunction. From the information you have been given there is no evidence of organ dysfunction.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Septic shock\",\"e\":\"Septic shock is sepsis with the presence of hypotension resistant to fluid resuscitation. This patient is not hypotensive\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Bacteraemia\",\"e\":\"The patient has had negative blood cultures. Bacteraemia is the presence of bacteria within the blood.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":124,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old man with known diverticular disease presents to the Emergency Department with left iliac fossa pain. He is not sure why, but he takes warfarin. His observations show a temperature of 38.2°C, heart rate 124 beats/min, respiratory rate 20 breaths/min and blood pressure 114/74 mmHg. On examination he is found to have an irregularly irregular pulse: Which one of the following would be found on a jugular venous pressure (JVP) wave for this patient?\",\"lead\":\"On examination he is found to have an irregularly irregular pulse: Which one of the following would be found on a jugular venous pressure (JVP) wave for this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ViS . Normal waveform\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Large a-wave\",\"e\":\"Large a-waves occur when there is resistance to emptying of the right atrium for example in tricuspid stenosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Absent a-wave\",\"e\":\"The a-wave on a VP waveform corresponds to right atrial contraction and would be absent in patients with atrial fibrillation.\\n\\nThe normal JVP waveform consists of the a-wave (atrial contraction), c-wave (ventricular contraction), x-descent (atrial relaxation), v-wave (atrial filling) and y-descent (ventricular filling). The history of this patient would suggest he has atrial fibrillation, which would result in an absent a-wave.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Absent p-wave\",\"e\":\"The JVP waveform does not include a p-wave. This would be the finding on an ECG in a patient with atrial fibrillation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Slow y-descent\",\"e\":\"The y-descent represents ventricular filling and this would be slow in tricuspid stenosis. 3293\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":126,\"stem\":\"A 33-year-old cyclist has been hit by a car at 30 mph. The patient is alert and talking, but has an open left femoral fracture. Observations are: respiratory rate 28/min, heart rate 112/min and blood pressure 122/86 mmHg. Which one of the following would be a blood loss volume consistent with this history?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would be a blood loss volume consistent with this history?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"250 ml\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"700 ml\",\"e\":\"700 ml blood loss would be consistent with class I haemorrhagic shock in which the observations are usually normal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"1150 ml\",\"e\":\"This patient has a significant injury with a respiratory rate between 20 and 30 breaths/min, heart rate 100-120 beats/min and normal blood pressure. It would be consistent with class Il haemorrhagic shock, in which there is between 750-1500 ml blood loss.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"1600 ml\",\"e\":\"Although a fracture of the left femur could cause a blood loss of 1600 ml, the information given in the history does not suggest he is in class I!! haemorrhagic shock, in which you would expect hypotensior.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"2050 ml\",\"e\":\"With this type of injury. the patient would be likely to have significant blood loss. A blood loss of 250 ml would not affect the patient's observations\\n\\nBlood loss of more than 2000 ml would result in the patient being hypotensive with heart rate >140 beats/min, respiratory rate >40 breaths/min and a patient that was confused and lethargic. 3893\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":128,\"stem\":\"A man weighing 100 kg was brought to the Emergency Department after being caught in a house fire. The primary survey is unremarkable with the exception of obvious burns of both of his anterior lower limbs and both palms. You want to start immediate fluid resuscitation. How much fluid should you give this patient over the first 8 h (according to ATLS 10th edition guidelines)?\",\"lead\":\"How much fluid should you give this patient over the first 8 h (according to ATLS 10th edition guidelines)?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1800 ml\",\"e\":\"The total body surface area affected is 21%, therefore the total fluid requirement = 2x100x21 = 4200 ml. Half this amount should be given in the first 8 h following the burn, therefore 1800 ml will be insufficient.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"2400 ml\",\"e\":\"You first need to determine the % area burn, which can then be used to calculate the fluid volume required using the ATLS formula (2 ml × weight in kg × % burn).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"2100 ml\",\"e\":\"Patients with burns require significant fluid resuscitation, which can be calculated using the formula 2 ml × weight in kg x % burn, as per the latest version of the ATLS guidelines. Adult patients with deep-partial and full-thickness burns involving more than 20% total body surface area (TBSA) should receive initial fluid resuscitation of 2 mi/kg/%TBSA. In paediatric burns 3 ml/kg/%TBSA should be used, and 4ml/kg/%TBSA used for electrical burns. The first half of the fluid should be given over the course of eight hours, and the remaining half is provided over the next 16 hours. The area of burn can be calculated using the rule of 9s or the Lund and Browder chart. Each lower limb is 18% (9% anterior and 9% posterior). In this patient both anteriorly have lower limbs been burnt. The palms are 1.5% each. Therefore, the total burn % would be 21%. Accordingly, 4200 ml should be given in 24 h, of which 2100 ml should be given within the first 8h.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"4800 ml\",\"e\":\"Fluid volume for the first 24 h can then be calculated using the formula (2 ml x weight in kg x21 (% burn)).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"7200 ml\",\"e\":\"Using the new ATLS formula for patients with over 20% TBSA burns, the fluid volume for the first 24 h can be calculated: 2 ml × 100 kg x 21(% burn) = 4200 mi. Only half of this amount should be given during the first 8 h\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":131,\"stem\":\"A 66-year-old man who was previously fit and well is diagnosed with gastric carcinoma. He is due to have a total gastrectomy. For which one of the following is he most likely to require parenteral replacement post- operatively?\",\"lead\":\"For which one of the following is he most likely to require parenteral replacement post- operatively?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Vitamin B1z\",\"e\":\"The absorption of vitamin Biz in the terminal ileum is dependent on intrinsic factor: Intrinsic factor is secreted by the parietal cells in the stomach. Therefore, following a total gastrectomy, the patient will require vitamin Big injections.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Calcium\",\"e\":\"Calcium is absorbed largely in the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Magnesium\",\"e\":\"No significant absorption occurs in the stomach. The predominant site of magnesium absorption appears to be the distal ileum and it is therefore not affected by total gastrectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"phosphate?\",\"e\":\"Phosphate is absorbed in the small intestine by both passive diffusion and active transport.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Zine\",\"e\":\"Zinc absorption is not affected by total gastrectomy as it is absorbed throughout the small bowel.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":133,\"stem\":\"Following a transient ischaemic attack, a 77-year-old man has an ultrasound scan, which shows left carotid artery stenosis. He is undergoing a carotid endarterectomy. The consultant asks you about the normal flow of blood through a vessel. Which one of the following statements accurately describes blood flow through a vessel?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements accurately describes blood flow through a vessel?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inversely proportional to the radius to the fourth power\",\"e\":\"Blood flow through a vessel is directly proportional to the radius to the fourth power.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Flow through a vessel is best described using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation\",\"e\":\"The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation is used for acid-base balance. Poiseuille's equation describes blood flow through a vessel.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Resistance to flow is directly proportional to the viscosity of blood\",\"e\":\"Resistance to blood flow through a vessel is directly proportional to blood viscosity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A two-fold increase in vessel radius decreases resistance by eight-fold\",\"e\":\"An increase in the vessel radius reduces resistance to the fourth power of the change in radius: Therefore a two-fold increase in radius decreases resistance by 16-fold.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vessel resistance is inversely proportional to the vessel length\",\"e\":\"Vessel resistance is directly proportional to the vessel length. A vessel that is twice the length of another with the same radius will have twice the resistance to flow.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":135,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old man with Crohn's disease has had recurrent hospital admissions due to strictures of the terminal ileum. He has not responded to medical treatment and opts to undergo an elective resection of the terminal ileum. Following the operation, the patient is likely to have malabsorption of which one of the following substances?\",\"lead\":\"Following the operation, the patient is likely to have malabsorption of which one of the following substances?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Iron\",\"e\":\"Iron is mainly absorbed in the duodenum and upper jejunum\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Vitamin Bi\",\"e\":\"Vitamin B, (thiamine) is a water-soluble vitamin and is absorbed in the jejunum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bile salts\",\"e\":\"The terminal ileum is responsible for the reabsorption of bile salts and vitamin Bez Bile salt malabsorption can cause chronic diarrhoea.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Zinc\",\"e\":\"Zinc absorption occurs throughout the entire small bowel. Therefore, resection of the terminal ileum alone would likely not result in zine malabsorption.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Calcium\",\"e\":\"Calcium is largely reabsorbed in the duodenum and not affected by resection of the terminal ileum. 3028\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":137,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old woman is assessed after a complicated laparoscopic cholecystectomy which required conversion to an open procedure. Her observations are: heart rate 113/min, respiratory rate 24/min, oxygen saturation 93% on room air, blood pressure 76/43 mmHg and temperature 38.9°C. She is given an immediate fluid bolus.\\n\\nWhich one of the following factors would increase the rate of flow through a cannula?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following factors would increase the rate of flow through a cannula?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Remove pressure around the fluid bag\",\"e\":\"The flow of liquid is related to a number of factors including pressure. Increasing pressure with the application of a pressure bag would maximise flow.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decrease the length of tubing\",\"e\":\"The flow of fluid can be described by Poiseuille's law, which demonstrates that flow is related to viscosity, pressure, length and diameter of tubing. Maximum flow would be achieved by a short length, large diameter, low viscosity fluid and high pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Reduce height of fluid bag\",\"e\":\"Reducing the height of a fluid bag decreases the pressure and reduces flow. By elevating a fluid bag and applying a pressure bag, a greater rate of flow can be achieved.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increase fluid viscosity\",\"e\":\"Increased fluid viscosity increases resistance and therefore decreases the rate of flow.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decrease diameter\",\"e\":\"The larger the diameter the greater the rate of flow.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":139,\"stem\":\"A. 72-year-old man has an elective sigmoid colectomy for malignancy. Post-operatively he has developed ileus and has not been able to tolerate oral intake for seven days. He is started on total parenteral nutrition. The dietician is concerned about refeeding syndrome and asks for some blood tests to be performed\\n\\nWhich one of the following blood levels would be helpful in detecting this?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following blood levels would be helpful in detecting this?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Vitamin D\",\"e\":\"Vitamin D levels would not be helpful in the detection of refeeding syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"White blood cells\",\"e\":\"White blood cells are often repeatedly taken post-operatively and can be helpful in detecting post-operative complications However, in this case, the biggest concern would be refeeding syndrome and white blood cells would not help to detect that.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Calcium\",\"e\":\"Calcium can be measured when assessing a patient for refeeding syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Haemoglobin\",\"e\":\"It is important to assess haemoglobin after major operations, in particular to assess if there has been any significant drop following the surgery that may require a blood transfusion. This patient is now seven days post-operatively and if there were a big intra-operative bleed this will have already been detected. There is nothing in the history that makes you think they are continuing to bleed and the biggest concern here would be refeeding syndrome, which haemoglobin is not useful in detecting.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Phosphate\",\"e\":\"In malnourished patients, the commencement of feed puts the patient at risk of refeeding syndrome, which can cause cardiac complications including cardiac arrhythmias resulting in sudden cardiac death. Refeeding causes increased glucose availability, increased insulin secretion and decreased glucagon secretion. This results in the synthesis of glycogen, fat and protein that requires phosphate and magnesium. Additionally, insulin stimulates the absorption of potassium. As a result, there are decreased levels of phosphate, potassium and magnesium.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":142,\"stem\":\"A 76-year-old man on the ward is five days post-operation from an emergency laparoscopic repair of a duodenal ulcer perforation. His observations reveal a heart rate of 126 beats/min and blood pressure 84/52 mmHg. An ECG has been performed that shows an irregularly irregular rhythm with loss of P-waves and tachycardia.\\n\\nWhich one of the following reasons would explain why the patient has hypotension?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following reasons would explain why the patient has hypotension?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased diastolic time\",\"e\":\"The tachycardia leads to reduced time in ventricular diastole and a subsequent reduce in stroke volume\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased cardiac contractility\",\"e\":\"Cardiac contractility is reduced due to decreased ventricular diastole.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased coronary artery filling\",\"e\":\"As a result of decreased time in ventricular diastole due to the tachycardia there is decreased coronary artery filling.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decreased ventricular filling\",\"e\":\"Loss of atrial contraction in combination with the tachycardia results in decreased ventricular filling\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Decreased vascular resistance\",\"e\":\"Hypotension would result from decreased ventricular filling.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":144,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old woman is undergoing a diagnostic laparoscopy for suspected appendicitis. She was previously fit and well and has never had any surgery. When she is brought into theatre, the operating department practitioner checks her temperature and it is 39°C. Which area of the brain is responsible for thermoregulation?\",\"lead\":\"Which area of the brain is responsible for thermoregulation?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypothalamus\",\"e\":\"The hypothalamus controls thermoregulation. When there is an increase in body temperature the hypothalamus activates cooling mechanisms, including vasodilatation of skin blood vessels and perspiration.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Thalamus\",\"e\":\"The thalamus acts as a relay station for information to and from the cortex. It has a role in sensation but is not directly involved in thermoregulation\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Anterior pituitary pl\",\"e\":\"The anterior pituitary is responsible for the secretion of hormones: thyroid stimulating hormone, growth hormones, adrenocorticotropic hormone, luteinising hormone, follicle stimulating hormone and prolactin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posterior pituitary\",\"e\":\"The posterior pituitary is responsible for the secretion of oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Basal ganglia\",\"e\":\"group of nuclei that are primarily involved in fine The basal ganglia are an interconnected motor control.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":146,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old woman with a penicillin allergy is admitted with diverticulitis. She is seen on the ward round and prescribed co-amoxiclav. During administration of this, she complains of shortness of breath. Observations are: respiratory rate 26/min, oxygen saturations 92% on room air, heart rate 114 beats/min, blood pressure 88/58 mmHg and temperature 38°C Which one of the following medications would it be important to administer to prevent a late-phase reaction?\",\"lead\":\"Observations are: respiratory rate 26/min, oxygen saturations 92% on room air, heart rate 114 beats/min, blood pressure 88/58 mmHg and temperature 38°C Which one of the following medications would it be important to administer to prevent a late-phase reaction?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"High-flow oxygen\",\"e\":\"This is an emergency and the patient should be assessed using an ABCDE approach. As part of that, high-flow oxygen must be administered but it does not treat the underlying problem or prevent any later reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline\",\"e\":\"Adrenaline is an emergency drug for the treatment of anaphylaxis. It should be administered intramuscularly at a dose of 0.5 ml in a 1:1000 concentration. However, it has a short duration of action, often has to be re-administered and used only in the early phase.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hydrocortisone\",\"e\":\"Hydrocortisone is given intravenously after the initial resuscitation and helps to prevent protracted reactions.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"0.9% sodium chloride\",\"e\":\"Fluid resuscitation is an important component of the emergency management of a patient with anaphylaxis but does not prevent late-phase reaction:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"PI Salbutamol\",\"e\":\"With this history the biggest concern is that the patient is having an anaphylactic reaction. In this case it would not treat the anaphylaxis and would likely make the tachycardia worse.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":148,\"stem\":\"A 19-year-old motorcyclist has collided with a bus at low speed. He is confused, with an obvious head injury. Observations are respiratory rate 24/min, saturations 94% on air, heart rate 41 bpm, blood pressure 74/48 mmHg and temperature 37.2°C. He receives a fluid bolus, out the blood pressure does not improve. What is the most likely explanation for his low blood pressure?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely explanation for his low blood pressure?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Haemorrhagic shock\",\"e\":\"Haemorrhagic (or hypovolaemic) shock is a major concern in trauma. However, in this case, it is unlikely to explain the patient's hypotension as there is no haemorrhage reported Furthermore, you would expect the patient to be tachycardic and responsive to fluid resuscitation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cardiogenic shock\",\"e\":\"Cardiogenic shock results from low cardiac output typically after a massive myocardial infarction, it occasionally occurs in trauma as a result of a direct cardiac injury. It would cause hypotension but typically the patient would be tachycardic.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Septic shock\",\"e\":\"There is nothing in the history that suggests this patient has developed sepsis and is in septic shock. In septic shock the patient would unlikely be bradycardic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Anaphylactic shock\",\"e\":\"Anaphylactic shock is a life-threatening allergic reaction to an antigen resulting in the release of chemical mediators from mast cells and basophils. It classically occurs in a hospital setting after administration of a drug to which the patient is allergic. It is a life- threatening emergency and patients are typically hypotensive and tachycardic\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neurogenic shock\",\"e\":\"The history suggests that the patient may have sustained a spinal cord injury with a resulting neurogenic shock. In neurogenic shock there is a loss of sympathetic tone below the lesion that causes bradycardia and loss of vasomotor tone resulting in hypotension. Neurogenic shock does not respond to fluids as the primary problem is loss of sympathetic tone.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":151,\"stem\":\"An 84-year-old man on rivaroxaban for atrial fibrillation has fallen downstairs and sustained a severe traumatic brain injury. A CT scan shows a left acute subdural haematoma causing midline shift. Which one of the following statements relating to the Monroe-Kellie doctrine is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements relating to the Monroe-Kellie doctrine is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Intracranial volume is made up primarily of brain tissue, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and lymphatics\",\"e\":\"Intracranial volume is primarily composed of brain, CSF and blood. There is no lymphatic system within the brain.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An intracranial pressure of 20 mmHg is typically considered within normal range\",\"e\":\"Normal intracranial pressure is typically considered to be between 5-15 mmg.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Any increase in the volume of one component within the skull results in an immediate increase in intracranial pressure (ICP)\",\"e\":\"The Monroe-Kellie doctrine states that the skull is a rigid structure with three components (brain tissue, CSF and blood). If the volume of one of these components increases there is a reciprocal decrease in another to maintain a constant ICP.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The Monroe-Kellie hypothesis applies to the newborn\",\"e\":\"The hypothesis only applied when the skull is fused\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"An average adult had an intracranial volume of 1700 ml\",\"e\":\"The intracranial volume in the adult is approximately 1700 ml; consisting of 1400 ml of brain tissue, 150 ml of cerebrospinal fluid and 150 ml of blood\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":153,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old man with a normally functioning right kidney is undergoing a left nephrectomy for suspected renal-cell carcinoma. Your consultant asks you about the renin- angiotensin-aldosterone system Where is aldosterone secreted from?\",\"lead\":\"Your consultant asks you about the renin- angiotensin-aldosterone system Where is aldosterone secreted from?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Juxtaglomerular apparatus of the kidney\",\"e\":\"The juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney secrete renin.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Zona glomerulosa of the adrenal gland\",\"e\":\"The zona glomerulosa is the superficial layer of the three layers of the adrenal cortex. It produces and secretes aldosterone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Zona fasciculata of the adrenal gland\",\"e\":\"The zona fasciculate is the middle layer of the adrenal cortex, it secretes cortisol in response to adrenccorticotropic hormone from the pituitary.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Zona reticularis of the adrenal gland\",\"e\":\"The zona reticularis in the inner most layer of the adrenal cortex. It is responsible for the production of androgens.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Adrenal medulla\",\"e\":\"The adrenal medulla is responsible for the secretion of catecholamines.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":155,\"stem\":\"A 33-year-old overweight woman presents to her GP with fatigue. On further questioning. she has recently experienced polyuria and polydipsia. Her GP asks for a urine sample and subsequent urinalysis is positive for glucose.\\n\\nWhere is glucose absorbed in the nephron?\",\"lead\":\"Where is glucose absorbed in the nephron?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Glomerulus\",\"e\":\"The glomerulus is the network of the capillaries at the beginning of the nephron. It produces a selective ultrafiltrate of the blood. The rate of glomerular filtrate is called the glomerular filtration rate (GFR). The glomerulus does not absorb particles, but is a filter to the proximal convoluted tubule.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Proximal convoluted tubule\",\"e\":\"Glucose, amino acids and bicarbonate are reabsorbed along sodium in the early portion of the proximal convoluted tubule. Glucose is removed from the urine by secondary active transport\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Loop of Henle\",\"e\":\"The descending loop of Henle is permeable to water due to the presence of acquaporin-1 but the ascending limb is impermeable to water. Nat, K*and Clare co-transported out of the ascending limb, which encourages the movement of water out of the descending limb, making the fluid in the descending limb hypertonic. In the ascending limb it becomes more dilute because of the movement of Nat and Clout of the lumen and at the top is now hypotonic to plasma. The loop of Henle is not involved in glucose resorption.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Distal convoluted tubule\",\"e\":\"The distal convoluted tubule is relatively impermeable to water and is involved in the continual removal of the solute, making the tubular fluid further diluted. It is not involved in glucose resorption.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Collecting ducts\",\"e\":\"The collecting ducts are under the influence of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) (vasopressin) to control water absorption. In the absence of ADH, water can enter the urine, promoting diuresis. In the presence of ADH, aquaporins allow the collecting duct to become permeable to water allowing reabsorption and inhibiting diuresis. ¿ngQ\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":158,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-oid man who weighs 132 kg would like to be considered for an elective total knee replacement. He has been advised to lose weight before the surgery can go ahead Which one of the following factors contribute to increased cardiovascular complications in obesity?\",\"lead\":\"He has been advised to lose weight before the surgery can go ahead Which one of the following factors contribute to increased cardiovascular complications in obesity?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased cardiac output\",\"e\":\"Blood volume is increased as a result of increased activity in the renin-angiotensin- aldosterone system. With an increase in blood volume there is an increase in ventricular filling and therefore cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased renal blood flow\",\"e\":\"Renal blood flow in obese patients is similar to the non-obese population.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fatty infiltration of the Purkinje fibres of the conduction system\",\"e\":\"Obese patients can have excess intramyocardial fat and deposition of fat within the myocytes. This results in LV hypertropy and diastolic dysfunction and increased arrhythmogenocity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Poor compliance of the left ventricle\",\"e\":\"Blood volume expansion leads to left ventricular dilatation. Over time there is reduced compliance of the left ventricle and impaired left ventricle filling leading to heart failure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"High blood volume to body mass ratio\",\"e\":\"While the absolute blood volume is increased in relation to body mass it is low.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":160,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-olc man attended the Emergency Department with severe abdominal pain and hypotension. He was found to have a bowel perforation and went to theatre for an emergency laparotomy. You review him in the intensive care unit the following day and he remains on inotropes\\n\\nWhich one of these is a commonly used inotrope in intensive care?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is a commonly used inotrope in intensive care?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dexmedetomidine\",\"e\":\"Dexmedetomidine is a centrally acting a2-agonist. It has sedative and analgesic effects. On initial administration it can cause vasoconstriction, raising the blood pressure. However. continuous infusion causes vasodilatation and subsequent hypotension.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Dobutamine\",\"e\":\"Dobutamine acts on B1 receptors and acts to increase cardiac contractility and cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Amiodarone\",\"e\":\"Amiodarone is primarily used in the treatment of arrhythmias.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Noradrenaline\",\"e\":\"Noradrenaline is an al agonist and increases blood pressure by vasoconstriction. It is not inotropic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Adrenaline\",\"e\":\"Adrenaline is an inotrope. It acts on al, B1 and B2 receptors. However, it would not be typically used in this case as its B activity would induce tachycardia.\"}]},{\"t\":38,\"p\":162,\"stem\":\"A homeless man is brought into the Emergency Department on a cold winter night. He is unresponsive on arrival with profound hypothermia. Which one of the following can be seen in hypothermia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following can be seen in hypothermia?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreases in the oxygen content of blood at any given tension\",\"e\":\"Hypothermia causes a shift of the oxygen dissociation curve to the left, so increasing the oxygen content of blood at a given tension.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A rise in brain activity\",\"e\":\"A fall in core body temperature is associated with a decrease in brain metabolism and activity. Therapeutic cooling is one of several methods that can be used to prevent secondary traumatic brain injury.\\n\\nBradycardia occurs due to depression of cardiac pacemaker cells.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sinus tachycardia\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Decrease in blood viscosity\",\"e\":\"Blood viscosity increases as body temperature falls.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cardiac arrest is likely below 25°C\",\"e\":\"Atrial and ventricular arrhythmias can result from hypothermia; asystole and ventricular fibrillation have been noted to begin spontaneously at core temperatures below 25-28°C.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man is found under a pile of rubble at a building site after an accident. His right thigh is severely crushed and unsalvageable. He is disorientated and confused. His urine is dark and tests positive for myoglobin. His wife states that since his brother lost his leg in Afghanistan the patient has said several times that he would rather go through anything than lose a leg. What is the correct management of this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the correct management of this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Manage the patient on ITU with aggressive fluid resuscitation waiting for the patient's conscious level to improve\",\"e\":\"This man has a severe crush injury and is at significant risk of renal failure and sepsis if the leg is not amputated. Even being managed in the. intensive care unit (ITU) setting in this manner he is unlikely to regain capacity to made this decision. Although he may have said to his family that he would not accept an amputation under any circumstances, without a legally witnessed advance directive this unfortunately is hearsay. Your job is to act in the best wishes of the patient while keeping the family involved as much as possible.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Manage the patient using ABC principles. Ask to speak to the patients brother to corroborate the wife's wishes and if both are in agreement then manage the patient in the palliative setting\",\"e\":\"In any emergency setting the patient should be managed according to the ABC principles. While it would be important to involve the family in the care of the patient without any witnessed advance directive they cannot refuse life-saving treatment. As a doctor in this scenario you must act in the best interests of the patient and perform amputation to prevent the risk of renal failure, sepsis and death.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Manage the patient using ABC principles. Attempt to contact the on call legal team and await their decision before embarking further\",\"e\":\"While it may be sensible to involve the legal team you do not need to await their decision before commencing the life-saving treatment and should act in the best interests of the patient when there is no valid advance directive.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Manage the patient using ABC principles. Following discussion with the patient's wife book the patient for a below knee amputation with a consent form 4 in the first instance\",\"e\":\"In this case a below knee amputation would not be an effective treatment as the crush injury is to the thigh. As the patient is disorientated and confused you would need to use a consent form 4 to proceed with any surgery (consent form for adults who lack capacity). You should discuss the treatment with the patient's wife but you do not need to get her permission.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Manage the patient using ABC principles. Following discussion with the patient's vife book the patient for a high amputation with a consent form 4\",\"e\":\"This man has a severe crush injury and is at significant risk of renal failure and sepsis if the leg is not amputated. As the injury is to the thigh it would require a high amputation. You should discuss with the patient's wife, but do not need to get permission and consent should be gained with a consent form 4 (consent for adults who lack capacity) as he is confused and disorientated.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"You are in the process of preparing for your appraisal and are collecting the relevant evidence required. It will be the closest appraisal prior to your revalidation date. Which of the following statements is most accurate regarding this appraisal?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements is most accurate regarding this appraisal?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A revalidation ready appraisal is required every 3 years for revalidation every 6 years\",\"e\":\"An annual appraisal is mandatory for doctors working within the NHS and is part of the 5 yearly process of revalidation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Feedback from colleagues has a lower weighting than feedback from patients\",\"e\":\"Colleague and patient feedback must be used as supporting evidence for appraisal for revalidation; both are equally important.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Your appraisal can be carried out by a non consultant grade doctor\",\"e\":\"Appraisals are carried out between yourself and a colleague who has been trained as an appraiser. Medical appraisers come from a variety of backgrounds and include locums and salaried general practitioners in primary care settings and staff and associate specialist doctors in secondary care settings.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Your appraisal should be carried out by any NHS consultant who has been on the specialist register for 5 years\",\"e\":\"Your appraisal needs to be performed by an individual trained to do so and is not necessarily related to their length of time on the specialist register.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"360 degree feedback is an essential part of your appraisal\",\"e\":\"Feedback from colleagues in the form of either multi-source feedback, or 360 degree feedback is an essential component of revalidation done every 5 years. It does not necessarily need to be included at every appraisal.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"You are a Registrar. You have just had your appraisal and despite having passed you feel that you were woefully underprepared. As such, you are determined to make your job easier for next year.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding the appraisal process?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding the appraisal process?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Demonstration of reflection on any compliments or complaints is an essential caspect of appraisal\",\"e\":\"According to the General Medical Council (GMC) reflection drives change in performance and is the key to effective professional development. It is therefore advised that doctors reflect regularly on all aspects of professional work including compliments and complaints and this forms part of your appraisal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The maximum number of appraisals you can have is one per year\",\"e\":\"Appraisal needs to be carried out at least once a year but can be as frequent as you want it to be. You may struggle to find an appraiser who has the time to do it more than once yearly however.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"You need to demonstrate 25h of continuing professional development (CPD) points every year\",\"e\":\"You need to demonstrate 50 h of CPD points each year as per Royal College of Surgeons guidelines.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"You need to accumulate 100 CPD points per year\",\"e\":\"The guidelines from the Royal College of Surgeons on Continuing Professional Development state surgeons should complete 50 h of CPD each year and at least 250 h in a revalidation cycle. Choose CPD activities that are genuinely developmental. Activities should be relevant to the surgeons practice and support current skills and knowledge or career development. Surgeons should aim to achieve a balance of activities between clinical, academic and professional. No more than 20 h should be from a single type. Recording of CPD activity should include reflection.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"360 degree feedback is an essential part of the appraisal process\",\"e\":\"360 degree feedback or multi-source feedback is only essential for revalidation every 5 vears although most doctors will collect data every year.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"Clinical governance has been an integral part of the NHS for many years. Which one of the following statements is true regarding this important subject?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding this important subject?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It has a strong relationship with research and development\",\"e\":\"Research and development is hugely important in clinical governance to achieve the aim of continually improving the quality of services provided by NHS organisations.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is a yearly process\",\"e\":\"Reports on clinical governance are typically produced annually, but it is a continuous process.\\n\\nClinical audit is one of the seven pillars of clinical governance with the others being; service user, carer and public involvement, risk management, staffing and staff management, education and training, clinical effectiveness and clinical information.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is entirely separate from clinical audit\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is only applicable to clinical members of staff\",\"e\":\"Clinical governance is applicable to all members of staff, clinical and non-clinical. It is 'A framework through which NHS organisations are accountable for continually improving the quality of their services and safeguarding high standards of care by creating an environment in which excellence in clinical care will flourish\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is synonymous with clinical effectiveness\",\"e\":\"Clinical effectiveness forms part of clinical governance but there are also six other areas; service user, carer and public involvement, risk management, clinical audit, staffing and staff management, education and training and clinical information. зо3g\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"An essential part of clinical governance is risk management. Unfortunately, your patient has been given flucloxacillin despite having a documented penicillin allergy. Thankfully they have a moderate urticarial reaction that responds to Piriton and steroids.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Analysis of risk is a locally driven system\",\"e\":\"Analysis of risk is a national initiative. All reported clinical and non-clinical risks are centrally collated and can be analysed to view trends in incidents over time.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Clinical risk has a different reporting system than non-clinical risk\",\"e\":\"Both clinical and non-clinical risks are reported using the same system and are a mandatory part of the NHS. It is confidential and centrally collated.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is only the severity of the risk (ie green, amber or red rating) that is important when assessing clinical risk\",\"e\":\"When assessing clinical risk it is both the severity of risk and its frequency that is important\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reporting of clinical errors is at the discretion of the senior clinician, nurse or manager on duty\",\"e\":\"Clinical errors can and should be submitted by any member of the team.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Risk reporting has a direct impact on CNST payments\",\"e\":\"The frequency of error and risk reporting is fed into the CNST (clinical negligence scheme for trusts) and may affect the insurance premiums the Trust pays.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"As you are aware the NHS has undergone significant change over the past few years: It is inevitable that it will change significantly in the future. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the 'new NHS'?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the 'new NHS'?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Certain healthcare providers are exempt from regulation by the care quality commission (CQC)\",\"e\":\"The CQC is an independent regulator of all health and social care services in England. It inspects and regulates hospitals, care homes, GP surgeries, dental practices and other care services to ensure they meet fundamental standards of quality and safety.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Commissioning is dealt with by Primary Care Trusts (PCT's)\",\"e\":\"Clinical Commissioning Groups (CCGs) replaced PCTs in 2013. CCGs are responsible for the planning and commissioning of healthcare services for their local area.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"GPs commission all operative interventions from local hospitals\",\"e\":\"Clinical Commissioning Groups, made up of GPs and other clinicians including nurses and consultants, are responsible for commissioning healthcare services for their local area. Some of the more complex surgical procedures, eg tertiary paediatric surgery services, are commissioned directly from the Department of Health:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"GPs nave a vital role ip commissioning services for their patients trom healthcare providers\",\"e\":\"GPs form part of the Clinical Commissioning Groups along with other healthcare professionals. CCGs are responsible for commissioning healthcare services for the local area.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"NHS healthcare can only be delivered by NHS hospitals\",\"e\":\"Any qualified provider (NHS. private, third sector, social enterprise) licensed by the CQC can compete for contracts to provide NHS healthcare.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"As a Senior House Officer (SHO) you are interested in teaching medical students to both improve your knowledge and make an impact on doctors of the future. You set up a teaching programme and run a practical session on basic surgical skills. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding teaching and training in the NHS?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding teaching and training in the NHS?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Any consultant who has been in post for more than 3 years can be an educational supervisor\",\"e\":\"Any consultant who would like to be an educational supervisor has to have passed the qualified educational supervisor programme or similar. It is essential that those clinicians who wish to supervise have appropriate training.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Any consultant who has been in post for more than 5 years can be a clinical supervisor\",\"e\":\"It is essential for consultants to be appropriately trained to be clinical supervisors. Therefore, only consultants that have undertaken and passed the qualified educational supervisor programme or similar can become clinical supervisors.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"1- _ Evidence or teaching is important and torms a tormal part of the appraisal process\",\"e\":\"Teaching is essential for your training and the training of others. Evidence of teaching forms an essential part of appraisals, as it forms part of 'duties of a doctor.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is only recognised trainers who have taken part in the necessary training themselves who can teach in the NHS\",\"e\":\"Ideally trainers should have undertaken some training relevant to their field but anyone interested in teaching should be allowed to teach.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Workplace based assessments can only be undertaken by consultants\",\"e\":\"Workplace-based assessments can be completed by middle grade doctors and consultants.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"You have walked into the doctors' mess late at night and see your Registrar smoking cannabis while on call. She says that she has had a very stressful week and has only had one joint to help her relax. You believe her.\\n\\nWhat would you do?\",\"lead\":\"What would you do?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"As it is likely to be a quiet night, ask for her assurance that this is the last time and carry on\",\"e\":\"It is your duty to make the situation safe and is therefore vital that the Registrar is removed from the clinical environment immediately to protect patient safety. She cannot be allowed to continue to work.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ask her to arrange for someone else to do her on call for her and nothing more will be said\",\"e\":\"Another doctor does need to complete her on call to protect patient safety. However, this situation needs to be escalated and dealt with, not forgotten, after this night shift, so that any future patient safety issues can be prevented.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ask her to discuss the matter with her consultant/supervisor in the morning and carry on\",\"e\":\"It is not safe for her to continue to work overnight, she needs to immediately stopped from working. Therefore, discussing with the consultant/supervisor in the morning is not sufficient to protect patient safety.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ask her to phone her consultant to self-report immediately\",\"e\":\"She should be strongly encouraged to report herself immediately to her consultant and if she does not then you should. It is her consultant's responsibility, out of hours, to deal with this in the first instance. It is vital that the Registrar is removed from the clinical environment immediately and the consultant should facilitate this by 'stepping down' to act as the Registrar. Once the situation is made safe, it is likely the Registrar will face both Trust and GMC disciplinary proceedings in due course.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Phone the GMC on call line for advice\",\"e\":\"The matter is likely to be escalated to both the Trust and GMC for disciplinary proceedings but it is her consultant's responsibility out of hours to deal with this in the first instance.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 31-year-old man is critically injured in a road traffic accident (RTA) and requires an emergency laparotomy. He is intubated at the scene and unaccompanied by any family or\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding informed consent is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding informed consent is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Informed consent must be obtained by the person doing the procedure\",\"e\":\"If it is not practical for the person performing the procedure to undertake consent it can be delegated provided that person is suitably trained and qualified, understands the principles of consent and has sufficient knowledge of the proposed investigation/treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Informed consent must describe alternative options\",\"e\":\"To obtain informed consent the patient must be informed of all options for treating or managing the condition including the option not to treat. In the case given it would not be informed consent, the patient needs emergency surgery so a consent form 4 would be used.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Informed consent can be gained from relatives or carers if the patient themselves cannot give consent\",\"e\":\"A valid consent must be voluntarily given by a competent patient making an informed choice. For consent to be valid the patient must have the capacity to understand, retain and process the information given. No one else can make a decision on behalf of an adult who has capacity. If a patient lacks capacity then they cannot give informed consent and any views or preferences expressed by the patient when they had capacity should be taken into account, it should be discussed with the next of kin and treatment should be given in the patient's best interests\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Informed consent is the sole responsibility of the consultant in charge of the case\",\"e\":\"Informed consent is the responsibility of the doctor undertaking an investigation or providing treatment which may not necessarily be the consultant in charge. If it is not practical then this responsibility can be delegated provided that person is suitably trained and qualified, understands the principles of consent and has sufficient knowledge of the proposed investigation/treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Previous expressed wishes of the patient made when they are competent are null and void when subsequently presenting in a lowered state of consciousness if their life is at risk\",\"e\":\"When a patient lacks capacity any wishes they have previously expressed when competent should be taken into consideration when making a decision to act in the patients best interests.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"Your consultant asks you to obtain consent from his patients for his elective list which is lengthy and you are short of time. You notice that one of the patients signed a consent form in an out patients' department about 6 months ago that covers all the issues you would discuss now. How would you proceed with this patient?\",\"lead\":\"How would you proceed with this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A consent form is legal proof that consent has been given. it will not be necessary for the patient to sign again\",\"e\":\"A signature on a consent torm, following a documented balanced discussion of alternative treatments, risks and benefits often indicates completion of the consent process. It is good practice to reconfirm consent on the day of surgery and offer opportunity for questions. If a significant delay has occurred between initial consent and the day of surgery, reconfirming consent by signing the relevant section of the consent form is good practice.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A consent form signed in an out-patients' department 6 months preoperatively is technically valid but it is strongly advisable to obtain the signature again on admission\",\"e\":\"It would be best practice to hold a further discussion with the patient, as there has been significant delay, to check their understanding and offer them another opportunity to ask any additional questions. On the consent form there is an additional section labelled confirmation of consent that can be signed as evidence that this has been done.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Having checked that all complications have been consented in the document, and that the patient had capacity to consent at the time of signature, no further contact is required\",\"e\":\"In this case there has been a 6 month delay from consent, which is a significant time period. In this time the patient may have lost their capacity to consent or have additional questions. As the surgeon it is your responsibility to ensure that the patient has capacity on the day of surgery, understands the operation and complications and offer the patient a further opportunity to ask questions.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tell the patient they must re-sign the document\",\"e\":\"It would be best practice to hold a further discussion about the surgery and answer any questions from the patient. The surgeon can clarify the patient's initial consent and reconfirm this by signing the relevant section on the day of surgery. The patient does not have to re-sign.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Verbal consent will be adequate in this scenario\",\"e\":\"Verbal consent is not enough in cases in which sedation or anaesthesia is being used or where the treatment is risky, lengthy or complex. In surgery written consent should be obtained.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"An 89-year-old woman with Alzheimer's dementia is placed on an elective list for excision of an ulcerated squamous-cell carcinoma (SCC) to her cheek, Her son shows you he has a lasting power of attorney (LPA) and will be able to consent to the treatment on her behalf. Which one of the statements regarding an LPA and consent is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the statements regarding an LPA and consent is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A health and welfare LPA allows decisions to be made on the donor's medical care: and life-sustaining treatment, daily care and living arrangements\",\"e\":\"An LPA is a legal document that may be made by a person (known as a donor) in anticipation of losing the mental capacity to make decisions for themselves. The document must be registered with the Office of the Public Guardian (OPG) to be valid. An 'attorney' or 'attorneys' are appointed to make decisions over either property and financial affairs, health and welfare affairs, or both. An attorney acting under a health and welfare LPA must act in the donor's best interests when deciding upon medical treatments.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A health and welfare LPA comes into effect as soon as it is registered\",\"e\":\"While a financial LPA can come into effect, with the permission of the donor, as soon as it is registered, a health and weifare PA is never valid until the donor loses the capacity to make such decisions in person.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"An LPA can be validly made by anyone 16 or over who has mental capacity at the time of making the document\",\"e\":\"A valid LPA can be made by anyone aged 18 or over at the time of creating the document and have mental capacıty.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An LPA cannot be validly made by anyone 18 or over\",\"e\":\"A valid LPA cannot be made by anyone under the age of 18.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"If there is more than one attorney registered they must both give consent for medical care decisions\",\"e\":\"Depending on the terms of the LPA, when two or more people are acting as attorney, they may be able to act individually or only with the consent of all attorneys.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"Many bodies exist with an aim to improving healthcare delivery throughout the UK. They will regularly produce documentation that clinicians should aim to keep up to date with. What does The National Institute of Clinical Excellence (NICE) issue guidance on?\",\"lead\":\"What does The National Institute of Clinical Excellence (NICE) issue guidance on?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Good medical practice\",\"e\":\"The General Medical Council issues guidance on good medical practice.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Interventional procedures\",\"e\":\"The role of NIcE is to improve outcomes for people using the NHS and other public health and social care services. This is achieved in part by producing evidence-based guidance and advice for health technologies, clinical practice, public health and social care. NICE issues interventional procedures guidance.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medical ethics\",\"e\":\"The General Medical Council issues guidance on medical ethics.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Patient safety\",\"e\":\"The National Patient Safety Agency monitored and recorded all incidents in which patient safety may be compromised. In 2012, the key functions and expertise were transferred to the NHS Commissioning Board Special Health Authority\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Risk management\",\"e\":\"Risk management is one component of clinical governance but it not directly part of the role ofnice\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"An adolescent aged 14 is seen at her General Practitioner's (GP) clinic requesting an abortion of an unwanted pregnancy. She does not wish her parents to know about the procedure.\\n\\nWhat is meant by Gillick competence?\",\"lead\":\"What is meant by Gillick competence?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A child cannot consent to their own medical treatment\",\"e\":\"If a child has capacity then they may consent to their own medical treatment regardless of age.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A child of any age can consent to treatment if he or she fully understands the medical treatment proposed\",\"e\":\"Gillick competence states 'as a matter of Law the parental right to determine whether or not their minor child below the age of 16 will have medical treatment terminates if and when the child achieves sufficient understanding and intelligence to understand fully what is proposed'_\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A child of any age can consent to treatment if he or she fully understands the medical treatment proposed but this must be supported by at least one parent / guardian\",\"e\":\"If a child has capacity then they may consent to their own medical treatment and no parent or guardian is required to support this decision.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A child of any age can consent to treatment if he or she fully understands the medical treatment proposed so long as it is supported by two doctors and is in the child's best interest\",\"e\":\"A child of any age who has sufficient intelligence, competence and understanding to fully appreciate what is involved in their treatment may consent to that treatment, this does not need to be supported by two doctors. If a child refuses treatment, the decision of which may lead to their death or severe injury their decision car be overruled by the Court of Protection.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Parental rights outweigh the rights of a minor\",\"e\":\"If a child is deemed Gillick competent then they may consent to their own treatment and this does not require parental involvement. If the child is not Gillick competent then someone with parental responsibility and capacity can consent for them. If a parent refuses to give consent to a particular treatment that is deemed to be in the best interests of the child this decision can be overruled by the courts.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man with a mental health disorder is seen in the Accident and Emergency Department with abdominal trauma and bleeding. He requires an urgent laparotomy but is deemed to be incapable of consent.\\n\\nWhich one of the following allows you to proceed to theatre?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following allows you to proceed to theatre?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A next of kin can give consent on his behalf\",\"e\":\"A discussion with the next of kin about the treatment would be best practice, however their consent is not required for this life-saving treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Consent form 4 can be signed, acting in his best interest\",\"e\":\"As this is an emergency life-saving procedure, he can be taken to theatre with a Consent 4 (England) or an adults with incapacity (AWI) document (Scotland) signed by his medical team.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"As this is a life-saving procedure, consent is not required\",\"e\":\"Even though the patient is unable to give informed written consent, written consent is still required to confirm that the patient does not have capacity to consent and the reasons why you cannot wait until the patient has regained capacity before performing the treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Court approval should be sought before any intervention\",\"e\":\"In cases of life-saving treatment court approval is not required. In these cases the doctor must provide medical treatment that the treatment is in the patient's best interests.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"He can be treated under the Mental Health Act, issued by the duty psychiatrist\",\"e\":\"The Mental Health Act covers the assessment, treatment and rights of people with a mental health disorder. People are detained under this Act when they seem to be at serious risk of harming themselves or others and need urgent treatment for a mental health disorder. In this case the patient requires life-saving treatment for his abdominal trauma not relating to his mental health disorder and it would therefore not be covered by the Mental Health Act.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"You are the core trainee and have just reviewed the case of a 30-year-old patient on the ward who has died following an emergency operation for a bowel perforation. You are required to fill in the death certificate in the bereavement office as soon as you are able. Cases that should be referred to the coroner in England include which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Cases that should be referred to the coroner in England include which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Death from a bowel perforation\",\"e\":\"The cause of death is known, explained and not unnatural. Assuming the patient has beer seen by a doctor during their final illness and had no surgery this would not need to be referred to the coroner\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Death from a notifiable disease such as meningitis\",\"e\":\"Notifiable diseases have to be reported to the Consultant in Communicable Disease Control (CCDC). They do not need to be referred to the coroner.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Death from AIDS or an HIV related illnesses\",\"e\":\"Deaths from AIDS or an HIV-related illnesses do not need to be reported to the coroner unless they meet another reason for reporting; unknown cause of death, violent or unnatural death, sudden and unexplained death, not visited by a medical practitioner in their final illness, medical certificate unavailable, not seen by a doctor who signed the medical certificate within 14 days before death or after they died, death during an operation or before the person came out of anaesthetic or medical certificate suggesting that the death may have been caused by an industrial disease or industrial poisoning.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Death in a patient under 50 years of age\",\"e\":\"Age does not affect whether a case should or should not be referred to the coroner.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Death related to industrial or occupational disease in former employment\",\"e\":\"Any death thought to have been caused by an industrial disease or industrial poisoning should be referred to the coroner. Recent surgery should also be referred to the coroner, particularly if it relates to the patient's death.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"You are the core surgical trainee and are consenting a patient for removal of a lipoma under local anaesthetic, a procedure you will perform yourself with the consultant as an unscrubbed supervisor. You go to consent the procedure in the admissions unit. Which one of the following is correct regarding your discussion during the consent process with the patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding your discussion during the consent process with the patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"You do not have to mention non-operative management\",\"e\":\"All alternative management options should be mentioned. In any case non-operative management is always an option provided the patient understands any associated risks with such an option.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"You do not have to mention the risk of reaction to local anaesthetic as it is so rare\",\"e\":\"All risks relating to any procedure need to be mentioned regardless of how rare they may be.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"You need not mark the site as it is a local anaesthetic procedure\",\"e\":\"Even though the procedure is to be performed under a local anaesthetic the site still needs to be marked before the operation to act as an additional check to prevent wrong site surgery, which would be a never event.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"You should put a single identifier like the patient's name on the consent form\",\"e\":\"At least three patient identifiers need to be put on the form. Commonly this is the patients name, date of birth and hospital or NHS number: 2952\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"You should state you will be doing the operation with supervision\",\"e\":\"You should inform the patient who will be doing the operation and be honest about your experience. As the consultant plans to remain in theatre to supervise the procedure you should also inform the patient of this.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man is involved in a road traffic accident and has extensive head injuries, he is Glasgow Coma Scale 3 on arrival and is unresponsive to intervention. Two sets of appropriate brainstem tests are performed and the patient shows no response. He is certified dead by brainstem testing (BST) and suitable for organ transplant. Who is authorised to diagnose brainstem death?\",\"lead\":\"Who is authorised to diagnose brainstem death?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"An Intensive Therapy Unit (ITU) consultant alone\",\"e\":\"This is not sufficient; brainstem death testing has to be performed by two doctors, one of whom should be a consultant and both should be 5 years post-registration.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Two consultants both 5 years post-registration must make the certification\",\"e\":\"Only one of the doctors needs to be a consultant but both do need to be 5 years post- registration. Neither doctor can be a member of the transplant team.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Two consultants, one of whom must be a member of the transplant team, both 5 vears post-registration\",\"e\":\"Both doctors must be 5 years post-registration but only one needs to be a consultant Neither doctor can be a member of the transplant team.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Two doctors both 5 years post-registration\",\"e\":\"Both need to be qualified for 5 years, and one of these doctors must be a consultant. Neither of them should be a member of the transplant team.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Two doctors, one of whom must be a consultant, both 5 years post-registration neither of whom are a member of the transplant team\",\"e\":\"This is correct, brainstem death testing should be carried out twice by two doctors, both 5 years post-registration, one of whom must be a consultant and neither should be a member of the transplant team.\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"Your consultant is participating in a trial of a new chemotherapy agent for breast cancer and asks you to help her collect the data. You begin to plan the study and set about plans to reduce as much bias as you can.\\n\\nIn a single-blinded trial who will be aware of the treatment allocation?\",\"lead\":\"In a single-blinded trial who will be aware of the treatment allocation?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Both the assessor and the patient will be aware of the treatment being used\",\"e\":\"When both the assessor and patient are aware of the treatment being given then the study is not blinded.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Neither the patient nor the assessor knows which treatment an individual is receiving\",\"e\":\"If the patient and the assessor are unaware of what treatment has been given this would be a double-blinded trial.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Usually only the patient will be unaware of which treatment they have been given\",\"e\":\"in single-blinded trials, patients are randomly allocated to treatment or placebo/standard groups. Typically the patient is unaware to which treatment group they have been allocated. but the assessor will know.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Usually only the assessor will be unaware of which treatment the patient has received\",\"e\":\"Usually in single-blinded trials it is the patient and not the assessor that is blind to the treatment being given.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neither the patient nor the assessor knows which treatment an individual is receiving until analysis is performed when both are informed\",\"e\":\"For single-blinded trials typically the patient remains blind to the treatment they have received for the entire length of the trial, while the assessor will be aware. 2011\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"An 18-month-old boy is admitted to the paediatric unit with burns sustained while in the care of his mother's partner. You are concerned that a non-accidental injury may have taken place. Of the following, which one is suggestive of a diagnosis of child abuse?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following, which one is suggestive of a diagnosis of child abuse?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bruising over the shins\",\"e\":\"Bruising over the shins is commonly seen, particularly in children who are just learning to walk.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Injury consistent with history!\",\"e\":\"There are no pathognomonic signs of child abuse, the diagnosis rests upon a combination of inconsistencies between the presenting history and the injury. With an injury consistent with the history a suspicion of child abuse is not necessarily raised.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mid-clavicular fracture\",\"e\":\"Clavicular fractures have a low association with child abuse.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Rib fractures\",\"e\":\"While there are no pathognomonic signs of child abuse some fractures raise a higher suspicion These include single fracture with multiple bruises, multiple fractures of different ages, metaphyseal and/or epiphyseal injuries, rib fractures, new periosteal bone formation and skull fractures in association with intracranial injury. An 18 month old should not be able to sustain these kind of injuries in normal circumstances.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supracondylar humeral fractures\",\"e\":\"Supracondylar fractures are common in children, falling onto an outstretched arm, and if consistent with the history, would not raise a suggestion of abuse. 2957\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"A child who has not yet started walking arrives in A&E for assessment. They have hurt themselves, but the parents give an inconsistent history. In addition the interaction between the mother and child does not seem normal. Which injury most raises suspicion of non-accidental injury?\",\"lead\":\"Which injury most raises suspicion of non-accidental injury?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior rib fractures\",\"e\":\"Anterior rib fractures are uncommon in a child and should raise the suspicion of non- accidental injury, however with the appropriate history of trauma this may be accidental. In non-accidental injury it is classically the postero-medial ribs that fracture.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Club foot deformity\",\"e\":\"Club foot deformity is a congenital deformity in which one or both feet points down and inwards, not associated with any injury.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Femoral shaft fracture\",\"e\":\"Red flags of child abuse include long bone fracture in a child who is not yet walking, multiple fractures in various stages of healing, corner fractures, posterior rib fractures and transphyseal separation of the distal humerus. Children suspected of being abused should be admitted to hospital and appropriate child protection professionals informed.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hip dislocation\",\"e\":\"Hip dislocation in a child who is not yet walking is not usually a presentation of child abuse. It could represent a condition such as developmental dysplasia of the hip in which the acetabulum is too shallow and the femoral head is not held tightly in place making it more prone to dislocation\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Bruising over both knees\",\"e\":\"Most accidental bruises are seen on the front of the body, particularly over bony prominences and bruising over the knees may be expected\"}]},{\"t\":39,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"A 7-year-old boy is brought in after a severe road traffic accident. He sustained a blunt abdominal injury, a fractured pelvis and a left haemopneumothorax. He is accompanied by his father who is unscathed. The family are Jehovah's witnesses. The child is in grade IV shock. The father states he would rather see his child die than receive a blood transfusion.\\n\\nWhat is the correct management of this child?\",\"lead\":\"What is the correct management of this child?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Accept and respect the father's wishes to avoid transfusion\",\"e\":\"According to the Children's Act 2005 the best interests of a child are paramount in all decisions regarding children. In cases with a chiid younger than 12 or older children that lack capacity, medical treatment or surgery cannot be performed without parent or guardian consent, unless for life-saving treatment when the doctor can act in the best interests of the child and in this case administer the blood transfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Discuss the situation with the father but manage the child using ABC principles and transfuse the child with 'Group O negative' blood while you await the results of a group and save\",\"e\":\"It would be appropriate to discuss and inform the parents but their permission is not required in accordance with the Children's Act 2005. For life-saving treatment the doctor must act in the best interests of the child and in this case administer the blood transfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Manage the child according to ABC first principles and use a non-blood product for resuscitation, while seeking urgent legal advice to make the child a ward of court.\",\"e\":\"In any emergency setting the patient should be managed using an ABC approach. In this case the child is in grade IV shock which requires immediate blood products for life-saving treatment. Following the Children's Act of 2005 it would not be necessary to make the child a ward of the court to administer life-saving treatment and in these scenarios where the child is less than 12 or older but lacking capacity the doctor must act in the best interests of\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Telephone the elders of the father's community to schedule a meeting with the father to discuss the situation further\",\"e\":\"In an elective setting if the parents wish this may be the best way to proceed to better understand the family beliefs and what if anything they would accept. However, for life- saving treatment in children under 12 or older children who lack capacity the parents or guardian cannot refuse and the doctor must act in the best interests of the child, in this case they should administer the blood transfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Telephone the on-call Trust barrister to try to make the child a ward of the court\",\"e\":\"Before the Children's Act of 2005 a court order was required to authorise a life-saving blood transfusion if the parents refused. Now, for life-saving treatment in children under the age of 12 or older children that lack capacity the parents cannot refuse a blood transfusion and the doctor should act in the best interests of the child.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman who is 36 weeks' pregnant is rushed to theatre for placental abruption. Following a C-section she is bleeding heavily around the uterus with oozing from suture sites. The anaesthetist notes bleeding from her cannula sites. Bloods show a prolongation of all clotting times, decreased platelet count and increased fibrin degradation products (FDP). Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome\",\"e\":\"Antiphospholipid syndrome is an autoimmune condition that predisposes to thrombosis and would not present at this point with increased bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)\",\"e\":\"DIC is characterised by the pathological activation of the coagulation pathway leading to clot formation. This clotting consumes coagulation factors and platelets resulting in subsequent bleeding. Common causes of DIC include sepsis, malignancy (especially adenocarcinoma), trauma and obstetric emergencies. Laboratory features include prolongation of all clotting times and a progressively falling platelet count and fibrinogen concentration. Increasing fibrin degradation products (FDP) indicate activation of the fibrinolytic system and, in the context of the other laboratory abnormalities, support a diagnosis of DIC. Treatment involves intensive blood-product support with correction of the underlying cause.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor V Leiden disease\",\"e\":\"Factor V Leiden causes thrombophilia and would not present as excessive bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Haemophilia A\",\"e\":\"An inherited bleeding disorder would not likely suddenly worsen in this context. This scenario is classic for DIC\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Haemophilia B\",\"e\":\"An inherited bleeding disorder would not likely suddenly worsen in this context. This scenario is classic for DIC.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old woman who was admitted for elective subtotal thyroidectomy gave a history of a bleeding disorder. Pre-operative evaluation showed a prolonged thrombin clotting time.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause for this abnormality?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause for this abnormality?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor VIlI deficiency .. C\",\"e\":\"Factor VIII deficiency (haemophilia A) would increase the activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT), but not the thrombin time (TT).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Factor XII deficiency\",\"e\":\"Factor XII deficiency would increase the APTT, but not the TT.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypofibrinogenaemia\",\"e\":\"The thrombin clotting time (TCT), also known as the thrombin time (TT), is a coagulation assay that is usually performed to assess fibrin formation. A prolonged TT is due to either fibrin abnormalities, impairment of formation, or due to inhibition of thrombin, eg heparin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Willebrand factor (vWF) deficiency\",\"e\":\"vWF deficiency would increase the APTT, but not the TT.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Warfarin therapy\",\"e\":\"Warfarin, which is obviously not a bleeding disorder (which the question asks for), would increase the prothrombin time (PT), but not the TT.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 27-year-old man has a deficiency of a coagulation factor that forms a complex with tissue factor (TF) to activate factors IX and X\\n\\nWhich one of the following coagulation factors is most likely to be deficient in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following coagulation factors is most likely to be deficient in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Prothrombin\",\"e\":\"Prothrombin does not form a complex with TF.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AiVI Fibrinogen\",\"e\":\"Fibrinogen does not form a complex with TF.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor VII\",\"e\":\"Factor VIl (formerly known as proconvertin) is one of the central proteins in the coagulation cascade. It is serine protease enzyme. The main role of factor VII (FVII) is to initiate the process of coagulation in conjunction with TF. On vessel injury, TF is exposed to the blood and circulating factor VII. Once bound to TF, FVIl is activated to FVIla by different proteases, among which are thrombin (factor lla), activated factor X and the FVlla-TF complex itself.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"ARL Factor XI\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor XII\",\"e\":\"Factor Xi does not form a complex with TF.\\n\\nFactor XII does not formia complex with TF\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old woman who was commenced on warfarin following mechanical aortic valve replacement for a congenital bicuspid valve has developed skin necrosis.\\n\\nDeficiency of which one of the following proteins could be responsible for this disorder?\",\"lead\":\"Deficiency of which one of the following proteins could be responsible for this disorder?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Heparin cofactor I\",\"e\":\"The likely cause is warfarin necrosis due to protein C deficiency. Heparin cofactor II deficiency would not cause this.\\n\\nThe likely cause is warfarin necrosis due to protein C deficiency. Plasmin deficiency would not cause this, but instead causes pseudomembranes to form.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AMiL Plasmin\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Protein C\",\"e\":\"Warfarin necrosis is an acquired protein C deficiency due to treatment with the vitamin K- inhibitor anticoagulant, warfarin. It is a feared (but rare) complication of warfarin treatment. This rare reaction, mainly in women, usually occurs between the second and fifth days of therapy with warfarin. Lesions are sharply demarcated, erythaematous, indurated and purpuric and can either resolve or progress to form large, irregular, haemorrhagic bullae, with eventual necrosis and slow-healing eschar formation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Protein S\",\"e\":\"The likely cause is warfarin necrosis due to protein C deficiency. Protein S deficiency would not cause this.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Protein Z\",\"e\":\"The likely cause is warfarin necrosis due to protein C deficiency. Protein Z deficiency would not cause this:\\n\\n4454(\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old woman who bled excessively after a therapeutic abortion was diagnosed as having a rare coagulation defect characterised by deficiency of an enzyme of the coagulation cascade that cross-links fibrin.\\n\\nWhich one of the following factors was deficient in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following factors was deficient in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor I\",\"e\":\"Factor II (prothrombin) does not cross-link fibrin. Factor lla (thrombin) converts fibrinogen to fibrin monomers.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Factor VII\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor VIII\",\"e\":\"Factor VII does not cross-link fibrin. When in combination with tissue factor, it initiates the extrinsic coagulation pathway.\\n\\nFactor VIlI does not cross-link fibrin. It is a component of the intrinsic coagulation pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Factor XII\",\"e\":\"Factor XII does not cross-link fibrin. It is a component of the intrinsic coagulation pathway.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor XIII\",\"e\":\"Factor XIII (FXIII), or 'fibrin stabilising factor' cross-links fibrin. Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin, to form a proteinaceous network. Factor XIll is activated by thrombin into factor Xilla, (requiring calcium as a cofactor) which then cross-links fibrin fibres to form an insoluble clot.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"4 39-year-oid builder fell off a ladder sustaining bilateral femoral fracture. His massive bleeding resulted in disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC).\\n\\nWhat should this patient be treated with?\",\"lead\":\"What should this patient be treated with?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antibiotics\",\"e\":\"Antibiotics may treat an infective process contributing towards DIC, but will not treat DIC in the short term\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anticoagulants\",\"e\":\"Anticoagulation will make the situation worse. In DIC, there is already an insufficiency of clotting.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) and platelet transfusion\",\"e\":\"Treatment may require intensive blood-product support while the underiying cause is corrected. This includes the administration of FFP (or cryoprecipitate) and platelets to replace deficiencies.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intravenous saline infusion\",\"e\":\"This will not treat the process of DIC.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thrombin infusion\",\"e\":\"A thrombin infusion may improve coagulation somewhat, but alone is not sufficient.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man is admitted with an extradural haemorrhage. They are known to be on a anticoagulant and the neurosurgeons recommend this be reversed as soon as possible.\\n\\nProthrombin complex concentrate (PCC) is used as a reversal agent for which anticoagulant?\",\"lead\":\"Prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC) is used as a reversal agent for which anticoagulant?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aspirin\",\"e\":\"Aspirin cannot be reversed by PCC. Aspirin binds irreversibly to platelets. If emergency surgery is required, a platelet transfusion may be warranted.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Fondaparinux\",\"e\":\"PCC is not effective in the reversal of fondaparinux.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Low-molecular-weight heparin\",\"e\":\"Low-molecular-weight heparin (eg enoxaparin or dalteparin) is not reversed by PCC. There is some evidence that protamine can be used to reverse the effects of low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Unfractioned heparin\",\"e\":\"Unfractionated heparin is not reversed by PCC, but rather protamine sulphate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Warfarin\",\"e\":\"PCC (eg Octaplex) is licensed for warfarin reversal. It is derived from human plasma (non- UK) and contains the clotting factors II, VII, IX and X. PCC provides immediate reversal of warfarin but the effect will begin to wear off after 6-12 h. Vitamin K can be used if the reversal of warfarin is less urgent.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"An 84-year-old woman, who was initially admitted and treated for a neck of femur fracture, remains an in-patient 3 weeks later as she cannot be safely discharged. The night FY1 sees the patient and concludes that she has developed a deep vein thrombosis (DVT). Which one of the following features is not a component of Wells' score for DVT?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following features is not a component of Wells' score for DVT?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Calf swelling compared with the contralateral leg\",\"e\":\"Calf swelling >3 cm compared with the non-affected leg is a component of Wells' score.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Being bedridden >3 days\",\"e\":\"Being bedridden or immobilised increases the risk of venous stasis and thrombosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Raised D-dimer\",\"e\":\"Patients with a low Wells' score should have a D-dimer test to further investigate for DVT, but the D-dimer value is not an initial component of the score.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An alternative diagnosis is more likely\",\"e\":\"The likelihood of another diagnosis (based on clinical judgement) is a component of the Wells' score for DVT.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Previously documented DVT\",\"e\":\"Patients who have previously had a DVT have a 50 times higher risk of DVT compared with those who have not.\\n\\nWells' score for deep vein thrombosis (DVT)\\n\\nWells PS, Anderson DR, Rodger M, Forgie M, Kearon C, Dreyer J, Kovacs G, Mitchell M, Lewandowski B, Kovacs MJ. Evaluation of D-dimer in the diagnosis of suspected deep vein thrombosis. N Eng| J Med. 2003 Sep 25;349(13):1227-35.\\n\\nFahrni J, Husmann M, Gretener SB, Keo HH. Assessing the risk of recurrent venous thromboembolism--a practical approach. Vasc Health Risk Manag. 2015 Aug 17;11:451-9.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old woman undergoes a dynamic hip screw fixation for a neck of femur fracture. Postoperatively she develops shortness of breath and chest pain. A computed tomography pulmonary angiography (CTPA) reveals a pulmonary embolus.\\n\\nWhich one of the following clinical signs would most commonly be seen in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following clinical signs would most commonly be seen in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A rise in pulmonary artery pressure\",\"e\":\"Occlusion of branches of the pulmonary artery can cause pulmonary artery pressure to rise. However, this presentation is not the most common.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Altered respiratory rate\",\"e\":\"The commonest symptom of pulmonary embolism is the sudden onset of dyspnoea, manifesting as an altered respiratory rate (usually a tachypnea).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"If a large embolus causes shock then a metabolic acidosis may supervene. However, this presentation is not the most common.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Bradycardia\",\"e\":\"The clinical presentation of a pulmonary embolus is often associated with a tachycardia rather than a bradycardia. Other common clinical signs include a low-grade fever, and peripheral vascular collapse.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Respiratory alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Increased ventilation of dead spaces formed as a result of embolisation will lead to tachypnoea and 'blowing off' of carbon dioxide. Hypocarbia and respiratory alkalosis may therefore occur. However, this presentation is not the most common.\\n\\nWells' score for pulmonary embolism (PE): Wells PS, Anderson DR, Rodger M, Stiell I, Dreyer JF, Barnes D, Forgie M, Kovacs G, Ward J. Kovacs MJ. Excluding pulmonary embolism at the bedside without diagnostic imaging: management of patients with suspected pulmonary embolism presenting to the emergency department by using a simple clinical model and D-dimer. Ann Intern Med 2001 Jul 17;135(2):98-107. PubMed PMID: 11453709.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old woman presents with acute shortness of breath. She has recently been diagnosed with metastatic colorectal cancer. The cause is suspected to be thromboembolism to the pulmonary tree.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is associated with this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is associated with this condition?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Tented T-waves on an electrocardiogram (ECG)\",\"e\":\"Tented T-waves are more suggestive of hyperkalaemia. ECG changes associated with pulmonary emboli (PE) include the S1Q3T3 (McGinn-White) pattern (seen in 10% of patients with massive PE. Other non-specific changes on the ECG include tachycardia, RBBB, atrial fibrillation (AF), atrial flutter or right axis deviation. Right atrial dilatation may lead to prominent P-waves in the inferior leads. The most common abnormality, however, is sinus tachycardia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Central chest pain\",\"e\":\"Pleuritic chest pain is more common.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left heart strain\",\"e\":\"Right heart strain is more common.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"V/Q overlay on radio-isotope lung scan\",\"e\":\"A mismatch rather than an overlay is more suggestive of a PE.\\n\\nWells' score for PE:\\n\\nWells PS, Anderson DR, Rodger M, Stiell 1, Dreyer JF, Barnes D, Forgie M, Kovacs G, Ward J. Kovacs MJ. Excluding pulmonary embolism at the bedside without diagnostic imaging management of patients with suspected pulmonary embolism presenting to the emergency department by using a simple clinical model and D-dimer: Ann Intern Med. 2001 Jul 17;135(2):98-107. PubMed PMID: 11453709\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Gallop rhythm\",\"e\":\"Pleural pain, tachypnoea, loud pulmonary second sound, S3 or S4 gallop sounds and cyanosis may be present.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old man undergoes pinning of his femur following a road traffic accident (RTA). On his third post-operative day he has difficulty breathing with reduced oxygen saturations and chest pain. He is diagnosed with a fat embolism.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding this diagnosis is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding this diagnosis is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Does not occur commonly in patients with long bone fractures\",\"e\":\"Fat embolisms occur most commonly following fractures (up to 90% of cases), mainly long bones.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fat emboli do not disseminate widely and have local effects\",\"e\":\"Fat emboli often have widespread dissemination, passing through the pulmonary vessels and into the systemic circulation, where they may become impacted in the capillaries of the brain, kidneys, skin, and other organs.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is associated with retinal petechiae\",\"e\":\"Retinal petechiae are found in half of cases.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased haematocrit occurs within the first few days as a result of intra-alveolar haemorrhage\",\"e\":\"Haematocrit is reduced in the first few days.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The most effective prophylactic measure is early mobilisation\",\"e\":\"Early reduction of a fracture is the most effective prophylactic measure.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient who has been admitted with major limb and chest trauma after a motorcycle accident. He begins to bleed from the cannula sites and you send an urgent blood screen, suspecting disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). Which one of the following is correct regarding this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding this condition?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is associated with decreased fibrinolysis\",\"e\":\"It is associated with increased fibrinolysis\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is best managed with whole blood transfusion\",\"e\":\"The mainstay of care is treatment of the underlying cause, replacement of the deficient factors with fresh-frozen plasma, cryoprecipitate and platelets.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is confirmed by elevated fibrin degradation products\",\"e\":\"Increased fibrinolysis is indicated by rising concentrations of fibrin degradation products or D-dimers.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It only occurs after major trauma\",\"e\":\"Common causes include sepsis, malignancy (especially adenocarcinoma), trauma and obstetric emergencies (placenta abruption, amniotic fluid embolism).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It praduces a characteristic raised platelet count\",\"e\":\"Thrombocytopenia (low platelet count) would be more characteristic.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"You are looking after an elderly patient with protein C deficiency, a congenital prothrombotic disorder and are aware that this condition carries an increased risk of deep vein thrombosis (DVT). You note they are on long term anticoagulation therapy.\\n\\nWhat is the reason for this increased risk?\",\"lead\":\"What is the reason for this increased risk?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It causes intimal injury\",\"e\":\"Intimal injury would increase the risk of thrombosis, but this is not a feature of protein C deficiency.\\n\\nIncreased endothelial damage would increase the risk of thrombosis, but this is not a feature of protein C deficiency.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased endothelial damage\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased venous stasis\",\"e\":\"Increased venous stasis would increase the risk of thrombosis, but this is not a feature of protein C deficiency.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"increased venous blood flow\",\"e\":\"A decrease in venous blood flow (venous stasis) would increase the risk of thrombosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypercoagulable state\",\"e\":\"Protein C inhibits the coagulation system by primarily inactivating FVa and FVilla. Deficiency of protein C therefore creates a prothrombotic environment.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"You are consenting a 58-year-old woman about to undergo surgery for ovarian cancer. You ensure that VTE prophylaxis is on the drug chart for their post-operative recovery. Which one of the following is the most common 'acquired risk factor' increasing the risk of perioperative venous thrombosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most common 'acquired risk factor' increasing the risk of perioperative venous thrombosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adenocarcinoma of the ovary\",\"e\":\"Malignancy is the most common 'acquired cause of deep venous thrombosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Antithrombin III deficiency\",\"e\":\"Antithrombin III deficiency is not an acquired state.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Protein C deficiency\",\"e\":\"Protein C deficiency is not an acquired state\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Protein S deficiency.\",\"e\":\"Protein S deficiency is not an acquired state.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sickle-cell disease\",\"e\":\"Sickle-cell disease is not an acquired state.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A 39-year-old patient presents to hospital with a painful tender calf having been on a long- haul flight. Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is suspected. Which one of the following statements regarding risk factors is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding risk factors is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The risk decreases with age\",\"e\":\"Risk increases with age.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The risk is higher in a patient with a body mass index (BMI) <35\",\"e\":\"The risk is higher in obese patients.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The risk is higher in patients homozygous for factor V Leiden\",\"e\":\"Thrombophilias such as factor V Leiden also increases risk of DVT, those being homozygous being at greater risk than those who are heterozygous.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The risk is reduced by using general anaesthesia rather than epidural anaesthesia\",\"e\":\"A GA would more likely increase the risk.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The risk is reduced in the presence of malignancy\",\"e\":\"The risk is increased in the presence of malignancy. Wells' score for DVT: Patients with a score of less than two were considered unlikely, and those with a score of two or more were considered likely, to have deep-vein thrombosis.\\n\\nWell's Criteria\\n\\nActive cancer treatment or palliation within 6 months\\n\\nNo O\\n\\nYes+1\\n\\nBedridden recently >3 days or major surgery within 12 weeks\\n\\nNo O\\n\\nYes+1\\n\\nCalf swelling >3 cm compared to the other leg measured 10 cm below tibial tuberosity\\n\\nNo O\\n\\nYes+1\\n\\nCollateral (non varicose) superficial veins present\\n\\nNo o\\n\\nYes+1\\n\\nEntire leg swollen No O\\n\\nYes+1\\n\\nNo U\\n\\nYes+1\\n\\nLocalized tenderness along the deep venous system\\n\\nNo O\\n\\nYes+1 Pitting oedema, confined to symptomatic leg No O\\n\\nYes+1\\n\\nParalysis, paresis, or recent plaster immobilization of the lower extremity\\n\\nNo O Yes+1 Previously documented DVT\\n\\nNo O Yes+1\\n\\nAlternative diagnosis to DVT as likely or more likely\\n\\nNo O\\n\\nYes -2 DVT can be safely ruled out in patients who are deemed to be unlikely to have a DVT and have a negative DDimer. Wells PS, Anderson DR, Rodger M, Forgie M, Kearon C, Dreyer J, Kovacs G, Mitchell M, Lewandowski B, Kovacs MJ. Evaluation of D-dimer in the diagnosis of suspected deep-vein thrombosis. N Engl J Med. 2003 Sep 25;349(13):1227-35.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man undergoes an emergency abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) repair. The operation is more difficult than usual due to abdominal adhesions and is prolonged. The anaesthetist comments that the patient is more unstable and thinks he is going into disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC).\\n\\nThis condition is most commonly associated with which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This condition is most commonly associated with which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased D dimer\",\"e\":\"It is associated with an increased D-dimer level.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased coagulation times\",\"e\":\"It is associated with increased coagulation times.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"High levels of fibrin degradation products\",\"e\":\"It is associated with high levels of fibrin degradation products. This is a very sensitive marker, but is not specific.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thrombocythaemia\",\"e\":\"It is associated with low platelet counts (thrombocytopaenia).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduction in APTT\",\"e\":\"It is associated with an increase in partial thromboplastin time (PTT) and activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT).\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old man is referred by the Emergency Department with right lower limb swelling and chest pain on deep inspiration. D-dimer is positive. His condition occurs as a result of Virchow's triad\\n\\nWhich one feature below is typical of Virchow's triad?\",\"lead\":\"Which one feature below is typical of Virchow's triad?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Previous deep vein thrombosis (DVT)\",\"e\":\"Consideration of previous DVT events is not part of the triad, though a previous DVT increased the risk of subsequent DVT, and is utilised in multiple clinical risk assessment tools.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Endothelial injury\",\"e\":\"This patient presenting with leg swelling and associated pleuritic chest pain is likely to have a pulmonary embolism secondary to a deep vein thrombosis of his right leg. Virchow's triad refers to the three circumstances required for venous thromboembolism to occur: hypercoagulability, venous stasis, and endothelial injury. It refers specifically to the pathophysiology of DVT development. Remembering the triad can help to identify risk factors of DVT in surgical patients, and understand how to reduce the risk. For example, venous stasis can occur during prolonged operations or hospital stays, and pneumatic compression devices can reduce this. Hypercoagulability can occur due to dehydration, trauma, or sepsis, and endothelial injury can occur due to inflammation, trauma, or activated platelets due to haemorrhage. Fluid resuscitation, and chemothromboprophylaxis can be utilised to minimise this risk.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased risk of gallstores\",\"e\":\"Venous thromboembolism, and Virchow's triad, are unrelated to the pathophysiology of gallstones.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is associated with muffled heart sounds\",\"e\":\"Muffled heart sounds can be a sign of cardiac tamponade, though this is unrelated to venous thromboembolism, and this is not a feature of Virchow's triad.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is associated with lower limb cellulitis\",\"e\":\"This is not a component of Virchow's triad, though reduced motion in the context of lower limb infection could theoretically lead to venous stasis, a component of the triad.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"You are called to see a 23-year-old man following a fall that had resulted in a long bone fracture. He has developed a fat embolism.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true regarding fat embolisms?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding fat embolisms?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is related to obesity\",\"e\":\"Fat embolism is not related to obesity.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Delays fracture union\",\"e\":\"There is no known association between fat embolism and delayed fracture union.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Results in a petechial rash in the retinae only\",\"e\":\"A petechial rash can be seen on the trunk, in the axillae and in the conjunctiva.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Causes confusion\",\"e\":\"Hypoxaemia is a consistent finding and may cause confusion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is characterised by fat seen in urine\",\"e\":\"It is characterised by fat in the blood. Urine fat stains are not sensitive or specific enough to diagnose a fat embolism.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A deep-sea diver loses consciousness soon after resurfacing. A diagnosis of air embolus is made.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding air embolus?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding air embolus?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Open-heart surgery may lead to air embolism\",\"e\":\"Air embolism is a well recognised cause of death in deep-sea divers. The other important causes include laparoscopic surgery; open-heart surgery, insertion of central venous catheter, injury to the chest wall, arterial or venous surgery and massive blood transfusions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Death is caused by air trapped in the left ventricular outflow tract\",\"e\":\"Death is caused by air being trapped in the right ventricular outflow tract.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"May lead to hypertension S\",\"e\":\"Hypotension is more typical. The other clinical features include: pulmonary hypertension, joint pain, visual disturbance, hypoxia, apnoea and arrhythmia. A large air embolism affecting the cerebral circulation arteries will cause immediate loss of consciousness and convulsions.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Accidental intravenous injection of 2-3 ml of air is frequently fatal\",\"e\":\"Accidental injection of a few millilitres of air is usually absorbed by the body without any serious effects. However, injections of 5 ml/kg of air can be fatal.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The cause of death is usually rupture of the pulmonary vessels\",\"e\":\"The usual cause of death in air embolism is from circulatory obstruction and cardiovascular collapse.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man who is being managed in the intensive care unit (ICU), following multiple fractures sustained in a high speed road traffic accident, is noted to have experienced a drop in his oxygen saturation level seven days after admission. It is felt he is becoming more difficult to ventilate and, following investigations, a diagnosis of fat embolus syndrome is made\\n\\nWhich one of the following features can be associated with this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following features can be associated with this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bradycardia\",\"e\":\"An early sign of fat embolus syndrome is a slight rise in heart rate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperpyrexia\",\"e\":\"An early sign of fat embolus syndrome is a slight rise in temperature, but the temperature does not usually rise over 39°C.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypothermia\",\"e\":\"Hyperthermia is more likely.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Petechiae\",\"e\":\"The characteristic rash over thorax, neck, soft palate and conjunctiva is petechial, and caused by microinfarction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pleural effusions\",\"e\":\"Pleural effusions due to fat embolism are not typical.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old patient develops acute shortness of breath with chest pain on inspiration 12 hours after a femoral nail. A pulmonary embolism is suspected.\\n\\nWhich one of the following features would be most suggestive of this diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following features would be most suggestive of this diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Central cyanosis\",\"e\":\"In massive pulmonary embolism, the patient may have acute circulatory failure with cyanosis, hypotension and a raised jugular venous pressure (JVP). Acute pulmonary embolism peaks in incidence between t2 and 10 days post-operatively. Although post-operative respiratory distress is a common occurrence, it is more commonly caused by basal atelectasis, pneumonia or aspiration. Orthopaedic operations such as femoral nailing can give rise to fat embolism syndrome, which can have a similar presentation, though is usually associated with skin petechiae, which are absent in this case. Nevertheless, pulmonary embolism remains an important differential diagnosis and reliance on non-specific investigations, such as d-dimer or characteristic ECG changes may lead to the condition being overlooked.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"1M. A high D-dimer value\",\"e\":\"D-dimer is a fibrin-degradation product (FDP) which is formed in the body anytime a clot is broken down by the fibrinolytic system. Although a raised D-dimer value can be used to aid diagnosis, it is not specific to pulmonary embolism, and can be raised in multiple other conditions, including trauma, aortic dissection, and pneumonia. A d-dimer is always raised after any operation, due to the degree of bleeding, coagulation, and trauma caused by surgery. It is therefore not routinely checked in the immediate post-operative phase.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypertension\",\"e\":\"Hypertension in a young patient would be rare. In the case of a pulmonary embolism with right-sided cardiac strain, hypotension is more common.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Low jugular venous pressure (JVP)\",\"e\":\"A pulmonary embolism leads to increased resistance in the pulmonary vasculature, leading to increased afterload on the right ventricle, If this is significant, this can lead to right heart failure, which would cause a raised JVP\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A Q-wave and inverted T-wave in lead VI on the electrocardiogram (ECG)\",\"e\":\"The most common ECG abnormality is a sinus tachycardia. The rarer but 'classical' description of the McGinn-White sign is an S-wave in lead I and a Q-wave and inverted T- wave in lead III. It is important to note that the McGinn-White sign is not pathognomonic for pulmonary embolism, and can occur in any condition leading to right heart strain.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":54,\"stem\":\"You are called to an orthopaedic ward as part of the cardiac arrest team. An elderly patient appears acutely short of breath having undergone recent surgery.\\n\\nPulmonary emboli are most likely to result from which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Pulmonary emboli are most likely to result from which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left atrial myxoma\",\"e\":\"Atrial myxomas are rare, benign tumours and are more common in the left atrium so giving rise to arterial circulation emboli.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Circumflex territory myocardial infarction\",\"e\":\"Myocardial infarction involving the right ventricle may lead to mural thrombus that could emboli to the pulmonary system. The circumflex artery, however, supplies the left atrium and ventricle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fractured femur\",\"e\":\"Fat emboli, a subtype of pulmonary embolism, occur from long bone fractures. They are small and multiple, causing widespread effects. Fat emboli may be caused by orthopaedic procedures breaching the medullary cavity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Mitral stenosis 1\",\"e\":\"The mitral valve is between the left atrium and left ventricle of the heart. An embolus from this valve would not embolise to the lungs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rhabdomyolysis\",\"e\":\"Rhabdomyolysis is the release of myoglobin and other muscle-derived products into the blood following muscle injury. It causes renal failure, but does not typically affect the pulmonary system.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-oid patient suffered recurrent deep vein thromboses and also one pulmonary embolism. She was extensively investigated and diagnosed with protein C deficiency.\\n\\nWhat pathological process is most likely to be responsible for her venous thromboembolisms?\",\"lead\":\"What pathological process is most likely to be responsible for her venous thromboembolisms?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Reduced degradation of factors Va and Villa\",\"e\":\"Protein C acts to inactivate the active forms of the procoagulant cofactors, factors Va and Villa. Protein C deficiency impairs this process.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Reduced factor xa complex\",\"e\":\"Protein C deficiency does not affect factor Xa complex.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Reduced inhibition of tissue-factor expression\",\"e\":\"Protein C deficiency does not affect tissue-factor expression.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduced protein S\",\"e\":\"Protein C deficiency does not affect protein S.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduced synthesis of antithrombin III\",\"e\":\"Protein C deficiency does not affect antithrombin III.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted with brisk PR bleeding and a low haemoglobin on the venous blood gas. Which one of the following accurately describes bleeding time?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following accurately describes bleeding time?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The time taken for blood to clot in a glass test tube\",\"e\":\"Bleeding time is assessed by making a superficial incision, often in the forearm or the earlobe.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The time taken for visible bleeding from a puncture wound to stop\",\"e\":\"Time is measured between making a wound and the bleeding stopping. Bleeding time is usually between 1 min and 9 min and is a test of platelet function. Bleeding time is prolonged in the presence of any platelet disorder.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The time taken for bleeding to commence from a wound\",\"e\":\"Time is measured between making a wound and the bleeding stopping.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The time taken for a bleeding wound to form a visible clot\",\"e\":\"Time is measured between making a wound and the bleeding stopping. A clot does not necessarily need to be seen.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The time taken for a clot to dissolve and bleeding to recommence\",\"e\":\"lime is measured between making a wound and the bleeding stopping. Recommencing of bleeding is not required to be seen.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man on warfarin for a mechanical mitral valve was admitted in the Accident and Emergency Department with persistent bleeding following dental extraction. He was told that his coagulation was deranged.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements about blood coagulation is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements about blood coagulation is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Absence of Cart promotes blood coagulation\",\"e\":\"Cat is a necessary cofactor for blood coagulation and chelation of Ca?+ ions by citrate inhibits coagulation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) results in depletion of fibrin degradation products\",\"e\":\"DIC results in depletion of coagulation factors and accumulation of fibrin degradation products.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Patients with haemophilia A usually have a normal bleeding time\",\"e\":\"Patients with haemophilia A or B (ie absence of factors VIII or IX, respectively) have a prolonged partial thromboplastin time (PTT), but do not have a prolonged bleeding time. The bleeding time assesses platelet function.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Willebrand factor suppresses platelet adhesion\",\"e\":\"von Willebrand factor is part of the factor VIII complex and also promotes platelet adherence to the vascular subendothelium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Willebrand factor suppresses blood coagulation\",\"e\":\"von Willebrand factor promotes haemostasis. Patients with von Willebrand's disease have both a prolonged PTT and a prolonged bleeding time\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"The coagulation factors are generally serine proteases. However, there are some exceptions.\\n\\nWhich one of the following coagulation factors is not a serine protease?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following coagulation factors is not a serine protease?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor XI\",\"e\":\"Factor Xl is a serine protease.\\n\\nFactor Il is a serine protease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Factor II\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor IX\",\"e\":\"Factor IX is a serine protease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"2' Factor XII\",\"e\":\"Factor XII is a serine protease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor XIII\",\"e\":\"Factor XIII is a transglutaminase.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old woman is awaiting a right-sided groin dissection for metastatic spread of melanoma. During her perioperative investigations she is noted to have a deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in her right leg. What should happen next in this case?\",\"lead\":\"What should happen next in this case?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cancel surgery and monitor her progress with three-monthly (CT) scans\",\"e\":\"Delaying her surgery will almost certainly lead to widespread metastatic spread of her cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Continue surgery, keep her on warfarin\",\"e\":\"Warfarin is not used in the perioperative phase since it has a long-half-life and it can be difficult to control the therapeutic level.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Continue surgery, admit to hospital and start her on intravenous heparin\",\"e\":\"Intravenous heparin has a short half-life and can be stopped before surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Postpone surgery until she has been warfarinised for 3 months\",\"e\":\"Delaying her surgery will almost certainly lead to widespread metastatic spread of her cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Postpone surgery until she has been warfarinised for 6 months\",\"e\":\"Delaying her surgery will almost certainiy lead to widespread metastatic spread of her cancer.\\n\\nTAC:\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"You are doing the post-surgery ward round and your consultant suggests giving overweight, immobile patients, aspirin to 'thin the blood'. You do not think this suggestion follows local guidelines. Regarding low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It cannot be used to treat pulmonary embolus\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It has a shorter half-life than unfractionated heparin\",\"e\":\"LMWH has a longer half-life and more predictable bioavailability than unfractionated heparin, and therefore can be administered subcutaneously, once daily and requires monitoring only if used long term or if anticoagulation control is critical.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It prolongs the activated partial thromboplastin time\",\"e\":\"The activated partial thromboplastin time is not prolonged and when monitoring is required, it is with the anti-Xa assay.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is not effective in thromboprophylaxis\",\"e\":\"It is at least as effective, and may be superior to unfractionated heparin in some patient groups in perioperative thromboprophylaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It can be used in pregnancy\",\"e\":\"LMWH is safe and has revolutionised long-term anticoagulation in pregnancy.\\n\\nLMWH can indeed be used to treat pulmonary embolus. A higher 'therapeutic' dose is given than compared with the prophylactic dose.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old man presents with a painful swollen right calf 14 days following an intramedullary tibial nail for a closed midshaft tibial fracture. The calf circumference is 3.5 cm greater on this side. What is the gold standard investigation for diagnosing a deep vein thrombosis (DVT)?\",\"lead\":\"What is the gold standard investigation for diagnosing a deep vein thrombosis (DVT)?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Computed tomography pulmonary angiography (CTPA)\",\"e\":\"CTPA is an investigation for pulmonary embolism.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"D-dimer blood test\",\"e\":\"The D-dimer will be raised in the patient in the above scenario, as there are multiple factors that can lead to a raised D-dimer, such as trauma and surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Duplex venous ultrasonography\",\"e\":\"Doppler ultrasound is most commoniy used to radiologically detect DVT, but is not the gold standard.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lower limb arteriogram\",\"e\":\"An arteriogram will examine the arterial system, not the venous system.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lower limb venography\",\"e\":\"Venography remains the gold standard investigation for DVT but is used less commonly than Doppler ultrasound as it involves the use of contrast and it is invasive.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old woman presents to the anaesthetic pre-op clinic. She is on the waiting list for right knee replacement. She has read about deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in the newspaper and asks about the risk of this.\\n\\nWhich one of the following factors predisposes to DVT?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following factors predisposes to DVT?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Early mobilisation\",\"e\":\"Early mobilisation would reduce the risk of DVT.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Early discharge from hospital, S\",\"e\":\"Early discharge would be associated with mobility and would therefore reduce the risk of\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pre-operative dehydration\",\"e\":\"Dehydration may result in hypercoagulable blood, which predisposes a patient to DVT\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pneumatic calf compression\",\"e\":\"Pneumatic calf compression would help prevent DVT.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Short anaesthesia\",\"e\":\"A long anaesthesia would more likely increase the risk of DVT.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":73,\"stem\":\"A 19-year-old man with no previous medical history presents to the orthopaedic clinic with recurrent episodes of spontaneous haemarthrosis of his knee. Clotting studies confirm a normal International Normalised Ratio (INR) and platelet count, but a raised activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT). Which haematological disorder is most likely to be the cause?\",\"lead\":\"Which haematological disorder is most likely to be the cause?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Haemophilia\",\"e\":\"Haemophilia, types A and B, are X-linked recessive disorders due to the deficiency of factors VIII and IX respectively. There is a significant increase in the risk of spontaneous haemorrhage, for example haemarthrosis or intracranial bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Idiopathic trombocytopenic purpura (ITP)\",\"e\":\"ITP does not typically present as haemarthrosis, but rather petechiae, mucosal bleeding and easy bruising. APTT is normal in ITP.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sickle cell disease\",\"e\":\"Sickle cell disease does not typically cause haemarthrosis and does not typically show deranged coagulation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Willebrand disease\",\"e\":\"Von Willebrand's disease is the commonest inherited haemostatic disorder, but most cases show only a mild bleeding tendency, usually with epistaxis, easy bruising, menorrhagia and gingival bleeding.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Leukaemia\",\"e\":\"Recurrent haemarthrosis due to a coagulopathy is unlikely to be due to leukaemia in the absence of other symptoms or pancytopenia.\"}]},{\"t\":40,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old woman is undergoing varicose vein surgery for symptomatic varicose veins in the distribution of the long saphenous vein on the right lower limb. It is noted by the anaesthetist that her saturations have dropped and the end-tidal CO_ has fallen. After assessing the patient, it is suspected that the patient has suffered an air embolism.\\n\\nWhich one of the following options is not associated with venous air embolism?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is not associated with venous air embolism?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A mill wheel' murmur heard on auscultation\",\"e\":\"A 'mill wheel murmur' is a classic sign of intracardiac air: A splashing sound is heard.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypotension\",\"e\":\"Hypotension may occur in air embolism, but is not specific. Hypotension occurs in other embolism types - including thromboembolism and fat embolism.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Petechial rash seen on the chest and upper limbs\",\"e\":\"A petechial rash in this more suggestive of a fat embolism and is part of the 'fat embolism syndrome'\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypoxia\",\"e\":\"Respiratory failure and hypoxia does occur in severe air embolism.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A normal initial chest X-ray\",\"e\":\"In the first instance, the chest X-ray is typically normal. Rarely, air can be seen in the main pulmonary artery.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A patient with thrombocytopenia is going to undertake an elective major surgical operation that carries high bleeding risk.\\n\\nWhen using platelets for transfusion, of which one of the following should you be aware?\",\"lead\":\"When using platelets for transfusion, of which one of the following should you be aware?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Are suspended in plasma\",\"e\":\"Platelet concentrates are suspended in plasma and so have some ability to correct clotting factor abnormalities.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Are usually derived from a single donation\",\"e\":\"Most platelet preparations are supplied as a pool of platelets from about four individual donations\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Must be cross-matched with the patient before transfusion\",\"e\":\"Cross-matching of platelets is not required, although because of red-cell contamination, platelets should be ABO compatible with the recipient. Rhesus D-negative women should receive rhesus D-negative platelets to prevent alloimmunisation and prevent rhesus haemolytic disease of the newborn.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Must be used within 3 days of donation\",\"e\":\"Platelets must be administered within 5 days of collection.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Should be stored in the fridge\",\"e\":\"Platelets are stored in an agitator at room temperature.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"Blood donors are screened and tested for various diseases.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is one of the core tests recommended by the World Health Organisation (WHO)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is one of the core tests recommended by the World Health Organisation (WHO)?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antibody to HIV\",\"e\":\"All blood donations in the UK are now screened for hepatitis B surface antigen, hepatitis C virus DNA, HIV 1 and 2 antibodies and I reponema pallidum antıbody.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Malaria\",\"e\":\"Transfusion-borne human T-cell lymphotrophic virus (HTLV)-1/2, malaria, cytomegalovirus, parvovirus, hepatitis A and G and Trypanosoma cruzi (Chagas' disease) infection are recognised, although the incidence of contamination of blood products is reduced by donor selection and questionnaire exclusion. These tests are based or local epidemiological evidence.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The human T-cell lymphotrophic virus (HTLV)-1\",\"e\":\"Transfusion-borne human T-cell lymphotrophic virus (HTLV)-1/2, malaria, cytomegalovirus, parvovirus, hepatitis A and G and Trypanosoma cruzi (Chagas' disease) infection are recognised, although the incidence of contamination of blood products is reduced by donor selection and questionnaire exclusion. These tests are based or local epidemiological evidence.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"West Nile virus\",\"e\":\"West Nile virus is not screened for.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Yersinia\",\"e\":\"Bacterial contamination of blood with cold-tolerant Pseudomonas or Yersinia organisms very rarely causes life-threatening septicaemia in the recipient, but is not routinely screened for.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old motorcyclist arrived to Accident & Emergency following a head on collision with a tree. He is disorientated with tachycardia and hypotension. X-ray shows pelvic fracture. Massive blood transfusion protocol was activated.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a recognised sequelae of massive transfusion?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a recognised sequelae of massive transfusion?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Myocardial infarction\",\"e\":\"Myocardial infarction is uncommon, but cardiac arrhythmias can occur due to either hypothermia or electrolyte abnormalities.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Elevated factor VIII and factor V levels\",\"e\":\"The levels of the labile clotting factors V and VIll decrease rapidly with storage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypercalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypocalcaemia may result from the high citrate load in transfused blood.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hyperkalaemia\",\"e\":\"There is a risk of hyperkalaemia as potassium leaks from red cells during storage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypernatremia\",\"e\":\"Hypernatraemia is uncommon.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 61-year-old woman who is known to have had a transfusion reaction in the past is undergoing a major revision hip replacement with anticipated high volume of blood loss.\\n\\nAutologous transfusion of pre-collected blood has the advantage of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Autologous transfusion of pre-collected blood has the advantage of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Being a safe technique in all patients\",\"e\":\"The procedure still carries a small risk of transfusion reaction either through bacterial contamination of the donor units, or by receiving the wrong blood through clerical error.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Being able to supply up to 10 units of blood\",\"e\":\"The finite shelf life of stored blood limits the maximum collection to 4 units.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Carrying no risk of transfusion reaction\",\"e\":\"Unfortunately, clerical errors can still occur and therefore the risk is not eliminated.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cheapness\",\"e\":\"There is a considerable cost. This includes the service to accommodate weekly visits to donate blood, blood screening and individual blood storage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Minimising the risks of infection transmission\",\"e\":\"Autologous transfusion of pre-operatively donated blood is a useful technique which minimises the hazards of infection transmission and alloantibody stimulation.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in the preoperative assessment clinic before a major spinal operation.\\n\\nIn assessing a patient's needs for peri-operative red-cell transfusion, which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"In assessing a patient's needs for peri-operative red-cell transfusion, which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cardiac output increases sharply in healthy individuals if the haemoglobin falls below 10 g/dl\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output in healthy individuals only sharply increases if the haemoglobin (Hb) falls to below 7 g/dl.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Men tolerate haemodilution better than women\",\"e\":\"Women tolerate haemodilution better than men.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Patients with sickle-cell disease must be transfused to haemoglobin >12.5 g/dl\",\"e\":\"Transfusion of sickle-cell disease patients to 'normal range' Hb concentrations may increase the chance of vaso-occlusion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An elderly patient with a haemoglobin of 5.2 g/l should receive 8 units as quickly as possible\",\"e\":\"Patients with severe anaemias, especially in the elderly, that are well compensated should be transfused with extreme caution (1-2 units/day) to reduce the risk of provoking cardiac failure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Patients with ischaemic heart disease should be considered for transfusion if Hb < 80g/l\",\"e\":\"IHD patients have limited myocardial oxygen delivery and cannot adapt as readily to anaemia. Postoperative morbidity and mortality are significantly higher in patients with ischaemic heart disease than in those without hence most international surgical guidelines recommend a transfusion trigger of 70-80g/l.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old man is 1-day post-anterior resection for rectal cancer. His haemoglobin has dropped significantly and it is decided to transfuse him as he has a history of ischaemic heart disease. Shortly after the first unit of blood he is noted to be tachycardic, febrile and hypotensive. An acute haemolytic transfusion reaction is diagnosed.\\n\\nWhich one of the following options is the most common cause for this reaction?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is the most common cause for this reaction?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Administration of group A to group AB\",\"e\":\"Group AB is known as the universal recipient, therefore acute haemolytic transfusion reactions would be rare in these patients.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Administration of group O to group A\",\"e\":\"Group O blood is known as the universal donor, therefore it should not produce an acute haemolytic transfusion reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Administration of group O to group AB\",\"e\":\"Group O blood is known as the universal donor, therefore it should not produce an acute haemolytic transfusion reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Human error\",\"e\":\"Acute haemolytic transfusion reactions are the result of administration of ABO-incompatible blood: group A, B or AB to a group O recipient; group A or AB to a group B recipient; or group Bor AB to a group A recipient. Human error outside the transfusion laboratory is the commonest cause.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rhesus incompatibility\",\"e\":\"In this context, rhesus incompatibility is very unlikely to cause an acute haemolytic reaction.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient in the resuscitation unit and the massive blood transfusion protocol is instigated.\\n\\nWhich one of the following options is the definition for major haemorrhage?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is the definition for major haemorrhage?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Loss of 100% blood volume in 12\",\"e\":\"The correct criterion is loss of 100% blood volume in 24 h.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Loss of ≥50% of blood volume in th\",\"e\":\"The correct criterion is loss of >50% of blood volume in 3 h\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Loss of >50% of blood volume in 3\",\"e\":\"This is a correct definition of massive haemorrhage. The correct definitions are loss of >50% of blood volume in 3h, or loss of of blood volume in in h, or or>150 ml of blood loss per minute. The definition is made when any one of these criteria is met.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Loss of >50% of blood volume in 2\",\"e\":\"The correct criterion is loss of >50% of blood volume in 3 h\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Blood loss of >100mL / minute\",\"e\":\"The correct criterion is blood loss of >150 ml/min.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"You have donated a unit of blood. Locally to you there is no requirement for your blood type so it is stored and transferred to a different region where there is currently a shortage.\\n\\nWhich one of the following anticoagulants will the blood bank technician prefer for storage of your donated blood?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following anticoagulants will the blood bank technician prefer for storage of your donated blood?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Citrate\",\"e\":\"The citrate ion combines with calcium in the blood to form a unionised calcium compound. The lack of ionic calcium prevents coagulation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Coumarin\",\"e\":\"Citrate is the anticoagulant of choice for blood storage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Enoxaparin\",\"e\":\"Citrate is the anticoagulant of choice for blood storage.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Heparin\",\"e\":\"Citrate is the anticoagulant of choice for blood storage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Oxalate\",\"e\":\"Citrate is the anticoagulant of choice for blood storage.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"An 88-year-old woman undergoes incision and drainage of a back abscess, resulting in a 3 x 3 cm packed cavity on her back. She is anticoagulated and post-operatively experiences bleeding to the surgical site.\\n\\nHow should she be managed initially?\",\"lead\":\"How should she be managed initially?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Apply direct pressure for 30 min to the bleeding point\",\"e\":\"The quickest way to attempt to gain haemostatic control is to apply direct pressure which will contain the majority of venous bleeding points.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Apply more dressing gauze to the area\",\"e\":\"Applying more dressings will apply pressure, but is not as effective as applying manual, direct pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Nurse on back\",\"e\":\"Nursing the patient on their back would apply pressure, but direct application of pressure to the wound site would be more effective.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Return to theatre for haemostasis\",\"e\":\"If compression fails, a definitive measure (eg tying off a visible bleeding vessel, or cautery) can be considered.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Any of the above, they are all equally effective\",\"e\":\"While all of the answers are acceptable, direct compression should be attempted first.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A 62-year-old woman undergoing a cholecystectomy experiences a large bleed from the liver and her haemoglobin drops to 72 g/L. A massive blood loss protocol is activated and the patient is transfused a number of products including cryoprecipitate.\\n\\nWhich one of these is contained in cryoprecipitate?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is contained in cryoprecipitate?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Red blood cells\",\"e\":\"Patients with a low haemoglobin count requiring transfusion will be given packed red cells, which are not present in cryoprecipitate.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Granulocytes\",\"e\":\"Cryoprecipitate does not contain granulocytes, which would be given as a separate transfusion. It is rarely required but occasionally used in infection with prolonged neutropenia after cytotoxic chemotherapy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Factor VII\",\"e\":\"Cryoprecipitate is rich in factor VIII not factor VII. It was developed as a treatment for haemophilia but since the development of factor VIII concentrate it is rarely used in its treatment now.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Platelets\",\"e\":\"Cryoprecipitate does not contain platelets, which would be given as a pool of platelets if required.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Fibrinogen\",\"e\":\"Cryoprecipitate is rich in fibrinogen, factor VIlI and von Willebrand factor. It is mainly used as a more concentrated source of fibrinogen than fresh-frozen plasma (FFP).\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old man underwent a right hemicolectomy, Five days post-operatively he becomes short of breath with abdominal pain. A pulmonary embolism (PE) is suspected and he is given treatment dose heparin. CT pulmonary angiogram and abdomen/pelvis with contrast shows no PE but detects an anastomotic leak. The patient is booked for an emergency laparotomy. Which one of these medications should be given in view of the recent dose of heparin?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these medications should be given in view of the recent dose of heparin?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"vi's Vitamin K\",\"e\":\"Warfarin inhibits the synthesis of vitamin K dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X Vitamin K is effective for the reversal of warfarin but not heparin.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Protamine sulphate\",\"e\":\"Protamine sulphate works by binding with heparin to reverse its effects. This patient requires urgent surgery and should be discussed with the haematologist and receive protamine.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Prothrombin complex concentrate\",\"e\":\"Prothrombin complex concentrate is made up factors II, IX and X all of which are vitamin K dependent. It is therefore useful in the reversal of warfarin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cryoprecipitate\",\"e\":\"Cryoprecipitate contains fibrinogen, factor VIII and von Willebrand factor. It is not used as a reversal agent for heparin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Factor Vill concentrate\",\"e\":\"Heparin binds to antithrombin III resulting in its activation, which in turn inactivates thrombin and factor Xa to prevent clotting. Factor VIII concentrate would not be used to reverse the effects of heparin\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"Following a massive blood transfusion during a repair of an abdominal aortic aneurysm the patient's clotting is deranged.\\n\\nWhich one of these blood pictures would be most consistent with a diagnosis of disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these blood pictures would be most consistent with a diagnosis of disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Low platelets, low fibrinogen, prolonged prothrombin time, increased D-dimer\",\"e\":\"During DIC there is increased platelet aggregation and coagulation factor consumption, which results in the low platelets, low fibrinogen with a prolonged prothrombin time. There are increased levels of D-dimers (fibrin degradation products).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Low platelets, high fibrinogen, prolonged prothrombin time, decreased D-dimer\",\"e\":\"D-dimer is increased in DIC as it represents fibrin degradation products. In addition in DIC low fibrinogen would be expected, due to overconsumption.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"High platelets, low fibrinogen, normal prothrombin time, decreased D-dimer\",\"e\":\"DIC is a hypercoagulable state. There is increased platelet aggregation, which results in decreased platelet count. D-dimer would be increased as a marker of fibrin degradation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Low platelets, low fibrinogen, normal prothrombin time, increased D-dimer\",\"e\":\"Consumption of clotting factors results in a prolonged prothrombin time (high INR).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"High platelets, low fibrinogen, normal prothrombin time, increased D-dimer:\",\"e\":\"Platelets would be reduced as a result of increased platelet aggregation. Prothrombin time would be prolonged due to the consumption of clotting factors.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old undergoes an elective inguinal hernia repair. This is his first surgery and it is noted that he has excessive bleeding during the procedure. Afterwards he is diagnosed with haemophilia A.\\n\\nHow is this inherited?\",\"lead\":\"How is this inherited?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Autosomal recessive\",\"e\":\"Haemophilia A is inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern. Males only have one X chromosome and so if they inherit the gene they will develop the condition.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Autosomal dominant\",\"e\":\"Haemophilia results from a deficiency in factor 8. The factor 8 gene is located on the X chromosome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"X-linked recessive\",\"e\":\"Haemophilia A is inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern and therefore is more common in men. Patients with mild forms of the disease may only be picked up the first time they undergo an operation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"X-linked dominant\",\"e\":\"It is inherited in an X-linked recessive condition therefore more commonly affecting men.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sporadic mutation\",\"e\":\"Haemophilia can occur as a sporadic mutation without any family history. This question specifically asks how it is inherited, which is in an X-linked recessive manner.\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A patient is being treated for disseminated intravascular coagulation and as part of the treatment is being transfused fresh-frozen plasma (FFP). Which one of these is correct regarding FFP?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is correct regarding FFP?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"FFP should be stored at - 15°C\",\"e\":\"FFP should be stored at - 25°C or below.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"After FFP is thawed it must be used within 8\",\"e\":\"After FFP has been thawed it can be stored for up to 24 h at 4°C before transfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has a shelf life of 36 months\",\"e\":\"FFP can be stored at -25°C or below for up to 36 months.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Each FFP comes from four donors\",\"e\":\"FFP is issued as single-donor packs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"FFP contains 0.5 lU/ml of factor VIII\",\"e\":\"Each FFP comes from a single donor and will have variable levels of clotting factors that is displayed on the pack\"}]},{\"t\":41,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in an endoscopy to coagulate a bleeding peptic ulcer using a laser. What type of laser is most commonly used in this type of procedure?\",\"lead\":\"What type of laser is most commonly used in this type of procedure?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PT Argon beam\",\"e\":\"Argon beam lasers have now superseded NdYAG laser in the treatment of upper Gl ulcers. Argon beam is also widely used in ophthalmology and dermatology, eg trabeculoplasty, diabetic retinopathy and port wine stain treatment\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"ivis CO2\",\"e\":\"CO_ lasers are used in otolaryngology and gynaecology, eg haemostasis, removal of benign tumours and premalignant changes in ear, nose and throat (ENT) and cervical and vulval lesions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Helium-neon\",\"e\":\"Helium-neon lasers are not used for medical procedures, but have a variety of uses including barcode scanning\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"NdYAG\",\"e\":\"NdYAG laşers have been used in gastrointestinal (GI) surgery, urology and ophthalmology in the past. However, this has now been superseded in gastrointestinal surgery by Argon beam plasma coagulation eg debulking oesophageal carcinoma, malignant GI strictures, transitional-cell lesions for the bladder, cataract surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulsed ruby laser\",\"e\":\"Pulsed ruby lasers are used for tattoo removal.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"Following an elective splenectomy in a 21-year-old man with thalassaemia, you are asked by the discharge co-ordinating team to review a discharge summary detailing the operative steps and subsequent treatment.\\n\\nWhich one of the following would be correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would be correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The splenic artery was ligated after the splenic vein and lifelong oral penicillin prophylaxis should not be offered to patients\",\"e\":\"The splenic artery is always divided before the vein. Penicillin prophylaxis must be offered to patients who have undergone splenectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The splenic artery was ligated after the splenic vein and lifelong oral penicillin prophylaxis should be offered to patients\",\"e\":\"Although it is correct to say that penicillin prophylaxis should be offered to patients undergoing splenectomy, the splenic vein should be divided after the artery is divided, as there is risk of rupture during the procedure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The splenic artery was ligated before the splenic vein and lifelong oral penicillin prophylaxis should be offered to patients\",\"e\":\"The splenic artery is always divided before the vein. This prevents congestion of the spleen, by leaving it with outflow. When the indication for splenectomy is haemorrhage from the spleen, it also allows for ongoing autotransfusion of blood from the spleen. Current guidelines dictate that, after splenectomy, lifelong prophylactic antibiotics should be offered to patients considered at continued high risk of pneumococcal infection using oral penicillins. Patients not at high risk should be counselled regarding the risks and benefits of lifelong antibiotics and may choose to discontinue them. All patients should carry a supply of appropriate antibiotics for emergency use.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The splenic artery was ligated before the splenic vein and lifelong oral penicillin prophylaxis should not be offered to patients\",\"e\":\"All patients should be offered penicillin prophylaxis; alternatively, macrolides may be prescribed and this depends on local pneumococcal resistance patterns.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The splenic artery was ligated at the hilum and lifelong oral penicillin prophylaxis should be offered to patients\",\"e\":\"The splenic artery can be ligated at the hilum, although dissection here puts the patient at risk of pancreatic injury. The short gastric vessels need to be divided. In this scenario, the splenic artery is usually ligated along the upper border of the tail of the pancreas. The artery is ligated before the vein to minimise splenic engorgement.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman undergoes hemicolectomy through a midline laparotomy incision. You are asked to close the abdominal wall.\\n\\nWhich one is the most appropriate suture for this task?\",\"lead\":\"Which one is the most appropriate suture for this task?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Catgut\",\"e\":\"Catgut is rarely used in current practice, due to higher rates of postoperative infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Poliglecaprone monofilament\",\"e\":\"This suture is also known as Monocryl. This is not an appropriate suture for abdominal wall closure as it loses its tensile strength quickly - it loses about 40% of its tensile strength within 14 days. The abdominal wall is subject to high stresses, strains and tensions, and so a suture which retains its tensile strength for longer is needed. Poliglecaprone is appropriate for closure of the skin as it is absorbed quickly and so less likely leave skin-marks behind; it also elicits a low tissue reaction. which is less likely to lead to infection and more likely to leave wound that heals with satisfactory cosmesis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Polydioxanone monoflament\",\"e\":\"Polydioxanone is commonly used in loop form for closure of the abdominal wall - it is sometimes known as loop poly(p-dioxanone) (PDS). It retains around 80% of its tensile strength by 2 weeks post-use; and is not completely absorbed until 6 months, so providing the wound support that an abdominal wound needs.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Polyglactin braided\",\"e\":\"This is known as Vicryl. It is used for soft tissue approximation and ligation. Because it is braided and relatively slow to be absorbed (although not as slow as polydioxanone/PDS), it should not be used in cutaneous wounds, as it is bulkier in comparison to a monofilament suture, and remain reactive over time, leading to a higher risk of infection and unsatisfactory cosmesis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Polypropylene monofilament.\",\"e\":\"Polypropylene, also known as Prolene, is used primarily for vascular and other anastomoses. It non-absorbable, and withstands enzymes, and passes through tissues with ease, which is why it used in these anastomoses. Polydioxanone has less plasticity than polypropylene, which is why it is preferred in abdominal wall closure.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man is admitted for an open appendicectomy. While the consultant is undertaking the pre-operative World Health Organization (WHO) checklist, it appears the diathermy plate has not yet been applied.\\n\\nWhich one of the following options most accurately describes the principles of surgical diathermy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options most accurately describes the principles of surgical diathermy?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Coagulation is produced by a continuous current with a square wave form\",\"e\":\"Coagulation involves an interrupted pulse of current (50-100/s) and a square wave form, whereas cutting is produced by a continuous current with a sinus wave form.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is always dangerous if the patient is touching earthed metal\",\"e\":\"This is only the case for older diathermy machines. When using an older machine it is important to ensure that the patient is not touching earthed metal. Most modern diathermy machines do not have earth-referenced generators.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Involves a high frequency current in the range of 30 MHz to 300 MHz\",\"e\":\"Diathermy uses the heating effect of electrical current at a frequency of 400 kHz to 10 MHz.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is contraindicated in patients with a pacemaker\",\"e\":\"Although diathermy can be used in a patient with a pacemaker, the pad of the monopolar diathermy should be positioned well away from the pacemaker. Bipolar diathermy which combines two electrodes in the forceps does not interfere with pacemakers as the current does not travel through the patient and may be more suitable.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"With bipolar diathermy two electrodes are combined in the instrument as opposed to monopolar diathermy\",\"e\":\"Bipolar diathermy combines two electrodes and so a diathermy plate on the patient is not needed if using only bipolar.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a sentinel node biopsy for a patient with known breast cancer. You have difficulty locating the sentinel node but do so with a combination of blue dye and radioisotope.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"At least 10 nodes must be sampled to give an accurate nodal stage\",\"e\":\"Sentinel node biopsy involves identifying the first nodes that drain the primary tumour and is usually 1-2 nodes but can be more.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sentinel node biopsy involves identifying the first four nodes of a basin through which the majority of lymph from the breast drains\",\"e\":\"Sentinel node biopsy involves identifying the first nodes that drain the primary tumour.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lymphoscintigraphy can be performed up to one week in advance of the procedure\",\"e\":\"Lymphoscintigraphy needs to be done on the day of the surgery; the isotopes used have a half-life of just a few hours.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Blue dye is contraindicated in pregnancy\",\"e\":\"Currently, the use of blue dye is not recommended during sentinel node biopsy in patients who are pregnant.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"There is a high incidence of brachial plexus traction following sentinel node\",\"e\":\"The axilla simply needs to be exposed during sentinel node biopsy, and there is no need to place the arm under great traction.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a free flap reconstruction of the breast. The consultant allows you to isolate the vascular pedicle so the free flap can be performed.\\n\\nWhich one of the following recipient vessels is commonly used in the microanastomosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following recipient vessels is commonly used in the microanastomosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Subclavian artery\",\"e\":\"The subclavian artery is not an ideal recipient vessel as it located relatively far away from where the free flap will sit, ie on the chest. This may put the microanastomosis under too much tension.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Internal mammary artery\",\"e\":\"The internal mammary vessels are found underneath the medial 2-4th ribs, which are resected during the procedure. These vessels are ideally located to receive the perforator vessels from the free flap. It is possible to use recipient vessels other than the internal mammary artery, such as those based on the thoracodorsal axis. However the dissection is harder. Examples of free flaps used in breast reconstruction include deep inferior epigastric perforator flap (DIEP flap) and the superior gluteal artery perforator flap (SGAP flap).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Axillary artery\",\"e\":\"It may be possible to use the axillary artery, but the dissection of this vessel is technically challenging due to the proximity of the brachial plexus to it. Damage to the vessel will also put the distal blood supply of the upper limb at risk\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thoracodorsal artery\",\"e\":\"This artery supplies a latissimus dorsi flap that can be used for breast reconstruction. However this is not a free flap, but regional flap\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serratus branch of thoracodorsal artery\",\"e\":\"The serratus branch of the thoracodorsal artery supplies what is known as a serratus muscle flap. It is similar to the latissimus dorsi flap in that it is a regional flap that may be used in breast reconstruction.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"An 84-year-old man with multiple co-morbidities presents with severe abdominal pain looking very unwell. Blood gas reveals a severe metabolic acidosis and a computed tomography (CT) scan confirms a diverticular perforation, but the extent of abdominal contamination is unclear. A diagnostic laparoscopy is performed to assess if the patient can avoid a laparotomy at present. This shows a purulent peritonitis, but no faecal contamination.\\n\\nHow would this be classified?\",\"lead\":\"How would this be classified?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hinchey I\",\"e\":\"Hinchey II describes the presence of pelvic abscesses.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hinchey !!\",\"e\":\"Hinchey Ill describes the presence of pus in the abdominal cavity (purulent peritonitis) as described in this case. It is sometimes possible for cases with purulent peritonitis to be managed laparoscopically and through inserting a drain. It may then be possible to perform the appropriate resection later as an elective procedure and stoma formation may not be\\n\\nThe Hinchey classification is used for colonic perforation secondary to diverticular disease. It is divided into four descriptions. Hinchey I describes localised (paracolic) abscesses. It can be subdivided into la - phlegmon, or 1b - presence of pericolic or mesenteric abscess.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hinchey !!I\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hinchey IV\",\"e\":\"Hinchey IV describes faecutent peritonitis. This usually requires surgical management, in the form of a Hartman's procedure, where the affected segmented would be resected and the proximal end of the colon be brought out as an end stoma.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hinchey V\",\"e\":\"There is no stage V in the Hinchey classification.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"During a daycase operating list, a 56-year-old man, weighing 84 kg, asks you to repair his direct left inguinal hernia under a local anaesthetic. You agree. What would be the most appropriate anaesthetic and dose to use with this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What would be the most appropriate anaesthetic and dose to use with this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"100 ml 0.25% bupivacaine with 1:200 000 adrenaline\",\"e\":\"The maximum safe dose of bupivacaine with or without adrenaline is 2 mg/kg, up to a maximum of 150 mg. The maximum volume of 0.25% to be used in this case is 60 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"67 ml of 0.5% bupivacaine\",\"e\":\"The maximum safe dose of bupivacaine with or without adrenaline is 2 mg/kg, up to a maximum of 150 mg. The maximum volume of 0.5% to be used in this case is 30 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"60 ml 0.25% bupivacaine\",\"e\":\"The maximum safe dose of bupivacaine with or without adrenaline is 2 mg/kg. Therefore the maximum dose of bupivacaine for an 84 kg man would be 168 mg. However, the maximum safe dose permissible is 150 mg using the formula '1 ml of 1% drug contains 10 mg', 15. ml of 1% drug, or 60 ml of 0.25% drug (ie in this case) will be needed.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"100 ml 0.25% bupivacaine with 1:20 000 adrenaline\",\"e\":\"The maximum safe dose of bupivacaine with or without adrenaline is 2 mg/kg. Therefore the maximum volume of 0.25% to be used in this case is 60ml. However, the concentration of adrenaline used is 1:200 000, not 1:20 000\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"50 ml of 1% lidocaine\",\"e\":\"The maximum dose of 1% lidocaine for this man would be 20 ml.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"You are being taken through a carpal tunnel decompression by your consultant and are prepping the patient with the operating room staff. Which one of the following should you be aware of when applying a tourniquet?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following should you be aware of when applying a tourniquet?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"can be safely for up to shat a time\",\"e\":\"Tourniquets should not be applied for longer than 1.5-2 h. If the tourniquet needs to be used for longer, it should be deflated for an interval of 5 min, before re-applying pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Can be safely applied to cause a pressure exceeding 300 mmHg\",\"e\":\"The pressure of tourniquets should not exceed 300 mmHg.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Can cause focal demyelination of peripheral nerves\",\"e\":\"The most common effect on peripheral nerves is neuropraxia. Mechanical effects of compression and ischaemia cause focal demyelination.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Usually produce an axonotmesis\",\"e\":\"'Axonotmesis' means disruption of the axons while the nerve sheath remains intact. It usually occurs when there is a severe crush or stretch injury\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is not suitable for procedures under local anaesthesia\",\"e\":\"Tourniquets can be used for procedures being done under local anaesthetic, however they may become uncomfortable so should only be used for short procedures.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A 71-year-old heavy smoker is listed for a left posterolateral thoracotomy for resection of a tumour. How should he be positioned on the table?\",\"lead\":\"How should he be positioned on the table?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left lateral position\",\"e\":\"As the surgery is being performed on the left side, this would not be appropriate; they should be placed on their right side with the left side uppermost.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Prone\",\"e\":\"Having the patient lying flat on their front is not appropriate for this type of surgery. It would not allow access to the left thoracic cavity, and would pose multiple problems with management of the airway.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Trendelenburg position\",\"e\":\"The Trendelenburg position, with the feet higher than the head by up to 30° is not used to allow access to the left thoracic cavity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right lateral position\",\"e\":\"Posterolateral thoracotomy is performed most commonly with the patient in a full lateral position. The skin incision extends from a point two fingerbreadths below the inferior angle of the scapula, posteriorly to a point halfway between the spine of the scapula and the vertebral column, and anteriorly following the line of the rib for a variable distance. The latissimus dorsi is routinely divided but the serratus anterior is usually spared. The chest is entered through the fifth intercostal space, which is counted as starting below the first rib. So the fifth space lies below the fifth rib. The intercostal muscles are divided from the superior border of the rib below the intercostal space, to be entered to avoid injury to the neurovascular bundle lying just inferior to each rib.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supine\",\"e\":\"A posterolateral thoracotomy is typically performed in a lateral position\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"Your consultant leaves you to close the skin wound after performing an open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF) of a fractured distal radius. He asks you to close with a subcuticular stitch.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is an acceptable suture in this area?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is an acceptable suture in this area?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"2-0 Vicryl\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"3-0 silk\",\"e\":\"The suture should absorbable - silk is not absorbable and would have to be removed, it would also produce a significant tissue reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"4-0 Ethilon\",\"e\":\"Ethilon is not absorbable, and may also produce a tissue reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"4-0 Monocryl\",\"e\":\"The ideal suture should produce minimal tissue reaction, have adequate tensile strength to support wound healing and not cut through tissue. In this scenario, a dissolvable suture should be used to place a subcuticular suture.\\n\\nThis is a large diameter, and Vicryl can produce a prominent tissue reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"5-0 Prolene\",\"e\":\"Prolene is not absorbable, and would have to be removed. A suture at 5-0 strength would probably not be heavy enough to hold the relatively thick skin of the arm.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"You are performing a zone 2 repair of a flexor tendon on a patient right middle finger. Which one of the following is most likely to be used?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most likely to be used?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Blunt point needle\",\"e\":\"A blunt point needle could not pass through the tendon with ease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Conventional cutting needle\",\"e\":\"This needle is not usually selected due to concerns regarding cutting through the tissues, leaving the tendon vulnerable to further rupture. Round body needle is more likely to be used.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Reverse cutting needle\",\"e\":\"This needle is not usually selected due to concerns regarding cutting through the tissues, leaving the tendon vulnerable to further rupture\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Round body needle\",\"e\":\"When repairing a tendon it is important not to cut through the tissues as they may be damaged leading to fraying of the edges and rupture. A round bodied needle does not cut the tissue as it passes through, and so is the most likely correct answer here.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Taper cutting needle\",\"e\":\"This needle could cut through the tissues, leaving the tendon vulnerable to further rupture.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"A 78-year old man presents with an infected dehisced sternal wound following a coronary artery bypass graft (CABG). He undergoes debridement and application of dressing.\\n\\nWhich one of the following dressings is most suitable?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following dressings is most suitable?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alg nate\",\"e\":\"Alginate dressings are derived from seaweed, and are of use in wounds that secrete fluid, including infected wounds. However, in this setting, this may not be the best option. Alginate dressings are useful in chronic, shallow wounds, such as venous ulcers.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Foam dressing\",\"e\":\"Foam is useful for wounds that are wet. In this scenario, it would be best combined with negative pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hydrocolloid\",\"e\":\"Hydrocolloid dressings should be used in clean wounds.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"ORL Hydrogel\",\"e\":\"Hydrogel dressings are useful in dry wounds, as they provide moisture: They are not indicated in wounds with heavy exudate/discharge.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Negative pressure topical dressings\",\"e\":\"This is a difficult scenario, and often patients have co-morbidities that delay or complicate wound healing. Negative pressure dressings apply a constant pressure to the wound to reduce extravascular and interstitial fluid, while promoting cellular proliferation. Once the wound has been covered with a layer of granulation tissue other reconstructive methods, such as skin grafting, can be carried out with the knowledge that there is a clean bed without bacterial contamination.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"You are performing a repair of a spaghetti wrist on a patient who has had his arm trapped in heavy machinery. In this situation what is the maximum continuous tourniquet time you should use?\",\"lead\":\"In this situation what is the maximum continuous tourniquet time you should use?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Less than 2\",\"e\":\"Ideally, a tourniquet should not be applied for longer than 1.5-2 h\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Less than 4\",\"e\":\"Ideally, a tourniquet should not be applied for longer than 1.5-2 h. This patient has already suffered a degree of ischaemia to his arm, as it was trapped in machinery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Less than 6\",\"e\":\"Ideally, a tourniquet should not be applied for longer than 1.5-2 h.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"There is no maximum\",\"e\":\"A tourniquet should not be applied for longer than 1.5-2 h before damage due to ischaemia and compression occurs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"This depends on his response to deep sedation\",\"e\":\"Sedation does not affect the length of time for which the tourniquet should be applied. In prolonged operations, surgeons will reduce the tourniquet every 2 h for a period of up to 15 min to establish re-perfusion.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old woman undergoes bilateral breast reduction. A drain is inserted before\\n\\nWhat is the purpose of inserting a drain?\",\"lead\":\"What is the purpose of inserting a drain?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"To assist in wound healing\",\"e\":\"A drain will not speed wound healing.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"To keep an eye on amount of exudate being released from the surgica site\",\"e\":\"There is no clinical reason to record the amount of exudate released from a surgical site.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"To minimise the risk of postoperative seroma or haematoma formation\",\"e\":\"The use of drains remains controversial, and prolonged drain insertion can lead to an increased risk of infection. They can be used in combination with low-grade suction to remove blood excess from a cavity. They can also prevent haematoma or seroma. Large amounts of blood in the drain bag can indicate ongoing bleeding\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"To reduce infection rates S\",\"e\":\"A drain is an infection risk, especially if kept in for a prolonged time.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"To seal off the environment from outside bacterial invasion\",\"e\":\"Drains will not prevent bacteria from entering the wound; in fact, they may act as a vector for them. The use of drains remains controversial, and prolonged drain insertion can lead to an increased risk of infection. They can be used in combination with low-grade suction to remove blood excess from a cavity. Large amounts of blood in the drain bag can indicate ongoing bleeding.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"A 60 kg woman is undergoing lipofilling. The consultant wants to ensure she is comfortable and administers local anaesthetic.\\n\\nWhat is the maximum volume of 1% lidocaine with adrenaline that can be administered?\",\"lead\":\"What is the maximum volume of 1% lidocaine with adrenaline that can be administered?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"50 mi\",\"e\":\"This dose is too low. The maximum dose is 420 mg, which is 42 ml of 1% lidocaine.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"35 mi\",\"e\":\"This dose is too low. The maximum dose is 420 mg, which is 42 ml of 1% lidocaine.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"42 ml\",\"e\":\"The maximum dose of lidocaine without adrenaline is 3 mg/kg, and with adrenaline this is 7 mg/kg; 1% lidocaine contains 10 mg/ml, and 2% 20 mg/ml. The maximum dose of lidocaine with adrenaline in a 60 kg patient is 420 mg, which equates to 42 ml.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"62 mi\",\"e\":\"This dose is too high. The maximum dose is 420 mg, which is 42 ml of 1% lidocaine:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"70 mi\",\"e\":\"This dose is too high. The maximum dose is 420 mg, which is 42 ml of 1% lidocaine:\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"You are completing the World Health Organisation (WHO) checklist before starting an inguinal hernia repair under general anaesthetic. You have just asked 'Is the diathermy plate correctly applied?' Which one of the following is true regarding the use of surgical diathermy?\",\"lead\":\"' Which one of the following is true regarding the use of surgical diathermy?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The plate must be placed on a limb\",\"e\":\"It may be placed on the trunk or the limbs and should avoid bony prominences, scar tissue, metal work and hairy surfaces.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The plate is required for use with bipolar machines\",\"e\":\"A diathermy plate is only required when using monopolar. When using monopolar the high frequency current passes through the patient from the active electrode and is returned via the diathermy plate. In bipolar surgery the current passes between the forceps to complete the electrical circuit and there is no need for a diathermy plate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"If care is not taken, diathermy may cause full thickness burns if the plate becomes wet\",\"e\":\"When the plate becomes wet its contact with the skin is partially lost. This causes heat generation as there is no appropriate exit for the current to pass safely and results in burns. Incorrect use of the diathermy plate is the commonest cause of diathermy burns.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Plate position should be checked by the theatre sister/ scrub nurse\",\"e\":\"While the theatre sister/scrub nurse often apply the diathermy plate, the overall responsibility of plate position lies with the operating surgeon.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The plate should cover a minimum area of 20 cm2\",\"e\":\"The plate should cover a minimum surface area of 70 cm?.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"You are briefing your scrub sister on which scalpel you would like for your 'lumps and bumps' list.\\n\\nWhich one of the following blades are correctly paired with its likely use?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following blades are correctly paired with its likely use?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The correct scalpel for excision of minor cutaneous lesions is a number 15 blade\",\"e\":\"The number 15 blade has a small curved cutting edge and is the most popular blade shape ideal for making short and precise incisions. It is utilised in a variety of surgical procedures including the excision of a skin lesion or recurrent sebacecus cyst.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The correct scalpel for an arteriotomy for example of the aorta is a number 10 blade\",\"e\":\"To make this stab incision an elongated triangular blade with a strong pointed tip, the number 11 blade, would be more suitable. It is used in various procedures such as the creation of incisions for chest drains, opening the aorta and removing calcifications in the aortic or mitral valves.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The correct scalpel for a small arteriotomy for example of a coronary artery is a number 10 blade\",\"e\":\"The number 10 blade with its curved cutting edge is used generally for making incisions in skin and muscle. However, the number 15 blade has a small curved cutting edge and is ideal for making short and precise incisions. It is utilised in a variety of surgical procedures including opening coronary arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The correct scalpe for dissection in the abdomen is a number 10 blade\",\"e\":\"When dissecting in the abdomen it is important to have precise controlled movements which is best suited for a number 15 blade which has a small curved cutting edge.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The correct scalpel for a laparotomy incision is a number 15 blade\",\"e\":\"To make a laparotomy incision a number 10 blade would be more suitable with its curved cutting edge compared with the small number 15 blade more suitable for short precise incisions.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"You are undertaking a teaching session for some of the student operating department professionals (ODPs) Which one of the following statements best describes how the bipolar diathermy machine works?\",\"lead\":\"You are undertaking a teaching session for some of the student operating department professionals (ODPs) Which one of the following statements best describes how the bipolar diathermy machine works?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The bipolar diathermy machine uses a low current at very high frequency and low voltage, which is passed through the patient's tissue using two electrodes to complete the circuit\",\"e\":\"Diathermy is used for cutting tissues and for haemostasis. The bipolar diathermy machine uses a low current at very high frequency and low voltage. The current passes between two limbs of diathermy forceps only, with no need for patient plate electrode. Bipolar diathermy is inherently safer than other settings but cannot be used to touch other instruments to transfer current. Bipolar diathermy is useful for surgery to extremities: scrotum, penis or on digits\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The bipolar diathermy machine uses a high current at very high frequency and high voltage, which is passed through the patient's tissue using two electrodes to complete the circuit\",\"e\":\"A high current results in cutting, which is not the use of bipolar diathermy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The bipolar diathermy machine uses a low current at very low frequency and high voltage, which is passed through the patient's tissue using two electrodes to complete the circuit\",\"e\":\"The bipolar diathermy machine is passed through the patient's tissue using a low current but at a very high frequency and low voltage. A high frequency is required to enable energy to pass through the patient's tissue with minimal neuromuscular stimulation and no risk of electrocution.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The bipolar diathermy machine uses a low current at very high frequency and high voltage, which is passed through the patient's tissue using two electrodes to complete the circuit\",\"e\":\"The bipolar diathermy machine uses low current at a very high frequency but low voltage to cause coagulation. Using high voltage causes fulguration - coagulating and charring the tissue over a wide area.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The bipolar diathermy machine uses a low current at very low frequency and low voltage, which is passed through the patient's tissue using two electrodes to complete the circuit\",\"e\":\"During bipolar diathermy a low current passes between the forceps tips. It uses high frequency to prevent neuromuscular stimulation or electrocution and a low voltage.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in laser cataract surgery using a neodymium-yttrium aluminium garnet (Nd:YAG) laser. You ensure that you and the theatre staff wear protective goggles and adhere to all safety precautions.\\n\\nTo what class of laser does this type of laser belong?\",\"lead\":\"To what class of laser does this type of laser belong?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1\",\"e\":\"A class 1 laser is safe under all conditions of normal use, a laser printer is an example of a class 1 laser in use.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"2\",\"e\":\"Class 2 lasers are limited to a maximum output power and must have a wavelength between 400 and 700 nm. A person whose eyes are exposed to a class 2 laser will be naturally protected by their involuntary blink response. Examples of class 2 lasers include laser pointers and barcode scanners.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"3R\",\"e\":\"Class 3R lasers are higher powered than classes 1 and 2. The laser beams exceed the maximal permissible exposure and your body is not able to protect itself from these lasers. Therefore, they have the potential to cause eye injuries although this risk is low in unintentional exposure as exposure is usually short. Some laser pointers are class 3R lasers with others belonging to class 2.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"3B\",\"e\":\"Class 3B lasers have sufficient power to cause eye injury and minor skin injuries from both direct beam and reflections. Examples include lasers used for physiotherapy treatment and research lasers.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"4\",\"e\":\"Lasers are classified according to their degree of hazard. Class 4 lasers are the most hazardous. They can burn the skin and cause permanent damage to the eye by direct, diffuse or indirect beam viewing. They also pose a fire risk. Most medical lasers, which includes Nd:YAG are class 4. Protective glasses should be worn when using the laser:\\n\\nA class 1 laser is safe under all conditions of normal use, a laser printer is an example of a class 1 laser in use.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a femoro-distal bypass and the consultant asks you what would be the most appropriate suture to select for the distal anastomosis to the tibial artery\\n\\nWhat suture would you select?\",\"lead\":\"What suture would you select?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Polyester 6-0\",\"e\":\"Polyester is a polyfilament and would not give a smooth running suture required for a vascular anastomosis. The size of the suture should be related to the size of the vessel with 2-0 and 3-0 suitable for the aorta and for this anastomosis to the tibial artery a 6-0 or 7-0 would be a suitable size.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Poliglecaprone 4-0\",\"e\":\"Poliglecaprone (Monocryl) is a monofilament, which would give the smooth running suture required for a vascular anastomosis. However, the size of the suture should be related to the size of the vessel with a 4-0 being more appropriate for the iliac vessels: For this anastomosis to the tibial artery a 6-0 or 7-0 would be a more suitable size.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Polyglycolic acid 6-0\",\"e\":\"Polyglactin (Vicryl) is a polyfilament and would not give a smooth running suture required for a vascular anastomosis. The size of the suture should be related to the size of the vessel with 2-0 and 3-0 suitable for the aorta and for this anastomosis to the tibial artery a 6-0 or 7-0 would be a suitable size.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Polypropylene 6-0\",\"e\":\"For a vascular anastomosis a smooth running suture, a monofilament, with a size suitable for the size of the vessel is required. Polypropylene is a monofilament and for this anastomosis to the tibial artery a 6-0 or 7-0 would be a suitable size.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Silk 4-0\",\"e\":\"Silk is a polyfilament and would not give a smooth running suture required for a vascular anastomosis. A 4-0 would also be too large for the size of this vessel. For this anastomosis to the tibial artery a 6-0 or 7-0 would be a more suitable size.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A knee arthroscopy is performed on a 30-year-old man with no medical problems, and the consultant prefers to use a tourniquet. This enables the arthoscopy to be bloodless and relatively little sterile fluids is required to keep the joint inflated as it is not becoming blood stained\\n\\nWhich type of tourniquet would be best for this procedure?\",\"lead\":\"Which type of tourniquet would be best for this procedure?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Double cuff pneumatic\",\"e\":\"Double cuff pneumatic tourniquets are used in orthopaedic surgery but mostly when a regional block, eg Bier's Block has been given. In a double cuff inflation and deflation of each can be controlled separately to allow a switch from proximal to distal after local anaesthetic injection. When the distal cuff is inflated the local anaesthetic is then effective to prevent pain caused by the inflated cuff.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Elastic\",\"e\":\"An elastic tourniquet is an example of a non-pneumatic tourniquet. Therefore, there is a fixed pressure which would not be suitable for a knee arthroscopy and is more commonly used in venepuncture.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Non-pneumatic\",\"e\":\"Tourniquets can be broadly divided into two types: non-pneumatic, (non-inflatable) and pneumatic. Examples of non-pneumatic tourniquets include elastic and rubber tourniquets, which are commonly used in venepuncture and surgery on digits. These would not be suitable for a knee arthroscopy as they give a fixed pressure to which there is limited control.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"0 Rubber\",\"e\":\"A rubber tourniquet is an example of a non-pneumatic tourniquet. These give a fixed pressure which would not be suitable for a knee arthroscopy and are more commonly used in venepuncture.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Single cuff pneumatic\",\"e\":\"Tourniquets are commonly used in orthopaedic surgery. Knee arthroscopy can be performed without tourniquet or with a single cuff pneumatic tourniquet. Pneumatic tourniquets have a number of safety features and can be inflated to different pressures.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"You are performing an open reduction and plate fixation of a distal radius fracture.\\n\\nWhich one of the following maximum tourniquet pressure and tourniquet time would you consider to be the most safe and effective for a tourniquet placed on the proximal part of the arm?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following maximum tourniquet pressure and tourniquet time would you consider to be the most safe and effective for a tourniquet placed on the proximal part of the arm?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pressure: 350 mmHg/time: 120 min\",\"e\":\"Tourniquets, even when used in an appropriate manner, can cause injury. A tourniquet used at pressures of greater than 250 mmHg in the upper limb and/or for more than 2 h increases the risk of vascular damage, thrombosis, soft-tissue injury and nerve injury. If more than 2 h is required the tourniquet should be deflated for 5 min during every 30 min of inflation time.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pressure: 150 mmHg/time: 90 min\",\"e\":\"Tourniquet pressure should be just above arterial pressure. While 150 mmHg would be enough for some individuals it would not work for all. If the pressure is too low then the operating field would be obscured by bleeding. At the correct pressure it would be safe to leave a tourniquet for 90 minutes in the upper limb.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pressure: 50 mmHg above diastolic blood pressure/time: 60 min\",\"e\":\"The tourniquet pressure should be just above arterial pressure - systolic blood pressure not diastolic blood pressure; 50 mmHg above diastolic pressure is unlikely to be sufficient and bleeding would obscure the operating field. It would be safe to leave a tourniquet for 60 min in the upper limb.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pressure: 100 mmHg above diastolic blood pressure/time: 90 min\",\"e\":\"The tourniquet pressure should be just above arterial pressure to prevent bleeding obscuring the field of view and also not be too great to cause injury. While, 100 mmg for many people would likely fulfil both of these criteria it is not the best answer for calculating tourniquet pressure. At the correct pressure it would be safe to leave a tourniquet for 90 min in the upper limb, beyond 2 h there is a higher risk of vascular damage, thrombosis, soft-tissue and nerve injury.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pressure: 50 mmHg above systolic blood pressure/time: 60 min\",\"e\":\"A pressure of 50 mmHg above systolic blood pressure would be just above arterial pressure and therefore be suitable to prevent blood from obscuring the surgical field while also minimising the risk of vascular damage, thrombosis, soft-tissue and nerve injury. A tourniquet time of 60 min would be safe, beyond 2 h there is a higher risk of injury. It is also good practice to release the tourniquet before suturing the wound to identify the bleeding vessels and so prevent post-operative haemorrhage.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"You are observing your consultant ablate a large exophytic oesophageal lesion using a laser. You ensure you adhere to the laser safety protocols, the lesion is successfully excised and the patient has a successful outcome.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true regarding the neodymium-yttrium aluminium garnet (Nd- YAG) laser?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding the neodymium-yttrium aluminium garnet (Nd- YAG) laser?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is fully portable and can be used in any theatre environment\",\"e\":\"The Nd-YAG laser must be used in a designated theatre that is clearly identified as a 'laser controlled area' with warning signs placed at every entry point. It should only be used by those trained to do so. It has the potential to burn the skin and cause permanent damage to the eye by direct, diffuse or indirect beam viewing\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is principally used for tattoo removal\",\"e\":\"The Nd-YAG laser can be used in tattoo removal but it is not its principle use. In tattoo removal its 1064 nm wavelength makes it ideal for removing black or dark tattoos. It has many other medical applications including the ablation of benign or malignant lesions such as vascular skin lesions and in the palliative treatment of oesophageal carcinoma.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It has a role in the curative treatment of oesophageal carcinoma\",\"e\":\"The Nd-YAG laser has many medical applications. In upper gastrointestinal surgery the major applications include control of haemorrhage and also in the palliative treatment of oesophageal cancer to reduce symptoms relating to obstruction.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It has very little tissue penetration\",\"e\":\"The wavelength of the laser determines the depth of penetration, the lower the wavelength the less tissue penetration. The Nd-YAG laser has a wavelength of 1064 nm, one of the greatest, and penetrates tissue deeply (3-5 mm).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It requires compulsory eye protection by the user\",\"e\":\"The Nd-YAG laser is a class 4 laser and therefore classified as the most hazardous. It can burn the skin and cause permanent damage to the eye by direct, diffuse or indirect beam viewing. Protective glasses must be worn when using the laser.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant on a breast reduction when you ask if a drain should be inserted at the end of the procedure.\\n\\nWhich one of the following regarding surgical drains is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regarding surgical drains is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Drains of the peritoneal cavity always prevent the accumulation of fluid\",\"e\":\"Intraperitoneal drains are of doubtful benefit, and cannot be relied upon to drain intra- abdominal fluid.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Drains of the pleural cavity must always be connected to an underwater seal drain\",\"e\":\"Drainage of the pleural cavity requires a closed system, usually an underwater seal drain. Alternatively a disposable flutter valve system or continuous wall suction may be used and these do not require an underwater seal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Drains prevent a haematoma\",\"e\":\"Simply placing a drain in a space does not prevent haematoma formation, a meticulous surgical technique to ensure haemostasis will reduce haematoma rates. However, gentle suction drains will aid the closure of cavities after evacuation of haematoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Drains may result in the formation of a fistula\",\"e\":\"Surgical drains, whether tubular or ribbon, form channels along which fluids travel to the skin surface, these may form fistulae. For example after colorectal surgery an uncommon but significant complication is the formation of an enterocutaneous fistula.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Drains should be performed through the main wound\",\"e\":\"Surgical drains can be placed in the wound (eg Yeates drain) but usually require a separate stab incision.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant on a breast reduction and he quizzes the medical student on suture type and material. Whilst this is happening you close the skin with a subcuticular suture using non-absorbable suture material.\\n\\nOf the following, which one is an example of these sutures?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following, which one is an example of these sutures?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Polydioxanone sulphate\",\"e\":\"Polydioxanone sulphate also known as PDS is an absorbable suture.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Polygalactin 910\",\"e\":\"Polygalactin 910 also known as Vicryl is an absorbable suture.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Polyglycolic acid\",\"e\":\"Polyglycolic acid (Dexon) is a multifilament absorbable suture.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Polyglyconate\",\"e\":\"Also known as Maxon is an absorbable suture.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Polypropylene\",\"e\":\"Polypropylene or 'Prolene' is an example of a non-absorbable suture, as are silk, nylon, polyester (eg 'Dacron'), PVDF (polyvinylidene fluoride - eg 'Novafil') and steel.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"You are position ng a patient for left breast mastectomy. During this stage, the theatre nurse asks you if you want a diathermy-\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding how diathermy works?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding how diathermy works?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Heat is generated by the passage of high-frequency alternating current chrough body tissues\",\"e\":\"Diathermy is used for cutting tissues and for haemostasis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Heat is generated by the passage of low-frequency alternating current through body tissue\",\"e\":\"The heat from diathermy is generated by the passage of high-frequency alternating current.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Heat is produced and required of up to 10000 °C\",\"e\":\"The heat produced is not this high, the concentrated current produces high heat up to 1000 °C\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Heat is produced and required of up to 100 °C\",\"e\":\"Locally concentrated current produces high heat up to 1000°C.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Currents of up to 400 mA are safe at frequencies of 500kHz to 10 MHz\",\"e\":\"Currents of up to 500 mA are safe at frequencies of 400 kHz to 10 MHz.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A 91-year-old woman is admitted to hospital with an infected diabetic foot ulcer. The nursing staff ask advice on which type of dressing to use.\\n\\nWhich one of the following dressings would you advise?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following dressings would you advise?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"An alginate dressing\",\"e\":\"Alginate dressings are highly absorbent, have haemostatic properties and are useful in packing cavities. They are useful in the management of exudative wounds. However, it is only bacteriostatic, as opposed to bacteriocidal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A hydrogel dressing\",\"e\":\"Hydrogels retain moisture or provide rehydration, and so are more useful in dry. necrotic wounds. This patient is likely to have a very wet wound with a large volume of exudate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"An iodine-based dressing\",\"e\":\"lodine-based dressings are effective in the management of infected and exuding wounds, particularly diabetic foot ulcers, as it has bacteriocidal qualities.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An occulsive dressing\",\"e\":\"Occlusive dressings should be avoided in infected wounds as they can worsen the infection by retaining the exudate and causing maceration of the surrounding skin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"No dressing required\",\"e\":\"This patient needs dressings to help treat the infection, promote healing, and to allow them to lead as normal a life as possible.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"You are asked by your consultant to directly close the abdominal skin wound following an open cholecystectomy. There is concern about wound contamination after slight bile spillage earlier in the operation. He leaves the choice of sutures to you.\\n\\nWhat properties would you look for?\",\"lead\":\"What properties would you look for?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A non-absorbable biological braided multifilament coated in wax such as silk\",\"e\":\"It is thought that multifilament sutures are more likely to be a vector for bacteria, and so should be avoided in potentially contaminated wounds.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A non-absorbable, synthetic monofilament such as Prolene™\",\"e\":\"In cases of possible wound contamination (ie in this scenario), non-absorbable monofilament sutures carry the lowest infection risk and can be removed easily if the wound becomes infected in the early postoperative period. Staples are a good alternative. Prolene™ (polypropylene) sutures in addition are generally used in a vascular anastomosis, and cosmetically important scars as it does not produce much of a tissue reaction. Smooth (monofilament) sutures are best for running stitches (eg vascular surgery) because they slide easily through tissues.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A non-absorbable, synthetic multifilament such as Dacron™\",\"e\":\"Multifilament sutures should be avoided in potentially contaminated wounds. Examples of monofilament sutures are polydioxanone sutures (PDS) and Prolene™: Vicry!™ is a polyfilament suture. Silk, linen and cotton are biological, multifilament, non-absorbable sutures.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"An absorbable, coated synthetic homopolymer such as Dexon™\",\"e\":\"Non-absorbable sutures, rather than absorbable sutures, are used for tissues that heal more slowly (eg abdominal wall closure).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"An absorbable synthetic copolymer coated with calcium stearate, glycolide and lactide such as Vicry!™\",\"e\":\"Non-absorbable sutures are used for tissues that heal more slowly (eg abdominal wall closure); Vicryl is also multifilament, and so thought to harbour bacteria. Absorbable sutures are used for tissues that heal quickly such as bowel anastomosis.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"At the start of a routine procedure your scrub nurse asks you if you would like to use diathermy.\\n\\nWhat is the most common cause of diathermy burns?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most common cause of diathermy burns?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Careless technique\",\"e\":\"Diathermy burns can be caused by careless technique, but fortunately this option is not the most common cause.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Incorrect placement of patient plate electrode\",\"e\":\"The most common cause of an accidental diathermy burn is the incorrect application of the diathermy plate on the patient. The principles of safe pad placement are as follows:\\n\\n• the pad should be placed close to the operative site • it should be placed away from prosthetic implants eg metal, hips • it should be placed on a well vascularised muscular area eg the thigh, and the area should be shaved • there must be good contact between the pad and the skin to prevent fluids from seeping in-between.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"use or bipolar diathermy\",\"e\":\"Bipolar diathermy is considered to be safer and avoids the need for a diathermy plate. However, burns can still be caused by carelessness.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Use of diathermy on appendages (eg penis)\",\"e\":\"Monopolar diathermy should not be used on end organs such as the penis, but bipolar diathermy can be used safely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Use of diathermy on large bowel\",\"e\":\"Diathermy can be used on the large bowel, provided it is used purposefully eg creating enterotomy for staplers, or to open the bowel to create a stoma. The safe use of diathermy includes ensuring that the patient is not touching earthed metal (older machines). Avoiding the pooling of inflammable agents (alcohol, inflammable gases); using the lowest practicable power setting; not using monopolar on narrow pedicles such as the penis, digits, dissected tissue block, spermatic cord; placing the plate away from metallic implants (eg prosthetic hips).\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"An 18-month-old boy presents with a painful ulcerated haemangioma and is listed for laser therapy. Which one of the following statements regarding the mechanism of action of lasers is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding the mechanism of action of lasers is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lasers produce a low-voltage monochromatic beam of energy\",\"e\":\"Lasers produce a highly directional monochromatic and in-phase (coherent) beam of electromagnetic radiation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lasers routinely absorb red pigments such as in blood vessels\",\"e\":\"The absorption of red pigment depends on the wavelength of the laser.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The amplitude defines the penetration depth of the light through the tissue\",\"e\":\"It is the wavelength that defines the penetration of depth.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The wavelength defines the penetration depth of the light through the tissue\",\"e\":\"This is because the skin has a range of chromophores which have scattering and absorption coefficients that wavelength dependent. Laser is an acronym for 'light amplification by the stimulated emission of radiation'.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Water is a common lasing medium\",\"e\":\"The lasing medium is commonly gaseous (eg carbon dioxide) or crystalline (eg neodymium/yttrium-aluminium garnet, NdYAG)\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":79,\"stem\":\"A 3-month-old boy presents with bile-stained vomit and abdominal tenderness. A plain X-ray is normal. A barium enema reveals mid-gut malrotation with volvulus.\\n\\nWhat will be the best treatment option?\",\"lead\":\"What will be the best treatment option?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Urgent laparotomy, rotate the volvulus anticlockwise, return the small bowel to the left of the abdomen and the caecum and colon to the right and perform an appendicectomy\",\"e\":\"The small bowel needs to be returned to the right of the abdomen, and the caecum and colon to the left.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Urgent laparotomy, rotate the volvulus anticlockwise, return the small bowel to the right of the abdomen and the caecum and colon to the left and perform an appendicectomy\",\"e\":\"This is the correct summary of the Ladd procedure, which is the treatment for mid-gut malrotation with volvulus. The patient in the scenario has malrotation and volvulus neonatorum. During normal embryogenesis, the bowel herniates into the base of the umbilical cord and rapidly elongates. As it returns to the abdominal cavity, it undergoes a complex 270 degree anticlockwise rotation, resulting in the duodenojejunal (DJ) flexure normally located to the left of the midline (at the level of L1 vertebrat body) and the terminal ileum located in the right iliac fossa. This results in a broad mesentery running obliquely down from the DJ flexure to the caecum, and prevents rotation around the superior mesenteric artery (SMA). In malrotation, this does not occur and as a result the mesentery has a short root, which allows it to act as a pedicle [through which the SMA and superior mesenteric vein (SMV) pass] around which volvulus can occur. Surgical treatment for malrotation remains the Ladd procedure - reduction of any volvulus, division of mesenteric bands, placement of the small and large bowel on the right and left of the abdomen respectively, and appendicectomy. A laparoscopic approach is becoming more common. When mid-gut volvulus is present, this is usually seen as a clockwise direction twist and is reduced by twisting in a counterclockwise direction, usually requiring two or three twists of the bowel. Once this is done it is possible to decide whether the bowel remains viable. The best outcomes are achieved when no or only a small area of gangrenous bowel is discovered and resection/primary anastomosis performed. Grossly necrotic bowel will require resection followed by primary anastomosis or diversion of the faecal stream with a proximal stoma formation depending on circumstances. The next step is identification and relief of any extrinsic obstruction to the duodenum and a check that no intrinsic obstruction remains by passing an nasogastric tube (NG) tube through the duodenum. The small and large bowel are replaced on the right and left of the abdomen respectively and appendicectomy is performed to avoid any future difficulty in the diagnosis of appendicitis at a later date due to the normal anatomical placement of the appendix having been disrupted.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Urgent laparotomy, rotate the volvulus clockwise, return the small bowel to the left of the abdomen and the caecum and colon to the right\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect, because it does not describe the need for an appendicectomy, the volvulus needs to be rotated anticlockwise; and the small bowel needs to be returned to the right of the abdomen, and the cacum and colon to the left:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urgent laparotomy, rotate the volvulus clockwise, return the small bowel to the left of the abdomen and the caecum and colon to the right and perform an appendicectomy\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect, because the volvulus needs to be rotated anticlockwise, and the small bowel needs to be returned to the right of the abdomen, and the caecum and colon to the\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urgent laparotomy, rotate the volvulus clockwise, return the small bowel to the right of the abdomen and the caecum and colon to the left and perform an appendicectomy\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect because the volvulus needs to be rotated anticlockwise.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":82,\"stem\":\"A 3-month-old girl presents with bile-stained vomit, abdominal tenderness and rectal bleeding. Subsequent imaging suggests malrotation and mid-gut volvulus. Intraoperatively. the small bowel is found to be ischaemic.\\n\\nWhat is the best treatment option?\",\"lead\":\"What is the best treatment option?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"If less than 50 cm of viable bowel is found, excise all the ischaemic bowel\",\"e\":\"The patient has malrotation and volvulus neonatorum. The abdominal tenderness and rectal bleeding are signs that there is bowel ischaemia. Urgent laparotomy is required. Once the volvulus has been derotated, and the bowel returned to the abdomen the ischaemic bowel needs to be dealt with. Efforts should be made to give a chance for the bowel to reperfuse as in a young patient excision of such a large amount of small bowel will lead to debilitating morbidity.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"If less than 50 cm of viable bowel is found, untwist the bowel, return it to the abdomen and close\",\"e\":\"This is not correct, as a second look laparotomy will be required\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"If less than 50 cm of viable bowel is found, untwist the bowel, return it to the abdomen and perform a second look laparotomy\",\"e\":\"This is the correct response; the second look laparotomy allows for assessment of the bowel and therefore preservation of more length.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"If more than 50 cm of viable bowel is found, untwist the bowel, return it to the abdomen and close\",\"e\":\"If there is more than 50 cm of viable bowel remaining the ischaemic section can be excised; a primary anastomosis can be created and the abdomen closed without a need for second look.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"If more than 50 cm of viable bowel is found, untwist the bowel, return it to the abdomen and perform a second look laparotomy\",\"e\":\"If there is more than 50 cm of viable bowel remaining the ischaemic section can be excised and the ends anastomosed; there should be no need for a second look.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":85,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in surgical exploration of a testicular torsion.\\n\\nIf the testis is viable, how should the fixation be performed?\",\"lead\":\"If the testis is viable, how should the fixation be performed?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Both testes should be invaginated in the tunica vaginalis and sutured to the midline septum with non-absorbable sutures\",\"e\":\"The treatment of a torted testis that is viable is to suture both testes to the midline with non- absorbable sutures, having invaginated the testes in the tunica vaginalis. Usually three point fixation of each testis should be performed with a stitch medially, laterally and inferiorly.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Both testes should be invaginated in the tunica vaginalis and sutured to the midline septum with absorbable sutures\",\"e\":\"Non-absorbable sutures should be used to maintain the fixation for life.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Both testes should be invaginated in the internal spermatic fascia and sutured to the midline septum with non-absorbable sutures\",\"e\":\"Both testes should be invaginated to the tunica vaginalis:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Both testes should be invaginated in the cremasteric fascia and sutured to the midline septum with absorbable sutures\",\"e\":\"The invagination should be to the tunica vaginalis with non-absorbable sutures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Both testes should be invaginated in the cartos fascia and sutured to the midline septum with non-absorbable sutures\",\"e\":\"Both testes should be invaginated to the tunica vaginalis. The layers of the scrotum are skin, dartos fascia and muscle, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, internal spermatic fascia, tunica vaginalis and tunica albuginea.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":88,\"stem\":\"An 87-year-old patient with perforated diverticular disease undergoes a lengthy laparotomy: The anaesthetists are concerned about maintaining the patient's temperature during the operation. Which one of the following would be most effective in reducing heat loss from the patient and maintain core body temperature during the procedure?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would be most effective in reducing heat loss from the patient and maintain core body temperature during the procedure?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Humidification of inspired gases\",\"e\":\"Humidification of inspired gases works to raise/maintain the core temperature, rather than to reduce the heat loss from a patient.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Lavage with warm saline\",\"e\":\"Using warmed infusions raises/maintains the core temperature of a patient, rather than minimising heat losses.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Maintaining the environmental temperature at 20 °C\",\"e\":\"Theatre temperature is usually maintained between 22°C and 24°C (but may need to be higher for neonatal surgery).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Use of a heated blanket set at 35 °C\",\"e\":\"Anaesthesia obtunds the normal mechanisms of heat production, so the patient is vulnerable to hypothermia from conductive, convective and evaporative heat loss. Using a Bair Hugger (heated blanket) acts to warm the patient rather than reduce losses and can be on three settings low (32 = 3°C), medium (38 = 3°C) and high (43 = 3°C). However, it is effective in maintaining the patient's temperature, and so has to be considered the most superior method.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Use of warm saline soaked packs to cover the bowel\",\"e\":\"This is one way of minimising heat loss, however, the packs will rapidly lose their warmth, and so is it not the most effective wav.\"}]},{\"t\":42,\"p\":90,\"stem\":\"You are performing the approach for a thyroidectomy. Which one of the following options is correct regarding this approach?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is correct regarding this approach?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The ansa cervicalis is seen readily\",\"e\":\"The ansa cervicalis embedded in the carotid sheath is not usually sought, unlike the recurrent laryngeal nerves.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The anterior jugular veins should be divided\",\"e\":\"The anterior jugular veins are typically paired veins running vertically along bilateral symmetrical paramedian lines in the superficial layer of the deep cervical fascia. To gain access to the strap muscles, and subsequently the surgical thyroid capsule, the fascia must be divided in the midline (ie between the paired anterior jugular veins, which are subsequently retracted laterally).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The flaps raised should be deep to platysma\",\"e\":\"The flaps must be raised deep to platysma, which lies superficially to the target planes.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The incision is made vertically at the level of the cricoid\",\"e\":\"The incision in thyroidectomy is made following the skin creases at about 4 cm above the sternoclavicular joints.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The strap muscles are commonly divided\",\"e\":\"The majority of surgeons rarely divide strap muscles (namely the sterothyroid muscle at its insertion on the thyroid cartilage) to gain exposure to the superior pole during thyroidectomy; they can usually be retracted out of the way.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old patient in the intensive care unit following liver transplant surgery has a metabolic alkalosis.\\n\\nWhich one of the following biochemical abnormalities is most specifically indicative of this?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following biochemical abnormalities is most specifically indicative of this?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acidic urine ..C.\",\"e\":\"Urine pH (normal 4.6-8.0) is highly variable (ie depending on time of day, diet, etc.) and is therefore not specific.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"High arterial blood ph (low hydrogen-ion concentration)\",\"e\":\"Arterial pH is increased in both metabolic and respiratory alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"High arterial partial pressure of carbon dioxide P(CO2)\",\"e\":\"A high P(COz) can occur in metabolic alkalosis as a result of respiratory compensation, but it is also a feature of respiratory acidosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"High plasma bicarbonate concentration\",\"e\":\"Plasma bicarbonate is always increased in metabolic alkalosis and can be low in chronic respiratory alkalosis. It can be raised due to compensation in respiratory acidosis, but overali is most specific for metabolic alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypochloraemia\",\"e\":\"Hypochloraemia is present in metabolic alkalosis due to a loss of gastric acid, but may not occur with alkalosis from other causes.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 77-year-old man presents with a history of vomiting undigested food. Routine biochemistry shows a serum bicarbonate concentration of 38 mmol/I (normal range HCO=: 24-30 mmol/1).\\n\\nWhich one of the following findings would most suggest that he had a chronic metabolic alkalosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following findings would most suggest that he had a chronic metabolic alkalosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alkaline urine\",\"e\":\"In prolonged metabolic alkalosis, the urine may become acidic (not alkalotic) as a result of increased proximal renal tubular bicarbonate resorption (a consequence of hypochloraemia)\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Base excess 18 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"The base excess provides no additional information: it is directly related to the high bicarbonate concentration\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Elevated arterial PaCO:\",\"e\":\"The development of hypercapnia as compensation for metabolic alkalosis, ie resulting in elevated arterial Pa(CO) tends to take some time.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemia\",\"e\":\"Gastric secretions contain about 10 mmol/1 potassium and, although potassium depletion is likely to worsen with prolonged vomiting, hypokalaemia itself can manifest at any time, and is not suggestive of a chronic metabolic alkalosis\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypomagnesaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypomagnesaemia is frequently found in patients with potassium depletion.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old woman, on nasogastric aspiration for paralytic ileus following surgery develops a metabolic alkalosis. She continues to have a high output through the NGT and her bowels are not yet working.\\n\\nWhich one of the following intravenous fluids would be the preferred treatment for the alkalosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following intravenous fluids would be the preferred treatment for the alkalosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"5% dextrose\",\"e\":\"Dextrose 5% contains no chloride so this would not be appropriate for correction of the alkalosis\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Dextrose saline\",\"e\":\"While dextrose saline does contain chloride ions it is insufficient for treatment of the alkalosis, and therefore would not be the intravenous fluid of choice\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Normal (0.9%) saline\",\"e\":\"The metabolic alkalosis secondary to a loss of gastric acid is a hypochloraemic alkalosis. This is perpetuated by the hypochloraemia, which prevents renal excretion of the excess bicarbonate since its proximal tubular reabsorption (with sodium) is enhanced. Provision of adequate chloride ions, as with normal (0.9%) saline, allows the excess bicarbonate to be excreted and corrects the alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ringer's lactate\",\"e\":\"Ringer's lactate is inappropriate, since the metabolism of the lactate that it contains produces bicarbonate which would exacerbate the alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Twice normal (1.8%) saline\",\"e\":\"Hypertonic saline is occasionally used for treating severe hyponatraemia but has no place in this clinical situation.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A child is admitted with likely pyloric stenosis. They have had persistent vomiting and appear very dehydrated. Which one of the following is associated with a metabolic alkalosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is associated with a metabolic alkalosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A fall in serum bicarbonate . C\",\"e\":\"A rise, not fall, in serum bicarbonate would be associated with a metabolic alkalosis\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypokalemia\",\"e\":\"Serum potassium levels are closely linked with serum H- levels via the sodium potassium ATPase pump. This cell membrane pump principally exchanges intracellular sodium ions with extracellular potassium ions to maintain the cell membrane potential. However, potassium ions compete with hydrogen ions in the exchange pump and therefore in the presence of hypokalaemia, more hydrogen ions will move into the intracellular compartment via this pump. Conversely, in the presence of hyperkalaemia, less hydrogen ions will move out of the extracellular compartment which result in a metabolic acidosis\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypovolaemic shock\",\"e\":\"Hypovolaemic shock results in reduced tissue perfusion and anaerobic respiration, which produces a metabolic lactic acidosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hyperventilation\",\"e\":\"During a metabolic alkalosis, respiratory compensation occurs with hypoventilation aiming to raise Pa(COz) and subsequently increase hydrogen ions, which of course lowers pH.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ketosis\",\"e\":\"Ketones are acidic, therefore ketosis would be associated with a metabolic acidosis\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient's VBG and note significant biochemical abnormalities. You note the pH is high, as well as the bicarbonate, with a normal pCO2 You suspect the patient has a metabolic alkalosis.\\n\\nWhich one the following conditions would result in a metabolic alkalosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one the following conditions would result in a metabolic alkalosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute shortness of breath\",\"e\":\"The question refers to a metabolic, not respiratory, alkalosis. Hyperventilation in respiratory distress lowers Pa(CO¿) and subsequently increases arterial pH.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastric outlet obstruction\",\"e\":\"Repeated vomiting in gastric outlet obstruction, results in the loss of hydrochloric acid and a rise in plasma bicarbonate. Bicarbonate levels rise partly because the gastric outlet obstruction results in a decrease in pancreatic exocrine bicarbonate secretion, which is the normal response to the presence of acidic gastric contents in the duodenum. A secondary hyperaldosteronism will also occur as a response to dehydration resulting in renal potassium loss in preference for sodium reabsorption. With the loss of chloride in hydrochloric acid, the net result is a hypokalaemic. hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Addison's disease\",\"e\":\"Addison's disease results in primary adrenal insufficiency which would ultimately result in hyperkalaemia, and subsequently a metabolic acidosis, not alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A patient taking potassium sparing diuretics\",\"e\":\"Potassium sparing diuretics may well result in a hyperkalaemia. Serum potassium levels are intimately linked with serum H- levels via the sodium potassium ATPase. This cell membrane pump principally exchanges intracellular sodium ions with extracellular potassium ions to maintain the cell membrane potential. However, potassium ions compete with hydrogen ions in the exchange pump. Consequently, in the presence of hyperkalaemia, less hydrogen ions move out of the extracellular compartment which results in a metabolic acidosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Salicylate overdose\",\"e\":\"In salicylate overdose, there is a mixed acid-base disorder of respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-oid woman is reviewed in the Emergency Department resus, after collapsing with abdominal pain. It is suspected that she may have small bowel ischaemia. What metabolic picture would most likely be seen on blood gas analysis?\",\"lead\":\"What metabolic picture would most likely be seen on blood gas analysis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Compensated metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"Given the acute nature of the patient's condition, they are likely to have a significant metabolic acidosis and while there may be an element of respiratory compensation through hyperventilation it is unlikely to be compensated.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Metabolic acidosis and increased anion gap\",\"e\":\"The most likely abnormality is a metabolic acidosis secondary to significant organ ischaemia and lactic acidosis. By definition, this patient will have a low arterial pH and a low bicarbonate concentration. An increased anion gap will also be seen\\n\\nThe anion gap may be calculated by:\\n\\n[Na*] + [K+] - [CI] - [НСО:-]. The normal anion gap is 8-16 mmol/l. The anion gap is a useful tool in differentiating between an acidosis due to the accumulation of organic acids eg lactic acid (as in this patient) and acidosis that are secondary to the loss of base or ingestion of inorganic acid where there will be a normal anion gap\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Metabolic acidosis and normal anion gap\",\"e\":\"As discussed, the anion gap will be increased. A normal anion gap would suggest the loss of base or ingestive of an inorganic acid.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Metabolic alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Due to mesenteric ischaemia and subsequent anaerobic respiration a metabolic acidosis will occur through the increased production of lactic acid.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Respiratory acidosis\",\"e\":\"A respiratory acidosis occurs through hypoventilation. Given the acute nature of their illness, hyperventilation is more likely, and the underlying pathology will produce a significant metabolic acidosis.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old patient was seen in the Emergency Department with metabolic acidosis and a decreased anion gap on their VBG. On the second VBG their parameters improve.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause for the first anion gap calculation in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause for the first anion gap calculation in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypoalbuminaemia\",\"e\":\"The anion gap is caused by a difference in the routinely measured positively charged (Na+ and K+ and negatively charged chloride Cl- and bicarbonate HCO3-) ions in the serum. The decreased anion gap is unrelated to metabolic acidosis, but a decreased gap caused by: (1) hypoalbuminaemia (decreased anions); (2) hypercalcaemia, hypermagnesaemia, lithium intoxication (increased cations); or (3) by plasma cell dyscrasia and monoclonal proteins or (4) halide (bromide or iodide) intoxication. Albumin is a negatively charged protein and its loss from the serum results in the retention of other negatively charged ions such as chloride and bicarbonate. This reduces the gap between measured anions and cations.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypocalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypocalcaemia will result in decreased cations which increases the anion gap.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypomagnesaemia\",\"e\":\"Again, hypomagnesaemia will result in decreased cations which increases the anion gap.\\n\\nThe anion gap may be calculated by:\\n\\n[Na*] + [K*]-[CI]— [HCOs] The normal anion gap is 8-16 mmol/L. The anion gap is a useful tool in differentiating between an acidosis due to the accumulation of organic acids (ie resulting from a lactic acidosis which increases the gap) and acidosis that are secondary to the loss of base or ingestion of inorganic acid where there will be a normal anion gap.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lactic acidosis\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Uraemia\",\"e\":\"Uraemia, most commonly seen in the context of chronic renal failure (which is unlikely in a young patient as described in the case), produces a metabolic acidosis with either a normal or increased anion gap.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old man is brought to the Emergency Department with a femoral fracture. He is in a lot of pain and his respiratory rate is 30. His blood pressure and heart rate are normal. Before proceeding to theatre, the anaesthetist requests a blood gas and this confirms respiratory alkalosis.\\n\\nRegarding the patient's acid-base balance, which one of the following statements is the most accurate?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding the patient's acid-base balance, which one of the following statements is the most accurate?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It could be corrected by rebreathing into a bag exercises\",\"e\":\"Rebreathing into a bag (and similar exercises) causes expired CO_ to be stored in this reservoir and on inspiration increases F CO2, increasing the CO, reabsorbed and having some effect in correcting the alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"High risk of tetany due to changes in calcium-protein binding\",\"e\":\"Hyperventilating leads to an increase in pH due to a decreased partial pressure of arterial carbon dioxide, causing an increase in affinity of albumin to bind Ca. This leads to a fall in free calcium causing hypocalcemia. Tetany is a symptom of severe hypocalcaemia. Although tetany secondary to hyperventilation has been documented, it is not a very high risk in both patients.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"An increase in the plasma carbonic acid concentration\",\"e\":\"There will be a decrease, not increase, in plasma carbonic acid concentration due to hyperventilation\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Elevation of serum bicarbonate concentration\",\"e\":\"Hyperventilation causes a respiratory alkalosis. If it were prolonged, then compensation would occur by reduction in serum bicarbonate.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The P(CO,) will be > 5 kPa\",\"e\":\"The P(COz) would be <5 kPa which represents hypocarbia. The normal range for P(COz) is 4.6-6.0 kPa.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"You are on the intensive care ward round reviewing a surgical patient. The intensive care doctor hands you a blood gas result and states that your patient has a metabolic alkalosis.\\n\\nThis state is most commonly seen in patients with which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"This state is most commonly seen in patients with which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aspirin poisoning\",\"e\":\"In salicylate overdose, there is a mixed acid-base disorder of respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hyperventilation\",\"e\":\"Hyperventilation would cause a respiratory alkalosis, if this period is prolonged then a compensatory metabolic acidosis may occur.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoglycaemia\",\"e\":\"During hypoglycaemia, metabolism of lipids occurs resulting in the production of ketones, which by nature are acidic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pancreatic fistula\",\"e\":\"A pancreatic fistula would cause a metabolic acidosis due to the loss of HCOs:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Protracted vomiting\",\"e\":\"Metabolic alkalosis is characterised by a primary rise in the extracellular HCO: concentration with a consequent fall in hydrogen ion concentration. Causes include loss of unbuffered hydrogen ion as in gastric aspiration, vomiting with pyloric stenosis, mineralocorticoid excess (Cushing's syndrome, Conn's syndrome) and potassium depletion. It is not usually a feature of vomiting if the pylorus s patent, as there is additional loss of HCO; secretion from the upper intestine.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A patient is critically unwell. While starting basic interventions you obtain a blood gas which shows abnormalities in their base excess. You make a management plan and on a repeat gas you are pleased to see the patient has deteriorated no further.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct with regard to the standard base excess?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct with regard to the standard base excess?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It has a normal range of +6 to -6\",\"e\":\"The normal range is +2 to -2 and is calculated from the amount of acid or base required to return the blood sample to a pH of 7.4\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is likely to be negative in a patient with ischaemic bowel\",\"e\":\"Base excess is likely to be negative in most conditions arising due to ischaemia. This is due to anaerobic respiration and increased production of lactic acid\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It requires albumin concentration for its calculation\",\"e\":\"Albumin concentration is not required for calculation of the base excess, unlike the anion gap calculation\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is likely to be negative in a patient with persistent vomiting\",\"e\":\"With persistent vomiting, there is a flow of hydrogen ions from the body, leading to a metabolic alkalosis\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is of little direct clinical use\",\"e\":\"It is a useful indicator of the severity of metabolic disturbance. The large the base excess the greater the metabolic disturbance.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old man undergoes anterior resection. His routine post-operative bloods are checked 12 h post-operatively\\n\\nWhat is the most likely metabolic response to this surgical trauma?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely metabolic response to this surgical trauma?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Urinary sodium loss\",\"e\":\"The endocrine response to surgery is aimed at galvanising the body's metabolism to achieve increased catabolism so mobilising substrates to provide glucose sources. This is characterised by increased secretion of pituitary hormones and activation of the sympathetic nervous system. Increased serum cortisol leads to sodium and water retention.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased secretion of antidiuretic hormone (ADH)\",\"e\":\"The most pronounced metabolic response to trauma begins to fall off approximately 24 h after the surgery. ADH levels rise as part of the endocrine response to stress\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Reduced urea production\",\"e\":\"The catabolism that accompanies trauma increases urea production and excretion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduced serum cortisol\",\"e\":\"During the stress response to trauma, such as surgery, cortisol levels rise.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased glomerular filtration rate\",\"e\":\"Glomerular filtration rate decreases in response to surgery, this is usually related to hypovolaemic pre-renal failure due to fluid losses intraoperatively.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old woman undergoes right breast mastectomy and immediate reconstruction with a free flap. She is closely monitored post-operatively. Her physiological parameters are monitored in an attempt to assess her stress response to surgery.\\n\\nWhich one of the following hormones would be most dramatically raised in this assessment?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following hormones would be most dramatically raised in this assessment?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cortisol\",\"e\":\"The endocrine response to surgery is aimed at galvanising the body's metabolism to achieve increased catabolism so mobilising substrates to provide glucose sources. This is characterised by increased secretion of pituitary hormones and activation of the sympathetic nervous system. Cortisol is produced in the adrenal cortex and its production is stimulated by ACTH it promotes gluconeogenesis and inhibits cell use of glucose leading to increased bleod glucose levels.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Insulin\",\"e\":\"Insulin inhibits catabolism and lipolysis, so insulin levels would be reduced post-surgery. Insulin secretion is thought to be inhibited by adrenergic action on beta-islet cells in the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oxytocin\",\"e\":\"Oxytocin levels are likely to be unaffected following surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Glucagon\",\"e\":\"Glucagon stimulates hepatic glycogenolysis and, while mildly rises in this hormone are seen during the stress response, it does not make a major contributor to the hyperglycaemic state during the stress response.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thyroxine\",\"e\":\"Thyroxine has a depressant effect on cortisol, and would not be raised in response to surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A patient with a paralytic ileus is draining 2 litres of fluid a day through a nasogastric tube, and is subsequently in metabolic alkalosis.\\n\\nWhich one of the following fluids would be the most appropriate to replace the fluid being lost in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following fluids would be the most appropriate to replace the fluid being lost in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Compound sodium lactate (Hartmann's solution)\",\"e\":\"Compound sodium lactate solution should be administered with particular caution to patients at risk for alkalosis. Lactate is metabolised to bicarbonate, administration may result in, or worsen, metabolic alkalosis. Seizure may be precipitated by the alkalosis induced by lactate but this is uncommon.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"5% dextrose\",\"e\":\"This dextrose solution does not contain chloride and therefore would be inappropriate to use in this case.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"10% dextrose\",\"e\":\"Again, this does not contain chloride and would not be useful in this clinical scenario.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"0.18% sodium chloride with 4% dextrose ('dextrose saline')\",\"e\":\"'Dextrose saline' contains insufficient chloride for the purpose of replacing lost chloride ions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"0.9% sodium chloride ('normal saline')\",\"e\":\"In this situation, it is essential to supply sufficient chloride ions to replace the chloride being lost in the gastric fluid (gastric juice is essentially dilute hydrochloric acid). If this is not done, a metabolic alkalosis can ensue. The appropriate fluid is 'normal saline'. If hypokalaemia is also present, potassium chloride can be added to the normal saline bags.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with chronic hyponatraemia and their sodium concentration is 112 mmol/l (normal range: 135-145 mmol/l)\\n\\nWhich one of the following findings would most suggest a diagnosis of syndrome of inappropriate (secretion of) antidiuretic hormone (SIADH) in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following findings would most suggest a diagnosis of syndrome of inappropriate (secretion of) antidiuretic hormone (SIADH) in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Normal cortisol response to ACTH C\",\"e\":\"Addison's disease, another cause of chronic hyponatraemia, is excluded by the cortisol response to adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH), as ultimately SIADH is a diagnosis of exclusion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Plasma albumin concentration 28 g/l (normal range albumin: 35-55 g/l)\",\"e\":\"The low plasma albumin concentration could just reflect dilution of the plasma by excess water, but it could also suggest chronic liver disease, another cause of chronic hyponatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Plasma osmolality 248 mOsmol/kg (normal range 275-295 mOsmol/kg)\",\"e\":\"The low plasma osmolality reflects the hyponatraemia, as sodium is the principal determinant of extracellular fluid osmolality, however this is not diagnostic of any particular aetiology of hyponatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urinary osmolality 850 mOsmol/kg (normal range 500-800 mOsmol/kg)\",\"e\":\"The most significant finding is that the urine is concentrated relative to plasma. This is inappropriate, in that a low plasma osmolality should suppress antidiuretic hormone (ADH) secretion and lead to the formation of a maximally dilute urine\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urinary sodium concentration < mmol/l (normal value 20 mmol/l)\",\"e\":\"A low urine sodium excretion would suggest extrarenal sodium depletion as a cause of hyponatraemia.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman with diet-controlled type II diabetes is making a good recovery 7 days after a partial small bowel resection for trauma. She receives parenteral nutrition with additional 0.9% saline and, because of a previous deep vein thrombosis, prophylactic heparin. Prior to admission she was otherwise well and on no medication. Serum electrolyte results are:\\n\\nInvestigation Result Normal value\\n\\nSodium (Na-) 129 mmol/l 135-145 mmol/l\\n\\nPotassium (K+) 6.5 mmol/i 3.5-5.0 mmol/l\\n\\nBicarbonate 24 mmol/l 24-30 mmol/l\\n\\n8.5 mmol/l 2.5-6.5 mmol/l Urea\\n\\nCreatinine 120 umol/l 50-120 umol/l\\n\\n10.2 mmol/l 3.5-5.5 mmol/1 Glucose\\n\\nHer potassium concentration has risen over the past 3 days. The potassium content of the parenteral feed has been reduced from 60 to 20 mmol/24 h during this period. Urine output is appropriate to her fluid input. Her urinary ketones, red cell, white cell and platelet counts are all in the normal range\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause of the hyperkalaemia?\\n\\nHer potassium concentration has risen over the past 3 days. The potassium content of the parenteral feed has been reduced from 60 to 20 mmol/24 h during this period. Urine output is appropriate to her fluid input. Her urinary ketones, red cell, white cell and platelet counts are all in the normal range.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause of the hyperkalaemia?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of the hyperkalaemia?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Heparin treatment\",\"e\":\"The heparin is most likely to be responsible: heparin inhibits aldosterone secretion by the adrenal cortex, leading to impaired renal potassium excretion, particularly in patients with diabetes or those who are acidotic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Overprovision of potassium in the parenteral feed\",\"e\":\"Approximately 20 mmol/24 h is the minimum obligatory potassium output, while the typical potassium requirements for patients on parenteral feeding are 40-80 mmol/24 h. As the potassium load of the parental feed has been reduced, this is an unlikely cause.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Primary adrenal failure (Addison's disease)\",\"e\":\"Incipient adrenal failure could have been made overt by the stress of surgery, but this is uncommon.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pseudohyperkalaemia\",\"e\":\"Pseudohyperkalaemia is hyperkalaemia occurring as a result of a loss of potassium from white cells and platelets during clotting, usually seen in patients with high white cell or platelet counts. Typically, the plasma potassium concentration is significantly lower than the serum potassium concentration in this condition.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Renal impairment\",\"e\":\"The elevated urea may be due to an excessive provision of amino acids, but neither it nor the creatinine level suggest sufficient renal impairment to cause such a severe hyperkalaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man is admitted to hospital with persistent vomiting. He is clinically dehydrated and hypotensive. His results are as follows:\\n\\nResult Investigation Normal value\\n\\n124 mmol/l 135-145 mmol/i Sodium (blood)\\n\\nPotassium (blood) 5.2 mmol/l 3.5-5.0 mmol/l\\n\\n9.8 mmol/l 2.5-6.5 mmol/l Urea (blood)\\n\\n96 mmol/l 50-120 mmol/l Creatinine (blood)\\n\\n20 mmol/l Urine sodium concentration (on admission) 62 mmol/i\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause of the hyponatraemia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely cause of the hyponatraemia?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adrenal failure\",\"e\":\"Natriuresis in a dehydrated, hyponatraemic patient suggests that there is uncontrolled renal loss of sodium, as occurs in adrenal failure:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cerebral salt wasting\",\"e\":\"Cerebral salt wasting can also cause dehydration and hyponatraemia due to excessive natriuresis, but typically occurs following a head injury or brain surgery, which is not the clinically history given.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastrointestinal fluid loss\",\"e\":\"Hyponatraemia and dehydration due to gastrointestinal fluid loss or sodium deficiency due to a low intake should lead to renal conservation of sodium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Low sodium intake\",\"e\":\"Again, low dietary sodium should lead to renal conservation of sodium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuresis (SIADH)\",\"e\":\"While SIADH is an important cause of hyponatraemia and sodium excretion may be high, the hyponatraemia is due to water excess and patients are not dehydrated.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"The serum electrolyte analysis of a patient shows hypokalaemia, with a serum potassium of 2.8 mmol/l. (normal range 3.5-5.0 mmol/l). The patient is sent for an electrocardiogram (ECG) to ensure there is no impact on the myocardium.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause of this abnormality?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of this abnormality?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Trimethoprim administration\",\"e\":\"Trimethoprim (an antibiotic commonly used to treat urinary tract infections) blocks apical sodium channels in the distal convoluted tubules and as a result reduces the transmembrane voltage potential and inhibits potassium secretion. This can result in hyperkalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Liddle syndrome\",\"e\":\"Hypokalaemia can be caused by decreased intake of potassium but is usually caused by excessive losses of potassium in the urine or from the gastrointestinal tract. Liddle's syndrome is a rare autosomal-dominant disorder characterised by severe hypertension and hypokalaemia. Liddle syndrome is caused by unrestrained sodium reabsorption in the distal nephron due to one of several mutations found in genes encoding for epithelial sodium channel subunits. Inappropriately high reabsorption of sodium results in both hypertension and renal potassium wasting.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"Serum potassium levels are intimately linked with serum H* levels via the sodium potassium ATPase. This cell membrane pump principally exchanges intracellular sodium ions with extracellular potassium ions to maintain the cell membrane potential. Potassium ions compete with hydrogen ions in the exchange pump and therefore in the presence of metabolic acidosis more hydrogen ions will move into the intracellular compartment via this pump, maintaining the serum potassium levels..\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal failure <\",\"e\":\"Significant renal impairment would cause a hyperkalaemia due to decreased excretion of potassium. Indeed, in chronic renal failure hyperkalaemia is an indication for dialysis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rhabdomyolysis\",\"e\":\"Due to muscle breakdown in rhabdomyolysis, increased serum potassium levels are seen due to leaching of potassium during cell lysis.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following conditions is most likely to cause hypervolaemic hypernatraemia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following conditions is most likely to cause hypervolaemic hypernatraemia?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PY Burns\",\"e\":\"Significant fluid losses occur with burns which can cause hypernatraemia, however this would be hypovolaemic in nature.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Central diabetes insipidus\",\"e\":\"In central diabetes insipidus there is a lack of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) secretion, this leads to excessive urination and thirst, ultimately resulting in hypovolaemic hypernatraemia. Treatment is with desmopressin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diarrhoea\",\"e\":\"Similarly with burns, diarrhoea causes significant fluid losses and a hypovolaemio hypernatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hyperalimentation)\",\"e\":\"Hypernatraemia in rare cases is associated with volume overload. In this case, hypernatraemia results from a grossly elevated sodium intake associated with limited access to water. One example is excessive administration of hypertonic sodium bicarbonate given during cardiopulmonary resuscitation or during the treatment of lactic acidosis. Hypernatraemia can also be caused by the administration of hypertonic saline or hyperalimentation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus\",\"e\":\"In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus the kidneys fail to respond to ADH causes significant fluid losses and hypovolaemic hypernatraemia.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old alcoholic is admitted with acute pancreatitis. Together with his admission screen, you check with nutritional status and he is noted be deficient in magnesium. Which one of the following can be seen with Mg depletion?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following can be seen with Mg depletion?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Has to be corrected by infusion only (\",\"e\":\"Mild magnesium deficiency may be treated with oral supplements but in severe cases slow intravenous infusion is required.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Magnesium in the plasma is a good indicator of total body magnesium\",\"e\":\"Plasma magnesium levels are not a good indicator of total body magnesium as fluctuations in protein levels will significantly affect the plasma level, however this only accounts for around 0.3% of total body magnesium. The rest is predominantly found in the soft tissues and bones.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is associated with large bowel resection\",\"e\":\"The main site of magnesium absorption is the ileum, so large bowel resection would not alter magnesium levels.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is associated with muscle weakness\",\"e\":\"Hypomagnesaemia should be suspected in patients with malnutrition, excess alcohol ingestion, prolonged diuretic treatment and chronic mineralocorticoid excess. Both alcohol and diuretics increase magnesium excretion. Hypomagnesaemia can produce hypocalcaemia (and subsequent muscle weakness) and contribute to the persistence of hypokalaemia (renal potassium wasting).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Produces similar clinical effects to hyponatraemia\",\"e\":\"The symptoms of hyponatraemia range from lethargy and anorexia to agitation, disorientation, seizures and coma depending on severity.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"One of your patients has a high output ileostomy. His blood tests come back showing a Na* result of 130 mmol/I (normal range 135-145 mmol/I). Which one of the following is a feature of sodium depletion?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a feature of sodium depletion?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bradycardia\",\"e\":\"Due to sodium depletion and subsequently loss of water causing hypovolaemia, tachycardia ensues to maintain cardiac output and tissue perfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased blood pressure\",\"e\":\"The hypovolaemic state in sodium depletion (due to fluid loss) results in hypotension.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased body weight\",\"e\":\"The loss of water associated with sodium depletion will reduce body weight.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased cardiac output\",\"e\":\"Cardiac output is reduced as a consequence of water loss associated with sodium depletion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Muscle weakness\",\"e\":\"In surgery, sodium depletion is synonymous with extracellular fluid loss: gastrointestinal losses (vomiting, diarrhoea, fistula). When there is loss of sodium, water follows, eventually causing hypovolaemia. In severe sodium depletion, the patient will be hypovolaemic and eventually will become tachycardic and hypotensive. This subsequently stimulates the body's homoeostatic mechanisms. The clinical features of sodium depletion are broad and can include muscle weakness, as well as seizures, coma and ultimately death.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"Routine blood tests on an 30-year-old woman on the Surgical Ward reveal the following results:\\n\\nNormal value Result Investigation\\n\\n129 mmol/l 135-145 mmol/l Sodium (Nat)\\n\\n3.5 mmol/l 3.5-5.0 mol/l Potassium (K)\\n\\n6.0 mmol/l 2.5-6.5 mmol/l Urea\\n\\nCreatinine 90 mmol/l 50-120 mmol/l\\n\\nWith regard to hyponatraemia, which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"With regard to hyponatraemia, which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyponatraemia could be due to syndrome of inappropriate antid uretic hormone (SIADH) if the urine osmolality is low\",\"e\":\"If hyponatraemia is due to SIADH, then urine osmolality would be high, as antidiuretic hormone results in fluid retention and concentrated urine.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Elevated plasma lipid levels falsely raise sodium levels\",\"e\":\"Elevated lipid levels can result in a pseudo-hyponatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyponatraemia is usually of no concern in such a patient\",\"e\":\"The patient can be dehydrated or well hydrated with hyponatraemia. In a dehydrated patient, it may be due to renal failure, diuretic excess, vomiting, diarrhoea or loss via a fistula. Post-operative hyponatraemia is most commonly dilutional. It may present as confusion, coma or convulsions and, as such, action must be taken to correct the abnormality.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It should be corrected with 0.18% dextrose saline\",\"e\":\"Hyponatraemia should be corrected slowly by fluid restriction to prevent central pontine myelinolysis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Fluid restriction may help in restoring sodium levels to within the normal reference range\",\"e\":\"Fluid restriction can be helpful in restoring the patient's sodium levels to within the normal range. A pragmatic approach to this clinical situation is to first assess the patient's fluid status. If the patient's fluid status is hypovolaemic, treatment with 0.9% saline may be required. If hypervolaemic, fluid restriction can be helpful. Urinary sodium and paired osmolalities will also be helpful in managing the hyponatraemic patient.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old woman is admitted with right iliac fossa pain, nitrate-positive urine dip and impaired renal function. Her blood results are as follows:\\n\\nResult Investigation Normal value\\n\\n24 mmol/l 2.5-6.5 mmol/l Urea\\n\\n215 mmol/l 50-120 umol/l Creatinine\\n\\nPotassium (K) 6.4 mmol/l 3.5-5.0 mmol/l\\n\\nTreatment needs to be initiated to counteract this.\\n\\nWhich treatment would be the best initial management?\",\"lead\":\"Which treatment would be the best initial management?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Calcichew*\",\"e\":\"Intravenous calcium should be given for myocardial stability in the context of hyperkalaemia. Oral Calcichew® is not indicated.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inhaled steroids\",\"e\":\"Inhaled salbutamol, rather than steroids, reduce high potassium.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Insulin and dextrose\",\"e\":\"Insulin increases cellular potassium uptake and is therefore useful in the treatment of hyperkalaemia. It is given with dextrose to prevent hypoglycaemia and aid driving potassium into the cells.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intravenous fluids\",\"e\":\"Intravenous fluids will help with improving pre-renal failure, but the raised potassium level can be life-threatening, so it needs urgent correction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Gentamicin\",\"e\":\"This patient potentially has a renal tract infection if nitrate-positive, though this needs correlation with the patient's history, but gentamicin is nephrotoxic and should be avoided, especially at the 5 mg/kg dose.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A patient undergoes a total hip replacement under the orthopaedic team. Post-operatively their bowels stop working and they develop abdominal distention. A diagnosis of ileus is made and the patient is referred for a general surgical opinion. In metabolic alkalosis associated with prolonged nasogastric aspiration in post-operative ileus, what is the most important cause of the acid-base disturbance?\",\"lead\":\"In metabolic alkalosis associated with prolonged nasogastric aspiration in post-operative ileus, what is the most important cause of the acid-base disturbance?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypoventilation\",\"e\":\"Hypoventilation is a compensatory change; on its own, hypoventilation causes carbon dioxide retention and respiratory acidosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased renal bicarbonate reabsorption\",\"e\":\"Increased renal bicarbonate reabsorption (needed to allow adequate renal sodium reabsorption in the presence of hypochloraemia) contributes to the alkalosis but does not cause it.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Loss of gastric acid\",\"e\":\"Loss of unbuffered gastric acid is the primary cause of the metabolic alkalosis seen under these circumstances of ileus and significant nasogastric aspiration. Adequate replacement of the fluid lost with intravenous physiological saline is paramount to managing this alkalosis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Potassium depletion\",\"e\":\"Gastric secretions contain about 10 mmol/l of potassium; this maintains the alkalosis but does not cause it.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Secondary aldosteronism\",\"e\":\"Secondary aldosteronism, as a result of extracellular fluid loss, helps to maintain the alkalosis through excretion of hydrogen ions:\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":60,\"stem\":\"On your post-operative ward round, you discover that one of your patients has third-space fluid loss following a cholecystectomy. Which of the following statements best describes these losses?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following statements best describes these losses?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Can be up to 50 ml/kg/hour\",\"e\":\"In moderately major operations, such as cholecystectomy, such losses would be approximately 3 ml/kg/hour.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Has a fluid composition similar to interstitial fluid\",\"e\":\"Third-space losses are the temporary internal losses of extracellular fluid into a space that does not participate in the normal transport of nutrients or waste products, with a composition similar to that of interstitial fluid. Examples would include peripheral oedema or pleural effusions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Include losses from perforated, bleeding duodenal ulcer\",\"e\":\"This would constitute an intravascular loss of fluid, not third-space losses.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is best replaced by dextrose saline\",\"e\":\"Dextrose saline increases the free water content and so leads to hyponatraemia. As such, it should be avoided in the management of third-space losses. Balanced salt solutions should be used to replace third-space losses.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is unrelated to the degree of tissue injury\",\"e\":\"In more extensive operations, such as aortic aneurysm repair, third-space fluid losses could initially be 10-20 ml/kg/hour.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man is admitted into the Emergency Department with the police. There is a concern the patient has been deliberately poisoned.\\n\\nWhich category of hypoxia results from tissue exposure to metabolic inhibitors such as cyanide?\",\"lead\":\"Which category of hypoxia results from tissue exposure to metabolic inhibitors such as cyanide?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Stagnant hypoxia\",\"e\":\"Stagnant hypoxia results from reduced perfusion of tissues, producing a fall in local arterial pressure of oxygen (p, (Oz)) when there is inadequate oxygen delivery and extraction to meet tissue demands. An example of this would be a crush injury.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Anaemic hypoxia\",\"e\":\"Anaemic hypoxia occurs with substantial reductions in blood haemoglobin concentration. In this case, although values of p, (02) may be in the normal range, blood oxygen content, and hence oxygen delivery, is reduced, resulting in local tissue hypoxia. Similarly, reducing the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen also results in a fall in blood oxygen content and may induce tissue hypoxia\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoxic hypoxia\",\"e\":\"Hypoxic hypoxia results from a fall in pa (02) secondary to a reduction in inspired pOz (such as occurs at altitude).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Historaxi hypoxia\",\"e\":\"Histotoxic hypoxia occurs in the presence of metabolic poisons, such as cyanide, that act intracellularly to inhibit the mitochondrial respiratory electron transport chain.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"None of the above\",\"e\":\"Hypoxic challenges can be categorised into several classes, according to the underlying cause. This question refers to histotoxic hypoxia.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"A patient is due to undergo a gastric bypass procedure for morbid obesity. You take an multi-disciplinary team approach to ensure the best post-operative outcome for the patient. This includes reviews by the gastroenterologist, dieticians and clinical psychologist.\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most important causative factor in the development of obesity in the majority of patients?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most important causative factor in the development of obesity in the majority of patients?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Energy intake in excess of expenditure\",\"e\":\"The ultimate cause of obesity is always an intake of energy in excess of expenditure, but many factors govern both intake and expenditure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Genetic predisposition\",\"e\":\"There is undoubtedly a genetic predisposition in some individuals. However, environmental causes are the greater contributor.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Insulin resistance\",\"e\":\"Insulin resistance is probably a consequence of obesity, not a cause.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Intrauterine malnutrition\",\"e\":\"Intrauterine malnutrition may be important in a select few cases\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Leptin deficiency\",\"e\":\"Leptin deficiency is a very rare cause of obesity. More frequently, obese individuals are resistant to the actions of obesity.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"A 49-year-old post-menopausal woman of Southern Asian origin complains of muscle weakness. She is found to have hypocalcaemia, and X-ray examination reveals two Looser's zones in her left upper femur.\\n\\nA defect in which of the following physiological processes is most likely to be the cause of her illness?\",\"lead\":\"A defect in which of the following physiological processes is most likely to be the cause of her illness?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Absorption of calcium from the gut\",\"e\":\"The findings in this woman suggest osteomalacia, and the most important reason for impaired mineralisation of bone is reduced intestinal calcium absorption and consequently vitamin D deficiency. Decreased availability of calcium to mineralise bone leads to increased osteoblastic activity (and hence increased osteoid formation).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Osteoblastic activity\",\"e\":\"Osteoblasts are involved in the formation of new bone, and a defect in osteoblastic activity would not result in hypocalcaemia. Indeed, hypocalcaemia triggers increased osteoblastic activity.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Osteoclastic activity\",\"e\":\"Osteoporosis (post-menopausal osteoporosis is due to increased osteoclastic activity) is not associated with hypocalcaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Parathyroid hormone (PTH) secretion\",\"e\":\"Hypocalcaemia causes increased PTH secretion (secondary hyperparathyroidism), which stimulates renal calcium reabsorption (hence reduced excretion). So while this woman may have increased osteoblastic activity and increased PTH secretion, they are both secondary to vitamin D deficiency and decreased intestinal absorption of calcium.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Renal excretion of calcium\",\"e\":\"As discussed, renal calcium reabsorption is stimulated by PTH secretion as a response to hypocalcaemia secondary to reduced gut absorption.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":70,\"stem\":\"A patient is recovering on ITU following a laryngectomy. They are slowly improving but a huge amount of surgical trauma has taken piace. The metabolic response to trauma has two phases: anabolism and catabolism.\\n\\nWhich of the following characterises catabolism?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following characterises catabolism?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Increased sodium output\",\"e\":\"Due to increased secretion of aldosterone, sodium retention is increased during the catabolic phase.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased urine output\",\"e\":\"During the catabolic phase of the metabolic response to trauma, increased levels of antidiuretic hormone lead to water retention, and hence reduced urine output.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gluconeogenesis\",\"e\":\"Gluconeogenesis in the liver is stimulated by cortisol which is secreted during the stress response to trauma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Posit ve nitrogen balance\",\"e\":\"Amino acids from the catabolism of muscle act as the substrate, leading to a negative nitrogen balance, which may persist for up to 2 weeks in patients with severe burns.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thrombocytopenia\",\"e\":\"Among the haematological responses are thrombocytosis and neutrophilia, primarily due to the secretion of corticosteroids such as cortisol.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":72,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old man attends the Emergency Department, feeling generally unwell and vomiting. He has well-controlled hypertension and is a heavy smoker, but is otherwise weil. He is found to be hypercalcaemic.\\n\\nTo which of the following is the hypercalcaemia most likely to be due?\",\"lead\":\"To which of the following is the hypercalcaemia most likely to be due?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypoparathyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hypoparathyroidism reduces serum calcium levels, whereas hyperparathyroidism raises it (as does hyperthyroidism).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Multiple myeloma\",\"e\":\"Bone lysis occurs in multiple myeloma, giving rise to hypercalcaemia. However, this is not the most likely cause in this patient.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Renal insufficiency\",\"e\":\"Renal failure can lead to secondary hyperparathyroidism, in turn leading to tertiary hyperparathyroidism, raising serum calcium levels through the increased secretion of parathyroid hormone, and subsequently increasing calcium resorption. This, however, is not the most likely cause, given the case history.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Carcinoma of the bronchus\",\"e\":\"Hypercalcaemia may be part of a paraneoplastic syndrome of numerous malignancies, including carcinoma of the bronchus. The possible answers in this case are multiple myeloma, renal insufficiency or carcinoma of the bronchus. Given the history that this patient is a heavy smoker, the best-fitting cause is carcinoma of the bronchus:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hyperthyroidism, not hypothyroidısm, would result in raised calcium levels.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old man has been unable to eat for 7 days because of an obstructing lesion in the oesophagus. In the endoscopy department a dilatation is carried out and an NGT placed distal to the obstruction. He is therefore able to take enteral nutrition.\\n\\nWhich of the following was the major source of fuel being oxidised by his skeletal muscles prior to NG feed starting?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following was the major source of fuel being oxidised by his skeletal muscles prior to NG feed starting?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Muscle creatine phosphate\",\"e\":\"Creatine cannot be oxidised to provide a fuel source. Its primary role is facilitating conversion of adenosine diphosphate (ADP) back to adenosine triphosphate (ATP) through the donation of phosphate groups.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Muscle glycogen\",\"e\":\"Muscle glycogen will undergo glycogenolysis to provide glucose for tissues that absolutely require it such as the brain and erythrocytes. These tissues are unable to metabolise other fuel substrates.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Muscle triglycerides\",\"e\":\"Precursors of glucose are not abundant, and most energy is stored in the fatty acyl moieties of triacylglycerols. Fatty acids cannot be converted into glucose, because acetyl coenzyme A cannot be transformed into pyruvate. In addition, the glycerol moiety of triacylglycerol can be converted into glucose, but only a limited amount is available.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Serum fatty acids\",\"e\":\"Starvation is a severe reduction in nutrient, vitamin and energy intake and is the most extreme form of malnutrition. In humans, prolonged starvation (in excess of 1-2 months) causes permanent organ damage and will eventually result in death. The only potential source of glucose is amino acids derived from the breakdown of proteins. However, proteins are not stored, and so any breakdown will necessitate a functional loss. So the second priority of metabolism in starvation is to preserve protein, which is accomplished by shifting the fuel being used from glucose to fatty acids and ketone bodies.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serum glucose\",\"e\":\"Note that the first priority of metabolism in starvation is to provide sufficient glucose to the brain and other tissues (eg erythrocytes) that are absolutely dependent on this fuel. As such, skeletal muscle would not be metabolising serum glucose.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":78,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old woman has undergone a difficult total thyroidectomy for a papillary carcinoma. Post-operatively it is noted on day 1 that tapping the facial nerve anterior and inferior to the ear causes spasms in the facial muscles.\\n\\nWhat is the name of this clinical sign?\",\"lead\":\"What is the name of this clinical sign?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Babinski's sign\",\"e\":\"Babinski's sign is extension of the big toe rather than flexion following plantar stimulation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Chvostek's sign\",\"e\":\"Chvostek's sign involves spasms of the facial muscles occurring after tapping the facial nerve just below and in front of the ear. It is a sign of hypocalcaemia, which can occur if the parathyroid glands have been removed inadvertently.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Trosseau's sign\",\"e\":\"Trosseau's sign is also a sign of hypocalcaemia and involves contractions of the small muscles of the hand caused by occlusion of the circulation to the hand.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cullen's sign\",\"e\":\"Cullen's sign manifests as a bluish periumbilical discoloration indicative of subcutaneous intraperitoneal haemorrhage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rovsing's sign\",\"e\":\"Rovsing's sign is an indication of appendicitis and is defined as palpation in the left iliac fossa producing pain at the site of tenderness in the right iliac fossa due to movement of the inflamed parietal peritoneum.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"You are asked to see a 68-year-old man who has undergone a laparotomy for a perforated appendix 3 days previously. He has deteriorated acutely and has a pulse rate of 140 beats/min, a systolic blood pressure of 60 mmHg unresponsive to fluid resuscitation, a temperature of 39.5°C and urine output of 5 ml/h. You promptly commence empirical antibiotic therapy for sepsis secondary to a presumed postoperative collection.\\n\\nHow would you define his state of sepsis?\",\"lead\":\"How would you define his state of sepsis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bacteraemia\",\"e\":\"Bacteraemia is the presence of bacteria in the bloodstream, and while this may be a component of this man's condition, it does not define his state of sepsis\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypovolaemic shock\",\"e\":\"While this patient is shocked, this is septic in nature given the clinical history - the shock is more likely to be distributive.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sepsis\",\"e\":\"Sepsis is defined as, 'life-threatening organ dysfunction due to a dysregulated host response to infection'. A patient with sepsis may not have end organ dysfunction at the time of diagnosis, but the terminology makes clear the seriousness of the condition. Life-threatening organ dysfunction can occur if not recognised and treated swiftly.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Septic shock\",\"e\":\"The clinical history and examination for this patient is of septic shock. They have a suspected source of infection, a systemic inflammatory response, evidence of end organ dysfunction and importantly, to qualify as septic shock (not just severe sepsis), hypotension which is not responsive to appropriate intravenous fluid resuscitation. The pathogenesis of sepsis, severe sepsis septic shock is becoming increasingly understood. The 'systemic inflammatory response syndrome' (SIRS) is thought to be a central component, involving cytokine cascades, free radical production, and the release of vasoactive mediators. Terminology has changed in recent years with the current definitions below:\\n\\n• Sepsis: life-threatening organ dysfunction due to a dysregulated host response to infection (or suspected infection) • Severe sepsis: Sepsis with evidence of organ dysfunction, eg hypoxaemia, oliguria, lactic acidosis, or altered cerebral function. • Septic shock: Severe sepsis with hypotension (systolic BP < 90 mmHg) despite adequate fluid resuscitation, or the requirement for vasopressors/inotropes to maintain blood pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Severe sepsis\",\"e\":\"Severe sepsis is defined as sepsis with evidence of end organ dysfunction, such as reduced urine output, raised lactate, or hypotension. Terminology has changed in recent years with the current definition: life-threatening organ dysfunction due to a dysregulated host response to infection (or suspected infection)\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"An 87-year-old arteriopath complains of heavy chest pain post-operatively and he is suspected of having a myocardial infarction. His blood examination reveals raised urea and creatinine.\\n\\nWhat type of renal failure is this termed?\",\"lead\":\"What type of renal failure is this termed?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"S r latrogenic\",\"e\":\"Renal failure can be classified as either pre-renal, intrinsic or post-renal, and while renal failure may have an iatrogenic cause (such as nephrotoxic drugs causing intrinsic renal failure) this clinical scenario does not refer to iatrogenic cause.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intrinsic renal failure\",\"e\":\"Given the clinical history, pre-renal failure through hypovolaemia and cardiogenic shock represents the most likely cause. Of note, if renal perfusion drops significantly, acute tubular necrosis could occur, in which case there would be both a pre-renal and intrinsic renal failure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pre-renal renal failure\",\"e\":\"Renal failure is defined as failure of the kidneys to maintain the correct composition and volume of the body's internal environment. Patients undergoing major surgery are at risk of developing renal failure, particularly in the postoperative period when inadequate fluid rehydration is not uncommon. Cardiogenic shock and hypoperfusion are the likely reasons for his renal failure in this example.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Post-renal renal failure\",\"e\":\"Post-renal failure is due to outflow obstruction, which can be at the level of the uretra, bladder, ureter or renal pelvis, causing back pressure on the kidneys and ultimately failure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"This is unlikely to be renal failure\",\"e\":\"The suspected myocardial infarction and deranged renal function is suggestive of a pre-renal failure.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient who is day 2 post-operation and has developed acute renal failure. You suspect a pre-renal cause for the acute renal failure.\\n\\nWhat urinary test results would confirm this?\",\"lead\":\"What urinary test results would confirm this?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Urinary osmolality <350 mOsm/l, urinary sodium ≥20 mmol/1 and urinary creatinine >20 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"These results would not confirm a pre-renal cause. A low urinary osmolality and high urinary sodium are suggestive of a renal cause of acute renal failure. Given the patient is post surgery this could be secondary to renal ischaemia and medullary hypoxia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Urinary osmolality <350 mOsm/l, urinary sodium >40 mmol/l and urinary creatinine <20 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"These results would suggest a renal cause for acute renal failure with significant sodium loss.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Urinary osmolality >500 mOsm/l, urinary sodium <20 mmol/l and urinary creatinine >40 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"Renal hypoperfusion is associated with avid sodium retention with minimal excretion into the urine (< 20 mmol/I), increased osmolality and creatinine excretion.\\n\\nPre-renal cause Renal cause\\n\\n< 350 mOsm/l Urinary osmolality > 500 mOsm/l\\n\\n> 40 mmol/l Urinary sodium excretion < 20 mmol/l\\n\\n< 20 mmol/l > 40 mmol/l Urinary creatinine\\n\\nWhile the osmolality and creatine results suggest a pre-renal cause, the excessive sodium loss is not consistent with a pre-renal cause of acute renal failure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urinary osmolality >500 mOsm/I, urinary sodium >20 mmol/l and urinary creatinine >40 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urinary osmolality >500 mOsm/l, urinary sodium >40 mmol/1 and urinary creatinine <20 mmol/l\",\"e\":\"These results do not confirm a pre-renal cause and instead provide a mixed picture. The high urinary osmolality is suggestive of a pre-renal cause, while the high urinary sodium and low urinary creatinine is suggestive of a renal cause for the acute kidney injury.\"}]},{\"t\":43,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"A patient presents to the preoperative clinic, they are on long-term renal dialysis and are listed for subtotal parathyroidectomy for tertiary hyperparathyroidism.\\n\\nRegarding calcium and parathyroid hormone levels, which is the most likely pattern of preoperative blood results?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding calcium and parathyroid hormone levels, which is the most likely pattern of preoperative blood results?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Raised Ca2+, , raised PTH\",\"e\":\"This patient has tertiary hyperparathyroidism and will be having 3.5 of their four parathyroid hormones (PTH) removed. After a long period of secondary hyperparathyroidism, in this case due to chronic kidney disease, a patient may develop tertiary hyperparathyroidism. There is autonomous and aberrant PTH secretion due to long-term parathyroid stimulation, which fails to respond to circulating calcium levels.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Raised Ca?+ , reduced PTH\",\"e\":\"This pattern would be appropriate in a patient with a cause of hypercalcaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Reduced a\\\", raised PTH\",\"e\":\"This would be seen in secondary hyperparathyroidism, and could precede the tertiary hyperparathyroidism described in the question.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reduced Ca?+, , reduced PTH\",\"e\":\"This would suggest hypoparathyroidism, the patient's magnesium levels should be checked as low magnesium levels can inhibit the release and action of PTH.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduced Ca?+, *, normal PTH\",\"e\":\"This could indicate pseudohypoparathyroidism (normal PTH levels but the tissues are insensitive to its effect).\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"You are managing a patient on the ward with a prolonged ileus requiring total parenteral nutrition (TPN). Which one of these statements is true of this therapy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these statements is true of this therapy?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is associated with a reduced incidence of cholecystitis\",\"e\":\"There is an increased risk of gall-bladder stasis and so cholecystitis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is associated with hyperuricaemia\",\"e\":\"TPN does not cause hyperuricaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is made up to a standard prescription\",\"e\":\"TPN formula is highly variable and can be tailored to the particular patient\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It may cause a fatty liver as an early complication due to linoleic acid\",\"e\":\"Liver function tests may become deranged, and a fatty liver and gall-bladder stasis may occur: These are late complications.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It may cause hypokalaemia\",\"e\":\"Metabolic complications include: hyper-/hypoglycaemia, hyper-/hyponatraemia, hyper-/hypokalaemia and hyper-/hypocalcaemia. Deficiencies in folate, zinc, phosphate, magnesium and vitamins may occur.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old woman undergoes excision of a skin cancer and direct closure. She is advised on the ideal conditions for wound healing.\\n\\nWhich one of the following nutritional factors is required for RNA and DNA synthesis?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following nutritional factors is required for RNA and DNA synthesis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"Explanation i\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Protein\",\"e\":\"Proteins are essential for extracellular matrix (ECM) formation and an effective immune response. However, DNA and RNA can be made without the intake of protein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Vitamin A\",\"e\":\"Vitamin A is required for epithelial cell proliferation and differentiation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Vitamin B6\",\"e\":\"Vitamin Be is required for collagen crosslinking.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vitamin C\",\"e\":\"Vitamin C is necessary for hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues and deficiency increases the risk of wound dehiscence.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Zinc\",\"e\":\"Zinc is an essential trace element required for RNA and DNA synthesis and for the function of some 200 metalloenzymes. If a patient is in a deficient state, zinc administration accelerates healing*. However, excessive zinc can interfere with copper metabolism, which plays a role in the crosslinking of collagen and elastin.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old man of average weight and height undergoes an abdominoperineal resection for bowel cancer. You are assisting the dietician to plan his postoperative nutritional input:\\n\\nWhich one of the following is accepted as the normal daily nutritional intake of carbohydrate for a surgical patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is accepted as the normal daily nutritional intake of carbohydrate for a surgical patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1g/kg/day\",\"e\":\"The requirement is higher: 2 g/kg/day.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"2g/kg/day\",\"e\":\"The following are the accepted normal daily nutritional requirements for a surgical patient\\n\\nNormal daily requirement\\n\\nPotassium 1 mmol/kg/day\\n\\nNitrogen 0.15-0.2 g/kg/day\\n\\nCarbohydrate 2 g/kg/day\\n\\n3g/kg/day Fat\\n\\n30 kcal/kg/day Energy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"3g/kg/day\",\"e\":\"The requirement is lower: 2g/kg/day.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"5g/kg/day\",\"e\":\"The requirement is lower: 2g/kg/day.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"10 g/kg/day\",\"e\":\"The requirement is lower: 2g/kg/day.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man weighing 70 kg is due to have an emergency appendicectomy. There is no capacity on CEPOD overnight, so the decision is made to keep him nil by mouth and perform the procedure first thing in the morning.\\n\\nWhich one of the following fluid regimens would be most appropriate for this man over a 24 h period?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following fluid regimens would be most appropriate for this man over a 24 h period?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"3 litres 0.9% saline with 20 mmol potassium chloride in each bag\",\"e\":\"Current guidelines for maintenance fluids recommend a combination of sodium chloride and glucose\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"3 litres dextrose-saline\",\"e\":\"Current guidelines for maintenance fluids recommend a combination of sodium chloride and glucose.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"3 litres Hartmann's solution\",\"e\":\"Hartman's is not currently in the NICE guidelines, but is used commonly in surgical patients as it is considered 'physiological'. Current guidelines for maintenance fluids recommend a combination of sodium chloride and glucose.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"1 litres 0.9% saline with 20 mmol potassium chloride and, 2 litres 5% dextrose with 20 mmol potassium chloride in each bag\",\"e\":\"The daily fluid and electrolyte requirements are 1-1.5 mmol Nat/kg/24 h, 1 mmol K+/kg/24 h and 40 ml H2O /kg/24 h.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"3 litres 5% dextrose with 20 mmol potassium chloride in each bag\",\"e\":\"Current guidelines for maintenance fluids recommend a combination of sodium chloride and glucose.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man with sepsis is admitted to A&E resus. He is tachycardic and hypotensive, so a fluid challenge is instituted.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true of 0.9% saline?\",\"lead\":\"9% saline?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Contains 9 mmol/1 sodium chloride -\",\"e\":\"It contains 154 mmol/l of sodium.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Contains 154 mmol/l sodium\",\"e\":\"It contains 154 mmol/l of sodium.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Contains 29 mmol/l bicarbonate\",\"e\":\"It does not contain bicarbonate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is a colloid solution.\",\"e\":\"It is a crystalloid solution containing water and electrolytes and pass freely through a semipermeable membrane.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is the fluid of choice in the early postoperative period\",\"e\":\"0.9% saline may not be a suitable fluid in the immediate postoperative period because of the salt and water retention caused by the sympathoadrenal surgical stress response. The ideal postoperative IV solution should be dictated by local/regional guidelines and by specialist preference.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man with a 1-week history of severe diarrhoea feels dizzy when he stands up. His blood pressure (supine) is 112/76 mmg with a pulse of 88/min; blood pressure (on standing) is 80/60 mmg with a pulse of 120/min. In addition to controlling his diarrhoea, the most appropriate initial therapy is intravenous administration of which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"In addition to controlling his diarrhoea, the most appropriate initial therapy is intravenous administration of which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"5% dextrose in water\",\"e\":\"Isotonic saline is preferred as dextrose will shift mostly from the intravascular space into the tissue and will not improve the blood pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Desmopressin\",\"e\":\"This patient requires rehydration. Desmopressin may cause some retention of water, but intravenous fluids is much easier and more effective.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fresh-frozen plasma (FFP)\",\"e\":\"This patient requires rehydration. FFP would have no benefit here.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Isotonic saline\",\"e\":\"0.9% sodium chloride solution, is often used because it is isotonic and therefore will not cause potentially dangerous fluid shifts.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Verapamil\",\"e\":\"This patient requires rehydration. Verapamil has no role here.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old woman undergoes total gastrectomy for a carcinoma of the stomach.\\n\\nFor which one of the following nutrients is she most likely to require parenteral replacement?\",\"lead\":\"For which one of the following nutrients is she most likely to require parenteral replacement?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ascorbic acid\",\"e\":\"Ascorbic acid (vitamin C) is not absorbed in the stomach, it is absorbed in the small intestine.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Folic acid\",\"e\":\"Folic acid is not absorbed in the stomach, it is absorbed mostly in the jejunum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Zinc\",\"e\":\"Zinc is absorbed in the small intestine by a carrier-mediated mechanism. Under normal physiological conditions, transport processes of uptake are not saturated.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vitamin B12\",\"e\":\"The absorption of vitamin B12, although it takes place in the terminal ileum, is critically dependent on the availability of intrinsic factor, which is only secreted by the parietal (oxyntic) cells of the stomach.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vitamin D\",\"e\":\"Vitamin D is not absorbed in the stomach, it is absorbed in the small intestine.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"Complex starches are mainly digested by enzymes secreted from which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Complex starches are mainly digested by enzymes secreted from which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Pancreas\",\"e\":\"Pancreatic secretion contains a large quantity cf u-amylase, a digestive enzyme that hydrolyses starches, glycogen and most other carbohydrates to form disaccharides and a few trisaccharides.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Large intestine\",\"e\":\"The complex starches should have already been digested before reaching the large intestine.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Salivary glands\",\"e\":\"Amylase from the salivary glands will digest non-complex starches down to maltose and glucose. However, pancreatic amylase is more significantly responsible.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Small intestine\",\"e\":\"Amylase is secreted into the duodenum, but the source is from the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stomach\",\"e\":\"Digestion in the stomach is due to mechanical action, pepsin and acid. However, these are not the major mechanisms for the digestion of complex starches.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"A 62-year-old woman undergoes a total gastrectomy for gastric cancer. She requires careful follow-up because of potential malabsorption and nutrient deficiencies.\\n\\nIn which one of the following nutrients might she become be deficient if left untreated?\",\"lead\":\"In which one of the following nutrients might she become be deficient if left untreated?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Iron, Vitamin B12\",\"e\":\"Intrinsic factor is a glycoprotein produced by the stomach and is a cofactor required for the absorption of vitamin Be (cyanocobalamin). Cyanocobalamin becomes firmly bound to intrinsic factor in the small intestine. This complex then becomes bound to specific receptors in the terminal ileum, and cyanocobalamin is transferred across the intestinal epithelium.\\n\\nIron absorption is facilitated by gastric acid by preventing the precipitation of the oxidised Fe3+ ion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Iron, Vitamin A\",\"e\":\"Vitamins A, D, E and K are fat-soluble vitamins - they require bile salts to be absorbed so therefore, if the terminal ileum was excised, bile salts and therefore fat-soluble vitamins cannot be absorbed.\\n\\nGlucose absorption would not be impaired.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glucose, Vitamin B12\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Glucose, Vitamin D\",\"e\":\"Vitamins A, D, E and K are fat-soluble vitamins - they require bile salts to be absorbed so therefore, if the terminal ileum was excised, bile salts and therefore fat-soluble vitamins cannot be absorbed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vitamin C\",\"e\":\"Vitamin C absorption would not be impaired.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"A patient has prolonged ileus secondary to an emergency laparotomy for small bowel obstruction. It becomes apparent that they have not eaten a meal in 10 days.\\n\\nIn deciding whether to provide nutritional support to a malnourished patient either enterally or parenterally, which one of the following is the most important consideration?\",\"lead\":\"In deciding whether to provide nutritional support to a malnourished patient either enterally or parenterally, which one of the following is the most important consideration?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"How long nutritional support is likely to be required\",\"e\":\"While this is important, either route can be sustained in the long term.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The patient's nutritional requirements\",\"e\":\"This should be carefully considered for either route chosen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Whether the small intestine is functioning normally\",\"e\":\"The paramount consideration in deciding whether to provide nutritional support enterally or parenterally is whether the small intestine is functioning normally. If it is, then enteral nutrition should be attempted into the stomach, or, if there is a significant risk of aspiration or delayed gastric emptying, into the jejunum. In the short term, this can be achieved by a nasogastric or nasojejunal tube; in the long term, a percutaneous gastrostomy or jejunostomy may be appropriate. Both enteral and parenteral nutrition can be continued long term if necessary. The patient's nutritional requirements only become a limiting factor if they cannot be absorbed fully from the gut, in which case parenteral supplementation may be required.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Whether the patient can swallow\",\"e\":\"A patient who cannot swallow does not necessarily need parenteral feeding. A nasogastric (NG) or nasojejunal (NJ) tube can be used - this is enteral feeding.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Whether there is a risk of aspiration\",\"e\":\"This is an important consideration, but enteral feeding (NG or NJ tubes) is possible and care should be taken, particularly in ensuring the patient is not lying flat when feeding occurs.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A patient on a total parenteral nutrition (TPN) regimen presents with drowsiness and abnormal serum electrolytes.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypocalcaemia\",\"e\":\"TPN does not cause hypocalcaemia\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypercalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Some typical symptoms of hypercalcaemia* are: weakened bones, kidney stones, constipation, polyuria, weakness, fatigue and altered mental state.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hyponatraemia\",\"e\":\"Hypernatraemia is more common in the context of TPN\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypophosphataemia\",\"e\":\"Administering carbohydrate stimulates the release of insulin, which moves phosphate and glucose into cells. This so-called re-feeding syndrome occurs when starving or chronically malnourished patients are re-fed or given intravenous (iv) glucose, and leads to low serum phosphate levels.\\n\\nPhosphate deficiency commonly impairs neurological function, which may be manifested by confusion, seizures, and coma. Peripheral neuropathy and ascending motor paralysis, similar to Guillain-Barré syndrome, may also oçcur. Weakness of skeletal or smooth muscle is the most common clinical manifestation of phosphate deficiency. It can involve any muscle group, alone or in combination, ranging from ophthalmoplegia to proximal myopathy, to dysphagia or ileus. Respiratory insufficiency may occur in some patients with severe hypophosphataemia, particularly when the underlying cause is malnourishment. Impaired cardiac contractility occurs, leading to generalised signs of myocardial depression. The hypophosphataemic myocardium also has a reduced threshold for ventricular arrhythmias.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypermagnesaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypomagnesaemia is more common in the context of TPN and re-feeding syndrome.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A patient on enteral nutrition develops constipation.\\n\\nWhat could explain the underlying clinical physiology?\",\"lead\":\"What could explain the underlying clinical physiology?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyperosmolar feed\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"N? Bacterial contamination\",\"e\":\"Bacterial contamination would more likely cause diarrhoea.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Low feed temperature\",\"e\":\"Low feed temperature may more likely cause diarrhoea.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inadequate fluid replacement\",\"e\":\"Inadequate fluid replacement (dehydration) is a common cause for constipation, especially in hospital.\\n\\nA hyperosmolar feed would more likely cause diarrhoea.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduced intestinal absorptive capacity\",\"e\":\"Reduced intestinal absorptive capacity would more likely cause diarrhoea.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"You are asked to prepare a list of patients on your ward who require dietetic input for enteral feeding before the weekend. The dieticians are extremely busy and cannot review all of the referrals you want to make.\\n\\nWhich one of these patients would you highlight?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these patients would you highlight?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A patient with 3% total body surface area (TBSA) burns\",\"e\":\"A 3% TBSA burn is unlikely to require enteral feeding.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A patient with mild alcoholic pancreatitis\",\"e\":\"Patients with mild pancreatitis are permitted a normal diet if tolerated.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A patient with traumatic brain injury (TBI) with prolonged recovery\",\"e\":\"Artificial nutrition is needed when oral intake is absent or likely to be absent for more than 5-7 days. Patients with severe TBI who have a continuously poor or slow recovering neurological state require assisted enteral feeding. This is usually with an nasogastric (NG)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A patient with a proximal Crohn's fistula\",\"e\":\"Enteral feeding is the route of choice in most cases, but it is contraindicated in short bowel syndrome, severe Crohn's disease and proximal fistula.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"A patient with small bowel obstruction\",\"e\":\"A patient with small bowel obstruction should be 'nil by mouth' and therefore enteral feeding is not suitable.\\n\\nYou are treating a post-operative patient who is day 3 following laparotomy and is known to have type I diabetes. The patient is managing free fluids and is going to be allowed to eat.\\n\\nWhich digestive enzyme is responsible for digesting a certain disaccharide in milk to form glucose and galactose?\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old girl is admitted with anorexia nervosa. She has not eaten a meal for 5 days.\\n\\nIn her case, which one of the following is the most likely direct energy source for the brain?\",\"lead\":\"In her case, which one of the following is the most likely direct energy source for the brain?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amino acids\",\"e\":\"Amino acids are not used as a direct source of energy for the brain.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"SIN Fat\",\"e\":\"Fats cannot be used as direct energy source for the brain, but could be metabolised to ketone bodies.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glucose\",\"e\":\"Glucose is the major direct source of energy for the brain under normal conditions. Glucose enters the brain via a glucose transporter system. Under starvation conditions, eg anorexia, the brain adapts to use ketones instead.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ketone bodies\",\"e\":\"In prolonged starvation, pregnancy and in the newborn, the brain develops the capacity to use ketone bodies for energy. Ketone bodies are formed from the condensation of acetyl- CoA following the degradation of fatty acids in the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lactic acid\",\"e\":\"Lactic acid is not used, instead the brain adapts to use ketones.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old obese man has recently been diagnosed with type II diabetes mellitus by his General Practitioner (GP). Part of his treatment has involved the GP commencing orlistat, a pancreatic lipase inhibitor to help reduce weight and improve glycaemic control. Lipase is also produced at the base of the tongue. What proportion of triglyceride digestion occurs due to lingual lipase in a healthy individual?\",\"lead\":\"What proportion of triglyceride digestion occurs due to lingual lipase in a healthy individual?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1%\",\"e\":\"It is higher - between 10-30%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"5%\",\"e\":\"It is higher - between 10-30%.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"20%\",\"e\":\"Orlistat is a gastric and pancreatic lipase inhibitor and prevents the breakdown of dietary fat to fatty acid and glycerols. Side-effects of its use can include abdominal cramps, flatus, oily spotting and intestinal borborygmi.\\n\\nNeural and hormonal signais control pancreatic exocrine secretion. The main trigger is the presence of acid and products of digestion in the duodenum, not the presence of triglycerides. Lipase is secreted by glands near the base of the tongue (responsible for up to 30% of triglyceride digestion), and the remainder is digested by pancreatic lipase - which requires a cofactor (co-lipase) to become active. The products of triglyceride breakdown are free fatty acids glycerol.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"50%\",\"e\":\"It is lower - between 10-30%.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"75%\",\"e\":\"It is lower - between 10-30%\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"You have just seen a woman in clinic as a follow-up for breast cancer. You notice she has lost a lot of weight and is now 50 kg.\\n\\nWhen attempting to assess her nutritional status which one of the following is the most useful measure of her recovering status?\",\"lead\":\"When attempting to assess her nutritional status which one of the following is the most useful measure of her recovering status?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1 Si Body mass index\",\"e\":\"Body mass index can be used, but it is not always reliable.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mid-arm muscle circumference\",\"e\":\"Mid-arm muscle circumference can be used, but it is not reliable.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Percentage of expected body weight\",\"e\":\"Percentage of expected body weight is not always reliable.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Serum albumin levels\",\"e\":\"A rising albumin can be the most useful marker as a measure of recovery. However, the serum albumin level alone is not a reliable absolute indicator of nutritional state. Albumin is. also a negative acute phase protein, the levels of which fall in sepsis and inflammation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Triceps skin-fold thickness\",\"e\":\"Triceps skin-fold thickness can be used, but is not always reliable.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"An 89-year-old woman is admitted with mechanical bowel obstruction and is commenced on total parenteral nutrition in the post-operative period. She eventually opens her bowels and enteral feeding can be reinstituted.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding this patient's nutrition is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding this patient's nutrition is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Parental nutrition can be administered through a peripheral vein\",\"e\":\"Total parenteral nutrition (TPN) solutions are hypertonic which is why they are usually given centrally, although the peripheral route is sometimes used.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hepatic dysfunction is a rare complication of parenteral nutrition\",\"e\":\"Hepatic dysfunction with cholestasis is a relatively common complication associated with TPN. According to the NICE guidelines, liver function tests should be performed twice weekly initially and until stable.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Perioperative administration leads to poor wound healing\",\"e\":\"Malnutrition in a surgical patient is associated with poor wound healing* so attempts to reverse malnutrition are of benefit.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Solutions are generally isotonic\",\"e\":\"TPN solutions are typically hypertonic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The nitrogen content should be 40g/day\",\"e\":\"About 14g of nitrogen is the usual daily amount given. 37÷÷\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"An 89-year-old man in hospital is on total parenteral nutrition (TPN). He is of average build and is not acutely unwell.\\n\\nWhich one of the following regarding a TPN regime for an average sized adult is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regarding a TPN regime for an average sized adult is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fatty acids provide 10% of the non-protein energy requirement\",\"e\":\"30-50% of the energy requirement comes in the form of fat.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"In a patient with liver failure fatty emulsions should be avoided\",\"e\":\"In patients with liver failure, fatty acids are not tolerated.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Only essential proteins are provided\",\"e\":\"40% are essential amino acids.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"There is a 100 kcal/kg/day requirement\",\"e\":\"The energy requirement is 25-35 kcal/kg/day. This requirement increases in acutely unwell patients.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"There is a 4-6 g nitrogen requirement per day\",\"e\":\"There is a 14-16 g nitrogen requirement.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"Your consultant wants to start an elderly patient on a regime of total parenteral nutrition (TPN) as they are unable to tolerate oral feeds.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements regarding TPN is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements regarding TPN is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It causes a hypochloraemic metabolic acidosis\",\"e\":\"The main acid-base disturbance caused by parenteral feeding is hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis especially in patients with renal impairment. This is due to metabolism of cationic amino acids with release of free hydrogen ions and the presence of excess chloride load in some feeding regimens that may restrict the ability of the kidney to excrete hydrogen ions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It decreases hepatic transaminases\",\"e\":\"Increases in serum hepatic enzymes are not infrequent with transaminases rising two-or three-fold about 8 days after the beginning of feeding. A similar rise may be seen in alkaline phosphatase.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It increases circulating phosphate levels\",\"e\":\"Extra nutritional support increases the cellular need for phosphate and so depletes circulating phosphate. Hypophosphataemia usually becomes evident 2-4 days after feeding has commenced unless phosphate has been provided.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It may lead to copper deficiency within several days of commencement\",\"e\":\"Symptoms of copper deficiency take several weeks to appear (hypochromic anaemia, neutropaenia and osteoporosis).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It may cause life-threatening pancytopaenia through acute folate deficiency\",\"e\":\"Parenteral nutrition without giving folate or vitamin Big will lead to megaloblastic anaemia and eventually pancytopenia.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"You are prescribing total parenteral nutrition (TPN) for one of your post-operative patients that leads you to consider nutritional requirements.\\n\\nWhat are the standard daily nutritional requirements for a surgical patient?\",\"lead\":\"What are the standard daily nutritional requirements for a surgical patient?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Carbohydrate is 2 g/kg per day\",\"e\":\"Carbohydrate requirement is approximately 2g/kg per day\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Nitrogen is 0.1 g/kg per day\",\"e\":\"Nitrogen requirement is approximately 0.5-1 g/kg per day\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Potassium is approximately 2 mmol/kg per day\",\"e\":\"Potassium requirement is approximately 1 mmol/kg per day\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sodium is approximately 3 mmol/kg per day\",\"e\":\"Sodium requirement is approximately 1-2 mmol/kg per day\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Water is 15ml/kg per day\",\"e\":\"Water requirement is approximately 35 ml/kg per day\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man is recovering in the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) following an emergency abdominal aortic aneurysm repair. He is intubated and ventilated, there is concern regarding a poss ble postoperative ileus and it is decided to commence total parenteral nutrition (TPN) via a central line:\\n\\nWhich one of the following are essential components of TPN?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following are essential components of TPN?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Emulsifiers\",\"e\":\"TPN does not contain emulsifiers\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fibrin\",\"e\":\"TPN does not contain fibrin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fibrinogen\",\"e\":\"TPN does not contain fibrinogen.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Insulin\",\"e\":\"TPN does not contain insulin.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Nitrogen\",\"e\":\"The essential components of TPN are nitrogen, carbohydrate, fat, minerals (calcium, magnesium, iron, zinc, manganese, copper, fluoride, iodine, chloride) and vitamins.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"A 14-year-old boy with non-specific abdominal pain presents to the acute general surgical take. He has a soft non-peritonitic abdomen and normal inflammatory markers. He is noted to be acidotic, hyperglycaemic and has glucosuria. He is diagnosed with diabetic ketoacidosis and admitted under the physicians.\\n\\nWhat is the main substrate used for the generation of ketone bodies?\",\"lead\":\"What is the main substrate used for the generation of ketone bodies?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fatty acids\",\"e\":\"Ketone bodies are an important alternative energy source for the brain in periods of prolonged starvation. They are generated from the breakdown of fatty acids which are metabolised into acetyl coenzyme A (acetyl-CoA), which normally enters the tri-carboxylic cycle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Triglycerides\",\"e\":\"Fatty acids are the main substrate for the generation of ketone bodies.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Amino acids\",\"e\":\"Fatty acids are the main substrate for the generation of ketone bodies.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Carbohydrates\",\"e\":\"Fatty acids are the main substrate for the generation of ketone bodies.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Glycogen\",\"e\":\"Fatty acids are the main substrate for the generation of ketone bodies.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":54,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man has a long history of Crohn's disease. Over 1 week he develops vomiting, abdominal pain and constant diarrhoea. He has a small bowel resection and requires assistance with meeting his daily nutritional requirements. He weighs 70 kg.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding daily nutritional requirements for this man?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding daily nutritional requirements for this man?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"He requires at least 70mmol sodium\",\"e\":\"Adults typically require 1-2 mmol/kg/day of sodium, which means 70-140 mmol/day for this patient.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"He requires 90mmol potassium\",\"e\":\"Adults typically require 1mmol/kg/day of potassium, which means 70 mmol/day for this patient.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"He requires 2500 calories?\",\"e\":\"His caloric requirement is 25-30 kcal kg-' day-1, therefore around 2000 calories per day in total.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"His protein: fat glucose ratio should be 25:25:50\",\"e\":\"Protein, fat and glucose ratio should be 20:30:50.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Parenteral nutrition should be used to induce remission with medical therapy\",\"e\":\"Nutritional alteration will not alter the course of disease.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":56,\"stem\":\"A 26-year-old man involved in a road traffic accident (RTA) suffers a high cord paralysis and has to be intubated. After discussion with the Multi-disciplinary Team (MDT) it is decided he should commence enteral feeding. Which one of the following is a complication of enteral feeding?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a complication of enteral feeding?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bacterial overgrowth in the gut\",\"e\":\"Enteral feeding is immunologically superior to parenteral feeding, and would therefore more likely prevent bacterial overgrowth.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Diarrhoea\",\"e\":\"Diarrhoea is a recognised complication.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoglycaemia\",\"e\":\"Hyperglycaemia is a more likely complication. Other complications of enteral feeding tubes include displacement or blockage, hyperglycaemia or micro-nutrient deficiencies and aspiration.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increase in the incidence of cholestasis by inhibition of biliary flow\",\"e\":\"The presence of nutrients in the upper small bowel stimulates the release of gut peptide hormones - it is by this mechanism that enteral nutrition promotes biliary flow and prevents cholestasis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"increased likelihood of stress ulceration\",\"e\":\"Enteral nutrition provides a degree of protection against stress ulceration.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 48 year old man had a laparotomy and small bowel resection with stoma formation for Crohn's disease 5 days ago. The operation notes state that he has 100 cm of smail bowel left. His pulse is 120 beats/min and temperature 37°C. He is oliguric and hypotensive with cool peripheries. Stoma output is over 3 litres since the surgery. He is receiving regular metoclopramide for nausea. CT earlier in the day showed fluid in the pelvis. What is the most likely cause of this patient's clinical state?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of this patient's clinical state?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Short-bowel syndrome causing high output ileostomy\",\"e\":\"This patient's tachycardia and oliguria is due to hypovolaemia. This patient has been producing high outputs since surgery. The length of 100 cm is the minimum amount of small bowel needed, and so he is clearly right at this threshold for short-bowel syndrome and subsequent high output.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AAI Metoclopramide\",\"e\":\"Metoclopramide is a pro-kinetic, and so can contribute to high stoma outputs, but it is unlikely to be the primary cause in this case. The patient was suffering high outputs before the metoclopramide was started. Such medications should be ceased in a scenario like this.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Intra-abdominal sepsis )\",\"e\":\"This patient has some features that suggest this to be the cause, such as the tachycardia, and the CT scan showing fluid in the pelvis. He is also 5 days post-surgery, and so in the right time-frame to develop a collection. However, he is not overtly septic, and while this may be contributing, it is unlikely to be main driver of his clinical state.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Bowel obstruction\",\"e\":\"This can be a driver of high output stoma, and this patient, having had multiple abdominal surgeries, is at risk, but it is not the most likely answer here.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Infective gastroenteritis\",\"e\":\"Of course, this can cause high enteric outputs like any gastroenteritis, but should not be considered the primary diagnosis in this case. One might expect this outcome a few days or weeks following surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":44,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old patient who was admitted 2 days ago with an acute ischaemic stroke and difficulty swallowing requires initial nutrition.\\n\\nWhich statement is most appropriate?\",\"lead\":\"Which statement is most appropriate?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The patient should be assessed by a dietician and considered for feeding gastrostomy, and be fed this way\",\"e\":\"A feeding gastrostomy is an invasive procedure with potential morbidity, and should only be considered when it is certain that the patient is going to need long term enteral nutritional support.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The patient should be assessed by a dietician and considered for total parenteral nutrition (TPN) via a central line\",\"e\":\"TPN should only be considered for patients who will not be able to have enteral nutrition for a prolonged period. So, this is incorrect, as the patient may recover swallow function soon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The patient should be given free fluids only per oral until they recover from their stroke\",\"e\":\"The patient has suffered a neurological event. Before being allowed to take anything orally, the patient should undergo a swallow assessment, preferably by an allied health care professional with expertise in the area.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The patient should be assessed by a dietician and have a nasogastric (NG) tube inserted, and be fed this way\",\"e\":\"All patients who are not feeding normally should be assessed by a dietician. Here, an NG tube should be able to provide the patient with short-u term nutrition. This will give time for the patient's neurological function and prognosis to be assessed, including the state of their swallow.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The patient should be assessed by a gastroenterologist and be considered for a nasojejunal (NJ) tube, and be fed this way\",\"e\":\"Although NJ tubes are a good way to provide enteral nutrition, they require insertion by a trained professional. They are usually inserted with fluoroscopic guidance by a radiologist, or via endoscopy. This is not necessary in this patient, as a simple NG tube will suffice.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A /b-year-old man undergoes a transurethral resection of the prostate (URP) for benign prostatic hyperplasia. The procedure takes over 2 h as he has a large prostate. The surgeon uses glycine as the irrigating fluid. In the postoperative period, he becomes confused.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely reason for this?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely reason for this?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hyponatraemia:\",\"e\":\"Postoperative confusion* is an important complication following surgery, of which there are several causes. In this case, hyponatraemia and hypo-osmolality secondary to the irrigation fluid used during this patient's TURP, is the most likely cause of confusion. The irrigating fluid causes osmotic diuresis with a resulting reduction in plasma osmolality and increase in plasma volume. Glycine also has inhibitory effects on the central nervous system. Fluid restriction, as well as hypertonic saline, can be used to correct the hyponatraemia. Due to the availability of bipolar diathermy, glycine is now rarely used. Other complications from TURP include mortality (0.5%), erectile dysfunction (10%), retrograde ejaculation (70%), incontinence (1%), bladder neck stenosis (2%) and urethral stricture (1%). Up to 2% of patients per vear require re-operation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypotension\",\"e\":\"Postoperative hypotension can cause confusion and altered conscious level through reduced cerebral perfusion, however, in this case there is no mention of fluid or blood loss during surgery that would cause significant hypotension. The percentage of patients requiring transfusion due to blood loss during TURP is 0.4-6.4%.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary oedema\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary oedema would present with breathlessness, tachypnoea and desaturation; these features are not described in the case above. Pulmonary oedema would be an uncommon cause of postoperative confusion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Septicaemia\",\"e\":\"Septicaemia is a recognised complication of TURP and is a cause of postoperative confusion. The incidence of septicaemia post-TURP is reported to be 1.5%. There are no indications in the case history above which would suggest the patient is suffering septicaemia, so another cause for the confusion should be sought\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transient ischaemic attack\",\"e\":\"A transient ischaemic attack would present with signs and symptoms of a stroke (facial/limb weakness or paraesthesia, dysphasia, altered level of consciences and/or confusion) which resolve within 24 h. These features are not described in the case history above.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man presents with severe upper abdominal pain following a prolonged course of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for chronic back pain. He is peritonitic and an erect chest X-ray shows free intra-abdominal air. He consents for an upper midline laparotomy to deal with a presumed perforated gastroduodenal ulcer.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is true regarding this condition?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is true regarding this condition?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A preoperative osophago-gastroduodenoscopy (OGD) is beneficial in locating the site of pathology\",\"e\":\"An OGD adds little information to the diagnosis and may make matters worse by increasing the perforation due to insufflation of CO_ to distend the stomach. Once the diagnosis of a gastric perforation is made then laparotomy is the next step.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"An effective repair for small perforations involves primary repair and a free omental patch\",\"e\":\"At operation, the defect is closed by oversewing an omental patch which remains attached to its blood supply. A free flap patch would involve removal from its native blood supply and anastomosis with an appropriate blood vessel at the donor site.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The surgical approach involves incising rectus abdominis\",\"e\":\"A midline laparotomy is not a muscle cutting incision. The incision passes through the skin and subcutaneous fat, fascia, the linea alba, transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat and parietal peritoneum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The transversalis fascia is incised in this surgical approach\",\"e\":\"A midline laparotomyt incision passes through the skin and subcutaneous fat, fascia, linea alba, transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat and parietal peritoneum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The parietal peritoneum is not broached during this approach\",\"e\":\"This approach broaches the parietal peritoneum. Most gastroduodenal ulcers are found on the anterior surface of the first part of the duodenum.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man with AIDS is in the preoperative clinic being assessed for a hip replacement. His HIV status is recorded and at the subsequent WHO meeting the surgical team are aware so that they can prepare properiy.\\n\\nRegarding precautions, which one of the following is true during the perioperative period?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding precautions, which one of the following is true during the perioperative period?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"This risk of HIV seroconversion following a needle stick injury is 2%\",\"e\":\"The human immunodeficiency viruses, HIV-1 and HIV-2, are retroviruses that cause acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS). The viruses are spread by sexual transmission, infected blood or blood products, or by organ transplant. The risk of seroconversion following a needlestick injury is as follows (per 1000 exposures):\\n\\n• HIV 0.3%. • hepatitis C 3% • hepatitis B 30%\\n\\nThe specific risk of a single injury depends on a number of factors when the patients harbour the virus of concern. Injuries with a hollow-bore needle, deep penetration, visible blood on the needle, a needle that was located in a deep artery or vein, or with blood from terminally ill patients are known to increase the risk for HIV infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Eye protection would be unnecessary as there is no transmission from HIV-infected blood splashes to the eye\",\"e\":\"The HIV virus can be spread through infected blood, therefore eye protection is necessary.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Suture of the wound should be carried out by two surgeons\",\"e\":\"Only one surgeon should close the wound to avoid the risk of inflicting a needlestick injury on the other.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Use of standard unenforced gowns is sufficient\",\"e\":\"Standard precautions should be taken intraoperatively to prevent transmission, these include double gloving and the use of enforced gowns and aprons.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Double gloving is advised\",\"e\":\"As above, double gloving is recommended to reduce the risk of transmission of HIV.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old man is receiving a blood transfusion several hours after a mitral valve replacement. The recovery nurse is concerned as the patient is flushed with abnormal observations. Blood tests show anaemia, raised bilirubin, but normal coagulation. What is the most likely cause for these symptoms?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause for these symptoms?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ABO-mismatched blood transfusion\",\"e\":\"Immune-mediated intravascular haemolysis is only usually encountered in the potent antibody-antigen interactions seen following mismatched ABO transfusion. Protocols for checking patient details should be followed by all staff involved in transfusion to avoid errors.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia\",\"e\":\"Most autoimmune haemolytic anaemias produce red cell destruction in the reticuloendothelial system (ie phagocytosis of red blood cells), it would be uncommon for this condition to present postoperatively.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)\",\"e\":\"DIC may cause microangiopathic haemolysis with red cell destruction in the microcirculation and is a cause of coagulopathy. Given this patient has normal coagulation, DIC would be unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Endocarditis from prosthetic mitral valve\",\"e\":\"As the patient is immediately post-surgery, endocarditis is unlikely as this would be a longer term complication of mitral valve replacement.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Mitral valve replacement\",\"e\":\"Intravascular haemolysis may result from the mechanical destruction of red cells by prosthetic heart valves or arterial grafts, in arteriovenous malformations or after severe physical exercise. Haemolysis caused by the replacement valve is unlikely to present quite so acutely post-surgery, therefore a transfusion reaction is more likely.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man undergoes drainage of a perianal abscess. A large amount of pus is extruded and there is a significant cavity left next to the anus.\\n\\nWhich is the best method to manage the wound?\",\"lead\":\"Which is the best method to manage the wound?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Primary closure\",\"e\":\"Given the infected nature of the wound, primary closure would be ill-advised, as this is likely to lead to re-accumulation of the abscess. Primary closure is achieved by suturing, stapling or Steri-Strips.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Delayed primary closure\",\"e\":\"Delayed primary closure is needed for contaminated wounds that are left open day 1 and closed by suturing by days 3 to 5 before excessive granulation has started. Healing by secondary intention is more appropriate, however, as the perianal region represents a contaminated area and delayed primary closure may lead to further infection or abscess formation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Healing by secondary intention\",\"e\":\"Infected wounds are best left open to heal by secondary intention. In abscess formation it may be prudent to place a temporary drain to ensure complete drainage of infected material and prevent re-accumulation. Healing by secondary intention requires daily wound cleaning as granulation tissue forms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Permanent fistula\",\"e\":\"A fistula is an abnormal communication between to epithelial surfaces. Permanent fistula formation should be avoided and is not desirable in such a young patient due to chronic implications.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tertiary closure by skin graft\",\"e\":\"There is no indication to close the described wound with a skin graft. Furthermore, a skin graft over an infected wound is unlikely to be viable.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":18,\"stem\":\"Having recently performed an appendicectomy, your patient has been readmitted with a postoperative wound infection.\\n\\nThe incidence of this in abdominal surgery is increased with which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The incidence of this in abdominal surgery is increased with which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Closing wounds with minimal tension\",\"e\":\"Excessive tension, not minimal tension, would be a factor increasing the risk of postoperative wound infection. Excessive pressure or tension in the tissues may impair the circulation of both blood and lymph, impeding healing.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Extensive diathermy use\",\"e\":\"The non-microbial factors influencing the incidence of postoperative infection* include: dead/damaged tissue within a wound; an excessive use of diathermy: and mass ligature. Additionally, inadequate haemostasis allows haematoma formation, and the presence of 'dead space' can allow a seroma to develop, bath of which provide a favourable nidus for bacterial growth.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory use\",\"e\":\"There is no association between non-steroidal anti-inflammatory use and increased postoperative wound infection. Steroids, and other immunosuppressants such as cyclasporin, suppress the host's response to infection by depressing antibody function, diminishing phagocytic activity and inhibiting new capillary formation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Not wearing masks\",\"e\":\"Face masks contribute little to the prevention of wound infection.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Well-controlled diabetes mellitus\",\"e\":\"A patient with well controlled diabetes mellitus is no more susceptible to infection than non- diabetic patients.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"You have a patient who is undergoing a hemihepatectomy and needs central venous pressure monitoring during anaesthesia and postoperatively while in the Intensive Care Unit. The anaesthetist is teaching you how to insert a central venous pressure line.\\n\\nRegarding insertion of the line, where should the tip lie?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding insertion of the line, where should the tip lie?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The tip should lie in the aorta\",\"e\":\"Placement in the aorta would provide arterial pressures not venous.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The tip should lie in the left atrium\",\"e\":\"Again, this would provide arterial pressures not venous.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The tip should lie in the right atrium\",\"e\":\"It is not necessary to place the central venous catheter into the right atrium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The tip should lie in the right ventricle\",\"e\":\"The tip should lie in the distal superior vena cava (SVC), at the level of the right atrium. This pressure reflects the right atrial pressure, and so the filling pressure of the right side of the heart.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The tip should lie in the lower superior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The tip of a central venous pressure (CVP) linet should lie low in the superior vena cava (SVC). The reading reflects pressure in the right atrium. The normal CVP reading is usually measured in cHO. Normal CVP is 2-8 cmH_O, and is raised during positive pressure ventilation.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"You are explaining to a patient that you need to place a central venous catheter and you have chosen to place a femoral line. The patient asks what are the complications of this\\n\\nWhich one of the following complications is most likely?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following complications is most likely?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Venous thrombosis\",\"e\":\"While all of the potential answers are known complications of central venous catheterisation, the question specifies the femoral route making intrathoracic complications unlikely, except for venous thrombosis, which is a common complication of all central lines. Other known complications of femoral central venous access include bowel penetration, bladder penetration, psoas abscess and femoral nerve injury.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Damage to the thoracic duct\",\"e\":\"This is a rare complication and very unlikely given the femoral approach\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Injury to the brachial plexus\",\"e\":\"Again, utilising a femoral approach greatly reduces the risk of intrathoracic complications.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"pericardial tamponde\",\"e\":\"Pericardial tamponade, injury to the brachial plexus and thoracic duct damage are rare\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"Recognised complications of central venous catheter insertion include pneumo- or haemothorax, and air embolism upon line insertion. Pneumothorax however would be unlikely while using a femoral approach. Other complications include pseudo-aneurysm formation, arterial puncture, haematoma formation and infection.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"You have a patient on the emergency list for an urgent laparotomy. The patient has an irregularly irregular pulse with a rate of 180 bpm, and the electrocardiogram (ECG) confirms fast atrial fibrillation. The patient only has peripheral intravenous access.\\n\\nIn order to control the rate, which would be the most appropriate treatment to quickly control the fast atrial fibrillation?\",\"lead\":\"In order to control the rate, which would be the most appropriate treatment to quickly control the fast atrial fibrillation?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amiodarone 300 mg intravenous infusion over 24\",\"e\":\"Amiodarone can chemically cardiovert atrial fibrillation (AF) to sinus rhythm. An infusion must be given via a central line, not a peripheral line which is all the patient has in this scenario.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Digoxin 62.5 mcg orally\",\"e\":\"AF may be treated by intravenous, or more commoniy; oral digoxin. The onset of action of oral digoxin is slower and given the patient requires urgent control this would not be appropriate.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Isoprenaline 2 mg/kg infusion over 2 min\",\"e\":\"Isoprenaline increases contractility and heart rate, and is used in severe bradycardia and heart block. Use of isoprenaline in this situation is likely to cause cardiac arrest.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Phentolamine 10 mg/ml in 20 min\",\"e\":\"Phentolamine is a short-acting drug used to control hypertensive crises due to phaeochromocytoma\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Metoprolol 5-10 mg intravenously.\",\"e\":\"Intravenous Metoprolol can help with rapid rate control. It can be given via a peripheral line with rapid onset of action, therefore given the urgency of the laparotomy, it would be most appropriate treatment in this case.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"You insert a subclavian central line for one of your patients. The procedure is uncomplicated out you forget to close the central line following insertion. Which recognised complication of central venous line insertion is most likely to occur as a result?\",\"lead\":\"Which recognised complication of central venous line insertion is most likely to occur as a result?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arterial air embolism\",\"e\":\"If placed correctly the central line would be in the subclavian venous, not in an artery, therefore an arterial air embolus would not be possible.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Chylothorax\",\"e\":\"A chylothorax is an accumulation of lymph from the gastrointestinal system in the picural cavity. Damage to the thoracic duct is a recognised complication of insertion of a subclavian central line, however, the question stem states the central line has been left open, thus venous air embolism of more correct, as there is no suggestion of damage to the duct during insertion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemorrhage\",\"e\":\"Central venous line insertion may cause trauma to adjacent tissues with consequent haemorrhage, particularly if the great vessels are damaged during insertion. In this question, there is no suggestion of damage during insertion and specifically refers to leaving the central line open.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"Again, damage to pleural and lung parenchyma could cause a subsequent air leak and pneumothorax. There is no suggestion of trauma upon insertion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Venous air embolism\",\"e\":\"If the central line is left open then air may enter the blood causing a venous air embolism.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":30,\"stem\":\"A 61-year-old woman with known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is about to have a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. Having established a pneumoperitoneum the anaesthetist informs you that he is having difficulty oxygenating the patient. Which one of the following factors is most likely responsible for this difficulty?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following factors is most likely responsible for this difficulty?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abdominal aorta compression\",\"e\":\"Compression of the abdominal aorta would cause hypotension and cardiovascular instability; difficulty in oxygenating the patient suggests a primary respiratory issue.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Increased FEV FVC ratio\",\"e\":\"An increased FEV,: FVC ratio is suggestive of a restrictive lung disease. COPD by definition is an obstructive lung disease causing a decreased FEV;: FVC ratio. Obstruction in the small airways decreases the amount of air a patient can exhale in 1 second (FEV, forced expiratory volume in 1 second). As such, this could not be the reason for difficulty with oxygenation in this patient.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased peak airway pressures\",\"e\":\"The raised intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) associated with CO_ insufflation splints the diaphragm, reducing diaphragmatic movements. Functional residual capacity, vital capacity, and pulmonary compliance all decrease with raised IAP and peak airway pressures may increase by > 50%. These changes are normally well tolerated but patients with underlying COPD lung disease are prone to airway collaspe and are unable to compensate. These patients often require positive end-expiratory pressure in order to achieve adequate gas exchange. In general, laparoscopic surgery is performed through a transperitoneal or retroperitoneal approach with insuffiation of CO2 under pressure to create a working space. Cardiovascular, respiratory, renal, and metabolic changes occur secondary to the raised intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) and absorption of CO_\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased respiratory rate\",\"e\":\"An increased respiratory rate will increase minute ventilation and therefore increase oxygenation. NB: Minute volume = respiratory rate x tidal volume. Any increase in either respiratory rate or tidal volume increases the minute volume which in turn increases gaseous exchange and therefore oxygenation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reduced venous return\",\"e\":\"Although increased intra-abdominal pressure can reduce venous return due to compression of veins within the abdomen (and thoracic cavity due to increased pleural pressure as the diaphragm is splinted), this is likely to cause cardiac instability. Furthermore, the question states the patient has COPD thus this respiratory complaint is more likely to be the cause of reduced oxygenation.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A 24-year-old rugby player sustains a femoral fracture which is treated with intramedullary nailing. The next day he is confused, which is thought to be due to his medication. He then drops his oxygen saturation to 87% and a petechial rash is noted over his thorax and neck. A CT pulmonary angiogram (CTPA) shows no arterial filling defects.\\n\\nWhat disease process is likely to be occurring?\",\"lead\":\"What disease process is likely to be occurring?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1'5' ARDS\",\"e\":\"Adult respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) is a medical phenomenon caused by extensive inflammation in the lungs causing accumulation of fluid within the alveoli and subsequent impairment of gas exchange. It is commonly triggered by trauma or systemic sepsis with a mortality as high as 50%. It is not however, associated with a petechial rash.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Aspiration pneumonia\",\"e\":\"Aspiration pneumonia can be a complication of difficult airway management (either at intubation or extubation) and aspiration of stomach contents in the perioperative period. There is no suggestion of difficult airway management in the question stem.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fat embolism\",\"e\":\"The symptoms and signs of fat embolism tend to be manifest 24 to 48 h after major trauma or fracture. Mental changes are frequently the first clinical features observed and may appear as confusion, drowsiness, convulsions or coma. A mild to moderate pyrexia is common, but does not usually rise over 39°C. The characteristic rash over thorax, neck, soft palate and conjunctiva is petechial, and caused by microinfarction. There is a low partial pressure of arterial oxygen (paOz) and hyperventilation. The resultant paCO, causes a respiratory alkalosis. Chest radiographs may show 'fluffy' opacification of the lung fields or pulmonary oedema, but pleural effusions are not a feature\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary emboli\",\"e\":\"The question clearly states that no filling defects to indicate thromboemboli where found on СТРА imaging. This is the gold standard investigation for a pulmonary embolus therefore, pulmonary emboli can be excluded as the cause.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary oedema\",\"e\":\"Pulmonary oedema, of any underlying cause, can cause poor oxygenation and thus reduced oxygen saturations, however, it would not be associated with a petechial rash.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A patient has undergone major plastic reconstructive surgery following severe burns. They have been admitted for prolonged support while they recover from their procedure.\\n\\nWhich one of the following biochemical parameters will drop immediately after major surgery as part of an acute phase response?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following biochemical parameters will drop immediately after major surgery as part of an acute phase response?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Glucose\",\"e\":\"Glucose rises partly because of release of other stress hormones including cortisol and growth hormone.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Albumin\",\"e\":\"Albumin is a transport protein which decreases in the acute phase response. The acute phase response is a humoral and biochemical response to any major insult on the body including surgery, sepsis and burns. This is a stress response designed to optimise the body's response to the trauma or infection and minimise ongoing tissue damage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"C3\",\"e\":\"C3 is part of the complement proteins important in fighting infection and will rise in the acute phase reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Alpha-1-antitrypsin\",\"e\":\"Alpha-1-antitrypsin helps minimise tissue damage from proteinases that may be released following tissue damage.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Haptoglobin\",\"e\":\"Haptoglobin is an iron binding protein that will pick up any released iron from damaged cells and prevent it being utilised by foreign micro- organisms.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"You assist in intubating and ventilating a patient at a trauma call.\\n\\nWhat complications of artificial ventilation should you observe the patient for?\",\"lead\":\"What complications of artificial ventilation should you observe the patient for?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Rasal atelectasis\",\"e\":\"Basal atelectasis is the collapse of the alveoli found most distal (Le. basal alveoli) in the lungs; this can be prevented by use of positive end-expiratory pressure to reduce alveolar closing/collapse\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"MS Cerebral oedema\",\"e\":\"Cerebral oedema as a result of intubation and ventilation is uncommon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypercarbia\",\"e\":\"Hypercarbia may occur due to hypoventilation during intubation, however, this can be quickly corrected by increased ventilation of the patient post-intubation, and is countered by hyperventilating the patient prior to intubation. As such, it is unlikely to be a significant issue.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypertension\",\"e\":\"Hypotension, not hypertension, is more likely following intubation and ventilation as anaesthetic agents reduce sympathetic tone. Furthermore, the use of positive end- expiratory pressure to reduce alveolar closing may lead to a positive intrathoracic pressure, which in turn may reduce venous return to the heart, thus reducing cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"Over forceful ventilation pressures may cause a pneumothorax. This requires immediate management to prevent a tension pneumothorax.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man has undergone an emergency laparotomy and is on the intensive care unit. He is maintained on controlled mechanical ventilation via an endotracheal route.\\n\\nWhat physiological changes would you expect in someone with this type of ventilation?\",\"lead\":\"What physiological changes would you expect in someone with this type of ventilation?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Decreased thoracic pressure, decreased venous return and decreased cardiac output\",\"e\":\"Increased thoracic pressure, not decreased thoracic pressure, secondary to mechanical ventilation causes decreased venous return and cardiac output. This answer is physiologically incorrect.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Decreased thoracic pressure, increased venous return and decreased cardiac output\",\"e\":\"Increased venous return would cause increased cardiac output\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Increased thoracic pressure, decreased venous return and decreased cardiac output\",\"e\":\"Increased thoracic pressure secondary to mechanical ventilation and positive airway pressures causes decreased venous return and subsequently reduced cardiac output. Controlled mechanical ventilation requires intubation and hence necessitates intensive therapy unit (ITU) admission. The patient must be sedated to tolerate the endotracheal tube. Oxygen concentration can reach 100%, although this is avoided to prevent its toxic effects. Mechanical ventilation causes increased thoracic pressure due to the need for positive end- expiratory airway pressure. Positive end-expiratory pressure ventilation increases the risk of barotrauma, which can result in the rapid formation of a tension pneumothorax.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Increased thoracic pressure, increased venous return and decreased cardiac output\",\"e\":\"Again, increased thoracic pressure would decrease venous return. Furthermore, increased venous return increases cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Increased thoracic pressure, increased venous return and increased cardiac output\",\"e\":\"Increased thoracic pressure decreased both venous return and cardiac output.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"A 70-year-old man is 1 hour postoperative following a laparotomy and Hartmann's procedure for perforated diverticulitis.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a response to injury/surgery?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a response to injury/surgery?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A fall in plasma insulin concentration immediately after surgery\",\"e\":\"In the ebb phase after injury, plasma insulin concentration falls because catecholamines and cortisol make the 6 islet cells of the pancreas less sensitive to glucose. The body responds to any form of injury (including surgery) with local and systemic responses that attempt to contain and heal the tissue damage, and also to protect the body whilst it is injured. The systemic response, produced by many different mediators (sympathetic nervous system, acute phase response, endocrine response and vascular endothelial cell system response). increases the metabolic rate, mobilises carbohydrate, protein and fat stores, conserves salt and water, stimulates immunological and coagulation systems, and diverts blood preferentially to vital organs. Blood is redistributed from the viscera and skin to the heart, brain and skeletal muscles, and there is an increase in heart rate and contractility.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A reduction in aldosterone secretion, leading to metabolic alkalosis\",\"e\":\"Adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) stimulates aldosterone secretion (in addition to glucocorticoids), which causes increased re-absorption of sodium and secretion of potassium (and hydrogen ions) in the distal convoluted tubules and collecting ducts, leading to metabolic alkalosis as well as a reduction in the urine volume.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A reduction in hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis\",\"e\":\"Arginine vasopressin acts on the distal tubules and collecting ducts in the kidney and leads to increased re-absorption of solute-free water; it causes peripheral vasoconstriction, especially in the splanchnic bed, and it also stimulates hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. In addition, glucagon secretion increases after injury, further stimulating hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"A rise in serum albumin\",\"e\":\"Serum albumin falls after trauma because production by the liver decreases and loss into damaged tissue increases due to the action of cytokines and prostaglandins on vessel permeability. The accompanying fluid shift out of the intra-vascular compartment contributes to dysfunction in various organs.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"An increase in the visceral blood flow\",\"e\":\"Blood is redistributed from the viscera and skin to the heart, brain and skeletal muscles.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old man is being prepared to undergo a Femoral Popliteal Bypass procedure for intermittent claudication. He is a smoker and has type II diabetes mellitus.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Chlorpropamide can usually be given or the day of surgery\",\"e\":\"Chlorpropamide is a long acting hypoglycaemic agent, of the sulfonylurea class, used to treat type I! diabetes. Tight blood sugar control perioperatively is less critical than avoiding hypoglycaemia, it is therefore important to avoid any hypoglycaemic agents which may have a prolonged effect.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypokalaemia may occur secondary to peri-operative insulin use\",\"e\":\"The use of insulin (ie in the form of a sliding scale) during this patient's preoperative care carries a risk of hypokalaemia, as insulin increases cellular uptake of potassium ions.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is important to maintain the blood sugar below 8 mmol/| throughout the operation\",\"e\":\"Hypoglycaemia is a severe threat to the anaesthetised diabetic patient, therefore to maintain the blood sugar below 8mmol/1 would be to risk hypoglycaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The metabolic response to surgery lowers blood sugar\",\"e\":\"Surgery causes an acute phase response, subsequent release of glucagon (inhibits peripheral action of insulin) and cortisol (stimulates hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis) increases blood sugar level.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The risk of cardiac death is not significant\",\"e\":\"Diabetics have a higher incidence of vascular disease and higher death rate from cardiac disease.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient on the ward who underwent a hemiarthroplasty for a fractured neck of femur 2 hours ago. The patient is hypotensive\\n\\nThe hypotension is most likely to be caused by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"The hypotension is most likely to be caused by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"Explanation i\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Narcotics producing vasoconstriction\",\"e\":\"Narcotics can produce hypotension through a vagal induced bradycardia. Additionally, vasoconstriction would cause hypertension not hypotension.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Narcotics producing myocardial depression\",\"e\":\"Narcotics can produce hypotension through vagal induced bradycardia; narcotics do not have any effect on myocardial function.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pain\",\"e\":\"Pain causes increased sympathetic tone and therefore hypertension not hypotension.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Regional anaesthesia producing vasoconstriction by increasing sympathetic outflow\",\"e\":\"Again, vasoconstriction would cause hypertension not hypotension.\\n\\n2203\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Regional anaesthesia producing vasodilation by blocking sympathetic outflow\",\"e\":\"Spinal and epidural anaesthesia produce hypotension via a vasodilator effect from blocking sympathetic outflow.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":51,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old female undergoes a thyroidectomy for a follicular carcinoma of the thyroid gland. Later that day you are called urgently to the ward as the patient has become acutely short of breath and dropped her oxygen saturations. On examination, a tense swelling is seen beneath the dressings.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A'S' Anaphylaxis\",\"e\":\"There is no precipitant, such as intravenous antibiotic administration, in the history to induce anaphylaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Infection\",\"e\":\"Although postoperative-operative infection and subsequent abscess formation may present with a neck swelling, and possible airway compromise, the history is too acute to make an infection likely.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tension haematoma (\",\"e\":\"A tension haematoma can occur following a thyroidectomy and represents a surgical emergency as the airway could be compromised as evidenced by the shortness of breath and drop in oxygen saturations in this case. Immediate measures to control this include removing any clips or using a scalpel to release blood and thereby relieve the pressure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thyroid storm\",\"e\":\"Thyroid storm is very unlikely to manifest as a compromised airway and more likely restlessness, confusion, tachycardia and pyrexia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tracheal injury\",\"e\":\"If tracheal injury had occurred intra-operatively, it is likely to present prior to the patient returning to the ward.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A female opera singer is being followed up after thyroid surgery for a total thyroidectomy for a locally aggressive papillary carcinoma. She complains that when singing she is no longer able to achieve high-pitched notes, although her voice is otherwise normal. Which one of the following nerves is most likely to have been affected during her surgery?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following nerves is most likely to have been affected during her surgery?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"External laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"The external laryngeal nerve is one of the two terminal branches of the superior laryngeal nerve, a branch of the vagus nerve. It supplies the cricothyroid muscle, and it can be damaged during thyroid surgery. The cricothyroid muscle contracts to stretch the vocal folds thereby increasing tension within them. This is important in higher pitch phonation\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Di'i Glossopharyngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the glossopharyngeal nerve will cause altered taste sensation, namely loss of bitter and sour taste, alongside impaired swallowing.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the hypoglossal nerve would cause tongue muscle wasting and deviation of the tongue towards the affected side.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Recurrent laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the recurrent laryngeal nerve will cause paralysis of the ipsilateral vocal cord, and therefore hoarseness of the voice.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the vagus nerve would produce a plethora of pathology given the multitude of functions including autonomic functions and parasympathetic supply to the cardiovascular. respiratory and gastrointestinal system.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man is undergoing a formal tracheostomy. The procedure is successful and they are initially managed on ITU while they recover from the operation. When performing this procedure which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"When performing this procedure which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is best performed with the neck slightly flexed\",\"e\":\"A flexed neck will reduce the surgical working space therefore the head and neck are extended to increase access to the trachea during surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Midline dissection provides a relatively avascular plane\",\"e\":\"Dissecting in the midline ensures a relatively avascular plane down to the pre-tracheal fascia. Large vessels, such as the subclavian vein and carotid arteries lie lateral to the midline, somewhat protected by the sternocleidomastoid muscles bilaterally.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Once the tracheostomy cuff is inflated further fixation is not required\",\"e\":\"Once the airway is in the trachea, the cuff may be inflated. Further fixation is then mandatory to prevent the tracheostomy tube from being displaced. A tracheostomy is a definitive surgical airway.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The first and second tracheal rings are incised\",\"e\":\"Care must be taken not to incise the first ring (the cricoid cartilage) as this will lead to airway compromise. A vertical incision through the second and third rings is usual.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The skin incision is placed between the cricoid cartilage and hyoid\",\"e\":\"A 3-4 cm transverse collar incision is made between the cricoid cartilage and sternal notch. The incision is deepened through the platysma muscle and subsequently the pre-tracheal fascia.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old female had a laparoscopic cholecystectomy 1 day ago, and has severe abdominal pain. You diagnose a bile duct injury. Which one of the following is true regarding this complication?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true regarding this complication?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It has a lower incidence than after open cholecystectomy\",\"e\":\"Rates of bile duct injury are comparable between open and laparoscopic cholecystectomy procedures, with current studies reporting an incidence of between 0.2-0.4%.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"If major, it is usually treated with balloon dilatation\",\"e\":\"Major duct transection is obvious early because of abdominal pain. It is difficult to treat and may require hepaticojejunostomy. Minor injury with a stricture causes biliary cirrhosis and malabsorption, in this situation balloon dilatation of the stricture may be of value.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It may be prevented by routine cholangiography\",\"e\":\"Bile duct injury does not seem to be prevented by routine cholangiography.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It occurs in 10% of cases\",\"e\":\"As mentioned above bile duct injury occurs in 0.2-0.4% of laparoscopic procedures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It usually results from mistaking the common bile duct (CBD) for the cystic duct\",\"e\":\"Due diligence is required when identifying the cystic duct to ensure the CBD is not damaged as this can have a lasting, and distressing, impact on the patient.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old woman is consented for a laparoscopic cholecystectomy and she asks which is the commonest of the complications that have been mentioned.\\n\\nHow would you respond?\",\"lead\":\"How would you respond?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Enterocutaneous fistula\",\"e\":\"Diathermy injury may cause a fistula, however, insufflation of the abdominal cavity with carbon dioxide during laparoscopic surgery thus reduces the risk of damage to adjacent structures as the surgical working space is increased.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemorrhage\",\"e\":\"All of the problems listed are recognised complications of laparoscopic cholecystectomy. Haemorrhage is the commonest of these. Treatment is with good surgical haemostasis and appropriate fluid resuscitation, including blood transfusion as required. As such, patients should also be consented for blood transfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Incisional hernia\",\"e\":\"This would be a long-term complication of a laparoscopic cholecystectomy and may require surgical treatment. The risk of an incisional hernia is low due to the small size of the port incisions\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"Pneumothorax may occur as a result of a diaphragmatic injury using from the diathermy hook. This is an uncommon complication.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Small bowel obstruction\",\"e\":\"Obstruction occurs due to adhesions or port site hernia and is considered a long-term complication and uncommon.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"During a carotid endarterectomy, the surgeon damages a nerve. Post-operatively the patient has a drooping lip. Which nerve was damaged?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve was damaged?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Greater auricular nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the greater auricular nerve would produce paraesthesiaesthesia/numbness around the ear lobe. This nerve arises from the cervical plexus and passes along the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid, thus it can be damaged during an endarterectomy, as it then passes into the deep fascia to the parotid gland below the platysma muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoglossal nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the hypoglossal nerve would cause tongue muscle wasting and deviation of the tongue towards the affected side. Damage to the hypoglossal nerve is a recognised complication of carotid endarterectomy, however it is rare.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Marginal mandibular nerve\",\"e\":\"The marginal mandibular nerve is a branch of the facial nerve and supplies muscles to the lower lip and chin, thus damage during an endarterectomy can manifest as a drooping lip. All the above nerves, as well as the glossopharyngeal nerve, may be damaged during carotid endarterectomy Patients must therefore be informed of possible postoperative complications including difficulty moving their tongue, the presence of a drooping lip, hoarseness, numbness around the jaw and ear lobe, and difficulty swallowing. Most of these are due to a neuropraxia from retraction as opposed to the nerves being accidentally divided and usually settle with time.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Glossopharyngeal\",\"e\":\"Damage to the glossopharyngeal would most commonly produce a loss of taste in the posterior third of the tongue.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the vagus nerve would produce a plethora of pathology given the multitude of functions including autonomic functions and parasympathetic supply to the cardiovascular, respiratory and gastrointestinal system.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"An 85-year-old woman who is unfit for a general anaesthesia (GA) undergoes spinal anaesthesia for debridement of a hamatoma. She comes through the anaesthetic without a complication and her pain control is good.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a common complication of spinal anaesthesia?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a common complication of spinal anaesthesia?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Spinal canal haematoma\",\"e\":\"In spinal anaesthesia, the local anaesthetic (around 1.5-3 ml) is placed into into the subarachnoid-arachnoid space below the termination of cord at L1. A haematoma can occur due to trauma to vessels during insertion of the spinal needle, however, this is rare.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypotension\",\"e\":\"Hypotension is a common complication of spinal anaesthesia. This is due to a reduction in sympathetic tone distal to the spinal block, subsequent venous pooling and reduced venous return. It can be managed with intravenous fluids or inotropic support if refractory to fluid replacement.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Spinal cord injury\",\"e\":\"As the insertion site is below the termination of the cord, spinal cord injury is unlikely as the true spinal cord is not at risk of damage from the spinal needle. Correct identification of an intra-vertebral space below L1 is important.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Epidural abscess\",\"e\":\"Epidural abscess is a recognised complication of spinal anaesthesia but it is rare, not common, complication. Presentation is likely to be meningitis, back pain and possible neurological deficit. Urgent spinal imaging, intravenous antibiotics and surgical drainage is required. Ensuring a sufficient sterile field, equipment and utilising aseptic technique is vitally important to reduce the risk of introducing infection.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Toxicity\",\"e\":\"Local anaesthetic toxicity can occur although this is rare. Significant toxicity occurs when local anaesthetic is injected intravenously. Drainage of CSF indicates the spinal needle is correctly in the subarachnoid space and not in a vein.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"You are assessing a patient who needs an emergency appendicectomy under general anaesthesia. The patient is 60 years of age and has a past medical history of hypertension, and suffered a myocardial infarction (MI) within the last year. The patient asks you about the risk of further MI.\\n\\nWhat is the correct information to convey?\",\"lead\":\"What is the correct information to convey?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Operation within 1 year of an MI carries a 10% risk of re-infarction\",\"e\":\"The risk of a perioperative re-infarction is approximately 5% after 1 year. A patient in extremis, or requiring an extensive surgical procedure, will have a higher risk of re-infarction due to increased cardiovascular stress.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Operation within 3 months of an MI carries a risk of re-infarction of more than 50%\",\"e\":\"The risk of a perioperative re-infarction is around 37% within 30 days. These figures are dependent upon the nature of the infarct and the nature of the operative intervention proposed.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Operation within 3-6 months of an MI carries a risk of re-infarction of approximately 16%\",\"e\":\"The risk of a perioperative re-infarction is approximately 16% at 3-6 months. Recent myocardial infarction is a significant risk factor for a further perioperative re-infarction for patients undergoing surgery. Optimisation of medical co-morbidities, such as hypertension, diabetes and hypercholestrolaemia, is paramount in reducing risk of re-infarction. Furthermore, delaying non-emergency procedures until at least 8-12 weeks post infarction is pragmatic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The resting electrocardiogram is the best indicator of ischaemic heart disease\",\"e\":\"The most important indicator of ischaemic heart disease is in the patient's history. Risk factors in the history include smoking, diabetes or hypercholesterolaemia. They should lead one to suspect myocardial disease. An electrocardiogram is more helpful in aiding the diagnosis of an acute myocardial event.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Well controlled hypertension carries a significant postoperative myocardial risk\",\"e\":\"Well controlled hypertension does not appear to be an adverse risk factor.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":70,\"stem\":\"An elderly man has presented to your service with a simple inguinal hernia. He has extensive co-morbidities and elects to have his operation under spinal anaesthesia.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding this technique?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding this technique?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It involves injecting local anaesthetic into the sub-dural space\",\"e\":\"Spinal anaesthesia involves infiltration of a local anaesthetic agent, most commonly bupivacaine, into the subarachnoid-arachnoid space below the termination of cord at L1. Onset of action is within around 5 minutes and causes complete blockage of motor and sensory nerve pathways below the level of the block\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It results in hypotension which can be due to splanchnic vasodilatation\",\"e\":\"Hypotension is a common complication of spinal anaesthesia. This is due to a reduction in sympathetic tone causing splanchnic vasodilatation and venous pooling. It can be managed with intravenous fluids or inotropic support if refractory to fluid replacement.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is associated with a symptomatic dural tap in 15-20% of cases\",\"e\":\"The incidence of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) loss (dural tap) is 1-5%, not all cases of which are clinically significant. Dural tap presents with occipital pain on standing and photophobia, and may need a blood patch if symptoms do not resolve at 48 hours.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is unsuitable for operations of duration greater than one hour\",\"e\":\"Spinal anaesthesia is suitable for operations of less than two hours duration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It can be used to provide anaesthesia for patients undergoing mastectomy operations\",\"e\":\"Spinal anaesthesia is only suitable for surgical procedures below the level of the umbilicus. A mastectomy would require a high spinal block which would risk damage to the spinal cord, paralysis of the intercostal muscles and diaphragm, and even the nervous supply to the heart. As such high spinal blocks are not undertaken.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":73,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old patient undergoes an appendicectomy and is prescribed a patient controlled analgesia (PCA) system for postoperative analgesia.\\n\\nWhat is the advantage of this?\",\"lead\":\"What is the advantage of this?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Dose cannot be changed therefore toxicity is not an issue\",\"e\":\"The PCA system usually utilises morphine as the drug of choice with a starting dose of 1 mg. If this is not sufficient to control pain the dose can be increased. Importantly, although the risk of opiate toxicity with a PCA machine is reduced, it can still occur.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Patient determines own analgesic requirements\",\"e\":\"PCA machines allow the patient to control how much analgesia is given. They usually start at 1mg morphine with a 5-min lock out time, after which they can receive a further dose, if required.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Patients have less nausea\",\"e\":\"As the PCA still uses opiate based medication and as such nausea is still a common side- effect. Prescription of anti-emetics is required.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Patients mobilise quicker with a PCA\",\"e\":\"As the patient often has a smoother analgesic profile they may well mobilise quicker, but this depends on the patient's general health. Furthermore, being attached to a PCA machine may paradoxically limit mobilisation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"There is no need to prescribe multiple analgesics\",\"e\":\"Background simple analgesics should also be given to reduce the need for breakthrough morphine.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old patient undergoes a prolonged procedure with inadequate support to his pressure areas and awakens with a wrist drop. The injury does not fully resolve and the patient pursues a medicolegal case.\\n\\nWhich nerve has been affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which nerve has been affected?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1Si Axillary\",\"e\":\"The motor function of the axillary nerve, a branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus, is to supply the teres minor and deltoid muscles. This would cause weakness in shoulder abduction and external rotation\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Median\",\"e\":\"The median nerve supplies the flexor compartment of the forearm (except for flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus). Damage to the median nerve can cause weakness in wrist flexion, loss of radial deviation, loss of thumb flexion, and loss of the function of the LOAF muscles (lateral two lumbricals, opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis and flexor pollicis brevis).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Musculocutaneous\",\"e\":\"Injury to the musculocutaneous nerve (which supplies biceps brachii, brachialis and coracobrachialis), would cause weakness of elbow flexion and forearm supination.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Radial\",\"e\":\"The radial nervet supplies brachioradialis, supinator, triceps and the wrist extensor muscles, thus a radial nerve palsy will present with wrist drop or weakness in wrist extension.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ulnar\",\"e\":\"An ulnar nerve palsy can present with both motor and sensory features. These could include: paraesthesiaesthesia in the little and ulnar half of the ring fingers, weakness of the intrinsic muscles of the hand (hypothenar muscles, interossel muscles, medial two lumbricals and adductor pollicis). Compression of the ulnar nerve can occur at the wrist or elbow.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":78,\"stem\":\"You are assisting your consultant on an elective orthopaedic theatre list and discussing the importance of surgical safety.\\n\\nShe asks you how many sections there are to the World Health Organisation (WHO) surgical safety checklist?\",\"lead\":\"She asks you how many sections there are to the World Health Organisation (WHO) surgical safety checklist?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"2\",\"e\":\"There are three sections to the WHO surgical safety checklist.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"3\",\"e\":\"There are three sections to the WHO surgical safety checklist\\n\\nThe World Health Organisation (WHO) developed the surgical safety checklist to aide in prevention of 'never events'. It is currently used worldwide, should be used for all operating lists and has improved surgical safety significantly. The surgical safety checklist consists of three sections, before the induction of anaesthesia, time out before skin incision, and sign out at the end of the procedure. It centres around good communication between all theatre staff (including introductions and role), correct identification of the patient, site of surgery and procedure to be performed, allows any anticipated problems to be highlighted, as well as estimated blood loss and if antibiotics are required.\\n\\nThere are three sections to the WHO surgical safety checklist.\\n\\nThere are three sections to the WHO surgical safety checklist.\\n\\nThere are three sections to the WHO surgical safety checklist.\\n\\nThere are three sections to the WHO surgical safety checklist\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"4\",\"e\":\"There are three sections to the WHO surgical safety checklist.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"5\",\"e\":\"There are three sections.to the WHO surgical safety checklist.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"6\",\"e\":\"There are three sections to the WHO surgical safety checklist.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"You are opening a suture kit in order to perform a wound closure.\\n\\nYour consultant asks you how you can sterilise medical equipment?\",\"lead\":\"Your consultant asks you how you can sterilise medical equipment?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Beta-radiation\",\"e\":\"Gamma radiation, not beta, can be used to sterilise medical equipment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Boiling in water for 10 min\",\"e\":\"Boiling in water only is a form of disinfection, it kills bacteria, some viruses and some spores but it does not sterilise.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Immersion in chlorhexidine and alcohol for 20 min\",\"e\":\"Low- temperature steam and formaldehyde will sterilise. Immersion in chlorhexidine and alcohol will only disinfect surgical instruments.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Nitrous oxide\",\"e\":\"Ethylene oxide can be used to sterilise, nitrous oxide, however, cannot 7ac=\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Treatment with hot air at 160°C for 2 hours\",\"e\":\"This will ensure adequate sterilisation for surgical use. Steam at 134°C can also be used for sterilisation purposes. Sterilisation is defined as a complete destruction of all viable micro- organisms including spores, viruses and microbacteria. In practice this is defined in terms of the probability of a single viable micro-organism surviving on one million items. Proper sterilisation is paramount to reduce the risk of iatrogenic infection.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":82,\"stem\":\"The trust in which you work is upgrading their theatre suite to accommodate an influx of elective cases.\\n\\nWhen designing/planning a new theatre suite, which of the following factors should be incorporated into the design?\",\"lead\":\"When designing/planning a new theatre suite, which of the following factors should be incorporated into the design?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Infected and non-infected cases must be completed in separate theatres\",\"e\":\"Infected and non-infected cases can be completed in the same theatre, either at the beginning or end of the operating list, as long as there is sufficient time to clean and decontaminate the theatre.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Individual recovery rooms are required for patient privacy\",\"e\":\"The postoperative-operative recovery phase is a critically important time where significant anaesthetic or surgical issues may arise. Individual rooms, although increasing patient privacy, could lead to a patient being unmonitored/alone in the room. An open plan recovery area allows monitoring of all patients and ease of access should issues occur.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Trauma and elective operating should be in the same theatre to utilise space\",\"e\":\"Ideally, a separate trauma theatre should be instituted as this ensures adequate theatre space for surgical emergencies. This is often the case in large, tertiary teaching hospitals, however, in smaller district general hospitals they may be constrained by theatre space and staff, and subsequently are more pragmatic in their approach to management of the trauma theatre list.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The need to maintair, a temperature of 19°C in theatres when they are operational\",\"e\":\"A comfortable temperature for theatres to run at for both patients and staff would be 21- 22°C; beneath this temperature patients may become hypothermic during long procedures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Proximity to intensive therapy unit (ITU)\",\"e\":\"In the design of a new theatre suite the operating theatres should be sited preferably away from main hospital traffic. Ideally the operating room should be on the same level and adjacent to the ITU and surgical wards, sterile supplies unit, A&E and radiology/CT. Other factors to consider would be the provision of adequate lighting, the adequate separation of clean areas and corridors from dirty areas and sluices, the proximity and size of anaesthetic rooms, air filters and provision of power, piped gas, anaesthetic gas scavenging systems and suction.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":85,\"stem\":\"You have to cancel the last case on your local anaesthetic list as the equipment you require has not been returned in time from CSSD (Central Sterile Services Department).\\n\\nWhat does the process of sterilisation of instruments involve?\",\"lead\":\"What does the process of sterilisation of instruments involve?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It involves pasteurisation\",\"e\":\"Pasteurisation is the process of heating food to decrease the number of viable pathogenic micro-organisms.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It involves steam at 34°C under pressure\",\"e\":\"Steam at 134°C can be used for sterilisation purposes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It may involves 2% glutaraldehyde when used for 2 hours\",\"e\":\"Glutaraldehyde and formaldehyde can be used as liquid sterilisation agents, but immersion times should be up to 22 hours to kill all spores for glutaraldehyde, and even longer for formaldehyde.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It involves 70% alcohol\",\"e\":\"Alcohol can be used to disinfect instruments but not sterilise.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It may involve ethylene oxide\",\"e\":\"Sterilisation is the complete destruction of all viable micro-organisms including spores, viruses and mycobacteria. A sterile medical device should have less than a one in a million probability of a surviving micro-organism. This can be achieved using steam at 134°C under pressure, ethylene oxide, hot air ovens, low-temperature steam and formaldehyde, and irradiation.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":87,\"stem\":\"You are preparing the skin for a laparotomy and wish to ensure your choice of skin prep covers against spores and fungi, in addition to bacteria.\\n\\nWhat would be the optimum choice of skin prep?\",\"lead\":\"What would be the optimum choice of skin prep?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CHI Alcohol in isolation\",\"e\":\"Alcohol, chlorhexidine and iodine are all commonly used as skin preps. Alcohol in isolation has a rapid onset against bacteria, mycobacteria, fungi and viruses but not spores and has no residual activity. It is best used in conjunction with another skin preparation agent. This is reflected in the current NICE guidelines.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AM - Chlorhexidine\",\"e\":\"Chlorhexidine is effective against most bacteria, but is inactivated by pus, soap and some plastics. Additionally, it is ineffective against some viruses.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glutaraldhyde\",\"e\":\"Glutaraldehyde is not a skin prep, and can cause sensitivity reactions. It is used to physically remove contamination, but does not necessarily destroy bacteria.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hydrogen peroxide\",\"e\":\"Hydrogen peroxide has only weak bactericidal activity, and is more often used in combination with other agents eg. iodine in infected orthopaedic procedures. Its use is now limited.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"V lodine\",\"e\":\"lodine is effective against bacteria, viruses, spores and fungi, however, it is inactivated by blood, faeces and pus. Alcoholic povidone iodine is most commonly used for skin preparation prior to surgery.\\n\\nAlthough iodine is the correct answer here, the most recent NICE guidelines 2019 [NG125] state that alcohol-based chlorhexidine should be the first choice unless contraindicated or the surgical site is next to a mucous membrane. If the surgical site is next to a mucous membrane, aqueous based chlorhexidine should be used, and iodine is used if there are contra-indications to chlorhexidine.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":90,\"stem\":\"The Department of Health has set out 'bundles' for the prevention of surgical site infections.\\n\\nWhich one of the following concerning prevention of the postoperative infection is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following concerning prevention of the postoperative infection is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Negative pressure filtered ventilation of the operating theatre prevents bacteria gaining entry with the air\",\"e\":\"By changing the operating room air 20-25 times each hour at positive pressure relative to outside the room, lower concentrations of airborne bacteria and particulate matter can be maintained\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The open wound is not at risk of contamination from airborne dust which carries bacteria derived from skin organisms of operating room personnel\",\"e\":\"Any open wound is at risk of contamination even in the operating room. As such, the number of people in the room and their movement should be minimised. A face mask which covers the nose and mouth prevents droplet spread of bacteria, is worn for any invasive procedure and is changed after each case. Eye protection in the form of plain plastic glasses or a visor attached to the face mask must be worn to protect against droplet spray of infected body fluids. Gloves are worn if there is a possibility of coming into contact with patients' body fluids. Clean theatre attire, dedicated theatre shoes, and a disposable hair cover are worn whilst in the operating suite.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The use of a plastic adhesive film (Opsite) through which the incision is made prevents contamination\",\"e\":\"The commonest source of bacterial contamination in the operating room is from the patient. Therefore, topical antiseptic agents are used to reduce the number of skin organisms prior to any skin incision or puncture. Use of an adhesive film will not prevent contamination.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The patient's own skin is a natural barrier of infection at abdominal operations\",\"e\":\"As above, the patient is the commonest source of infection. Skin prep is paramount prior to abdominal surgery. The different agents can be seen below.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ultra-clean ventilation with laminar flow and fine filter reduces the rate of wound infection by two-fold\",\"e\":\"The operating room is a dedicated area for surgical procedures and must be conducive to performing surgery to the highest standards of safety for patients and staff. The principal purpose of such a dedicated area is to reduce the risk of infection of patients. The use of ultra-clean ventilation, laminar flow and fine filter has been shown to reduce wound infection by two-fold. Skin preparation agents used prior to incision are listed below;\\n\\n• Aqueous chiorhexidine (0.5%): this is used to disinfect mucous membranes and parts of the body adjacent to structures which would be adversely affected by more stringent antiseptics (eg the skin around the eyes). • Aqueous chlorhexidine is bactericidal and has low tissue toxicity. • Cetrimide (2%) - this is bactericidal. • lodine-based antiseptics (e.g. povidone iodine (10%) [Betadine], alcoholic iodine solution): these destroy a wide range of bacteria, especially staphylococci. by iodisation of microbial proteins. • Alcohol-based (70%) antiseptics: these kill bacteria by evaporation.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":94,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old woman who had recurrent cholecystitis and confirmed gallstones on ultrasound, is having an abdominal wound closed following an open cholecystectomy. Which one of the following statements concerning incision and skin closure is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements concerning incision and skin closure is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Keloid scarring results in a proliferation of scar tissue beyond the boundaries of the original wound\",\"e\":\"Keloid scarring results in a proliferation of scar tissue beyond the boundaries of the original wound, whereas hypertropic scarring results in a proliferation of scar tissue within the boundaries of the original wound. Keloid scarring is more common in Afro-Caribbean patients, whilst injection of corticosteroid into the scar at time of skin closure may reduce degree of keloid scarring\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Midline incisions are associated with higher blood loss than paramedian incisions\",\"e\":\"Midline incisions are a non-muscle cutting incision and thus are associated with lesser blood loss than paramedian incisions, they also allow more rapid access into the abdominal cavity.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Paramedian incisions have a high incisional hernia rate\",\"e\":\"Paramedian incisions are associated with a lower incisional hernia rate, but take longer to make and close.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"the wound length/suture length ratio should be 1:5\",\"e\":\"The wound/suture length ratio is 1:4, with 1cm 'bites' 1cm apart - the Jenkins rule.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transverse incisions must be muscle cutting\",\"e\":\"Transverse incisions should ideally be muscle splitting as this aids healing, reduces recovery time, and long-term complications such as hernias are reduced.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":96,\"stem\":\"Surgical fires in the operating room represent a significant hazard. At an induction you are asked to highlight which item/s must be present for there to be a risk of combustion.\\n\\nSelect the necessary item or items.\",\"lead\":\"\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"Explanation mt\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fuel\",\"e\":\"The three requisites for a fire or an explosion are a fuel, an oxidising agent and a source of ign tion, all in close proximity, in the right conditions and in appropriate proportions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fuel and source of ignition in close proximity\",\"e\":\"This combination lacks an oxidising agent which is also required for risk of combustion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fuel and oxidizing agent in close proximity\",\"e\":\"This combination lacks a source of ignition. Diathermy and laser are the most common sources of ignition.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Fuel, source of ignition and oxidizing agent in close proximity\",\"e\":\"In situation there is no fuel or oxidising agent therefore a fire is not possible. With the decline in use of flammable anaesthetic agents, such as diethyl ether and cyclopropane, there has been a decrease in the incidence of fires and explosions in the operating room. Nevertheless, surgical fires still occur and cause significant morbidity and mortality. Fires are preventable. There are still many flammable agents in the operating room, such as tracheal tubes, surgical drapes, alcohol-based cleaning fluids and bowel gas. Diathermy and laser are the most common sources of ignition and supplemental oxygen and nitrous oxide are readily available oxidising agents.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Source of ignition only\",\"e\":\"Without fuel or an oxidising agent alongside a source of ignition (diathermy or laser), there will be no risk of combustion.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":99,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old woman with myasthenia gravis undergoes an open thymectomy for a suspected large thymoma displacing the heart. Immediately post-operatively the chest X-ray is abnormal. Compared to the original X-ray the right lower zone is opacified and the right hemidiaphragm is significantly elevated above the left.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Effusion\",\"e\":\"Both an effusion and haemothorax should be considered immediately post-operatively but in both cases the diaphragm will be obscured, in the question it states the right hemidiaphragm is seen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemothorax\",\"e\":\"If there is a haemothorax then the diaphragm would be obscured. The right lower zone is opacified as the raised hemidiaphragm has collapsed this zone.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lung consolidation\",\"e\":\"Lung consolidation, often a sign of infection, may occur with collapse of lung tissue, however this would occur later post-operatively.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Phrenic nerve injury\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve (C3, C4, C5) descends obliquely with the internal jugular vein across the anterior scalene. The right phrenic crosses anterior to the second part of the subclavian artery and, in the mediastinum, passes over the brachiocephalic artery, posterior to the subclavian vein, and then crosses the root of the right lung anteriorly. The right phrenic nerve passes over the right atrium and then leaves the thorax by passing through the vena cava hiatus opening in the diaphragm at the level of T8 Unilateral damage to the phrenic nerve manifests clinically as raised a hemidiaphragm. Phrenic nerve injury is a recognised risk during cardiothoracic surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve injury\",\"e\":\"A vagal nerve injury would not cause the features described on the chest radiograph. Damage to the vagus nerve would produce a plethora of pathology including autonomic dysfunction and disruption of the parasympathetic supply to the cardiovascular, respiratory and gastrointestinal system.\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"You are attending the surgical department's morbidity and mortality meeting. The case being discussed is regarding a patient who re-presented with a surgical site infection following their operation. Your consultant asks you about prophylactic antibiotics. Which one of the following is an indication for perioperative prophylactic antibiotics?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is an indication for perioperative prophylactic antibiotics?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ankle joint injection\",\"e\":\"This risk of introducing infection, if using appropriate aseptic technique and sterile equipment, is low for an ankle joint injection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Arthroscopic knee meniscectomy\",\"e\":\"This qualifies as clean, non-prosthetic, uncomplicated surgery and therefore would not require antibiotic prophylaxis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Carpal tunnel decompression™\",\"e\":\"Again, this is clean, non-prosthetic, uncomplicated surgery that does not necessitate prophylactic antibiotics.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Excision of lipoma under local anaesthetic\",\"e\":\"Surgical procedures under local anaesthetic do not routinely require prophylactic antibiotics as the risk of surgical site infection is low.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Total hip replacement\",\"e\":\"National Institute for Health and Clinical Excellence (NICE) recommend giving prophylactic antibiotics to patients undergoing surgery involving the placement of a prosthesis or implant. As such, total hip replacement qualifies for antibiotic cover at time of induction. Surgical site infection is a serious postoperative complication, particularly if the infection involves a prosthesis in a deep space. The National Institute for Health and Clinical Excellence recommended giving antibiotic prophylaxis to patients before:\\n\\n• clean surgery involving the placement of a prosthesis or implant • clean-contaminated surgery • contaminated surgery.\\n\\nNICE also recommended withholding antibiotic prophylaxis routinely for clean non- prosthetic uncomplicated surgery-\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":105,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man is noted to have a low haemoglobin following a low anterior resection for rectal cancer. It is decided to proceed with a blood transfusion. Shortly after this is commenced he is noted to be short of breath and feels feverish. His observations demonstrate a heart rate of 120 beats per minute, and blood pressure of 100/90 mmHg. It is thought he is experiencing an acute haemolytic transfusion reaction.\\n\\nWhich one of the following measures would be appropriate in managing this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following measures would be appropriate in managing this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fluid bolus of human albumin solution\",\"e\":\"This would not be the fluid of choice given the case history. Use of intravenous fluids, such as 0.9% saline, is appropriate while electrolyte monitoring is required. Hyperkalaemia and disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) may both occur and require specific treatment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fluid restriction to avoid pulmonary oedema\",\"e\":\"Fluid restriction alone will not improve this clinical situation and the patient requires active diuresis which includes plentiful administration of intravenous fluids to maintain urine output > 1.5 ml/kg/h and the use of furosemide can encourage diuresis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Furosemide (frusemide)\",\"e\":\"The blood transfusion should be stopped and the reaction treated. Furosemide (frusemide) would potentiate hypotension and not protect against renal injury.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"1 litre of 20% mannitol\",\"e\":\"100 ml/m2 of 20% mannitol, not 1 litre, is recommended for 'renal protection' during an acute haemolytic transfusion reaction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Stop the intravenous blood transfusion\",\"e\":\"The transfusion should be stopped and the intravenous giving set should be removed\"}]},{\"t\":45,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man with no previous medical history has been placed on the emergency list for an appendicectomy. He is asked to remain nil by mouth and may not go to theatre until later in the day. On examination, he is normotensive and does not appear to be dehydrated.\\n\\nWhat is the most appropriate fluid regime for this 70-kg patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most appropriate fluid regime for this 70-kg patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1 litre of 0.9% sodium chloride (NaCI) over 8 hours, then 2 litres of 5% dextrose with 40 mmol/i over 16 hours\",\"e\":\"This patient requires maintenance fluids. This means 25-30 ml fluid/kg/day. For this patient, that equates to 1750-21 000 ml. This option gives the patient too much fluid, as well as too much sodium (Nat). The electrolyte content of common fluid prescriptions is as below: Nat HCO; Glu Type Osm K+ Cl Ca?t Crystalloid 154 0.9% Saline 154 308 0 Crystalloid 5% Dextrose 50 250 0 0 0 Hartmann's 280 111 Crystalloid 131 2 5 Solution Colloid Volplex® or 274 154 125 <0.5 0 <0.5 0 Gelofusine@ (gelatin) Extracellular 142 280-310 103 26 0.9-1.1 5 4 Fluid Intracellular 160 10 280 - 310 0.0001 10 3 0 Fluid\\n\\nraman. Taa\\n\\nIntracellular 280 - 310 0.0001 160 3 10 10 0 Fluid\\n\\nSource: Teachmesurgery.com\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"1 litre of 0.9% NaCI with 20 mmol/l of potassium (K+) over 8 hours, then 1 litre of 5% dextrose over 16 hours\",\"e\":\"This patient requires maintenance fluids and electrolytes. This means they require approximately 2 litres of fluids and 1mmol/kg/day of Na- and 1 mmol/kg/day of K+. A volume of 1 litre of 0.9% NaCI contains 154 mmol of Nat. This is too much. This patient is also not getting enough K.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"2 litres of Hartmann's solution over 24 hours\",\"e\":\"This regime provides an appropriate volume of fluid, but the wrong quantities of electrolytes. A volume of 1 litre of Hartmann's solution provides 131 mmol of Nat and 5 mmol of K-. This regime could overload the patient with Nat and provide inadequate K-in a fully fasted patient.\\n\\nThis patient needs maintenance fluids as he is nil by mouth. Patients need 25-30 ml/kg/day of fluid. A volume of 500 ml given as a bolus would not be sufficient.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"500 ml of 0.9% NaCi bolus\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"500 ml of 0.9% NaCI with 20 mmol/l of K* over 8 hours, then 1 litre of 5% dextrose with 20 mmol/l of K* over 8 hours, then 500 ml of 5% dextrose with 20 mmol/1 of K+ over 8 hours\",\"e\":\"The fluid and electrolyte requirements for patients are as follows:\\n\\n• maintenance fluid requirements for patients: 25-30 ml/kg/day • maintenance electrolyte requirements: Nat 1 mmol/kg/day • K-1mmol/kg/day • maintenance glucose requirements: 50-100 g/day.\\n\\nThe electrolyte content of common fluids is as below:\\n\\nK+ Glu Cast Na* HCO; Osm cl Type 154 308 Crystalloid 0 154 0.9% Saline 0 0 0 0 250 50 Crystalloid 5% Dextrose 0 0 Crystalloid Hartmann's 280 131 2 111 5 0 Solution <0.5 Volplex® or <0.5 Colloid 154 274 125 0 0 Gelofusine® (gelatin) 142 Extracellular 0.9-1.1 280 - 310 5 103 4 26 Fluid 280 - 310 10 Intracellular 160 0.0001 3 10 Fluid\\n\\nSource: Teachmesurgery.com\\n\\nSource: Teachmesurgery.com\\n\\nThis regime means this patient gets the required amount of fluid (25-30 ml/kg/day × 70 kg = 1.7-2.1 litres) and their approximate requirements for Na* and K- (1 mmol/kg/day × 70 kg) and glucose. A volume of 500 ml of 0.9% NaCl provides 77 mmol of Na. A volume of 1.5 litres of 5% dextrose provides 75g of glucose. They will receive 60 mmol of K- with this regime.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 28-year-old woman is consented for resection of the terminal ileum. The patient is concerned about the impact of this surgery on their long term health. What potential complications can occur following this surgery?\",\"lead\":\"What potential complications can occur following this surgery?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Constipation\",\"e\":\"Increased faecal loss of bile salts will result in watery diarrhoa.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Folic acid deficiency\",\"e\":\"Folate is absorbed in the jejunum, and therefore deficiency does not occur after ileal resection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gallstones\",\"e\":\"Bile salts are normally reabsorbed in the terminal ileum as part of the enterohepatic circulation. In the absence of this recirculation, the bile-salt pool is decreased and the solubility of cholesterol in the bile diminished. This can in turn lead to the formation of gallstones.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Iron deficiency\",\"e\":\"Iron is mainly absorbed in the duodenum and upper jejunum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pernicious anaemia\",\"e\":\"Vitamin Bia is almost exclusively absorbed in the terminal ileum. Although pernicious anaemia leads to vitamin Bi2 deficiency, this is a specific autoimmune condition characterised by gastric atrophy and autoantibodies to intrinsic factor (which is essential for vitamin Bz absorption).\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 24-year-old woman undergoes resection of the terminal ileum with fashioning of an ileostomy for Crohn's disease. Two weeks after surgery, she is making a good recovery, and is eating a high-energy, low-residue diet, but has a high ileostomy volume that necessitates intravenous fluid replacement. Her results pre and post surgery are as follows:\\n\\nResult postsurgery Result presurgery Normal value Investigation\\n\\n2.18 mmol/l 2.20-2.60 mmol/l Serum calcium 1.82 mmol/l\\n\\n1.28 mmol/l 0.70-1.40 mmol/l Phosphate\\n\\n<150 U/l Alkaline phosphate 82 U/l\\n\\n36 g/l 30 g/l Albumin 35-50 g/l\\n\\n80 umol/l Creatine 60-110 umol/l\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause of her hypocalcaemia?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of her hypocalcaemia?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Formation of insoluble calcium salts in the intestine\",\"e\":\"Impaired fat absorption can lead to the formation of insoluble calcium salts in the gut. Although bile salts are absorbed distally, and impaired absorption can lead to a secondary decrease in proximal fat absorption, this is unlikely to be responsible for hypocalcaemia developing so quickly.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoalbuminaemia\",\"e\":\"The albumin level is not particularly low.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypomagnesaemia\",\"e\":\"Patients with ileostomies can lose large amounts of magnesium through their stomas; hypomagnesaemia impairs parathyroid hormone secretion and can cause hypocalcaemia that is resistant to an increased provision of calcium.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Malabsorption of calcium\",\"e\":\"Calcium is absorbed in the proximal small intestine.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Malabsorption of vitamin D\",\"e\":\"Vitamin D is absorbed in the proximal small intestine. The normal alkaline phosphatase level also mitigates against vitamin D deficiency.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old motorcyclist is involved in a road traffic accident. She sustains extensive pelvic fractures and is immobilised while awaiting fixation, post-operatively she is not at her previous level of mobility. Ten days into her hospital admission she develops an acutely swollen right leg.\\n\\nRegarding deep vein thrombosis, which one of the following is the gold standard investigative tool?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding deep vein thrombosis, which one of the following is the gold standard investigative tool?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"MIS Venography\",\"e\":\"Although most healthcare institutions employ venous duplex ultrasonography to investigate deep vein thrombosis, venography remains the gold standard investigation for this purpose. Duplex ultrasound and venography both have their disadvantages - eg venograms are more invasive and the patient is exposed to radiation. A duplex scan, however, is not so beneficial at looking at the deep veins of the calf.\\n\\nWhile most hospital will investigate for deep vein thrombosis using venous duplex ultrasonography, venography is the gold standard\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Venous duplex ultrasonography\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"125|- labelled fibrinogen scanning\",\"e\":\"125|- labelled fibrinogen scanning is typically confined to academic purposes (eg research).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Plan radiography\",\"e\":\"Plain radiography is a poor choice for investigating deep vein thrombosis as it has a low diagnostic yield.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"All of the above are equally used\",\"e\":\"This is not true. While venography is the gold standard, most hospitals will investigate for deep vein thrombosis using venous duplex ultrasonography.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old man is seen in the Emergency Department 2 weeks after an elective left total hip replacement due to pain and swelling of his left calf. On examination, the left leg has an increased circumference relative to the right and he is started on appropriate therapy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following options increases the risk of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options increases the risk of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Enoxaparin\",\"e\":\"Enoxaparin is a low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH) given to reduce the risk of DVT in post-operative patients.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haemophilia A\",\"e\":\"A haemophilia disorder would not increase the risk of DVT. Haemophilia is a group of hereditary bleeding disorders in which coagulation is impaired. Haemophilia A is caused by a deficiency in factor VIII.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemophilia B\",\"e\":\"A haemophilia disorder would not increase the risk of DVT. Haemophilia B is caused by a deficiency in factor IX\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Malignancy\",\"e\":\"Acquired pro-thrombotic risk factors include the combined oral contraceptive pill, pregnancy, malignant disease, myeloproliferative disorders (including polycythaemia), diabetes mellitus, hyperviscosity syndrome (eg some myelomas) and lupus anticoagulant.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Rivaroxaban\",\"e\":\"Rivaroxaban is an oral anticoagulant that acts as a direct factor Xa inhibitor. This treatment would reduce the risk of DVT.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"An 18-year-old man is being followed up for idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP). which is frequently causing him problems due to his low platelet count. He has missed significant periods of his first term at university and after careful consideration he consents to a splenectomy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following blood changes is most likely to be seen at 1 week post- operation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following blood changes is most likely to be seen at 1 week post- operation?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Lymphopenia\",\"e\":\"Lymphopenia does not typically occur.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AAI Neutropenia\",\"e\":\"An early leucocytosis (usually neutrophils) is seen within hours and may last for several weeks.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Polycythaemia\",\"e\":\"Polycythaemia does not tend to occur, but there is an increase in the number of red blood cells (RBCs) with Howell-Jolly bodies or Heinz bodies. There is also an increase in the number of target cells and sideroblasts.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thrombocytopenia\",\"e\":\"There is an early thrombocytosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Thrombocytosis\",\"e\":\"Early mobilisation following splenectomy may lead to a transient increase in platelets and predispose to thrombi. There is an early thrombocytosis, usually peaking between 7 and 10\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old man develops oedema and ascites post anterior resection for rectal carcinoma. He has a history of congestive cardiac failure. His serum albumin is 35 g/dl (normal range 35-50 g/dl).\\n\\nWhat is the most useful investigation to determine the cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most useful investigation to determine the cause?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abdominal utrasound scan (US)\",\"e\":\"A USS will confirm ascites but will give less specific information about the cause.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Ascitic tap\",\"e\":\"An ascitic tap will differentiate between exudates and transudates, hence differentiating malignant from non-malignant causes. In this case, given the borderline hypoalbuminaemia, proximity to major surgery and cardiac failure a transudate is more likely.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"computed tomography (CT) scan\",\"e\":\"A CT scan will confirm ascites but will give less specific information about the cause.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Plain abdominal film\",\"e\":\"A plain film may or may not show free fluid, and will certainly not give information about the cause.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Serum carcinoembryonic albumin (CEA)\",\"e\":\"The CEA level will probably be high given the diagnosis, but it will not inform us to whether fluid collection is due to cancer or due to heart failure.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old woman is treated after a partial gastrectomy with duodenal exclusion (Polya gastrectomy). She is seen in the clinic post-operatively and is advised of ongoing risks to her health.\\n\\nWhat bone complication of this surgery might you commonly expect?\",\"lead\":\"What bone complication of this surgery might you commonly expect?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Osteomalacia 1 year after surgery,\",\"e\":\"In this context, osteomalacia tends to occur later (10-20 years after surgery).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Osteomalacia 5 years after surgery\",\"e\":\"In this context, osteomalacia tends to occur later (10-20 years after surgery).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Osteomalacia 10 years after surgery\",\"e\":\"Post-partial gastrectomy with duodenal exclusion (Polya gastrectomy) results in a reduction of calcium absorption causing osteomalacia 10-20 years after surgery. Under normal circumstances, calcium absorption, which occurs in the duodenum and proximal small bowel, is modest at best. Following gastric surgery. calcium absorption is even less efficient as a result of rapid emptying of the stomach and bypassing of the duodenum in the case of a Billroth II. Calcium also binds tightly to unabsorbed dietary fat which further interferes with its absorption.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Osteonecrosis 1 year after surgery\",\"e\":\"In this context, osteomalacia is more common after surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Osteonecrosis 5 years after surgery\",\"e\":\"In this context, osteomalacia is more common after surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 43-year-old woman undergoes a radical neck dissection for metastatic spread of malignant melanoma. During her operation, the registrar accidentally cuts the thoracic duct. Of the following options, which one is a management option in chyle leak?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following options, which one is a management option in chyle leak?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Adequate drainage and pressure dressing\",\"e\":\"The optimum management of chyle leak includes adequate drainage, pressure dressings, serial aspirations and nutritional modifications. Monitoring and maintenance of fluid and electrolyte status are essential. Surgical options include re-exploration and ligation of the thoracic duct branches in the neck or trans-hiatal ligation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Antibiotics IV\",\"e\":\"There is no role for prophylactic antibiotics.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Blood transfusion\",\"e\":\"There is no role for blood transfusion for the management of chyle leak alone.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"High-fat diet\",\"e\":\"Nutritional modification may take the form of enteral nutrition comprising of an elemental diet supplemented with medium-chain triglycerides. These are absorbed directly into the portal system, bypassing the lymphatic system. Others have advocated total parenteral nutrition.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Keep patient nil by mouth until leak resolves with intravenous (IV) fluids.\",\"e\":\"Patients should not be kept nil by mouth for prolonged periods without nutritional support, particularly when recovering from radical neck dissection.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man complains of low-grade fever and calf pain 3 weeks after a total knee replacement. He returns to A&E with these symptoms and is subsequently referred to the Orthopaedic surgeons with a post-operative complication.\\n\\nWhat is the next appropriate step in management of this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the next appropriate step in management of this patient?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Knee aspiration\",\"e\":\"The scenario does not give us any features (apart from fever) that indicates infection at the knee joint. In the first instance, pain in the calf is more suspicious of deep vein thrombosis and this should be ruled out. If a knee aspirate is indicated, the patient should be referred to orthopaedics and the procedure should be performed in theatre (sterile environment).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Oral antibiotics\",\"e\":\"Asides from fever, there is nothing in the scenario that is strongly suggestive of infection. Investigating for deep vein thrombosis should be the primary concern.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Physiotherapy\",\"e\":\"The history post-operative calf pain and fever is suspicious for a deep vein thrombosis (DVT). If there is a DVT and it is not treated, physiotherapy may cause venous thromboembolism.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Reassure\",\"e\":\"The history post-operative calf pain and fever is suspicious for a deep vein thrombosis (DVT). Reassurance should not be given until this has been investigated. Firstly, the Wells' score for DVT should be calculated to guide the need for ultrasound or first perform a D- dimer test.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Venous duplex ultrasound\",\"e\":\"Diagnosis is confirmed with venous ultrasound. This would come after examining the patient and calculating the Wells' deep vein thrombosis score. If clinical suspicion is very high and there is a delay in scanning treatment dose clexane can be commenced.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A 71-year-old man complains of calf pain and a fever 3 weeks after a total hip replacement. What is the next appropriate step in management of this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the next appropriate step in management of this patient?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Physiotherapy\",\"e\":\"The history post-operative calf pain and fever is suspicious for a deep vein thrombosis (DVT). If there is a DVT and it is not treated, physiotherapy may cause venous thromboembolism.\\n\\nCalf pain is unlikely due to a complication of the hip replacement. The timing of symptoms is suspicious for deep vein thrombosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"X-rays\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Venous duplex scan\",\"e\":\"Diagnosis is confirmed with venous ultrasound. This would come after examining the patient and calculating the Wells' deep vein thrombosis score. Venous duplex scan is 96% sensitive and 98% specific.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Give more pain killers and review in an hour\",\"e\":\"The history post-operative calf pain and fever is suspicious for a deep vein thrombosis (DVT): Reassurance (with or without pain killers) should not be given until this has been investigated. Firstly, the Wells' score for DVT should be calculated to guide the need for ultrasound or first perform a D-dimer test.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reassure and watch\",\"e\":\"The history post-operative calf pain and fever is suspicious for a deep vein thrombosis (DVT). Reassurance should not be given until this has been investigated. Firstly, the Wells' score for DVT should be calculated to guide the need for ultrasound or first perform a D- dimer test\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":25,\"stem\":\"Following emergency surgery for an open femoral fracture the anaesthetist notices petechial haemorrhages. The patient was intubated on arrival at the Emergency Department and taken straight to theatre - his medical history is unknown.\\n\\nWhat is the likely cause of these petechial haemorrhages?\",\"lead\":\"What is the likely cause of these petechial haemorrhages?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Factor V Leiden\",\"e\":\"Factor V Leiden would cause a hypercoagulable state leading to thrombophilia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fat embolus\",\"e\":\"Fat embolism can occur following long bone fracture and its treatment. Clinical features often include fever, shortness of breath, cyanosis, tachypnoea and petechial haemorrhages. Given the history, fat embolism is the most likely cause in this case.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Scurvy\",\"e\":\"Scurvy is classically associated with swollen and spongy gums that bleed spontaneously. Early cutaneous signs of bleeding in scurvy include peri-follicular and petechial haemorrhages. It is not the most likely cause in this scenario.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vitamin K deficiency\",\"e\":\"Vitamin K deficiency causes a raised International Normalised Ratio (INR) and coagulopathy. it can be easily address with intravenous/per oral (IV/PO) vitamin K supplementation pre- operatively in the same way that warfarin can be reversed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Willebrand's disease\",\"e\":\"Von Willebrand's disease would cause easy bleeding.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":27,\"stem\":\"Following a difficult laparotomy for division of adhesions, a 56-year-old man presents with an obvious infection in his midline wound. This is easily resolved by removed several of the wound staples and allowing pus to come out of the wound.\\n\\nWhich one of the following factors is most likely to predispose him to this complication?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following factors is most likely to predispose him to this complication?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hypercalcaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypercalcaemia is not associated with a higher risk of wound infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypocarbia\",\"e\":\"Hypoxia, rather than hypocarbia, is associated with a higher risk of wound infection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypoglycaemia\",\"e\":\"Hyperglycaemia, rather than hypoglycaemia, is associated with a higher risk of wound infection.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Jaundice\",\"e\":\"General host factors predisposing to wound infection include hypoxia, jaundice, anaemia, increasing age, obesity, uraemia, malnutrition, diabetes mellitus, hypovolaemic shock, corticosteroids and immunosuppressants.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pyrexia\",\"e\":\"Pyrexia is not associated with a higher risk of wound infection.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A poorly controlled diabetic man attends with a chronic wound to the back of his finger. An extensor tendon has been eroded and he requires flap cover\\n\\nWhich complication is this patient at higher risk of developing post-operatively?\",\"lead\":\"Which complication is this patient at higher risk of developing post-operatively?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Keloid scarring\",\"e\":\"There is no reported increased risk of keloid scars.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Haematoma formation\",\"e\":\"There is no reported increased risk of haemorrhage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Non-clostricial, gas-forming wound infections\",\"e\":\"Poorly controlled diabetics have an increased risk of infection with unusual organisms due to impairment of leucocyte phagocytic activity.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Seroma formation\",\"e\":\"There is no reported increased risk of seroma formation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tendon contracture\",\"e\":\"There is no reported increased risk of tendon contracture.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A 52-year-old woman was admitted into the surgical intensive care unit after a prolonged and complex cardiac surgical operation. Her tissue perfusion was poor in the immediate post-operative period and arterial blood gas analysis showed lactic acidosis. What type of lactic acidosis did this woman have in the immediate post-operative period?\",\"lead\":\"What type of lactic acidosis did this woman have in the immediate post-operative period?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Type A\",\"e\":\"Type-A lactic acidosis occurs with decreased tissue ATP in the setting of poor tissue perfusion or oxygenation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Type B1\",\"e\":\"Type B lactic acidosis occurs when evidence of poor tissue perfusion or oxygenation is absent. Type B1 lactic acidosis occurs in association with systemic disease such as renal and hepatic failure, diabetes and/or malignancy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Type B2\",\"e\":\"Type B lactic acidosis occurs when evidence of poor tissue perfusion or oxygenation is absent. Type B2 lactic acidosis is caused by several classes of drugs and toxins, including biguanides, alcohols, iron, isoniazid and salicylates.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Type B3\",\"e\":\"Type B lactic acidosis occurs when evidence of poor tissue perfusion or oxygenation is absent. Type B3 lactic acidosis is due to inborn errors of metabolism\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Type C\",\"e\":\"There is no such thing as type C lactic acidosis. Candidates should be aware of this classification and lactic acidosis in general, however a detailed knowledge is unlikely to be required for the examination.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old woman with hypertension is being monitored on the post-operative ward. Her blood pressure is found to be very high.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause of hypertension in this patient?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of hypertension in this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Urinary retention\",\"e\":\"Urinary retention can cause hypertension indirectly by causing pain.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pain\",\"e\":\"Pain often causes an elevation of systemic arterial pressure via sympathetic nervous system stimulation. This is often related to the wound or, occasionally, to urinary retention.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Isoflurane\",\"e\":\"Isoflurane is not typically associated with hypertension in the post-operative period.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Epidural analgesia\",\"e\":\"Epidurals most frequently cause post-operative hypotension due to sympathetic blockade and loss of vasomotor tone.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Acute tubular necrosis\",\"e\":\"There is no association in this context between acute tubular necrosis and post-operative hypertension\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"You are called to see a post-operative patient in the High Dependency Unit, who underwent an elective abdominal aortic aneurysm repair 36 h ago. The patient has a reduced cardiac output.\\n\\nWhich one of the following can cause this?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following can cause this?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Long-term inotropic therapy\",\"e\":\"Positive inotropic drugs act by different mechanisms to increase the contractility of the heart, and as such have been proposed for use in patients with cardiac failure to increase myocardial contractility and hence cardiac output. However, in the long-term, excessive inotropic stimulation may increase the myocardial oxygen demand, leading ultimately to a reduced cardiac output and myocardial ischaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Respiratory acidosis\",\"e\":\"In the systemic circulation, respiratory acidosis is known to reduce left ventricular contractility. However, this is compensated for by an increased heart rate and an reduced systemic vascular resistance, thus leading to an increased cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypervolaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypovolaemia (reduced filling) will reduce cardiac output. Hypervolaemia will lead to increased filling, greater stroke volume and higher cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Systemic vasodilatat.on\",\"e\":\"Systemic vasodilatation will decrease afterload on the heart during ejection. Reducing the afterload reduces the pressure required to pump blood out of the heart therefore increasing cardiac output as the cardiac muscle does not need to work as hard to expel the same\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Administration of dobutamine\",\"e\":\"Dobutamine works primarily by directly stimulating B-1 receptors, which increase the strength of the heart's contraction. Therefore, the use of this drug primarily increases cardiac output. It has little effect on the heart rate.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"You have a patient in the intensive care unit with a Swan-Ganz catheter in situ, which was inserted approximately 4 h ago. Electrocardiogram (ECG) changes are noted following insertion.\\n\\nWhich ECG changes are most often seen following Swan-Ganz catheterisation?\",\"lead\":\"Which ECG changes are most often seen following Swan-Ganz catheterisation?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Transient left bundle-branch block occurs in up to 5% of patients and usually resolves within 24\",\"e\":\"Transient right bundle-branch block is more common.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Transient left bundle-branch block occurs in up to 5% of patients and usually resolves within 1 week\",\"e\":\"Transient left bundle-branch block is unusual.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Transient right bundle-branch block occurs in up to 5% of patients and usually resolves within 24\",\"e\":\"Transient right bundle-branch block is the commonest cardiac anomaly with Swan-Ganz catheterisation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Transient right bundle-branch block occurs in up to 5% of patients and usually resolves within 1 week\",\"e\":\"It usually resolves within 24 h.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transient bifascicular block occurs in up to 5% of patients and usually resolves on removal of the catheter\",\"e\":\"Transient right bundle-branch block is more common.\\n\\n1592\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"One of your patients suffers a cardiac arrest on the ward. The monitor shows pulseless electrical activity (PEA). The patient underwent an abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) repair 1 day ago and complained of abdominal pain before arresting.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause of the PEA arrest?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the most likely cause of the PEA arrest?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cardiac tamponade\",\"e\":\"Cardiac tamponade can cause PEA, but the most likely cause in this case is rupture at the aneurysm site causing massive internal haemorrhage and sudden hypovolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Electric shock\",\"e\":\"Electric shock usually causes a ventricular fibrillatory arrest or occasionally an asystolic arrest.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypovolaemia\",\"e\":\"The causes of a PEA arrest include hypovolaemia, hypoxia, hypothermia, hyperkalaemia/hypokalaemia, tension pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade, drug toxicity. electrolyte abnormalities, thromboembolism and mechanical obstruction. The most likely cause in this case is rupture at the aneurysm site causing massive internal haemorrhage and sudden hypovolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypoxia\",\"e\":\"Hypoxia can cause PEA, but the most likely cause in this case is rupture at the aneurysm site causing massive internal haemorrhage and sudden hypovolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Tension pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"Tension pneumothorax can cause PEA, but the most likely cause in this case is rupture at the aneurysm site causing massive internal haemorrhage and sudden hypovolaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"You have a postoperative patient on the Intensive Care Unit following an emergency laparotomy 12 h ago. The patient's central venous pressure is decreased.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is most likely to cause this?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is most likely to cause this?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epidural anaesthesia\",\"e\":\"Decreased central venous pressure can result from vasodilation due to epidural anaesthesia.\\n\\nHigh central venous pressure is an indication of overfilling or cardiac or pulmonary pathology and can be caused by MI.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Myocardial infarction (MI)\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pericardial effusion with tamponade\",\"e\":\"High central venous pressure is an indication of overfilling or cardiac or pulmonary pathology and would be present in tamponade.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Vasoconstriction in response to sepsis\",\"e\":\"Sepsis can result in a decreased central venous pressure due to vasodilation. Note that the answer states vasoconstriction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"500 ml gelofusine challenge\",\"e\":\"A fluid challenge will result in a transient high central venous pressure (CVP) in adequately filled patients and a maintained high CVP in over-filled patients.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man underwent an abdominoperineal (AP) resection for bowel carcinoma. Three days post-operation, he is pain-free but tachypnoeic and tachycardic at rest. His oxygen requirements remain high (Sp(Oz), 85% on air). His other vital signs are normal. Examination of his chest is unremarkable\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Deep vein thrombosis\",\"e\":\"Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) gives rise to swelling and pain, but alone is not associated with hypoxaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pulmonary embolus\",\"e\":\"Considering this patient's multiple risk factors for venous thromboembolism, recent major surgery, and normal chest examination, pulmonary embolismt should be suspected. Current practice dictates that therapeutic dose low-molecular-weight heparin be commenced, and urgent imaging (eg computed tomography pulmonary angiogram, CTPA) performed.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pneumonia\",\"e\":\"Given that the examination of the chest is normal, pneumonia is unlikely. The recent surgery and lack of pyrexia make a diagnosis of pulmonary emboli (PE) more likely\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pneumothorax\",\"e\":\"A pneumothorax from a central line is unlikely given that these are placed under ultrasound guidance, and would be apparent earlier. Inappropriate ventilation pressures could also lead to pneumothorax but would again be apparent much sooner.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Haemorrhage\",\"e\":\"Systemic effects of haemorrhage are hypovolaemic shock, unless very severe this would be unlikely to lead to desaturation and would be associated with a drop in blood pressure.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"A ward nurse asks you to look at one of her elderly patients whom she is concerned has developed postoperative respiratory problems. Which one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"(O=) 7 kPa and pa(COz) 8 kPa on 40% O_ suggests type 1 respiratory failure\",\"e\":\"Hypoxia and hypercarbia indicate type 2 respiratory failure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"P,(O_) 7 kPa and p,(CO2) 5 kPa on 40% O, suggests type 2 respiratory failure\",\"e\":\"Hypoxia without hypercarbia indicates type 1 respiratory failure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Adequate analgesia is an important component of management\",\"e\":\"Adequate postoperative analgesia is important to allow patients to breathe deeply and cough up secretions.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urgent chest physiotherapy is mandatory when a patient has a pulmonary embolism (PE)\",\"e\":\"Chest physiotherapy helps patients clear secret ons but has no role in the management of\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"(02) 14 kPa. p,(CO,) 2.9 kPa with a pH of 7.33 is type 2 respiratory failure\",\"e\":\"Acidosis with a normal p (0) and a low Pa (COz) would suggest a metabolic acidosis with partial respiratory compensation rather than respiratory failure.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"A patient is admitted to the Accident and Emergency department with fulminant necrotising fasciitis and taken to theatre where this is debrided. Postoperatively, he develops low urine output and raised creatinine.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"LANIAlIALIVII\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute kidney injury is drug induced in 75% of cases\",\"e\":\"About 20% of acute kidney injury* (AKI) is drug induced. The common causes of AKI include postoperative hypovolaemia, congestive cardiac failure, radiocontrast and drugs (aminoglycosides, NSAIDs).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acute kidney injury does not increase the likelihood of mortality and morbidity\",\"e\":\"Loss of renal function does lead to an increase in mortality, with reports suggesting that mortality may be up to 60%.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Acute kidney injury occurs in approximately 90% of critically ill patients\",\"e\":\"AKI is observed in about 30%-50% of critically ill patients.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Acute kidney injury is associated with distinct histological changes\",\"e\":\"The histological appearance of the kidney has not been shown to correlate to the level of renal dysfunction - a severe functional renal deficit can occur with minimal histological changes.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Acute kidney injury is usually the result of damage to the proximal tubule\",\"e\":\"The proximal tubule is the segment that is most susceptible to injury and reflects the relative hypoxia in the outer medulla and inner cortex.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old man undergoes a tonsillectomy as a day case procedure and is discharged later that day. He presents to the Emergency Department 5 days later with haemoptysis. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Secondary hacmorrhage that often requires a return to theatre\",\"e\":\"Haemorrhage following tonsillectomy (be it primary or secondary) does not often require a return to theatre; most bleeds are self-limiting.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Secondary haemorrhage that usually requires blood transfusion\",\"e\":\"Haemorrhage following tonsillectomy (be it primary or secondary) does not usually require a blood transfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Primary haemorrhage, as it has occurred within 1 week of the primary procedure\",\"e\":\"Primary haemorrhage occurs within 24 h of the primary procedure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Secondary haemorrhage, as it has occurred more than 24 h after the primary procedure\",\"e\":\"Primary haemorrhage occurs within 24 h of the primary procedure and is commonly due to technical failure, such as a ligature coming off. Secondary haemorrhage occurs 5-10 days postoperatively and can occur due to local sepsis, disiodgement of clot/slough or ligature erosion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Secondary haemorrhage that is likely to be due to anticoagulation\",\"e\":\"Anticoagulation may cause bleeding problems, but this is not classified as secondary haemorrhage. While secondary haemorrhage is more likely in patients on anticoagulation this patient is 22 years old and there is no suggestion that he is on any anticoagulation; making this highly unlikely.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A patient who had a soft tissue lump resected 1 week ago is reviewed in the Emergency Department. The wound is red, discharging pus and the patient is confused, pyrexial, tachycardic and clinically hypovolaemic with a low blood pressure. A diagnosis of septic shock is made.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding septic shock?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding septic shock?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gram-negative organisms are the most common cause\",\"e\":\"Septic shock is most commonly due to Gram-positive organisms.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Tissue uptake of oxygen by cells is normal\",\"e\":\"Tissue uptake of oxygen by cells is reduced\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Endotoxins (lipopolysaccharides) can act as negative inotropes\",\"e\":\"Endotoxins (lipopolysaccharides) act as a negative inotrope and can initiate both ARDS ano disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Blood cultures are almost always positive\",\"e\":\"Blood cultures are not always positive, with reports of up to only half of patients with sepsis having a positive blood culture.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Peripheral vasoconstriction is seen\",\"e\":\"Peripheral vasodilation is typically seen in septic shock.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient on the ward who has just come back from theatre and are asked to write up fluids by the nursing staff, as per the operation note.\\n\\nRegarding fluids, which one of the following should be borne in mind in the early postoperative period?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding fluids, which one of the following should be borne in mind in the early postoperative period?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Excessive nasogastric fluid loss should be replaced with 5% dextrose 0.45% saline\",\"e\":\"Nasogastric aspirate is high in sodium and potassium and so both saline and potassium should be replaced, in addition to standard maintenance fluids.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A low serum sodium usually indicates insufficient sodium replacement\",\"e\":\"A low sodium is usually dilutional.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fluid may be retained due to an antidiuretic response\",\"e\":\"Fluid retention may occur in response to the increased production of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) in the early postoperative period due to the stress response. This causes a dilutional hyponatraemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Isotonic dextrose is an important source of nutrition\",\"e\":\"Isotonic dextrose is only 5% dextrose and its calorific content is negligible.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Oliguria is usually caused by acute tubular necrosis\",\"e\":\"Postoperative oliguria is usually due to pre-renal fluid depletion, and not a renal cause such as acute tubular necrosis. The kidney's response to hypovolaemia is to reduce glomerular filtration rate (GFR) to maintain the circulating volume.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":57,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient on the high-dependency unit (HDU), who underwent a laparotomy 2 days ago. His bloods reveal a decreased magnesium level, for which he is receiving an intravenous magnesium sulphate infusion.\\n\\nWhat side-effects of magnesium sulphate infusion would you observe for?\",\"lead\":\"What side-effects of magnesium sulphate infusion would you observe for?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cardiac arrhythmias\",\"e\":\"Intravenous (IV) magnesium can be used in the treatment of cardiac arrhythmias, but cardiac arrhythmias are also a known side-effect of treatment with IV magnesium sulphate. Other side-effects of the infusion (due to hypermagnesaemia) include nausea, vomiting. thirst, hypotension, coma, respiratory depression, drowsiness, confusion, loss of tendon reflexes, and muscle weakness.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cerebral vasospasms.\",\"e\":\"Cerebral vasospasm is more likely to be due to a low magnesium level.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Coronary vasospasms\",\"e\":\"Coronary vasospasm is more likely to be due to a low magnesium level.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypertension\",\"e\":\"IV magnesium is more likely to cause hypotension as a side-effect.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Muscle cramps\",\"e\":\"IV magnesium is more likely to cause muscle weakness as a side-effect.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":59,\"stem\":\"You review a 60-year-old woman in clinic who underwent a laparotomy 8 days ago for bowel obstruction secondary to adhesions and who has returned with unilateral ankle oedema. You learn from the patient that she has been in bed mostly after being discharged from hospital due to developing a cold. She has no other past medical history other than having a hysterectomy 10 years ago. What is the most likely cause of ankle oedema?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of ankle oedema?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Heart failure\",\"e\":\"Cardiac failure is more likely to cause bilateral oedema. Unilateral oedema could be due to either deep vein thrombosis (DVT) or acute inflammation, but in a patient 7-10 days post- operative DVT is the most likely cause.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Alcoholic liver disease\",\"e\":\"Osmotic shift caused by alcoholic liver disease (due to a reduction in albumin production) would more likely cause bilateral oedema.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Deep vein thrombosis\",\"e\":\"Unilateral cedema could be due to either deep vein thrombosis (DVT) or acute inflammation, but in a patient 7-10 days post-operative, exacerbated by the reported immobility, DVT is the most likely cause.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lymphoedema\",\"e\":\"Obstructed lymphatic drainage to the lower limb is unlikely to have occurred from this operation. While it could be caused by malignancy, lymph node dissection and enlarged lymph nodes, which could cause unilateral oedema, it is unlikely as there is no history of cancer. The recent surgery and period of immobility makes deep vein thrombosis (DVT) more likely in this scenario.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypoproteinaemia\",\"e\":\"Osmotic shift caused by a reduction in serum protein would more likely cause bilateral oedema.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":62,\"stem\":\"An 80-year-old retired orthopaedic surgeon presents to the Emergency Department with 5 days of faeculent vomiting, peri-umbilical colic, abdominal distension and absolute constipation. He undergoes a laparotomy with division of band adhesion. His postoperative course is complicated by prolonged ileus. Which one of the following is correct with regard to this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct with regard to this patient?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epidural infusion of opiates for pain control is contraindicated\",\"e\":\"Epidural opiates are an effective way of pain control, which is more important in the elderly to allow adequate cough reflex and ventilation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"He should be commenced on total parenteral nutrition\",\"e\":\"This man has been without adequate calories for at least 7 days in view of his clinical presentation and prolonged ileus. It is recommended that total parenteral nutrition be commenced if there has been inadequate intake of calories for at least 7 days.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Low urine output is commonly due to a renal cause\",\"e\":\"Low urine output is commonly due to a pre-renal cause. Poor absorption of fluids from the intestine leaves the patient in a state of hypovolaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Nasogastric tube fluid losses are best replaced with intravenous 0.9% saline\",\"e\":\"Nasogastric tube (NG) losses will cause depletion of K=, Cland H*. Fluid replacement should include dextrose/saline with the addition of KCI.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Oral intake is not contraindicated\",\"e\":\"Oral intake with an ileus will cause vomiting.\\n\\n2D03\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":64,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man is undergoing a gastroenterostomy. He is consented for the procedure and is counselled about the possible risks of the operation.\\n\\nWhat is the best way to prevent dumping syndrome?\",\"lead\":\"What is the best way to prevent dumping syndrome?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Excise antrum\",\"e\":\"Preserving the pylorus is the best strategy to prevent dumping syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Excise pylorus\",\"e\":\"Preserving the pylorus is the best strategy to prevent dumping syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Preserve antrum\",\"e\":\"Preserving the pylorus is the best strategy to prevent dumping syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Preserve cardia\",\"e\":\"Preserving the pylorus is the best strategy to prevent dumping syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Preserve pylorus\",\"e\":\"If the pylorus is removed, the risk of dumping syndrome is increased, as this increases the rate of gastric emptying. Dumping syndrome can be categorised as early (30-60 min after a meal) or late (1-3 h following a meal).\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"A 19-year-old boy undergoes an emergency appendicectomy. The appendix had perforated with free pus in all four quadrants of the abdomen. He is noted to have a pyrexia 12 hours postoperatively.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is most applicable to postoperative fever in this scenario?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is most applicable to postoperative fever in this scenario?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It cannot be due to thrombophlebitis\",\"e\":\"Thrombophlebitis can be a cause of fever, particularly if it migrates to become deep venous thrombosis. This is rare, however.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is frequently due to urinary tract infection (UTI)\",\"e\":\"Unless the patient has been catheterised, it is unlikely for a 19-year-old man to develop a spontaneous UTI.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is most likely due to wound infection\",\"e\":\"It is possible for a wound infection to cause fever. However, wound infections would generally occur at least 72 hours following surgery, and thus it is unlikely that this is the source of pyrexia in this patient, in the immediate post-operative period.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is most likely due to the systemic inflammatory response to surgery\",\"e\":\"Post-operative pyrexia* is an extremely common presentation. It is something you will deal with clinically, in the exam, and in interview scenarios. A structured approach (ABCDE) is required to ensure you do not miss a cause, and that you instigate treatment while assessing the patient. The most common cause in the initial post-operative period is a normal systemic inflammatory response to the insult of surgery. It would be dangerous to assume this in all cases however, and therefore you should actively seek and consider alternative causes, such as ongoing infection, Addisonian crisis etc. Other causes must be actively sought and excluded- post-operative inflammatory response should be considered a diagnosis of exclusion\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Within the first 6 his always due to transfusion reaction\",\"e\":\"Other causes should be actively sought and excluded, eg a thyroid crisis, adrenocortical insufficiency in patients with Addison's disease or transfusion reaction if the patient has received a blood transfusion.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A patient is seen on the ward after undergoing a routine open cholecystectomy 36 h ago. The patient is pyrexial with a temperature of 37.9°C.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause of this pyrexia?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of this pyrexia?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Atelectasis\",\"e\":\"Atelectasis can cause fever without bacterial infection.The potential causes of postoperative pyrexia* vary based on time since surgery:\\n\\n• Day 1: Systemic response to trauma/surgery, pre-op infection • Day 2-3: Atelectasis, chest infection • Day 3-7: Infection (wound, urinary, abdominal, chest), anastomotic leak • Day 7-10: Thromboembolic event (deep vein thrombosis (DVT), PE)\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Blood transfusion\",\"e\":\"A blood transfusion can cause fever, but there is no mention of this patient having a transfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) I\",\"e\":\"Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is possible, but DVT more commonly occurs 7-10 days postoperatively.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"lleus\",\"e\":\"A paralytic ileus per se does not cause postoperative pyrexia, but may indicate possible sepsis. J\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urinary tract infection\",\"e\":\"A UTI is possible, but this tends to manifest after 72 h postoperatively. In the current scenario, atelectasis is more likely\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":72,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old Afro-Caribbean woman undergoes repair of her flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus of her little finger under general anaesthesia and a tourniquet. Postoperatively she becomes confused and reports severe chest pain. Her haemoglobin (Hb) iS 7.\\n\\nGiven her condition, which one of the following statements can be applied?\",\"lead\":\"Given her condition, which one of the following statements can be applied?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Conjugated bilirubin is raised\",\"e\":\"Conjugated bilirubin is not typically raised in sickle cell anaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Her anaemia will be characterised as chronic haemolytic anaemia with a high reticulocyte count\",\"e\":\"This patient has sickle cell anaemia, common in patients of Afro-Caribbean and Asian descent. Sickle cell anaemia is typically associated with a normocytic, normochromic blood picture with a high reticulocyte count. Normal haemoglobin has two alpha and two beta chains. In sickle cell disease at least one of the beta chains is replaced by haemoglobin S, resulting in formation of characteristic sickling of cells. These block small vessels resulting in vaso-occlusive events.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Her anaemia will be microcytic and normochromic\",\"e\":\"Sickle cell anaemia is typically associated with a normocytic, normochromic bicod picture.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Her low Hb is a result of reduced haemoglobin production\",\"e\":\"The rate of Hb production is normal, but the cells are sickled and sequestered and removed in the spleen.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The treatment of choice is long term iron supplements\",\"e\":\"The treatment of an acute crisis is analgesia, along with intravenous fluids or blood transfusion. Iron supplements are not recommended in sickle cell anaemia. Iron overload can occur and increase the risk of sickling crises.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"A patient who underwent dynamic hip screw fixation for fractured neck of femur 6 days ago is reviewed. Postoperatively the patient required a 2 unit transfusion. The patient appears jaundiced and has a haemoglobin of 8 g/di, which has decreased from 10 g/dl 2 days previousiy. Their clotting screen is normal.\\n\\nWhich of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Disseminated intravascular coagulation\",\"e\":\"Acute haemolytic and bacterial transfusion reactions may both lead to disseminated intravascular coagulation, occurring more rapidly following transfusion. In critically ill patients the only sign of disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) may be prolonged bleeding from venepuncture sites. The clotting screen in this patient is normal making DIC unlikely.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fat embolism\",\"e\":\"While fat embolism can cause an anaemia, there are no other features (eg hypoxia, dyspnoea, rash, or coma) that indicate this. Fat embolism would likely have manifested before day 6 following surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haemolytic reaction following transfusion\",\"e\":\"A delayed haemolytic reaction (>24 h following a transfusion), resulting from antibodies developing against transfused red cells, can cause the scenario described. Clinically an unexplained jaundice and a drop in haemoglobin may be the only features:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"lleus\",\"e\":\"Ileus is common following surgery for a fractured neck of femur in elderly patients, particularly those with other medical comorbidities and these patients commonly have electrolyte disturbances on their bloods. However, it is not the cause of the haemoglobin drop described.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Upper gastrointestinal bleed\",\"e\":\"Upper gastrointestinal bleeding may present with melana, which has not described in the scenario. A rise in the urea level is often also found in addition to the drop in haemoglobin. The history of transfusion makes a transfusion reaction more likely.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":78,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old woman undergoes a breast reduction and is discharged home. She re- presents 10 days later with pain, erythema and discharge of pus.\\n\\nWhich one of the following are correct with regard to wound infection?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following are correct with regard to wound infection?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is associated with overzealous use of diathermy\",\"e\":\"Overuse of diathermy is linked to increased risk of wound infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It commonly results from aerial contamination\",\"e\":\"Airborne organisms have a minor role in wound infections.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It is commonly observed before the third postoperative day\",\"e\":\"Wound infection is observed 3-5 days after surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is not usually due to endogenous organisms\",\"e\":\"Recent work has shown that the main source of bacteria is the patient's own tissues.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"There is no relationship between the length of operation and the risk of infection\",\"e\":\"Longer operations are associated with an increase in the risk of surgical site infection.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"You have performed a tracheostomy and are writing the post-operative notes. The patient's airway is secure but they are initially going to be managed on ITU and you are not seeing the patient again until after the weekend.\\n\\nWhen would you recommend the earliest time, the first tube change takes place?\",\"lead\":\"When would you recommend the earliest time, the first tube change takes place?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Day 1 post-operation\",\"e\":\"Tube changing should be avoided until at least 3 days post-operation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Day 3 post-operation\",\"e\":\"Tube changing should be avoided until at least 3 days post-operation, until a track is well established and the tube changed easily.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Day 2 post-operation\",\"e\":\"Tube changing should be avoided until at least 3 days post-operation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"on post operation.\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"12 h post-operation\",\"e\":\"Tube changing should be avoided until at least 3 days post-operation.\\n\\nTube changing should be avoided until at least 3 days post-operation.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":82,\"stem\":\"A 76-year-old man underwent tracheostomy for long-term ventilation. A few weeks later the tube was removed but the patient had hoarseness of voice.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely complication?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely complication?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Surgical emphysema\",\"e\":\"Surgical emphysema is more likely to have occurred during the initial tracheostomy procedure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Pneumonia\",\"e\":\"Pneumonia is unlikely to cause hoarseness, and is unlikely in the absence of other symptoms or signs.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Air embolism\",\"e\":\"Air embolism is more likely to have occurred during the initial tracheostomy procedure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Haemorrhage\",\"e\":\"Significant haemorrhage causing compression is unlikely at this point postoperatively.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Subglottic stenosis\",\"e\":\"Subglottic stenosis occurs if the first and second tracheal rings are damaged.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":84,\"stem\":\"A 29-year-old woman underwent total thyroidectomy. Post-operatively she complained of difficulty in breathing. What would be the most likely cause of this?\",\"lead\":\"What would be the most likely cause of this?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve damage during dissection of the upper pole vesseís\",\"e\":\"Damage to one recurrent laryngeal nerve will cause hoarseness. Damage to both nerves will cause respiratory distress.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypoparathyroidism in 10% cases\",\"e\":\"Damage to or resection of the parathyroid glands causes hypocalcaemia. Severe hypocalcaemia presents as confusion, tetany, paraesthesia, seizures, and arrhythmias. Hypoparathyroidism would not cause the airway obstruction described.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Significant Haematoma\",\"e\":\"An expanding haematoma is one of the immediate complications following thyroid surgery. If there is airway obstruction, the patient needs to be considered peri-arrest and should be seen by an anaesthetist immediately. Surgical clips can also be removed from the operating site to allow release of the haematoma.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tachycardia, hyperpyrexia and confusion\",\"e\":\"A thyroid crisis is a potentially life-threatening condition that is rarely seen but classically occurs in a patient who has undergone surgery without adequate preoperative preparation. It presents with tachycardia, fever and confusion.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Damage to the external superior laryngeal nerve\",\"e\":\"Damage to the external superior laryngeal nerve may go unnoticed. This nerve innervates the cricothyroid muscle and the ability to make high pitched sounds. The classical exam scenario is of a professional singer who can no longer make his or her high notes.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":87,\"stem\":\"An otherwise healthy 77-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia returns to the urology ward following a trans urethral resection of the prostate (TURP). Three hours later he becomes very confused and then has a seizure. The operation notes indicate it was a particularly difficult TURP which took much longer than expected.\\n\\nWhat is the most likely cause of this deterioration?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause of this deterioration?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SN Delirium tremens\",\"e\":\"There is no mention of alcohol excess in the history. Although delirium tremens can cause sudden confusion and seizures, it typically occurs a few days following the last alcohol consumption.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN Epilepsy\",\"e\":\"It is too much of a coincidence for a patient's first seizure of epilepsy to come so soon after a Transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP) procedure. A single seizure with a known cause is not a diagnosis of epilepsy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Postoperative infection\",\"e\":\"It is too soon postoperatively for infection to cause a patien: to become unwell. Even if infection was present, it would unlikely manifest as a seizure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Stroke\",\"e\":\"It is rare for a stroke to present as a seizure, and the absence of other neurological deficits makes this diagnosis unlıkely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP) syndrome\",\"e\":\"TURP syndrome is an uncommon, but important complication of a TURP. It occurs due to excessive absorption of hypotonic irrigation fluids used during the operation. It is most common in larger resections that are difficult with heavy bleeding. The main problems that result include electrolyte disturbances - in particular hyponatraemia, fluid overload and cerebral oedema. This can result in confusion, impaired consciousness and seizures. The management consists of fluid restriction, the use of diuretics as required and supportive management, usually in an high dependency unit (HDU) setting.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":90,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old woman who is 8 days post anterior resection with a stapled anastomosis reports some discomfort in the left iliac fossa while mobilising around the ward. She is tachycardic and this morning spiked a temperature of 37.7°C. She has some left iliac fossa discomfort.\\n\\nWhich is the best dynamic test to organise?\",\"lead\":\"Which is the best dynamic test to organise?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Flexible sigmoidoscopy\",\"e\":\"A flexible sigmoidoscopy can be useful to look at the mucosa for evidence of ischaemia but should be used with great caution as this could disrupt the anastomosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Barium enema\",\"e\":\"A barium enema will demonstrate a leak but leakage of barium into the peritoneal cavity can cause peritonitis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Computed tomography (CT) scan with intravenous (IV) contrast\",\"e\":\"A CT scan with just IV contrast gives a static image - it may show a collection of fluid or a gas bubble next to the anastomosis with a high index of suspicion for leak but is not as accurate as showing an active leak such as with a contrast enema. In a relatively well patient a CT scan may show fluid, which may be difficult to differentiate between normal postoperative fluid and an active leak:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gastrografin enema\",\"e\":\"A dynamic test is best to demonstrate an active leak from the anastomosis - a water-soluble enema such as Gastrografin is the best test. A wisp of contrast may be seen from the anastomosis on X-ray screening, which diagnoses an active leak\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ultrasound Scan\",\"e\":\"Ultrasound would not be particularly useful. It may show a fluid collection, but will not prove it is from the anastomosis.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":92,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old woman is found to have a low rectal cancer through her local bowel screening programme. Following multi-disciplinary team discussion with review of her MRI (which suggests a radiological T3 N1 MO tumour she has had 6 weeks of chemoradiotherapy followed by a laparoscopic low anterior resection and defunctioning stoma. She follows an enhanced recovery programme postoperatively.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is the most applicable to this programme?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is the most applicable to this programme?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A lumbar epidural for analgesia also minimises the duration of postoperative ileus\",\"e\":\"Thoracic, rather than lumbar, epidurals reduce the duration of postoperative ileus, as does goal directed fluid therapy and judicious use of intravenous fluids postoperatively.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Insufflation with warm and humidified carbon dioxide gas has a positive benefit towards pain control and maintenance of normothermia\",\"e\":\"Insufflating the abdomen with warm and humidified carbon dioxide has not been shown to improve postoperative pain scores, core temperature or length of hospital stay\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Preoperative carbohydrate loading reduces postoperative Insulin resistance\",\"e\":\"Preoperative carbohydrate loading reduces postoperative insulin resistance, and has also been shown to reduce anxiety, thirst, nausea and hunger.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Preoperative carbohydrate loading should be given within 2 hours of surgery\",\"e\":\"Preoperative carbohydrate loading is feasible up to 2 h before administering general anaesthesia. The patient should then be fasted in the 2h before surgery to reduce aspiration risk during anaesthetic. It would therefore be inappropriate to give carbohydrate drinks during this time.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Postoperative early feeding has no effect on nitrogen equilibrium\",\"e\":\"Carbohydrate loading with postoperative early feeding results in a lower postoperative nitrogen loss.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":94,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old obese diabetic man is admitted with leg cellulitis. He soon becomes more unwell and complains of increased pain. His cellulitis spreads, his Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) level drops and he becomes hemodynamically unstable requiring intropic support to maintain his blood pressure, urine output and cerebral perfusion.\\n\\nWhere should his care be managed?\",\"lead\":\"Where should his care be managed?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acute medical ward\",\"e\":\"This patient is too unwell to remain on the medical ward. The ward is not equipped to support a patient requiring invasive monitoring and intropic agents.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Acute surgical ward\",\"e\":\"This patient is too unwell to remain on a surgical ward. Moving the patient from a medical ward to a surgical ward will not offer invasive monitoring or intropic support.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Coronary care ward\",\"e\":\"A coronary care unit is best equipped to treat patients with cardiac conditions:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"High dependency unit (HDU)\",\"e\":\"HDU care (level 2) is indicated in reversible single organ failure requiring single agent support, except for advanced respiratory support which is deemed level 3 [Intensive Treatment Unit (ITU)]. This patient has multi-organ failure (brain, kidney and the cardiovascular system) and therefore needs level 3 care.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Intensive care unit\",\"e\":\"ICU or Intensive Treatment Unit (ICU/ITU) care (level 3*) is indicated in the presence of two or more reversible organ failures, or the requirement for advanced respiratory support. The ITU will be able to provide inotropic support, invasive monitoring, invasive ventilation if required, and one-to-one nursing.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":96,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old man has just undergone an abdominoperineal (AP) resection for bowel cancer. You are asked to prescribe postoperative fluids.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is the best indicator to assess adequacy of fluid balance/status?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the best indicator to assess adequacy of fluid balance/status?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"STI Blood pressure\",\"e\":\"While blood pressure is useful when assessing the patient's volume status, particularly to see if they respond to a fluid challenge, urine output is an indicator of end-organ perfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Heart rate\",\"e\":\"While heart rate is useful when assessing the patient's volume status, urine output is a better indicator of end-organ perfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Respiratory rate\",\"e\":\"Respiratory rate can be elevated in severe hypovolaemia; however urine output is a better indicator of end-organ perfusion.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Temperature\",\"e\":\"Febrile patients have higher fluid requirements. However, temperature alone does not indicate end-organ perfusion and does not dictate fluid requirements.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urine output\",\"e\":\"Urine output is the single best indicator of end-organ perfusion, 0.5 ml/<g/h in adults and 1 ml/kg/h in children should be the minimum target. Level of consciousness can also be an indicator of cerebral end-organ perfusion.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"A 77-year-old woman develops a low-grade pyrexia 12 h after dynamic hip screw fixation for a fractured neck of femur. Examination is unremarkable and her vital signs are otherwise normal.\\n\\nWhat one option is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What one option is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Basal lung atelectasis\",\"e\":\"Basal atelectasis from inadequate ventilation of the lung bases that is often a consequence of insufficient analgesia (which reduces respiratory excursion and coughing) can be associated with a low-grade pyrexiat. This often responds to chest physiotherapy (not antibiotics). The body's stress response to the surgical insult can also cause a pyrexia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"DVT\",\"e\":\"DVT is possible, but is unlikely with a normal examination. DVT more commonly occurs at 7 -10 days post operation. Fever is not a component of Wells' score for DVT, which is used to stratify the suspicion of DVT.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"PE\",\"e\":\"PE is possible, but is unlikely with no cardiorespiratory signs or compromise. Fever is not a component of Wells' score for PE, which is used to stratify the suspicion of PE\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urine infection\",\"e\":\"A high temperature alone is not enough to be called sepsis. Most pyrexia in the first 24 h post operation is unrelated to infection - either related to the systemic response to surgery or to atelectasis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wound infection\",\"e\":\"12 h post operation is too soon in most surgical cases for wound infection to manifest.\\n\\nWells' score for DVT: The Wells' DVT Criteria can be used in the outpatient and emergency department setting. By risk stratifying to low risk (Wells' Score <2) and a negative D-dimer, the clinician can exclude the need for ultrasound (US) to rule out DVT.\\n\\nActive cancer treatment or palliation within 6 months\\n\\nNo 0 Yes+1\\n\\nBedridden recently >3 days or major surgery within 12 weeks\\n\\nNo 0 Yes+1\\n\\nCalf swelling >3 cm compared to the other leg measured 10 cm below tibial tuberosity No 0 Yes+1 Collateral (nonvaricose) superficial veins present\\n\\nNo 0 Yes+1\\n\\nEntire leg swollen No 0 Yes+1 Localized tenderness along the deep venous system No 0 Yes+1\\n\\nWells PS, Anderson DR, Rodger M, Forgie M. Kearon C, Dreyer J, Kovacs G, Mitchell M. Lewandowski B, Kovacs MJ. Evaluation of D-dimer in the diagnosis of suspected deep-vein thrombosis: N Engl J. Med. 2003;349:1227-35.\\n\\nLewandowski B, Kovacs MJ. Evaluation of D-dimer in the diagnosis of suspected deep-vein thrombosis. N Engl J Med. 2003;349:1227-35. Wells' score for PE:\\n\\nPatients with suspected pulmonary embolism can be stratified on the basis of pretest probability and D-dimer result. A score of less than 2 is considered unlikely for PE and can safely and decreases the need for diagnostic imaging\\n\\nSymptoms of DVT (3 points) No alternative diagnosis better explains the illness (3 points)\\n\\nTachycardia with pulse > 100(1.5 points) Immobilization (>= 3 days) or surgery in the previous four weeks (1.5 points)\\n\\nPrior history of DVT or pulmonary embolism (1.5 points) Presence of hemoptysis (1 point)\\n\\nPresence of malignancy (1 point)\\n\\nWells PS, Anderson DR, Rodger M, Still I, Dreyer JF, Barnes D, Forgie M, Kovacs G, Ward J, Kovacs MJ. Excluding pulmonary embolism at the bedside without diagnostic imaging: management of patients with suspected pulmonary embolism presenting to the emergency department by using a simple clinical model and D-dimer. Ann Intern Med. 2001,135:98-107. PubMed PMID: 11453709.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old woman undergoes resection of a vaginal carcinoma and 2 h postoperatively she develops confusion, anuria and low blood pressure that fails to respond to a fluid challenge: Her early warning score is deteriorating.\\n\\nWhere should her care be continued?\",\"lead\":\"Where should her care be continued?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Coronary Care Unit (CCU)\",\"e\":\"The coronary care unit is a more appropriate care setting for those patients with cardiac disease requiring monitoring, eg arrhythmias, unstable angina, myocardial infarction etc.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN ITU\",\"e\":\"This patient has multiple organs/systems (brain, cardiovascular system and kidneys) that are beginning to fail due to a postoperative complication. This patient needs invasive, multi- system monitoring (for example, an arterial line), cardiac support (inotropes or vasopressors) and one-to-one caret. • Given the operation was just two hours ago haemorrhage could be present, which would need to be managed in the operating theatre if not responsive to early resuscitation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medical HDU\",\"e\":\"As there is more than one system failing in this patient, intensive care unit (ITU) (level three care) is required rather than HDU (level two care).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Surgical HDU<\",\"e\":\"As there is more than one system failing in this patient, intensive care unit (ITU) (level three care) is required rather than HDU (level two care)\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ward based\",\"e\":\"Due to the severity of this patients condition, the patient requires level 3 care in ITU, rather than on a general ward.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":105,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) undergoes bladder resection for carcinoma. Postoperatively he develops breathing difficulties. Blood gas analysıs reveal type 1 respiratory failure. What is the most likely cause?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely cause?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alveolar hypoventilation leading to collapse\",\"e\":\"Atelectasis is a common cause of eariy type 1 respiratory failure following surgery. In the context of postoperative patients, alveolar hypoventilation is commonly caused by inadequate breathing due to pain or impaired clearing of secretions.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Infective exacerbation of COPD causing CO_ retention\",\"e\":\"COPD causing a low P, (02) and a high P(CO) retention is type 2 respiratory failure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Excess morphine\",\"e\":\"Excess morphine can impair the spontaneous rhythm of breathing generated by the cardiorespiratory centre in the brainstem. This causes ventilatory failure, and so type 2 respiratory failure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Myasthenia gravis\",\"e\":\"Myasthenia gravis causes muscle weakness and in myasthenic crises may cause ventilatory failure, and so type 2 respiratory failure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Raised intracranial pressure\",\"e\":\"Raised intracranial pressure may cause type 2 respiratory failure by affecting the pattern of spontaneous breathing.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old man who has undergone an AP resection for bowel cancer develops respiratory distress. He is found to have bilateral pulmonary infiltrates on chest X-ray. A diagnosis of ARDS is made and he is provided with ventilatory support. Which one of the following supportive measures should be considered next?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following supportive measures should be considered next?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Nutritional supplementation\",\"e\":\"Nutritional support is important, typically through a nasal tube rather than intravenously, especially if the patient requires invasive ventilation.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fluid boluses\",\"e\":\"Fluid boluses will not improve acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inhaled nitric oxide\",\"e\":\"Nitric oxide has no clinically proven, important benefit in large studies:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ventilatory support with high tidal volumes\",\"e\":\"Patients with ARDS should be managed with ventilator support with low tidal volumes to reduce further lung injury.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ward based management\",\"e\":\"Patients with ARDS should be managed in a critical care setting.\\n\\n2858\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":109,\"stem\":\"You are the paediatric surgical CT1. A 1-year-old child is admitted for cleft palate repair, and shortly after the procedure you are called to see her urgently as there is bleeding from the surgical site.\\n\\nWhat should you do first?\",\"lead\":\"What should you do first?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Call for help and manage the child using ABC first principles\",\"e\":\"All such cases require management according to first principles. The most probable cause of haemorrhage at this stage is that a suture has come undone. Children can become haemodynamically unstable very quickly. The child may need to be returned to theatre as an emergency to stop the bleeding, but this decision should be made by your seniors following assessment.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Commence fluid resuscitation with colloids\",\"e\":\"Fluid resuscitation should be commenced to replace past, present and ongoing losses however this is almost always done with crystalloids. Blood transfusion may be required if the bleeding is significant.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Cross match and transfuse 2 units immediately\",\"e\":\"Transfusion should aim to replace blood loss. Transfusion should be preceded by an ABCDE assessment, and the patient reassessed after each unit.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Immediately take the child back to theatre\",\"e\":\"Transfer to theatre would only occur following assessment using an ABCDE approach and a senior decision.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"This is normal - It is not necessary to do anything immediately\",\"e\":\"Acute haemorrhage from the site of any operation is never normal. Patients should be seen urgently and ABC principles should be applied to initial assessment.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":112,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man is admitted with pancreatitis (modified Glasgow Score 4) and then develops acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) for which invasive ventilation is required.\\n\\nWhere should this patient have his continued care?\",\"lead\":\"Where should this patient have his continued care?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Coronary care unit (CCU)\",\"e\":\"The coronary care unit is a setting most appropriate for patients with cardiac disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intensive treatment unit (ITU)\",\"e\":\"Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) is a life-threatening, inflammatory lung condition that can be a complication of a severe pancreatitis. This patient requires invasive ventilationt, which requires an ITU\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Medical high-dependency unit (HDU)\",\"e\":\"This patient requires invasive ventilation which is not offered in the HDU.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Surgical ward\",\"e\":\"The patient requires an intensive care unit (ITU) for invasive ventilation, one-to-one care and close monitoring.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Surgical high-dependency unit (HDU)\",\"e\":\"This patient requires invasive ventilation, which is not offered in the HDU.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":114,\"stem\":\"You have been asked to review an elderly patient who underwent a radical retropubic prostatectomy during the day. The nurses are concerned as he is confused and agitated.\\n\\nWhich one of the following should you be aware of regarding postoperative neuropsychiatric disturbances in the elderly?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following should you be aware of regarding postoperative neuropsychiatric disturbances in the elderly?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"May be precipitated by hypoxia\",\"e\":\"Hypoxia can cause delirium in any patient, but the elderly will be particularly susceptible.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"May acutely manifest as impairment of long term memory\",\"e\":\"Isolated impairment of long-term memory is not typical in delirium - short-term memory is more likely to be affected.\\n\\nBiochemical disturbances can cause delirium and hyponatraemia is a common cause, rather than hypokalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"May result from hypokalaemia\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Need urgent psychiatric referral\",\"e\":\"Psychiatric referral is usually unnecessary and an organic cause for the delirium should be investigated and treated. This includes a general 'confusion screen' including review of medications, blood tests and investigation for infection (eg urine dip) and a computed tomography (CT) head scan if indicated or no other cause is found.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Usually does not require any active management\",\"e\":\"In all cases of postoperative confusion a search for a reversible underlying cause should be undertaken for example: review of medications, treatment of hypoxia, infection or biochemical disturbance, further investigation such as a chest X-ray or CT head scan. This is called a 'confusion screen' in some hospitals.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":116,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man who underwent cholecystectomy 1 day ago develops postoperative acute kidney injury (AKI). Which factor directly increases the risk of this?\",\"lead\":\"Which factor directly increases the risk of this?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Benign prostatic hypertrophy\",\"e\":\"Benign prostatic hypertrophy can lead to acute on chronic urinary retention, which can lead to acute kidney injury (AKI) if left untreated. Catheterisation in the short-term relieves the pressure on the kidneys and facilitates accurate fluid balance - helpful in monitoring fluid balance. Preoperative hypovolaemia has a direct impact on the rate of AKI (while BPH does not always lead to AKI) and is therefore the better answer in this case.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hypercholesterolaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypercholesterolaemia is not a significant risk factor for developing AKI.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypothyroidism\",\"e\":\"Hypothyroidism is not a significant risk factor for developing AKI.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Perioperative hypertension\",\"e\":\"Hypotension, rather than hypertension, is a risk factor for developing AKI. Hypotension leads to impaired renal perfusion.\\n\\n2867\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Hypovolaemia\",\"e\":\"Hypovolaemia is a common cause of 'pre-renal' AKI and be caused by several surgical factors: including dehydration, haemorrhage and sepsis.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":118,\"stem\":\"A 32-year-old man undergoes replantation of his right thumb. You are asked to see him in the recovery room as he is in a lot of pain.\\n\\nPostoperative pain can be most reliably assessed by which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Postoperative pain can be most reliably assessed by which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Healthcare assistants assessing on a subjective basis by observation\",\"e\":\"Pain is an 'experience' of the patient and is therefore determined by the patient whenever possible, rather than the subjective observation of an onlooker.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"High respiratory rate\",\"e\":\"Patients can become tachypneic when they are in pain, but this is not a-specific sign. A patient reported measure is best used.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Tachycardia\",\"e\":\"Patients can become tachycardic when they are pain, but this is not a-specific sign. A patient reported measure is best used.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Urine output\",\"e\":\"Urine output does not correlate with pain. PASTEST Verbal Scale:\\n\\nNo pain\\n\\nMild pain\\n\\nModerate pain\\n\\nSevere pain\\n\\nVery severe pain\\n\\nWorst possible pain\\n\\nNumerial Scale:\\n\\n0 = no pain.. 5 = Moderate pain ... 10 = Worst possible pain\\n\\nVisual analogue scale:\\n\\nPatient marks on scale where their pain lies\\n\\nAssessing pain in children from about the age of 3, using a 'smiley faces/sad faces' picture range.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Categorical rating scale\",\"e\":\"Pain is defined as 'an unpleasant sensory and emotional experience associated with actual or potential tissue damage'. So, pain is an 'experience' of the patient and is therefore best determined by the patient whenever possible. Pain should be assessed and recorded by categorical rating scales such as a visual analogue scale, or verbal/numerical reporting scale.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":121,\"stem\":\"A 31-year old man sustained an open tibial fracture requiring intramedullary nailing and a latissimus dorsi free flap. Eight months post operation he reports pain, swelling and erythema at the fracture site. Inflammatory markers are raised and X-ray reveals periosteal elevation.\\n\\nWhat is the underlying pathology?\",\"lead\":\"What is the underlying pathology?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SI Non-union\",\"e\":\"Non-union does occur and would result in pain and problems with the leg, but the rising inflammatory markers, swelling and erythema, and discharge suggests an infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Failure of metal work\",\"e\":\"Failure of metal work does occur, but the rising inflammatory markers, swelling and erythema, and discharge suggests infection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Osteomyelitis\",\"e\":\"He has likely developed osteomyelitis, as periosteal reaction on the X-ray is highly suggestive of this. Treatment of osteomyelitis involves long-course intravenous antibiotics and surgical debridement.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Sinus tract\",\"e\":\"Sinus tracts can occur, but the periosteal reaction on the X-ray described makes the option of osteomyelitis more likely.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Soft tissue cellulitis\",\"e\":\"It may well be that there is infection of the skin and soft tissue, but the periosteal reaction on the X-ray is highly suggestive of underlying bone involvement in keeping with osteomyelitis.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":123,\"stem\":\"A 54-year-old man undergoes skin grafting to a fasciotomy wound on the lower leg. The graft fails to take and he is found to have a foul smelling, green discharge from the wound\\n\\nWhat is the most likely pathogen?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely pathogen?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Fungi\",\"e\":\"Fungal infection of these wounds is uncommon.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Mixed anaerobes\",\"e\":\"Fungal infection of these wounds is uncommon.\\n\\nPolymicrobial wound infections do occur, but the features described (green discharge and foul smell) are typical of Pseudomonas infection.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pseudomonas aeruginosa\",\"e\":\"Pseudomonas is strongly associated with graft failure. Topical silver can be used to treat the wound bed and eradicate micro-organisms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"staphylococcus aureus\",\"e\":\"Staphylococcus aureus can indeed cause infection of wounds and skin grafts, but the features described (green discharge and foul smell) are typical of Pseudomonas.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Streptococcus\",\"e\":\"Streptococcus can cause infection of wounds and skin grafts, but the features described (green discharge and foul smell) are typical of Pseudomonas.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":125,\"stem\":\"You are discussing the ventilation techniques that will be employed in an oesophagectomy that is happening later in the day\\n\\nWhich of the following cases absolutely must have one-lung ventilation?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following cases absolutely must have one-lung ventilation?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Elective pneumonectomy\",\"e\":\"One lung ventilation may be used in elective pneumonectomy to help the surgeon with access, but it is not essential to do so.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Open Ivor-Lewis oesophagectomy\",\"e\":\"Single lung ventilation/one lung ventilation is not essential for open Ivor-Lewis oesophagectomy, it would however be required during minimally invasive oesophagectomy using video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Open thoracic aortic aneurysm repair\",\"e\":\"Open thoracic aortic aneurysm repair is a relative indication for single lung ventilation, however it is not an absolute indication and therefore is not mandatory.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Middle lobectomy of the lung\",\"e\":\"One lung ventilation may be used in middle and lower lobe lobectomy of the lung to aid access for the operating surgeon, however it is not essential to do so.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"None of the above\",\"e\":\"None of the aforementioned operations MUST have one-lung ventilation. One-lung ventilation in these cases is to help the surgeon with access, and is not essential. The absolute indications for one-lung ventilation are any condition in which one lung has to be isolated and protected, e.g. when the other lung is severely contaminated. Another absolute indication is when the distribution of ventilation needs to be controlled, e.g. there is traumatic damage to a main bronchus, or a unilateral bullae that may blow. The only surgical procedure that absolutely needs one-lung ventilation is video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS).\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":128,\"stem\":\"The recurrent laryngeal nerve is at risk during surgery to the cervical spine using the anterior approach. What clinical features would suggest bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy?\",\"lead\":\"What clinical features would suggest bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hoarseness\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Stridor\",\"e\":\"Stridor is in keeping with a bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy.\\n\\nHoarseness can occur with a unilateral paisy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"breathy' voice.\",\"e\":\"This can occur in a unilateral palsy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Dyspnoea on exertion.\",\"e\":\"Shortness of breath on exertion can occur with unilateral palsy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Loss of low pitched voice\",\"e\":\"Pitch is controlled by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":130,\"stem\":\"Which one of the following patients will most likely require potassium replacement?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following patients will most likely require potassium replacement?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A patient with acute kidney injury\",\"e\":\"Severe acute kidney injury (AKI) can cause hyperkalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A patient commenced on an insulin sliding scale\",\"e\":\"Insulin increases cellular update of potassium and commencing an insulin sliding scale poses the risk of hypokalaemia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"A patient with an Addisonian crisis\",\"e\":\"Hyperkalaemia occurs in an Addisonian crisis, so replacement is not indicated.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH)\",\"e\":\"SIADH can be a cause of hyponatraemia, but does not usually affect potassium levels.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"As first-line treatment in a patient who develops torsades de pointes\",\"e\":\"Intravenous (IV) magnesium sulphate is the first-line treatment 'torsade de pointes', however K- should also be normalised if it is found to be low.\"}]},{\"t\":46,\"p\":132,\"stem\":\"You have a 60-year-old patient who had a previous myocardial infarction (MI) just over a year ago. He is going to undergo an elective surgical abdominal aortic aneurysm repair. On which post-operative day is MI most likely to occur?\",\"lead\":\"On which post-operative day is MI most likely to occur?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Day 1\",\"e\":\"A number of studies have shown that post operative MI is most likely to occur in day 1 post operatively. However, they can be delays in detection as patients are less likely to be able to report symptoms. Perioperative myocardial ischaemia occurs most commonly in those with a history of a previous MI, in the intraoperative period (during surgery). The more recent the MI, the greater the risk of re-infarction: Patients complaining of chest pain at this point should be investigated thoroughly.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Day 2\",\"e\":\"MI is more likely to occur later.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Day 3\",\"e\":\"Perioperative myocardial ischaemia occurs most commonly in those with a history of a previous MI, in the intraoperative period (during surgery). A number of studies have shown that PMis are detected late - between POD3 and POD5, although they occur earlier. This may be due to the fact that patients are less likely to be able to report symptoms.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Day 4\",\"e\":\"MI is more likely to occur on day 3.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Day 5\",\"e\":\"MI is less likely at this stage.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"Two patients are being consented for the elective urology list - one nephrectomy followed by a transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP).\\n\\nWhen obtaining consent for a TURP, what are the complications you would mention?\",\"lead\":\"When obtaining consent for a TURP, what are the complications you would mention?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bladder perforation, erectile dysfunction, incontinence, haematuria, retrograde ejaculation, UTI, urethral stricture, deep vein thrombosis (DVT), pulmonary embolism (PE) and death\",\"e\":\"Procedure-specific complications for a TURP include: retrograde ejaculation, erectile dysfunction, incontinence, haematuria, UTI, urethral stricture and bladder perforation. Systemic complications include: DVT, PE and there is a small risk of death. In addition there is a risk of TURP syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Bladder perforation, erectile dysfunction, incontinence, priapism, retrograde ejaculation\",\"e\":\"Haematuria should also be discussed when gaining consent for a TURP. Additionally, priapism (a persistent erection without sexual stimulation) is not a recognised complication.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Bladder perforation, priapism, deep vein thrombosis, urethral stricture\",\"e\":\"While some of these conditions are potential complications of TURP. priapism is not a recognised complication, Furthermore, there are other potential complications such as erectile dysfunction, retrograde ejaculation, UTI and haematuria that need discussion to gain informed consent\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, death\",\"e\":\"When counselling a patient about a TURP systemic complications should be discussed, such as deep vein thrombosis (DVT), pulmonary embolism (PE) and death (which occurs in 0.5- 1% of cases). This, however, neglects the other local complications discussed above\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Bladder perforation, erectile dysfunction, pulmonary embolism, retrograde ejaculation, death\",\"e\":\"This does not include all of the potential complications of TURP that require discussion with the patient before surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old man with Crohn's disease is admitted with vomiting, colicky peri-umbilical pain and abdominal distension. He has had a previous ileocolic resection. A computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen reveals multiple small bowel strictures. What should you be aware of regarding the management of this man?\",\"lead\":\"What should you be aware of regarding the management of this man?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Intravenous fluids do not have a role in managing this patient\",\"e\":\"Depending on the patient's current hydration status, they may require intravenous fluids. Furthermore, intravenous fluids will be required during surgery to prevent complications such as acute kidney injury.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intravenous hydrocortisone and parenteral nutrition are required\",\"e\":\"In Crohn's disease the inflammation is classically described as transmural. 'Rose thorn' ulcers are deep ulcers that traverse beyond the lamina propria and have a characteristic appearance. Aphthous ulcers occur anywhere from the mouth to the anus. Short-bowel syndrome is why it is best to try to avoid surgery whenever possible with Crohn's disease, but in very severe cases in which less than 50 cm of small bowel remains, malabsorption of essential fat soluble vitamins (A, D, E and K) as well as other essential nutrients requires lifelong parenteral nutrition. Crohn's colitis is not uncommon but the small bowel is more often involved and usually seen by the time colitis occurs. Crohn's disease spreads as 'skip lesions' and, unlike ulcerative colitis, the spread is not usually in a continuous fashion. All of these options are possible in Crohn's disease. It is usually not possible to differentiate between inflammatory and fibrous strictures and the best course of management is a trial of medical therapy (intravenous steroid and parental nutrition). If this fails then surgery is indicated.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Stricturoplasty is immediately required\",\"e\":\"Given the challenges of surgery in patients with Crohn's disease, a trial of medical therapy would be appropriate before any surgical intervention, including stricturoplasty.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The patient needs a small bowel resection\",\"e\":\"The main aim of surgery is bowel preservation and strictureplasty is the primary option, if not then, small bowel resection together with strictureplasty.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The symptoms are definitely caused by adhesions\",\"e\":\"The patient has undergone significant abdominal surgery in the past and therefore there is a risk of adhesion formation causing small bowel obstruction, however given the case history it is not possible to say the strictures are definitely caused by adhesions.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old man is in burns intensive care having sustained 42% burns in a house fire: It is day 12 post injury and he remains intubated and ventilated. The Consultant Anaesthetist feels he will not be ready for extubation for a while and the decision is made to perform a tracheostomy. A size 7 tracheostomy tube is used\\n\\nWhich one of the following does the tube size relate to?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following does the tube size relate to?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Distance from tracheostomy tube tip to carina\",\"e\":\"Sizing refers to the internal diameter, not the distance from tracheostomy tube tip to the carina.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Distance of tracheostomy tube from vocal cords\",\"e\":\"Given individual anatomical variation, creating standardised sizing in relation to distance from the vocal cords would be challenging.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Internal diameter of the tracheostomy tube\",\"e\":\"A size 7 tracheostomy tube relates to an internal diameter (ID) of 7 mm, not the outer diameter or length. Tracheostomy should be performed if prolonged intubation is expected; either percutaneous or open approaches are used. Fenestrated tubes allow air to be diverted superiorly through the vocal cords so speech can be possible, but this can also be attained if the tube is occluded with a finger. The first tracheal ring is complete in children and must not be excised for fear of tracheal stenosis. The preferred method is excision of rings 2-4.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Length of the tracheostomy tube\",\"e\":\"It is the internal diameter, not the length, of the tracheostomy tube that sizing refers to.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Outer diameter of the tracheostomy tube\",\"e\":\"Sizing refers to the internal diameter of the tracheostomy tube, not the outer diameter.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":11,\"stem\":\"A patient diagnosed with a thyroglossal cyst is going to be admitted for surgery on the cyst. What is the most important preoperative test?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most important preoperative test?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"T3 and T4\",\"e\":\"Thyroid function tests are usually normal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH) levels\",\"e\":\"As discussed, thyroid function tests, including TSH, are usually normal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Echocardiogram\",\"e\":\"While dysfunction in thyroid function may result in dysrhythmias, thyroid function is usually normal in these patients.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Isotope scan\",\"e\":\"Thyroglossal cyst results from the persistence of the thyroglossal tract. It presents clinically as a neck swelling. Although it most commonly occurs at the level of the hyoid bone in the midline, it can be anywhere between the foramen caecum, above the manubrium, and between the sternomastoid muscles. As thyroglossal cysts can contain the only functioning thyroid tissue in the body, isotope scan should be planned before surgery to identify all functioning thyroid tissue.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cyst ultrasound scan\",\"e\":\"The cystic nature can be confirmed using ultrasound scan, however, an isotope scan is the most important as thyroglossal cysts can contain the only functioning thyroid tissue in the body.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"A 30-year-old man undergoes a diagnostic laparoscopy for right iliac fossa pain. At operation an inflamed appendix is visualised and removed without spillage of bowel content. When classifying surgical procedures according to the risk of wound contamination, uncomplicated appendicectomy is an example of which of the following?\",\"lead\":\"When classifying surgical procedures according to the risk of wound contamination, uncomplicated appendicectomy is an example of which of the following?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Clean\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Clean contaminated\",\"e\":\"In a clean contaminated wound the viscus wall is breached, but the contents are contained and no spillage occurs.\\n\\nIn a clean wound the viscus wall is not breached.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Contaminated\",\"e\":\"In a contaminated wound the viscus wall is breached and contents spilled.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Dirty\",\"e\":\"In a dirty wound there is already pus or spilled bowel contents.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Infected\",\"e\":\"Infected is not part of the classification of risk of wound contamination.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"You are undertaking an audit and need to record P-POSSUM scores for your patients.\\n\\nWhich one of these is a physiological component of the P-POSSUM score?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is a physiological component of the P-POSSUM score?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"PT Creatinine\",\"e\":\"Creatinine is not assessed but urea is included in the P-POSSUM score.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gender\",\"e\":\"The only demographic parameter included in the P-POSSUM score is age.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Glasgow Coma scale (GCS)\",\"e\":\"P-POSSUM score (Physiological and Operative Severity Score for the enUmeration of Mortality and Morbidity) comprises 12 physiological factors and six operative factors. Scores for the physiology components can be either 1, 2, 4 or 8 depending on the severity, and operative factors are scored similarly (except for two components where 4 is the maximum score). The overall physiology and operation scores are then applied to a logistic regression formula to calculate morbidity and mortality. It can be used for both emergency and elective general surgical operations, in most UK hospitals it is mandatory before emergency laparotomy. It should be calculated at the time of surgery, not on admission. It should not be used in trauma patients.\\n\\nPhysiological factors Operative factors\\n\\nOperalive complexily Age\\n\\nNumber of procedures being performed Cardiac status\\n\\nElectrocardiogram (ECG) Blood loss\\n\\nRespiratory status Peritoneal contaminationExtent of malignant spread\\n\\nBlood pressure Mode of surgery (elective or emergency)\\n\\nPulse rate\\n\\nGlasgow coma scale\\n\\nHaemoglobin\\n\\nWhite cell count\\n\\nUrea\\n\\nSodium\\n\\nPotassium\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"RY Temperature\",\"e\":\"Temperature does not form part of the parameters assessed by the P-POSSUM score.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Urine output\",\"e\":\"Renal function, including sodium, urea and potassium are considered by the P-POSSUM score, however, urine output is not.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"You request lung function tests for a pre-operation patient. You are concerned that they will not be fit for their elective procedure and refer them on to the respiratory physicians for optimisation.\\n\\nTo measure which one of the following lung volumes, would you have to additionally request a helium wash-out technique?\",\"lead\":\"To measure which one of the following lung volumes, would you have to additionally request a helium wash-out technique?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Forced expiratory volume in one second\",\"e\":\"Forced expiratory volume in 1 second is the maximal volume of air the patient can exhale in 1 S:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Functional residual capacity\",\"e\":\"Functional residual capacity (FRC) includes residual volume that cannot be expelled into a spirometer. FRC (residual volume plus expiratory reserve volume) is measured by a helium wash-out technique. Body plethysmography can also be used to assess FRC, and is replacing the helium dilution technique.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"inspiratory capacity\",\"e\":\"Inspiratory capacity is the maximum inspiration starting from the normal expiratory position.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Maximum ventilation volume\",\"e\":\"Maximum ventilation volume is the greatest volume of air that can be breathed in a given time (litres/min).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vital capacity\",\"e\":\"Vital capacity is the maximum volume of air that can be expelled from the lungs by forceful effort after a maximum inspiration.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"You are asked to pre-assess a 64-year-old woman who has been listed for elective abdomino-perineal resection for bowel cancer. Among her medications, you find that she s on postmenopausal hormone therapy.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is true of her hormone therapy?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is true of her hormone therapy?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It reduces the risk of breast cancer\",\"e\":\"Postmenopausal hormone-replacement therapy also increases the risk of breast cancer. In addition, hormone therapy may increase the risk of ovarian cancer but lower the risk of colon cancer.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It decreases the risk of thrombosis\",\"e\":\"Postmenopausal hormone-replacement therapy increases the risk of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary emboli (PE) due the procoagulant effect of ostrogen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"No changes need to be made before surgery\",\"e\":\"Current National Institute of Clinical Excellence (NICE) guidelines suggest patients should consider stopping hormone-replacement therapy 4 weeks before elective surgery. For emergency surgery, peri-operative thromboprophylaxis is required.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It should be increased before surgery\",\"e\":\"Hormone replacement should be stopped, not increased, before elective surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It can contain ostrogen alone in patients who have had a hysterectomy\",\"e\":\"The current NICE guidelines suggest that HRT containing ostrogen alone should be offered to women without a uterus. The addition of a progestogen is required to women who have a uterus.\\n\\nThe association between unopposed ostrogen therapy and endometrial hyperplasia/neoplasia has been previously established, with a 20% incidence of endometrial hyperplasia shown at one year of unopposed ostrogen use.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"As part of the preoperative investigations for a patient requiring urgent surgery the anaesthetist requests that the patient's functional residual capacity (FRC) is checked.\\n\\nHow can FRC be measured?\",\"lead\":\"How can FRC be measured?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Exercise stress test\",\"e\":\"An exercise stress test is used to assess patients with suspected ischaemic heart disease. The patient undergoes a set programme on a treadmill while connected to continuou: electrocardiogram (ECG) monitoring; a positive stress test would be when there are ischaemic changes on the ECG or the patient experiences chest pain.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Helium dilution technique\",\"e\":\"Functional residual volume (FRC) is measured by the helium dilution technique or body plethysmography. The FRC is the residual volume plus the expiratory reserve volume and represents < 50% of the vital capacity. FRC decreases when supine and increases on standing. It also varies with height and body build.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Nitrogen dilution technique\",\"e\":\"It is the helium, not the nitrogen, dilution technique that is used to assess FRC.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spirometry\",\"e\":\"Expiratory reserve can be measured directly by spirometry but FRC cannot be assessed by spirometry alone.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Wrights peak flow meter\",\"e\":\"This is the standard peak flow meter used by asthmatics that measures their peak expiratory flow rate through the bronchi and so the degree of obstruction in the airways.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A patient presenting with poorly controlled asthma requires a lung function test. What is lung spirometry routinely used to directly measure?\",\"lead\":\"What is lung spirometry routinely used to directly measure?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"FEV1\",\"e\":\"The spirometer is able to measure the forced vital capacity (FVC) (not the total lung capacity) and the FEV1 (forced expiratory volume in 1 s).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"FEV1.FVC ratio\",\"e\":\"From spirometry measurements the FEV1:FVC ratio can be derived but the spirometer does not directly measure this ratio. FEV1 is the dynamic measure of flow in spirometry.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Functional residual volume T\",\"e\":\"Functional residual volume is measured by helium dilution or body plethysmography.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The peak expiratory flow rate\",\"e\":\"The peak expiratory flow rate is routinely measured with a Wright's peak flow meter.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Total lung capacity\",\"e\":\"This is the total lung capacity and cannot be measured directly by spirometry. It can be calculated by adding the inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume, expiratory reserve volume and the residual volume.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"You are reviewing a patient in pre-operation clinic before a total knee replacement and he tells you that he has type 1 diabetes mellitus.\\n\\nWhich one of the following should you bear in mind when assessing him?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following should you bear in mind when assessing him?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"He will have normal function of the Langerhans cells of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"Type 1 diabetes mellitus results from disruption in the function of B-cells in the islets of Langerhans.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"He will normally have ketones present on dipstick testing of his routine pre-op urine sample\",\"e\":\"Ketonuria is present when the patient is in a catabolic state and metabolising fats instead of carbohydrates. A patient will not routinely have ketonuria in the presence of well controlled diabetes.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"He will be more prone to superficial infection as a post-op complication\",\"e\":\"Infection has an incidence of 1-2% following total knee replacement. Factors increasing the risk of infection include diabetes mellitus, rheumatoid arthritis, poor nutrition, obesity and poor surgical technique.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"On glucose tolerance testing he will have a resting blood glucose of 5 mmol/1 which rises to 10 mmol/l in half an hour and then returns to 5 again after 2\",\"e\":\"On oral glucose tolerance testing the plasma concentration of glucose will increase to above 11.1 mmol/L 2 h following administration of 75g of glucose.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"He will have low levels of glucagon in the blood\",\"e\":\"Type 1 DM leads to a reduction in blood insulin. Loss of insulin mediated inhibition of glucagon leads to higher than expected blood levels. If the preoperative starvation period results in more than one meal being missed, then National Institute of Clinical Health (NICE) guidance recommends the use of variable rate insulin infusions with glucose and electrolyte infusion to prevent hypoglycaemia.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"You are prescribing pre-operation medications for a patient undergoing elective joint replacement surgery, including prophylactic antibiotics.\\n\\nWhat does the choice of pre-op antibiotics in joint replacement depend on?\",\"lead\":\"What does the choice of pre-op antibiotics in joint replacement depend on?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Age of patient\",\"e\":\"A patient's age should not alter the choice of antibiotic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Means of excretion of the antibiotic\",\"e\":\"This factor should not alter the choice of prophylactic antibiotic, however guidance from British National Formulary (BNF) should be sought with regards to dosing.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sensitivity of most likely organisms\",\"e\":\"The antibiotic chosen will depend of local resistance patterns.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Surgical approach used\",\"e\":\"The surgical approach used should not influence the choice of antibiotic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Which joint is being replaced\",\"e\":\"The individual joint should not alter the choice of antibiotic.\\n\\nThe choice of prophylactic antibiotics should be based on the type of operation being performed (but not the individual joint in the case of joint replacement) and the type of bacteria you wish to prevent from causing infection. Antimicrobial susceptibility will vary according to local resistance patterns.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":33,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old woman is consented for laparoscopic cholecystectomy. She is worried about the procedure and wants to know as much information as possible.\\n\\nWhich one of the following would you mention?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would you mention?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Liver cirrhosis alone is an absolute contraindication\",\"e\":\"Cirrhosis is not a contraindication for laparoscopic cholecystectomy, however there is increased risk of bleeding and postoperative infection.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Operative cholangiography is mandatory during laparoscopic cholecystectomy\",\"e\":\"Cholangiography is only required in the presence of common bile duct (CBD) stones. These can be detected by preoperative MRCP and if this is negative there is no need for intra- operative cholangiography.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Previous abdominal surgery is an absolute contraindication\",\"e\":\"Previous abdominal surgery can make the procedure more challenging due to the presence of adhesions but is not a contraindication.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"On average, patients go home on the same day as the procedure\",\"e\":\"There is an NHS initiative to ensure that 60% of laparoscopic cholecystectomies are performed as day case procedures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"20% require conversion to open cholecystectomy\",\"e\":\"Around 5% of laparoscopic cholecystectomies require conversion to open procedures.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":35,\"stem\":\"An overweight 46-year-old woman is being referred to general surgery with gallstones and biliary colic, she has never suffered from cholecystitis. Following careful consultation she is offered laparoscopic cholecystectomy\\n\\nWhich one of the following should be discussed, as it is a recognised complication?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following should be discussed, as it is a recognised complication?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"2% mortality rate\",\"e\":\"Mortality rates following laparoscopic cholecystectomy are around 0.22-0.4%\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"50% risk of conversion to open surgery\",\"e\":\"There is a 5% risk of conversion to open surgery\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"5% common bile duct injury\",\"e\":\"Common bile duct injury occurs in around 0.5% of laparoscopic\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"5% risk of venous thromboembolism\",\"e\":\"Risk of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) (without prophylaxis) is around 1-2%\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Overall 0.5% risk of common bile duct injury\",\"e\":\"Risk of bile duct injury is around 0.5%.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old woman with a recently excised malignant melanoma of the leg returns to the clinic with a palpable groin lump suggestive of lymphadenopathy. She is concerned the cancer may have spread.\\n\\nWhat is the next best step in her management?\",\"lead\":\"What is the next best step in her management?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Computed tomography (CT) scan Cy\",\"e\":\"This option should be performed to assess for distant metastases, but fine-needle aspiration (FNA) of the groin lump is indicated.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fine needle aspiration\",\"e\":\"First line investigation is FNA (under ultrasound guidance) to assess for the presence of regional metastases.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Groin dissection\",\"e\":\"This investigation is only required if biopsies are positive for metastatic disease.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Open node biopsy\",\"e\":\"This biopsy should be performed in the eventuality of inconclusive fine-needle aspiration.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"PET scan\",\"e\":\"A PET scan is not currently recommended by NICE as part of the work-up for metastasis in malignant melanoma.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old man is seen in clinic seeking an abdominoplasty following rapid weight loss. He currently weights 120 kg. down from 160 kg.\\n\\nWhich one of the following regarding obesity is correct?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regarding obesity is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Body mass index (BMI) is a widely used means of estimating obesity in patients\",\"e\":\"A BMI is a simple measure for estimating obesity using the patient's weight and height measurements.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"A BMI greater than 35 is consistent with 'morbid obesity\",\"e\":\"A BMI of greater than 40 is described as morbidly obese\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"BMI = weight (kg) /height (m)\",\"e\":\"BMI is calculated as weight (kg)/height? (m=)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Obese patients do not normally require HDU/Intensive Therapy Unit (ITU) care\",\"e\":\"Obesity increases the risk of requiring mechanical ventilation, tracheostomy placement and stays on critical care.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vena caval filters should be used to reduce the risk of a pulmonary embolus\",\"e\":\"Obesity is a risk factor for venous thromboembolism, however the mainstay of prophylaxis is pharmacological and mechanical (TED stockings and/or calf pumps). The routine use of vena caval filters is not currently recommended by NICE\\n\\nA normal BMI lies between 20-25, while a BMI of between 25-30 is classed as being 'overweight'. A BMI of between >30-40 is obese; and a BMI of over 40 is 'morbidly obese' BMI is equal to weight (kg)/height (m):. Obese patients encounter significantly greater complications, both intra- and post-operatively. ITU care must be considered and beds booked before admission.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":42,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old insulin-dependent diabetic is seen in clinic. He is due to undergo elective circumcision under general anaesthetic.\\n\\nWhich one of the following interventions are most appropriate?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following interventions are most appropriate?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"He should be placed first on the theatre list\",\"e\":\"Diabetics should ideally be placed first on the list to prevent prolonged starvation. Often other factors may change this, but in an ideal scenario this would be the case.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"He should have a sliding scale, this should be taken down in recovery\",\"e\":\"Glucose/potassium/insulin (GKI) infusions should remain up until the patient is eating and drinking normally.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"He should have addition oral hypoglycaemics on the morning of his surgery\",\"e\":\"There is no need for further oral hypoglycaemics as the patient will be in a state of starvation; this would risk making them dangerously hypoglycaemic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"He should not be fasted for 6 h before surgery\",\"e\":\"The requirement for fasting is dependent on the general anaesthetic rather than co- morbidities, and so the patient should be fasted for 6h. If anything, this is more important in diabetics as they often have a degree of gastroparesis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"He should remain solely on long-acting insulin\",\"e\":\"There is no need for the patient to be solely on long-acting insulin as he will be starved. If he becomes hyperglycaemic, this can be controlled with a sliding scale, which usually employs short acting insulin. He should go back to his usual insulin regime once the surgery is complete and he is eating and drinking.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":44,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man is admitted to the Emergency Department with a compound tibial fracture. You are asked to book him for theatre urgently. Which confidential enquiry into peri-operative deaths (CEPOD) category do you use?\",\"lead\":\"Which confidential enquiry into peri-operative deaths (CEPOD) category do you use?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"CEPOD category 1 - Immediate\",\"e\":\"CEPOD 1 refers to conditions threatening the patient's life or limb, eg a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, or compartment syndrome:\\n\\n• 1 IMMEDIATE - Immediate life, limb or organ-saving intervention - resuscitation simultaneous with intervention. Normally within minutes of decision to operate:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"CEPOD category 2 - Urgent\",\"e\":\"Compound fractures should be washed out within 6 h and so fall into category 2:\\n\\n• 2 URGENT - Intervention for acute onset or clinical deterioration of potentially life- threatening conditions, for those conditions that may threaten the survival of limb or organ, for fixation of many fractures and for relief of pain or other distressing symptoms. Normally within h of decision to operate. Compound fractures should be washed out within 6 h and so fall into category 2. Another example is an appendicectomy for acute appendicitis\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"CEPOD category 3 - Expedited\",\"e\":\"CEPOD category 3 refers to patients in whom early treatment will suffice to improve their prognosis. An example is an acute cholecystectomy ('hot gallbladder')\\n\\n• 3 EXPEDITED - Patient requiring early treatment where the condition is not an immediate threat to life, limb or organ survival. Normally within days of decision to operate.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"CEPOD category 4 - Elective\",\"e\":\"CEPOD 4 refers to elective cases:\\n\\n• 4 ELECTIVE - Intervention planned or booked in advance of routine admission to hospital. Timing to suit patient, hospital and staff. There are many examples of this, such as a reducible inguinal hernia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"CEPOD category 5\",\"e\":\"There is no CEPOD category 5. The National Confidential Enquiry into Patient Outcome and Death (NCEPOD) classification of intervention rationalises available theatre resources and delivers them to those who require care by urgency of injuries.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man with hypertension requiring two antihypertensives is seen in the pre- assessment clinic. He gets out of breath when walking up two flights of stairs. What American Society of Anaesthesiologists (ASA) grade is he?\",\"lead\":\"What American Society of Anaesthesiologists (ASA) grade is he?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"ASA I\",\"e\":\"The correct answer is ASA III by virtue of the need for two antihypertensives and that he has severe systemic disease with limitation to his activity. The ASA grade predicts patient mortality based on medical co-morbidities. The correct answer is ASA III by virtue of the need for two antihypertensives he has severe systemic disease and his activity is also limited.\\n\\nMortality ASA Definition grade\\n\\n0.06 Normal healthy individual\\n\\n0.4 Mild systemic disease that does not limit activity\\n\\nIII 4.5 Severe systemic disease that limits activity but is not incapacitating\\n\\n23 Incapacitating systemic disease which is constantly life threatening IV\\n\\n51 V Moribund, not expected to survive 24 h with or without surgery\\n\\nA declared brain-dead person whose organs are being removed for N/A VI donor purposes\\n\\nA patient who is ASA I is a healthy individual with no co-morbidities.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"ASA II\",\"e\":\"A patient who is ASA Il has mild systemic disease that does not limit activity. An example might be a patient with hypertension that is controlled by one agent.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"ASA III\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"ASA IV\",\"e\":\"A patient classified as ASA IV has an incapacitating systemic disease that is always life threatening eg someone with unstable angina secondary to coronary artery disease.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"ASA V\",\"e\":\"A patient who is ASA V is moribund, and not expected to survive 24 h with or without surgery. An example would be a patient who has severe systemic disease, and has now suffered further acute pathology eg gross faecal peritonitis.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A 61-year-old with poorly-controlled asthma is sent for lung function testing as part of his preoperative assessment.\\n\\nWhat result would you most expect?\",\"lead\":\"What result would you most expect?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Forced vital capacity (FVC) is reduced but FEV/FVC is normal\",\"e\":\"If the FVC is reduced, it is impossible for the ratio of FEV, to FVC to be normal as it would also be reduced.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"FVC normal or reduced and FEV./FVC is reduced\",\"e\":\"In asthma (an obstructive lung disorder) the forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV,) is usually decreased, the FVC is usually normal and the ratio FEV /FVC is decreased. In restrictive lung disorders, eg fibrosis, the FEV, is reduced; as is the FVC, but to a lesser extent, and so the FEV /FVC ratio is normal. Lung function tests measure for:\\n\\n• forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV:) - the volume of air that can be exhaled in the very first second of forced expiration • FVC - total volume of air that can be forcibly exhaled in one breath • the ratio of FEV, to FVC, usually expressed as a percentage.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Normal FVC and normal FEV:/FVC\",\"e\":\"Patients with obstructive lung disorders eg asthma may not give normal lung function tests as their FEV, is usually reduced.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"All of the above\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option; the patient will have a reduced FEV,.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"None of the above\",\"e\":\"This is not the correct option.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":53,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old woman is due to undergo an ORIF for an ankle fracture. She is seen pre- operatively by the anaesthetist.\\n\\nOf the following, which one is an indicator for performing urea and electrolyte (U+E) blood tests?\",\"lead\":\"Of the following, which one is an indicator for performing urea and electrolyte (U+E) blood tests?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Age over 50\",\"e\":\"Age over 60 is an indication for urea and electrolyte blood tests.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"All women\",\"e\":\"Gender is not an indication for these blood tests.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diabetics\",\"e\":\"Patients over 60 years, those with cardiovascular and renal disease, diabetes and those on steroids/ACE-I or diuretics should have U+Es monitored pre-operatively.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Respiratory disorders\",\"e\":\"Urea and electrolyte blood tests would not give any idea of the extent of respiratory disease. Clinical assessment in combination with chest X-ray and lung function tests are important here.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Those undergoing prolonged anaesthetic time > 60 minutes\",\"e\":\"This is not an absolute indication for this blood test.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man is due to have an elective laparoscopic sigmoid colectomy for symptomatic diverticular disease. He has a history of atrial fibrillation, controlled with a b- blocker, and angina pectoris. Four months ago he had a NSTEMI and is recovering well. He is taking warfarin and has an international normalised ratio (INR) of 2.5.\\n\\nHow would you proceed?\",\"lead\":\"How would you proceed?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Arrange surgery and obtain consent on the day of the procedure\",\"e\":\"Consent should ideally be performed in the outpatient clinic and confirmed on the day of surgery. This patient is complex and is undergoing major surgery for benign disease, and does not appear to have had a full preoperative work up. He is also not a good candidate for surgery as he has had a recent MI. Routine surgery should be purposed until six months post MI.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Arrange surgery but ensure Warfarin is stopped 3 days prior to his procedure\",\"e\":\"Warfarin should be stopped approximately 5 days before the procedure and the INR checked to ensure it is less than 1.5. It is not sufficient to simply stop the warfarin without checking the INR. He is also not a good candidate for surgery as he has had a recent MI.. Routine surgery should be purposed until six months post MI\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Initiate the recommended investigations, which include full blood count, urea and electrolytes, chest X-ray and electrocardiogram\",\"e\":\"The patient will need these tests, but also a group and save test and echocardiogram to assess ventricular function. However, it is not advisable for patients to undergo routine surgery within six months of an MI.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Postpone his surgery\",\"e\":\"Major elective surgery is not recommended within 6 months of a myocardial infarction. The patient needs a more thorough work up and has benign disease; so his operation can be delayed.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The warfarin should be replaced with therapeutic dose of dalteparin\",\"e\":\"This patient is not a good candidate for surgery as he has had a recent MI. Routine surgery should be purposed until six months post MI. Additionally, a therapeutic dose of dalteparin is not mandatory for a patient taking warfarin for atrial fibrillation. A prophylactic dose together with graduated compression stockings and pneumatic calf compression boots during the surgery are adequate.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old, otherwise fit and healthy woman, is seen at the pre-assessment clinic before undergoing an elective, laparoscopic cholecystectomy. She is found to be hypertensive with a diastolic blood pressure >110 mmHg\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding preoperative hypertension?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding preoperative hypertension?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"As most antihypertensive agents react adversely with anaesthetics the hypertension should be treated after the surgery\",\"e\":\"Most antihypertensive agents are compatible w th anaesthetic agents so may be continued as normally prescribed.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"As the diastolic blood pressure is >110 mmg the procedure should be discussed with the anaesthetist but should still go ahead\",\"e\":\"A diastolic blood pressure of >110 mmg before elective surgery prompts discussion with the anaesthetist and possible postponement until better control can be achieved.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hypertension must be corrected pre-operatively in a patient over 50 years of age undergoing emergency surgery\",\"e\":\"While the presence of hypertension should not delay an emergency operation as the risks of delay will outweigh the dangers of an elevated blood pressure, this is not the case with elective surgery. This is the case for all patients, regardless of age.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hypertension has no bearing on mortality in this type of operation and thus does not require any intervention\",\"e\":\"Poorly controlled hypertension in the immediate preoperative period predisposes the patient to perioperative cardiac morbidity and must be avoided.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ideally, hypertension should be corrected in all patients undergoing elective surgery\",\"e\":\"Hypertension needs to be corrected before elective surgery in all patients as it increases the risk of cardiovascular pathology including myocardial complications and stroke. Hypertension is defined as a blood pressure reading persistently at or above 140/90 mmHg with subsequent ambulatory blood pressure monitoring (ABPM) daytime average or home blood pressure monitoring (HBPM) that is 135/85 mmHg or higher.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old woman is noted to be positive for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) on a wound swab. Steps are subsequently taken to minimise risk to the other patients on the ward.\\n\\nWhat is the appropriate infection control procedure?\",\"lead\":\"What is the appropriate infection control procedure?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"All MRSA positive patients must be nursed in the same bay, away from non-infected patients\",\"e\":\"Most hospitals do not place patients with one MRSA positive swab in an exclusive bay. If a single side-room is not available patients may be able to be nursed in a bay alongside other MRSA patients. Some hospitals have made arrangements for patients with multiple MRSA infections to stay in one ward.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Barrier nursing\",\"e\":\"Barrier nursing with apron and gloves is the current accepted method.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gloves and face masks for all entering the room\",\"e\":\"Face masks are unnecessary when seeing patients with MRSA.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"0 None\",\"e\":\"Patients with MRSA do require care with some infection control precautions.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Reversed barrier nursing\",\"e\":\"Reversed barrier nursing is implemented to protect patients who are at high risk of contracting infection from common organisms carried by other people such as neutropenic individuals.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"An 8-year-old boy fell from a monkey bar on an outstretched arm, and sustained a completely displaced distal humeral supracondylar fracture: On examination, his hand is cold with reduced capillary refill.\\n\\nWhat is the most important next step in management?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most important next step in management?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Admit and prepare for theatre next day\",\"e\":\"This patient has a fracture with vascular compromise and needs urgent treatment\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Application of above elbow plaster with the elbow at 90 degrees flexion\",\"e\":\"Reduction of the fracture is needed\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Below elbow plaster\",\"e\":\"Reduction of the fracture is needed here.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Discharge home with fracture clinic follow up\",\"e\":\"This patient has a fracture with a vascular injury and needs urgent treatment. Vascular injury is a common and potentially catastrophic complication of displaced supracondylar fractures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Emergent fracture reduction\",\"e\":\"Vascular insufficiency should be managed initially by rapid reduction and fixation.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old man presents to the Urology Rapid Access Clinic with a right testicular lump. He undergoes an ultrasound the same day, which suggests that this is a testicular tumour. He is counselled regarding a radical inguinal orchidectomy.\\n\\nWhich one of these must form part of his preoperative management?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these must form part of his preoperative management?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), beta-human chorionic gonadotrophin (B-HCG) and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)\",\"e\":\"Baseline testicular cancer tumour markers are required before any treatment is instigated. These will aid monitoring of disease.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Staging CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis\",\"e\":\"Full staging with a CT scan can of the chest, abdomen and pelvis forms part of the mandatory pre-operative work-up.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sperm banking\",\"e\":\"All patients undergoing an orchidectomy must be counselled about fertility and offered sperm banking. This typically happens after the orchidectomy but in cases in which the patient is known to have fertility problems or their contralateral testis is atrophic it should be offered before orchidectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Testicular biopsy to confirm malignancy\",\"e\":\"Unlike many other cancers, testicular biopsies are not indicated to confirm malignancy. If suspected on imaging then the patient should undergo a radical inguinal orchidectomy after which it will be sent for histology to confirm the diagnosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA)\",\"e\":\"CEA is not a tumour marker for testicular cancer. The patient should have pre-operative bloods taken for alpha feto-protein, human chorionic gonadotrophin and lactate dehydrogenase. Tumour markers help with staging and monitoring response to treatment.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"Before anaesthetising a patient, staff carry out their checks to ensure that the operation is being performed safely using the World Health Organization (WHO) surgical safety checklist. This is referred to as the 'sign in' stage of the procedure.\\n\\nWhich one of these would form part of these checks?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these would form part of these checks?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Antibiotic prophylaxis\",\"e\":\"Antibiotic prophylaxis forms part of the WHO surgical checklist before skin incision (the 'time out' stage) but not at the induction of anaesthesia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Essential imaging displayed\",\"e\":\"This is an important part of the WHO surgical safety checklist before skin incision along with ensuring that the site of surgery has been correctly marked and corresponds with that documented in the consent form.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Instrument sterility\",\"e\":\"It is important to confirm instrument sterility with the nursing team. This takes place during the 'time out' stage of the WHO checklist.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pulse oximeter attached to patient and in working order\",\"e\":\"This is correct and forms part of the WHO check before induction of anaesthesia. Additional checks at this stage include confirming patient identity, site, procedure, consent, site marked, anaesthesia machine, allergies, difficult airway/aspiration risk and risk of large blood loss.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Normal coagulation tests\",\"e\":\"Coagulation tests should be checked by the surgeon and anaesthetist before any procedure but does not form part of the WHO surgical safety checklist.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":70,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old man is scheduled to have a right inguinal hernia mesh repair. He is normally fit and well and takes aspirin 75 mg daily as he had an MI two years ago. The surgeon is concerned about the risk of bleeding with aspirin and wishes to stop it.\\n\\nHow long do the effects of aspirin last?\",\"lead\":\"How long do the effects of aspirin last?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"6h\",\"e\":\"Unfractionated heparin should be discontinued 6 h before surgery to reduce risk of bleeding. Unfractionated heparin can be used for high risk patients normally on anticoagulation to ensure they continue to receive appropriate anticoagulation during the perioperative phase.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"12h\",\"e\":\"Low-molecular-weight heparins often given prophylactically in hospital should typically be stopped 12 h before surgery, although some surgeons will proceed with surgery provided it was prophylactic not treatment dose, depending on the type of procedure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"2 days\",\"e\":\"The non-vitamin K antagonist oral anticoagulants (NOACs) including Apixaban and Rivaroxaban are typically stopped 2 days before surgery depending on the patient's renal function and bleeding risk.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"5 days\",\"e\":\"Warfarin is typically stopped 5 days pre-operatively with the International Normalised Ratio (INR) checked before surgery to ensure it is at an adequate level. INR < 1.5 is usually acceptable to proceed with surgery but some surgeons prefer an INR < 1.3.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"10 days\",\"e\":\"Aspirin is an antiplatelet medication with irreversible action. Therefore, the effects of aspirin last for the duration of the life of the platelet which is up to 10 days. Although most surgeons will stop aspirin 7 days before surgery.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":73,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man attends the Emergency Department with severe abdominal pain Observations are: respiratory rate 22/min, heart rate 102/min, blood pressure 124/78 mmHg. temperature 38.1°C. CT of the abdomen and pelvis suggests a localised diverticular perforation. He is known to have hypertension, and type 2 diabetes, which is well controlled with metformin.\\n\\nWhat is this patient's American Society of Anaesthesiologists' (ASA) grade?\",\"lead\":\"What is this patient's American Society of Anaesthesiologists' (ASA) grade?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"1\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"2\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"3\",\"e\":\"ASA 3 describes a patient with severe systemic disease. In this case if the patient's hypertension was not well controlled or he was a poorly controlled diabetic then he would fit into the ASA 3 category.\\n\\nASA 4 describes a patient with severe systemic disease that is a constant threat to life. For example a patient with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) on long-term oxygen therapy.\\n\\nASA 5 describes a moribund patient that is not expected to survive without the operation. For example, a patient with a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm having an emergency open repair.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"4\",\"e\":\"ASA 4 describes a patient with severe systemic disease that is a constant threat to life. For example a patient with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) on long-term oxygen therapy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"5\",\"e\":\"ASA 5 describes a moribund patient that is not expected to survive without the operation. For example, a patient with 3 ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm having an emergency open repair.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man is awaiting a left total knee replacement. He has hypertension that is controlled with amlodipine and takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation. He takes no other regular medications.\\n\\nWhich one of these investigations should form part of this patient's pre-operative assessment?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these investigations should form part of this patient's pre-operative assessment?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Full blood count only\",\"e\":\"This patient is going for a major operation and takes warfarin. He would need his clotting checked before proceeding with surgery although this may not take place until the day of surgery after the warfarin has been stopped.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Full blood count, renal function, coagulation\",\"e\":\"This would be a suitable request for blood tests for this patient as recommended by for ASA 2 patients. However, he should also have an ECG.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Full blood count, renal function, coagulation, ECG\",\"e\":\"According to the NICE guidelines (NG45) on routine pre-operative tests for elective surgery this would be correct. This patient is ASA 2 and is undergoing a major operation.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Full blood count, renal function, coagulation, ECG, lung function tests\",\"e\":\"The patient has no history of respiratory disease and lung function tests would not be routinely requested for such a patient.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Full blood count, renal function, coagulation, lung function tests\",\"e\":\"All patients undergoing a major operation that are either ASA 1 and over the age of 65 or ASA 2 and above should have an ECG performed. In addition, lung function tests would not routinely be arranged for patients like this without a history of respiratory disorder.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"A 68-year-old woman has been on the waiting list for an elective hernia repair for one year It causes occasional discomfort and impacts on her mobility. Her medications include aspirin and clopidogrel, after a myocardial infarction (MI) seven months previously which required two cardiac stents. She wants to proceed with surgery under general anaesthetic as soon as possible.\\n\\nWhat advice should the patient be given?\",\"lead\":\"What advice should the patient be given?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Proceed with the surgery on current medication\",\"e\":\"This patient will be at high risk of bleeding if the surgery were performed now as she is on dual antiplatelet therapy following her recent cardiac stenting. As this is an elective procedure, it should be postponed until it can be safely performed.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Stop clopidogrel before surgery\",\"e\":\"Unless agreed by the cardiologists, the clopidogrel should not be stopped in view of her recent cardiac stents.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Stop all medication before surgery\",\"e\":\"Stopping all medication including the antiplatelets puts the patient at risk of cardiac stent thrombosis, which could have potentially life-threatening complications for the patient. This patient only had their cardiac stents recently and therefore medications should not be stopped without consultation with the cardiologist.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Postpone surgery\",\"e\":\"The patient has had a recent Mi and is on dual antiplatelet therapy. She would, therefore, be a high anaesthetic risk and would be high risk for bleeding. She has had recent cardiac stents and therefore these medications cannot be stopped at present. Clopidogrel is typically stopped after the first year and given that this is an elective procedure it would be sensible to postpone the surgery until the risk of bleeding and anaesthetic can be reduced.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cancel surgery with no plan to perform in the future\",\"e\":\"The patient has been awaiting this surgery for some time and it appears to be impacting her quality of life. There is also a risk that the hernia could strangulate or become incarcerated. At this stage the surgery does not need to be completely cancelled just postponed until it can be safely performed in view of her recent MI.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"Many patients on warfarin that are due to undergo surgery have their anticoagulation stopped pre-operatively. Patients that are high risk of thrombosis require bridging with alternative agents such as low-molecular-weight heparin in the perioperative phase.\\n\\nWhich one of these patients would it be safe to stop warfarin on without bridging?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these patients would it be safe to stop warfarin on without bridging?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Metallic aortic valve\",\"e\":\"Patients with metallic valves are very high risk for embolic events should their anticoagulation be stopped. Plans for these patients should be made in conjunction with the cardiology team. Often, these patients are admitted pre-operatively and converted onto a unfractionated heparin infusion, which can be stopped 6 h pre-operatively.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Atrial fibrillation in a patient with an ischaemic stroke two months ago\",\"e\":\"Patients that are on warfarin for atrial fibrillation and have had a previous stroke or transient ischaemic attack (TIA) or a thromboembolic event within 3 months should have bridging when stopping their warfarin pre-operatively.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Atrial fibrillation in a patient who is otherwise fit and well\",\"e\":\"Patients with uncomplicated, low risk atrial fibrillation can have their warfarin stopped pre- operatively and they do not need to be bridged with low-molecular-weight heparin.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Recurrent pulmonary emboli/deep vein thrombosis with the last episode one year ago\",\"e\":\"Even though this patient has had no thromboembolic event for a year their recurrent episodes put the patient at high risk. Therefore, these patients will require bridging for their anticoagulation.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Unprovoked pulmonary embolism two months ago\",\"e\":\"This patient would be at high risk for a further thromboembolic event and therefore will require bridging with the warfarin being stopped for the shortest time possible.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"A 74-year-old man who has been on nasogastric (NG) feed following a stroke, is consented for a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) tube to be done under a general anaesthetic. He has a history of dementia and hypertension. He does not understand the treatment proposed but his daughters are in agreement.\\n\\nWhich consent form should be used for this patient?\",\"lead\":\"Which consent form should be used for this patient?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"AS i Consent form 1\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Consent form 2\",\"e\":\"Consent form 1 would only be suitable if this patient had capacity to make a decision regarding the proposed treatment, which he does not.\\n\\nConsent form 2 is used to consent children and would not be appropriate in this case.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Consent form 3\",\"e\":\"Consent form 3 is used for patients who have capacity, which this patient does not, and when their consciousness will not be impaired during the procedure. In this case of a general anaesthetic, this would not be the case.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Consent form 4\",\"e\":\"Consent form 4 would be the correct choice. This is used for patients who lack capacity and it should be checked whether the patient has a lasting power of attorney or advanced directive before proceeding with this. It would be best practice to also have the daughters sign the consent form.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Verbal consent\",\"e\":\"This patient would not have the capacity to give verbal consent to any investigation or treatment. Even if the patient did have capacity, verbal consent would not be sufficient for a procedure under a general anaesthetic.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":85,\"stem\":\"A 13-year-old boy who was previously fit and well presents to the Emergency Department with a 4 h history of severe left testicular pain. He is accompanied by his parents. He is able to give a clear history and understands that he needs a scrotal exploration to investigate a possible testicular torsion.\\n\\nHow should this patient be consented?\",\"lead\":\"How should this patient be consented?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Consent form 1 (Patient agreement to investigation, treatment or procedure)\",\"e\":\"Consent form 1 is used for adult patients with capacity who are agreeing to an investigation or treatment in which their consciousness will be impaired as is the case in any procedure performed under general anaesthetic.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Consent form 2 (Parental agreement to investigation/treatment for a child or young person)\",\"e\":\"Consent form 2 is used when parental consent is also required for a child up to age of 16 Although, if the child is deemed to have capacity (can comprehend and retain information relating to the decision, weigh it up and then communicate their decision) they may also sign the consent form.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Consent form 3 (Patient agreement to investigation or treatment where consciousness is not impaired\",\"e\":\"Consent form 3 is used for adult patients when they are consenting for a procedure or investigation that will not impair their consciousness such as a flexible cystoscopy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Consent form 4 (Adult patients who lack capacity to make decisions regarding the investigation or treatment)\",\"e\":\"Consent form 4 is used for patients who lack capacity to make a decision regarding a proposed investigation or treatment. It must be deemed that the procedure cannot be delayed until the patient regains capacity and is in the patient's best interests.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Verbal consent\",\"e\":\"Verbal consent is not enough for patients undergoing this type of procedure. Verbal consent would be suitable for example for a patient who is going to have a CT scan and agrees that they are happy to have it performed.\"}]},{\"t\":47,\"p\":88,\"stem\":\"It is 10:00 and a 38-year-old man has been awaiting an incision and drainage of a perianal abscess under general anaesthetic. He has been nil by mouth since 02:00 and is now called for his surgery. He is found to be chewing gum when called.\\n\\nHow long does the patient need to wait for the surgery?\",\"lead\":\"How long does the patient need to wait for the surgery?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The surgery can go ahead now\",\"e\":\"It would not be safe to proceed with the surgery now. Although the patient has had nothing else to eat or drink since 2 am chewing gum mimics eating and causes gastric secretions. Therefore, there would be a risk of aspiration.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Th\",\"e\":\"The patient should be managed as if they have just eaten as chewing gum stimulates gastric secretions and therefore this patient would be at risk of aspiration.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"2h\",\"e\":\"Chewing gum increases the volume of gastric fluid but not gastric acidity, hence the risk of dangerous aspiration is less. The AAGBI recommends a 2 h wait between chewing gum and an anaesthetic.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"6h\",\"e\":\"Chewing gum causes an increase in gastric fluid from saliva and therefore the gastric pH does not change. This is thought to reduce the risk of dangerous aspiration. Therefore, the AAGBI currently recommends a 2 h prior between chewing gum and an anaesthetic.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"8h\",\"e\":\"8 his too long for a patient to be fasted before surgery. Patients should delayed by 2 hours, having nothing to eat, including chewing gum, in this time.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"You review a 54-year-old man with a history of alcoholism; you are concerned that there may be evidence of portal hypertension. They may require an emergency laparotomy and you are reluctant to go ahead due to the increased bleeding risk. Which one of the following stems best describes blood flow to the liver?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following stems best describes blood flow to the liver?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The hepatic artery supplies 75% of the total liver blood flow\",\"e\":\"The hepatic artery (proper) accounts for 25% of total blood flow to the liver. The hepatic artery proper is a branch of the common hepatic artery. It maintains constant blood flow to the liver by autoregulation - arterial flow decreases when portal venous flow increases, ano vice versa. The remaining 75% is from the portal vein. Blood flow to the liver via the hepatic artery and portal vein accounts for 25% of resting cardiac output.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The normai portal vein pressure is 8-12 mmHg\",\"e\":\"Normal portal venous pressure is approximately 5-7 mmg. The pressure should be less chan the pressure in the inferior vena cava by 5 mmg. If the pressure difference (hepatic venous pressure gradient - HVPG) is greater than 5 mmg then portal hypertension is diagnosed.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The portal vein supplies 75% of liver blood flow\",\"e\":\"It is formed by the union of the splenic and superior mesenteric veins posterior to the neck of the pancreas. This occurs at the level of L1 in the transpyloric plane of Addison.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The caudate lobe of the liver does not have direct drainage into the inferior vena cava (IVC)\",\"e\":\"The caudate lobe often has direct connection to the inferior vena cava (IVC) through separate branches of the hepatic veins.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Only the portal vein enters the liver via the porta hepatis\",\"e\":\"Following its formation, the portal vein passes posterior to the duodenum in the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) along with the hepatic artery proper and the common bile duct. These three structures enter the porta hepatis, with the duct the most anterior and lateral, the artery medial to the duct and the portal vein posteriorly. The portal vein branches into left and right branches as it enters the liver.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man is undergoing a laparotomy and they are found to have caput medusae during the incision. Before much progress is made they lose a significant amount of blood and a transfusion is required Which of the vessels listed below drain(s) into the inferior vena cava?\",\"lead\":\"Before much progress is made they lose a significant amount of blood and a transfusion is required Which of the vessels listed below drain(s) into the inferior vena cava?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Renal veins\",\"e\":\"Both renal veins drain directlv into the inferior vena cava* . which also receives tributaries from each of the structures labelled in the image below.\\n\\nHepatic veins\\n\\nLeft adrenal vein Inferior vena cava Left renal Right vein renal vein Left testicular vein Right testicular Left common iliac vein vein\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior mesenteric vein: 2)\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric vein (IMV) usually drains into the splenic vein before it forms the portal vein along with the superior mesenteric vein. Occasionally the IMV will drain into this confluence or into the SMV.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric vein\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric vein, along with the splenic vein, forms the hepatic portal vein. which then passes in the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) and enters the liver via the porta hepatis, giving off left and right branches.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Splenic vein 2.\",\"e\":\"The splenic veins joins with the superior mesenteric vein to form the hepatic portal vein. This occurs posterior to the neck of the pancreas at the level of L1 (the transpyloric plane of Addison).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"All of the above\",\"e\":\"The renal veins drain directly into the vena cava. The splenic vein and superior mesenteric vein join together to form the hepatic portal vein. The inferior mesenteric vein drains into the splenic vein.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A patient has splenic haemorrhage. They remain haemodynamically stable and are sent to the interventional radiologist for embolisation of the bleeding vessel. Which of the following arteries branches directly from the coeliac trunk?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following arteries branches directly from the coeliac trunk?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The right gastric artery\",\"e\":\"This is a branch of the common hepatic artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The left gastric artery\",\"e\":\"The coeliac trunk arises from the aorta at the level of T12. It trifurcates into the left gastric. splenic and common hepatic arteries. These three branches are coloured individually below.\\n\\nCoeliac trunk\\n\\nLeft gastric a.\\n\\nCommon hepatic a.\\n\\nSplenic a.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"This is a branch of the gastroduodenal artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The superior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"This is a direct branch of the aorta, arising at the level of L1.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The inferior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"This is a direct branch of the aorta*, arising at the level of L3.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man has a suspected aortic dissection. There is concern the flap has continued into the abdomen thereby devascularising vital organs. What is (are) the first arterial branch or branches arising from the abdominal aorta?\",\"lead\":\"What is (are) the first arterial branch or branches arising from the abdominal aorta?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The renal arteries\",\"e\":\"The renal arteries are paired branches arising from the aorta at the level of L1-L2, inferior to the origin of the superior mesenteric artery\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The right adrenal artery\",\"e\":\"The right adrenal (or suprarenal artery) branches from the aorta just above the origin of the right renal artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The left adrenal artery\",\"e\":\"The left adrenal (or suprarenal artery) branches from the aorta just above the origin of the left renal artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The inferior phrenic arteries\",\"e\":\"The inferior phrenic arteries branch from the aorta* immediately after it enters the abdomen through the aortic hiatus - they can be seen as the first branches in the image below. They pass superolaterally and run under the crura to supply the diaphragm.\\n\\nInferior phrenic Coellac trunk artery - Superior mesenteric Renal artery arterles\\n\\naorta Testicular artery Inferior mesenteric artery\\n\\nC TramtoAnatony\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The superior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric artery branches from the aorta at the level of L1.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":15,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with profuse haematemesis. At emergency OGD distended and tortuous veins are visualised in the distal oesophagus and the patient is diagnosed with variceal bleeding Concerning the portal vein, which of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"At emergency OGD distended and tortuous veins are visualised in the distal oesophagus and the patient is diagnosed with variceal bleeding Concerning the portal vein, which of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It carries blood trom three major veins, the superior and interior mesenteric vein and the left gastric vein\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is formed by the union of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins. The inferior mesenteric vein may also empty into this confluence but usually drains to the splenic: vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is formed posterior to the head of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"The portal vein forms posterior to the neck of the pancreas at the level of L1 (transpyloric plane of Addison). It then passes in the free edge of the lesser omentum (the hepatoduodenal ligament) along with the common bile duct and the hepatic artery proper\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies posterior to the bile duct in the free edge of the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is posterior to both the common bile duct and the hepatic artery proper, with the duct the more lateral of the latter two.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"At the porta hepatis the portal vein divides into four branches\",\"e\":\"The portal vein divides into two branches (left and right) at the porta hepatis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It lies anterior to the hepatic artery in the free edge of the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is posterior to both the hepatic artery and the common bile duct in the free edge of the lesser omentum. 30\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"In a patient with gastric carcinoma, the radiologist reported that the lymph nodes around the coeliac trunk are enlarged. Subsequently at the upper GI MDT the decision is made not to proceed to gastrectomy Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the coeliac trunk?\",\"lead\":\"Subsequently at the upper GI MDT the decision is made not to proceed to gastrectomy Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the coeliac trunk?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is the arterial supply to the embryonic mid-gut\",\"e\":\"The coeliac trunk is the supply to the embryonic foregut.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is accompanied by the coeliac vein\",\"e\":\"There is no coeliac vein. The venous drainage of the abdominal viscera is via the hepatic portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It arises from the lateral surface of the abdominal aorta, at about the level of the first lumbar vertebra\",\"e\":\"The coeliac trunk arises from the anterior surface of the aorta at the level of T12.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It gives rise to the splenic, left gastric and common hepatic arteries\",\"e\":\"The coeliac trunk trifurcates into the splenic, left gastric and common hepatic arteries, which are coloured individually in the image below. It occasionally gives off one of the inferior phrenic arteries.\\n\\nCoeliac trunk\\n\\nLeft gastric a.\\n\\nCommon hepatic a.\\n\\nSplenic a.\\n\\nнaUIL4ha122\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is surrounded by the aorticorenal ganglion\",\"e\":\"The coeliac trunk is related to the right coeliac ganglion and the caudate process of the liver on the right, and the left coeliac ganglion and cardia of the stomach on the left. Inferiorly, it is in relation to the upper border of the pancreas and the splenic vein.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"4 55-year-old man executive who had a history of a chronic duodenal ulcer was admitted to the Emergency Department exhibiting signs of a severe internal haemorrhage. He was quickly diagnosed with perforation of the posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum and erosion of an artery behind it by the gastric expellant. What is the most likely artery to be involved?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely artery to be involved?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"iS ' Common hepatic\",\"e\":\"The common hepatic artery is a branch of the coeliac trunk and is superior to the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastroduodenal\",\"e\":\"The gastroduodenal artery is a branch of the common hepatic artery. It passes posteriorly to Di before bifurcating into the right gastroepiploic and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. A duodenal ulcer in the posterior wall of D1 could therefore erode into it, as demonstrated in the image below, causing an upper Gl haemorrhage.\\n\\nSuperlor pa duodenal a.\\n\\nAnterior superior bancroatico-duodenal a. sterior inferior reatico-duodenal a. inferior pancreatio Anterior inferior ncreatico duodenal a. duodenal a.\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left gastric\",\"e\":\"The left gastric artery is a branch of the coeliac trunk and supplies the lesser curve of the stomach.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Proper hepatic\",\"e\":\"The proper hepatic artery is a branch of the common hepatic artery. It travels in the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) along with the common bile duct and the portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric artery arises from the aorta at the level of Li, posterior to the neck of the pancreas. It passes over the third part of the duodenum to supply the small bowel, the right colon and the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":23,\"stem\":\"Whilst performing a hysterectomy. the surgeon ligates the uterine artery on either side. They then proceed to remove the uterus relatively bloodlessiy. The uterine artery arises from which one of the following structures?\",\"lead\":\"The uterine artery arises from which one of the following structures?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abdominal aorta\",\"e\":\"The uterine artery is not a direct branch of the abdominal aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"External iliac artery\",\"e\":\"The branches of the external iliac artery are the inferior epigastric, deep circumflex iliac artery and the common femoral artery. They supply the lower abdominal wall, the iliac bone and the lower limb respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inferior rectal artery\",\"e\":\"This is a branch of the internal pudendal artery and supplies the lower third of the rectum, the anal canal and gives some branches to the superficial gluteal region.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Internal iliac artery\",\"e\":\"The uterine artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and runs medially on the levator ani towards the uterine cervix, It crosses above and in front of the ureter, to which it supplies a small branch, about 2 cm from the cervix. Reaching the side of the uterus, it ascends in a tortuous manner between the two layers of the broad ligament, to the junction of the Fallopian tube and uterus. It then runs laterally towards the hilum of the ovary and ends by anastomosing with the ovarian artery: It supplies branches to the uterine cervix, the vagina, the body of the uterus, and from its terminal portion branches are distributed to the Fallopian tube and the round ligament of the uterus.\\n\\nOvarian artery\\n\\nUterine artery\\n\\nInternal iliac artery i % vaginal arteries * C teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ovarian artery\",\"e\":\"The ovarian artery is a branch of the abdominal aorta.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"An 85-year-old man with prostatic cancer is found to have metastatic disease. The cecision is made that surgery is not in the patient's best interests.\\n\\nTheir disease is most likely to have spread through which one of the following veins?\",\"lead\":\"Their disease is most likely to have spread through which one of the following veins?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Testicular vein\",\"e\":\"The testicular veins drain the testes. The right testicular vein drains directly to the inferior vena cava, the left drains to the left renal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cephalic vein\",\"e\":\"The cephalic vein is a superficial vein of the upper limb. it originates from the dorsal venous network of the hand and travels up the radial border of the forearm. At the elbow, it communicates with the basilic vein via the median cubital vein. In the arm, it runs in the superficial fascia along the anterolateral surface of biceps brachii before entering the deltopectoral groove, where it empties into the axillary vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Basilic vein\",\"e\":\"The basilic vein is a superficial vein of the arm. It originates from the dorsal venous network of the hand and runs up the unar border of the forearm. in the arm, it joins with the brachial veins to form the axillary vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"External iliac vein\",\"e\":\"The external iliac vein drains the lower limb. It is a tributary (along with the internal iliac) of the common iliac vein, which joins with its partner to form the inferior vena cava.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Internal vertebral venous plexus\",\"e\":\"The veins of the internal vertebral venous (Batson's) plexus are clinically significant because they are valveless and can serve as a route for metastases.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"During the course of an abdominal hysterectomy, you are asked to ligate and divide the uterine arteries: The consultant warns you to ensure that you do not damage the nearby ureters.\\n\\nFrom which vessel do the uterine arteries originate from?\",\"lead\":\"From which vessel do the uterine arteries originate from?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abdominal aorta\",\"e\":\"The uterine arteries are not direct branches of the abdominal aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Common iliac artery\",\"e\":\"The common iliac artery bifurcates to form the external and internal iliac arteries. The uterine artery then arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"External liac artery ~\",\"e\":\"The branches of the external iliac artery are the inferior epigastric. deep circumflex iliac artery and the common femoral artery. They supply the lower abdominal wall, the iliac bone and the lower limb respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"internal llac artery\",\"e\":\"The uterine artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and runs medially on the levator ani towards the uterine cervix. it crosses above and in front of the ureter, to which it supplies a small branch, about 2 cm from the cervix. Reaching the side of the uterus, it ascends in a tortuous manner between the two layers of the broad ligament to the junction of the Fallopian tube and uterus. It then runs laterally towards the hilum of the ovary and ends by anastomosing with the ovarian artery. It supplies branches to the uterine cervix, the vagina, the body of the uterus, and from its terminal portion branches are distributed to the Fallopian tube and the round ligament of the uterus.\\n\\nOvarian artery\\n\\nUterine artery\\n\\nvaginal arteries teachmeanatomy\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Median sacral artery\",\"e\":\"The median sacral artery is a small posterior branch of the aorta that supplies the coccyx, lumbar vertebrae and sacrum.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"During an elective repair of an aortic aneurysm you are asked to identify a vessel that passes behind the inferior vena cava, the head of the pancreas and the duodenum. The registrar is unsure whether or not it is safe to divide\\n\\nWhat is the correct answer?\",\"lead\":\"What is the correct answer?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Common hepatic artery\",\"e\":\"The common hepatic artery is a branch of the coeliac trunk.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left renal artery\",\"e\":\"The left renal artery arises from the left side of the aorta at approximately the level of L1-L2 and passes posterior to the left renal vein, the body of the pancreas and the splenic vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Right renal artery\",\"e\":\"The right renal artery usually arises slightly lower than the left. It passes posterior to the inferior vena cava (IVC), the right renal vein, the head of the pancreas and the duodenum - it can be seen emerging from behind the IVC in the image below. It divides before reaching the renal hilum and, as with the left renal artery, gives branches which supply the adrenal gland and the proximal ureter. It would not be safe to divide this vessel, although occasionally there can be accessory renal arteries present branching directly off of the aorta.\\n\\nInfePlor phrenic artery Coellac trunk - Superior orsenteric Renal arteries Abdominal orti Testicular artery Inferior mesenteric artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric artery is a direct branch of the abdominal aorta, arising from the anterior surface at the level of Li, posterior to the neck of the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Testicular artery\",\"e\":\"The paired testicular arteries are direct branches of the abdominal aorta. They descend retroperitoneally, running along the psoas muscle before passing into the inguinal canal in the spermatic cord to reach the testes.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-oid woman is admitted for an elective right hemicolectomy, following a diagnosis of colonic adenocarcinoma. During the procedure, you are advised to avoid injury to a vessel arising from the abdominal aorta at the level of the second lumbar vertebra, and passing anterior to the inferior vena cava. Which vessel is at risk?\",\"lead\":\"Which vessel is at risk?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Common iliac artery\",\"e\":\"The common iliac arteries are terminal branches of the aorta, which bifurcates at the level of L4.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric artery arises from the anterior surface of the aorta at the level of L3. It briefly descends on the aorta then crosses to the left. Its branches supply the Gi tract from the distal third of the transverse colon to the upper rectum\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Middle suprarenal artery\",\"e\":\"The middle suprarenal arteries are small direct branches of the abdominal aorta at the level of L1.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right ovarian artery\",\"e\":\"The gonadal vessels originate from the abdominal aorta at the level of the L2 vertebra and descend into the pelvis anterior to the inferior vena cava as well as the ureters ('water under the bridge'). It is important to identify the ureter and gonadal vessels during mobilisation of the left and right colon.\\n\\nInferior vena Abdominal Left renal cava aorta artery Right renal artery\\n\\nLeft renal vein\\n\\nRight renal vein\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right renal artery\",\"e\":\"The right renal artery passes posteriorly to the inferior vena cava (IVC), not anteriorly.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old male with a history of atrial fibrillation presents with severe abdominal pain, leucocytosis and fever. At laparotomy, there is noted to be infarction of the ascending colon Which branch of the aorta has an embolus passed through?\",\"lead\":\"At laparotomy, there is noted to be infarction of the ascending colon Which branch of the aorta has an embolus passed through?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"SH Coeliac axis\",\"e\":\"The coeliac axis is a direct branch of the aorta, arising from its anterior surface at the level of T12. Its branches supply the embryonic toregut le. the lower oesophagus, stomach, spleen, liver, body and tail of pancreas, gallbladder and the duodenum as far as the major duodenal papilla.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ileocolic artery\",\"e\":\"The ileocolic artery is a branch of the superior mesenteric artery and supplies the ileum, caecum and appendix.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"inferior mesenteric artery.\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric artery arises from the anterior surface of the aorta at the level of L3. It supplies the GI tract from the distal third of the transverse colon to the upper third of the rectum ie. the embryonic hindgut.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric artery arises from the aortat at the level of Li. It is the artery of the embryonic mid-gut and supplies the head and uncinate process of the pancreas, the small bowel from the major duodenal papilla to the terminal ileum and the colon from the caecum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The distribution of the SMA (yellow) and IMA (green) with respect to the colon are shown below.\\n\\nMarginal artery Middle colle artary Right colic artery Left colle artery lleocolic artery\\n\\nSigmold arteries\\n\\nreachMeAnatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lumbar artery\",\"e\":\"The paired lumbar arteries (of which there are usually four pairs) arise directly from the aorta and supply quadratus lumborum.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":40,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old male is stabbed in his abdomen. He is taken to theatre for an emergency laparotomy and is noted to have a significant liver injury. The consultant is concerned at the severity of the haemorrhage and clamps the hepatoduodenal ligament. Which vessels have been occluded?\",\"lead\":\"Which vessels have been occluded?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Coeliac trunk + hepatic portal vein\",\"e\":\"The coeliac trunk arises from the anterior surface of the aorta at the level of T12. whilste the portal vein forms at the level of L1.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hepatic artery + gastroduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"The gastroduodenal artery and the hepatic artery proper are branches of the common hepatic artery (the other being the right gastric artery. Whilste the hepatic artery proper ascends and crosses to the right, the gastroduodenal artery descends vertically behind the duodenum before bifurcating into the right gastroepiploic and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hepatic artery + hepatic portal vein\",\"e\":\"Following its formation by the confluence of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins at the level of L1, the hepatic portal vein ascends in the free edge of the lesser omentum (the hepatoduodenal ligament). It is posterior to the hepatic artery proper and the common bile duct, with the bile duct lateral to the artery. These three structures enter the liver at the porta hepatis. Compression of the free edge of the lesser omentum for haemostasis is known as Pringle's manoeuvre.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right hepatic artery + hepatic portal vein\",\"e\":\"The right hepatic artery is a branch of the hepatic artery proper.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right hepatic artery + gastroduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"The right hepatic is a branch of the hepatic artery proper. The gastroduodenal artery descends vertically posterior to the duodenum before bifurcating into the right gastroepiploic and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"A 49-year-old woman is diagnosed with a malignancy of the upper rectum. She subsequently undergoes laparoscopic anterior resection with a covering defunctioning loop ileostomy. From which artery is the lesion's blood supply most likely to be derived?\",\"lead\":\"From which artery is the lesion's blood supply most likely to be derived?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"A 49-year-old woman is diagnosed with a malignancy of the upper rectum. She subsequently undergoes laparoscopic anterior resection with a covering defunctioning loop ileostomy. From which artery is the lesion's blood supply most likely to be derived?\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inferior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) arises from the aorta at the level of L3. It supplies the Gl tract from the distal third of the transverse colon to the upper third of the rectum. The branch supplying the upper rectum is the superior rectal artery, which can be seen as the most inferior branch of the IMA in green in the image below. The middle rectal artery supplies the lower part of the rectum (a branch from the internal iliac artery).\\n\\nMarginal artery Middle colic artery Right colic artery Left colic lleocolic artery artery\\n\\nSigmold arteries\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"External pudendal artery\",\"e\":\"There are two external pudendal arteries on each side (superficial and deep). They are branches of the common femoral artery and supply the lower abdominal wall, the perineum, and the scrotum/labia majora.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left external iliac artery\",\"e\":\"The branches of the external iliacs supply the lower abdominal wall, the external genitalia and the lower limbs.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Obturator artery\",\"e\":\"The obturator artery is a branch of the anterior trunk of the internal iliac artery. It runs anteriorly along the margin of the pelvic inlet before exiting the pelvis via the obturator canal. It supplies the obturator externus muscle, the medial compartment of the thigh, and the femur.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric artery arises from the aorta at the level of L1. It is the artery of the embryonic mid-gut and supplies the head and uncinate process of the pancreas, the small bowel from the major duodenal papilla to the terminal ileum and the colon from the caecum to the distal two-thirds of the transverse colon.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":47,\"stem\":\"A 31-year-old intravenous drug user presents to the Emergency Department with a pseudoaneurysm of the right femoral artery caused by repeated injections in the groin. He requires urgent surgical intervention.\\n\\nWhich one of the following describes the course of the femoral artery in the lower limb most accurately?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following describes the course of the femoral artery in the lower limb most accurately?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The femoral artery has no branch in the adductor cana\",\"e\":\"The femoral artery gives off the descending genicular artery in the adductor canal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The femoral artery is anterior to the vein in the adductor canal\",\"e\":\"The femoral vein* is posterior to the artery in the adductor canal. As it exits the canal, it comes to lie medial to the artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The femoral artery is posterior to the adductor brevis\",\"e\":\"The femoral artery lies anterior to adductor brevis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The femoral artery is posterior to the adductor longus\",\"e\":\"The femoral artery lies anterior to the adductor longus. The profunda femoris is deep to adductor longus.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The femoral artery is posterior to the psoas muscle\",\"e\":\"The femoral artery is anterior to the psoas muscle. It enters the thigh halfway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the symphysis pubis, passing underneath the inguinal ligament. It lies on the psoas tendon which separates it from the capsule of the hip joint\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":49,\"stem\":\"To achieve temporary haemostasis during a trauma laparotomy for liver injury, Pringle's manoeuvre can be performed by manually compressing the free edge of the lesser omentum. It also has utility in severe and unexpected haemorrhage as a result of cholecystectomy. Which vein is compressed during this manoeuvre?\",\"lead\":\"Which vein is compressed during this manoeuvre?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gastroduodenal vein\",\"e\":\"There is no gastroduodenal vein in humans:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Hepatic vein\",\"e\":\"The hepatic veins exit the liver posteriorly and drain directly to the inferior vena cava.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Portal vein\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is formed by the confluence of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins posterior to the neck of the pancreas at the level of L1 (transpyloric plane of Addison). It then passes in the free edge of the lesser omentum (the hepatoduodenal ligament) along with the common bile duct and the hepatic artery proper. The portal vein can be seen forming in the image below, and the CBD can be seen alongside it in the free edge of the lesser omentum.\\n\\nPortal vein (and tributaries) Splenic Vein (and tributaries) Superior mesenteric vein Inferior (and tributaries) mesenteric vein (and tributaries)\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Splenic vein\",\"e\":\"The splenic vein terminates as it forms the portal vein with the superior mesenteric vein posterior to the neck of the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric vein\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric vein terminates as it forms the portal vein with the splenic vein posterior to the neck of the pancreas.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"A 42-year-old man is stabbed in the right upper quadrant of his abdomen. During the subsequent emergency laparotomy the surgeon clamps the free edge of the lesser omentum in order to achieve rapid haemostasis. Which artery has been occluded?\",\"lead\":\"Which artery has been occluded?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Common hepatic artery\",\"e\":\"The common hepatic artery is a branch of the coeliac trunk. Its branches are the right gastric artery, the gastroduodenal artery and the hepatic artery proper.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AirI Cystic artery\",\"e\":\"The cystic artery is a branch of the right hepatic artery. It passes laterally to enter Calot's triangle and supply the gallbladder.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastroduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"The gastroduodenal artery is a branch of the common hepatic artery. It passes posterior to the duodenum and bifurcates into the right gastroepiploic and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hepatic artery\",\"e\":\"The hepatic artery (proper) is a branch of the common hepatic artery - it can be seen branching from it in the image below. It passes in the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) medial to the common bile duct and anterior to the portal vein. Clamping the free edge of the lesser omentum to achieve haemostasis in the liver is known as Pringle's manoeuvre.\\n\\nCommon hepatic artery Splenic artery Hepatic artery proper\\n\\nCystic! gastric artery artery:\\n\\neft gastro-omenta Right gastro-omental rter artery\\n\\ntachrasnatiny C\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right gastric artery\",\"e\":\"The right gastric artery is a branch of the common hepatic artery. It runs along and supplies the lesser curve of the stomach, where it anastomoses with the left gastric artery, a branch of the coeliac trunk.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man presents with rectal bleeding, weight loss and deep pelvic pain. Subsequent computed tomography (CT) imaging of the abdomen and pelvis reveals a large rectal tumour with hepatic metastases. By which anatomical route are metastatic cells usually likely to 'settle' in the liver?\",\"lead\":\"By which anatomical route are metastatic cells usually likely to 'settle' in the liver?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Inferior mesenteric vein to superior mesenteric vein\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric vein does not usually drain to the superior mesenteric vein, instead terminating in the splenic vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior mesenteric artery to hepatic artery\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric artery is a direct branch of the aorta, arising at the level of L3. This question concerns venous drainage as related to metastasis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Middle rectal vein to internal rectal plexus\",\"e\":\"The middle rectal veins are part of the systemic circulation. They drain to the internal iliac vein. The internal rectal venous plexus lies above the dentate line and drain to the superior rectal vein and then the inferior mesenteric vein (portal venous system).\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inferior mesenteric vein to Splenic vein\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric vein drains the upper rectum and left sided colon - highlighted in green below. Its tributaries are the superior rectal vein, rectosigmoid veins, sigmoid veins and the left colic vein. It drains to the splenic vein, which then forms the hepatic portal vein with the superior mesenteric vein. Occasionally, the inferior mesenteric vein drains into the confluence of the portal vein or into the superior mesenteric vein.\\n\\nPortal vein (and tributaries) Splenic Vein (and tributarips) Superior mesenteric vein Inferior (and tributarios) mesenteric vein (and tributaries)\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric vein to portal vein\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric vein does not directly drain the rectum.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old alcoholic presents with massive upper gastrointestinal bleeding and is too unstable to undergo emergency gastroscopy. A Sengstaken-Blakemore tube is applied to achieve haemostasis. The bleeding has arisen from which vessels?\",\"lead\":\"The bleeding has arisen from which vessels?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left gastric arteries\",\"e\":\"The left gastric artery is a branch of the coeliac trunk and supplies the lesser curve of the stomach.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left gastric veins\",\"e\":\"The left gastric vein drains the lesser curve of the stomach, It receives the superficial desophageal veins.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Oesophageal arteries\",\"e\":\"The blood supply of the oesophagus can be divided into thirds. The upper third is supplied by oesophageal branches from the inferior thyroid artery, the middle third by branches from the thoracic aorta, and the lower third by oesophageal branches from the left gastric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Oesophageal veins\",\"e\":\"The oesophageal veins are veins draining the oesophagus to the systemic circulation (mostly via the azygous and inferior thyroid veins).\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superficial oesophageal veins.\",\"e\":\"The question text describes 'massive' upper GI bleeding in an alcoholic patient. This makes oesophageal varices the most likely cause of the bleeding. The superficial oesophageal veins drain the lower third of the oesophagus to the left gastric vein. Portal hypertension secondary to alcoholic liver cirrhosis causes reversal of flow at portacaval anastomoses resulting in dilated superficial oesophageal veins (cf. caput medusae, anorectal varices).\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":60,\"stem\":\"A 46-year-old woman presents with massive haematemesis and upper abdominal pain. She is resuscitated and proceeds to emergency gastroscopy, which reveals a deep ulcer in the posterior wall of the gastric body. The patient eventually requires an emergency laparotomy to achieve haemostasis. Which vessel is likely to be the source of the haemorrhage?\",\"lead\":\"Which vessel is likely to be the source of the haemorrhage?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Common hepatic artery\",\"e\":\"The common hepatic artery is a branch of the coeliac axis. It is not related to the stomach during its course.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastroduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"The gastroduodenal artery is a branch of the common hepatic artery. It runs behind the first part of the duodenum and is usually implicated in a bleeding posterior duodenal ulcer.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Splenic artery\",\"e\":\"The splenic artery is a branch of the coeliac axis as shown below. It has a tortuous course. passing along the superior border of the pancreas and posterior to the body of the stomach It can be eroded into by a posterior gastric ulcer.\\n\\nPortal Splenic vein vein\\n\\nGastroduodenal artery\\n\\nInferior pancreaticduodenal artery\\n\\nSuperior mesenteric artery & vein teachmeanatomy\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superficial oesophageal veins\",\"e\":\"These veins drain the lower third of the osophagus and are the cause of variceal bleeding in patients with portal hypertension.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric artery branches from the aorta at the level of L1. It courses anteroinferiorly, crossing the left renal vein and third part of the duodenum, before running in the root of the mesentery. It is not related to the stomach.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"A 2-year-old boy presents with a large mass in the left flank and proceeds to laparotomy with excision of Wilms' tumour. During the procedure, the lienorenal ligament is divided Which vessels are carried by this ligament?\",\"lead\":\"During the procedure, the lienorenal ligament is divided Which vessels are carried by this ligament?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left colic\",\"e\":\"The left colic artery is a branch of the inferior mesenteric artery and the left colic vein drains to the inferior mesenteric vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left gastric\",\"e\":\"The left gastric vessels run in the lesser curve of the stomach. The artery is a branch of the coeliac axis and the vein drains to the portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pancreaticoduodenal\",\"e\":\"There are superior and interior pancreaticoduodenal vesses. The superior artery is a branch of the gastroduodenal artery and the inferior branches from the superior mesenteric artery. The veins accompany the corresponding arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Renal\",\"e\":\"The renal arteries are direct branches of the aorta and the renal veins drain directly to the inferior vena cava.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"splenic\",\"e\":\"The lienorenal ligament connects the hilum of the spleen to the left kidney. It is highlighted in blue below. The splenic artery and vein are carried within it, as is the pancreatic tail. Wilms' tumour (nephroblastoma) is the commonest type of renal tumour of childhood, representing 20% of all childhood malignancies.\\n\\nStomach Gastrosplenic ligament\\n\\nPeritoneum\\n\\nSpleen\\n\\nLeft Splenorenal Kidney ligament TeachmeAnatomy\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":66,\"stem\":\"A patient with severe pelvic injuries is deemed to be at very high risk for DVT following fixation. An inferior vena cava (IVC) filter is fitted to reduce risk of pulmonary embolism. Which one of the following is correct regarding the anatomy of the IVC?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding the anatomy of the IVC?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Commences at the level of the fourth lumbar vertebra\",\"e\":\"The inferior vena cava is formed by the confluence of the common iliac veins at the level of L 5\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Drains the portal vein at the porta hepatis\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is formed by the confluence of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins at the level of L1 (transpyloric plane of Addison): The portal vein supplies the liver; hepatic veins subsequently drain to the inferior vena cava (IVC).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Forms the anterior boundary of the epiploic foramen\",\"e\":\"The IVC forms the posterior boundary of the epiploic foramen (of Winslow). The anterior boundary is the portal vein, whilst the superior and inferior boundaries are the caudate lobe of liver and the first part of the duodenum, respectively.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lies posterior to the right renal artery\",\"e\":\"The right renal artery passes posterior to the IVC.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lies posterior to the third part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The inferior vena cava* (IVC) passes posterior to third part of the duodenum. After the IVC receives the hepatic veins, it passes through its aperturet in the diaphragm at the level of T8. Each of the three holes has three structures passing through them:\\n\\n• Caval foramen - IVC, lymphatics, right phrenic nerve (left phrenic nerve pierces diaphragm separately) • Oesophageal hiatus - oesophagus. vagal trunks, left gastric vessels • Aortic hiatus - aorta, thoracic duct, azygous vein.\\n\\nHepatic veins\\n\\nLeft adrenal vein Inferior vena cava Left renal Right vein renal vein Left testicular vein Right testicular Left co vein llac vein\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"A patient has a leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm. This is detected on CT angiography and the patient is transferred directly to the operating theatre. Which one of the following best describes the abdominal aorta?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following best describes the abdominal aorta?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Bifurcates at the level of the second lumbar vertebra\",\"e\":\"The aorta bifurcates at the level of the fourth lumbar vertebra.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gives oft the gonadal arteries interior to the origin of the interior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The gonadal arteries originate at the level of L2, whilste the inferior mesenteric artery arises at L3.\\n\\nThe gonadal arteries originate at the level of L2, whilste the inferior mesenteric artery arises at L3.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is crossed by the left renal vein inferior to the origin of the superior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta and inferior to the superior mesenteric artery - it can be seen travelling under the SMA and in front of the aorta in the image below.\\n\\nInferior phrenic Coeliac trunk arry Superior mesenteric Renal artery arteries Abdominal aorta Testicular artery Inferior mesenteric artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pierces the diaphragm at the level of the tenth thoracic vertebra\",\"e\":\"The aorta pierces the diaphragm at the level of the 12twelfth thoracic vertebra.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Supplies midgut derivatives via the coeliac axis\",\"e\":\"The coeliac axis is the artery of the embryonic foregut. The mid-gut is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery, whilst the hindgut is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":72,\"stem\":\"A patient is brought in with severe abdominal pain and found to have new onset atrial fibrillation. They are taken for a laparotomy where a significant amount of small bowel has become ischaemic requiring resection.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct concerning the blood supply to the foregut?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct concerning the blood supply to the foregut?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The gastroduodenal artery passes anterior to the first part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The gastroduodenal artery passes posterior to the first part of the duodenum. This is important clinically as a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode into it, causing an upper GI haemorrhage.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The hepatic artery usually divides into its left and right branches in the free edge of the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The hepatic artery (proper) branches before entering the porta hepatis, but not in the free edge of the lesser omentum (the hepatoduodenal ligament).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The main blood supply to the pancreas is the common hepatic artery\",\"e\":\"The gastroduodenal artery, a branch of the common hepatic, gives off the suprior pancreaticoduodenal artery which supplies the head and uncinate process of the pancreas. However, the body and tail of the pancreas are supplied mainly by the pancreatic branches of the splenic artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The short gastric arteries are found within the gastrosplenic ligament\",\"e\":\"The short gastric arteries are branches of the splenic artery and course in the gastrosplenic ligament. which connects the greater curve of the stomach to the hilum of the spleen. It also contains the left gastroepiploic vessels.\\n\\nStomach Gastrosplenic ligament\\n\\nPeritoneum\\n\\nSpleen\\n\\nLeft Splenorenal kidney ligament TeachmeAnatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries are branches of the gastroduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"Only the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery is a branch of the gastroduodenal artery. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is a branch of the superior mesenteric artery.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":75,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old female presents with colicky right upper quadrant pain which is exacerbated by eating. She is diagnosed with gallstones and undergoes laparoscopic cholecystectomy. From which branch of the abdominal aorta does the gall bladder's blood supply arise?\",\"lead\":\"From which branch of the abdominal aorta does the gall bladder's blood supply arise?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Coeliac trunk\",\"e\":\"The coeliac trunk is a direct branch of the aorta*, arising at the level of T12. It trifurcates into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries. The common hepatic artery bifurcates into the gastroduodenal artery and the hepatic artery proper, the latter then bifurcating into left and right branches. The right hepatic artery gives off the cystic artery, which supplies the gallbladder.\\n\\n.. •\\n\\nL Cystic a.\\n\\n..=\\n\\nSuperior pancreatico-duodenal a.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left gastric artery\",\"e\":\"The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curve of the stomach and the lower third of the oesophagus\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inferior mesenteric artery!\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric artery is a direct branch of the aorta, arising at the level of L3. It is the artery of the embryonic hindgut, supplying the Gi tract from the distal third of the transverse colon to the rectum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right gastric artery\",\"e\":\"The right gastric artery is a branch of the common hepatic artery. It supplies the lesser curve of the stomach and anastomoses with the left gastric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric artery is a branch of the aorta, arising at the level of L1. It is the artery of the embryonic mid-gut, supplying the GI tract from the major duodenal papilla to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":78,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old motorcyclist presents with hypovolaemic shock following blunt abdominal trauma. He undergoes emergency laparotomy and is noted to have a liver laceration. The surgeon clamps the hepatoduodenal ligament just anterior to the epiploic foramen. Which vein is carried within the ligament?\",\"lead\":\"Which vein is carried within the ligament?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cystic vein\",\"e\":\"The cystic vein (when present) usually drains to the right branch of the portal vein It does not run in the hepatoduodenal ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Gastroduodenal vein\",\"e\":\"There is no gastroduodenal vein in humans.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Hepatic vein\",\"e\":\"The hepatic veins exit the liver posteriorly and drain directly to the inferior vena cava.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Hepatic portal vein\",\"e\":\"The hepatic portal vein is formed by the confluence of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins at the level of L1 (transpyloric plane of Addison). The portal vein runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament (the free edge of the lesser omentum) posterior to the common bile duct and the hepatic artery proper (the artery is medial to the duct). The portal vein forms the anterior boundary of the epiploic foramen of Winslow. Clamping the hepatoduodenal ligament for haemostasis of the liver is known as Pringle's manoeuvre.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right gastric vein\",\"e\":\"The right gastric vein drains the lesser curve of the stomach.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old male presents with rectal bleeding, tenesmus and loose stools. Digital rectal examination reveals the presence of a fixed mass, and biopsy confirms a rectal adenocarcinoma. He proceeds to a low-anterior resection of the rectum. Into which vessel does the inferior mesenteric vein usually drain?\",\"lead\":\"Into which vessel does the inferior mesenteric vein usually drain?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hepatic portal vein\",\"e\":\"The hepatic portal vein is usually formed by the confluence of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins. The inferior mesenteric vein occasionally empties into the confluence, but this is not the norm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric vein is part of the portal circulation and does not drain into the inferior vena cava\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Left gastric veins\",\"e\":\"The left gastric veins drain the lesser curve of the stomach.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Splenic vein\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric vein (in yellow) usually drains into the splenic vein before it forms the portal vein with the superior mesenteric vein.\\n\\nHepatic portal Splenic vein vein\\n\\nInferior Superior mesenteric mesenteric veln vein\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric vein\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric vein occasionally drains into the superior mesenteric vein, however this is not the norm.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":83,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old woman presents with a large, pulsatile abdominal mass, hypotension and back pain. She undergoes emergency endovascular aneurysm repair. At which vertebral level does the aorta bifurcate to give the common iliac arteries?\",\"lead\":\"At which vertebral level does the aorta bifurcate to give the common iliac arteries?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"LI\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"L2\",\"e\":\"This is the level of the origin of the gonadal arteries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"L3\",\"e\":\"L3 is the level of the origin of the inferior mesenteric artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"L4\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"L5\",\"e\":\"This is the level of the origin of the gonadal arteries.\\n\\nL3 is the level of the origin of the inferior mesenteric artery\\n\\nThis is the level at which the common iliac veins form the inferior vena cava.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":86,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient with portal hypertension and request a scan to look for a portal vein thrombosis. The radiologist reports the scan as positive and the patient is commenced initially on a treatment dose of enoxaparin.\\n\\nWhich one of the following would help you locate the portal vein on the scan images?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following would help you locate the portal vein on the scan images?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It arises at the union of the splenic and inferior mesenteric veins\",\"e\":\"at the confluence of the splenic and superior mesenteric veins, at The portal vein is formed the level of L1.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It lies directly anterior to the aorta\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is anterior to the inferior vena cava\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies in the free edge of the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is formed by the confluence of the splenic and superior mesenteric veins (the inferior mesenteric vein usually joins the splenic vein as seen in the image below). Following its formation, the portal vein runs in the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament).\\n\\nHepatic portal Splenic vein vein\\n\\nInferior Superior mesenteric mesenteric. vein vein\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It lies in front of the hepatic artery at the porta hepatis\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is posterior to the hepatic artery proper and the common bile duct, with the artery medial to the duct.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It receives the ligamentum teres at its right branch\",\"e\":\"The ligamentum teres (hepatis) is the free edge of the falciform ligament. It contains the obliterated umbilical vein and paraumbilical veins\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":89,\"stem\":\"You suspect a patient you are treating has ischaemic bowel secondary to a thrombosis of the superior mesenteric artery. You are considering whether this could be treated by an interventional radiologist or whether a major bowel resection will be required. In reviewing the scans which of the following would allow you to locate the superior mesenteric artery?\",\"lead\":\"In reviewing the scans which of the following would allow you to locate the superior mesenteric artery?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It crosses posterior to the third part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) crosses anterior to the third part of the duodenum. If the angle of the SMA is particularly acute, it can cause duodenal obstruction at this level (Wilkie's syndrome).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It lies to the left of the inferior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The SMA arises at the level of L1. The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) arises at L3. Both are midline branches of the aorta, however whilst the SMA runs inferiorly and to the right, the IMA runs to the left.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies to the right of the superior mesenteric vein\",\"e\":\"The SMA lies to the left of the superior mesenteric vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It passes posterior to the left renal vein\",\"e\":\"The SMA passes anterior to the left renal vein, Compression of the left renal vein between the SMA and the aorta is known as nutcracker syndrome.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It passes infero-posterior to the splenic vein\",\"e\":\"The SMA is posterior to the splenic vein - it can be seen passing beneath the neck of the pancreas and under the splenic vein in the image below.\\n\\nPortal Splenic vein vein\\n\\nSplenic artery Gastroduodenal artery\\n\\nInferior pancreaticduodenal artery\\n\\nSuperior mesenteric artery & vein teachmeanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":92,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient with portal hypertension. They are admitted with hypotension and tachycardia with a low haemoglobin on the venous blood gas. Where is the most likely location of the porto-systemic anastomosis responsible for variceal bleeding?\",\"lead\":\"Where is the most likely location of the porto-systemic anastomosis responsible for variceal bleeding?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Between the left gastric veins and the azygos system\",\"e\":\"The left gastric vein receives blood from the superficial desophageal veins, which drain the lower third of the oesophagus. The left gastric vein drains into the portal system. The oesophageal veins, which drain the upper two-thirds of the oesophagus, drain to the azygous system. The superficial oesophageal veins become engorged (as varices) in portal hypertension due to them being a porto-systemic anastomosis. The Azygos system is shown below in the image, with the azygos vein draining the right side of the thorax, and the hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins draining the left side.\\n\\nArch of Superior aorta vena cava\\n\\nAccessory hemiazygos vein Azygos vein\\n\\nHemiazygos vein\\n\\nieachMeAnatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Between portal system and anterior abdominal wall veins\",\"e\":\"Portal hypertension can lead to engorgement of paraumbilical veins which give rise to the clinical sign of caput medusae.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Between the superior rectal vein and the internal pudendal vein\",\"e\":\"This portacaval anastomosis causes anorectal varices. These are not to be confused with haemorrhoids, which are prolapsed rectal venous plexuses.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"At the bare area of the liver\",\"e\":\"Intraparenchymal branches of the right portal system anastomose with the phrenic vein at this portacaval anastomosis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"in the falciform ligament\",\"e\":\"The free edge of the falciform ligament is the ligamentum teres hepatis. It contains the obliterated umbilical vein and paraumbilical veins. In portal hypertension, the umbilical vein can become recanalised.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":95,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old alcoholic patient has severe liver cirrhosis. They are subsequently diagnosed with portal hypertension. In an attempt to reduce portal pressure they undergo a porto- systemic bypass procedure.\\n\\nHow would you describe the portal vein?\",\"lead\":\"How would you describe the portal vein?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"At the epiploic foramen, it is anterior to the hepatic artery\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is posterior to the hepatic artery and the common bile duct in the free edge of the lesser foramen, with the artery medial to the duct\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It is formed in the confluence of the superior mesenteric and splenic vein\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is formed by the confluence of the superior mesenteric and splenic vein at the level of L1. The inferior mesenteric vein usually drains in to the splenic vein, as seen in the image below.\\n\\nPortal vein {and tributaries) Splenic Vein (and tributaries) Superior mesenteric vein Inferior (and tributaries) mesenteric vein (and tributaries)\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies behind the epiploic foramen\",\"e\":\"The portal vein forms the anterior boundary of the epiploic foramen of Winslow, which connects with lesser sac with the peritoneal cavity. The posterior boundary is the inferior vena cava, superiorly it is bounded by the caudate lobe of liver, and inferiorly by the first part of the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It lies deep within the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The portal vein runs in the free edge of the lesser omentum (the hepatoduodenal ligament). It gives off left and right branches, the former supplying segments II, Ill and IV of the liver. The right branch gives off anterior and posterior divisions, the former supplying segments V and VIll, and the latter supplying segments Vl and VIl. Segment | (the caudate lobe) may be supplied by the right or left portal veins. The branches of the portal vein continue to branch within the liver parenchyma and form portal venules. Each portal venule courses alongside a hepatic arteriole, the two vessels forming the vascular components of the portal triad. These vessels ultimately empty into the hepatic sinusoids to supply blood to the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It receives tributaries from the pancreatic neck\",\"e\":\"The pancreatic neck is drained by the splenic vein.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in fitting an inferior vena cava (IVC) filter. The consultant asks you about the anatomy of the IVC Which one of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"The consultant asks you about the anatomy of the IVC Which one of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It commences at the level of L3\",\"e\":\"The inferior vena cava is formed by the confluence of the common iliac veins at the level of L5.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It enters the thoracic cavity at the level of T8\",\"e\":\"The IVC* passes through the diaphragm t at the level of T8, along with the right phrenic nerve and lymph nodes. The caval hiatus in the central tendon is shown below. Each of the three holes has three structures passing through them:\\n\\n• Caval foramen - IVC, lymphatics, right phrenic nerve (left phrenic nerve pierces diaphragm separately) • oEsophageal hiatus - oesophagus, vagal trunks, left gastric vessels • Aortic hiatus - aorta, thoracic duct, azygous vein.\\n\\nSternal Central tendon attachment\\n\\nCaval hiatus Desophageal niatus\\n\\nAortic hiatus\\n\\nVertebral attachment © reachmeAnstony\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies anterior to the aorta\",\"e\":\"The IVC lies to the right of the aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It lies anterior to the caudate lobe of the liver\",\"e\":\"The IVC is posterior to the caudate lobe.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It lies to the left of the aorta\",\"e\":\"The IVC lies to the right of the aorta.\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":101,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in an open elective abdominal aortic aneurysm repair and your consultant points to an artery that comes directly from the branch of the aorta given off at the level of\\n\\nWhich one of these is that artery most likely to be?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is that artery most likely to be?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The gastroduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"This artery is a branch of the common hepatic artery. It runs posterior to the first part of the duodenum and bifurcates into the right gastroepiploic and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"This artery is a branch of the superior mesenteric artery, which arises from the aorta at the level of L1.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The right gastric artery\",\"e\":\"The right gastric artery is a branch of the common hepatic artery, and runs in the lesser curve of the stomach, where it anastomoses with the left gastric artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"artery V The splenic artery\",\"e\":\"This artery is one of the three branches of the coeliac trunk, which arises from the aorta at the level of T12. The other two branches are the common hepatic artery and the left gastric artery - all three of which are shown below in the image.\\n\\nCoeliac trunk\\n\\nLeft gastric a.\\n\\nCommon hepatic a.\\n\\nSplenic a.\\n\\nalicAnste\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"This artery is a branch of the gastroduodenal artery\"}]},{\"t\":48,\"p\":104,\"stem\":\"A patient with known vascular disease develops erectile dysfunction. He denies any leg pain. On examination he has marked wasting of the right buttock area.\\n\\nWhich vessel is likely to be affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which vessel is likely to be affected?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Superficial femoral artery\",\"e\":\"The superficial femoral artery* (SFA) is the continuation of the common femoral artery, after it has given off profunda femoris. It gives off descending genicular branches before it runs. in the subsartorial canal of Hunter. When it exits the adductor hiatus it becomes the popliteal artery, which branches into the anterior tibial arteries and the tibial-peroneal trunk, the latter then dividing into the posterior tibial and the peroneal artery. These vessels supply the leg. The absence of leg pain in the patient would suggest the SFA is not involved\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Internal iliac artery\",\"e\":\"The internal iliac artery is a branch of the common iliac artery - it is shown with its major branches in the image. It divides into two branches, the posterior division and the anterior division. The posterior division gives off the superior gluteal artery (shown in turquoise) which supplies gluteus medius and minimis, and the lateral sacral (green). The anterior division gives off the inferior gluteal artery which supplies gluteus maximus. All the muscles of the buttock are therefore supplied by the internal iliac artery, so compromise of this vessel would lead to visible buttock wasting. The blood supply of the penis is mainly derived from the pudendal artery (a branch of the internal iliac artery) and so this would also suggest the internal iliac artery was affected\\n\\nInternal: Lateral liac Sacral Superior gluteal Obturator\\n\\nInferior: Superior glures vesical\\n\\nInferior nternal vesical udenda\\n\\nMiddle rectal\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Profunda femoris\",\"e\":\"Profunda femoris supplies the thigh. It gives off medial and lateral circumflex branches as well as perforating branches.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"External iliac artery\",\"e\":\"The external iliac artery is a branch of the common iliac. It becomes the common femoral artery when it passes underneath the inguinal ligament. Before it does so, it gives off the inferior epigastric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Common Niac artery\",\"e\":\"As the patient has no leg symptoms, this makes the common iliac artery a less likely answer than internal iliac. However, impotence and buttock claudication would raise suspicion of aortoiliac occlusive disease. If these two symptoms are present along with absent or weak femoral pulses, they form a triad known as Leriche syndrome.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A 56-year-old man is undergoing a routine cholecystectomy Suddenly there is uncontrolled the surgeon converts to open and performs Pringle's manoevure. haemorrhage.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is a boundary of the omental foramen (epiploic foramen or foramen of Winslow)?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is a boundary of the omental foramen (epiploic foramen or foramen of Winslow)?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anteriorly the free edge of the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The free edge of the lesser omentum forms the anterior border of the epiploic foramen and it can be seen to the right of the stomach in the first image below. The lesser omentum is a double fold of peritoneum and between these layers are the common bile duct, hepatic artery, and portal vein. The lesser sac itself can be seen from the side in the second image below, posterior to the stomach. ASTEST Stomach Gastrosplenic ligament\\n\\nPeritoneum\\n\\nSpleen\\n\\nLeft Splenorenal kidney ligament TeachmeAnatomy\\n\\nBare area of the liver Subphrenic recess\\n\\nStomach Subhepatic recess\\n\\nTransverse colon\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Posteriorly the hepatic vein. Posteriorly the hepatic vein\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Superiorly the quadrate lobe of the liver\",\"e\":\"The caudate, not the quadrate lobe of the liver is the superior border of the foramen of Winslow.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inferiorly the hepatic vein\",\"e\":\"The posterior boundary is formed by the peritoneum covering the inferior vena cava.\\n\\nThe inferior boundary comprises the peritoneum covering the commencement of the duodenum and the hepatic artery, the latter passing forward below the foramen before ascending between the two layers of the lesser omentum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"None of the above\",\"e\":\"This is incorrect as the free edge of the lesser omentum forms the anterior border of the epiploic foramen.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man presents with severe loin to groin pain and microscopic haematuria. On piain AXR a calcification is visible projected over the right ureter. In the normal ureter the diameter of its lumen narrows at which point?\",\"lead\":\"In the normal ureter the diameter of its lumen narrows at which point?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The pelviureteric junction . Ca\",\"e\":\"The pelviureteric junction (PUJ) is one narrowing of the ureter.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The vesicoureteric junction\",\"e\":\"The vesicoureteric junction (VUJ) is a narrowing of the ureter.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The point at which the ureter crosses the iliac vessels\",\"e\":\"The iliac vessels cause the ureter to narrow when they cross it.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"All of the above\",\"e\":\"The pelviureteric junction (PUJ), the vesicoureteric junction (VUJ) and the point at which the iliac vessels cross the ureter are the three points at which the ureter narrows.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"None of the above\",\"e\":\"The ureter has three distinct narrowings in its passage from the kidney to the bladder. The lumen of the ureter is not of equal diameter throughout the ureters entire length. It has three distinct narrowings, these are the pelviureteric junction proximally, then the crossing of the iliac vessels, and distally the vesicoureteric junction in the pelvis. These are possible sites of ureteric calculus impaction and are useful landmarks when assessing a plain radiograph of the abdomen or a computed tomography scan of the kidneys, ureters and bladder (CTKUB).\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":8,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man is undergoing a right hemicolectomy for a caecal tumour. initially dissection is slow with the harmonic scalpel as the consultant identifies critical structures. Which one of the following structures is most important to identify during this operation?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures is most important to identify during this operation?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The left ureter\",\"e\":\"The left ureter is not encountered during a right hemicolectomy. However the right ureter can be easily damaged\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The left gonadal artery\",\"e\":\"The left gonadal artery is not encountered in a right hemicolectomy. However along with the right ureter; the right gonadal artery can be easily damaged during a right hemicolectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The right colic artery\",\"e\":\"The right colic artery is a branch of the superior mesenteric artery and supplies the ascending colon and must be identified and ligated in a right hemicolectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The inferior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the large bowel from the distal one third of the transverse colon and is therefore not involved in a right hemicolectomy.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The third part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The transverse colon crosses the second part of the duodenum rather than the third. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the large bowel from the transverse colon distally and is not encountered in a right hemicolectomy which involves excision of the caecum, ascending colon and the middle third of the transverse colon. The transverse colon crosses the second part of the duodenum. The right ureter and gonadal vessels although both retroperitoneal, are both posteriorly related to the ascending colon and are easily injured during dissection. The ileocolic artery is the terminal branch of the superior mesenteric artery and its right branch is usually ligated during a right hemicolectomy.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old woman is admitted with obstructive jaundice: MRCP confirms a stone obstructing the CBD and they are booked for an ERCP for sphincterotomy. What is the normal location of the major duodenal papilla?\",\"lead\":\"What is the normal location of the major duodenal papilla?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Superior part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"This is the first part of the duodenum and does not contain the major duodenal papilla.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ascending part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"This is the fourth part of the duodenum and joins to the jejunum at the duodenojejunal flexure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Descending part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The common bile duct and pancreatic duct join and enter the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla, in the descending part of the duodenum (D2).\\n\\nBile duct Major duodenal papilla (controsed by the sphincter of codi) Pancreatic duct\\n\\nDuodenum teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Horizontal part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The horizontal or third part of the duodenum does not contain the major duodenal papilla.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Duodenal bulh\",\"e\":\"The duodenal bulb is the part of the duodenum closest to the stomach. The major duodenal papilla is found in the descending part of the duodenum, rather than the duodenal bulb.\\n\\nThe major duodenal papilla is situated at the medial side of the descending portion of the duodenum, a little below its middle and about 7 to 10 cm from the pylorus. The common bile and pancreatic ducts unite and open by a common orifice on the summit of the duodenal papilla.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":13,\"stem\":\"While reviewing the abdominal aortogram of a 67-year-old man with an abdominal aortic aneurysm, a radiologist notices an occluded inferior mesenteric artery. However, on enquiry. the patient denies having any abdominal symptoms. Occlusion of the inferior mesenteric artery is seldom symptomatic because its territory may be supplied by branches of which one of the following?\",\"lead\":\"Occlusion of the inferior mesenteric artery is seldom symptomatic because its territory may be supplied by branches of which one of the following?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gastroduodenal artery\",\"e\":\"The gastroduodenal artery is a branch off the common hepatic artery, which supplies parts of the duodenum, pancreas and stomach and is too superior to help with an occluded inferior mesenteric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"AN leocolic artery\",\"e\":\"The ileocolic artery supplies the caecum and appendix and helps contribute to the marginal artery. However it is too proximal to help supply the descending colon, sigmoid colon and\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Middle colic artery\",\"e\":\"The middle colic artery is the branch from the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) that supplies the transverse colon. This is the most distal part of the colon that receives blood from the SMA. Branches from the middle colic form an anastomosis with the marginal artery. which would be able to supply the descending colon, sigmoid colon and rectum if the inferior mesenteric artery was occluded. The distribution of the superior (yellow) and inferior (green) mesenteric artery supplies are shown below.\\n\\nMarginal artery Middla colic artory Right colic artery Left colic lleocolic artery artery\\n\\nSigmold arteries\\n\\nreacteAnatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right colic artery\",\"e\":\"The right colic artery also helps supply the marginal artery but the middle colic artery supplies the proximal two thirds of the transverse colon so is more likely to supply the descending colon, sigmoid colon and rectum if the inferior mesenteric artery was occluded.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Splenic artery\",\"e\":\"The splenic artery is a branch of the collac trunk and supplies the spleen, pancreas and stomach. It is too superior to help with an occluded inferior mesenteric artery.\\n\\nThe middle colic artery is the branch from the superior mesenteric artery that supplies the transverse colon. This area is the most distal part of the colon that receives blood from the SMA. Branches from the middle colic anastomose with the marginal artery, which would be able to supply the descending colon, sigmoid colon and rectum if the inferior mesenteric artery was occluded. The ileocolic and right colic arteries are also branches of the SMA that supply the colon (and contribute to the marginal artery), but the middle colic, which serves a more distal part of the colon, is a better answer. The gastroduodenal artery is a branch off the common hepatic artery, which supplies parts of the duodenum, pancreas and stomach. The splenic artery is one of the three branches of the coeliac trunk. It supplies the spleen, pancreas and curvature of the stomach\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":16,\"stem\":\"The spleen normally does not descend below the costal margin. However, it pushes downward and medially when pathologically enlarged What structure limits the straight-vertical-downward movement?\",\"lead\":\"However, it pushes downward and medially when pathologically enlarged What structure limits the straight-vertical-downward movement?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Left colic flexure . C-\",\"e\":\"The left colic flexure, also called the splenic flexure, is the point where the colon takes a sharp downward turn. This flexure is the point where the transverse colon ends and the descending colon begins. It is located immediately inferior to the spleen, so an enlarged spleen must move medially to avoid this colic flexure.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left suprarenal gland\",\"e\":\"The suprarenal gland is a retroperitoneal structure and would not prevent the spleen from descending inferiorly.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pancreas\",\"e\":\"The pancreas lies medial to the spleen and would not prevent the spleen from descending inferiorly.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Stomach\",\"e\":\"The stomach lies medial to the spleen and would not prevent the spleen from descending inferiorly.\\n\\nThe left colic flexure, also called the splenic flexure, is the point where the colon takes a sharp downward turn. This flexure is the point where the transverse colon ends and the descending colon begins. It is located immediately inferior to the spleen, so an enlarged spleen must move medially to avoid this colic flexure. The left suprarenal gland is a retroperitoneal structure, which sits superior to the kidney. The suspensory muscle of the duodenum or ligament of Treitz is a thin sheet of muscle derived from the right crus of the diaphragm - it suspends the fourth part of the duodenum from the posterior abdominal wall. Both the pancreas and stomach lie medial to the spleen. These organs would not prevent the spleen from descending inferiorly.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ligament of Treitz\",\"e\":\"The suspensory muscle of the duodenum or ligament of Treitz is a thin sheet of muscle derived from the right crus of the diaphragm - it suspends the fourth part of the duodenum from the posterior abdominal wall. It has no effect on the spleen.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":19,\"stem\":\"A patient is undergoing a liver resection for a colorectal metastastic deposit. You are assisting the HPB consultant. In the surgical anatomy of the liver which of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"In the surgical anatomy of the liver which of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Segment I lies to the left of the portal vein\",\"e\":\"It lies posterior to the portal vein\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Segment I! lies lateral to the porta hepatis\",\"e\":\"Segment Il lies medial to the porta hepatis.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The caudate lobe lies anterior to the portal vein\",\"e\":\"It lies posterior, rather than anterior, to the portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The portal vein runs in the anterior border of Winslow's foramen\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is an anterior border of the foramen of Winslow, or epiploic foramen. The caudate lobe (segment I according to Couinaud's classification, 1957) lies posterior to the portal vein but anterior to the inferior vena cava. Three main hepatic veins divide the liver into four sectors, each of which receives a portal pedicle, with an alternation between hepatic veins and portal pedicles. According to this functional anatomy, the liver is divided into hemilivers (right and left) by the main portal scissura called Cantlie's line.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Three hepatic arteries divide the liver into four sectors\",\"e\":\"The three main hepatic veins - the right, left and middle hepatic veins - divide the liver into four sectors.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":21,\"stem\":\"A 57-year-old woman presents with large bowel obstruction and is diagnosed with an ascending colon adenocarcinoma. During the subsequent right hemicolectomy you are advised not to injure a layer of fascia that lies posterior to the ascending colon.\\n\\nWhat fascia is this?\",\"lead\":\"What fascia is this?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Colles' fascia\",\"e\":\"Colle's fascia affixes the root of the penis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Denonvilliers! fascia\",\"e\":\"Denonvilliers' fascia separates the rectum from the prostate gland and urinary bladder.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gerota's fascia\",\"e\":\"Gerota's fascia, or the renal fascia, surrounds the kidney and the adrenal gland. It is found in the retroperitoneum, posterior to the colon and is labelled in red in the image below.\\n\\nKidney Renal capsule Perirenal fat Renal fascia\\n\\nPararenal fat\\n\\nTeachMeAnatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Scarpa's fascia\",\"e\":\"Scarpa's fascia is the membranous layer of the superficial fascia of the anterior abdominal wall. It is found deep to the thicker Camper's fascia and superficial to the external oblique muscle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spigelian fascia\",\"e\":\"The Spigelian fascia is part of the anterior abdominal wall. It lies between the rectus abdominis muscle and the semilunar line and so is an area of weakness through which Spigelian hernias can develop.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A 22-year-old man is stabbed in the right side of his abdomen sustaining a laceration to the upper part of the portal vein close to the liver. He is brought to the Accident and Emergency Department (A&E) in hypovolaemic shock. Where is the surgeon most likely to gain access to the portal vein initially?\",\"lead\":\"Where is the surgeon most likely to gain access to the portal vein initially?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Anterior to the hepatic artery and common bile duct\",\"e\":\"It is posterior to the hepatic artery and common bile duct.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Behind the body of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"It is found behind the head of the pancreas rather the body.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inside the greater sac\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is not inside the greater sac.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inside the lesser sac\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is not found in the lesser sac.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"On the free right edge of the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The portal vein lies between the two layers of the lesser omentum running in the free edge. The portal vein is formed by the confluence of the superior mesenteric vein (lying to the right of the artery) and the splenic vein, behind the neck of the pancreas. It is about 8 cm long. The portal vein lies between the two layers of the lesser omentum, running almost vertically in the free edge where the lesser omentum forms the anterior boundary of the epiploic foramen. The portal vein is found behind the hepatic artery and common bile duct.. The terminals of the portal vein and the hepatic artery form, with the hepatic ductules, the triads of the liver in the corners of the hexagonal lobules. The central veins drain into the hepatic veins. In trauma scenarios, liver haemorrhages can be controlled by temporary compression of the portal vein and hepatic artery in the edge of the lesser omentum (hepato-duodenal ligament). This is known as Pringle's manoeuvre.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old woman develops a perinephric abscess following an episode of severe pyelonephritis: She presents with severe tenderness in the flank with the impression of a mass in the area\\n\\nWhich one of the following structures is likely to be in contact with the purulent exudates?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures is likely to be in contact with the purulent exudates?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Psoas muscle\",\"e\":\"The kidney is directly related to the psoas muscle at its medial posterior surface.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Splenocolic ligament\",\"e\":\"Splenocolic ligament attaches to the hilum of the spleen to the left transverse mesocolon. It has no relation to the kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gonadal vein\",\"e\":\"The gonadal vein is not in direct contact with the kidneys.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Cisterna chyli\",\"e\":\"The cisterna chyli is found on the right of the aorta in front of Li and L2. It is at a similar level to the kidneys and lies between them without direct contact.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Ligament of Treitz\",\"e\":\"The suspensory muscle of the duodenum or ligament of Treitz is a thin sheet of muscle derived from the right crus of the diaphragm - it suspends the fourth part of the duodenum from the posterior abdominal wall. It is not in contact with the kidney.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":28,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man is diagnosed with an adenocarcinoma of the head of pancreas. He undergoes a Whipple's procedure. Which one of the following options best describes the third part of the duodenum?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options best describes the third part of the duodenum?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Is anterior to the lett ureter .C~\",\"e\":\"It is anterior to the right ureter rather than the left.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is posterior to the superior mesenteric artery\",\"e\":\"The third part of the duodenum lies in the subcostal plane (L3) and follows the inferior margin of the pancreatic head. It lies posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels, which can be seen in the image below travelling anteriorly to D3. It overlies the aorta and the beginning of the inferior mesenteric artery. The root of the small bowel mesentery is attached near its termination on the left. The second part of the duodenum overlies the right ureter and renal vessels in the hilum of the right kidney, separated from the renal artery by the vein.\\n\\nPortal Splenic vein vein\\n\\nSplenic artery Gastroduodenal artery\\n\\nInferior pancreaticduodenal artery\\n\\nSuperior mesenteric artery & vein © teachmoanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is anterior to the root of the mesentery of the small bowel\",\"e\":\"The root of the small bowel is to the left of the third part of the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is anterior to the superior mesenteric vessels.\",\"e\":\"It lies posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is immediately anterior to the right renal artery\",\"e\":\"The second part of the duodenum, rather than the third part, is anterior to the right renal artery. 102\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":31,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with central abdominai pain radiating to the back. Serum amylase levels are significantly elevated and he is diagnosed with acute severe necrotising pancreatitis confirmed on a computed tomography (CT) scan. Which one of the following options best describes the anatomy of the pancreas?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options best describes the anatomy of the pancreas?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Gives attachment to the small bowel mesentery\",\"e\":\"The transverse mesocolon is attached to the head, neck and body of the pancreas rather than the small bowel mesentery:\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Has an uncinate process lying anterior to the superior mesenteric vein\",\"e\":\"The uncinate process lies posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Has the inferior mesenteric vein passing behind the neck\",\"e\":\"The inferior mesenteric vein passes behind the body of the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lies in the transpyloric plane\",\"e\":\"The head of the pancreas lies in the transpyloric plane. The head of the pancreas is related to the hilum but does not overlie the right kidney. It is, however, anterior to the left kidney. The transpyloric plane (L1) transects the pancreas obliquely, passing through the midpoint of the neck, with most of the head below the plane, and most of the body and tail above. The transverse mesocolon is attached to the head, neck and body of the pancreas. The uncinate process lies posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels, and the inferior mesenteric vein passes behind the body of the pancreas, where it joins the splenic vein. The various parts of the pancreas are labelled in the image below.\\n\\n(Uncinate process Head Neck Body Tail\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Overlies the right kidney\",\"e\":\"The head of the pancreas lies within the C-shaped concavity of the duodenum medial to the hilum of the right kidney, but not overlying it. The tail of the pancreas is, however, anterior to the left kidney.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"A 69-year-old man with recent onset fast atrial fibrillation presents to the Emergency Department with acute, severe periumbilical abdominal pain. Arterial blood gas measurement reveals raised lactate levels. CT scanning confirms an extensive ischaemic small bowel. The patient is taken to theatre for a laparotomy and small bowel resection. Which feature best differentiates the upper jejunum from the lower ileum?\",\"lead\":\"Which feature best differentiates the upper jejunum from the lower ileum?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A thinner wall\",\"e\":\"The jejunum has a thicker wall than the ileum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fewer circular folds\",\"e\":\"The jejunum has more circular folds compared with the ileum (plicae circulares).\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Less fat at the mesenteric border\",\"e\":\"There is less mesenteric fat at the border of the jejunum compared to the ileum. The jejunum has a thicker wall, less mesenteric fat, more plicae circulares, a wider lumen, fewer Peyer's patches and fewer arterial arcades than the ileum\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"More aggregated lymphatic follicles (Peyer's patches)\",\"e\":\"The jejunum has fewer Peyer's patches than the ileum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"More arterial arcades:\",\"e\":\"There are less arterial arcades in the jejunum compared with the ileum.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":36,\"stem\":\"A 49-year-old man develops an abscess in the lesser sac following an episode of severe pancreatitis. He undergoes laparotomy for wash-out of the abscess. The lesser omentum is incised to access the lesser sac.\\n\\nWhich one of the following options best describes the anatomy of the lesser omentum?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options best describes the anatomy of the lesser omentum?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Encloses the right gastric vessels\",\"e\":\"The right gastric vessels like between the two layers of omentum. The right and left gastric arteries supply the lesser omentum as they lie between its two peritoneal layers. The free edge of the lesser omentum is attached to the first 2 cm of the first part of the duodenum below and the fissure of the ligamentum venosum above. The common hepatic duct is joined by the cystic duct to form the common bile duct in the free edge of the lesser omentum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Has the left hepatic duct in its free edge\",\"e\":\"The common bile duct runs in the free edge.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"is attached to the liver in the fissure of the ligamentum teres\",\"e\":\"It is attached to the liver via the ligamentum venosum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"is attached to the third part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"It is attached to the first 2 cm of the first part of the duodenum\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is supplied by gastroepiploic arteries\",\"e\":\"It is supplied by the right and left gastric arteries.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":38,\"stem\":\"A 48-vear-old man presents with symptoms of fulminant hepatic failure secondary to alcohol abuse. He is noted to have hepatosplenomegaly, anorectal varices and caput medusa. Which anatomical structure is just anterior to the origin of the hepatic portal vein?\",\"lead\":\"Which anatomical structure is just anterior to the origin of the hepatic portal vein?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hepatic flexure\",\"e\":\"The hepatic flexure is lateral to the origin of the portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The inferior vena cava is posterior to the origin of the hepatic portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pancreatic body\",\"e\":\"It is the pancreatic neck, rather than the pancreatic body that is anterior to the origin of the portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pancreatic neck\",\"e\":\"The pancreatic neck is anterior to the origin of the portal vein. The hepatic portal vein is formed of the confluence of the splenic and superior mesenteric veins, just posterior to the pancreatic neck, as shown below. It supplies 75% of all blood flow to the liver. The inferior vena cava lies posterior to its origin.\\n\\nPortal Splenic vein vein\\n\\nSplenic artery Gastroduodenal artery\\n\\nInferior pancreaticduodenal artery\\n\\nSuperior mesenteric artery & vein teachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Pancreatic tail\",\"e\":\"The pancreatic neck is anterior to the hepatic portal vein.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"A 65-year-old man is admitted with a leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm. During open repair your consultant points to the epiploic foramen and asks what forms its inferior border. What would be the appropriate response?\",\"lead\":\"What would be the appropriate response?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aorta\",\"e\":\"The aorta is not a boundary of the epiploic foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Caudate lobe of the liver\",\"e\":\"The caudate lobe is the superior border of the epiploic foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"First part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The first part of the duodenum is the inferior border of the epiploic foremen. The epiploic foramen of Winslow lies behind the free edge of the lesser omentum. The posterior wall of the epiploic foramen is formed by the peritoneum of the posterior abdominal wall, which passes as a smooth layer from the hepatorenal pouch to the duodenum. Behind this peritoneum lies the inferior vena cava. The superior border of the epiploic foramen is formed by the inferior surface of the liver (caudate lobe). The first part of the duodenum forms part of the inferior border. The portal vein lies anteriorly in the free edge of the opening.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Portal vein V\",\"e\":\"The portal vein lies anteriorly in the free edge of the opening.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Second part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The first part, rather than the second part of the duodenum is the inferior border of the epiploic foramen\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"A patient has been diagnosed with a hepatocellular carcinoma. During hemihepatectomy your consultant points to an H-shaped arrangement of structures on the visceral surface between the limbs of which lie the caudate and quadrate lobes. Which structures form this H shape?\",\"lead\":\"Which structures form this H shape?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aorta, inferior vena cava (IVC), ligamentum teres\",\"e\":\"The aorta is not part of the H. The IVC and ligamentum teres along with the gall-bladder and porta hepatis divide the visceral surface of the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Aorta, gall-bladder, IVC, ligamentum teres, porta hepatis\",\"e\":\"All apart from the aorta form the H shape that divides the visceral surface of the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Aorta, gall-bladder. IVC, ligamentum teres\",\"e\":\"The aorta is not part of the H shape.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Gall-bladder. IVC, ligamentum teres, ligamentum venosum, porta hepatis\",\"e\":\"These five structures form the H shape that divides the visceral surface of the liver. The gall bladder can be seen to the right of the image, the IVC superiorly and the ligamentum teres towards the lower left\\n\\nInferior Surface Left Right\\n\\nCaudate Quadrate\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"IVC. ligamentum teres. porta hepatis\",\"e\":\"Although these form the H shape, it is also formed by the gall-bladder and so this option is not the single best answer.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":46,\"stem\":\"A 12-year-old boy undergoes diagnostic laparoscopy for suspected right iliac fossa pain. The appendix looks normal at operation but you notice prominent lymph nodes in the small bowel mesentery. How would you describe the mesentery of the small bowel?\",\"lead\":\"How would you describe the mesentery of the small bowel?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Contains arteries that supply the large bowel\",\"e\":\"The small bowel mesentery contains the superior mesenteric artery supplies the mid-gut, which includes the large bowel as far as the distal transverse colon. The veins in the mesentery are all tributaries of the portal system. The root of the small bowel mesentery extends from a point to the left of the L2 vertebra, at the duodenojejunal junction, down to the right sacroiliac joint. The nerves are postganglionic sympathetic and preganglionic parasympathetic fibres (vagus nerve) from the superior mesenteric plexus. The superior mesenteric artery supplies the mid-gut, which includes the large bowel as far as the distal transverse colon. The transverse mesocolon lies anterior to the small bowel mesentery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Contains autonomic fibres of the phrenic nerve\",\"e\":\"The nerves are postganglionic sympathetic and preganglionic parasympathetic fibres (vagus nerve) from the superior mesenteric plexus rather than the phrenic nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Contains veins that drain directly into the inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The mesenteric veins drain into the portal system rather than directly into the vena cava.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Has a root overlying the left sacroiliac joint\",\"e\":\"The root overlies the right sacroiliac joint.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Overlies the transverse mesocolon\",\"e\":\"The transverse mesocolon is anterior to the small bowel mesenterv.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"A patient attends hospital with severe left sided testicular pain. They are taken to theatre for exploration of a scrotum where a torted testicle is found Which one of the following options is correct regarding the testis?\",\"lead\":\"They are taken to theatre for exploration of a scrotum where a torted testicle is found Which one of the following options is correct regarding the testis?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is covered by the thick fibrous tunica vaginalis\",\"e\":\"The thick fibrous covering is the tunica albuginea\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It usually descends after birth\",\"e\":\"They are normally descended by birth.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sympathetic nerve fibres reach the organ via the splanchnic nerves\",\"e\":\"The nerve supply is via the lesser splanchnic nerve and the coeliac ganglion with postganglionic fibres accompanying the testicular arteries. The testis is usually descended by birth having developed from the gonadal ridge on the posterior abdominal wall. Its thick. whitish covering is the tunica albuginea. Lymphatic drainage follows the arterial supply to Lhe para-aortic nodes. The spermalic cord conleins the pampiniform plexus - a single testicular vein is formed more distally over psoas major. The nerve supply is via the lesser splanchnic nerve and the coeliac ganglion with postganglionic fibres accompanying the testicular arteres.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The testicular lymph drains into the internal iliac nodes\",\"e\":\"Testicular lymph drains into the para-aortic nodes as it follows the arterial supply.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The testicular vein runs alongside the artery in the spermatic cord\",\"e\":\"The spermatic cord contains the pampiniform plexus - a single testicular vein is formed more distally over the psoas major.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A patient is involved in a severe RTA. CT imaging shows a haemoperitoneum secondary to a ruptured spleen. Which one of the following options is the most accurate regarding the spleen?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is the most accurate regarding the spleen?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"Which one of the following options is the most accurate regarding the spleen?\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is a retroperitoneal structure\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"May be absent and replaced with multiple splenunculi\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Will be injured by a stab wound over the left sixth rib\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The splenic artery passes to the splenic hilum in the gastrosplenic ligament\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Venous drainage from the spleen passes directly to the inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"Rate this question: ©\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old woman undergoes a right nephrectomy for a staghorn calculus. You are the surgeon performing the procedure.\\n\\nFrom an anatomical point of view, what are you likely to note?\",\"lead\":\"From an anatomical point of view, what are you likely to note?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Parietal pleura does not extend below the 12th rib\",\"e\":\"The lower border of the parietal pleura crosses the12th rib, the lateral border of the erector spinae and passes in horizontally to the lower border of the 12th thoracic vertebra. There is therefore a triangle of pleura in the costovertebral angle below the medial part of the 12th rib, behind the upper pole of the kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The adrenal glands are at easy risk of injury\",\"e\":\"The adrenal glands are anatomically well protected. They lie anterosuperior to the upper part of each kidney. within their own compartment of the renal fascia.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The perinephric fat surrounds the renal fascia of Gerota\",\"e\":\"The fascia of Gerota is superficial to the perinephric fat.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The renal artery lies anterior to the renal vein\",\"e\":\"The renal artery lies posterior to the renal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The subcostal neurovascular bundle is relatively protected from risk of injury\",\"e\":\"The subcostal neurovascular bundle is relatively protected from injury by lying in the costal groove, seen lying inferiorly on the internal aspect of the rib Articular Facet Articulates with the vertebrae above\\n\\nArticular Facet Articulates with numerically corresponding vertebrae\\n\\nTubercle Articulates with numerically corresponding vertebrac\\n\\nCostal Groove For intercostal vein, artery and nerve\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man with a history of alcohol abuse presents with torrential upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage. He is resuscitated, undergoes emergency desophagogastroduodenoscopy, banding of pesophageal varices and is transferred to the high dependency unit.\\n\\nWhich anatomical structure lies anterior to the origin of the hepatic portal vein?\",\"lead\":\"Which anatomical structure lies anterior to the origin of the hepatic portal vein?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Body of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"The origin of the hepatic portal vein is posterior to the neck of the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Fourth part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The fourth part of the duodenum is not anterior to the origin of the portal vein, which is formed by the confluence of the splenic vein and superior mesenteric vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Gastric pylorus\",\"e\":\"The gastric pylorus is not anterior to the origin of the hepatic portal vein\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Head of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"Is the neck of the pancreas, rather than the head that is anterior to the origin of the portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Neck of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"The neck of the pancreas is anterior to the origin of the hepatic portal vein. The hepatic portal vein is formed of the confluence of the splenic and superior mesenteric veins, just posterior to the neck of the pancreas. Portal hypertension arises secondary to hepatic pathology, and causes portosystemic venous shunts. This leads to the development of oesophageal varices, which can rupture and cause torrential, often fatal, haemorrhage.\\n\\nPortal Splenic vein vein\\n\\nSplenic artery Gastroduodenal artery\\n\\nInferior pancreaticduodenal artery\\n\\nSuperior mesenteric artery & vein teachmeanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-olc man presents with hypovolaemic shock following a penetrating abdominal trauma. He undergoes emergency laparotomy and is noted to have a liver laceration. The surgeon clamps the hepatoduodenal ligament just anterior to the epiploic foramen.\\n\\nWhat structure lies posterior to the epiploic foramen?\",\"lead\":\"What structure lies posterior to the epiploic foramen?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Abdominal aorta\",\"e\":\"The abdominal aorta is not posterior to the epiploic foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Common bile duct\",\"e\":\"The common bile duct lies in the hepatoduodenal ligament with the hepatic artery and portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"First part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The first part of the duodenum is part of the inferior border of the epiploic foramen.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The inferior vena cava is the posterior boundary of the epiploic foramen. Pringle's manoeuvre (clamping or grasping of the hepatoduodenal ligament) is performed to achieve rapid haemostasis in cases of severe hepatic trauma. The ligament contains the common bile duct, the hepatic portal vein and the hepatic artery proper. It lies anterior to the epiploic foramen which itself lies anterior to the inferior vena cava.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Left lobe of the liver\",\"e\":\"The left lobe of the liver is superior to the epiploic foramen.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":60,\"stem\":\"A 78-year-old woman presents with obstructive jaundice, weight loss and general malaise. A CT scan of the abdomen reveals a mass in the head of the pancreas, involving the common bile duct. The fluids produced by the pancreas drain into which part of the gastrointestinal tract?\",\"lead\":\"The fluids produced by the pancreas drain into which part of the gastrointestinal tract?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"First part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The maior duodenal papilla enters in the second part rather than the first part of the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"First part of the jejunum\",\"e\":\"The pancreatic duct joins the common bile duct and enters the gastrointestinal tract through the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Fourth part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The pancreatic duct does not join with the bile duct at the fourth part of the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Second part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The joining of the pancreatic duct and the bile duct forms the ampulla of Vater, which enters the second part of the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla. The hepatopancreatic ampulla enters the second part of the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla. It is through this papilla that endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography can enable the relief of obstructive jaundice via placement of a biliary stent\\n\\nBile duct Major duodenal papilla (controlled by the sphincter of Dadi) Pancreatic duct\\n\\nDuodenum feachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Third part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"It is the second part, rather than the third part, of the duodenum where the pancreatic duct and bile duct join the gastrointestinal tract.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"A 40-year-old man is involved in a road traffic accident. He complains of having haematuria and clinically is tachycardic and hypotensive. A computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen reveals a ruptured left kidney with retroperitoneal haemorrhage. He requires an emergency nephrectomy. You call the consultant who asks you about the anatomical relations and approach for nephrectomy.\\n\\nHow would you respond?\",\"lead\":\"How would you respond?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is best approached through the 12th rib and simple nephrectomy is indicated in cases of non-functioning kidneys\",\"e\":\"The best approach for a ruptured kidney is through the anterior abdominal wall by a midline incision.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The adrenal gland lies under the medial border below the hilum and radical nephrectomy is indicated in cases of non-functioning kidneys\",\"e\":\"The adrenal gland lies over the medial border and above the hilum and simple nephrectomy is indicated in cases of non-functioning kidneys.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The adrenal gland lies over the medial border above the hilum and simple nephrectomy is indicated in cases of non-functioning kidneys\",\"e\":\"A simple nephrectomy is indicated in cases of a non-functioning kidney. The adrenal gland lies over the medial border and superior to the hilum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The lower pole is separated from the peritoneum by the tail of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"The hilum of the kidney is separated from the tail of the pancreas by the peritoneum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The renal artery which lies in front of the renal vein should be ligated\",\"e\":\"The renal artery lies posterior to the renal vein.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"A 36-year-old woman presents with visible haematuria, uraemia and a palpable right kidney. An ultrasound of kidneys, ureters, bladder (KUB) confirms the presence of multiple renal cysts and she undergoes a laparoscopic right nephrectomy. What structure lies immediately anterior to the right renal artery as it enters the renal hilum?\",\"lead\":\"What structure lies immediately anterior to the right renal artery as it enters the renal hilum?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ascending colon\",\"e\":\"The right colic flexure lies anteriorly to the right kidney, but not immediately anterior to the right renal artery. The ascending colon as a whole lies antero-inferiorly to the right kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Duodenum\",\"e\":\"The duodenum is an anterior relation of the right kidney but, again, does not lie immediately anterior to the right renal artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Pancreas\",\"e\":\"The pancreas lies anteriorly to the left renal vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right renal vein\",\"e\":\"The right renal vein lies immediately anterior to the right renal artery Cit can be seen in grey anterior to the right renal artery below) and, therefore, is the most anterior structure in the renal hilum. The renal vein lies immediately anterior to the renal artery as it enters the hilum The ureter is posterior in relation to the artery and is the posterior-most structure which enters the right renal hilum. So the renal hilum, from anterior te posterior, includes the renal vein, renal artery and ureter\\n\\nphrenic artery Coeliac trunk - Suparior mesenteric artery arterles Abdominal aorta Terticular artery Inferior mesenteric artery\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right ureter\",\"e\":\"The right ureter is the most posterior structure at the hilum of the right kidney and is a posterior relation of the right renal artery.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":68,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old woman with a history of alcohol abuse presents with hamatemesis, jaundice and increasing abdominal distension. After the initial period of resuscitation she is moved to a 'Gastro' ward. Clinical examination and blood tests confirm a diagnosis of portal hypertension.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct about the portal vein?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct about the portal vein?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Formed by the confluence of the splenic vein and inferior mesenteric vein\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is classically formed by the confluence of the splenic vein and superior mesenteric vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The portal vein provides 75% of the oxygen supply to the liver\",\"e\":\"The portal vein provides 75% of blood supply to the liver, but only 50% of its oxygen supply.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The portal vein divides into three main branches\",\"e\":\"Normally. (in non-variant anatomy cases) the portal vein divides into two main branches:\\n\\n• left portal vein • right portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Segments IV and V of the liver are supplied by the left portal vein\",\"e\":\"The left portal vein supplies segments I!, Ill and IV of the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The portal vein is located posteriorly to the neck of the pancreas at the level of L1\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is indeed located posteriorly to the neck of the pancreas at the level of L1. also known as the transpyloric plane. The hepatic portal vein is formed of the confluence of the splenic and superior mesenteric veins, just posterior to the pancreatic neck at the level of L1. There is some anatomical variance, specifically that the inferior mesenteric vein may also confluence to form the portal vein. It divides into two main branches, the left and right portal veins. The right portal vein goes on to divide into the anterior and posterior portal veins supplying segments V, VIII and VI, VII respectively.\\n\\nHepatic portal Splenic vein vein\\n\\nInferior Superior mesenteric mesenteric. vein vein\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":71,\"stem\":\"A 67-year-old man presents with a 3-week history of painless jaundice, weight loss and general malaise. He undergoes a CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis that confirms the presence of a mass in the head of the pancreas. An urgent staging CT scan is requested.\\n\\nWhich portion of the pancreas lies between the abdominal aorta and the superior mesenteric artery?\",\"lead\":\"Which portion of the pancreas lies between the abdominal aorta and the superior mesenteric artery?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Body\",\"e\":\"The body of the pancreas lies lateral to the abdominal aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Head\",\"e\":\"The head of the pancreas lies in the duodenal C loop in front of the inferior vena cava (IVC) and the right renal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Neck\",\"e\":\"The neck of the pancreas lies in front of the junction of the superior mesenteric vein, splenic vein and portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tail\",\"e\":\"The tail of the pancreas is the most lateral part of the organ and lies medially to the spleen. without being in contact with the abdominal aorta.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Uncinate process\",\"e\":\"The uncinete process is an extension of the inferior half of the head toward the left; it is of varying size and is wedged between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta - seen as the most inferior part of the pancreas below. The uncinate process is 4 curved extension of the pancreatic head, and is bound by the superior mesenteric wessels anteriorly and the abdominal aorta posteriorly. The superior mesenteric-artery passes posterior to the pencreatic neck as it- arises from the abdominal aorta.\\n\\nPortal Splenic vein vein x 4 ia Splenic artery Bs . 2! . fi Ly Gastroduodenal \\\"Saar. - jn \\\\ artery aya Ins OY Ee y i er, 4 aad wey By Inferior r igo 3 Wh pancreaticduodenal ff \\\\. artery eT ‘EN NN Bt NS 4 1 AY\\n\\nSuperior mesenteric artery & vein © feachmeanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"A 72-year-old man presents with a 2-week history of visible haematuria. Blood tests confirm a polycythaemia and the urine cytology is positive for malignant cells. Imaging confirms a left-sided renal cell carcinoma and he undergoes a laparoscopic left nephrectomy. Which muscle lies posterior to the left kidney?\",\"lead\":\"Which muscle lies posterior to the left kidney?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Erector spinae\",\"e\":\"The erector spinae muscles are a group of muscles of the back. It is made up of three muscles, from medial to lateral, the spinalis, longissimus and iliocostalis. These muscles lie posteromedial to the kidneys, not immediately posterior.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"liacus\",\"e\":\"The iliacus muscle, as the hame would suggest, originates in the iliac fossa. Along with the psoas muscle it makes up the iliopsoas muscle, which acts synergistically to produce hip flexion and lateral rotation of the thigh. The iliacus muscle is not closely related anatomically to the kidneys.\\n\\nThe iliacus muscle, as the name would suggest, originates in the iliac fossa. Along with the psoas muscle it makes up the iliopsoas muscle, which acts synergistically to produce hip flexion and lateral rotation of the thigh. The iliacus muscle is not closely related anatomically to the kidneys.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Internal oblique\",\"e\":\"The internal oblique muscle has no direct relation to the kidney. It is an anterior abdominal wall muscle which lies deep to the external oblique muscle and superficial to the transversus abdominus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Psoas major\",\"e\":\"The psoas major muscle is one of the posterior abdominal wall muscles that lies posterior to the kidneys. The psoas major lies immediately posterior to the kidneys bilaterally and arises from the transverse processes, lateral aspects of vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs of T12 to L5. Internal oblique and transverses abdominis form part of the anterior abdominal wall, while the erector spinae group support the trunk.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Latissimus dorsi\",\"e\":\"Is the wide muscle of the back responsible for movement at the shoulder joint. It has no anatomical relationship to the left kidney.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":77,\"stem\":\"While performing an exploratory laparotomy for small bowel obstruction you identify a polypoid mass within the small bowel. You decide to resect the lesion and perform a primary\\n\\nWhich one of the following anatomical features would enable you to best distinguish between the jejunum and ileum?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following anatomical features would enable you to best distinguish between the jejunum and ileum?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The jejunum has more arcades (arterial loops) compared with the ileum\",\"e\":\"The ileum has more arcades than the jejunum.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The jejunum has a narrower lumen\",\"e\":\"The ileum has a narrower lumen when compared with the jejunum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The jejunum has a thicker wall\",\"e\":\"The jejunum has a thicker intestinal wall compared with the ileum. The jejunum makes up the proximal two-fifths of the small intestine while the ileum makes up the distal three-fifths. There is no macroscopic demarcation between the two, but macroscopic features help differentiate the two. The jejunum has a wider lumen, thicker wall and valvulae conniventes. The jejunal mesenteric vessels form one or two arcades with long and infrequent branches (vasa recta) stretching to the intestinal wall, in comparison with the ileum which has shorter vasa recta (see image).\\n\\nlejunal end of small Intestine Longer vasa recta\\n\\nlical end of small intestine\\n\\nShorter vasa recta\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The ileum has longer vasa recta compared with the jejunum\",\"e\":\"The ileum has shorter vasa recta compared to the jejunum\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"There is an absence of valvulae conniventes in the jejunum\",\"e\":\"Valvulae conniventes are mucosal folds of the small intestine which start at the second part of the duodenum. They are present in the jejunum, where they are large and thick, decrease in size in the distal ileum.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":80,\"stem\":\"A 34-year-old man has been blue-lighted to Accident and Emergency resuscitation unit following a stab wound to the right upper quadrant of the abdomen. He is persistently hypotensive, despite fluid resuscitation and is rushed to theatre for an emergency laparotomy. Profuse hepatic bleeding is encountered and your consultant performs a successful Pringle's manoeuvre. Which of the following is correct concerning the lesser sac and its related anatomy?\",\"lead\":\"Which of the following is correct concerning the lesser sac and its related anatomy?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Its left lateral border is formed by the falciform ligament\",\"e\":\"The lateral border of the lesser sac is formed by the gastrosplenic ligament.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"One of the anterior relations of the lesser sac is the visceral peritoneum along the posterior aspect of the stomach\",\"e\":\"The anterior relations of the lesser sac are:\\n\\n• visceral peritoneum along the posterior stomach • lesser omentum • gastrocolic omentum.\\n\\nThe lesser sac is a potential peritoneal space within the peritoneal cavity, seen in the images below as the space behind the stomach - it is shown in both axial and sagittal views. It may be entered by passing a finger into the epiploic foramen, immediately behind the portal vein in the free edge of the lesser omentum. The transverse mesocolon and visceral/parietal peritoneum that covers the diaphragm, pancreas, left kidney and duodenum form the posterior wall. The gastrosplenic ligament forms its lateral border. The falciform ligament arises from the anterior abdominal wall and demarcates the left and the right lobe of the liver. Pringle's manoeuvre involves clamping the (proper) hepatic artery and portal vein in the free edge of the lesser omentum, interrupting the arterial and portal venous inflow to the liver and is often used when uncontrollable bleeding is encountered following trauma in an emergency surgery setting.\\n\\n-\\n\\nStomach Gastrosplenic ligament\\n\\nPeritoneum\\n\\nSpleen\\n\\nLeft Splenorenal kidney ligament TeachMeAnatomy\\n\\nBare area - of the liver Subphrenic recess 2R22 Stomach\\n\\nSubhepatic recess\\n\\n• Transverse colon\\n\\nC) TeachMeSeries\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Its posterior relations include the spleen.\",\"e\":\"The spleen is located in the left upper quadrant, hence is not a posterior relation of the lesser sac.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The lesser omentum is a posterior relation\",\"e\":\"The lesser omentum is anterior to the lesser sac.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The hepatic flexure forms part of the posterior boundary of the lesser sac\",\"e\":\"The transverse mesocolon is the only part of the colon that contributes to the posterior border of the lesser sac.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":84,\"stem\":\"You are performing a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. You are concerned that there may be variant anatomy present but after careful dissection you obtain the critical view and are satisfied you have delineated Calot's triangle.\\n\\nIn locating the common bile duct, which of the following is true?\",\"lead\":\"In locating the common bile duct, which of the following is true?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It crosses anteriorly to the second part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The common bile duct drains into the second part of the duodenum through the major duodenal papilla, it does not pass anteriorly to it.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The common hepatic duct and pancreatic duct combine to form the common bile duct\",\"e\":\"The common bile duct is formed by the union of the cystic duct and common hepatic duct.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies to the left of the hepatic artery in the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The common bile duct lies to the right of the hepatic artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It passes anteriorly to the right renal vein\",\"e\":\"The biliary drainage system is shown below broken down section by section. The distal part of the common bile duct lies in front of the right renal vein. The common bile duct lies to the right of the hepatic artery. Joining the pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater, which opens into the second part of the duodenum. It crosses a groove between the head of the pancreas and the second part of the duodenum, posteriorly to the second part of the duodenum, in front of the right renal vein.\\n\\nCystic Common duct hepatic duct\\n\\nCommon bile duct\\n\\nHepatopancreatic Pancreatic ampulla of Water duct\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It usually opens into the duodenum separately from the pancreatic duct\",\"e\":\"The common bile duct joins the pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater which drains into the second part of the duodenum through the major duodenal papilla.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":87,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient with an abdominal stab wound, which you suspect has lacerated the hepatic portal vein. Which one of the following facts would allow you to locate the hepatic portal vein?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following facts would allow you to locate the hepatic portal vein?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It forms anterior to the neck of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"The portal vein originates posterior to the neck of the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It lies anterior to the first part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The portal vein lies posterior in relation to the first part of the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies posterior to the hepatic artery proper\",\"e\":\"The portal vein lies behind the hepatic artery proper and bile duct. The proper hepatic artery (to the left) and the common bile duct (in white to the right) can be seen in the image below; the portal vein lies behind them. The portal vein typically measures 8 cm in length and is formed by the convergence of the superior mesenteric vein and the splenic vein. It lies in front of the inferior vena cava, and lies behind the pancreas and the first part of the duodenum Common hepatic artery Hepatie artery Splenic artery proper\\n\\nCystic .ef artery astric arter\\n\\nLeft gastro-omental Right gastro-omental artery artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It lies posterior to the inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The portal vein lies in front of the inferior vena cava.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It runs posterior to the epiploic foramen\",\"e\":\"The portal vein forms the anterior boundary of the epiploic foramen, lying behind the bile duct and hepatic artery proper.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":90,\"stem\":\"A patient is found to a have a phaeochromocytoma due to a right sided adrenal adenoma. You are excising the right suprarenal (adrenal) gland and your consultant asks you about its anatomy Which one of the following descriptions is correct?\",\"lead\":\"You are excising the right suprarenal (adrenal) gland and your consultant asks you about its anatomy Which one of the following descriptions is correct?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is crescentic in shape\",\"e\":\"The right suprarenal gland is pyramidal in shape while the left suprarenal gland is semi-lunar or crescentic shape.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It has a longer vein than the left suprarenal gland\",\"e\":\"The left suprarenal vein is longer than the right.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies anterior to the inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The anterior surface of the right suprarenal gland is overlapped medially by the inferior vena cava.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It receives an arterial supply from the subcostal artery\",\"e\":\"The subcostal artery is not involved in the arter al supply of the right suprarenal gland.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It touches the bare area of the liver\",\"e\":\"The right lobe of the liver is an anterior relation of the right (pyramidal) suprarenal gland - the image below shows the abdomen seen from the posterior aspect, and the right adrenal gland can be seen against the liver. The suprarenal glands lie in the posterior abdomen, lying between the upper pole of the kidneys and the diaphragm. The suprarenal glands have a rich blood supply, which is supplied via three arteries:\\n\\n• superior adrenal artery - arises from the inferior phrenic artery • middle adrenal artery - arises from the abdominal aorta • inferior adrenal artery - arises from the renal arteries\\n\\nThe right and left adrenal veins drain the glands. The right adrenal vein drains into the inferior vena cava, whereas the left adrenal vein drains into the left renal vein.\\n\\nLiver Spleen\\n\\n_ Right adrenal Left adrenal gland gland\\n\\nRight kidney Left ureter\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":93,\"stem\":\"While performing a cholecystectomy your consultant points out Calot's triangle and asks about its boundaries. Which one of the following options is the correct description?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following options is the correct description?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It lies between the right and left hepatic arteries and inferior border of the liver\",\"e\":\"The right and left hepatic arteries are not part of Calot's triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It lies between the left hepatic duct, left hepatic artery and inferior border of the liver\",\"e\":\"It is the common hepatic duct, cystic duct and inferior border of the liver that make up the boundaries.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It lies between the common hepatic duct, common hepatic artery and inferior border of the liver\",\"e\":\"The common hepatic artery is not part of Calot's triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It lies between the lert hepatic artery, right hepatic artery and interior border of the liver\",\"e\":\"The left and right hepatic arteries are not part of Calot's triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It lies between the cystic duct, common hepatic duct and inferior border of the liver\",\"e\":\"The three borders of Calot's triangle are cystic duct, common hepatic duct and inferior border of the liver. Calot's triangle lies between the inferior border of the liver, cystic duct and common hepatic duct and usually contains the cystic artery. The cystic artery must be divided in cholecystectomy, which is why the triangle is very important to identify.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":95,\"stem\":\"You are treating a patient with ischaemic bowel due to an inferior mesenteric artery thrombosis. Which one of the following regions of the bowel will be affected?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following regions of the bowel will be affected?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ascending colon\",\"e\":\"The ascending colon is supplied by the right colic artery which is a branch of the superior mesenteric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ileum\",\"e\":\"The ileum is supplied via the superior mesenteric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Jejunum\",\"e\":\"The jejunum is part of the mid-gut and is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Descending colon\",\"e\":\"The descending colon is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery (shown in green in the image below), and would be affected by a thrombosis. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the left third of transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon and rectum to the dentate line. The remainder of the sections of bowel mentioned are supplied by the superior mesenteric artery (shown in yellow).\\n\\nlarginal rtery Middle colic artery Right colic artery Left colle artery lleocolic artery\\n\\nigmoit arterie\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Third part of duodenum\",\"e\":\"The third part of the duodenum is the first part of the bowel that is formed from the mid-gut and is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":98,\"stem\":\"Your consultant shows you the porta hepatis and asks you what the most posterior structure passing through it is.\\n\\nWhat would you respond?\",\"lead\":\"What would you respond?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Hepatic artery proper\",\"e\":\"The hepatic artery proper runs in the porta hepatis and is anterior to the portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Left hepatic duct\",\"e\":\"It is the common hepatic duct that runs in the porta hepatis and it is anterior and right to the portal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Portal vein\",\"e\":\"The porta vein is the most posterior structure in the porta hepatis. Within the porta hepatis, the common hepatic duct and proper hepatic artery typically are located anterior to the portal vein, with the common hepatic duct to the right and the proper hepatic artery to the left.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right hepatic artery\",\"e\":\"It is the proper hepatic artery rather than the right hepatic artery that runs in the porta hepatis.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Right hepatic duct\",\"e\":\"The common hepatic duct runs in the porta hepatis rather than the right hepatic duct.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":100,\"stem\":\"In hepatobiliary surgery which of the following is correct?\",\"lead\":\"In hepatobiliary surgery which of the following is correct?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The common bile duct runs anterior to the portal vein in the free edge of the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The common bile duct runs anterior to the portal vein. The common bile duct contains no muscle. Calot's triangle is bounded by the cystic duct, common hepatic duct and inferior edge of the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The hepatic artery runs to the right of the common bile duct\",\"e\":\"The hepatic artery runs to the left of the common bile duct.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The common bile duct contains non-striated muscle in its wall\",\"e\":\"It contains no muscle\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Calot's triangle is bordered by the cystic duct, common hepatic duct and superior edge of the liver\",\"e\":\"It is bordered by the cystic duct, the common hepatic duct and the inferior edge of the liver.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The cystic artery usually arises from the left hepatic artery\",\"e\":\"The cystic artery normally arises from the right hepatic artery.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":102,\"stem\":\"A 75-year-old man undergoes an elective open abdominal aortic aneurysm repair. The aneurysm is infra-renal. To expose the aorta and the aneurysm, the vascular surgeon needs to mobilise the third part of the duodenum. Which one of the following is the anterior relation of the third part of the duodenum?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is the anterior relation of the third part of the duodenum?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Coeliac artery\",\"e\":\"The coeliac artery is at T12 in adults whereas the third part of the duodenum is at L3.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Head of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"The third part of the duodenum is inferior to the head of the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Right psoas muscle\",\"e\":\"The right psoas muscle is posterior to the third part of the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Right renal artery\",\"e\":\"The right renal artery is posterior to the second part of the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior mesenteric vessels\",\"e\":\"The third part of the duodenum (in red in the image) lies in the subcostal plane (L3) and follows the inferior margin of the pancreatic head. The third part of the duodenum lies posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels, which can be seen just overlying it above in the image. It overlies the aorta and the beginning of the inferior mesenteric artery. The root of the small bowel mesentery is attached near its termination on the left. The second part of the duodenum overlies the right ureter and renal vessels in the hilum of the right kidney. separated from the renal artery by the vein. The transpyloric plane is a guide to the surface marking of the duodenum.\\n\\nPylorus Pancreas Parts of the Duodenum I Superior Descending Inferior Ascending\\n\\nvodenojajuna unctior\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":105,\"stem\":\"During a laparoscopic low anterior resection for a mid-rectal neoplasm, the splenic flexure may have to be mobilised to ensure a tension-free anastomosis. If this step is not performed an anastomotic leak becomes more likely.\\n\\nDuring dissection, which one of the following is the anatomical structure most closely related to the body of the pancreas?\",\"lead\":\"During dissection, which one of the following is the anatomical structure most closely related to the body of the pancreas?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The greater curve of the stomach.\",\"e\":\"The body of the pancreas is not related to the greater curve of the stomach\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The superior mesenteric vein\",\"e\":\"The uncinate process lies posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The right kidney\",\"e\":\"The body of the pancreas overlies the left renal vessels.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The transverse mesocolon\",\"e\":\"The transverse mesocolon is attached to the head, neck and body of the pancreas. The head of the pancreas overlies the right renal vessels (but not the right kidney itself). The transpyloric plane (L1) transects the pancreas obliquely, passing through the midpoint of the neck, with most of the head below the plane, and most of the body and tail above. The uncinate process lies posterior to the superior mesenteric vesseis, and the inferior mesenteric vein passes behind the body of the pancreas, where it joins the splenic vein. Entering the lesser sac between the transverse mesocolon and body of pancreas is a common method for laparoscopic mobilisation of the splenic flexure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lies in the transpyloric plane\",\"e\":\"The neck of the pancreas, rather than the body, is in the transpyloric plane. 231\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":107,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old man with familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) undergoes a Whipple's procedure for a malignant duodenal polyp. During the dissection, the superior mesenteric artery is identified What is the closest anatomical relation to its origin?\",\"lead\":\"During the dissection, the superior mesenteric artery is identified What is the closest anatomical relation to its origin?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Neck of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"The origin of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) lies behind the neck of the pancreas (at the L1 level) - it can be seen inferiorly in the image. The SMA supplies the entire smal! intestine except the proximal duodenum. The SMA also supplies the caecum, ascending colon and half the transverse colon. The origin of the SMA lies behind the neck of the pancreas (at the L1 level). The SMA passes forwards and downwards in front of the uncinate process and the third part of the duodenum. Throughout, the superior mesenteric vein lies to its right. The splenic vein grooves the posterosuperior aspect of the pancreas and passes above the SMA.\\n\\nPortal Splenic vein vein\\n\\nSplenic artery Gastroduodonal artery\\n\\nInferior pancreaticduoden al artery\\n\\nSuperior mesenteric artery & voin © #cimeanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The hepatic flexure of the colon\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric artery is more closely related to the neck of the pancreas than the hepatic flexure of the colon.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The splenic vein as it passes below\",\"e\":\"The splenic vein is superior to the superior mesenteric artery\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Tail of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"The superior mesenteric artery originates behind the neck of the pancreas, rather than the body.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The third part of the duodenum\",\"e\":\"The third part of the duodenum is at L3, whereas the superior mesenteric artery arises at L1.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":110,\"stem\":\"A patient is undergoing a low anterior resection for a rectal cancer: To avoid excess tension on the anastomosis, the splenic flexure may need to be mobilised. As part of the procedure for mobilising the splenic flexure of the colon, the lesser sac needs to be entered. What is the most accurate anatomical description of the lesser sac?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most accurate anatomical description of the lesser sac?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Its left lateral border is formed by the falciform ligament\",\"e\":\"The falciform ligament arises from the anterior abdominal wall and demarcates the left and the right lobe of the liver. The left lateral border of the lesser sac is the gastro-splenic and lieno-renal ligaments.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Its posterior relations include the spleen\",\"e\":\"The posterior relations are the left kidney, upper surface of pancreas and the greater omentum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Its superior relation is the posterior aspect of the stomach\",\"e\":\"The superior boundary is the liver rather than the stomach\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It communicates with the general peritoneal cavity via the epiploic foramen\",\"e\":\"It may be entered by passing a finger into the epiploic foramen. The lesser sac is a peritoneal cavity located behind the stomach, seen in axial and sagittal views below. It may be entered by passing a finger into the epiploic foramen, immediately behind the portal vein in the free edge of the lesser omentum. The greater omentum and the pancreas form its posterior wall. The gastrosplenic and lienorenal ligaments form its left lateral border. The falciform ligament arises from the anterior abdominal wall and demarcates the left and the right lobe of the liver.\\n\\nStomach Gastrosplenic ligament\\n\\nPeritoneum\\n\\nSpleen\\n\\nLeft Splenorenal kidney ligament , TeachMeAnatomy\\n\\n- Bare area T of the liver Subphrenic recess\\n\\nStomach\\n\\nSubhepatic recess\\n\\nTeachMeseries\\n\\nLower entainm Fatefore ligament ry firer\\n\\nGastrolicant Topsire enter Грани - portal tim\\n\\nInferior sona can\\n\\nPArmiculienal ligament orla\\n\\nImage ref: Henry Vandyke Carter [Public domain]\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The posterior wall is formed partly by the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The greater omentum, rather than the lesser omentum, forms the posterior wall.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":114,\"stem\":\"A 64-year-old man, who is a smoker presents with haematuria and straining at micturition. He is diagnosed with a malignant ureteric neoplasia and treated accordingly.\\n\\nWhich type of epithelium lines the ureter?\",\"lead\":\"Which type of epithelium lines the ureter?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"15. Pseudostratified\",\"e\":\"Pseudostratified epithelium can be found in the trachea, endometrium, vas deferens, and epididymis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Simple columnar\",\"e\":\"Simple columnar epithelium lines most of the digestive tract.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Simple sqaumous\",\"e\":\"Simple squamous epithelium is found in the capillaries, alveoli and glomeruli.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Stratified squamous\",\"e\":\"Stratified squamous epithelium is not found in the ureter. Instead it is found on the skin if its keratinised, or in the oral cavity, oesophagus, and anal canal if non-keratinised.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transitional\",\"e\":\"The ureter is lined by transitional epithelium. The ureter is lined by transitional epithelium. consisting of multiple layers of epithelial cells that contract and expand\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":116,\"stem\":\"During a rotation on Paediatric Surgery, a surgical trainee encounters a 4-year-old girl who presents to the Emergency Department with abdominal pain and vomiting. She is eventually found to have an annular pancreas.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is correct regarding an annular pancreas?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is correct regarding an annular pancreas?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"An annular pancreas develops due to embryological failure of the ventral bud to: rotate with the jejunum, resulting in encasement of the jejunum\",\"e\":\"An annular pancreas develops due to failure of the ventral bud to rotate with the duodenum, resulting in its encasement.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The duodenum typically expands in the 5th week of gestation resulting in rotation of the ventral bud which fuses with the dorsal bud\",\"e\":\"This process typically happens in the 7th week of gestation.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Characteristic computed tomography (CT) scan findings include: complete or incomplete encasing of the 2nd part of the duodenum by the pancreas, duodenal narrowing and dilatation of the proximal duodenum\",\"e\":\"Annular pancreas can either partially or completely encompass the second part of the duodenum resulting in stenosis and proximal dilatation of the proximal small bowel. Clinically, this may result in abdominal pain and vomiting. Annular pancreas is an embryological anomaly that can result in duodenal obstruction. It can be complete or incompete. It affects both children and adults causing post-prandial satiety, abdominal pain and vomiting and is diagnosed using a CT scan or MRI.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Annular pancreas is associated with Li Fraumeni's syndrome\",\"e\":\"Down's syndrome, pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer are associated with having an annular pancreas: Li Fraument's syndrome, conversely, has no association with an annular pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Clinical presentation is similar in children and adults with an annular pancreas\",\"e\":\"A third of cases are an incidental finding and patients are asymptomatic. In children, it commonly presents as duodenal obstruction, whereas adults typically present with signs and symptoms of pancreatitis.\"}]},{\"t\":49,\"p\":119,\"stem\":\"A 60-year-old man presents with a perforated duodenal ulcer into the retroperitoneum. The decision is made to initially manage the case conservatively. Regarding the ascending duodenum, which is the correct answer?\",\"lead\":\"Regarding the ascending duodenum, which is the correct answer?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It is a retroperitoneal structure C\",\"e\":\"The second, third and fourth (ascending) parts of the duodenum are all retroperitoneal structures. Only the first part (superior, D1) is intraperitoneal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Receives the common bile duct\",\"e\":\"The common bile duct inserts into the second part of the duodenum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lies lateral to the head of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"The descending part of the duodenum lies lateral to the head of the pancreas.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lies anterior to the hilum of the right kidney\",\"e\":\"The descending duodenum lies anterior to the right kidney.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It is related posteriorly to the right psoas muscle and the aorta.\",\"e\":\"The fourth part of the duodenum, also known as D.4, or the ascending duodenum, ascends to the inferior border of the body of the pancreas, curves anteriorly and forms the jejunum, where it terminates at the duodenal-jejunal junction or flexure, as you can see in the image below. Superiorly it is related to the stomach, posteriorly to the left psoas muscle and the aorta, and inferiorly to the jejunal loops.\\n\\nPylorus Pancreas Parts of the Duodenum • Superior Descending Inferior Ascending\\n\\nDuodenojejunal junctior schrantony\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"A specialist registrar while performing inguinal hernia repair made an incision parallel to and 5 cm (2 inches) above the inguinal ligament. The supervising consultant warned him to look out for the inferior epigastric vessels. The specialist registrar is most likely to find these between which layers of the abdominal wall?\",\"lead\":\"The specialist registrar is most likely to find these between which layers of the abdominal wall?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Camper's fascia and Scarpa's fascia\",\"e\":\"These are two superficial layers of fascia. Camper's is the fatty superficial layer and Scarpa's is the membranous layer. The epigastric vessels are found deeper than this.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"External oblique and internal oblique abdominal muscles.\",\"e\":\"The inferior epigastric arteries are found deeper than the internal and external oblique abdominal muscles. They are deep to the transversus abdominis muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Internal abdominal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles\",\"e\":\"The vessels are found deep to the transversus abdominis rather than between the transversus abdominus and the internal oblique muscles.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Skin and superficial fascia of the abdominal wall\",\"e\":\"The superficial abdominal fascia is the first layer deep to the skin and its associated subcutaneous fat. The superficial fatty layer is called Camper's fascia, and the deep membranous layer is known as Scarpa's fascia. The inferior epigastric vessels are deeper structures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transversus abdominis muscle and peritoneum\",\"e\":\"The inferior epigastric vessels lie on the inner surface of the transversus abdominis and are covered by parietal peritoneum. The inferior epigastric artery starts off as a branch of the external iliac artery, proximal to the inguinal ligament. Soon thereafter, it ascends on the anterior abdominal wall, medial to the deep ingunal ring, as illustrated below. After piercing the transversalis fascia, the inferior epigastric artery entres the rectus sheath, ascending posterior to the rectus abdominis muscle - the inferior epigastric vein mirrors its course Their superficial position is marked on the image below. Remember: the peritoneum lies over the inferior epigastric vessels to make the lateral umbilical fold\\n\\nOpening to the inguinal canal (deep inguinal ring) Inferior epigastric vessels\\n\\nInguinal canal Exit from the inguinal canal (superficial inguinal ring)\\n\\nImage source: Teachmeanatomy.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":6,\"stem\":\"A 23-year-old man presents with a painful, tender swelling inferior and lateral to the pubic tubercle. They present to the emergency general surgery service for assessment What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"They present to the emergency general surgery service for assessment What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Amyand's hernia\",\"e\":\"This is an inguinal hernia containing an inflamed appendix.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Femoral hernia\",\"e\":\"Femoral hernias present with a swelling inferior and lateral to the pubic tubercle, and are due to peritoneum herniating into the femoral canal - the boundaries of which are shown below. Although they are more common in women, they can occur in men. They are likely to strangulate due to the presence of the lacunar ligament medially.\\n\\n• Femoral canal inguinal ligament (anterior border) Pectineus (posterior border) Femoral vein (lateral border) • Lacunar ligament (medial border)\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inguinal hernia\",\"e\":\"An inguinal hernia would arise superior and medial to the pubic tubercle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Obturator hernia\",\"e\":\"Obturator hernias overall very rare but are more common in women. The pain worsens with hip extension, medial rotation and abduction.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Spigelian hernia\",\"e\":\"This occurs through a defect in the Spigelian fascia at the edge of the rectus abdominus muscle. They are uncommon.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":9,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old athlete presents with a tender mass in the abdomen on the lateral aspects of his rectus abdominis muscle. This occurred after a session of intense exercise. He is diagnosed with a Spigelian hernia and is scheduled for surgery. How would you describe the linea semilunaris of the rectus sheath?\",\"lead\":\"How would you describe the linea semilunaris of the rectus sheath?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Forms the lateral side of Hesselbach's triangle\",\"e\":\"The medial border of Hasselbach's triangle is the semilunaris. The lateral border is the inferior epigastric artery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Is crossed by the inferior epigastric vessels\",\"e\":\"The inferior epigastric vessels ascend posterior to the rectus sheath.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Is crossed by the median umbilical ligament.\",\"e\":\"The linea semilunaris is the lateral margin of the rectus muscle. The median umbilical ligament is found in the midline.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Is formed by interdigitation of the internal and external oblique aponeuroses\",\"e\":\"Linea semilunaris marks the lateral border of the rectus abdominis muscle. It corresponds to, but is not necessarily formed by the division of the aponeurosis of the internal oblique above the arcuate line.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Is one of the preferred lines of incision in abdominal surgery\",\"e\":\"The anterior abdominal wall would be greatly weakened by cutting along the semilunar line as the nerve supply to the rectus abdominis muscle and overlying skin would be interrupted. The linea semilunaris marks the lateral margin of the rectus muscle and sheath, passing from the pubic tubercle to the costal cartilage at the tip of the ninth rib, which overlies the fundus of the gall-bladder on the right. The inferior epigastric vessels and medial umbilical ligament cross the linea posteriorly below the arcuate line, before ascending in the posterior compartment of the rectus sheath. The anterior abdominal wall would be greatly weakened both physically and functionally by cutting along the semilunar line as the nerve supply to the rectus abdominis muscle and overlying skin would be interrupted. Furthermore, Langer's lines on the abdomen are horizontal. Spigelian hernias occur at the edge of the linea semilunaris, typically at the level of the arcuate line. The three borders of Hesselbach's triangle are the inferior epigastric artery, the inguinal ligament and the lateral border of the rectus abdominis muscle (linea semilunaris).\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"During an elective laparoscopic inguinal hernia repair you correctly identify a direct subtype, with the sac protruding through Hesselbach's triangle. You reduce the hernia and place the mesh laparoscopically, the patient is discharged the same day. What is the medial border of this triangle?\",\"lead\":\"What is the medial border of this triangle?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"15, 1l Ilioinguinal nerve\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior epigstric vessels.\",\"e\":\"The inferior epigastric vessels form the supero-lateral border of Hesselbach's triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"This is the infero-lateral border of Hesselbach's triangle.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Linea alba.\",\"e\":\"This is the fibrous band of tissue that runs in the midline of the abdomen from the xiphoid process to the pubic symphysis. It is not part of Hesselbach's triangle\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Linea semilunaris\",\"e\":\"The linea semilunaris is the lateral border of the rectus sheath and forms the medial border of Hesselbach's triangle.\\n\\nThe ilioinguinal nerve travels through the superficial ring but does not form a border of Hesslebach's triangle.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":14,\"stem\":\"A 48-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer presents with a painful, erythematous swelling of the right calf. She undergoes a procedure to insert a caval filter, intended to reduce the risk of pulmonary embolus At which vertebral level does the inferior vena cava pierce the diaphragm?\",\"lead\":\"She undergoes a procedure to insert a caval filter, intended to reduce the risk of pulmonary embolus At which vertebral level does the inferior vena cava pierce the diaphragm?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"T6\",\"e\":\"The inferior vena cava passes through the diaphragm at T8 rather than T6.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ts\",\"e\":\"The inferior vena cava crosses the diaphragm at T8, through the central tendon at the caval hiatus.\\n\\nCentral Sternal tendon sy ; ‘attachment\\n\\nfy? he, aval fs S , . a - iif (mae “= aa _ to tigen. ier WJ EB gl i rte is » 8 OE Bey aa TE! ‘ wth ok ts if ~ *y 42 . ‘ oh \\\\\\\\e DEES wae a4 i. SF eS 7 1 J mes\\n\\nVertebral attachment © wexctwieanatomy\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"TiO\",\"e\":\"T10 is the level at which the cesophagus crosses the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Ti2\",\"e\":\"The aorta passes behind the right and left crus of the diaphragm at T12 not the inferior vena cava.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Li\",\"e\":\"The left and right crus of the diaphragm insert into the lumbar vertebrae at L1/L2.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":17,\"stem\":\"A 21-year-old man presents with signs and symptoms of acute appendicitis, and the decision is made to perform an open appendicectomy via a Lanz skin incision.\\n\\nWhich fascia will be divided first?\",\"lead\":\"Which fascia will be divided first?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Camper's fascia\",\"e\":\"Camper's fascia is the first fatty layer that is encountered in the abdomen when performing an appendicectomy via a Lanz incision. In the attached image it is coloured in yellow and Scarpa's fascia is coloured in green.\\n\\nSkin Camper's fascia\\n\\nScarpa's fascia\\n\\n- Muscle\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Colles' fascia\",\"e\":\"This is a continuation of Scarpa's fascia into the perineal tissues and is not encountered during a Lanz incision.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Denonvillier's fascia\",\"e\":\"Denonvillier's fascia is otherwise known as rectoprostatic fascia and is not divided during an appendicectomy via a Lanz incision.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Scarpa's fascia\",\"e\":\"Scarpa fascia is just below Camper's fascia and is the second fascia to be divided.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transversus abdominis fascia\",\"e\":\"Transversus abdominus fascia is not the first fascia incised during a Lanz procedure.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":20,\"stem\":\"Before performing a hernia repair your consultant is quizzing you regarding the inguinal region and asks for the eponymous names for the anatomical structures. Which one of the following is correctly paired?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correctly paired?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The femoral canal is also known as Alcock's canal\",\"e\":\"Alcock's canal is a fascial tunnel on the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa, which conveys the pudendal nerve and vessels: It is not related to the femoral canal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The inguinal ligament is also known as Astley Cooper's ligament\",\"e\":\"The pectineal ligament is also known as Cooper's ligament, and should not be confused with the suspensory ligaments of the breast, also known as the ligaments of Cooper.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The inguinal ligament is also known as Poupart's ligament\",\"e\":\"The inguinal ligament is also known as Poupart's ligament, named after Francois Poupart who gave it relevance with regards to inguinal hernia repairs.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The transversalis fascia is also known as Scarpa's fascia\",\"e\":\"The transversalis fascia and Scarpa's fascia are not related structures.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The vas deferens is also known as the duct of Santorini\",\"e\":\"The duct of Santorini is the accessory pancreatic duct.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":22,\"stem\":\"A patient presented with bowel obstruction on a background of hard and painful groin lump. It could not be reduced and they proceed to theatre for treatment of the lesion. During inguinal hernia repair, which structures would be encountered?\",\"lead\":\"During inguinal hernia repair, which structures would be encountered?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Colles' tascia • C-\",\"e\":\"Colle's fascia is the continuation of Scarpa's fascia once it continues over the scrotum and penis and therefore is not encountered during an inguinal hernia repair.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"iliohypogastric nerve\",\"e\":\"The iliohypogastric nerve is not encountered during normal inguinal hernia repair.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Iloinguinal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve is usually seen during an inguinal hernia repair.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve\",\"e\":\"The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is usually visualised in the anterior approach to the hip during hip surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Superior epigastric vein\",\"e\":\"The superior epigastric vein anastomoses with the inferior epigastric vein at the level of the umbilicus and drains the upper abdominal wall and some of the diaphragm. It is not encountered during an inguinal hernia repair.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":24,\"stem\":\"A patient is brought into the Emergency Department as part of a trauma call. A FAST scan demonstrates free fluid in the abdominal cavity Which of the following is a correct description of the hepatorenal space?\",\"lead\":\"A FAST scan demonstrates free fluid in the abdominal cavity Which of the following is a correct description of the hepatorenal space?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"It communicates freely with the greater sac\",\"e\":\"The hepatorenal space communicates with the lesser sac.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"It communicates freely with the right paracolic gutter\",\"e\":\"The hepatorenal space is a potential space and lies between the right lobe of the liver and right kidney. It forms the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity, apart from the pelvis, in the supine abdomen and communicates freely with the lesser sac, the right paracolic gutter and right subphrenic space. Intraperitoneal fluid or pus can easily accumulate in the hepatorenal pouch.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"It communicates freely with the left subphrenic space\",\"e\":\"It communicates with the right rather than the left subphrenic space.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"It is closed inferiorly by the inferior coronary ligament\",\"e\":\"The coronary ligament is a fold of peritoneum that holds the liver to the inferior surface of the diaphragm and does not close the hepatorenal space\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"It lies posterior to the right kidney\",\"e\":\"It is anterior to the right kidney.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":26,\"stem\":\"A patient presents with a painful lump in their groin. They are diagnosed with an inguinal hernia and listed for elective repair. Which one of the following is correct concerning the inguinal canal?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct concerning the inguinal canal?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"In women the inguinal canal transmits only the round ligament of the uterus\",\"e\":\"The inguinal canal also carries the ilioinguinal nerve along with the round ligament in women.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The deep inguinal ring is an hiatus in the external oblique aponeurosis\",\"e\":\"The deep inguinal ring is hiatus in the transversalis fascia whereas the superficial ring is a hiatus in the external oblique aponeurosis\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The inferior epigastric vesseis are found lateral to the deep inguinal ring\",\"e\":\"The inferior epigastric vessels are medial to the deep inguinal ring.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve is found within the spermatic cord\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve is carried along with the spermatic cord in the inguinal canal in men but not within the cord.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The posterior wall is formed by the conjoint tendon and the transversalis fascia\",\"e\":\"The inguinal canal is a short oblique passage through the anterior abdominal wall. The relatively weak transversalis fascia (laterally) and the conjoint tendon (medially) form the posterior wall.\\n\\nRoof Aponeurosis of the external oblique Internal oblique and transversus abdominis. Anterior • Tranversalia fascia Posterior wall wall Inguinal ligament\\n\\nPubis bone\\n\\nFloor\\n\\nteachmeanatomy\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":29,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man presents with a swelling in the right groin. He is diagnosed with an inguinal hernia and proceeds to surgery. During the procedure, you notice a direct hernia through the posterior wall of the inguinal canal. Which structure lies in the medial one-third of the posterior wall of the inguinal canal?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure lies in the medial one-third of the posterior wall of the inguinal canal?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aponeurosis of external oblique\",\"e\":\"This forms the anterior wall of the inguinal canal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"IN Conjoint tendon\",\"e\":\"The conjoint tendon is formed by the confluence of tissues from the internal oblique and transverses abdominis muscles of the abdominal wall. It lies in the medial one-third of the inguinal canal, posterior to the superficial ring. The lateral 2/3 of the posterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed by the transversalis fascia.\\n\\nRoof Aponeurosis of the external oblique Intomal oblique and transversus abdominis. Anterior Tranversalis fascia Posterior wall wall Inguinal ligament\\n\\nPubis bone\\n\\nFloor\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Lacunar ligament\",\"e\":\"The lacunar ligament forms the medial aspect of the floor of the canal.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Superficial ring\",\"e\":\"The superficial ring is the exit to the inguinal canal 1cm above and lateral to the pubic tubercule.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transversus abdominis\",\"e\":\"Transversus abdominus muscle forms the roof of the inguinal canal.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":32,\"stem\":\"A 58-year-old man undergoes abdominoperineal resection after developing a malignant melanoma of the anus. During dissection several fascial planes are encountered. Colles' fascia is contiguous with which layer of the anterior abdominal wall?\",\"lead\":\"Colles' fascia is contiguous with which layer of the anterior abdominal wall?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aponeurosis of external oblique:\",\"e\":\"This forms the inguinal ligament and has no part of Colles' fascia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"rivi- Camper's fascia\",\"e\":\"This is the fascia that is superficial to Scarpa's fascia and is not contiguous with Colles'\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Posterior rectus sheath\",\"e\":\"The posterior rectus sheath terminates at the arcuate line. Below this, the entire aponeurosis passes in front of the rectus muscles\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Scarpa's fascia\",\"e\":\"Scarpa's fascia is contiguous with Colles' fascia in the perineum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transversalis fascia\",\"e\":\"Transversalis fascia continues to become the internal spermatic fascia.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":34,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man presents with a swelling of the right groin. He is diagnosed with an inguinal hernia and proceeds to surgery. During the procedure, you notice a direct hernia through the poster or wall of the inguinal canal.\\n\\nWhich structure forms the lateral one-third of the posterior wall of the inguinal canal?\",\"lead\":\"Which structure forms the lateral one-third of the posterior wall of the inguinal canal?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Aponeurosis of external oblique\",\"e\":\"The aponeurosis of the external oblique helps form the anterior wall and is assisted laterally by the internal oblique.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Transversus abdominis\",\"e\":\"The transverus abdominus muscle helps form the roof of the inguinal canal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"This forms the floor of the inguinal canal along with the lacunar ligament medially.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Internal oblique\",\"e\":\"This helps form the lateral third of the anterior wall of the inguinal canal.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transversalis fascia\",\"e\":\"This forms the lateral aspect of the posterior wall throughout the inguinal canal and is joined by the conjoint tendon medially. Roof → Aponeurosis of the externol oblique Internal oblique and tranaversus Anterior •Tranversalis fascia wall Posterior Inguinal ligament wall\\n\\n- Pubis bone\\n\\nFloor\\n\\nteachment. C\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":37,\"stem\":\"During an open inguinal hernia repair in a 76-year-old man. you find that the patient has an indirect sac that you begin to dissect free. You encounter multiple anatomical structures and do not want to damage anything important\\n\\nWhich one of the following structures travels within the inguinal canal but remains external to the spermatic cord?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures travels within the inguinal canal but remains external to the spermatic cord?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The Cremasteric artery\",\"e\":\"The cremasteric artery travels through the inguinal canal as part of the spermatic cord.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The Genitofemoral nerves\",\"e\":\"The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve travel with the spermatic cord through the inguinal canal.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The pampiniform plexus\",\"e\":\"The pampiniform plexus is part of the spermatic cord\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The Vas deferens\",\"e\":\"The vas deferens is part of the spermatic cord.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The llioinguinal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve is separate to the spermatic cord in the inguinal canal. The external (or superficial) inguinal ring is formed by a V-shaped defect in the external oblique aponeurosis - an indirect hernia, that may emerge from this ring, is typically felt above and lateral to the pubic tubercle. It transmits the spermatic cord with all its contents, plus the ilioinguinal nerve. The following are the contents of the spermatic cord: three fascial layers (external spermatic, cremasteric, internal spermatic); three arteries. (testicular, cremasteric, artery to the vas deferens); the pampiniform plexus, veins from the cremaster and vas deferens; and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, sympathetic fibres, vas deferens and lymphatics.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":39,\"stem\":\"A 45-year-old man with a history of gastro-oesophageal reflux disease undergoes laparoscopic reduction of hiatus hernia and fundoplication. During the procedure, dissection is performed to identify the boundaries of the cesophageal hiatus. What structure accompanies the oesophagus through this hiatus?\",\"lead\":\"What structure accompanies the oesophagus through this hiatus?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"151 Azygos vein\",\"e\":\"The azygos vein, along with the aorta and thoracic duct travel through the diaphragm at T12.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The IVC travels through the diaphragm at T8 along with the right phrenic nerve\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Right phrenic nerve A\",\"e\":\"The right phrenic nerve travels with the inferior vena cava through the diaphragm at T8.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Thoracic duct\",\"e\":\"The thoracic duct accompanies the aorta and ażygos vein through the diaphragm at T12.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerves\",\"e\":\"The left and right branches of the vagus travel through the diaphragm with the besophagus at the level of T10.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":41,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old man motorcyclist is involved in a road traffic accident and sustains direct blunt trauma to his pelvis, causing a fracture of the ischiopubic ramus. Which layer of the abdominal wall is contiguous with Colles' fascia?\",\"lead\":\"Which layer of the abdominal wall is contiguous with Colles' fascia?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"cX\\\" Anterior rectus sheath\",\"e\":\"The anterior rectus sheath is not contiguous with Colles' fascia; instead it is Scarpa's fascia that is continuous.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Camper's fascia\",\"e\":\"Camper's fascia is the superficial fascia of the abdominal wall.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Posterior rectus sheath\",\"e\":\"Posterior rectus sheath terminates at the arcuate line, below which all of the aponeuroses then passes in front of the rectus.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Scarpa's fascia\",\"e\":\"Scarpa's fascia is contiguous with Colles' fascia in the perineum. Colles' fascia forms the superficial fascial boundary of the superficial perineal space and attaches to the margin of the ischiopubic ramus. The fascia is continuous with the dartos fascia of the scrotum, with the superficial penile fascia (that covers the clitoris and penis, and with Scarpa's fascia of the abdominal wall.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Transversalis fascia\",\"e\":\"Transversalis fascia continues to become the internal spermatic fascia.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":43,\"stem\":\"A 3-year-old Ugandan child presents with anterior abdominal wall swelling and pain. The admitting consultant suggests a Mayo repair is the most suitable surgical intervention. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Epigastric hernia.\",\"e\":\"Epigastric hernias are not repaired by a Mayo repair.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Inguinal hernia\",\"e\":\"Halstead's operation would be an inguinal hernia repair.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Paraumbilical hernia T\",\"e\":\"Paraumbilical hernias are more frequently repaired using a mesh, rather than the Mayo technique. They are also less common than umbilical hernias in this age group.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Spigelian hernia\",\"e\":\"These are hernias of the linea semilunaris and are typically repaired with mesh. They are not repaired with a Mayo repair.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Umbilical hernia\",\"e\":\"An umbilical hernia protrudes through the umbilical cicatrix to lie in the subcutaneous tissues. They will often resolve as the child grows and few will require surgical treatment Those that do can be repaired with the Mayo 'vest-over-pants' approach. There is a higher incidence in Afro-Caribbean children than in Caucasian.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":45,\"stem\":\"A 50-year-old man is being examined for an inguinal hernia.\\n\\nWhich one of the following is correct regarding the inguinal rings?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is correct regarding the inguinal rings?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The deep inguinal ring lies 5 cm above the midinguinal point\",\"e\":\"It is above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament rather than the midinguinal point.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The deep inguinal ring lies above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"The deep inguinal ring is at the midpoint of the inguinal ligament (shown below), which is midway between the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) and the pubic tubercle.\\n\\nOpening to the Inguinal canal (deep inguinal ring) / Inferlor epigastric vessels\\n\\nInguinal canal Exit from the inguinal canal (superficial inguinal ring)\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The deep inguinal ring lies 5 cm above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"The deep inguinal point is about 1.5 cm above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament rather than 5 cm.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The superficial inguinal ring lies 1.5cm above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"The superficial ring is 1 cm above and medial to the pubic tubercle.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The superficial inguinal ring lies 5cm above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament\",\"e\":\"The superficial inguinal ring is the exit of the inguinal canal and is found 1 cm above and medial to the pubic tubercle.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":48,\"stem\":\"You are assisting in a nephrectomy using a posterior approach. The consultant's previous patient had a complication following the operation and today they are being more cautious than usual. Which one of the following is important during this approach?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is important during this approach?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The adrenal glands are at easy risk of injury\",\"e\":\"The adrenal glands are anatomically well protected. They lie anterosuperior to the upper part of each kidney. within their own compartment of the renal fascia. Care must be taken during a nephrectomy to avoid damage to the adrenals, but these are not easily damaged by a careful surgeon using a posterior approach.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The parietal pleura does not extend below the twelfth rib\",\"e\":\"The lower border of the parietal pleura crosses the twelfth rib at the lateral border of the erector spinae, and passes in horizontally to the lower border of the twelfth thoracic vertebrae. There is, therefore, a triangle of pleura in the costovertebral angle below the medial part of the twelfth rib, behind the upper pole of the kidney\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The perinephric fat surrounds the renal fascia\",\"e\":\"The renal fascia of Gerota surrounds the perinephric fat, which lies outside the renal capsule.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The renal artery lies anterior to the renal vein in the anatomical position\",\"e\":\"The renal artery is posterior to the renal vein.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The subcostal neurovascular bundle is relatively protected from risk of injury\",\"e\":\"The subcostal neurovascular bundle is relatively protected from risk of injury because it lies in the costal groove\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":50,\"stem\":\"A 35-year-old builder has a left inguinal hernia repair: During the operation, the external oblique is opened and you commence lifting the spermatic cord\\n\\nWhich one structure lies within this?\",\"lead\":\"Which one structure lies within this?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Cremasteric artery ....\",\"e\":\"Nine structures lie within the spermatic cord: three arteries (testicular, cremasteric, artery to the vas deferens); three nerves (genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, sympathetic fibres, ilioinguinal nerve - this is outside the cord but travels with it) and three other structures (pampiniform plexus, vas deferens, lymphatics). There are three covering fascial layers - external and internal spermatic and cremasteric fascia.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"External spermatic fascia\",\"e\":\"The external spermatic fascia is a thin membrane covering the spermatic cord and is derived from the aponeurosis of the external oblique. It surrounds but is not contained within the cord.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve\",\"e\":\"The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve travels within the spermatic cord rather than the femoral branch.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Illoinguinal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve travels within the inguinal canal but not within the spermatic cord\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Lipoma of the cord\",\"e\":\"Lipomas of the cord are sometimes found during inguinal hernia repairs but they are not contained within the cord\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":52,\"stem\":\"A 25-year-old footbail player is undergoing an inguinal hernia repair. First Camper's and then Scarpa's fascia are divided and the first muscle layer is visualised What would the surgeon note to identify the superficial inguinal ring?\",\"lead\":\"First Camper's and then Scarpa's fascia are divided and the first muscle layer is visualised What would the surgeon note to identify the superficial inguinal ring?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"A W-shaped defect in the external oblique\",\"e\":\"The superficial inguinal ring is a triangular hiatus in the external oblique aponeurosis.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Intercrural fibres\",\"e\":\"The visible landmark for the superficial inguinal ring are the intercrural fibres that run at right angles across the external oblique aponeurosis. The inguinal canal with its borders is shown in the image below, with the deep inguinal ring seen laterally and the superficial ring seen medially at either end.\\n\\nRoof Transversalis fascis Internal oblique Transversus abdominus\\n\\nPosterior Wall Transversalls fascia\\n\\nFloor Inguinal ligament Anterior Wall Lacunar ligament Aponeurosis of the external oblique Internal oblique\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The conjoint tendon and the transversalis fascia\",\"e\":\"These form the posterior wall of the inguinal canal - the transversalis fascia laterally and the conjoint tendon medially.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"That the ilioinguinal nerve is within the spermatic cord\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve travels through the inguinal canal with the spermatic cord but not inside it.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"That the inferior epigastric vessels are lateral to the deep inguinal ring\",\"e\":\"The inferior epigastric vessels are medial to the deep inguinal ring.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":55,\"stem\":\"You are a Foundation Year 2 (FY2) student assisting in a laparotomy for small bowel obstruction. While opening the abdomen via a midline laparotomy. the consultant points out the most superficial fascial layer, which is fatty She asks you what this fascia is called. What is the most likely name of the structure described?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely name of the structure described?\",\"ans\":\"A\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Camper's fascia\",\"e\":\"The superficial fascia of the anterior abdominal wall is divided into a superficial adipose layer known as Camper's fascia and a deeper membranous layer known as Scarpa's fascia. Camper's fascia is fatty to varying degrees and is the more superficial, and therefore is the correct answer here. The layers of the anterior abdominal wall are: skin, subcutaneous tissue, superficial fascia (Camper's fascia and Scarpa's fascia), external oblique muscle, internal oblique muscle, transversus abdominis muscle, transversalis fascia, preperitoneal fatty tissue, and peritoneum. These are clinically important to know for all abdominal surgery.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Scarpa's fascia\",\"e\":\"Scarpa's fascia is the deeper layer to Camper's fascia and is thin and membranous. It contains very little, or no. fat and so is not the correct answer here. Scarpa's fascia continues below the inguinal ligament and fuses with the fascia lata of the thigh. It also forms the fundiform ligament of the penis and is known as Colle's fascia once it advances posterioriy along the perineum.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Colle's fascia\",\"e\":\"Colle's fascia is the continuation of Scarpa's fascia in the perineum. It is the deeper membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia. It attaches to the ischiopubic rami and the perineal membrane, which divides the prostate from the base of the penis.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Dartos fascia\",\"e\":\"The Dartos fascia is the continuation of Scarpa's fascia into the scrotum. It contains smooth muscle fibres.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Fascia lata\",\"e\":\"The fascia lata is the deep fascia of the thigh and surrounds the muscles of the thigh Scarpa's fascia fuses with the fascia lata just under the inguinal ligament.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":58,\"stem\":\"You are assisting during a Hartmann's procedure. While dividing the anterior abdominal layers during the operation, your registrar points out a triangular structure, anterior to the rectus abdominis, which has its base on the pubis and is attached superiorly to the linea alba. He remarks that this structure is sometimes absent. What is the most likely name of the structure?\",\"lead\":\"What is the most likely name of the structure?\",\"ans\":\"D\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Linea alba\",\"e\":\"The linea alba ('white line') is a fibrous structure running down in the midline from the xiphoid process to the pubic symphysis. Although it attaches inferiorly to a similar place, it is not triangular and has not been frequently reported to be absent. The linea alba is tensed by the pyramidalis muscle, which is the correct answer here.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Plantaris muscle\",\"e\":\"Although the plantaris muscle has been noted to be absent in as much as 12% of the population, it is found in the superficial posterior compartment of the leg, and so would not be seen during a Hartmann's procedure, which requires a midline laparotomy.\\n\\nDiastasis recti, or divarication, is not a structure, but a condition in which an abnormal distance is created between the right and left abdominal muscles - generally taken to be above 2 cm. It occurs due to the laxity of the linea alba, and risk factors include pregnancy and obesity. It is apparent on clinical examination by a prominent abdominal ridge seen when a patient with the condition raises his/her head. It does not often require surgical repair.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Diastasis recti\",\"e\":\"\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pyramidalis muscle\",\"e\":\"This is a small, triangular, vertical muscle that lies anterior to the rectus abdominis and attaches to the linea alba having originated at the front of the pubis and pubic symphysis. It is innervated by the ventral portion of T12 and supplied by the inferior and superior epigastric arteries. Its action is to tense the linea alba. It is naturally absent* in up to 12% of the population.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Peroneus tertius\",\"e\":\"This is a muscle in the lower leg that effects eversion and dorsiflexion of the foot, so it will not be seen during the operation described. However, it can be absent in up to 17% of the population.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":61,\"stem\":\"You are scrubbed in during an inguinal hernia repair case on a 74-year-old man who presented as an emergency with bowel obstruction. During the operation you correctly identify the spermatic cord. Which one of the following structures is found outside the spermatic cord?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following structures is found outside the spermatic cord?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Testicular artery\",\"e\":\"The testicular artery is found in the spermatic cord and is one of three arteries that travels within it. A paired artery, it branches directly on each side from the abdominal aorta, below the origin of the renal arteries and passes downwards on the anterior surface of the psoas major to supply the testes\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Cremasteric artery\",\"e\":\"The cremasteric artery is found in the spermatic cord and is one of the three arteries travelling within it. It is a branch of the inferior epigastric artery and supplies the cremaster muscle and scrotal skin.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Ilioinguinal nerve\",\"e\":\"The ilioinguinal nerve is a branch of the first lumbar nerve and accompanies the spermatic cordt through the inguinal canal, exiting through the superficial inguinal ring. It is commonly seen overlying the cord, rather than within it.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Pampiniform plexus\",\"e\":\"The pampiniform plexus is a network of veins found within the spermatic cord. It provides venous return from the testes and plays a part in testicular temperature regulation. A varicocele is an abnormal enlargement of this structure.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Vas deferens\",\"e\":\"The vas deferens is a muscular structure that does run within the spermatic cord and transports sperm from the epididymis to the ejaculatory duct. If inadvertently damaged during a hernia repair, it may contribute to infertility and the patient must be counselled regarding this during the consent process.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":63,\"stem\":\"You have been holding a retractor for an hour during an emergency laparotomy for small bowel obstruction. Your consultant notices that you are losing concentration and asks you to distinguish between intraperitoneal and retroperitoneal structures. Which one of the following is an intraperitoneal structure?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following is an intraperitoneal structure?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Descending part of duodenum.\",\"e\":\"The descending part of the duodenum is the second part of the duodenum and is retroperitoneal. Only the proximal first segment of the duodenum is intraperitoneal.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Ascending part of duodenum\",\"e\":\"The ascending part of the duodenum is the fourth part and is the shortest section. It is a retroperitoneal structure.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Inferior vena cava\",\"e\":\"The inferior vena cava lies posteriorly to the main abdominal cavity and is therefore a retroperitoneal structure.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Suprarenal glands\",\"e\":\"These endocrine glands produce varying hormones and are retroperitoneal glands, sitting on top of the kidneys and surrounded by Gerota's fascia.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Sigmoid coion\",\"e\":\"The sigmoid colon is suspended in the mesentery and is found within the peritoneal cavity. ine organs or the abdominal cavity can be classitied as intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal according to their relationship with the peritoneal membrane. Intraperitoneal organs are those that are within the peritoneal space and lined with peritoneum, and retroperitoneal are those that lie behind the space and are not lined with peritoneum. These organs are mainly close to the posterior wall of the abdomen.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":65,\"stem\":\"You are a surgical Foundation Year 2 (FY2) doctor observing an emergency small bowel resection in a patient with small bowel obstruction. You ask the registrar to explain the anatomical differences between the jejunum and ileum.\\n\\nWhich one of the following statements is the most accurate?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of the following statements is the most accurate?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The jejunum is larger in diameter than the ileum and displays more prominent arterial arcades\",\"e\":\"The jejunum is larger in diameter and has a thicker wall than the ileum, but it is characterised by less prominent arterial arcades and ionger vasa recta\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The ileum is mainly situated in the left lower quadrant and has thinner walls than the jejunum\",\"e\":\"The ileum does have thinner walls in comparison with the jejunum but the ileum is mainly found in the right lower quadrant of the abdomen.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The jejunum lies mainly in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen and has less prominent arterial arcades than the ileum\",\"e\":\"The jejunum is mainly found in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen and is characterised by less prominent arterial arcades and longer vasa recta when compared with the ileum.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The ileum has less mesenteric fat, but more prominent plicae circularis (valvulae conniventes) when compared with the jejunum\",\"e\":\"The ileum has more mesenteric fat and less prominent plicae circularis (mucosal folds) than the jejunum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The ileum has thicker walls and less prominent plicae circularis in comparison with the jejunum\",\"e\":\"The ileum has less prominent plicae circularis in comparison with the jejunum. The jejunum has a thicker wall, and more prominent plica circularis, with long straight arteries. The ileum, on the other hand, has less prominent plica circularis but has thinner walls.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":67,\"stem\":\"You are teaching a group of medical students about the embryological anatomy of the abdomen when one student asks about the boundaries of the foregut.\\n\\nWhich one of these abdominal structures is most closely related to the junction between the foregut and the midgut?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these abdominal structures is most closely related to the junction between the foregut and the midgut?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The origin of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA)\",\"e\":\"The SMA originates from the anterior abdominal aorta anterior to L1. It supplies the midgut, which begins inferior to the major duodenal papilla.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Major duodenal papilla\",\"e\":\"The foregut begins at the mouth and ends just inferior to the major duodenal papilla, where it gives way to the midgut.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Head of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"The head of the pancreas is closely related to the duodenum as it lies within its concavity. The major duodenal papilla, below which point the foregut ends and midgut begins, is the point at which the common bile duct and pancreatic duct empty into the duodenum. Therefore, the head of the pancreas is closely related to the junction between foregut and midgut but the major duodenal papilla is a more accurate answer.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Distal third of transverse colon\",\"e\":\"The distal third of the transverse colon is the point at which the hindgut begins and is not related to the foregut and midgut\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The origin of the coeliac trunk\",\"e\":\"The coeliac trunk arises from the abdominal aorta immediately below the hiatus of the diaphragm at T12. It originates just above the superior mesenteric artery and supplies the foregut but is not related to the junction of the foregut and midgut.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":69,\"stem\":\"You are a CT1 doctor working in a hepatobiliary ward when you review a computed tomography arterial portography (CTAP) scan of a patient with portal vein thrombosis. As you scroll through the images you try to remember the anatomy of the portal vein.\\n\\nWhich one of these statements is the most accurate with regard to the portal vein?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these statements is the most accurate with regard to the portal vein?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The portal vein is formed by the union of the splenic vein and inferior mesenteric vein posterior to the neck of the pancreas\",\"e\":\"Although the portal vein forms posterior to the neck of the pancreas, it is formed by the union of the splenic vein and the superior mesenteric vein.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The portal vein is formed by the union of the splenic vein and the superior mesenteric vein at the L3 level\",\"e\":\"The portal vein is formed at the L1 level.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The portal vein receives tributaries including the right and left gastric veins and para-umbilical veins\",\"e\":\"This is correct, and the portal vein also receives cystic veins from the gall-bladder. The portal vein is formed by the union of the splenic vein and superior mesenteric veins posterior to the neck of the pancreas. It runs along with the hepatic artery proper and common bile duct to form the three components of the portal triad. The hepatoduodenal ligament is a double layer of peritoneum - the free edge of the lesser omentum - that encompasses the triad. This is clinically relevant in liver surgery where the hepatoduodenal ligament may be clamped with a large traumatic haemostat to halt blood flow and control bleeding from the liver. This is known as the Pringle manoeuvre.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The portal vein passes anterior to the superior part of the duodenum to enter the right edge of the lesser omentum\",\"e\":\"The portal vein passes posterior to the superior part of the duodenum but does enter the right edge of the lesser omentum.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The portal vein passes anteriorly to the bile duct and hepatic artery proper.\",\"e\":\"The portal vein passes posteriorly to both these structures.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":72,\"stem\":\"You are a Foundation Year 2 (FY2) student working in general surgery and are called to a patient with known diverticular disease who now presents with guarding and rebound. A computed tomography (CT) scan confirms a perforation and you later assist in an emergency Hartmann's procedure. The consultant asks about the features of the small bowel.\\n\\nWhich one of these is anatomical features is most associated with the small bowel?\",\"lead\":\"Which one of these is anatomical features is most associated with the small bowel?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Omental appendices\",\"e\":\"Omental appendices are peritoneal-covered fatty accumulations associated with the large bowel.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Taeniae coli\",\"e\":\"The longitudinal muscles of the large bowel are segregated into three bands, which are known as the taeniae coli.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Haustra\",\"e\":\"The large bowel's circular muscles form sacculations known as haustra.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Peritoneal - covered fatty tags\",\"e\":\"These are most evident in the large bowel and are known as omental appendices.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Valvulae conniventes\",\"e\":\"Valvulae conniventes, also known as plicae circulares, are thin circular mucosal folds in the small bowel. These structures give the small bowel the distinct radiological appearances of lines appearing to pass across the full width of the lumen.\"}]},{\"t\":50,\"p\":74,\"stem\":\"You are working in the Urology Department and have been referred a patient with severe loin to groin pain and haematuria. His pain only settles with intravenous opioid analgesia: You request a computed tomography scan of kidneys, ureters and bladder (CTKUB), which shows an obstructing ureteric calculus Which one of these is most likely to be the location of the obstruction?\",\"lead\":\"His pain only settles with intravenous opioid analgesia: You request a computed tomography scan of kidneys, ureters and bladder (CTKUB), which shows an obstructing ureteric calculus Which one of these is most likely to be the location of the obstruction?\",\"ans\":\"C\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"The ureteropelvic junction\",\"e\":\"This is the point at which the renal pelvis becomes continues with the ureter and is the first point of constriction at which renal stones can become lodged.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"The ureteral crossing of the iliac vessels\",\"e\":\"At the point at which the ureters cross the common iliac vessels the ureters are slightly constricted. Stones lodged here are more unusual than stones lodged at the ureteropelvic or ureterovesical junction.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"The ureterovesical junction\",\"e\":\"This is the point at which the ureters enter the wall of the bladder, and is a site of ureteral constriction at which renal stones may lodge. It is the most common site of impaction as it has the smallest diameter of the options listed. Generally for a stone to impact it must have a diameter of 2 mm or greater.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"The ureteral crossing of the renal veins\",\"e\":\"The ureters do not cross the renal veins.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"The prostatic urethra\",\"e\":\"This is the widest part of the urethra, so an unlikely site for obstruction.\"}]},{\"t\":51,\"p\":3,\"stem\":\"The coeliac branch of the posterior vagal trunk sustained iatrogenic injury during repair of a hiatus hernia. The damage to this nerve would affect the muscular movements, as well as some secretory activities, of the gastrointestinal tract Which segment is least likely to be affected by the nerve damage?\",\"lead\":\"The damage to this nerve would affect the muscular movements, as well as some secretory activities, of the gastrointestinal tract Which segment is least likely to be affected by the nerve damage?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Ascending colon\",\"e\":\"The ascending colon is innervated by the vagus nerve so would likely be affected\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Caecum\",\"e\":\"The caecum is part of the mid-gut which is innervated by the vagus nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Jejunum\",\"e\":\"The jejunum is part of the midgut and is therefore innervated by the vagus nerve.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"lleum\",\"e\":\"As the ileum receives its blood supply from the superior mesenteric artery it is also innervated by the vagus nerve. The vagus nerve supplies parasympathetic fibres to all of the abdominal organs, which receive blood from the coeliac trunk or superior mesenteric artery. This means that the vagus supplies parasympathetic to the entire gastrointestinal tract, up to the last part of the transverse colon at the splenic flexure. The end of the transverse colon and all gastrointestinal structures distal to that point receive parasympathetic innervation from the peivic splanchnic nerves and blood from the inferior mesenteric artery. So, the ascending colon, caecum. jejunum and ileum would all be affected by damage to the vagus nerve. The sigmoid colon, which receives parasympathetic innervation from the pelvic splanchnics, would not be affected.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"sigmoid colon\",\"e\":\"The sigmoid colon (part of the hindgut) has its parasympathetic innervation originating from the pelvic splanchnic nerves.\"}]},{\"t\":51,\"p\":5,\"stem\":\"A 47-year-old woman undergoes low anterior resection to treat a rectal adenocarcinoma. During the surgery, great care is taken to preserve the pelvic splanchnic nerves. From which nerve roots do these arise?\",\"lead\":\"From which nerve roots do these arise?\",\"ans\":\"E\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"L2-L4\",\"e\":\"L2-L4 are part of the lumbar plexus of nerves.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"L3-L5\",\"e\":\"The pelvic splanchnic nerves are formed by S2-S4 rather than the lumbar nerves.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"L4. L5- S1\",\"e\":\"These form part of the sciatic nerve but not the pelvic splanchnic nerves:\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"SI-S3\",\"e\":\"The pelvic plexus is formed by S2-S4 rather than S1-S3.\\n\\nThe pelvic plexuses supply the viscere of the pelvic cavity. They are situated at the sides of the rectum in men and at the sides of the rectum and vagina in women. The pelvic plexuses are formed on either side by a continuation of the superior hypogastric plexus (the hypogastric nerve), by the sacral parasympathetic efferent fibres from the second, third and fourth sacral nerves (pelvic splanchnic nerves) and sacral splanchnic nerves from the sympathetic trunk.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"S2-S4\",\"e\":\"The sacral parasympathetic efferent fibres from the second, third and fourth sacral nerves contribute to the pelvic plexus.\"}]},{\"t\":51,\"p\":7,\"stem\":\"A 55-year-old man presents with metastatic lung cancer. On CR there is an obvious large opacity at the right apex. His pupil is constricted on the right. What is the autonomic control of the pupil?\",\"lead\":\"What is the autonomic control of the pupil?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Parasympathetic innervation from superior cervical ganglia of T1 and sympathetic innervation from cranial nerve (CN) IM\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic fibres originate from the thoracic cord (T1) and enter the sympathetic chain. Post-ganglionic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion supply the eye. The parasympathetic supply is from the ciliary ganglion via the oculomotor nerve (CNIII).\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Sympathetic innervation from superior cervical ganglia of T1 and parasympathetic innervation from cranial nerve III\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic responses are related to the fight-or-flight response and the pupil will dilate. Sympathetic fibres originate from the thoracic cord (T1) and enter the sympathetic chain. Post-ganglionic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion supply the eye, removal of which results in unbalanced parasympathetic innervation. In this scenario, the metastatic disease has damaged the sympathetic innervation of the pupil on the side ipsilateral to T1.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Mixed sympathetic and parasympathetic innervation from superior cervical ganglia of T1 and cranial nerve ill\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic responses are related to the fight-or-flight response and the pupil will dilate. Sympathetic fibres originate from the thoracic cord (T1) and enter the sympathetic chain Post-ganglionic fibres from the superior cervical ganglion supply the eye, removal of which results in unbalanced parasympathetic innervation. In this scenario, the metastatic disease has damaged the sympathetic innervation of the pupil on the side ipsilateral to T1.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Parasympathetic innervation from cranial nerves III-Vl and sympathetic innervation from T1-3\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic responses are related to the fight-or-flight response and the pupil will dilate. removal of which results in unbalanced parasympathetic innervation. Although cranial nerves (CNs) III, IV and VI are involved in eye movements, only CNIll provides parasympathetic innervation to the pupil. T1 accounts for the sympathetic innervation of the pupil.\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Parasympathetic innervation from T1-3 and sympathetic innervation from cranial nerves II-VI\",\"e\":\"Sympathetic responses are related to the fight-or-flight response and the pupil will dilate. removal of which results in unbalanced parasympathetic innervation. Although cranial nerves (CNs) III, IV and VI are involved in eye movements, only CNIll provides parasympathetic innervation to the pupil. T1 accounts for the sympathetic innervation of the pupil.\"}]},{\"t\":51,\"p\":10,\"stem\":\"A 43-year-old man is seen in the clinic with autonomic dysfunction of his gastrointestinal tract, caused by his poorly controlled diabetes.\\n\\nWhat is the parasympathetic innervation of the gastrointestinal tract?\",\"lead\":\"What is the parasympathetic innervation of the gastrointestinal tract?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Phrenic nerve\",\"e\":\"The phrenic nerve is involved in innervation of the diaphragm.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Vagus nerve\",\"e\":\"The vagus nerve. cranial nerve X. innervates a variety of abdominal viscera and stimulates activity via parasympathetic activity.\"},{\"l\":\"C\",\"x\":\"Sacral plexus\",\"e\":\"The sacral plexus innervates pelvic organs and, although it does supply some of the large bowel, the vagus nerve is the predominant influence.\"},{\"l\":\"D\",\"x\":\"Coeliac plexus via thoracic spinal nerves\",\"e\":\"The coeliac plexus does form a main junction for abdominal autonomic innervation; however. the parasympathetic innervation comes from the vagus nerve, not thoracic nerve routes:\"},{\"l\":\"E\",\"x\":\"Cranial nerve XI\",\"e\":\"The spinal accessory nerve innervates the trapezius muscles and does not have a parasympathetic element\"}]},{\"t\":51,\"p\":12,\"stem\":\"A 59-year-old builder is admitted after falling from a ladder. He has not sustained any significant injury however his pupils are dilated, he is tachycardic, very sweaty and he feels sick.\\n\\nWhat is the predominant post-ganglionic neurotransmitter in the sympathetic nervous system?\",\"lead\":\"What is the predominant post-ganglionic neurotransmitter in the sympathetic nervous system?\",\"ans\":\"B\",\"gen\":\"\",\"img\":null,\"opts\":[{\"l\":\"A\",\"x\":\"Acetylcholine\",\"e\":\"The neurotransmitter acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter in post-ganglionic sweat gland innervation, but noradrenaline forms the overwhelming majority because it is involved in all other sites.\"},{\"l\":\"B\",\"x\":\"Noradrenaline\",\"e\":\"Unlike pre-ganglionic neurotransmission, which uses acetylcholine (ACh), and sweat glands, which also use ACh in post-ganglionic");